{"version":1,"lang":"en","places":{"Q7296":{"t":"Africa's rooftop, a snowy island above the plains","s":"Kilimanjaro belongs to no range: it rises alone, a broad dark volcano swelling out of the savanna. Its summit, Uhuru Peak on the Kibo cone, is 5,895 m high — the top of Africa, yet still about five kilometres below you. Look for the white rim of ice on Kibo, shrinking year by year but still there, just three degrees south of the equator.","w":"A vast lone dome with a flat, white-streaked top and the jagged Mawenzi peak on its east flank, near the Kenya border.","f":["Kilimanjaro is built from three volcanic cones: Kibo, Mawenzi and Shira.","It is the highest free-standing mountain in the world, rising about 4,900 m above the plains at its base.","Its ice fields have shrunk by more than 80% since the early 20th century."],"q":{"q":"Which of Kilimanjaro's three volcanic cones holds its summit?","a":"Kibo","x":["Mawenzi","Shira"]}},"Q39231":{"t":"Japan's lone volcano, white-capped and nearly symmetrical","s":"On a clear day Fuji is unmistakable: one broad, even cone standing by itself west of Tokyo, its upper slopes white from autumn to spring. It last erupted in 1707. The summit, 3,776 m, sits roughly seven kilometres below you, and in summer the snow shrinks to thin streaks in the gullies, leaving a dark, ash-coloured mountain.","w":"A lone snow-topped cone about 100 km south-west of Tokyo, with small lakes strung along its northern foot.","f":["The five lakes around Fuji's northern foot formed where old lava flows dammed the valleys.","The 1707 eruption blew out a side crater on the south-east flank that breaks the cone's symmetry.","Fuji became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2013 — as a cultural site, not a natural one."],"q":{"q":"How many lakes make up the famous group at Fuji's northern foot?","a":"Five","x":["Three","Seven"]}},"Q513":{"t":"The world's highest point, only about 2 km below you","s":"Everest rarely looks tallest from the air: it hides among giant neighbours, a dark pyramid often trailing a plume of snow blown east by the jet stream. Its summit, 8,848 m, is only about two kilometres below cruising height. The rock at the very top is limestone that formed on an ancient sea floor and was lifted as India pushed into Asia.","w":"A dark three-sided pyramid, often with a snow plume, rising above the glaciers on the Nepal–Tibet border.","f":["Tibetans call it Chomolungma; in Nepal it is Sagarmatha.","Tenzing Norgay and Edmund Hillary first reached the summit on 29 May 1953.","The English name, adopted in 1865, honours George Everest, a British surveyor-general of India."],"q":{"q":"Who reached the summit of Everest with Tenzing Norgay in 1953?","a":"Edmund Hillary","x":["George Mallory","Reinhold Messner"]}},"Q43512":{"t":"Earth's second summit, a pyramid of the Karakoram","s":"The name K2 is just a surveyor's label — peak 2 on a Karakoram list drawn up in 1856, when no widely used local name was found. It is the second-highest mountain on Earth, 8,611 m, a steep, almost perfect pyramid ringed by rivers of ice. Look for a sharp summit at the head of a long glacier, far from any road or town.","w":"A lone sharp pyramid at the head of broad glacier tongues, on the border crest of the Karakoram.","f":["The Italians Lino Lacedelli and Achille Compagnoni made the first ascent in 1954.","The first winter ascent came only in January 2021, by a team of Nepali climbers.","Its pyramid rises more than 3,000 m above the glaciers at its foot."],"q":{"q":"What does the 'K' in K2 stand for?","a":"Karakoram","x":["Kashmir","Kunlun"]}},"Q1374":{"t":"The Alps' sharpest tooth, facing all four compass points","s":"Even from cruise height the Matterhorn looks carved: a hooked pyramid whose four faces point almost exactly north, south, east and west. It stands on the Swiss–Italian border above Zermatt, 4,478 m high and roughly six and a half kilometres below you. On a sunny morning its east face is lit while the north face stays in shadow, so the peak looks sliced in two.","w":"A lone, hooked rock pyramid among glaciers, west of the big Monte Rosa massif on the Swiss–Italian border.","f":["Edward Whymper's party made the first ascent on 14 July 1865.","Italians call it Monte Cervino; in French it is Mont Cervin.","A summit ridge about 100 m long links two tops, a Swiss one and an Italian one."],"q":{"q":"From which Swiss village is the Matterhorn most often climbed?","a":"Zermatt","x":["Grindelwald","Saas-Fee"]}},"ne-pk-1159105947":{"t":"The volcano that buried Pompeii, still watching Naples","s":"Look for a cone inside a broken ring: Vesuvius grew within the collapsed crater of an older volcano, Monte Somma, whose ridge wraps around its north side. Naples spreads right up to its lower slopes, a dense grey carpet along the bay. On a clear day the summit crater, 1,281 m high, shows as a dark hole. Vesuvius has been quiet since 1944, but it is watched around the clock.","w":"A dark two-humped volcano on the Bay of Naples, with the city spread along the shore to its north-west.","f":["In AD 79 its ash buried Pompeii and Herculaneum, preserving the Roman towns for archaeologists.","It is the only volcano on mainland Europe to have erupted in the last hundred years.","Its observatory, founded in 1841, is the oldest volcano observatory in the world."],"q":{"q":"Which Roman town, buried in AD 79, lies at the foot of Vesuvius?","a":"Pompeii","x":["Ostia","Paestum"]}},"Q583":{"t":"Western Europe's highest dome, white all year","s":"Mont Blanc is less a spike than a huge white shoulder: a broad dome of ice on a massif shared by France, Italy and Switzerland. At about 4,807 m it is the highest point of the Alps, yet still more than six kilometres below you. On its north side long glaciers pour down toward the valley of Chamonix; on the Italian side the walls drop steeply to Courmayeur.","w":"A broad, rounded white dome above deep valleys, with long glacier tongues reaching toward Chamonix on its north-west side.","f":["Jacques Balmat and Michel-Gabriel Paccard first reached the summit in 1786.","The summit is a cap of ice, and its height shifts by a metre or two from year to year.","The Mont Blanc road tunnel, 11.6 km long, runs beneath the massif between France and Italy."],"q":{"q":"In which year was Mont Blanc first climbed?","a":"1786","x":["1865","1741"]}},"ne-pk-1159107423":{"t":"Europe's restless giant, often trailing a plume","s":"Etna is the dark giant of eastern Sicily, a broad volcano whose summit, about 3,300 m high, often smokes. Its lower slopes are green with orchards and vineyards, the upper ones black with fresh lava, and in winter snow lies over both. Look for a thin plume drifting downwind: Etna erupts so often that its height changes every few years.","w":"A vast dark cone above Sicily's east coast, north of Catania, often with a steam plume and snow on top.","f":["Etna covers about 1,200 km², and its base is roughly 140 km around.","It is the highest active volcano in Europe outside the Caucasus.","Etna became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2013."],"q":{"q":"Which city lies at the southern foot of Etna?","a":"Catania","x":["Palermo","Messina"]}},"ne-gl-antarctic-ice-sheet":{"t":"Most of the world's ice, on one frozen continent","s":"If your route crosses Antarctica, the whiteness below is not snow on land but ice kilometres thick. The Antarctic Ice Sheet covers about 14 million km² and holds roughly 90% of all the ice on Earth. Its surface is so smooth and bright that the horizon can vanish; only nunataks — rocky peaks poking through the ice — break the white.","w":"An endless white surface with faint wind ripples; dark rock shows only near the coast and along mountain ranges.","f":["If it all melted, global sea level would rise by about 58 metres.","The ice is about two kilometres thick on average.","Its weight has pressed the bedrock beneath it down by hundreds of metres."],"q":{"q":"Roughly what share of Earth's ice lies in the Antarctic Ice Sheet?","a":"About 90%","x":["About 50%","About 70%"]}},"ne-gl-greenland-ice-sheet":{"t":"A 3-kilometre-thick lid on the world's largest island","s":"Crossing the North Atlantic, many flights pass over this white desert. The ice sheet covers about 80% of Greenland, some 1.7 million km², and rises into a dome more than 3,000 m high. Near the coast it breaks into crevassed outlet glaciers that snake down to fjords full of icebergs; in summer, bright blue meltwater lakes dot its western edge.","w":"Flat white expanse inland; at the edges, dark mountains, glacier tongues and fjords speckled with icebergs.","f":["If the whole ice sheet melted, sea level would rise by roughly 7 metres.","Its weight has pushed the bedrock in the centre of Greenland below sea level.","Ice cores drilled through it hold a climate record reaching back more than 100,000 years."],"q":{"q":"How much would sea level rise if Greenland's ice sheet melted?","a":"About 7 metres","x":["About 1 metre","About 60 metres"]}},"Q1001294":{"t":"The coldest high desert on the planet","s":"Around the South Pole the ice rises into a vast, gently sloping plateau, mostly around 3,000 m high and as flat as a frozen sea. It is a true desert: each year the snowfall adds up to only a few centimetres of water. Vostok Station, on this plateau, recorded −89.2 °C in 1983 — the lowest air temperature ever measured at the Earth's surface.","w":"A featureless white plain to the horizon, scored only by long wind-carved snow ridges called sastrugi.","f":["The South Pole itself lies on the plateau, about 2,835 m above sea level.","Antarctica's fierce coastal winds start here, as heavy cold air slides off the plateau toward the sea.","Its cold, dry, still air makes it one of the best places on Earth for telescopes."],"q":{"q":"What temperature did Vostok Station record in 1983?","a":"−89.2 °C","x":["−71.2 °C","−79.8 °C"]}},"Q165440":{"t":"The white mountain that once topped the world's list","s":"Dhaulagiri means 'white mountain' in Sanskrit, and from the air it earns the name: a massive snowy wall rising straight out of central Nepal. Its main summit, about 8,170 m, is the seventh-highest on Earth. Just east of it the Kali Gandaki river cuts through the Himalaya between Dhaulagiri and Annapurna, carving one of the deepest valleys on the planet.","w":"A huge snowy massif west of the deep Kali Gandaki gorge, with Annapurna's white ridges on the other side.","f":["After surveys in 1808 it was believed for some 30 years to be the highest mountain in the world.","A Swiss-Austrian expedition made the first ascent in 1960.","The Kali Gandaki gorge beside it was for centuries a trade route between Tibet and India."],"q":{"q":"Which river gorge separates Dhaulagiri from Annapurna?","a":"Kali Gandaki","x":["Arun","Trisuli"]}},"Q130736":{"t":"The 'naked mountain' at the western end of the Himalaya","s":"Nanga Parbat stands apart: the westernmost 8,000-metre peak of the Himalaya, with the Indus curving around its foot. Its name means 'naked mountain', perhaps for walls too steep to hold snow. The Rupal Face on its south side rises about 4,600 m from valley to summit, one of the tallest mountain walls on Earth. The top, 8,125 m, is less than three kilometres below you.","w":"An isolated giant massif south of a bend in the Indus, with a sheer southern wall and glaciers spilling north.","f":["Hermann Buhl made the first ascent in 1953, reaching the summit alone.","It is the ninth-highest mountain on Earth.","The Indus flows past its northern flank at around 1,100 m — seven kilometres below the summit."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Nanga Parbat mean?","a":"Naked mountain","x":["White mountain","Mother of snows"]}},"Q82019":{"t":"Five treasures of snow above Sikkim and Nepal","s":"Kangchenjunga is not one summit but a crowded crown of them, five main tops on a single massif. At 8,586 m it is the third-highest mountain on Earth, and until the 1850s it was thought to be the highest. Look for a broad white wall towering over the green hills of Darjeeling and Sikkim; at sunrise its snow turns pink long before the valleys wake.","w":"A massive white massif rising above green foothills, north of Darjeeling on the Nepal–India border.","f":["Its name is usually translated from Tibetan as 'Five Treasures of the Snow'.","The first climbers, in 1955, stopped just short of the top out of respect for local beliefs.","From the hill town of Darjeeling, about 75 km away, it fills the northern horizon."],"q":{"q":"Where does Kangchenjunga rank among the world's highest mountains?","a":"Third","x":["Second","Fifth"]}},"Q604846":{"t":"A red tableland of some of Earth's oldest rock","s":"For hours on a flight across Australia the ground barely changes: rust-red sand, pale salt lakes and dark scrub, a few hundred metres above sea level. This is the Western Plateau, spread over most of the western two-thirds of the continent. Its bedrock is among the oldest on Earth, and the deserts on top — the Gibson, the Great Sandy, the Great Victoria — are combed with long dune lines.","w":"Endless red and ochre ground streaked with parallel dunes and dotted with white salt lakes that shine like mirrors.","f":["Zircon crystals from the Jack Hills in Western Australia are about 4.4 billion years old, the oldest known pieces of Earth.","Its dunes often run in parallel lines for tens of kilometres, aligned with the prevailing winds.","Salt lakes such as Lake Carnegie hold water only after rare heavy rains."],"q":{"q":"What record do zircons from Western Australia's Jack Hills hold?","a":"Oldest known Earth minerals","x":["Largest gold nuggets","Most radioactive rocks"]}},"Q179842":{"t":"The roof of the world, averaging over 4,500 m","s":"Below you lies the highest large plateau on Earth, averaging more than 4,500 m and covering about 2.5 million km². From cruise it looks like another planet: brown and ochre plains, turquoise lakes with salt-white shores, snowy ranges ruled across it. Asia's great rivers — the Yangtze, Yellow, Mekong, Indus and Brahmaputra among them — begin up here.","w":"Bare brown highland with scattered turquoise lakes and white ridges; the Himalaya forms its sharp southern rim.","f":["It holds so much glacier ice that it is often called the Third Pole.","Lhasa, in the south of the plateau, lies at about 3,650 m.","The plateau is still rising as India keeps pushing north into Asia."],"q":{"q":"Which nickname does the Tibetan Plateau get for its glaciers?","a":"The Third Pole","x":["The White Continent","The Frozen Crown"]}},"ne-gl-flade-isblink":{"t":"Greenland's biggest ice cap that stands on its own","s":"Near Greenland's north-eastern tip a separate dome of ice sits apart from the main ice sheet: Flade Isblink, some 8,000 km² of white. Its Danish name means roughly 'flat iceblink' — the glow that ice casts on low clouds. The ice is up to about 600 m thick, and parts of its edge surge forward from time to time, pushing ice out to sea.","w":"A smooth, low white dome on a peninsula of north-east Greenland, cut off from the main ice sheet by bare land.","f":["It is the largest ice cap in Greenland that is not joined to the main ice sheet.","Station Nord, the only inhabited place in the area, lies just to its north-west.","The whole area is part of Northeast Greenland National Park, the largest national park in the world."],"q":{"q":"What does the word 'iceblink' describe?","a":"The glow of ice on clouds","x":["A crack in sea ice","A glacier's calving wall"]}},"Q163758":{"t":"The highest point of the frozen continent","s":"Vinson is the summit of Antarctica, 4,892 m, a long snowy massif in the Sentinel Range of the Ellsworth Mountains, some 1,300 km from the South Pole. US Navy aircraft spotted it in 1958, and climbers first reached the top in 1966. From above the range looks like a spine of dark rock and ice breaking out of the ice sheet, with glaciers flowing away on both sides.","w":"A long, dark rock-and-ice ridge rising sharply above the flat ice sheet in the Ellsworth Mountains.","f":["It is named after Carl Vinson, a US congressman who backed Antarctic exploration.","An American expedition made the first ascent in December 1966.","Climbers fly in to a blue-ice runway at Union Glacier to start their approach."],"q":{"q":"In which mountains does Vinson Massif rise?","a":"Ellsworth Mountains","x":["Transantarctic Mountains","Prince Charles Mountains"]}},"Q1537198":{"t":"Twin summits of ice above Ladakh's Suru valley","s":"Nun and its twin, Kun, form the Nun Kun massif, a knot of ice in the western Himalaya of Ladakh. Nun is the higher of the pair at 7,135 m, only about four kilometres below you. Between the two tops lies a snowy plateau some 4 km long, and from above it looks like a white saddle slung between two peaks. Glaciers pour off both sides toward the Suru valley.","w":"Two white summits joined by a snowy plateau, with long glaciers running down to a green valley at their foot.","f":["Nun was first climbed in 1953 by Claude Kogan and Pierre Vittoz of a French–Swiss expedition.","Kun, the lower twin, is a little over 7,000 m high.","The Parkachik Glacier flows off the massif almost down to the road in the Suru valley."],"q":{"q":"What is the name of Nun's twin summit?","a":"Kun","x":["Stok Kangri","Kang Yatse"]}},"Q811064":{"t":"The crown of the Karakoram's jagged western wall","s":"Batura Sar tops a long, serrated wall of ice in the far west of the Karakoram, the Batura Muztagh. At 7,795 m it is the highest point of that wall, yet it is little known outside climbing circles. Below it lies one of the longest glaciers outside the polar regions, the Batura: a grey-and-white river of ice that ends right beside the Karakoram Highway in the Hunza valley.","w":"A long, serrated ice ridge north-west of the Hunza valley, with a broad glacier sliding east toward the river.","f":["The Batura Glacier is about 57 km long.","Muztagh means 'ice mountain' in Turkic languages.","The Karakoram Highway, one of the highest paved roads in the world, passes the glacier's snout."],"q":{"q":"Which famous road passes the snout of the Batura Glacier?","a":"Karakoram Highway","x":["Pamir Highway","Grand Trunk Road"]}},"Q6654097":{"t":"The Kunlun's highest peak, lost in a cold desert","s":"This is one of the emptiest corners of Asia. Liushi Shan, also called the Kunlun Goddess, is the highest peak of the Kunlun Mountains at 7,167 m, rising from bleak high desert on the border of Tibet and Xinjiang. At cruise height it shows as a cluster of white ice domes and glaciers in a sea of brown, wind-scoured ridges.","w":"A cluster of white ice domes on the northern edge of the Tibetan Plateau, surrounded by bare brown ridges.","f":["A Japanese university team made the first ascent in 1986.","In Chinese it is also known as Kunlun Nüshen Feng, 'Kunlun Goddess Peak'.","The Keriya River rises in this part of the Kunlun, flows north and vanishes into the Taklamakan Desert."],"q":{"q":"Liushi Shan is the highest peak of which mountain system?","a":"Kunlun Mountains","x":["Tian Shan","Karakoram"]}},"Q1050238":{"t":"Where the Himalaya ends and a great river turns","s":"Namcha Barwa marks the eastern end of the Himalaya, beside one of the strangest river bends on Earth: the Yarlung Tsangpo, flowing east across Tibet, swings around the mountain and plunges south through a gorge far deeper than the Grand Canyon. The peak is 7,782 m high, a steep white pyramid above dark, forested valleys, often wrapped in monsoon cloud.","w":"A steep white peak where the brown Tibetan plateau gives way to green, cloudy gorges; a river loops around its foot.","f":["It was the highest unclimbed peak in the world until a Chinese–Japanese team reached the top in 1992.","Gyala Peri, 7,294 m, rises only about 20 km away on the other side of the gorge.","Downstream from the gorge, the Yarlung Tsangpo becomes the Brahmaputra."],"q":{"q":"Which river bends sharply around Namcha Barwa?","a":"Yarlung Tsangpo","x":["Salween","Mekong"]}},"Q26794321":{"t":"A goddess inside a fortress of peaks","s":"Nanda Devi rises inside a natural fortress: a ring of mountains, broken only by a narrow gorge, encloses the high glacial basin known as the Sanctuary. The double-topped peak, 7,817 m, is the highest mountain lying entirely within India and is revered as the goddess Nanda. The ring itself is the thing to spot: a near-circle of white summits around a bowl of ice.","w":"A double-topped white peak inside a near-circle of snowy summits, in the Himalaya west of the Nepal border.","f":["Bill Tilman and Noel Odell made the first ascent in 1936.","Eric Shipton and Tilman were the first to force a way up the Rishi Ganga gorge into the Sanctuary, in 1934.","Nanda Devi National Park and the nearby Valley of Flowers form a UNESCO World Heritage site."],"q":{"q":"What is the ring-walled basin around Nanda Devi called?","a":"The Sanctuary","x":["The Amphitheatre","The Citadel"]}},"Q41413":{"t":"The Pamirs' high point, a peak of three names","s":"For decades this was the highest point of the Soviet Union, first as Stalin Peak, then as Communism Peak. Since 1998 it has carried the name of Ismoil Somoni, a Samanid ruler of the 9th and 10th centuries. The summit, 7,495 m, crowns a massive white wall in the heart of the Pamirs, close to the Fedchenko Glacier, one of the great ice rivers of Asia.","w":"A massive white peak at a junction of ridges, west of the long, straight Fedchenko Glacier in the high Pamirs.","f":["Yevgeny Abalakov made the first ascent in 1933.","The Fedchenko Glacier nearby is about 77 km long, the longest outside the polar regions.","Tajikistan's currency, the somoni, is named after the same ruler."],"q":{"q":"What was the peak called from 1962 to 1998?","a":"Communism Peak","x":["Lenin Peak","Stalin Peak"]}},"Q1537157":{"t":"The easternmost 7,000-metre giant, above Sichuan","s":"Gongga, also known as Minya Konka, stands where the Tibetan highlands drop toward the Sichuan Basin. At 7,556 m it is the highest peak in Sichuan and the easternmost 7,000-metre summit on Earth. Look for a sharp white pyramid ringed by glaciers, with the deep Dadu River valley to the east — more than six kilometres of relief in a short distance.","w":"A sharp white pyramid with a skirt of glaciers, west of the deep Dadu River gorge in western Sichuan.","f":["An American team made the first ascent in 1932.","The Hailuogou Glacier on its eastern side reaches down into forest below 3,000 m.","Gongga is the highest point of the Daxue Shan, the 'Great Snowy Mountains'."],"q":{"q":"Which glacier descends from Gongga's east side into forest?","a":"Hailuogou","x":["Baltoro","Rongbuk"]}},"Q6362901":{"t":"A cloud-wrapped giant of the eastern Himalaya","s":"East of Bhutan the Himalaya grows wilder and wetter, and Kangto is one of its tallest summits, just over 7,000 m. It sits on the main crest, where the dry Tibetan uplands meet steep valleys draining south toward the Brahmaputra plains of Assam. Thick forest climbs its lower flanks, and monsoon cloud hides the peak for much of the summer, so a clear view is a rare prize.","w":"A white summit on the Himalayan crest north of the Brahmaputra plains, between dry brown highlands and forested valleys.","f":["Kangto remained unclimbed for decades; the first recorded ascent came only in 2025.","Its southern valleys drain toward the Kameng River, a tributary of the Brahmaputra.","The eastern Himalaya receives far more monsoon rain than the ranges around Everest."],"q":{"q":"Toward which great river do Kangto's southern valleys drain?","a":"Brahmaputra","x":["Ganges","Irrawaddy"]}},"Q1109058":{"t":"A mountain god guarding a turquoise lake","s":"Nyenchen Tanglha is the highest peak of a long range running across central Tibet north-west of Lhasa. Look for its white summit, 7,162 m, just south-east of Namtso, a huge salt lake of startling turquoise. In Tibetan tradition the mountain is a protector deity, and he and the lake are often spoken of as a couple.","w":"A white ridge just south-east of a large turquoise lake, Namtso, on the brown plateau north-west of Lhasa.","f":["Namtso, at its foot, lies about 4,700 m above sea level.","The lake covers nearly 2,000 km², one of the largest in Tibet.","The railway and highway from Qinghai to Lhasa run along the range's southern foot."],"q":{"q":"Which lake lies at the foot of Nyenchen Tanglha?","a":"Namtso","x":["Manasarovar","Yamdrok"]}},"Q1560829":{"t":"The 'great ice mountain' of Tibet's empty north","s":"Ulugh Muztagh means 'great ice mountain' in Uyghur, and it rules one of the loneliest places on Earth: the high, cold uplands on the border of Tibet and Xinjiang, far from any town. The summit, 6,973 m, crowns a compact knot of ice-covered ridges. Around it the ground is pale brown desert, dotted with salt lakes that flash white and blue.","w":"A compact cluster of white summits and glaciers rising from pale brown desert and scattered salt lakes.","f":["After his journey of 1895, the explorer George Littledale guessed it was over 7,700 m high.","A Chinese–American expedition made the first ascent in 1985 and measured its true height.","Its meltwater drains into closed basins and never reaches the sea."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Ulugh Muztagh mean?","a":"Great ice mountain","x":["White goddess","Father of rivers"]}},"Q229107":{"t":"A sacred rock pyramid that no one has climbed","s":"Kailash looks almost designed: a lone, four-sided dome of dark rock streaked with snow, standing apart in the far west of Tibet. It is sacred to Hindus, Buddhists, Jains and followers of Bon, and climbing it is not permitted. Pilgrims instead walk around it, a circuit of about 52 km. Just to the south, two big lakes lie side by side: Manasarovar and Rakshastal.","w":"A lone snow-streaked rock dome north of two large lakes lying side by side on the brown plateau.","f":["The Indus, Sutlej, Brahmaputra and Karnali all rise within about 100 km of it.","Lake Manasarovar, at about 4,590 m, is fresh, while its neighbour Rakshastal is salty.","The pilgrim circuit crosses a pass at about 5,600 m."],"q":{"q":"Which sacred freshwater lake lies just south of Mount Kailash?","a":"Manasarovar","x":["Namtso","Yamdrok"]}},"Q207132":{"t":"King of the Hindu Kush, above the Chitral valley","s":"Tirich Mir is the highest mountain of the Hindu Kush, 7,708 m, and it rules the valley of Chitral in northern Pakistan. From cruise height it looks like a great snowy tent with long ridges, standing north of the green thread of the Chitral river. Beyond it the land runs north toward Afghanistan's Wakhan Corridor, a narrow strip squeezed between high ranges.","w":"A massive snowy peak with long ridges, north of the town of Chitral and close to the Afghan border.","f":["A Norwegian expedition made the first ascent in 1950.","The Hindu Kush stretches about 800 km from northern Pakistan into central Afghanistan.","The town of Chitral lies about 40 km to its south."],"q":{"q":"Tirich Mir is the highest peak of which range?","a":"Hindu Kush","x":["Karakoram","Pamir"]}},"Q332762":{"t":"The northernmost 7,000-metre peak on Earth","s":"Jengish Chokusu, long known as Victory Peak, is the highest point of the Tian Shan, 7,439 m, on the border of Kyrgyzstan and China. No mountain over 7,000 m stands farther north. Look for a long, heavy ridge of ice rather than a spike; just to its north rises the sharper pyramid of Khan Tengri, whose marble summit glows pink at sunset.","w":"A long, heavy white ridge on the Kyrgyz–Chinese border, with the sharp pyramid of Khan Tengri about 16 km north.","f":["It was recognised as the Tian Shan's highest peak only in the 1940s; Khan Tengri had long been thought higher.","The South Inylchek Glacier at its foot is about 60 km long.","Jengish Chokusu is Kyrgyz for 'Victory Peak'."],"q":{"q":"Which sharp peak rises just north of Jengish Chokusu?","a":"Khan Tengri","x":["Lenin Peak","Muztagh Ata"]}},"Q271700":{"t":"Afghanistan's highest summit, on the Hindu Kush crest","s":"Noshaq is the highest point of Afghanistan and the second-highest mountain of the Hindu Kush, 7,492 m, standing right on the border with Pakistan. It appears as a broad white massif with several summits in a row, a short way north of Tirich Mir. Its northern glaciers drain into the Wakhan valley, where the rivers gather that later form the Amu Darya.","w":"A broad white massif with a row of summits on the Afghan–Pakistani border, just north of Tirich Mir.","f":["A Japanese expedition made the first ascent in 1960.","It stands just south of the Wakhan Corridor, a thin arm of Afghanistan reaching east to China."],"q":{"q":"Noshaq is the highest point of which country?","a":"Afghanistan","x":["Tajikistan","Iran"]}},"Q8536063":{"t":"The top of the Transhimalaya, Tibet's second wall","s":"North of the great Himalayan wall runs a second, lesser-known chain: the Transhimalaya, or Gangdise. Its highest summit is Lunpo Gangri, 7,095 m, an ice-capped massif in south-western Tibet. Look for a line of white peaks rising from brown uplands, with the upper Yarlung Tsangpo — the future Brahmaputra — winding through a broad valley to the south.","w":"An ice-capped massif north of the broad Yarlung Tsangpo valley, part of a white chain parallel to the Himalaya.","f":["Mount Kailash belongs to the same Gangdise range, about 300 km to the west.","The Swedish explorer Sven Hedin named the Transhimalaya after travelling through it in 1906–1908."],"q":{"q":"Which explorer gave the Transhimalaya its name?","a":"Sven Hedin","x":["Francis Younghusband","Nikolai Przhevalsky"]}},"ne-pk-1159105819":{"t":"The ice giant above the Karakoram Highway","s":"Kongur Tagh, 7,649 m, is a massive dome of ice at the western edge of the Tarim Basin, not far from Kashgar. Its neighbour to the south, Muztagh Ata, is rounder and more famous, but Kongur is higher. Between the two the Karakoram Highway runs past Karakul Lake, a blue mirror at about 3,600 m that reflects both peaks on calm days.","w":"A huge ice massif east of the Karakoram Highway, with the rounder Muztagh Ata to the south and a small blue lake between.","f":["A British team including Chris Bonington made the first ascent in 1981.","Kashgar, the old Silk Road oasis, lies roughly 100 km to the north-east.","Its summit rises more than 6,000 m above the floor of the Tarim Basin."],"q":{"q":"Which famous peak stands just south of Kongur Tagh?","a":"Muztagh Ata","x":["Khan Tengri","Tirich Mir"]}},"Q1322521":{"t":"The northern sentinel of Tibet's great gorge","s":"Gyala Peri and Namcha Barwa face each other across the Yarlung Tsangpo, only about 20 km apart. Between them the river squeezes through a gorge of extraordinary depth before turning south toward India. Gyala Peri, 7,294 m, is the northern guard: a white dome above slopes so wet and steep that dense forest climbs far up its sides.","w":"A white dome on the north side of a tight river bend, facing the higher Namcha Barwa across a dark gorge.","f":["Between Gyala Peri and Namcha Barwa the gorge is more than 5,000 m deep.","The botanist Frank Kingdon-Ward explored the gorge in 1924 in search of new plants."],"q":{"q":"Which peak faces Gyala Peri across the Yarlung Tsangpo gorge?","a":"Namcha Barwa","x":["Kangchenjunga","Nyenchen Tanglha"]}},"Q219986":{"t":"The highest mountain no one has climbed","s":"Gangkhar Puensum, 7,570 m, is the highest point of Bhutan and very likely the highest unclimbed mountain on Earth. Attempts in the 1980s failed, and since 1994 Bhutan has banned climbing peaks over 6,000 m out of respect for the belief that mountains are home to spirits. Look for a broad white summit on the northern border, above deep, forested valleys.","w":"A broad white summit on Bhutan's northern border with Tibet, above deep, green, forested valleys.","f":["Its name is usually translated as 'White Peak of the Three Spiritual Brothers'.","Since 2003 Bhutan has banned mountaineering altogether.","In 1999 a Japanese team climbed a neighbouring summit, Liankang Kangri, from the Tibetan side."],"q":{"q":"Since which year has Bhutan banned climbing peaks over 6,000 m?","a":"1994","x":["1974","2010"]}},"Q43105":{"t":"A double-humped volcano crowning the Caucasus","s":"Elbrus stands a little north of the main Caucasus ridge, and from the air its shape gives it away: two rounded white domes, the remains of a dormant volcano, joined by a saddle. The western summit, 5,642 m, is the highest point in Russia and is often counted as the top of Europe. Its ice cap feeds more than twenty glaciers that radiate outward like the spokes of a wheel.","w":"Two rounded snow domes side by side, standing just north of the jagged main Caucasus ridge.","f":["The eastern summit, 5,621 m, is only about 20 m lower than the western one.","Its last eruption is thought to have happened around AD 50.","Cable cars carry visitors from the Baksan valley up to about 3,800 m on its southern slope."],"q":{"q":"How many main summits does Elbrus have?","a":"Two","x":["One","Three"]}},"Q130018":{"t":"North America's summit, rising from near sea level","s":"Denali is the highest mountain in North America, about 6,200 m, and one of the bulkiest on Earth. It rises from lowlands only about 600 m above sea level, so its climb from base to summit — some 5,500 m — is greater than Everest's. Look for a huge white mass with long glaciers flowing out of it, dwarfing the rest of the Alaska Range.","w":"An enormous white massif towering over the Alaska Range, about 210 km north of Anchorage.","f":["Its name means 'the high one' in the Koyukon Athabaskan language.","An expedition led by Hudson Stuck and Harry Karstens made the first ascent in 1913.","The Kahiltna Glacier on its south side is about 70 km long, the longest in the Alaska Range."],"q":{"q":"What does 'Denali' mean in the Koyukon language?","a":"The high one","x":["The white one","Father of rivers"]}},"Q235539":{"t":"The Lower 48's highest summit, above a desert valley","s":"Mount Whitney is the highest point of the contiguous United States, 4,421 m, on the sharp eastern edge of the Sierra Nevada. Its granite wall drops steeply to the dry Owens Valley, more than three kilometres below. Only about 135 km to the south-east lies Badwater Basin in Death Valley, the lowest point in North America — the continent's high and low points almost within sight of each other.","w":"A saw-toothed granite crest above the brown Owens Valley, directly west of the small town of Lone Pine.","f":["Badwater Basin, 86 m below sea level, lies only about 135 km away.","Three fishermen from Lone Pine first reached the summit in 1873.","It is named after Josiah Whitney, head of the California Geological Survey."],"q":{"q":"Which low point lies about 135 km from Mount Whitney?","a":"Badwater Basin","x":["Salton Sea","Great Salt Lake"]}},"Q178167":{"t":"Australia's rooftop, a gentle granite hump","s":"Mount Kosciuszko is the highest mountain on mainland Australia, yet at 2,228 m it is a rounded summit that walkers reach from the top of a chairlift. From above, the Snowy Mountains look like a rumpled plateau, white for a few months each winter and grey-green the rest of the year. Look for the long blue arms of Lake Jindabyne and other reservoirs winding through the valleys below.","w":"A rounded, often snowy plateau south-west of Canberra, with long blue reservoirs winding through the valleys.","f":["The explorer Paweł Strzelecki named it in 1840 after the Polish patriot Tadeusz Kościuszko.","The Snowy Mountains Scheme, built from 1949 to 1974, sends the mountains' water through tunnels to power stations and farms.","Mainland Australia's only glacial lakes, including Blue Lake, lie near its summit."],"q":{"q":"Who was Mount Kosciuszko named after?","a":"A Polish patriot","x":["A British governor","A Scottish explorer"]}},"Q39739":{"t":"The highest peak on Earth outside Asia","s":"Aconcagua is the tallest mountain outside Asia, 6,959 m, and the summit of both the Western and Southern hemispheres. It rises in Argentina just east of the Chilean border, a huge brown-and-white massif rather than a slender spike. On flights between Santiago and Mendoza it often looms off the wing, barely four kilometres below you.","w":"A massive white-topped peak above the Andes' dry brown ridges, just north of the route between Santiago and Mendoza.","f":["Matthias Zurbriggen made the first recorded ascent in 1897.","Its normal route needs no technical climbing, only stamina and careful acclimatisation.","The name is often traced to Quechua words meaning 'stone sentinel', though its origin is debated."],"q":{"q":"Aconcagua is the highest peak outside which continent?","a":"Asia","x":["Africa","Europe"]}},"ne-pk-1159108759":{"t":"A lonely summit above Venezuela's rainforest","s":"Deep in Venezuela's Amazonas state, far from any road, Cerro La Raya rises to about 2,000 m above an unbroken green sea of forest. It belongs to the Guiana Highlands, a region of ancient sandstone mountains and flat-topped tepuis. Look for dark ridges poking through the canopy, often wrapped in afternoon cloud, with brown rivers winding away toward the Orinoco.","w":"Forested ridges and cloud-capped heights rising from unbroken rainforest, with brown rivers winding west toward the Orinoco.","f":["Venezuela's Amazonas state is larger than many European countries but very thinly populated.","In the same state the Casiquiare, a natural channel, links the Orinoco and Amazon river systems.","Nearby tepuis such as Cerro Autana have sheer walls rising straight out of the forest."],"q":{"q":"Which natural channel in Venezuela's Amazonas links the Orinoco and Amazon?","a":"Casiquiare","x":["Ventuari","Caroní"]}},"Q1108926":{"t":"Copper country on the roof of central Africa","s":"South-eastern DR Congo rises into the Katanga Plateau: high savanna and open woodland mostly over 1,000 m, crossed by the divide between Congo and Zambezi waters. Beneath it lie some of the richest copper and cobalt deposits on Earth. Look for enormous open-pit mines and tailings ponds — grey and turquoise scars — around the cities of Lubumbashi and Kolwezi.","w":"Green-brown savanna and woodland dotted with grey open-pit mines and bright turquoise ponds near Kolwezi and Lubumbashi.","f":["DR Congo produces around 70% of the world's cobalt, most of it from this region.","The Lualaba, the upper course of the Congo River, rises on the plateau.","The copper belt continues south across the border into Zambia's Copperbelt."],"q":{"q":"Which battery metal is mined heavily on the Katanga Plateau?","a":"Cobalt","x":["Nickel","Platinum"]}},"Q208807":{"t":"Tabletop mountains rising from the rainforest","s":"Across northern South America, sheer-sided table mountains called tepuis rise from the rainforest like islands, their flat tops often lost in cloud. They are carved from sandstone laid down about 1.7 billion years ago, among the oldest sedimentary rocks on the continent. Look for dark, cliff-edged blocks above the green, and in the rainy season thin white threads of waterfalls dropping from their rims.","w":"Flat-topped, cliff-edged blocks of dark rock standing above unbroken rainforest, often capped with cloud.","f":["Angel Falls, dropping 979 m from Auyán-tepui, is the highest uninterrupted waterfall in the world.","Mount Roraima, about 2,810 m, marks the meeting point of Venezuela, Brazil and Guyana.","Tepui is a Pemón word usually translated as 'house of the gods'."],"q":{"q":"Which record-breaking waterfall pours off a tepui here?","a":"Angel Falls","x":["Victoria Falls","Iguazu Falls"]}},"Q118899":{"t":"The old stone heart of Brazil","s":"Much of eastern and central Brazil is a vast upland of ancient rock, worn into long flat-topped plateaus called chapadas with steep edges. Brasília was built on it in the late 1950s, at over 1,000 m. From the air look for red-earth tablelands, cerrado savanna in olive and gold, and huge green circles of irrigated farmland laid out like coins on a table.","w":"Flat-topped red tablelands with steep edges, olive-green savanna and neat green circles of centre-pivot irrigation.","f":["The São Francisco, the longest river entirely within Brazil, flows north across the highlands for much of its course.","Brasília was inaugurated as the capital in 1960.","The cerrado savanna covering much of it is one of the most species-rich savannas on Earth."],"q":{"q":"Which is the longest river flowing entirely within Brazil?","a":"São Francisco","x":["Paraná","Tocantins"]}},"Q155197":{"t":"Taiga, rivers and old lava to the horizon","s":"Between the Yenisei and the Lena lies a vast raised upland, mostly 500 to 700 m high and cut by deep river valleys. Much of it is built from the Siberian Traps, lava that poured out about 250 million years ago. From cruise height it is endless larch taiga: dark green in summer, gold in autumn, and white under snow for most of the year.","w":"Endless dark taiga cut by winding rivers in deep valleys, with flat-topped uplands and, in autumn, golden larch.","f":["The Siberian Traps eruptions are linked to the greatest mass extinction in Earth's history, about 252 million years ago.","In 1908 the Tunguska explosion flattened some 2,000 km² of forest on the plateau.","Permafrost underlies nearly all of it, in places hundreds of metres deep."],"q":{"q":"Which 1908 event flattened forest on the Central Siberian Plateau?","a":"The Tunguska explosion","x":["The Sikhote-Alin meteorite","The Chelyabinsk meteor"]}},"Q560198":{"t":"Grassland under a huge sky, a kilometre up","s":"The Mongolian Plateau spreads across Mongolia and northern China at around 1,000 to 1,500 m: rolling steppe, bare hills and, to the south, the stony Gobi. Few places look so open from the air. Roads are faint tracks, rivers wander freely, and lakes shine like scattered coins. Ulaanbaatar, on the plateau at about 1,350 m, is the coldest national capital on Earth.","w":"Rolling green-brown steppe with few roads, meandering rivers and, to the south, pale stony desert.","f":["Ulaanbaatar has the lowest average temperature of any national capital.","Lake Khövsgöl, in the north, holds about 70% of Mongolia's fresh water.","At the Flaming Cliffs in the Gobi, scientists found the first recognised dinosaur egg nests in 1923."],"q":{"q":"Which is the coldest national capital, on this plateau?","a":"Ulaanbaatar","x":["Astana","Moscow"]}},"Q169966":{"t":"India's tilted tableland of ancient lava","s":"The Deccan fills most of southern India, a triangular plateau raised along its western edge by the Western Ghats and tilted toward the east. So its great rivers — the Godavari, Krishna and Kaveri — rise near the Arabian Sea yet flow right across to the Bay of Bengal. Much of the north-west is built of Deccan Traps basalt, poured out as lava about 66 million years ago.","w":"A dry brown tableland of flat-topped hills, reservoirs and rivers draining east; much greener in the monsoon months.","f":["Its name comes from the Sanskrit word dakshina, 'south'.","Hyderabad and Bengaluru both stand on the plateau, at about 500 and 900 m.","The Deccan Traps eruptions took place around the time the dinosaurs died out."],"q":{"q":"Where do most of the Deccan's great rivers flow?","a":"East to the Bay of Bengal","x":["West to the Arabian Sea","North to the Ganges"]}},"Q1908889":{"t":"Where the Amazon forest meets the fields","s":"Mato Grosso means 'thick bush' in Portuguese, and this plateau sits in the heart of Brazil, where Amazon rainforest gives way to cerrado savanna. From the air it is a patchwork: huge rectangles of soy and maize, strips of forest along the rivers, and red dirt roads ruled straight for kilometres. Its rivers split two ways, north to the Amazon and south toward the Pantanal.","w":"A vast chessboard of green and tan fields with ruler-straight roads, cut by dark lines of forest along rivers.","f":["The Xingu and the rivers that form the Tapajós, big Amazon tributaries, rise on this plateau.","The Xingu Indigenous Park, founded in 1961, shows from above as a great block of forest ringed by farmland.","Mato Grosso state is Brazil's leading soybean producer."],"q":{"q":"What does 'Mato Grosso' mean in Portuguese?","a":"Thick bush","x":["Great river","High plain"]}},"Q7680307":{"t":"A snowy ice dome above the Ross Sea coast","s":"Talos Dome is a broad, gentle summit of the East Antarctic ice, about 2,300 m high, inland from the coast of northern Victoria Land. Unlike the bone-dry domes of the deep interior, it catches a good deal of snow from storms off the Ross Sea and the Southern Ocean. That leaves thick, readable yearly layers in its ice, and scientists have drilled deep into them.","w":"A barely noticeable swell in the white ice sheet, with the dark peaks of Victoria Land between it and the coast.","f":["A European ice-coring project, TALDICE, drilled more than 1,600 m into the dome in the 2000s.","Its ice core holds a climate record of roughly 250,000 years."],"q":{"q":"Which sea lies toward the coast from Talos Dome?","a":"Ross Sea","x":["Weddell Sea","Amundsen Sea"]}},"Q470049":{"t":"An icy upland named on a 1939 flight","s":"American Highland is a high, ice-covered upland in East Antarctica, beside the vast valley of the Lambert Glacier. The American explorer Lincoln Ellsworth flew over it in 1939 and gave it its name. Seen from the window it is a smooth white slope crossed by faint ridges, falling toward the giant ice stream of the Lambert and the flat Amery Ice Shelf beyond.","w":"A smooth, faintly ridged white upland sloping toward a vast glacier valley and the flat Amery Ice Shelf.","f":["The Lambert Glacier nearby is one of the largest glaciers on Earth, several hundred kilometres long.","Ellsworth's 1939 flight took off from his expedition ship, the Wyatt Earp.","The Amery Ice Shelf, fed by the Lambert, floats at the head of Prydz Bay."],"q":{"q":"Who named the American Highland after a flight in 1939?","a":"Lincoln Ellsworth","x":["Richard Byrd","Hubert Wilkins"]}},"Q5881339":{"t":"Named for the pilot of the first flight across Antarctica","s":"This broad ice plateau in West Antarctica carries the name of Herbert Hollick-Kenyon, who in 1935 flew Lincoln Ellsworth across the continent for the first time. The crossing took about two weeks, because they had to land on the open ice several times to wait out storms. Below you is an endless, gently rolling white expanse with hardly a rock in sight.","w":"A featureless, gently rolling white plateau between the Ellsworth Mountains and the ranges of Marie Byrd Land.","f":["The 1935 flight ran out of fuel short of the Bay of Whales, and the two men walked the last stretch.","Ellsworth named the plateau after his pilot."],"q":{"q":"What did Herbert Hollick-Kenyon do in 1935?","a":"Flew across Antarctica","x":["Reached the South Pole","Mapped the Ross Sea"]}},"Q370807":{"t":"A white upland named after an expedition backer","s":"The Rockefeller Plateau is a vast, ice-buried upland in West Antarctica, inland from the Ross Ice Shelf. Richard Byrd's expedition flew over it in 1934 and named it after John D. Rockefeller Jr., one of Byrd's sponsors. From above there is only smooth white ice to the horizon; the bedrock lies well over a kilometre down, much of it below sea level.","w":"Smooth white ice to the horizon, east of the flat Ross Ice Shelf, with no rock visible at all.","f":["Much of the bedrock beneath West Antarctica's ice lies below sea level.","Byrd's base, Little America, stood on the Ross Ice Shelf to the west."],"q":{"q":"Whose expedition discovered and named the Rockefeller Plateau?","a":"Richard Byrd's","x":["Roald Amundsen's","Ernest Shackleton's"]}},"Q3045552":{"t":"A year-round outpost on the ice, 3,200 m up","s":"Dome C is one of the high summits of the East Antarctic ice, about 3,200 m above sea level, where France and Italy run Concordia, a station staffed all year. Winter temperatures here fall below −80 °C, and for months the sun never rises. An ice core drilled at Dome C holds a record of Earth's climate reaching back some 800,000 years.","w":"A flat white summit of the ice sheet with nothing to mark it — the station is far too small to see.","f":["The European EPICA project drilled 3,270 m of ice here, spanning about 800,000 years.","Space agencies study Concordia's winter crews to learn how people cope with isolation on long missions.","In 2025 the Beyond EPICA project reached ice over a million years old at nearby Little Dome C."],"q":{"q":"Which two countries run Concordia Station at Dome C?","a":"France and Italy","x":["Germany and Norway","UK and Belgium"]}},"Q593197":{"t":"The highest summit of Antarctica's ice","s":"Dome A is the highest point of the Antarctic ice sheet, about 4,090 m above sea level, deep in East Antarctica. Under some three kilometres of ice lie the Gamburtsev Mountains, a range roughly the size of the Alps that no one has ever seen. China set up its Kunlun Station here in 2009, and satellites have found some of Earth's coldest surface temperatures nearby.","w":"An utterly flat white horizon; the summit of the ice rises so gently that you cannot pick it out.","f":["Soviet scientists discovered the buried Gamburtsev Mountains in 1958 with seismic soundings.","Satellite readings on the ice ridge near Dome A have dropped below −90 °C.","A Chinese traverse team first reached Dome A in 2005."],"q":{"q":"What lies hidden beneath the ice at Dome A?","a":"The Gamburtsev Mountains","x":["Lake Vostok","The Wilkes Basin"]}},"Q2007598":{"t":"Japan's station on a summit of the ice","s":"Dome Fuji, also called Dome F, is a high summit of the East Antarctic ice sheet, about 3,800 m up, far inland in Queen Maud Land. Japan opened a research station here in 1995 and drilled deep into the ice beneath it; the cores reach back roughly 700,000 years. Up here the air is so cold and still that temperatures below −79 °C have been recorded.","w":"A broad white summit of the ice sheet, featureless to the horizon, far inland from the coast of Queen Maud Land.","f":["Japanese expeditions named the dome after Mount Fuji.","Only a few centimetres of water a year fall here as snow, making it one of the driest places on Earth.","The ice ridge between Dome Fuji and Dome A holds some of the coldest surface temperatures ever measured."],"q":{"q":"Which country runs the research station at Dome Fuji?","a":"Japan","x":["China","Norway"]}},"ne-gl-academy-of-sciences-ice-cap":{"t":"Russia's largest ice cap, a round lid on an Arctic island","s":"Near the top of Severnaya Zemlya, an almost perfectly round dome of ice covers most of Komsomolets Island. The Academy of Sciences Ice Cap is about 80 km across and some 5,500 km² in area, the largest single ice mass in Russia. It looks like a white lid dropped on a dark sea, its edges crumbling into ice cliffs and drifting floes.","w":"A round, smooth white dome filling most of an island, with ice cliffs along its edges and sea ice around.","f":["Severnaya Zemlya was one of the last archipelagos on Earth to be discovered, in 1913.","Georgy Ushakov and Nikolay Urvantsev first mapped the islands in 1930–1932, travelling by dog sled.","The ice is hundreds of metres thick at the centre of the dome."],"q":{"q":"On which island does the Academy of Sciences Ice Cap lie?","a":"Komsomolets Island","x":["Wrangel Island","Kotelny Island"]}},"ne-gl-agassiz-ice-cap":{"t":"An Arctic ice cap named for the Ice Age's discoverer","s":"On northern Ellesmere Island, Canada's northernmost, the Agassiz Ice Cap spreads over the high interior like poured icing. It carries the name of Louis Agassiz, the Swiss-born scientist who in the 1830s argued that great ice sheets had once covered much of the northern world. Look for a smooth white dome cut at its edges by dark rock ridges and glacier tongues sliding toward the fjords.","w":"A smooth white dome over Ellesmere's high interior, fringed by dark rock ridges and glacier tongues heading for the fjords.","f":["Ice cores drilled here since the 1970s record Arctic temperatures over thousands of years.","Ellesmere Island is the tenth-largest island in the world."],"q":{"q":"Louis Agassiz is famous for which idea?","a":"Past ice ages","x":["Continental drift","Natural selection"]}},"ne-gl-asgardfonna":{"t":"An ice plateau named after the home of the Norse gods","s":"In the north-east of Spitsbergen, between two long fjords, lies Åsgardfonna, a plateau of ice some 1,200 km² in area. Its name comes from Åsgard, the home of the gods in Norse myth, and neighbouring ice fields borrow names from the same stories. Out of the window it reads as a high white tableland, with dark ridges and valley glaciers spilling off its sides.","w":"A high white ice plateau between two long fjords, Wijdefjorden to the west and Lomfjorden to the east.","f":["Nearby ice fields are called Valhallfonna and Balderfonna, also after Norse mythology.","Wijdefjorden, on its western side, is Svalbard's longest fjord, over 100 km long."],"q":{"q":"In Norse myth, what is Åsgard?","a":"The home of the gods","x":["The realm of the dead","The world of the giants"]}},"ne-gl-barnes-ice-cap":{"t":"The last surviving piece of the great Ice Age sheet","s":"In the middle of Baffin Island lies a smooth white dome some 150 km long, and it is older than it looks. The Barnes Ice Cap is the last remnant of the Laurentide Ice Sheet, which buried most of Canada during the last ice age, and its deepest layers still hold ice from that time. Around it the land is brown tundra scattered with thousands of lakes.","w":"A long, smooth white dome in the centre of Baffin Island, surrounded by brown tundra freckled with lakes.","f":["It covers about 6,000 km².","Scientists expect it to disappear within a few centuries as the Arctic warms.","Baffin Island is the fifth-largest island in the world."],"q":{"q":"The Barnes Ice Cap is a remnant of which ice sheet?","a":"The Laurentide Ice Sheet","x":["The Cordilleran Ice Sheet","The Greenland Ice Sheet"]}},"ne-gl-bering-glacier":{"t":"North America's longest glacier, a river of ice","s":"Flying along the Gulf of Alaska, look for a vast lobe of ice spreading onto the coastal plain: the Bering Glacier, roughly 190 km long, the longest and largest glacier in North America. It flows from the Bagley Icefield in the Chugach and Saint Elias mountains. Dark stripes of rock debris ripple across its surface, bent into waves by past surges.","w":"A huge ice lobe spilling onto the coastal plain east of the Copper River delta, streaked with dark, wavy lines.","f":["It is named after the explorer Vitus Bering and has surged forward several times in the past century.","At its snout lies Vitus Lake, crowded with floating icebergs."],"q":{"q":"Which icefield feeds the Bering Glacier?","a":"Bagley Icefield","x":["Harding Icefield","Juneau Icefield"]}},"ne-gl-brasvellbreen":{"t":"The ice wall that leapt 20 km in two years","s":"On Nordaustlandet, Svalbard's second-largest island, the Austfonna ice cap ends in a long, low cliff, and Bråsvellbreen is its biggest outlet. In 1936–38 it surged forward by up to 20 km along a 30 km front, one of the largest glacier surges ever recorded. In summer, meltwater pours off the ice cliff in dozens of waterfalls straight into the sea.","w":"A long, low ice cliff running along the sea, the edge of a huge white ice cap on Svalbard's second-largest island.","f":["Its Norwegian name means roughly 'sudden swell', after that surge.","Austfonna's ice front runs for well over 100 km, almost without a break.","Austfonna is one of the largest ice caps in Europe."],"q":{"q":"What did Bråsvellbreen do in 1936–38?","a":"Surged forward up to 20 km","x":["Retreated 20 km","Split into two glaciers"]}},"ne-gl-cambria-icefield":{"t":"An icefield feeding blue glaciers above Stewart","s":"South-east of the little port of Stewart, at the head of the long fjord of Portland Canal, the Cambria Icefield covers some 700 km² of the Coast Mountains. Its glaciers spill down on all sides into forested valleys. One of them, Bear Glacier, ends right beside a highway, its snout glowing an intense blue above a meltwater lake.","w":"A white icefield among jagged coastal peaks, glaciers radiating into green valleys, east of a long, narrow fjord.","f":["Portland Canal, the fjord below, is more than 100 km long and forms part of the Alaska–Canada border.","Stewart, at the fjord's head, calls itself Canada's northernmost ice-free port.","Its outlet glaciers have been retreating since the early 1900s."],"q":{"q":"Which Cambria glacier ends beside a highway near Stewart?","a":"Bear Glacier","x":["Athabasca Glacier","Mendenhall Glacier"]}},"ne-gl-columbia-icefield":{"t":"The rooftop of the Rockies, draining to three oceans","s":"The Columbia Icefield is the largest in the Rocky Mountains, some 200 km² of ice on the Continental Divide between Alberta and British Columbia. Its meltwater runs to three oceans: west to the Pacific, north to the Arctic, and east through Hudson Bay to the Atlantic. Look for the Athabasca Glacier, a broad white tongue ending near the Icefields Parkway.","w":"A high white plateau of ice ringed by rock peaks, with a broad glacier tongue reaching down toward a road.","f":["Mount Columbia, 3,747 m, on its rim, is the highest point in Alberta.","Snow Dome, on the icefield, is a triple divide: its meltwater can reach three different oceans.","The Athabasca Glacier has retreated more than 1.5 km since the late 1800s."],"q":{"q":"How many oceans does Columbia Icefield meltwater reach?","a":"Three","x":["Two","Four"]}},"ne-gl-devon-ice-cap":{"t":"Ice on the largest uninhabited island on Earth","s":"Devon Island, north of Lancaster Sound, is the largest island on Earth with no permanent residents, and its eastern part lies under the Devon Ice Cap, about 14,000 km² of white. West of the ice the land is a bare, rust-brown plateau, so Mars-like that scientists use the Haughton crater there to rehearse missions to the Red Planet. From the air the white-brown contrast is striking.","w":"A white dome covering the eastern end of the island, beside bare, rust-brown plateaus cut by canyons.","f":["The Haughton crater, about 23 km wide, was blasted out by an impact tens of millions of years ago.","The Haughton-Mars Project has run field seasons at the crater since 1997."],"q":{"q":"What makes Devon Island stand out?","a":"Largest uninhabited island","x":["Northernmost island","Largest Arctic island"]}},"ne-gl-edgeoyjokulen":{"t":"A white dome on Svalbard's wild eastern island","s":"Edgeøya, Svalbard's third-largest island, has no permanent residents, and much of its eastern part lies under ice caps such as Edgeøyjøkulen. Look for a smooth white dome whose edges break into short glaciers reaching the sea, while the west of the island shows bare, terraced brown plateaus. In winter the whole island merges with the frozen sea around it.","w":"A smooth white dome on a big island south-east of Spitsbergen, with short glaciers reaching the sea and bare brown uplands.","f":["The island is named after Thomas Edge, an English merchant in the 17th-century whaling trade.","Edgeøya lies within a nature reserve where polar bears roam freely."],"q":{"q":"Where does Edgeøya rank among Svalbard's islands by size?","a":"Third","x":["First","Fifth"]}},"ne-gl-harding-icefield":{"t":"A frozen sea spilling dozens of glaciers","s":"On the Kenai Peninsula south of Anchorage, the Harding Icefield buries some 1,800 km² of mountains, with only rocky summits poking through. More than 30 glaciers flow off it, several all the way down to the fjords of Kenai Fjords National Park. From above it is a white sea studded with dark islands of rock — nunataks — that ends abruptly at the green coast.","w":"A white expanse dotted with dark rock peaks, sending glacier tongues down to fjords on the Kenai Peninsula's south coast.","f":["It is named after US President Warren G. Harding, who visited Alaska in 1923.","Exit Glacier, near the town of Seward, is the one part of the icefield you can reach by road."],"q":{"q":"Which national park lies below the Harding Icefield?","a":"Kenai Fjords","x":["Glacier Bay","Denali"]}},"ne-gl-holtedahlfonna":{"t":"The ice field that feeds the glaciers of Kongsfjorden","s":"North-west Spitsbergen is crowned by Holtedahlfonna, the island's second-largest ice cap, about 1,400 km² of white. Its ice drains west through Kronebreen, one of Svalbard's fastest glaciers, which ends in a crumbling blue wall at the head of Kongsfjorden. On the same fjord stands Ny-Ålesund, one of the northernmost research settlements on Earth.","w":"A broad white ice field east of Kongsfjorden, sending a wide glacier into the head of the fjord.","f":["It is named after the Norwegian geologist Olaf Holtedahl.","Kronebreen can flow up to about 3 m a day.","Ny-Ålesund hosts research stations from about ten countries."],"q":{"q":"Which fast glacier drains Holtedahlfonna into Kongsfjorden?","a":"Kronebreen","x":["Negribreen","Bråsvellbreen"]}},"ne-gl-juneau-icefield":{"t":"The icefield behind Alaska's roadless capital","s":"Right behind Juneau, Alaska's capital, the mountains vanish under the Juneau Icefield, nearly 4,000 km² of ice straddling the US–Canada border. Dozens of glaciers pour off it: the Mendenhall reaches almost to the city's suburbs, and the Taku runs down toward the sea. The ice is one reason no road links Juneau to the rest of North America.","w":"A white plateau with dark peaks behind Juneau, glaciers spilling toward the sea, one ending in a lake by the town.","f":["Juneau is the only US state capital on the North American mainland with no road link to the rest of the continent.","Students have studied the icefield every summer since the late 1940s through the Juneau Icefield Research Program.","For decades the Taku was one of the few advancing glaciers in Alaska."],"q":{"q":"Which Juneau Icefield glacier ends in a lake near the city?","a":"Mendenhall Glacier","x":["Hubbard Glacier","Columbia Glacier"]}},"ne-gl-karpinsky-ice-cap":{"t":"Severnaya Zemlya's highest point is made of ice","s":"The Karpinsky Ice Cap is the largest of the seven ice caps on October Revolution Island, about 2,800 km². Its summit, at about 965 m, is the highest point of the whole Severnaya Zemlya archipelago — and it is ice, not rock. Look for a broad dome sloping toward the Laptev Sea, its edges split into outlet glaciers and bays choked with sea ice most of the year.","w":"A broad white dome on the eastern side of a large Arctic island, with glaciers reaching into ice-choked bays.","f":["It is named after Alexander Karpinsky, a geologist who became the first elected president of the Russian Academy of Sciences.","October Revolution Island is the largest island of Severnaya Zemlya."],"q":{"q":"What forms the highest point of Severnaya Zemlya?","a":"An ice cap","x":["A volcano","A rock pinnacle"]}},"ne-gl-kluane-ice-cap":{"t":"The biggest icefields outside the polar regions","s":"Between Yukon and Alaska lies a sea of ice that swallows whole mountain ranges: the icefields of the Saint Elias Mountains, the largest outside the polar regions. Rising from them is Mount Logan, 5,959 m, Canada's highest peak and one of the bulkiest mountains on Earth. Watch for rivers of ice many kilometres wide, striped with dark moraines like highways.","w":"Endless white icefields with dark rock peaks and huge striped glaciers, inland from the Gulf of Alaska.","f":["Kluane National Park and its neighbours form a single UNESCO World Heritage site straddling the Canada–US border.","Mount Logan is thought to have the largest base of any non-volcanic mountain on Earth.","The Hubbard Glacier, fed from these icefields, calves into the sea near Yakutat."],"q":{"q":"Which mountain, rising from these icefields, is Canada's highest?","a":"Mount Logan","x":["Mount Robson","Mount Saint Elias"]}},"ne-gl-lyell-icefield":{"t":"Five summits named for Swiss mountain guides","s":"South of the Columbia Icefield, another sheet of ice spreads along the Continental Divide: the Lyell Icefield. Above it rise the five summits of Mount Lyell, named after the geologist Charles Lyell; the individual peaks honour Swiss guides who worked in the Canadian Rockies. Look for a white plateau feeding glaciers west into British Columbia and east into the valleys of Banff National Park.","w":"A white ice plateau on the Rockies' crest south of the larger Columbia Icefield, with glaciers pouring off both sides.","f":["Charles Lyell, whose name it bears, was one of the founders of modern geology.","Its eastern glaciers feed the North Saskatchewan River, whose water ends up in Hudson Bay."],"q":{"q":"Which river is fed by the Lyell Icefield's eastern glaciers?","a":"North Saskatchewan","x":["Athabasca","Bow"]}},"ne-gl-malaspina-glacier":{"t":"A giant fan of ice spread over the Alaskan plain","s":"Look for a vast, round lobe of ice sprawled across the coastal plain at the foot of Mount Saint Elias: the Malaspina, the largest piedmont glacier in the world. The Seward Glacier squeezes out of the mountains through a gap and spreads like dough on a table, about 65 km wide. Its surface is marbled with dark bands of moraine, folded into zigzags by repeated surges.","w":"A huge rounded lobe of ice on the coastal plain, marbled with dark zigzag stripes, west of Yakutat Bay.","f":["It covers about 3,900 km², more than the US state of Rhode Island.","It is named after Alessandro Malaspina, who explored this coast for Spain in 1791.","Its bed lies below sea level, so the ocean could one day flood in if the ice thins enough."],"q":{"q":"What kind of glacier is the Malaspina?","a":"A piedmont glacier","x":["A tidewater glacier","A cirque glacier"]}},"ne-gl-manson-icefield":{"t":"A lonely ice sheet on Ellesmere's southern shore","s":"Along the south-east coast of Ellesmere Island, at the top of Baffin Bay, the Manson Icefield covers roughly 6,000 km² of mountains. Its glaciers drop steeply off a broad white plateau into the sea, calving icebergs that drift slowly south. There are no roads or settlements on the ice, and in winter it merges with the frozen sea into a single white plain.","w":"A broad white plateau on Ellesmere's south-east coast, with glaciers dropping steeply into dark, ice-strewn water.","f":["Grise Fiord, Canada's northernmost civilian community, lies on the same southern coast of Ellesmere.","Just offshore, the North Water polynya is a stretch of sea that stays partly open even in winter."],"q":{"q":"Which is Canada's northernmost civilian community, on Ellesmere?","a":"Grise Fiord","x":["Resolute","Alert"]}},"ne-gl-monarch-icefield":{"t":"A crown of ice above the Bella Coola valley","s":"South-east of the fjord town of Bella Coola, the Coast Mountains vanish under the Monarch Icefield, the northernmost of a chain of big icefields in the Pacific Ranges. Its namesake, Monarch Mountain, 3,555 m, towers over the peaks around it. In clear weather you will see a white plateau with dark summits, glaciers spilling toward deep green valleys, and the long fjords of the coast beyond.","w":"A white icefield with dark rock summits south-east of a long fjord, glaciers draining toward deep green valleys.","f":["Henry Hall and Hans Fuhrer made the first ascent of Monarch Mountain in 1936.","The larger Ha-Iltzuk Icefield, the biggest of this group, lies to the south.","The Bella Coola valley below is known for grizzly bears fishing for salmon."],"q":{"q":"Which fjord town lies north-west of the Monarch Icefield?","a":"Bella Coola","x":["Prince Rupert","Kitimat"]}},"ne-gl-mount-brown-icefield":{"t":"Home of a famous mountain-height blunder","s":"Around Athabasca Pass on the Continental Divide lies the Mount Brown Icefield, a quiet sheet of ice in the backcountry south of Jasper. Its namesake has a story: in 1827 the Scottish botanist David Douglas declared Mount Brown and its neighbour Mount Hooker to be about 16,000 feet high, the tallest peaks in North America. For decades maps repeated the claim. Both are in fact well under 3,500 m.","w":"A white icefield among dark peaks on the Rockies' crest, beside a low notch that marks an old fur-trade pass.","f":["Athabasca Pass was a main fur-trade route across the Rockies in the early 1800s.","Mount Brown is named after the botanist Robert Brown, known for describing Brownian motion.","The myth of the giant peaks lasted until climbers and surveyors checked them in the 1890s."],"q":{"q":"What did David Douglas get wrong about Mount Brown in 1827?","a":"Its height","x":["Its name","Its location"]}},"ne-gl-muller-ice-cap":{"t":"Arctic ice on an island of fossil forests","s":"Axel Heiberg Island, west of Ellesmere, is crowned by two ice caps, and the Müller is the northern one. Its outlet glaciers creep down toward long fjords. Nearby, White Glacier has been measured every year since 1959, one of the longest such records in the Arctic. Elsewhere on the island lie mummified tree stumps from forests that grew here some 45 million years ago.","w":"A broad white ice cap in northern Axel Heiberg Island, glaciers reaching down toward long, dark fjords.","f":["The island is named after Axel Heiberg, a Norwegian brewery director who funded Otto Sverdrup's expedition.","McGill University has run a summer research station at Expedition Fiord since 1960."],"q":{"q":"What remarkable fossils have been found on Axel Heiberg Island?","a":"Mummified tree stumps","x":["Dinosaur footprints","Mammoth skeletons"]}},"ne-gl-myrdalsjokull":{"t":"An ice cap hiding the volcano Katla","s":"Near Iceland's southern tip, a round white cap of ice sits above black sand plains: Mýrdalsjökull, about 500 km². Beneath it lies Katla, one of Iceland's most powerful volcanoes, which last erupted in a big way in 1918, sending floods of meltwater across the coast. Just to the west is the smaller Eyjafjallajökull, whose 2010 eruption grounded flights across Europe for days.","w":"A rounded white ice cap above black sand plains on Iceland's south coast, with smaller Eyjafjallajökull just to the west.","f":["The 1918 Katla flood carried so much sediment that it pushed the shoreline several kilometres out to sea.","Vík, near its southern foot, is the southernmost village in Iceland.","Mýrdalsjökull is Iceland's fourth-largest glacier."],"q":{"q":"Which volcano lies beneath Mýrdalsjökull?","a":"Katla","x":["Hekla","Grímsvötn"]}},"ne-gl-negribreen":{"t":"A glacier that wakes up about once a century","s":"On Spitsbergen's east coast, Negribreen flows into the wide Storfjorden, a broad river of ice with a heavily crevassed surface. It is a surge-type glacier: in 1935–36 it lurched almost 12 km forward in a single year. In 2016 it woke again, and by 2017 parts were racing along at about 20 m a day. Its surface is a chaos of shattered white ice, a rough texture easy to pick out even from cruise height.","w":"A broad, heavily crevassed glacier on Spitsbergen's east coast, ending in an ice cliff in the wide Storfjorden.","f":["In its 1935–36 surge the front advanced along a stretch about 15 km wide.","Surging glaciers are common in Svalbard; many lie quiet for decades between surges."],"q":{"q":"How far did Negribreen lurch forward in 1935–36?","a":"About 12 km","x":["About 1 km","About 50 km"]}},"ne-gl-north-ellesmere-ice-cap":{"t":"Ice at the very top of North America","s":"The northern end of Ellesmere Island is some of the northernmost land on Earth, and much of it lies under ice caps and icefields. Here, in Quttinirpaaq National Park, rise the highest mountains of the Canadian Arctic, including Barbeau Peak, 2,616 m. From above look for white plateaus broken by dark nunataks and, along the coast, the ragged remains of ancient floating ice shelves.","w":"White plateaus pierced by dark peaks, fringed along the Arctic Ocean coast by the remains of floating ice shelves.","f":["Cape Columbia, on Ellesmere's north coast, is the northernmost point of Canada.","Quttinirpaaq means 'top of the world' in Inuktitut.","Barbeau Peak, 2,616 m, is the highest mountain in Nunavut."],"q":{"q":"What does 'Quttinirpaaq' mean in Inuktitut?","a":"Top of the world","x":["Land of ice","Place of bears"]}},"ne-gl-northern-patagonian-ice-field":{"t":"Where a glacier meets the sea closest to the equator","s":"The Northern Patagonian Ice Field is the smaller of Patagonia's two great ice fields, yet it still drapes some 4,000 km² of ice over the Andes of southern Chile. Its highest point, Monte San Valentín, about 4,000 m, is the tallest summit in Patagonia. On its western side the San Rafael Glacier reaches Laguna San Rafael, a lagoon open to the Pacific, at only about 46.7° south.","w":"An icy plateau on the Andes of southern Chile, glaciers spilling west to a milky lagoon and east toward turquoise lakes.","f":["San Rafael is often called the tidewater glacier closest to the equator.","Lake General Carrera, east of the ice field, is the largest lake in Chile."],"q":{"q":"Which is the highest summit of the Northern Patagonian Ice Field?","a":"Monte San Valentín","x":["Cerro Fitz Roy","Cerro Torre"]}},"ne-gl-penny-ice-cap":{"t":"The ice cap of 'the land that never melts'","s":"On Baffin Island's Cumberland Peninsula, the Penny Ice Cap spreads some 6,000 km² of white just north of the Arctic Circle. It is the heart of Auyuittuq National Park — 'the land that never melts' in Inuktitut. From its edges glaciers pour down into deep U-shaped valleys and fjords, walled by some of the tallest granite cliffs on Earth.","w":"A broad white ice cap feeding glaciers into deep, steep-walled valleys, just north of the Arctic Circle on Baffin Island.","f":["Mount Thor, in the park, has one of the tallest sheer cliffs on Earth, about 1,250 m.","Akshayuk Pass, a well-known trekking route, cuts right across the peninsula below the ice."],"q":{"q":"What does Auyuittuq mean in Inuktitut?","a":"The land that never melts","x":["The land of the bears","The great white wall"]}},"ne-gl-prince-of-wales-icefield":{"t":"A vast ice sheet on Ellesmere, facing Greenland","s":"The Prince of Wales Icefield spreads along the eastern side of central Ellesmere Island, roughly 20,000 km² of ice — larger than many small countries. Across the channels to the east lies Greenland. Below the wing is an immense white plateau split by outlet glaciers that snake down to the sea, releasing icebergs into the ice-choked straits.","w":"An immense white plateau on eastern Ellesmere, with glaciers snaking east to the sea channels that separate it from Greenland.","f":["It is one of the largest icefields in the Canadian Arctic.","Smith Sound and Nares Strait, off its eastern side, link Baffin Bay with the Arctic Ocean."],"q":{"q":"Which land lies across the strait east of this icefield?","a":"Greenland","x":["Baffin Island","Devon Island"]}},"ne-gl-sargent-icefield":{"t":"Glaciers pouring straight into Prince William Sound","s":"On the eastern Kenai Peninsula, the Sargent Icefield caps the mountains above the maze of fjords and islands of Prince William Sound. Its glaciers spill down steep valleys right to the sea, calving icebergs into quiet green inlets. Some carry unexpected names: Princeton Glacier, for example, was named in 1909 after the university.","w":"A white icefield above a maze of fjords and forested islands, with glaciers ending at the water's edge.","f":["It is named after Rufus Harvey Sargent, a US Geological Survey mapmaker who surveyed the Kenai Peninsula.","Chenega Glacier, one of its outlets, takes its name from nearby Chenega Island."],"q":{"q":"Which sound do the Sargent Icefield's glaciers flow into?","a":"Prince William Sound","x":["Norton Sound","Kotzebue Sound"]}},"ne-gl-southern-patagonian-ice-field":{"t":"The largest ice in the south outside Antarctica","s":"For some 350 km along the Andes, the mountains of Patagonia disappear under one continuous sheet of ice, the largest in the Southern Hemisphere outside Antarctica. On a clear day you can follow glaciers flowing west to Pacific fjords and east to milky turquoise lakes in Argentina. Granite spires such as Fitz Roy and the Torres del Paine rise from its edges like broken teeth.","w":"A continuous white sheet on the Andes, glaciers streaming west to fjords and east into turquoise lakes like Lago Argentino.","f":["Perito Moreno Glacier, one of its outlets, ends in an ice wall about 5 km wide in Lago Argentino.","Pío XI, on the Chilean side, is the largest glacier in South America."],"q":{"q":"Which famous glacier of this ice field ends in Lago Argentino?","a":"Perito Moreno","x":["San Rafael","Pío XI"]}},"ne-gl-steacie-ice-cap":{"t":"The southern ice cap of an uninhabited Arctic island","s":"Axel Heiberg Island, west of Ellesmere, carries two big ice caps, and the Steacie covers the island's southern highlands. Seen from cruise height it is a broad white dome, its outlet glaciers winding between dark, flat-topped plateaus toward the fjords. Nobody lives on the island; in summer only a few research camps dot its valleys, and in winter the sea around it freezes solid.","w":"A broad white dome over southern Axel Heiberg Island, glaciers winding between dark flat-topped plateaus to the fjords.","f":["More than a quarter of Axel Heiberg Island, some 43,000 km² in all, lies under ice.","Several glaciers on the island's west side have kept advancing for decades, against the global trend."],"q":{"q":"Axel Heiberg Island lies next to which larger island?","a":"Ellesmere Island","x":["Baffin Island","Victoria Island"]}},"ne-gl-stikine-icecap":{"t":"An icefield split by a wild river","s":"Along the Alaska–British Columbia border near Wrangell and Petersburg, the Stikine Icecap buries the Coast Mountains in white. The Stikine River slices right through the range here, one of only a few rivers that do. On the Alaskan side LeConte Glacier reaches the sea, calving icebergs into LeConte Bay. Look too for Devils Thumb, a sharp rock spire rising from the ice on the border.","w":"A broad white icefield on the coastal mountains east of Petersburg, cut by a wide river valley and fjords full of icebergs.","f":["LeConte Glacier is the southernmost tidewater glacier in the Northern Hemisphere.","John Muir compared the Stikine valley to 'a Yosemite a hundred miles long'."],"q":{"q":"Which glacier here is the Northern Hemisphere's southernmost tidewater glacier?","a":"LeConte Glacier","x":["Mendenhall Glacier","Hubbard Glacier"]}},"ne-gl-university-ice-cap":{"t":"One of seven ice caps on a remote Arctic island","s":"October Revolution Island, the largest of Severnaya Zemlya, wears seven ice caps, and the University Ice Cap lies south of the biggest, the Karpinsky. Its dome rises to about 800 m. Between the caps the bare ground is stony polar desert, brown and grey in summer, with no trees or shrubs at all. In winter everything merges with the frozen seas into one white expanse.","w":"A white dome on a large Arctic island, flanked by stony brown polar desert and other ice caps to the north.","f":["Severnaya Zemlya separates the Kara Sea from the Laptev Sea.","The archipelago was mapped only in 1930–32, by a four-man expedition led by Georgy Ushakov."],"q":{"q":"Which two seas does Severnaya Zemlya separate?","a":"The Kara and Laptev seas","x":["The Barents and Kara seas","The Laptev and East Siberian"]}},"ne-gl-vatnajokull":{"t":"Iceland's giant ice cap, sitting on volcanoes","s":"Vatnajökull covers about 8% of Iceland, some 7,700 km² of ice up to nearly a kilometre thick. From above it is a white plateau spilling glacier tongues in every direction, some ending in lagoons full of icebergs near the south coast. Beneath the ice lie active volcanoes, including Grímsvötn and Bárðarbunga, whose eruptions can melt the ice and unleash sudden floods.","w":"A vast white ice cap in south-east Iceland, glacier tongues radiating outward and a lagoon of icebergs by the coast.","f":["Hvannadalshnúkur, on its southern rim, is Iceland's highest peak at about 2,110 m.","Jökulsárlón, the lagoon at the foot of one of its glaciers, is crowded with floating icebergs.","Vatnajökull National Park became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2019."],"q":{"q":"Which Icelandic volcano lies beneath Vatnajökull?","a":"Grímsvötn","x":["Hekla","Katla"]}},"ne-gl-vestfonna":{"t":"The western twin of Svalbard's great ice caps","s":"Nordaustlandet, Svalbard's second-largest island, is mostly wrapped in ice, split between two great caps. Vestfonna, the western one, covers about 2,500 km²: a low white dome crossed by ridges and outlet glaciers that end in ice cliffs along the fjords. Its bigger sibling, Austfonna, spreads to the east, and together they make the island look frosted from above.","w":"A low white dome on the western half of a large, ice-covered island north-east of Spitsbergen.","f":["Nordaustlandet has no permanent residents and is protected as a nature reserve.","Walruses haul out on beaches along the island's coasts."],"q":{"q":"Which is Vestfonna's larger neighbour on the same island?","a":"Austfonna","x":["Holtedahlfonna","Åsgardfonna"]}},"Q358755":{"t":"A huge snowy volcano named after a rare bird","s":"Mount Takahe is a broad shield volcano rising alone from the ice of Marie Byrd Land, 3,460 m high and with a volume of about 780 km³. It looks like a gently swelling white dome with a wide, shallow crater on top. Its last known eruption was about 7,500 years ago, and layers of its ash turn up in Antarctic ice cores.","w":"A broad, gently rounded snow dome rising alone from the flat ice, with a wide shallow crater at its summit.","f":["It is named after the takahē, a rare flightless bird of New Zealand.","Its summit caldera is about 8 km across."],"q":{"q":"What is Mount Takahe named after?","a":"A flightless bird","x":["A ship","A mountaineer"]}},"Q924312":{"t":"Antarctica's highest volcano, with a broken crater","s":"Mount Sidley is the tallest of the Executive Committee Range, a line of volcanoes standing in the ice of Marie Byrd Land, and at 4,285 m it is the highest volcano in Antarctica. Its south side is torn open by a caldera about 5 km wide, a dramatic bite out of the white cone. Richard Byrd spotted it from the air in 1934.","w":"A massive white volcano in a north–south line of snowy cones, its southern flank cut by a huge crater.","f":["Byrd named it after Mabelle Sidley, daughter of one of his expedition's sponsors.","It is the Antarctic entry on the 'Volcanic Seven Summits', the highest volcano on each continent.","The first recorded ascent was made in 1990."],"q":{"q":"Which is the highest volcano in Antarctica?","a":"Mount Sidley","x":["Mount Erebus","Mount Takahe"]}},"Q3321842":{"t":"A giant beside Amundsen's road to the Pole","s":"In November 1911 Roald Amundsen's party climbed from the Ross Ice Shelf up the Axel Heiberg Glacier toward the polar plateau, and beside their route stood this massive peak. Amundsen named it after Fridtjof Nansen, the Norwegian explorer who had lent him his ship, the Fram. At 4,069 m it is one of the great summits of the Queen Maud Mountains, a wall of rock and ice across the way to the Pole.","w":"A massive ice-clad peak at the edge of the Ross Ice Shelf, beside a steep glacier climbing toward the polar plateau.","f":["Amundsen reached the South Pole on 14 December 1911, about a month after passing this mountain.","The Axel Heiberg Glacier beside it climbs roughly 3,000 m from the ice shelf to the plateau."],"q":{"q":"Who named Mount Fridtjof Nansen in 1911?","a":"Roald Amundsen","x":["Robert Falcon Scott","Ernest Shackleton"]}},"Q19459387":{"t":"The high point above Pangong's blue lake","s":"Kangju Kangri is the highest summit of the Pangong Range, 6,725 m, a white crest in the high desert of eastern Ladakh. Its most striking neighbour is water rather than rock: Pangong Tso, a narrow lake some 130 km long at about 4,200 m, lies at its foot. Even from 11 km the lake is an unmistakable ribbon of blue that shifts from turquoise to deep navy with the light.","w":"A white crest in bare brown mountains beside a long, narrow, vividly blue lake that winds away to the east.","f":["Pangong Tso is brackish, yet it freezes over in winter.","Kangju Kangri was first climbed in 1983.","Villagers on the lake's southern shore also call the peak Thinguru."],"q":{"q":"Which long high-altitude lake lies at the foot of Kangju Kangri?","a":"Pangong Tso","x":["Tso Moriri","Namtso"]}},"Q28794266":{"t":"The 'white mountain' of the central Kunlun","s":"Aktag means 'white mountain' in Turkic, a plain name for a remote summit in the Kunlun, the long range that walls the Tibetan Plateau off from the Tarim Basin. At 6,748 m it is among the highest peaks of the Kunlun, rising in one of the least visited corners of Asia. Scan for a white massif over khaki ridges, with the sandy Tarim lowlands far to the north.","w":"A white massif rising from bare khaki ridges, with the pale desert lowlands of the Tarim Basin far to the north.","f":["The Kunlun runs for more than 2,000 km along the northern rim of the Tibetan Plateau.","North of the range lies the Taklamakan, one of the largest sand deserts in the world."],"q":{"q":"What does the Turkic name Aktag mean?","a":"White mountain","x":["Blue lake","Red rock"]}},"ne-pk-1159105667":{"t":"The sacred giant the Yellow River bends around","s":"Amne Machin is the holy mountain of the Golog Tibetans, a broad massif of ice rising to 6,282 m from the grasslands of Qinghai. The Yellow River, flowing east from its source, swings in a great loop around the range before turning north. Pilgrims walk a long circuit around it. The landmark from the air is a white crest of glaciers ringed by high green pastures.","w":"A broad ice massif in rolling green-brown grassland, with a big river looping around it in a wide bend.","f":["Early 20th-century explorers guessed it might rival Everest; modern surveys put it at 6,282 m.","Amne means 'grandfather' in the local Amdo dialect, an honorific for the mountain's protector deity."],"q":{"q":"Which great river loops around the Amne Machin range?","a":"Yellow River","x":["Yangtze","Mekong"]}},"Q724367":{"t":"Siberia's highest peak, twin-topped and white","s":"Belukha is the highest mountain of the Altai and of all Siberia, 4,506 m, straddling the border of Russia and Kazakhstan. Its name comes from its whiteness: two snowy summits joined by a saddle, draped in glaciers that feed the Katun, one of the rivers that form the Ob. Its bright white crown stands out above greener, lower ridges and turquoise lakes.","w":"A twin-summited white massif above green valleys and turquoise lakes, on the Russia–Kazakhstan border in the Altai.","f":["The Katun River begins at the Gebler Glacier on Belukha's southern side.","The Tronov brothers made the first ascent of the eastern summit in 1914.","Belukha is part of the Golden Mountains of Altai, a UNESCO World Heritage site."],"q":{"q":"Which river rises from the glaciers of Belukha?","a":"Katun","x":["Yenisei","Irtysh"]}},"Q4062402":{"t":"A lonely ice massif in Tibet's far west","s":"Far out in the dry uplands of western Tibet, the Nganglong massif rises to about 6,720 m, a knot of ice with more than 40 peaks over 6,000 m. It stands roughly 85 km east-north-east of the town of Ali, in a land of gravel plains and salt lakes. What you see is a cluster of white summits and glaciers set in a vast brown landscape.","w":"A cluster of white summits and glaciers in a vast brown upland of gravel plains, north-east of the town of Ali.","f":["The massif holds 37 glaciers, two of them more than 6 km long.","Modern Chinese maps give about 6,720 m, although older sources still quote 7,315 m."],"q":{"q":"Which town lies about 85 km west-south-west of the Nganglong massif?","a":"Ali","x":["Lhasa","Shigatse"]}},"Q5530346":{"t":"Where the Yangtze is born, in Tibet's ice","s":"The longest river in Asia starts here. Meltwater from glaciers on Geladaindong, the highest peak of the Tanggula Mountains at 6,621 m, gathers into the Tuotuo River, the traditional source of the Yangtze. From this cold, empty upland the water will travel more than 6,000 km to the sea at Shanghai. Look for a white massif of glaciers above braided silver streams on the brown plateau.","w":"A white massif of glaciers above braided silver streams spreading across the brown plateau of central Tibet.","f":["The Jianggendiru Glacier on its south-west flank is usually named as the Yangtze's source.","East of the peak, the Qinghai–Tibet railway crosses the Tanggula range at about 5,070 m."],"q":{"q":"Which great river begins on Geladaindong's glaciers?","a":"Yangtze","x":["Yellow River","Mekong"]}},"Q2102394":{"t":"A remote ice peak of the eastern Karakoram","s":"This summit of the eastern Karakoram, charted at 6,934 m and known as Shahi Kangri, stands above the upper Shyok valley in Ladakh, west of the high, empty plains of Aksai Chin. It is a world of ice and bare rock: glaciers pour down between brown ridges, and the braided Shyok threads its gravel bed below. The higher Saser Kangri group rises to the south.","w":"Glaciers and white crests above a braided grey river valley, with bare brown plateaus stretching away to the east.","f":["Saser Kangri I, the highest summit of the neighbouring Saser group, reaches about 7,670 m.","The Shyok River, flowing below, is a major tributary of the Indus."],"q":{"q":"The Shyok River below this peak is a tributary of which river?","a":"Indus","x":["Ganges","Brahmaputra"]}},"Q2118899":{"t":"The biggest ice field of Tibet's empty north","s":"In the heart of the Changtang, the high, cold and almost empty north of Tibet, Purog Kangri rises to 6,929 m above a dome of ice some 400 km² in area. It is the largest ice field of the northern Tibetan Plateau. From above it looks like a white starfish on a brown plain, its glacier arms reaching out toward shallow lakes and salt flats.","w":"A white, star-shaped ice field with radiating glacier lobes on the brown plateau, surrounded by lakes and salt flats.","f":["In 2000 Chinese and American scientists drilled ice cores here, the longest 213 m deep.","The ice field holds about 52 km³ of ice."],"q":{"q":"In which remote region of Tibet does Purog Kangri lie?","a":"The Changtang","x":["Kham","Amdo"]}},"Q4848635":{"t":"The unclimbed crown of a hidden Tibetan range","s":"In the wet, deeply cut south-east of Tibet rises the Kangri Garpo range, and Bairiga, 6,882 m, is its highest peak. It has never been climbed. The range was so little known that until 2009 none of its 47 summits above 6,000 m had been reached. Expect a chain of white peaks feeding long glaciers, their tongues reaching down toward dark forested valleys.","w":"A chain of white peaks with long glaciers reaching down toward dark, forested valleys in south-eastern Tibet.","f":["The botanist-explorer Frank Kingdon-Ward photographed the peak in 1933, when it was known as Choembo.","The Lhagu Glacier, the largest in the range, is about 30 km long."],"q":{"q":"Bairiga is the highest peak of which range?","a":"Kangri Garpo","x":["Nyenchen Tanglha","Gangdise"]}},"Q572801":{"t":"A modest mountain with famously savage weather","s":"At 1,917 m Mount Washington is small by world standards, yet it is the highest peak in the north-eastern United States and notorious for its weather. Storm tracks from several directions converge over the Presidential Range, and in April 1934 observers on the summit measured a gust of 372 km/h. Look for a bare summit ridge above New Hampshire's dark green forests, white with snow from October to May in most years.","w":"A bare grey summit ridge above endless dark forest in northern New Hampshire, the tallest of a chain of treeless peaks.","f":["The 1934 gust of 231 mph stood as the world's strongest measured wind for over 60 years.","The Mount Washington Cog Railway, opened in 1869, was the world's first mountain-climbing cog railway.","The harsh climate keeps the treeline here at only about 1,300 m."],"q":{"q":"What record did Mount Washington's summit set in 1934?","a":"Strongest measured wind gust","x":["Lowest temperature","Deepest snowfall"]}},"Q1858056":{"t":"The finish line of the Appalachian Trail","s":"Katahdin rises alone from the forests and lakes of northern Maine, a granite massif of 1,606 m with a bare, bouldery top. Its name means 'the greatest mountain' in the Penobscot language. For hikers it is the northern end of the Appalachian Trail, which runs about 3,500 km from Georgia. Look for its grey summit plateau and sharp ridges above dark green woods.","w":"A lone grey granite massif rising from dark forest dotted with countless lakes in northern Maine.","f":["Henry David Thoreau climbed toward its summit in 1846 and wrote about it in The Maine Woods.","Former governor Percival Baxter bought the land around it and gave it to Maine as a park.","The trail's southern end is Springer Mountain in Georgia."],"q":{"q":"Which long-distance trail ends on Katahdin?","a":"Appalachian Trail","x":["Pacific Crest Trail","Continental Divide Trail"]}},"Q748355":{"t":"A lone volcano ruling northern California","s":"Mount Shasta stands alone, a huge white cone rising some 3,000 m above the surrounding country of northern California. At 4,322 m it is the second-highest peak of the Cascades and the largest of their stratovolcanoes by volume. Look for a smaller cone, Shastina, bulging from its western flank; on a clear day the pair can be seen from well over 100 km away.","w":"A lone white volcanic cone with a smaller cone on its western shoulder, rising far above forested plateaus.","f":["Shastina, its satellite cone, would rank among the Cascades' tallest volcanoes on its own.","Its Whitney Glacier is the longest glacier in California."],"q":{"q":"What is the name of Shasta's satellite cone?","a":"Shastina","x":["Lassen Peak","Black Butte"]}},"Q2078034":{"t":"A wall of peaks with no foothills at all","s":"The Tetons rise straight out of the flat valley of Jackson Hole with no foothills in between, and Grand Teton, 4,199 m, is the sharpest tooth among them. The range stands about 2,100 m above the valley floor, pushed up along a fault that is still active. The giveaway is a jagged grey line of peaks, with the blue ovals of Jackson Lake and smaller glacial lakes along its eastern foot.","w":"A short, jagged range rising abruptly from a flat valley, with lakes along its eastern foot, just south of Yellowstone.","f":["French-speaking fur trappers called the peaks les trois tétons, 'the three breasts'.","The Teton Range is among the youngest in the Rocky Mountains, less than 10 million years old.","Yellowstone National Park lies just to the north."],"q":{"q":"Which valley lies at the eastern foot of the Tetons?","a":"Jackson Hole","x":["Yosemite Valley","Sun Valley"]}},"Q275662":{"t":"Portland's snowy volcano, skiable even in summer","s":"Mount Hood is Oregon's highest peak, about 3,400 m, a sharp snowy volcano some 80 km east of Portland that crowns the city's skyline on clear days. It is still considered potentially active, with steam vents near the summit. On its south slope the Palmer Snowfield keeps skiers busy well into summer, and Timberline Lodge, built in the 1930s, sits just below the treeline.","w":"A sharp, lone white cone rising above dark forest, east of Portland and south of the Columbia River Gorge.","f":["A British naval officer named it in 1792 after Admiral Samuel Hood.","Its last significant eruptions came in the 1780s, shortly before Europeans arrived.","Timberline Lodge played the exterior of the hotel in the film The Shining."],"q":{"q":"Which city sees Mount Hood on its skyline, about 80 km away?","a":"Portland","x":["Seattle","Boise"]}},"Q756244":{"t":"A snowy giant east of Santiago","s":"Tupungato is a massive, snow-capped volcano over 6,500 m high, straddling the border of Chile and Argentina east of Santiago. It is an old, eroded giant; its smaller, younger neighbour, Tupungatito, has erupted repeatedly in the last two centuries. From above look for a broad white massif rising from a tangle of dry brown Andean ridges, with green vineyards in the valleys on the Argentine side.","w":"A broad white massif on the Chile–Argentina border, rising above dry brown ridges about 80 km east of Santiago.","f":["Matthias Zurbriggen and Stuart Vines made the first ascent in 1897.","The Argentine wine district of Tupungato, in the Uco Valley, takes its name from the volcano."],"q":{"q":"Who made the first ascent of Tupungato in 1897?","a":"Zurbriggen and Vines","x":["Whymper and Carrel","Hillary and Tenzing"]}},"Q233836":{"t":"The highest volcano on Earth, in the driest desert","s":"Ojos del Salado is the highest volcano on Earth, nearly 6,900 m, rising on the border of Chile and Argentina at the edge of the Atacama. The air here is so dry that the mountain carries only thin patches of snow despite its height. Steaming vents near the summit show it is not extinct. Out of the window it is a broad, pale massif in a moonscape of ochre, rust and white salt flats.","w":"A broad, pale volcanic massif in a moonscape of ochre and rust, with white salt flats spread around it.","f":["It is also the highest point of Chile.","A small crater lake near the summit, at about 6,390 m, is often called the highest lake in the world."],"q":{"q":"Which country's highest point is Ojos del Salado?","a":"Chile","x":["Peru","Bolivia"]}},"Q214916":{"t":"An Inca shrine on a volcano in the sky","s":"Llullaillaco rises to 6,739 m on the border of Chile and Argentina, in one of the driest places on Earth. Five centuries ago the Inca climbed to its summit to build a shrine, and in 1999 archaeologists found three remarkably preserved Inca burials there — the highest archaeological site in the world. Watch for a lone cone with thin snow above rust-red desert and salt flats.","w":"A lone, sharp volcanic cone with thin snow patches above rust-red desert and bright salt flats in the Atacama.","f":["The three Inca children found on the summit are now kept in a museum in Salta, Argentina.","The air is so dry that snow on the summit often turns straight to vapour without melting."],"q":{"q":"What did archaeologists find on Llullaillaco's summit in 1999?","a":"Inca burials","x":["Spanish silver","Dinosaur bones"]}},"Q148521":{"t":"The snowy guardian on La Paz's skyline","s":"Illimani is the mountain that seems to appear in every photo of La Paz: a long white wall with several summits, towering over the city and the high plain of El Alto. At about 6,400 m it is the highest peak of the Cordillera Real and the second-highest in Bolivia. From above look for a massive glacier-clad ridge rising south-east of the city, with the dark gash of La Paz's canyon below.","w":"A long, glacier-clad ridge with several summits, south-east of La Paz and the flat, built-up plateau of El Alto.","f":["Martin Conway and two Alpine guides climbed its south summit in 1898.","La Paz and El Alto together form one of the highest large urban areas on Earth, between about 3,600 and 4,100 m."],"q":{"q":"Which city lies at the foot of Illimani?","a":"La Paz","x":["Cusco","Quito"]}},"Q272593":{"t":"Bolivia's highest summit, a lone volcanic cone","s":"Nevado Sajama rises alone from the Altiplano, a white-topped cone of 6,542 m and the highest point of Bolivia. Just to the west, on the Chilean border, two more volcanoes stand side by side, Parinacota and Pomerape, known together as the Payachatas. On Sajama's lower slopes grows one of the highest forests on Earth, made of small, twisted queñua trees.","w":"A lone, snow-capped volcanic cone on the brown Altiplano, with twin white cones on the Chilean border to its west.","f":["Sajama National Park, created in 1939, was Bolivia's first national park.","Ice cores drilled on its summit in 1997 reach back roughly 25,000 years."],"q":{"q":"What name is given to the twin volcanoes west of Sajama?","a":"Payachatas","x":["Licancabur","Aucanquilcha"]}},"Q758574":{"t":"A volcano once mined almost to its summit","s":"Aucanquilcha is a big, pale volcano of 6,176 m in the Atacama highlands of northern Chile, and for decades it was a workplace. Sulphur was dug near its summit, at about 5,950 m — for years the highest mine in the world. In 1972 a switchback road for 20-tonne trucks was built almost to the top. Its slopes show pale yellow-grey above bright white salt flats.","w":"A broad pale volcano in the dry highlands near the Bolivian border, with white salt flats spread at its foot.","f":["The mine opened in 1913, and at first llamas carried the sulphur down the mountain.","The sulphur was refined far below at the camp of Amincha, about 3,900 m, linked by an aerial cableway."],"q":{"q":"What was mined near the summit of Aucanquilcha?","a":"Sulphur","x":["Copper","Silver"]}},"Q777794":{"t":"The sacred ice peak beside Rainbow Mountain","s":"Ausangate is the great white summit of southern Peru, 6,372 m, rising about 100 km south-east of Cusco. For the Quechua people it is an apu, a mountain spirit, and every year thousands of pilgrims gather in a valley near it for the festival of Qoyllur Rit'i. Close by stands Vinicunca, the 'Rainbow Mountain', striped in red, ochre and green.","w":"A massive white peak rising above brown puna grassland and small turquoise lakes, south-east of Cusco.","f":["Ausangate is the highest peak of the Cordillera Vilcanota.","Qoyllur Rit'i, held each year before Corpus Christi, blends Andean and Catholic traditions.","Vinicunca's colours come from layers of mineral-rich sediment."],"q":{"q":"Which famous striped mountain lies near Ausangate?","a":"Vinicunca","x":["Huayna Picchu","El Misti"]}},"Q946035":{"t":"The largest ice cap in the tropics","s":"Coropuna is a broad volcanic massif in southern Peru, more than 6,300 m high, crowned by several summits and a sheet of ice. That ice, around 44 km², is the most extensive in the tropics, and its meltwater feeds valleys and farms far below. The big white cap sprawled over a dark volcanic plateau, north-west of Arequipa, is hard to miss.","w":"A sprawling white ice cap with several summits on a dark volcanic plateau, north-west of Arequipa.","f":["Coropuna is the highest volcano in Peru.","The Inca held it sacred, and ruins of their shrines lie on its slopes.","Scientists expect its ice to keep supplying water for roughly another century."],"q":{"q":"What record does Coropuna's ice cap hold?","a":"Largest ice cap in the tropics","x":["Oldest ice in South America","Highest glacier on Earth"]}},"Q200935":{"t":"The highest summit in the tropics","s":"Huascarán is the highest mountain in Peru and in all of the Earth's tropics, 6,768 m. It crowns the Cordillera Blanca, the 'white range', the most heavily glaciated range in the tropics. From above look for a twin-topped white massif towering over the green valley of the Santa River, with turquoise glacial lakes tucked into the side valleys.","w":"A twin-summited white massif above a long green valley, in a chain of ice peaks running north–south in central Peru.","f":["Annie Peck reached its north summit in 1908, a landmark climb for women in mountaineering.","Huascarán National Park became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1985.","The Cordillera Blanca holds hundreds of glacial lakes."],"q":{"q":"Which range does Huascarán crown?","a":"Cordillera Blanca","x":["Cordillera Real","Cordillera Vilcanota"]}},"Q14081":{"t":"The summit farthest from the centre of the Earth","s":"Measured from sea level, Chimborazo's 6,267 m is nowhere near Everest. But the Earth bulges at the equator, and this Ecuadorian volcano stands just south of it, so its summit is the point on the planet's surface farthest from the Earth's centre — about 2 km farther out than Everest's. Look for a broad, glacier-capped dome rising alone over the highlands.","w":"A broad, glacier-capped volcanic dome standing alone above green and brown highlands, south-west of Quito.","f":["In 1802 Alexander von Humboldt climbed to nearly 5,900 m on its slopes, then a world altitude record.","Edward Whymper and his guides Jean-Antoine and Louis Carrel made the first ascent in 1880."],"q":{"q":"What makes Chimborazo's summit special?","a":"Farthest point from Earth's centre","x":["Highest point of the Andes","Highest active volcano"]}},"Q27489229":{"t":"A pale limestone tableland above the Mediterranean","s":"Between the Mediterranean and Egypt's Western Desert, the Libyan Plateau spreads as a pale, stony limestone tableland cut by dry wadis. Along the coast it ends in a steep escarpment above a thin strip of shore. From 11 km it is all bleached sand-and-stone colours, broken only by winding ravines, with the deep blue sea to the north and emptiness to the south.","w":"A pale, flat stony plateau cut by dry ravines, ending in a steep escarpment above the blue Mediterranean.","f":["To the south-east lies the Qattara Depression, whose floor sinks to about 133 m below sea level.","The plateau's limestone formed on the floor of a sea millions of years ago.","Siwa Oasis, famous in antiquity for its oracle of Amun, lies at the plateau's southern edge."],"q":{"q":"Which deep depression lies south-east of the Libyan Plateau?","a":"Qattara Depression","x":["Danakil Depression","Turpan Depression"]}},"Q1881042":{"t":"India's mineral heartland of forests and waterfalls","s":"East-central India rises into the Chota Nagpur Plateau, a hilly tableland of ancient rock patched with sal forest. It holds much of India's coal and iron ore, so from the air you may spot black scars of open-cast mines and the haze of steel towns. In the monsoon its rivers tumble off the plateau edges in waterfalls, and reservoirs fill the valleys.","w":"Rolling green-brown hills and forest patches, with dark open-cast mines, reservoirs and a few hazy industrial towns.","f":["Jamshedpur, one of India's first planned industrial cities, grew around a steel works founded here in 1907.","The Damodar valley on the plateau has long been one of India's main coalfields.","Its sal forests are home to many of India's Adivasi communities."],"q":{"q":"Which steel city grew up on the Chota Nagpur Plateau from 1907?","a":"Jamshedpur","x":["Kanpur","Surat"]}},"Q2740752":{"t":"The highland that keeps the rain off the sertão","s":"In north-eastern Brazil, just inland from the green Atlantic coast, the land steps up onto the Borborema Plateau, rolling uplands several hundred metres high. Moist ocean winds drop their rain on its seaward side, so beyond it lies the dry sertão, a land of thorny caatinga scrub. Often you can see the change from the window: greener to the east, dusty grey-brown to the west.","w":"Rolling uplands inland from the coast: greener on the eastern side, grey-brown dry scrub to the west.","f":["Campina Grande, on the plateau, hosts one of Brazil's biggest São João festivals every June.","Caatinga, the dry thorny forest beyond, is found only in Brazil."],"q":{"q":"Which dry scrubland lies west of the Borborema Plateau?","a":"Caatinga","x":["Pampa","Pantanal"]}},"Q916997":{"t":"South Africa's vast 'land of thirst'","s":"The Karoo covers a huge part of South Africa's interior: a semi-desert of flat plains and flat-topped hills capped with dark dolerite. Its name is usually traced to a Khoekhoe word for a dry, thirsty land. From above it is a palette of khaki, rust and grey, crossed by long straight roads and the occasional green thread of a river.","w":"Flat khaki plains dotted with flat-topped dark hills, crossed by long straight roads and dry riverbeds.","f":["Its rocks are rich in fossils of mammal-like reptiles more than 250 million years old.","The MeerKAT radio telescope, part of the Square Kilometre Array project, stands in the Karoo."],"q":{"q":"Which giant radio-telescope project is being built in the Karoo?","a":"Square Kilometre Array","x":["Atacama Large Millimeter Array","Event Horizon Telescope"]}},"Q482274":{"t":"Cameroon's water tower, draining three ways","s":"Across central Cameroon runs the Adamawa Plateau, a high grassy upland mostly around 1,000 m, dotted with old volcanic cones and crater lakes. It is often called Cameroon's water tower: rivers born here flow north toward Lake Chad, west to the Niger by way of the Benue, and south toward the Atlantic and the Congo basin. Below you: green savanna, wooded valleys and red earth.","w":"A green, rolling upland of savanna and wooded valleys, dotted with small volcanic cones and crater lakes.","f":["It is named after Modibo Adama, a Fulani leader who founded an emirate here in the early 19th century.","Ngaoundéré, the main town on the plateau, is the northern end of the railway from Douala."],"q":{"q":"Why is the Adamawa Plateau called Cameroon's 'water tower'?","a":"Many rivers rise there","x":["It stores water in dams","It has Africa's highest rainfall"]}},"Q776345":{"t":"Angola's high plateau, where the Okavango is born","s":"The Bié Plateau is the high heart of Angola, rolling grassland and woodland mostly 1,500–1,800 m above the sea. Its rains feed rivers that set off in every direction: the Kwanza to the Atlantic, the Cunene south toward Namibia, and the Cubango, which becomes the Okavango and ends in the sands of Botswana. From above look for green-and-ochre uplands threaded with meandering rivers.","w":"Rolling green-and-ochre uplands and woodland, laced with meandering rivers and wetlands in central Angola.","f":["The Okavango never reaches the sea; it spreads into a vast inland delta in Botswana.","Huambo, on the plateau at about 1,700 m, is one of Angola's largest cities."],"q":{"q":"Which famous river starts on the Bié Plateau as the Cubango?","a":"Okavango","x":["Limpopo","Orange"]}},"Q1321468":{"t":"An old worn plateau riddled with caves and springs","s":"The Ozarks are not really mountains but an old plateau, deeply cut by rivers into forested hills. The limestone beneath is riddled with caves and springs, some of the largest in North America. The country below is dark green and crinkled, with winding clear rivers and big, branching reservoirs — dammed valleys shaped like spilled ink.","w":"Crinkled, dark green forested hills cut by winding rivers, with large reservoirs spreading in branching, fingered shapes.","f":["Missouri, much of it in the Ozarks, has more than 7,000 known caves.","Big Spring in Missouri releases around a billion litres of water on an average day.","The Buffalo River in Arkansas became the first national river in the United States, in 1972."],"q":{"q":"Which Ozark river became the first US 'national river' in 1972?","a":"Buffalo River","x":["White River","Arkansas River"]}},"Q4492616":{"t":"A rolling lava plateau of lakes and pine forest","s":"Between the Coast Mountains and the Rockies, central British Columbia flattens into the Fraser Plateau, a rolling upland of pine forest, meadows and countless lakes, much of it resting on old lava flows. Along its eastern edge the Fraser River cuts a deep trench on its way to the Pacific. Seen from the window it is a green-grey carpet speckled with lakes and slashed by that sharp river canyon.","w":"A rolling green-grey upland speckled with lakes, cut on its eastern side by the deep trench of the Fraser River.","f":["The Fraser, about 1,375 km long, is the longest river entirely within British Columbia.","In the 2000s a mountain pine beetle outbreak killed vast areas of pine here, turning forests red and then grey."],"q":{"q":"Which ocean does the Fraser River finally reach?","a":"Pacific","x":["Arctic","Atlantic"]}},"Q15241934":{"t":"The continent's ancient shield rock, full of lakes","s":"The Laurentian Upland is the worn-down core of the Canadian Shield, a vast sweep of ancient rock around Hudson Bay and the Great Lakes. Ice-age glaciers scraped it bare and left behind a maze of lakes, bogs and rocky knolls. From above, especially near Lake Superior, look for dark forest scattered with thousands of lakes shining like broken glass.","w":"Dark forest and bare rock scattered with countless lakes, especially around the shores of Lake Superior.","f":["Much of its rock is more than 2.5 billion years old, among the oldest in North America.","Minnesota's Mesabi Range, on the upland, has long been one of the world's great iron-ore districts.","Lake Superior, on its southern edge, is the largest freshwater lake in the world by area."],"q":{"q":"Which is the largest freshwater lake by area, on the upland's edge?","a":"Lake Superior","x":["Lake Victoria","Lake Huron"]}},"Q293730":{"t":"A land of wind-blown yellow dust, carved into gullies","s":"The Loess Plateau is built of dust: fine yellow silt blown in from the deserts of Central Asia over hundreds of thousands of years, piled in places more than 100 m thick. Rain carves it easily, so from above it looks like a crumpled yellow-brown sheet shot through with branching gullies and neat terraced fields. The silt washing off it gives the Yellow River its colour and its name.","w":"Crumpled yellow-brown land cut by countless branching gullies, with contour-terraced hillsides and a muddy river.","f":["The plateau covers about 640,000 km², larger than Spain.","Millions of people have lived in yaodong, homes dug into the loess hillsides.","A restoration project from the 1990s terraced slopes and replanted hillsides, sharply cutting erosion."],"q":{"q":"What gives the Yellow River its colour?","a":"Silt from the Loess Plateau","x":["Algae in the water","Sulphur springs"]}},"Q1126906":{"t":"Gentle hills of black earth, where great rivers begin","s":"South of Moscow the land swells gently into the Central Russian Upland, hills only a couple of hundred metres high but important on the map: the Oka, the Don and tributaries of the Dnieper all start here. Its soils include rich black chernozem. Below lies a patchwork of huge fields cut by branching, tree-lined ravines and, in the south, vast open-pit iron mines.","w":"A vast patchwork of fields cut by tree-lined, branching ravines, with huge grey open-pit mines in the south.","f":["The Kursk Magnetic Anomaly beneath it is one of the largest iron-ore deposits on Earth.","The Don rises near Novomoskovsk, south of Moscow, and flows about 1,900 km to the Sea of Azov.","Its ravines grow fast because the soft soils erode easily once the forest is cleared."],"q":{"q":"Which river rises here and flows to the Sea of Azov?","a":"Don","x":["Volga","Dnieper"]}},"Q277576":{"t":"A basalt plateau sliced by canyon lakes","s":"North of the Arctic Circle in Siberia, the Putorana Plateau rises as a giant layer cake of dark basalt, cut into flat-topped mesas by canyons hundreds of metres deep. Many canyons hold long, narrow lakes, like fjords far from any sea. No roads lead in. Look for stepped cliffs and ribbons of water; in winter the whole plateau becomes black-and-white graphic art.","w":"Flat-topped dark mesas with stepped sides, cut by deep canyons holding long, narrow lakes, east of Norilsk.","f":["The plateau became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2010.","It has the greatest concentration of waterfalls in Russia.","The geographic centre of Russia, at Lake Vivi, lies on its southern edge."],"q":{"q":"What lies at Lake Vivi, on the plateau's southern edge?","a":"Russia's geographic centre","x":["Russia's coldest point","Russia's deepest lake"]}},"Q604329":{"t":"Karst hills, rice terraces and eternal spring","s":"South-west China lifts into the Yunnan–Guizhou Plateau, a high, rumpled land mostly 1,000–2,000 m up, eaten by rain into karst: sinkholes, stone pinnacles and rivers that vanish underground. From above look for green hills pocked with hollows, red-earth fields, big blue lakes around Kunming and, on steep southern slopes, rice terraces stacked like contour lines.","w":"Rumpled green hills pocked with sinkholes, patches of red earth, and big lakes around the city of Kunming.","f":["Kunming, at about 1,900 m, is nicknamed the 'City of Eternal Spring' for its mild climate.","The Stone Forest near Kunming is a maze of grey limestone pillars tens of metres tall.","The Hani rice terraces of Honghe, in the south of the plateau, are a UNESCO World Heritage site."],"q":{"q":"Which city on the plateau is called the 'City of Eternal Spring'?","a":"Kunming","x":["Guiyang","Chengdu"]}},"Q874816":{"t":"A shallow saucer of rice fields beside the Mekong","s":"North-east Thailand sits on the Khorat Plateau, a vast shallow saucer only about 100–200 m above the sea, tilted gently toward the Mekong. Its two main rivers, the Mun and the Chi, meander across it before joining the Mekong at the Lao border. The ground below is a patchwork of rice paddies — bright green in the rainy season, gold and brown in the dry — dotted with villages and ponds.","w":"A flat patchwork of rice paddies and ponds, rimmed by low hills, with the Mekong curving along its northern and eastern edges.","f":["Thick layers of rock salt lie beneath the plateau, and salt is still produced there.","Dinosaur fossils found at Phu Wiang made the plateau famous among palaeontologists.","The region, known as Isan, is home to about a third of Thailand's people."],"q":{"q":"Which great river bounds the Khorat Plateau to the north and east?","a":"Mekong","x":["Chao Phraya","Salween"]}},"Q27696359":{"t":"Myanmar's cool highland with a famous lake","s":"East of the Irrawaddy plain, Myanmar climbs abruptly onto the Shan Plateau, a rolling upland around 1,000 m high, green with forest and fields. The Salween River slices through it in a deep gorge on its way to the sea. Look for Inle Lake, a long blue sliver in a valley, where villages stand on stilts and gardens float on the water.","w":"A green, rolling upland rising sharply above the flat Irrawaddy plain, with long, narrow Inle Lake in a valley.","f":["Inle Lake's fishermen are known for rowing with one leg wrapped around the oar.","The plateau's western edge rises as a steep escarpment above the central plain.","Taunggyi, capital of Shan State, lies at about 1,400 m near Inle Lake."],"q":{"q":"What are Inle Lake's fishermen famous for?","a":"Rowing with one leg","x":["Fishing with cormorants","Diving for pearls"]}},"Q925906":{"t":"Red-rock country where the Earth shows its layers","s":"The Colorado Plateau is one of the most spectacular things you can fly over: a high desert of red, orange and cream rock laid down in layers, then cut by rivers into canyons, mesas and buttes. The Grand Canyon, Monument Valley, Arches and Canyonlands all lie on it. On a clear day look for the winding blue arms of Lake Powell among bare red rock.","w":"Vast red-and-cream rock desert carved by winding canyons and flat-topped mesas, with the branching blue arms of Lake Powell.","f":["The rocks exposed in the Grand Canyon span nearly two billion years of Earth's history.","It holds one of the greatest concentrations of national parks in the United States.","The Four Corners, where four US states meet, lies on the plateau."],"q":{"q":"Which great canyon on the plateau did the Colorado River carve?","a":"Grand Canyon","x":["Hells Canyon","Copper Canyon"]}},"Q401815":{"t":"Lava floods and ice-age floods, written in the land","s":"Between the Cascades and the Rockies lies the Columbia Plateau, built from vast floods of basalt lava that poured out mostly 17 to 15 million years ago. At the end of the last ice age, colossal floods from a burst glacial lake raced across it and scoured the Channeled Scablands. Two things stand out: the rolling, dune-like wheat hills of the Palouse and the broad blue curve of the Columbia River.","w":"Rolling golden wheat hills and bare, channel-scarred basalt, with the wide blue Columbia River curving through.","f":["Some single lava flows travelled hundreds of kilometres, all the way to the Pacific.","The floods came from glacial Lake Missoula, which emptied again and again as its ice dam broke.","The rolling Palouse hills are made of wind-blown silt and farmed for wheat."],"q":{"q":"Floods from which ice-age lake carved the Channeled Scablands?","a":"Lake Missoula","x":["Lake Bonneville","Lake Agassiz"]}},"Q3623001":{"t":"Gold-rush country along the Yukon River","s":"Across central Yukon the land rolls away as the Yukon Plateau: forested uplands and broad valleys cut by the Yukon River and its tributaries. In 1896 gold was found in a creek near the Klondike River, and within two years tens of thousands of prospectors poured in. In summer it is dark spruce and pale gravel bars; in autumn, great swathes of yellow aspen and birch.","w":"Rolling forested uplands cut by wide, braided river valleys, with the Yukon River winding north-west.","f":["Parts of the plateau escaped the ice sheets of the last ice age, as part of Beringia.","Whitehorse, the Yukon's capital, stands on the plateau beside the Yukon River.","The Yukon River runs about 3,190 km to the Bering Sea."],"q":{"q":"Which gold rush brought prospectors here from 1896?","a":"Klondike Gold Rush","x":["California Gold Rush","Fraser Canyon Gold Rush"]}},"Q118574":{"t":"The high plain of Titicaca and the great salt flat","s":"Between two chains of the Andes lies the Altiplano, a high plain averaging about 3,750 m — the most extensive high plateau on Earth after Tibet. From cruise it is an astonishing sight: brown steppe, snowy volcanoes along the rim, the deep blue of Lake Titicaca in the north and, in the south, the dazzling white of Salar de Uyuni, the largest salt flat in the world.","w":"A vast brown high plain ringed by snowy volcanoes, with a huge blue lake in the north and white salt flats to the south.","f":["Salar de Uyuni covers about 10,000 km², and after rain it becomes a giant mirror.","Lake Titicaca, at about 3,810 m, is often called the highest navigable lake in the world.","Beneath the salt flats lie some of the world's largest known lithium reserves."],"q":{"q":"What is Salar de Uyuni?","a":"The world's largest salt flat","x":["The world's highest lake","The world's driest desert"]}},"ne-rg-1730071967":{"t":"A lonely plateau in the heart of the eastern Sahara","s":"Far out in the eastern Sahara of north-western Sudan, the Jebel Abyad Plateau rises from the sand as a low, weathered tableland. Its name is Arabic for 'white mountain'. In a typical year hardly any rain falls here at all. From above look for dark rocky uplands streaked with wind-blown sand and, to the south, the faint winding line of Wadi Howar, a dry valley that was once a river.","w":"A dark, weathered tableland rising from pale sand, streaked by dunes, in the empty desert of north-western Sudan.","f":["Wadi Howar, to the south, carried a Nile tributary when the Sahara was green, about 9,500–4,500 years ago.","Rock art across the eastern Sahara shows cattle and giraffes from that wetter time."],"q":{"q":"What does 'Jebel Abyad' mean in Arabic?","a":"White mountain","x":["Black rock","Red sand"]}},"ne-rg-1730072941":{"t":"A cerrado tableland named after a Brazilian classic","s":"West of the São Francisco River the land rises onto broad, flat cerrado tablelands laced with veredas — marshy valleys lined with buriti palms. This is the backcountry of João Guimarães Rosa's 1956 novel Grande Sertão: Veredas, and a national park carries its name. Today many of the flat plateaus are covered in huge circles and rectangles of farmland, cut by green strips along the streams.","w":"Vast flat tablelands of farmland and olive savanna, cut by dark green lines of palm-lined streams running east.","f":["Guimarães Rosa's novel is often ranked among the greatest works of Brazilian literature.","Buriti palms, which line the veredas, can grow to about 30 m.","Its streams feed the São Francisco, the longest river lying entirely within Brazil."],"q":{"q":"Which writer's novel gave the Grande Sertão Veredas park its name?","a":"João Guimarães Rosa","x":["Jorge Amado","Machado de Assis"]}},"Q1294308":{"t":"Texas Hill Country, a limestone tableland of springs","s":"West of Austin and San Antonio the land steps up onto the Edwards Plateau, a limestone tableland of ranches, oak and juniper scrub. Its southern and eastern edge is the Balcones Escarpment, where clear springs burst out at the foot of the slope. Look for a grey-green plain crossed by tree-lined rivers, with the grey sprawl of Austin and San Antonio along the escarpment below.","w":"A grey-green limestone tableland of scrub and ranches, dropping at its south-east edge toward Austin and San Antonio.","f":["The Edwards Aquifer beneath its edge supplies most of San Antonio's drinking water.","Comal Springs, at New Braunfels, are among the largest springs in Texas.","Bracken Cave, near San Antonio, hosts one of the world's largest bat colonies."],"q":{"q":"Which city gets most of its water from the Edwards Aquifer?","a":"San Antonio","x":["Houston","Dallas"]}},"ne-rg-1730072971":{"t":"Kazakhstan's 'yellow ridge' of worn-down hills","s":"The Kazakh Uplands, known as Saryarka, fill the heart of Kazakhstan: a vast land of low, rounded hills, the worn stumps of ancient mountains, set in dry steppe. It looks like crumpled felt in shades of tan and olive, dotted with salt lakes. The coal city of Karaganda lies here, and to the south the long, thin Lake Balkhash marks the edge of the uplands.","w":"Crumpled tan and olive steppe with low rounded hills and salt lakes; the long, thin Lake Balkhash lies to the south.","f":["The highest point, Aksoran, reaches about 1,565 m.","Lake Balkhash, to the south, is fresh in its western part and salty in the east.","Saryarka's steppe and lakes, including Korgalzhyn, are a UNESCO World Heritage site."],"q":{"q":"Which lake, fresh in the west and salty in the east, lies to the south?","a":"Lake Balkhash","x":["Lake Issyk-Kul","Lake Zaysan"]}},"ne-rg-1730072981":{"t":"Rugged highlands north-east of Lake Baikal","s":"North-east of Lake Baikal, rugged ridges and deep basins make up the Stanovoy Highlands, a wild land of larch taiga and bare summits. The Baikal–Amur Mainline railway threads through them in tunnels and along valleys. In the Kodar Range, the highest part, small glaciers survive. Look out for a surprise in the Chara basin: a patch of golden sand dunes in the middle of the taiga.","w":"Rugged grey ridges and deep taiga-filled basins north-east of Baikal, with a small patch of golden sand dunes.","f":["The Severomuysky Tunnel on the railway here, about 15 km long, is the longest in Russia.","The Kodar Range rises to about 3,070 m.","The Chara Sands cover a few dozen square kilometres, a real desert patch inside the taiga."],"q":{"q":"Which railway threads through the Stanovoy Highlands?","a":"Baikal–Amur Mainline","x":["Trans-Siberian Railway","Amur–Yakutsk Mainline"]}},"ne-rg-1730073599":{"t":"Ancient rock and gold in southern Yakutia","s":"In southern Yakutia rise the Aldan Highlands, a rolling upland of taiga-covered hills built on some of the oldest rock in Asia. Gold was found here in 1923, and the rush that followed drew thousands of prospectors into the wilderness. Look for dark larch forest, pale river valleys and, near Neryungri, the vast terraced pit of a coal mine cut into the hills.","w":"Rolling taiga-covered hills cut by pale river valleys, with a vast terraced open-pit mine near Neryungri.","f":["The town of Aldan grew up during the gold rush of the 1920s.","The Amur–Yakutsk railway and the Lena highway both cross the highlands on their way north to Yakutsk."],"q":{"q":"What was found in the Aldan Highlands in 1923, sparking a rush?","a":"Gold","x":["Diamonds","Oil"]}},"Q1753873":{"t":"A high desert tableland between two Sierras","s":"Most of northern and central Mexico is a high tableland walled in by two mountain chains, the Sierra Madre Occidental to the west and the Sierra Madre Oriental to the east. Its northern part is the Chihuahuan Desert, all pale basins, dry lake beds and long ridges; farther south it rises and turns greener toward Mexico City. The pattern to spot: tan basins between parallel dark ranges.","w":"Tan desert basins and white dry lake beds between parallel dark ridges, walled by higher mountains on both sides.","f":["The Chihuahuan Desert is the largest hot desert in North America.","Mexico City lies on its southern part at about 2,240 m.","Copper Canyon, cut into its western rim, is deeper in places than the Grand Canyon."],"q":{"q":"Which mountains wall the Mexican Plateau to the west?","a":"Sierra Madre Occidental","x":["Sierra Nevada","Sierra Madre del Sur"]}},"Q654947":{"t":"Wooded hills and the long blue Finger Lakes","s":"Across southern New York and western Pennsylvania runs the Allegheny Plateau, an old tableland worn by rivers into a maze of steep wooded hills. On its northern edge, ice-age glaciers gouged the Finger Lakes, long, narrow and parallel as if scratched by a giant hand. From above they are unmistakable: a row of thin blue slivers running north–south.","w":"Wooded, deeply cut hills, with a row of long, narrow parallel lakes running north–south along the northern edge.","f":["Seneca Lake, the largest of the Finger Lakes, is about 190 m deep at its deepest.","The first commercial oil well in the United States was drilled in 1859 at Titusville, on the plateau in Pennsylvania."],"q":{"q":"Which chain of long, narrow lakes lies on the plateau's northern edge?","a":"Finger Lakes","x":["Kawartha Lakes","Rideau Lakes"]}},"Q1143656":{"t":"A sandstone tableland of gorges and waterfalls","s":"Rising above the Tennessee Valley, the Cumberland Plateau is a long sandstone tableland running from Kentucky through Tennessee into Alabama. Its flat, forested top ends in steep bluffs, and streams plunge off the edges into deep gorges. The plateau shows as a dark green, flat-topped ridge with sharp escarpments, its rim notched by the V-shaped valleys of its waterfalls.","w":"A long, flat-topped forested tableland with steep escarpments, its edges notched by deep, V-shaped gorges.","f":["Fall Creek Falls, about 78 m high, is one of the highest waterfalls in the eastern United States.","At Cumberland Falls in Kentucky, a 'moonbow' can appear in the spray on clear full-moon nights.","Its hard sandstone cap resists erosion, which is why the plateau keeps its flat top."],"q":{"q":"What rare sight can appear at Cumberland Falls on full-moon nights?","a":"A moonbow","x":["A green flash","Ball lightning"]}},"Q1458409":{"t":"Where the rivers of northern Karelia gather","s":"Its name is usually read as 'main lake', and Pyaozero earns it: rivers from the forested hills pour in from all sides, among them the Olanga from Paanajärvi National Park. The water then leaves by the Kuma and travels on to the White Sea. In 1966 a dam raised the level, and the lake became part of the Kumskoye Reservoir. The Arctic Circle runs only about 60 km to the north.","w":"A large, ragged lake full of islands and bays in dark forest, just south of the Arctic Circle and west of the White Sea.","f":["Its open water covers about 659 km², not counting the islands.","Water leaving by the Kuma reaches the White Sea through Kovdozero and the Kovda River.","Topozero, to the south, drains into Pyaozero by the Sofyanga River."],"q":{"q":"Which sea does Pyaozero's water eventually reach?","a":"The White Sea","x":["The Baltic Sea","The Barents Sea"]}},"Q1247231":{"t":"The largest lake wholly inside New York State","s":"Lake Champlain and the Great Lakes are bigger, but New York shares them; Oneida is the largest lake that lies entirely within the state. It is long and surprisingly shallow — about 34 km from end to end, yet on average only some 7 m deep — so a strong wind quickly whips up steep waves. The modern Erie Canal route runs straight across it.","w":"A long, pale-blue lake lying east–west, just north-east of the sprawl of Syracuse.","f":["Boats on the Erie Canal enter the lake at Sylvan Beach in the east and leave it at Brewerton in the west.","In winter the lake usually freezes over and fills with ice-fishing shanties.","It is named after the Oneida, one of the six nations of the Haudenosaunee (Iroquois) Confederacy."],"q":{"q":"Which canal's modern route runs across Oneida Lake?","a":"The Erie Canal","x":["The Champlain Canal","The Welland Canal"]}},"Q1335725":{"t":"The Y-shaped Finger Lake with a flying past","s":"All the other Finger Lakes are straight blades of water; only Keuka forks, and from the air it looks like a slightly crooked letter Y — early settlers simply called it Crooked Lake. At its southern tip lies Hammondsport, home town of the aviation pioneer Glenn Curtiss. In 1908 his June Bug made the first publicly announced aeroplane flight in America there.","w":"A forked, Y-shaped lake among long parallel lakes, just west of the much longer, straight Seneca Lake.","f":["Keuka drains into Seneca Lake through a short outlet stream that drops about 80 m on the way.","Glenn Curtiss was born in Hammondsport in 1878 and built engines and aircraft there.","The steep slopes around the lake are at the heart of the Finger Lakes wine region."],"q":{"q":"What shape sets Keuka apart from the other Finger Lakes?","a":"A letter Y","x":["A ring","A letter S"]}},"Q1552706":{"t":"The deepest Finger Lake, its floor below sea level","s":"Glaciers gouged the Finger Lakes like claw marks, and Seneca is the deepest scratch of all: about 188 m. Its surface lies roughly 136 m above sea level, so the bottom of this inland lake sits well below the level of the ocean. With so much water, it cools slowly and almost never freezes over, even in hard upstate winters.","w":"A long, narrow north–south lake at the centre of the Finger Lakes, with Geneva at its northern end.","f":["The lake is about 60 km long but nowhere much more than 5 km wide.","Watkins Glen, at its southern tip, is famous for a narrow gorge with 19 waterfalls.","Seneca holds more water than any other Finger Lake."],"q":{"q":"Roughly how deep is Seneca Lake at its deepest point?","a":"About 190 m","x":["About 60 m","About 400 m"]}},"Q731175":{"t":"The longest Finger Lake, with Ithaca at its tip","s":"At about 61 km, Cayuga is the longest of the Finger Lakes — a slim blue line you can follow almost from end to end on a clear day. Ithaca and Cornell University sit at its southern tip, where gorges cut down through the hills. At the flat northern end the lake melts into the Montezuma marshes, a major stop for migrating geese and ducks.","w":"The longest lake of the group, running north–south just east of Seneca Lake, with Ithaca at its southern end.","f":["Taughannock Falls, near the west shore, drops about 65 m — higher than Niagara Falls.","At some 133 m deep, Cayuga's floor also lies below sea level.","A canal links Cayuga to Seneca Lake, its neighbour to the west."],"q":{"q":"Which university stands at the southern end of Cayuga Lake?","a":"Cornell","x":["Syracuse","Colgate"]}},"Q945023":{"t":"A wild lake on the sandstone of the Athabasca Basin","s":"Cree Lake spreads across about 1,435 km² of northern Saskatchewan, a maze of islands and bays in unbroken boreal forest. It lies almost wholly within the Athabasca Basin, a vast bowl of ancient sandstone that also hides the world's richest uranium ore. Its water leaves northward by the Cree River and, lake by lake, finds its way to Lake Athabasca.","w":"A big, island-studded lake in continuous dark forest, about 200 km south of Lake Athabasca.","f":["At about 1,435 km², it is the fourth-largest lake in Saskatchewan.","The Cree River carries its water north to Black Lake, which drains into Lake Athabasca.","The Athabasca Basin holds the highest-grade uranium deposits known anywhere."],"q":{"q":"Which way does water leave Cree Lake?","a":"North, by the Cree River","x":["South, to the Churchill River","West, to the Peace River"]}},"Q719538":{"t":"Source of the Kazan, near Canada's 'four corners'","s":"Only a few dozen kilometres to the south is the one point in Canada where four provinces and territories meet: Saskatchewan, Manitoba, Nunavut and the Northwest Territories, at 60° N, 102° W. Kasba Lake itself is the source of the Kazan River, which runs about 1,000 km north to Baker Lake. Here the spruce forest is already thinning towards the tundra.","w":"A large, many-armed lake in sparse spruce forest, just north of the point where four Canadian provinces and territories meet.","f":["The lake covers about 1,341 km²; most of it is in the Northwest Territories, a small part in Nunavut.","The Kazan River flows from Kasba about 1,000 km north to Baker Lake in Nunavut.","It lies close to the tree line, where the northern forest breaks up into open tundra."],"q":{"q":"Which river begins at Kasba Lake?","a":"The Kazan","x":["The Thelon","The Back"]}},"Q1699973":{"t":"Two round lakes, two asteroids, 175 million years apart","s":"Two almost perfectly round lakes lie side by side here, and both fill meteorite craters. The western one, about 36 km across, holds a striking ring of islands left by the impact. For decades the pair was taken for twins made by one double asteroid, but newer dating shows the eastern crater is roughly 175 million years older. In English they are the Clearwater Lakes.","w":"Two round lakes side by side in the northern forest, the larger western one with a neat ring of islands inside it.","f":["The western crater formed about 286 million years ago, the eastern one about 460–470 million years ago.","The western lake is about 36 km across, the eastern about 26 km.","The lakes lie in northern Quebec, roughly 100 km inland from the east coast of Hudson Bay."],"q":{"q":"What made the two round Clearwater Lakes?","a":"Two meteorite impacts","x":["Two volcanic calderas","Two glacial kettle holes"]}},"Q1356838":{"t":"The fur traders' shortcut from Montreal to the west","s":"For two centuries of canoe travel, this lake was the hinge of the route west: paddlers came up the Ottawa and Mattawa rivers from Montreal, carried their canoes over a low height of land, then crossed Lake Nipissing and ran down the French River to Georgian Bay. Samuel de Champlain came this way in 1615. Today North Bay spreads along its north-eastern shore.","w":"A wide, shallow lake with a scalloped shore and several large islands, with the city of North Bay on its north-eastern corner.","f":["Although it covers about 870 km², the lake averages only around 4.5 m deep.","Its water flows west down the French River into Georgian Bay, part of Lake Huron.","Champlain crossed it in 1615 on his way to the country of the Huron-Wendat."],"q":{"q":"Which river carries Lake Nipissing's water to Georgian Bay?","a":"The French River","x":["The Mattawa River","The Ottawa River"]}},"Q1799611":{"t":"A northern lake turned into a channel for diverted rivers","s":"East of James Bay the taiga is a patchwork of lakes, bogs and black spruce, and Lake Sakami is one of its bigger pieces. It has also become part of an engineered water route: rivers farther south are diverted northward, and their water passes through Sakami on its way to the Robert-Bourassa Reservoir and the turbines of the La Grande River. A weir at its outlet keeps the lake from rising too high.","w":"An irregular lake in flat, lake-strewn taiga, just south of the vast Robert-Bourassa Reservoir, about 140 km east of James Bay.","f":["Water diverted from the Eastmain and Opinaca rivers flows through Sakami towards the La Grande power stations.","A canal with a concrete weir at the outlet limits how high the lake can rise.","The land around it is part of Eeyou Istchee, the Cree territory of James Bay."],"q":{"q":"Which reservoir receives the water flowing through Lake Sakami?","a":"Robert-Bourassa","x":["Manicouagan","Smallwood"]}},"Q7359166":{"t":"A lake cut in two by the 60th parallel","s":"The ruler-straight line that divides Saskatchewan from the Northwest Territories, the 60th parallel, runs right through Scott Lake, so its water lies in two jurisdictions at once. On the ground nothing marks the border: only rocky shores, islands and dark spruce. The nearest community, Stony Rapids, is about 80 km to the south-east, and the lake's fishing lodge is reached by air.","w":"A branching lake of rocky shores and islands in forest, lying on the 60° N line north-west of Stony Rapids.","f":["The 60th parallel forms the whole northern border of Saskatchewan, about 445 km long.","At this latitude the midsummer sun stays up for nearly 19 hours.","Like most lakes here, it fills a hollow scraped into the Canadian Shield by Ice Age glaciers."],"q":{"q":"Which line runs through Scott Lake?","a":"The 60th parallel north","x":["The Arctic Circle","The 49th parallel north"]}},"Q923693":{"t":"A mountain lake inside a supervolcano","s":"At 2,357 m above sea level, Yellowstone Lake is the largest lake in North America at such a height — and it rests inside the caldera of one of the world's great volcanic systems. Its western bay, West Thumb, is itself a smaller caldera, and hot springs vent from the lake floor. Ice usually seals the surface from December until late May or June.","w":"A big lake shaped like an open hand, ringed by dark forest in the middle of the Yellowstone plateau.","f":["Even from 11,000 m you are only about 8.6 km above its surface.","West Thumb formed in a volcanic eruption roughly 170,000 years ago.","Hot springs and steam vents on the lake bottom warm the water in places."],"q":{"q":"What is West Thumb, the western bay of Yellowstone Lake?","a":"A small volcanic caldera","x":["A drowned river valley","A meteorite crater"]}},"Q3518981":{"t":"The giant among Arctic Alaska's thaw lakes","s":"Alaska's North Slope is freckled with thousands of oval lakes, many lined up in the same direction by the prevailing wind. Teshekpuk is the giant among them: some 800 km² of shallow water barely above sea level, close to the Beaufort Sea. In summer tens of thousands of geese gather here to moult, and a caribou herd named after the lake calves on the tundra around it.","w":"A huge, pale lake among countless smaller oval ponds on flat tundra, a short way inland from the Arctic Ocean coast.","f":["It is the largest lake on Alaska's Arctic coastal plain, about 130 km south-east of Utqiaġvik.","Most lakes around it are thermokarst lakes, formed where permafrost thawed and the ground sank.","The Teshekpuk caribou herd takes its name from the lake."],"q":{"q":"What formed most of the lakes around Teshekpuk?","a":"Thawing permafrost","x":["Volcanic craters","Meteorite impacts"]}},"Q1427364":{"t":"The biggest natural freshwater lake in the western US","s":"Glaciers carved this basin at the foot of the Rockies, and Flathead Lake now fills it, roughly 45 km long. By surface area it is the largest natural freshwater lake west of the Mississippi in the lower 48 states. Sweet-cherry orchards line its eastern shore, and Wild Horse Island, near the west side, is a state park where bighorn sheep and a few wild horses roam.","w":"A large blue lake at the head of a broad valley, with steep, often snowy mountains along its eastern side.","f":["The southern half of the lake lies within the Flathead Indian Reservation.","A dam at the southern outlet near Polson controls the top few metres of the lake's level.","The peaks of Glacier National Park rise to the north-east."],"q":{"q":"Which animals share Wild Horse Island with a few wild horses?","a":"Bighorn sheep","x":["Bison","Mountain goats"]}},"Q1534419":{"t":"Alberta's biggest lake, lost in a vast delta","s":"Lake Claire is the largest lake lying entirely inside Alberta, yet it is very shallow — a broad sheet of water spread across the Peace–Athabasca Delta, one of the largest inland deltas in the world. Around it stretch sedge meadows, winding channels and willow flats inside Wood Buffalo National Park, where herds of free-roaming wood bison still graze.","w":"A broad, shallow lake just west of Lake Athabasca, in a green-and-brown maze of delta channels and meadows.","f":["Wood Buffalo is Canada's largest national park — bigger than Switzerland.","The Peace and Athabasca rivers feed the delta, and the lake rises and falls with their floods.","The park became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1983."],"q":{"q":"Which national park surrounds Lake Claire?","a":"Wood Buffalo","x":["Jasper","Prince Albert"]}},"Q4539001":{"t":"The smaller of Yamal's two 'sandy-shore' lakes","s":"In the Nenets language 'to' means lake, and Yarroto is usually explained as 'lake with sandy shores'. There are two of them in southern Yamal; this one, the Second, covers about 154 km² but is no more than 8 m deep. For eight or nine months a year it is locked in ice, and in summer it glitters among thousands of smaller tundra lakes.","w":"A large rounded lake with pale margins among countless smaller lakes, north-west of its bigger twin, Yarroto First.","f":["The lake is about 21 km long and 8 km wide.","Nenets herders drive their reindeer across Yamal on long seasonal migrations.","Its fish include the peled, a whitefish common in Siberian lakes."],"q":{"q":"What does the Nenets word 'to' mean in names like Yarroto?","a":"Lake","x":["River","Hill"]}},"Q1520895":{"t":"Western Siberia's biggest lake — and a shallow one","s":"Lake Chany is the largest lake in Western Siberia, yet you could wade across much of it: the average depth is only about two metres. It has no outlet, so it swells and shrinks with wet and dry decades, and its water is slightly salty. From above it is a ragged patchwork of open water, reed beds and islands on the flat Baraba steppe.","w":"A big, irregular sheet of grey-blue water with reedy bays and islands on flat steppe, about 350 km west of Novosibirsk.","f":["Two rivers, the Kargat and the Chulym, flow into the lake, and none flows out.","The lake is made up of several linked basins, including Big Chany and Small Chany.","Its reed beds shelter huge numbers of ducks, grebes and gulls in summer."],"q":{"q":"How deep is Lake Chany on average?","a":"About 2 m","x":["About 20 m","About 50 m"]}},"Q537374":{"t":"Where a river from Mongolia ends on the grasslands","s":"The Kherlen River rises in the mountains north-east of Ulaanbaatar, loops across Mongolia and finally ends here, in Hulun Lake, one of the largest lakes in China. Around it roll the treeless Hulunbuir grasslands, green in summer and brown and white in winter. When the ice is thick, fishermen still haul long nets out from under it in a famous winter catch.","w":"A large oval lake on open, treeless grassland, south of the border town of Manzhouli.","f":["Its Russian name, Dalainor, comes from the Mongolian for 'sea lake'.","In wet years the lake spills through a channel into the Argun River, part of the Amur basin.","Its area changes with rainfall and has exceeded 2,000 km² in wet periods."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows from Mongolia into Hulun Lake?","a":"The Kherlen","x":["The Selenge","The Orkhon"]}},"Q673169":{"t":"The lake 'where the water meets the sky'","s":"Bangweulu is said to mean 'where the water meets the sky', and on a hazy day it lives up to it: a flat, shining lake fading into endless swamps and floodplains. The wetlands shelter the shoebill, a huge grey bird with a clog-shaped bill, and the black lechwe, an antelope found nowhere else. David Livingstone died near its southern swamps in 1873.","w":"A flat, silvery lake that merges into vast green-brown swamps to the east and south.","f":["Its water leaves by the Luapula River and ends up in the Congo, not the Nile.","Livingstone was still searching for the source of the Nile when he died at Chitambo's village.","In the rainy season the floodplains around the lake spread far beyond its open water."],"q":{"q":"Which great river system does Lake Bangweulu drain into?","a":"The Congo","x":["The Nile","The Zambezi"]}},"Q203137":{"t":"A Sámi lake of three thousand islands","s":"More than 3,000 islands are scattered across Lake Inari, so from the air it looks less like one lake than a shattered mirror dropped into the pine and birch forest of Lapland. It is Finland's third-largest lake and a heartland of Sámi culture; the rocky islet of Ukonsaari was once a sacred place of offerings. From about November to June the lake lies frozen under snow.","w":"A huge, shattered-looking lake crowded with islands in low northern forest, close to the Norwegian and Russian borders.","f":["It covers about 1,040 km², making it the third-largest lake in Finland.","The lake drains north by the Paatsjoki River to the Barents Sea.","Inari lies roughly 270 km north of the Arctic Circle."],"q":{"q":"Roughly how many islands dot Lake Inari?","a":"More than 3,000","x":["About 300","About 30"]}},"Q19253":{"t":"An hourglass of a lake with a famous winter battle","s":"Two broad basins joined by a narrow waist give Lake Peipus the look of an hourglass, and the border between Estonia and Russia runs down its length. It is one of Europe's largest lakes, yet only about 7 m deep on average, so it freezes over most winters. In 1242 Alexander Nevsky's army defeated the Livonian knights on its ice, in what Russians call the Battle on the Ice.","w":"A huge hourglass-shaped lake — a wide northern basin, a narrow middle strait, a smaller southern basin — between Tartu and Pskov.","f":["Its parts are Peipus proper in the north, narrow Lämmijärv ('Warm Lake') and Lake Pskov in the south.","The Narva River carries its water north to the Gulf of Finland.","Villages of Russian Old Believers have lined its western shore for centuries."],"q":{"q":"What does Lämmijärv, the narrow middle part of Lake Peipus, mean?","a":"Warm Lake","x":["Cold Lake","Deep Lake"]}},"Q125912":{"t":"A lovely lake with gas dissolved in its depths","s":"Lake Kivu looks idyllic from above: a long, deep-blue lake folded among steep green hills, with the big island of Idjwi in its middle. Its depths hide something rare — huge amounts of methane and carbon dioxide dissolved in the bottom water, held down by the weight of the lake. Today some of that methane is pumped up and burned to generate electricity on the Rwandan shore.","w":"A long, many-bayed blue lake among steep green hills, with a large island in the middle and volcanoes to the north.","f":["The lake lies about 1,460 m above sea level and is nearly 500 m deep.","Idjwi, in the middle of the lake, is one of the largest lake islands in Africa.","Kivu drains south by the Ruzizi River into Lake Tanganyika."],"q":{"q":"Which gas is pumped from Lake Kivu's depths to make electricity?","a":"Methane","x":["Hydrogen","Helium"]}},"Q125211":{"t":"Hippos, a royal name and the Mountains of the Moon","s":"Just north of this lake the Rwenzori, the legendary Mountains of the Moon, sometimes show their glaciers above the clouds. Lake Edward itself lies on the floor of the Albertine Rift, shared by two national parks where hippos crowd the shallows. The explorer Henry Morton Stanley named it after the Prince of Wales, who later became King Edward VII.","w":"A broad blue lake on the floor of the rift valley, with a narrow channel running north-east to smaller Lake George.","f":["The Kazinga Channel, about 32 km long, links it to Lake George and is famous for its hippos.","Its water leaves by the Semliki River, which flows to Lake Albert and on into the Nile.","The equator passes just north of the lake."],"q":{"q":"Which future king is Lake Edward named after?","a":"Edward VII","x":["Edward VI","Edward VIII"]}},"Q140712":{"t":"The lake behind China's strangest garden stones","s":"The holey, twisted limestone rocks that decorate Suzhou's classical gardens come from here: for centuries they were hauled out of Tai Hu and prized as 'Taihu stones'. The lake itself is broad and remarkably shallow, about two metres deep on average, and dotted with dozens of islands. Suzhou and Wuxi crowd its eastern and northern shores, a ring of lights at night.","w":"A huge, roughly round lake with islands and bays along its eastern side, west of Suzhou and the sprawl towards Shanghai.","f":["At about 2,250 km², it is one of China's largest freshwater lakes.","The Grand Canal runs past its eastern side on the way between Suzhou and Hangzhou.","Biluochun, one of China's famous green teas, grows on the Dongting hills beside the lake."],"q":{"q":"What are 'Taihu stones' traditionally used for?","a":"Decorating classical gardens","x":["Building city walls","Making ink stones"]}},"Q753898":{"t":"Argentina's largest freshwater lake, tinted by glacier flour","s":"Glaciers grind the Andes into fine rock flour, and it tints Lago Argentino an unreal milky turquoise. This is the largest freshwater lake in Argentina, and its western arms reach deep into the ice: the Perito Moreno Glacier pushes right across one of them, now and then damming the water until it breaks through. The town of El Calafate sits on the southern shore, where mountains give way to dry steppe.","w":"A large turquoise lake with long fjord-like arms reaching west into snowy mountains and glaciers, and brown steppe to the east.","f":["Its western arms lie within Los Glaciares National Park, a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1981.","The front of the Perito Moreno Glacier is about 5 km wide and rises some 60–70 m above the water.","The Upsala Glacier flows into the lake's long northern arm."],"q":{"q":"What gives Lago Argentino its milky turquoise colour?","a":"Fine rock flour from glaciers","x":["Algae blooms","Dissolved copper"]}},"Q651294":{"t":"Tibet's 'Heavenly Lake', 4.7 km above the sea","s":"Namtso means 'heavenly lake' in Tibetan, and at about 4,718 m it sits closer to a cruising airliner than to the sea: the gap is only some 6 km. It is one of the largest lakes on the Tibetan Plateau, salty and startlingly blue, and one of Tibet's three holy lakes. To the south-east the snowy Nyenchen Tanglha range rises above 7,000 m.","w":"A large, intensely blue lake on a brown plateau, with a line of glaciated peaks along its south-eastern side.","f":["Pilgrims walk around the lake; a circuit in the Tibetan Year of the Sheep is thought especially auspicious.","Nyenchen Tanglha, the highest peak of the range beside it, reaches 7,162 m.","In winter the whole lake freezes over."],"q":{"q":"What does the Tibetan name Namtso mean?","a":"Heavenly lake","x":["Turquoise lake","Mirror lake"]}},"Q1774501":{"t":"A Kolyma tundra lake named after the seal","s":"Nerpichye means 'seal lake', a hint of how close the Arctic Ocean is: the lake lies in the Kolyma Lowland, on the left side of the Kolyma River's delta. It formed where permafrost thawed and the ground slumped, and its low, boggy shores barely rise above the water. Around it thousands of smaller lakes make the tundra look like lace.","w":"A large lake stretched from south-east to north-west in flat tundra riddled with lakes, just west of the Kolyma delta.","f":["The lake covers about 237 km² and is one of the largest in Yakutia's Nizhnekolymsky district.","Its only outflow, a channel called the Nerpichya Viska, leads to the neighbouring lake Maloye Pokhodskoye.","Ice covers it for most of the year in this far north-eastern corner of Yakutia."],"q":{"q":"What kind of lake is Nerpichye by origin?","a":"A thermokarst lake","x":["A volcanic crater lake","A tectonic rift lake"]}},"Q4538999":{"t":"The bigger twin: 247 km² of shallow tundra water","s":"Yarroto First is the larger of a pair of lakes in southern Yamal: some 247 km² of water, 22 km long, yet nowhere deeper than about 8 m. Like its twin a little to the north-west, it carries a Nenets name read as 'lake with sandy shores'. In June the sun here does not set at all, and around then the ice finally breaks up.","w":"A big oval lake with pale edges on flat tundra dotted with thousands of ponds, south-east of its smaller twin.","f":["It is about 22 km long, and its greatest depth is only about 8 m.","It is one of the largest lakes on the Yamal Peninsula.","The lake lies about 150 km north of the Arctic Circle."],"q":{"q":"Roughly how deep is Yarroto First at its deepest?","a":"About 8 m","x":["About 80 m","About 300 m"]}},"ne-lk-1159113765":{"t":"Surprisingly deep lakes in the heart of Yamal","s":"Most tundra lakes are only a few metres deep, but the Neyto lakes in the middle of Yamal break the rule: the deepest of them goes down more than 50 m. Together the group covers about 364 km², and its waters drain two ways — towards the Gulf of Ob and towards the Kara Sea. Around them lies flat, treeless tundra.","w":"A group of large, linked lakes in flat treeless tundra in the middle of the Yamal Peninsula.","f":["The group's three main lakes are Neyto First, Yorto and Malto.","The deepest lake of the group reaches about 53 m.","In midwinter the sun does not rise here for more than a month."],"q":{"q":"How deep is the deepest of the Neyto lakes?","a":"More than 50 m","x":["About 5 m","About 500 m"]}},"ne-lk-1159113781":{"t":"Where the Mordyyakha starts its run to the Kara Sea","s":"Yambuto is the source of the Mordyyakha, one of the main rivers of central Yamal, which winds north-west to the Kara Sea. The lake spreads over roughly 119 km² of flat tundra, where permafrost lies just beneath the moss and summer lasts barely two months. In June and July the sun circles the sky without setting, and the water shines like foil.","w":"A large lake with a ragged outline in flat tundra, just east of the Neyto lakes in central Yamal.","f":["The Mordyyakha flows out of Yambuto and reaches the Kara Sea at Sharapov Shar Bay.","Permafrost under central Yamal can reach down hundreds of metres."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows out of Lake Yambuto?","a":"The Mordyyakha","x":["The Yuribey","The Nadym"]}},"Q1315765":{"t":"A forest lake drafted into a canal to the White Sea","s":"Since 1933 ships travelling between the Baltic and the White Sea have crossed Vygozero: the White Sea–Baltic Canal uses the lake as part of its route, and dams raised its level to make it deep enough. The result is a sprawling, island-strewn expanse in the Karelian forest. From its northern end the Vyg River runs on to meet the White Sea at Belomorsk.","w":"A large, ragged lake full of islands in Karelian forest, with the town of Segezha near its western shore.","f":["Raising the water for the canal made the lake much larger than it had been.","The Segezha River brings in water from Segozero, which lies to the west.","The lake is frozen for about half the year."],"q":{"q":"Which canal runs through Vygozero?","a":"The White Sea–Baltic Canal","x":["The Volga–Baltic Waterway","The Moscow Canal"]}},"Q1416209":{"t":"One of Karelia's deepest lakes, raised by a dam","s":"In its northern part Segozero plunges to more than 100 m, remarkably deep for a lake hidden in the Karelian forest. In 1957 a dam at the source of the Segezha River lifted the water by about six metres and turned the lake into a reservoir. More than 70 islands are scattered across it, while its south-western bays are shallow, rarely deeper than ten metres.","w":"A large, irregular lake with scattered islands in dense forest, west of the even bigger Vygozero.","f":["The 1957 dam raised the lake by about 6.3 m.","Its water flows out by the Segezha River into Vygozero.","The deepest water, more than 100 m down, lies in the north of the lake."],"q":{"q":"What happened to Segozero in 1957?","a":"A dam raised its level","x":["It was drained for farmland","A canal linked it to the Volga"]}},"Q845891":{"t":"The lake behind Finland's 'national landscape'","s":"From the bare hilltops of Koli, on Pielinen's western shore, Finnish artists of the 1890s looked down on a view that became a national symbol: long wooded islands afloat on a vast pale lake. From a plane you can take in far more of it than they could. In hard winters an ice road crosses the lake from Koli to the far shore.","w":"A long, wide lake running north–south through forested hills, with a steep wooded ridge on its western shore.","f":["Koli's highest top, Ukko-Koli, rises to 347 m, about 250 m above the lake.","Pielinen drains south by the Pielisjoki River, through Joensuu, towards Lake Saimaa.","It covers about 894 km², making it one of Finland's largest lakes."],"q":{"q":"Which famous viewpoint rises on Pielinen's western shore?","a":"Koli","x":["Pallastunturi","Aavasaksa"]}},"Q709323":{"t":"A 75-km lake strung with 144 islands","s":"Topozero runs for some 75 km through the forests of northern Karelia, a long sheet of water strewn with 144 islands. It is the fourth-largest lake in the republic and deep for this region, reaching 56 m. Its water spills north by the Sofyanga River into Pyaozero, and since the 1960s both lakes have been part of the same hydropower reservoir.","w":"A long, broad lake with many islands in dense forest, south of Pyaozero and not far below the Arctic Circle.","f":["The lake's open water covers about 986 km², and its islands add some 63 km² more.","Its average depth is around 16 m, and the deepest point is about 56 m.","It is about 75 km long and up to 30 km wide."],"q":{"q":"How many islands does Topozero have?","a":"144","x":["14","1,440"]}},"Q611206":{"t":"A freshwater lake at the edge of the Tarim desert","s":"Snowmelt from the Tian Shan gathers in the Kaidu River and pools here, in Bosten Lake — the largest freshwater lake in Xinjiang, set among dry mountains and desert. Its outflow, the Peacock River, cuts through a gorge to water the oasis city of Korla. Wide reed beds fringe its shores, turning gold in autumn against the blue water.","w":"A large blue lake with reedy margins in a brown basin, south of the Tian Shan and north of the Taklamakan.","f":["The lake covers roughly 1,000 km² and lies about 1,050 m above sea level.","The Kaidu River brings in most of its water, fed by Tian Shan snow and glaciers.","Korla, downstream on the Peacock River, is famous for its fragrant pears."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows out of Bosten Lake towards Korla?","a":"The Peacock (Kongque) River","x":["The Tarim River","The Ili River"]}},"Q207690":{"t":"China's largest freshwater lake — for half the year","s":"In the summer floods Poyang is China's largest freshwater lake, a brown-green sea thousands of square kilometres wide. In the dry winter it shrinks back into channels and pools, exposing vast grassy flats. That is when the Siberian cranes arrive: nearly the entire world population of these white birds spends the winter here. Mount Lu rises above the north-western shore.","w":"A huge branching lake, or in winter a maze of channels and green flats, south of the Yangtze with Mount Lu to the north-west.","f":["In some dry winters the lake shrinks to a small fraction of its summer size.","It meets the Yangtze at Hukou, whose name means 'lake mouth'.","Five main rivers of Jiangxi, the Gan among them, flow into it."],"q":{"q":"Nearly the world's entire population of which bird winters at Poyang?","a":"The Siberian crane","x":["The red-crowned crane","The crested ibis"]}},"ne-lk-1159114031":{"t":"One of a chain of lakes on the Yangtze's north bank","s":"North of the Yangtze, just downstream of where Poyang Lake empties into it, a chain of shallow lakes spreads across the floodplain: Longgan, Huang, Daguan and Bo. Together they form one of the largest lake groups along the river's middle and lower course. Daguan's level rises with the summer floods and drops in winter, when migrating waterbirds gather on its shores.","w":"A shallow lake with a ragged outline among fields and other lakes, north of the Yangtze and north-east of Poyang Lake's outlet.","f":["Daguan lies in Anhui province, in Susong and Wangjiang counties.","Neighbouring Longgan Lake is a national nature reserve for wintering waterbirds."],"q":{"q":"Which great river flows just south of Daguan Lake?","a":"The Yangtze","x":["The Yellow River","The Pearl River"]}},"Q6673852":{"t":"A lake shared by two provinces, busy with winter birds","s":"Longgan Lake straddles the border of Hubei and Anhui provinces on the north side of the Yangtze, the westernmost of a chain of floodplain lakes. At high water it covers some 420 km², a shallow sheet among rice fields and fish ponds. Its Hubei part is a national nature reserve, where big flocks of ducks, geese and other waterbirds spend the winter.","w":"A broad, shallow lake in flat farmland just north of the Yangtze, a short way north of the river's junction with Poyang Lake.","f":["The lake lies partly in Huangmei County, Hubei, and partly in Susong County, Anhui.","The Hubei part is protected as a national nature reserve for wetland birds.","About 180 bird species, most of them waterbirds, have been recorded in the reserve."],"q":{"q":"Which two provinces share Longgan Lake?","a":"Hubei and Anhui","x":["Hunan and Jiangxi","Henan and Shandong"]}},"Q7374197":{"t":"Mother lake of Wuhan's most famous fish","s":"Wuhan's best-known dish, Wuchang fish, is a bream that scientists first described from specimens caught here, and locals call Liangzi its 'mother lake'. The fish's fame spread across China after Mao Zedong mentioned it in a poem in 1956. From above, Liangzi is a many-armed lake with countless bays and islands, spread through green countryside south-east of Wuhan's sprawl.","w":"A many-armed lake with dozens of bays and islands in green farmland south of the Yangtze, between Wuhan and Ezhou.","f":["The type specimen of the blunt-snout bream, sold as Wuchang fish, came from Liangzi Lake.","Mao's 1956 line 'and now I eat Wuchang fish' made the dish famous across China.","It is one of the largest lakes of Hubei, long nicknamed the 'province of a thousand lakes'."],"q":{"q":"Which fish, famous in Wuhan cooking, was first described from Liangzi Lake?","a":"The Wuchang bream","x":["The mandarin fish","The grass carp"]}},"Q1404973":{"t":"The Tibetan lake that grew past Namtso","s":"For a long time Namtso was the largest lake in Tibet, but Siling Co has overtaken it: fed by melting glaciers and heavier rain, it has swollen by hundreds of square kilometres in recent decades. It lies at about 4,530 m in a wide, empty basin of the Changtang plateau. The salty water is an intense blue, and black-necked cranes breed on the marshes around it.","w":"A large, deep-blue lake with a lobed outline on a bare brown plateau, north-west of Namtso.","f":["Its area grew from roughly 1,600 km² in the 1970s to more than 2,300 km² in recent years.","The lake lies at the heart of a reserve for the black-necked crane, the only crane of high plateaus.","Even at 4,530 m, the lake is still some 6.5 km lower than a cruising airliner."],"q":{"q":"Which lake did Siling Co overtake as Tibet's largest?","a":"Namtso","x":["Yamdrok Tso","Manasarovar"]}},"Q1323267":{"t":"Mongolia's second-largest freshwater lake, in a dry basin","s":"The Khovd River comes down from the Altai and spreads into a vast marshy delta before it reaches Khar-Us Nuur, Mongolia's second-largest freshwater lake. Reed beds, channels and a big island called Agbash fill much of it, so from above it looks more like a green-and-blue mosaic than open water. All around lies the dry, brown Great Lakes Depression of western Mongolia.","w":"A large lake broken up by reeds and one big island, with a wide green delta on its western side, east of Khovd city.","f":["Agbash Island, inside the lake, covers about 274 km².","A channel carries water from Khar-Us Nuur on to neighbouring Khar Nuur.","The lake and its delta are protected as a national park and a Ramsar wetland."],"q":{"q":"Which river forms the big delta at Khar-Us Nuur?","a":"The Khovd","x":["The Zavkhan","The Selenge"]}},"Q6403":{"t":"A blue crescent with Mont Blanc on the horizon","s":"From cruise height Lake Geneva is an unmistakable blue crescent some 73 km long, with Geneva at one tip and the Rhône valley at the other. The northern shore is Swiss, most of the southern shore French. The Rhône flows in grey with glacier silt at the eastern end and leaves clear at Geneva. On a clear day Mont Blanc's white dome shines to the south-east.","w":"A large crescent-shaped lake curving between the Jura and the Alps, with Geneva at its south-western tip.","f":["The lake is about 310 m deep at its deepest and covers around 580 km².","The terraced Lavaux vineyards on its northern shore are a UNESCO World Heritage site.","Its French name, Lac Léman, comes from the Latin Lacus Lemannus."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Lake Geneva?","a":"The Rhône","x":["The Rhine","The Aare"]}},"Q206942":{"t":"The British Isles' biggest lake — a giant's handiwork?","s":"Legend says the giant Fionn mac Cumhaill scooped up a huge clod of earth and hurled it at a rival, leaving the hollow that filled to become Lough Neagh; the clod, they say, landed in the sea as the Isle of Man. The real origin is geological, but the lake is truly big — the largest in the British Isles by area. Belfast International Airport sits close to its eastern shore.","w":"A broad, roughly square lake in green farmland west of Belfast, with Belfast International Airport near its eastern shore.","f":["Five of Northern Ireland's six counties touch its shores.","It supports one of Europe's largest commercial fisheries for wild eels.","Although it covers about 390 km², it averages only around 9 m deep."],"q":{"q":"How many of Northern Ireland's six counties touch Lough Neagh?","a":"Five","x":["Three","All six"]}},"Q188195":{"t":"Sweden's long, clear, cold blade of water","s":"Vättern is a slim blue blade about 135 km long, cutting north–south through southern Sweden. Its water is so clean and cold that it supplies drinking water to about a quarter of a million people. Jönköping curls around the southern tip, the long island of Visingsö floats in its southern half, and the Göta Canal crosses it on the way between the Baltic and Lake Vänern.","w":"A long, narrow lake running north–south, with Jönköping at its southern tip and a slim island in its southern half.","f":["Vättern is Sweden's second-largest lake and reaches about 128 m deep.","The Göta Canal crosses the lake, linking the Baltic coast with Lake Vänern.","Vadstena, on the eastern shore, grew up around the abbey founded by St Birgitta's order in the 14th century."],"q":{"q":"Which canal crosses Lake Vättern?","a":"The Göta Canal","x":["The Kiel Canal","The Trollhätte Canal"]}},"Q845868":{"t":"A big lake at 74° north, free of ice for only weeks","s":"At about 74° N, Lake Taymyr is one of the most northerly large lakes on Earth, and its ice usually breaks up only in July. It is vast but shallow, on average just a few metres deep, and its long arms reach towards the bare ridges of the Byrranga Mountains to the north. Musk oxen, brought back to Taymyr in the 1970s, graze the tundra around it.","w":"A long, many-armed lake on flat tundra, with the low, bare Byrranga Mountains rising to the north.","f":["The Upper Taymyra River flows in, and the Lower Taymyra carries the water north to the Kara Sea.","Musk oxen were reintroduced to the Taymyr Peninsula from Canada and Alaska in 1974–1975.","Its area runs to thousands of square kilometres, yet its average depth is only about 3 m."],"q":{"q":"Which animals were brought back to Taymyr's tundra in the 1970s?","a":"Musk oxen","x":["Bison","Wild horses"]}},"Q240398":{"t":"A lake of 580 islands that doubled in size","s":"More than 580 islands are scattered across Kovdozero, so from above it reads like a jigsaw of dark forest pieces floating on grey-blue water. In 1955 a hydropower dam downstream raised its level by more than six metres, and the lake's surface roughly doubled. Its water runs on by the Kovda River to the White Sea's Kandalaksha Gulf, only a short distance to the east.","w":"A sprawling lake crowded with hundreds of wooded islands, just north of the Arctic Circle and west of the White Sea's Kandalaksha Gulf.","f":["Before the dam the lake covered about 294 km²; its surface is now about 608 km².","Its islands, more than 580 of them, add up to some 70 km².","The Kovda cascade of power stations uses the lake's water on its short run to the sea."],"q":{"q":"What happened to Kovdozero's area after the 1955 dam?","a":"It roughly doubled","x":["It halved","It barely changed"]}},"Q1345130":{"t":"A maze of 451 islands near Finland's eastern edge","s":"Some 451 islands are scattered across Koitere, a maze of wooded strips and open water deep in the forests of Ilomantsi, in North Karelia. The easternmost point of Finland — and of the European Union — lies not far to the east. The Koitajoki River flows out towards the Pielisjoki, although most of the lake's outflow is sent through a tunnel to a hydropower plant at Pamilo.","w":"An island-choked lake in unbroken forest near Finland's eastern border, about 60 km north-east of Joensuu.","f":["The lake covers about 164 km² and holds some 451 islands.","Finland's easternmost point, on the lake Virmajärvi, is in the same municipality of Ilomantsi.","A tunnel sends most of the outflow to the Pamilo power station, bypassing about 20 km of river."],"q":{"q":"Where is most of Koitere's outflow sent?","a":"Through a hydropower tunnel","x":["Into a canal to Lake Saimaa","Into irrigation channels"]}},"Q7334058":{"t":"The lake that broke through and dropped 9.5 metres","s":"In 1859 workers were digging a canal to lower this lake and win new farmland; instead the water broke through the sandy ridge of Jaamankangas and carved its own outlet. Within about a month Höytiäinen fell some 9.5 m and lost roughly a third of its area. Even so, it still spreads over about 283 km² of North Karelia, just north of Joensuu.","w":"A large lake in forest just north of Joensuu, separated from the Pyhäselkä basin to the south-west by a sandy ridge.","f":["The water level fell about 9.5 m within a month in 1859.","The lake shrank by roughly a third, and the exposed bed became new land.","Today it drains south-west into Pyhäselkä through the channel the water cut."],"q":{"q":"What happened to Höytiäinen in 1859?","a":"Its level fell about 9.5 m","x":["It froze to the bottom","It was dammed for hydropower"]}},"Q203462":{"t":"The lake on the old road from the Varangians to the Greeks","s":"A thousand years ago, merchants rowing from the Baltic towards Byzantium crossed Lake Ilmen: the route from the Varangians to the Greeks ran up the Volkhov, across the lake and on up the Lovat. At its northern tip the Volkhov leaves for Lake Ladoga, passing Veliky Novgorod. The lake is shallow and restless, swelling hugely each spring with meltwater from some fifty rivers.","w":"A broad, shallow lake in flat, marshy country, with Veliky Novgorod just beyond its northern tip on the Volkhov River.","f":["In the spring flood its area can nearly triple compared with the low-water season.","More than 50 rivers, including the Msta, Lovat and Shelon, flow in; only the Volkhov flows out.","In the Novgorod tale of Sadko, the minstrel's music charms a king who rises from Lake Ilmen."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows out of Lake Ilmen?","a":"The Volkhov","x":["The Msta","The Lovat"]}},"Q580019":{"t":"The 'Sea of Kainuu' and its old tar route","s":"Locals call Oulujärvi the Sea of Kainuu, and on a windy day its steep grey waves earn the name. In the 19th century the lake was a highway for tar: boats loaded with barrels crossed it and shot the rapids of the Oulujoki down to Oulu, then one of the world's great tar ports. Sandy islands and beaches rim its open water.","w":"A wide, open lake in Finland's forested interior, with Kajaani near its south-eastern end and a long wooded island in the middle.","f":["The lake covers roughly 900 km², one of the largest areas of open water in Finland.","Its water flows west by the Oulujoki River to the Gulf of Bothnia at Oulu.","Manamansalo, a long sandy island, lies in the middle of the lake."],"q":{"q":"What cargo did boats carry across Oulujärvi towards Oulu in the 1800s?","a":"Tar","x":["Iron ore","Salted fish"]}},"Q1888211":{"t":"A lake raised 3 m to send a river another way","s":"Southern Indian Lake was once simply a wide stretch of the Churchill River. In 1976 a dam at Missi Falls raised it by about 3 m, and now much of the Churchill's water leaves by a new channel on the lake's southern side, heading for the Rat and Burntwood rivers and the power stations of the Nelson. From above it is a huge lake of many broad basins in dense forest.","w":"A huge lake of many broad basins and islands in dense northern forest, on the Churchill River in northern Manitoba.","f":["The dam at Missi Falls, at the lake's natural outlet, holds the water about 3 m above its old level.","A channel at South Bay sends water into the Rat River and on to the Nelson River's hydro plants.","The diversion carries on average about 875 cubic metres of water a second."],"q":{"q":"Which river's water is diverted out of Southern Indian Lake?","a":"The Churchill","x":["The Saskatchewan","The Hayes"]}},"Q1052532":{"t":"Where the Saskatchewan River pauses before Lake Winnipeg","s":"The Saskatchewan River, which drains the prairies all the way from the Rocky Mountains, spreads into Cedar Lake before its final drop at Grand Rapids into Lake Winnipeg. Since the 1960s a hydroelectric dam at Grand Rapids has held the lake higher than before, drowning low shoreline forest and marsh. It is one link in the chain of big lakes that dominates the map of Manitoba.","w":"A broad, irregular lake in flat forest and marsh, just north of Lake Winnipegosis and west of the north basin of Lake Winnipeg.","f":["The Saskatchewan River flows through the lake and leaves by Grand Rapids for Lake Winnipeg.","The Grand Rapids generating station, finished in the 1960s, turned Cedar Lake into a reservoir.","Some of the river's water begins in glaciers of the Canadian Rockies."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Cedar Lake on its way to Lake Winnipeg?","a":"The Saskatchewan","x":["The Red River","The Assiniboine"]}},"Q979922":{"t":"A near-round lake that feeds the Saguenay fjord","s":"Lac Saint-Jean is almost round, about 1,000 km² of water in a flat, farmed lowland ringed by forested hills, an unmistakable shape from the air. It is the source of the Saguenay River, which leaves from the eastern side at Alma and runs down to its famous fjord. People from around here are nicknamed 'Bleuets', after the wild blueberries that grow in abundance.","w":"A large, almost circular lake in a flat lowland with forested hills all around, north of Quebec City.","f":["Each summer swimmers race about 32 km across it in the Traversée internationale du lac Saint-Jean.","Its big tributaries include the Péribonka, Mistassini and Ashuapmushuan rivers.","The ouananiche, a landlocked salmon, is the lake's emblem fish."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows out of Lac Saint-Jean?","a":"The Saguenay","x":["The Saint-Maurice","The Péribonka"]}},"Q22617464":{"t":"The sprawling reservoir of the James Bay hydro giant","s":"The Robert-Bourassa dam flooded some 2,800 km² of taiga in the late 1970s, turning hills into a maze of islands and long drowned valleys. Its water drives the Robert-Bourassa power station, carved deep into the rock and one of the largest underground power plants in the world. The spillway, ten giant steps blasted down a hillside, is nicknamed the Giant's Staircase.","w":"A vast, branching reservoir full of islands in flat taiga, stretching east from the lower dams of the La Grande River.","f":["The reservoir covers a little more than the area of Luxembourg.","It is named after Robert Bourassa, the Quebec premier who launched the James Bay project in 1971.","The nearby town of Radisson was built in the 1970s for the project's workers."],"q":{"q":"What is the Robert-Bourassa spillway nicknamed?","a":"The Giant's Staircase","x":["The Devil's Slide","The Stairway to Heaven"]}},"Q2179011":{"t":"The next giant step up the La Grande River","s":"Upstream of the Robert-Bourassa Reservoir, the La Grande River widens again into this second great artificial lake, created when the La Grande-3 dam closed in the early 1980s. It covers about 2,400 km², a ragged tangle of drowned valleys and hilltop islands that is hard to tell from the natural lakes around it. It is one step in a staircase of reservoirs and power stations descending towards James Bay.","w":"A huge ragged reservoir with countless islands in flat taiga, east of the Robert-Bourassa Reservoir along the La Grande River.","f":["The La Grande River drops through a series of dams and power stations before it reaches James Bay.","The gravel Trans-Taiga Road runs east past the reservoir deep into the interior of Quebec.","The La Grande complex is one of the largest hydroelectric systems in the world."],"q":{"q":"Into which bay does the La Grande River flow?","a":"James Bay","x":["Ungava Bay","Frobisher Bay"]}},"Q654515":{"t":"A desert reservoir with a pale 'bathtub ring'","s":"Lake Mead fills the canyons of the Colorado behind the Hoover Dam, and when full it is the largest reservoir in the United States by capacity. Years of drought have pulled the water far below that mark, leaving a pale band of minerals on the canyon walls — the famous 'bathtub ring' that frames the dark-blue water in the reddish desert.","w":"Deep-blue water branching through reddish desert canyons, with a pale rim along the shores, about 40 km east of Las Vegas.","f":["The Hoover Dam, at the lake's south-western end, was completed in 1936.","Las Vegas draws most of its water from the lake.","The lake is named after Elwood Mead, head of the Bureau of Reclamation while the dam was built."],"q":{"q":"Which dam holds back Lake Mead?","a":"The Hoover Dam","x":["The Glen Canyon Dam","The Grand Coulee Dam"]}},"Q667507":{"t":"A blue maze drowned in red-rock canyons","s":"Lake Powell is a startling sight from the air: fingers of deep-blue water threading through red and orange sandstone, filling the side canyons of the Colorado for nearly 300 km. It formed behind Glen Canyon Dam, closed in 1963, and took seventeen years to fill. It is named after John Wesley Powell, who led the first known expedition by boat through these canyons in 1869.","w":"Deep-blue water snaking through red-rock desert in countless branching canyons, with the dam and the town of Page at its south-western end.","f":["Its winding shoreline is longer than the whole Pacific coast of the contiguous United States.","Rainbow Bridge, one of the world's largest natural stone arches, stands in one of its side canyons.","The lake straddles the border between Utah and Arizona."],"q":{"q":"Which explorer is Lake Powell named after?","a":"John Wesley Powell","x":["Zebulon Pike","John C. Frémont"]}},"Q1539726":{"t":"A labyrinth of flooded lakes behind a silenced waterfall","s":"In the early 1970s dams and dykes on the Labrador plateau joined dozens of lakes into the Smallwood Reservoir, a labyrinth of water covering roughly 6,500 km². Its water now drives the underground Churchill Falls power station instead of pouring over Churchill Falls itself, a cascade of about 75 m that is now mostly a trickle. The reservoir is named after Joey Smallwood, Newfoundland's first premier as a Canadian province.","w":"A vast tangle of lakes, channels and islands on the flat, forested Labrador plateau, far inland from the Atlantic coast.","f":["The Churchill Falls power station, built deep underground, is one of the largest hydroelectric plants in the world.","The reservoir swallowed older lakes, among them Michikamau and Lobstick.","Joey Smallwood led Newfoundland into Canada in 1949 and became its first premier."],"q":{"q":"What happened to Churchill Falls once the reservoir was built?","a":"Most of its water was diverted","x":["It doubled in height","It froze over for good"]}},"Q770141":{"t":"A lake on the Niger that drowned Mungo Park's rapids","s":"The Scottish explorer Mungo Park died in 1806 in the rapids of the Niger at Bussa, while trying to follow the river to its mouth. Those rapids, and the old town of Bussa, now lie beneath Kainji Reservoir, formed when the Kainji Dam closed in the late 1960s. From above it is a long, many-armed lake in dry savanna, fed by the Niger from the north.","w":"A long, branching lake on the Niger River, running north–south through dry brown savanna in western Nigeria.","f":["The Kainji Dam was completed in 1968, and a new town, New Bussa, replaced the drowned one.","Kainji Lake National Park stretches beside the reservoir.","When full, the reservoir covers about 1,200 km²."],"q":{"q":"Which explorer died in rapids now drowned by Kainji Reservoir?","a":"Mungo Park","x":["David Livingstone","Richard Lander"]}},"Q1540436":{"t":"A former reservoir where a lost river meadow returns","s":"For almost seventy years this stretch of the Dnipro was the 'Kakhovka Sea', a reservoir more than 200 km long. Since 2023 it has largely emptied, and the Dnipro once again winds through the old bed, where young willows and poplars have sprung up with astonishing speed. The floodplain drowned in the 1950s was the Great Meadow, a legendary haunt of the Zaporozhian Cossacks.","w":"A long, wide valley of the Dnipro where the reservoir used to be: a green band of young forest with the river winding through it.","f":["The reservoir was created in the 1950s by a dam at Nova Kakhovka.","When full, it covered about 2,150 km², one of the largest reservoirs on the Dnipro.","The Great Meadow was a vast floodplain of channels, woods and islands below the Dnipro rapids."],"q":{"q":"What was the floodplain under the Kakhovka Reservoir called?","a":"The Great Meadow","x":["The Golden Steppe","The Cossack Marsh"]}},"Q1431500":{"t":"The widest stretch of the Dnipro, an inland 'sea'","s":"Here the Dnipro spreads so wide that people call it the Kremenchuk Sea: the reservoir is almost 30 km across in places and roughly 150 km long, the largest by area on the whole river. It filled in the early 1960s behind a dam near Kremenchuk. Cherkasy lines its western shore, where a long causeway and bridge carry road and rail across the water.","w":"A very wide blue stretch of the Dnipro with flat farmland all round, and the city of Cherkasy along its western shore.","f":["With about 2,250 km² of water, it is the largest reservoir on the Dnipro by area.","The Sula River enters from the east through wide marshes and reed beds."],"q":{"q":"Which river does the Kremenchuk Reservoir lie on?","a":"The Dnipro","x":["The Dniester","The Southern Bug"]}},"Q745937":{"t":"Europe's largest reservoir, where the Volga meets the Kama","s":"No reservoir in Europe covers more water. Behind the Zhiguli dam near Tolyatti the Volga backs up for some 500 km, and the Kama joins it in a vast bay south of Kazan. The reservoir filled in the late 1950s, drowning old riverside towns; Stavropol was moved and grew into Tolyatti. Near Ulyanovsk the water spreads so wide that the bridges across it run for kilometres.","w":"A huge, branching expanse of the Volga, many kilometres wide, with the city of Ulyanovsk on its western bank.","f":["With about 6,450 km² of water, it is the largest reservoir in Europe.","Sviyazhsk, an old town near Kazan, became an island when the reservoir filled in 1957.","The ruins of Bolgar, capital of Volga Bulgaria, stand on its eastern shore and are a UNESCO site."],"q":{"q":"Which river joins the Volga inside the Kuybyshev Reservoir?","a":"The Kama","x":["The Oka","The Sura"]}},"Q3234165":{"t":"Where the backlands really did turn into sea","s":"'The backlands will become sea,' goes an old prophecy of the Brazilian sertão, and at Sobradinho it came true. In the late 1970s a dam on the São Francisco River flooded more than 4,000 km² of dry scrubland, creating one of the largest artificial lakes in the world by area. Several riverside towns had to be rebuilt on higher ground.","w":"A huge, long lake of blue-grey water stretching through dry scrubland along the São Francisco River in northern Bahia.","f":["The São Francisco is the longest river that flows entirely within Brazil.","Towns including Remanso, Casa Nova, Sento Sé and Pilão Arcado were rebuilt away from the rising water.","The reservoir also evens out the river's flow for the power stations farther downstream."],"q":{"q":"Which river is dammed to form the Sobradinho Reservoir?","a":"The São Francisco","x":["The Paraná","The Tocantins"]}},"Q1464758":{"t":"The Caribbean's lowest point — with crocodiles","s":"Lago Enriquillo lies well below sea level, the lowest point in the Caribbean, in a hot, dry trough between two mountain ranges. Its water is saltier than the ocean, yet American crocodiles live here, and rhinoceros iguanas roam Isla Cabritos in the middle. The lake takes its name from Enriquillo, a Taíno chief who held out against the Spanish in the nearby mountains.","w":"A long, pale-blue lake with a large island, in a hot dry valley between mountain ranges in the south-west of the Dominican Republic.","f":["No point in the Caribbean islands lies lower than the surface of this lake.","Isla Cabritos, in the middle of the lake, is a national park known for its iguanas.","Since the early 2000s the lake has risen markedly, drowning roads and farmland."],"q":{"q":"Which reptile lives in salty Lago Enriquillo?","a":"The American crocodile","x":["The Nile crocodile","The spectacled caiman"]}},"Q199551":{"t":"A great salt lake turned red and white","s":"Lake Urmia was once one of the largest salt lakes in the world, a long turquoise sheet in north-western Iran. In recent decades it has lost most of its water, and much of its bed is now a glaring white salt flat. What remains can turn blood-red in summer, when salt-loving algae and bacteria bloom. A long causeway still runs straight across the middle.","w":"A long basin of white salt flats and patches of red or turquoise water, with a straight causeway crossing its middle.","f":["Its water became so salty that little but brine shrimp and microbes can live in it.","Flamingos have long bred on its islands in years with enough water.","The lake has no outlet; water leaves only by evaporation."],"q":{"q":"What turns Lake Urmia's water red in some summers?","a":"Salt-loving algae and bacteria","x":["Iron-rich mud from rivers","Volcanic gases"]}},"Q257416":{"t":"A desert lake filled by water drained from fields","s":"Before the 1960s this was largely a dry, salty hollow in the Karakum desert; today it holds one of the largest lakes in Central Asia. The water comes from farming: salty runoff from the irrigated cotton and rice fields of the lower Amu Darya is carried here by drainage canals. Long ago the river itself sometimes turned this way, and its water flowed on towards the Caspian.","w":"A large, irregular turquoise lake in brown desert, south of the pale cliffs of the Ustyurt plateau.","f":["The lake fills a depression whose floor lies below sea level.","Nothing flows out, so its water grows steadily saltier.","The Uzboy, a dry ancient riverbed, once carried water from here towards the Caspian Sea."],"q":{"q":"Where does most of Sarygamysh Lake's water come from?","a":"Drainage from irrigated fields","x":["Melting glaciers","Underground hot springs"]}},"Q310547":{"t":"A 'motley lake' at the mouth of a windy gate","s":"Alakol means 'motley lake', and its colours shift from turquoise to steel-grey as clouds race across it. They race for a reason: to the south-east lies the Dzungarian Gate, a mountain corridor between Kazakhstan and China through which fierce winds funnel. Holidaymakers prize its salty water, and islands in the lake shelter colonies of the rare relict gull.","w":"A large salt lake in a dry basin, with a narrow gap between mountains to its south-east leading towards China.","f":["The Ebe, a cold wind that blasts through the Dzungarian Gate, can reach storm force.","The lake has no outlet, which is why its water is salty.","For thousands of years the Dzungarian Gate has been a route for nomads and traders."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Alakol mean?","a":"Motley lake","x":["Salt lake","Windy lake"]}},"Q21837060":{"t":"South America's largest salt lake, pink with flamingos","s":"Mar Chiquita means 'little sea', and in wet years this salt lake on the plains of Córdoba spreads over thousands of square kilometres, the largest in South America. Its size swings with the rains: in the 1970s and 80s it rose so fast that part of the resort town of Miramar was swallowed. Big flocks of flamingos feed in its shallows, turning stretches of shore pink.","w":"A vast, pale, shallow lake on the flat pampas, often edged with white salt, north-east of the city of Córdoba.","f":["Three species of flamingo feed on the lake.","Since 2022 the lake and its wetlands have been protected as Ansenuza National Park.","The Dulce River brings in much of its water from the north."],"q":{"q":"What does the Spanish name Mar Chiquita mean?","a":"Little sea","x":["Salty sea","Silver sea"]}},"Q211823":{"t":"Turkey's salt pan, white as snow in summer","s":"In summer most of Lake Tuz dries out into a dazzling white crust of salt, so bright it looks like snow lying in the middle of the Anatolian steppe. It is Turkey's second-largest lake, yet only a metre or two deep, and one of the saltiest lakes on Earth. Salt works along its shores produce much of the salt eaten in Turkey.","w":"A huge white or pale-blue patch on the brown Anatolian plateau, roughly midway between Ankara and Konya.","f":["Its name simply means 'Salt Lake' in Turkish.","Greater flamingos gather on its islands to breed.","The lake lies about 905 m above sea level in a basin with no outlet."],"q":{"q":"What does 'Tuz' mean in Turkish?","a":"Salt","x":["White","Mirror"]}},"Q126307":{"t":"A soda sea on the Anatolian plateau, 1,640 m up","s":"Lake Van is so big and so alkaline that it feels like an inland sea: its water is rich in soda and feels soapy to the touch. The lake formed when lava from the Nemrut volcano blocked its outlet, and the snowy volcano Süphan, over 4,000 m high, still rises above the north shore. Only one fish, the pearl mullet, has adapted to its water.","w":"A huge blue lake on a high plateau, with a snowy volcano on its northern shore and a crater-topped mountain at its western end.","f":["Van is Turkey's largest lake, covering about 3,700 km² at some 1,640 m above sea level.","Each spring pearl mullet swim up freshwater streams to spawn.","On Akdamar Island stands a 10th-century church famous for its stone carvings."],"q":{"q":"What blocked Lake Van's outlet and created the lake?","a":"Lava from the Nemrut volcano","x":["A glacial moraine","A landslide from Süphan"]}},"Q199903":{"t":"A calm lake filling the crater of a super-eruption","s":"Lake Taupō, New Zealand's largest lake, fills a caldera left by some of the most violent eruptions of recent geological time. About 25,500 years ago the Oruanui eruption buried much of the North Island in ash and pumice, and around 1,800 years ago another eruption sent flows of hot pumice racing across the land. Today the calm blue water drains into the Waikato, New Zealand's longest river.","w":"A large, rounded blue lake in the middle of the North Island, with the snowy volcanoes of Tongariro rising to the south.","f":["The lake covers about 616 km² and is up to about 186 m deep.","The Waikato River, New Zealand's longest, flows out at the town of Taupō.","Trout introduced in the 19th century draw anglers from around the world."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows out of Lake Taupō?","a":"The Waikato","x":["The Whanganui","The Clutha"]}},"Q940488":{"t":"The short river that carries all of Lake Superior","s":"Every drop of water leaving Lake Superior passes down the St. Marys River on its way to Lake Huron. At the twin cities of Sault Ste. Marie it tumbles about 6 m over rapids, which is why ships climb and descend through the Soo Locks beside them. Below the rapids the river braids around large islands and widens into lakes before reaching Huron.","w":"A broad river channel with big islands and lake-like widenings, running south-east from the twin cities of Sault Ste. Marie.","f":["The river is about 120 km long and forms part of the border between the United States and Canada.","The Soo Locks let ships bypass the rapids, where Superior's level drops about 6 m to Huron's.","Sault Ste. Marie grew from a French mission founded in 1668, among the oldest in the Great Lakes region."],"q":{"q":"Which two lakes does the St. Marys River connect?","a":"Superior and Huron","x":["Huron and Erie","Michigan and Huron"]}},"Q188203":{"t":"A funnel of sea with one of the world's biggest tides","s":"Twice a day the sea surges up the Bristol Channel into the Severn Estuary, where the tide can rise and fall by around 14 m, one of the greatest tidal ranges in the world. The funnel shape does it: the channel narrows and shallows between the coasts of Wales and south-west England, squeezing the incoming water higher. Near its mouth the granite island of Lundy stands guard.","w":"A wide inlet narrowing eastward between the green coasts of south Wales and Devon, with a small island near its mouth.","f":["Where the channel meets the Severn Estuary, the tidal range can reach about 14 m.","The rising tide pushes a wave called the Severn Bore far up the river.","Lundy's name comes from the Old Norse for 'puffin island'."],"q":{"q":"What does the name of Lundy island mean in Old Norse?","a":"Puffin island","x":["Seal island","Storm island"]}},"Q1971856":{"t":"An island-studded sea where planes land on a beach","s":"Between the long chain of the Outer Hebrides and the mountainous islands of Skye, Rùm, Coll and Tiree lies the Sea of the Hebrides, scattered with islands of every size. On Barra, at its western edge, scheduled flights land on the sands of Traigh Mhòr, and the timetable follows the tides. In summer basking sharks and minke whales feed in these cold, rich waters.","w":"Grey-blue sea scattered with islands, between the long Outer Hebrides chain to the west and Skye's jagged mountains to the east.","f":["Barra's airport uses a tidal beach as its runway, a rarity among scheduled airports.","The Cuillin ridge on Skye is one of the most rugged mountain ranges in Britain.","Basking sharks, the world's second-largest fish, are often seen here in summer."],"q":{"q":"What serves as the runway at Barra's airport?","a":"A tidal sandy beach","x":["A frozen lake","A floating pontoon"]}},"Q35000":{"t":"The world's shallowest sea, rimmed by pink lagoons","s":"No sea on Earth is shallower: the Sea of Azov is nowhere deeper than about 14 m, and much of it is far less. Two great rivers, the Don and the Kuban, pour in fresh water, so it is only mildly salty and often freezes in winter. On its western side the thin Arabat Spit, over 100 km long, walls off the Sivash, a maze of lagoons tinted pink and red.","w":"An enclosed greenish sea with a long, thread-thin sand spit along its western shore and pinkish lagoons behind it.","f":["Its greatest depth is only about 14 m.","The narrow Kerch Strait connects it with the Black Sea.","The Sivash lagoons are so salty that microscopic algae tint them pink."],"q":{"q":"Roughly how deep is the Sea of Azov at its deepest?","a":"About 14 m","x":["About 140 m","About 1,400 m"]}},"Q14686":{"t":"Two capitals and an imperial city on one Baltic gulf","s":"Helsinki and Tallinn face each other across the Gulf of Finland, only about 80 km apart, and at its far eastern end lies St Petersburg, Russia's capital for two centuries. The gulf is shallow and barely salty, so in cold winters its eastern part freezes over. Near St Petersburg a thin line crosses the water: a flood barrier running through Kotlin Island.","w":"A long, narrow arm of the Baltic running east, with Helsinki on the north shore, Tallinn on the south and St Petersburg at its head.","f":["The Neva, entering at St Petersburg, carries more water than any other river flowing into the Baltic.","The St Petersburg flood barrier stretches about 25 km across the head of the gulf.","Thousands of rocky islands and skerries line the Finnish coast."],"q":{"q":"Which two capitals face each other across the Gulf of Finland?","a":"Helsinki and Tallinn","x":["Helsinki and Riga","Stockholm and Tallinn"]}},"Q210118":{"t":"A curving coast of lagoons, flamingos and pink salt pans","s":"The Gulf of Lion sweeps in a broad arc from the Pyrenees to Provence, and its shore is a string of lagoons cut off from the sea by thin sandbars. In the middle sprawls the Camargue, the Rhône delta, where salt pans glow pink and flamingos wade. Two cold winds, the Mistral and the Tramontane, often rush down here, whipping the sea white.","w":"A wide, gently curved bay of the Mediterranean with a lagoon-lined coast, the Rhône delta in the middle and the Pyrenees at its south-western end.","f":["The salt pans of Aigues-Mortes and Salin-de-Giraud turn pink thanks to salt-loving microalgae.","The Camargue is one of the main breeding grounds of greater flamingos in the Mediterranean.","The Canal du Midi ends in the Étang de Thau, the big lagoon beside the port of Sète."],"q":{"q":"Which river's delta forms the Camargue?","a":"The Rhône","x":["The Garonne","The Loire"]}},"Q232912":{"t":"Where the Gulf Stream races out into the Atlantic","s":"A river runs through this sea: the Florida Current, the start of the Gulf Stream, pours north through the strait carrying more water than all the world's rivers combined. From above, the deep channel shows dark indigo, sharply edged by the glowing turquoise shallows of the Bahama Banks to the east. Florida's coast and the curve of the Keys frame it to the west.","w":"Deep indigo water between the Florida coast and the brilliant turquoise shallows of the Bahama Banks.","f":["At its core the current moves faster than a person walks.","Ponce de León's pilots noticed the powerful current here in 1513.","The strait separates the Florida Keys from Cuba, about 150 km to the south."],"q":{"q":"Which famous current flows through the Florida Strait?","a":"The Gulf Stream","x":["The Humboldt Current","The Labrador Current"]}},"Q140290":{"t":"Named by Cook for the food it gave his crew","s":"When James Cook sailed along this coast in 1769, Māori communities supplied his ship generously, and he called it the Bay of Plenty. The name still fits: behind long white beaches spread kiwifruit and avocado orchards, and the town of Te Puke calls itself the kiwifruit capital of the world. The volcanic cone of Mauao stands guard at the entrance to Tauranga Harbour.","w":"A broad, open bay on the North Island's northern coast, with long beaches, a small cone at Tauranga harbour and islands offshore.","f":["Cook named Poverty Bay, farther east, after a stop where he could get nothing he wanted.","Mayor Island (Tūhua), offshore, was an important source of obsidian for early Māori.","Mauao, at Tauranga's harbour entrance, is an extinct volcano about 230 m high."],"q":{"q":"Why did Cook name it the Bay of Plenty?","a":"Food and supplies were plentiful","x":["It had many islands","Its beaches seemed endless"]}},"Q2565098":{"t":"Open Pacific between Haida Gwaii and Vancouver Island","s":"Between the southern tip of Haida Gwaii and the northern end of Vancouver Island, the coast of British Columbia opens straight onto the Pacific, and Queen Charlotte Sound takes the full force of its swells. Ships following the sheltered Inside Passage must cross this exposed stretch. To the east the mainland shatters into fjords and islands cloaked in the Great Bear Rainforest.","w":"Open ocean between the north tip of Vancouver Island and Haida Gwaii, with a coast of fjords and forested islands to the east.","f":["Haida Gwaii, to the north-west, was known as the Queen Charlotte Islands until 2010.","The Great Bear Rainforest on the mainland coast is one of the largest temperate rainforests in the world.","Humpback and grey whales pass along this coast on their long migrations."],"q":{"q":"What was Haida Gwaii called until 2010?","a":"The Queen Charlotte Islands","x":["The Vancouver Islands","The Prince Rupert Islands"]}},"Q212428":{"t":"A gulf of limestone towers and one tide a day","s":"In the north-western corner of the Gulf of Tonkin, some 1,600 limestone islands rise straight out of green water — Ha Long Bay, one of Asia's most famous seascapes. The gulf is shallow and sheltered by the big island of Hainan to the east. Its tides are unusual too: along much of this coast the sea rises and falls only once a day, not twice.","w":"A broad, shallow gulf between Vietnam's coast and the island of Hainan, with a maze of rocky islets in its north-western corner.","f":["Ha Long Bay's limestone islands and islets are a UNESCO World Heritage site.","The Red River pours reddish-brown silt into the gulf's western side.","In Vietnamese it is called Vịnh Bắc Bộ, the Gulf of the North."],"q":{"q":"How many high tides a day does much of the Gulf of Tonkin get?","a":"One","x":["Two","Four"]}},"Q181857":{"t":"The highest tides on Earth, twice a day","s":"Twice a day the Bay of Fundy fills and empties, and in its upper reaches the tide rises by as much as 16 m — the highest tides on Earth. At low water, vast red-brown mudflats appear where boats floated a few hours before. The long funnel of the bay does the work: its natural rhythm almost matches the ocean tide, so the water piles up higher and higher.","w":"A long, funnel-shaped bay between New Brunswick and Nova Scotia, its upper arms edged with wide red-brown mudflats.","f":["In the Minas Basin the difference between high and low tide can reach about 16 m.","At Hopewell Rocks, visitors walk on the sea floor at low tide around rocks shaped like giant flowerpots.","The Joggins Fossil Cliffs on its shore preserve forests from some 300 million years ago."],"q":{"q":"Roughly how high can the Bay of Fundy's tides reach?","a":"About 16 m","x":["About 4 m","About 40 m"]}},"Q35827":{"t":"A brown 'river' so wide the far shore vanishes","s":"The Paraná and Uruguay rivers meet and pour into the Atlantic through the Río de la Plata, a funnel of brown, silty water up to about 220 km wide at its mouth. It is often called the widest river in the world, though geographers class it as an estuary. Buenos Aires spreads along one shore and Montevideo sits on the other, where brown river water meets the blue sea.","w":"A huge funnel of brown water opening to the south-east, with the sprawl of Buenos Aires on its south-western shore and Montevideo on the north.","f":["Spanish explorers named it the 'River of Silver', hoping it led to silver mines inland.","Two capitals, Buenos Aires and Montevideo, face each other across it.","Its muddy colour comes from sediment carried down by the Paraná, one of South America's great rivers."],"q":{"q":"Which two rivers join to form the Río de la Plata?","a":"The Paraná and the Uruguay","x":["The Paraná and the Paraguay","The Amazon and the Uruguay"]}},"Q205655":{"t":"A strait so busy that ships dot it like grains of rice","s":"On a clear day the Singapore Strait looks peppered with white dots: tankers, container ships and bulk carriers under way or waiting at anchor, each trailing a thin wake. This narrow channel, only about 16 km wide, links the Strait of Malacca with the South China Sea and is one of the busiest shipping lanes on Earth. Singapore lies on the north side, Indonesia's Riau Islands on the south.","w":"A narrow channel crowded with ships, between the dense city of Singapore to the north and the green islands of Batam and Bintan.","f":["The strait is about 105 km long.","The Port of Singapore is one of the busiest container ports in the world.","At night the anchored ships glitter like a floating city."],"q":{"q":"Which two waters does the Singapore Strait link?","a":"Malacca Strait and South China Sea","x":["Java Sea and Indian Ocean","Andaman Sea and Gulf of Thailand"]}},"Q52052":{"t":"The sea gate between Korea and Japan, split by Tsushima","s":"Only about 200 km of water separate Korea from Japan's island of Kyushu here, and the island of Tsushima sits in the middle, splitting the strait into two channels. On a clear day Busan's coast and Tsushima's green hills can be seen at the same time from cruise height. A warm current from the south flows through, keeping the Japanese shores of the Sea of Japan milder.","w":"A broad strait between Korea's south coast and Kyushu, with the long, hilly island of Tsushima in the middle.","f":["Tsushima lies only about 50 km from the Korean coast.","The warm Tsushima Current, a branch of the Kuroshio, flows north-east through the strait.","Ferries link Busan and Fukuoka across the strait in a matter of hours."],"q":{"q":"Which island divides the Korea Strait into two channels?","a":"Tsushima","x":["Jeju","Sado"]}},"Q231312":{"t":"A sea of three thousand islands stitched by bridges","s":"Some 3,000 islands are scattered across the Seto Inland Sea, the calm water enclosed by Honshu, Shikoku and Kyushu. Three chains of bridges now stitch Honshu to Shikoku across it; from cruise height they show as fine threads hopping from island to island. At the Naruto Strait, on its eastern edge, the tide spins whirlpools up to about 20 m across.","w":"A long, calm inland sea crowded with green islands between Honshu and Shikoku, crossed by several chains of bridges.","f":["The Akashi Kaikyō Bridge, at its eastern end, held the record for the longest suspension span from 1998 to 2022.","Itsukushima Shrine on Miyajima, with its torii gate standing in the sea, is a UNESCO site.","In 1934 the Inland Sea became one of Japan's first national parks."],"q":{"q":"Which two main islands of Japan are linked by bridges across this sea?","a":"Honshu and Shikoku","x":["Honshu and Hokkaido","Kyushu and Shikoku"]}},"Q127031":{"t":"A shallow strait that was dry land in the Ice Age","s":"Between the mountainous island of Taiwan and the coast of Fujian lies a strait about 130 to 180 km wide, yet surprisingly shallow, mostly less than 100 m deep. During the last Ice Age, when sea level fell, much of it was dry land that animals could walk across. In the middle lie the low, flat Penghu Islands, fringed with pale reefs.","w":"A wide channel between the high, cloud-capped mountains of Taiwan and the Fujian coast, with a cluster of flat islands in the middle.","f":["Its narrowest crossing is about 130 km.","The Penghu Islands are famous for cliffs of columnar basalt.","Fishing boats sometimes haul up fossils of Ice Age animals from its sea floor."],"q":{"q":"What was much of the Taiwan Strait during the last Ice Age?","a":"Dry land","x":["A freshwater lake","A glacier"]}},"Q1129590":{"t":"A tidal inlet guarded by a row of volcanoes","s":"Tikahtnu, the Dena'ina name for Cook Inlet, runs about 290 km from the Gulf of Alaska up to Anchorage, and its tides are among the biggest in North America. Along its western shore stands a line of volcanoes — Augustine, Iliamna, Redoubt and Spurr — often snow-capped and sometimes steaming. James Cook sailed in here in 1778, hoping it might lead to a passage to the Atlantic.","w":"A long, silty-grey inlet running north-east towards Anchorage, with snowy volcanic cones along its western shore.","f":["At Anchorage the tide can rise and fall by about 9 m.","Augustine volcano forms its own island near the inlet's mouth.","A small, isolated population of beluga whales lives in these waters."],"q":{"q":"Which city stands at the head of Cook Inlet?","a":"Anchorage","x":["Juneau","Fairbanks"]}},"Q917872":{"t":"Home of the world's biggest sockeye salmon run","s":"Every summer tens of millions of sockeye salmon swim in from the Bering Sea and head up the rivers that drain into Bristol Bay — the largest run of these fish anywhere on Earth. Fishing boats crowd its muddy estuaries in July, and brown bears line the streams inland. James Cook named the bay in 1778 after Admiral the Earl of Bristol.","w":"A broad, shallow bay of the Bering Sea, with the long Alaska Peninsula forming its southern shore.","f":["Male walruses haul out by the thousand on Round Island, in the north of the bay.","The Kvichak River carries water from Lake Iliamna, Alaska's largest lake, into the bay.","Katmai National Park, famous for bears fishing at Brooks Falls, lies on its south-eastern side."],"q":{"q":"Which fish makes Bristol Bay famous?","a":"Sockeye salmon","x":["Atlantic cod","Bluefin tuna"]}},"Q2336534":{"t":"Where Greenland's ice sheet spills into the sea","s":"Along Melville Bay the Greenland ice sheet reaches the sea almost without a break, pouring through dozens of glaciers that calve icebergs into the water. For 19th-century whalers this was one of the most feared stretches of the Arctic: many ships were crushed by drifting ice while trying to pass. Today much of it is a nature reserve, home to polar bears and narwhals.","w":"A wide bay on Greenland's north-west coast where the white ice sheet spills straight into a sea full of icebergs.","f":["The Melville Bay Nature Reserve protects polar bears and narwhals.","John Ross named it in 1818 after Viscount Melville, First Lord of the Admiralty.","Sea ice covers the bay for much of the year."],"q":{"q":"Which Arctic whale with a single long tusk lives in Melville Bay?","a":"The narwhal","x":["The beluga","The bowhead whale"]}},"Q876470":{"t":"A cold, whale-rich sea closed off by an undersea bank","s":"From the curled arm of Cape Cod to the tip of Nova Scotia, the Gulf of Maine is almost a sea of its own, cut off from the open Atlantic by the shallow Georges Bank. Its cold, rich water draws humpback and right whales to feed. Along the Maine coast, thousands of rocky islands and drowned valleys make one of the most ragged shorelines in America.","w":"A broad, cold gulf edged by the sandy hook of Cape Cod to the south-west and the rocky, island-studded coast of Maine to the north.","f":["The Bay of Fundy, with the world's highest tides, is one of its arms.","Georges Bank, at its mouth, was for centuries one of the richest fishing grounds on Earth.","For part of the year, Cadillac Mountain on its coast is among the first places in the US to see sunrise."],"q":{"q":"Which shallow undersea bank partly encloses the Gulf of Maine?","a":"Georges Bank","x":["The Grand Banks","Dogger Bank"]}},"Q216868":{"t":"America's largest estuary, over an ancient impact crater","s":"Chesapeake Bay runs some 300 km from the mouth of the Susquehanna to the Atlantic, a branching estuary fed by dozens of rivers and the largest in the United States. Its mouth hides a surprise: an asteroid crater about 35 million years old, buried under sediment. There a thin bridge line crosses the water and twice seems to vanish — the Chesapeake Bay Bridge-Tunnel diving underwater.","w":"A long, branching bay with many river mouths, running north–south from Maryland to Virginia's Atlantic coast.","f":["The Chesapeake Bay Bridge-Tunnel is about 28 km long, with two tunnels that let ships pass over them.","The bay is famous for its blue crabs.","More than 150 rivers and streams flow into it."],"q":{"q":"What lies buried beneath the mouth of Chesapeake Bay?","a":"An asteroid impact crater","x":["A sunken volcano","A glacial valley"]}},"Q1536292":{"t":"A Patagonian bay where water-seekers struck oil","s":"In 1907 drillers at Comodoro Rivadavia, on the shore of this gulf, were searching for fresh water in the parched Patagonian steppe. They struck oil instead, and Argentina's oil industry was born. Today the San Jorge Gulf is a broad, windswept bay about 250 km across, its dry brown shores dotted with oil wells and scoured by strong westerly winds.","w":"A wide, semicircular bay on the Patagonian coast, edged by dry brown tablelands, with Comodoro Rivadavia on its western shore.","f":["The gulf stretches about 250 km between Cape Dos Bahías and Cape Tres Puntas.","The San Jorge basin around it is one of Argentina's main oil-producing regions."],"q":{"q":"What did drillers looking for water find at Comodoro Rivadavia in 1907?","a":"Oil","x":["Gold","Coal"]}},"Q200712":{"t":"Turquoise shallows between Spain and its islands","s":"The Balearic Sea lies between Spain's Mediterranean coast and the islands of Mallorca, Ibiza and Formentera. Where it is shallow, meadows of Posidonia seagrass over pale sand make the water glow turquoise, especially in the channel between Ibiza and Formentera. On the mainland side the Ebro delta pushes a green-and-brown triangle far out into the blue.","w":"Deep-blue sea between the Spanish coast and the Balearic Islands, with turquoise shallows around Ibiza and the Ebro delta jutting from the mainland.","f":["The Posidonia meadows between Ibiza and Formentera are part of a UNESCO World Heritage site.","Posidonia is not a seaweed but a flowering plant found only in the Mediterranean.","Much of the Ebro delta, one of the largest wetlands in the western Mediterranean, is covered with rice fields."],"q":{"q":"What makes the shallows around Ibiza and Formentera glow turquoise?","a":"Seagrass meadows over pale sand","x":["Coral reefs","Volcanic minerals"]}},"Q34575":{"t":"A sea of islands with a drowned volcano at its heart","s":"Thousands of islands are scattered across the Aegean between the coasts of Greece and Anatolia, and from cruise height they look like crumbs of brown and white on deep blue. In the south, a broken ring of cliffs around a flooded bay is Santorini, the rim of a volcano that erupted about 3,600 years ago. In summer the strong meltemi wind streaks the sea with whitecaps.","w":"Deep-blue sea scattered with rocky brown islands, with a broken ring-shaped island in the south.","f":["In Greek myth it is named after King Aegeus, who threw himself into the sea believing his son Theseus was dead.","The Cyclades take their name from the circle they form around the sacred island of Delos.","The meltemi, a dry northerly wind, blows across it through much of the summer."],"q":{"q":"In Greek myth, which king gave his name to the Aegean Sea?","a":"Aegeus","x":["Minos","Priam"]}},"Q622811":{"t":"A sea edged by a chain of sandbanks called Adam's Bridge","s":"Between India and Sri Lanka runs a strange line of shoals and sandbanks about 48 km long, pale against turquoise water: Adam's Bridge, known in India as Rama Setu, the bridge that the Ramayana says Rama's army built. It marks the northern edge of the Gulf of Mannar, a warm sea of coral islets, seagrass and dugongs. For centuries its oyster beds were famous for pearls.","w":"A string of pale sandbanks linking India's Pamban Island with Sri Lanka's Mannar Island, with turquoise shallows to the south.","f":["Twenty-one small islands in the gulf make up India's Gulf of Mannar Marine National Park.","Dugongs, the sea cows that graze on seagrass, still live in the gulf.","The pearl fisheries off Thoothukudi (Tuticorin) have been known since ancient times."],"q":{"q":"What is the chain of shoals between India and Sri Lanka called?","a":"Adam's Bridge (Rama Setu)","x":["Palk Causeway","Mannar Reef"]}},"Q181969":{"t":"China's inner sea, where the Yellow River ends","s":"The Bohai is almost enclosed: the Liaodong and Shandong peninsulas reach towards each other and leave only a narrow, island-strewn gap to the Yellow Sea. It is shallow, averaging less than 20 m, and in hard winters ice forms along its shores. On the south-western side a brown plume fans into the sea — the mouth of the Yellow River, whose delta keeps growing.","w":"A nearly enclosed, often murky sea between two peninsulas, with a brown river plume on its south-western shore.","f":["The Great Wall meets the sea at Shanhaiguan, on its north-western shore.","Tianjin, one of China's biggest ports, stands on its western shore.","The Bohai Strait between the two peninsulas is only about 100 km wide."],"q":{"q":"Which great river flows into the Bohai Sea?","a":"The Yellow River","x":["The Yangtze","The Amur"]}},"Q844917":{"t":"Coral reefs, mangroves and a salt desert next door","s":"The Gulf of Kutch pushes about 160 km into the coast of Gujarat, a funnel of muddy water with strong tides. Yet its southern shore hides coral reefs and mangrove islands, protected since 1982 as India's first marine national park. To the north stretches the Rann of Kutch, a vast salt marsh that turns into a dazzling white desert in the dry season.","w":"A long, funnel-shaped gulf of brownish water on India's west coast, with mangrove-fringed islands in the south and white salt flats to the north.","f":["Tides in the inner gulf rise and fall by several metres, among the largest on India's coast.","Big cargo ports at Kandla and Mundra stand on its northern shore.","Dolphins, sea turtles and a few dugongs live in the marine national park."],"q":{"q":"What was set up in the Gulf of Kutch in 1982?","a":"India's first marine national park","x":["India's first nuclear power plant","India's first oil refinery"]}},"Q645012":{"t":"Where ships queue for the Panama Canal","s":"At the head of the Gulf of Panama, dozens of ships often lie at anchor, waiting their turn to enter the Panama Canal and cross to the Caribbean. Behind them rise the towers of Panama City. Out in the gulf, the green Pearl Islands earned their name: La Peregrina, one of the most famous pearls in history, was found there in the 16th century.","w":"A broad Pacific bay with Panama City on its northern shore, ships anchored offshore and a cluster of green islands to the south-east.","f":["The canal's Pacific entrance lies at the head of the gulf, next to Panama City.","Surprisingly, the Pacific end of the canal lies farther east than its Atlantic end.","From about July to October humpback whales from the Southern Hemisphere come here to breed."],"q":{"q":"Which Panama Canal entrance lies farther east?","a":"The Pacific entrance","x":["The Atlantic entrance","Neither: they align north–south"]}},"Q1974744":{"t":"The western doorway of the Northwest Passage","s":"Ships threading the Northwest Passage from the Pacific side enter the Canadian Arctic islands here, between Banks Island to the north and the mainland to the south. Roald Amundsen sailed through in 1905 aboard the little Gjøa, completing the first voyage along the whole passage the following year, and the gulf now bears his name. For most of the year it is covered in ice.","w":"A broad arm of the Arctic Ocean between flat Banks Island to the north and the mainland coast, usually white with ice.","f":["An area of open water that often stays unfrozen, the Cape Bathurst polynya, forms at its western end.","Bowhead and beluga whales pass through it in summer.","Victoria Island, one of the largest islands in the world, borders it to the east."],"q":{"q":"Whose ship first sailed the whole Northwest Passage, via this gulf?","a":"Roald Amundsen's","x":["John Franklin's","Robert McClure's"]}},"Q5605258":{"t":"An ice-choked sound where Parry won a prize","s":"In September 1819 William Parry's ships pushed west into this sound and crossed 110° W, winning a £5,000 reward offered by Parliament for getting that far along the Northwest Passage. Beyond it the ice stopped them. Viscount Melville Sound is still one of the passage's hardest stretches: thick, old sea ice drifts in from the Arctic Ocean and often lingers all summer.","w":"A wide channel of white sea ice between Melville Island to the north and Victoria Island to the south.","f":["It is named after Viscount Melville, First Lord of the Admiralty at the time.","Parry's crews wintered at Winter Harbour on Melville Island in 1819–1820.","The sound is part of Parry Channel, the main east–west route of the Northwest Passage."],"q":{"q":"How much prize money did Parry's crews win for crossing 110° W?","a":"£5,000","x":["£500","£50,000"]}},"Q1165951":{"t":"An Arctic bay whose tides rival the Bay of Fundy","s":"Ungava Bay's tides are contenders for the world record: in Leaf Basin, on its western side, the sea can rise and fall by around 16 m, rivalling the Bay of Fundy. The bay is frozen for much of the year, and floes grind along its shores as the tide lifts and drops them. Kuujjuaq, the largest community in Nunavik, lies up the Koksoak River near its southern end.","w":"A broad bay in far northern Quebec opening north into Hudson Strait, with a large, flat-topped island near its mouth.","f":["Akpatok Island, in the bay, is ringed by high limestone cliffs where seabirds nest.","Kuujjuaq is the administrative centre of Nunavik, the Inuit region of northern Quebec.","The bay opens into Hudson Strait, the passage between the Atlantic and Hudson Bay."],"q":{"q":"Which famous bay's tides do Ungava Bay's tides rival?","a":"The Bay of Fundy","x":["The Bay of Biscay","Hudson Bay"]}},"Q243125":{"t":"A sea at the top of the world, locked in old ice","s":"The Lincoln Sea lies at about 83° N, between the northern tips of Ellesmere Island and Greenland, less than 800 km from the North Pole. Its sea ice is among the thickest and oldest in the Arctic, pressed against the coast by the drifting pack. On its shore stands Alert, the northernmost permanently inhabited place on Earth.","w":"A frozen expanse of jumbled white pack ice, bordered by the dark, mountainous northern coasts of Ellesmere Island and Greenland.","f":["Cape Columbia on Ellesmere Island, the northernmost point of Canada, overlooks it.","Greenland's Cape Morris Jesup, near its eastern edge, is among the northernmost points of land on Earth.","Scientists call this region the 'Last Ice Area', expected to keep its summer ice the longest."],"q":{"q":"Which settlement on the Lincoln Sea is the northernmost inhabited place?","a":"Alert","x":["Qaanaaq","Longyearbyen"]}},"Q171846":{"t":"The stormy strait that proved Tasmania an island","s":"Until 1798 Europeans did not know whether Tasmania was joined to the mainland. Then George Bass and Matthew Flinders sailed right around it through this strait, which now bears Bass's name. It is about 240 km wide but shallow, mostly 50 to 70 m deep, so the westerly gales of the Roaring Forties whip up short, steep and dangerous seas.","w":"A wide stretch of sea between Victoria's coast and Tasmania, dotted with islands: King Island in the west, the Furneaux group in the east.","f":["During the last Ice Age the strait was dry land, and people walked from the mainland to Tasmania.","Oil and gas have been produced from platforms in its eastern part since the late 1960s.","Flinders Island, the largest of the Furneaux group, is named after Matthew Flinders."],"q":{"q":"Who is Bass Strait named after?","a":"George Bass","x":["Abel Tasman","Matthew Flinders"]}},"Q1135336":{"t":"An ice-choked strait that trapped McClure's ship","s":"Thick, old ice from the Arctic Ocean jams into M'Clure Strait, and ships that try this western route of the Northwest Passage rarely get through. Robert McClure's expedition tried in 1851; his ship Investigator became trapped in the ice of Mercy Bay on Banks Island and had to be abandoned. His men were rescued by sledge from the east — and so became the first to cross the whole passage.","w":"A broad channel of white pack ice between the flat north coast of Banks Island and Melville Island.","f":["The wreck of HMS Investigator was found in shallow water in Mercy Bay in 2010.","The strait opens west onto the Beaufort Sea and the Arctic Ocean.","It is named after Robert McClure, the Irish-born naval officer who led the expedition."],"q":{"q":"Which ship did McClure abandon in the ice near this strait?","a":"HMS Investigator","x":["HMS Erebus","HMS Terror"]}},"Q2328243":{"t":"The blue whales' dining room below a hunchbacked volcano","s":"Each summer the largest animals that have ever lived come here to feed: blue whales gather in the Gulf of Corcovado, between Chiloé Island and the Chilean mainland, to eat swarms of krill. The gulf takes its name from the Corcovado volcano on the mainland shore, whose lopsided summit looks like a hunched back. Rain-soaked forest covers almost every slope around it.","w":"A wide gulf between the green island of Chiloé and the mountainous mainland, with a snow-capped volcano on the eastern shore.","f":["Corcovado volcano rises about 2,300 m above the gulf.","The gulf is one of the most important feeding grounds for blue whales in the Southern Hemisphere.","Chiloé's wooden churches, a UNESCO site, stand on the island's shores to the west."],"q":{"q":"What does 'Corcovado' mean in Spanish?","a":"Hunchbacked","x":["Crowned","Smoking"]}},"Q281638":{"t":"An Antarctic gulf named after a chewing-gum magnate","s":"Wrigley Gulf curves along the coast of Marie Byrd Land, in front of the long Getz Ice Shelf, and very few people have ever seen it. An American expedition discovered it in December 1940 and named it after Philip Wrigley, the Chicago chewing-gum manufacturer who had helped fund the venture. Nearby, the huge snowy dome of Mount Siple, a volcano, rises above the ice.","w":"A broad bay of sea ice and icebergs along the edge of a flat white ice shelf, with a large volcanic dome to the east.","f":["The gulf is about 185 km wide.","Mount Siple, on Siple Island nearby, is a volcano more than 3,000 m high.","The Getz Ice Shelf along its shore stretches for hundreds of kilometres."],"q":{"q":"Which product made Philip Wrigley's company famous?","a":"Chewing gum","x":["Canned soup","Motor oil"]}},"Q281737":{"t":"An ice-locked bay named after a New York publisher","s":"Richard Byrd's expedition flew over this coast of Marie Byrd Land in December 1929 and named the bay after Arthur Hays Sulzberger of The New York Times, a supporter of the venture. Along its shore spreads the Sulzberger Ice Shelf, studded with ice-covered islands and hemmed in by sea ice for most of the year. It remains one of the least visited coasts of the continent.","w":"A wide bay of white sea ice and ice-shelf edge, dotted with ice-covered islands, along the coast of Marie Byrd Land.","f":["The bay was first seen from an aircraft during Byrd's expedition in 1929.","Arthur Hays Sulzberger later became publisher of The New York Times.","Marie Byrd Land, around it, is named after Admiral Byrd's wife."],"q":{"q":"Which newspaper is linked to the man Sulzberger Bay honours?","a":"The New York Times","x":["The Washington Post","The Chicago Tribune"]}},"Q898839":{"t":"An Antarctic strait with a ring-shaped volcano","s":"At the western end of the strait lies a ring-shaped island: Deception Island, a volcano whose flooded crater ships can sail right into through a narrow gap. The Bransfield Strait separates the South Shetland Islands from the icy mountains of the Antarctic Peninsula. It is named after Edward Bransfield, who charted part of the peninsula in January 1820, one of the first sightings of the Antarctic mainland.","w":"A wide channel between the snowy South Shetland Islands and the glaciated mountains of the Antarctic Peninsula, with a ring-shaped island to the west.","f":["The entrance to Deception Island's flooded crater, Neptune's Bellows, is only a few hundred metres wide.","King George Island, on its northern side, hosts research stations of several countries.","Humpback whales feed in the strait in summer."],"q":{"q":"What is Deception Island, at the edge of the Bransfield Strait?","a":"A flooded volcanic caldera","x":["A coral atoll","A giant stranded iceberg"]}},"Q1056751":{"t":"Antarctica's front door, watched by a smoking volcano","s":"Mount Erebus, the southernmost active volcano on Earth, rises 3,794 m above the eastern side of McMurdo Sound, often trailing steam from a lava lake in its crater. Across the water the Dry Valleys lie bare and brown, among the driest places on the planet. The sound is the gateway to the largest research station in Antarctica and to the old routes towards the South Pole.","w":"A wide bay of sea ice beside a large island crowned by a smoking volcano, with bare brown valleys in the mountains to the west.","f":["Erebus holds one of the few long-lasting lava lakes in the world.","Scott and Shackleton built expedition huts on Ross Island, and several still stand.","James Clark Ross named the sound in 1841 after Archibald McMurdo, an officer of HMS Terror."],"q":{"q":"Which active volcano rises over McMurdo Sound?","a":"Mount Erebus","x":["Mount Terror","Mount Vinson"]}},"Q938327":{"t":"A long Arctic sound, once a whalers' winter home","s":"Cumberland Sound cuts about 250 km into Baffin Island, a broad arm of the sea walled by rugged, glaciated mountains. In the 19th century Scottish and American whalers wintered here, and the remains of a whaling station still stand on Kekerten Island. Today the hamlet of Pangnirtung sits beside a fjord on its north-eastern shore, the gateway to Auyuittuq National Park.","w":"A long, wide inlet slicing into Baffin Island, lined with snowy mountains and glaciers, often partly covered in sea ice.","f":["The old whaling station on Kekerten Island is now a territorial historic park.","Auyuittuq, near Pangnirtung, means 'land that never melts' in Inuktitut.","Beluga whales gather in the sound's waters."],"q":{"q":"What does Auyuittuq, the park near Cumberland Sound, mean?","a":"Land that never melts","x":["Land of many bears","Land of long nights"]}},"Q1695":{"t":"Where two seas collide at the tip of Denmark","s":"At Grenen, the sandy northern tip of Denmark, waves from the Skagerrak and the Kattegat run into each other along a line you can stand beside. The Skagerrak itself is a broad channel between Norway's rocky south coast and Jutland, linking the North Sea with the approaches to the Baltic. Along the Norwegian side it plunges to about 700 m, the deepest part of the Norwegian Trench.","w":"A wide channel between Norway's island-fringed south coast and the flat, sandy tip of Denmark, with Sweden's west coast to the east.","f":["The Skagen painters of the late 1800s were drawn to the town by its famous clear light.","The strait is about 240 km long and between about 80 and 140 km wide.","It is one of the busiest shipping routes in Europe."],"q":{"q":"Which sea meets the Skagerrak at Grenen?","a":"The Kattegat","x":["The Wadden Sea","The Bothnian Sea"]}},"Q131716":{"t":"The shallow gateway between the North Sea and the Baltic","s":"Most ships sailing between the Baltic and the open ocean cross the Kattegat, a shallow sea hemmed in by Denmark's Jutland on the west and Sweden's west coast on the east. It averages only about 23 m deep and is dotted with sandbanks and low islands such as Læsø and Anholt. At its southern end it narrows into the Øresund and the Danish belts.","w":"A shallow, rounded sea between flat Danish shores to the west and Sweden's rocky, island-fringed coast to the east, with Gothenburg in the north-east.","f":["Part of Anholt, in the middle of the Kattegat, is a desert-like expanse of dunes and lichen.","Gothenburg, Sweden's second city, stands where the Göta River enters it.","Its water grows fresher towards the south, as brackish Baltic water flows out on the surface."],"q":{"q":"Which Swedish city stands on the Kattegat?","a":"Gothenburg","x":["Stockholm","Kalmar"]}},"Q654010":{"t":"A muddy funnel of huge tides and river mouths","s":"The Gulf of Khambhat narrows like a funnel into Gujarat, and the tide rushes up it so hard that the range can reach around 10 m, among the largest in India. Four big rivers — the Narmada, Tapi, Mahi and Sabarmati — pour in silt, turning the water an opaque brown. On its western shore at Alang, huge old ships are run aground on the beach to be broken up.","w":"A long, funnel-shaped gulf of opaque brown water on India's west coast, north of Mumbai and east of the Kathiawar peninsula.","f":["Alang is one of the largest ship-breaking yards in the world.","The rising tide can race up the gulf as a tidal bore.","Its old name, the Gulf of Cambay, comes from Khambhat, once a great trading port at its head."],"q":{"q":"What happens to ships run aground at Alang?","a":"They are broken up for scrap","x":["They are repaired in dry docks","They are sunk as artificial reefs"]}},"Q209530":{"t":"A dun-coloured river with diamonds in its gravels","s":"Vaal means 'pale' in Afrikaans, and it fits: a dun, silty river winding across flat khaki veld. Around 1870 prospectors found diamonds in its gravels at Klipdrift, today's Barkly West, just before the great Kimberley rush began some 30 km away. Further downstream the Vaal ends in the Orange, as its longest tributary.","w":"A thin brown line looping across flat, dusty veld north-west of Kimberley, edged by a dark fringe of trees.","f":["At about 1,120 km, the Vaal is the longest tributary of the Orange River.","The Vaal Dam, finished in 1938, stores water for Johannesburg and the rest of Gauteng.","Barkly West began as Klipdrift, a camp of diggers washing the river gravels for diamonds."],"q":{"q":"Which river does the Vaal flow into?","a":"The Orange","x":["The Limpopo","The Zambezi"]}},"Q272074":{"t":"Where Oregon Trail wagons finally left the river","s":"For hundreds of kilometres across Idaho, wagon trains on the Oregon Trail followed this river through dry sagebrush country. Here, at Farewell Bend, they turned away from it for good and struck north-west into the Oregon hills. Downstream the Snake plunges into Hells Canyon, deeper than the Grand Canyon, before it joins the Columbia.","w":"A blue-green river in a dry, tan valley along the Oregon–Idaho border, widening into a long reservoir to the north.","f":["At about 1,735 km, the Snake is the largest tributary of the Columbia River.","Hells Canyon, downstream, is about 2,400 m deep – the deepest river gorge in North America.","Shoshone Falls on the Snake in southern Idaho drop about 65 m, more than Niagara."],"q":{"q":"Which river does the Snake flow into?","a":"The Columbia","x":["The Missouri","The Colorado"]}},"Q142148":{"t":"Brazil's 'Old Chico', crossing the dry backlands","s":"Brazilians call it Velho Chico, Old Chico, and it earns the affection: it carries water right across the dry sertão, where many rivers run only in the rainy season. Here in northern Minas Gerais it winds north through scrub and cattle country, flanked by pale sandbars. Its name dates from 4 October 1501, St Francis's day, when Portuguese ships reached its mouth.","w":"A wide, sandy-banked river winding north through olive-brown scrub, with long pale sandbars in the dry season.","f":["At roughly 2,800 km, it is the longest river that flows entirely within Brazil.","Its Sobradinho reservoir in Bahia covers over 4,000 km², one of the largest artificial lakes on Earth.","Boatmen once fixed carved wooden heads called carrancas to their bows to ward off river spirits."],"q":{"q":"What do Brazilians fondly call the São Francisco?","a":"Velho Chico","x":["Rio Mar","Rio Doce"]}},"Q82812":{"t":"A northern river that sometimes flows backwards","s":"In spring the Sukhona can do something strange: when its tributary the Vologda is in full flood, its upper course runs backwards into Lake Kubenskoye. Here it winds past Totma through dark taiga and hay meadows. Totma sent many men to sea, among them Ivan Kuskov, who in 1812 founded Fort Ross in California.","w":"A narrow dark river looping through unbroken taiga, with pale strips of meadow along its banks and small Totma on a bend.","f":["At Veliky Ustyug the Sukhona meets the Yug, and together they form the Northern Dvina.","Below Totma the river cuts through the Opoki, high cliffs of pale rock where rapids once scared boatmen.","About 560 km long, the Sukhona carried the old trade route from Vologda to Arkhangelsk."],"q":{"q":"Which California outpost did Totma-born Ivan Kuskov found?","a":"Fort Ross","x":["Fort Yukon","Fort Vancouver"]}},"Q15277375":{"t":"One of the Yenisei's first threads, in a lost valley","s":"Few rivers start as remotely as this. The Shishged gathers its water in the Darkhad basin, a flat valley about 1,600 m up, ringed by larch-covered ranges and scattered with lakes. From here it slips north into Siberia and becomes one of the headwaters of the Yenisei, which reaches the Arctic Ocean thousands of kilometres away.","w":"A wide flat valley speckled with small lakes, ringed by dark forested mountains, one range west of long blue Lake Khövsgöl.","f":["Ice-age glaciers dammed the Darkhad basin more than once, filling it with a large lake.","Dukha reindeer herders, also called Tsaatan, live in the taiga around the valley.","Across the border in Tuva it continues as the Kyzyl-Khem, then as the Little Yenisei."],"q":{"q":"Which great river does the Shishged help to start?","a":"The Yenisei","x":["The Lena","The Ob"]}},"Q776155":{"t":"One of the two rivers that make the Paraná","s":"Pastures, coffee and soya fields spread across these uplands, and through them the Paranaíba runs in a green, wooded groove. Downstream it is barely a river any more but a chain of big reservoirs, one after another, along the border of Goiás and Minas Gerais. Where it finally meets the Rio Grande, the two become the Paraná.","w":"A thin, tree-lined river in a patchwork of green and red-brown fields; further west it swells into long branching reservoirs.","f":["The Paranaíba is roughly 1,000 km long and marks much of the border between Goiás and Minas Gerais.","Dams such as Emborcação, Itumbiara and São Simão turn its lower course into a staircase of lakes.","It is easy to confuse with the Parnaíba, a different river that reaches the Atlantic in north-east Brazil."],"q":{"q":"The Paranaíba joins which river to form the Paraná?","a":"The Rio Grande","x":["The Tietê","The Paraguay"]}},"Q31379":{"t":"A wild taiga river rebuilt as a chain of giant lakes","s":"What looks like a string of vast, ragged lakes across the taiga is a river remade by engineers. Since the 1970s the La Grande has been dammed step by step for hydropower, and neighbouring rivers were diverted into it, roughly doubling its flow. In winter the reservoirs freeze into white shapes with drowned, fingered shorelines.","w":"Huge irregular reservoirs full of islands and inlets in dark spruce forest, strung east to west towards James Bay.","f":["The turbines of the Robert-Bourassa power station sit in a cavern about 140 m underground.","Its spillway is cut into ten giant steps and is nicknamed the Giant's Staircase.","The river is about 900 km long and empties into James Bay."],"q":{"q":"What is the stepped spillway on the La Grande nicknamed?","a":"The Giant's Staircase","x":["The Devil's Ladder","The Beaver Steps"]}},"Q80240":{"t":"A steppe river long drawn as Europe's edge","s":"Loops, oxbows and crescent lakes crowd the Ural's floodplain here, a ribbon of dark woodland across bare, sunburnt steppe. The river runs about 2,400 km from the Ural Mountains to the Caspian, and many maps use it as part of the line between Europe and Asia. Until 1775 it was called the Yaik.","w":"A tightly meandering river with bright oxbow lakes and a dark strip of floodplain forest across flat yellow-brown steppe.","f":["Catherine the Great renamed the Yaik in 1775, after Pugachev's rebellion broke out among its Cossacks.","Pushkin came to Uralsk in 1833 to collect memories of Pugachev for his book on the revolt.","Its lower course has no dams, so Caspian sturgeon can still swim upstream to spawn."],"q":{"q":"What was the Ural River called before 1775?","a":"The Yaik","x":["The Emba","The Samara"]}},"Q3047":{"t":"The 'swift-flowing river' of the fur-trade canoes","s":"The name comes from a Cree phrase for 'swift-flowing river', and for generations it was a highway for fur-trade canoes heading west. Here, near The Pas, the current slows and spreads into a vast delta of channels, marshes and lakes. Downstream the river ends in Lake Winnipeg, carrying water that fell as snow on the Rockies.","w":"A brown river snaking through a maze of marsh, reed beds and shallow lakes on a flat, forested plain.","f":["The Saskatchewan River Delta, around 10,000 km², is one of the largest inland deltas in North America.","Cumberland House, the Hudson's Bay Company's first inland post, was built on this river in 1774.","The river is born where the North and South Saskatchewan meet, east of Prince Albert."],"q":{"q":"What does the Cree name behind 'Saskatchewan' mean?","a":"Swift-flowing river","x":["River of many islands","Muddy water"]}},"Q217818":{"t":"A Rhine branch strung with old Hanseatic towns","s":"Of the Rhine's three Dutch branches, the IJssel is the one that turns north. It winds for about 125 km through green floodplain between dikes, past brick towns that grew rich in the Hanseatic League: Doesburg, Zutphen, Deventer, Kampen. It ends close to the lake that bears its name, the IJsselmeer, once the salty Zuiderzee.","w":"A winding river in a wide green floodplain between dikes, with the compact town of Deventer on its east bank.","f":["IJ counts as a single letter in Dutch, so both letters are capitalised: IJssel, IJsselmeer.","Deventer, Zutphen and Kampen on the IJssel were all members of the Hanseatic League.","Under the Room for the River programme, side channels were dug here so floods can spread safely."],"q":{"q":"Why is IJssel written with two capital letters?","a":"IJ is one letter in Dutch","x":["It joins two river names","It honours a royal house"]}},"Q40855":{"t":"Europe's fourth-longest river, still young here","s":"Here the Dnieper is still a modest river, meandering through flat fields and pine woods, with silver oxbows marking its old beds. It has a long way to go: about 2,200 km in all, through three countries to the Black Sea. A thousand years ago this was part of the trade route 'from the Varangians to the Greeks'.","w":"A modest winding river with silvery oxbow lakes, crossing a flat patchwork of fields and dark pine forest.","f":["Among Europe's rivers only the Volga, the Danube and the Ural are longer.","It rises in the Valdai Hills, not far from the sources of the Volga and the Western Dvina.","Gogol wrote that rare is the bird that can fly to the middle of the Dnieper."],"q":{"q":"Which sea does the Dnieper flow into?","a":"The Black Sea","x":["The Baltic Sea","The Caspian Sea"]}},"Q2237":{"t":"Glacier water crossing the prairie city of Edmonton","s":"The water here set out as meltwater from the Saskatchewan Glacier on the Columbia Icefield, high in the Rockies. By Edmonton it has become a broad, silty river in a deep, wooded valley that snakes right through the city, lined with parks and trails. From here it keeps heading east for hundreds of kilometres across the prairie.","w":"A brown river in a deep, tree-filled valley winding through Edmonton's grid of streets, then out across flat farmland.","f":["The river runs roughly 1,300 km from the Rockies to its meeting with the South Saskatchewan.","The first Fort Edmonton, a fur-trade post, was built on its banks in 1795.","Its valley in Edmonton is one of the longest continuous stretches of urban parkland in North America."],"q":{"q":"Where does the North Saskatchewan begin?","a":"A glacier in the Rockies","x":["A lake on the prairie","A spring in the Cypress Hills"]}},"Q180558":{"t":"The 'black river' that becomes the Senegal","s":"Bafing means 'black river', and its partner, the Bakoye, is the 'white river'. They meet at Bafoulabé, whose name means 'meeting of two rivers', and from there on the stream is called the Senegal. Upstream, the Bafing drains the rainy Fouta Djallon highlands; the Manantali dam holds it back in a wide, branching lake.","w":"A dark ribbon through wooded savanna and red laterite hills; to the north, the long branching Manantali reservoir.","f":["The Bafing rises in Guinea's Fouta Djallon, the highlands that also feed the Niger and the Gambia.","The Manantali dam, completed in the late 1980s, created one of the largest reservoirs in West Africa.","Counted from the Bafing's source, the Senegal is about 1,600 km long."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Bafing mean?","a":"Black river","x":["Great river","Red river"]}},"Q8168":{"t":"An undammed northern giant running to the Barents Sea","s":"No dam stands anywhere on the Pechora's main stream, and from the air it shows: a broad, braided river of sandbars and wooded islands, running free through taiga toward the Barents Sea. It rises in the northern Urals and flows about 1,800 km. For roughly half the year it is frozen into a white road through the forest.","w":"A wide river split by sandbanks and wooded islands, winding north through dark taiga and pale bogs, the Urals to the east.","f":["The Virgin Komi Forests in its upper basin became Russia's first natural World Heritage site in 1995.","Atlantic salmon still run up the Pechora to spawn.","It ends in a wide delta near Naryan-Mar, opening into the shallow Pechora Sea."],"q":{"q":"Which sea does the Pechora flow into?","a":"The Barents Sea","x":["The White Sea","The Kara Sea"]}},"Q10362010":{"t":"A palm-named stream feeding an inland sea","s":"Red earth, cattle pasture and patches of cerrado scrub make up this upland of Minas Gerais, and the Indaiá threads through it in a dark line of gallery forest. It takes its name from a native palm. Its water flows into Três Marias, a huge reservoir on the São Francisco, joining it from the west bank.","w":"A narrow dark line of riverside forest across red and green uplands, flowing north into the branching arms of Três Marias.","f":["Indaiá is the Brazilian name of a native palm; the town of Dores do Indaiá, in its basin, shares it.","The Três Marias reservoir it feeds holds around 21 billion cubic metres of water.","The cerrado it crosses is considered the most species-rich savanna on Earth."],"q":{"q":"Which reservoir does the Indaiá flow into?","a":"Três Marias","x":["Furnas","Sobradinho"]}},"Q3334104":{"t":"A wild gorge river racing to become the Irrawaddy","s":"This river has two names and hardly a calm stretch. In China it is the Dulong, home of the Derung people; in Myanmar it becomes the N'Mai Hka, a fast, cold torrent hidden deep among steep forested ridges. North of Myitkyina it merges with the Mali Hka, and together they form the Irrawaddy.","w":"A thin silvery line deep in a maze of steep green ridges running north to south, often patched with cloud.","f":["The meeting point with the Mali Hka is called Myitsone, Burmese for 'confluence'.","The Derung of its upper valley number only a few thousand, one of China's smallest ethnic groups.","Ridges above its valley rise to more than 3,000 m."],"q":{"q":"The N'Mai Hka joins which river to form the Irrawaddy?","a":"The Mali Hka","x":["The Chindwin","The Salween"]}},"Q191829":{"t":"Colombia's river road between two Andean ranges","s":"Two ranges of the Andes wall in this valley, and down its middle runs Colombia's great river, broad and brown, heading north for the Caribbean. For centuries the Magdalena was the country's main road: goods and travellers went by boat between the coast and the highlands. García Márquez sent Simón Bolívar on his last journey down it in The General in His Labyrinth.","w":"A wide brown river with pale sandbars running north down a hot, flat valley between two mountain walls.","f":["About 1,500 km long, the Magdalena reaches the Caribbean at Barranquilla.","Most Colombians live within its basin.","Rapids at Honda, a little downstream, once forced boats to unload and cargo to go round overland."],"q":{"q":"Which novelist sent Bolívar down the Magdalena in a novel?","a":"Gabriel García Márquez","x":["Jorge Isaacs","Isabel Allende"]}},"Q1472009":{"t":"A thin green line through the mesas of Patagonia","s":"Brown, flat-topped mesas and grey steppe stretch in every direction, and the Neuquén cuts through them in a thin green line. It is fed by snow on the Andes near Domuyo, the highest peak in Patagonia. Downstream its water fills the big artificial lakes of Cerros Colorados, then meets the Limay to form the Río Negro.","w":"A narrow river with a green fringe winding south-east through bare, brown plateau; further on, large blue reservoirs in the desert.","f":["Domuyo, near its headwaters, rises to about 4,700 m and is often called the roof of Patagonia.","Water from the Neuquén fills Los Barreales and Mari Menuco, two large lakes of the Cerros Colorados scheme.","Neuquén province is rich in dinosaur fossils, including some of the largest ever found."],"q":{"q":"Which city stands where the Neuquén meets the Limay?","a":"Neuquén","x":["Bariloche","Mendoza"]}},"Q1569231":{"t":"The top of the Pearl River, winding through karst","s":"Karst country: green valleys studded with steep limestone hills, red soil and terraced fields, and the Nanpan winding between them. This is the uppermost reach of the Pearl River system. The river rises on Maxiong Mountain in eastern Yunnan, flows south, then swings east in a wide arc before meeting the Beipan to become the Hongshui.","w":"A winding river among red-soiled fields and lumpy green limestone hills, in a broad upland of eastern Yunnan.","f":["A spring on Maxiong Mountain near Qujing is officially marked as the source of the Pearl River.","The Nanpan is roughly 900 km long.","Its partner the Beipan – the North Pan – joins it on the Guizhou–Guangxi border."],"q":{"q":"Which river system does the Nanpan belong to?","a":"The Pearl River","x":["The Yangtze","The Mekong"]}},"Q1471":{"t":"The river that draws Paris in one long arc","s":"Paris is easy to read even from cruise height: a vast grey-beige city with the Seine drawing one long, gentle arc through its middle. On a clear day you may pick out the split in the stream at its heart, the Île de la Cité, where the city began. Beyond the suburbs the river loops back and forth in huge meanders on its way to the sea.","w":"A dark river curving east to west through a vast grey city, then looping in big meanders to the north-west.","f":["The banks of the Seine in central Paris have been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1991.","Paris's coat of arms shows a ship, a nod to the medieval guild of river merchants who ran the city.","The Seine is about 775 km long and rises on the Langres plateau in Burgundy."],"q":{"q":"Which island in the Seine is the historic heart of Paris?","a":"Île de la Cité","x":["Île Saint-Germain","Île aux Cygnes"]}},"Q552":{"t":"A wandering river fringed with floodplain forest","s":"Silver loops, oxbow lakes and dark bands of riverside forest mark the Oder as it swings north-west across the Silesian lowland. It rises in the hills of Czechia and runs about 840 km to the Baltic, forming part of the border between Germany and Poland on the way. Upstream, Wrocław is built across its islands and branches.","w":"A broad meandering river with bright oxbow lakes and strips of dark floodplain woods, across flat fields and villages.","f":["Wrocław, on the Oder, has more than 100 bridges and footbridges.","The river reaches the sea through the Szczecin Lagoon, a large shallow bay behind the Baltic coast.","Its source lies in the Oder Mountains of Czechia, at about 630 m."],"q":{"q":"Which sea does the Oder flow into?","a":"The Baltic Sea","x":["The North Sea","The Black Sea"]}},"Q3569":{"t":"A green ribbon between Senegal and Mauritania","s":"Rice paddies and gardens make a green stripe along the Senegal River, and beyond it the land turns dry and tawny in both directions. For hundreds of kilometres the river forms the border between Senegal and Mauritania. Its floods once spread across the whole valley floor each rainy season, and farmers still plant the damp land as the water drops back.","w":"A winding river with bright green, rectangular fields along both banks, set in dry, sandy-brown country.","f":["Near its mouth the river runs behind the Langue de Barbarie, a long, thin sand spit, before reaching the Atlantic.","The old colonial town of Saint-Louis, on an island near the mouth, is a UNESCO World Heritage site.","The Diama dam near the mouth, finished in the 1980s, stops salty seawater from pushing upstream."],"q":{"q":"Which country faces Senegal across the Senegal River?","a":"Mauritania","x":["Mali","The Gambia"]}},"Q1511505":{"t":"Half of the Yenisei, hurrying to meet the other half","s":"Tuvans call it the Ka-Khem. It comes down from Mongolia and runs west through wooded mountains in a chain of rapids and gravel islands. At Kyzyl it meets the Great Yenisei, and from that confluence on the river is simply the Yenisei, one of the longest on Earth, heading north for the Arctic Ocean.","w":"A river of gravel bars and braided channels in a mountain valley, dark taiga on the slopes, flowing west towards the Tuvan basin.","f":["An obelisk marking the 'Centre of Asia' stands at the confluence in Kyzyl.","The Little Yenisei is about 560 km long.","Its headwaters lie in Mongolia's Darkhad basin, west of Lake Khövsgöl."],"q":{"q":"In which city do the Great and Little Yenisei meet?","a":"Kyzyl","x":["Abakan","Krasnoyarsk"]}},"Q35591":{"t":"A palm-green thread through the Mesopotamian plain","s":"A narrow band of palm groves and fields follows the Tigris, and on either side the plain fades to sand and stone. This is Mesopotamia, the 'land between the rivers', where some of the first cities rose. Tikrit stands on the bank here; Saladin, who became sultan of Egypt and Syria, was born in the town around 1137.","w":"A dark ribbon of river with a thin green fringe of palms and fields, winding south through pale desert.","f":["The Tigris runs about 1,850 km from the mountains of south-eastern Turkey to its meeting with the Euphrates.","Upstream stood Assur and Nineveh, capitals of ancient Assyria, both on its banks.","Baghdad grew up on the Tigris, founded in 762 as a round city."],"q":{"q":"Which famous sultan was born in Tikrit on the Tigris?","a":"Saladin","x":["Suleiman the Magnificent","Baybars"]}},"Q8187":{"t":"Sandbanks and taiga on the road to the White Sea","s":"Pale sandbanks and long green islands split the Northern Dvina into several channels as it winds north through the taiga. Born at Veliky Ustyug from the Sukhona and the Yug, it runs about 740 km to the White Sea at Arkhangelsk. Before St Petersburg was built, this river was Russia's main route to sea trade with Western Europe.","w":"A wide, pale-brown river with sandbanks and wooded islands, winding north through dark taiga and scattered villages.","f":["Mikhail Lomonosov was born in 1711 in a village near Kholmogory, on the Dvina's lower course.","Arkhangelsk, near the mouth, was founded in 1584 and became Russia's first major seaport.","The Vaga, one of its largest tributaries, flows in a little upstream of here."],"q":{"q":"Which scientist was born near Kholmogory on the Northern Dvina?","a":"Mikhail Lomonosov","x":["Nikolai Lobachevsky","Ivan Pavlov"]}},"Q204806":{"t":"The Niger's great partner, wide and sandy","s":"In the dry season the Benue looks like a braid of thin channels across a broad bed of pale sand; after the rains it fills from bank to bank and spills onto its floodplain. It rises in Cameroon and runs about 1,400 km before meeting the Niger at Lokoja, where the two rivers join in a giant Y.","w":"A wide, pale sandy riverbed with braided channels and a green floodplain, winding west across flat savanna.","f":["The Benue is the largest tributary of the Niger.","In the flood season, steamers once travelled up it as far as Garoua in Cameroon.","Lagdo dam in northern Cameroon holds back its upper course in a large reservoir."],"q":{"q":"Which river does the Benue join at Lokoja?","a":"The Niger","x":["The Volta","The Congo"]}},"Q817337":{"t":"A green irrigated valley slicing through grey steppe","s":"The contrast is stark: a strip of poplars, orchards and fields a few kilometres wide, and beyond it grey-brown Patagonian steppe as far as the eye can see. The Río Negro begins where the Neuquén and the Limay meet, then runs about 635 km across Patagonia to the Atlantic. Its valleys grow most of Argentina's apples and pears.","w":"A winding blue-green river inside a narrow green valley of fields and tree rows, cut into a flat grey-brown plateau.","f":["It carries more water than any other river in Patagonia.","Tall rows of poplars shelter the orchards from the strong Patagonian wind.","Near its mouth, the towns of Viedma and Carmen de Patagones face each other across the river."],"q":{"q":"Which fruits is the Río Negro valley best known for?","a":"Apples and pears","x":["Grapes and olives","Oranges and lemons"]}},"Q1152951":{"t":"A quiet Rhine branch between forest and orchards","s":"North of the river rise the wooded, sandy hills of the Veluwe; south of it lies the Betuwe, flat and neatly lined with orchards. Between them runs the Nederrijn, the 'Lower Rhine', one of the branches into which the great river splits after entering the Netherlands. Weirs manage its flow, deciding how much Rhine water goes on to the IJssel.","w":"A modest river with green floodplains between dikes, dark forest to the north and a neat grid of orchards to the south.","f":["Past Wijk bij Duurstede the same river takes another name, the Lek.","The weir at Driel controls how much Rhine water flows on towards the IJssel.","The Veluwe to the north is one of the largest forested areas in the Netherlands."],"q":{"q":"What is the Nederrijn called below Wijk bij Duurstede?","a":"The Lek","x":["The Waal","The IJssel"]}},"Q1143852":{"t":"A Rhine branch ending by the windmills of Kinderdijk","s":"Pastures below sea level, long straight ditches and dikes: this is the heart of polder country, and the Lek runs through it on its last kilometres. At Kinderdijk it meets the Noord, and the two carry on towards Rotterdam as the Nieuwe Maas. Nineteen 18th-century windmills there once pumped the surrounding polders dry.","w":"A broad river between dikes, with flat green polders cut by straight silver ditches on both sides and Rotterdam's sprawl to the west.","f":["The Kinderdijk windmills became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1997.","The Lek is about 60 km long, from Wijk bij Duurstede to Kinderdijk.","Much of the land along its lower course lies below sea level."],"q":{"q":"Which river does the Lek meet at Kinderdijk?","a":"The Noord","x":["The Amstel","The Vecht"]}},"Q2076850":{"t":"Tigris water carried south to Sumer's plain","s":"Also known as the Shatt al-Gharraf, this waterway leaves the Tigris at Kut and runs south across the flat, dusty plain of southern Iraq. Irrigation channels branch off it like the veins of a leaf, feeding date groves and fields. The ruins of the Sumerian cities of Girsu and Lagash lie in the plain near its lower course.","w":"A thin dark waterway running south across a pale flat plain, with a green lace of irrigated fields along it.","f":["A barrage across the Tigris at Kut sends water into it.","Near its end it splits into smaller channels that fade into the southern marshes.","Excavations at Girsu began in 1877 and revealed one of the oldest cities in the world."],"q":{"q":"Which river does the Shatt al-Hayy branch off?","a":"The Tigris","x":["The Euphrates","The Karun"]}},"Q615328":{"t":"The 'red water river' of Guangxi's karst","s":"Hongshui means 'red water', and in the rainy season its current runs rusty with silt from the red soils upstream. Here it snakes through deep karst country, between rows of steep, forested limestone peaks. Formed by the Nanpan and the Beipan, it is stepped with big hydropower dams, among them Longtan, one of the largest in China.","w":"A winding river in a deep valley, lumpy green limestone peaks crowding both banks and long reservoirs behind dams.","f":["The Hongshui forms the main upper course of the Xi River, the chief branch of the Pearl.","Downstream it meets the Liu River and continues as the Qian.","Its cascade of dams, including Yantan and Longtan, makes it one of China's great hydropower rivers."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Hongshui mean?","a":"Red water","x":["Clear water","Rainbow river"]}},"Q2509519":{"t":"A short link in the Pearl River's chain, with a gorge","s":"This short river begins where two big Guangxi streams, the Hongshui and the Liu, have just combined. It pushes south-east through low green hills and sugarcane fields, then squeezes through Datengxia, the Great Rattan Gorge, before meeting the Yu River at Guiping. There the name changes again, to the Xun.","w":"A wide brown river winding between low green hills and fields, entering a narrow gorge above the town of Guiping.","f":["A large dam now closes the Datengxia gorge for flood control, power and shipping.","Guangxi grows more sugarcane than any other province of China.","The Taiping Rebellion broke out in 1851 at Jintian, in the hills near Guiping."],"q":{"q":"Which two rivers join to form the Qian?","a":"The Hongshui and the Liu","x":["The Xun and the Gui","The Yu and the Zuo"]}},"Q856509":{"t":"The last stretch before the Xi River begins","s":"Cargo barges ply this broad brown river, one of the busiest waterways of southern China. The Xun begins at Guiping, where the Qian and the Yu meet, and flows east through rolling green hills and fish ponds. At Wuzhou it takes in the Gui River from Guilin, and from there on it is called the Xi, the main stem of the Pearl River.","w":"A broad brown river bending east through low green hills, with fish ponds and small towns along its banks.","f":["The Gui River that joins it at Wuzhou flows down from the karst landscapes of Guilin.","Wuzhou has long been Guangxi's gateway for river trade with Guangzhou and Hong Kong.","Fish ponds line its floodplain, a checkerboard of silver rectangles when the sun catches them."],"q":{"q":"At which city does the Xun become the Xi River?","a":"Wuzhou","x":["Guilin","Nanning"]}},"Q584":{"t":"Where the Rhine leaves the hills for the open plain","s":"Here the Rhine is leaving the hills behind. The dark wooded knobs of the Siebengebirge, old volcanoes, crowd its east bank, while to the north the valley opens into the flat, busy lowland towards Cologne. Bonn stands on the west bank: Beethoven was born there in 1770, and from 1949 to 1990 it was West Germany's capital.","w":"A broad, busy river heading north-west, with wooded volcanic hills on its east bank and the city of Bonn along the west.","f":["The Rhine is about 1,230 km long, from the Swiss Alps to the North Sea.","Upstream between Bingen and Koblenz, its castle-lined gorge is a UNESCO World Heritage site.","A rack railway has climbed the Drachenfels in the Siebengebirge since 1883."],"q":{"q":"Which composer was born in Bonn on the Rhine?","a":"Ludwig van Beethoven","x":["Johannes Brahms","Richard Wagner"]}},"Q216171":{"t":"The Rhine's main branch, running out to the sea","s":"Most of the Rhine's water takes this route: about two-thirds of it flows into the Waal soon after the river enters the Netherlands. The Waal runs west past Nijmegen, one of the oldest Dutch towns, carrying some of Europe's heaviest barge traffic. Its water finally reaches the North Sea through wide estuaries such as the Haringvliet, closed off by a long dam with sluices.","w":"A broad grey-blue estuary between flat green islands, shut off from the North Sea to the west by a long dam.","f":["Nijmegen on the Waal grew from a Roman settlement and celebrated its 2,000th anniversary in 2005.","The Haringvliet dam, finished in 1971, has huge sluices that let river water out to sea.","The Waal is one of the busiest inland waterways in the world."],"q":{"q":"What share of the Rhine's water flows into the Waal?","a":"About two-thirds","x":["About a tenth","About a third"]}},"Q639478":{"t":"The small arm wrapped around a giant river island","s":"The Javaés is the Araguaia's 'smaller arm': it splits off from the main river and loops round the east side of Bananal Island before rejoining it. That island, around 20,000 km² of flooded grassland and forest, is usually called the largest river island in the world. In the rainy season much of it disappears under water.","w":"A winding dark river along the east edge of a vast flat island of green savanna and lakes, the wider Araguaia to the west.","f":["Brazilians also call it the Braço Menor, the 'smaller arm' of the Araguaia.","The river is named after the Javaé people, who live on Bananal Island.","Araguaia National Park covers the northern part of the island."],"q":{"q":"What is Bananal Island, wrapped by the Javaés, usually called?","a":"The largest river island","x":["The largest delta in Brazil","The wettest place in Brazil"]}},"Q10965":{"t":"The river that gave Iberia its name","s":"Iberia takes its name from this river, which the Romans knew as the Iberus. Near Tudela it runs through a green belt of orchards and vegetable fields, while just to the north lies the Bardenas Reales, a startling badland desert of pale, eroded clay. The Ebro flows about 910 km from Cantabria to its delta on the Mediterranean.","w":"A green river valley of fields and orchards, with the pale, furrowed desert of the Bardenas Reales spreading just to the north.","f":["It is the longest river flowing entirely within Spain.","Its delta on the Mediterranean is carpeted with rice fields.","Zaragoza, downstream, grew from a Roman colony named after the emperor Caesar Augustus."],"q":{"q":"Which peninsula is named after the Ebro?","a":"The Iberian Peninsula","x":["The Italian Peninsula","The Balkan Peninsula"]}},"Q1139679":{"t":"A great jungle river through the old Maya world","s":"Broad, green-brown and full of loops, the Usumacinta slides out of the hills onto the flat, wet plain of Tabasco here. Upstream it forms the border between Mexico and Guatemala, and Maya cities stood on its banks: Yaxchilán sits inside a tight loop of the river. Together with the Grijalva, it carries more water than any other river system in Mexico.","w":"A wide, muddy river swinging in big loops across a flat green plain of pasture, lagoons and patches of forest.","f":["The easiest way to reach Yaxchilán is still by boat along the river.","It rises in the highlands of Guatemala and runs about 1,000 km to the Gulf of Mexico.","Near the coast it joins the Grijalva in a vast maze of wetlands, the Pantanos de Centla."],"q":{"q":"Which Maya city lies inside a loop of the Usumacinta?","a":"Yaxchilán","x":["Palenque","Tikal"]}},"Q1644":{"t":"Meadows, oxbows and brick towns on a wild-edged river","s":"Wide meadows, oxbow ponds and patches of floodplain forest line the Elbe here, and for long stretches no town crowds its banks. Tangermünde, a red-brick Hanseatic town, sits on a low bluff above the water. The river rises in the Czech mountains and runs about 1,100 km to the North Sea, passing Dresden, Magdeburg and Hamburg.","w":"A broad river winding north-west through wide green meadows, with silver oxbow ponds and a small town on its west bank.","f":["Near Magdeburg a canal crosses the Elbe on a 918 m water bridge, one of the longest in the world.","Much of its middle course is protected as the UNESCO biosphere reserve River Landscape Elbe.","The Elbe rises in the Krkonoše, or Giant Mountains, at about 1,400 m."],"q":{"q":"What crosses the Elbe near Magdeburg on a bridge?","a":"A canal with ships","x":["A cable car","An oil pipeline"]}},"Q2416760":{"t":"A green oasis of trees far out in the tundra","s":"Out here the tundra runs to the horizon, a lake-freckled plain of greens and browns, white for most of the year. Yet the Thelon valley holds pockets of spruce far north of the treeline, an oasis that shelters muskoxen, moose and caribou. The river flows east into a string of big lakes and on to Hudson Bay via Chesterfield Inlet.","w":"A river threading a chain of long lakes across treeless, lake-speckled tundra, with dark patches of spruce along parts of the valley.","f":["The Thelon Wildlife Sanctuary was created in 1927 to protect the region's muskoxen.","The Thelon was named a Canadian Heritage River in 1990.","Its water reaches Hudson Bay through Baker Lake and Chesterfield Inlet."],"q":{"q":"Which animal was the Thelon sanctuary created to protect?","a":"The muskox","x":["The polar bear","The wood bison"]}},"ne-rv-1159112855":{"t":"The Colorado when it was still called the Grand","s":"Until 1921 this stretch was called the Grand River, and the city of Grand Junction is named after its meeting with the Gunnison. From above, the Colorado is a green irrigated strip of orchards and vineyards between the grey terraces of the Book Cliffs and the high, flat top of Grand Mesa. Canyon country begins just downstream.","w":"A green strip of fields and a small city along the river, grey cliffs to the north, a huge flat mesa to the east.","f":["Grand Mesa, to the east, is often called the largest flat-topped mountain in the world.","The Colorado runs about 2,330 km from the Rockies towards the Gulf of California.","Palisade, just upstream, is known for its peaches and wine."],"q":{"q":"What was this stretch of the Colorado called until 1921?","a":"The Grand River","x":["The Green River","The Gunnison River"]}},"Q386331":{"t":"A staircase of reservoirs between two Brazilian states","s":"The Rio Grande here is less a river than a staircase of lakes: dam after dam holds it back in long, branching reservoirs between São Paulo and Minas Gerais. Around them stretch sugarcane, soy and pasture, laid out in huge geometric fields. Further west it joins the Paranaíba, and from that meeting on the river is called the Paraná.","w":"A wide, branching reservoir along a state border, ringed by vast rectangular fields of green cane and red earth.","f":["It rises in the Serra da Mantiqueira and runs about 1,300 km.","The Furnas dam upstream, finished in the 1960s, was one of Brazil's first big hydropower projects.","For much of its course it forms the border between the states of São Paulo and Minas Gerais."],"q":{"q":"What do people in Minas Gerais call the vast Furnas reservoir?","a":"The sea of Minas","x":["The mirror of Minas","The lake of gold"]}},"Q14294":{"t":"The river that wraps itself around Toledo","s":"Toledo is built on a rocky hill that the Tagus almost surrounds, curling round it in a deep, tight gorge. From cruise height the loop shows as a sharp bend in a green river crossing the tawny Castilian plain. The Tagus is the longest river of the Iberian Peninsula, flowing about 1,000 km to the Atlantic at Lisbon.","w":"A green river making a sharp horseshoe bend around a compact hilltop city in brown, dry plain.","f":["The old city of Toledo has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1986.","Its estuary at Lisbon is so wide that part of it is called the Sea of Straw.","Upstream, the royal palace town of Aranjuez was built in a green bend of the river."],"q":{"q":"At which city does the Tagus reach the Atlantic?","a":"Lisbon","x":["Porto","Cádiz"]}},"Q548":{"t":"Poland's great river, still braided with sandbanks","s":"Sandbars, willow islands and shallow side channels give the Vistula a wild look, even this close to Warsaw. It is Poland's longest river, running about 1,050 km from the Silesian Beskids past Kraków and Warsaw to the Baltic near Gdańsk. Much of its course is still unregulated, which is rare for a river this size in Europe.","w":"A wide river with pale sandbars and green willow islands, running north through a flat patchwork of fields towards Warsaw.","f":["The Vistula basin covers more than half of Poland.","Kraków's Wawel Castle stands on a hill above the Vistula.","Near the sea its branches built the flat Żuławy delta, parts of which lie below sea level."],"q":{"q":"Near which city does the Vistula reach the Baltic?","a":"Gdańsk","x":["Szczecin","Kaliningrad"]}},"Q193737":{"t":"A river bending into a moccasin at Chattanooga","s":"Look for a huge meander shaped like a foot: Moccasin Bend, where the Tennessee coils round a flat peninsula beneath Lookout Mountain. Chattanooga spreads along its banks between wooded ridges. The river runs a strange U-shaped course, south into Alabama, then west and north across Tennessee and Kentucky to the Ohio.","w":"A tight, foot-shaped loop of river beside a city, with a long wooded ridge rising just to the south-west.","f":["Since 1933 the Tennessee Valley Authority has turned the river into a chain of dammed lakes.","The Tennessee is about 1,050 km long and joins the Ohio at Paducah, Kentucky.","Muscle Shoals, downstream in Alabama, gave its name to a famous recording scene."],"q":{"q":"Which river does the Tennessee join at Paducah?","a":"The Ohio","x":["The Missouri","The Arkansas"]}},"Q483159":{"t":"The ancient Jaxartes, winding through rice and desert","s":"A green, glinting strip of rice paddies and reed marsh follows the Syr Darya across the desert, with the Kyzylkum sands spreading to the south. The river, known to the Greeks as the Jaxartes, runs about 2,200 km from the Tian Shan to the North Aral Sea. A dike built there in 2005 has helped that part of the shrunken sea to refill.","w":"A meandering river in a green belt of flooded rice fields and reeds, with pale desert sand to the south.","f":["Further downstream, the Baikonur Cosmodrome stands near its banks.","It is formed in the Fergana Valley, where the Naryn and the Kara Darya meet.","Alexander the Great founded Alexandria Eschate, 'the Furthest', on its banks in 329 BC."],"q":{"q":"What did the ancient Greeks call the Syr Darya?","a":"The Jaxartes","x":["The Oxus","The Hydaspes"]}},"Q651195":{"t":"A glacier-fed torrent in the heart of the Tian Shan","s":"Snow-capped ranges, bare brown slopes and a thin grey-green river at the bottom of the valley: this is the Naryn in the heart of the Tian Shan. The town of Naryn stretches along the river for kilometres. Fed by glaciers, the river runs west into the long Toktogul reservoir, then out to the Fergana Valley, where it becomes the Syr Darya.","w":"A thin river in a narrow valley between bare brown ranges, snow on the peaks and a long strip of town along its banks.","f":["The Toktogul dam holds back one of the largest reservoirs in Central Asia.","Counted from the Naryn's source, the Syr Darya is over 3,000 km long.","The town of Naryn lies at about 2,000 m above sea level."],"q":{"q":"Which river does the Naryn help to form?","a":"The Syr Darya","x":["The Amu Darya","The Ili"]}},"Q2216":{"t":"An icefield's river winding north through boreal forest","s":"The Athabasca begins as meltwater from the Columbia Icefield and ends some 1,200 km later in a vast delta on Lake Athabasca. In between, as here, it winds north through an endless boreal forest of spruce, poplar and muskeg. Downstream of Fort McMurray, open-pit oil sands mines spread across the forest along its banks, plainly visible from the air.","w":"A wide brown river snaking through dark boreal forest dotted with bogs and small lakes.","f":["The Athabasca Glacier, where the river begins, is one of the most visited glaciers in North America.","The Peace–Athabasca Delta at its mouth is one of the largest freshwater deltas in the world.","Its name comes from a Cree word describing reeds or grasses growing here and there."],"q":{"q":"Where does the Athabasca River begin?","a":"The Columbia Icefield","x":["Lake Louise","Great Slave Lake"]}},"Q1256528":{"t":"Burkina Faso's great hairpin river","s":"In Burkina Faso it is called the Mouhoun. The river rises near Bobo-Dioulasso, runs north-east for a long way, then turns sharply and heads south, as it does here, through wooded savanna and farm plots. Later its dark-green band marks borders with Côte d'Ivoire and Ghana before it ends in Lake Volta, one of the world's largest reservoirs by area.","w":"A dark ribbon of gallery forest winding south through dry, patchy savanna and small fields.","f":["The Black Volta meets the White Volta in Lake Volta, in Ghana.","Lake Volta, which it feeds, covers about 8,500 km².","The river is about 1,350 km long."],"q":{"q":"What is the Black Volta called in Burkina Faso?","a":"Mouhoun","x":["Nakambé","Nazinon"]}},"Q1644776":{"t":"Where the Xingu begins, between forest and soy","s":"The Culuene is the main headstream of the Xingu, flowing north across the Mato Grosso plateau. Here a dark ribbon of forest follows it through a landscape largely turned into vast soy and cattle farms. Downstream it enters the Xingu Indigenous Park, where the forest suddenly becomes unbroken, a boundary so sharp it shows up from space.","w":"A dark, forest-lined river winding north across a checkerboard of enormous pale fields; further north, solid green forest.","f":["The Xingu Indigenous Park, created in 1961, was the first large indigenous territory recognised in Brazil.","Peoples of the upper Xingu hold the Kuarup, a great ceremony honouring the dead.","With the Ronuro and other rivers, the Culuene forms the Xingu."],"q":{"q":"Which great river does the Culuene help to form?","a":"The Xingu","x":["The Tapajós","The Araguaia"]}},"Q191314":{"t":"The 'Ganges of the South', held back in a wide lake","s":"Indians call it Dakshina Ganga, the Ganges of the South, and it is India's second-longest river after the Ganges itself. Here in Telangana the Sriram Sagar dam holds it in a wide, branching lake among dry, rocky plains. From its source near Nashik, only about 80 km from the Arabian Sea, it crosses the whole peninsula to the Bay of Bengal.","w":"A broad, branching reservoir with ragged fingers in flat, dry country, the river continuing east as a pale sandy ribbon.","f":["The Godavari runs about 1,465 km, ending in a wide delta on the Bay of Bengal.","The Pushkaram bathing festival is held on the Godavari once every 12 years.","Sriram Sagar was built mainly to irrigate the dry plains of northern Telangana."],"q":{"q":"What is the Godavari's popular nickname?","a":"The Ganges of the South","x":["The Sorrow of Bengal","The Lifeline of Punjab"]}},"Q124653":{"t":"A steppe river on its way to Siberia's old capital","s":"Birch groves scattered like islands, round lakes by the hundred and ploughed steppe: the Tobol winds through this West Siberian plain in tight loops. It rises in Kazakhstan and runs about 1,600 km north to join the Irtysh at Tobolsk, the town named after it. Tobolsk was once the capital of all Siberia.","w":"A tightly looping river across flat steppe dotted with birch copses and countless round lakes.","f":["Tobolsk, at its mouth, has the only stone kremlin in Siberia.","Dmitri Mendeleev, creator of the periodic table, was born in Tobolsk in 1834.","Its tributary the Tura carried Yermak's Cossacks deep into Siberia in the 1580s."],"q":{"q":"Which famous scientist was born in Tobolsk?","a":"Dmitri Mendeleev","x":["Mikhail Lomonosov","Ivan Pavlov"]}},"Q8197":{"t":"One river, two names, three countries","s":"In Belarus and Russia it is the Western Dvina; in Latvia, the Daugava. Here it curves through Polotsk, one of the oldest towns of the Eastern Slavs, first mentioned in the chronicles in 862. From here it winds on through lake-studded forest and farmland to the Gulf of Riga, about 1,000 km from its source.","w":"A winding river through forest and farmland, a town spread across both banks and many small lakes scattered around.","f":["Its source lies in the Valdai Hills, close to the sources of the Volga and the Dnieper.","In Latvia three hydropower dams turn the river into a chain of reservoirs.","Riga grew up near its mouth and became a major Hanseatic port."],"q":{"q":"What is the Daugava called in Belarus and Russia?","a":"The Western Dvina","x":["The Neman","The Northern Dvina"]}},"Q200174":{"t":"Where an Amazon source bursts out of the Andes","s":"For hundreds of kilometres the Marañón runs north through one of the deepest canyons in the Andes; here it swings east and cuts its way out of the mountains through narrow gorges called pongos. Beyond them lies the endless green of the Amazon lowlands. Downstream it meets the Ucayali, and from that confluence on the river is called the Amazon.","w":"A brown river forcing its way through forested ridges in narrow gaps, then spreading into flat, endless rainforest to the east.","f":["For centuries the Marañón was regarded as the main source of the Amazon.","In places its canyon in northern Peru is deeper than the Grand Canyon.","The Marañón is about 1,700 km long."],"q":{"q":"Which river does the Marañón meet to form the Amazon?","a":"The Ucayali","x":["The Napo","The Huallaga"]}},"Q269710":{"t":"A gold-rush river cut deep into the plateau","s":"The Fraser here runs in a deep trench carved into the forested Interior Plateau, with the Quesnel River joining it at the town of Quesnel. In the 1860s this was the gateway to the Cariboo gold fields, and thousands of miners came up the valley. At about 1,375 km, the Fraser is the longest river in British Columbia.","w":"A brown river in a deep, steep-sided trench across a forested plateau patched with clearings, a town at a river junction.","f":["Simon Fraser followed the river to the sea in 1808, and it bears his name.","In good years millions of salmon swim up the Fraser to spawn.","Downstream it squeezes through Hell's Gate, a gorge only about 35 m wide."],"q":{"q":"Which fish runs up the Fraser in millions to spawn?","a":"Salmon","x":["Sturgeon","Trout"]}},"Q82207":{"t":"A river born near one of the coldest villages on Earth","s":"The Indigirka starts its journey near Oymyakon, where winter temperatures have dropped close to −70 °C. It then cuts north through bare, rocky ranges, braiding into gravel channels, before spreading out across lake-strewn lowlands to the East Siberian Sea. For about eight months a year it lies frozen, a white line through the mountains.","w":"A pale, braided river among bare grey-brown mountains, patched with snow for much of the year.","f":["Russkoye Ustye, in its delta, was settled by Russians in the 17th century, who kept their old speech and songs.","The Indigirka is about 1,700 km long.","It usually freezes in October and stays frozen until late May or June."],"q":{"q":"Which famously cold village lies on the Indigirka's upper course?","a":"Oymyakon","x":["Verkhoyansk","Tiksi"]}},"Q65383":{"t":"The tributary longer than the river it joins","s":"Sandbanks glowing pale against dark taiga, oxbow lakes and long meanders: the Vychegda crosses the Komi Republic like this for hundreds of kilometres. Near Syktyvkar, the capital of Komi, it takes in the Sysola. At about 1,130 km it is longer than the Northern Dvina itself, which it joins at Kotlas.","w":"A wide meandering river with bright sandbanks and silver oxbow lakes in dark taiga, a city to the south-east.","f":["Syktyvkar means 'town on the Sysola' in the Komi language.","In the 14th century Stephen of Perm preached along the Vychegda and created an alphabet for the Komi language.","The Vychegda is the largest tributary of the Northern Dvina."],"q":{"q":"Which river does the Vychegda join at Kotlas?","a":"The Northern Dvina","x":["The Pechora","The Kama"]}},"ne-rv-1159113559":{"t":"The reddish river where Patagonia begins","s":"Colorado means 'reddish', for the silt that tints its water. The river rises in the Andes and runs south-east for roughly 1,000 km to the Atlantic, and by long tradition it marks the northern edge of Patagonia. Here it crosses dry, gravelly steppe in a thin green line, also serving as a border between Argentine provinces.","w":"A thin river with a narrow green fringe winding south-east across bare, pale-brown gravel plains.","f":["It is formed by the Grande and the Barrancas rivers, which meet in the Andean foothills.","The river separates Mendoza from Neuquén, then La Pampa from Río Negro.","The Casa de Piedra dam downstream holds a large reservoir in the dry plains."],"q":{"q":"Which region traditionally begins south of the Río Colorado?","a":"Patagonia","x":["The Pampas","The Gran Chaco"]}},"Q8542":{"t":"A mountain river with two countries on its banks","s":"Deep in a gorge between huge bare mountains, the Panj runs grey-green and fast, with villages and orchards clinging to fans of gravel on both banks. On one side lies Tajikistan, on the other Afghanistan; the river is the border for hundreds of kilometres. Downstream it meets the Vakhsh, and together they become the Amu Darya.","w":"A thin river at the bottom of a deep gorge between massive bare ranges, small green patches of villages on both sides.","f":["Its headwaters include the Pamir River, which flows out of Lake Zorkul.","In 1838 the British explorer John Wood reached Lake Zorkul and hailed it as the source of the Oxus.","Panj is the Persian word for 'five'."],"q":{"q":"The Panj and which other river form the Amu Darya?","a":"The Vakhsh","x":["The Zeravshan","The Kafirnigan"]}},"Q49544":{"t":"A clear, rocky river in a land of forest and cattle","s":"Unlike the muddy Amazon, the Xingu runs clear and dark green over rocks and rapids, spattered with forested islands. Around São Félix do Xingu, cattle pasture has cut into the forest in huge pale blocks, while the Kayapó indigenous lands nearby remain solid green. The river flows north for well over 1,500 km before merging into the Amazon.","w":"A wide, dark river full of rocky islands and rapids, pale blocks of pasture on one side and unbroken forest on the other.","f":["Its rapids are home to fish found nowhere else, such as the black-and-white zebra pleco.","Near its mouth the river widens into a lake-like channel several kilometres across.","Its headwaters lie in the Xingu Indigenous Park, far to the south in Mato Grosso."],"q":{"q":"Which striped fish lives only in the rapids of the Xingu?","a":"The zebra pleco","x":["The neon tetra","The red-bellied piranha"]}},"Q8493":{"t":"A braided desert river that once filled the Aral Sea","s":"Two deserts face each other across the Amu Darya: the Karakum on one side, the Kyzylkum on the other. Between them the river spreads into a wide brown bed of shifting channels and sandbars, edged by a thin green strip of fields. Its water once filled the Aral Sea, but so much is now taken for irrigation that it rarely reaches the old shore.","w":"A wide, braided brown river with sandbars and a thin green strip of fields, pale desert on both sides.","f":["The Karakum Canal carries its water westward across the desert for more than 1,000 km.","Counted from its Pamir sources, the Amu Darya is about 2,500 km long.","Khiva and the other oasis towns of Khorezm grew up on its lower course."],"q":{"q":"Which desert faces the Kyzylkum across the Amu Darya?","a":"The Karakum","x":["The Taklamakan","The Gobi"]}},"Q752674":{"t":"A blue river of rapids in the southern Amazon","s":"The Tapajós is a clear-water river: in the dry season it runs blue-green over rocks and rapids, striking against the dense forest. Downstream it widens into a huge, lake-like channel with white sand beaches before meeting the muddy Amazon at Santarém, where the two waters flow side by side. It is formed by the Juruena and the Teles Pires.","w":"A dark blue-green river with rocky rapids and islands, winding north through dense forest cut by a few clearings and roads.","f":["In 1928 Henry Ford founded Fordlândia on its banks to grow rubber for his cars.","Alter do Chão, near its mouth, is famous for white river beaches and is often called the Amazon's Caribbean.","Near Santarém the Tapajós is more than 10 km wide in places."],"q":{"q":"Which industrialist built a rubber town on the Tapajós?","a":"Henry Ford","x":["Thomas Edison","Harvey Firestone"]}},"Q204040":{"t":"An Alpine river that ends in 'Europe's Amazon'","s":"The Mur starts high in the Austrian Alps and flows through Graz, but here on the Pannonian plain it has turned wild: a winding, willow-lined river with gravel bars and backwaters, marking borders as it goes. Soon it meets the Drava, and its floodplains form part of a vast protected wetland along the Mura, Drava and Danube.","w":"A winding river lined with dark floodplain forest and backwaters, crossing flat farmland patterned in long narrow strips.","f":["In 2021 UNESCO named the Mura–Drava–Danube area the world's first five-country biosphere reserve.","The Mur flows through Graz, Austria's second-largest city.","Along its lower course it forms parts of the borders of Austria, Slovenia, Croatia and Hungary."],"q":{"q":"Which Austrian city does the Mur flow through?","a":"Graz","x":["Salzburg","Linz"]}},"Q979518":{"t":"Borneo's brown highway to Banjarmasin","s":"A milky-brown river swings in wide loops through the forests and plantations of Central Kalimantan. For people here the Barito has long been the main road, carrying boats, logs and coal barges down to the coast. At its mouth stands Banjarmasin, a city of canals whose floating markets trade from boats at dawn.","w":"A wide brown river looping through dark green forest and paler plantation blocks, with small towns on the bends.","f":["The Barito is about 900 km long, one of the longest rivers of Borneo.","Banjarmasin is nicknamed the 'city of a thousand rivers' for its many canals.","Proboscis monkeys live in the mangroves and riverside forests of its lower course."],"q":{"q":"Which city stands at the mouth of the Barito?","a":"Banjarmasin","x":["Pontianak","Balikpapan"]}},"Q135477":{"t":"The river that keeps Lake Chad alive","s":"Most of the water that reaches Lake Chad comes down this river. The Chari rises in the Central African Republic and flows north-west for about 1,400 km, meandering through wide grassy floodplains that turn to marsh in the rainy season. At N'Djamena it meets the Logone, and together they run on to the lake.","w":"A broad, winding river with a wide, pale floodplain of grass and marsh, crossing flat savanna dotted with trees.","f":["The Chari supplies about 90% of the water that flows into Lake Chad.","Lake Chad has shrunk dramatically since the 1960s, which makes this inflow all the more vital.","N'Djamena, the capital of Chad, stands at the meeting of the Chari and the Logone."],"q":{"q":"Which lake does the Chari feed?","a":"Lake Chad","x":["Lake Victoria","Lake Volta"]}},"Q131210":{"t":"A river that twists through its canyon like a ribbon","s":"Few rivers loop as extravagantly as the Dniester here: it coils through a deep canyon cut into the Podolian plateau, bend after bend, between wooded slopes and pale rocky cliffs. Part of the canyon is now flooded behind a dam, forming a long, sinuous reservoir. The river runs about 1,350 km from the Carpathians to the Black Sea.","w":"A winding, snake-like river and reservoir in a deep wooded canyon, carved into a flat plateau of fields.","f":["The Dniester rises in the Carpathians, not far from the Polish border.","Khotyn Fortress, on a bluff above the river upstream, dates back to the Middle Ages.","It ends in the Dniester Estuary, a wide lagoon behind a sandbar on the Black Sea."],"q":{"q":"In which mountains does the Dniester rise?","a":"The Carpathians","x":["The Crimean Mountains","The Sudetes"]}},"Q60067":{"t":"Alaska's winter highway of river ice","s":"In summer the Kuskokwim is a wide, silty river looping through spruce forest and tundra; in winter it freezes so hard that people drive trucks and snowmobiles along it between villages. About 1,100 km long and without a single dam, it is the longest free-flowing river entirely within the United States. Its lower course crosses a flat delta freckled with lakes.","w":"A wide grey-brown river looping through dark spruce and tundra, with oxbow lakes and gravel bars along its banks.","f":["The Kuskokwim 300 sled dog race starts and ends at Bethel, on its lower course.","Its lower basin is part of the Yukon–Kuskokwim Delta, one of the largest river deltas in the world.","Salmon from the Kuskokwim have fed Yup'ik villages along its banks for thousands of years."],"q":{"q":"Which town on the Kuskokwim hosts a famous sled dog race?","a":"Bethel","x":["Juneau","Kodiak"]}},"Q393769":{"t":"A glacial river of grey silt below the Alaska Range","s":"Milky grey with glacial silt, the Tanana spreads into a maze of braided channels across a wide gravel floodplain, with the snowy wall of the Alaska Range to the south. Near Big Delta the Trans-Alaska Pipeline crosses it on its own suspension bridge. The river flows about 940 km north-west to the Yukon, passing close to Fairbanks.","w":"A wide, grey braided river on a broad gravel floodplain, snowy peaks of the Alaska Range rising to the south.","f":["Since 1917 people in Nenana have bet on the exact minute the Tanana's ice breaks up in spring.","The Alaska Highway ends at Delta Junction, just south of the river.","Its grey colour comes from rock flour ground up by glaciers in the Alaska Range."],"q":{"q":"What do people bet on each spring at Nenana on the Tanana?","a":"When the river ice breaks up","x":["The first salmon catch","The first snowfall"]}},"Q284603":{"t":"The river that named a caribou herd","s":"Old Crow, the only community in Yukon without a road, stands on a bend of the Porcupine well north of the Arctic Circle. Around it the Old Crow Flats spread out as a vast sheet of lakes and ponds, glittering in summer. The river flows west into Alaska and joins the Yukon at Fort Yukon, about 900 km from its source.","w":"A winding river through flat, lake-strewn tundra and thin spruce, with a small village on a river bend.","f":["The Porcupine caribou herd, roughly 200,000 animals, is named after this river.","The Old Crow Flats hold thousands of lakes and ponds and are a protected wetland.","Old Crow is home to the Vuntut Gwitchin, whose name means 'people of the lakes'."],"q":{"q":"Which animal herd is named after the Porcupine River?","a":"Caribou","x":["Muskoxen","Wood bison"]}},"Q1546553":{"t":"A river of endless oxbows in the Alaskan wilds","s":"The Koyukuk is a lesson in how rivers wander: tight loops, cut-off oxbow lakes and crescent-shaped scars fill its broad, wooded valley. It rises in the Brooks Range north of the Arctic Circle and flows about 680 km south-west to the Yukon. Only a handful of small villages stand on its banks.","w":"A heavily meandering river surrounded by countless oxbow lakes and crescent-shaped ponds in dark spruce forest.","f":["Gold strikes around Wiseman and Coldfoot, on its upper course, drew miners around 1900.","The Nogahabara Dunes, a field of active sand dunes, lie within its wildlife refuge.","The Koyukuk joins the Yukon at the village that shares its name."],"q":{"q":"Which great river does the Koyukuk flow into?","a":"The Yukon","x":["The Kuskokwim","The Tanana"]}},"ne-rv-1159114673":{"t":"An Ethiopian highland river heading for Somalia","s":"Rain on the Bale Mountains, some of the highest land in Ethiopia, feeds the Genale. It drops south-east from the highlands into dry, bushy lowlands, where it winds in a green thread of riverside trees. At Dolo, close to where Ethiopia, Kenya and Somalia meet, it joins the Dawa, and together they become the Jubba.","w":"A thin green line of trees along a winding river, crossing dry, reddish bush country that falls away from the highlands.","f":["The Bale Mountains at its source are home to the Ethiopian wolf, the world's rarest canid.","It is the main headstream of the Jubba, one of Somalia's two great rivers.","Its upper course is also known as the Ganale Doria."],"q":{"q":"The Genale and the Dawa join to form which river?","a":"The Jubba","x":["The Shabelle","The Omo"]}},"Q559283":{"t":"A Karakoram river feeding a Silk Road oasis","s":"The Yarkand comes down from glaciers of the Karakoram, grey with silt, and fans out into a braided channel as it leaves the mountains for the Tarim Basin. Its water sustains one of the great oases on the southern edge of the Taklamakan: Yarkant, an old Silk Road town. Downstream it is the longest source of the Tarim River.","w":"A grey, braided river fanning out from bare mountains onto a desert plain, with a big green oasis of fields beside it.","f":["The Yarkand is about 1,100 km long.","Yarkant was for centuries a caravan town on the southern Silk Road, between Kashgar and Khotan.","Its headwaters lie among Karakoram glaciers above 5,000 m."],"q":{"q":"Which river does the Yarkand help to form?","a":"The Tarim","x":["The Indus","The Ili"]}},"Q1241991":{"t":"A fur-trade river crossing the great northern bogs","s":"Near here the Albany leaves the rocky Canadian Shield for the Hudson Bay Lowlands, one of the largest wetlands on Earth, where bogs and fens form stripes and teardrop shapes. The river runs about 980 km from Lake St. Joseph to James Bay. At its mouth, Fort Albany was one of the Hudson's Bay Company's earliest trading posts.","w":"A broad brown river winding east through dark spruce and vast bogs patterned with stripes and pools.","f":["The Hudson's Bay Company set up its post at the mouth of the Albany in the 1670s.","Since 1943 the Ogoki Diversion has sent part of its upper water south into the Great Lakes.","At about 980 km, the Albany is one of the longest rivers in Ontario."],"q":{"q":"Where does the Albany River end?","a":"James Bay","x":["Lake Superior","Lake Winnipeg"]}},"Q81555":{"t":"A lonely northern river of the lost town Mangazeya","s":"Pale bogs, thousands of small lakes and thin larch forest stretch to the horizon as the Taz meanders north across the West Siberian Plain. About 1,400 km long, it ends in the Taz Estuary, a long arm of the Gulf of Ob. In the 17th century the fur-trading town of Mangazeya thrived on its lower course, then was abandoned.","w":"A winding river through flat, pale bog and larch woodland spattered with countless small lakes.","f":["Mangazeya, founded around 1601, was nicknamed 'gold-boiling' for the wealth of its fur trade.","The Selkup people have fished and herded reindeer along the Taz for centuries.","The river stays frozen for more than half the year."],"q":{"q":"Which 17th-century fur town stood on the Taz?","a":"Mangazeya","x":["Beryozovo","Obdorsk"]}},"Q1063190":{"t":"A green river of old trading towns in western Hunan","s":"The Yuan winds north-east through the forested hills of western Hunan, past terraced fields and old river towns. Hongjiang, on its bank here, grew rich for centuries shipping timber, tung oil and salt by boat. After more than 1,000 km, the river empties into Dongting Lake, one of China's great freshwater lakes.","w":"A winding green river between wooded ridges and terraced fields, with small towns clustered on its bends.","f":["Taoyuan county on the lower Yuan claims to be the setting of Tao Yuanming's tale 'Peach Blossom Spring'.","In Guizhou, where it rises, the river is called the Qingshui.","The Yuan is about 1,030 km long."],"q":{"q":"Which lake does the Yuan River flow into?","a":"Dongting Lake","x":["Poyang Lake","Tai Lake"]}},"Q1068297":{"t":"Atlanta's river, stretched into long lakes","s":"A long reservoir with dozens of branching fingers spreads through the Georgia woods here: West Point Lake, one of several on the Chattahoochee. The river rises in the Blue Ridge Mountains, supplies most of metro Atlanta's water, and then forms the border between Georgia and Alabama. Far to the south it joins the Flint to become the Apalachicola.","w":"A long, many-fingered reservoir along a river in rolling forest and farmland on the Georgia–Alabama border.","f":["Lake Lanier, upstream, provides most of the drinking water for metro Atlanta.","The river runs about 690 km, from the Blue Ridge to Lake Seminole.","A national recreation area protects a green strip of the river inside metro Atlanta."],"q":{"q":"Which river does the Chattahoochee join to form the Apalachicola?","a":"The Flint","x":["The Coosa","The Savannah"]}},"Q558643":{"t":"A slow, looping river through the Black Belt","s":"The Alabama River takes its time, looping in wide, lazy meanders through the Black Belt, a crescent of dark, fertile soil that once grew much of the South's cotton. It forms near Montgomery where the Coosa and the Tallapoosa meet, and joins the Tombigbee north of Mobile. In 1965 civil rights marchers set out from Selma across the Edmund Pettus Bridge over this river.","w":"A brown river swinging in wide loops through a patchwork of dark fields, pine plantations and a long, narrow reservoir.","f":["The Black Belt region takes its name from its dark, rich soil.","With the Tombigbee it forms the Mobile River, which drains into Mobile Bay.","The river is about 500 km long."],"q":{"q":"Which two rivers meet to form the Alabama?","a":"The Coosa and the Tallapoosa","x":["The Tombigbee and the Black Warrior","The Flint and the Chattahoochee"]}},"Q602":{"t":"A strong river in a corridor of vineyards","s":"South of Lyon the Rhône runs straight and strong through a narrow corridor, with vineyards on its steep slopes and a road, a railway and a motorway squeezed along its banks. This is the heart of the northern Côtes du Rhône: Côte-Rôtie and Condrieu lie just upstream. From its Swiss glacier, the river runs about 810 km to its delta in the Camargue.","w":"A wide river running north–south in a narrow valley, lined by canals, roads and railways, with steep hillsides on its west bank.","f":["The Rhône starts at the Rhône Glacier in the Swiss Alps and passes through Lake Geneva.","It carries more water to the sea than any other French river.","The cold mistral wind funnels down this valley towards the Mediterranean."],"q":{"q":"Which lake does the Rhône flow through before entering France?","a":"Lake Geneva","x":["Lake Constance","Lake Maggiore"]}},"Q171675":{"t":"The longest of the five rivers of Punjab","s":"Punjab means 'five waters', and the Sutlej is the longest of the five. It rises in Tibet near the sacred lakes below Mount Kailash, cuts straight through the Himalaya in deep gorges, and then crosses the flat plains of Punjab. Here its wide, sandy bed often carries only a narrow stream, as much of its water is taken for irrigation upstream.","w":"A wide, pale sandy riverbed with a thin braided stream, across a flat green grid of irrigated fields.","f":["The Sutlej is about 1,450 km long.","It is one of the few rivers that cut right across the main Himalayan range.","Joined by the Chenab, it becomes the Panjnad, which flows into the Indus."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Punjab mean?","a":"Five waters","x":["Land of wheat","Green plain"]}},"Q798339":{"t":"A wild tundra river racing to the Arctic Ocean","s":"Almost 1,000 km of lakes, rapids and falls, and hardly a tree for most of the way: the Back River crosses the barren lands of Nunavut to the Arctic Ocean. It is named after George Back, a British naval officer who travelled down it in 1834. For most of the year it lies frozen under snow.","w":"A river threading a chain of irregular lakes and white rapids across bare, rolling tundra speckled with ponds.","f":["It was once known as the Great Fish River.","It reaches the sea at Chantrey Inlet, on the Arctic coast.","Along its course the river drops through dozens of rapids."],"q":{"q":"What was the Back River once called?","a":"The Great Fish River","x":["The Copper River","The Caribou River"]}},"Q2242":{"t":"Rocky Mountain water on its way across the prairie","s":"The line on the map here traces the Bow River, the South Saskatchewan's main headstream, just downstream of Calgary. Born at Bow Lake in Banff National Park, it runs clear and green across rolling prairie, lined with cottonwoods. Further east it meets the Oldman to form the South Saskatchewan, which carries the water on across the dry plains.","w":"A green river winding south-east across prairie fields and round irrigation circles, with Calgary's sprawl just to the north-west.","f":["Counting from the Bow's source, the South Saskatchewan system runs well over 1,000 km.","Lake Diefenbaker, a large reservoir on the South Saskatchewan, supplies water across much of southern Saskatchewan.","Below Calgary the Bow is famous among anglers for its trout."],"q":{"q":"Which two rivers meet to form the South Saskatchewan?","a":"The Bow and the Oldman","x":["The Red Deer and the Bow","The Milk and the Oldman"]}},"Q1259188":{"t":"Burkina's Nakambé, pooled behind the Bagré dam","s":"In Burkina Faso the White Volta is called the Nakambé. Here in the south of the country it is held back by the Bagré dam, spreading into a long, branching lake among dry savanna and small farms. Downstream it crosses into Ghana, flows through the northern plains not far from Tamale, and ends in the vast Lake Volta.","w":"A long, branching reservoir in dry, tan savanna dotted with trees and small fields, fed from the north.","f":["The Bagré dam supplies water for rice fields and hydropower in southern Burkina Faso.","The White Volta rises in northern Burkina Faso, towards the edge of the Sahel.","Its tributary the Red Volta joins it just across the border in Ghana."],"q":{"q":"What is the White Volta called in Burkina Faso?","a":"Nakambé","x":["Mouhoun","Comoé"]}},"Q875573":{"t":"The river that gave the Han Chinese their name","s":"The name Han, borne by a dynasty and then by China's largest ethnic group, traces back to this river. Here on the Jianghan Plain it winds between dikes through a flat, watery land of rice fields, fish ponds and lakes. It joins the Yangtze at Wuhan, where the district of Hankou, 'Han mouth', stands at the confluence.","w":"A winding river between dikes across a flat plain of rice paddies, silver fish ponds and scattered lakes.","f":["The Danjiangkou reservoir on its upper course sends water north towards Beijing along a canal over 1,000 km long.","About 1,500 km long, the Han is one of the Yangtze's largest tributaries.","Hankou, at its mouth, is one of the three towns that merged to form Wuhan."],"q":{"q":"At which city does the Han River meet the Yangtze?","a":"Wuhan","x":["Chongqing","Nanjing"]}},"Q271044":{"t":"The 'great river' of India's rice bowl","s":"Mahanadi means 'great river' in Sanskrit. Here in Chhattisgarh its basin is a flat, green-and-gold plain of rice fields, crossed by wide, sandy riverbeds that fill only in the monsoon. Downstream in Odisha the river is held back by the Hirakud dam, then spreads into a broad delta on the Bay of Bengal.","w":"A wide, pale sandy riverbed with a thin stream, crossing a flat patchwork of rice fields and village ponds.","f":["With its dikes, the Hirakud dam stretches for about 25 km, one of the longest dams in the world.","Chhattisgarh is often called the rice bowl of central India.","The Mahanadi is roughly 850 km long."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Mahanadi mean?","a":"Great river","x":["Holy river","Black river"]}},"Q1535828":{"t":"A West African river named by Portuguese sailors","s":"Portuguese navigators named its mouth after Saint Andrew, and the name stuck to the whole river. The Sassandra runs south for about 650 km from the Guinean highlands, through savanna and then rainforest, to the Gulf of Guinea. Here it winds through mixed forest and farmland, some 200 km before the Buyo dam holds it back in a large lake.","w":"A winding brown river through a mosaic of forest patches, plantations and fields, heading south towards a large reservoir.","f":["Taï National Park, one of West Africa's last large rainforests, lies west of its lower course.","The Buyo reservoir on the Sassandra is one of the largest lakes in Côte d'Ivoire.","The river reaches the sea at the small port town of Sassandra."],"q":{"q":"Which saint is the name Sassandra thought to come from?","a":"Saint Andrew","x":["Saint Anthony","Saint Sebastian"]}},"Q3292":{"t":"Lake Winnipeg's outlet, harnessed on its way to the Bay","s":"Everything that drains into Lake Winnipeg, from the Rockies to the edge of Ontario, leaves through the Nelson. The river runs about 640 km north-east across the Canadian Shield to Hudson Bay, and on its lower course a chain of big dams turns its power into electricity for Manitoba. Around it lie endless spruce forest, bog and lakes.","w":"A wide river cutting north-east through dark spruce and pale bog, with dams and long reservoirs along its lower course.","f":["Thomas Button named it in 1612 after Robert Nelson, his ship's master, who died there.","York Factory, near its mouth, was long the Hudson's Bay Company's main depot on the Bay.","Its basin covers about a million km² in Canada and the United States."],"q":{"q":"Which lake does the Nelson River drain?","a":"Lake Winnipeg","x":["Lake Superior","Great Slave Lake"]}},"Q1229":{"t":"A quiet steppe river under white chalk cliffs","s":"White chalk cliffs rise on the Don's right bank, bright against green woods and yellow fields, while the left bank spreads into a low floodplain of meadows, oxbows and sandbars. The Don runs about 1,870 km from near Tula to the Sea of Azov. The ancient Greeks knew it as the Tanais and saw it as the boundary between Europe and Asia.","w":"A winding river with pale chalk cliffs on one side and a wide green floodplain with oxbow lakes on the other.","f":["Mikhail Sholokhov's novel And Quiet Flows the Don is set among the Cossacks of its middle course.","The Volga–Don Canal, opened in 1952, links it with the Volga near Volgograd.","The Don ends in the Sea of Azov, often called the shallowest sea in the world."],"q":{"q":"Which sea does the Don flow into?","a":"The Sea of Azov","x":["The Caspian Sea","The Baltic Sea"]}},"Q1046685":{"t":"The river that gave Jiangxi its short name","s":"Jiangxi province's one-character name, Gan, comes from this river, which runs the length of the province from south to north. Here it winds through red hills and bright green rice paddies near Taihe. After about 750 km it spreads into Poyang Lake, China's largest freshwater lake, which drains into the Yangtze.","w":"A wide, winding river through a patchwork of red-earth hills and green rice paddies, with sandbars on the bends.","f":["Nanchang, Jiangxi's capital, stands on the Gan; its Tengwang Pavilion is one of China's most famous towers.","The upper Gan was once feared for its 'eighteen rapids', now largely tamed by dams.","Poyang Lake, where it ends, swells hugely in the summer floods and shrinks in winter."],"q":{"q":"Which province's short name, Gan, comes from this river?","a":"Jiangxi","x":["Hunan","Guangdong"]}},"Q193499":{"t":"A Deccan river of dams and legendary diamonds","s":"The Krishna crosses the dry Deccan plateau from west to east, and here it widens into the great Almatti reservoir, a sheet of water among brown fields. It rises in the Western Ghats near Mahabaleshwar and runs about 1,400 km to the Bay of Bengal. Some of history's most famous diamonds came from mines along its lower course.","w":"A broad, branching reservoir with jagged arms spreading over a flat, brown plateau of fields.","f":["The Koh-i-Noor is thought to have come from the Kollur mine on the lower Krishna.","Nagarjuna Sagar dam, downstream, is one of the largest masonry dams in the world.","The Almatti dam stores water to irrigate the dry plains of northern Karnataka."],"q":{"q":"Which famous diamond is thought to come from a mine on the Krishna?","a":"The Koh-i-Noor","x":["The Cullinan","The Excelsior"]}},"Q2249":{"t":"The river where the Rocky Mountains end","s":"The Canadian Rockies run out at the Liard's valley, and here the river swings through their rugged northern tip in a canyon of rapids, with forested slopes and bare grey peaks around it. Its French name, from the fur traders, means 'cottonwood', after the poplars on its banks. It flows about 1,100 km to meet the Mackenzie at Fort Simpson.","w":"A green river winding through a deep forested valley between grey mountain ridges, with gravel bars on its bends.","f":["Liard River Hot Springs, just upstream, is one of the largest natural hot springs in Canada.","Wood bison graze along the Alaska Highway near its valley.","Below Great Slave Lake, no river brings more water to the Mackenzie."],"q":{"q":"What does the fur traders' name Liard mean?","a":"Cottonwood","x":["Rapids","Beaver"]}},"Q732790":{"t":"A river of rapids between Angola and Congo","s":"The Kwango rises on the high plateaus of northern Angola and flows north for about 1,100 km through savanna and gallery forest, for a long stretch marking the border with the Democratic Republic of the Congo. Falls and rapids break its course. Downstream it joins the Kasai, and its water finally reaches the Congo River.","w":"A winding river with a dark ribbon of gallery forest across rolling, grassy plateau cut by small valleys.","f":["The Kwango is the largest tributary of the Kasai.","Its valley in Angola is known for diamonds washed from the river gravels.","It meets the Kasai near the town of Bandundu."],"q":{"q":"Which river does the Kwango flow into?","a":"The Kasai","x":["The Ubangi","The Zambezi"]}},"Q846063":{"t":"A river through Zambia's oldest national park","s":"The Kafue winds through one of Africa's great wildernesses: Kafue National Park, Zambia's oldest and largest, with wooded savanna, floodplains and grassy dambos stretching to the horizon. Upstream the river crosses the Copperbelt; downstream it spreads over the Kafue Flats, home to herds of lechwe antelope, before joining the Zambezi.","w":"A dark, winding river with pale floodplain grassland, through endless olive-green woodland dotted with shallow dambos.","f":["Kafue National Park covers about 22,400 km², a little larger than Wales.","The Kafue lechwe, a water-loving antelope, lives only on the Kafue Flats.","At about 960 km, the Kafue is the longest river flowing entirely within Zambia."],"q":{"q":"Which antelope lives only on the Kafue Flats?","a":"The Kafue lechwe","x":["The sitatunga","The sable antelope"]}},"Q2742774":{"t":"A river of gems and clay dolls in dry Minas Gerais","s":"The Jequitinhonha rises near Diamantina, where diamonds were found in the 18th century, and flows north-east through dry hills of scrub and pasture to the Atlantic in Bahia. Its valley is famous across Brazil for folk art, especially painted clay dolls made by local women. Gemstones such as tourmaline and aquamarine are mined in its hills.","w":"A winding, sandy river in a valley of rounded, dry hills, grey-green scrub and patches of pasture.","f":["Diamantina, near its source, is a UNESCO World Heritage site.","It runs about 1,100 km to the Atlantic at Belmonte, in Bahia.","The Irapé dam on its upper course is one of the tallest dams in Brazil."],"q":{"q":"What folk art is the Jequitinhonha valley famous for?","a":"Painted clay dolls","x":["Lace tablecloths","Carved wooden masks"]}},"Q955354":{"t":"A lonely river across Patagonia to Welsh villages","s":"For hundreds of kilometres the Chubut crosses almost empty Patagonian plateau, a thin green thread between ochre cliffs and grey-brown steppe. It rises in the Andes and runs about 800 km east to the Atlantic. In its lower valley Welsh settlers arrived in 1865, and towns like Gaiman still serve Welsh tea.","w":"A thin, winding green line across a vast brown plateau, with layered ochre cliffs along parts of the valley.","f":["Some families in the Chubut valley still speak Welsh.","Fossils of Patagotitan, one of the largest known dinosaurs, were found in Chubut province.","The Florentino Ameghino dam holds a reservoir in a canyon on its lower course."],"q":{"q":"Settlers from which country arrived in the lower Chubut valley in 1865?","a":"Wales","x":["Scotland","Ireland"]}},"Q1162365":{"t":"A salty river winding across the hot Chaco plain","s":"Salado simply means 'salty', and the river earns the name: its water picks up salt from the dry soils it crosses. Rising in the Andes of Salta, where it is called the Juramento, it winds south-east for more than 1,000 km across the flat, scrubby plains of Santiago del Estero. It ends in the Paraná near Santa Fe.","w":"A thin meandering river with pale banks, winding across flat olive-green scrub and large cleared fields.","f":["Its upper course in Salta is known as the Pasaje or Juramento.","Summer temperatures on the plains it crosses can climb above 45 °C.","Other Argentine rivers share its name, so it is often called the Salado del Norte."],"q":{"q":"What does the river's name, Salado, mean?","a":"Salty","x":["Sacred","Sandy"]}},"Q1165761":{"t":"The 'white river' of Brazil's far north","s":"Branco means 'white', for its pale, silty water, which stands out among the dark rivers of the Amazon. Here it curves past Boa Vista, the only Brazilian state capital lying wholly north of the equator, across the open savannas of Roraima known as the lavrado. Downstream it enters the rainforest and flows into the Rio Negro.","w":"A broad, pale river curving past a fan-shaped city, with open grassy savanna and scattered lakes around it.","f":["Boa Vista's streets fan out from the riverbank in a radial plan that is easy to see from the air.","The Branco is formed by the Uraricoera and the Tacutu, which meet north of Boa Vista.","It is the largest river of the state of Roraima."],"q":{"q":"Which river does the Branco flow into?","a":"The Rio Negro","x":["The Madeira","The Orinoco"]}},"Q655251":{"t":"A great river of the Colombian llanos","s":"The llanos stretch flat to every horizon, green grassland striped with dark gallery forest, and through them the Meta loops east towards the Orinoco. In the rainy season much of this plain floods into a shimmering sheet of water; in the dry season it turns gold. Along its last stretch the river forms the border between Colombia and Venezuela.","w":"A broad, looping brown river with sandbars, crossing flat green grassland striped by dark lines of gallery forest.","f":["The Meta is about 1,000 km long and one of the Orinoco's main tributaries.","It joins the Orinoco at Puerto Carreño.","Llaneros, the cowboys of the plains, have herded cattle along its banks for centuries."],"q":{"q":"Which river does the Meta flow into?","a":"The Orinoco","x":["The Amazon","The Magdalena"]}},"Q849693":{"t":"A rainforest river rich in oxbow lakes","s":"The Madre de Dios is a textbook rainforest river: a broad brown ribbon swinging in huge loops, leaving oxbow lakes that glint like horseshoes in the green. It rises in the Andes near Manu National Park in Peru and flows east into Bolivia, where it joins the Beni. Giant otters and caimans live in its quiet lakes.","w":"A wide brown river looping through unbroken forest, with shining horseshoe-shaped lakes along its course.","f":["Manu National Park, near its source, is one of the most biodiverse places on Earth.","Macaws gather in their hundreds at clay licks on its riverbanks.","Its name means 'Mother of God' in Spanish."],"q":{"q":"Which river does the Madre de Dios join in Bolivia?","a":"The Beni","x":["The Mamoré","The Ucayali"]}},"ne-rv-1159116437":{"t":"The river where Brazil's patron saint was found","s":"In 1717 three fishermen on the Paraíba do Sul pulled a small clay statue of the Virgin from the water near today's Aparecida; it became Our Lady Aparecida, patron saint of Brazil. The river runs along a long valley between the Serra do Mar and the Serra da Mantiqueira, linking the states of São Paulo and Rio de Janeiro, and reaches the Atlantic after about 1,100 km.","w":"A winding river in a wide valley between two mountain ranges, lined with towns, a highway and farmland.","f":["The basilica at Aparecida is one of the largest Catholic churches in the world.","Much of Rio de Janeiro's water supply is pumped from the Paraíba do Sul.","In the 19th century its valley was Brazil's great coffee-growing region."],"q":{"q":"What did fishermen pull from the Paraíba do Sul in 1717?","a":"A statue of the Virgin Mary","x":["A gold nugget","A giant catfish"]}},"Q18278":{"t":"The river that gave a country its name","s":"A whole country took its name from this river: Uruguay lies on its eastern bank. Here, far upstream, the Uruguay is a green-brown river winding through rolling hills of forest and farmland, with Brazil on one bank and Argentina on the other. A little upstream it tumbles over the Moconá Falls, which run lengthwise along the river rather than across it.","w":"A winding river in a deep green valley between rolling hills, forest and patchwork farmland on its banks.","f":["Uruguay's official name means 'Eastern Republic of the Uruguay' – the land east of this river.","It runs about 1,800 km and, with the Paraná, forms the Río de la Plata.","Its name is often translated from Guaraní as 'river of the painted birds'."],"q":{"q":"Which river joins the Uruguay to form the Río de la Plata?","a":"The Paraná","x":["The Paraguay","The Iguazú"]}},"Q21806688":{"t":"A muddy river that loses itself in the Chaco","s":"The Pilcomayo carries so much silt down from the Bolivian Andes that it keeps choking its own bed. Here it leaves the foothills and enters the flat Gran Chaco, where three countries meet on its banks. Further on, much of its water spills into shifting marshes, and its channel moves from year to year.","w":"A pale, silty river with a wide sandy bed across flat, olive-green Chaco scrub, with dry channels branching off.","f":["Its sediment load is among the highest of any river in the world.","It rises in the Andes of Bolivia, near Potosí.","For much of its course it forms the border between Argentina and Paraguay."],"q":{"q":"On which river do Argentina, Bolivia and Paraguay meet?","a":"The Pilcomayo","x":["The Bermejo","The Paraguay"]}},"Q2635327":{"t":"A dark rainforest river in bonobo country","s":"Endless rainforest, broken only by the dark, twisting line of the Tshuapa: this is the heart of the Congo Basin. The river winds west for about 1,000 km, joining other streams to reach the Congo near Mbandaka, a city on the equator. Its basin lies south of the Congo River, the only region in the world where wild bonobos live.","w":"A narrow, dark river twisting through unbroken, deep-green rainforest, its banks almost hidden by the canopy.","f":["The province of Tshuapa, in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, is named after the river.","Its water reaches the Congo by way of the Busira and the Ruki.","Salonga National Park, Africa's largest tropical rainforest reserve, lies to its south-west."],"q":{"q":"Which great ape lives wild only south of the Congo River?","a":"The bonobo","x":["The gorilla","The chimpanzee"]}},"Q2629730":{"t":"An Arctic outpost where the Kolyma meets the ice","s":"Where the wide, braided Kolyma finally reaches the East Siberian Sea, a small bay bites into the tundra: Ambarchik. In Soviet times ships sailing the Northern Sea Route anchored here to hand their cargo over to river boats heading upstream. Today the settlement is all but abandoned, surrounded by lake-speckled tundra and a sea that stays frozen for most of the year.","w":"A small bay on the sea coast just east of the Kolyma's wide mouth, framed by flat tundra dotted with lakes.","f":["The Kolyma, which meets the sea close by, flows for more than 2,100 km.","At almost 70° N, Ambarchik lies roughly 350 km north of the Arctic Circle.","The nearest town, Chersky, is about 100 km to the south."],"q":{"q":"Which river reaches the sea near Ambarchik?","a":"The Kolyma","x":["The Indigirka","The Lena"]}},"Q212219":{"t":"An empty Arctic island beside the continent's northern tip","s":"Mainland North America runs out just across the water from this island. Bellot Strait, about 2 km wide at its narrowest, separates Somerset Island from the tip of the Boothia Peninsula, the continent's northernmost point. The island is a bare, brownish plateau cut by steep valleys, with no permanent residents. In summer, belugas gather in Cunningham Inlet on its northern shore.","w":"A flat, brownish plateau between wide Barrow Strait to the north and the hair-thin Bellot Strait to the south.","f":["Somerset Island covers about 24,800 km², yet nobody lives here permanently.","Bellot Strait is named after the French officer Joseph René Bellot, who joined the search for Franklin's lost expedition.","Fort Ross, a trading post at the island's southern tip, operated from 1937 until 1948."],"q":{"q":"Which strait separates Somerset Island from the mainland?","a":"Bellot Strait","x":["Fury and Hecla Strait","Hudson Strait"]}},"Q1044754":{"t":"Green volcanic ridges and a lagoon strung with islets","s":"The island is green right down to the water, and its eastern side is edged by one of the world's great lagoons: Marovo, a maze of turquoise channels and forested islets behind a double line of reef. Roviana Lagoon traces the south-western coast in much the same way. Inland, steep volcanic ridges carry unbroken rainforest, and clouds often snag on them.","w":"A long green island with turquoise lagoons along its coasts; the round volcanic cone of Kolombangara rises just to the north-west.","f":["Marovo Lagoon is often described as the largest saltwater lagoon in the world.","New Georgia covers about 2,000 km² and gives its name to a whole group of islands in the west of Solomon Islands."],"q":{"q":"Which huge lagoon lines New Georgia's eastern side?","a":"Marovo Lagoon","x":["Aitutaki Lagoon","Rangiroa Lagoon"]}},"Q913470":{"t":"Flat coral tables afloat east of New Caledonia","s":"No mountains here: the Loyalty Islands are old coral reefs lifted out of the sea, so they look like flat green tables edged with pale cliffs and white sand. Lifou is the largest; Ouvéa is the showpiece, a thin crescent of land hugging a vast turquoise lagoon. Most islanders are Kanak, and each main island has its own language.","w":"A line of low, flat green islands east of New Caledonia's long, mountainous main island; Ouvéa's lagoon glows turquoise.","f":["Lifou, the largest island of the group, covers more than 1,100 km².","Ouvéa's lagoon is lined by a white beach that runs for about 25 km.","These are uplifted atolls: their flat tops are old reef, raised out of the sea by movements of the Earth's crust."],"q":{"q":"Which is the largest of the Loyalty Islands?","a":"Lifou","x":["Maré","Ouvéa"]}},"Q274388":{"t":"Coral specks and reef rings in the South China Sea","s":"Out in the open sea, scattered rings of pale reef and tiny coral islands break the deep blue: the Paracels. There are two main clusters, the Amphitrite Group in the north-east and the Crescent Group in the south-west, whose islets curve around a shallow lagoon. None of them rises much above the waves, and the largest, Woody Island, covers only about 2 km².","w":"Tiny pale islets and turquoise reef rings in open water, south-east of Hainan and east of central Vietnam.","f":["The archipelago is made up of more than a hundred small coral islands, reefs and banks.","The Crescent Group owes its name to the arc its islets form around a lagoon."],"q":{"q":"In which sea do the Paracel Islands lie?","a":"The South China Sea","x":["The East China Sea","The Sulu Sea"]}},"Q1720063":{"t":"Namibia's wild north-west of red rock and dry rivers","s":"This is one of the emptiest corners of southern Africa: a jumble of rust-red and ochre mountains cut by sandy riverbeds that carry water only after rare rains. To the west the land drops towards the fog-bound Skeleton Coast, and in the north the green thread of the Kunene River marks the border with Angola. Kaokoland is home to the Himba, semi-nomadic cattle herders.","w":"Bare red-brown ranges and pale dry riverbeds, with Atlantic fog to the west and the Kunene's green ribbon to the north.","f":["Himba women coat their skin and hair with otjize, a paste of butterfat and red ochre.","Desert-adapted elephants roam the region, following dry riverbeds in search of water and food.","On the Kunene, the Epupa Falls tumble through a belt of palm-fringed channels."],"q":{"q":"Which people live in Kaokoland?","a":"The Himba","x":["The Maasai","The Zulu"]}},"Q193264":{"t":"An island the size of Switzerland in the Amazon's mouth","s":"Before it reaches the Atlantic, the Amazon's brown water parts around an island almost as big as Switzerland. Marajó is really two landscapes: dense forest in the west and, in the east, flat grassland that floods in the rainy season and glitters with lakes. Its ranches are famous for water buffalo; in the town of Soure even the police have patrolled on them.","w":"A huge flat island between brown river channels, forest-dark in the west, pale and lake-spotted in the east.","f":["At about 40,000 km², Marajó is often called the world's largest island formed between river and sea.","Long before Europeans, people here built earth mounds and made elaborate painted pottery now known as Marajoara."],"q":{"q":"Marajó is roughly the size of which country?","a":"Switzerland","x":["Luxembourg","Portugal"]}},"Q648391":{"t":"An Arctic island named after a forgotten kettle","s":"In 1773 the merchant Ivan Lyakhov and his companions found a copper kettle on this island, left by visitors no one could name, and 'Kettle Island' it became. Kotelny is the largest of the New Siberian Islands: low hills, bare tundra and long snowbound months. To the south-east it merges with its neighbour Faddeyevsky through Bunge Land, a vast, almost bare sandy plain.","w":"A broad tundra island, white for most of the year; to the south-east, the pale, almost featureless sand flats of Bunge Land.","f":["Kotelny alone covers about 11,700 km²; together with Bunge Land and Faddeyevsky, the landmass is roughly twice as big.","The New Siberian Islands were once famous for mammoth tusks, gathered here by ivory hunters."],"q":{"q":"Kotelny belongs to which archipelago?","a":"The New Siberian Islands","x":["Severnaya Zemlya","Franz Josef Land"]}},"Q21162":{"t":"Pointed peaks and glaciers at the top of Europe","s":"The Dutch explorer Willem Barentsz named it in 1596 for what he saw: 'pointed mountains'. From the air it is exactly that, with sharp dark ridges, tongues of glacier spilling into fjords and snow almost everywhere. The main town, Longyearbyen, stands by Isfjorden, the long fjord that cuts deep into the west coast. In summer the sun does not set for four months.","w":"Jagged dark mountains between white glaciers, and long fjords, Isfjorden the largest, opening westward to the sea.","f":["Spitsbergen covers about 37,600 km², more than Belgium.","About 2,500 people live in Longyearbyen, one of the northernmost towns in the world.","The Svalbard Global Seed Vault, dug into a mountainside near Longyearbyen, stores seeds from all over the world."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Spitsbergen mean?","a":"Pointed mountains","x":["White island","Cold harbour"]}},"Q134116":{"t":"Where Parry's ships crossed 110° west and won a prize","s":"In 1819 William Edward Parry's two ships forced their way west through the ice and crossed 110° W off this island, winning his crews a £5,000 prize offered by Parliament. They then spent the winter frozen in at a bay they named Winter Harbour. Today Melville Island is uninhabited: brown, rolling, lake-dotted tundra where muskoxen and caribou graze.","w":"A large, low, brownish island on the north side of the wide Viscount Melville Sound, snow-covered most of the year.","f":["At about 42,000 km², Melville Island is roughly the size of Denmark.","The boundary between the Northwest Territories and Nunavut crosses the island along 110° W.","Parry's men carved their names on a sandstone boulder at Winter Harbour, still known as Parry's Rock."],"q":{"q":"Who wintered at Melville Island in 1819–20?","a":"William Edward Parry","x":["John Franklin","Roald Amundsen"]}},"Q207751":{"t":"The island that half-closes the door to Hudson Bay","s":"Hudson Bay's northern entrance is half blocked by one big, flat island. Southampton is mostly a low limestone plain speckled with lakes, rising to hills only in the north-east, and sea ice surrounds it for much of the year. About a thousand people live in its only settlement, Coral Harbour, named for the fossil corals found nearby. Walruses, polar bears and huge colonies of snow geese share the rest.","w":"A large, flat, lake-speckled island across the northern mouth of Hudson Bay, often ringed by sea ice.","f":["Covering about 41,000 km², Southampton is one of Canada's ten largest islands.","Most of Coral Harbour's roughly 1,000 residents are Inuit."],"q":{"q":"What is the only settlement on Southampton Island?","a":"Coral Harbour","x":["Rankin Inlet","Iqaluit"]}},"Q207925":{"t":"An empty island on the Franklin expedition's route","s":"In 1846 John Franklin's Erebus and Terror sailed south down Peel Sound, the channel along this island's eastern shore, and never came home. Prince of Wales Island is still empty: a low, stony plateau scattered with countless lakes, frozen and snow-covered for most of the year. Summer turns it brown and blue, the lakes shining like shattered glass.","w":"A broad, low island between Somerset Island and Victoria Island, its surface freckled with countless lakes.","f":["The island covers about 33,000 km² and has no permanent residents.","Peel Sound separates it from Somerset Island to the east; M'Clintock Channel separates it from Victoria Island to the west."],"q":{"q":"Which channel separates Prince of Wales Island from Somerset Island?","a":"Peel Sound","x":["Bellot Strait","Davis Strait"]}},"Q207666":{"t":"Arctic ice caps and a forest that never turned to stone","s":"Ice caps crown the mountains and glaciers slide down into deep fiords, yet this was once warm forest. In the Geodetic Hills lie stumps of dawn redwoods about 45 million years old, so well preserved that the wood can still be cut and burned. The island is named after Axel Heiberg, a Norwegian businessman who helped fund Otto Sverdrup's expedition here around 1900.","w":"Mountains under white ice caps and glaciers reaching the sea, with Ellesmere Island to the east and the flat Ringnes islands to the west.","f":["Axel Heiberg Island covers about 43,000 km² and has no permanent residents.","The neighbouring Ringnes islands are named after two brewer brothers who also sponsored Sverdrup.","A McGill University research station has studied the island's glaciers since the early 1960s."],"q":{"q":"What is preserved in Axel Heiberg's Geodetic Hills?","a":"A fossil forest","x":["A meteorite crater","Viking ruins"]}},"Q186841":{"t":"The largest island on Earth with nobody living on it","s":"Scientists come here to rehearse for Mars. The Haughton crater, about 20 km across and blasted out by a meteorite millions of years ago, lies in a bare, rusty-grey polar desert that looks remarkably like the Red Planet. The eastern part of Devon Island is buried under a thick ice cap. At about 55,000 km², it is the largest island in the world with no permanent residents.","w":"A huge island north of Lancaster Sound: a white ice cap in the east, a bare brown plateau cut by canyons in the west.","f":["Since the late 1990s the Haughton-Mars Project has used the crater to test equipment and methods for exploring Mars.","The Devon Ice Cap covers about 14,000 km² in the east of the island."],"q":{"q":"Which record does Devon Island hold?","a":"Largest uninhabited island","x":["Northernmost island on Earth","Canada's largest island"]}},"Q148839":{"t":"Remote volcanic islands where La Pérouse vanished","s":"The lost French expedition of La Pérouse ended here: in 1788 both its ships were wrecked on the reefs of Vanikoro, and the mystery was solved only nearly forty years later. The Santa Cruz Islands are the far eastern outpost of Solomon Islands, green and reef-fringed, with Nendö the largest. To the north, the near-perfect cone of Tinakula volcano rises straight out of the sea.","w":"Scattered green islands in coral reefs; the lone, sometimes steaming cone of Tinakula stands north of the largest island, Nendö.","f":["In 1595 the Spanish navigator Álvaro de Mendaña tried to found a colony on Nendö and died there that same year.","The islands were known for feather money: long coils made from the red feathers of a small honeyeater."],"q":{"q":"Whose expedition was wrecked on Vanikoro in 1788?","a":"La Pérouse's","x":["Bougainville's","James Cook's"]}},"Q271674":{"t":"Eight lonely atolls inside a giant marine reserve","s":"Eight atolls and reef islands are scattered across the central Pacific here, and almost nobody lives on them: only Kanton has a small community. The ocean around them is protected. The Phoenix Islands Protected Area covers about 400,000 km², roughly the size of California, and has been a World Heritage site since 2010. Its seldom-visited reefs are full of fish, sharks and seabirds.","w":"Tiny pale rings of reef with bright lagoons, far apart in deep blue ocean just south of the equator.","f":["Kanton, the only inhabited island, is home to just a few dozen people.","The group lies just south of the equator, roughly midway between Hawaii and Fiji."],"q":{"q":"Which is the only inhabited Phoenix Island?","a":"Kanton","x":["Nikumaroro","Enderbury"]}},"Q153732":{"t":"Green islands beside the deepest trench on Earth","s":"Just offshore, the ocean floor drops into the Mariana Trench, which curves along the islands and reaches nearly 11 km at the Challenger Deep, about as deep as you are flying high. The islands form an arc some 800 km long: bigger, flatter Guam, Rota, Tinian and Saipan in the south, steep volcanic cones in the north. Magellan's expedition reached them in 1521.","w":"A north–south chain of green islands; the big ones, Guam, Saipan and Tinian, in the south, small volcanic cones to the north.","f":["They are named after Mariana of Austria, the Spanish queen, in whose honour Jesuit missionaries renamed them in 1668.","Magellan's crew had called them the Islands of Thieves, Islas de los Ladrones."],"q":{"q":"Which expedition reached the Mariana Islands in 1521?","a":"Magellan's","x":["Columbus's","James Cook's"]}},"Q504043":{"t":"Volcanic peaks inside one vast turquoise lagoon","s":"Picture Bora Bora on a larger scale: a ring of reef encloses a wide lagoon, and inside it rise the steep green remains of an old volcano. The largest island, Mangareva, climbs to about 440 m at Mount Duff. The Gambiers mark the south-eastern end of French Polynesia, and their lagoon farms are known for black pearls.","w":"A turquoise lagoon inside a thin ring of reef, with a cluster of steep dark-green islands within it.","f":["About 1,500 people live in the Gambier Islands, most of them on Mangareva.","In 1797 the captain of the missionary ship Duff named the islands after the British admiral James Gambier."],"q":{"q":"Which is the largest of the Gambier Islands?","a":"Mangareva","x":["Rangiroa","Moorea"]}},"Q172697":{"t":"Wild volcanic spires with no reef to soften them","s":"Unlike most of Polynesia, the Marquesas have almost no coral reefs or lagoons: the ocean hits the cliffs head-on, and green ridges and basalt spires shoot up from deep blue water. Tahiti is about 1,400 km away, and these are among the most remote islands on the planet. In 2024 the archipelago, with its seabird cliffs and ancient stone platforms, became a UNESCO World Heritage site.","w":"Steep dark-green islands with jagged ridges and no lagoons around them; surf breaks straight onto the cliffs.","f":["The Spanish navigator Mendaña named them in 1595 after the Marquis of Cañete, viceroy of Peru.","Marquesan tattoo is among the most elaborate in Polynesia, sometimes covering the whole body."],"q":{"q":"What do the Marquesas lack that most Polynesian islands have?","a":"Lagoons and barrier reefs","x":["Volcanic peaks","Rainforest"]}},"Q38095":{"t":"Darwin's islands of black lava and giant tortoises","s":"Dark lava fields, shield volcanoes with calderas like sunken bowls, and patches of green in the highlands: the Galápagos sit on the equator nearly 1,000 km off the coast of Ecuador. Charles Darwin spent about five weeks here in 1835, and the islands' finches, tortoises and iguanas later fed his thinking on evolution. Isabela, the largest island, is shaped a little like a seahorse.","w":"Dark volcanic islands with round calderas; the largest, Isabela, is a long, seahorse-shaped chain of volcanoes on the western side.","f":["'Galápago' is an old Spanish word for tortoise, after the giant tortoises that live here.","In 1978 the Galápagos were among the first twelve places inscribed as UNESCO World Heritage sites.","Marine iguanas, found only here, dive into the sea to graze on algae."],"q":{"q":"Who visited the Galápagos aboard HMS Beagle in 1835?","a":"Charles Darwin","x":["Alfred Russel Wallace","Alexander von Humboldt"]}},"Q162126":{"t":"Volcanic islands where tomorrow is next door","s":"The chain runs west to east as a line of green volcanic islands: big Savai'i and Upolu first, then Tutuila and the tiny Manu'a group. They are shared by Samoa and American Samoa, whose capitals are little more than 100 km apart yet a calendar day apart, because in 2011 Samoa moved to the other side of the International Date Line. Coral reefs draw pale borders around the dark-green shores.","w":"A west–east line of dark-green volcanic islands with pale reef edges; the two largest, Savai'i and Upolu, lie at the western end.","f":["Samoa skipped 30 December 2011 entirely when it moved west of the International Date Line.","Robert Louis Stevenson spent his last years on Upolu and is buried on Mount Vaea above Apia.","Fa'a Samoa, the Samoan way of life, is built around the extended family and its chiefs."],"q":{"q":"Which date did Samoa skip in 2011?","a":"30 December","x":["31 December","1 January"]}},"Q913624":{"t":"Forested islands between a deep sea and a shallow one","s":"A long, low, forested island leads the group: Yamdena, with smaller islands scattered around it, set between the deep Banda Sea and the shallow Arafura Sea. This is a quiet corner of eastern Indonesia, far from the tourist trail. Its forests shelter the Tanimbar corella, a small white cockatoo that lives in the wild nowhere else, and villages still weave richly patterned ikat cloth.","w":"A long green island, Yamdena, running roughly north-east to south-west, with smaller islands and pale reef edges around it.","f":["The main town, Saumlaki, lies at the southern end of Yamdena.","North of the islands the Weber Deep in the Banda Sea plunges more than 7 km."],"q":{"q":"Between which two seas do the Tanimbar Islands lie?","a":"The Banda and Arafura seas","x":["The Java and Flores seas","The Celebes and Sulu seas"]}},"Q1980628":{"t":"Volcanic islands in the gap between Luzon and Taiwan","s":"North of Luzon, the strait between the Philippines and Taiwan is dotted with a loose cluster of volcanic islands: Calayan, Camiguin, Dalupiri, Fuga and Babuyan itself. Several peaks here are active volcanoes, and the cone of Babuyan Claro stands out from far away. In late winter and spring, humpback whales come to these warm waters to breed.","w":"A scatter of green islands in open sea north of Luzon, several topped by volcanic cones; the Batanes lie farther north.","f":["The Calayan rail, a flightless bird found only on Calayan island, was first described by scientists in 2004.","The islands belong to the Philippine province of Cagayan, on the northern tip of Luzon."],"q":{"q":"Which flightless bird was discovered in the Babuyan Islands in 2004?","a":"The Calayan rail","x":["The Guam rail","The weka"]}},"Q171132":{"t":"Dry, sunlit islands circling sacred Delos","s":"Scattered across the deep blue Aegean, the Cyclades are brown, bare and sharp-edged, each fringed with a thin white line of surf. Their name comes from the Greek word for a circle: the ancients saw them ringing the sacred island of Delos. At the southern end lies Santorini's broken ring, the drowned crater of a volcano; Naxos, the largest island, sits near the centre.","w":"Dozens of brown, bare islands in deep blue sea; in the south, the ring of cliffs of Santorini around a flooded caldera.","f":["The group counts about 220 islands and islets.","Bronze Age islanders carved strikingly simple marble figures, now known as Cycladic figurines.","The Venus de Milo was found in 1820 on Milos, one of the Cyclades."],"q":{"q":"Which sacred island do the Cyclades 'circle'?","a":"Delos","x":["Crete","Rhodes"]}},"Q4991540":{"t":"The 'endless plains' of the great migration","s":"The Maasai name means 'endless plains', and from the air that is exactly what you see: golden or green grassland to the horizon, broken by dots of acacia and piles of granite boulders called kopjes. More than a million wildebeest, with zebras and gazelles, circle through it every year, following the rains. The highlands and the great crater of Ngorongoro rise at its south-eastern edge.","w":"Vast flat grassland, tawny or green, with scattered trees and granite outcrops; Lake Victoria to the west, crater highlands to the south-east.","f":["Serengeti National Park covers about 14,750 km² and has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1981.","In the north the plains continue across the border into Kenya as the Maasai Mara."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Serengeti mean in the Maasai language?","a":"Endless plains","x":["Land of lions","Red earth"]}},"Q83857":{"t":"Central Europe's great flat bowl of fields and puszta","s":"The Tisza once flooded this plain every spring; today it is a flat chessboard of fields, its rivers tamed behind embankments and old meanders left behind as curved oxbow lakes. The plain fills about half of Hungary and spills into neighbouring countries. In the Hortobágy the open grassland, the puszta, survives, with grey cattle and spiral-horned sheep grazing to the horizon.","w":"A perfectly flat patchwork of fields, crossed by the winding Tisza and its crescent-shaped oxbow lakes.","f":["Hortobágy, Hungary's first national park, is a UNESCO World Heritage site.","On hot summer days the puszta is known for mirages, called délibáb in Hungarian.","Hungary's lowest point, about 78 m above sea level, lies on the plain near Szeged."],"q":{"q":"Which river winds across the Great Hungarian Plain?","a":"The Tisza","x":["The Vistula","The Elbe"]}},"Q1052758":{"t":"A vast table of land dotted with green circles","s":"Flat as a table and just as sharply edged, the Llano Estacado ends in steep escarpments, the Caprock in the east, that drop to lower country all around. On top the land is so level that roads run ruler-straight for tens of kilometres. The dominant pattern is green and brown circles: fields watered by pivot sprinklers that draw on the Ogallala Aquifer.","w":"A flat plateau chequered with round irrigated fields, its eastern edge frayed by the Caprock Escarpment and Palo Duro Canyon.","f":["Covering roughly 97,000 km², it is one of the largest tablelands in North America.","Coronado's expedition crossed it in 1541; he wrote that there were no landmarks at all, as if they were at sea.","Lubbock and Amarillo are the largest cities on the plateau."],"q":{"q":"Which aquifer waters the Llano Estacado's round fields?","a":"The Ogallala","x":["The Edwards","The Floridan"]}},"Q26787455":{"t":"A green oval cradled between snowy ranges","s":"Walled in by the Pir Panjal range to the south-west and the Great Himalaya to the north-east, the valley floor lies about 1,600 m up and is strikingly flat and green. The Jhelum River meanders through it, feeding lakes such as Dal, beside Srinagar, and the broad Wular. Mughal emperors laid out terraced gardens here and came to escape the summer heat of the plains.","w":"A flat oval valley, green in summer, ringed by snowy ranges; lakes glint around Srinagar in the middle.","f":["The valley is roughly 135 km long and about 30 km wide.","Saffron is grown on the terraces around Pampore, south-east of Srinagar.","Wular Lake, fed by the Jhelum, is one of the largest freshwater lakes in South Asia."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through the Kashmir Valley?","a":"The Jhelum","x":["The Sutlej","The Chenab"]}},"Q18677875":{"t":"Hedgerows, chalk cliffs and the land of the Northmen","s":"Normandy takes its name from the Northmen, Viking settlers granted this land in 911; their descendant William sailed from here to conquer England in 1066. From the air the countryside is a fine green mesh of small fields bounded by hedgerows, the bocage. White chalk cliffs line the coast north of the Seine estuary, and at the western end Mont-Saint-Michel rises from its tidal bay.","w":"A patchwork of small green fields and hedges, with the wide Seine estuary to the east and the English Channel along the north.","f":["Normandy's apple orchards give cider and Calvados, an apple brandy.","Tides in the Bay of Mont-Saint-Michel can rise and fall by more than 10 m.","Camembert, Livarot and Pont-l'Évêque cheeses are all named after places in Normandy."],"q":{"q":"In which year were Viking settlers granted the land that became Normandy?","a":"911","x":["1066","843"]}},"Q12130":{"t":"A ragged granite prow thrust into the Atlantic","s":"Brittany juts into the Atlantic like the bow of a ship, and its coast is frayed into headlands, rocky islets and deep estuaries. Inland, small fields and woods make a green quilt. The westernmost department is called Finistère, 'the end of the land'. Breton, a Celtic language related to Welsh and Cornish, is still spoken and taught here.","w":"A broad peninsula pointing west into the ocean, its edges frayed into headlands, islands and inlets; the island-filled Gulf of Morbihan on the south coast.","f":["Near Carnac, about 3,000 standing stones form long rows raised more than 5,000 years ago.","The Gulf of Morbihan is dotted with dozens of islands; its name means 'little sea' in Breton."],"q":{"q":"Which Celtic language is spoken in Brittany?","a":"Breton","x":["Basque","Occitan"]}},"Q1370633":{"t":"A maze of blue water and dark forest","s":"Water and forest interlock so tightly here that it is hard to say which is the background: blue lakes full of islands, threaded by narrow straits and long gravel ridges left by the ice sheet. Saimaa, the largest lake system, sprawls across the south-east. In winter it all turns white, and the lakes show only as smooth, flat patches among the trees.","w":"An endless labyrinth of island-studded lakes and dark green forest; long, narrow ridges cross the water in places.","f":["Finland has around 188,000 lakes, and a great many of them lie in this region.","The Saimaa ringed seal, one of the rarest seals on Earth, lives only in Lake Saimaa.","Punkaharju is an esker, a gravel ridge laid down by a river under the ice sheet, and a road now runs along it between lakes."],"q":{"q":"Which rare seal lives only in Lake Saimaa?","a":"The Saimaa ringed seal","x":["The Baikal seal","The Caspian seal"]}},"Q6146133":{"t":"A broad ice-free lowland beside the world's biggest fjord","s":"Most of east Greenland is mountains and ice, which makes Jameson Land stand out: a broad, rolling, snow-patched lowland with hardly a glacier on it. It lies on the north side of Scoresby Sund, the largest fjord system in the world. The land is built of soft sedimentary rocks, and muskoxen graze its tundra. The airstrip at Constable Point serves the remote village of Ittoqqortoormiit.","w":"A wide brownish lowland north of the mouth of Scoresby Sund, between the mountains of Liverpool Land and the ice-capped ranges inland.","f":["William Scoresby Jr named it in 1822 after Robert Jameson, his geology professor in Edinburgh.","Scoresby Sund reaches about 350 km inland from the open sea."],"q":{"q":"Which huge fjord system lies beside Jameson Land?","a":"Scoresby Sund","x":["Sognefjord","Ilulissat Icefjord"]}},"Q1081204":{"t":"China's tropical island on the South China Sea","s":"Hainan is China's tropical south: a big, rounded island with forested mountains in the middle and a rim of rice paddies, rubber plantations and beaches. The narrow Qiongzhou Strait separates it from the mainland, and the capital, Haikou, sits on the north coast facing it. At the southern tip, Sanya's bays draw crowds seeking winter sun.","w":"A large rounded island with green mountains in the south-centre and flatter farmland in the north, across a narrow strait from the mainland.","f":["Wuzhi Shan, 'Five Finger Mountain', the island's highest peak, rises to more than 1,800 m.","The Qiongzhou Strait is only about 20–30 km wide.","The Li people, one of China's officially recognised ethnic groups, live mainly in Hainan's mountainous interior."],"q":{"q":"Which strait separates Hainan from mainland China?","a":"The Qiongzhou Strait","x":["The Taiwan Strait","The Strait of Malacca"]}},"Q248654":{"t":"An island almost entirely under one great ice dome","s":"A single smooth dome of ice, the Academy of Sciences Ice Cap, hides most of Komsomolets; it is among the largest in Russia. Only a thin rim of bare, stony ground shows at its edges. At the northern tip, Cape Arktichesky is a traditional starting point for skiers heading to the North Pole, a little under 1,000 km away.","w":"The northernmost island of Severnaya Zemlya: a smooth white ice dome with sharp edges, surrounded by sea ice most of the year.","f":["The ice cap covers about 5,600 km², most of the island's roughly 9,000 km².","Severnaya Zemlya was discovered only in 1913 and first mapped in 1930–32 by Georgy Ushakov and Nikolai Urvantsev.","The island's name honours the Komsomol, the Soviet youth organisation."],"q":{"q":"Which cape of Komsomolets is a starting point for North Pole treks?","a":"Cape Arktichesky","x":["Cape Chelyuskin","Cape Dezhnev"]}},"Q650720":{"t":"The island facing Eurasia's northernmost cape","s":"Across the Vilkitsky Strait from this island lies Cape Chelyuskin, the northernmost point of mainland Eurasia. Bolshevik is the southernmost of Severnaya Zemlya's big islands, and less of it is buried than on its neighbours: ice caps hold the high ground, while bare, stony tundra and deep valleys fill the rest. The strait between it and the mainland is icebound for much of the year.","w":"A large island north of the Taymyr Peninsula, ice caps on its heights and grey-brown tundra around them, across a wide strait from the mainland.","f":["Bolshevik Island covers about 11,000 km².","The Vilkitsky Strait, named after the explorer who found these islands in 1913, is a key passage of the Northern Sea Route."],"q":{"q":"Which cape lies across the strait from Bolshevik Island?","a":"Cape Chelyuskin","x":["Cape Dezhnev","Cape Arktichesky"]}},"Q217372":{"t":"The biggest island of Severnaya Zemlya, crowned with ice","s":"Several ice caps crown this island, the largest in Severnaya Zemlya, and between them run bare, deeply cut valleys. In the 2010s the Vavilov Ice Cap on its western side suddenly began to slide fast into the Kara Sea, pushing out a broad lobe of ice that shows clearly in satellite images. The highest point of the whole archipelago, Mount Karpinsky, rises here to about 965 m.","w":"A large island in the middle of Severnaya Zemlya: several white ice domes separated by dark, bare valleys and fjords.","f":["October Revolution Island covers about 14,000 km².","Its name, given in the early 1930s, commemorates the revolution of October 1917."],"q":{"q":"Which is the largest island of Severnaya Zemlya?","a":"October Revolution Island","x":["Bolshevik Island","Komsomolets Island"]}},"Q779867":{"t":"Low specks of land lost in the Kara Sea ice","s":"For most of the year it is hard to tell these islands from the sea around them: both are white and flat. They are a small cluster of low, bare islets in the Kara Sea, only about 15 km across in all, with nobody living on them. The group is named after the Arctic Institute in Leningrad, whose scientists explored and mapped much of the Soviet Arctic.","w":"A few tiny, low islets in the open Kara Sea, grey-brown in brief summer, lost under snow and ice otherwise.","f":["The islands lie at about 75° N, nearly 1,000 km north of the Arctic Circle.","The Arctic Institute, founded in 1920, still works in St Petersburg as the Arctic and Antarctic Research Institute."],"q":{"q":"In which sea do the Arctic Institute Islands lie?","a":"The Kara Sea","x":["The Laptev Sea","The Barents Sea"]}},"Q944346":{"t":"A lonely dome of ice on the northern Kara Sea","s":"Imagine a white shield dropped on the sea: Schmidt Island is almost entirely covered by a smooth ice cap that reaches nearly to the shore. It stands alone in the Kara Sea off the north-west coast of Severnaya Zemlya, and nobody lives on it. The island was discovered in 1930 by an icebreaker expedition led by Otto Schmidt, and now bears his name.","w":"A small, lone white dome in the sea north-west of Severnaya Zemlya, hard to tell from the ice around it for most of the year.","f":["The Schmidt Ice Cap covers about 385 km², most of the island.","In 1937 Otto Schmidt led the landing of the first drifting research station near the North Pole."],"q":{"q":"Who is Schmidt Island named after?","a":"Otto Schmidt","x":["Georgy Ushakov","Fridtjof Nansen"]}},"Q504449":{"t":"A rain-soaked island of wooden churches and ghost ships","s":"Green, rolling and often under cloud, Chiloé lies just off the Chilean mainland across the narrow Chacao Channel. Its eastern side is a quilt of small farms and inlets facing an inner sea dotted with smaller islands; the Pacific side is wilder, with dense forest. Islanders built dozens of wooden churches, and their folklore tells of the Caleuche, a ghost ship that sails these waters.","w":"A large green island south-west of Puerto Montt: farmland and inlets on the east side, dark forest facing the open Pacific.","f":["Sixteen of Chiloé's wooden churches together form a UNESCO World Heritage site.","Covering about 8,400 km², Chiloé is Chile's second-largest island after Tierra del Fuego."],"q":{"q":"Which ghost ship sails through the folklore of Chiloé?","a":"The Caleuche","x":["The Flying Dutchman","The Mary Celeste"]}},"Q185532":{"t":"The 'mother island' of the Moluccas, steep and forested","s":"Seram is long and mountainous, its ridges clothed in rainforest and often lost in cloud. Local tradition calls it Nusa Ina, 'mother island', the place the peoples of the central Moluccas came from. Manusela National Park covers much of the centre, climbing towards Mount Binaiya at about 3,000 m, and the smaller island of Ambon lies just off Seram's south-western coast.","w":"A long island, over 300 km from east to west, with a steep green mountain spine; smaller Ambon lies off its south-western end.","f":["The salmon-crested cockatoo, a large pink-tinged parrot, lives in the wild only on Seram and a few nearby islands.","Covering about 17,000 km², Seram is the second-largest island of the Moluccas after Halmahera."],"q":{"q":"What does Seram's local name, Nusa Ina, mean?","a":"Mother island","x":["Island of cloves","Land of fire"]}},"Q201398":{"t":"A four-armed island ringed by clove volcanoes","s":"Halmahera looks like a smaller copy of Sulawesi: four mountainous arms reaching out around three deep bays. It is the largest island of the Moluccas, the original Spice Islands. Just off its west coast rise the neat volcanic cones of Ternate and Tidore, rival sultanates that grew rich on cloves. On the northern arm, Dukono volcano often trails a plume of ash.","w":"A K-shaped island with four long arms around deep bays; small round volcanic islands, Ternate and Tidore, line its western shore.","f":["Wallace's standardwing, a bird of paradise, lives on Halmahera and a few nearby islands.","Alfred Russel Wallace's famous 1858 letter on natural selection was sent from Ternate, just off Halmahera."],"q":{"q":"Which spice made the sultanates beside Halmahera rich?","a":"Cloves","x":["Cinnamon","Pepper"]}},"Q83067":{"t":"A mountain island that legend says was once a crocodile","s":"A Timorese legend tells that the island is the body of a giant crocodile that turned to land, and its long, tapering outline does suggest one. Unlike its volcanic neighbours, Timor was pushed up by the collision of the Australian plate with the edge of Asia. Its mountains stay brown and dry for much of the year and turn green only in the rainy season.","w":"A long island running south-west to north-east, with rugged brown-green mountains along its spine and the Timor Sea to the south.","f":["The name comes from 'timur', the Malay and Indonesian word for east.","The island is shared by Indonesia in the west and Timor-Leste in the east.","Mount Ramelau, the highest peak, rises to nearly 3,000 m."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Timor mean?","a":"East","x":["Mountain","Crocodile"]}},"Q1061269":{"t":"A long green blade between two seas","s":"Palawan is a long, narrow blade of green, more than 400 km from end to end and rarely more than 40 km wide, with the South China Sea on one side and the Sulu Sea on the other. Its northern end breaks up into limestone islands around El Nido, sheer grey towers rising from turquoise water. Near Puerto Princesa, a river runs for kilometres through a cave before reaching the sea.","w":"A long, thin, mountainous island running north-east to south-west, with scattered limestone islets off its northern tip.","f":["The Puerto Princesa Underground River is a UNESCO World Heritage site.","Tubbataha Reefs, out in the Sulu Sea south-east of the island, is another World Heritage site, famous for diving."],"q":{"q":"Which sea lies along Palawan's eastern side?","a":"The Sulu Sea","x":["The Celebes Sea","The Philippine Sea"]}},"Q6497558":{"t":"The island of the Chocolate Hills","s":"In the middle of Bohol, more than a thousand grassy, rounded hills rise from the plain like upturned bowls, so regular they look almost man-made. In the dry season the grass turns brown and they become the famous Chocolate Hills. The island itself is a rough oval fringed with reefs, and Panglao, joined to it by bridges, draws divers with its white beaches.","w":"An oval green island between Cebu and Mindanao; the Chocolate Hills make a stippled patch in its interior.","f":["The Philippine tarsier, a tiny primate with enormous eyes, lives in Bohol's forests.","The Chocolate Hills are limestone mounds shaped by rain and erosion."],"q":{"q":"Why are Bohol's Chocolate Hills so called?","a":"They turn brown in the dry season","x":["Cocoa is grown on them","Their soil is dark volcanic ash"]}},"Q212777":{"t":"Sugar fields beneath a smoking volcano","s":"Negros is the Philippines' sugar island: its western plains are a patchwork of cane fields, bright green while growing and bare brown after the harvest. Above them rises Kanlaon, an active volcano about 2,400 m high and the tallest peak in the Visayas. A mountain spine runs down the island's length, dividing the broad sugar lands of the west from the narrower east coast.","w":"A long, boot-shaped island west of Cebu, with the volcanic cone of Kanlaon in its northern half and green fields on the west.","f":["Negros is often called the 'Sugarbowl of the Philippines'.","The island is split between two provinces, Negros Occidental and Negros Oriental."],"q":{"q":"Which crop dominates the plains of Negros?","a":"Sugarcane","x":["Coffee","Tea"]}},"Q211377":{"t":"Green hills, a slender strait and a Mass from 1521","s":"Leyte is ruggedly green, with a mountain backbone down its length and coastal plains planted with coconut palms and rice. At its north-eastern corner, the strait separating it from Samar narrows to a winding channel. Off the southern tip lies little Limasawa, where Magellan's crew celebrated the first Christian Mass in the Philippines in 1521.","w":"A green mountainous island east of Cebu, separated from Samar in the north-east by a thin, winding strait.","f":["The San Juanico Bridge, more than 2 km long, spans the strait between Leyte and Samar.","Volcanic heat in Leyte's mountains feeds some of the country's largest geothermal power fields."],"q":{"q":"Which island does the San Juanico Bridge link to Leyte?","a":"Samar","x":["Cebu","Bohol"]}},"Q219737":{"t":"A big, forested island facing the open Pacific","s":"Samar's eastern coast looks straight out over thousands of kilometres of open Pacific, and it is often the first land that ocean storms reach in the Philippines. Inside, low mountains are cloaked in one of the country's largest surviving lowland rainforests, riddled with rivers, gorges and caves. Tiny Homonhon, off its southern tip, is where Magellan first stepped ashore in the Philippines in 1521.","w":"A big, rugged green island north of Leyte, its eastern shore open to the Pacific, its interior forested and cut by rivers.","f":["Samar covers about 13,400 km², making it the third-largest island in the Philippines.","Samar Island Natural Park protects the largest stretch of its forest."],"q":{"q":"Where did Magellan first land in the Philippines in 1521?","a":"Homonhon","x":["Cebu","Mactan"]}},"Q332359":{"t":"A triangle of rice plains with Boracay off its tip","s":"Panay is almost a perfect triangle: a mountain range runs along its western side, and broad, bright-green rice plains spread east towards Iloilo. Just off the north-western corner lies little Boracay, a thin sliver of land with a dazzling white beach. Every January the town of Kalibo holds the Ati-Atihan, one of the oldest and liveliest festivals in the Philippines.","w":"A large triangular island in the central Philippines: mountains in the west, a flat green plain in the east, Boracay off the north-west tip.","f":["The fortress-like church of Miag-ao, near Iloilo, is part of a UNESCO World Heritage site.","Boracay's White Beach runs for about 4 km."],"q":{"q":"Which beach island lies off Panay's north-western tip?","a":"Boracay","x":["Siargao","Bantayan"]}},"Q211800":{"t":"Home of the tamaraw, a buffalo found nowhere else","s":"South of Luzon, Mindoro's mountainous interior climbs to Mount Halcon at around 2,600 m and is often wrapped in cloud. The forests and grasslands of those mountains are the last refuge of the tamaraw, a small, stocky wild buffalo that lives only here. The island's Mangyan peoples still write in old Indic-derived scripts, carving verses into bamboo.","w":"A large, compact island south of Luzon across the Verde Island Passage, with forested mountains in its centre.","f":["The Verde Island Passage off Mindoro's north coast is among the world's richest waters for marine life.","Apo Reef, off the west coast, is one of the largest coral reef systems in the Philippines."],"q":{"q":"Which wild animal lives only on Mindoro?","a":"The tamaraw","x":["The anoa","The banteng"]}},"Q644636":{"t":"The copper island with a long, thin panhandle","s":"Cyprus has an unmistakable outline: a broad body with a long, thin 'panhandle', the Karpas Peninsula, pointing north-east towards the Levant. In the west, the dark, pine-covered Troodos Mountains rise to about 1,950 m; a sharp limestone ridge runs along the north coast, and the flat Mesaoria plain fills the gap between. In winter, the salt lakes near Larnaca and Limassol fill with flamingos.","w":"A large island with a long, narrow peninsula pointing north-east; dark mountains in the west, a flat, pale plain in the middle.","f":["The Latin word for copper, cuprum, comes from the island's name; its copper mines were famous in antiquity.","Cyprus is the third-largest island in the Mediterranean, after Sicily and Sardinia.","Legend places the birth of Aphrodite at a sea rock off the south-west coast."],"q":{"q":"Which metal takes its Latin name from Cyprus?","a":"Copper","x":["Tin","Silver"]}},"Q200226":{"t":"Muskox country at the western edge of the Arctic islands","s":"Flat to gently rolling tundra, laced with rivers and lakes, Banks Island faces the Beaufort Sea across its western shore. It is muskox country and has long held one of the largest herds of these shaggy animals in the world. The only village, Sachs Harbour, has around a hundred residents. In the north, Aulavik National Park protects the valley of the Thomsen River.","w":"A big, flat, brownish island at the western end of the Arctic archipelago, facing the Beaufort Sea, with lakes and river valleys scattered across it.","f":["At about 70,000 km², Banks is Canada's fifth-largest island.","In 2006 a hunter near Sachs Harbour took the first confirmed wild grizzly–polar bear hybrid."],"q":{"q":"What is the only village on Banks Island?","a":"Sachs Harbour","x":["Tuktoyaktuk","Resolute"]}},"Q27508031":{"t":"Land of wood and water, under blue-hazed mountains","s":"The Taíno called it Xaymaca, 'land of wood and water', and from the air the name fits: green forest, rivers and a white-edged coast. In the east, the Blue Mountains rise to about 2,250 m, often veiled in a bluish haze. In the west-centre lies Cockpit Country, a limestone landscape pitted with steep hollows that make it look like an egg carton.","w":"An oval green island south of Cuba; the high Blue Mountains in the east, a curving sand spit enclosing Kingston's harbour on the south coast.","f":["Blue Mountain coffee, grown on the eastern slopes, is among the most prized in the world.","Jamaica is the third-largest island in the Caribbean, after Cuba and Hispaniola.","Kingston Harbour, sheltered by a long sand spit called the Palisadoes, is one of the world's largest natural harbours."],"q":{"q":"What does the Taíno name Xaymaca mean?","a":"Land of wood and water","x":["Island of flowers","Blue mountain"]}},"Q2076337":{"t":"Green mountains above the Atlantic's deepest trench","s":"Puerto Rico is a nearly rectangular island, about 160 km long, with a spine of green mountains, the Cordillera Central, running from east to west. In the north-east, the peaks of El Yunque catch the trade winds and stay wrapped in rainforest and cloud. Off the north coast the seabed plunges into the Puerto Rico Trench, the deepest part of the Atlantic, more than 8 km down.","w":"A rectangular green island east of Hispaniola, with mountains along its centre and the sprawl of San Juan on the north coast.","f":["El Yunque is the only tropical rainforest in the US National Forest System.","Mosquito Bay on the nearby island of Vieques glows at night with bioluminescent plankton.","Cerro de Punta, the highest peak, reaches about 1,340 m."],"q":{"q":"What makes Mosquito Bay on Vieques famous?","a":"Glowing plankton","x":["Nesting giant turtles","Pink sand"]}},"Q128323":{"t":"Three hills, a lake of tar and the steelpan","s":"Columbus named Trinidad in 1498 after the Holy Trinity, reportedly for three hills he saw on the horizon. The island is almost a rectangle, with a green mountain range along its north coast that continues the mountains of Venezuela across the water. The south-west holds a curiosity: Pitch Lake, the world's largest natural deposit of asphalt, a grey, wrinkled surface you can walk on.","w":"A roughly rectangular island off Venezuela's coast, with a green mountain ridge along the north and the Gulf of Paria to the west.","f":["The steelpan, made from oil drums, was invented in Trinidad in the 20th century.","The scarlet ibis, Trinidad and Tobago's national bird, roosts in the Caroni Swamp.","At its closest, Trinidad is only about 11 km from Venezuela."],"q":{"q":"What fills Trinidad's famous Pitch Lake?","a":"Natural asphalt","x":["Salt","Hot mud"]}},"Q188489":{"t":"An island with an inland sea at its heart","s":"Cape Breton hides a sea inside itself: Bras d'Or Lake, a branching body of salt water that fills much of the island's middle. In the north, the Cape Breton Highlands rise as a forested plateau that ends in steep cliffs above the Gulf of St Lawrence, blazing red and gold in autumn. Scottish settlers brought Gaelic here, and it still lives on in local music.","w":"A ragged island off Nova Scotia's north-eastern tip, with the branching blue Bras d'Or Lake in its centre and highlands in the north.","f":["Since 1955 the Canso Causeway has linked the island to the Nova Scotia mainland.","In the 18th century the French built the Fortress of Louisbourg on its east coast."],"q":{"q":"What fills the centre of Cape Breton Island?","a":"Bras d'Or Lake","x":["Minas Basin","Lake Champlain"]}},"Q25277":{"t":"The Caribbean's highest peaks and its lowest lake","s":"Hispaniola packs the extremes of the Caribbean into one island. Pico Duarte, at about 3,100 m, is the highest peak in the region, while Lake Enriquillo, a salty lake in a sunken valley, lies well below sea level. Between them run parallel mountain ranges separated by fertile valleys. The island is shared by Haiti in the west and the Dominican Republic in the east.","w":"A big mountainous island between Cuba and Puerto Rico; its western end splits into two long peninsulas like a lobster's open claws.","f":["Santo Domingo, founded in the 1490s, is the oldest continuously inhabited European-founded city in the Americas.","Lake Enriquillo is home to American crocodiles and rhinoceros iguanas.","Hispaniola is the second-largest island in the Caribbean, after Cuba."],"q":{"q":"Which lake on Hispaniola lies below sea level?","a":"Lake Enriquillo","x":["Lake Nicaragua","Lake Maracaibo"]}},"Q586657":{"t":"A long green crocodile stretched across the Caribbean","s":"Cubans call their island El Caimán, the crocodile, and its long, slender outline, more than 1,200 km from snout to tail, really does look like one. Much of it is gently rolling farmland of sugarcane, tobacco and royal palms, but the Sierra Maestra in the south-east rises to nearly 2,000 m. Along the north coast, strings of low cays sit in startlingly turquoise water.","w":"A very long, narrow island, mostly green and flat, with mountains in the far south-east and turquoise shallows off both coasts.","f":["Cuba is the largest island in the Caribbean, covering about 105,000 km².","In the west, the Viñales Valley is dotted with mogotes, steep-sided limestone hills rising from tobacco fields.","More than 4,000 smaller islands and cays surround the main island."],"q":{"q":"Which animal do Cubans see in the shape of their island?","a":"A crocodile","x":["A lizard","A shark"]}},"Q215089":{"t":"A long, thin island where men still call sharks","s":"New Ireland is one of the skinniest big islands anywhere: some 350 km long but in places only about 10 km wide, a green, mountainous ribbon curving across the Bismarck Sea. In a few villages, men still practise shark calling, luring sharks to their canoes with coconut-shell rattles. The island is also famous for malagan, intricate carved figures made for funeral ceremonies.","w":"A very long, narrow island curving from north-west to south-east, north-east of New Britain, with a mountain spine along its length.","f":["Kavieng, the main town, sits at the island's north-western tip.","Malagan carvings from New Ireland are kept in major museums around the world.","Under German rule the island was called Neu-Mecklenburg."],"q":{"q":"Which traditional skill is New Ireland known for?","a":"Shark calling","x":["Pearl diving","Fire walking"]}},"Q714573":{"t":"A steep rainforest island at the Solomons' south-eastern end","s":"Makira rises from the sea as a long, steep ridge of rainforest, with few roads and small villages tucked along the coast. It lies at the south-eastern end of the main Solomon Islands chain, beyond Guadalcanal. Sailors of Mendaña's Spanish expedition named it San Cristóbal in 1568, and both names are still used. Its forests shelter birds found nowhere else.","w":"A long, narrow, forested island south-east of Guadalcanal, its ridges often in cloud.","f":["Makira covers roughly 3,000 km² but has only a few short stretches of road.","The provincial capital, Kirakira, lies on its north coast."],"q":{"q":"What name did Spanish sailors give Makira in 1568?","a":"San Cristóbal","x":["Santa Isabel","Guadalcanal"]}},"Q713182":{"t":"The long island where Europeans first met the Solomons","s":"In 1568 Álvaro de Mendaña's ships reached this island, the first of the Solomons seen by Europeans, and named it after Saint Elizabeth. Santa Isabel is long and slender, about 200 km from end to end, with a forested mountain spine and a coast broken into bays and small islands. Few people live here, and most of the island is still covered by rainforest.","w":"A long, slender, forested island running north-west to south-east between Choiseul and Guadalcanal, fringed with islets.","f":["The Arnavon Islands, between Santa Isabel and Choiseul, are a major nesting site for hawksbill turtles.","The island's highest point, Mount Sasari, rises to about 1,200 m."],"q":{"q":"Which Solomon island did Mendaña's expedition reach first, in 1568?","a":"Santa Isabel","x":["Guadalcanal","Makira"]}},"Q618594":{"t":"One island, some thirty languages","s":"Malakula, Vanuatu's second-largest island, is a forested land of rugged hills, deep valleys and mangrove-lined bays. It is also one of the most linguistically diverse places on Earth: roughly thirty languages are spoken here, some by only a few hundred people. In the interior live the Big Nambas and Small Nambas, peoples named after their traditional dress.","w":"A large green island with ragged, mangrove-lined coasts; Espiritu Santo lies to the north and volcanic Ambrym to the east.","f":["Malakula covers about 2,000 km².","Lakatoro, on the east coast, is the main town and a provincial capital."],"q":{"q":"Roughly how many languages are spoken on Malakula?","a":"About 30","x":["About 3","About 300"]}},"Q713127":{"t":"The busy heart of the Galápagos","s":"Round, green-topped Santa Cruz sits in the middle of the Galápagos and is the busiest island: Puerto Ayora, on its south coast, is the archipelago's largest town. The land rises gently to misty highlands, where giant tortoises wander through pastures and forests of tree daisies. Lonesome George, the last tortoise of his kind, lived out his life at the research station here.","w":"A round volcanic island in the centre of the Galápagos, green in its highlands, with the small airport island of Baltra off its north coast.","f":["Twin pit craters called Los Gemelos open up in the highland forest.","The Charles Darwin Research Station at Puerto Ayora has worked to study and protect the islands since 1964."],"q":{"q":"Which famous tortoise lived at the station on Santa Cruz?","a":"Lonesome George","x":["Harriet","Jonathan"]}},"Q337519":{"t":"A huge shield volcano with a black lava coast","s":"Savai'i is one broad shield volcano, rising gently from the reef to forested heights of nearly 1,860 m at Mount Silisili, the highest point in Samoa. Its north coast still carries a wide black scar: lava from the Matavanu eruption of 1905–11 flowed through villages down to the sea. The island's name is related to Hawaiki, the legendary homeland of the Polynesians.","w":"A big, rounded green island west of Upolu, a gentle volcanic dome with dark lava fields along its northern coast.","f":["Savai'i is the largest island in Polynesia outside Hawaii and New Zealand.","At the Alofaaga blowholes on the south coast, waves shoot seawater high into the air."],"q":{"q":"What is the highest point in Samoa?","a":"Mount Silisili","x":["Mount Vaea","Mount Matavanu"]}},"Q847008":{"t":"The sacred island where Polynesian voyages began","s":"Raiatea shares one lagoon with its smaller neighbour Taha'a: two green, mountainous islands enclosed by a single ring of reef. For centuries this was the religious heart of eastern Polynesia, and from the great marae of Taputapuatea voyagers set out across the ocean. On the flat top of Mount Temehani grows the tiare apetahi, a white flower found nowhere else in the world.","w":"Two high green islands, Raiatea and Taha'a, inside one shared turquoise lagoon; Bora Bora lies to the north-west, Huahine to the east.","f":["Taputapuatea became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2017.","Raiatea's old name was Havai'i, echoed in the name of Hawai'i far to the north."],"q":{"q":"Which flower grows only on Raiatea's Mount Temehani?","a":"Tiare apetahi","x":["Tiare Tahiti","Frangipani"]}},"Q937908":{"t":"The island where Gauguin and Brel came to rest","s":"Hiva Oa is a rugged crescent of green ridges and plunging cliffs, with no reef to shelter its black-sand bays. Paul Gauguin spent his last years in the village of Atuona and was buried there in 1903; the Belgian singer Jacques Brel, who flew his own plane around the islands, lies in the same cemetery. At Puamau stand some of the largest stone tiki in Polynesia.","w":"A long, curving, mountainous island in the southern Marquesas, green ridges dropping steeply to the sea around a deep southern bay.","f":["Mount Temetiu, the highest point, rises to about 1,200 m.","Hiva Oa is the second-largest of the Marquesas, after Nuku Hiva."],"q":{"q":"Which painter is buried on Hiva Oa?","a":"Paul Gauguin","x":["Henri Matisse","Vincent van Gogh"]}},"Q21064":{"t":"The Marquesan valleys that inspired Melville's 'Typee'","s":"In 1842 a young sailor named Herman Melville jumped ship in Taiohae Bay and spent weeks in a valley on this island; the adventure became his first book, Typee. Nuku Hiva is the largest of the Marquesas, a jagged mass of ridges around deep green valleys, with a high plateau in the middle. Its main village, Taiohae, is the administrative centre of the archipelago.","w":"The biggest island of the northern Marquesas: steep green ridges and deep bays around a broad, grassy central plateau.","f":["Vaipo waterfall in the Hakaui valley drops about 350 m, one of the highest in French Polynesia.","Nuku Hiva covers about 340 km²."],"q":{"q":"Which Melville book grew out of his stay on Nuku Hiva?","a":"Typee","x":["Moby-Dick","Billy Budd"]}},"Q193331":{"t":"Earth's largest coral atoll, early to greet the New Year","s":"Kiritimati is the biggest coral atoll in the world by land area, and from above it looks like a pale lobster claw filled with turquoise and green ponds. James Cook arrived here on Christmas Eve 1777, hence the name. Kiribati's Line Islands keep the world's earliest time zone, UTC+14, so every new year begins here before almost anywhere else on Earth.","w":"A large, pale, claw-shaped atoll just north of the equator, its lagoon broken into countless turquoise and green ponds.","f":["Kiritimati is simply 'Christmas' written in the spelling of the Kiribati language.","Millions of seabirds, including huge colonies of sooty terns, nest on the atoll."],"q":{"q":"Why is Kiritimati also called Christmas Island?","a":"Cook arrived on Christmas Eve","x":["Its palms look like fir trees","It was settled on Christmas Day"]}},"Q131347":{"t":"Two old volcanoes, a city and the crater of Diamond Head","s":"Oahu is built from two old volcanoes, and you can still see them as two parallel mountain ranges: the Wai'anae in the west and the Ko'olau in the east, their cliffs fluted like organ pipes. Between them lies a green central plain. On the south coast, Honolulu spreads out beneath the crisp crater of Diamond Head, and at night the whole shoreline glitters.","w":"An island with two parallel mountain ranges; the city spreads along the south coast and ends at the round crater of Diamond Head.","f":["About two-thirds of Hawaii's people live on Oahu.","The island's name is often translated as 'the gathering place'.","Diamond Head got its English name from sailors who mistook calcite crystals on its slopes for diamonds."],"q":{"q":"Which crater rises at the eastern end of Waikiki?","a":"Diamond Head","x":["Haleakalā","Kīlauea"]}},"Q201026":{"t":"The Garden Isle, carved by rain into canyons and cliffs","s":"Kauai is the oldest of the main Hawaiian islands, and millions of years of rain have carved it deeply. Near its centre, Mount Wai'ale'ale is one of the rainiest places on Earth; to the west, Waimea Canyon cuts a red-and-green gorge about 900 m deep. Along the north-west coast, the Nā Pali cliffs drop in sharp green pleats straight into the ocean.","w":"A round green island north-west of Oahu, with the red gash of Waimea Canyon in the west and the pleated Nā Pali cliffs.","f":["In 1778 James Cook landed at Waimea on Kauai, the first recorded European visit to Hawaii.","Waimea Canyon is often called the Grand Canyon of the Pacific.","Mount Wai'ale'ale receives roughly 10 m of rain in an average year."],"q":{"q":"Who landed on Kauai in 1778?","a":"James Cook","x":["Ferdinand Magellan","Louis de Bougainville"]}},"Q327733":{"t":"Fiji's quieter big island behind a great reef","s":"Vanua Levu, Fiji's second-largest island, is a long, irregular sweep of green hills, with sugarcane plains around Labasa in the north and palm-fringed bays in the south. Deep Natewa Bay almost cuts its eastern end in two. Off the north coast runs the Great Sea Reef, one of the longest barrier reefs in the world, a pale line drawn across the blue.","w":"A long, ragged green island north-east of Viti Levu, a deep bay biting into its eastern arm and a reef line to the north.","f":["Vanua Levu covers about 5,600 km².","Its name means 'great land' in Fijian.","Savusavu, on the south coast, is a sheltered harbour popular with yachts."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Vanua Levu mean?","a":"Great land","x":["Sacred island","Land of reefs"]}},"Q16635":{"t":"Where America's day begins, on a limestone plateau","s":"Guam narrows at the waist like a footprint: the north is a flat limestone plateau ending in sheer cliffs above the sea, the south a jumble of volcanic hills with red earth. It is the largest and southernmost of the Mariana Islands. Because it lies so far west across the Pacific, its motto is 'Where America's Day Begins'.","w":"An elongated island narrowing in the middle: a flat, forested plateau in the north, hillier south, reefs all round.","f":["Latte stones, pillars topped with stone half-spheres, once supported the houses of the Chamorro people.","Covering about 540 km², Guam is the largest island in Micronesia."],"q":{"q":"Guam is the largest island of which chain?","a":"The Mariana Islands","x":["The Caroline Islands","The Marshall Islands"]}},"Q818749":{"t":"A row of smoking volcanoes at the top of the Kurils","s":"Paramushir is essentially a row of volcanoes joined into one island, their cones capped with snow for much of the year. Chikurachki, the tallest at about 1,800 m, and Ebeko, close to the town of Severo-Kurilsk, regularly puff out ash. Just to the north lies little Shumshu, and beyond it Cape Lopatka, the southern tip of Kamchatka.","w":"A long, snowy volcanic island in the northern Kurils, its peaks in a line from north-east to south-west, with Kamchatka's tip just to the north.","f":["Severo-Kurilsk, the island's only town, has a couple of thousand residents.","Covering about 2,000 km², Paramushir is one of the largest Kuril Islands."],"q":{"q":"Which peninsula's tip lies just north of Paramushir?","a":"Kamchatka","x":["Chukotka","Taymyr"]}},"Q697278":{"t":"Subtropical forests and a rabbit from another age","s":"Rugged and deeply green, Amami Ōshima lies in the chain of islands between Kyushu and Okinawa, its hills covered in dense subtropical forest and its bays edged with coral. These forests shelter the Amami rabbit, a dark, short-eared species regarded as a living fossil. Since 2021 the island has been part of a UNESCO World Heritage site. Its mud-dyed silk, Ōshima tsumugi, is among Japan's most prized fabrics.","w":"A rugged, deeply indented green island in the chain between Kyushu and Okinawa, with coral-fringed bays.","f":["Amami rabbits live only on Amami Ōshima and neighbouring Tokunoshima.","Ōshima tsumugi threads are dyed with the tannin of a local tree and iron-rich mud."],"q":{"q":"Which rare animal lives only on Amami Ōshima and Tokunoshima?","a":"The Amami rabbit","x":["The Iriomote cat","The Okinawa rail"]}},"Q1061195":{"t":"Stepping stones from Mindanao to Borneo","s":"Like stepping stones, the Sulu islands run south-west from the tip of Mindanao towards Borneo: Basilan, Jolo, Tawi-Tawi and hundreds of smaller ones, many ringed by turquoise shallows. For centuries this was the realm of the Sultanate of Sulu, a trading power dealing in pearls and sea cucumbers. Bajau sea people still live here in villages built on stilts over the water.","w":"A long chain of green islands and reefs curving south-west from Mindanao's Zamboanga Peninsula towards Borneo.","f":["The Sultanate of Sulu was founded in the 15th century.","The archipelago separates the Sulu Sea from the Celebes Sea.","Seaweed farming is one of the islands' main livelihoods."],"q":{"q":"Which two seas does the Sulu Archipelago separate?","a":"The Sulu and Celebes seas","x":["The Java and Banda seas","The Philippine and South China seas"]}},"Q5813":{"t":"Seven volcanic islands and Spain's highest peak","s":"Seven volcanic islands lie scattered off the coast of Africa, each with its own character: dark lava fields and craters on Lanzarote, pale dunes on Fuerteventura, deep green ravines on La Palma. In the middle of the chain, Tenerife's Teide rises to 3,715 m, the highest point in Spain, often poking above a sea of cloud. The islands are named after dogs, not birds.","w":"A west–east chain of islands off Morocco; Tenerife's volcanic cone, Teide, often stands above the clouds.","f":["The Romans knew one island as Canaria, 'island of dogs'; the canary bird was later named after the islands.","Fuerteventura lies only about 100 km from the African coast.","Before the Spanish conquest the islands were home to the Guanches and related peoples."],"q":{"q":"Which peak on Tenerife is the highest point in Spain?","a":"Teide","x":["Mulhacén","Aneto"]}},"Q210907":{"t":"Misty islands of the Haida on the edge of the continent","s":"Across the wide, stormy Hecate Strait from the British Columbia coast, Haida Gwaii rises as a triangle of dark-green islands. Some 150 of them, cloaked in rainforest of giant cedar and spruce, are often wrapped in mist. This is the homeland of the Haida, famed for their carved poles and ocean-going canoes. Unusual wildlife has earned the islands the nickname 'Galápagos of the North'.","w":"A triangular group of dark-forested islands well off the British Columbia coast, largest in the north, tapering to many small ones southward.","f":["At SGang Gwaay, a UNESCO site, weathered Haida poles still stand in an abandoned village.","The islands were long known in English as the Queen Charlotte Islands; the official name changed in 2010."],"q":{"q":"Which strait separates Haida Gwaii from the mainland?","a":"Hecate Strait","x":["Strait of Juan de Fuca","Johnstone Strait"]}},"Q140510":{"t":"A thousand forested islands along the Inside Passage","s":"Some 1,100 islands crowd the coast of south-east Alaska here, steep, dark with spruce and hemlock, and separated by narrow, fjord-like channels. Ships following the sheltered Inside Passage wind between them for hundreds of kilometres. On Baranof Island, Sitka was the capital of Russian America until Alaska was sold to the United States in 1867.","w":"A maze of steep, dark-green islands and narrow channels along the Alaska coast, with snowy mountains and glaciers on the mainland behind.","f":["Most of the islands lie within the Tongass, the largest national forest in the United States.","The archipelago is named after the Russian tsar Alexander II.","These islands are the homelands of the Tlingit and Haida peoples."],"q":{"q":"Which town in the archipelago was the capital of Russian America?","a":"Sitka","x":["Juneau","Anchorage"]}},"Q306147":{"t":"Wild islands off a crowded coast, home of a dwarf fox","s":"Just off one of the most crowded coastlines in America lie eight rugged, dry islands where the land looks almost untouched. Five of them form Channel Islands National Park; Santa Catalina, farther south, has a small resort town. Here lives the island fox, no bigger than a house cat, and in the Ice Age pygmy mammoths roamed Santa Rosa Island.","w":"A chain of brownish, cliff-edged islands off the coast between Santa Barbara and Los Angeles, the largest to the north-west.","f":["Remains found on Santa Rosa Island, about 13,000 years old, are among the oldest human remains known in North America.","Santa Cruz is the largest island in California."],"q":{"q":"Which dwarf animal lived on Santa Rosa Island in the Ice Age?","a":"The pygmy mammoth","x":["The pygmy hippo","The dwarf bison"]}},"Q179528":{"t":"A green fan pressed against the desert","s":"Nowhere is the line between life and desert sharper: the Nile Delta spreads as a dense green fan, and at its edges the sand begins abruptly. North of Cairo the river splits into two branches, Rosetta to the west and Damietta to the east, and between them lies one of the most intensively farmed landscapes on Earth. At night the whole triangle glitters with towns and villages.","w":"A huge green triangle between yellow deserts, pointing south towards Cairo, its coast edged with shallow lagoons.","f":["The ancient Greeks named it after the triangular letter delta.","The Rosetta Stone, the key to Egyptian hieroglyphs, was found in 1799 near the delta town of Rashid, or Rosetta.","Tens of millions of people live in the delta, though it covers only a small fraction of Egypt."],"q":{"q":"What are the Nile's two delta branches called?","a":"Rosetta and Damietta","x":["Alexandria and Suez","Giza and Luxor"]}},"Q2298744":{"t":"A frozen fan of channels on the Laptev Sea","s":"Before it reaches the Laptev Sea, the Lena splits into a vast fan of channels, islands and lakes about 400 km wide, the largest delta in the Arctic. In summer it is a mosaic of silver water and green-brown tundra; for the rest of the year it lies frozen and white. Tiksi, a port on the Northern Sea Route, stands just to the south-east.","w":"A huge fan of silver channels and countless lakes spreading into the Laptev Sea, white and frozen for most of the year.","f":["The delta covers about 32,000 km² and holds thousands of lakes.","Much of it is protected as part of the Ust-Lensky Nature Reserve.","The delta stays frozen for about seven to eight months of the year."],"q":{"q":"Into which sea does the Lena Delta spread?","a":"The Laptev Sea","x":["The Kara Sea","The East Siberian Sea"]}},"Q1277365":{"t":"A thirsty delta of creeks and mangroves","s":"The Indus ends not in a lush fan but in a pale, braided maze of tidal creeks, mudflats and mangroves on the Arabian Sea coast. Dams and canals upstream now take most of its water, so the sea pushes salt far inland along the channels. Even so, its mangrove forests are among the largest in any arid climate, sheltering fish, shrimp and migrating birds.","w":"A wide web of grey-brown tidal creeks and dark mangrove fringes on the coast south-east of Karachi.","f":["The river's ancient name, Sindhu, is the root of the words India and Hindu.","The Indus rises in Tibet and flows more than 3,000 km to reach this coast."],"q":{"q":"Which big city lies just west of the Indus Delta?","a":"Karachi","x":["Lahore","Mumbai"]}},"Q1163759":{"t":"The world's largest delta, woven from three rivers","s":"Three great rivers, the Ganges, the Brahmaputra and the Meghna, braid together here into the largest delta on Earth. It is a green world of rice paddies and ponds, cut by wide, silty rivers whose sandbars shift with every monsoon. More than a hundred million people live on this low, fertile land, shared by Bangladesh and India, and its southern edge dissolves into the mangroves of the Sundarbans.","w":"A vast flat green plain crossed by wide brown rivers and shifting sandbanks, fraying into dark mangrove islands along the Bay of Bengal.","f":["The delta covers about 100,000 km², more than any other on Earth.","Its rivers carry around a billion tonnes of sediment to the sea each year.","Kolkata stands on the Hooghly, the delta's westernmost major branch."],"q":{"q":"Which river joins the Ganges to form this delta?","a":"The Brahmaputra","x":["The Indus","The Godavari"]}},"Q182561":{"t":"A long, straight sandy shore on the Bay of Bengal","s":"The Coromandel Coast runs almost ruler-straight along the Bay of Bengal, a pale line of beach backed by lagoons, rice fields and palm groves. Its name probably comes from Chola Mandalam, 'realm of the Cholas', the dynasty that once ruled here. European traders followed: Chennai grew from an English fort, Puducherry was French and Tranquebar Danish.","w":"A long, smooth, sandy coastline running north–south, with Chennai's sprawl and the large Pulicat lagoon just north of it.","f":["Unlike most of India, this coast gets much of its rain from the north-east monsoon, between October and December.","Pulicat Lake, north of Chennai, is India's second-largest brackish lagoon.","The Shore Temple at Mahabalipuram, south of Chennai, has faced the sea since the 8th century."],"q":{"q":"Which dynasty probably gave the Coromandel Coast its name?","a":"The Cholas","x":["The Mughals","The Guptas"]}},"Q34709":{"t":"A high salt desert striped by wind-carved ridges","s":"Ringed by the Kunlun, Qilian and Altyn-Tagh ranges, the Qaidam is a closed basin about 3 km above sea level, with no outlet to the ocean. Its floor is a pale desert of salt flats and briny lakes, some patched blue and green by evaporation ponds. In the north-west, wind has carved the ground into thousands of parallel ridges called yardangs, a pattern that looks like the surface of Mars.","w":"A vast, pale basin between snowy ranges, with white salt flats, coloured evaporation ponds and streaky wind-carved ridges.","f":["Qarhan Salt Lake, in the basin, is China's largest source of potash.","The Qinghai–Tibet Railway crosses the basin at Golmud before climbing towards Lhasa.","Scientists study its yardangs and salt flats as a stand-in for Mars."],"q":{"q":"What are the Qaidam's wind-carved ridges called?","a":"Yardangs","x":["Drumlins","Eskers"]}},"Q2155625":{"t":"Flat irrigated plains between two rivers","s":"The Riverina is flat, very flat: towards the west, the Hay Plain is often called one of the flattest places on Earth. Rivers give it life, the Murray along the south and the Murrumbidgee through the middle, and irrigation turns parts of the dry plain into a geometric grid of rice paddies, orchards and vineyards. Elsewhere, sheep and wheat stretch to a bare horizon.","w":"Wide flat plains, geometric green irrigated fields along winding rivers lined with red gums, dry brown land beyond.","f":["Australia's rice industry is centred on the Riverina's irrigation areas around Leeton and Griffith.","Wagga Wagga, on the Murrumbidgee, is the region's largest city.","The name comes from 'riverine', meaning land beside rivers."],"q":{"q":"Which crop, surprising in dry Australia, grows here in paddies?","a":"Rice","x":["Tea","Sugarcane"]}},"Q1052359":{"t":"The Silk Road's green corridor between snow and sand","s":"Snowy peaks of the Qilian Shan on one side, stony desert on the other, and between them a long, narrow belt of green oases: this is the Hexi Corridor, the natural route of the Silk Road from China towards Central Asia. Meltwater rivers from the mountains feed a chain of oasis towns such as Wuwei, Zhangye and Jiuquan. At Jiayuguan the Ming Great Wall reached its western end.","w":"A long green strip of fields between a snow-capped range to the south-west and pale desert to the north-east.","f":["Hexi means 'west of the river': the corridor lies west of the Yellow River.","The corridor stretches for roughly 1,000 km.","Near Zhangye, the Danxia landforms are banded in red, orange and yellow."],"q":{"q":"Which fortress marks the western end of the Ming Great Wall?","a":"Jiayuguan","x":["Shanhaiguan","Badaling"]}},"Q673287":{"t":"Where the Yangtze squeezes between limestone walls","s":"For about 200 km the Yangtze cuts through the Wu Mountains in three narrow gorges, Qutang, Wu and Xiling, with limestone walls rising hundreds of metres above the water. From the air the river becomes a thin, winding band of grey-green between folded, forested ridges. Near the eastern end, the Three Gorges Dam has turned the river upstream into a reservoir some 600 km long.","w":"A narrow, twisting river threading between steep, forested ridges, between Chongqing and Yichang.","f":["Kuimen, the gateway of Qutang Gorge, appears on the back of the 10-yuan banknote.","The Three Gorges Dam is the world's largest hydroelectric power station by installed capacity."],"q":{"q":"Which Chinese banknote shows Kuimen, the gateway of Qutang Gorge?","a":"The 10-yuan note","x":["The 100-yuan note","The 1-yuan note"]}},"Q19742":{"t":"The deepest canyon on Earth, bent around a giant peak","s":"Here the Yarlung Tsangpo, Tibet's great river, makes a hairpin turn around Namcha Barwa, a 7,782 m peak, and plunges into the deepest canyon on Earth. Between that summit and Gyala Peri across the gorge, the canyon is more than 5 km deep. Below the bend the river turns south towards the plains of Assam, where it becomes the Brahmaputra.","w":"A sharp hairpin of river gorge wrapped around a huge, glaciated peak, Namcha Barwa, at the eastern end of the Himalaya.","f":["The canyon is about 500 km long, longer than the Grand Canyon in Arizona.","Namcha Barwa, guarding the bend, was first climbed only in 1992.","A waterfall hidden deep in the gorge was reached by explorers only in the late 1990s."],"q":{"q":"What does the Yarlung Tsangpo become downstream?","a":"The Brahmaputra","x":["The Ganges","The Mekong"]}},"Q1031793":{"t":"Chile's long garden between two mountain walls","s":"Squeezed between the high Andes to the east and the lower Coastal Range to the west, the Central Valley runs for hundreds of kilometres down the middle of Chile. It is the country's farm and orchard, with vineyards in the north giving way to wheat, pastures and pine plantations farther south. Around Temuco, snow-capped volcanoes such as Llaima and Villarrica stand guard along its eastern edge.","w":"A long, flat, farmed lowland running north–south between the white Andes and a lower green coastal range, with volcanic cones to the east.","f":["Most Chileans live in the Central Valley, which also holds Santiago.","Its northern half, with a Mediterranean-like climate, is one of South America's great wine regions.","Villarrica, rising near the valley's edge south-east of Temuco, is one of Chile's most active volcanoes."],"q":{"q":"Which range borders Chile's Central Valley on the west?","a":"The Coastal Range","x":["The Andes","The Sierra Nevada"]}},"Q27026":{"t":"America's great orchard, flat as a table","s":"Between the Sierra Nevada and the Coast Ranges lies a flat valley some 700 km long, one of the most productive farming regions on Earth. It is an endless grid of orchards, vineyards and fields, crossed by canals carrying water south. Near its southern end lies the bed of Tulare Lake, once the largest freshwater lake west of the Mississippi, now farmland that floods only in very wet years.","w":"A vast flat valley chequered with green and brown fields, the snowy Sierra Nevada to the east and lower Coast Ranges to the west.","f":["The valley grows about a quarter of the food produced in the United States.","Pumping groundwater has made parts of the valley floor sink by several metres.","In winter a dense, low 'tule fog' can fill the valley for days."],"q":{"q":"Which lake once filled part of the southern Central Valley?","a":"Tulare Lake","x":["Lake Tahoe","Mono Lake"]}},"Q140948":{"t":"Ice-capped islands and a volcano you can sail into","s":"Glacier-covered and ringed by dark cliffs, the South Shetlands form a long chain off the tip of the Antarctic Peninsula. Deception Island is the strangest: a ring-shaped volcano whose flooded crater ships enter through a narrow gap called Neptune's Bellows. King George Island hosts more research stations than almost anywhere else in Antarctica, run by many different countries.","w":"A chain of white, glacier-capped islands north of the Antarctic Peninsula; Deception Island's ring shape lies on the south side of the chain.","f":["William Smith sighted the islands in 1819 after being blown south while rounding Cape Horn.","Visitors sometimes bathe in volcanically warmed water on Deception Island's beaches."],"q":{"q":"How do ships enter Deception Island's flooded crater?","a":"Through Neptune's Bellows","x":["Through the Lemaire Channel","Through Drake Passage"]}},"Q192188":{"t":"Remote atolls around the largest atoll structure on Earth","s":"In the middle of the Indian Ocean, some 60 low coral islands are scattered across the Chagos atolls. The Great Chagos Bank is the largest atoll structure in the world, a vast, mostly submerged ring with only a few specks of land on its rim. The reefs here are among the most pristine on Earth, and much of the surrounding ocean has been protected as a marine reserve.","w":"Tiny low islands and pale reef rings with turquoise lagoons, far out in deep blue ocean south of the Maldives.","f":["The Great Chagos Bank covers about 18,000 km², yet its islands add up to only a few square kilometres.","The Chagos lie at the southern end of a line of atolls that also includes the Maldives and the Laccadives."],"q":{"q":"Which chain of atolls lies north of the Chagos?","a":"The Maldives","x":["The Seychelles","The Comoros"]}},"Q118223":{"t":"A lonely arc of smoking volcanoes in the Southern Ocean","s":"Far out in the stormy Southern Ocean, a curved chain of volcanic islands rises from the sea, most of them capped with ice and several still active. James Cook sighted them in 1775 and named them after the Earl of Sandwich. Nobody lives here, but Zavodovski Island hosts one of the largest penguin colonies on Earth, with chinstrap penguins in the hundreds of thousands.","w":"A north–south arc of small, white, cone-shaped islands in open ocean, some trailing plumes of steam.","f":["East of the islands, the South Sandwich Trench plunges more than 8 km.","Mount Belinda, the volcano on Montagu Island, erupted through its ice cap in the early 2000s."],"q":{"q":"Who named the South Sandwich Islands in 1775?","a":"James Cook","x":["Ernest Shackleton","James Clark Ross"]}},"Q1266371":{"t":"A 'hundred thousand islands' that add up to 32 km²","s":"The name Lakshadweep means 'a hundred thousand islands', yet there are only 36, and together they cover about 32 km². They are delicate rings of turquoise lagoon and white sand, each with a thin green strip of coconut palms along its eastern edge. The islands lie 200–400 km off the coast of Kerala, and only ten of them are inhabited.","w":"Scattered turquoise lagoons with thin green islets, far out in the Arabian Sea west of India's south-western coast.","f":["Minicoy, the southernmost island, speaks a language close to that of the Maldives.","Coconuts and tuna fishing are the mainstays of island life."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Lakshadweep mean?","a":"A hundred thousand islands","x":["Islands of coconuts","Pearl islands"]}},"Q25389":{"t":"A low green peninsula where two seas collide","s":"Jutland is the only part of Denmark joined to mainland Europe: a low peninsula of fields, heath and dunes pointing north towards Norway. At its tip, at Skagen, the Skagerrak and the Kattegat meet in a line of clashing waves. The long Limfjord cuts right across the north from sea to sea, and on the south-west coast the Wadden Sea's tidal flats appear and vanish twice a day.","w":"A long, low peninsula of green and brown fields between the North Sea and the Kattegat, crossed in the north by the winding Limfjord.","f":["Råbjerg Mile, near Skagen, is a migrating sand dune that moves up to about 15 m a year.","Lego was born in Billund, in the middle of Jutland.","The peninsula continues south into Germany as Schleswig-Holstein."],"q":{"q":"Which two seas meet at Skagen, Jutland's northern tip?","a":"Skagerrak and Kattegat","x":["Baltic and Barents","North Sea and Irish Sea"]}},"Q171617":{"t":"Coffee money, twin motorways and an opera composer","s":"Two motorways run side by side out of São Paulo and end in a big, sprawling grid on the rolling plateau: that is Campinas. In the 19th century it was one of the richest coffee towns in Brazil. Today universities, research labs and a busy cargo airport have taken coffee's place, and cane fields fill the country around.","w":"A large pale sprawl on hilly farmland north-west of São Paulo, threaded by two parallel motorways; Viracopos airport's runway to the south-west.","f":["Composer Carlos Gomes was born here in 1836; his opera Il Guarany premiered at La Scala in Milan in 1870.","The Anhanguera and Bandeirantes motorways link Campinas with São Paulo, about 90 km away.","Viracopos airport, south-west of the centre, is one of Brazil's busiest air-cargo hubs."],"q":{"q":"Which composer of the opera Il Guarany was born in Campinas?","a":"Carlos Gomes","x":["Heitor Villa-Lobos","Antônio Carlos Jobim"]}},"Q5092":{"t":"The harbour that gave America its anthem","s":"Follow the Chesapeake Bay north until a river estuary splits into fingers of water: Baltimore wraps around their tips. In 1814 British ships bombarded Fort McHenry at the harbour mouth all night, and seeing the flag still flying at dawn, Francis Scott Key wrote the words of 'The Star-Spangled Banner'. Today the old harbour front is lined with museums and moored ships.","w":"A city clasping a branching estuary on the west side of the Chesapeake Bay, about 60 km north-east of Washington.","f":["The Baltimore and Ohio, chartered in 1827, was one of the first railways in the United States.","Edgar Allan Poe died in Baltimore in 1849; the city's football team, the Ravens, is named after his poem.","Baseball's Babe Ruth was born in Baltimore in 1895."],"q":{"q":"Which song was inspired by the defence of Baltimore's Fort McHenry?","a":"The Star-Spangled Banner","x":["Yankee Doodle","America the Beautiful"]}},"Q165972":{"t":"Canals like a comb, the Everglades at its back","s":"Look closely at the shore: rows of narrow finger islands jut into the water like the teeth of a comb, each lined with houses and boat docks. These canals earned Fort Lauderdale its nickname, the 'Venice of America'. A thin barrier beach faces the Atlantic, and inland the suburbs stop abruptly at the flat, grassy Everglades.","w":"Comb-like canals along the coast north of Miami; the deep-water cruise port by the ocean inlet; flat green Everglades to the west.","f":["The city grew around a fort built in 1838 and named after Major William Lauderdale.","Port Everglades, at the city's south edge, is one of the busiest cruise ports in the world.","Westward, the suburbs end at long levees that fence off the Everglades wetlands."],"q":{"q":"What nickname do Fort Lauderdale's many canals earn it?","a":"Venice of America","x":["Amsterdam of the South","Little Holland"]}},"Q16739":{"t":"Oil wells disguised as tropical islands","s":"A long stone breakwater fences off the bay, and behind it container terminals spread over land reclaimed from the sea. Look offshore for four small islands: palm trees and colourful towers there hide working oil wells. Long Beach and its twin port of Los Angeles handle a large share of America's trade with Asia.","w":"Straight breakwaters enclosing San Pedro Bay at the southern end of the Los Angeles sprawl; grids of container docks behind them.","f":["The ocean liner Queen Mary has been moored here as a hotel since 1967.","The four oil islands off the beach were built in the 1960s and dressed up to look like resort islands.","Together the ports of Long Beach and Los Angeles form the busiest container port complex in the US."],"q":{"q":"Which famous ocean liner is permanently moored in Long Beach?","a":"Queen Mary","x":["Queen Elizabeth 2","SS United States"]}},"Q62823":{"t":"Where Colombia's great river finally meets the sea","s":"The Magdalena has flowed some 1,500 km from the Andes by the time it reaches Barranquilla, and here its brown water spills into the blue Caribbean. The city lines the west bank just before the mouth. Every year before Lent it throws one of the largest carnivals in the world, a feast of costumes, masks and cumbia.","w":"A wide brown river entering the Caribbean between long jetties; the city on its west bank; a large lagoon beyond the river to the east.","f":["The Carnival of Barranquilla was proclaimed a UNESCO masterpiece of intangible heritage in 2003.","SCADTA, the airline that later became Avianca, was founded here in 1919.","The river mouth is called Bocas de Ceniza, 'Mouths of Ash', for its grey, silty water."],"q":{"q":"Which river meets the Caribbean at Barranquilla?","a":"The Magdalena","x":["The Atrato","The Orinoco"]}},"Q486168":{"t":"A city pressed against a mountain wall and a fault","s":"The sprawl of Los Angeles runs east until it slams into a steep wall of mountains: that edge is San Bernardino. Along the foot of the slope runs the San Andreas Fault. North-west, a deep notch — the Cajon Pass — carries the railway and the freeway to the desert. And in 1940 two brothers opened a drive-in here called McDonald's.","w":"A grid of suburbs ending sharply at dark, steep mountains; a pass cutting north-west through them; in winter, snow on the peaks to the east.","f":["Richard and Maurice McDonald opened their first restaurant in San Bernardino in 1940.","San Gorgonio Mountain, about 3,500 m, is the highest peak in Southern California.","Old Route 66 entered the Los Angeles basin through the Cajon Pass and San Bernardino."],"q":{"q":"Which fast-food chain began in San Bernardino in 1940?","a":"McDonald's","x":["Burger King","KFC"]}},"Q81882":{"t":"Latin America's busiest port, under a wall of forest","s":"A winding channel lined with docks, a long curve of beach, and behind it all a green escarpment rising almost straight up: that is Santos. São Paulo sits on the plateau above, and much of what Brazil sells to the world leaves through this port. Coffee made the city rich; football made it famous.","w":"A flat island city at the foot of the steep, forested Serra do Mar, south-east of São Paulo; docks along a winding channel.","f":["Pelé played for Santos FC from 1956 to 1974.","Santos shares its island with São Vicente, founded in 1532 as the first Portuguese town in Brazil.","The old Coffee Exchange, where Brazil's crop was once priced, is now a coffee museum."],"q":{"q":"Which football legend played most of his career for Santos FC?","a":"Pelé","x":["Garrincha","Zico"]}},"Q168376":{"t":"An island capital guarding an iron-ore port","s":"Vitória is built on a hilly island in a bay, tied to the mainland by bridges, with granite hills poking up between the neighbourhoods. The state capital of Espírito Santo, it was founded by the Portuguese in the 16th century. On the island's northern edge, one of the world's biggest iron-ore terminals loads ships bound for Asia and Europe.","w":"A rocky island in a bay on the Atlantic coast, linked by bridges to the mainland; Vila Velha across the water to the south.","f":["It is one of three Brazilian state capitals on an island, with Florianópolis and São Luís.","The clay pots of Goiabeiras, used for local moqueca, were the first craft listed as Brazil's intangible heritage, in 2002.","Iron ore reaches the Tubarão port by rail from the mines of Minas Gerais."],"q":{"q":"Vitória is the capital of which Brazilian state?","a":"Espírito Santo","x":["Minas Gerais","Bahia"]}},"Q42763":{"t":"Tobacco capital of the fertile Cibao valley","s":"In the green heart of the Dominican Republic, between two mountain ranges, lies the broad Cibao valley, and Santiago is its capital in all but name. Tobacco from these fields rolls into some of the world's most prized cigars. The city is the country's second largest; its full name honours the thirty caballeros said to have settled it.","w":"A large city in a wide green valley between the island's central mountains and a lower northern range; a river curving past its west side.","f":["The Dominican Republic is one of the world's leading exporters of premium cigars, most of them made around Santiago.","Pico Duarte, 3,098 m and the highest peak in the Caribbean, rises in the Cordillera Central south-west of the city.","The Yaque del Norte river flows past the city on its way to the Atlantic."],"q":{"q":"Which valley is Santiago de los Caballeros the heart of?","a":"The Cibao","x":["The Constanza","The San Juan"]}},"Q209034":{"t":"Mexico's highest state capital, under a broken volcano","s":"Toluca sits at about 2,660 m, higher than any other state capital in Mexico, on a broad valley west of Mexico City. South-west of the city stands the Nevado de Toluca, an old volcano whose crater has been torn open on one side. Inside it lie two lakes, and in winter the rim often turns white.","w":"A city on a flat, high valley; to the south-west a large volcano with a broken, often snowy crater rim.","f":["The Nevado de Toluca rises to about 4,680 m, one of the highest peaks in Mexico.","The two crater lakes are called the Lake of the Sun and the Lake of the Moon.","The Lerma River, which flows on to Lake Chapala, rises from springs in the Toluca valley."],"q":{"q":"What are the two lakes inside the Nevado de Toluca crater called?","a":"The Sun and the Moon","x":["Day and Night","Gold and Silver"]}},"Q17042":{"t":"The working shore of San Francisco Bay","s":"Across the bay from San Francisco, the giant cranes of the Port of Oakland line the waterfront, loading ships for the Pacific. Behind them the city climbs to wooded hills. In its middle is a round lake: Lake Merritt, a tidal lagoon that became America's first official wildlife refuge in 1870. The Bay Bridge ties Oakland to the city opposite.","w":"The east shore of San Francisco Bay: container cranes along the estuary, a small lake in the centre, the Bay Bridge heading west.","f":["Lake Merritt was declared a wildlife refuge in 1870, the first such official refuge in the United States.","Writer Jack London spent much of his youth on the Oakland waterfront.","The Bay Bridge reaches San Francisco by way of Yerba Buena Island, in mid-bay."],"q":{"q":"Which Oakland lagoon became America's first official wildlife refuge?","a":"Lake Merritt","x":["Lake Temescal","Lake Chabot"]}},"Q178725":{"t":"The city with the best view of Rio","s":"Guanabara Bay has two shores. Rio de Janeiro gets the fame; Niterói, on the eastern side, gets the view of Sugarloaf and Corcovado across the water. A bridge some 13 km long crosses the bay between them. Niterói traces its founding to 1573 and the Temiminó chief Araribóia, and its ocean beaches face the open Atlantic.","w":"The eastern shore of Guanabara Bay, opposite Rio; a long bridge crossing the bay; green granite hills behind the beaches.","f":["The Rio–Niterói Bridge is about 13 km long, one of the longest in the Southern Hemisphere.","The name Niterói comes from Tupi and is usually translated as 'hidden water'.","Oscar Niemeyer's Contemporary Art Museum, shaped like a flying saucer, perches on a cliff above the bay."],"q":{"q":"Which bay separates Niterói from Rio de Janeiro?","a":"Guanabara Bay","x":["Sepetiba Bay","Todos os Santos Bay"]}},"Q49259":{"t":"Watch the road dive under the bay","s":"At the mouth of the Chesapeake Bay, look for a thin line crossing open water that suddenly vanishes: the Chesapeake Bay Bridge-Tunnel dives underground at two shipping channels. Virginia Beach lies at its southern end, a resort strip facing the Atlantic. Just here, at Cape Henry, the English colonists of 1607 first stepped ashore.","w":"Where the Chesapeake Bay meets the Atlantic: a long causeway crossing the bay mouth, a straight ocean beach running south.","f":["The Chesapeake Bay Bridge-Tunnel is about 28 km long from shore to shore.","The Jamestown colonists landed at Cape Henry in April 1607 before sailing up the James River.","Virginia Beach is the most populous city in Virginia."],"q":{"q":"Where did the Jamestown colonists first land in 1607?","a":"Cape Henry","x":["Cape Hatteras","Cape Cod"]}},"Q189128":{"t":"Mexico's shoe capital on the high Bajío plain","s":"If you own Mexican leather boots, there's a good chance they came from here. León is a big industrial city on the high Bajío plain, surrounded by a patchwork of farmland, and it has made shoes, belts and saddles for generations. Founded in 1576, it now honours two independence heroes, the Aldama brothers, in its name.","w":"A large city on a flat, farmed plain, with low mountains rising to its north; Guanajuato's hills to the south-east.","f":["León is the centre of Mexico's footwear and leather industry.","The city was founded in 1576 as the Villa de León.","Juan and Ignacio Aldama fought alongside Miguel Hidalgo in the independence war that began in 1810."],"q":{"q":"What is León, Mexico, best known for making?","a":"Shoes and leather goods","x":["Silver jewellery","Tequila"]}},"Q41819":{"t":"One name, two states, two great rivers","s":"Where the Kansas River flows into the Missouri, two cities share one name: Kansas City, Missouri, and Kansas City, Kansas, with the state line running between them. The larger one is on the Missouri side. The city grew as a jumping-off point for the Santa Fe and Oregon trails, and later swung to its own style of jazz.","w":"Two rivers meeting at a sharp bend of the Missouri, with a downtown cluster on the bluffs just south-east of the confluence.","f":["It calls itself the City of Fountains and has some 200 of them.","Count Basie's band and saxophonist Charlie Parker came out of Kansas City's 1930s jazz scene.","Covered wagons bound for the Santa Fe and Oregon trails outfitted in the towns here in the 1840s."],"q":{"q":"Which two rivers meet at Kansas City?","a":"The Missouri and the Kansas","x":["The Missouri and the Platte","The Arkansas and the Kansas"]}},"Q16558":{"t":"Where the West begins, as the city likes to say","s":"Fort Worth began in 1849 as an army outpost on the Trinity River and grew rich as a stop for cattle drives heading north on the Chisholm Trail. Its Stockyards still keep that cowboy spirit alive. Today it merges eastward with Dallas into one huge metro area; the giant DFW airport sits between the two.","w":"A city at the forks of the Trinity River, joined to Dallas by suburbs; the huge DFW airport with parallel runways halfway between.","f":["Herds of longhorns passed through Fort Worth on the Chisholm Trail from the late 1860s.","DFW Airport, between Fort Worth and Dallas, covers more ground than Manhattan.","The Stockyards still stage a small cattle drive down the street twice a day."],"q":{"q":"What is Fort Worth's nickname?","a":"Where the West Begins","x":["Big D","The Alamo City"]}},"Q6346":{"t":"A planned capital with a racetrack you can spot","s":"Indianapolis was drawn on paper in 1821: a 'Mile Square' at the centre with four diagonal avenues cutting across the grid. It sits almost in the middle of flat, farmed Indiana, which is why it calls itself the Crossroads of America. North-west of downtown, a big oval stands out — the Speedway, home of the Indianapolis 500 since 1911.","w":"A city on flat farmland with motorways radiating like spokes; a large oval racetrack north-west of the centre.","f":["The Indianapolis 500 has been raced at the Speedway since 1911.","The Speedway's oval is about 4 km around, big enough to pick out from cruise on a clear day.","The White River runs through the city from north to south-west."],"q":{"q":"Which famous race is held in Indianapolis every May?","a":"The Indy 500","x":["The Daytona 500","The 24 Hours of Le Mans"]}},"Q49233":{"t":"A city of round lakes and theme parks","s":"Count the lakes: Orlando is freckled with dozens of round blue ponds, many of them old sinkholes where the limestone below gave way. Until the late 1960s this was a quiet citrus town in central Florida. Then Walt Disney World opened south-west of the city in 1971, and Orlando became one of the most visited places on the planet.","w":"A city scattered with many small round lakes in flat central Florida; big theme-park complexes and resorts to the south-west.","f":["Walt Disney World opened in 1971; it covers an area about the size of San Francisco.","Many of the region's round lakes formed as sinkholes in the limestone bedrock.","Kennedy Space Center lies about 80 km east, on the Atlantic coast."],"q":{"q":"What formed many of Orlando's round lakes?","a":"Sinkholes in limestone","x":["Meteorite impacts","Retreating glaciers"]}},"Q26590":{"t":"Half of a desert metropolis split by a river","s":"In the Chihuahuan Desert, a narrow river divides one large city between two countries: Ciudad Juárez in Mexico on the south bank, El Paso in Texas on the north. For centuries this was the 'pass of the north', a gap in the mountains on the royal road from Mexico City to Santa Fe. Factories making goods for export now ring the city.","w":"A large grid on the desert on the south bank of the Rio Grande, facing El Paso and the dark Franklin Mountains across the river.","f":["Until 1888 the city was called El Paso del Norte; it was renamed after President Benito Juárez.","The mission of Our Lady of Guadalupe, founded in 1659, is the city's oldest building.","Together with El Paso it forms one of the largest cross-border urban areas in the world."],"q":{"q":"What was Ciudad Juárez called before 1888?","a":"El Paso del Norte","x":["Santa Fe del Norte","San Elizario"]}},"Q18383":{"t":"A refuge for free thinkers at the head of a bay","s":"Narragansett Bay narrows northward to a point, and there sits Providence, the capital of tiny Rhode Island. Roger Williams founded it in 1636 after being expelled from Massachusetts for his beliefs, and he promised freedom of conscience to all. Later it grew rich on trade and jewellery making. Two small rivers meet in its compact downtown.","w":"The northern tip of a long bay with islands; a compact city at its head, and Boston about 80 km to the north-east.","f":["Roger Williams named the settlement for God's 'merciful providence' in finding him a refuge.","Brown University, founded in 1764, is one of the oldest in the United States.","On summer evenings, WaterFire sets dozens of bonfires burning on the rivers downtown."],"q":{"q":"Who founded Providence in 1636?","a":"Roger Williams","x":["William Penn","John Winthrop"]}},"Q16567":{"t":"A capital planned on a riverbank, ringed by a beltway","s":"Look for a ring road drawn around a city on flat farmland, with two rivers meeting right at its heart: that is Columbus, the capital of Ohio. It was laid out in 1812 as a brand-new capital on the Scioto. Today it is the state's largest city, and its mix of residents mirrors America so closely that companies test new products here first.","w":"A compact downtown where two rivers meet, inside a round beltway, amid flat, square-cut Ohio farmland.","f":["Columbus was chosen as Ohio's capital in 1812, when the site was little more than forest.","Fast-food chains and retailers often trial new products in Columbus because its population resembles the US average.","Ohio State University, on the Olentangy River, is one of the largest universities in the country."],"q":{"q":"Which two rivers meet in downtown Columbus, Ohio?","a":"The Scioto and the Olentangy","x":["The Muskingum and the Scioto","The Maumee and the Olentangy"]}},"Q163749":{"t":"The mainland town built to serve an island resort","s":"A narrow lagoon runs along Florida's coast here, and a skinny barrier island lies beyond it. The island is Palm Beach, where Henry Flagler built grand hotels in the 1890s; West Palm Beach, on the mainland, was laid out for the people who worked in them. Now the working town is the bigger city, and inland lies the vast Lake Okeechobee.","w":"A long lagoon between the mainland and a thin barrier island; far to the west, the huge round Lake Okeechobee.","f":["Henry Flagler founded West Palm Beach in 1894 as a town for workers at his island resorts.","Lake Okeechobee, one of the largest freshwater lakes in the US, lies about 60 km west.","The Lake Worth Lagoon separates the city from the island of Palm Beach."],"q":{"q":"Which railway tycoon founded West Palm Beach?","a":"Henry Flagler","x":["Cornelius Vanderbilt","Henry Plant"]}},"ne-pp-1159148899":{"t":"A quilombo village on a river in Brazil's far north","s":"This Vila Velha is not the big city in Espírito Santo but a small village of the same name, far to the north on the Cassiporé River in the state of Amapá. In 2016 it was recognised as a quilombo, a community descended from people who escaped slavery. Around it, forest gives way to wet grasslands and mangroves on the way to the Atlantic.","w":"A river winding through green forest and pale flooded grassland near the Atlantic coast, north of the Amazon's mouth.","f":["The village belongs to the municipality of Oiapoque, which borders French Guiana.","At about 3° north, it lies only some 350 km from the equator.","The Cassiporé River runs from the forested interior of Amapá to the Atlantic."],"q":{"q":"In which Brazilian state is Vila Velha do Cassiporé?","a":"Amapá","x":["Pará","Roraima"]}},"Q334197":{"t":"The old town facing the island capital","s":"The name means 'Old Town', and it's earned: founded in 1535, this is the oldest settlement in Espírito Santo. The colony's seat soon moved across the bay to the island of Vitória, but Vila Velha kept growing. Today it is the bigger of the two, a line of beaches and high-rises facing the ocean, with a convent crowning a granite hill.","w":"Long ocean beaches south of the bay mouth, facing the island city of Vitória; a steep granite hill near the water.","f":["The Penha Convent, begun in the 16th century, sits on a granite hill above the bay.","The Garoto chocolate factory, one of Brazil's best-known brands, is in Vila Velha.","A tall bridge completed in 1989, the Terceira Ponte, links the city with Vitória."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Vila Velha mean?","a":"Old Town","x":["New Harbour","Holy Hill"]}},"Q52535":{"t":"The flag's birthplace on a mighty brown river","s":"The Paraná here is a broad brown river, and beyond its main channel a maze of green islands and wetlands stretches for many kilometres. Rosario spreads along the western bank, and from its river ports much of Argentina's grain heads to sea. In 1812 General Manuel Belgrano first raised the country's blue-and-white flag on this riverbank. Lionel Messi grew up here.","w":"A large city on the west bank of a wide brown river; opposite, a maze of green islands crossed by a long straight causeway.","f":["Manuel Belgrano first raised the Argentine flag on the bank of the Paraná here on 27 February 1812.","A bridge and a causeway about 60 km long cross the delta islands to the town of Victoria.","The river ports around Rosario ship a large part of Argentina's soybean harvest."],"q":{"q":"Which football star was born in Rosario?","a":"Lionel Messi","x":["Diego Maradona","Gabriel Batistuta"]}},"Q168888":{"t":"Turquoise reefs on one side, a lagoon on the other","s":"Maceió is squeezed between two waters: a big brown lagoon inland and a turquoise sea edged by reefs. At low tide, sail rafts called jangadas carry swimmers out to natural pools on the reef. Rows of coconut palms line the shore. The whole state, Alagoas, is named after its lagoons, and Maceió has been its capital since 1839.","w":"A coastal city between a large lagoon and the Atlantic; pale reef lines and turquoise water just offshore.","f":["The state's name, Alagoas, means 'lagoons' in Portuguese.","Maceió took over as the capital of Alagoas in 1839.","The natural pools off Pajuçara beach are reached by jangada only at low tide."],"q":{"q":"What does the name of Maceió's state, Alagoas, mean?","a":"Lagoons","x":["Mangroves","Coconut groves"]}},"Q16559":{"t":"Hill Country meets prairie, and a million bats","s":"Austin sits right on a geological seam: to the west the wooded Hill Country, to the east flat prairie farmland. The Colorado River has been dammed into a chain of narrow lakes that wind through the hills. The capital of Texas calls itself the live-music capital of the world, and on summer evenings a cloud of bats spirals out from under a downtown bridge.","w":"A chain of narrow, winding lakes in wooded hills west of the city; flat farmland to the east.","f":["Up to 1.5 million Mexican free-tailed bats roost under the Congress Avenue Bridge in summer.","The Balcones Escarpment, the edge between hills and plains, runs through the city.","The Texas Capitol in Austin stands a few metres taller than the US Capitol in Washington."],"q":{"q":"What colony lives under Austin's Congress Avenue Bridge?","a":"Free-tailed bats","x":["Brown pelicans","Monarch butterflies"]}},"Q386991":{"t":"A green oasis on a riverbed that is usually dry","s":"In the middle of the northern Mexican desert, a large green patch of irrigated fields appears: La Laguna. Torreón is its biggest city, and across a mostly dry riverbed lie its twins, Gómez Palacio and Lerdo, in another state. It is a young city: a ranch with a watchtower became a railway junction and was declared a city only in 1907.","w":"A large city on pale desert at the foot of bare, rocky ridges, beside a green irrigated oasis and a wide, dry riverbed.","f":["The name comes from the torreón, or watchtower, that stood on the original ranch.","Across the Nazas River, Gómez Palacio and Lerdo belong to the state of Durango, not Coahuila.","La Laguna's irrigated desert is one of Mexico's main dairy regions."],"q":{"q":"What gave Torreón its name?","a":"A watchtower","x":["A bullring","A silver mine"]}},"Q79867":{"t":"The Magic City, built on iron, coal and limestone","s":"Long wooded ridges run in parallel lines here, the southern end of the Appalachians, and Birmingham fills the valley between them. It was founded only in 1871, yet grew so fast it was nicknamed the Magic City. The reason lay in the ground: iron ore, coal and limestone, everything needed to make iron, were all within easy reach.","w":"A city in a long valley between parallel wooded ridges running south-west to north-east.","f":["Red Mountain, the ridge south of downtown, is named for the iron ore once mined in it.","A statue of Vulcan on Red Mountain is the largest cast-iron statue in the world.","Birmingham is the most populous metro area in Alabama."],"q":{"q":"What is Birmingham, Alabama, nicknamed?","a":"The Magic City","x":["The Queen City","The Gate City"]}},"Q4709":{"t":"A city of music and a sea of pilgrims","s":"On a dry plateau above the green valley of the Turbio River, Barquisimeto spreads out in long, straight avenues. Around it, the hills are scrubby and full of cacti. Venezuelans call it their musical capital. Every 14 January the city fills with an enormous crowd following the image of the Divina Pastora, one of the largest religious processions in the Americas.","w":"A large, regular grid on a plateau above a green river valley, surrounded by dry, brownish hills.","f":["The Divina Pastora procession carries the image from the town of Santa Rosa to the cathedral.","The city was founded in the 1550s by Spanish settlers and moved more than once before settling here.","Lara state is the home of the tamunangue, a cycle of folk dances in honour of St Anthony."],"q":{"q":"What is Barquisimeto often called?","a":"Venezuela's musical capital","x":["Venezuela's oil capital","Venezuela's garden city"]}},"Q210307":{"t":"A giant grid on a vanished lake","s":"Right beside Mexico City's airport runways lies an astonishingly regular grid of streets, packed tight with houses. That is Ciudad Nezahualcóyotl, built on the dried-out bed of Lake Texcoco. As the lake shrank in the 20th century, settlers flocked to the new land, and within a few decades it had over a million people. Its name honours a poet-king.","w":"A dense, rectangular street grid immediately east of Mexico City airport; the flat, pale remains of Lake Texcoco to the north-east.","f":["The city is named after Nezahualcóyotl, the 15th-century poet-king of Texcoco.","Every street stands on land that was the bed of Lake Texcoco.","It became a separate municipality only in 1963."],"q":{"q":"Who was Nezahualcóyotl, the city's namesake?","a":"A poet-king of Texcoco","x":["A Spanish viceroy","A revolutionary general"]}},"Q131620":{"t":"Born on Christmas Day, surrounded by dunes","s":"Near the north-eastern corner of Brazil, where the coast turns, white dunes pile up beside the sea and a river snakes into the Atlantic. Natal sits between them. The name means 'Christmas': the city was founded on 25 December 1599. At the river mouth, a star-shaped Portuguese fort still stands on the reef.","w":"A coastal city between a winding river mouth and bright white dunes, near the corner where Brazil's coast turns west.","f":["The Forte dos Reis Magos, begun in 1598, guards the mouth of the Potengi River.","Just south of the city, a single cashew tree at Pirangi spreads over some 8,500 m².","Dune buggies race over the Genipabu dunes, just north of the city."],"q":{"q":"What does the city name Natal mean?","a":"Christmas","x":["Easter","New Year"]}},"Q49261":{"t":"Desert fields watered by thousand-year-old canals","s":"East of Phoenix, the Valley of the Sun becomes one immense grid of streets, and Mesa is its eastern anchor. Mormon settlers founded it in 1878 and reopened canals first dug by the Hohokam people many centuries before. Beyond the last suburbs, the jagged Superstition Mountains rise out of the desert.","w":"An endless street grid in the desert east of Phoenix; the Salt River's pale bed to the north, jagged mountains to the east.","f":["The Hohokam dug hundreds of kilometres of irrigation canals here long before Europeans arrived.","Mesa is the third-largest city in Arizona, after Phoenix and Tucson.","The Superstition Mountains are said to hide the legendary Lost Dutchman's gold mine."],"q":{"q":"Which ancient people dug the canals that Mesa's settlers reused?","a":"The Hohokam","x":["The Ancestral Puebloans","The Olmecs"]}},"Q28441":{"t":"The island capital the French founded","s":"On Brazil's north coast a wide, muddy bay holds a large island, and on its north-western corner sits São Luís. The French founded it in 1612, the only Brazilian state capital with that origin, though the Portuguese took it within a few years. The tides here are among the biggest in Brazil, laying bare broad mudflats twice a day.","w":"A large island in a broad, silty bay on the northern coast; wide tidal flats and river mouths around it.","f":["The old town, with its tiled colonial facades, has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1997.","Tides in São Marcos Bay can rise and fall by about 7 m.","The city is named after King Louis XIII of France."],"q":{"q":"Which European nation founded São Luís in 1612?","a":"France","x":["The Netherlands","Spain"]}},"Q49174":{"t":"The showman's city on Long Island Sound","s":"Connecticut's biggest city faces Long Island across a broad sound, with ferries crossing the water between them. In the 19th century Bridgeport was a factory town of sewing machines, corsets and brass, and home to the showman P. T. Barnum, who wintered his circus here and even served as mayor. Its harbour sits at the mouth of a small river.","w":"A city on the north shore of Long Island Sound, about 90 km north-east of Manhattan; Long Island visible across the water.","f":["P. T. Barnum was elected mayor of Bridgeport in 1875.","General Tom Thumb, Barnum's famous small performer, was born here in 1838.","A car ferry runs from Bridgeport across the Sound to Port Jefferson on Long Island."],"q":{"q":"Which famous showman was once mayor of Bridgeport?","a":"P. T. Barnum","x":["Buffalo Bill Cody","Harry Houdini"]}},"Q243766":{"t":"A city of parks perched on a plateau","s":"Bucaramanga sits on a flat-topped shelf in the eastern Andes, and its western edge breaks off in steep, eroded slopes. It is known as the City of Parks for its many green squares. To the south-west, the Chicamocha River has carved a spectacular canyon. Locals snack on roasted big-bottomed ants, a Santander speciality.","w":"A city on a flat plateau whose edge drops in eroded slopes; higher Andean ridges to the east, a deep canyon to the south-west.","f":["Roasted hormigas culonas — 'big-bottomed ants' — are a traditional snack of the Santander region.","A cable car in Chicamocha National Park crosses the canyon from rim to river and up the far side.","The city lies at about 1,000 m, so its climate is warm but not stifling."],"q":{"q":"What is Bucaramanga's nickname?","a":"City of Parks","x":["City of Eternal Spring","City of Bridges"]}},"Q333928":{"t":"The garden city between the mountains and a lake","s":"North of Maracay, the coastal mountains are clothed in cloud forest; to the south-west spreads Lake Valencia, which has no outlet to the sea. The city fills the green valley between them. Its tree-lined avenues earned it the name Garden City. The forest above became Henri Pittier National Park, Venezuela's first, in 1937.","w":"A city in a green valley between forested coastal mountains and a large lake to its south-west.","f":["Henri Pittier National Park was created in 1937 as Venezuela's first national park.","Lake Valencia has no natural outlet: its rivers flow in, but none flows out.","The park's Portachuelo Pass is a famous crossing point for migrating birds."],"q":{"q":"What is special about Lake Valencia near Maracay?","a":"It has no outlet to the sea","x":["It is salt water","It fills a volcanic crater"]}},"Q183124":{"t":"A valley of eternal spring, watched by a giant Christ","s":"In the Bolivian Andes, a broad green valley opens between bare brown mountains, and Cochabamba fills its floor. At about 2,500 m, the climate is mild all year, so it is called the City of Eternal Spring. On a hill at the city's edge stands a statue of Christ with open arms, a little taller than the one in Rio.","w":"A green, cultivated valley floor ringed by bare mountains; a higher, often snow-dusted range along the north side.","f":["The Cristo de la Concordia, completed in 1994, is taller than Rio de Janeiro's Christ the Redeemer.","The Tunari range north of the city rises above 5,000 m.","The valley's farms have long made Cochabamba one of Bolivia's breadbaskets."],"q":{"q":"What is Cochabamba's nickname?","a":"City of Eternal Spring","x":["The White City","City of Kings"]}},"Q204271":{"t":"Named in hope of silver riches","s":"When gold and silver were found in the hills nearby in 1592, the Spanish added 'Potosí' to this city's name, hoping it would rival the fabled silver mountain in Bolivia. It never quite did, but it prospered. San Luis Potosí sits at about 1,860 m on a dry plateau of scrub and cactus, and its colonial core is full of pink stone.","w":"A large city on a high, dry plateau; rugged mountains to the south-west, pale scrubland in every direction.","f":["Gold and silver were found at Cerro de San Pedro, just east of the city, in 1592.","Its historic centre is part of the UNESCO-listed Camino Real de Tierra Adentro.","The city is the capital of the state of the same name in north-central Mexico."],"q":{"q":"Which famous mining town inspired the name San Luis Potosí?","a":"Potosí in Bolivia","x":["Zacatecas","Guanajuato"]}},"Q16568":{"t":"The biggest city in the lower 48 — by area","s":"Jacksonville covers more land than any other city in the contiguous United States, sprawling over forests, marshes and suburbs. Through the middle runs the broad St. Johns River, which flows north for most of its length before turning east to the Atlantic. In 1564 French Huguenots built Fort Caroline near its mouth, decades before the first English colony.","w":"A wide, dark river curving north then east through the city to the sea, crossed by several bridges; forests and marsh all around.","f":["By land area, Jacksonville is the largest city in the contiguous United States.","The St. Johns River flows north for most of its roughly 500 km.","Fort Caroline, founded in 1564, was one of the first European settlements in what is now the US."],"q":{"q":"Which way does Jacksonville's St. Johns River mainly flow?","a":"North","x":["South","West"]}},"Q44989":{"t":"An oil boomtown on the sandy Arkansas River","s":"In 1905 a gusher at Glenn Pool, just south of town, changed everything: within a few years Tulsa was calling itself the Oil Capital of the World. The money built a downtown full of Art Deco towers. The city stretches along the broad, sandy Arkansas River, where green prairie meets the wooded hills of north-eastern Oklahoma.","w":"A city on a wide, sandy river with pale sandbars; wooded hills to the north-west and a large reservoir to the west.","f":["Oil from the Glenn Pool field, struck in 1905, turned Tulsa into a boomtown.","Cyrus Avery of Tulsa, called the 'Father of Route 66', helped plan the highway, which runs through the city.","The name comes from Tallasi, the old town of the Creek people who settled here in 1836."],"q":{"q":"What did Tulsa call itself during its early 1900s boom?","a":"Oil Capital of the World","x":["Cattle Capital of the World","Cotton Capital of the World"]}},"Q43421":{"t":"Whitewater rapids right below the capital's towers","s":"Few capitals have whitewater downtown, but Richmond does: here the James River tumbles over the fall line in rocky, braided rapids between wooded islands. The city grew at this spot because ships from the sea could go no farther. In 1775 Patrick Henry cried 'Give me liberty, or give me death!' in one of its churches; five years later Richmond became Virginia's capital.","w":"A river breaking into rocky rapids and islands just below the downtown towers, where the hills meet the flat coastal plain.","f":["Two US presidents, James Monroe and John Tyler, are buried in Hollywood Cemetery on the river bluffs.","Thomas Jefferson designed the Virginia State Capitol, modelled on a Roman temple in Nîmes.","The James River drops about 30 m through the city over a stretch of roughly 10 km."],"q":{"q":"Which river tumbles over rapids in downtown Richmond?","a":"The James","x":["The Potomac","The Rappahannock"]}},"Q165341":{"t":"The north-eastern capital that turned its back on the sea","s":"Every other state capital in north-east Brazil sits on the coast; Teresina alone lies inland, in the fork between two rivers, the Parnaíba and the Poti. It was planned from scratch in 1852 and named after Empress Teresa Cristina. It is one of the hottest cities in the country, and its many trees earned it the name Green City.","w":"A city in the fork of two winding rivers on a flat plain, with another town across the larger river to the west.","f":["Teresina was built in 1852 to replace the old capital, Oeiras, and to be closer to the river trade.","The Parnaíba River divides it from the neighbouring state of Maranhão.","The empress it honours was the wife of Emperor Pedro II."],"q":{"q":"Whom is Teresina named after?","a":"Empress Teresa Cristina","x":["Saint Teresa of Ávila","Princess Isabel"]}},"Q34804":{"t":"Watermelon mountains and a sky full of balloons","s":"At sunset the long ridge east of Albuquerque glows pink, which may be why the Spanish called it Sandía, 'watermelon'. The city spreads over high desert mesas at about 1,500 m, cut by a green ribbon of cottonwoods along the Rio Grande. Each October hundreds of hot-air balloons lift off together at the International Balloon Fiesta.","w":"A green river ribbon through a sprawling desert city; a steep mountain wall to the east, small dark volcanic cones to the west.","f":["The Balloon Fiesta, held every October, is the largest hot-air balloon festival in the world.","Founded in 1706, the town was named after the Duke of Alburquerque, then viceroy of New Spain.","On the West Mesa, a line of small volcanoes overlooks thousands of ancient petroglyphs."],"q":{"q":"What does 'Sandía', in the Sandia Mountains, mean in Spanish?","a":"Watermelon","x":["Sand","Saint"]}},"Q199440":{"t":"Green fields end at a line: the border and the desert","s":"Here the desert is cut into a vivid green checkerboard by water from the Colorado River, and a razor-straight line runs across it: the border between Mexico and the United States. Mexicali, capital of Baja California, lies right against it, facing the smaller Calexico. The two names are mirror images, blending 'Mexico' and 'California' in opposite order.","w":"A city on a straight border line, in a checkerboard of irrigated fields that ends abruptly at bare desert and mountains to the west.","f":["Chinese workers who came to farm cotton in the early 1900s made Chinese food a local staple.","The Cerro Prieto geothermal field south of the city is one of the largest in the world.","Summer temperatures here regularly climb above 40 °C."],"q":{"q":"Which US town faces Mexicali across the border?","a":"Calexico","x":["Nogales","Yuma"]}},"Q44255":{"t":"Where Argentina declared its independence","s":"On 9 July 1816, in a colonial house in this city, delegates declared the independence of the United Provinces of South America — today's Argentina. Tucumán lies on a green plain at the foot of a steep, forested mountain wall. Its province is the smallest in Argentina, and so fertile with sugar cane and lemons that it is called the Garden of the Republic.","w":"A large city on a green plain, with a forested mountain wall rising sharply to the west; cane fields all around.","f":["Argentina's Independence Day, 9 July, commemorates the declaration signed here in 1816.","Tucumán province is one of the world's leading producers of lemons.","Behind the city, the Sierra de San Javier and higher Aconquija ranges catch the rain that feeds its forests."],"q":{"q":"What was declared in Tucumán on 9 July 1816?","a":"Argentina's independence","x":["Argentina's first constitution","The abolition of slavery"]}},"Q18575":{"t":"A desert city guarded by giant cacti","s":"Tucson spreads across a desert basin ringed by mountains, and on both its east and west sides lie the two halves of Saguaro National Park, a forest of giant cacti. North of the city the Santa Catalina Mountains rise steeply, often with snow on top in winter. The city began as a Spanish fort in 1775.","w":"A pale street grid in a desert basin; steep mountains close to the north, others to the east and west.","f":["In 2015 Tucson became the first UNESCO City of Gastronomy in the United States.","Mount Lemmon, in the Santa Catalinas, rises to about 2,790 m.","The white Mission San Xavier del Bac, south of the city, was completed in 1797."],"q":{"q":"Which giant cactus gives Tucson's national park its name?","a":"The saguaro","x":["The organ pipe","The prickly pear"]}},"Q159273":{"t":"A white city beneath three great volcanoes","s":"Three giant volcanoes stand over Arequipa, and the nearest, El Misti, is an almost perfect cone of 5,822 m. The city lies in a green valley in the desert at about 2,300 m, and its colonial centre is built of sillar, a white volcanic stone — hence the nickname the White City. Even from cruise height, Misti's cone is unmistakable.","w":"A green valley in brown desert; right above it a symmetrical cone, a broader massif to its north-west and a jagged ridge to its south-east.","f":["The historic centre, built of white sillar, has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 2000.","Chachani, the broad massif beside Misti, rises to about 6,050 m.","The Colca Canyon, north of Arequipa, is more than twice as deep as the Grand Canyon."],"q":{"q":"What is Arequipa's old town built from?","a":"White volcanic stone","x":["Adobe bricks","Pink granite"]}},"Q211760":{"t":"Two rivers become one in a farming heartland","s":"Culiacán grew where two rivers from the Sierra Madre, the Tamazula and the Humaya, join to form the Culiacán River. Around the city, a green checkerboard of irrigated fields stretches toward the Gulf of California. Those fields send tomatoes and other vegetables north across the border all winter. Founded in 1531, it is one of the oldest Spanish towns in north-western Mexico.","w":"A city where two rivers merge, on a green coastal plain of irrigated fields; forested Sierra Madre foothills to the east.","f":["The Tamazula and Humaya rivers meet in the heart of the city.","San Miguel de Culiacán was founded in 1531 by the conquistador Nuño de Guzmán.","Culiacán is the capital of the state of Sinaloa."],"q":{"q":"Which two rivers join at Culiacán?","a":"The Tamazula and the Humaya","x":["The Fuerte and the Mayo","The Yaqui and the Sonora"]}},"Q170762":{"t":"The very centre of South America","s":"Cuiabá claims the heart of the continent: a monument in the city marks the geodesic centre of South America. The capital of Mato Grosso grew from a gold rush in 1719 on the winding Cuiabá River. To the south stretch the flat wetlands of the Pantanal; to the north-east rise the red sandstone cliffs of the Chapada dos Guimarães. It is famously, fiercely hot.","w":"A city on a flat plain, split by a winding river; red cliffs of a plateau to the north-east, low wetlands to the south.","f":["The centre of South America was calculated by Marshal Rondon's survey in 1909 and marked in Cuiabá.","Gold found in the river in 1719 brought the first settlers.","Afternoon temperatures in the dry season often pass 40 °C."],"q":{"q":"What is Cuiabá said to mark?","a":"The centre of South America","x":["The source of the Amazon","The Tropic of Capricorn"]}},"Q49218":{"t":"A waterfall downtown and the birthplace of Kodak","s":"The Genesee River flows north through Rochester to Lake Ontario, and right in the city centre it drops over a waterfall nearly 30 m high. Those falls powered the mills that first made the town. Later, George Eastman founded Kodak here, and the Kodak camera went on sale in 1888. Every May, the city's lilac festival lives up to its nickname, the Flower City.","w":"A city on the south shore of Lake Ontario, with a river running north through it into the lake.","f":["George Eastman's Kodak camera, sold loaded with film, went on sale in 1888.","The original Erie Canal crossed the Genesee River in Rochester on a stone aqueduct.","Suffragist Susan B. Anthony lived in Rochester for four decades."],"q":{"q":"Which photography company was founded in Rochester?","a":"Kodak","x":["Polaroid","Fujifilm"]}},"Q16562":{"t":"A Texas city split by a mountain range","s":"A bare, rocky mountain range runs right into the heart of El Paso, dividing the city into east and west. At its southern tip, the Rio Grande separates El Paso from Ciudad Juárez, and the two cities blend into one big desert metropolis. El Paso is so far west that, unlike almost all of Texas, it keeps Mountain Time.","w":"A dark mountain ridge pushing into a desert city from the north; a thin river and the border along its south side.","f":["El Paso keeps Mountain Time, an hour behind Dallas and Houston.","Franklin Mountains State Park, inside the city limits, is one of the largest urban parks in the US.","Spanish travellers called this gap between mountains El Paso del Norte, 'the pass of the north'."],"q":{"q":"Which time zone does El Paso use?","a":"Mountain Time","x":["Central Time","Pacific Time"]}},"Q28848":{"t":"The other Twin City, once called Pig's Eye","s":"Where the Mississippi swings through a deep valley between limestone bluffs, Saint Paul sits on the east, and Minneapolis's towers rise a few kilometres to the west. Together they are the Twin Cities. Saint Paul is Minnesota's capital, yet its first settlement bore a less dignified name: Pig's Eye, after a local tavern keeper.","w":"A city on the bluffs of a winding Mississippi, with Minneapolis's taller towers just to the west and lakes all around.","f":["The settlement was renamed after a log chapel dedicated to St Paul, built in 1841.","F. Scott Fitzgerald was born in Saint Paul in 1896.","The Saint Paul Winter Carnival has been held on and off since 1886."],"q":{"q":"What was Saint Paul's early nickname?","a":"Pig's Eye","x":["Bear's Den","Beaver Dam"]}},"Q128069":{"t":"Salt City, where the lake snow never quits","s":"Syracuse sits at the south end of little Onondaga Lake, whose salt springs gave the city its first fortune and its nickname, Salt City. The Erie Canal once ran right through downtown. In winter, cold air picking up moisture over Lake Ontario dumps so much snow here that Syracuse is regularly among the snowiest large cities in the United States.","w":"A city at the southern tip of a small lake; the long, narrow Finger Lakes to the south-west, Lake Ontario to the north-west.","f":["Salt from Onondaga Lake's springs once supplied much of the United States.","The old route of the Erie Canal through downtown is now Erie Boulevard.","The city was named after Syracuse in Sicily, which also had salt springs nearby."],"q":{"q":"Where does Syracuse, New York, get its name?","a":"From a city in Sicily","x":["From a Native American word","From an English duke"]}},"Q185582":{"t":"Where the Tennessee River begins","s":"Two rivers flowing out of the mountains, the Holston and the French Broad, meet just east of Knoxville, and from there the water is called the Tennessee River. The city fills a long Appalachian valley, with the blue wall of the Great Smoky Mountains on the south-eastern horizon. Knoxville was Tennessee's first capital, in 1796.","w":"A river city in a long valley between wooded ridges; the high, blue-grey Smoky Mountains to the south-east.","f":["The Tennessee River officially begins at Knoxville and flows about 1,050 km to the Ohio.","Great Smoky Mountains National Park, the most visited in the US, lies about 50 km to the south-east.","The city hosted the World's Fair in 1982."],"q":{"q":"Which river begins at Knoxville?","a":"The Tennessee","x":["The Cumberland","The Ohio"]}},"Q43301":{"t":"In the middle of America's great garden valley","s":"Fresno sits right in the middle of the San Joaquin Valley, a flat patchwork of orchards and vineyards that is one of the most productive farming regions on Earth. Most of America's raisins are dried in the vineyards around it. To the east the Sierra Nevada rises abruptly, snowy in winter, hiding Yosemite and the giant sequoias.","w":"A large flat grid amid a vast patchwork of orchards and vineyards; the wall of the Sierra Nevada rising to the east.","f":["Fresno is Spanish for 'ash tree', after the trees that grew along the San Joaquin River.","Yosemite, Kings Canyon and Sequoia national parks are all within a couple of hours' drive.","Nearly all of America's raisins come from the San Joaquin Valley, much of it around Fresno."],"q":{"q":"What does 'Fresno' mean in Spanish?","a":"Ash tree","x":["Fresh water","Oak tree"]}},"Q189138":{"t":"A desert capital around a lonely hill","s":"Hermosillo sprawls across the flat Sonoran Desert, with a lone rocky hill, the Cerro de la Campana, rising near its centre. Summers here are fierce, with temperatures that can pass 45 °C. The capital of Sonora is known for beef, carne asada and enormous, paper-thin flour tortillas. About 100 km west, the desert meets the Gulf of California.","w":"A city on flat brown desert with a single rocky hill near its centre; a reservoir to the east, bare ranges around.","f":["The city is named after José María González de Hermosillo, a fighter in Mexico's independence war.","Kino Bay, on the Gulf of California, lies about 100 km to the west.","Sonora is famous for its huge, thin flour tortillas, often wider than a dinner plate."],"q":{"q":"Which sea lies about 100 km west of Hermosillo?","a":"The Gulf of California","x":["The Gulf of Mexico","The Caribbean Sea"]}},"Q334577":{"t":"A highland city beside a steaming volcano","s":"Quetzaltenango, which everyone calls Xela, sits in a high valley at about 2,300 m, and its skyline is volcanoes. Just to the south rises the tall cone of Santa María, and on its flank a younger lava dome, Santiaguito, has been active since 1922 and often puffs out ash and steam. Guatemala's second city is cool, stony and full of Spanish-language students.","w":"A city in a high basin; to the south a tall volcanic cone, often with a plume of steam or ash on its flank.","f":["Xela is short for Xelajú, the city's name in the K'iche' Maya language.","The Santa María volcano, about 3,770 m, rises just south of the city.","Santiaguito, a lava dome on Santa María's side, has been growing since 1922."],"q":{"q":"What do locals call Quetzaltenango?","a":"Xela","x":["Chichi","Tikal"]}},"Q194328":{"t":"A farming hub on the red plateau of the cerrado","s":"Look for huge rectangular fields and green circles of centre-pivot irrigation stretching to the horizon: that is the Triângulo Mineiro, the western wedge of Minas Gerais, and Uberlândia is its biggest city. It grew up as a crossroads for grain, trucks and warehouses on the high cerrado plateau. Until 1929 it was called São Pedro de Uberabinha.","w":"A large city on flat farmland of vast rectangular fields and irrigation circles; reservoirs on the rivers around.","f":["The city took the name Uberlândia in 1929.","The name mixes Latin 'uber', fertile, with 'land'.","The Triângulo Mineiro lies between two big rivers, the Grande and the Paranaíba."],"q":{"q":"What was Uberlândia called before 1929?","a":"São Pedro de Uberabinha","x":["Uberaba","Nova Minas"]}},"Q188901":{"t":"From mule fair to Brazil's 'Manchester'","s":"For more than a century, drovers brought great herds of mules up from southern Brazil to Sorocaba's annual fair, and from here the animals carried goods all over the country. Later, textile mills earned the city the nickname Manchester of São Paulo. Today it is a big industrial city on rolling hills about 90 km west of São Paulo.","w":"A sizeable city on rolling green hills west of São Paulo, with a long reservoir to its south-east.","f":["Sorocaba was founded in 1654.","Its mule fair was the biggest in Brazil through much of the 18th and 19th centuries.","One of Brazil's first ironworks, the Ipanema foundry, opened nearby in the early 1800s."],"q":{"q":"What was Sorocaba nicknamed for its textile mills?","a":"Manchester of São Paulo","x":["Liverpool of Brazil","Detroit of São Paulo"]}},"Q117040":{"t":"A pocket bay behind a castle, under Cuba's highest mountains","s":"Santiago de Cuba hides behind a narrow gap in the cliffs: the bay opens up only once you are through it, and the city climbs the hills around its inner shore. A stone castle has guarded that entrance since the 17th century. Westward rise the dark ridges of the Sierra Maestra. This is the city of son, the music that grew into salsa.","w":"A pouch-shaped bay behind a narrow entrance on Cuba's south-east coast; the city on hills around it, dark mountains to the west.","f":["The Castillo de San Pedro de la Roca at the bay mouth has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1997.","Pico Turquino, Cuba's highest peak at about 1,970 m, rises in the Sierra Maestra west of the city.","Diego Velázquez founded Santiago in 1515, making it one of Cuba's oldest cities."],"q":{"q":"Which mountain range rises west of Santiago de Cuba?","a":"The Sierra Maestra","x":["The Sierra del Escambray","The Sierra de los Órganos"]}},"Q188892":{"t":"Coffee capital turned ocean of sugar cane","s":"Around Ribeirão Preto the land is a sea of sugar cane cut into huge rectangles, and where a field is bare, the soil shows a deep purplish red. That rich 'terra roxa' once made this the heart of Brazil's coffee empire. Today cane and ethanol rule, and the city is so prosperous that it's been called the Brazilian California.","w":"A large city in an endless patchwork of cane fields, with patches of dark-red bare soil, in the north of São Paulo state.","f":["Ribeirão Preto means 'black stream'.","Around 1900 its plantations made it known as the world capital of coffee.","Each year the city hosts Agrishow, one of Latin America's largest farm-machinery fairs."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Ribeirão Preto mean?","a":"Black stream","x":["Red river","Stony creek"]}},"Q738353":{"t":"The last border city before the river meets the Gulf","s":"The Rio Grande makes its final loops through flat delta land here, and Matamoros sits on the south bank, facing Brownsville in Texas. A few dozen kilometres east, the river slips into the Gulf of Mexico. Around the city stretch shallow lagoons and marsh. It takes its name from Mariano Matamoros, a priest who fought for Mexico's independence.","w":"Twin cities on the last big loops of the Rio Grande; flat delta and shallow lagoons between them and the Gulf coast.","f":["The city is named after Mariano Matamoros, a priest and hero of Mexico's independence war.","The Rio Grande reaches the Gulf of Mexico some 35 km east of the city.","The Laguna Madre along the coast is one of the few hypersaline lagoons in the world."],"q":{"q":"Which Texan city faces Matamoros across the Rio Grande?","a":"Brownsville","x":["Laredo","McAllen"]}},"Q131429":{"t":"A green-stone city watched by a flattened mountaintop","s":"Three valleys meet in the mountains of southern Mexico, and Oaxaca lies where they join, at about 1,550 m. More than 2,000 years ago the Zapotecs levelled the top of a ridge above the valley and built a city there, Monte Albán. Below, the colonial town grew in pale green stone. Today it is known for its markets, mezcal and seven kinds of mole.","w":"A city where three valley arms meet, ringed by mountains; a long ridge rising just west of the centre.","f":["The historic centre and Monte Albán have been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1987.","The Tule tree, a cypress in a village just east of the city, has one of the thickest trunks in the world.","Every July the Guelaguetza festival gathers dancers from all the state's regions."],"q":{"q":"Which people built Monte Albán above Oaxaca?","a":"The Zapotecs","x":["The Aztecs","The Maya"]}},"Q234053":{"t":"The older Vancouver, across the river from Portland","s":"Not the Canadian one: this Vancouver sits on the north bank of the broad Columbia River in Washington State, facing Portland, Oregon. It is actually the older of the two. In 1825 the Hudson's Bay Company built Fort Vancouver here as the hub of its fur trade in the Pacific Northwest. On a clear day, Mount Hood and Mount St. Helens stand on the horizon.","w":"A city on the north bank of a wide river, opposite Portland; snowy Mount Hood to the east, Mount St. Helens to the north-east.","f":["Fort Vancouver, built in 1825, was the Hudson's Bay Company's headquarters on the Pacific coast.","The Canadian city of Vancouver was incorporated only in 1886.","Both cities are named after George Vancouver, the British captain who charted this coast in the 1790s."],"q":{"q":"Which city faces Vancouver, Washington, across the Columbia River?","a":"Portland","x":["Seattle","Salem"]}},"Q36307":{"t":"Salta the Beautiful, gateway to the high Andes","s":"Argentines call it Salta la Linda, 'Salta the Beautiful', for its colonial arcades and churches. The city fills a green valley at about 1,150 m, ringed by forested hills, with the bare, high Andes rising beyond. From here the Train to the Clouds climbs to a viaduct above 4,000 m, and roads lead to red canyons and high-altitude vineyards.","w":"A city on a green valley floor ringed by wooded hills; higher, bare Andean ranges to the west.","f":["Salta was founded in 1582 by Hernando de Lerma, who gave his name to the valley.","The Train to the Clouds reaches the La Polvorilla viaduct at about 4,200 m.","Vineyards in the province, some above 2,000 m, are famous for the white Torrontés wine."],"q":{"q":"What is Salta's famous mountain railway called?","a":"The Train to the Clouds","x":["The Train at the End of the World","The Andean Explorer"]}},"Q49258":{"t":"The mountain that inspired 'America the Beautiful'","s":"The Great Plains run west as flat as a table until they hit a wall of mountains, and Colorado Springs sits right at the join. Above it looms Pikes Peak, 4,302 m high and often white with snow. In 1893 the view from its summit moved Katharine Lee Bates to write the words of 'America the Beautiful'. Red sandstone fins stand at the city's western edge.","w":"A city where the flat plains meet the Rockies, with the massive, often snowy bulk of Pikes Peak directly to the west.","f":["Pikes Peak rises more than 2,400 m above the city.","The red rock fins of the Garden of the Gods are a free public park on the city's west side.","The US Olympic and Paralympic Training Center is based in Colorado Springs."],"q":{"q":"Which song was inspired by the view from Pikes Peak?","a":"America the Beautiful","x":["This Land Is Your Land","Home on the Range"]}},"Q44244":{"t":"A capital between lagoons on the Paraná floodplain","s":"Santa Fe sits on a strip of land between lagoons and river channels, on the wide, watery floodplain of the Paraná. Across the water, on a high bank, stands its sister city, Paraná, linked by a tunnel under the river. In 1853 Argentina's constitution was adopted here. The city's emblem is the Puente Colgante, a suspension bridge over the lagoon.","w":"A city between lagoons and river channels on a wide, green floodplain; another city on the high bank opposite.","f":["Argentina's national constitution was adopted in Santa Fe in 1853.","Juan de Garay founded the city in 1573; it moved to its present site in the 1650s.","The Hernandarias tunnel, opened in 1969, carries traffic beneath the Paraná River."],"q":{"q":"Which city faces Santa Fe across the river, linked by a tunnel?","a":"Paraná","x":["Rosario","Corrientes"]}},"Q193289":{"t":"The big city no road can reach","s":"Iquitos is surrounded by rainforest in every direction, and no highway leads to it: you arrive by plane or by boat. It is often called the largest city in the world that cannot be reached by road. The Amazon flows past its edge, joined by two smaller rivers, and in the Belén district houses stand on stilts or float with the seasonal flood.","w":"A city on a bend of a huge brown river, hemmed in by smaller rivers and oxbow lakes, with unbroken forest all around.","f":["About 100 km upstream, the Marañón and Ucayali rivers join to form the Amazon.","The rubber boom of the late 1800s filled the city with mansions faced with European tiles.","In the rainy season the river can rise by several metres, lifting Belén's floating houses."],"q":{"q":"How do most people travel to Iquitos?","a":"By air or by river","x":["By highway from Lima","By railway"]}},"Q44241":{"t":"A vineyard oasis in a sun-baked Andean valley","s":"In the rain shadow of the Andes, San Juan is a green island of vineyards and poplar windbreaks in a brown desert valley, watered by the San Juan River. The province is Argentina's second-biggest wine producer. The sun shines here almost every day. San Juan is also the birthplace of Domingo Faustino Sarmiento, the president who spread public schools across Argentina.","w":"A green oasis of vineyards in a brown desert valley; a reservoir to the west, bare Andean foothills beyond.","f":["Domingo Faustino Sarmiento was born in San Juan in 1811.","San Juan is Argentina's second-largest wine province, after Mendoza.","Ischigualasto, in the north of the province, holds some of the oldest known dinosaur fossils."],"q":{"q":"Which Argentine president was born in San Juan?","a":"Domingo F. Sarmiento","x":["Bartolomé Mitre","Julio A. Roca"]}},"Q16868":{"t":"America's busy trade gateway on the Rio Grande","s":"Look for lorries: long lines of them crawl over the bridges at Laredo, one of the busiest trade crossings between the United States and Mexico. The city sits on the north bank of the Rio Grande in Texas, facing Nuevo Laredo. It was founded in 1755 as a Spanish ranching town, and for a few months in 1840 it was the capital of a would-be republic.","w":"Twin cities across a looping river in flat, dry brush country; big bridges and rail yards on both banks.","f":["Laredo was founded in 1755 by Tomás Sánchez as a settlement of New Spain.","In 1840 it was the capital of the short-lived Republic of the Rio Grande.","The city's Washington's Birthday Celebration, held since 1898, is the largest in the US."],"q":{"q":"Which Mexican city faces Laredo across the Rio Grande?","a":"Nuevo Laredo","x":["Reynosa","Piedras Negras"]}},"Q28218":{"t":"A red stick on the banks of the Mississippi","s":"In 1699 French explorers paddling up the Mississippi saw a red-painted pole on the bluff here, marking a hunting boundary between two tribes. They called the spot Baton Rouge, 'red stick'. Today it is Louisiana's capital, with refineries and docks lining the river. It is as far up the Mississippi as big ocean-going ships can sail.","w":"A city on the east bank of the broad, winding Mississippi; docks and industry along the river, swamps and oxbow lakes around.","f":["The name means 'red stick', after the painted pole the French saw in 1699.","Louisiana's State Capitol, about 137 m tall, is the tallest state capitol in the US.","Louisiana State University's campus lies south of downtown, by the river."],"q":{"q":"What does 'Baton Rouge' mean?","a":"Red stick","x":["Red river","Red rock"]}},"Q468782":{"t":"A high Andean valley of farms, fairs and railways","s":"At about 3,250 m, the Mantaro Valley opens wide and flat between the ranges of the central Peruvian Andes, a patchwork of potato and maize fields. Huancayo is its busy market capital. A railway climbs here from Lima over one of the highest passes in the world. On clear days, the snowy peaks of the Huaytapallana range shine to the north-east.","w":"A broad, flat valley floor high in the Andes, a braided river along it; snowy peaks to the north-east.","f":["The Central Railway from Lima to Huancayo crosses the Andes at more than 4,700 m.","The Mantaro River leaves the valley through deep gorges and eventually joins the Apurímac.","Huancayo's Sunday market spreads for blocks along one of its main avenues."],"q":{"q":"Which valley is Huancayo the main city of?","a":"The Mantaro Valley","x":["The Sacred Valley","The Colca Valley"]}},"Q38453":{"t":"A planned capital where two rivers become one","s":"Columbia was one of the first planned cities in America: in 1786 South Carolina's lawmakers laid out a new capital in the middle of the state. The Broad and Saluda rivers meet here to form the Congaree. Just south-east lies Congaree National Park, a dark, swampy old-growth forest, and to the west spreads the branching blue of Lake Murray.","w":"A city at a river confluence, with a large branching lake to the west and dark floodplain forest to the south-east.","f":["Columbia was laid out in 1786 to replace Charleston as the capital.","Congaree National Park protects the largest old-growth bottomland hardwood forest left in the south-eastern US.","Lake Murray was created by a dam on the Saluda River completed in 1930."],"q":{"q":"Which two rivers meet at Columbia to form the Congaree?","a":"The Broad and the Saluda","x":["The Santee and the Saluda","The Broad and the Pee Dee"]}},"Q223966":{"t":"Peru's first Spanish city, in the sun-baked north","s":"In 1532, before he marched inland to meet the Inca, Francisco Pizarro founded a town in this northern desert: Piura, the first Spanish city in Peru. It moved a few times before settling on the river. Around it spreads the pale Sechura Desert, dotted with dry algarrobo forest. Its residents call it the City of Eternal Heat.","w":"A city on a river in flat, pale desert near the Pacific coast, with green irrigated strips along the water.","f":["Piura was founded in 1532 as San Miguel de Piura.","The algarrobo, a kind of mesquite, is the tree of the northern Peruvian desert and a local emblem.","In strong El Niño years, heavy rains can briefly fill the Sechura Desert with lakes."],"q":{"q":"Who founded Piura in 1532?","a":"Francisco Pizarro","x":["Diego de Almagro","Hernán Cortés"]}},"Q320015":{"t":"A carnival city at the foot of a volcano","s":"Pasto lies in a high green valley of the southern Colombian Andes at about 2,500 m, and the Galeras volcano rises right at its western edge. Every January the city explodes into the Carnival of Blacks and Whites, when people paint each other's faces and giant floats parade through the streets. To the south-east lies the large mountain lake of La Cocha.","w":"A city in a high green valley with a large volcano rising at its western edge; a big lake to the south-east.","f":["The Carnival of Blacks and Whites has been on UNESCO's intangible heritage list since 2009.","Galeras volcano rises to about 4,280 m, more than 1,700 m above the city.","Barniz de Pasto, a lacquer made from the resin of the mopa-mopa shrub, is a centuries-old local craft."],"q":{"q":"Which carnival is Pasto famous for?","a":"The Carnival of Blacks and Whites","x":["The Carnival of the Devil","The Flower Festival"]}},"Q110017":{"t":"Italian vineyards in Brazil's southern highlands","s":"In 1875 families from the Veneto began clearing the araucaria forest on these high, hilly lands of southern Brazil, and they brought their vines with them. Caxias do Sul grew from those colonies into an industrial city, but grapes still define it. Every few years the Festa da Uva celebrates the harvest. Winters here are cold, and snow is not unknown.","w":"A city on high, hilly ground with dark araucaria woods and vineyards; deep river valleys cutting into the plateau.","f":["Italian immigrants, mostly from the Veneto, began settling the area in 1875.","The Festa da Uva, a grape festival, was first held in 1931.","Marcopolo, one of the world's big bus-body makers, is based in Caxias do Sul."],"q":{"q":"Where did most of Caxias do Sul's first settlers come from?","a":"Northern Italy","x":["Germany","The Azores"]}},"Q235190":{"t":"A city balanced on a knife-edge ridge","s":"Manizales was built along the crest of a narrow Andean ridge, so its streets run along the top and the slopes fall away steeply on both sides. Settlers from Antioquia founded it in 1849, in the heart of coffee country. On clear mornings, the snowy summit of the Nevado del Ruiz shines to the south-east. Cable cars serve as public transport here.","w":"A city strung along a narrow ridge with steep green slopes on both sides; a snow-capped volcano to the south-east.","f":["Manizales was founded in 1849 by settlers from Antioquia.","The Nevado del Ruiz, about 5,320 m, rises some 30 km south-east of the city.","The area is part of the Coffee Cultural Landscape of Colombia, a UNESCO site since 2011."],"q":{"q":"Which snowy volcano looms over Manizales?","a":"Nevado del Ruiz","x":["Galeras","Cotopaxi"]}},"Q518431":{"t":"An anchovy boomtown on a desert bay","s":"Chimbote curves around a sheltered bay on Peru's bone-dry coast, with rocky islands at its mouth. In the 1960s it boomed as few towns ever have: the cold Humboldt Current teemed with anchovies, and Chimbote became one of the biggest fishing ports in the world. Just to the north, the Santa River brings a green valley down from the Andes.","w":"A city around a bay with islands at its mouth, on bare desert coast; a green river valley just to the north.","f":["Most of the anchovy catch landed here is turned into fishmeal for animal feed.","The Santa River, fed by the glaciers of the Cordillera Blanca, flows all year.","The bay is also known as Ferrol Bay."],"q":{"q":"Which fish made Chimbote a boomtown?","a":"Anchovy","x":["Tuna","Cod"]}},"Q328846":{"t":"Founded by those who chose to stay Mexican","s":"When the war of 1846–48 turned the Rio Grande into the border, Laredo ended up in the United States. Some of its families refused to change country: they crossed to the south bank and founded Nuevo Laredo in 1848. Today the two Laredos, joined by several bridges, form the busiest trade crossing on the whole border.","w":"A city on the south bank of a looping river, facing Laredo; flat, dry brush country all around.","f":["Nuevo Laredo was founded in 1848 by families who wanted to remain Mexican.","The city belongs to the state of Tamaulipas.","More US–Mexico trade passes through the two Laredos than any other crossing."],"q":{"q":"Why was Nuevo Laredo founded in 1848?","a":"For Laredo families staying Mexican","x":["As a railway town","As a silver-mining camp"]}},"Q187805":{"t":"Waterfalls downtown, flood-scoured land to the south-west","s":"Spokane grew around a set of waterfalls right in the city centre, where the Spokane River drops through basalt ledges. Look south-west and you'll see a strange landscape of bare channels and scattered lakes: the Channeled Scablands, gouged by colossal floods at the end of the last ice age. Father's Day was first celebrated here, in 1910.","w":"A city on a river among pine-covered hills; to the south-west, dark scoured channels and small lakes; wheat fields to the south.","f":["Father's Day was first celebrated in Spokane in June 1910.","When Spokane hosted the 1974 World's Fair, it was the smallest city ever to have done so.","The Ice Age floods that carved the Scablands burst out of glacial Lake Missoula, in today's Montana."],"q":{"q":"Which holiday was first celebrated in Spokane in 1910?","a":"Father's Day","x":["Mother's Day","Labor Day"]}},"Q23551":{"t":"A university town under Venezuela's highest peaks","s":"Mérida sits on a long, flat terrace in the deep valley of the Chama River, at about 1,600 m, walled in by two high ranges of the Venezuelan Andes. To the south-east tower the country's highest mountains, led by Pico Bolívar. A cable car climbs from the edge of town almost to the summits. Students of the old University of the Andes fill the streets.","w":"A city on a long, flat terrace in a deep valley between two high ranges; bare rocky peaks to the south-east.","f":["Pico Bolívar, about 4,980 m, is the highest mountain in Venezuela.","The Mukumbarí cable car rises from the city to Pico Espejo at about 4,765 m.","The Coromoto ice-cream parlour once held a world record for the most flavours on sale."],"q":{"q":"Which mountain, towering over Mérida, is Venezuela's highest?","a":"Pico Bolívar","x":["Pico Humboldt","Mount Roraima"]}},"Q651906":{"t":"Capital of the backlands of northern Minas","s":"North of the mining towns of Minas Gerais, the land grows drier and wider: this is the sertão, a country of cerrado scrub, cattle and dry-season dust. Montes Claros is its biggest city and its market town, a crossroads of highways and trade. These backlands are the setting of one of Brazil's greatest novels, Guimarães Rosa's Grande Sertão: Veredas.","w":"A city on a broad plain between low ridges, amid brownish cerrado in the dry season that turns green in the rains.","f":["Montes Claros means 'clear hills'.","It is the largest city in the north of Minas Gerais.","Pequi, a pungent cerrado fruit, is cooked with rice and chicken throughout the region."],"q":{"q":"Which famous Brazilian novel is set in the northern Minas backlands?","a":"Grande Sertão: Veredas","x":["Dom Casmurro","Os Sertões"]}},"Q49225":{"t":"The Biggest Little City, at the Sierra's dry foot","s":"On the dry, eastern side of the Sierra Nevada, Reno fills a high basin at about 1,370 m. Through downtown flows the Truckee River, which leaves Lake Tahoe just over the mountains and ends in Pyramid Lake in the desert, never reaching the sea. An arch over the main street proudly calls Reno 'The Biggest Little City in the World'.","w":"A city in a high desert basin at the eastern foot of forested mountains; a river winding east, Lake Tahoe hidden beyond the ridge.","f":["Pyramid Lake, where the Truckee ends, has no outlet to the sea.","In the 1930s Reno became famous for quick divorces, after Nevada cut residency to six weeks.","Lake Tahoe lies only about 35 km south-west, on the other side of the Carson Range."],"q":{"q":"Which lake does the Truckee River flow out of?","a":"Lake Tahoe","x":["Pyramid Lake","Mono Lake"]}},"Q192235":{"t":"Where three countries meet by a mighty dam","s":"Two big rivers meet just south of here, and three countries touch: Paraguay, Brazil and Argentina. Ciudad del Este, Paraguay's second city, faces Brazil across the Paraná, joined by the Friendship Bridge, and its streets are crowded with shoppers from across the border. Just upstream stands the Itaipú Dam, and a short way south thunder the Iguazu Falls.","w":"A city on the west bank of a wide river facing Brazil; a huge reservoir behind a dam just to the north.","f":["The Itaipú Dam, shared by Paraguay and Brazil, is one of the largest hydroelectric plants in the world.","The Friendship Bridge across the Paraná links the city with Foz do Iguaçu in Brazil.","Ciudad del Este was founded only in 1957."],"q":{"q":"Which huge dam lies just north of Ciudad del Este?","a":"Itaipú","x":["Yacyretá","Guri"]}},"Q109188":{"t":"Salt beef, sweet pastries and two vast lagoons","s":"Pelotas lies on a flat plain beside the channel that links Brazil's two great coastal lagoons, the Lagoa dos Patos and the Lagoa Mirim. In the 19th century its salt-beef works made it one of the richest towns in the country, and the wealth built grand mansions. Its Portuguese-style egg and sugar sweets are still famous.","w":"A flat city on a channel between two huge lagoons; the enormous Lagoa dos Patos stretching to the north-east.","f":["The name comes from pelotas, small boats of stretched hide once used to cross the rivers.","The sweets of Pelotas were declared part of Brazil's cultural heritage in 2018.","The Lagoa dos Patos is the largest coastal lagoon in Brazil."],"q":{"q":"What were the 'pelotas' that gave the city its name?","a":"Small hide boats","x":["Clay balls","Cattle ranches"]}},"Q217071":{"t":"Where the road from Lima meets the rivers of the Amazon","s":"The Ucayali twists across the green plain in huge loops, leaving behind crescent-shaped lakes wherever it changes course. Pucallpa sits on its bank, at the point where the long road over the Andes from Lima finally reaches a river that ships can follow to the Amazon. Right next to the city lies Yarinacocha, an old river bend turned into a lake.","w":"A wide brown river looping wildly across green forest, with crescent lakes everywhere; the city on its bank beside one of them.","f":["Yarinacocha, the oxbow lake on the city's northern edge, was once a bend of the Ucayali.","Riverboats sail from Pucallpa down the Ucayali to Iquitos, a trip of several days.","The Shipibo-Konibo people are known for kené, intricate geometric patterns on cloth and pottery."],"q":{"q":"On which river does Pucallpa stand?","a":"The Ucayali","x":["The Marañón","The Huallaga"]}},"Q22062839":{"t":"A cool plateau city in the Bahian backlands","s":"Vitória da Conquista stands on a plateau at about 900 m in south-western Bahia, and that height gives it cool nights that surprise visitors to north-eastern Brazil. Coffee grows on the surrounding hills. It is the third-largest city in Bahia and a crossroads on the highway between the north-east and the south. Film-maker Glauber Rocha was born here.","w":"A city on a high plateau of farms and coffee plantations; the land drops away to lower, drier country around it.","f":["Glauber Rocha, a leader of Brazil's Cinema Novo, was born here in 1939.","At about 900 m, it is one of the coolest cities in north-eastern Brazil.","It is Bahia's third-largest city, after Salvador and Feira de Santana."],"q":{"q":"Which film-maker was born in Vitória da Conquista?","a":"Glauber Rocha","x":["Nelson Pereira dos Santos","Walter Salles"]}},"Q172512":{"t":"A railway built for rubber, on the Amazon's big tributary","s":"Porto Velho grew up where boats on the Madeira River met a stretch of rapids they could not pass. To get Bolivian rubber around them, a railway was cut through the jungle, finished in 1912. The capital of Rondônia now sits beside a big hydroelectric dam, and around it the forest is cut into long clearings like the bones of a fish.","w":"A city on the east bank of a wide brown river, a dam just upstream; forest cut into fishbone-shaped clearings around it.","f":["The Madeira–Mamoré Railway, finished in 1912, ran about 366 km around the rapids.","The Madeira is the Amazon's largest tributary.","The Santo Antônio hydroelectric dam stands at the edge of the city."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows past Porto Velho?","a":"The Madeira","x":["The Tapajós","The Xingu"]}},"Q39178":{"t":"Carnival capital between mountains and a calm gulf","s":"Port of Spain lies on the calm Gulf of Paria, with the forested Northern Range rising right behind it. Just north of downtown, look for a big green oval: the Queen's Park Savannah, heart of one of the world's great carnivals. Trinidad gave the world the steelpan, an instrument hammered out of oil drums. Venezuela is only a short hop across the water.","w":"A city on a calm gulf at the foot of forested mountains; a large green oval north of downtown, mangrove swamp to the south.","f":["The steelpan, made from oil drums, was invented in Trinidad in the 20th century.","At the Dragon's Mouths, Trinidad is only about 11 km from the coast of Venezuela.","The Caroni Swamp, just south of the city, is famous for flocks of scarlet ibis at dusk."],"q":{"q":"Which musical instrument was invented in Trinidad?","a":"The steelpan","x":["The marimba","The bongo drums"]}},"Q242906":{"t":"Stone goblets on the grassy plains of Paraná","s":"Ponta Grossa lies on the Campos Gerais, rolling natural grasslands on a high plateau of Paraná. Its name, 'big point', recalls a wooded hill that stood out on the open plain. The city grew as a stop on the old cattle trail to São Paulo. Nearby, in Vila Velha State Park, wind and rain have sculpted sandstone into towers, one shaped like a giant goblet.","w":"A city on rolling open grassland with dark patches of pine plantations; a low escarpment to the east.","f":["Vila Velha State Park's sandstone pillars include one known as 'the Cup'.","The park also has deep water-filled sinkholes called furnas.","Drovers taking mules from the south to Sorocaba once rested their herds here."],"q":{"q":"What is Vila Velha State Park near Ponta Grossa known for?","a":"Sandstone pillars","x":["Hot springs","Salt flats"]}},"Q54886":{"t":"Where Panama hats are actually made","s":"Cuenca sits in a green Andean basin at about 2,550 m, crossed by four rivers, and its colonial centre is a UNESCO World Heritage site. Here's a surprise: the famous Panama hat comes from here and the surrounding towns, woven from toquilla straw. It got its name because it was sold to travellers and canal workers in Panama. West of the city, the Cajas moors glitter with lakes.","w":"A city in a green mountain basin crossed by several rivers; high moorland dotted with small lakes to the west.","f":["Cuenca's historic centre has been on the UNESCO World Heritage list since 1999.","The Inca built a major city here called Tomebamba before the Spanish arrived.","Cajas National Park, just west, holds more than 200 lakes and ponds."],"q":{"q":"Which famous hat is actually made in and around Cuenca?","a":"The Panama hat","x":["The bowler hat","The sombrero vueltiao"]}},"Q49242":{"t":"Behind the longest barrier island on Earth","s":"Look along the Texas coast for a thin ribbon of sand that seems to go on forever: Padre Island, the longest barrier island in the world. Corpus Christi sits on a round bay sheltered behind its northern end. Between the island and the mainland lies the shallow, salty Laguna Madre. On the quiet beaches, rare sea turtles come ashore to lay eggs.","w":"A round bay behind a thin sandy island that runs far to the south; a narrow lagoon between the island and the mainland.","f":["Padre Island stretches about 180 km along the Texas coast.","Padre Island National Seashore is the main US nesting beach of the Kemp's ridley sea turtle.","Tradition says the bay was named on the feast of Corpus Christi in 1519."],"q":{"q":"Which long barrier island lies off Corpus Christi?","a":"Padre Island","x":["Galveston Island","Hatteras Island"]}},"Q842143":{"t":"Grapes and mangoes in the heart of the dry backlands","s":"In the grey-brown scrub of Brazil's semi-arid north-east, a blaze of green appears along the São Francisco River: irrigated vineyards and mango orchards. Petrolina, on the Pernambuco bank, faces its twin Juazeiro across the river. With sun almost every day and water from the river, vines here can give more than two harvests a year.","w":"Bright green irrigated fields along a big river in brownish scrubland; twin cities joined by a bridge; a vast reservoir upstream.","f":["Mangoes and grapes from this valley are exported to Europe and North America.","Upstream, the Sobradinho reservoir is one of the largest artificial lakes in the world by area.","The valley also makes wine, unusual so close to the equator."],"q":{"q":"Which city faces Petrolina across the São Francisco River?","a":"Juazeiro","x":["Juazeiro do Norte","Paulo Afonso"]}},"Q171612":{"t":"A rubber town that joined Brazil in 1903","s":"Rio Branco grew on the meandering Acre River, deep in the western Amazon, from a rubber-tapping settlement of the 1880s. Back then this land was claimed by Bolivia; it became Brazilian by treaty in 1903, and the city was named after the diplomat who negotiated it, the Baron of Rio Branco. Today it is the capital of Acre.","w":"A city on a winding river in the rainforest, surrounded by cattle pastures cut out of the forest.","f":["The Treaty of Petrópolis, signed in 1903, made Acre part of Brazil.","The city began in 1882 as a rubber estate on the Acre River.","The Santo Daime religion was founded near Rio Branco in the 1930s."],"q":{"q":"Which country did Acre belong to before 1903?","a":"Bolivia","x":["Paraguay","Colombia"]}},"Q80517":{"t":"Named after the man who cleared a giant logjam","s":"For centuries a vast tangle of fallen trees, the Great Raft, choked the Red River for well over 200 km. In the 1830s Captain Henry Shreve cleared it with his snag boats, opening the river to steamboats, and the town that sprang up was named after him. Shreveport faces Bossier City across the rust-coloured river, amid pine forests and lakes.","w":"A city on a winding, reddish-brown river with oxbow lakes, surrounded by pine forest; a large lake just west of downtown.","f":["Henry Shreve's snag boats pulled apart the Great Raft logjam in the 1830s.","Elvis Presley made some of his first broadcasts on Shreveport's Louisiana Hayride in 1954.","The Red River takes its colour from the reddish soils it carries."],"q":{"q":"What did Henry Shreve clear from the Red River?","a":"A giant logjam","x":["A sandbar","A waterfall"]}},"Q44239":{"t":"Two rivers join to make one, in dinosaur country","s":"In the brown Patagonian steppe, two rivers from the Andes meet — the Limay and the Neuquén — and together become the Río Negro. Neuquén sits right at the fork, at the head of a green strip of apple and pear orchards. The province is famous for giants: some of the largest dinosaurs ever found were dug up here.","w":"A city at the fork of two rivers in brown steppe; a green ribbon of orchards running east along the merged river.","f":["Argentinosaurus and Giganotosaurus, two of the largest known dinosaurs, were found in Neuquén province.","The city was founded in 1904 and became the provincial capital that same year.","The Vaca Muerta shale formation in the province holds huge oil and gas reserves."],"q":{"q":"Which two rivers meet at Neuquén to form the Río Negro?","a":"The Limay and the Neuquén","x":["The Colorado and the Neuquén","The Limay and the Chubut"]}},"Q43919":{"t":"A capital in the green Willamette Valley","s":"Oregon's capital lies in the heart of the Willamette Valley, a flat patchwork of farms, hazelnut orchards and grass-seed fields between two mountain ranges. The city grew from a Methodist mission of the 1840s on the Willamette River. On clear days, the snowy cones of the Cascades line the eastern horizon, and the lower Coast Range closes the view to the west.","w":"A city on a winding river in a flat green-and-gold valley; the Coast Range to the west, snowy Cascade volcanoes to the east.","f":["Willamette University in Salem, founded in 1842, is the oldest university in the western US.","Oregon grows nearly all of America's hazelnuts, mostly in the Willamette Valley.","A gilded statue of a pioneer stands on top of the Oregon State Capitol."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Salem, Oregon?","a":"The Willamette","x":["The Columbia","The Snake"]}},"Q61320":{"t":"A city of palms beneath a smoking volcano","s":"Colima is a warm, low city of palms in western Mexico, and its horizon is dominated by two volcanoes. The nearer one, the Volcán de Colima, is among the most active in the country and often trails a plume. Behind it rises the older, higher Nevado de Colima, sometimes white with snow. In ancient tombs here, archaeologists found hundreds of plump clay dogs.","w":"A city on a green plain with two large volcanoes to the north, one often smoking.","f":["The Volcán de Colima, about 30 km north of the city, is one of Mexico's most active volcanoes.","The neighbouring Nevado de Colima rises to over 4,200 m.","The clay dogs of ancient Colima were buried in deep shaft tombs."],"q":{"q":"Which animal do Colima's famous ancient clay figures show?","a":"Dogs","x":["Jaguars","Eagles"]}},"Q44214":{"t":"The 'beautiful bend' of the Paraguay River","s":"Old Spanish sailors called this bend of the Paraguay River the Vuelta Formosa, the 'beautiful bend', and the name stuck. The city, capital of Argentina's Formosa province, faces Paraguay across the wide brown river. Behind it spreads the flat, humid Chaco: palm savannas, forest islands and wetlands under a big sky.","w":"A city on a bend of a wide brown river facing Paraguay; flat green plains with palm savanna and wetlands to the west.","f":["Formosa was founded in 1879 by Luis Jorge Fontana.","Across the river lies the Paraguayan town of Alberdi.","The Bañado La Estrella, in the west of the province, is one of Argentina's largest wetlands."],"q":{"q":"Which river separates Formosa city from Paraguay?","a":"The Paraguay River","x":["The Uruguay River","The Bermejo"]}},"Q37043":{"t":"A capital picked for being halfway","s":"In 1824 Florida's two main towns, Pensacola and St. Augustine, lay more than 500 km apart, so lawmakers met in the middle and built a capital in the forest: Tallahassee. It sits among rolling red-clay hills, unusual for flat Florida, and its old 'canopy roads' run under arches of live oaks hung with Spanish moss. The Gulf coast lies about 35 km south.","w":"A city in rolling, forested hills dotted with lakes; the flat, dark pine forests of the Gulf coast to the south.","f":["Hernando de Soto's expedition spent the winter of 1539–40 in the area.","The Apalachicola National Forest, the largest in Florida, begins at the city's south-western edge.","Florida State University and Florida A&M are both in Tallahassee."],"q":{"q":"Why was Tallahassee chosen as Florida's capital in 1824?","a":"It lay halfway between two main towns","x":["It had the best harbour","It was the oldest settlement"]}},"Q61301":{"t":"A walled port that stood up to the pirates","s":"In the 1600s pirates sacked Campeche again and again, so the Spanish finally ringed it with thick walls and bastions. Much of that fortress still stands, and inside it the streets are painted in pastel colours. The city faces the shallow, greenish Gulf of Mexico on the west coast of the Yucatán Peninsula, and the sun sets straight over the sea.","w":"A city on the flat western coast of the Yucatán Peninsula facing the Gulf; pale-green shallow water offshore, low forest inland.","f":["The city walls were built between 1686 and 1704 to keep out pirates.","The fortified town has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1999.","The Maya city of Calakmul, deep in the forest of Campeche state, is also a UNESCO site."],"q":{"q":"Why did the Spanish wall in Campeche?","a":"To keep out pirates","x":["To hold back the sea","To guard a silver mint"]}},"Q29364":{"t":"Where a bus seat changed American history","s":"On 1 December 1955, Rosa Parks refused to give up her bus seat in Montgomery, and the boycott that followed made the city a birthplace of the civil rights movement. Alabama's capital sits on a looping stretch of the Alabama River, amid the dark, fertile farmland of the Black Belt. The Wright brothers opened one of the first flying schools here in 1910.","w":"A city on a looping river amid flat farmland and forest in central Alabama.","f":["The Montgomery bus boycott lasted more than a year, from 1955 to 1956.","In 1910 the Wright brothers opened a flying school on the edge of the city.","The Black Belt around Montgomery is named for its dark, rich soil."],"q":{"q":"Whose refusal to give up a bus seat began the Montgomery bus boycott?","a":"Rosa Parks","x":["Harriet Tubman","Coretta Scott King"]}},"Q36168":{"t":"A capital on the island that stands apart","s":"Barbados sits alone in the Atlantic, east of the arc of Caribbean islands, a low, green, pear-shaped island of coral limestone. Bridgetown lies on Carlisle Bay on its south-west coast, where the water turns turquoise over the reefs. The old town and its British garrison are a UNESCO World Heritage site. George Washington stayed here in 1751.","w":"A low green island alone to the east of the island chain; the capital on a bay on its sheltered south-west coast.","f":["George Washington visited Barbados in 1751, his only journey outside mainland North America.","Historic Bridgetown and its Garrison became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2011.","Barbados is made largely of coral limestone rather than volcanic rock."],"q":{"q":"Which future US president visited Barbados in 1751?","a":"George Washington","x":["Thomas Jefferson","John Adams"]}},"Q214215":{"t":"Sunset over the Sea of Cortez","s":"Near the southern tip of the Baja California peninsula, a round bay is closed off by a long sandbar, and La Paz stretches along its shore. Thanks to the bay's shape, this Gulf city watches the sun set over the water. Desert mountains rise behind it, while the islands of Espíritu Santo lie just to the north, and whale sharks visit the bay.","w":"A city on a round bay partly closed by a long sandbar; rugged islands to the north, desert mountains behind.","f":["Hernán Cortés tried to found a colony here in 1535.","The Espíritu Santo islands are part of a UNESCO World Heritage site covering the Gulf of California's islands.","La Paz is the capital of the state of Baja California Sur."],"q":{"q":"Who tried to found a colony at La Paz in 1535?","a":"Hernán Cortés","x":["Francisco Pizarro","Juan Rodríguez Cabrillo"]}},"Q2203":{"t":"Home of mummies older than Egypt's","s":"Arica sits where the bare Atacama Desert meets the Pacific, beneath a steep headland called the Morro. It is Chile's northernmost city and one of the driest inhabited places on Earth: rain may not fall for years. Yet some 7,000 years ago, the Chinchorro fishing people here were already mummifying their dead, long before the Egyptians.","w":"A coastal city at the foot of a steep headland; two thin green valleys cutting through bare brown desert.","f":["Arica averages less than a millimetre of rain a year.","The Chinchorro mummification sites became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2021.","The green Azapa Valley, just inland, grows olives and tomatoes in the middle of the desert."],"q":{"q":"What is special about Arica's Chinchorro mummies?","a":"Oldest known artificial mummies","x":["Found frozen on a volcano summit","Buried with Inca gold"]}},"Q51691":{"t":"Yellow city on a table-flat plain","s":"Few places on Earth are as flat as the Llano Estacado, the high tableland of the Texas Panhandle, and Amarillo sits right in the middle of it. Circles of irrigated crops and vast cattle feedlots dot the plain. Then, to the south-east, the ground suddenly falls away into Palo Duro Canyon, a gash of red and gold rock.","w":"A city on an utterly flat plain of circles and squares; a red canyon cutting into the plateau to the south-east.","f":["Amarillo means 'yellow' in Spanish.","Palo Duro Canyon is often called the second-largest canyon in the United States.","Amarillo was once called the Helium Capital of the World for the gas stored and produced nearby."],"q":{"q":"What does 'Amarillo' mean in Spanish?","a":"Yellow","x":["Windy","Plain"]}},"Q159288":{"t":"The California coast that faces south","s":"Here the California coast takes a turn and runs east–west, so Santa Barbara's beaches face south into the sun. The city fills a narrow shelf between the Pacific and the steep Santa Ynez Mountains, all white walls and red-tiled roofs. It calls itself the American Riviera. Out on the horizon float the Channel Islands.","w":"A city on a narrow coastal shelf between the ocean and a steep mountain wall; the coast running east–west, islands offshore.","f":["Mission Santa Barbara, founded in 1786, is known as the Queen of the Missions.","The Channel Islands, about 40 km offshore, form a national park.","The Santa Ynez Mountains rise to over 1,200 m just behind the city."],"q":{"q":"Which way do Santa Barbara's beaches face?","a":"South","x":["West","North"]}},"Q83813":{"t":"A city planned around squares, in a sea of marsh","s":"Savannah was designed on paper in 1733: James Oglethorpe laid out a grid of neighbourhoods, each built around a small square, and 22 of those squares survive, shaded by live oaks and Spanish moss. The city sits on a bluff above the Savannah River, which winds through wide golden-green salt marshes to the Atlantic.","w":"A city on the south bank of a river winding through wide golden-green salt marshes toward the Atlantic.","f":["James Oglethorpe founded Savannah in 1733 as the first city of the Georgia colony.","Juliette Gordon Low founded the Girl Scouts of the USA in Savannah in 1912.","The Port of Savannah is one of the busiest container ports in the United States."],"q":{"q":"Who founded Savannah in 1733?","a":"James Oglethorpe","x":["William Penn","John Smith"]}},"Q79783":{"t":"Twin towns named for walnut trees","s":"In a narrow valley between dry hills, the border runs straight over the ridges and splits one town in two: Nogales, Sonora, in Mexico and the much smaller Nogales, Arizona. The name means 'walnut trees', which once grew in the pass. In winter, lorries loaded with Mexican tomatoes, peppers and cucumbers queue here to cross into the United States.","w":"Twin towns in a narrow valley of dry hills, cut by a straight border line; a highway heading north toward Tucson.","f":["Nogales means 'walnut trees' in Spanish.","A large share of the fresh vegetables Mexico sends to the US in winter crosses at Nogales.","Tucson lies about 100 km to the north."],"q":{"q":"What does 'Nogales' mean?","a":"Walnut trees","x":["Pine trees","Oak trees"]}},"Q34109":{"t":"Flat as a table, on a river that runs north","s":"Around Fargo the land is astonishingly flat: this is the bed of glacial Lake Agassiz, a vast lake that covered the region at the end of the last ice age. Fields stretch in perfect squares to the horizon. Along the city's eastern edge winds the Red River of the North, the border with Minnesota, flowing north toward Canada.","w":"A grid city on dead-flat farmland cut into huge squares; a thin, winding river along its east side.","f":["The Red River of the North flows north into Lake Winnipeg, in Canada.","Fargo faces its twin, Moorhead, Minnesota, across the river.","The city was named after William Fargo, a director of the Northern Pacific Railway."],"q":{"q":"Which ancient lake bed makes the land around Fargo so flat?","a":"Lake Agassiz","x":["Lake Bonneville","Lake Missoula"]}},"Q659400":{"t":"Home turf of the Venus flytrap","s":"Wilmington sits on the Cape Fear River, some 40 km before it reaches the Atlantic, with barrier-island beaches just to the east. The pine savannas around it hide a botanical wonder: the Venus flytrap, which grows wild only in this small corner of the Carolinas. Film studios have also turned the city into a busy location for movies and TV.","w":"A city on the east bank of a wide river, with barrier-island beaches to the east; pine forest and swamp around.","f":["The Venus flytrap grows wild only within about 120 km of Wilmington.","The Cape Fear River meets the Atlantic about 40 km south of the city.","Wilmington's film studios have produced dozens of movies and TV series."],"q":{"q":"Which carnivorous plant grows wild only near Wilmington?","a":"The Venus flytrap","x":["The cobra lily","The waterwheel plant"]}},"Q35004":{"t":"Bolivia's wine valley, high in the Andes","s":"In the far south of Bolivia, a warm green valley opens at about 1,850 m, and vineyards cover its floor: this is Tarija, Bolivia's wine country. Its vines are among the highest-growing in the world. The valley also makes singani, the national grape brandy. The city was founded in 1574, and its easy-going charm earned it the name 'Andalusia of Bolivia'.","w":"A green valley of vineyards in the southern Andes, ringed by eroded, pale badlands; a reservoir to the south-west.","f":["Luis de Fuentes founded Tarija in 1574.","Singani, Bolivia's national grape spirit, is distilled in the valleys around Tarija.","Fossils of Ice Age giants such as mastodons have been found in the valley's eroded badlands."],"q":{"q":"Which spirit, besides wine, is Tarija known for?","a":"Singani","x":["Pisco","Mezcal"]}},"Q1384520":{"t":"Gateway to a new frontier of soy and cotton","s":"Barreiras sits in a river valley in western Bahia, and above it, on the vast flat plateau to the west, lies one of Brazil's newest farming frontiers. Since the 1980s, the native cerrado there has been turned into huge fields of soy, maize and cotton, many watered by circular pivots. The town grew from a river port into a busy agricultural hub.","w":"A city in a river valley below the edge of a vast flat plateau covered in huge rectangular fields and green irrigation circles.","f":["Barreiras stands on the Rio Grande, a tributary of the São Francisco.","Many of the plateau's farmers came from southern Brazil, especially Rio Grande do Sul.","Western Bahia is one of Brazil's leading cotton-growing regions."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Barreiras?","a":"The Rio Grande","x":["The Rio Pardo","The Rio Doce"]}},"Q42735":{"t":"A small capital at the tip of Puget Sound","s":"Puget Sound ends in a spread of long, narrow fingers of water, and at the tip of one of them sits Olympia, the capital of Washington State. Despite its title, it is a small city of trees and water. On clear days, the white cone of Mount Rainier dominates the eastern horizon, and the Olympic Mountains, which gave the city its name, rise to the north-west.","w":"The southern fingers of Puget Sound; a small city at the tip of one inlet, Mount Rainier's white cone to the east.","f":["Olympia is named after the Olympic Mountains, visible to the north-west on clear days.","The Nisqually River delta, just north-east of the city, is a protected estuary rich in birds.","Washington's Capitol dome is one of the tallest masonry domes in the world."],"q":{"q":"Olympia takes its name from which mountains?","a":"The Olympic Mountains","x":["The Cascades","The Blue Mountains"]}},"Q131335":{"t":"Pink stone waterfalls on the prairie","s":"Out on the flat farmland of eastern South Dakota, the Big Sioux River loops around the centre of Sioux Falls and tumbles over wide ledges of pink quartzite. The falls gave the city its name and its start. The same hard pink stone, quarried nearby, built many of its old buildings. Today it is the largest city in South Dakota.","w":"A city on flat prairie farmland, with a river curling around its centre.","f":["The falls cascade over Sioux quartzite, a hard pink rock about 1.6 billion years old.","Sioux Falls is the most populous city in South Dakota.","Local quartzite was used for many of the city's 19th-century buildings."],"q":{"q":"What rock forms the falls at Sioux Falls?","a":"Pink quartzite","x":["Black basalt","White limestone"]}},"Q14467":{"t":"Chile's second-oldest city, under the clearest skies","s":"La Serena spreads along a wide bay where the green Elqui Valley reaches the sea through bare desert hills. Founded in 1544, it is the second-oldest city in Chile. Inland, the Elqui's dry, cloudless nights have drawn some of the world's great observatories to the mountaintops, and its vineyards distil pisco. Its twin, Coquimbo, sits on the headland to the south.","w":"A city on a wide bay where a green valley meets the sea through desert hills; its twin city on a headland to the south.","f":["Founded in 1544, La Serena is Chile's second-oldest city after Santiago.","Observatories such as Cerro Tololo and La Silla crown mountaintops inland from the city.","Poet Gabriela Mistral, Nobel laureate in 1945, grew up in the Elqui Valley."],"q":{"q":"Which poet, a Nobel laureate, grew up in the Elqui Valley?","a":"Gabriela Mistral","x":["Pablo Neruda","Vicente Huidobro"]}},"Q16910":{"t":"Chile's own Los Ángeles, below the southern volcanoes","s":"Yes, Chile has a Los Ángeles too: a farming and forestry town in the Central Valley, some 450 km south of Santiago. Around it, flat green fields alternate with dark pine plantations. To the east the Andes rise, with the snowy cone of Antuco above Laguna del Laja. Just north, the Laja River plunges over a wide horseshoe of falls.","w":"A town on a flat green valley with dark pine plantations; snowy volcanic cones in the Andes to the east.","f":["The town was founded in 1739 as the Villa de Santa María de los Ángeles.","The Laja Falls lie about 25 km north of the city.","The Antuco volcano rises to about 2,980 m above Laguna del Laja."],"q":{"q":"Which famous waterfalls lie north of Los Ángeles, Chile?","a":"The Laja Falls","x":["The Petrohué Falls","The Huilo-Huilo Falls"]}},"Q41057":{"t":"A prairie capital where a landmark case began","s":"Kansas's capital lies on the winding Kansas River amid rolling farmland, with the tallgrass hills of the Flint Hills to the south-west. In 1951 a Topeka father sued the school board so his daughter could attend a nearby whites-only school. The case, Brown v. Board of Education, ended in 1954 with the Supreme Court outlawing school segregation.","w":"A city on a winding river in rolling farmland; the grassy Flint Hills to the south-west.","f":["The name is often said to mean 'a good place to dig potatoes'.","Charles Curtis, the first US vice president of Native American descent, was born in Topeka in 1860.","The Flint Hills hold one of the largest surviving tracts of tallgrass prairie in North America."],"q":{"q":"Which landmark court case began in Topeka?","a":"Brown v. Board of Education","x":["Roe v. Wade","Marbury v. Madison"]}},"Q3868":{"t":"A silver-rush town in the Atacama","s":"A thin thread of green winds through bare brown mountains: the valley of the Copiapó River, and in it the city of Copiapó. In 1832 a silver strike nearby made it rich, and in 1851 it got Chile's first railway, down to the port of Caldera. When rare rains fall, the desert around it bursts into flower, a spectacle called the desierto florido.","w":"A thin green valley winding through bare desert mountains; the city in the valley, the high Andes far to the east.","f":["Chile's first railway, opened in 1851, ran from Copiapó to the port of Caldera.","The silver of Chañarcillo, found in 1832, brought a rush to the region.","Ojos del Salado, the world's highest volcano at about 6,890 m, rises on the border to the east."],"q":{"q":"Where did Chile's first railway, from Copiapó, lead?","a":"The port of Caldera","x":["Valparaíso","Antofagasta"]}},"Q44564":{"t":"A capital squeezed into an Appalachian valley","s":"In West Virginia there is almost no flat land, and Charleston makes the most of a narrow river valley, strung along the Kanawha where the Elk River flows in. Steep, forested ridges rise straight from the edge of town. In the early 1800s salt boiled from brine wells here was shipped all over the country; later came coal, gas and chemicals.","w":"A city squeezed into a narrow, winding river valley between steep, forested ridges.","f":["Charleston lies where the Elk River flows into the Kanawha.","Frontiersman Daniel Boone lived in the Kanawha valley in the 1790s.","The gold-leaf dome of the West Virginia Capitol is slightly taller than the US Capitol's."],"q":{"q":"Which river meets the Kanawha at Charleston?","a":"The Elk","x":["The Ohio","The Monongahela"]}},"Q44215":{"t":"A small capital on the endless pampas","s":"Santa Rosa is a neat grid of streets set down on the flat pampas of central Argentina, with nothing but fields and sky around it. It was founded only in 1892 and is the capital of La Pampa province. On its western edge lies a shallow lake ringed by a park. Further west, farmland gives way to dry woods of caldén.","w":"A small grid city on endlessly flat plains; a shallow lake on its western edge, farmland squares turning to scrub westward.","f":["Santa Rosa was founded in 1892.","Laguna Don Tomás, the lake at the city's edge, is surrounded by a large park.","The caldén, a hardy tree of the mesquite family, grows wild only in central Argentina."],"q":{"q":"Santa Rosa is the capital of which Argentine province?","a":"La Pampa","x":["Córdoba","San Luis"]}},"Q279009":{"t":"Under the volcano where you can see two oceans","s":"David spreads across the hot, green Pacific lowlands of western Panama, not far from the border with Costa Rica. Look north: the land climbs steadily to Volcán Barú, the highest point in Panama. On a clear morning, climbers on its summit can see both the Pacific and the Caribbean. The cool highlands on its slopes grow some of the world's most prized coffee.","w":"A city on a green coastal plain near the Pacific, mangrove estuaries to the south, a large volcano to the north.","f":["Volcán Barú, about 3,470 m, rises roughly 40 km north of the city.","The highland town of Boquete, above David, grows the famous Geisha coffee.","David is the capital of Chiriquí province."],"q":{"q":"What is Panama's highest peak, rising north of David?","a":"Volcán Barú","x":["Cerro Chirripó","Cerro Pirre"]}},"Q166304":{"t":"Montana's biggest city, under the Rimrocks","s":"Along the northern edge of Billings runs a long line of pale sandstone cliffs, the Rimrocks, and below them the city fills the valley of the Yellowstone River. It is the largest city in Montana, a railway and ranching hub for a huge stretch of the northern plains. On the south-western horizon, the snowy Beartooth Mountains stand guard.","w":"A city in a wide river valley below long, pale cliffs; dry plains around, snowy mountains to the south-west.","f":["At Pompeys Pillar, about 40 km east, William Clark carved his name into the rock in 1806.","Billings is the most populous city in Montana.","The Yellowstone, which flows through the city, is one of the longest undammed rivers in the lower 48 states."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Billings?","a":"The Yellowstone","x":["The Missouri","The Clark Fork"]}},"Q985521":{"t":"A deep harbour among jagged desert hills","s":"On the desert coast of Sonora, the Gulf of California slips into a many-armed bay ringed by bare, jagged hills, and Guaymas wraps around it. It is one of the best natural harbours on the Gulf, and Mexicans know it above all for its big shrimp. Just to the north-west, the twin horns of Tetakawi mountain rise over the resort of San Carlos.","w":"A port around a rocky, many-armed bay on the desert coast, with bare jagged hills behind.","f":["Guaymas shrimp are famous across Mexico for their size.","The twin-peaked Tetakawi mountain stands above the neighbouring resort of San Carlos.","Hermosillo, the state capital, lies about 130 km to the north."],"q":{"q":"Which seafood is Guaymas famous for?","a":"Shrimp","x":["Lobster","Oysters"]}},"Q271215":{"t":"Capital of the Pantanal, on the Bolivian border","s":"Corumbá stands on a low limestone bluff above the Paraguay River, and around it stretch the endless flatlands of the Pantanal, the world's largest tropical wetland. In the wet season much of it lies under water. South of the city, a dark mountain block, the Urucum massif, rises from the plain. The Bolivian border is just to the west.","w":"A town on a bluff above a winding river amid immense flat wetlands; a dark mountain block rising from the plain to the south.","f":["Corumbá is often called the capital of the Pantanal.","The Urucum massif south of the city holds large deposits of iron and manganese.","The town was founded in 1778 as a Portuguese outpost on the Paraguay River."],"q":{"q":"Which vast wetland is Corumbá the gateway to?","a":"The Pantanal","x":["The Llanos de Moxos","The Iberá Wetlands"]}},"Q47772":{"t":"Alpine chocolate town on a deep-blue Andean lake","s":"A huge, many-armed lake of deep blue, forested mountains all around and snowy peaks on the Chilean border: that is Nahuel Huapi, and Bariloche sits on its southern shore. The town looks almost Alpine, with stone and timber buildings, ski slopes on Cerro Catedral and a main street full of chocolate shops. It is the gateway to Argentina's oldest national park.","w":"A town on the southern shore of a large, branching deep-blue lake among forested mountains; snowy peaks to the west.","f":["Nahuel Huapi National Park, created in 1934, is the oldest national park in Argentina.","Monte Tronador, an extinct volcano on the Chilean border, carries several glaciers.","Nahuel Huapi lake covers more than 500 km² and is over 400 m deep."],"q":{"q":"On which lake does Bariloche stand?","a":"Nahuel Huapi","x":["Lago Argentino","Lago General Carrera"]}},"Q31058":{"t":"The small big city on Lake Champlain","s":"Lake Champlain runs long and narrow between two ranges: the Adirondacks of New York on the west, Vermont's Green Mountains on the east. Burlington sits on the Vermont shore, looking across the water to the sunset. It is the largest city in Vermont, yet small enough that it's said to be the smallest largest city of any US state. Ben & Jerry's began here.","w":"A city on the eastern shore of a long, narrow lake; the Adirondacks across the water to the west, the Green Mountains to the east.","f":["Ben & Jerry's opened its first ice-cream shop in a converted petrol station here in 1978.","Lake Champlain stretches about 190 km from New York State into Quebec.","The University of Vermont was founded in Burlington in 1791."],"q":{"q":"Which ice-cream company started in Burlington in 1978?","a":"Ben & Jerry's","x":["Häagen-Dazs","Baskin-Robbins"]}},"Q30620":{"t":"Where Columbus dropped anchor in Costa Rica","s":"On his fourth and last voyage, in 1502, Christopher Columbus anchored off a small island here on Costa Rica's Caribbean coast. Limón grew into the country's main port on this side. In the 1870s workers from Jamaica came to build the railway and stayed, giving the city its Caribbean flavour: rice and beans cooked in coconut milk, calypso and English Creole.","w":"A small port on a green Caribbean coast, with a little island just offshore and banana plantations inland.","f":["Columbus anchored off Uvita Island, just off the city, in September 1502.","Limón is Costa Rica's main port for banana exports.","North of the city, the Tortuguero canals lead to one of the Caribbean's key nesting beaches for green turtles."],"q":{"q":"Who anchored off Limón in 1502?","a":"Christopher Columbus","x":["Vasco Núñez de Balboa","Hernán Cortés"]}},"Q34986":{"t":"A mission town on the flooding plains of Moxos","s":"In north-eastern Bolivia the land is flat to the horizon: grassy savannas, islands of forest and winding rivers, which in the rainy season flood into a vast shallow sheet of water. These are the Llanos de Moxos, and Trinidad is their capital. It began in 1686 as a Jesuit mission. Long before that, local peoples built raised fields and causeways across these plains.","w":"A town on flat savanna with islands of forest, lakes and winding rivers; in the wet season, sheets of floodwater.","f":["Trinidad was founded in 1686 as the Jesuit mission of La Santísima Trinidad.","Thousands of ancient mounds, canals and raised fields dot the Llanos de Moxos.","Trinidad is the capital of the Beni department."],"q":{"q":"Trinidad is the capital of which Bolivian department?","a":"Beni","x":["Pando","Santa Cruz"]}},"Q986634":{"t":"The locks between Lake Superior and Lake Huron","s":"Lake Superior drains into Lake Huron through the short St. Marys River, and at its rapids stand twin cities: Sault Ste. Marie, Michigan, and its larger namesake in Ontario. Ships bypass the rapids through the Soo Locks, which lift them about 6 m between the lakes. French missionaries founded the Michigan side in 1668, making it the oldest city in the state.","w":"Twin towns on a short river between two great lakes; the locks and rapids side by side, forest all around.","f":["Sault Ste. Marie is the oldest city in Michigan, founded in 1668.","The first Soo Locks opened in 1855.","The International Bridge links the city with Sault Ste. Marie, Ontario."],"q":{"q":"Which two lakes does the St. Marys River connect?","a":"Superior and Huron","x":["Superior and Michigan","Huron and Erie"]}},"Q189602":{"t":"A town that once lay under an ice-age lake","s":"Look at the bare hills above Missoula: faint horizontal lines run across them like bathtub rings. They are old shorelines of glacial Lake Missoula, which filled these valleys at the end of the ice age and several times burst out in colossal floods. Today five valleys meet at the town, and the Clark Fork River flows through it.","w":"A town where several mountain valleys meet, a river flowing west; bare grassy hills around with faint horizontal lines.","f":["At its deepest, the ice-age lake stood a few hundred metres above today's town.","Norman Maclean's novella A River Runs Through It is set in and around Missoula.","The University of Montana has its main campus here."],"q":{"q":"What drew the horizontal lines on Missoula's hills?","a":"An ice-age lake's shorelines","x":["Old mining roads","Terraced farming"]}},"Q466190":{"t":"The falls that stalled Lewis and Clark","s":"Here the Missouri River drops in a series of five waterfalls through a canyon cut into the plains. In 1805 Lewis and Clark had to haul their boats and supplies around them overland, a gruelling task that took nearly a month. Today dams harness the falls, earning Great Falls the nickname Electric City. Wheat fields stretch to the mountains in the west.","w":"A city on the Missouri where the river steps down through a shallow canyon; wheat fields on the plains, mountains to the west.","f":["Giant Springs, on the edge of the city, is one of the largest freshwater springs in the US.","Western artist Charles M. Russell lived and painted in Great Falls.","Lewis and Clark's portage around the falls covered about 30 km."],"q":{"q":"Which explorers portaged around the Great Falls in 1805?","a":"Lewis and Clark","x":["Zebulon Pike","John C. Frémont"]}},"Q79842":{"t":"Pluto's discovery, pines and a dark night sky","s":"Flagstaff sits at about 2,100 m in a vast, dark-green forest of ponderosa pines, with the old volcanic San Francisco Peaks rising just north. In 1930, at Lowell Observatory on a hill above town, Clyde Tombaugh discovered Pluto. The city still guards its dark skies. East of town, look for a round pale scar in the desert: Meteor Crater.","w":"A town in a huge dark-green pine forest below snowy volcanic peaks; a round crater in the desert about 60 km east.","f":["In 2001 Flagstaff became the world's first International Dark Sky City.","Humphreys Peak, at 3,851 m the highest point in Arizona, rises in the San Francisco Peaks.","Meteor Crater is about 1.2 km across and 170 m deep."],"q":{"q":"Which dwarf planet was discovered in Flagstaff?","a":"Pluto","x":["Ceres","Eris"]}},"Q223177":{"t":"Where the wagon trails crossed the North Platte","s":"In the mid-1800s, tens of thousands of emigrants bound for Oregon, California and Utah crossed the North Platte River here, some on ferries and later on a bridge. Casper grew later, first on the railway and then on oil. It sits on the river among wide, dry sagebrush plains, with the dark, forested ridge of Casper Mountain rising right to the south.","w":"A city on a river in wide, dry plains, with a dark forested ridge just to the south.","f":["The Oregon, Mormon and California trails all crossed the North Platte near Casper.","Oil from the Salt Creek field, north of the city, made Casper a boomtown in the early 1900s.","Casper Mountain rises about 900 m above the city."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Casper, Wyoming?","a":"The North Platte","x":["The Yellowstone","The Green"]}},"Q372920":{"t":"Granite domes and a river of rapids","s":"Here the mighty Orinoco breaks into white rapids among black rocks, and huge bare domes of granite rise out of the green forest and savanna. Puerto Ayacucho, the capital of Venezuela's Amazonas state, sits beside the Atures Rapids, where boats cannot pass. Colombia lies directly across the river. Alexander von Humboldt came this way in 1800.","w":"A town beside a wide river broken by rocky rapids; bare granite domes rising from green forest and savanna.","f":["A road runs past the Atures Rapids so that river cargo can continue upstream.","Cerro Pintado, near the city, bears some of the largest known petroglyphs, including a giant serpent.","Humboldt and Bonpland travelled the Orinoco past these rapids in 1800."],"q":{"q":"Which river rushes over rapids at Puerto Ayacucho?","a":"The Orinoco","x":["The Amazon","The Meta"]}},"Q216258":{"t":"A rainy bay town beneath two giant volcanoes","s":"Hilo curves around a bay on the wet, green eastern side of Hawaii's Big Island, and behind it rise two giants: Mauna Kea and Mauna Loa, their slopes so gentle they hardly look like mountains. Clouds pile up against them, so Hilo is one of the rainiest cities in the United States. In winter, Mauna Kea's summit can wear snow.","w":"A town on a crescent bay on the island's green east coast; two huge, gently sloping volcanoes behind.","f":["Hilo gets more than 3 m of rain in an average year.","Measured from its base on the sea floor, Mauna Kea is over 10,000 m tall — more than Everest.","A lava flow from Mauna Loa in 1880–81 stopped just short of the town."],"q":{"q":"Which volcano above Hilo is taller than Everest from base to summit?","a":"Mauna Kea","x":["Kīlauea","Haleakalā"]}},"Q912253":{"t":"Gateway to the north on the voyageurs' lake","s":"North Bay sits on the shore of Lake Nipissing, a big lake in the endless forests of northern Ontario. For two centuries fur traders' canoes crossed this lake on the water route from Montreal to the upper Great Lakes, paddling on down the French River to Georgian Bay. The railways later made the town the 'Gateway to the North'.","w":"A town on the north-east shore of a large lake, among endless forest and countless smaller lakes.","f":["The French River carries Lake Nipissing's water west to Georgian Bay.","The Dionne quintuplets, the first quintuplets known to survive infancy, were born near North Bay in 1934.","Algonquin Provincial Park lies to the south-east."],"q":{"q":"Which lake lies on the edge of North Bay?","a":"Lake Nipissing","x":["Lake Simcoe","Lake Nipigon"]}},"Q41474":{"t":"The island where Captain Bligh's breadfruit landed","s":"Kingstown curls around a bay on the green, mountainous island of St Vincent, and to the south the little Grenadines trail away across the sea. Its botanical garden, founded in 1765, is the oldest in the Western Hemisphere. In 1793 Captain Bligh delivered breadfruit trees from Tahiti here. In the north of the island rises the La Soufrière volcano.","w":"A small town around a bay on a green, mountainous island; a chain of small islands stretching away to the south.","f":["The St Vincent Botanical Gardens, founded in 1765, are the oldest in the Western Hemisphere.","A breadfruit tree grown from one of Bligh's 1793 plants still stands in the gardens.","La Soufrière, about 1,230 m, is the highest point on St Vincent."],"q":{"q":"Which tree did Captain Bligh bring to St Vincent in 1793?","a":"Breadfruit","x":["Nutmeg","Cacao"]}},"Q3810":{"t":"A Patagonian town around a five-sided square","s":"Coyhaique lies in a green valley of Chilean Patagonia, surrounded by mountains, forests and rivers, and it is the capital of the wild Aysén region. Founded only in 1929, it was laid out around a square with five sides, from which streets radiate like a star. The Carretera Austral, the long road through Chile's far south, passes here.","w":"A small town in a green valley where rivers meet, ringed by forested and bare mountains; snowy peaks around.","f":["Coyhaique was founded in 1929.","Its main square is a pentagon, with streets fanning out from it in all directions.","The jagged Cerro Castillo, shaped like a castle, rises to the south."],"q":{"q":"What is unusual about Coyhaique's main square?","a":"It has five sides","x":["It is perfectly round","It sits on an island"]}},"Q488702":{"t":"A foggy redwood town on a hidden bay","s":"Behind a pair of long, thin sand spits on California's far north coast hides Humboldt Bay, and Eureka sits on its shore. The hills around are dark with forests of coast redwoods, the tallest trees on Earth, and the town grew rich milling their timber. Fog rolls in off the cold Pacific much of the summer.","w":"A town on a long, narrow bay behind thin sand spits; dark forested hills inland, often a blanket of coastal fog.","f":["Humboldt Bay is California's second-largest natural bay, after San Francisco Bay.","Eureka is Greek for 'I have found it!' — also California's state motto.","The town is known for its ornate wooden Victorian houses, built with local redwood."],"q":{"q":"What does 'Eureka' mean?","a":"I have found it","x":["Land of gold","Safe harbour"]}},"Q433574":{"t":"A town named for the forest it replaced","s":"Alta Floresta means 'tall forest', and when the town was founded in 1976, that's exactly what surrounded it. It was a planned settlement in the far north of Mato Grosso, carved out of the southern Amazon for farmers from the south. Today pastures and fields spread around it, but to the north unbroken rainforest still stretches toward the Teles Pires River.","w":"A town in a patchwork of pastures cut out of the rainforest; unbroken dark-green forest further north.","f":["Alta Floresta was founded in 1976 as a private colonisation project.","Its name means 'tall forest' in Portuguese.","The forests along the Cristalino River, to the north, are famous among birdwatchers."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Alta Floresta mean?","a":"Tall forest","x":["Green river","Red earth"]}},"Q487201":{"t":"A prairie town with a Scandinavian heart","s":"Minot sits in a broad valley that the Souris River has cut into the flat prairie of North Dakota. It sprang up almost overnight in 1886, when the Great Northern Railway arrived, and was quickly dubbed the Magic City. Many of the farmers around descend from Norwegian settlers, and each autumn the city throws a huge Scandinavian festival.","w":"A town in a broad valley cut into flat prairie, a river looping through it; square farm fields all around.","f":["Minot's Norsk Høstfest is billed as the largest Scandinavian festival in North America.","The Souris River rises in Canada, loops through North Dakota and flows back into Canada.","The city's Scandinavian Heritage Park has a full-size copy of a Norwegian stave church."],"q":{"q":"Which heritage does Minot's big autumn festival celebrate?","a":"Scandinavian","x":["German","Irish"]}},"Q41699":{"t":"A tiny island capital with two Nobel laureates","s":"Castries lies at the head of a deep, sheltered harbour on the north-west coast of St Lucia, a green, steep volcanic island. Look far to the south-west for two sharp spires rising from the sea: the Pitons. This small place has produced two Nobel laureates, poet Derek Walcott and economist Arthur Lewis, both born here.","w":"A harbour town in a deep inlet on a green, mountainous island; two sharp volcanic spires on the coast far to the south-west.","f":["Derek Walcott, born in Castries in 1930, won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1992.","Sir Arthur Lewis, also born in Castries, won the Nobel Prize in Economics in 1979.","The Pitons, two volcanic spires, have been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 2004."],"q":{"q":"Which Nobel-winning poet was born in Castries?","a":"Derek Walcott","x":["V. S. Naipaul","Aimé Césaire"]}},"Q932261":{"t":"Cape Breton's steel town, far from its namesake","s":"Not the one in Australia: this Sydney sits on a branching harbour on the Atlantic coast of Cape Breton Island. For a century it was a steel town, fed by coal from mines that ran far out under the sea. The mills are gone and the land has been cleaned and turned into parks. Across the harbour, ferries leave for Newfoundland.","w":"A town on a branching harbour on the east coast of Cape Breton Island; dark forest and the open Atlantic beyond.","f":["Sydney was founded in 1785 as the capital of the separate colony of Cape Breton.","Its steelworks ran for about a century, until 2001.","Ferries to Newfoundland sail from North Sydney, across the harbour."],"q":{"q":"On which island is Sydney, Nova Scotia?","a":"Cape Breton Island","x":["Prince Edward Island","Newfoundland"]}},"Q36262":{"t":"An island with a beach for every day of the year","s":"Antigua is a low, scalloped island ringed by coves, reefs and white sand, and it likes to say it has 365 beaches, one for every day of the year. St John's, the capital, sits on a deep bay on the north-west coast. On the south coast, English Harbour sheltered the Royal Navy in the age of sail, and its restored dockyard still stands.","w":"A harbour town on the north-west coast of a low island with a heavily indented shoreline, white beaches and pale reefs.","f":["Nelson's Dockyard at English Harbour has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 2016.","Cricket great Viv Richards was born in St John's in 1952.","Barbuda, the country's second island, lies about 40 km to the north."],"q":{"q":"How many beaches is Antigua said to have?","a":"365","x":["100","1,000"]}},"Q991310":{"t":"A gold-rush town in the northern forest","s":"In 1909 prospectors found gold in the forests of northern Ontario, and the Porcupine gold rush began. Timmins grew out of it and has been mining ever since. From above it is a town surrounded by endless boreal forest and lakes, with pale scars of tailings and pits around it. Singer Shania Twain grew up here.","w":"A town amid endless dark forest and lakes, with pale mine tailings and open pits nearby; a river running north.","f":["The Porcupine gold rush of 1909 gave birth to the town.","The Kidd Creek mine north of the city goes down nearly 3 km, one of the deepest base-metal mines on Earth.","The Mattagami River, which flows through the city, runs north toward James Bay."],"q":{"q":"Which rush founded Timmins in 1909?","a":"A gold rush","x":["A silver rush","A copper rush"]}},"Q855645":{"t":"A peach of a town in the heart of Uruguay","s":"Durazno means 'peach', and this quiet town sits almost in the geographic centre of Uruguay, on the tree-lined Yí River. All around it roll the green grasslands of cattle country, with hardly a hill in sight. It was founded in 1821 under Fructuoso Rivera, who later became Uruguay's first president.","w":"A small town on a winding, tree-lined river amid rolling green grassland; cattle country in every direction.","f":["Durazno means 'peach' in Spanish.","The town was founded in 1821 on the orders of Fructuoso Rivera.","The Yí River flows west into the Río Negro, the river that crosses the whole of Uruguay."],"q":{"q":"What does 'Durazno' mean in Spanish?","a":"Peach","x":["Apricot","Plum"]}},"Q41547":{"t":"A horseshoe harbour on the Spice Isle","s":"St George's wraps around a small horseshoe-shaped harbour on the south-west tip of Grenada, its red-roofed houses climbing the green hills. It is often called one of the prettiest harbours in the Caribbean. Grenada is the Spice Isle: nutmeg grows so well here that it appears on the national flag. Just north of town lies the world's first underwater sculpture park.","w":"A small horseshoe harbour on the south-west tip of a green, mountainous island; red roofs climbing the hills.","f":["Grenada's flag bears a nutmeg, the island's most famous spice.","The world's first underwater sculpture park opened in Molinere Bay, near the capital, in 2006.","Fort George, begun in 1705, guards the entrance to the harbour."],"q":{"q":"Which spice appears on Grenada's flag?","a":"Nutmeg","x":["Cinnamon","Clove"]}},"Q1376242":{"t":"A Maroon river village on the French Guiana border","s":"Deep in the rainforest of south-eastern Suriname, the village of Cottica sits on the Lawa, the river that forms the border with French Guiana. Its story is tied to the Aluku, a Maroon people descended from Africans who escaped plantation slavery in the 18th century and built free villages along these rivers. Around it, the forest stretches unbroken for hundreds of kilometres.","w":"A dark river winding through unbroken green rainforest, with small clearings along its banks.","f":["The Lawa River, on which Cottica stands, forms part of the border between Suriname and French Guiana.","Downstream, the Lawa joins the Tapanahony to form the Marowijne, or Maroni, River.","Most travel in this region is by boat or small plane rather than by road."],"q":{"q":"Which territory lies across the Lawa River from Cottica?","a":"French Guiana","x":["Guyana","Brazil"]}},"Q61432":{"t":"The Wheat City in a prairie valley","s":"Brandon grew in 1881, when the Canadian Pacific Railway reached the Assiniboine River here on its way west. It sits in the river's shallow valley, surrounded by prairie farmland cut into enormous squares of wheat and canola. Its nickname, the Wheat City, says it all. To the north the land rises to the forested plateau of Riding Mountain.","w":"A town in a shallow river valley amid flat, square-cut prairie farmland; a forested upland to the north.","f":["Brandon is Manitoba's second-largest city, after Winnipeg.","The Assiniboine River flows east through the city toward Winnipeg.","Riding Mountain National Park lies about 100 km to the north."],"q":{"q":"What is Brandon's nickname?","a":"The Wheat City","x":["The Steel City","The Gateway City"]}},"Q1805608":{"t":"A frontier town named after the company that built it","s":"Colíder grew up along the BR-163, the long highway pushed north from Cuiabá toward the Amazon in the 1970s. A land company called Colonizadora Líder divided the forest into plots for settlers, and its name gave the town its own. Around it, fields and pastures now cut into the southern edge of the Amazon rainforest.","w":"A small town on a straight highway through a patchwork of pastures and rainforest remnants; a large river to the north.","f":["The name joins the words 'Colonizadora Líder', the company that settled the area.","The settlement dates from 1974 and became a municipality in 1979.","A hydroelectric plant on the Teles Pires River bears the town's name."],"q":{"q":"Where does the name Colíder come from?","a":"A land-settlement company","x":["A Tupi word for river","A pioneer family"]}},"Q44208":{"t":"Founded by Welsh settlers in Patagonia","s":"In 1865 about 150 settlers from Wales landed on this barren Patagonian coast, hoping to build a community where their language could live on. That same year they founded Rawson near the mouth of the Chubut River. Today it is the small capital of Chubut province, and Welsh tea houses still serve cakes in the green valley upriver.","w":"A small town near a river mouth on the Atlantic coast; a green irrigated valley winding inland through pale steppe.","f":["The Welsh settlers arrived on the ship Mimosa in July 1865.","The town is named after Guillermo Rawson, the Argentine minister who backed the Welsh colony.","Black-and-white Commerson's dolphins are often seen near the port at Playa Unión."],"q":{"q":"Who founded Rawson in 1865?","a":"Welsh settlers","x":["Italian settlers","Basque settlers"]}},"Q867573":{"t":"A planned port on a flat, pine-covered island","s":"Grand Bahama is long, low and green with pines, and on its south coast lies Freeport, a city that was planned rather than grown. In 1955 an agreement with the government created a free-trade zone here, and a deep harbour and resort town followed. North of the island, the sea turns pale turquoise over the shallow Little Bahama Bank.","w":"A planned grid and harbour on a long, flat, pine-covered island; turquoise shallows to the north, deep blue to the south.","f":["Freeport was created under the Hawksbill Creek Agreement of 1955.","Lucayan National Park, east of the city, protects one of the longest known underwater cave systems.","Florida's coast lies only about 90 km west of Grand Bahama's western tip."],"q":{"q":"In which year was the agreement that created Freeport signed?","a":"1955","x":["1905","1973"]}},"Q28206":{"t":"A Pilgrim trading post turned state capital","s":"Maine's small capital lies on the Kennebec River, amid thick forest dotted with lakes. Its story goes back to 1628, when the Pilgrims of Plymouth Colony set up a trading post here to buy furs. A wooden fort followed in 1754 and still stands. The State House was designed by Charles Bulfinch, who also finished the US Capitol.","w":"A small city on a river winding south through dense forest dotted with lakes toward the Atlantic coast.","f":["The Plymouth Colony's Cushnoc trading post stood here from 1628.","Fort Western, built in 1754, is one of the oldest wooden forts in the United States.","Augusta is one of the smallest state capitals in the US by population."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Augusta, Maine?","a":"The Kennebec","x":["The Penobscot","The Androscoggin"]}},"Q36281":{"t":"Capital of the Nature Island, where a lake boils","s":"Dominica rises steeply from the sea, covered in rainforest and cut by countless rivers, and Roseau sits on its sheltered west coast at the mouth of one of them. The island calls itself the Nature Island. In the cloudy mountains east of town lies the Boiling Lake, a flooded volcanic vent that bubbles and steams, one of the largest hot lakes on Earth.","w":"A small town on the west coast of a steep, densely forested volcanic island; mountains in cloud behind.","f":["Morne Trois Pitons National Park, home of the Boiling Lake, has been a UNESCO site since 1997.","Roseau is French for 'reed', after the reeds that grew at the river mouth.","Morne Diablotins, about 1,450 m, is the island's highest peak."],"q":{"q":"What is Dominica's nickname?","a":"The Nature Island","x":["The Spice Isle","The Helen of the West Indies"]}},"Q41295":{"t":"An island capital facing Hamilton's birthplace","s":"St Kitts is shaped a bit like a cricket bat: a round volcanic body in the north and a thin handle pointing south-east toward the cone of Nevis. Basseterre lies on the south-west coast where the two meet. The French founded it in 1627. Just across a narrow strait is Nevis, the island where Alexander Hamilton was born.","w":"A town on the south coast of a green island with a volcano in its north; a thin peninsula pointing toward round Nevis.","f":["Brimstone Hill Fortress, on St Kitts' west coast, has been a UNESCO site since 1999.","The strait between St Kitts and Nevis, called the Narrows, is about 3 km wide.","Mount Liamuiga, about 1,156 m, is the highest point of St Kitts."],"q":{"q":"Which US founding father was born on Nevis, near Basseterre?","a":"Alexander Hamilton","x":["Benjamin Franklin","John Jay"]}},"Q126624":{"t":"Boomtown on the oil sands, deep in the forest","s":"In the boreal forest of northern Alberta, where the Clearwater River joins the Athabasca, lies Fort McMurray. North of town the forest gives way to something astonishing from the air: vast open-pit mines, dark scraped earth and pale tailings ponds. They are digging the Athabasca oil sands, one of the largest oil deposits on Earth.","w":"A town at a river confluence in dense forest; to the north, huge dark open pits and pale ponds cut into the woods.","f":["The Athabasca oil sands hold one of the largest oil reserves in the world.","Fur traders reached the Clearwater from Hudson Bay waters over the Methye Portage.","The Athabasca River flows north from here to Lake Athabasca."],"q":{"q":"What resource drives the economy around Fort McMurray?","a":"Oil sands","x":["Gold","Potash"]}},"Q945084":{"t":"Cowboy poets under Nevada's Alps","s":"Elko is a small railway town on the Humboldt River, in a wide valley of silvery sagebrush. To the south-east rise the Ruby Mountains, their glacier-carved peaks often white with snow — people call them Nevada's Alps. Every winter the town hosts the National Cowboy Poetry Gathering, and its Basque restaurants recall the shepherds who settled here.","w":"A small town on a river in a wide sagebrush valley; snowy, glacier-carved mountains to the south-east.","f":["The National Cowboy Poetry Gathering has been held in Elko since 1985.","Basque shepherds who settled here gave Elko its Basque boarding houses and festival.","The Carlin Trend gold mines nearby make this one of the richest gold districts in the world."],"q":{"q":"Which event has Elko hosted every winter since 1985?","a":"A cowboy poetry gathering","x":["A chili cook-off","An ice sculpture festival"]}},"Q23021":{"t":"An old fur post in the Peace River country","s":"Fort St. John sits on a plateau above the deep valley of the Peace River, in north-eastern British Columbia, east of the Rockies. Around it, square farm fields cut into the forest: the Peace River country is the province's main grain region. The town traces its origin to a fur-trading post of 1794, often called the oldest European settlement in mainland BC.","w":"A town on a plateau above a deep river valley; square farm fields cut into forest, the Rockies far to the west.","f":["The Alaska Highway runs through the city on its way north-west from Dawson Creek.","Rocky Mountain Fort, the town's forerunner, was built by the North West Company in 1794.","The Peace River flows east from here into Alberta and on toward the Arctic."],"q":{"q":"Which famous highway runs through Fort St. John?","a":"The Alaska Highway","x":["The Trans-Canada Highway","The Dempster Highway"]}},"Q1022":{"t":"A city poured into a bowl of vineyards","s":"Stuttgart looks poured into a bowl. Its centre fills a steep hollow that locals call the Kessel, 'the cauldron', while the suburbs spill over the wooded rim and down to the Neckar. Look at the sunny slopes: those neat striped terraces are vineyards, many of them inside the city limits. Wine has been made here for centuries.","w":"A dense centre in a wooded hollow, the Neckar curving past to the east, striped vineyards on the slopes.","f":["Both Mercedes-Benz and Porsche have their headquarters in Stuttgart.","In 1885 Gottlieb Daimler's garden workshop in Cannstatt, now part of Stuttgart, produced one of the first motorcycles.","The mineral springs of Bad Cannstatt are among the most abundant in Europe."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Stuttgart?","a":"The Neckar","x":["The Main","The Moselle"]}},"Q588":{"t":"Where a dozen towns have grown into one","s":"Here the towns have run out of gaps between them. Katowice is the hub of the Upper Silesian conurbation, where more than a dozen cities merge into one mesh of housing estates, rail yards and old pitheads. Small ponds dot the patchwork: many fill hollows where the ground sank over worked-out coal seams. To the south, green Beskid ridges close the view.","w":"A sprawling grey-green urban mesh with scattered ponds and rail lines; wooded Beskid hills rise to the south.","f":["Katowice received town rights only in 1865, as coal was turning Upper Silesia into an industrial powerhouse.","The Vistula, Poland's longest river, rises on Barania Góra in the Silesian Beskids, about 70 km to the south.","Silesian Park in neighbouring Chorzów is one of the largest city parks in Europe."],"q":{"q":"Which historical region is Katowice part of?","a":"Upper Silesia","x":["Lesser Poland","Pomerania"]}},"Q2119":{"t":"A city laid out like a chessboard","s":"Mannheim's centre is a horseshoe of square blocks, and its streets have no names: addresses read like squares on a chessboard, such as Q6 or N1. The grid fills the angle where the Neckar flows into the Rhine. Across the Rhine, Ludwigshafen's enormous chemical works stretch along the bank, and on a clear night the two cities glow as one.","w":"A rounded grid of square blocks in the angle between the Rhine and the Neckar, with a vast chemical works across the Rhine.","f":["In 1817 Karl Drais rode his two-wheeled 'running machine', an ancestor of the bicycle, out of Mannheim.","In 1888 Bertha Benz drove her husband's motor car from Mannheim to Pforzheim — the first long-distance car journey.","The BASF site in Ludwigshafen, across the Rhine, is one of the largest chemical complexes in the world."],"q":{"q":"What did Karl Drais ride out of Mannheim in 1817?","a":"A two-wheeled running machine","x":["A steam-powered carriage","A pedal tricycle"]}},"Q2256":{"t":"Britain's canal city, far from any big river","s":"No great river runs through Birmingham, so in the 18th century the city dug its own waterways. Canals still thread between the red-brick districts, and locals like to claim the city has more of them than Venice. From cruise height, look instead for a knot of motorways on the north-eastern side: the tangle that earned the nickname Spaghetti Junction.","w":"A huge brick-red and green urban spread in the English Midlands; a knot of motorway loops on its north-eastern side.","f":["The Gravelly Hill Interchange, better known as Spaghetti Junction, opened in 1972.","In the 18th century Matthew Boulton and James Watt built steam engines at their Soho works in Birmingham.","Birmingham is the most populous British city after London."],"q":{"q":"What is Birmingham's Gravelly Hill Interchange better known as?","a":"Spaghetti Junction","x":["Pretzel Junction","The Magic Roundabout"]}},"Q18125":{"t":"The first industrial city, at the foot of the Pennines","s":"The Pennine hills rise just east of Manchester, and their damp air and fast streams helped cotton turn it into the world's first industrial city. Look west for a dark ribbon heading towards the Mersey estuary: the Ship Canal. It was dug in the 1880s and 1890s so that ocean-going ships could sail right up to the city without stopping at Liverpool.","w":"A broad urban spread with the Pennine moors rising to the east; the Ship Canal runs west towards the Mersey estuary.","f":["The Manchester Ship Canal, opened in 1894, is about 58 km long.","The Liverpool and Manchester Railway, opened in 1830, was the world's first inter-city passenger railway.","In 1948 the Manchester 'Baby' became the first computer to run a program stored in its own memory."],"q":{"q":"Which waterway lets ships sail from the Mersey estuary to Manchester?","a":"The Manchester Ship Canal","x":["The Grand Union Canal","The Caledonian Canal"]}},"Q2066":{"t":"Coal country that has turned green","s":"This is where the Ruhr's chain of cities is at its densest, and yet much of what you see is green. Coal and steel built Essen; the pits have closed, leaving parks, woods and grassed-over spoil heaps. South of the centre, the river Ruhr widens into a long reservoir, the Baldeneysee, shining among wooded hills.","w":"A dense, green-flecked city in the Ruhr chain, with a long lake winding through wooded hills to the south.","f":["The Zollverein colliery, once one of Europe's largest coal mines, has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 2001.","Essen was named European Green Capital for 2017.","The Golden Madonna in Essen Cathedral, made around 980, is the oldest known fully sculpted figure of the Virgin Mary."],"q":{"q":"Which title did Essen hold in 2017?","a":"European Green Capital","x":["European Capital of Culture","European Youth Capital"]}},"Q495":{"t":"An Alpine wall behind a Roman grid","s":"Snowy Alps wrap around Turin in a great arc, often in plain sight from its streets. The city follows the Po, and its centre still keeps the rigid street grid of a Roman camp. Across the river rises a line of wooded hills. For four years, from 1861 to 1865, this was the capital of newly united Italy.","w":"A large, regular city on the Po, with the Alps curving round to the west and north and green hills across the river.","f":["Monviso (3,841 m), where the Po rises, stands about 65 km south-west of the city.","Fiat was founded in Turin in 1899.","Turin hosted the Winter Olympic Games in 2006."],"q":{"q":"Which car maker was founded in Turin in 1899?","a":"Fiat","x":["Alfa Romeo","Ferrari"]}},"Q39121":{"t":"A wool town grown big in a Pennine valley","s":"Wool made Leeds rich: fleece from the Yorkshire hills was spun and woven in mills along the River Aire. The river and a canal still run side by side through the city, then head north-west into the Pennines. North of the centre, the land rolls up towards the Yorkshire Dales in green waves split by dry-stone walls.","w":"A large city in the Aire valley, with the river and canal running north-west towards the moors of the Pennines.","f":["The Leeds and Liverpool Canal, about 204 km long, is the longest single canal in Britain.","Marks & Spencer began in 1884 as a penny stall in Leeds Kirkgate Market.","Louis Le Prince's Roundhay Garden Scene, filmed in Leeds in 1888, is the oldest surviving motion picture."],"q":{"q":"Which retailer began as a market stall in Leeds in 1884?","a":"Marks & Spencer","x":["Tesco","Sainsbury's"]}},"Q2044":{"t":"A red-roofed bowl on the Arno","s":"Hills close in on three sides, and in the bowl between them lies a tight mass of terracotta roofs cut by the Arno. That is the Florence of the Renaissance, a centre small enough to cross on foot in half an hour. Around it Tuscany unrolls in olive groves, lines of cypress and vineyards; to the south lie the hills of Chianti.","w":"A compact reddish-brown city in a valley ringed by hills, with the Arno crossing it from east to west.","f":["Brunelleschi's cathedral dome, completed in 1436, is still the largest brick dome ever built.","The gold florin, first struck here in 1252, became a standard coin of trade across medieval Europe.","The historic centre of Florence has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1982."],"q":{"q":"Which gold coin was first minted in Florence in 1252?","a":"The florin","x":["The ducat","The guilder"]}},"Q42308":{"t":"A university city on the edge of the steppe","s":"Three small rivers, the Kharkiv, the Lopan and the Udy, meet within the city, and Kharkiv spreads out from their valleys across a plateau of black earth. Broad avenues and big green parks read clearly from the air. The city grew around a 17th-century fortress and became a centre of learning when its university opened in 1805.","w":"A large city on a flat plateau cut by shallow river valleys, surrounded by dark fields and patches of forest.","f":["Kharkiv University, founded in 1805, is one of the oldest universities in the country.","Freedom Square, about 12 hectares, is one of the largest city squares in Europe.","The Derzhprom building, finished in 1928, was one of the first Soviet skyscrapers."],"q":{"q":"In which year did Kharkiv University open?","a":"1805","x":["1755","1834"]}},"Q36600":{"t":"The Dutch seat of power that isn't the capital","s":"Parliament, the government and the king's working palace are all here, yet The Hague is not the Dutch capital; that title belongs to Amsterdam. The city runs straight into a line of pale dunes and the North Sea at Scheveningen. To the south-west, look for acres of glinting glass: the greenhouses of the Westland, one of the most intensively farmed corners of Europe.","w":"A city pressed against pale coastal dunes, with the shining glasshouses of the Westland spreading to the south-west.","f":["The Peace Palace, opened in 1913, houses the International Court of Justice.","Scheveningen, the city's seaside district, grew from a fishing village and has a pier over the North Sea.","The city's formal Dutch name, 's-Gravenhage, means 'the Count's hedge'."],"q":{"q":"Which UN court sits in The Hague?","a":"The International Court of Justice","x":["The European Court of Human Rights","The European Court of Justice"]}},"Q23482":{"t":"France's oldest city, framed by white cliffs","s":"Blinding white limestone hems Marseille in: bare hills behind, and to the south-east the Calanques, narrow fjord-like inlets cut into cliffs above turquoise water. Offshore lie the pale, treeless Frioul islands. Greek sailors from Phocaea founded a trading post here around 600 BC, which is why Marseille is called the oldest city in France.","w":"A large pale city around a bay, with white limestone hills behind, bare islands offshore and cliffs to the south-east.","f":["The Calanques became a national park in 2012, reaching right to the edge of the city.","The Château d'If, on a small island offshore, is the prison of 'The Count of Monte Cristo'.","Marseille-Fos is the largest port in France."],"q":{"q":"Greek settlers from which city founded Marseille?","a":"Phocaea","x":["Corinth","Athens"]}},"Q36433":{"t":"Steep banks and bridges over the Douro","s":"The Douro runs its last few kilometres in a deep gorge, with Porto piled up on the north bank and Vila Nova de Gaia facing it from the south. Six bridges stitch the two together. Boats once brought port wine down from vineyard terraces far upriver, and the lodges where it matures still line the Gaia shore. Beyond, Atlantic surf draws a white line.","w":"A city on steep banks where the Douro gorge meets the Atlantic, the river crossed by a string of bridges.","f":["Portugal takes its name from Portus Cale, the Roman-era settlement at the mouth of the Douro.","The two-deck Dom Luís I bridge, finished in 1886, was designed by Théophile Seyrig, a former partner of Gustave Eiffel.","Porto's historic centre has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1996."],"q":{"q":"Which river meets the Atlantic at Porto?","a":"The Douro","x":["The Tagus","The Minho"]}},"Q42448":{"t":"Steel city with one foot in the Peak District","s":"Where the houses stop, the moors begin. Sheffield climbs over steep hills where five rivers meet, and its western edge runs straight into the Peak District; a sizeable slice of the city lies inside the national park. Fast streams once turned the grinding wheels of the cutlers here, and the city's name became a byword for steel.","w":"A hilly city in green valleys, its western edge meeting the brown moorland of the Peak District.","f":["Harry Brearley developed stainless steel in Sheffield in 1913.","Sheffield FC, founded in 1857, is recognised as the world's oldest football club.","The city takes its name from the River Sheaf, one of the five rivers that meet here."],"q":{"q":"Which material did Harry Brearley develop in Sheffield in 1913?","a":"Stainless steel","x":["Vulcanised rubber","Duralumin"]}},"Q2100":{"t":"A sea of docks where the Ruhr meets the Rhine","s":"Harbour basins, rail yards and container stacks lace the water where the Ruhr flows into the Rhine. This is Duisport, often called the largest inland port in the world, and barges from the North Sea ports come up the Rhine to unload here. North of the city, an old ironworks has been kept as a park and is lit up in colour after dark.","w":"Long, finger-like harbour basins on the Rhine at the mouth of the Ruhr, with industry and rail lines around them.","f":["The cartographer Gerardus Mercator lived and worked in Duisburg for more than 40 years.","Landschaftspark Duisburg-Nord turned an ironworks, closed in 1985, into a public park.","The Rhine at Duisburg is a busy barge highway linking the Ruhr with Rotterdam and the North Sea."],"q":{"q":"Which famous mapmaker lived and worked in Duisburg?","a":"Gerardus Mercator","x":["Abraham Ortelius","Martin Waldseemüller"]}},"Q1718":{"t":"The village on the Düssel that grew rich on the Rhine","s":"The Rhine swings through Düsseldorf in wide loops, with the old town on the east bank and broad green meadows along the water. The name means 'village on the Düssel', after a small stream that joins the big river here. Today the capital of North Rhine-Westphalia is a city of fashion, advertising and art.","w":"Wide meanders of the Rhine with the city mostly on the east bank; the airport lies just to the north.","f":["The poet Heinrich Heine was born in Düsseldorf in 1797.","Düsseldorf is home to one of the largest Japanese communities in Europe.","The Königsallee, a shopping boulevard, runs along both sides of an old moat."],"q":{"q":"What does 'Dorf' mean in the name Düsseldorf?","a":"Village","x":["Castle","Bridge"]}},"Q898":{"t":"A fortress city where the Om meets the Irtysh","s":"Across the flat West Siberian plain the Irtysh loops broad and brown, and at the point where the little Om joins it, Omsk spreads over both banks. It began in 1716 as a wooden fortress on Russia's southern frontier. Beyond the last suburbs the land is almost perfectly level: birch groves, fields and lakes stretching away in every direction.","w":"A large city on a sweeping bend of the Irtysh where a smaller river joins from the east, in flat farmland.","f":["Dostoevsky served four years of penal labour in Omsk, later retold in 'Notes from the House of the Dead'.","The Trans-Siberian Railway crosses the Irtysh at Omsk.","The Om, which gives the city its name, flows for more than 1,000 km before joining the Irtysh."],"q":{"q":"Which river does the Om flow into at Omsk?","a":"The Irtysh","x":["The Ob","The Tobol"]}},"Q906":{"t":"An industrial giant among the lakes of the Urals","s":"Lakes are scattered around Chelyabinsk like dropped coins, catching the light where the Urals flatten into the West Siberian plain. Several reach right up to the city's edge. Founded in 1736 as a fortress on the Miass river, the city grew into a giant of steel and tractor building after the railway arrived.","w":"A large grey city on flat land peppered with round lakes, with low wooded Ural ridges to the west.","f":["Chelyabinsk was founded in 1736 as a fortress on the Miass river.","In 1892 the West Siberian section of the Trans-Siberian Railway began to be built eastwards from Chelyabinsk.","Lake Smolino, inside the city limits, is a salt lake."],"q":{"q":"On which river was Chelyabinsk founded?","a":"The Miass","x":["The Iset","The Ural"]}},"Q908":{"t":"Gateway to the Caucasus on the lower Don","s":"The Don makes its final run to the Sea of Azov here, broad and slow, and Rostov lines its high right bank. Across the river lie low meadows and reed beds, and a little downstream the Don begins to split into the channels of its delta. The city grew from a customs post set up in 1749, and grain and river trade made it rich.","w":"A big city on the high north bank of the Don, facing flat green floodplain, with the river's delta to the west.","f":["Rostov began in 1749 as the Temernik customs post, set up by decree of Empress Elizabeth.","Nakhichevan-on-Don, founded by Armenian settlers from Crimea in 1779, is now part of the city.","The Sea of Azov, fed by the Don, is the shallowest sea in the world, nowhere much deeper than 14 m."],"q":{"q":"What was Rostov-on-Don founded as in 1749?","a":"A customs post","x":["A monastery","A naval shipyard"]}},"Q48256":{"t":"A long city on a river as wide as a lake","s":"The Dnieper is so broad here that it looks more like a lake than a river, with islands in mid-stream and bridges strung between the banks. Dnipro stretches for kilometres along the water. It was founded in the late 18th century as Yekaterinoslav, 'the glory of Catherine', in honour of Catherine the Great, and grew into a city of steel and rocket-building.","w":"A long city on both banks of a very wide Dnieper, with wooded islands in mid-river and several bridges.","f":["Just downstream the river once tumbled over the Dnieper Rapids; since the 1930s they have lain under a reservoir.","The Dnieper, about 2,200 km long, is the fourth-longest river in Europe.","Space launchers such as the Zenit and the Cyclone were designed in Dnipro."],"q":{"q":"What was Dnipro called when it was founded?","a":"Yekaterinoslav","x":["Yelisavetgrad","Nikolayev"]}},"Q648":{"t":"Flemish brick and a star-shaped citadel","s":"Flat Flanders stretches in every direction, and Lille's brick-red sprawl runs on without a break into Roubaix and Tourcoing, right up to the Belgian border. Look for a green five-pointed shape north-west of the centre: the citadel that Vauban built after 1667, still ringed by water. High-speed trains now put Lille about an hour from Paris and within easy reach of London and Brussels.","w":"A reddish urban mass on flat farmland merging into neighbouring towns to the north-east; a green pentagon marks the citadel.","f":["Vauban's citadel in Lille is nicknamed the 'Queen of Citadels'.","Charles de Gaulle was born in Lille in 1890.","The Braderie de Lille, held each September, is one of Europe's largest flea markets."],"q":{"q":"Which French leader was born in Lille in 1890?","a":"Charles de Gaulle","x":["Georges Pompidou","François Mitterrand"]}},"Q34370":{"t":"Europe's biggest port, reaching out to sea","s":"Follow the Maas from Rotterdam towards the sea and you trace Europe's largest port: some 40 km of docks, tank farms and container stacks. At its far end the port has pushed out into the North Sea on land built from sand. The city itself, with its skyline of towers and bridges, sits among flat green polders cut by straight canals.","w":"A long band of docks and pale storage tanks running west to the North Sea, with the city at its inland end.","f":["The port of Rotterdam is the largest in Europe.","Maasvlakte 2, a port extension on land reclaimed from the North Sea, opened in 2013.","The Renaissance scholar Erasmus was born in Rotterdam in the 1460s."],"q":{"q":"Which river branch flows through the centre of Rotterdam?","a":"The Nieuwe Maas","x":["The Amstel","The Scheldt"]}},"Q365":{"t":"A Roman city in a half-circle on the Rhine","s":"Cologne draws a half-moon on the west bank of the Rhine: its old centre is ringed by boulevards that follow the line of the medieval walls, with the river as the straight edge. The Romans made it a colony in AD 50, and the name Cologne comes from the Latin colonia. Across the river, the Deutz district faces the old town.","w":"A semicircular city on the west bank of the Rhine, its centre outlined by a ring of boulevards.","f":["Cologne Cathedral's twin spires reach 157 m; from 1880 to 1884 it was the tallest building in the world.","Eau de Cologne was first made here in the early 18th century by the Italian perfumer Johann Maria Farina.","The city's Roman name was Colonia Claudia Ara Agrippinensium."],"q":{"q":"What record did Cologne Cathedral hold from 1880 to 1884?","a":"World's tallest building","x":["World's largest church","Europe's oldest cathedral"]}},"Q1874":{"t":"A grid of streets on a Black Sea bluff","s":"A neat grid of streets on a high bluff above the Black Sea: from the air, Odessa's centre looks drawn with a ruler. North and south-west of the city, long salty lagoons called limans reach inland like fingers. The city was founded in 1794 and grew quickly as a free port shipping grain from the steppe.","w":"A gridded city on a coastal bluff with harbour moles, and long narrow lagoons reaching inland on either side.","f":["Odessa was founded in 1794 on the orders of Catherine the Great.","Beneath the city lie the Odessa Catacombs, old limestone quarries often said to total some 2,500 km.","The Potemkin Stairs, leading down to the port, have 192 steps."],"q":{"q":"In which year was Odessa founded?","a":"1794","x":["1703","1856"]}},"Q43070":{"t":"Pyramids of coal waste on the open steppe","s":"Scattered across the steppe around Donetsk stand dark cones, some as tall as small hills. They are spoil heaps from more than a century of coal mining in the Donbas, the Donets coal basin. The city grew from a works settlement founded in 1869 by the Welsh industrialist John Hughes, and its first name, Yuzovka, came from his surname.","w":"A large city on open steppe, with dark conical spoil heaps, ponds and industrial sites scattered around it.","f":["The name Donbas is short for 'Donets Basin', after the Siverskyi Donets river to the north.","Donetsk is known for its parks and rose gardens.","The Kalmius river runs through the city on its way south to the Sea of Azov."],"q":{"q":"Where did John Hughes, founder of Donetsk's ironworks, come from?","a":"Wales","x":["Scotland","Belgium"]}},"Q8692":{"t":"An iron town reborn in a green Basque valley","s":"Steep green hills crowd in on Bilbao, leaving room only for the winding estuary of the Nervión and the city strung along it. Ships and iron made the city rich; the river then leads about 15 km to the open Bay of Biscay. In 1997 a titanium-clad art museum by Frank Gehry gave the old port a new face and a new name abroad.","w":"A city squeezed into a narrow, winding estuary between green hills, opening out north-west to the Bay of Biscay.","f":["The Vizcaya Bridge near the estuary mouth, opened in 1893, is the world's oldest transporter bridge.","Bilbao was founded as a town in 1300.","The Guggenheim Museum Bilbao, designed by Frank Gehry, opened in 1997."],"q":{"q":"What kind of bridge crosses the estuary mouth near Bilbao?","a":"A transporter bridge","x":["A drawbridge","A pontoon bridge"]}},"Q3426":{"t":"Where Peter the Great built his first fleet","s":"A long, narrow reservoir runs right through Voronezh, splitting it into two banks joined by bridges; locals call it the Voronezh sea. Around the city lies some of Russia's blackest, richest soil. In the 1690s Peter the Great built shipyards on the river here, and the ships launched from them became the first Russian navy.","w":"A big city split by a long, narrow reservoir running north–south, with dark farmland all around.","f":["The Voronezh reservoir is about 30 km long.","Russia's first naval squadron was built in Voronezh in 1695–1696 for Peter the Great's campaign against Azov.","A replica of Peter's ship of the line Goto Predestination, launched here in 1700, is moored in the city."],"q":{"q":"Which ruler built a fleet at Voronezh in the 1690s?","a":"Peter the Great","x":["Ivan the Terrible","Catherine the Great"]}},"Q5332":{"t":"Two cities facing each other across the Volga","s":"The Volga here spreads kilometres wide, with Saratov climbing the hills of the right bank and Engels spread out on the flat left bank opposite. A bridge nearly 3 km long ties them together. Wooded hills close in on the city from the west. A young Yuri Gagarin learned to fly at the Saratov aero club in the 1950s.","w":"A very wide stretch of the Volga with a big city on the hilly west bank and another on the flat east bank.","f":["The Saratov road bridge over the Volga, opened in 1965, is about 2.8 km long.","Yuri Gagarin landed near Engels, across the Volga from Saratov, after the first human spaceflight.","The Saratov accordion is known for the little bells fitted to its body."],"q":{"q":"Who landed near Saratov after the first human spaceflight?","a":"Yuri Gagarin","x":["Alexei Leonov","Valentina Tereshkova"]}},"Q36036":{"t":"An old city on Europe's great watershed","s":"Rain falling on Lviv can end up in two different seas. The city sits on the main European watershed: streams on one side drain towards the Baltic, those on the other to the Black Sea. Its medieval centre is ringed by green hills, and even its own river, the Poltva, is out of sight, running in a tunnel beneath the streets.","w":"A compact city among green hills and patches of forest, surrounded by rolling farmland.","f":["Lviv was founded in the mid-13th century and named after Prince Lev, son of King Danylo of Galicia.","The historic centre of Lviv has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1998.","The Poltva river, hidden under the centre, eventually drains to the Baltic via the Bug and the Vistula."],"q":{"q":"After whom was Lviv named?","a":"Prince Lev","x":["Prince Roman","King Casimir"]}},"Q1479":{"t":"A moon-shaped bend of the Garonne among vineyards","s":"The Garonne curves through Bordeaux in a perfect crescent, which is why the old harbour is called the Port of the Moon. Around the city the land is striped with vineyards, among the most famous in the world. A little way downstream the Garonne joins the Dordogne to form the Gironde, a wide brown estuary running out to the Atlantic.","w":"A crescent-shaped bend of the river with the city on its inner bank; vineyards and the broad Gironde estuary to the north.","f":["The Port of the Moon, the crescent-shaped historic city, became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2007.","The Gironde is the largest estuary in Western Europe.","The Dune of Pilat, the tallest sand dune in Europe at over 100 m, lies about 60 km to the south-west."],"q":{"q":"Which two rivers meet below Bordeaux to form the Gironde?","a":"The Garonne and the Dordogne","x":["The Garonne and the Lot","The Loire and the Dordogne"]}},"Q31487":{"t":"A royal city in a bend of the Vistula","s":"The Vistula swings round Wawel Hill, where Polish kings were crowned and buried, and the medieval town fans out to the north. A ring of park, the Planty, now follows the line of the old walls. On clear days the jagged Tatras show on the southern horizon. Kraków was Poland's capital until the royal court moved to Warsaw at the end of the 16th century.","w":"A large city on a loop of the Vistula, with a big steelworks to the east and hills rising towards the south.","f":["Kraków's old town was among the first sites put on UNESCO's World Heritage List, in 1978.","The Wieliczka Salt Mine, just south-east of the city, was worked from the 13th century to the late 20th.","The Planty park, about 4 km long, rings the old town where medieval walls once stood."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows past Wawel Hill in Kraków?","a":"The Vistula","x":["The Oder","The Warta"]}},"Q1792":{"t":"Amber, grain and a sand spit across the bay","s":"A thin arm of sand curls across Gdańsk Bay: the Hel Peninsula, some 35 km long and in places only a few hundred metres wide. Gdańsk sits at the bottom of the bay, joined to Sopot and Gdynia in one long coastal city. For centuries grain floated down the Vistula to this Hanseatic port, and amber washed up on its beaches.","w":"A city at the head of a wide bay, a long thin sand spit curving across it, the flat Vistula delta to the east.","f":["Sopot's wooden pier, just north of Gdańsk, is about 500 m long — the longest wooden pier in Europe.","The Vistula, Poland's longest river, reaches the Baltic just east of the city.","Baltic amber is still worked in Gdańsk, which calls itself the world capital of amber."],"q":{"q":"Which trading league was Gdańsk a leading member of?","a":"The Hanseatic League","x":["The Swabian League","The Kalmar Union"]}},"Q1903":{"t":"A city built of black lava at the foot of Etna","s":"Etna fills the view to the north: a vast dark mountain, often trailing a plume of steam and snow-capped in winter. Catania spreads along the coast at its foot, and much of the old city is built from the volcano's own dark lava stone. South of the city, the flat Plain of Catania opens out, a patchwork of citrus groves and fields.","w":"A coastal city beneath a huge, dark volcanic cone to the north, with a broad flat plain opening to the south.","f":["Etna rises to more than 3,300 m, about 30 km from the city centre, and is one of the most active volcanoes on Earth.","Mount Etna became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2013.","The composer Vincenzo Bellini was born in Catania in 1801."],"q":{"q":"Which composer was born in Catania in 1801?","a":"Vincenzo Bellini","x":["Gioachino Rossini","Giuseppe Verdi"]}},"Q3646":{"t":"Cossack city on the looping Kuban","s":"The Kuban wanders across the flat plain in great loops and cut-off oxbows, and Krasnodar grew on its northern bank. Just upstream, a large reservoir shines on the plain. All around lie some of Russia's most fertile fields, and downstream, towards the Sea of Azov, many of them are flooded paddies: this is Russia's main rice-growing region.","w":"A large city on a meandering river, with a big reservoir just to the east and a chequerboard of fields all around.","f":["Black Sea Cossacks founded the city in 1793 as Yekaterinodar, 'Catherine's gift'.","The Krasnodar reservoir on the Kuban is the largest reservoir in the North Caucasus.","The Kuban flows about 870 km from the glaciers of Elbrus to the Sea of Azov."],"q":{"q":"What was Krasnodar called until 1920?","a":"Yekaterinodar","x":["Yekaterinoslav","Yekaterinburg"]}},"Q5627":{"t":"A high bank above Europe's largest reservoir","s":"No longer a river but a broad inland sea: that is the Volga at Ulyanovsk, part of the Kuybyshev reservoir. The old town sits high on the steep right bank, with newer districts across the water on the flat left bank, joined by long bridges. The city was founded in 1648 as the fortress of Simbirsk; its present name honours Lenin, born here in 1870.","w":"A city on both sides of a very wide stretch of the Volga, linked by long bridges, the old town on the higher west bank.","f":["The Kuybyshev reservoir on the Volga is the largest reservoir in Europe.","Ivan Goncharov, author of 'Oblomov', was born in Simbirsk in 1812.","The city was renamed Ulyanovsk in 1924, after Lenin's family name, Ulyanov."],"q":{"q":"What was Ulyanovsk called until 1924?","a":"Simbirsk","x":["Samara","Syzran"]}},"Q1731":{"t":"Florence on the Elbe, framed by green meadows","s":"The Elbe curves gently through Dresden between wide green meadows that have been left unbuilt, so the river keeps its countryside look right in the city centre. The baroque old town rises on the south bank. Upstream to the south-east, the river heads into Saxon Switzerland, a maze of sandstone towers and deep gorges.","w":"A city along a gently curving river bordered by broad green meadows, with wooded hills to the north-east.","f":["Its baroque skyline earned Dresden the nickname 'Florence on the Elbe'.","European hard-paste porcelain was first made in Dresden in 1708; production moved to nearby Meissen in 1710.","The sandstone towers of Saxon Switzerland begin about 30 km upstream."],"q":{"q":"What nickname did its baroque skyline earn Dresden?","a":"Florence on the Elbe","x":["Venice of the North","Paris of the East"]}},"Q2423":{"t":"A thousand-year-old city where two rivers meet","s":"Look for the point where the Kotorosl flows into the Volga: that spit of land, the Strelka, is where Yaroslavl began. According to tradition, Prince Yaroslav the Wise founded the city around 1010. Its old centre, with streets fanning out from the riverfront, is one of the jewels of the Golden Ring of old Russian towns.","w":"A big city on the Volga, with a smaller river joining from the south-west near the centre.","f":["The historic centre of Yaroslavl has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 2005.","Valentina Tereshkova, the first woman in space, was born in a village near Yaroslavl in 1937.","The city's coat of arms shows a bear carrying an axe."],"q":{"q":"Which animal appears on Yaroslavl's coat of arms?","a":"A bear","x":["A wolf","An elk"]}},"Q4454":{"t":"The city on the banknote, where the Amur is vast","s":"A maze of channels, islands and sandbanks kilometres wide: that is the Amur at Khabarovsk, joined by the Ussuri just upstream. The city lines the high right bank. A long bridge carrying both a road and a railway spans the river. The city began in 1858 as a military post and took its name from the 17th-century explorer Yerofey Khabarov.","w":"A long city on the high east bank of a huge braided river, with a long bridge across it and flat wetlands beyond.","f":["Khabarovsk and its Amur bridge appear on the Russian 5,000-rouble banknote.","The Ussuri joins the Amur just south-west of the city.","In winter the Amur freezes hard enough for people to walk far out onto the ice."],"q":{"q":"Which explorer is Khabarovsk named after?","a":"Yerofey Khabarov","x":["Vitus Bering","Semyon Dezhnev"]}},"Q5337":{"t":"A steppe city on the river between two continents","s":"Open steppe runs to the horizon, and through it the Ural river winds in tight loops, lined by a green ribbon of floodplain woods. Orenburg stands on the high bank above. The river is often taken as a boundary between Europe and Asia, and a footbridge in the city lets you walk from one to the other. Gas fields spread across the plain to the south.","w":"A city on a high riverbank above a meandering, tree-lined river, with flat yellow-brown steppe all around.","f":["Orenburg's gossamer down shawls are said to be fine enough to pull through a wedding ring.","Yuri Gagarin trained at the military flight school in Orenburg and married a local girl here in 1957.","The Orenburg gas field, one of the largest in Europe, lies just south of the city."],"q":{"q":"What is Orenburg famous for making?","a":"Fine down shawls","x":["Painted clay toys","Lacquered boxes"]}},"Q93910":{"t":"Steel smoke in a valley of the Kuzbass","s":"Mountains close in around Novokuznetsk: the Kuznetsk Alatau to the east, the dark taiga of Mountain Shoria to the south. The city follows the Tom along the valley floor, with vast steelworks on its edges and coal pits scarring the hills. It began in 1618 as the Kuznetsk fortress, named after the local people's skill as blacksmiths.","w":"An industrial city strung along a river valley, with steelworks, coal pits and forested mountains to the east and south.","f":["Sheregesh, one of Siberia's best-known ski resorts, lies in the mountains of Mountain Shoria to the south.","Fyodor Dostoevsky married Maria Isaeva in Kuznetsk in 1857.","Novokuznetsk is one of the main steel-making centres of Siberia."],"q":{"q":"Which writer married in Kuznetsk, now Novokuznetsk, in 1857?","a":"Fyodor Dostoevsky","x":["Leo Tolstoy","Anton Chekhov"]}},"Q2746":{"t":"An old kremlin above the Oka's green meadows","s":"The Oka swings past Ryazan in wide loops, and across the river a sea of flat meadows and forest stretches north towards the lakes and marshes of Meshchera. The city climbs the higher southern bank, where the kremlin stands on a hill above two small streams. In late spring the Oka floods its meadows for kilometres, turning them into a shining lake.","w":"A city on the south bank of a looping river, facing a broad green floodplain and dark forests to the north.","f":["Ivan Pavlov, the first Russian Nobel laureate, was born in Ryazan in 1849.","The poet Sergei Yesenin was born in 1895 in Konstantinovo, a village on the Oka in the Ryazan region.","Until 1778 the city was called Pereyaslavl-Ryazansky."],"q":{"q":"Which Nobel-winning scientist was born in Ryazan?","a":"Ivan Pavlov","x":["Ilya Mechnikov","Pyotr Kapitsa"]}},"Q5815":{"t":"Siberia's first Russian town, now an oil capital","s":"Tyumen was the first foothold: founded in 1586 on the Tura, it is considered the oldest Russian town in Siberia. From the air the city is ringed by pine and birch forest, with bogs and lakes glinting beyond. Today it runs the oil and gas fields far to the north, and its hot springs let people bathe outdoors in the depths of winter.","w":"A large city on a winding river in flat forest land, with lakes and bogs scattered around it.","f":["The Trans-Siberian Railway runs through Tyumen on its way from the Urals to Omsk.","Warm water from deep thermal springs around Tyumen fills outdoor pools that stay open all winter.","The Tura, on which the city stands, flows into the Tobol, a tributary of the Irtysh."],"q":{"q":"In which year was Tyumen founded?","a":"1586","x":["1703","1480"]}},"Q5540":{"t":"An old frontier fortress in the forest-steppe","s":"Penza marks the meeting of forest and steppe: patches of woodland give way to wide fields, and the city spreads along the Sura in between. It was founded in 1663 as a fortress guarding Russia's south-eastern frontier. South-west of the city a large reservoir fills the Sura valley. The poet Lermontov grew up on an estate in this region.","w":"A city on a small river among fields and patches of forest, with a long reservoir to the south-west.","f":["Penza was founded in 1663 as a fortress on the Sura.","Mikhail Lermontov grew up at Tarkhany, his grandmother's estate in the Penza region, where he is buried.","The Sura, which flows through the city, is a tributary of the Volga."],"q":{"q":"Which poet grew up at Tarkhany in the Penza region?","a":"Mikhail Lermontov","x":["Alexander Pushkin","Sergei Yesenin"]}},"Q3927":{"t":"Where the Volga shatters into a maze of channels","s":"South of Astrakhan the Volga falls apart: hundreds of channels, reed beds and islands fan out towards the Caspian Sea in the largest river delta in Europe. The city itself stands on islands in the upper delta, around a white-walled 16th-century kremlin. In summer, whole bays of the lower delta turn pink with blooming lotus.","w":"A city among river channels at the head of a vast delta of reed beds and waterways spreading south-east to the Caspian.","f":["The Volga delta, which begins just upstream of Astrakhan, is the largest river delta in Europe.","Astrakhan's kremlin was built of stone in the late 16th century.","The curly lamb fur called astrakhan takes its name from the city."],"q":{"q":"Which river's delta begins near Astrakhan?","a":"The Volga","x":["The Don","The Ural"]}},"Q384":{"t":"A city strung along the Vardar under a mountain","s":"Mountains frame Skopje on every side, and the city stretches for kilometres along the Vardar on the floor of the basin. Mount Vodno rises steeply right above its southern edge. A little to the south-west, the Treska river cuts the deep Matka canyon, now a narrow reservoir. In the old bazaar across the Stone Bridge, streets keep an Ottoman pattern.","w":"A long city on a river in a mountain-ringed basin, with a steep wooded peak rising right above its southern edge.","f":["Mother Teresa was born in Skopje in 1910.","The Stone Bridge over the Vardar was built in the 15th century.","Matka Canyon, with its caves and small reservoir, lies about 15 km south-west of the centre."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Skopje?","a":"The Vardar","x":["The Morava","The Drina"]}},"Q6066":{"t":"The birthplace of Kuzbass coal","s":"The Tom cuts through Kemerovo in a broad curve, with the city on both banks and dark taiga beyond. Around it the land is stripped in places by open-pit coal mines, terraced like giant steps. This is the Kuzbass, one of the world's great coalfields, and the story of its coal began on this riverbank three centuries ago.","w":"A city on both banks of a curving river, with grey terraced open-pit mines and dark forest around it.","f":["In 1721 the ore prospector Mikhail Volkov found coal on the Tom here — the first recorded find in the Kuzbass.","The city grew from the villages of Shcheglovo and Kemerovo and took its present name in 1932.","The Kuznetsk Basin, or Kuzbass, is one of the largest coalfields in the world."],"q":{"q":"In which year was coal first found at Kemerovo?","a":"1721","x":["1812","1905"]}},"Q25270":{"t":"A hill city where the Kosovo plain begins","s":"Pristina climbs over low hills where the flat Kosovo plain meets forested uplands to the east. Around 650 m above sea level, the city is the largest in Kosovo. West of it, look for grey open-pit mines and the smokestacks of power stations: the area sits on some of the largest lignite deposits in Europe. Wooded Germia Park reaches into the city from the east.","w":"A city on low hills with a broad plain to the west, grey open-pit mines beyond, and wooded uplands to the east.","f":["The Kosovo plain west of the city is drained by the Sitnica river, which flows north.","The ruins of Ulpiana, a Roman city, lie about 10 km to the south-east.","Germia Park, the forested hills on the city's eastern edge, is Pristina's largest green space."],"q":{"q":"Which ancient Roman city's ruins lie just south-east of Pristina?","a":"Ulpiana","x":["Stobi","Naissus"]}},"Q5384":{"t":"A river-bend city of clay toys and taiga","s":"The Vyatka loops lazily through a green sea of forest, and Kirov stands on the high bank of one of its great bends. The city has worn three names: Khlynov, Vyatka and, since 1934, Kirov. Across the river lies the village of Dymkovo, where craftswomen have long shaped and painted bright clay toys, one of Russia's best-known folk crafts.","w":"A city on a high bank inside a big loop of a river, with endless taiga around it.","f":["Dymkovo toys — clay figures whitewashed and painted in bright patterns — are named after a village across the river.","The city's founding is traditionally dated to 1374.","The Vyatka flows for more than 1,300 km before joining the Kama."],"q":{"q":"What are Dymkovo toys, made near Kirov, made of?","a":"Painted clay","x":["Carved birch wood","Woven straw"]}},"Q10686":{"t":"A shipbuilding city at the head of a sea lough","s":"Belfast Lough runs inland like a long funnel, and the city fills its head, where the river Lagan comes in. Dark hills rise steeply on the north-western side: Cave Hill's cliffs are said to resemble Napoleon's profile. Shipbuilding made the city famous, and two huge yellow gantry cranes still stand over the docks. Just west glints Lough Neagh.","w":"A city at the end of a funnel-shaped sea inlet, with steep dark hills to the north-west and a huge lake further west.","f":["Lough Neagh, about 25 km west of the city, is the largest lake in the British Isles.","The shipyard's yellow gantry cranes, Samson and Goliath, are each about 100 m tall.","Queen's University Belfast was founded in 1845."],"q":{"q":"Which is the largest lake in the British Isles, just west of Belfast?","a":"Lough Neagh","x":["Loch Lomond","Lough Erne"]}},"Q1829":{"t":"Königsberg's bridges, amber and sand spits","s":"Look for the Pregolya river winding into a shallow lagoon, and beyond it two impossibly thin sand spits running along the Baltic coast: the Vistula Spit to the south-west and the Curonian Spit to the north-east. Kaliningrad sits between them. Until 1946 it was Königsberg, the city of the philosopher Kant, and its bridges gave rise to a famous puzzle.","w":"A city on a river flowing into a lagoon, with long, pencil-thin sand spits along the sea coast to the south-west and north-east.","f":["In 1736 Leonhard Euler proved that no walk could cross Königsberg's seven bridges exactly once each, founding graph theory.","The philosopher Immanuel Kant was born in Königsberg in 1724 and lived there almost all his life.","The Curonian Spit, about 98 km long, has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 2000."],"q":{"q":"Which mathematician solved the Seven Bridges of Königsberg puzzle?","a":"Leonhard Euler","x":["Carl Friedrich Gauss","Gottfried Leibniz"]}},"Q2801":{"t":"A city named after the thickets of the Desna","s":"Deep forests have always surrounded Bryansk, and its name is thought to come from the old Russian word for dense thickets. The city spreads across both banks of the Desna, broken up by steep, wooded ravines. The river wanders across a wide green floodplain, and beyond it the woods run on to the south for many kilometres.","w":"A city on both sides of a winding river, cut by ravines and surrounded by broad forests.","f":["Bryansk was first mentioned in a chronicle in 1146.","The poet Fyodor Tyutchev was born in 1803 at Ovstug, a family estate in the Bryansk region.","The Desna, which flows through the city, is a large tributary of the Dnieper."],"q":{"q":"Which poet was born at Ovstug near Bryansk in 1803?","a":"Fyodor Tyutchev","x":["Afanasy Fet","Alexander Pushkin"]}},"Q1780":{"t":"A capital on the Danube that touches two borders","s":"Austria and Hungary both begin at the city limits: Bratislava is the only capital that borders two other countries. The Danube divides it, with the castle hill and old town on the north bank and the vast high-rise estate of Petržalka across the water. The Little Carpathians start right in the city, their slopes striped with vineyards running away to the north-east.","w":"A city on both banks of the Danube, with wooded hills rising to the north and flat farmland to the south.","f":["Vienna and Bratislava are about 55 km apart, the closest pair of capitals in Europe after Rome and Vatican City.","Hungarian kings and queens were crowned in Bratislava's St Martin's Cathedral from 1563 to 1830.","Petržalka, on the south bank, is one of the largest housing estates in Central Europe."],"q":{"q":"Which two countries border Bratislava?","a":"Austria and Hungary","x":["Czechia and Austria","Hungary and Poland"]}},"Q95449":{"t":"A steel giant built beside a mountain of iron","s":"Magnitogorsk was built from scratch from 1929 beside Magnitnaya, a mountain so rich in magnetic iron ore that it has now been largely dug away. The steelworks stretches for kilometres along a reservoir on the Ural river. Because the river runs through the city, Magnitogorsk lies partly in Europe and partly in Asia.","w":"A huge steelworks along a long reservoir on a river, with the city on both sides and open steppe beyond.","f":["Construction of the city and its steelworks began in 1929 on the open steppe.","The Magnitogorsk steelworks is one of the largest in Russia."],"q":{"q":"What gave Magnitogorsk its name?","a":"A magnetic iron-ore mountain","x":["A meteorite crater","A salt lake"]}},"Q2288":{"t":"A Volga city halfway between two capitals","s":"Halfway between Moscow and St Petersburg, the railway and main road linking the two cities cut straight through Tver. The Volga, still young here, crosses the city in a gentle curve and is joined by the Tvertsa and the Tmaka. From Tver in the 15th century, a merchant named Afanasy Nikitin set off on a journey that took him all the way to India.","w":"A city on both banks of a medium-sized river, with a straight railway and motorway running north-west to south-east.","f":["Afanasy Nikitin described his journey to India in 'A Journey Beyond Three Seas'.","The Volga's source in the Valdai Hills is only a few hundred kilometres upstream.","In the 14th century Tver was a powerful principality and a rival of Moscow."],"q":{"q":"Which Tver merchant travelled to India in the 15th century?","a":"Afanasy Nikitin","x":["Semyon Dezhnev","Vitus Bering"]}},"Q98967":{"t":"Where the Demidovs forged iron for Europe","s":"Nizhny Tagil sprawls between forested Ural ridges, around a long dammed pond that once drove the ironworks' waterwheels. The Demidov family founded the works in the 1720s, and the town grew into one of the great iron centres of the Russian Empire. Look for the huge terraced pit on the edge of town: Mount Vysokaya, a hill of iron ore mined into a hole.","w":"An industrial city around a long pond, with deep terraced mining pits and forested Ural ridges around it.","f":["In 1834 the Cherepanovs, father and son, built Russia's first steam locomotive here.","Demidov iron, stamped with a sable, was prized in 18th-century England as 'Old Sable'.","The Nizhny Tagil Charter of 2003, the international charter for industrial heritage, is named after the city."],"q":{"q":"What did the Cherepanovs build in Nizhny Tagil in 1834?","a":"Russia's first steam locomotive","x":["Russia's first steamship","Russia's first blast furnace"]}},"Q8745":{"t":"A deep Atlantic inlet dotted with mussel rafts","s":"The Ría de Vigo reaches deep into the green Galician hills, and at its mouth the granite Cíes Islands stand guard against the Atlantic. Look closely at the calm water: it is freckled with hundreds of small dark squares, rafts where mussels are grown on hanging ropes. Vigo climbs the southern shore, one of Europe's busiest fishing ports.","w":"A long sea inlet between green hills, with rocky islands at its mouth and rows of tiny rafts on the water.","f":["The Cíes Islands are part of the Atlantic Islands of Galicia National Park.","The Rande Bridge spans the ría at its narrowest point, upstream of the city.","Galicia's rías produce most of Spain's mussels, grown on thousands of floating rafts."],"q":{"q":"Which islands guard the mouth of the Ría de Vigo?","a":"The Cíes Islands","x":["The Ons Islands","The Columbretes"]}},"Q11974":{"t":"A city on a sandy neck between two shores","s":"At the north-eastern tip of Gran Canaria, a volcanic knob called La Isleta is tied to the island by a narrow sandy isthmus. Las Palmas fills that neck, with a long beach on one side and a busy port on the other, then runs south along the coast. Behind it, bare ravines climb towards the island's volcanic heights.","w":"A city on the north-east corner of a round island, stretched along the coast and spreading onto a rocky peninsula.","f":["Las Palmas was founded in 1478.","Columbus stopped at Gran Canaria in 1492 on his first voyage across the Atlantic.","Las Canteras beach is sheltered by a natural offshore reef called La Barra."],"q":{"q":"In which year was Las Palmas founded?","a":"1478","x":["1402","1571"]}},"Q5206":{"t":"The 'city of the cross' on a high plateau","s":"Stavropol has a head start on height: it sprawls over the Stavropol Upland, with woods on its slopes and the steppe spreading out far below. It was founded in 1777 as one of a chain of fortresses guarding the empire's new southern frontier, and its Greek name means 'city of the cross'. On a clear day the snowy Caucasus may show far to the south.","w":"A large city on a raised, partly wooded plateau, with a patchwork of farmland spreading away in every direction.","f":["Stavropol was founded in 1777 as a fortress on the Azov–Mozdok defensive line.","The city is the capital of Stavropol Krai, one of southern Russia's main grain-growing regions."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Stavropol mean?","a":"City of the cross","x":["City of the steppe","City of the eagle"]}},"Q6816":{"t":"Where the Trans-Siberian turns off for Mongolia","s":"Bare steppe hills and dark forested ridges meet around Ulan-Ude, where the little Uda flows into the broad, braided Selenga. The name means 'Red Uda'. Lake Baikal lies less than 100 km to the north-west, and the Mongolian border about 200 km to the south. This is the heart of Buryatia, where Buddhist monasteries stand among the hills.","w":"A city where two rivers meet in a broad valley, with bare steppe hills and forested ridges around.","f":["Just outside the city the Trans-Mongolian Railway branches off the Trans-Siberian towards Ulaanbaatar and Beijing.","The Ivolginsky datsan, south-west of the city, is a leading centre of Buddhism in Russia.","A bronze head of Lenin about 7.7 m tall, one of the largest ever made, stands in the main square."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Ulan-Ude mean?","a":"Red Uda","x":["White Water","Great Steppe"]}},"Q39420":{"t":"A subtropical coast under snowy peaks","s":"Palm trees on the beach and snow on the peaks: Sochi squeezes a thin ribbon of subtropical coast between the Black Sea and the forested slopes of the Caucasus. Greater Sochi runs for about 145 km along the shore. Up in the mountains to the east lies Krasnaya Polyana, where the ski and sledding events of the 2014 Winter Olympics were held.","w":"A long, thin strip of town along the sea, backed by steep green mountains with snowy summits behind.","f":["Greater Sochi stretches about 145 km along the Black Sea coast.","Sochi hosted the Winter Olympic Games in 2014.","Tea has grown on the hills around Sochi since the early 20th century, in some of the world's most northerly tea gardens."],"q":{"q":"In which year did Sochi host the Winter Olympics?","a":"2014","x":["2010","2018"]}},"Q46852":{"t":"The old Moldavian capital on seven hills","s":"Like Rome, Iași is said to stand on seven hills, rolling above the valley of the little Bahlui. For three centuries this was the capital of the Principality of Moldavia, and it remains a city of churches, theatres and students. East of the city, the land drops to the wide green floodplain of the Prut.","w":"A city spread over low hills above a small river valley, with a wide green floodplain to the east.","f":["Iași was the capital of Moldavia from 1564 to 1859.","Romania's first modern university was founded in Iași in 1860.","The Prut river flows about 15 km east of the city."],"q":{"q":"Until 1859, Iași was the capital of which principality?","a":"Moldavia","x":["Wallachia","Transylvania"]}},"Q5818":{"t":"A caliphs' city on the Guadalquivir","s":"The Guadalquivir winds across a plain of olive groves and wheat, and dark hills of the Sierra Morena rise just north of the city. A thousand years ago Córdoba was the capital of a caliphate and one of the largest cities in Europe. In high summer the plain bakes: afternoon temperatures here often climb past 40 °C.","w":"A compact city on a winding river, with dark wooded hills to the north and olive groves on the plain to the south.","f":["Córdoba's Great Mosque, begun in 785, has had a cathedral built inside it since the 16th century.","Medina Azahara, a ruined 10th-century palace city about 8 km west, became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2018.","The Roman bridge over the Guadalquivir was first built in the 1st century BC."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Córdoba?","a":"The Guadalquivir","x":["The Guadiana","The Ebro"]}},"Q2337":{"t":"An old fortress city on the upper Dnieper","s":"Wooded hills crowd the upper Dnieper, still a modest river here, and Smolensk climbs both its banks. The city was first mentioned in 863, which makes it one of the oldest in Russia. Around 1600 it was ringed by a massive brick wall with dozens of towers, and long stretches of it still stand.","w":"A hilly city on both banks of a winding river, surrounded by forest and farmland.","f":["Smolensk's fortress wall, built in 1595–1602, was about 6.5 km long with 38 towers.","The composer Mikhail Glinka was born in 1804 at Novospasskoye, an estate in the Smolensk region.","Yuri Gagarin was born in 1934 in a village in the Smolensk region."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Smolensk?","a":"The Dnieper","x":["The Western Dvina","The Oka"]}},"Q437":{"t":"A small capital between the Alps and a marsh","s":"Alpine peaks to the north, a flat marsh to the south, and Ljubljana tucked between them around a wooded castle hill. The little Ljubljanica river curls through the old town below the castle. On those southern marshes, archaeologists found prehistoric pile dwellings and a wooden wheel more than 5,000 years old.","w":"A modest city around a wooded hill, flat marshland to the south and a wall of Alpine peaks to the north.","f":["The Ljubljana Marshes wheel, about 5,150 years old, is one of the oldest wooden wheels ever found.","Prehistoric pile dwellings on the Ljubljana Marshes are part of a UNESCO World Heritage site.","The architect Jože Plečnik reshaped much of the city centre in the 20th century."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through the old town of Ljubljana?","a":"The Ljubljanica","x":["The Drava","The Soča"]}},"Q5265":{"t":"A green spa town with Elbrus on the skyline","s":"The plains end abruptly at Nalchik, where the first forested ridges of the Caucasus rise. On clear days the twin white summit of Elbrus stands out to the south-west. The city's name is said to mean 'little horseshoe', after the curve of the hills around it. Parks and mineral springs have made it a spa town.","w":"A city at the edge of the plain against forested foothills, with the snowy main Caucasus ridge beyond.","f":["Elbrus, the highest peak in Europe at 5,642 m, stands about 100 km to the south-west.","Nalchik is the capital of Kabardino-Balkaria.","The city's large park runs up into the forested foothills."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Nalchik mean?","a":"Little horseshoe","x":["Eagle's nest","White water"]}},"Q79808":{"t":"Ovid's place of exile, now a busy Black Sea port","s":"Long breakwaters and a maze of docks mark Romania's main gateway to the sea. North of the city, Mamaia's hotels line a narrow strip of sand between the Black Sea and a freshwater lake. In ancient times this was the Greek town of Tomis, and the Roman poet Ovid spent his last years here in exile, complaining of the cold.","w":"A port city with long breakwaters, a canal joining from the west and a thin sandbar between a lake and the sea to the north.","f":["The Danube–Black Sea Canal, opened in 1984, reaches the sea just south of the city.","Constanța is Romania's largest port and one of the biggest on the Black Sea.","Mamaia resort runs along a sandbar between Lake Siutghiol and the sea."],"q":{"q":"Which Roman poet was exiled to Tomis, today's Constanța?","a":"Ovid","x":["Virgil","Horace"]}},"Q140147":{"t":"A border river and a break of gauge","s":"The Western Bug and the Mukhavets meet at Brest, and the Bug forms the border here. The land is flat, with forests and fields to the horizon. Brest is a railway crossroads where two track widths meet, Europe's standard gauge and the wider one used across the former Russian Empire, so long-distance trains change their wheel sets here.","w":"A city where two rivers meet on a flat, forested plain; the larger river runs north–south along the border.","f":["Brest was first mentioned in a chronicle, as Berestye, in 1019.","At Brest, 1,435 mm standard-gauge track meets the 1,520 mm gauge used further east.","Białowieża Forest, one of the last primeval forests of Europe, lies a short way to the north."],"q":{"q":"Which river forms the border at Brest?","a":"The Western Bug","x":["The Neman","The Pripyat"]}},"Q1957":{"t":"Lace, butter and a white cathedral in the north","s":"Flat forest and marsh stretch around Vologda, and the little Vologda river loops through the middle. The city dates its founding to 1147, the same year as Moscow. Its white St Sophia Cathedral was built on the orders of Ivan the Terrible, and whole streets of carved wooden houses survive around the centre. Lake Kubenskoye glints to the north-west.","w":"A city on a small winding river in flat, forested lowland, with a long lake to the north-west.","f":["Vologda lace, woven on bobbins, is one of Russia's best-known crafts.","St Sophia Cathedral in Vologda was built in 1568–1570.","Vologda butter, with its nutty taste, has a protected regional name."],"q":{"q":"What kind of lace is Vologda famous for?","a":"Bobbin lace","x":["Needle lace","Crochet lace"]}},"Q156713":{"t":"Granite cliffs and the birthplace of a space pioneer","s":"North of Zhytomyr the dark forests of Polesia begin, and the city spreads along the Teteriv, which cuts through outcrops of grey granite here. According to tradition, Zhytomyr was founded in 884. Its best-known son is Sergei Korolev, who became chief designer of the Soviet space programme. The city now has a museum of space flight in his name.","w":"A city on a winding river with rocky banks, farmland to the south and dark forests to the north.","f":["Sergei Korolev, who led the design of the first satellite and Gagarin's rocket, was born in Zhytomyr in 1907.","The Teteriv river runs through granite cliffs in the city.","The Teteriv is a tributary of the Dnieper."],"q":{"q":"Which space pioneer was born in Zhytomyr?","a":"Sergei Korolev","x":["Konstantin Tsiolkovsky","Valentin Glushko"]}},"Q70":{"t":"An old town wrapped in a loop of green river","s":"The Aare, milky green with Alpine meltwater, curls around Bern's old town in a tight loop, leaving the medieval streets on a narrow peninsula. On clear days the white wall of the Bernese Alps fills the southern horizon. Officially, Switzerland has no capital: Bern is the 'federal city', where the government and parliament sit.","w":"A tight loop of a green river around a narrow old town, with snowy Alpine peaks far to the south.","f":["The Old City of Bern has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1983.","Albert Einstein developed his special theory of relativity while living in Bern in 1905.","The Aare gets its green colour from fine rock flour carried down by Alpine glaciers."],"q":{"q":"Which theory did Einstein publish while living in Bern in 1905?","a":"Special relativity","x":["General relativity","Quantum electrodynamics"]}},"Q641":{"t":"A fish-shaped city floating in a lagoon","s":"In the middle of a shallow lagoon lies a tight cluster of islands shaped like a fish, cut through by one reversed S of water: the Grand Canal. That is Venice. A long straight causeway ties it to the mainland, and a chain of thin barrier islands, including the Lido, shields the lagoon from the Adriatic. Cars stop where the causeway ends; beyond, the city moves on foot and by boat.","w":"A fish-shaped island city in a shallow lagoon, linked to the mainland by a long causeway, with thin barrier islands offshore.","f":["Venice is built on about 118 small islands separated by canals.","The Ponte della Libertà, about 3.8 km long, carries a road and railway to the mainland.","Venice and its Lagoon have been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1987."],"q":{"q":"About how many islands is Venice built on?","a":"About 118","x":["About 30","About 400"]}},"Q2211":{"t":"A bridge that dives under the sea to Denmark","s":"A thin line leaves the coast south-west of Malmö and heads out to sea: the Øresund Bridge. It runs about 8 km to an artificial island, then vanishes into a tunnel for the rest of the way to Copenhagen. Malmö itself spreads over the flat, fertile farmland of Scania, Sweden's southern tip.","w":"A city on a flat coast, with a long bridge heading west over the strait to an island and Copenhagen beyond.","f":["The Øresund link, opened in 2000, joins a bridge, an artificial island and a tunnel under the sea.","Turning Torso, a twisting tower about 190 m tall, was the tallest building in Scandinavia when completed in 2005.","Malmö is Sweden's third-largest city."],"q":{"q":"Which city does the Øresund link connect Malmö with?","a":"Copenhagen","x":["Helsingør","Aarhus"]}},"Q1895":{"t":"Peter's ironworks on a great northern lake","s":"Lake Onega stretches north like a scattered puzzle of bays, islands and rocky peninsulas, and Petrozavodsk lines its western shore. The city grew from an ironworks that Peter the Great founded in 1703 to cast cannon, and its name simply means 'Peter's works'. Out on the lake lies Kizhi island, famous for its wooden churches.","w":"A city along the curved shore of a huge lake, with long bays and forested peninsulas reaching north.","f":["Lake Onega is the second-largest lake in Europe, after Ladoga.","Kizhi's wooden churches, north-east of the city on the lake, are a UNESCO World Heritage site.","The ironworks that gave the city its name was founded in 1703."],"q":{"q":"On which lake does Petrozavodsk stand?","a":"Lake Onega","x":["Lake Ladoga","Lake Peipus"]}},"Q40840":{"t":"A factory town between two lakes","s":"Two big lakes nearly touch at Tampere, and the city fills the narrow neck between them. Water pours from the northern lake to the southern one down the Tammerkoski rapids, and that drop powered the first factories in the 19th century. Red-brick mills made this the 'Manchester of Finland'. All around, forest and lakes stretch into the distance.","w":"A city on a narrow strip of land between a large lake to the north and another to the south.","f":["The Tammerkoski rapids fall about 18 m between lakes Näsijärvi and Pyhäjärvi.","Scottish engineer James Finlayson founded a cotton mill beside the rapids in 1820.","Tampere is the largest inland city in the Nordic countries."],"q":{"q":"What connects Tampere's two big lakes?","a":"The Tammerkoski rapids","x":["A flight of locks","An underground river"]}},"Q103565":{"t":"A planned city on the river that leaves Baikal","s":"Angarsk was laid out from 1948 as a planned city, and its neat grid of blocks still stands out between the Angara and the Kitoy. On its edge sprawls one of Siberia's biggest refinery and chemical complexes. The Angara here has only recently left Lake Baikal, and the city of Irkutsk lies a short way upstream.","w":"A neat grid of blocks between two rivers, with a large industrial complex alongside and taiga beyond.","f":["Construction of Angarsk began in 1948, and it became a city in 1951.","The Angara is the only river that flows out of Lake Baikal.","Angarsk has a museum devoted entirely to clocks."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows out of Lake Baikal past Angarsk?","a":"The Angara","x":["The Selenga","The Lena"]}},"Q42810":{"t":"The harbour that gave Impressionism its name","s":"The Seine ends in a wide estuary, and Le Havre guards its northern shore. Look for the fine line of the Pont de Normandie arching across the river mouth. The city was founded in 1517 by King Francis I as a port. In 1872 Claude Monet painted its harbour at dawn, and the title, 'Impression, Sunrise', gave a whole art movement its name.","w":"A port city on the northern shore of a wide estuary, with long docks and a slender bridge across the river mouth.","f":["Auguste Perret's concrete city centre has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 2005.","The Pont de Normandie, opened in 1995, is a cable-stayed bridge more than 2 km long.","Le Havre is one of France's busiest container ports."],"q":{"q":"Which painting of Le Havre gave Impressionism its name?","a":"Impression, Sunrise","x":["The Water Lilies","The Hay Wain"]}},"Q5196":{"t":"An oil city on the Sunzha below the Caucasus","s":"The plain north of the Caucasus is crossed here by the Sunzha, and Grozny spreads along its banks with low ridges rising to the north. On the southern horizon, forested foothills climb towards the snowy main range. The city began in 1818 as a Russian fortress, and oil made it grow: wells were drilled here in the 1890s, among the earliest in the empire.","w":"A large city on a river crossing a plain, low ridges to the north and forested mountains rising to the south.","f":["Grozny was founded in 1818 as the fortress of Groznaya on the Sunzha river.","Industrial oil production around Grozny began in the 1890s.","The Sunzha is a tributary of the Terek, which it joins east of the city."],"q":{"q":"On which river does Grozny stand?","a":"The Sunzha","x":["The Terek","The Kuban"]}},"Q6934":{"t":"A border river with a city on each bank","s":"Two countries, two cities, one river: across the Amur from Blagoveshchensk, less than a kilometre away, stands Heihe in China. At night their lights face each other over the dark water. Blagoveshchensk began in 1856 as a military post where the Zeya pours into the Amur, and flat, fertile farmland of the Amur region spreads all around it.","w":"Two cities facing each other across a wide river, where a large tributary joins from the north; flat farmland all around.","f":["The Chinese city of Heihe lies directly across the Amur, less than a kilometre away.","The city's name means 'Annunciation', after its first church.","The Zeya, which joins the Amur here, is more than 1,200 km long."],"q":{"q":"Which river joins the Amur at Blagoveshchensk?","a":"The Zeya","x":["The Ussuri","The Shilka"]}},"Q2214":{"t":"A stone kremlin on a spit between two rivers","s":"On a rocky spit where the little Pskova joins the broad Velikaya rises the Krom, Pskov's kremlin, and the city spreads around it on both banks. A short way downstream the Velikaya flows into Lake Pskov, the southern part of the vast Lake Peipus. Pskov was first mentioned in 903, making it one of Russia's oldest cities.","w":"A city on a broad river flowing north-west into a huge lake, with a smaller river joining in the centre.","f":["Pskov was first mentioned in the chronicles in 903.","The churches of the Pskov school of architecture became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2019.","Lake Peipus, into which the Velikaya flows, is one of the largest lakes in Europe."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows past the Pskov Krom?","a":"The Velikaya","x":["The Volkhov","The Narva"]}},"Q7859":{"t":"A ski slope right above an island capital","s":"A ski slope drops almost into the eastern streets of Yuzhno-Sakhalinsk, which lines a long valley between two ranges of forested mountains near the southern end of Sakhalin. To the south the valley opens onto Aniva Bay, where the port of Korsakov sits. Sakhalin is Russia's largest island, and its southern tip is only about 40 km from Japan's Hokkaido.","w":"A long city in a green valley between two forested mountain ranges, opening south to a wide bay.","f":["Sakhalin, about 950 km long, is the largest island in Russia.","Russia's first liquefied natural gas plant opened in 2009 at Prigorodnoye on Aniva Bay.","The Gorny Vozdukh ski area rises right at the city's eastern edge."],"q":{"q":"Which bay lies south of Yuzhno-Sakhalinsk?","a":"Aniva Bay","x":["Terpeniya Bay","Peter the Great Gulf"]}},"Q875":{"t":"A steppe capital among ancient burial mounds","s":"In a wide basin ringed by the Sayan and Kuznetsk Alatau mountains, the Abakan river joins the mighty Yenisei, and the city of Abakan stands in the angle between them. The basin is open steppe, warm by Siberian standards, and scattered with ancient burial mounds and standing stones. Across the Yenisei lies the older town of Minusinsk.","w":"A city where a river joins a much bigger one in open steppe, with mountains far around the basin.","f":["Abakan is the capital of Khakassia.","Abakan received city status only in 1931.","The Minusinsk Basin around Abakan is rich in ancient kurgans, standing stones and rock carvings."],"q":{"q":"Which great river does the Abakan join here?","a":"The Yenisei","x":["The Ob","The Angara"]}},"Q175517":{"t":"The biggest city this far north, cut off from roads","s":"No city of more than 100,000 people lies farther north than Norilsk, set in bare tundra near the foot of the Putorana Plateau. No road or railway links it to the rest of Russia: people fly in, and freight comes up the Yenisei to the port of Dudinka. Much of the year the land is white, and from early December the sun does not rise for weeks.","w":"A compact city and huge smelters on white or brown tundra, with lakes and the dark cliffs of a plateau to the east.","f":["Norilsk is the world's leading producer of palladium, a metal used in car exhaust catalysts.","The Putorana Plateau, rising east of the city, has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 2010.","A short railway links Norilsk only with Dudinka, its port on the Yenisei."],"q":{"q":"Norilsk is the world's leading producer of which metal?","a":"Palladium","x":["Tin","Uranium"]}},"Q23564":{"t":"A hot capital between canyons and a great lake","s":"Mountains wall in Podgorica on three sides, while to the south the flat Zeta plain runs down to Lake Skadar, the largest lake in the Balkans. The Morača river cuts through the city in a rocky channel and is joined here by the Ribnica. The name means 'below Gorica', after the small wooded hill beside the centre.","w":"A city on a flat plain ringed by bare mountains, with a river gorge through it and a large lake to the south.","f":["Lake Skadar, shared by Montenegro and Albania, is the largest lake in the Balkans.","Summer afternoons in Podgorica often pass 35 °C.","North of the city, the Morača has carved a canyon hundreds of metres deep."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Podgorica mean?","a":"Below Gorica hill","x":["Between two rivers","Beside the lake"]}},"Q6049":{"t":"The claimed centre of Asia, where the Yenisei is born","s":"Two mountain rivers, the Big and Little Yenisei, meet at Kyzyl and flow on together as the Yenisei, one of the longest rivers on Earth. Around the city spreads a dry basin of steppe, ringed by the mountains of Tuva. On the riverbank an obelisk marks what is claimed to be the geographical centre of Asia. The name Kyzyl simply means 'red'.","w":"A small city where two rivers meet, in a dry steppe basin ringed by mountains.","f":["The Biy-Khem and the Kaa-Khem, the Big and Little Yenisei, join at Kyzyl.","Kyzyl is the capital of Tuva, a republic famous for throat singing.","The Arzhan burial mounds in Tuva held some of the richest Scythian-era gold ever found."],"q":{"q":"What is said to lie at Kyzyl?","a":"The centre of Asia","x":["The source of the Ob","Asia's coldest point"]}},"Q1842":{"t":"A fortress city on cliffs above deep gorges","s":"Sheer cliffs drop from Luxembourg's old town, on its rocky plateau, into the green gorges of the Alzette and the Pétrusse. Its fortifications were once so formidable that it was called the Gibraltar of the North. East of the old town, the Kirchberg plateau carries European institutions, and all around lie the rolling woods and fields of a small country.","w":"A city cut by deep, green river gorges, with an old town on a rocky plateau and new districts on a plateau to the east.","f":["Luxembourg's old quarters and fortifications have been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1994.","The Bock casemates, tunnels carved into the rock, once ran for about 23 km.","Luxembourg, with Brussels and Strasbourg, is one of the main seats of European Union institutions."],"q":{"q":"What nickname did Luxembourg's fortress earn?","a":"The Gibraltar of the North","x":["The Rock of Europe","The Key of the Rhine"]}},"Q196946":{"t":"A dam town where the Ilim meets the Angara","s":"Taiga runs unbroken to the horizon, and through it the Angara has been turned into a long, branching reservoir. At its lower end stands the Ust-Ilimsk dam, and beside it the town, built from the late 1960s. Its name means 'mouth of the Ilim': the reservoir drowned the lower valley of that river too, reaching deep into the forest.","w":"A long, many-armed reservoir in endless taiga, with a straight dam across the river and a town beside it.","f":["The Ust-Ilimsk hydroelectric station on the Angara has a capacity of about 3,840 MW.","The Ust-Ilimsk reservoir stretches for hundreds of kilometres up the Angara and the Ilim.","Timber and pulp from the surrounding taiga are the town's other main industry."],"q":{"q":"What does 'Ust' mean in the name Ust-Ilimsk?","a":"River mouth","x":["Upper reaches","Fortress"]}},"Q133529":{"t":"A coal town in the tundra with no road out","s":"About 100 km beyond the Arctic Circle, in open tundra near the Polar Urals, stands Vorkuta. No road connects it to the rest of Russia; the railway is its lifeline. Coal mines are scattered around it, and a ring road links the city with a chain of mining settlements. In winter the sun disappears for weeks.","w":"A small city on white or brown tundra, linked by a looping road to outlying settlements, with mountains to the east.","f":["Vorkuta is one of the largest towns north of the Arctic Circle in Europe.","The Pechora coal basin around Vorkuta produces high-quality coking coal."],"q":{"q":"What is mined around Vorkuta?","a":"Coal","x":["Gold","Diamonds"]}},"Q26476":{"t":"A stone town between two hills on a Baltic bay","s":"Two steep, wooded hills stand guard over Sundsvall, which fills the gap between them at the head of a bay on the Gulf of Bothnia. In the 19th century the coast here was lined with sawmills, and timber made the town rich. Its centre, rebuilt in stone in the 1890s, is still called Stenstaden, 'the stone town'. Alnö island lies just offshore to the east.","w":"A town at the head of a bay between two wooded hills, with a large island just offshore.","f":["In the late 19th century, dozens of sawmills made the Sundsvall area one of the world's great timber exporters.","The stone-built centre, Stenstaden, dates from the 1890s.","The High Coast, a UNESCO site known for rapid land uplift, lies a short way to the north."],"q":{"q":"What made Sundsvall rich in the 19th century?","a":"Sawmills and timber","x":["Iron mines","Herring fishing"]}},"Q2415":{"t":"Norway's southern tip, laid out by a king","s":"A tangle of rocky skerries and pine-clad islets fringes the coast at Kristiansand, near Norway's southern tip. The town was founded in 1641 by King Christian IV, who gave it his name and a strict grid of square blocks, still visible in the centre. Ferries leave from here across the Skagerrak to Denmark.","w":"A small gridded town on a rocky coast full of islets, with forested hills behind.","f":["Kristiansand's centre, Kvadraturen, keeps the square grid laid out in 1641.","Ferries link Kristiansand with Hirtshals in Denmark, across the Skagerrak.","Kristiansand is the largest city of Sørlandet, Norway's southern coast."],"q":{"q":"Which king founded Kristiansand in 1641?","a":"Christian IV","x":["Frederick III","Haakon VII"]}},"Q208149":{"t":"A mid-Atlantic port beside blue and green crater lakes","s":"São Miguel rises alone from the middle of the Atlantic, and Ponta Delgada lines its southern shore. West of the city, look for a great round crater holding two lakes side by side, one blue and one green: Sete Cidades. The island is patched with green pastures and hedges of blue hydrangeas.","w":"A white town along the south coast of a green volcanic island, with a large crater holding two lakes to the north-west.","f":["São Miguel is the largest island of the Azores.","Tea has been grown on São Miguel since the 19th century, one of the few tea plantations in Europe.","The Sete Cidades crater, about 5 km across, lies about 15 km north-west of the city."],"q":{"q":"On which island does Ponta Delgada stand?","a":"São Miguel","x":["Terceira","Madeira"]}},"Q125080":{"t":"A coast where the land keeps rising from the sea","s":"Off Vaasa, the sea is scattered with thousands of low islands and skerries, and every year there is a little more land. Freed from the weight of the Ice Age glaciers, the ground here still rises by several millimetres a year, so new islets surface and bays turn into meadows. The Kvarken archipelago is a UNESCO site for this very reason.","w":"A coastal town facing a sea filled with countless low, rocky islands, with flat farmland inland.","f":["The land around Vaasa rises about 8 mm a year as it rebounds from the Ice Age.","The Replot Bridge, about 1 km long, is the longest bridge in Finland.","The Kvarken Archipelago shares a UNESCO listing with Sweden's High Coast."],"q":{"q":"Why is new land slowly appearing off Vaasa?","a":"Post-glacial land uplift","x":["River silt","Volcanic activity"]}},"Q1863":{"t":"Europe's highest capital, deep in the Pyrenees","s":"Steep Pyrenean slopes, snowy for much of the year, leave room only for a narrow valley, and Andorra la Vella fills its floor along the Gran Valira river. At about 1,023 m above sea level, it is the highest capital city in Europe. From the air the whole country looks tiny: a knot of mountain valleys between France and Spain.","w":"A narrow town squeezed along a mountain valley floor, surrounded by high, often snowy Pyrenean ridges.","f":["Andorra la Vella lies at about 1,023 m, the highest of Europe's capitals.","Andorra has two co-princes: the Bishop of Urgell and the President of France.","Andorra covers about 468 km²."],"q":{"q":"Who is Andorra's co-prince alongside the Bishop of Urgell?","a":"The President of France","x":["The King of Spain","The Pope"]}},"Q26268":{"t":"A town that moved to follow the sea","s":"The Lule river meets the Gulf of Bothnia among an archipelago of more than a thousand islands, and Luleå sits on a peninsula in their midst. The town moved here in 1649: the land was rising so fast after the Ice Age that its old harbour had become too shallow. In winter the sea freezes, and roads are ploughed across the ice to the islands.","w":"A town on a peninsula at a river mouth, surrounded by a maze of forested islands in a shallow sea.","f":["Gammelstad, the old church town with more than 400 wooden cottages, is a UNESCO World Heritage site.","The Luleå archipelago has about 1,300 islands.","In winter, ice roads are ploughed across the frozen sea to some of the islands."],"q":{"q":"Why did Luleå move to a new site in 1649?","a":"Rising land made the harbour too shallow","x":["The river changed its course","The king wanted a sea fortress"]}},"Q105477":{"t":"A giant diamond pit at the edge of town","s":"Look for a vast round hole beside the town, its sides spiralling down in terraces: the Mir mine, one of the largest man-made pits on Earth. Diamond-bearing rock was found here in 1955, and the town of Mirny grew up in the Yakutian taiga beside it. In winter the temperature here can drop below −40 °C.","w":"A huge round pit with terraced sides at the edge of a small town, in endless taiga.","f":["The Mir pit is about 525 m deep and about 1.2 km across.","Mirny is the centre of Russia's diamond industry.","Open-pit mining at Mir ended in 2001; mining continued underground."],"q":{"q":"What was mined in the great pit at Mirny?","a":"Diamonds","x":["Gold","Copper"]}},"Q235":{"t":"A whole country squeezed onto a strip of coast","s":"Between the mountains and the sea, a tiny country packs itself into a strip of coast only a few kilometres long. Monaco's towers crowd around a harbour full of yachts, and some districts stand on land reclaimed from the Mediterranean. Behind it, bare limestone slopes rise steeply towards the Alps, and France surrounds it on every landward side.","w":"A dense strip of towers around a small harbour on a steep coast, between the sea and bare mountains.","f":["At about 2 km², Monaco is the world's second-smallest country, after Vatican City.","The Grimaldi family has ruled Monaco, with interruptions, since 1297.","The Fontvieille district was built on land reclaimed from the sea."],"q":{"q":"Which is the only country smaller than Monaco?","a":"Vatican City","x":["San Marino","Liechtenstein"]}},"Q1844":{"t":"A tiny capital on the Rhine, twice cut off from the sea","s":"Straight and wide, the Rhine runs down a flat-floored Alpine valley here, marking the border with Switzerland. On its eastern side, Vaduz spreads along the foot of steep, forested mountains, with the prince's castle perched on a ledge above. Liechtenstein itself is only about 25 km long, so from cruise height the whole country fits in a single glance.","w":"A small town beside a straight, broad river in a flat Alpine valley, backed by steep forested slopes.","f":["Liechtenstein is one of only two doubly landlocked countries in the world; the other is Uzbekistan.","Liechtenstein covers about 160 km².","Vaduz Castle, above the town, is the residence of the Prince of Liechtenstein."],"q":{"q":"Which other country is, like Liechtenstein, doubly landlocked?","a":"Uzbekistan","x":["Kazakhstan","Switzerland"]}},"Q103717":{"t":"Lapland's capital, a stone's throw from the Arctic Circle","s":"The Kemijoki and the Ounasjoki, two big rivers, meet at Rovaniemi amid endless pine forest and bog. The Arctic Circle runs only about 8 km north of the centre, and a whole tourist village has grown up on the line itself, home to Santa Claus. In December the sun barely clears the horizon, and the northern lights often flicker overhead.","w":"A town where two wide rivers meet in flat, forested country, snow-covered for about half the year.","f":["Santa Claus Village stands right on the Arctic Circle, about 8 km north of the centre.","Alvar Aalto's town plan lays out Rovaniemi's main roads in the shape of a reindeer's head and antlers.","The Kemijoki, which flows through the town, is Finland's longest river."],"q":{"q":"Whose official 'village' stands on the Arctic Circle near Rovaniemi?","a":"Santa Claus","x":["The Snow Queen","The Moomins"]}},"Q1848":{"t":"The world's oldest republic, on a three-towered ridge","s":"A lone limestone ridge rears up from the rolling hills behind the Adriatic coast: Monte Titano, crowned by three towers and the old town of San Marino. The whole country, about 61 km², is surrounded by Italy. Tradition says a stonemason named Marinus founded a community here in 301, which would make it the oldest surviving republic in the world.","w":"A steep, isolated ridge with a town along its crest, rising from green hills a short way inland from the Adriatic coast.","f":["Monte Titano rises to 739 m, with three medieval towers along its ridge.","San Marino covers about 61 km² and is entirely surrounded by Italy.","The historic centre and Monte Titano became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2008."],"q":{"q":"According to tradition, in which year was San Marino founded?","a":"301","x":["1291","1054"]}},"Q177782":{"t":"Sakhalin's northern oil town","s":"Near the northern end of Sakhalin, the island flattens into a low, boggy plain of larch woods and lakes, and Okha grew up among the oil fields there. Oil seeping from the ground was noticed in the 19th century. The town's name is thought to come from an Evenki word meaning 'bad', after the oily, undrinkable water of the local river.","w":"A small town on a flat, boggy plain near the northern tip of a long, narrow island, with oil fields around it.","f":["The Okha oil field was discovered in 1880, and industrial production began in 1921.","Okha received town status in 1938.","Okha is the northernmost town on Sakhalin."],"q":{"q":"What resource did Okha grow up around?","a":"Oil","x":["Coal","Gold"]}},"Q157493":{"t":"A Lena river port feeding the diamond fields","s":"Wide and brown, the Lena winds through the Yakutian taiga, and Lensk strings out along its bank. Much of what the diamond mines of Mirny need, from fuel to machinery, comes upriver by barge in the short summer and is unloaded here for the long road north. Until 1963 the town was called Mukhtuya.","w":"A town strung along a wide river winding through endless taiga, with a road heading north through the forest.","f":["The Lena, about 4,400 km long, is one of the longest rivers in the world.","Mirny, the diamond town supplied through Lensk, lies about 200 km to the north.","The river is navigable here only in the ice-free months, roughly from late May to October."],"q":{"q":"What was Lensk called until 1963?","a":"Mukhtuya","x":["Nezametny","Ust-Kut"]}},"Q103273":{"t":"A gold-rush town named after an 'unnoticeable' creek","s":"Rounded, forested hills of the Aldan Highlands roll away in every direction, scarred here and there by old gold workings. In 1923 prospectors found gold beside a stream called Nezametny, 'the unnoticeable one', and a rush followed. The town that grew up took the stream's name and became Aldan only in 1939. The railway and highway to Yakutsk now pass through it.","w":"A small town in rolling, forested highlands, with a railway and a road running north through the taiga.","f":["Aldan was founded in 1923 beside a newly found gold deposit and became a town in 1932.","Despite its name, the town stands not on the Aldan river but on a small stream in the Aldan Highlands.","The Amur–Yakutsk railway runs through Aldan on its way north towards Yakutsk."],"q":{"q":"What was Aldan called until 1939?","a":"Nezametny","x":["Mukhtuya","Zolotoy"]}},"Q721374":{"t":"A far-north town that lit its streets first","s":"Hammerfest clings to a bay on the island of Kvaløya, among bare, rounded hills above the Norwegian Sea. It is one of the northernmost towns in the world: the sun does not set from mid-May to late July, and in midwinter it does not rise. Just offshore, on the islet of Melkøya, gas from beneath the Barents Sea is chilled into liquid for shipping.","w":"A small town around a bay on a bare, hilly island, with an industrial plant on an islet just offshore.","f":["In 1891 Hammerfest became the first town in Northern Europe with electric street lighting.","The Meridian Column here marks the northern end of the Struve Geodetic Arc, a UNESCO World Heritage site.","The Melkøya plant, opened in 2007, was Europe's first large liquefied natural gas export plant."],"q":{"q":"What does Hammerfest's Meridian Column mark?","a":"The end of the Struve Geodetic Arc","x":["The Arctic Circle","Europe's northernmost point"]}},"Q1025866":{"t":"A gold town in the frozen heart of Yakutia","s":"Deep among the bare ridges of the Chersky Range, the Nera flows into the Indigirka, and Ust-Nera takes its name from that meeting point. It is the centre of the Oymyakon district, one of the coldest inhabited places on Earth, where winter temperatures often fall below −50 °C. Gold brought people here, and the Kolyma Highway crosses the Indigirka by the settlement.","w":"A small settlement where two rivers meet in a wide valley among bare, often snowy mountain ridges.","f":["Ust-Nera is the administrative centre of the Oymyakon district, known as a pole of cold.","A concrete bridge carrying the Kolyma Highway across the Indigirka opened here in 1978.","Gold mining is the main industry around Ust-Nera."],"q":{"q":"Which river does the Nera flow into at Ust-Nera?","a":"The Indigirka","x":["The Kolyma","The Yana"]}},"Q7054":{"t":"A mountain mining town famous for its crystals","s":"A narrow valley in the forested Sikhote-Alin mountains holds Dalnegorsk, about 30 km from the Sea of Japan. The hills around it are riddled with ore: lead, zinc and boron have been mined here for decades. To mineral collectors worldwide, the name means superb crystals, from glassy calcite to pale green datolite. Until 1972 the town was called Tetyukhe.","w":"A small town strung along a narrow valley in forested mountains, with a river running south-east to the sea.","f":["Dalnegorsk is the only place in Russia where boron is mined.","The boron plant at Dalnegorsk began work in 1965.","The Rudnaya river flows from the town to the Sea of Japan."],"q":{"q":"What was Dalnegorsk called until 1972?","a":"Tetyukhe","x":["Iman","Suchan"]}},"Q648799":{"t":"Russia's first foothold on the Pacific","s":"A low, narrow spit between the open sea and a shallow estuary carries Okhotsk, where the Okhota and Kukhtuy rivers reach the coast. Founded in 1647, it was the first Russian settlement on the Pacific shore, and for more than a century its main port for voyages to Kamchatka and Alaska. Behind it, flat, lake-dotted lowland runs to low hills.","w":"A small settlement on a thin spit between a wide sea and a river estuary, backed by flat lowland and low hills.","f":["For about half the year the sea off Okhotsk is covered with ice.","Vitus Bering's expeditions built ships at Okhotsk in the 18th century.","The Sea of Okhotsk is named after the town, and the town after the Okhota river."],"q":{"q":"Which explorer's expeditions built ships at Okhotsk?","a":"Vitus Bering","x":["Yerofey Khabarov","Semyon Dezhnev"]}},"Q2359080":{"t":"A tiny town next to a giant permafrost scar","s":"About 10 km south-east of Batagay, look for a pale, tadpole-shaped scar on a forested hillside: the Batagaika crater, a slump about 1 km long where the permafrost is thawing. Each summer its cliffs retreat further, exposing ice and soil tens of thousands of years old. Batagay itself sits on the Yana, in one of the coldest corners of Yakutia.","w":"A small settlement on a river in taiga, with a large, pale, tadpole-shaped scar on a hillside to the south-east.","f":["The Batagaika crater is about 1 km long, around 800 m wide and in places 50–100 m deep.","The crater began as a small gully after the forest on the slope was cleared in the 1960s, and has grown ever since.","Batagay is the centre of the Verkhoyansk district, one of the coldest places in the Northern Hemisphere."],"q":{"q":"What is the Batagaika crater near Batagay?","a":"A thawing permafrost slump","x":["A meteorite crater","A volcanic caldera"]}},"Q1003237":{"t":"A coal-mining settlement in the Transbaikal hills","s":"Low, forested ridges of Transbaikalia roll to the horizon, larch and pine on the slopes and open meadows in the valleys. In one of those valleys lies Bukachacha, which grew up around a coal mine opened in 1930. A branch railway carried its coal south to the Trans-Siberian. The mine closed in 1998, and the settlement has been shrinking ever since.","w":"A small settlement in a valley among rolling, forested ridges, with a railway line heading off to the south.","f":["The Bukachacha coal mine began work in November 1930.","A railway branch from Chernyshevsk, on the Trans-Siberian, reached the settlement in 1932.","In 1979 Bukachacha had more than 9,000 inhabitants."],"q":{"q":"What was mined at Bukachacha?","a":"Coal","x":["Gold","Tin"]}},"Q1305638":{"t":"Chukotka's airport settlement across the estuary","s":"Across the wide Anadyr estuary from Anadyr, on a flat, lake-dotted shore of tundra, sits Ugolnye Kopi, whose name means 'coal mines'. It began in 1929 as a miners' settlement on a brown-coal deposit that fuelled Anadyr. Today it is best known for the airport serving Chukotka's capital: passengers cross the estuary by boat in summer and over the ice in winter.","w":"A small settlement on flat tundra beside a wide estuary, with a long runway nearby and a town on the opposite shore.","f":["The settlement was founded in 1929 beside a brown-coal deposit.","Ugolny airport, beside the settlement, serves Anadyr, the capital of Chukotka.","Brown-coal mining remains the settlement's main industry."],"q":{"q":"Which city does the airport at Ugolnye Kopi serve?","a":"Anadyr","x":["Pevek","Provideniya"]}},"Q2621673":{"t":"An old Cossack fort just past the Arctic Circle","s":"A giant of a river, kilometres wide and braided with sandy islands, the Lena flows north here through larch taiga towards the Arctic Ocean. Zhigansk stands on its left bank, just north of the Arctic Circle. Cossacks built a fort here in 1632, the same year Yakutsk was founded, which makes it one of the oldest Russian settlements in Yakutia.","w":"A small village on the west bank of a huge river full of sandy islands, with larch forest all around.","f":["In 1783 Zhigansk became a district town, but it lost that status in 1805.","Zhigansk is the centre of a district where many Evenks live."],"q":{"q":"In which year was the fort at Zhigansk founded?","a":"1632","x":["1586","1703"]}},"Q918830":{"t":"The last stop before the North Pole","s":"Far out on the Taymyr tundra, the Khatanga river winds towards the Laptev Sea, and on its high bank stands the settlement of the same name. It began in the 17th century as a winter outpost for collecting fur tribute. Today its long runway makes it a staging point for expeditions to the North Pole, and a museum here keeps mammoth remains found in the frozen ground.","w":"A small settlement on the high bank of a broad river winding across flat tundra dotted with lakes.","f":["Khatanga was founded in 1626.","Khatanga's mammoth museum opened in 2007.","Cellars dug into the permafrost of the riverbank stay below freezing all year round."],"q":{"q":"Khatanga is a staging point for expeditions to where?","a":"The North Pole","x":["The Pole of Cold","Wrangel Island"]}},"Q2312088":{"t":"A tundra outpost on the lower Indigirka","s":"Beyond the Arctic Circle, the lower Indigirka winds across tundra so riddled with lakes that there seems to be more water than land. Chokurdakh stands on the river's left bank, the centre of a vast and almost empty district. Nearby, the Kytalyk protected area guards a nesting ground of the Siberian white crane, one of the rarest cranes on Earth.","w":"A small settlement on a big river crossing flat tundra covered with countless round lakes.","f":["Chokurdakh is the centre of the Allaikhovsky district, which covers more than 100,000 km².","The Indigirka reaches the East Siberian Sea through a delta north of the settlement.","Kytalyk, near Chokurdakh, is world-famous as a nesting place of the Siberian crane."],"q":{"q":"Which rare bird nests in the Kytalyk area near Chokurdakh?","a":"The Siberian crane","x":["The Dalmatian pelican","The great bustard"]}},"Q237":{"t":"The world's smallest country, inside a city","s":"Inside Rome, a short way west of the Tiber, lies a walled patch less than a kilometre across: Vatican City, the smallest country in the world. It covers about 44 hectares, less than many city parks. From cruise height it is only a speck within the sprawl of Rome, yet it has its own government, flag, stamps and even a railway station.","w":"A tiny walled patch in the western part of Rome, a short way west of the Tiber — too small to pick out easily.","f":["At about 0.44 km², Vatican City is the smallest country in the world.","The dome of St Peter's Basilica, designed by Michelangelo, rises to about 136 m.","The Swiss Guard has protected the Pope since 1506."],"q":{"q":"Since which year has the Swiss Guard protected the Pope?","a":"1506","x":["1648","1870"]}},"Q818106":{"t":"A 'city of lakes' on the gold reef east of Johannesburg","s":"Scattered sheets of water and flat-topped, pale-yellow mine dumps give Benoni away. The town grew with the gold mines of the East Rand, and its many lakes and pans earned it the nickname 'City of Lakes'. Its name is biblical, Hebrew for 'son of my sorrow', reportedly chosen by a surveyor who struggled to map the awkwardly shaped farm.","w":"A grid of suburbs dotted with small lakes and pale mine dumps, just east of Johannesburg's main airport.","f":["O. R. Tambo International, Johannesburg's main airport, lies only about 10 km to the west.","Benoni stands on the Highveld at roughly 1,600 metres above sea level.","The actress Charlize Theron was born in Benoni."],"q":{"q":"What does the Hebrew name Benoni mean?","a":"Son of my sorrow","x":["City of gold","Place of many lakes"]}},"Q81788":{"t":"Where the city stops and the pyramid plateau begins","s":"The line here is razor-sharp: green fields and dense streets on the Nile's west bank, then, abruptly, the tan desert plateau where the pyramids stand. The Great Pyramid, built for the pharaoh Khufu around 2560 BC, remained the tallest structure made by humans for about 3,800 years. Across the river lies Cairo, one continuous sprawl with Giza.","w":"The western edge of greater Cairo, where the green Nile valley ends against a pale desert plateau.","f":["The three great pyramids belong to Khufu, his son Khafre and Khafre's son Menkaure.","The Great Pyramid was about 146 metres tall when built; with its casing stones gone, it is now a little lower.","Ancient Egyptians buried their dead on the west bank of the Nile, the side of the setting sun."],"q":{"q":"For which pharaoh was the Great Pyramid built?","a":"Khufu","x":["Khafre","Ramesses II"]}},"Q183298":{"t":"A sea of rust-brown roofs on low green hills","s":"Ibadan's old heart is famous for the colour of its roofs: countless sheets of corrugated iron, weathered over decades to rust-brown, spread across a string of low hills. The city began around 1829 as a military camp and grew into one of the largest in West Africa. Its name is often explained as Eba Odan, 'beside the savanna', for here the forest gives way to open country.","w":"A vast, brownish city spread over low hills, about 120 km north-east of Lagos.","f":["The University of Ibadan, founded in 1948, is the oldest university in Nigeria.","Ibadan is the capital of Oyo State."],"q":{"q":"Ibadan is the capital of which Nigerian state?","a":"Oyo","x":["Ogun","Osun"]}},"Q35997":{"t":"A city wrapped around the end of a long blue gulf","s":"A long gulf snakes in from the Aegean, and İzmir wraps around its innermost end, climbing the hills on three sides. As ancient Smyrna it was one of the cities that claimed to be the birthplace of Homer. Towards the gulf's mouth, the Gediz delta spreads flat and marshy, its rectangular salt pans flashing white in the sun.","w":"A big city ringing the head of a long, narrow Aegean gulf, with a flat delta and salt pans towards its mouth.","f":["İzmir is Turkey's third-largest city, after Istanbul and Ankara.","Flocks of flamingos feed among the salt pans of the Gediz delta.","The ruins of ancient Ephesus lie about 60 km to the south."],"q":{"q":"What was İzmir called in ancient times?","a":"Smyrna","x":["Pergamon","Halicarnassus"]}},"Q180921":{"t":"On the west bank where the Blue and White Niles meet","s":"Right opposite, two great rivers join: the Blue Nile from the Ethiopian highlands and the White Nile from the south. Omdurman stretches along the west bank, a low, sand-coloured city that fades into the desert. In the 1880s the Mahdists made it their capital; today it forms the 'Three Towns' with Khartoum and Khartoum North across the water.","w":"A low, sandy city on the west bank, facing the point where two branches of the Nile join.","f":["From here the Nile still has roughly 3,000 km to flow north to the Mediterranean.","The Blue Nile flows out of Lake Tana in the Ethiopian highlands.","In the flood season, the Blue Nile brings most of the water that passes Omdurman."],"q":{"q":"Which two rivers meet opposite Omdurman?","a":"The Blue Nile and the White Nile","x":["The Nile and the Atbara","The White Nile and the Sobat"]}},"Q160320":{"t":"Where Saudi Arabia's oil story began","s":"In 1938 a well on the Dammam Dome, just outside the city, finally struck oil in commercial quantities, the first in Saudi Arabia. A fishing coast turned into one sprawling metropolis with Khobar and Dhahran. Look east over the milky-turquoise shallows: a causeway strides about 25 km across the sea to the island of Bahrain.","w":"A flat, pale city on the Gulf shore, with a long causeway running east across turquoise shallows to Bahrain.","f":["The well that struck oil in 1938, Dammam No. 7, was later nicknamed the Prosperity Well.","King Fahd International Airport, north-west of the city, is one of the largest airports in the world by area.","The King Fahd Causeway to Bahrain opened in 1986."],"q":{"q":"Which country does the causeway from Dammam's coast reach?","a":"Bahrain","x":["Qatar","Kuwait"]}},"Q132830":{"t":"The prawn river that gave Cameroon its name","s":"Portuguese sailors in the 1470s found the Wouri estuary teeming with prawns and called it Rio dos Camarões, the river of prawns. The name spread to the whole country. Today Douala, Cameroon's biggest city and main port, sprawls over the muddy banks among dark mangroves. On a clear day the huge volcano of Mount Cameroon rises about 60 km to the west.","w":"A sprawling city on a brown estuary fringed with dark-green mangroves; the bulk of Mount Cameroon rises to the west.","f":["Mount Cameroon, an active volcano over 4,000 m high, is the highest peak in West and Central Africa.","Douala is Cameroon's largest city and busiest port, although Yaoundé is the capital."],"q":{"q":"What was the Wouri, which named Cameroon, teeming with?","a":"Prawns","x":["Crocodiles","Oysters"]}},"Q182059":{"t":"The Asante capital, with a meteorite lake nearby","s":"Kumasi was the seat of the Asante kings, whose wealth rested on gold and whose sacred symbol is the Golden Stool. The city spreads over green, rolling hills in the forest belt. About 30 km to the south-east, look for an almost perfectly round lake in a ring of hills: Lake Bosumtwi fills a crater blasted by a meteorite about a million years ago.","w":"A large city in green, forested hills; to the south-east, a round lake inside a ring of wooded hills.","f":["Lake Bosumtwi is about 8 km across and is Ghana's only natural lake.","Kejetia Market in the city centre is one of the largest markets in West Africa.","Its parks and trees earned Kumasi the nickname 'Garden City'."],"q":{"q":"What formed Lake Bosumtwi, near Kumasi?","a":"A meteorite impact","x":["A volcanic eruption","A landslide dam"]}},"Q40738":{"t":"The Ottomans' first capital, beneath a snowy mountain","s":"A long band of city runs along the foot of Uludağ, the mountain the Greeks knew as Mysian Olympus, white with snow through much of the winter. The Ottomans took Bursa in 1326 and made it their first great capital; the founders of the dynasty are buried here. Silk from its bazaars once travelled far along the caravan routes.","w":"A city stretched along the northern foot of a high, often snowy massif, with a flat green plain in front.","f":["Uludağ rises to 2,543 metres and is one of Turkey's best-known ski resorts.","İskender kebab was created in Bursa in the 19th century.","Bursa and the nearby village of Cumalıkızık are a UNESCO site marking the birth of the Ottoman Empire."],"q":{"q":"Which mountain rises above Bursa?","a":"Uludağ","x":["Erciyes","Palandöken"]}},"Q3861":{"t":"A crescent bay on a flat desert peninsula","s":"Doha curls around an almost round bay on the east coast of the Qatar peninsula. The seven-kilometre Corniche follows the curve, with a cluster of towers at its northern end. Not long ago this was a small town of pearl divers and fishermen. Beyond the towers, an artificial island called The Pearl reaches into the sea, a nod to that past.","w":"A crescent-shaped bay on a flat, sandy peninsula, a cluster of towers on its north side and artificial islands beyond.","f":["The Qatar peninsula juts north into the Persian Gulf for roughly 180 km.","Before oil, pearls were Doha's main source of wealth.","Summer afternoons here usually climb above 40°C."],"q":{"q":"Before oil, which trade sustained Doha?","a":"Pearl diving","x":["Spice trading","Copper mining"]}},"Q208318":{"t":"A colonial capital named after crocodiles","s":"The Kaduna River swings through the city in wide, lazy loops, and it gave the place its name: kada is the Hausa word for crocodile, and the river was once full of them. In 1916 the British moved the capital of Northern Nigeria here from Zungeru. The city grew into a major centre of textiles and industry on the high, open savanna.","w":"A large city on open savanna, cut by a broad, looping river.","f":["The Kaduna River flows on to join the Niger, Nigeria's greatest river.","Kaduna is the capital of Kaduna State.","The city stands on a plateau at around 600 metres above sea level."],"q":{"q":"Which animal gave Kaduna its name?","a":"The crocodile","x":["The hippopotamus","The lion"]}},"Q36529":{"t":"Tehran's western neighbour at the foot of the Alborz","s":"Tehran's sprawl barely breaks before Karaj begins, about 40 km to the west. Behind the city the Alborz rises like a wall, and the Karaj River cuts a valley north into the mountains, where the Amir Kabir Dam holds a reservoir for the capital. The same valley carries the Chalus road, a famous mountain route over the range to the Caspian Sea.","w":"A large city west of Tehran at the foot of a high mountain wall, with a valley and reservoir leading north.","f":["Karaj stands at about 1,300 metres, on the edge of the Iranian plateau.","The Chalus road crosses the Alborz and drops through dense forest to the Caspian coast.","Karaj is the capital of Alborz province."],"q":{"q":"Which mountain range rises behind Karaj?","a":"The Alborz","x":["The Zagros","The Kopet Dag"]}},"Q80053":{"t":"A bazaar city between a volcano and a salt lake","s":"Tabriz fills a broad valley in Iran's north-west, with the massive old volcano Sahand to the south and, further west, the shallow salt expanse of Lake Urmia, which can turn reddish in summer. For centuries caravans on the Silk Road stopped here. Its covered bazaar, a vast maze of brick vaults, is one of the oldest in the Middle East.","w":"A big city in a wide valley; a massive volcanic mountain rises to the south and a pale salt lake lies to the west.","f":["The Tabriz Historic Bazaar Complex has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 2010.","Mount Sahand rises to about 3,700 metres.","From 1501 Tabriz was the first capital of the Safavid dynasty."],"q":{"q":"Which volcanic mountain rises south of Tabriz?","a":"Sahand","x":["Damavand","Sabalan"]}},"Q83317":{"t":"On the Tigris, facing the ruins of Nineveh","s":"The Tigris runs through Mosul, and on its east bank lie the remains of Nineveh, once the capital of the Assyrian Empire. In its royal library, archaeologists found clay tablets that include parts of the Epic of Gilgamesh. Mosul later grew rich on trade and weaving, and became famous enough to give a fabric its name: muslin.","w":"A city on both banks of the Tigris, amid the rolling farmland of northern Mesopotamia.","f":["The library of King Ashurbanipal at Nineveh held many thousands of clay tablets.","The Tigris rises in the mountains of Turkey and joins the Euphrates in southern Iraq."],"q":{"q":"Which fabric is named after Mosul?","a":"Muslin","x":["Damask","Calico"]}},"Q319660":{"t":"The diamond town on the Kasai savanna","s":"Mbuji-Mayi grew around diamonds. Under the red earth of the Kasai lie some of the world's richest deposits of industrial diamonds, and the city spread fast around the mines along the Mbuji-Mayi River. From high above it is a vast, low sprawl of rust-red soil and tin roofs, scarred by diggings and ringed by savanna and strips of forest.","w":"A large, low city of red soil and tin roofs by a river, with mining scars and savanna around it.","f":["Until 1966 the city was called Bakwanga.","Most diamonds mined here are industrial stones rather than gems.","Mbuji-Mayi is the capital of Kasai-Oriental province."],"q":{"q":"Which gem made Mbuji-Mayi's fortune?","a":"Diamonds","x":["Emeralds","Sapphires"]}},"Q38545":{"t":"A cotton-plain city on the Seyhan","s":"The Seyhan River leaves a big reservoir and flows straight through Adana, then out across the Çukurova, the flat, fertile plain of ancient Cilicia, chequered with cotton fields and citrus groves. The Taurus Mountains wall it in to the north. In the old centre a Roman stone bridge still spans the river, one of the oldest surviving bridges in the world.","w":"A large city where a river leaves a reservoir and crosses a flat, chequered plain, with mountains to the north.","f":["Adana kebab, hand-minced meat grilled on a wide skewer, is named after the city.","The Çukurova is one of Turkey's main cotton-growing regions."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Adana?","a":"The Seyhan","x":["The Göksu","The Kızılırmak"]}},"Q320704":{"t":"Bronze casters and giant earthworks of an old kingdom","s":"For centuries this was the capital of the Kingdom of Benin, famous for its bronze and brass plaques and heads. Its rulers ringed their lands with ramparts and moats, the Walls of Benin, a web of earthworks estimated to run for thousands of kilometres. Today the city spreads through the rainforest belt, its main roads radiating from a central square like spokes of a wheel.","w":"A big city in the green forest belt, main roads converging on a round central square like wheel spokes.","f":["Bronze casters still work in guilds on Igun Street, as they have for centuries.","Benin City is in Nigeria; the neighbouring country of Benin took its name from the Bight of Benin.","The city is the capital of Edo State."],"q":{"q":"Benin City is the capital of which Nigerian state?","a":"Edo","x":["Delta","Ondo"]}},"Q289693":{"t":"An emirate with shores on two different seas","s":"Sharjah and Dubai have grown into one continuous city along the Gulf shore, with a lagoon curling into Sharjah's heart. Yet the emirate also holds stretches of coast on the far side of the peninsula, on the Gulf of Oman, the only one in the UAE with both. Long before the skyscrapers, Imperial Airways flights to India stopped here, at the region's first airport, opened in 1932.","w":"A dense city merging with Dubai to the south-west, wrapped around a lagoon; the desert begins at its inland edge.","f":["Sharjah's first airfield, Al Mahatta, is now an aviation museum.","UNESCO named Sharjah the Cultural Capital of the Arab World in 1998.","Its exclaves on the Gulf of Oman include the port of Khor Fakkan."],"q":{"q":"What opened in Sharjah in 1932, a first for the region?","a":"An airport","x":["A university","An oil refinery"]}},"Q3320803":{"t":"A market town among the rocky Yoruba Hills","s":"Bare rock domes and outcrops poke through the green here: this is Akoko country, a cluster of dozens of small towns in the Yoruba Hills of northern Ondo State. Ikare, one of the largest, grew as a market at a crossroads where roads from Owo, Okene, Kabba and Ado-Ekiti meet. Farmers from the surrounding hills bring in kola nuts, cocoa and coffee.","w":"A town spread among bare rocky hills and green farmland, about 70 km north-east of Akure.","f":["Ikare is the headquarters of the Akoko North-East local government area.","The Akoko area counts more than 40 small towns and villages, many of them built among rocky outcrops."],"q":{"q":"In which Nigerian state is Ikare?","a":"Ondo","x":["Ekiti","Kogi"]}},"Q503056":{"t":"A coal town on the Vaal, named after a mining company","s":"The Vaal River slides past Vereeniging in wide, muddy-green bends, and upstream spreads the branching reservoir of the Vaal Dam. The town was founded in 1892 by a coal-mining company whose long Dutch name ended in 'Vereeniging', meaning 'association'. Coal fed steelworks and power stations, and the town grew into the industrial Vaal Triangle with Vanderbijlpark and Sasolburg.","w":"An industrial city on a winding river, with a large, many-armed reservoir upstream to the south-east.","f":["The Vaal River here forms the border between Gauteng and the Free State.","The industrialist Sammy Marks was behind the coal company that founded the town."],"q":{"q":"What does the Dutch name Vereeniging mean?","a":"Association","x":["River crossing","Coal field"]}},"Q93338":{"t":"Pistachio orchards and a capital of baklava","s":"Rows of grey-green trees stripe the limestone hills around Gaziantep: pistachios, the city's pride, known across Turkey simply as Antep nuts. They end up in its baklava, the first Turkish product to win protected status in the European Union. About 45 km to the east flows the Euphrates, where the Roman city of Zeugma yielded a trove of floor mosaics, now in the city's museum.","w":"A large pale city on a limestone plateau, ringed by orchards in neat rows; the Euphrates and its reservoirs lie to the east.","f":["The 'Gypsy Girl' mosaic from Zeugma is the best-known piece in Gaziantep's mosaic museum.","UNESCO named Gaziantep a Creative City of Gastronomy in 2015.","The city sits on a plateau at about 850 metres above sea level."],"q":{"q":"Which nut is Gaziantep famous for?","a":"Pistachio","x":["Hazelnut","Almond"]}},"Q217432":{"t":"A port on the creeks of the Niger Delta","s":"South of the city, dry land dissolves into a maze of creeks, mangroves and river channels: the Niger Delta. Port Harcourt was founded in 1912 on the Bonny River to ship coal from the mines at Enugu. Oil later made it the hub of Nigeria's petroleum industry. At night, look for bright points among the dark swamps: flares burning off gas.","w":"A big city on a broad estuary, with a maze of creeks and dark-green mangroves spreading south to the sea.","f":["The city is named after Lewis Harcourt, Britain's Colonial Secretary when it was founded.","Its many parks once earned it the nickname 'Garden City'.","Port Harcourt is the capital of Rivers State."],"q":{"q":"What was Port Harcourt founded to export?","a":"Coal","x":["Cocoa","Rubber"]}},"Q41621":{"t":"A city climbing a green ridge above a curved bay","s":"Mount Carmel runs down to the Mediterranean like a green prow, and Haifa climbs its slopes from the port to the ridge top. North of it a smooth, curving bay sweeps round to the old walled town of Acre. The mountain gave its name to the Carmelites, a Catholic order that grew from hermits living on its slopes in the 12th century.","w":"A city on the slopes of a long ridge ending at the sea, with a smooth curved bay stretching north.","f":["Haifa's underground funicular, the Carmelit, is one of the world's smallest metro systems, with six stations.","The terraced Bahá'í Gardens on Mount Carmel are part of a UNESCO World Heritage site.","The Carmel ridge rises to just over 500 metres."],"q":{"q":"Which mountain ridge rises above Haifa?","a":"Mount Carmel","x":["Mount Tabor","Mount Hermon"]}},"Q35484":{"t":"An oasis city ringed by black lava fields","s":"Around Medina, dark lava fields spread like spilled ink across the tan desert: the harrats of western Arabia. In 1256 a lava flow from Harrat Rahat, to the south-east, stopped short of the city. Medina itself grew as a palm oasis, and in 622 the Prophet Muhammad settled here; his mosque makes it one of the holiest cities of Islam.","w":"A large city in a basin, hemmed in by black lava fields to the south and east and a rocky ridge to the north.","f":["Mount Uhud, a reddish granite ridge, rises on the city's northern edge.","The Hejaz Railway from Damascus reached Medina in 1908.","Harrat Rahat is one of the largest lava fields in Arabia."],"q":{"q":"In which year did the Prophet Muhammad move to Medina?","a":"622","x":["570","632"]}},"Q131301":{"t":"At the gap where Syria opens to the sea","s":"Coastal mountains wall inland Syria off from the Mediterranean, except at the Homs Gap, a broad, low corridor running west to the coast. Homs grew on the Orontes where that route crosses the road from north to south. Just south-west, the river widens into a lake held back by an ancient dam. As Emesa, the city gave Rome an empress, Julia Domna.","w":"A city on a broad plain by a river, with a lake to the south-west and a low gap in the mountains to the west.","f":["The crusader castle Krak des Chevaliers watches over the Homs Gap from a hill to the west.","Many of the old city's buildings are made of dark basalt."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows past Homs?","a":"The Orontes","x":["The Euphrates","The Litani"]}},"Q170366":{"t":"Iran's hot river city on the Karun","s":"The Karun, Iran's largest river by volume, winds through Ahvaz in broad loops, crossed by a string of bridges. Around the city lies the flat Khuzestan plain, the northern edge of Mesopotamia, pale and dusty in summer, green with fields in winter and spring. Summers here are among the hottest of any big city on Earth, and afternoons above 50°C are not unheard of.","w":"A large, low city on a looping river across a flat plain, with the Zagros foothills far to the north-east.","f":["The Karun is Iran's only navigable river; downstream it joins the Shatt al-Arab.","The steel White Bridge over the Karun, built in the 1930s, is the city's best-known landmark."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Ahvaz?","a":"The Karun","x":["The Karkheh","The Zayandeh"]}},"Q500366":{"t":"A hunter's town grown large on the savanna","s":"North of the forest belt the land opens into rolling farmland and savanna, and Ogbomosho spreads across it as a wide, low city. By tradition it was founded around 1650 by a hunter named Ogunlola, and his successors still reign with the title of Soun. In 1855 American Baptist missionaries opened one of their first stations in Nigeria here.","w":"A wide, low city on open, rolling savanna, about 90 km north of Ibadan.","f":["The city is home to Ladoke Akintola University of Technology.","Ogbomosho lies in Oyo State, on the road between the towns of Oyo and Ilorin."],"q":{"q":"What title does Ogbomosho's traditional ruler hold?","a":"Soun","x":["Alaafin","Ooni"]}},"Q3604":{"t":"A port on a flat spit at the Red Sea's gate","s":"Djibouti city juts into the Gulf of Tadjoura on a flat, low peninsula, not far from the Bab-el-Mandeb strait that links the Red Sea to the Indian Ocean. Inland lies a scorched land of lava fields and rifts. About 80 km to the west, look for a blinding-white crust of salt: Lake Assal, some 155 metres below sea level.","w":"A small city on a flat spit in a deep gulf; to the west, a white salt lake among dark lava.","f":["Lake Assal is the lowest point in Africa and one of the saltiest lakes in the world.","A railway links the port with Addis Ababa; the first line was completed in 1917.","The French founded the town in the late 1880s as a port on the route to Ethiopia."],"q":{"q":"What makes Lake Assal, west of Djibouti city, special?","a":"It is Africa's lowest point","x":["It is Africa's deepest lake","It is Africa's largest crater"]}},"Q79857":{"t":"Rumi's city on a vast, flat plain","s":"The Konya plain is one of the flattest places in Turkey, the bed of a lake that dried up long ago, now chequered with wheat fields. Konya sits on its western rim at about 1,000 metres. It was the capital of the Seljuk Sultanate of Rum, and the mystic poet Rumi is buried here; the whirling dervishes of his order made the city famous.","w":"A large city at the edge of a huge flat plain of geometric fields, with low mountains to the west.","f":["Çatalhöyük, one of the world's oldest known towns, lies about 40 km to the south-east.","Rumi's tomb, under a green-tiled dome, is now the Mevlana Museum.","Sinkholes called obruks pock the plain around the city."],"q":{"q":"Which mystic poet is buried in Konya?","a":"Rumi","x":["Hafez","Omar Khayyam"]}},"Q221436":{"t":"A Sahel city at the heart of old Borno","s":"The land here is flat to the horizon: a pale, sandy plain in the dry season, speckled with trees and fields after the rains. Maiduguri stands in the basin of Lake Chad. This was the core of the Kanem–Bornu empire, which ruled the lands around the lake for about a thousand years, and the city is the seat of the Shehu of Borno, heir to its rulers.","w":"A large, low city on a flat, sandy plain with scattered trees, towards the basin of Lake Chad to the north-east.","f":["Maiduguri is the capital of Borno State.","Rain falls mainly from June to September; the rest of the year is dry."],"q":{"q":"Maiduguri is the capital of which Nigerian state?","a":"Borno","x":["Yobe","Adamawa"]}},"Q48195":{"t":"Sinbad's port on the palm-lined Shatt al-Arab","s":"Above Basra, the Tigris and Euphrates merge into one wide waterway, the Shatt al-Arab, which carries their water past the city to the Gulf. Its banks were once lined with one of the largest date-palm forests in the world, criss-crossed by canals. Founded in the 630s, Basra became a great port of Islam's golden age, and in the Arabian Nights Sinbad the Sailor sets out from here.","w":"A city on a broad river flowing south-east to the Gulf, with dark palm groves along its banks.","f":["The Tigris and Euphrates meet at al-Qurnah, about 65 km upstream.","Its canals once earned Basra the nickname 'Venice of the East'.","The Mesopotamian Marshes, to the north-west, are a UNESCO World Heritage site."],"q":{"q":"Which Arabian Nights hero sets sail from Basra?","a":"Sinbad","x":["Aladdin","Ali Baba"]}},"Q3859":{"t":"A capital spread over ridges and green valleys","s":"Kigali doesn't sit on one hill but on dozens: red-roofed neighbourhoods follow the ridge tops, while the valleys between them stay green with wetlands and fields. Rwanda calls itself the land of a thousand hills, and its capital proves the point. At around 1,500 metres, just south of the equator, the city enjoys mild, spring-like weather all year.","w":"A city draped over many green ridges, with red roofs along the crests and wetlands in the valleys between.","f":["Rwanda banned plastic bags in 2008, and Kigali is known as one of Africa's cleanest cities.","Kigali lies close to the geographic centre of Rwanda."],"q":{"q":"Rwanda is known as the land of a thousand what?","a":"Hills","x":["Lakes","Waterfalls"]}},"Q574593":{"t":"An old river kingdom in the heart of oil country","s":"Creeks and mangrove channels thread through the land around Warri, glinting between dark-green swamp forest. Portuguese traders reached the Itsekiri kingdom of Warri in the 15th century, and around 1600 one of its princes studied in Portugal. Today the city lives on oil: a refinery, a port on the Warri River and, at night, gas flares burning out in the delta.","w":"A city on a winding river in the western Niger Delta, among creeks and dark mangrove swamps.","f":["The Olu of Warri is the traditional ruler of the Itsekiri people.","Warri is one of the main oil and gas centres of the Niger Delta."],"q":{"q":"Which Europeans reached the Warri kingdom in the 15th century?","a":"The Portuguese","x":["The Dutch","The English"]}},"Q205327":{"t":"A railway town at the heart of the Kasai","s":"A sprawl of low roofs and red laterite roads spreads over the Kasai plateau, where savanna alternates with dark strips of forest along the rivers. Until 1966 Kananga was called Luluabourg, after the Lulua River that flows west of it. It grew as a station on the railway linking the copper mines of Katanga with the river port of Ilebo.","w":"A large, low city on a savanna plateau, with dark strips of forest following the river valleys.","f":["Kananga is the capital of Kasai-Central province.","The Lulua River flows into the Kasai, which in turn feeds the Congo."],"q":{"q":"What was Kananga called before 1966?","a":"Luluabourg","x":["Bakwanga","Stanleyville"]}},"Q466216":{"t":"A highland city beneath a 3,000-metre mountain","s":"Jabal Sabir rises steeply behind Ta'izz, its terraced slopes climbing some 1,600 metres above the city to a summit near 3,000 m. The city itself fills a green highland basin at about 1,400 metres, far cooler than the scorching Red Sea coast to the west. From the 13th to the 15th century the Rasulid sultans made it one of their capitals.","w":"A city in a highland basin at the foot of a massive terraced mountain, with the Red Sea coastal plain to the west.","f":["Terraced fields on Jabal Sabir climb almost to the summit.","Mocha, the old coffee port that gave its name to mocha coffee, lies about 90 km to the south-west."],"q":{"q":"Which mountain towers over Ta'izz?","a":"Jabal Sabir","x":["Jabal an-Nabi Shu'ayb","Jabal Haraz"]}},"Q168193":{"t":"A shrine city on the edge of the western desert","s":"Najaf stands on a low ridge at the edge of the desert, where the green of the Euphrates valley gives out to open sand and gravel. It grew around the shrine of Ali ibn Abi Talib and for centuries has been a great centre of Shia learning. Beside it spreads Wadi al-Salam, one of the largest cemeteries in the world, a grey expanse of tombs.","w":"A pale city on a low ridge between the green Euphrates valley and open desert, with a vast cemetery beside it.","f":["Wadi al-Salam covers roughly 6 square kilometres and holds millions of graves.","Kufa, one of the early capitals of the caliphate, lies about 10 km to the north-east on the Euphrates."],"q":{"q":"Najaf is home to one of the world's largest what?","a":"Cemeteries","x":["Bazaars","Palm groves"]}},"Q223920":{"t":"Congo's Atlantic port, named for a dark headland","s":"Portuguese navigators coasting past in the 15th century noted a dark, rocky point jutting into the Atlantic, and the name stuck: Pointe-Noire, 'Black Point'. Today it is the main port and oil town of the Republic of the Congo, spread along long sandy beaches. At night, look offshore for pinpricks of light from oil platforms. A railway runs about 500 km inland from here to Brazzaville.","w":"A city on a long, straight sandy coast with a harbour breakwater; green savanna and patches of forest inland.","f":["Just to the north lies Loango, once the capital of the Kingdom of Loango.","The Diosso Gorge, north of the city, is a red amphitheatre of eroded cliffs."],"q":{"q":"What did Portuguese sailors see that named Pointe-Noire?","a":"A dark rocky headland","x":["A black sand beach","A burnt-out shipwreck"]}},"Q134509":{"t":"The Suez Canal's Mediterranean gateway","s":"A ruler-straight line of water heads south from the coast, often with a string of ships on it: the Suez Canal begins here. Port Said was founded in 1859, as digging started, and named after Egypt's ruler Said Pasha. Across the canal lies Port Fuad, on the Asian side, so a short ferry ride here takes you from Africa to Asia.","w":"A city on a flat coast at the mouth of a straight canal, with a wide lagoon to the west and ships at anchor offshore.","f":["Bartholdi first imagined a colossal lighthouse-statue for the canal entrance here, before he designed the Statue of Liberty.","The canal runs about 190 km from Port Said to Suez.","Lake Manzala, a broad, shallow lagoon, stretches west of the city."],"q":{"q":"Which ruler is Port Said named after?","a":"Said Pasha","x":["Muhammad Ali","Ismail Pasha"]}},"Q160450":{"t":"A Black Sea port between two river deltas","s":"Two great bulges of flat, green farmland push into the Black Sea on either side of Samsun: the delta of the Kızılırmak to the west and that of the Yeşilırmak to the east. The city lies in the curve of coast between them, backed by forested hills. Greek colonists founded Amisos here in antiquity, and it has been a busy port ever since.","w":"A long coastal city in a shallow bay, with a broad, flat river delta bulging out to either side.","f":["The Kızılırmak, the 'Red River', is the longest river flowing entirely within Turkey.","The Kızılırmak delta is one of Turkey's most important wetlands for birds.","Samsun is the largest city on Turkey's Black Sea coast."],"q":{"q":"Which river's delta lies west of Samsun?","a":"The Kızılırmak","x":["The Sakarya","The Çoruh"]}},"Q187346":{"t":"A city on a rock, stitched together by bridges","s":"The Rhumel River has sawn a deep, narrow gorge around a rocky plateau, and Constantine sits on top, tied to the land around it by a series of bridges. As Cirta, it was the capital of the Numidian kings. The emperor Constantine rebuilt it in the early 4th century and gave it his name. From above, the gorge shows as a dark crack curling around the old town.","w":"A city on a rock plateau wrapped by a dark, narrow gorge, amid rolling, wheat-coloured hills.","f":["In places the gorge walls drop well over 100 metres to the river.","The Sidi M'Cid suspension bridge, opened in 1912, spans the gorge high above the river.","Constantine is often called the 'City of Bridges'."],"q":{"q":"What was Constantine called in ancient times?","a":"Cirta","x":["Hippo Regius","Thugga"]}},"Q173310":{"t":"An ancient citadel near fires that never go out","s":"Kirkuk grew around a citadel mound by the Khasa River, a hill built up by thousands of years of settlement on the same spot. The Zagros foothills rise to the north-east, and around the city lie rolling steppe and fields. Just outside, at Baba Gurgur, natural gas seeps from the ground and has burned in open flames since ancient times.","w":"A city on a rolling plain by a seasonal river, with the first folds of the Zagros rising to the north-east.","f":["Kirkuk stands on the site of ancient Arrapha, a city of the Hurrians and later the Assyrians.","The giant Kirkuk oil field was first struck at Baba Gurgur in 1927."],"q":{"q":"What has burned at Baba Gurgur, near Kirkuk, since antiquity?","a":"Gas seeping from the ground","x":["A temple's sacred lamp","A buried coal seam"]}},"Q3929":{"t":"A harbour capital in an amphitheatre of peaks","s":"Port Louis fills a narrow coastal plain on the north-west side of Mauritius, hemmed in by a horseshoe of steep, jagged mountains. Look for Pieter Both, with a boulder balanced on its summit, and the thumb-shaped peak of Le Pouce. The French founded the port in the 1730s. Around the island, a coral reef draws a white line of surf beyond a turquoise lagoon.","w":"A city on a small bay backed by a ring of sharp green peaks, on an island girdled by a turquoise lagoon.","f":["The dodo lived only on Mauritius and was extinct by the late 17th century.","Aapravasi Ghat in the harbour, where indentured labourers from India landed, is a UNESCO site.","Le Pouce, French for 'the thumb', rises to about 810 metres."],"q":{"q":"Which extinct bird lived only on Mauritius?","a":"The dodo","x":["The moa","The great auk"]}},"Q188693":{"t":"A Scottish mission town in Malawi's green highlands","s":"Blantyre spreads across the Shire Highlands at about 1,000 metres, a greener, cooler world than the hot Shire valley below. Scottish missionaries founded it in 1876 and named it after David Livingstone's birthplace near Glasgow. Look east on a clear day: the great granite massif of Mulanje rises abruptly from the plain, and between the two stretch the neat green rows of tea estates.","w":"A city on green, hilly uplands; to the east, a huge, isolated mountain massif stands above tea-covered slopes.","f":["Sapitwa, the highest summit of Mulanje, reaches about 3,000 metres.","Blantyre is Malawi's main commercial city and one of its oldest towns."],"q":{"q":"Blantyre is named after the birthplace of which explorer?","a":"David Livingstone","x":["Henry Morton Stanley","Mungo Park"]}},"Q146723":{"t":"Where the Congo's rapids end and the river road begins","s":"Just upstream of Kisangani, the Congo tumbles through the Boyoma Falls, a chain of seven cataracts spread over about 100 km. Below the city the river is navigable all the way to Kinshasa, more than 1,700 km downstream, so a river port grew here naturally. Henry Morton Stanley founded a station on the spot in 1883, and the town was long called Stanleyville.","w":"A city on a broad brown river in unbroken green rainforest, with rapids breaking the water just upstream.","f":["Kisangani lies only about 60 km north of the equator.","Wagenia fishermen fix wooden tripods in the rapids and hang huge conical fish traps from them."],"q":{"q":"What was Kisangani called in colonial times?","a":"Stanleyville","x":["Léopoldville","Élisabethville"]}},"Q271000":{"t":"Where a wedge of savanna cuts into the forest","s":"Côte d'Ivoire's forest belt is broken here by a tongue of open grassland pushing south, known as the V Baoulé after the Baoulé people who live in it. Bouaké, the country's second city, sits near its heart, at a crossroads of highways and the railway running north from Abidjan. To the south-west lies the vast, branching reservoir of Lake Kossou.","w":"A big city on open, patchy savanna, with darker forest to the south and a huge branching lake to the south-west.","f":["Lake Kossou, created by a dam in the 1970s, is the largest lake in Côte d'Ivoire.","The Baoulé are renowned for their carved wooden masks and figures."],"q":{"q":"Which people give their name to the savanna 'V' around Bouaké?","a":"The Baoulé","x":["The Bété","The Dioula"]}},"Q3882":{"t":"An island capital where fresh springs rise in the sea","s":"Bahrain is an archipelago, and Manama crowds its north-east tip, its shoreline pushed outward year after year onto reclaimed land. The name Bahrain means 'two seas', often explained by freshwater springs that bubble up through the salty Gulf. Four thousand years ago this was Dilmun, a trading hub between Mesopotamia and the Indus Valley.","w":"A dense city of towers on the north-east corner of a flat, sandy island, edged with straight reclaimed shorelines.","f":["Qal'at al-Bahrain, the ancient harbour and capital of Dilmun, is a UNESCO World Heritage site.","Bahrain's pearling heritage, once the island's main wealth, is also on the UNESCO list.","The King Fahd Causeway, about 25 km long, links Bahrain to Saudi Arabia."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Bahrain mean?","a":"Two seas","x":["Pearl island","Palm harbour"]}},"Q244232":{"t":"Green circles of farmland in the northern desert","s":"Around Tabuk, the desert is dotted with perfect green circles: fields watered by centre-pivot sprinklers that draw on deep groundwater. The city grew as an oasis and a stop on the pilgrim road to Mecca, and later on the Hejaz Railway. To the west the land rises towards the mountains of Midian, where snow sometimes dusts the peaks in winter.","w":"A city on a broad desert plain dotted with green circular fields, with dark mountains rising to the west.","f":["An Ottoman-era castle and the old Hejaz Railway station still stand in the city.","Jabal al-Lawz, north-west of Tabuk, rises to about 2,600 metres and sometimes gets snow."],"q":{"q":"Which railway once linked Tabuk with Damascus?","a":"The Hejaz Railway","x":["The Baghdad Railway","The Anatolian Railway"]}},"Q28587":{"t":"The Medes' ancient capital, beneath Mount Alvand","s":"Hamadan lies at about 1,850 metres, at the foot of Mount Alvand, whose summit stays white for much of the year. This was Ecbatana, capital of the Medes and later a summer residence of the Persian kings. The great physician and philosopher Avicenna is buried here, and on the mountain's flank Darius and Xerxes carved inscriptions in three languages.","w":"A city on a high green plain, set against the long bulk of a mountain that is snowy for much of the year.","f":["Mount Alvand rises to about 3,580 metres.","Avicenna's Canon of Medicine served as a standard medical textbook for centuries.","Winters here are cold and snowy."],"q":{"q":"Which famous physician is buried in Hamadan?","a":"Avicenna","x":["Rhazes","Averroes"]}},"Q134514":{"t":"The canal's southern gate, at the head of the Red Sea","s":"Here the ruler-straight Suez Canal meets the Gulf of Suez, the long northern arm of the Red Sea. On one side rise the pale, sharp mountains of the Eastern Desert; across the water lie the dunes and ridges of Sinai. Ships waiting to go north gather at anchor off the city, while the canal's line runs away across the desert towards the Mediterranean.","w":"A city where a straight canal ends at the head of a long gulf, with desert mountains to the south-west and Sinai to the east.","f":["The Suez Canal opened in 1869 and has no locks, since both seas lie at almost the same level.","Jebel Ataqa, the mountain south-west of the city, rises to about 870 metres."],"q":{"q":"In which year did the Suez Canal open?","a":"1869","x":["1859","1882"]}},"Q47492":{"t":"An ancient port at the end of the incense road","s":"The coast here runs in a smooth sandy arc from Sinai towards the Levant, backed by low dunes and a flat coastal plain. Gaza is one of the oldest cities on this shore. In antiquity it was one of the five Philistine cities and the end point of the incense route, which carried frankincense and myrrh from southern Arabia to the Mediterranean.","w":"A dense city on a smooth, sandy stretch of coast, on the flat plain between Sinai and the Levant.","f":["Caravans on the ancient incense route crossed the Negev to reach Gaza's harbour.","Gaza lay on the Via Maris, the ancient coastal road between Egypt and Mesopotamia."],"q":{"q":"Which ancient trade route ended at Gaza?","a":"The Incense Route","x":["The Silk Road","The Amber Road"]}},"Q45942":{"t":"Saint Augustine's Hippo, under a forested massif","s":"The forested Edough massif rises straight from the sea west of Annaba, and the city spreads across the plain at its foot, where the Seybouse River reaches the Mediterranean. As Hippo Regius, this was a Numidian royal city and later a Roman port. Its most famous citizen was Augustine, bishop here for over thirty years and author of the Confessions and The City of God.","w":"A port city on a bay where a river meets the sea, with a dark, forested mountain block rising to the west.","f":["Ruins of Roman Hippo, with baths and a forum, lie on the edge of the modern city.","The Edough massif is covered in cork-oak forest and rises to about 1,000 metres."],"q":{"q":"Which saint was bishop of Hippo, today's Annaba?","a":"Augustine","x":["Cyprian","Ambrose"]}},"Q110218":{"t":"The 'Rock City' on Lake Victoria's southern shore","s":"Giant granite boulders are piled across the hills around Mwanza, and some stand right in the water, like Bismarck Rock just offshore. The city fans out along the southern coast of Lake Victoria, the largest lake in Africa, its shore broken into gulfs and rocky islands. John Hanning Speke reached the lake near here in 1858 and judged it to be the source of the Nile.","w":"A city on a rocky, boulder-strewn shore of a vast lake, among long gulfs and islands.","f":["Lake Victoria covers about 68,000 km².","Mwanza is one of Tanzania's largest cities and its main port on Lake Victoria."],"q":{"q":"Which explorer reached Lake Victoria near Mwanza in 1858?","a":"John Hanning Speke","x":["Richard Burton","David Livingstone"]}},"Q182394":{"t":"A mud-brick city of wind towers between two deserts","s":"Yazd sits on a dry plain between Iran's two great deserts, the Dasht-e Kavir to the north and the Dasht-e Lut to the south-east. Its old town is a honey-coloured maze of mud brick, crowned by windcatchers, towers that funnel breezes down into houses. Water arrives by qanats, underground channels dug from the mountains. To the south-west rises Shir Kuh, over 4,000 metres high.","w":"A pale, flat city on a desert plain, with a high, often snowy mountain massif to the south-west.","f":["The historic city of Yazd became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2017.","Yazd is a centre of Iran's Zoroastrian community, with a fire temple and old towers of silence."],"q":{"q":"What are the towers on Yazd's rooftops for?","a":"Catching wind to cool houses","x":["Storing grain","Signalling to caravans"]}},"Q154814":{"t":"A port on the Strait of Hormuz, facing colourful islands","s":"Bandar Abbas faces the Strait of Hormuz, the narrow gateway to the Persian Gulf, with bare brown mountains close behind. Offshore lie the long island of Qeshm and little Hormuz, whose soils flash red, ochre and yellow. Shah Abbas I made this his main port in the 1620s and gave it his name.","w":"A long coastal city at the foot of bare mountains, facing a large island and a small, reddish one across the strait.","f":["Qeshm is the largest island in the Persian Gulf.","Hormuz Island's red earth is used locally both as a spice and as a pigment."],"q":{"q":"Which is the largest island in the Persian Gulf?","a":"Qeshm","x":["Kish","Kharg"]}},"Q528432":{"t":"A highland city split by a green escarpment","s":"Bamenda spills down a steep escarpment on the edge of Cameroon's Western High Plateau: the old administrative quarter of Up Station on the heights, the busy town spread over the valley below. All around lie the Grassfields, rolling green uplands of old kingdoms ruled by Fons. To the north-east, look for the Oku massif, which hides a crater lake.","w":"A city spilling down a steep green escarpment, with rolling grassy uplands around and a higher massif to the north-east.","f":["Mount Oku rises to about 3,000 metres; in Cameroon only Mount Cameroon is higher.","The Ring Road, a loop of several hundred kilometres, circles the Grassfields from Bamenda."],"q":{"q":"What is the traditional ruler of a Grassfields kingdom called?","a":"Fon","x":["Oba","Emir"]}},"Q157915":{"t":"A river port on the Benue, deep inside Africa","s":"The Benue winds broad and sandy past Garoua, shrunken in the dry season and swollen after the summer rains. At high water, for a few weeks a year, boats could once travel downstream all the way to the Niger in Nigeria, which made Garoua a river port deep in the interior. Upstream to the south spreads the big reservoir of the Lagdo Dam, amid hot, open savanna.","w":"A city on a broad, sandy river in open savanna, with a large reservoir to the south.","f":["Garoua is the capital of Cameroon's North Region.","Bénoué National Park, south of the city, protects savanna along the upper river."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows past Garoua?","a":"The Benue","x":["The Logone","The Sanaga"]}},"Q1015631":{"t":"A planned gold town where flamingos gather","s":"Welkom rose from open Free State farmland after the gold discoveries of the 1940s, a town laid out on the drawing board with broad streets and a circular centre. Around it, mine headgear, pale tailings dams and shallow pans break up the maize country. Water pumped from the mines filled some of those pans, and flocks of flamingos now feed in them.","w":"A planned town in flat farmland, dotted with pale, flat-topped mine dumps and shallow pans of water.","f":["The town was founded in 1947 on a farm of the same name.","The Free State goldfields around Welkom are part of the rich Witwatersrand Basin."],"q":{"q":"What does Welkom mean in Afrikaans?","a":"Welcome","x":["Gold field","New town"]}},"Q29962":{"t":"Upper Egypt's big city on a narrow ribbon of green","s":"In Middle Egypt the Nile valley shrinks to a narrow strip of green squeezed between pale limestone desert cliffs, and Asyut sits right in the middle of it. For centuries caravans set out from here along the Forty Days Road to Darfur. The Greeks called the town Lycopolis, 'wolf city', after the jackal-headed god worshipped here.","w":"A city on the Nile's west bank in a narrow green valley, with pale desert cliffs on both sides.","f":["The Asyut Barrage across the Nile, completed in 1902, feeds the large Ibrahimiya irrigation canal.","Asyut is one of the largest cities of Upper Egypt."],"q":{"q":"What did the ancient Greeks call Asyut?","a":"Lycopolis","x":["Heliopolis","Hermopolis"]}},"Q193802":{"t":"Morocco's eastern gateway, a thousand years old","s":"Oujda spreads over the Angad plain in the far north-east of Morocco, only about 15 km from the border with Algeria. It was founded in 994 by the Zenata chief Ziri ibn Atiyya, and its position on the road between Fes and Tlemcen made it a crossroads of trade. North of the city rise the Beni Snassen mountains, with the Mediterranean beyond.","w":"A large city on a broad, flat plain, with a line of mountains to the north and the Algerian border just to the east.","f":["Oujda is the capital of Morocco's Oriental region.","The Mediterranean coast at Saïdia is about 50 km to the north-west."],"q":{"q":"In which year was Oujda founded?","a":"994","x":["789","1070"]}},"Q239580":{"t":"Heart of the Copperbelt, beside the Kafue","s":"Kitwe and Ndola are the two big cities of Zambia's Copperbelt, a mining region running along the border with Congo's Katanga. Kitwe grew up beside the Nkana mine in the 1930s, and from above you can pick out broad, pale tailings dams and dark slag heaps among the green bush. The Kafue River winds past the city, heading south towards the Zambezi.","w":"A large town in green bush, with pale tailings dams and dark slag heaps around it and a winding river nearby.","f":["The Copperbelt straddles the border between Zambia and the DR Congo.","The Kafue is one of Zambia's major rivers and a tributary of the Zambezi."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows past Kitwe?","a":"The Kafue","x":["The Luangwa","The Chambeshi"]}},"Q309475":{"t":"The gum arabic market of Kordofan","s":"El Obeid stands on a sandy plain in the middle of Sudan, where acacias dot the reddish ground. Some of those trees yield gum arabic, a hardened sap that ends up in soft drinks, sweets and medicines worldwide, and the city has long been its main market. A railway from the Nile reached El Obeid in 1912, and roads fan out from here across Kordofan.","w":"A sprawling town on a flat, sandy plain dotted with trees, far from any river.","f":["Sudan has long been the world's leading source of gum arabic, gathered from acacia trees.","El Obeid is the capital of North Kordofan state."],"q":{"q":"Which product is El Obeid's market famous for?","a":"Gum arabic","x":["Coffee","Cotton"]}},"Q323801":{"t":"A city among giant granite domes","s":"Northern Mozambique looks strange from above: smooth granite domes, some hundreds of metres high, rise abruptly out of a flat green plain like the backs of whales. Nampula, the biggest city of the north, sits among them. A railway runs through it from the deep-water port of Nacala towards Malawi, and the old island capital, Mozambique Island, lies to the east on the coast.","w":"A city on a flat green plain from which bare, rounded granite domes rise here and there.","f":["Such isolated rock domes are called inselbergs, German for 'island mountains'.","Mozambique Island, the country's first capital, is a UNESCO World Heritage site."],"q":{"q":"What do geographers call the granite domes around Nampula?","a":"Inselbergs","x":["Mesas","Drumlins"]}},"Q131323":{"t":"Where Libya's coast turns into the Gulf of Sidra","s":"Just east of Misrata, the coast of Libya turns sharply south-east and the long, empty curve of the Gulf of Sidra begins. The city spreads over the flat coastal plain around the headland of Cape Misrata, its palm groves and fields giving way to desert and a broad salt flat to the south. Its port and steelworks make it one of Libya's main industrial centres.","w":"A city on a flat coast by a headland, where the shoreline turns south-east into a vast gulf; salt flats inland.","f":["Misrata is Libya's third-largest city.","In Roman times a small port called Thubactis stood near here."],"q":{"q":"Which gulf begins just east of Misrata?","a":"The Gulf of Sidra","x":["The Gulf of Gabès","The Gulf of Bomba"]}},"Q223761":{"t":"A mud-mosque city in Burkina Faso's green south-west","s":"Bobo-Dioulasso lies in the greenest corner of Burkina Faso, where the Sahel gives way to wooded savanna, streams and mango orchards. Its name joins two peoples, the Bobo and the Dioula traders. The old Grand Mosque, built of mud in the late 19th century and bristling with wooden beams, is a classic of Sudano-Sahelian architecture.","w":"A large, low city in green, wooded savanna and orchards, with a railway line running through it.","f":["Bobo-Dioulasso is Burkina Faso's second-largest city.","The railway from Abidjan to Ouagadougou passes through the city."],"q":{"q":"Which two peoples does the name Bobo-Dioulasso refer to?","a":"The Bobo and the Dioula","x":["The Mossi and the Dioula","The Bobo and the Fula"]}},"Q183405":{"t":"Potters' hill and sardine boats on the Atlantic","s":"Safi sits on a stretch of Morocco's Atlantic coast where cliffs drop to the surf. Its potters have worked for centuries on a hill just outside the old medina, firing glazed bowls and tiles in kilns. The harbour lands huge catches of sardines, and plants south of the city process phosphate from the interior. The Portuguese held the town in the early 16th century and left a sea fort.","w":"A port city on a cliff-lined Atlantic coast, with a harbour and industrial plants stretching south along the shore.","f":["The Portuguese sea fortress, Dar el-Bahar, still stands on the seafront.","Safi is one of Morocco's main sardine ports."],"q":{"q":"Which craft is Safi famous for?","a":"Pottery","x":["Carpet weaving","Leather tanning"]}},"Q322149":{"t":"A highland farm town in running country","s":"Eldoret sits on the Uasin Gishu Plateau at over 2,000 metres, surrounded by a patchwork of big wheat and maize fields. Just to the east the land falls away abruptly into the deep Kerio Valley, part of the Great Rift. The thin air and high trails of this region have produced a remarkable number of world-class distance runners.","w":"A town on a high, open plateau of large fields, with a steep escarpment dropping into a rift valley to the east.","f":["The small town of Iten, on the escarpment edge nearby, is famous as a training base for runners.","Eldoret lies only about 60 km north of the equator."],"q":{"q":"Which sport made the Eldoret area world-famous?","a":"Distance running","x":["Cycling","Boxing"]}},"Q4301":{"t":"Safari gateway at the foot of Mount Meru","s":"Mount Meru rises steeply just north of Arusha, a big volcanic cone whose summit crater has collapsed on the eastern side into a great horseshoe. On a clear day the snows of Kilimanjaro float on the horizon about 80 km to the east. The city, at around 1,400 metres, is the starting point for safaris to the Serengeti, Ngorongoro and Tarangire.","w":"A city spread over the southern slopes below a big volcanic cone, with Kilimanjaro's white summit far to the east.","f":["Mount Meru reaches 4,566 metres, one of the highest peaks in Africa.","Arusha National Park covers Meru's slopes and crater."],"q":{"q":"Which volcano rises just north of Arusha?","a":"Mount Meru","x":["Mount Kenya","Ol Doinyo Lengai"]}},"Q399119":{"t":"Harare's big neighbour on the Highveld","s":"Just south of Harare, about 20 km from the centre, a dense grid of small houses stands out on the open grassland of the Highveld: Chitungwiza. It grew as a satellite town for the capital, and every morning many of its residents head into Harare to work. At around 1,400 metres the air is fresh, and rolling fields and patches of woodland surround the town.","w":"A dense grid of small houses on open grassland, just south of the larger sprawl of Harare.","f":["Chitungwiza is one of Zimbabwe's largest urban areas.","It became a town in 1978, joining the townships of Seke, Zengeza and St Mary's."],"q":{"q":"Chitungwiza is a satellite town of which capital?","a":"Harare","x":["Lusaka","Gaborone"]}},"Q209537":{"t":"The river port where a 'living fossil' surfaced","s":"East London grew around the mouth of the Buffalo River, and it is South Africa's only major river port. Green hills roll down to a coast of long beaches and rocky points. In 1938 a local museum curator, Marjorie Courtenay-Latimer, spotted a strange blue fish in a trawler's catch: a coelacanth, a creature thought to have died out tens of millions of years ago.","w":"A coastal city around a river mouth, with green rolling hills and long sandy beaches on the Indian Ocean.","f":["The coelacanth's scientific name, Latimeria, honours Marjorie Courtenay-Latimer.","The fish was caught near the mouth of the Chalumna River, south-west of the city.","The Wild Coast, a rugged stretch of cliffs and river mouths, begins to the north-east."],"q":{"q":"Which 'living fossil' fish was identified at East London in 1938?","a":"The coelacanth","x":["The lungfish","The sturgeon"]}},"Q3854":{"t":"A lakeside city under a mountain wall","s":"Bujumbura lies at the northern tip of Lake Tanganyika, on a narrow plain squeezed between the water and a steep green escarpment rising more than 1,000 metres behind it. To the west, the Rusizi River wanders into the lake through a marshy delta where hippos live. The lake itself is one of the deepest on Earth, reaching about 1,470 metres.","w":"A city at the head of a long, deep lake, pressed against a steep green escarpment, with a river delta to the west.","f":["Lake Tanganyika is the longest freshwater lake in the world, about 670 km.","At Mugere, 12 km south of the city, Livingstone and Stanley stayed two nights in November 1871."],"q":{"q":"Which lake does Bujumbura stand on?","a":"Lake Tanganyika","x":["Lake Kivu","Lake Malawi"]}},"Q180717":{"t":"A walled medina amid a sea of olive trees","s":"Behind Sousse the land becomes a vast grid of olive trees, row after row across the Tunisian Sahel. The city itself hugs the coast, its walled medina climbing a gentle slope from the harbour. Phoenicians founded it as Hadrumetum. The medina, with its fortress-like ribat and Great Mosque, is on the UNESCO World Heritage list.","w":"A coastal city with a compact walled old town by the harbour, surrounded by endless grids of olive groves.","f":["The ribat, a fortified monastery on the medina's edge, is more than 1,100 years old.","Beneath the city lie early Christian catacombs with thousands of tombs."],"q":{"q":"What was Sousse called in Roman times?","a":"Hadrumetum","x":["Utica","Leptis Magna"]}},"Q203299":{"t":"A green oasis cupped in a desert depression","s":"West of the Nile, a round green bowl sits inside the desert: the Faiyum, a depression watered by a canal from the river, the Bahr Yussef. Its lowest part holds Lake Qarun, a salty lake whose surface lies more than 40 metres below sea level. The Faiyum gave the world its famous mummy portraits, painted faces of Roman-era Egyptians that look startlingly alive.","w":"A roughly circular patch of green fields in the desert west of the Nile, with a long lake along its north-west edge.","f":["Wadi El Hitan, with fossil whale skeletons lying in the desert, is to the west and is a UNESCO site.","Old wooden waterwheels still turn in the city's canals.","In ancient times the city was called Crocodilopolis, after the crocodile god Sobek."],"q":{"q":"Which lake lies in the Faiyum depression?","a":"Lake Qarun","x":["Lake Nasser","Lake Manzala"]}},"Q170322":{"t":"Red roofs, a pinched-shut bay and a famous beer","s":"Jiaozhou Bay is squeezed almost closed, with a long, hair-thin bridge drawn across it and Qingdao guarding the narrow mouth. East of the city, the granite massif of Lao Shan climbs straight out of the sea. Germany leased this coast in 1898, and the red-roofed old town and the Tsingtao brewery are both left over from that era.","w":"A nearly enclosed bay on the south coast of the Shandong Peninsula, a bridge across it, dark mountains just east of the city.","f":["The Tsingtao brewery was founded in 1903 by German and British settlers.","The Jiaozhou Bay Bridge, opened in 2011, runs for more than 25 km over the water.","The sailing races of the 2008 Beijing Olympics were held off Qingdao."],"q":{"q":"Which country leased Qingdao from 1898?","a":"Germany","x":["Britain","Russia"]}},"Q26600":{"t":"Kyushu's big city, with its airport almost downtown","s":"Few big cities keep their airport this close: Fukuoka's runway lies only a few kilometres from the centre, so jets skim low over the rooftops. The city curves around Hakata Bay, closed off to the north by a thin sandbar that leads out to Shikanoshima island. In 1274 and 1281, fleets sent by Kublai Khan landed on these very shores.","w":"A city wrapped around a round bay in northern Kyushu, with a long thin sandspit closing the bay to the north.","f":["Fukuoka Airport is just two subway stops from Hakata Station.","A gold seal given by a Han emperor to a local king was found on Shikanoshima in 1784.","A typhoon wrecked much of the second Mongol fleet; it was remembered as kamikaze, the “divine wind”."],"q":{"q":"Which empire twice landed troops at Hakata Bay, in 1274 and 1281?","a":"The Mongol Empire","x":["Ming China","Portugal"]}},"Q245023":{"t":"Central Taiwan's sunny city between mountains and sea","s":"Wide riverbeds of pale gravel streak the plain around Taichung, which fills a broad basin halfway down Taiwan's west coast. To the east the Central Mountain Range climbs in dark green folds; to the west a low plateau hides the sea. The climate is famously mild and sunny, and since 2017 Taichung has been Taiwan's second most populous city.","w":"A large grid city in a basin halfway down Taiwan's west coast, with mountains rising steeply to the east.","f":["The name Taichung simply means “central Taiwan”.","Chun Shui Tang, a Taichung teahouse, is one of two places that claim to have invented bubble tea in the 1980s.","Sun Moon Lake lies in the mountains about 40 km to the south-east."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Taichung mean?","a":"Central Taiwan","x":["Northern Taiwan","Eastern Taiwan"]}},"Q215755":{"t":"A bend of the Xiang, and a painter's home country","s":"Three cities crowd the Xiang River here — Xiangtan, Zhuzhou and Changsha — each within about 50 km of the others. Xiangtan sits on a wide bend of the river among low red hills and rice paddies. Its countryside produced two very different men: the painter Qi Baishi, born in 1864, and Mao Zedong, born in 1893 in the village of Shaoshan.","w":"A wide bend of the Xiang River, with Zhuzhou just to the east and the bigger sprawl of Changsha to the north.","f":["Qi Baishi, famous for his lively ink shrimps, was born in the Xiangtan countryside in 1864.","Shaoshan, Mao Zedong's birthplace, is administered by Xiangtan and lies about 40 km to the west.","The name means roughly “deep pool of the Xiang”."],"q":{"q":"Which painter, famous for his ink shrimps, was born near Xiangtan?","a":"Qi Baishi","x":["Xu Beihong","Zhang Daqian"]}},"Q20934":{"t":"Korea's air gateway, on land won from the sea","s":"At low tide the sea here simply leaves: grey-brown mudflats stretch for kilometres, laced with glinting channels. Incheon's airport stands on reclaimed land that joined two islands, and a bridge more than 21 km long strides across the shallows to the new district of Songdo. Seoul presses in from the east, so the two cities blur into one.","w":"Wide grey mudflats and islands on the Yellow Sea coast west of Seoul; a long bridge links the airport island to the mainland.","f":["The tide here can rise and fall by more than 8 metres.","Incheon Airport, opened in 2001, was built on reclaimed land between Yeongjong and Yongyu islands.","Jajangmyeon, noodles in black-bean sauce, is said to have been born in Incheon's Chinatown."],"q":{"q":"Which dish is said to have been born in Incheon's Chinatown?","a":"Jajangmyeon","x":["Bibimbap","Naengmyeon"]}},"Q68744":{"t":"An island city with a car-free islet at its tip","s":"Most of Xiamen fits on one rounded island, tied to the mainland by bridges and a causeway that fan out across the water. Off its south-western corner floats tiny Gulangyu, where old villas climb the hills and private cars are not allowed. Foreign traders knew the port as Amoy, and in 1842 it was among the first Chinese ports opened to them.","w":"A rounded island packed with city, bridges fanning out to the mainland, and a small green islet off its south-west tip.","f":["Gulangyu has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 2017.","Gulangyu is nicknamed “piano island” for its many pianos and musicians.","The Gaoji Causeway, the island's first land link, was finished in 1955."],"q":{"q":"What did English-speaking traders call Xiamen?","a":"Amoy","x":["Swatow","Canton"]}},"Q20927":{"t":"A bowl of mountains that traps Korea's summer heat","s":"Mountains close around Daegu on every side, and the basin they form traps the heat: the city is known for some of South Korea's hottest summers. To the north, the ridge of Palgongsan climbs to about 1,190 m, while the Nakdong River curves past the western edge. Daegu grew rich on textiles and was long famous for apples from the surrounding slopes.","w":"A large city filling a round basin ringed by mountains, with the wide Nakdong River curving along its western side.","f":["Yangnyeongsi, Daegu's herbal-medicine market, dates back to the 17th century.","Palgongsan, north of the city, is scattered with Buddhist temples and hermitages.","Koreans jokingly call the city “Daefrica” for its summer heat."],"q":{"q":"Which fruit was Daegu long famous for?","a":"Apples","x":["Tangerines","Persimmons"]}},"ne-pp-1159149137":{"t":"A gourd-shaped peninsula on the road to Manchuria","s":"Since 1994 Jinxi has gone by a new name, Huludao — “gourd island”, after the bulbous peninsula poking into Liaodong Bay beside it. The city lies on the Liaoxi Corridor, the narrow strip between hills and sea that has always carried the road from Beijing to the north-east. Just to the south-west, Xingcheng keeps a complete Ming-dynasty walled town.","w":"A coastal city on the western shore of Liaodong Bay, a knobbly peninsula jutting into the sea and hills close behind.","f":["Xingcheng's walls, first built in 1428, enclose one of China's best-preserved Ming towns.","Shanhaiguan, where the Great Wall meets the sea, guards the south-western end of the same coastal corridor.","Huludao means “gourd island”, although the gourd is really a peninsula."],"q":{"q":"What is Jinxi called today?","a":"Huludao","x":["Jinzhou","Yingkou"]}},"Q58401":{"t":"A railway junction that outgrew its village name","s":"A little over a century ago this was a village, and the name still says so: Shijiazhuang means “the Shi family's village”. Then railway lines met here, and a provincial capital grew up on the flat North China Plain. To the west, the Taihang Mountains rise like a wall. Some 40 km south-east, a stone bridge about 1,400 years old still spans its river.","w":"A large grid city on the flat plain, with the Taihang Mountains rising abruptly to the west.","f":["The Anji Bridge, built around 600 AD, is the world's oldest standing open-spandrel stone arch bridge.","Zhengding, on the city's northern edge, is home to Longxing Temple, founded in 586.","Shijiazhuang is the capital of Hebei, the province that surrounds Beijing."],"q":{"q":"What turned the village of Shijiazhuang into a city?","a":"Railways","x":["The Grand Canal","A gold rush"]}},"Q92277":{"t":"Where winter mist turns riverside trees to crystal","s":"In the depths of winter the Songhua River keeps flowing through Jilin: water released from the Fengman Dam upstream is too warm to freeze. Its mist settles on the willows along the banks and freezes into feathery rime, turning the city silver. The river draws a broad S through the streets, and the long reservoir of Songhua Lake stretches away to the south-east.","w":"In winter a dark, unfrozen river snaking through a snowy city; a long, branching reservoir lies upstream to the south-east.","f":["The province of Jilin takes its name from this city.","The name comes from Manchu girin ula, “along the river”.","In 1976 a meteorite shower fell near Jilin; its largest stone weighs about 1.7 tonnes."],"q":{"q":"Which river loops through Jilin city?","a":"The Songhua","x":["The Amur","The Yalu"]}},"Q42635":{"t":"A river-mouth city with a dialect all its own","s":"Hemmed in by green mountains, Wenzhou spreads along the south bank of the Ou River as it widens into the East China Sea. Its people are famous traders: shops and workshops founded by Wenzhou families dot Italy, France and far beyond. At home they speak a dialect so distinct that other Chinese struggle to understand it.","w":"A city on the south bank of a wide, muddy river mouth, with an islet in mid-river and mountains all around.","f":["Southern opera, nanxi, one of China's earliest mature forms of drama, arose here under the Song dynasty.","Jiangxin Islet, in the Ou River, carries two pagodas more than a thousand years old.","Many Chinese communities in Italy and France trace their roots to the Wenzhou area."],"q":{"q":"Which river meets the sea at Wenzhou?","a":"The Ou River","x":["The Qiantang","The Min"]}},"Q46747":{"t":"The “city of trees”, founded by a one-eyed warlord","s":"Sendai calls itself the “city of trees”, and avenues of zelkova really do run like green rivers through the centre. The castle town was founded around 1600 by Date Masamune, the warlord remembered as the “one-eyed dragon”. To the north-east, Matsushima Bay is scattered with some 260 pine-topped islets, praised for centuries as one of Japan's three finest views.","w":"The largest city of north-eastern Japan, on a plain by the Pacific, with an island-dotted bay to the north-east.","f":["At the Tanabata festival each August, huge paper streamers hang over Sendai's shopping arcades.","Matsushima is one of the Three Views of Japan, alongside Miyajima and Amanohashidate.","In the Zaō mountains to the south-west, winter rime turns trees into “snow monsters”."],"q":{"q":"Which warlord founded Sendai?","a":"Date Masamune","x":["Oda Nobunaga","Takeda Shingen"]}},"Q426996":{"t":"Silk town in the loops of the Jialing River","s":"The Jialing River seems unable to run straight here: it swings in wide loops through the red hills of the Sichuan Basin, and Nanchong has settled inside them. Mulberry trees and silk have kept the town busy for centuries, earning it a name as a silk city of western China. The 3rd-century historian Chen Shou, author of the Records of the Three Kingdoms, was born here.","w":"A city folded into the loops of a broad river, among terraced red hills in the north-east of the Sichuan Basin.","f":["Chen Shou's Records of the Three Kingdoms later inspired the famous novel Romance of the Three Kingdoms.","Langzhong, within Nanchong's borders, is one of the best-preserved old towns in China.","The Han astronomer Luoxia Hong, who helped draw up a new calendar in 104 BC, came from Langzhong."],"q":{"q":"Which river loops around Nanchong?","a":"The Jialing","x":["The Min","The Tuo"]}},"Q147187":{"t":"Coal, jujubes and an old town on the Grand Canal","s":"Its name means “jujube village”, but Zaozhuang grew on coal: modern mines opened here in the late 19th century, among the first run by a Chinese company. The city lies among low hills in the south of Shandong. On its southern edge the Grand Canal passes Taierzhuang, a canal town of stone lanes and waterways, and to the west stretch the long, reedy Weishan lakes.","w":"A spread-out city among low hills in southern Shandong, with the Grand Canal to the south and a chain of lakes to the west.","f":["The Zhongxing coal company, founded here in 1878, was one of China's first modern mining firms.","Tengzhou, within Zaozhuang, is traditionally named as the birthplace of the philosopher Mozi.","Weishan Lake belongs to the Nansi Lakes, a chain of four lakes more than 100 km long."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Zaozhuang mean?","a":"Jujube village","x":["Pear village","Salt village"]}},"Q210493":{"t":"Beacon smoke, sea mirages and China's oldest winery","s":"Pirates gave Yantai its name: in the Ming era, beacon fires on the hill above the harbour warned of their ships, and “Yantai” means “smoke terrace”. The city stretches along the north coast of the Shandong Peninsula, facing the islands of the Bohai Strait. Further west, at Penglai, rare summer mirages of phantom towns over the sea fed legends of the islands of the immortals.","w":"A long coastal city on the north shore of the Shandong Peninsula, a small island tied to land by a neck of sand, islands offshore.","f":["Changyu, China's first modern winery, was founded here in 1892.","Zhifu Island, joined to the mainland by a neck of sand, gave the port its old European name, Chefoo.","The hills around Yantai are one of China's best-known apple-growing regions."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Yantai mean?","a":"Smoke terrace","x":["Swallow terrace","Salt terrace"]}},"Q57719":{"t":"A crossroads city on the Yellow River's old bed","s":"For centuries the Yellow River flowed right through Xuzhou, until it swung north in 1855; its abandoned bed still winds across the city as a ribbon of water and trees. Low hills ring the centre, and Yunlong Lake shines on the southern side. Liu Bang, who founded the Han dynasty in 202 BC, came from nearby Pei, so the area is full of Han tombs.","w":"A big city ringed by low green hills on the flat plain, with a lake on its southern side and rail lines radiating out.","f":["Han-era terracotta soldiers, much smaller than those of Xi'an, were found at Shizishan in 1984.","Two of China's main railways, Beijing–Shanghai and the east–west Longhai line, cross here.","In ancient times the city was called Pengcheng."],"q":{"q":"Which dynasty's founder came from the Xuzhou area?","a":"The Han","x":["The Tang","The Ming"]}},"Q373346":{"t":"Home town of calligraphy's greatest master","s":"Dams have turned the Yi River into a chain of broad, still pools running right through Linyi, shining like a string of mirrors. North of the city rise the rugged Yimeng hills. The old name of this land was Langya, and it produced two of China's most famous men: the calligrapher Wang Xizhi, born in 303, and Zhuge Liang, the strategist of the Three Kingdoms.","w":"A wide river turned into a chain of broad pools running north–south through a large city, with hills to the north.","f":["Wang Xizhi's Preface to the Orchid Pavilion is the most celebrated work in Chinese calligraphy.","In 1972, bamboo slips from a tomb at Yinqueshan here revealed Sun Bin's lost Art of War.","Zhuge Liang was born in Yinan county, now part of Linyi."],"q":{"q":"Which famous calligrapher was born in Linyi?","a":"Wang Xizhi","x":["Su Shi","Mi Fu"]}},"Q189823":{"t":"Hainan's gateway across a narrow strait","s":"Haikou means “mouth of the sea”, and it sits exactly there, where the Nandu River meets the Qiongzhou Strait on Hainan's north coast. Across roughly 30 km of water lies the Leizhou Peninsula, part of mainland China. South-west of the city, extinct volcanic cones pock the green countryside; one of them, Ma'anling, still has a clear crater.","w":"A flat, sprawling city on Hainan's north coast, a river delta with islands, and the mainland across the strait.","f":["Old downtown Haikou is lined with qilou, arcaded shophouses from the early 20th century.","The volcano field around Haikou is part of the Leiqiong UNESCO Global Geopark.","Trains cross the Qiongzhou Strait on ferries — the tracks run straight onto the ships."],"q":{"q":"Which strait separates Haikou from mainland China?","a":"The Qiongzhou Strait","x":["The Taiwan Strait","The Bohai Strait"]}},"Q185684":{"t":"A science city beside a lake called “the nest”","s":"South-east of Hefei spreads Chaohu, one of China's five great freshwater lakes; its name means “nest lake”, and the city has now grown right down to its shore. Hefei lies between the Yangtze and the Huai, and it has become a science city, home to a leading university and an experimental fusion reactor nicknamed the “artificial sun”.","w":"A large city on a plain, with a big, irregular lake to its south-east and the Yangtze further south.","f":["Judge Bao, the incorruptible magistrate of Chinese opera, was born in Hefei in 999.","Hefei's EAST tokamak is one of China's main fusion-energy experiments.","The University of Science and Technology of China moved to Hefei in 1970."],"q":{"q":"Which lake lies just south-east of Hefei?","a":"Chaohu","x":["Taihu","Poyang Lake"]}},"Q360643":{"t":"The other Suzhou, on the plain where empires fell","s":"Don't confuse it with the garden city near Shanghai: this Suzhou, in northern Anhui, is written with a different character and sits among the endless flat fields of the Huai plain. Its history is bigger than its skyline. In 209 BC the first great peasant revolt against the Qin broke out at Dazexiang, now in the city, and seven years later Xiang Yu's army was surrounded at nearby Gaixia.","w":"A mid-sized city on a vast, flat patchwork of fields, crossed by straight canals and rail lines.","f":["The two Suzhous sound the same, but their first characters differ; this one means “lodging”.","Gaixia, where Xiang Yu was cornered in 202 BC, lies in today's Lingbi county.","Lingbi county is famous for Lingbi stones, dark scholar's rocks that ring when struck."],"q":{"q":"Which revolt against the Qin began near Suzhou, Anhui, in 209 BC?","a":"Chen Sheng and Wu Guang's uprising","x":["The Yellow Turban Rebellion","The Red Eyebrows rebellion"]}},"Q404763":{"t":"A basin of scholars, jade and a canal heading north","s":"Mountains wrap Nanyang on three sides, leaving its basin open only to the south. On the western edge lies the great Danjiangkou Reservoir, and from its shore a man-made river sets off north towards Beijing, more than 1,200 km away. Nanyang produced Zhang Heng, the Han scientist who built a seismoscope in 132 AD, and Zhang Zhongjing, revered as China's sage of medicine.","w":"A city in a wide basin ringed by hills on three sides, with a large, branching reservoir far to the west.","f":["The central route of the South–North Water Transfer begins at Taocha, on the reservoir in Nanyang's west.","Dushan jade, from a hill north of the city, is counted among China's four famous jades.","Zhang Zhongjing's treatise on fevers, written around 200 AD, is still studied in Chinese medicine."],"q":{"q":"What did Nanyang native Zhang Heng build in 132 AD?","a":"A seismoscope","x":["A magnetic compass","A printing press"]}},"Q42780":{"t":"Three rivers meet at a port like no other","s":"Three rivers meet in the heart of Ningbo and flow on as one to the sea, past kilometres of cranes and quays. Together with the Zhoushan islands offshore, this is the world's busiest port by cargo tonnage. To the north-west, the Hangzhou Bay Bridge draws a thin S-curve 36 km across the brown water. Ningbo has traded by sea for more than a thousand years.","w":"A city where rivers join, docks stretching to the coast, an archipelago to the east and a long bridge across the bay to the north-west.","f":["Tianyi Pavilion, built in 1561, is China's oldest surviving private library.","At Hemudu, west of the city, people were growing rice about 7,000 years ago.","The Hangzhou Bay Bridge opened in 2008."],"q":{"q":"Which bridge links Ningbo's side of Hangzhou Bay to the north shore?","a":"The Hangzhou Bay Bridge","x":["The Donghai Bridge","The Jiaozhou Bay Bridge"]}},"Q58422":{"t":"Coal, cement and China's first standard railway","s":"Modern Chinese industry started in places like Tangshan. Coal from the Kaiping mines had to be hauled away, so in 1881 the country's first standard-gauge railway was laid here, and in 1889 its first cement works followed. The city spreads over the plain between the Yan Mountains and the Bohai Sea, and on the coast to the south, the port of Caofeidian stands on land built out into the sea.","w":"An industrial city on the flat coastal plain, hills to the north, reclaimed land and port basins on the Bohai coast to the south.","f":["The Tangxu railway of 1881 ran about 10 km, from Tangshan to Xugezhuang.","Tangshan is called the porcelain capital of North China and is known for its bone china.","Caofeidian port was built on sandbanks and reclaimed land in the Bohai Sea."],"q":{"q":"What did China's first standard-gauge railway, opened at Tangshan in 1881, carry?","a":"Coal","x":["Salt","Iron ore"]}},"Q140631":{"t":"Taiwan's oldest city, fringed with glittering fish ponds","s":"Along Tainan's coast, land and water blur into a mosaic of fish ponds, salt pans and lagoons that glitter in the sun. This is Taiwan's oldest city: the Dutch built Fort Zeelandia at Anping in 1624, Koxinga drove them out in 1662, and Tainan remained the island's capital until the 1880s. Hundreds of temples still crowd its lanes.","w":"A flat coastal city in south-west Taiwan, with a shimmering patchwork of fish ponds and lagoons along the shore.","f":["Rare black-faced spoonbills spend the winter at the mouth of the Zengwen River, north of the city.","The closed Qigu salt works left behind a white “salt mountain” that visitors can climb.","Fort Zeelandia at Anping still has stretches of its original Dutch brick walls."],"q":{"q":"Which European power built Fort Zeelandia at Tainan?","a":"The Dutch","x":["The Spanish","The Portuguese"]}},"Q72877":{"t":"Buddhas in the cliffs at the edge of the steppe","s":"Datong stands high on the Shanxi plateau, where brown loess hills reach towards Inner Mongolia and fragments of the Great Wall run along the ridges. As Pingcheng, it was the capital of the Northern Wei, who in the 5th century carved the Yungang Grottoes into a sandstone cliff west of the city. Coal later made it rich, and dark mining scars mark the hills.","w":"A walled city in a broad brown basin on the Shanxi plateau, with dark coal-mining areas in the hills to the west.","f":["The Yungang Grottoes hold about 51,000 Buddhist statues; the largest is nearly 17 m tall.","The Northern Wei ruled from here from 398 until they moved their capital to Luoyang in 494.","The Hanging Temple, clinging to a cliff about 60 km south-east, is some 1,500 years old."],"q":{"q":"Which Buddhist grottoes lie on Datong's western edge?","a":"Yungang","x":["Longmen","Mogao"]}},"Q34600":{"t":"Japan's capital for a thousand years, in a bowl of hills","s":"A grid in a bowl: straight streets fill a basin closed by wooded hills on three sides and open only to the south. The plan was copied from the Chinese capital Chang'an when the emperor moved here in 794, and Kyoto remained the imperial capital until 1868. Just over the eastern hills shines Lake Biwa, the largest lake in Japan.","w":"A grid city in a basin with wooded hills on three sides, open to the south; Lake Biwa lies just beyond the eastern hills.","f":["Every 16 August, bonfires shaped as giant characters blaze on the hills around the city.","A canal completed in 1890 brings water from Lake Biwa through the hills into Kyoto.","Seventeen sites in and around Kyoto together form one UNESCO World Heritage listing."],"q":{"q":"In which year did Kyoto become Japan's capital?","a":"794","x":["710","1185"]}},"ne-pp-1159140995":{"t":"A county that grows flowers for all of China","s":"Shuyang is only a county, but a huge one, spread across the flat, watery plain of northern Jiangsu. Greenhouses and nursery beds glint all over its fields: Shuyang is one of China's biggest centres for garden plants, flowers and saplings, many of them sold online to buyers across the country. Straight channels between high levees cross the plain, dug to lead floods to the sea.","w":"A flat plain of fields and greenhouses crossed by wide, straight flood channels, with a large town at its centre.","f":["The name means “north bank of the Shu River”: yang is the sunny side of a river.","Hundreds of villages here sell plants online, a trade that grew out of old family nurseries.","The New Yi River, a flood channel dug in the mid-20th century, crosses the county to the sea."],"q":{"q":"What does Shuyang grow on a huge scale?","a":"Flowers and nursery plants","x":["Tea","Tobacco"]}},"Q404817":{"t":"An old town laid out like an ancient coin","s":"Shangqiu's old town, Guide, is a square of walls set inside a round moat and an outer ring of earth — the design of a traditional Chinese coin, round outside and square within. That ring of water can stand out on the flat plain of eastern Henan. The name recalls the Shang, who are said to have had early seats in the area.","w":"A small square old town inside a ring of water, beside a large modern city on the flat plain.","f":["Yingtian Academy here was one of the four great academies of the Northern Song.","Guide's walls were rebuilt in the early 16th century after floods buried the older town.","The name means “mound of the Shang”."],"q":{"q":"What does the layout of Shangqiu's old town resemble?","a":"An ancient coin","x":["A turtle","A lotus flower"]}},"Q42651":{"t":"The city named for tin that ran out","s":"Taihu, a vast shallow lake scattered with islands, washes against Wuxi's southern edge, and the Grand Canal threads straight through the city. The name means “without tin”: legend says the local tin was mined out in ancient times. On the western shore a bronze Buddha 88 m tall gazes over the water, and the long peninsula of Yuantouzhu, “turtle-head islet”, turns pink with cherry blossom in spring.","w":"A large city on the north shore of a vast lake with peninsulas and islands, a canal cutting through the centre.","f":["Taihu covers more than 2,000 km² and is one of China's largest freshwater lakes.","The Lingshan Grand Buddha, cast in bronze, was completed in 1996.","Huishan clay figurines, plump painted dolls, have been made in Wuxi for centuries."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Wuxi mean?","a":"Without tin","x":["Five streams","Western lake"]}},"Q535534":{"t":"The Blue City beneath the Yin Mountains","s":"A dark mountain wall runs along Hohhot's northern edge: the Daqing range, part of the Yin Shan, beyond which the Mongolian plateau begins. The city's name is Mongolian for “blue city”. Altan Khan founded it in the late 16th century around a Buddhist monastery. Today it is China's dairy capital, home to the country's two biggest milk companies.","w":"A large city on a flat plain, a straight dark mountain wall to the north, the Yellow River further south-west.","f":["Dazhao, the monastery the city grew around, dates from 1579.","The Yili and Mengniu dairy companies both have their headquarters in Hohhot.","South of the city lies the tomb of Wang Zhaojun, a Han court lady married to a Xiongnu ruler."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Hohhot mean?","a":"Blue city","x":["Red city","Golden city"]}},"Q187136":{"t":"Ancient capital of peonies and cliff-carved Buddhas","s":"Few places have been China's capital as often as Luoyang, strung along the Luo River in a basin between mountains. South of the city, the Yi River slips through a gap in the hills — the Longmen gorge, whose limestone cliffs hold about 100,000 Buddhist statues. The Yellow River flows past to the north. Every April the city bursts into peonies, its emblem.","w":"A long city on the Luo River in a basin, a narrow gap in the hills to the south, the Yellow River to the north.","f":["The White Horse Temple, traditionally founded in 68 AD, is considered China's first Buddhist temple.","The Longmen Grottoes have been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 2000.","Luoyang served as the capital of more than a dozen dynasties and kingdoms."],"q":{"q":"Which flower is Luoyang famous for?","a":"The peony","x":["The plum blossom","The lotus"]}},"ne-pp-1159146993":{"t":"Home of the sage who wrote the first book on tea","s":"The city at this spot is Tianmen, on the flat Jianghan Plain of Hubei, where rice paddies, fish ponds and lakes break the land into a watery mosaic. The Han River curves along its southern side. In the 8th century a foundling raised in a monastery here, Lu Yu, grew up to write The Classic of Tea, the world's first book about the drink.","w":"A city on a flat plain of paddies, fish ponds and lakes, with the Han River curving to the south.","f":["In Lu Yu's day the town was called Jingling; it was renamed Tianmen in 1726.","The name, “Heaven's Gate”, comes from hills north-west of the city.","The Jianghan Plain, built up by the Yangtze and Han rivers, is one of China's great rice and cotton lands."],"q":{"q":"Which book did Lu Yu, a native of Tianmen, write?","a":"The Classic of Tea","x":["The Art of War","The Book of Songs"]}},"Q304848":{"t":"China's great oilfield on a plain of a hundred lakes","s":"Hundreds of small lakes and marshes dot the flat Songnen Plain here, glinting between grassland and the grids of oil-well pads. Oil was struck in 1959, and the city was named Daqing, “great celebration”, to mark the tenth anniversary of the People's Republic. For decades Daqing produced more oil than any other field in China. In winter the whole plain turns white and frozen.","w":"A flat plain dotted with countless small lakes, criss-crossed by roads and regular rows of oil wells.","f":["For more than 25 years in a row, Daqing pumped over 50 million tonnes of oil a year.","Nodding-donkey pumps still work among the city's streets and lakes.","Winter temperatures here regularly fall below −25 °C."],"q":{"q":"What was discovered at Daqing in 1959?","a":"Oil","x":["Gold","Iron ore"]}},"Q360702":{"t":"Tea country at the foot of the Dabie Mountains","s":"Lu'an sits where the Huai plain meets the Dabie Mountains, which rise in green folds to the south-west. Big reservoirs glitter among those hills; built from the 1950s, they feed canals that water a vast spread of farmland. The slopes are also tea country: Lu'an Guapian, one of China's famous green teas, is made from single leaves, with no buds or stems.","w":"A city at the edge of the plain, with wooded mountains and several large reservoirs to the south and south-west.","f":["The Pishihang irrigation system, fed by these reservoirs, is one of the largest in China.","Guapian means “melon slices”, after the shape of the rolled leaves.","On the border with Hubei, the Dabie Mountains rise above 1,700 m."],"q":{"q":"Which tea is Lu'an famous for?","a":"Guapian green tea","x":["Pu'er","Oolong"]}},"ne-pp-1159146887":{"t":"A Yangtze city that climbed uphill from the water","s":"Above the Three Gorges Dam, the Yangtze no longer races past here brown and fast: it has become a broad, calm reservoir winding between steep green hills. When the water rose in the 2000s, the lower parts of old Wanzhou went under, and new districts grew higher up the slopes. The city, called Wanxian until 1998, is a gateway to the gorges downstream.","w":"A hilly city spread along both banks of a wide, winding reservoir on the Yangtze, with bridges across it.","f":["The Wanxian Yangtze Bridge, opened in 1997, spans 420 m — then a record for a concrete arch.","Wanzhou lies roughly 300 km upstream of the Three Gorges Dam.","Wanzhou grilled fish, served in a spicy broth, is known all over China."],"q":{"q":"Which reservoir now fills the Yangtze at Wanzhou?","a":"The Three Gorges","x":["Xiaolangdi","Danjiangkou"]}},"Q42622":{"t":"Canals and gardens between great lakes","s":"Water is everywhere around Suzhou: vast Taihu to the west, the Yangcheng and Jinji lakes to the east, and canals threading the old town in between. Founded in 514 BC as the capital of the Wu kingdom, it has stood on roughly the same spot ever since. Silk and scholars' gardens made it famous, and nine of those gardens are now on the UNESCO list.","w":"A large city between a vast lake to the west and smaller lakes to the east, criss-crossed by canals.","f":["The Grand Canal still carries barges along the western and southern sides of old Suzhou.","The brick pagoda on Tiger Hill, finished in 961, leans noticeably.","Suzhou embroidery, using silk thread split finer than a hair, is one of China's four great styles."],"q":{"q":"What is Suzhou's UNESCO World Heritage listing for?","a":"Classical gardens","x":["Rock-cut grottoes","Imperial tombs"]}},"Q74957":{"t":"A steel city beneath the “thousand lotus peaks”","s":"Anshan was built on steel: ore from the surrounding hills has fed its blast furnaces since the early 20th century, and Anshan Iron and Steel grew into one of China's biggest producers. South-east of the city rise the Qianshan hills, called the “thousand lotus peaks” for their crowd of rocky summits. The name Anshan itself means “saddle mountain”, after a saddle-shaped hill.","w":"A large industrial city on the edge of the Liao River plain, with rugged, rocky hills to the south-east.","f":["Anshan's Jade Buddha, one of the largest in the world, was carved from a single jade boulder.","The local blast furnaces produced their first iron in 1919.","The Qianshan hills have been sacred to both Buddhists and Taoists for centuries."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Anshan mean?","a":"Saddle mountain","x":["Iron mountain","Peace mountain"]}},"Q172146":{"t":"A halfway town on a tableland of a thousand ponds","s":"Hundreds of ponds glint on the Taoyuan tableland around Zhongli, dug over the centuries to store water for the rice fields. The name is said to recall the town's position halfway along the old road between Tamsui and Hsinchu. Many residents are Hakka, and Taiwan's main international airport is only a short drive to the north.","w":"A dense city on a low plateau south-west of Taipei, surrounded by fields dotted with many small ponds.","f":["The tableland once held thousands of irrigation ponds; hundreds survive today.","Zhongli is a district of Taoyuan, one of Taiwan's six special municipalities.","Taoyuan International Airport, Taiwan's busiest, lies about 15 km to the north."],"q":{"q":"What dots the Taoyuan tableland around Zhongli?","a":"Irrigation ponds","x":["Tea terraces","Salt pans"]}},"Q58570":{"t":"An ancient capital that gave China hundreds of proverbs","s":"A man once came to Handan to copy the elegant local walk, failed, and forgot his own, so he had to crawl home. That is just one of hundreds of Chinese idioms born in this city. From 386 BC Handan was the capital of the state of Zhao. Today it spreads over the plain at the foot of the Taihang Mountains, a city of steel and coal.","w":"A large city on the flat plain, with the Taihang Mountains rising to the west.","f":["Handan was the capital of Zhao from 386 BC until the Qin took it in 228 BC.","At Cishan, west of the city, people stored millet in pits more than 8,000 years ago.","Around 307 BC, King Wuling of Zhao put his soldiers in nomad trousers so they could fight on horseback."],"q":{"q":"Which ancient state had its capital at Handan?","a":"Zhao","x":["Qin","Chu"]}},"Q217681":{"t":"The city at the foot of China's most sacred mountain","s":"Tai'an grew up at the very foot of Tai Shan, a mountain that rises abruptly from the Shandong plain to more than 1,500 m. For two thousand years emperors climbed it to offer sacrifices to heaven and earth, and pilgrims still toil up thousands of stone steps to watch the sunrise from the top. The city's great Dai Temple marks the spot where the climb traditionally begins.","w":"A city spread along the southern foot of a dark, isolated mountain massif that rises steeply from the plain.","f":["More than 6,000 stone steps lead to the summit of Tai Shan.","In 1987 Tai Shan was among China's first UNESCO World Heritage sites, listed as both cultural and natural.","Qin Shi Huang, China's first emperor, performed sacrifices on Tai Shan in 219 BC."],"q":{"q":"Which sacred mountain rises over Tai'an?","a":"Tai Shan","x":["Hua Shan","Emei Shan"]}},"Q59144":{"t":"A deep harbour once leased to France","s":"At the base of the Leizhou Peninsula, Zhanjiang wraps around a deep, sheltered harbour, shielded by Donghai Island, the largest island in Guangdong. From 1898 to 1945 this was the French leased territory of Kouang-Tchéou-Wan. Mangroves fringe the coast all around, and south-west of the city lies Huguangyan, an almost perfectly round lake in a volcanic crater.","w":"A port city on a large sheltered bay on the east side of the Leizhou Peninsula, with big, low islands offshore.","f":["Huguangyan is a maar: a crater blasted out by volcanic steam explosions.","Zhanjiang's mangrove reserve is the largest in China.","The territory the French called Kouang-Tchéou-Wan was returned to China in 1945."],"q":{"q":"Which European country once leased Zhanjiang?","a":"France","x":["Portugal","Germany"]}},"Q71455":{"t":"A town on a watery plain that raised Olympic gymnasts","s":"The flat Jianghan Plain around Xiantao is more water than land: rice paddies, fish ponds and lakes, cut by straight canals, with the Han River winding along the northern edge. Gymnastics made this modest city famous. Its sports school has turned out world and Olympic champions, including the all-around gold medallists Li Xiaoshuang and Yang Wei.","w":"A city on a flat, lake-strewn plain cut by straight canals, with the Han River curving along its north side.","f":["Until 1986 Xiantao was called Mianyang — not to be confused with Mianyang in Sichuan.","Li Xiaoshuang won Olympic all-around gold in 1996, Yang Wei in 2008; both were born here.","The town of Pengchang is a major centre for non-woven fabric used in masks and medical gowns."],"q":{"q":"Which sport made Xiantao famous?","a":"Gymnastics","x":["Table tennis","Diving"]}},"Q217698":{"t":"The world capital of kites","s":"Every April the sky over Weifang fills with dragons, centipedes and swallows of silk and bamboo: the city has held an international kite festival since 1984 and calls itself the world's kite capital. It lies on the flat plain of central Shandong and reaches north to Laizhou Bay, where salt pans form a bright chequerboard along the shore. Nearby Yangjiabu has printed colourful New Year pictures for centuries.","w":"A big city on the Shandong plain; to the north, along Laizhou Bay, a grid of salt pans and ponds.","f":["Yangjiabu's New Year woodblock prints have been made since the Ming dynasty.","Zheng Banqiao, famous for painting bamboo, served as magistrate of Weixian, today's Weifang, in the 18th century.","Weifang's kite museum has a roof shaped like a dragon kite."],"q":{"q":"What is Weifang called the world capital of?","a":"Kites","x":["Lanterns","Fireworks"]}},"Q404873":{"t":"Tea hills where northern China turns southern","s":"Somewhere around Xinyang, northern China gives way to southern China: the Huai River, the traditional line between wheat and rice, between dry winters and damp ones, crosses its land. The hills south of the city grow Xinyang Maojian, one of China's famous green teas. On the border with Hubei, Jigong Shan rises with its old summer villas, and reservoirs gleam among the forests.","w":"A city among green hills and reservoirs in southern Henan, with the Huai River plain opening to the north.","f":["Xinyang Maojian is counted among China's ten famous teas.","Foreign missionaries and merchants built stone villas on Jigong Shan from the early 1900s.","Nanwan Lake, a reservoir just west of the city, is dotted with dozens of wooded islands."],"q":{"q":"Which green tea comes from Xinyang?","a":"Maojian","x":["Longjing","Biluochun"]}},"Q425492":{"t":"Where the Tuo meets the Yangtze and baijiu is king","s":"The Tuo River slides into the Yangtze at Luzhou, wrapping the old centre in water on three sides. This is one of the homes of baijiu, China's fiery grain spirit, and some of the local fermentation pits have been in constant use since the Ming dynasty. Around the city, the low red hills of southern Sichuan roll away towards the mountains of Guizhou.","w":"A city on a tongue of land where a smaller river joins the wide, brown Yangtze, among low red hills.","f":["Luzhou Laojiao's “1573” cellars are named after the year their fermentation pits were dug.","Oil-paper umbrellas are still made by hand in the nearby town of Fenshui.","The rich “strong-aroma” style of baijiu is also known as Luzhou style."],"q":{"q":"Which drink is Luzhou famous for?","a":"Baijiu","x":["Rice wine","Beer"]}},"Q48320":{"t":"A city squeezed between the mountains and the sea","s":"Kobe is a ribbon: the city runs for kilometres along Osaka Bay but is rarely more than a few kilometres deep, pinned against the steep green Rokko mountains. The only way left to grow was into the sea. Port Island and Rokko Island are square slabs of land built from earth cut out of those very mountains. To the west, a great suspension bridge leaps to Awaji Island.","w":"A narrow strip of city between steep green mountains and the bay, square artificial islands offshore, a long bridge to the west.","f":["The Akashi Kaikyō Bridge held the world record for main span, 1,991 m, from 1998 to 2022.","Kobe's port opened to foreign ships in 1868, and a quarter of Western-style houses survives.","Mount Rokko, the highest point of the range, is 931 m high."],"q":{"q":"Which island does the Akashi Kaikyō Bridge link Kobe to?","a":"Awaji","x":["Shōdoshima","Sado"]}},"Q363166":{"t":"Two rivers meet here, and even their names merge","s":"The Zhang and Gong rivers meet at the tip of old Ganzhou, and even their names merge: their two characters combine into “Gan”, the river they form, which flows north to Poyang Lake. The old town is still guarded by Song-dynasty walls along the water. Around it, red hills are striped with navel-orange groves, and the mines of southern Jiangxi yield tungsten and rare earths.","w":"An old town on a point of land where two rivers join, surrounded by hills striped with orchards.","f":["Fushou Gou, a drainage system built under the Song dynasty, still keeps the old town from flooding.","Southern Jiangxi around Ganzhou is one of China's biggest navel-orange regions.","Ganzhou is a Hakka heartland, with fortified family compounds called weiwu."],"q":{"q":"Which two rivers meet at Ganzhou to form the Gan?","a":"The Zhang and the Gong","x":["The Xiang and the Zi","The Min and the Tuo"]}},"Q38968":{"t":"A river loop, karst hills and a notorious noodle soup","s":"The Liu River swings almost all the way round the centre of Liuzhou, drawing a nearly closed loop, while green karst hills poke up between the city blocks. It is an industrial city — Wuling minivans are built here — but it is best known for luosifen, a river-snail rice-noodle soup whose pungent smell divides China. The Tang poet Liu Zongyuan governed Liuzhou and died here in 819.","w":"A city inside a big, almost closed loop of a river, with steep green karst hills scattered among the buildings.","f":["Liu Zongyuan's memorial temple stands in Liuhou Park in the city centre.","Luosifen's smell comes mostly from fermented bamboo shoots, not from the snails.","Liuzhou is one of Guangxi's biggest industrial cities, making cars, steel and machinery."],"q":{"q":"Which famously pungent dish comes from Liuzhou?","a":"Luosifen","x":["Stinky tofu","Guilin rice noodles"]}},"Q75026":{"t":"A giant coal pit and the cradle of the Qing","s":"A huge terraced hole scars Fushun's south-western edge: the West Open-Pit Mine, some 6 km long and hundreds of metres deep, one of the largest coal pits in Asia. The Hun River runs through the city. In the hills to the east lies Hetu Ala, where the Manchu leader Nurhaci set up his first capital in 1603 — the start of a dynasty that would go on to rule all of China.","w":"A city along a river valley, with a huge grey terraced pit on its south-western edge.","f":["Coal has been mined at Fushun on an industrial scale for more than a century.","Yongling, the tombs of Nurhaci's ancestors, is part of a UNESCO World Heritage site.","The Dahuofang Reservoir, upstream on the Hun River, supplies water to much of central Liaoning."],"q":{"q":"What is the giant hole on Fushun's south-western edge?","a":"An open-pit coal mine","x":["A meteorite crater","A volcanic crater"]}},"Q416544":{"t":"The gateway to the Peach Blossom Spring","s":"Changde stands on the Yuan River just before it spreads into the western side of Dongting Lake, in a land of paddies and shallow lakes. A flood wall along the river is carved with poems and calligraphy for kilometres. Upriver, Taoyuan county is identified with the Peach Blossom Spring, the hidden valley of Tao Yuanming's 5th-century fable — China's picture of a lost paradise.","w":"A city on a wide river just west of a vast lake plain, with hills rising to the west.","f":["The poetry wall on the Yuan River flood dyke runs for about 3 km.","Liuye Lake, “willow-leaf lake”, lies on the city's north-eastern edge.","In Tao Yuanming's fable a fisherman finds a valley whose people had lived cut off from the world for centuries."],"q":{"q":"Which fabled paradise is linked to the Changde area?","a":"The Peach Blossom Spring","x":["Shangri-La","Mount Penglai"]}},"Q20921":{"t":"A “big field” that grew into Korea's science city","s":"Little more than a century ago Daejeon was a village called Hanbat, “big field”. The railway from Seoul to Busan arrived in 1905, a line to the south-west soon branched off here, and the junction became a city. Today Daejeon is Korea's science town: dozens of research institutes cluster in Daedeok to the north, and in 1993 the city hosted an international Expo.","w":"A large city in a basin of low hills in central South Korea, where railways and motorways converge.","f":["The Honam line to the south-west, which branches off here, was completed in 1914.","The Yuseong hot springs in western Daejeon have been known for centuries.","Many government agencies moved to the Government Complex in Daejeon in the late 1990s."],"q":{"q":"Which world event did Daejeon host in 1993?","a":"An international Expo","x":["The Summer Olympics","The Asian Games"]}},"Q426756":{"t":"A “sweet city” of sugar cane on the looping Tuo","s":"The Tuo River winds through Neijiang in tight, glinting loops, cutting through the red hills of the Sichuan Basin. Sugar cane once covered these slopes, and the sugar trade earned Neijiang the nickname “sweet city”. The painter Zhang Daqian was born here in 1899; he went on to work across the world, from the caves of Dunhuang to Brazil and California.","w":"A city among red hills, threaded by a river coiling in tight meanders, halfway between Chengdu and Chongqing.","f":["Zhang Daqian spent more than two years copying the murals of the Mogao Caves at Dunhuang.","Neijiang sits on the railway and motorway midway between Chengdu and Chongqing.","Sugar refining made the city prosperous in the early 20th century."],"q":{"q":"Which painter was born in Neijiang?","a":"Zhang Daqian","x":["Qi Baishi","Xu Beihong"]}},"Q68695":{"t":"Zayton, the medieval port that dazzled Marco Polo","s":"Seven centuries ago, travellers from Venice and Morocco sailed into Quanzhou and called it Zayton, one of the greatest ports on earth. Arab, Persian and Indian merchants lived here, and a mosque founded in 1009 still stands. Today the city spreads around the estuary of the Jin River, and its Song and Yuan trading sites are on the UNESCO list.","w":"A city on a river estuary on the Fujian coast, with a bay to the south and green hills behind.","f":["Quanzhou's Song–Yuan port sites have been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 2021.","The Luoyang Bridge, a stone bridge across an estuary here, was completed in 1059.","Anxi county, north-west of the city, is the home of Tieguanyin oolong tea."],"q":{"q":"What did Marco Polo and Ibn Battuta call Quanzhou?","a":"Zayton","x":["Cathay","Quinsai"]}},"Q41283":{"t":"The “city of light” under a mountain of stone pillars","s":"Gwangju's name means “district of light”. The city fills a basin in Korea's south-west, the old heart of the rice-growing Jeolla region. To the east rises Mudeungsan, about 1,190 m high, its upper slopes crowned with clusters of tall, many-sided stone columns. Gwangju is proud of its food and its art, and it hosts the Gwangju Biennale, one of Asia's leading contemporary-art shows.","w":"A large city in a basin in south-western Korea, with a big rounded mountain rising to the east.","f":["Mudeungsan's stone columns, Ipseokdae and Seoseokdae, formed as cooling lava cracked.","The Gwangju Biennale has been held since 1995.","Mudeungsan became a national park in 2013."],"q":{"q":"What does Gwangju's name mean?","a":"District of light","x":["Great hill","Big field"]}},"Q1475":{"t":"Once the capital, now the most populous Philippine city","s":"For almost three decades this was the official capital of the Philippines, and today no Philippine city has more people. Quezon City fills the north-eastern side of Metro Manila, spread across a low plateau above the bay. At its northern edge a rare patch of dark green interrupts the grey: the forested watershed of the La Mesa reservoir, part of the capital region's water supply.","w":"The north-east corner of Metro Manila's grey sprawl, with a dark green forest and reservoir at its northern edge.","f":["Quezon City was the official capital of the Philippines from 1948 to 1976, when Manila regained the title.","It is named after Manuel L. Quezon, president of the Philippine Commonwealth, who founded it in 1939.","The main campus of the University of the Philippines is in its Diliman district."],"q":{"q":"Who is Quezon City named after?","a":"Manuel L. Quezon","x":["José Rizal","Emilio Aguinaldo"]}},"Q47916":{"t":"Nawabs, kebabs and a looping river on the Ganges plain","s":"Follow the Gomti as it loops lazily across the flat Ganges plain, and you will find Lucknow wrapped around its bends. In 1775 the Nawabs of Awadh moved their capital here and filled it with domed imambaras, gardens and a court culture of poetry and fine manners. The city still trades on that refinement — and on its slow-cooked kebabs.","w":"A large city strung along the winding Gomti River on the flat, green Ganges plain.","f":["Lucknow is famous for chikankari, a delicate white-on-white hand embroidery.","The Bara Imambara, built in the 1780s, hides a maze of narrow passages known as the Bhulbhulaiya.","Lucknow is the capital of Uttar Pradesh, India's most populous state."],"q":{"q":"Which river winds through Lucknow?","a":"The Gomti","x":["The Yamuna","The Chambal"]}},"Q173985":{"t":"A colonial canal town that grew into a textile giant","s":"Until the late 19th century this was dry scrubland between the Chenab and Ravi rivers. Then British engineers dug irrigation canals, and in the 1890s a market town called Lyallpur rose among the new fields. At its centre stands a clock tower, with eight bazaars fanning out like the arms of a Union Jack. Renamed Faisalabad in 1977, it is now one of Pakistan's great textile centres.","w":"A big, flat city amid a chequerboard of irrigated fields, with straight canals running between two rivers.","f":["Lyallpur was named after Sir James Lyall, a British lieutenant-governor of Punjab.","In 1977 the city was renamed in honour of King Faisal of Saudi Arabia.","The eight bazaars radiating from the clock tower were laid out to echo the British flag."],"q":{"q":"What was Faisalabad called before 1977?","a":"Lyallpur","x":["Montgomery","Campbellpur"]}},"Q61092":{"t":"An island port tied to the mainland by two long bridges","s":"Two hair-thin lines stretch across the strait to Penang Island — road bridges of about 13 and 24 km. George Town fills the island's north-eastern cape, beneath a green, hilly interior. Francis Light founded it in 1786 as a British trading post, and its old quarter of shophouses, temples, mosques and clan houses is now a UNESCO World Heritage site.","w":"A small, hilly green island off Malaysia's west coast, a city on its north-east tip and two bridges to the mainland.","f":["The second Penang bridge, opened in 2014, is about 24 km long.","George Town and Melaka were listed together as a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2008.","Penang Hill rises above the city to more than 800 m; a funicular has climbed it since 1923."],"q":{"q":"Who founded George Town in 1786?","a":"Francis Light","x":["Stamford Raffles","James Brooke"]}},"Q10392":{"t":"Where Jakarta's sprawl rolls east without a seam","s":"Try to find where Jakarta ends and Bekasi begins — from cruise height you can't. The city merges into the capital's eastern flank, a grey carpet laced with toll roads, rail lines and the brown Bekasi River. Further east, the roofs of huge industrial estates glint, and beyond them green rice fields finally take over. Bekasi is the “Bek” in Jabodetabek, the name of Greater Jakarta.","w":"Grey sprawl merging with Jakarta's eastern edge, crossed by toll roads; factory roofs and rice fields farther east.","f":["Jabodetabek stands for Jakarta, Bogor, Depok, Tangerang and Bekasi — one of the largest urban areas on Earth.","An elevated toll road about 36 km long runs above the Jakarta–Cikampek motorway through Bekasi."],"q":{"q":"Bekasi supplies the last syllable of which metro-area name?","a":"Jabodetabek","x":["Gerbangkertosusila","Mebidangro"]}},"Q66616":{"t":"A plateau city famed for its night-time street food","s":"By day Sarafa is a jewellers' lane; after dark the shutters come down and food stalls move in, frying jalebis and kachoris until late. That switch sums up Indore, the largest city of Madhya Pradesh and a busy trading hub on the Malwa plateau. Around it, dark soil and flat fields stretch in every direction, broken by small reservoirs that glint in the sun.","w":"A large, compact city on a flat plateau of dark fields, dotted with small lakes and reservoirs.","f":["Indore was the seat of the Holkar dynasty, whose Rajwada palace still stands in the old centre.","India's national cleanliness survey has ranked Indore the country's cleanest city many times.","The city stands at about 550 m on the Malwa plateau, above the Narmada valley to the south."],"q":{"q":"Which dynasty ruled from Indore?","a":"The Holkars","x":["The Scindias","The Gaekwads"]}},"Q72818":{"t":"The Red River delta's seaport, framed by karst islands","s":"Brown delta rivers fan out into the Gulf of Tonkin here, and Haiphong grew up on one of them as northern Vietnam's main seaport. East of the city the flat delta breaks off at Cat Ba, an island of jagged limestone peaks next to Ha Long Bay. In early summer the streets blaze red with flamboyant trees, the flower that gave Haiphong its nickname.","w":"A port city among brown river mouths on a flat delta; jagged green limestone islands rise just to the east.","f":["Cat Ba, the largest island in the Ha Long Bay area, belongs to Haiphong.","On the nearby Bạch Đằng River, Vietnamese forces twice trapped invading fleets on hidden stakes, in 938 and 1288.","A sea bridge about 5 km long links the city to the deep-water port of Lạch Huyện."],"q":{"q":"Haiphong's nickname comes from which red-flowering tree?","a":"The flamboyant","x":["The lotus","The hibiscus"]}},"Q378879":{"t":"A valley capital in the foothills of the Hindu Kush","s":"Snowy ridges crowd the horizon, and between them the green floor of the Swat valley opens like a corridor. Saidu Sharif and its twin town, Mingora, fill a bend of the valley beside the Swat River. The town grew around the shrine of a 19th-century saint, the Akhund of Swat, and until 1969 it was the capital of the princely state of Swat.","w":"Twin towns on a green valley floor beside a braided river, hemmed in by steep ridges that turn white in winter.","f":["The town is named after Saidu Baba, the Akhund of Swat, whose shrine stands here.","Swat was a princely state until 1969, with Saidu Sharif as its capital.","The ruins of the Butkara Buddhist stupa, at the edge of town, are more than 2,000 years old."],"q":{"q":"Saidu Sharif was the capital of which princely state?","a":"Swat","x":["Chitral","Dir"]}},"Q93230":{"t":"The old bazaar city beside Pakistan's planned capital","s":"Two cities, one sprawl: Rawalpindi's dense, irregular streets run straight into the tidy grid of Islamabad to the north. Beyond, the wooded Margalla Hills form the first ridge of the Himalayan foothills, while to the south the Potohar plateau is cut by deep ravines. When Islamabad was chosen as the new capital in 1959, Rawalpindi stood in for it until the planned city was ready.","w":"A dense city merging into a neat grid to the north, below a wooded ridge; gullied plateau to the south.","f":["Rawalpindi served as Pakistan's interim capital in the 1960s while Islamabad was being built.","The Grand Trunk Road, one of Asia's oldest highways, runs through the city."],"q":{"q":"Rawalpindi stood in as capital while which city was built?","a":"Islamabad","x":["Karachi","Lahore"]}},"Q11909":{"t":"A princely city with crocodiles in its river","s":"A lone hill on the north-eastern horizon — Pavagadh, crowned with temples — is the landmark for finding Vadodara on the flat Gujarat plain. The Gaekwad maharajas made Baroda one of India's richest princely states and finished the vast Laxmi Vilas Palace here in 1890. Their river, the Vishwamitri, winds right through town, and it is still home to a large population of mugger crocodiles.","w":"A flat city on the Gujarat plain with a winding river through it; a lone steep hill rises about 45 km north-east.","f":["Laxmi Vilas Palace is often said to be four times the size of Buckingham Palace.","Pavagadh hill and the ruined city of Champaner at its foot form a UNESCO World Heritage site."],"q":{"q":"Which reptile lives in large numbers in Vadodara's river?","a":"The mugger crocodile","x":["The gharial","The saltwater crocodile"]}},"Q200123":{"t":"Punjab's workshop city in a sea of wheat and rice","s":"Around Ludhiana the land is ruled into squares of wheat in winter and rice in summer, some of the most intensively farmed fields in India. The city itself is the largest in Indian Punjab and its industrial engine, turning out bicycles, knitwear and machine parts. The Sutlej runs a few kilometres to the north, and one of its old channels, the Buddha Nullah, still threads through town.","w":"A large grey city on a flat plain of neat green or golden fields, with the broad Sutlej to the north.","f":["Hero Cycles, founded in Ludhiana, grew into one of the world's largest bicycle makers.","Punjab Agricultural University in Ludhiana played a key role in India's Green Revolution of the 1960s."],"q":{"q":"Which product made Ludhiana's industry famous?","a":"Bicycles","x":["Carpets","Glass bangles"]}},"Q42941":{"t":"The Taj Mahal's city, on a bend of the Yamuna","s":"The Yamuna swings in a wide bend here, and on its bank stands the reason millions come: the Taj Mahal, the white marble tomb Shah Jahan built for his wife Mumtaz Mahal. Upstream, the red sandstone walls of Agra Fort follow the same river. From cruise height the monuments are too small to pick out, but the river's curve and the city packed along it are easy to find.","w":"A large city on the flat plain, wrapped around a sweeping bend of the Yamuna River south-east of Delhi.","f":["The Taj Mahal took about 20 years to build; the whole complex was finished around 1653.","Agra Fort and Fatehpur Sikri, Akbar's red sandstone city about 35 km west, are World Heritage sites too.","Under Akbar, Jahangir and Shah Jahan, Agra was for long periods the capital of the Mughal Empire."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows past the Taj Mahal?","a":"The Yamuna","x":["The Ganges","The Chambal"]}},"Q366754":{"t":"An ancient port on a winding creek east of Mumbai","s":"Follow the Ulhas River as it snakes west through mangroves towards Thane Creek, and Kalyan appears on its bank, merged with Dombivli into one dense suburb of Mumbai. Behind it, the flat-topped hills of the Western Ghats rise like a wall. Traders knew this port nearly two thousand years ago, and in the 17th century Shivaji built some of the first Maratha warships here.","w":"Dense suburbs along a winding, mangrove-lined river north-east of Mumbai, with the dark wall of the Western Ghats to the east.","f":["At Kalyan Junction the main line from Mumbai splits, one branch climbing to Pune, the other to Nashik.","A 1st-century Greek sailing guide, the Periplus, mentions a port called Kalliena, identified with Kalyan.","Shivaji began building a Maratha fleet at Kalyan in the 1650s."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows past Kalyan?","a":"The Ulhas","x":["The Mithi","The Godavari"]}},"Q243325":{"t":"The last city before the Sundarbans mangroves","s":"South of Khulna the land dissolves into water: rivers split and rejoin, shrimp ponds glint like broken glass, and then a solid, dark green mass begins — the Sundarbans, the largest mangrove forest on Earth, home to Bengal tigers. Khulna, one of Bangladesh's largest cities, stands on the Rupsha and Bhairab rivers, and boats leave from here for the forest and the port of Mongla.","w":"A city on brown rivers in a flat delta of glinting ponds; the dark green Sundarbans mangroves begin to the south.","f":["The Sundarbans continue across the border into India and are a UNESCO World Heritage site on both sides.","The Sixty Dome Mosque in nearby Bagerhat actually has more than sixty domes."],"q":{"q":"Which forest lies south of Khulna?","a":"The Sundarbans","x":["The Madhupur forest","The Lawachara forest"]}},"Q1800":{"t":"A port of islands, backwaters and Chinese fishing nets","s":"Water and land interlock here like puzzle pieces: islands, peninsulas and lagoons edged with coconut palms, where the Kerala backwaters meet the Arabian Sea. Kochi has traded pepper and spices with the world for centuries. Chinese, Arab, Portuguese, Dutch and British merchants all left their mark, and cantilevered Chinese fishing nets still rise and dip along the shore of Fort Kochi.","w":"A city scattered over islands and peninsulas at a gap in the coast, with wide lagoons and palm-green land behind.","f":["Vasco da Gama died in Kochi in 1524 and was first buried in St Francis Church.","In 2015 Kochi's airport became the first in the world to run entirely on solar power.","Willingdon Island, home to the port, was built in the 20th century from sand dredged out of the harbour."],"q":{"q":"Which explorer died in Kochi in 1524?","a":"Vasco da Gama","x":["Ferdinand Magellan","Afonso de Albuquerque"]}},"Q243322":{"t":"Punjab's “city of wrestlers”, on the old trunk road","s":"Between Lahore and the Chenab River, Gujranwala stretches along the Grand Trunk Road across a flat, irrigated plain. It is proud of two things above all: its wrestlers — it is known as the City of Wrestlers — and its hearty food. Maharaja Ranjit Singh, who went on to build the Sikh Empire, was born here in 1780.","w":"A large, flat city along a straight highway north of Lahore, amid green irrigated fields; the Chenab lies to the north.","f":["Ranjit Singh, born in Gujranwala, later made Lahore the capital of the Sikh Empire.","The city stands in the Rechna Doab, the fertile land between the Chenab and Ravi rivers."],"q":{"q":"Gujranwala is nicknamed the city of…?","a":"Wrestlers","x":["Gardens","Saints"]}},"Q200235":{"t":"A sacred city near the source of the Godavari","s":"The Godavari, one of India's great rivers, is still young when it flows through Nashik: its source at Trimbakeshwar is only about 30 km away. Every twelve years the riverbanks fill with pilgrims for the Kumbh Mela. Around the city the basalt Western Ghats step down to the Deccan plateau, and the fields are lined with vineyards — Nashik is the centre of India's wine industry.","w":"A city on a small river at the foot of flat-topped basalt hills, with the Gangapur reservoir glinting to the west.","f":["By legend, Lakshmana cut off the demoness Shurpanakha's nose here — nasika in Sanskrit — giving the city its name.","A government press in Nashik prints Indian rupee banknotes."],"q":{"q":"Which great river rises near Nashik?","a":"The Godavari","x":["The Krishna","The Narmada"]}},"Q200237":{"t":"Where the great uprising of 1857 began","s":"On 10 May 1857, Indian soldiers of the East India Company's army rose up in the cantonment of Meerut and marched on Delhi, about 70 km away. It was the start of the largest revolt against British rule in that century. Today Meerut is a busy city on the flat land between the Ganges and the Yamuna, known for its sports goods — cricket bats and balls especially.","w":"A sizeable city on the flat, green plain between two rivers, north-east of Delhi's sprawl.","f":["Hastinapur, the Kuru capital of the Mahabharata, is traditionally placed about 40 km north-east of Meerut.","Meerut is one of India's main centres for making cricket equipment."],"q":{"q":"In which year did the great uprising begin at Meerut?","a":"1857","x":["1757","1885"]}},"Q200663":{"t":"Delhi's industrial neighbour at the end of the Aravallis","s":"The last low ridges of the Aravalli Hills, one of the oldest mountain ranges in the world, fade out on Faridabad's western side, while the Yamuna floodplain spreads to the east. Between them, the city runs south from Delhi in a long industrial strip. Sheikh Farid, a treasurer of the emperor Jahangir, founded it in 1607 to guard the highway, and it still carries his name.","w":"A long urban strip south of Delhi: rocky, scrubby hills to the west and the flat Yamuna floodplain to the east.","f":["Every February the Surajkund crafts fair on the city's edge draws artisans from across India and abroad.","Surajkund itself is an ancient reservoir shaped like an amphitheatre, attributed to the 10th century."],"q":{"q":"Which crafts fair is held each February near Faridabad?","a":"The Surajkund fair","x":["The Pushkar fair","The Sonepur fair"]}},"Q207098":{"t":"Where Delhi spills east across the Yamuna","s":"Cross the Yamuna eastward out of Delhi and the city simply keeps going: Ghaziabad is where the capital's sprawl spills into Uttar Pradesh. The Hindon River runs through it, a thin line winding south to join the Yamuna. The city was founded in 1740 by the Mughal noble Ghazi-ud-Din, who called it Ghaziuddinnagar; the shorter name came later.","w":"Dense suburbs east of the Yamuna, merged with Delhi, with a small river winding south through them.","f":["Ghaziabad Junction is one of the main rail gateways from Delhi towards eastern India.","It is often called the Gateway of Uttar Pradesh, since the main roads from Delhi into the state pass through it."],"q":{"q":"What was Ghaziabad originally called?","a":"Ghaziuddinnagar","x":["Shahjahanabad","Firozabad"]}},"Q185518":{"t":"Burma's last royal city, built around a moated square","s":"A perfect square about 2 km on each side, ringed by a moat that flashes silver in the sun, marks the heart of Mandalay: the walled grounds of the royal palace. King Mindon founded the city in 1857 beside the broad Irrawaddy, and it became the last royal capital of Burma. Mandalay Hill rises just to the north-east, its stairways lined with pagodas.","w":"A city beside the wide Irrawaddy with a huge moated square at its centre and a lone hill to the north-east.","f":["Kuthodaw Pagoda holds 729 marble slabs carved with Buddhist scripture, often called the world's largest book.","The U Bein Bridge nearby, about 1.2 km of teak posts across a lake, is often called the longest teak bridge in the world.","Mandalay was the royal capital for less than 30 years, until the British took it in 1885."],"q":{"q":"Which king founded Mandalay in 1857?","a":"Mindon","x":["Thibaw","Bodawpaya"]}},"Q200878":{"t":"Where the Narmada cuts through cliffs of marble","s":"Just outside Jabalpur the Narmada squeezes into a gorge of white and grey marble a few kilometres long, then tumbles over the Dhuandhar Falls — the “smoke cascade”. Upstream the river is held back by the Bargi Dam, and the reservoir's long, branching arms spread across the forested hills south-west of the city. Jabalpur itself stands among rocky outcrops at the edge of the Narmada valley.","w":"A city among rocky hills near a large, many-armed reservoir to the south-west, with the Narmada winding past.","f":["Boat rides through the Marble Rocks gorge at Bhedaghat are popular on moonlit nights, when the white cliffs glow.","The Narmada is one of the few large Indian rivers that flow west, into the Arabian Sea."],"q":{"q":"Which river cuts the Marble Rocks gorge near Jabalpur?","a":"The Narmada","x":["The Tapti","The Son"]}},"Q11854":{"t":"Where young Gandhi went to school","s":"Before he was a lawyer, a campaigner or the Mahatma, Mohandas Gandhi was a schoolboy in Rajkot, where his father served as chief minister of the small princely state. The city spreads across the dry, flat Kathiawar peninsula, beside the Aji River and its reservoirs. Today Rajkot is one of Gujarat's biggest cities, known for its engineering workshops and gold jewellery.","w":"A large city on a dry, flat plain in western India, with small lakes and reservoirs around its edges.","f":["Gandhi studied at Rajkot's Alfred High School, whose building is now a museum dedicated to him.","His father, Karamchand Gandhi, was diwan — chief minister — of the princely state of Rajkot."],"q":{"q":"Which future leader went to school in Rajkot?","a":"Mahatma Gandhi","x":["Jawaharlal Nehru","Sardar Patel"]}},"Q244159":{"t":"India's coal capital, pocked with open-cast mines","s":"Grey and black scars break up the green around Dhanbad: open-cast pits, spoil heaps and railway sidings of the Jharia coalfield, one of India's richest. Coal has been dug here since the late 19th century, and some seams have been burning underground for more than a hundred years. To the east, reservoirs on the Damodar and Barakar rivers add the only big patches of blue.","w":"Dark patches of open-cast mines and spoil heaps around a city, with large reservoirs on the rivers to the east.","f":["Some coal seams under Jharia have been on fire since the early 20th century.","IIT (ISM) Dhanbad began in 1926 as the Indian School of Mines."],"q":{"q":"Dhanbad is nicknamed India's capital of what?","a":"Coal","x":["Steel","Tea"]}},"Q25563103":{"t":"Capital of the Kakatiyas, ringed by old walls and lakes","s":"Warangal's old fort was built in rings: the Kakatiya kings, who ruled from here in the 12th to 14th centuries, surrounded it with concentric walls of earth and stone. Their carved stone gateways still stand in an open field. The Kakatiyas were also master builders of irrigation tanks, and their lakes still glint among the rice fields of the Deccan all around.","w":"A city on the Deccan plateau with lakes scattered around it and granite hills rising from the fields.","f":["One of the fort's stone gateways, the Kakatiya Kala Thoranam, appears on Telangana's state emblem.","The Ramappa Temple, a Kakatiya masterpiece north-east of the city, became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2021.","The Thousand Pillar Temple in Hanamkonda was built by the Kakatiyas in the 12th century."],"q":{"q":"Which dynasty ruled from Warangal?","a":"The Kakatiyas","x":["The Cholas","The Hoysalas"]}},"Q162442":{"t":"Where two great rivers meet, and millions gather","s":"Here the muddy Ganges and the greener Yamuna meet, and on a clear day you can see the two colours run side by side before they blend. Hindus call this confluence the Triveni Sangam, adding a third, invisible river, the Saraswati. Allahabad, officially Prayagraj since 2018, hosts the Kumbh Mela, one of the largest gatherings of people on Earth.","w":"A city in the angle where two broad rivers merge; at its south-eastern tip, paler and darker waters meet.","f":["Akbar built a fort right at the confluence in 1583 and named the city Ilahabad.","During the Kumbh Mela a temporary tent city rises on the sandy riverbanks.","The University of Allahabad, founded in 1887, is one of India's oldest modern universities."],"q":{"q":"Which two rivers meet at Allahabad?","a":"The Ganges and the Yamuna","x":["The Ganges and the Gomti","The Yamuna and the Chambal"]}},"Q170315":{"t":"A pine-scented hill town that kept a British officer's name","s":"At more than 1,200 m, Abbottabad sits in a green bowl of pine-covered hills, cool enough to draw summer visitors from the hot Punjab plains. It was founded in 1853 by Major James Abbott, who wrote a poem of farewell to the town when he left. The Karakoram Highway passes through on its long climb north towards the Khunjerab Pass and China.","w":"A town in a green, bowl-shaped valley among forested hills, where the plains give way to mountains.","f":["Abbottabad is a gateway to the Galyat hill resorts, such as Nathia Gali.","The Karakoram Highway runs from here towards the Khunjerab Pass, more than 4,600 m high."],"q":{"q":"Who is Abbottabad named after?","a":"James Abbott","x":["John Nicholson","Henry Lawrence"]}},"Q12478970":{"t":"Java's southern harbour, sheltered by a long island","s":"Java's south coast is mostly a wall of surf facing the Indian Ocean, which makes Cilacap unusual: a long, forested island, Nusa Kambangan, runs just offshore and shelters a natural harbour. Behind the island spreads Segara Anakan, a lagoon fringed with mangroves. On the mainland shore stand the tanks and chimneys of Indonesia's largest oil refinery.","w":"A long, dark green island just off Java's south coast, a mangrove lagoon behind it and a port town at its eastern end.","f":["The Cilacap refinery, run by Pertamina, is the largest in Indonesia.","The Segara Anakan lagoon is slowly shrinking as rivers fill it with silt."],"q":{"q":"Which long island shelters Cilacap's harbour?","a":"Nusa Kambangan","x":["Madura","Bawean"]}},"Q170115":{"t":"A lake city of houseboats in a ring of Himalayan peaks","s":"The Kashmir Valley is a flat green floor about 1,600 m up, walled in by snowy ranges, and Srinagar spreads across its middle. On its eastern edge lies Dal Lake, dotted with floating gardens and lined with carved wooden houseboats. The Jhelum River loops through the old city, while Mughal gardens climb in terraces up the hillsides above the lake.","w":"A city on a flat valley floor beside a large lake, with snowy ranges all around; the Jhelum winds through it.","f":["Shalimar Bagh, laid out for the Mughal emperor Jahangir in the 1600s, is one of Dal Lake's terraced gardens.","Many of Dal Lake's vegetable gardens float on mats of reeds and soil.","Houseboats became popular in the 19th century, when outsiders were not allowed to own land in Kashmir."],"q":{"q":"Which lake, famous for houseboats, borders Srinagar?","a":"Dal Lake","x":["Pangong Lake","Loktak Lake"]}},"Q200017":{"t":"Where the Krishna leaves the hills for its delta","s":"Watch the Krishna River break through a line of low hills and spread onto the plain: Vijayawada stands right in that gap. A barrage more than a kilometre long stretches across the river here, and from it canals fan out to water the rice fields of the Krishna delta. On a hill beside the river stands the Kanaka Durga temple, one of the most visited in Andhra Pradesh.","w":"A city at a gap in low hills where a long barrage crosses a wide river, with canals fanning out into green fields.","f":["The Prakasam Barrage, over 1 km long, was completed in 1957 on the site of a 19th-century dam.","The Undavalli cave temples, cut into a hillside across the river, date from around the 4th–5th centuries."],"q":{"q":"Which river does the Prakasam Barrage span?","a":"The Krishna","x":["The Godavari","The Kaveri"]}},"Q1645":{"t":"A river city of gold, rafts and pineapple fields","s":"The name promises gold, and there really was gold in the river that gave Cagayan de Oro its name. Today the same river is better known for whitewater rafting as it rushes down from the Bukidnon highlands into Macajalar Bay. Up on that plateau, vast pineapple plantations cover the land in geometric patches, while the city spreads along the bay on Mindanao's north coast.","w":"A city at a river mouth on a wide bay on Mindanao's north coast; a plateau of geometric plantations rises inland.","f":["Its nickname is the City of Golden Friendship.","The pineapple plantations on the Bukidnon plateau are among the largest in the world."],"q":{"q":"What does “de Oro” mean in the city's name?","a":"Of gold","x":["Of the river","Of the east"]}},"Q216075":{"t":"Capital of the Mekong Delta, where markets float","s":"Green land sliced into thin strips by canals, brown water everywhere: this is the Mekong Delta, and Cần Thơ is its largest city. It stands on the Hậu River, the delta's southern main branch, which is well over a kilometre wide here. At dawn, boats laden with pineapples, melons and pumpkins gather at the Cái Răng floating market.","w":"A city on a very wide brown river amid a flat green delta, cut into strips by countless canals.","f":["At Cái Răng, sellers hang their produce from a tall pole on the boat, so buyers can see from afar what is on offer.","The Hậu River is also known as the Bassac."],"q":{"q":"Which famous floating market is near Cần Thơ?","a":"Cái Răng","x":["Bến Thành","Đồng Xuân"]}},"Q200713":{"t":"Gateway to Ajanta and Ellora, with its own “Taj”","s":"Brown basalt hills and flat-topped ridges surround Aurangabad, and they hide some of India's greatest treasures. Ellora's rock-cut temples are about 30 km to the north-west, and the painted caves of Ajanta about 100 km to the north. The city itself has the Bibi Ka Maqbara, built in the 1660s for the wife of the emperor Aurangzeb and often called the Taj of the Deccan.","w":"A city in a basin of brown basalt hills; a conical fortress hill stands on its north-western outskirts.","f":["Daulatabad fort, on a steep cone of rock nearby, briefly became the capital of the Delhi Sultanate in 1327.","Ajanta and Ellora have both been UNESCO World Heritage sites since 1983."],"q":{"q":"Which monument is called the “Taj of the Deccan”?","a":"Bibi Ka Maqbara","x":["Gol Gumbaz","Charminar"]}},"Q237847":{"t":"A Deccan mill town of bedspreads and towels","s":"Solapur has been weaving since the days of the cotton mills, and its name still turns up on bedspreads and towels across India: Solapuri chaddars carry a protected geographical indication. The city spreads over dry, open Deccan country not far from the border with Karnataka. Near its centre, the white Siddheshwar temple stands on an island in a small lake.","w":"A flat city in dry, open plateau country, with a small lake near its centre and reservoirs on the plains around.","f":["Solapur chaddars and Solapur terry towels both have geographical-indication status in India.","A sanctuary near Solapur protects the great Indian bustard, one of the heaviest flying birds in the world."],"q":{"q":"Solapur's weavers are famous for which item?","a":"Chaddars (bedspreads)","x":["Silk saris","Pashmina shawls"]}},"Q174461":{"t":"A plateau capital of waterfalls and red earth","s":"At about 650 m on the Chota Nagpur Plateau, Ranchi is cooler and greener than the plains below, and its outskirts mix forest, red earth and rocky hills. Rivers tumble off the plateau edge in a string of waterfalls, which gave it the nickname City of Waterfalls. The capital of Jharkhand is also the hometown of the cricket captain MS Dhoni.","w":"A city on a rolling plateau of green forest and red soil, with several reservoirs close by.","f":["Hundru Falls, where the Subarnarekha drops off the plateau, is nearly 100 m high.","The Subarnarekha River rises just outside Ranchi.","Ranchi became a state capital in 2000, when Jharkhand was created."],"q":{"q":"Which cricketer calls Ranchi home?","a":"MS Dhoni","x":["Sachin Tendulkar","Virat Kohli"]}},"Q25282":{"t":"A long white beach between a mountain pass and a peninsula","s":"Few coastlines are this easy to read: a long white beach curving south, a green, mountainous peninsula — Sơn Trà — hooking out to the north-east, and the Hải Vân range plunging into the sea to the north-west. Da Nang fills the bay between them, split by the Hàn River. South-east of the centre, five marble hills rise abruptly from the flat sand.","w":"A bay city with a green peninsula to the north-east, mountains dropping into the sea to the north-west and a long white beach southward.","f":["The five Marble Mountains are named after the five elements: metal, water, wood, fire and earth.","Sơn Trà peninsula is home to red-shanked douc langurs, among the most colourful primates in the world.","The Hải Vân Pass has long been seen as a climate divide between northern and southern Vietnam."],"q":{"q":"How many hills make up Da Nang's Marble Mountains?","a":"Five","x":["Three","Seven"]}},"Q200019":{"t":"The Blue City under a fortress cliff on the desert's edge","s":"Jodhpur's old town is painted in shades of blue, and above it Mehrangarh Fort rises straight out of a rocky ridge, about 120 m above the plain. From cruise height the blue fades into a pale city on the edge of the Thar Desert, but the rocky ridge and the lakes beside it are easy to spot. Rao Jodha founded the city in 1459 as the capital of Marwar.","w":"A pale city beside a dark rocky ridge on the eastern edge of the Thar Desert, with lakes to the west.","f":["Jodhpur breeches, the riding trousers, take their name from the city.","Umaid Bhawan, finished in 1943, is one of the largest private homes in the world; part is still the royal residence."],"q":{"q":"Which riding garment is named after the city?","a":"Jodhpurs","x":["Chinos","Bermudas"]}},"Q199605":{"t":"Gateway to the Northeast on the mighty Brahmaputra","s":"Elsewhere in Assam the Brahmaputra sprawls across its valley in braided channels many kilometres wide, but at Guwahati hills squeeze it into a single channel little more than a kilometre across. That narrow point made the city the natural crossing and gateway to India's Northeast. On the south bank, Nilachal Hill carries the Kamakhya temple, one of the most revered Hindu shrines.","w":"A city stretched along the south bank of a huge river, where green hills pinch it into one channel.","f":["In 1671 an Ahom fleet defeated a Mughal army on the river here, at the Battle of Saraighat.","Umananda, in mid-river, is often called one of the world's smallest inhabited river islands.","The first bridge across the Brahmaputra, at Saraighat, opened here in 1962."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows past Guwahati?","a":"The Brahmaputra","x":["The Barak","The Teesta"]}},"Q43433":{"t":"Le Corbusier's grid city at the foot of the Shivaliks","s":"Chandigarh is one of the few cities whose plan you can read from cruise altitude: a neat grid of rectangular sectors, laid out in the 1950s by Le Corbusier. The Shivalik foothills rise along its north-eastern edge, and an artificial reservoir, Sukhna Lake, fills the top corner. The city serves as the shared capital of two states, Punjab and Haryana.","w":"A regular grid of rectangular blocks at the foot of forested hills, with a lake at its north-eastern corner.","f":["Each sector is roughly 800 by 1,200 m and was designed to be self-contained, with shops, schools and green space.","The Capitol Complex is part of a UNESCO site that groups Le Corbusier's buildings on three continents.","Nek Chand's Rock Garden is built from waste such as broken tiles, bottles and electrical sockets."],"q":{"q":"Which architect planned Chandigarh?","a":"Le Corbusier","x":["Edwin Lutyens","Oscar Niemeyer"]}},"Q158467":{"t":"A fortress on a sandstone mesa, and an early zero","s":"A long, flat-topped sandstone hill rises straight out of the middle of Gwalior, about 3 km long and crowned with the walls of one of India's most famous forts. The Mughal emperor Babur called it the pearl among the fortresses of Hind. Inside stands a small 9th-century temple with one of the oldest known inscriptions that writes zero as a digit.","w":"A long, narrow, flat-topped hill in the middle of a city on the plain; the Chambal ravines lie to the north.","f":["The inscription in the Chaturbhuj temple, dated 876, writes the number 270 with a round zero.","Tansen, the legendary musician of Akbar's court, is buried in Gwalior, and a music festival is held at his tomb.","Jai Vilas Palace, still the home of the Scindia family, was built in the 1870s."],"q":{"q":"Which great musician of Akbar's court is buried in Gwalior?","a":"Tansen","x":["Amir Khusrau","Baiju Bawra"]}},"Q1286":{"t":"A white crescent across the heart of Europe","s":"From cruise height the Alps look like crumpled white paper laid across Europe: sharp ridges, dark valleys and glaciers poured into the gaps. The arc runs about 1,200 km, from the Mediterranean near Nice almost to Vienna. Even Mont Blanc, the highest summit at about 4,800 m, sits some six kilometres below you.","w":"White ridges broken by deep valleys, with long lakes such as Garda, Como and Geneva at their feet.","f":["The Aletsch Glacier in Switzerland is the longest in the Alps: about 20 km of ice.","Around 14 million people live in the Alps.","The Gotthard Base Tunnel, opened in 2016, runs 57 km beneath the Alps — the longest railway tunnel in the world."],"q":{"q":"Which is the longest glacier in the Alps?","a":"The Aletsch Glacier","x":["The Mer de Glace","The Pasterze"]}},"Q5451":{"t":"The highest wall on Earth, almost at your altitude","s":"No other mountains come this close to your wing. The Himalayas rise from the green plains of India in steps — foothills, forested ridges, then a white wall — and Everest, at 8,849 m, reaches to within about two kilometres of cruise height. The range is still growing as India keeps pushing north into Asia.","w":"A white wall of peaks between the green plains of India and the brown, high Tibetan Plateau.","f":["Himalaya means “abode of snow” in Sanskrit.","Ten of the world’s fourteen peaks above 8,000 m are in the Himalayas; the other four are in the Karakoram.","Marine fossils are found near Everest’s summit: its top layers are limestone laid down on an ancient sea floor."],"q":{"q":"What does the Sanskrit name “Himalaya” mean?","a":"Abode of snow","x":["Roof of the world","Mountain of the gods"]}},"Q5456":{"t":"A 7,000-km spine down the whole of South America","s":"Seven thousand kilometres long, the Andes change character as you fly along them: glaciers and fjords in Patagonia, a wall of snow above Santiago, and in Bolivia a high plateau, the Altiplano, where Salar de Uyuni lies like a sheet of white paper. No mountain chain on any continent is longer.","w":"Snowy peaks and volcanic cones on one side, desert or rainforest on the other; in Bolivia, dazzling white salt flats.","f":["Aconcagua, at 6,961 m, is the highest mountain outside Asia.","Salar de Uyuni is so flat that it is used to calibrate satellite altimeters.","La Paz, in the Bolivian Andes at around 3,600 m, is the highest seat of government in the world."],"q":{"q":"Which is the highest peak of the Andes?","a":"Aconcagua","x":["Chimborazo","Huascarán"]}},"Q5463":{"t":"Where North America’s rivers choose their ocean","s":"Somewhere along these ridges runs the Continental Divide: rain on one side flows to the Pacific, on the other towards the Atlantic or the Arctic. From the air the Rockies are range after range of grey-brown peaks, dark with pine forest and streaked with snow well into summer. They stretch some 4,800 km, from northern British Columbia to New Mexico.","w":"Parallel ridges of grey rock and dark forest, snowfields on the peaks, and flat sagebrush basins between the ranges.","f":["Mount Elbert in Colorado, 4,401 m, is the highest peak of the Rockies.","Yellowstone, in the Wyoming Rockies, holds about half of the world’s active geysers.","By mountain standards the Rockies are young: their main uplift began around 80 million years ago."],"q":{"q":"Which is the highest peak of the Rocky Mountains?","a":"Mount Elbert","x":["Pikes Peak","Longs Peak"]}},"Q42070":{"t":"Stony, cold and vast — a desert of dinosaur eggs","s":"Expect beige, grey and rust rather than golden dunes: most of the Gobi is bare rock and gravel, scored by dry riverbeds. It is a cold desert, lying high on a plateau across Mongolia and northern China, with winters that can drop below −30 °C. Sand seas do exist, in patches, and from above they stand out as golden ripples in the grey.","w":"Flat grey-brown gravel plains with dry riverbeds, patches of golden dunes and white salt pans.","f":["In 1923 explorers at the Gobi’s Flaming Cliffs found nests of eggs that were the first to be recognised as dinosaur eggs.","In Badain Jaran, in the Chinese Gobi, dunes rise to about 500 m, with small lakes lying between them.","Wild Bactrian camels survive in the Gobi; they are a separate species, not runaway domestic camels."],"q":{"q":"What covers most of the Gobi’s surface?","a":"Bare rock and gravel","x":["Sand dunes","Salt marsh"]}},"Q6583":{"t":"Almost the size of the USA — and once green","s":"You can fly for hours and still be over the Sahara. At about 9 million km² it is the largest hot desert on Earth, painted in every shade from white salt to black basalt and orange sand. Dunes cover only part of it; the rest is stony plateaus, gravel plains and dark volcanic mountains.","w":"Endless tan and orange ground: look for rippled dune fields, dark volcanic massifs and pale dry lakebeds.","f":["Until about 5,000 years ago much of the Sahara was savanna with lakes; rock paintings show giraffes and cattle.","Saharan dust blown across the Atlantic helps fertilise the Amazon rainforest.","“Sahara” simply comes from the Arabic word for “desert”, so “Sahara Desert” says the same thing twice."],"q":{"q":"What does the name “Sahara” mean in Arabic?","a":"Desert","x":["Sand","Sun"]}},"Q5474":{"t":"Celestial Mountains around a lake that never freezes","s":"Tian Shan means “Celestial Mountains” in Chinese, and from above the name fits: snowy ranges roll for some 2,500 km across Central Asia. In their Kyrgyz heart lies Issyk-Kul, a deep blue lake about 180 km long sitting at 1,600 m. Despite the altitude and harsh winters, it never freezes — its water is slightly salty.","w":"Rows of snowy ridges running east–west; north of them, the long blue oval of Issyk-Kul.","f":["Jengish Chokusu, also called Victory Peak, is the highest summit of the Tian Shan at 7,439 m.","Issyk-Kul is one of the deepest lakes in the world, reaching about 670 m.","Khan Tengri glows pinkish-red at sunset because its summit pyramid is made of marble."],"q":{"q":"Which Tian Shan lake never freezes despite its altitude?","a":"Issyk-Kul","x":["Song-Kul","Balkhash"]}},"Q35600":{"t":"A modest ridge that divides two continents","s":"Don’t expect peaks: the Urals are so old and worn down that from cruise height they look like low, forested wrinkles. Yet this line, running some 2,500 km from the Arctic to the steppes of Kazakhstan, is where Europe traditionally ends and Asia begins. Around Yekaterinburg the mountains are so low that the Trans-Siberian crosses them almost unnoticed.","w":"A band of dark taiga and low ridges running north–south; east of Yekaterinburg and Chelyabinsk, a scatter of lakes.","f":["The Urals rose around 300 million years ago, when Europe and Siberia collided — long before the Alps existed.","The highest peak, Narodnaya, reaches only 1,895 m.","The malachite columns of St Isaac’s Cathedral in St Petersburg are faced with stone from the Urals."],"q":{"q":"Which is the highest peak of the Urals?","a":"Narodnaya","x":["Yamantau","Manaraga"]}},"Q5477":{"t":"A snowy wall between the Black Sea and the Caspian","s":"Between two seas rises a single, sharp-edged barrier: the Greater Caucasus runs about 1,200 km from the Black Sea to the Caspian, with a white crest along its spine. In the east, over Dagestan, it breaks into a maze of deep canyons and high plateaus. Elbrus, a dormant volcano with two summits, is usually counted as Europe’s highest peak at 5,642 m.","w":"A continuous line of snowy peaks running north-west to south-east between two seas.","f":["Dagestan means “land of mountains”, and more than 30 languages are spoken in its valleys.","Elbrus’s two summits stand about 1.5 km apart and differ in height by only about 20 m.","Kazbek, 5,054 m, is also an old volcano; its white cone rises above the Darial Gorge."],"q":{"q":"Which peak is usually counted as Europe’s highest?","a":"Elbrus","x":["Mont Blanc","Kazbek"]}},"Q60140":{"t":"A former island continent that crashed into Asia","s":"The land below you was once an island continent. Tens of millions of years ago it drifted north across the ocean and rammed into Asia, crumpling the crust into the Himalayas — and it is still pushing, a few centimetres a year. From above, look for the flat Ganges plain in the north and the old, worn Deccan plateau in the south.","w":"Flat, intensely farmed plains in the north; brown plateaus, rivers and reservoirs further south; at night, countless clusters of lights.","f":["The Indian plate still moves north by about 5 cm a year.","The Deccan Traps, layers of lava up to 2 km thick, poured out about 66 million years ago.","Around a quarter of all people on Earth live on the subcontinent."],"q":{"q":"Which mountains rose when India collided with Asia?","a":"The Himalayas","x":["The Urals","The Zagros"]}},"Q31945":{"t":"The world’s largest peninsula, tilted towards the Gulf","s":"Seen from above, Arabia is a giant tilted slab: mountains rise along the Red Sea in the west, and the land slopes gently east to the Gulf. In between lie gravel plains, black lava fields and the dunes of the Empty Quarter. Saudi Arabia, which fills most of the peninsula, has no permanent rivers at all.","w":"Beige plains and orange dune seas, dark mountain ridges along the Red Sea coast and black lava fields inland.","f":["At more than 3 million km², Arabia is the largest peninsula on Earth.","Arabia is slowly pulling away from Africa: the Red Sea widens by about a centimetre a year.","The Empty Quarter in its south is the largest continuous sand desert on Earth."],"q":{"q":"Which is the largest peninsula in the world?","a":"The Arabian Peninsula","x":["The Indochina Peninsula","Scandinavia"]}},"Q27611":{"t":"A land bridge built by volcanoes","s":"Two oceans, one narrow strip of land: on a clear day you may see water on both sides of the plane. A chain of volcanoes runs along the Pacific side, many of them near-perfect cones, while the Caribbean coast is low, green and swampy. When this land bridge closed about 3 million years ago, animals began crossing between the Americas.","w":"A string of volcanic cones along the Pacific coast, with lakes such as Nicaragua and Atitlán shining between them.","f":["Lake Nicaragua is the largest lake in Central America, and bull sharks swim in it.","Tajumulco in Guatemala, 4,220 m, is the highest peak in Central America.","Guatemala alone has more than 30 volcanoes."],"q":{"q":"Which Central American lake is home to bull sharks?","a":"Lake Nicaragua","x":["Lake Atitlán","Lake Yojoa"]}},"Q185605":{"t":"Antarctica’s long finger pointing at South America","s":"Ice-capped mountains, glaciers spilling into the sea and a coast shattered into islands: the Antarctic Peninsula reaches some 1,300 km north towards Cape Horn. It is the mildest and most visited corner of the continent, and one of the fastest-warming places on Earth. Along its eastern side, vast ice shelves float on the Weddell Sea.","w":"Jagged, snow-covered peaks along a narrow spine, and glaciers flowing into a dark sea dotted with icebergs.","f":["In 2002 part of the Larsen Ice Shelf larger than Luxembourg broke apart in about five weeks.","The Drake Passage between the peninsula and Cape Horn is about 800 km wide.","Most tourists who reach Antarctica come here, on ships from Ushuaia."],"q":{"q":"Which sea lies east of the Antarctic Peninsula?","a":"The Weddell Sea","x":["The Ross Sea","The Amundsen Sea"]}},"Q319671":{"t":"A 3,500-km wall holding back the polar ice","s":"Dark rock peaks break through the white here, marking one of the longest mountain ranges on Earth. The Transantarctic Mountains cross the whole continent, splitting East Antarctica from West, and hold back the thick East Antarctic ice sheet like a dam. Glaciers squeeze through the gaps between the summits and flow down to the Ross Ice Shelf.","w":"Rows of dark, flat-topped peaks poking through the ice, with broad glaciers flowing between them.","f":["Both Shackleton and Scott reached the polar plateau by climbing the Beardmore Glacier, about 200 km long.","Bones of a crested dinosaur, Cryolophosaurus, were found high on Mount Kirkpatrick in this range.","Coal seams and fossil Glossopteris leaves found here show that Antarctica was once forested."],"q":{"q":"Which glacier did Shackleton and Scott climb to the polar plateau?","a":"The Beardmore Glacier","x":["The Axel Heiberg Glacier","The Lambert Glacier"]}},"Q192583":{"t":"Australia’s green seam between coast and outback","s":"This is less a wall than a long, worn backbone: the Great Dividing Range runs some 3,500 km down eastern Australia, and from the air you notice the change of colour more than the mountains. On the coast side lie green forest and river valleys; inland, the land fades to dry brown plains. Its western rivers flow away from the nearby sea, towards the Murray–Darling or the dry heart of the continent.","w":"Green, forested ridges near the east coast; inland of them, farmland and scrub turning to brown plains.","f":["Mount Kosciuszko, 2,228 m, is its highest point and the highest in mainland Australia.","The Blue Mountains near Sydney are part of the range; their blue haze comes from eucalyptus oil in the air."],"q":{"q":"Which is the highest peak of the Great Dividing Range?","a":"Mount Kosciuszko","x":["Mount Townsend","Mount Bogong"]}},"Q21195":{"t":"A peninsula still rising after the Ice Age","s":"From cruise height Scandinavia is a mosaic of dark forest and countless lakes, with mountains along the Norwegian side and deep fjords cutting into the Atlantic coast. All of it once lay under an ice sheet up to about 3 km thick. Freed of that weight, the land is still rebounding — in places by nearly a centimetre a year.","w":"Dark green forest speckled with hundreds of lakes; to the west, snowy mountains and fjords like narrow blue cracks.","f":["Sweden’s High Coast has risen about 285 m since the ice melted, the greatest such rebound known.","Sweden alone has close to 100,000 lakes.","At North Cape the midnight sun shines for more than two months each summer."],"q":{"q":"Why is the land of Scandinavia still rising?","a":"The weight of Ice Age ice has gone","x":["Volcanic activity below it","A collision of tectonic plates"]}},"Q27617163":{"t":"Between India and China, a land of ridges and rivers","s":"Its old name, Indochina, says it all: the land between India and China. From the window you can read its structure — long mountain ridges fanning south from the Himalayas, with great rivers in the valleys between. The Mekong, Chao Phraya, Salween and Irrawaddy all run roughly north to south. In the dry season, smoke from burning fields often softens the view.","w":"Parallel green ridges running north–south, brown rivers winding between them, rice fields shining in the valleys.","f":["Cambodia’s Tonle Sap River reverses its flow every rainy season, and the lake it drains swells several times over.","The Mekong flows through or along six countries: China, Myanmar, Laos, Thailand, Cambodia and Vietnam.","Thailand and Vietnam are among the world’s largest rice exporters."],"q":{"q":"Which river reverses its flow every rainy season?","a":"The Tonle Sap","x":["The Chao Phraya","The Irrawaddy"]}},"Q12837":{"t":"A sun-baked tableland at the edge of Europe","s":"On the map Iberia is almost a square, and much of its interior is a lofty tableland, the Meseta, where ochre wheat fields and olive groves run to the horizon. Mountain ranges ring it and cross it, and the Pyrenees seal it off from the rest of Europe. At the southern tip, only about 14 km of sea separate it from Africa.","w":"Ochre and green plains broken by bare, rocky sierras, and reservoirs with branching, finger-like shapes.","f":["Madrid, at about 650 m, is one of the highest capital cities in Europe.","Spain has more than 1,000 large dams, one of the highest counts in the world.","Near Almería, plastic greenhouses cover so much land that they show up as a white patch from space."],"q":{"q":"How wide is the Strait of Gibraltar at its narrowest?","a":"About 14 km","x":["About 4 km","About 40 km"]}},"Q181528":{"t":"The Sahara’s driest corner, where years pass without rain","s":"This eastern part of the Sahara, spread across Egypt, Libya and Sudan, is among the driest places on Earth — some spots go years without a drop of rain. From cruise height it is a study in beige: flat gravel plains, the long parallel dunes of the Great Sand Sea, and the brown sandstone plateau of the Gilf Kebir rising like an island.","w":"Pale sand drawn into long, straight dune lines running north–south, and the dark, flat-topped Gilf Kebir plateau.","f":["A carved piece of yellow Libyan desert glass, found only in this region, sits in a pectoral from Tutankhamun’s tomb.","The Cave of Swimmers in the Gilf Kebir has ancient paintings of people who seem to be swimming — in today’s driest desert."],"q":{"q":"Whose tomb held a jewel set with Libyan desert glass?","a":"Tutankhamun’s","x":["Ramesses II’s","Khufu’s"]}},"Q37056":{"t":"Where Eurasia runs out of land","s":"You are over the very top of Eurasia. Taymyr’s tip, Cape Chelyuskin, is the northernmost point of any continent’s mainland; across a narrow strait lie only the islands of Severnaya Zemlya and the pack ice. Below you: treeless tundra, the low Byrranga Mountains and a web of lakes and rivers that stay frozen for most of the year.","w":"Flat, treeless tundra laced with lakes, and the long, low ridges of the Byrranga Mountains running east–west.","f":["Musk oxen, gone from here for thousands of years, were brought back from Canada and Alaska in the 1970s and thrived.","Taymyr is home to the largest herd of wild reindeer in Eurasia.","Cape Chelyuskin was first reached in 1742 by Semyon Chelyuskin, travelling by dog sled."],"q":{"q":"What is the northernmost point of mainland Eurasia?","a":"Cape Chelyuskin","x":["Cape Dezhnev","North Cape"]}},"Q488266":{"t":"“The end of the land”, freckled with countless lakes","s":"In the Nenets language Yamal means “the end of the land”, and from above the name feels right: a flat, pale tundra reaching north into the Kara Sea, so pocked with lakes that it seems more water than ground. Nenets herders still cross it with their reindeer, travelling hundreds of kilometres each year between summer and winter pastures.","w":"A flat peninsula between the Kara Sea and the Gulf of Ob, scattered with thousands of round lakes.","f":["Beneath Yamal lie some of the largest natural gas fields on Earth.","In 2014 a crater tens of metres wide appeared in the tundra, blown out by gas from thawing permafrost.","The peninsula is about 700 km long."],"q":{"q":"What does “Yamal” mean in the Nenets language?","a":"The end of the land","x":["Land of reindeer","White water"]}},"Q993":{"t":"A land of fire and ice on the edge of the Pacific","s":"White cones, one after another, some trailing a thin plume: Kamchatka has around 30 active volcanoes, one of the densest clusters on Earth. On a clear day you can pick out several at once, rising from dark forest and tundra. The tallest, Klyuchevskaya Sopka, reaches about 4,750 m — the highest active volcano in Eurasia.","w":"Snow-capped volcanic cones in a row along the peninsula’s spine and east coast, often with steam or ash at the top.","f":["The Valley of Geysers, discovered in 1941, is one of the largest geyser fields in the world.","Kamchatka is about 1,200 km long, yet no road links it to the rest of Russia.","The Volcanoes of Kamchatka have been a UNESCO World Heritage Site since 1996."],"q":{"q":"Which is the highest active volcano in Eurasia?","a":"Klyuchevskaya Sopka","x":["Koryaksky","Shiveluch"]}},"Q18758":{"t":"Asia’s long green tail, reaching for the equator","s":"Mainland Asia ends at the tip of this peninsula, barely 150 km from the equator. From 11 km up, its spine is a line of rainforest-covered mountains; on either side the green turns into the neat, dotted rows of oil palm and rubber plantations. Off the west coast runs the Strait of Malacca, busy with ships day and night.","w":"A forested mountain spine down the middle, geometric plantations on both sides, and ships dotting the Strait of Malacca to the west.","f":["Tanjung Piai, at the peninsula’s tip, is the southernmost point of mainland Asia.","The Strait of Malacca is one of the busiest shipping lanes in the world.","Gunung Tahan, 2,187 m, is the highest peak of Peninsular Malaysia."],"q":{"q":"Which is the southernmost point of mainland Asia?","a":"Tanjung Piai","x":["Cape Cà Mau","Cape Comorin"]}},"Q319288":{"t":"Africa’s rooftop, where the Nile gets its flood","s":"A vast green-and-brown plateau, mostly above 1,500 m, rises out of the surrounding lowlands like a fortress. Its flat tops are farmed right to the edge, then drop into canyons — the Blue Nile Gorge is up to 1.5 km deep. Every summer, monsoon rain on these highlands sends down the flood that has fed Egypt for millennia.","w":"A high tableland of fields and eucalyptus groves, split by deep brown canyons; Lake Tana shines to the north.","f":["Most of the Nile water that reaches Egypt falls as rain on the Ethiopian Highlands.","Arabica coffee comes from here: it still grows wild in Ethiopia’s forests.","Addis Ababa, at about 2,350 m, is one of the highest capital cities in the world."],"q":{"q":"Which lake is the source of the Blue Nile?","a":"Lake Tana","x":["Lake Victoria","Lake Turkana"]}},"Q167021":{"t":"Mountains folded like a rumpled carpet","s":"From above, the Zagros are one of the clearest pictures of how mountains are made: long parallel ridges curve for hundreds of kilometres like folds in a pushed rug, as Arabia presses into Iran. In the south, dark round blotches stand out between the folds — salt domes, where ancient salt has squeezed up through the rock and flowed out like a glacier.","w":"Long, curving parallel ridges like wrinkles in cloth, with round dark salt domes between them near the Gulf coast.","f":["The Zagros run about 1,500 km, from near the Turkish border to the Strait of Hormuz.","The Middle East’s first oil strike came in the Zagros foothills, at Masjed Soleyman, in 1908.","Goats were domesticated in the Zagros around 10,000 years ago."],"q":{"q":"Which collision is folding up the Zagros?","a":"Arabia pushing into Eurasia","x":["India pushing into Asia","Africa pushing into Europe"]}},"Q5955":{"t":"Golden mountains where four countries meet","s":"Snowy peaks, turquoise rivers and larch forest shading into steppe: the Altai sit in the middle of Asia, where Russia, Kazakhstan, China and Mongolia come together. Look for the milky turquoise ribbon of the Katun River and the long, narrow blade of Lake Teletskoye. Belukha, the highest peak at 4,506 m, wears a white, twin-headed cap all year.","w":"White peaks above dark larch forest, the turquoise Katun River and the narrow blue strip of Lake Teletskoye.","f":["The Golden Mountains of Altai have been a UNESCO World Heritage Site since 1998.","Ancient Pazyryk burials, sealed in permafrost, preserved tattooed human skin about 2,400 years old.","Belukha is the highest point in Siberia."],"q":{"q":"Which is the highest peak of the Altai?","a":"Belukha","x":["Munku-Sardyk","Khan Tengri"]}},"Q293713":{"t":"The ridge where forest ends and the steppe begins","s":"Look for a sharp change of colour: the Greater Khingan divides the damp, green Manchurian side in the east from the dry grasslands of the Mongolian Plateau in the west. The range itself is gentle, rarely much above 2,000 m, but it catches the summer monsoon and casts a rain shadow over the steppe beyond. It runs about 1,200 km from north to south.","w":"Low, forested ridges running north–south, with greener fields to the east and tawny steppe to the west.","f":["Its northern slopes carry one of China’s largest forests, mostly of larch.","West of the range lie the Hulunbuir grasslands, some of the finest pastures in China."],"q":{"q":"What lies to the west of the Greater Khingan?","a":"The Mongolian Plateau","x":["The Manchurian Plain","The North China Plain"]}},"Q214284":{"t":"A remote divide between the Arctic and the Pacific","s":"Dark taiga, bare stony summits and barely a road: the Stanovoy Range is one of Siberia’s emptiest places. It is the region’s great watershed — rivers on its north side flow to the Lena and the Arctic, those on the south to the Amur and the Pacific. In winter the ridges turn white and the valleys vanish under snow.","w":"Long, dark-green ridges with pale rocky tops running east–west, and winding rivers in empty valleys.","f":["For nearly two centuries after the 1689 Treaty of Nerchinsk, these mountains roughly marked the border between Russia and China.","The railway to Yakutsk crosses the range near Neryungri, a coal-mining town in southern Yakutia."],"q":{"q":"Which great river takes the water from the Stanovoy’s north side?","a":"The Lena","x":["The Yenisei","The Kolyma"]}},"Q26813923":{"t":"The Himalayas’ first and youngest wrinkle","s":"Coming from the Indian plains, these are the first hills you meet: low, green, steep-sided ridges rising abruptly from the flat Terai. The Siwaliks are the youngest and outermost fold of the Himalayas, made of gravel and sand washed down from the high peaks and then pushed up again. Behind them rise higher ranges, and on a clear day the snows.","w":"The first line of low, forested hills rising sharply from the flat, farmed plain, parallel to the snowy peaks beyond.","f":["The name is usually explained as “belonging to Shiva”.","Their rocks are rich in fossils of ancient apes, elephants and giraffes, some more than 10 million years old.","In Nepal these hills are called the Chure range."],"q":{"q":"Where do the Siwalik Hills lie within the Himalayas?","a":"At the outermost southern edge","x":["Along the main snowy crest","On the Tibetan side"]}},"Q161750":{"t":"The Roof of the World, cut by giant glaciers","s":"Locals called it Bam-i-Dunya, the Roof of the World, and from the air it looks the part: a high, bare knot of mountains where the Tian Shan, Hindu Kush and Karakoram all meet. In the eastern Pamirs even the valley floors lie around 4,000 m. The Fedchenko Glacier, more than 70 km long, is the longest glacier outside the polar regions.","w":"A dense knot of snowy peaks and long glaciers; to the east, high, bare brown plateaus with turquoise lakes.","f":["Ismoil Somoni Peak, 7,495 m, is the highest point of the Pamirs.","Lake Karakul, at about 3,900 m, fills a crater left by a meteorite impact millions of years ago.","The Pamir Highway crosses the Ak-Baital Pass at about 4,650 m, one of the highest roads in the world."],"q":{"q":"What is the traditional nickname of the Pamirs?","a":"The Roof of the World","x":["The Abode of Snow","The Celestial Mountains"]}},"Q5472":{"t":"A snowy wall on the old roads of Central Asia","s":"A sea of sharp, snowy peaks cut by deep valleys stretches about 800 km from central Afghanistan into northern Pakistan. For thousands of years traders, pilgrims and armies threaded the Hindu Kush’s high passes between Central Asia and India. Tirich Mir, the highest summit at 7,708 m, rises near its eastern end.","w":"Row upon row of jagged white peaks, with narrow green valleys and braided grey rivers deep below.","f":["Lapis lazuli mined in these mountains for more than 6,000 years ended up in the jewellery of Egyptian pharaohs.","Alexander the Great crossed the Hindu Kush on his way to India in the 4th century BC."],"q":{"q":"Which is the highest peak of the Hindu Kush?","a":"Tirich Mir","x":["Nanga Parbat","Noshaq"]}},"Q5469":{"t":"K2’s kingdom of rock, ice and giant glaciers","s":"Few places outside the polar regions hold this much mountain ice: glaciers such as the Siachen and the Baltoro, each over 60 km long, fill the Karakoram’s valleys like frozen motorways striped with dark rock. Above them stand K2 and three more peaks over 8,000 m. The name is usually translated from Turkic as “black gravel”.","w":"Long, straight glaciers with dark stripes of rock down their middle, between clusters of sharp granite peaks.","f":["K2, 8,611 m, is the second-highest mountain on Earth.","Some Karakoram glaciers have held steady or even grown while most mountain glaciers shrink — the “Karakoram anomaly”.","The Karakoram Highway crosses the range at the Khunjerab Pass, about 4,700 m high."],"q":{"q":"Which is the second-highest mountain on Earth?","a":"K2","x":["Kangchenjunga","Lhotse"]}},"Q187871":{"t":"The stern northern rampart of the Tibetan Plateau","s":"Look for the line where the sand stops. The Kunlun rise like a rampart along the northern edge of the Tibetan Plateau, with the yellow Tarim Basin and the Taklamakan desert at their feet and cold, high Tibet behind them. The range runs some 3,000 km, and in Chinese myth its peaks were the home of immortals.","w":"A sharp boundary: a yellow desert basin on the north side, snow-streaked grey ridges and the high plateau to the south.","f":["The Qinghai–Tibet Railway crosses the Kunlun at a pass about 4,770 m high.","Nephrite jade from the Kunlun rivers near Hotan has been prized in China for thousands of years.","In Chinese legend the Kunlun were the home of Xiwangmu, the Queen Mother of the West."],"q":{"q":"Which basin lies at the northern foot of the western Kunlun?","a":"The Tarim Basin","x":["The Sichuan Basin","The Dzungarian Basin"]}},"Q130135":{"t":"The barrier between the Mediterranean and the Sahara","s":"Green on one side, red and ochre on the other: the Atlas Mountains hold back the Sahara from the Mediterranean coast for some 2,500 km, from Morocco’s Atlantic shore to Tunisia. Their northern slopes catch rain and even snow, while the southern flanks look scorched. In the Middle Atlas, dark cedar forests shelter the Barbary macaque, the only macaque living in Africa.","w":"Snow-dusted ridges with dark cedar forest on the north side, and bare red and ochre slopes on the Saharan side.","f":["Jebel Toubkal, 4,167 m, in the High Atlas, is the highest peak in North Africa.","The Atlas cedar grows wild only in these mountains of Morocco and Algeria.","The mountains are named after Atlas, the Titan of Greek myth who holds up the sky."],"q":{"q":"Which is the highest peak of the Atlas Mountains?","a":"Jebel Toubkal","x":["Jebel Ayachi","Mount Chélia"]}},"Q93332":{"t":"Ancient ridges combed into parallel lines","s":"Look down for stripes: in the Ridge-and-Valley country the Appalachians form long, forested ridges running side by side for hundreds of kilometres, as if the land had been combed. These are some of the oldest mountains on Earth, raised more than 250 million years ago and once far higher than today. Erosion has worn them down to rounded, green hills.","w":"Long, parallel forested ridges with farmed valleys between them, running south-west to north-east.","f":["The Appalachian Trail follows the range for about 3,500 km, from Georgia to Maine.","The Blue Ridge owes its blue haze to natural compounds released by its trees.","Mount Mitchell in North Carolina, 2,037 m, is the highest peak in the eastern United States."],"q":{"q":"Why do the Blue Ridge Mountains look blue?","a":"Haze from compounds released by trees","x":["Blue-tinted granite","Reflections from many lakes"]}},"Q929910":{"t":"America’s wild northern rampart above the Arctic plain","s":"Above the Arctic Circle, the Brooks Range sweeps across northern Alaska for about 1,000 km: grey, treeless peaks, braided rivers and hardly a settlement anywhere. To the north the land drops to the flat Arctic coastal plain, dotted with countless lakes. In summer the snow melts from the valleys; in winter everything lies white under a low sun.","w":"Grey, jagged, treeless ridges running east–west and wide braided riverbeds; to the north, flat tundra full of lakes.","f":["Gates of the Arctic National Park, in the Brooks Range, has no roads or trails at all.","The Trans-Alaska Pipeline crosses the range at Atigun Pass, about 1,400 m high.","Its crest divides rivers flowing to the Arctic Ocean from those heading to the Bering Sea."],"q":{"q":"Which pipeline crosses the Brooks Range at Atigun Pass?","a":"The Trans-Alaska Pipeline","x":["The Keystone Pipeline","The Colonial Pipeline"]}},"Q920070":{"t":"Ice, rainforest and fjords on Canada’s Pacific edge","s":"Between the Pacific and the interior of British Columbia rises a wall of granite peaks and icefields some 1,600 km long. Fjords push deep inland between them, and their lower slopes are cloaked in dense temperate rainforest. On a clear day you look down on glaciers feeding milky rivers that wind to the sea.","w":"Jagged snowy peaks and icefields, with long fjords and green valleys cutting in from the Pacific coast.","f":["Mount Waddington, 4,019 m, is the highest peak lying entirely within British Columbia.","The range is built largely of granite and forms one of the largest granite bodies on Earth.","The Skeena and Stikine rivers slice right through the range on their way to the Pacific."],"q":{"q":"Which is the highest peak lying entirely within British Columbia?","a":"Mount Waddington","x":["Mount Robson","Mount Fairweather"]}},"Q4558":{"t":"A line of lone white volcanoes above dark forest","s":"The Cascades are easy to spot: a chain of big, isolated snow cones — Baker, Rainier, Adams, Hood, Shasta — standing high above the forest from British Columbia to northern California. Between them, the range is dark green on its rainy western side and brown and dry on the east. In the north, the jagged North Cascades hold hundreds of glaciers.","w":"Solitary white volcanic cones in a north–south line far above the forest; green western slopes, dry brown eastern ones.","f":["Mount Rainier, 4,392 m, carries more glacier ice than any other peak in the contiguous United States.","Crater Lake in Oregon fills the collapsed top of a Cascade volcano and is the deepest lake in the US, about 590 m.","The range is named after the cascades of the Columbia River, where it cuts through the mountains."],"q":{"q":"Which lake fills the collapsed top of a Cascade volcano?","a":"Crater Lake","x":["Lake Tahoe","Lake Chelan"]}},"Q156684":{"t":"Home of Denali, North America’s giant","s":"Even from cruise height Denali looks enormous: at 6,190 m it is North America’s highest peak, and it rises from lowlands only a few hundred metres above sea level, so its full bulk is on show. Around it the Alaska Range curves some 650 km across southern Alaska, a white wall of ice and rock above spruce forest and tundra.","w":"One massive white peak towering above all the others, with long glaciers streaming from a curved wall of snowy summits.","f":["From base to summit Denali rises about 5,500 m — more than Everest does above the Tibetan Plateau.","The Kahiltna Glacier, the climbers’ route to Denali, is about 70 km long.","The mountain has two names in use: Denali and Mount McKinley."],"q":{"q":"How high is Denali, North America’s highest peak?","a":"6,190 m","x":["5,642 m","6,961 m"]}},"Q40556":{"t":"Africa’s sharp eastern tip, jutting into the ocean","s":"On the map it is exactly what its name says: a horn thrust out into the sea. The Horn of Africa juts east between the Gulf of Aden and the Indian Ocean — a dry land of red-brown plains, thorny scrub and long, straight beaches. From the window it is mostly ochre, crossed by the green threads of the Shabelle and Juba rivers.","w":"Ochre and red-brown plains with sparse scrub, a long sandy coastline, and thin green ribbons along the rivers.","f":["Ras Hafun in Somalia is the easternmost point of mainland Africa.","Somalia has the longest coastline of any country on the African mainland, over 3,000 km.","Frankincense and myrrh, prized in the ancient world, come from trees that grow wild on the Horn’s dry hills."],"q":{"q":"Which is the easternmost point of mainland Africa?","a":"Ras Hafun","x":["Cape Agulhas","Ras ben Sakka"]}},"Q23522":{"t":"A knot of mountains wrapped in blue seas","s":"Look down and you’ll see why travel here was always slow: the Balkan Peninsula is a jumble of mountain ranges, deep valleys and high basins, with the Adriatic on one side and the Aegean and Black Sea on the other. The main north–south routes follow the valleys of the Morava and the Vardar. The peninsula takes its name from the Balkan Mountains in Bulgaria.","w":"Tightly packed green and grey ridges, small basins with towns and fields, and deep river valleys running north–south.","f":["The word “Balkan” is usually traced to a Turkish term for a chain of wooded mountains.","Musala in Bulgaria, 2,925 m, is the highest peak of the peninsula, just ahead of Mount Olympus.","Lake Ohrid is one of the oldest lakes in Europe, more than a million years old."],"q":{"q":"Which is the highest peak of the Balkan Peninsula?","a":"Musala","x":["Mount Olympus","Triglav"]}},"Q1359850":{"t":"Kazakhstan’s “Hungry Steppe”, flat to every horizon","s":"Between the Chu River and Lake Balkhash lies a clay-and-gravel plateau so empty that it is also known as the Northern Hungry Steppe. From cruise height it is a vast, flat sheet of grey-beige, crossed by faint tracks and dotted with white salt pans. Only in spring, for a few short weeks, does a green tinge spread across it.","w":"A featureless grey-beige plain with white salt pans; Lake Balkhash to the north-east, the Chu River’s green thread to the south-west.","f":["Betpak-Dala is home to one of Kazakhstan’s main herds of saiga, the antelope with a trunk-like nose.","The desert covers about 75,000 km²."],"q":{"q":"Which animal with a trunk-like nose roams Betpak-Dala?","a":"The saiga","x":["The goitered gazelle","The kulan"]}},"Q169132":{"t":"The Land of Five Rivers, a patchwork of fields","s":"Punjab means “five waters” in Persian, after the five rivers — the Jhelum, Chenab, Ravi, Beas and Sutlej — that cross this flat plain to join the Indus. From 11 km up it is one of the most intensely farmed landscapes on Earth: a fine patchwork of wheat and rice fields laced with a dense grid of irrigation canals. At night, towns and villages sparkle everywhere.","w":"A flat, finely divided patchwork of green and gold fields, straight canals and wide, braided riverbeds.","f":["The Indus basin irrigation network, fed by Punjab’s rivers, is one of the largest in the world.","Lahore, on the Ravi, is famous for its Mughal fort and Shalimar Gardens, both listed by UNESCO.","Punjab is often called the breadbasket of the subcontinent."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Punjab mean?","a":"Five waters","x":["Green land","Great plain"]}},"Q229269":{"t":"The Empty Quarter: the largest sand sea on Earth","s":"Nothing but sand for hundreds of kilometres: the Rub’ al Khali, the Empty Quarter, is the largest continuous sand desert in the world, about 650,000 km². From above, look for giant dunes lined up like waves, some over 200 m high, with pale salt flats in the hollows between them. Its colours shift from yellow to deep orange-red.","w":"Endless rows of orange dunes like frozen waves, with white or grey salt flats in the corridors between them.","f":["In 1930–31 Bertram Thomas made the first recorded crossing by a Westerner, travelling by camel.","Wilfred Thesiger crossed it twice in the 1940s and described it in his classic book “Arabian Sands”.","The Rub’ al Khali is larger than France."],"q":{"q":"What does the name “Rub’ al Khali” mean?","a":"The Empty Quarter","x":["The Red Sands","The Great Sea"]}},"Q375816":{"t":"Brazil’s “white forest” that turns green in days","s":"In the dry season the Caatinga looks grey and lifeless from above: thorny scrub that has dropped every leaf, dotted with cacti. Its name comes from the Tupi for “white forest”. But when the rains arrive, the whole landscape flushes green within days. This semi-arid land of north-eastern Brazil exists nowhere else on Earth.","w":"Grey-brown scrubland in the dry season, bright green after rain, with reservoirs shining like scattered mirrors.","f":["The Caatinga is the only major biome that lies entirely within Brazil.","The mandacaru, a tall columnar cactus, is a symbol of this dry back country, the sertão.","Serra da Capivara, in the Caatinga, has one of the largest concentrations of prehistoric rock art in the Americas."],"q":{"q":"What does the Tupi name “Caatinga” mean?","a":"White forest","x":["Dry land","Thorny field"]}},"Q47700":{"t":"Rust-red sand beneath a sea of grass and thorn","s":"The Kalahari is not the bare dune field you might imagine: its deep red sand is held down by grass and thorn trees, so from above it looks like rust-coloured ground brushed with pale green. Long parallel dunes run for kilometres, lined up by the wind. Far to the north, the same sand swallows the Okavango River, which never reaches the sea.","w":"Red-brown ground speckled with grey-green scrub, long straight dune ridges and white pans of dry salt lakes.","f":["The Okavango Delta, where a river vanishes into the Kalahari sand, is a UNESCO World Heritage Site.","The sand’s red colour comes from a thin coating of iron oxide on each grain.","The San people have lived in the Kalahari for many thousands of years."],"q":{"q":"Which river ends in the Kalahari without reaching the sea?","a":"The Okavango","x":["The Zambezi","The Orange"]}},"Q2913098":{"t":"Dark peaks stranded in a white ocean of ice","s":"Here the Transantarctic Mountains fray into their last, lonely ranges: dark summits and ridges sticking out of an endless white sheet, near the inner edge of the Filchner–Ronne Ice Shelf. The Pensacola Mountains stretch about 500 km. They were discovered only in 1956, spotted from a US Navy aircraft.","w":"Scattered dark rock ridges and peaks poking through flat, endless white ice.","f":["The mountains are named after the US Naval Air Station at Pensacola, Florida.","The Dufek Massif in this group is one of the largest layered igneous intrusions on Earth."],"q":{"q":"What are the Pensacola Mountains named after?","a":"A US Navy air station","x":["A Spanish explorer","A whaling ship"]}},"Q1139110":{"t":"The roof of Antarctica, crowned by Mount Vinson","s":"The highest mountains of the coldest continent rise here as a sharp, dark crest above the West Antarctic ice. Their Sentinel Range holds Mount Vinson, Antarctica’s highest peak at 4,892 m, and a string of other summits over 4,000 m. From the window, the black rock faces and long glaciers stand out sharply against the white.","w":"A sharp north–south line of dark peaks and ridges above flat white ice, with long glaciers streaming off both sides.","f":["Lincoln Ellsworth discovered the range in 1935, during the first flight across Antarctica.","Climbers reach Vinson via a seasonal camp on the Union Glacier, where planes land on bare blue ice."],"q":{"q":"Which is the highest peak in Antarctica?","a":"Mount Vinson","x":["Mount Erebus","Mount Kirkpatrick"]}},"Q1796029":{"t":"Amundsen’s stairway to the South Pole","s":"In November 1911 Roald Amundsen and his team climbed through these mountains with their dog sledges, up the steep Axel Heiberg Glacier to the polar plateau. From here the South Pole lies only about 400 km away. From cruise height, the Queen Maud Mountains are a jumble of dark peaks and broad glaciers pouring down to the flat Ross Ice Shelf.","w":"Dark, rugged peaks between wide glaciers that flow north onto the smooth white Ross Ice Shelf.","f":["Amundsen named the range after Queen Maud of Norway.","The Axel Heiberg Glacier is named after a Norwegian brewer who helped pay for Amundsen’s expeditions.","Amundsen’s team reached the South Pole on 14 December 1911."],"q":{"q":"Which glacier did Amundsen climb through these mountains?","a":"The Axel Heiberg Glacier","x":["The Beardmore Glacier","The Scott Glacier"]}},"Q842139":{"t":"A misty green spine that hid an unknown animal","s":"Cloud often clings to this long green spine between the Mekong valley and the coast of Vietnam. The Annamites run some 1,100 km along the border of Laos and Vietnam, their steep forested ridges so remote that in 1992 scientists discovered a large mammal entirely new to science: the saola, a shy forest ox with long, straight horns.","w":"Steep, forest-green ridges, often capped with cloud, separating the hills of Laos from Vietnam’s narrow coastal plain.","f":["In Vietnamese the range is called Truong Son, “the long mountains”.","Son Doong cave, in Vietnam’s Phong Nha–Ke Bang park in these mountains, has the largest known cave passage in the world."],"q":{"q":"Which large mammal was discovered in the Annamites in 1992?","a":"The saola","x":["The kouprey","The gaur"]}},"ne-rg-1730074073":{"t":"An ocean of green threaded with brown rivers","s":"Below you the Amazon rainforest — the selva — rolls to every horizon, a green so continuous it looks like moss. The only breaks are rivers: wide, coffee-coloured and looping back on themselves, leaving crescent-shaped lakes where old bends were cut off. In the afternoon, towering rain clouds bubble up over the canopy.","w":"Unbroken green canopy, brown rivers in huge loops and crescent-shaped oxbow lakes; in the afternoon, storm towers above.","f":["Iquitos, in the Peruvian selva, is often called the largest city in the world that cannot be reached by road.","Upstream of Iquitos, the Marañón and Ucayali rivers join to form the Amazon.","In Spanish, selva simply means “forest” or “jungle”."],"q":{"q":"Which two rivers join in Peru to form the Amazon?","a":"The Marañón and the Ucayali","x":["The Napo and the Putumayo","The Madeira and the Negro"]}},"Q748435":{"t":"Genghis Khan’s sacred mountains, birthplace of rivers","s":"Here the Siberian taiga reaches south into Mongolia: dark larch forest on the slopes, open grassy valleys below. The Khentii are the heartland of the Mongols — Burkhan Khaldun, the mountain Genghis Khan is said to have worshipped, rises among them. Three great rivers, the Onon, Kherlen and Tuul, begin in these hills.","w":"Dark forested ridges with open, grassy valleys; Ulaanbaatar spreads along the Tuul at the south-western edge.","f":["The Great Burkhan Khaldun Mountain was listed by UNESCO in 2015 as a sacred landscape.","The Kherlen flows east into Lake Hulun in China, while the Onon becomes part of the Amur system."],"q":{"q":"Which sacred Khentii mountain is linked to Genghis Khan?","a":"Burkhan Khaldun","x":["Otgontenger","Khüiten Peak"]}},"Q22449163":{"t":"Remote northern ranges famous for green jade","s":"Rounded grey peaks, dark spruce valleys and hardly a light at night: the Cassiar Mountains straddle the border of northern British Columbia and Yukon. Their rocks hold rich deposits of nephrite jade, making this one of the world’s main sources of the green stone. Few roads cross them; the Stewart–Cassiar Highway winds past the hamlet of Jade City.","w":"Rounded grey-green peaks, broad forested valleys and scattered lakes, with no towns in sight.","f":["Jade City, a tiny settlement on the Stewart–Cassiar Highway, sells nephrite mined in these mountains.","The Cassiar gold rush of the 1870s first drew miners into these remote valleys."],"q":{"q":"Which green stone is mined in the Cassiar Mountains?","a":"Nephrite jade","x":["Malachite","Emerald"]}},"Q1774690":{"t":"Kenya’s only true desert, a salt-white ancient lakebed","s":"East of Lake Turkana the land flattens into a pale, almost white expanse: the Chalbi, often called Kenya’s only true desert. It is the floor of an ancient lake, and after rare rains it floods into a shallow sheet of water before drying back to a cracked, salty crust. Gabbra herders bring their camels to the few oases on its edge.","w":"A flat, pale grey-white basin east of jade-green Lake Turkana, with the forested hump of Mount Marsabit to the south-east.","f":["Lake Turkana, just to the west, is the largest permanent desert lake in the world.","The Chalbi is so flat and bare that heat mirages often make it look like a lake even when it is dry."],"q":{"q":"Which big lake lies just west of the Chalbi Desert?","a":"Lake Turkana","x":["Lake Naivasha","Lake Victoria"]}},"Q624170":{"t":"Dark mountains between the Nile and the Red Sea","s":"Unlike the sandy west, Egypt’s Eastern Desert is a land of jagged, dark mountains and dry valleys — wadis — running down to the Red Sea on one side and to the Nile on the other. From 11 km up, look for the sudden contrast: turquoise sea, a thin strip of coast, then a chaos of brown and black peaks. The ancient Egyptians mined gold here.","w":"Jagged brown and black mountains cut by pale, branching wadis, with the turquoise Red Sea along the eastern edge.","f":["The Turin Papyrus Map, drawn about 1150 BC, shows quarries and gold mines here — perhaps the oldest geological map known.","The Romans quarried rare purple porphyry for their emperors at Mons Porphyrites, in these mountains."],"q":{"q":"Which rare stone did the Romans quarry in the Eastern Desert?","a":"Imperial porphyry","x":["Carrara marble","Lapis lazuli"]}},"Q22041906":{"t":"A lava plateau in southern Brazil, cut by sheer canyons","s":"About 130 million years ago, as South America and Africa began to split, lava flooded this region layer after layer, building a plateau more than a kilometre thick in places. Its edge, the Serra Geral, drops in steep escarpments split by deep canyons such as Itaimbezinho. On top, look for dark stands of Paraná pine among the fields.","w":"A high, flat plateau of fields and dark pine groves ending in steep cliffs and canyons; its rivers flow west, away from the sea.","f":["The Iguaçu Falls pour over the same layers of basalt, on the plateau’s western side.","The Paraná pine, whose flat-topped crowns dot the plateau, belongs to a tree family that grew alongside the dinosaurs.","The same lava flows appear across the ocean in Namibia, torn apart when the Atlantic opened."],"q":{"q":"Which famous waterfall pours over the same basalt layers?","a":"Iguaçu Falls","x":["Angel Falls","Kaieteur Falls"]}},"Q22529966":{"t":"Snowy ranges and long lakes in Canada’s inland rainforest","s":"West of the Rockies, British Columbia’s interior rises into another tangle of snowy ranges — the Selkirks, Monashees, Purcells and Cariboos, together called the Columbia Mountains. Between them lie long, narrow lakes such as Kootenay, Arrow and Slocan, like strips of blue ribbon. The western slopes catch so much snow and rain that they grow a rare inland rainforest of giant cedars.","w":"Tight clusters of snowy peaks separated by long, narrow blue lakes running north–south, with dark forest on the slopes.","f":["Rogers Pass, where the railway and the Trans-Canada Highway cross the Selkirks, gets about 10 m of snow in a typical winter.","The Columbia River loops right around the northern Selkirks in a giant curve known as the Big Bend.","Their eastern edge is the Rocky Mountain Trench, a straight valley hundreds of kilometres long."],"q":{"q":"Which pass takes the railway and highway across the Selkirks?","a":"Rogers Pass","x":["Kicking Horse Pass","Yellowhead Pass"]}},"Q747843":{"t":"Mongolia’s green heart, where an empire’s capital stood","s":"In the middle of Mongolia the land lifts into the Khangai: broad, rounded mountains where larch forest clings only to the north-facing slopes, so the hills look striped dark and light. Herders have grazed these pastures for thousands of years. At the range’s eastern foot, in the Orkhon Valley, stood Karakorum, the 13th-century capital of the Mongol Empire.","w":"Broad, rounded mountains with dark forest on the north slopes and pale grass on the south, and green river valleys between.","f":["The Orkhon Valley, with the ruins of Karakorum, is a UNESCO World Heritage Site.","Otgontenger, the highest peak of the Khangai, is revered by Mongolians as a sacred mountain.","Rivers from the Khangai’s northern slopes flow through the Selenga into Lake Baikal."],"q":{"q":"Which old imperial capital stood at the foot of the Khangai?","a":"Karakorum","x":["Xanadu","Samarkand"]}},"Q863798":{"t":"The wall that splits China into north and south","s":"Cross this range and you cross a climate line: the Qinling split China into a dry, wheat-growing north and a wet, rice-growing south. From above, yellow loess and the plain of Xi’an lie on one side, green terraced valleys on the other. In the bamboo forests on its slopes live giant pandas and golden snub-nosed monkeys.","w":"A long east–west wall of forested ridges, with brownish plains around Xi’an to the north and greener hills to the south.","f":["Together with the Huai River, the Qinling mark China’s traditional boundary between north and south.","Taibai Shan, about 3,770 m, is the highest peak in the eastern half of mainland China.","Xi’an, at the range’s northern foot, was the starting point of the Silk Road."],"q":{"q":"Which river pairs with the Qinling as China’s north–south line?","a":"The Huai River","x":["The Yellow River","The Pearl River"]}},"Q20155858":{"t":"A stone desert as flat and dark as a slate roof","s":"Not every desert is sand. A hamada is a bare, rocky plateau swept clean by wind, and the Hamada du Draa is a classic: a vast, flat pavement of dark stones south of the Draa valley. From the window it looks like slate or scorched leather, broken only by dry riverbeds. Along its northern edge runs the Draa, a river that rarely reaches the sea.","w":"A dark, flat stony plain to the horizon, cut by pale dry riverbeds, with the green Draa oases to the north.","f":["The Draa is Morocco’s longest river, about 1,100 km, yet it reaches the Atlantic only in rare wet years.","Desert varnish — a thin dark film of manganese and iron — gives the hamada’s stones their black sheen."],"q":{"q":"What is a hamada?","a":"A bare, rocky desert plateau","x":["A field of shifting dunes","A dried-up salt lake"]}},"Q272521":{"t":"A desert within the desert, once home to the loneliest tree","s":"Ténéré means “desert” in the Tuareg language — a desert within the Sahara. East of the Aïr Mountains it spreads as a vast, smooth sea of pale sand, some of the flattest and emptiest land on Earth. For decades a single acacia stood here, the only tree for some 400 km and a landmark for caravans, until a truck knocked it down in 1973.","w":"An immense, smooth sheet of pale yellow sand with few landmarks; the dark Aïr Mountains rise along its western edge.","f":["What remains of the Tree of Ténéré stands in the National Museum in Niamey; a metal sculpture marks its old spot.","At Gadoufaoua the shifting sands have uncovered dinosaur skeletons and the giant crocodile Sarcosuchus.","Salt caravans still cross the Ténéré to the oasis of Bilma."],"q":{"q":"What happened to the famous Tree of Ténéré in 1973?","a":"A truck knocked it down","x":["Lightning struck it","It was cut for firewood"]}},"Q307143":{"t":"A golden sea of dunes, frozen mid-storm","s":"Few views from the air say “Sahara” like this one: a golden sea of dunes stretching across eastern Algeria into Tunisia. Look for the patterns — long ridges, crescents and, in places, huge star-shaped dunes with arms radiating from a peak. Beyond its northern edge, the white salt crust of Chott el Djerid gleams in the sun.","w":"Golden dunes in regular patterns — ridges, crescents and stars — with the white salt flat of Chott el Djerid to the north.","f":["Sets for Tatooine, the desert planet of Star Wars, were built near Tozeur and Nefta on the erg’s Tunisian edge.","“Erg”, a word taken from Arabic, is what geographers call a large sea of dunes.","Hassi Messaoud, Algeria’s largest oilfield, lies at the erg’s western edge; at night its gas flares glow."],"q":{"q":"Which film saga shot desert scenes on the erg’s Tunisian edge?","a":"Star Wars","x":["Lawrence of Arabia","Dune"]}},"Q2064799":{"t":"The Sahara’s great empty basin, far from any oasis","s":"Here lies one of the most remote corners of the Sahara: El Djouf, a vast basin of dunes and gravel on the border of Mauritania and Mali, with no roads, villages or permanent wells across much of it. From cruise height, look for endless parallel dune ridges running north-east to south-west, combed by the steady trade wind. Its Arabic name means “the belly” — a hollow in the desert.","w":"Endless parallel orange dune ridges and gravel plains, with no roads, towns or green patches anywhere in sight.","f":["On its north-eastern edge lies Taoudenni, where salt has been cut in slabs and carried by camel to Timbuktu for centuries.","The French naturalist Théodore Monod spent decades exploring this empty basin by camel."],"q":{"q":"Which salt-mining oasis lies on El Djouf’s north-eastern edge?","a":"Taoudenni","x":["Bilma","Siwa"]}},"Q855658":{"t":"A high desert of cactus, gypsum dunes and the Rio Grande","s":"Much of the Chihuahuan Desert lies on high plateaus between two Mexican mountain ranges, so it is cooler than you might expect, and most of its rain falls in summer storms. From 11 km up it is a mosaic of tan basins, dark volcanic hills and dry lakebeds. On the Texas border the Rio Grande makes its Big Bend, carving deep canyons through the ranges.","w":"Tan basins with dark volcanic hills and pale dry lakebeds; the Rio Grande’s green ribbon winds through canyons.","f":["White Sands, at the desert’s northern end in New Mexico, is the largest gypsum dune field in the world.","The Chihuahuan Desert is one of the richest places on Earth for cactus species."],"q":{"q":"Which field of white gypsum dunes lies in the Chihuahuan Desert?","a":"White Sands","x":["Death Valley","Great Sand Dunes"]}},"Q189429":{"t":"The land of the saguaro, surprisingly green","s":"For a desert, the Sonoran looks surprisingly green from above: two rainy seasons a year feed a dense scatter of shrubs, trees and giant cacti. It spreads across Arizona, California and north-western Mexico, broken by rugged little mountain ranges. Look for the sharp green squares of irrigated farms along the Colorado and Gila rivers, and the vast grid of Phoenix to the east.","w":"Olive-dotted desert basins between small, jagged ranges, bright irrigated fields along the rivers, and Phoenix’s sprawling grid.","f":["The giant saguaro cactus grows wild almost only in the Sonoran Desert.","A saguaro can grow more than 12 m tall and live for over 150 years.","Phoenix, in the heart of the desert, is one of the largest cities in the United States."],"q":{"q":"Which giant cactus is the symbol of the Sonoran Desert?","a":"The saguaro","x":["The Joshua tree","The prickly pear"]}},"Q1670841":{"t":"A desert cradled in the Yellow River’s great loop","s":"Look for the Yellow River swinging north in a huge, almost rectangular loop: the land inside it is the Ordos Plateau, and its sandy south is the Mu Us Desert. Over recent decades much of the Mu Us has been fixed with planted shrubs and trees, so from above you may see as much grey-green as yellow. The Great Wall once ran along its southern edge.","w":"A plateau of pale sand patches and grey-green scrub inside the Yellow River’s big loop, crossed by straight roads.","f":["Kubuqi, the desert on the northern side of the Ordos, is known for huge solar farms laid out on the sand.","The Ordos Plateau is rich in coal, making it one of China’s main energy regions."],"q":{"q":"Which river loops around the Ordos Plateau?","a":"The Yellow River","x":["The Yangtze","The Amur"]}},"Q132956":{"t":"A sea of shifting sand ringed by snowy mountains","s":"The Taklamakan fills the Tarim Basin like sand poured into a bowl: dunes in every direction, walled in by the snowy Tian Shan to the north and the Kunlun to the south. It is one of the largest shifting-sand deserts on Earth. Old Silk Road oases such as Kashgar and Hotan ring its edges, fed by meltwater streams that die out in the sand.","w":"Huge dune fields in a closed basin, ringed by snowy mountains and green oasis strips where rivers leave the foothills.","f":["The name is popularly translated as “go in and you won’t come out”, though its real origin is uncertain.","The Tarim Desert Highway crosses the dunes for more than 500 km, protected by planted belts of shrubs.","The Tarim River, fed by mountain meltwater, runs along the desert’s northern edge and never reaches the sea."],"q":{"q":"Which mountains wall in the Taklamakan to the north?","a":"The Tian Shan","x":["The Kunlun","The Altai"]}},"Q483134":{"t":"A mountainous peninsula with a rugged eastern spine","s":"About 70% of the Korean Peninsula is hills and mountains, and from above you can see how they crowd towards the east: a long spine, the Baekdudaegan, runs close to the eastern coast, while the land slopes west to broad plains and a shore of islands and vast mudflats. In autumn the forests turn every shade of red and gold.","w":"Forested ridges crowding the east coast, flatter farmland and cities in the west, and a western shore of islands and mudflats.","f":["The tidal flats of the west coast are a UNESCO World Heritage Site; tides there can rise more than 8 m.","From north to south the peninsula is about 1,000 km long."],"q":{"q":"Roughly how much of the Korean Peninsula is mountainous?","a":"About 70%","x":["About 30%","About 50%"]}},"Q584279":{"t":"North Africa’s highest wall, snow above the desert","s":"From Marrakesh you can see its snow in winter, and from above the High Atlas is a sharp, rust-and-white barrier running some 700 km across Morocco. To the north lie green plains and the Atlantic; to the south the desert begins almost at once. Deep gorges such as Todgha and Dadès cut its southern flank, with thin green strips of palm oases below.","w":"A rust-red mountain wall topped with snow in winter; narrow green oasis strips wind along the valleys on its southern side.","f":["The road from Marrakesh to Ouarzazate crosses the range at the Tizi n’Tichka pass, about 2,260 m high.","Aït Benhaddou, a fortified earthen village on the southern side, is a UNESCO site and a favourite film location."],"q":{"q":"Which pass carries the road from Marrakesh to Ouarzazate?","a":"Tizi n’Tichka","x":["Tizi n’Test","Col du Zad"]}},"Q183295":{"t":"The Dragon Mountains, a basalt wall around Lesotho","s":"Seen from above, the Drakensberg is a single colossal cliff: the edge of a high basalt plateau that drops more than a kilometre to the green hills of KwaZulu-Natal. On top lies Lesotho, a mountain kingdom where even the lowest ground is about 1,400 m above sea level. In winter the rim of the escarpment is often dusted with snow.","w":"A long, dark escarpment edge: a flat, bare high plateau on one side, folded green foothills far below on the other.","f":["Its Zulu name, uKhahlamba, means “barrier of spears”; Drakensberg is Afrikaans for “dragon mountains”.","Thabana Ntlenyana in Lesotho, 3,482 m, is the highest peak in southern Africa.","The Maloti-Drakensberg Park holds thousands of rock paintings made by the San people."],"q":{"q":"What does the Zulu name uKhahlamba mean?","a":"Barrier of spears","x":["Dragon’s back","Mountain of thunder"]}},"Q5191296":{"t":"Green hills where Central Africa’s rivers rush to the sea","s":"A band of low, rainforest-covered hills runs parallel to the Atlantic coast of Central Africa, from Equatorial Guinea through Gabon towards the mouth of the Congo. Rivers crossing it tumble down in rapids, and the mighty Congo itself breaks through in the Livingstone Falls, a chain of cataracts below Kinshasa. From above it is an almost unbroken carpet of dark green.","w":"Low, rolling hills under dense green rainforest a short way inland from the Atlantic, with brown rivers cutting through them.","f":["Gabon’s Monts de Cristal National Park, at the northern end of the range, is one of Africa’s richest places for plant species.","Below Kinshasa the Congo drops about 270 m through the Livingstone Falls on its way to the ocean."],"q":{"q":"Which cataracts carry the Congo through these hills to the sea?","a":"The Livingstone Falls","x":["The Victoria Falls","The Boyoma Falls"]}},"Q309104":{"t":"Dark volcanoes rising from the heart of the Sahara","s":"In the middle of the Sahara, dark volcanic mountains rise out of the sand like islands. The Tibesti in northern Chad holds the highest summit of the Sahara, Emi Koussi, at about 3,400 m — a huge shield volcano whose crater is some 12 km across. Nearby lies Trou au Natron, a deep caldera whose floor is streaked white with salt.","w":"Dark brown and black volcanic massifs rising from pale sand, with wide craters and white salt crusts inside some calderas.","f":["Trou au Natron is about 1 km deep, and its floor is crusted with white natron salt.","Rock art in the Tibesti shows cattle and giraffes from the time when the Sahara was green."],"q":{"q":"Which is the highest summit of the Sahara?","a":"Emi Koussi","x":["Mount Tahat","Jebel Toubkal"]}},"Q23836082":{"t":"Black volcanic spires in the heart of the Algerian Sahara","s":"Out of the Algerian Sahara rises the Ahaggar, or Hoggar: a high granite plateau crowned with dark volcanic peaks and needle-like plugs of old lava, weathered into fantastic shapes. It is the home ground of the Tuareg, and the town of Tamanrasset sits at its foot. Its highest peak, Mount Tahat, reaches about 2,900 m.","w":"A dark, rugged highland of black peaks and spires rising from pale desert, with dry valleys radiating outwards.","f":["On the Assekrem plateau, about 2,700 m up, stands the hermitage the French monk Charles de Foucauld built in 1911.","Wild Saharan olive trees survive in the Ahaggar’s high valleys, relics of a wetter past."],"q":{"q":"Which is the highest peak of the Ahaggar?","a":"Mount Tahat","x":["Emi Koussi","Assekrem"]}},"Q170383":{"t":"Dark granite islands in a sea of sand","s":"The Aïr rises from the Sahara like a cluster of dark islands: granite massifs, some almost perfectly round, surrounded by yellow sand. The rings are the roots of ancient volcanoes laid bare by erosion, and they show up clearly from the air. Between them, dry valleys hold acacias and a few Tuareg villages; the old caravan town of Agadez lies at the southern edge.","w":"Dark, rounded rock massifs, some ring-shaped, standing out against pale sand, with the smooth Ténéré dunes to the east.","f":["The Aïr and Ténéré Natural Reserves together form one of the largest protected areas in Africa, listed by UNESCO.","The old town of Agadez, with its tall mud-brick minaret, is a UNESCO World Heritage Site."],"q":{"q":"Which caravan town lies at the southern edge of the Aïr?","a":"Agadez","x":["Timbuktu","Tamanrasset"]}},"Q404600":{"t":"Forested hills that the Amur River slices in two","s":"Low, rolling and dark with forest, the Lesser Khingan rise between the plains of Manchuria and the Amur. The range is modest, mostly under 1,000 m, but here the great Amur cuts straight through it in a narrow, winding gorge. The hills are covered with Korean pine, larch and birch, turning gold and red in autumn.","w":"Low, dark-green forested hills with the broad Amur River squeezing through them in a winding gorge.","f":["At the range’s western foot lies Wudalianchi, a cluster of volcanoes that last erupted in 1719–21, damming a chain of five lakes.","Korean pine, whose seeds are sold as pine nuts, is one of the range’s main trees."],"q":{"q":"Which great river cuts through the Lesser Khingan in a gorge?","a":"The Amur","x":["The Songhua","The Ussuri"]}},"Q155322":{"t":"Tiger country on Russia’s Pacific shore","s":"A long, forested range runs some 1,000 km along Russia’s Pacific coast, north of Vladivostok. Its forests are unique: northern taiga mixes with southern broadleaf woods, Amur tigers share them with brown and Himalayan bears, and spruce grows beside wild grapes and lianas. From the window it is a sea of ridges, dark green in summer and flecked with gold in autumn.","w":"Endless parallel forested ridges between the Ussuri valley and the sea, with small river mouths along the coast.","f":["On 12 February 1947 one of the largest iron meteorite showers ever seen fell here, leaving dozens of craters.","Central Sikhote-Alin is a UNESCO World Heritage Site that protects the Amur tiger’s home."],"q":{"q":"What fell on the Sikhote-Alin in February 1947?","a":"An iron meteorite shower","x":["Volcanic ash","A giant hailstorm"]}},"Q215917":{"t":"A frozen arc beside the Pole of Cold","s":"A great arc of bare, jagged mountains curves some 1,200 km along the east bank of the lower Lena, rising steeply above its broad, braided valley. Beyond the range lie Verkhoyansk and Oymyakon, the coldest inhabited places in the Northern Hemisphere, where winter can bring −67 °C. In summer the snow retreats to the summits; in winter everything is white and silent.","w":"A long wall of grey, snow-streaked peaks east of the wide, braided Lena, with empty, larch-dotted valleys between them.","f":["Verkhoyansk, beyond the range, recorded −67.8 °C in 1892, one of the lowest temperatures ever measured in an inhabited place.","Summer there can top +30 °C — a swing of about 100 degrees across the year.","Permafrost beneath the range reaches hundreds of metres deep."],"q":{"q":"Which town beyond the range is famed as a Pole of Cold?","a":"Verkhoyansk","x":["Norilsk","Vorkuta"]}},"Q1072879":{"t":"A snowy wall above the Silk Road’s Hexi Corridor","s":"Along the north-eastern edge of the Tibetan Plateau, the Qilian Mountains form a long snowy wall, their glaciers feeding the rivers that water the Hexi Corridor below. That narrow strip of oases, squeezed between the mountains and the Gobi, was the main route of the Silk Road into China. At the range’s northern foot, the Zhangye hills are striped red, orange and yellow.","w":"A long, white-capped range to the south, a green strip of oases and fields along its foot, and the grey-brown Gobi beyond.","f":["The Hexi Corridor was the main Silk Road route between China’s heartland and Central Asia.","Jiayuguan fort, the western end of the Ming Great Wall, stands at the foot of the Qilian."],"q":{"q":"Which Silk Road corridor runs along the Qilian’s northern foot?","a":"The Hexi Corridor","x":["The Wakhan Corridor","The Dzungarian Gate"]}},"Q1156557":{"t":"Green hills between the Yangtze and the Huai","s":"Between the Yangtze and the Huai River, the Dabie Mountains rise as a forested knot of peaks in central China, often wrapped in mist. They look gentle from above — green ridges, terraced tea slopes and reservoirs in the valleys — but their rocks tell a violent story: they were dragged more than 100 km down into the Earth and then brought back up.","w":"A compact cluster of green, forested ridges with reservoirs in the valleys, between two broad river plains.","f":["Tiny diamonds and coesite in the Dabie rocks prove they were once buried more than 100 km deep.","The mountains divide the basins of the Yangtze and the Huai.","Lu’an Guapian, a well-known Chinese green tea, is grown on the Dabie slopes."],"q":{"q":"How deep were the Dabie Mountains’ rocks once buried?","a":"More than 100 km","x":["About 1 km","About 10 km"]}},"Q1195285":{"t":"The ridges that shield southern China from winter","s":"Across southern China runs a jumble of rounded, forested ranges, the Nanling. They divide the Yangtze basin from the Pearl River basin and shelter Guangdong from the coldest winter winds, so subtropical crops grow on their southern side. From cruise height, look for green hills scarred with red earth, rice terraces and, towards Guilin, jagged limestone pinnacles.","w":"Rounded green ridges running east–west, red earth and rice terraces, and sharp limestone pinnacles to the west near Guilin.","f":["Nanling simply means “southern ridges” in Chinese.","The Lingqu Canal, dug in the 3rd century BC, crossed these mountains to link the Yangtze and Pearl River systems."],"q":{"q":"Which two river basins do the Nanling divide?","a":"The Yangtze and the Pearl","x":["The Yellow and the Huai","The Mekong and the Red"]}},"Q4527":{"t":"India’s rain-soaked green wall above the Arabian Sea","s":"Running some 1,600 km parallel to India’s west coast, the Western Ghats are the first thing the summer monsoon hits: their seaward slopes are drenched and deep green, while the land behind them lies in a rain shadow. Around the Nilgiri Hills they rise to a high, rolling plateau of tea gardens and grassland. Just to the south, the Palghat Gap cuts a rare low pass through the wall.","w":"A steep, dark-green escarpment parallel to the coast, tea gardens on the Nilgiri plateau and the wide Palghat Gap to the south.","f":["The Western Ghats are a UNESCO World Heritage Site and one of the world’s biodiversity hotspots.","The neelakurinji shrub blooms here only once every 12 years, turning whole slopes blue-purple.","Anamudi, 2,695 m, is the highest peak in India south of the Himalayas."],"q":{"q":"How often does the neelakurinji of the Western Ghats bloom?","a":"Once every 12 years","x":["Once every 3 years","Once every 50 years"]}},"Q465055":{"t":"Broken hills where India’s great rivers break through","s":"Unlike the continuous wall of the Western Ghats, the Eastern Ghats are a broken chain of old, worn hills along India’s east coast. The great rivers of the Deccan — the Godavari, Krishna and Mahanadi — cut right through them on their way to the Bay of Bengal, fanning out into fertile deltas. In the north, forested hills around the Araku Valley grow coffee.","w":"Low, forested, broken hills parallel to the east coast, with wide rivers cutting through them to green deltas.","f":["The Borra Caves in the Eastern Ghats are among the largest caves in India.","Chilika Lake, near the northern end of the Ghats, is India’s largest coastal lagoon."],"q":{"q":"Where do the rivers that cut through the Eastern Ghats flow?","a":"Into the Bay of Bengal","x":["Into the Arabian Sea","Into the Gulf of Khambhat"]}},"Q271435":{"t":"The old line between northern and southern India","s":"For centuries Indians saw the Vindhya as the dividing line between north and south — the Gangetic plains on one side, the Deccan on the other. From 11 km up it is less a range than a long, broken escarpment of flat-topped sandstone hills. Along its southern foot flows the Narmada, one of the few big Indian rivers that runs west.","w":"A long line of flat-topped, scrub-covered sandstone hills and cliffs, with the Narmada valley along the southern side.","f":["The Bhimbetka rock shelters in the Vindhya foothills hold prehistoric paintings and are a UNESCO World Heritage Site.","The Buddhist stupas of Sanchi, begun in the 3rd century BC, stand on a hilltop in this region."],"q":{"q":"Which west-flowing river runs along the Vindhya’s southern foot?","a":"The Narmada","x":["The Godavari","The Chambal"]}},"Q207692":{"t":"Red cliffs, a winding river and the home of rock tea","s":"On the border of Fujian and Jiangxi, the Wuyi Mountains rise as forested ridges and bare red sandstone cliffs. The Nine-Bend River loops between them, and on narrow terraces among the rocks grows Wuyi rock tea, including the famous Da Hong Pao. From above, look for red rock walls standing out against the deep green.","w":"Dense green forest broken by bare red cliffs and domes, with a winding river threading between them.","f":["The Wuyi Mountains are a UNESCO World Heritage Site for both their nature and their culture.","Da Hong Pao tea from the few ancient bushes here has fetched record prices at auction.","The Neo-Confucian philosopher Zhu Xi taught in these mountains in the 12th century."],"q":{"q":"Which famous tea comes from the Wuyi Mountains?","a":"Da Hong Pao","x":["Longjing","Pu’er"]}},"Q128500":{"t":"The marmot mountains on the Kazakh–Chinese border","s":"A long, bare ridge runs east–west along the border of Kazakhstan and China, rising abruptly from dry steppe. The Tarbagatai take their name from the Mongolian word for marmot, and those burrowing animals still whistle on the grassy slopes. To the north lies the blue sheet of Lake Zaysan; to the south, the Dzungarian Basin opens out.","w":"A lone east–west ridge, snow-streaked in winter, rising from brown steppe, with Lake Zaysan to the north-east.","f":["The range’s highest point, Mount Tastau, falls just short of 3,000 m.","South of the range lies the Dzungarian Gate, a windswept pass that has long linked Central Asia and China."],"q":{"q":"Which animal gave the Tarbagatai their name?","a":"The marmot","x":["The golden eagle","The wild horse"]}},"Q682334":{"t":"The golden mountains at the edge of Tibet","s":"Altyn-Tagh means “golden mountains” in Turkic — a fitting name for these bare, sun-baked ridges. The range forms the north-eastern rim of the Tibetan Plateau, dropping steeply to the Tarim Basin and the salt flats of Lop Nur. Along it runs a huge fault, where the plateau slides sideways past the rest of Asia.","w":"Bare, tawny ridges rising steeply south of the desert, with the pale, ear-shaped salt flats of Lop Nur to the north-east.","f":["The Altyn Tagh Fault runs for about 1,600 km, one of the longest active faults in Asia.","Lop Nur, a dried-up lake, is ringed with salt bands that make it look like a giant ear from space.","The Lop Nur area is one of the last refuges of the wild Bactrian camel."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Altyn-Tagh mean?","a":"Golden mountains","x":["Black mountains","Heavenly mountains"]}},"Q186547":{"t":"Norway’s high spine of ice, fjords and bare plateaus","s":"The Scandinavian Mountains run about 1,700 km along the peninsula, mostly through Norway. From the window they are less a line of peaks than a vast, bare highland scraped by ice, scattered with lakes and snowfields and slashed on the west by deep fjords. In Jotunheimen, the “home of the giants”, the land rises to the highest summits in northern Europe.","w":"Bare grey-brown plateaus with snowfields and countless lakes, deep fjords cutting in from the west, glaciers on the highest ground.","f":["Galdhøpiggen, 2,469 m, in Jotunheimen, is the highest peak in Scandinavia.","Jostedalsbreen is the largest glacier in mainland Europe.","Musk oxen roam the Dovrefjell plateau, brought back from Greenland in the mid-20th century."],"q":{"q":"Which is the highest peak in Norway?","a":"Galdhøpiggen","x":["Glittertind","Snøhetta"]}},"Q1288":{"t":"A great forested arc wrapped around Transylvania","s":"The Carpathians curve through Central and Eastern Europe in an arc about 1,500 km long, and from above you can see how they cradle the Transylvanian Basin like a horseshoe. Their slopes carry some of Europe’s largest surviving beech and spruce forests, home to many brown bears, wolves and lynx. Only in the Tatras do they rise to sharp, alpine-looking peaks.","w":"A curving band of dark-forested ridges, with the patchwork fields and towns of the Transylvanian Basin inside the arc.","f":["Gerlachovský štít in Slovakia’s High Tatras, 2,655 m, is the highest peak of the Carpathians.","Romania’s Carpathians hold one of the largest brown bear populations in Europe.","The Danube cuts through the range at the Iron Gates, a gorge on the border of Serbia and Romania."],"q":{"q":"Where does the Danube cut through the Carpathians?","a":"The Iron Gates","x":["The Wachau Valley","The Schlögen Loop"]}},"Q377264":{"t":"The long, bony spine of Italy","s":"The Apennines run some 1,200 km through Italy, from Liguria to the toe of Calabria, and from above they are its unmistakable backbone: grey limestone ridges, beech forest and towns clustered on hilltops. They reach their highest in Abruzzo, at the Gran Sasso. Nearby, look for a strangely flat oval of fields ringed by mountains — the bed of Lake Fucino, drained in the 19th century.","w":"A grey-green mountain spine down the middle of Italy; in Abruzzo, the flat oval of the drained Fucino basin among the peaks.","f":["Corno Grande in the Gran Sasso massif, 2,912 m, is the highest peak of the Apennines.","Under Emperor Claudius the Romans dug a 5.6-km tunnel to drain Lake Fucino, but it was fully drained only in the 1870s."],"q":{"q":"Which is the highest peak of the Apennines?","a":"Corno Grande","x":["Monte Vettore","Monte Amaro"]}},"Q12431":{"t":"A wall between Spain and France, with a country inside","s":"From the Atlantic to the Mediterranean, the Pyrenees form an almost unbroken wall about 430 km long between Spain and France. The French side is greener and steeper; the Spanish side is drier, with long ridges and ochre valleys. Tucked high in the middle is Andorra, a small country of deep valleys and ski slopes.","w":"A continuous snowy crest from sea to sea, with greener valleys on the French side and drier ochre ones on the Spanish side.","f":["Aneto, 3,404 m, on the Spanish side, is the highest peak of the Pyrenees.","The waterfall in the Cirque de Gavarnie drops about 420 m, one of the tallest in Europe.","Andorra la Vella, at about 1,020 m, is the highest capital city in Europe."],"q":{"q":"Which is the highest peak of the Pyrenees?","a":"Aneto","x":["Monte Perdido","Vignemale"]}},"Q1029702":{"t":"Mexico’s tangled southern ranges above the Pacific","s":"Along Mexico’s southern Pacific coast, from Jalisco to Oaxaca, the land crumples into the Sierra Madre del Sur: a maze of steep, forested ridges and deep valleys with few roads across it. From cruise height it looks endlessly folded, green in the rainy season and brown in the dry. Hidden in its highlands lies the Valley of Oaxaca, with the ancient Zapotec city of Monte Albán on a levelled hilltop.","w":"Endless steep, folded ridges between the Pacific coast and the highlands, with deep river valleys and few roads.","f":["Monte Albán, founded around 500 BC on a flattened mountaintop above Oaxaca, is a UNESCO World Heritage Site.","Acapulco’s famous bay is framed by the foothills of the Sierra Madre del Sur."],"q":{"q":"Which ancient city stands on a levelled hilltop in these mountains?","a":"Monte Albán","x":["Teotihuacan","Chichén Itzá"]}},"Q660304":{"t":"Fog, redwoods and faults along America’s Pacific edge","s":"Hugging the Pacific from southern California to Washington, the Coast Ranges are a long band of rugged, forested hills rarely far from the surf. On summer mornings, fog pours in from the ocean and fills their valleys like milk. That fog helps sustain the coast redwoods here in northern California — the tallest trees on Earth.","w":"Dark-green forested ridges right along the ocean, often with white fog filling the valleys between them in the morning.","f":["Hyperion, a coast redwood in northern California, is the tallest known living tree, about 116 m.","The San Andreas Fault runs through the southern Coast Ranges, where the Pacific plate slides past North America.","Coast redwoods grow only in a narrow strip of fog-bathed land in California and southern Oregon."],"q":{"q":"Which trees, the tallest on Earth, grow in these ranges?","a":"Coast redwoods","x":["Giant sequoias","Douglas firs"]}},"Q144040":{"t":"Folded limestone ridges curling around Monterrey","s":"From 11 km up, the Sierra Madre Oriental is one of the most striking fold belts on Earth: long, parallel limestone ridges bend and curve like the lines of a fingerprint. Near Monterrey the folds swing from north–south to east–west in one great curve. The range runs about 1,000 km through north-eastern Mexico, a dry, grey-green wall between the coastal plain and the high central plateau.","w":"Long, parallel grey limestone ridges curving like fingerprint lines, with dry basins between them and Monterrey at the eastern foot.","f":["Monterrey’s saddle-shaped landmark, the Cerro de la Silla, is a spur of the range.","The range forms the eastern edge of the Mexican Plateau, rising above the coastal plain.","Cumbres de Monterrey National Park protects the rugged ridges around the city."],"q":{"q":"Which city lies at the foot of the Sierra Madre Oriental’s great curve?","a":"Monterrey","x":["Guadalajara","Chihuahua"]}},"Q26777":{"t":"California’s granite wall, with Yosemite carved into it","s":"Think of a giant granite slab tilted up on one side: a long, gentle slope rising from California’s Central Valley, and a sheer eastern wall dropping some three kilometres to the desert. That is the Sierra Nevada. Ice Age glaciers carved it into deep valleys, the most famous being Yosemite, with its sheer walls and domes.","w":"Pale granite peaks and domes, deep glacier-carved valleys and lakes, snow on the crest; Lake Tahoe’s deep blue to the north.","f":["Mount Whitney, 4,421 m, is the highest peak in the contiguous United States.","Giant sequoias, the most massive trees on Earth, grow wild only on the Sierra’s western slopes.","Lake Tahoe, high in the range, is about 500 m deep."],"q":{"q":"Which is the highest peak in the contiguous United States?","a":"Mount Whitney","x":["Mount Rainier","Mount Elbert"]}},"Q1124584":{"t":"Mexico’s great volcanic plateau, split by canyons","s":"Running about 1,200 km down western Mexico, the Sierra Madre Occidental is a high plateau built from enormous volcanic eruptions tens of millions of years ago and now cut by deep canyons. The most famous, the Copper Canyon system in Chihuahua, is in places deeper than the Grand Canyon. From above, look for pine-covered uplands suddenly split by dark, winding gorges.","w":"A high, pine-dotted plateau sliced by deep, winding canyons, with dry desert basins on both sides.","f":["The plateau is built from layers of volcanic ash laid down by huge eruptions some 30 million years ago.","The Rarámuri, or Tarahumara, people of the Copper Canyon are famous for running very long distances.","The Chepe railway crosses the range from Chihuahua to the Pacific coast through 86 tunnels."],"q":{"q":"Which canyon system cuts the Sierra Madre Occidental?","a":"The Copper Canyon","x":["The Grand Canyon","The Sumidero Canyon"]}},"Q1089367":{"t":"Ice-buried peaks on the stormy Gulf of Alaska","s":"The Chugach rise straight out of the Gulf of Alaska, catching storm after storm off the Pacific, and much of the range lies buried under ice. From the window you see a white sea of glaciers with dark peaks poking through, and tongues of ice creeping down to the fjords of Prince William Sound. Thompson Pass, near Valdez, is among the snowiest places in North America.","w":"A white expanse of icefields and glaciers with dark peaks poking through, ending in fjords and the grey-blue Gulf of Alaska.","f":["The Bagley Icefield in the eastern Chugach is one of the largest icefields in North America outside the polar regions.","The Columbia Glacier, which flows into Prince William Sound, has retreated more than 20 km since the 1980s.","In the winter of 1952–53 Thompson Pass recorded about 25 m of snow."],"q":{"q":"Which icefield lies in the eastern Chugach Mountains?","a":"The Bagley Icefield","x":["The Juneau Icefield","The Columbia Icefield"]}},"Q1817102":{"t":"Wild peaks where the Nahanni carves its canyons","s":"Almost roadless and nearly empty of people, the Mackenzie Mountains stretch along the border of Yukon and the Northwest Territories. From cruise height you see bare grey ridges, braided rivers and, in autumn, tundra turning red and gold. At their southern end the South Nahanni River carves deep canyons and plunges over Virginia Falls, about twice the height of Niagara.","w":"Bare, grey, folded ridges and wide braided rivers, with almost no roads or settlements in sight.","f":["In 1978 Nahanni National Park became one of the first places listed as UNESCO World Heritage Sites.","Keele Peak, about 2,950 m, is the highest point of the range."],"q":{"q":"Which famous falls lie on the South Nahanni River?","a":"Virginia Falls","x":["Kakabeka Falls","Hunlen Falls"]}},"Q1132302":{"t":"Bolivia’s Royal Range, a snowy wall above the Altiplano","s":"East of the Altiplano rises a line of high, often snowy peaks — the Cordillera Real, the “Royal Range”. At its northern end Illimani, 6,438 m, towers over La Paz, its white bulk visible from much of the city. Further south the mountains grow drier and redder, cradling Potosí, which grew rich on the silver of Cerro Rico.","w":"A wall of snowy peaks and glaciers east of the flat, brown Altiplano, with deep valleys dropping away on the far side.","f":["Potosí, at about 4,000 m, was one of the richest cities in the world in the 16th and 17th centuries, thanks to its silver.","The range’s glaciers help supply water to La Paz and El Alto."],"q":{"q":"Which mountain towers over La Paz?","a":"Illimani","x":["Sajama","Licancabur"]}},"ne-rg-1159103969":{"t":"Peru’s western wall of volcanoes and deep canyons","s":"Between the coastal desert and the high puna, Peru’s Western Cordillera rises as a chain of snow-capped volcanoes — Coropuna, Ampato, Misti. Rivers have sawn canyons through it, and Colca and Cotahuasi are among the deepest on Earth, over 3 km from rim to river. From 11 km up, look for white cones standing on brown plateaus, with the desert falling away towards the Pacific.","w":"Snow-capped volcanic cones on a high brown plateau, deep dark canyons, and bare desert slopes falling towards the ocean.","f":["Coropuna, about 6,400 m, is the highest volcano in Peru.","The Colca Canyon is famous for Andean condors riding the morning updrafts.","The Nazca Lines lie on the desert plain at the western foot of this cordillera."],"q":{"q":"Which is the highest volcano in Peru?","a":"Coropuna","x":["El Misti","Ubinas"]}},"ne-rg-1159103969-2":{"t":"The Andes’ westernmost ridge, facing the rainy Chocó","s":"In Colombia the Andes split into three parallel ranges, and this is the westernmost. On its Pacific side lies the Chocó, one of the rainiest regions on Earth, draped in rainforest and usually under cloud; to the east the Cauca River runs through a deep valley. Southwards the same range continues into Ecuador, where it carries the ice-capped Chimborazo.","w":"A forested ridge with cloud piled up on its western, Pacific side and the deep Cauca valley on its eastern side.","f":["Towns in the Chocó below its western slopes are among the rainiest places on Earth.","Because Earth bulges at the equator, Chimborazo’s summit is the point on its surface farthest from the planet’s centre.","The steep Farallones de Cali, part of the range, rise just west of the city of Cali."],"q":{"q":"Which summit of this range is farthest from Earth’s centre?","a":"Chimborazo","x":["Cotopaxi","Huascarán"]}},"Q804330":{"t":"The White Range: the tropics’ greatest mass of ice","s":"Peru’s Cordillera Blanca is well named: a white wall of glaciers and fluted ice peaks only about nine degrees south of the equator, with more tropical glaciers than any other range on Earth. Its highest summit, Huascarán, reaches 6,768 m. Below, look for turquoise glacial lakes in the valleys and the green trench of the Santa River along its western foot.","w":"A line of brilliant white, fluted ice peaks with turquoise lakes in the valleys, running parallel to the Pacific coast.","f":["Huascarán, 6,768 m, is the highest mountain in Peru.","Huascarán National Park, which covers the range, is a UNESCO World Heritage Site.","The giant Puya raimondii, a relative of the pineapple, grows on its slopes; its flower spike can reach about 10 m."],"q":{"q":"Which is the highest mountain in Peru?","a":"Huascarán","x":["Alpamayo","Yerupajá"]}},"Q4794486":{"t":"The Andes’ eastern arm, reaching into Venezuela","s":"The easternmost of Colombia’s three Andean ranges carries Bogotá on its high plateau, then splits near the Venezuelan border. One arm, the Sierra de Perijá, heads north; the other, the Mérida Andes, runs north-east through Venezuela. Between them lies the vast blue sheet of Lake Maracaibo, so from above the mountains seem to hold it in a pincer.","w":"Rugged green-and-brown ridges; to the north-west, the wide blue expanse of Lake Maracaibo held between two mountain arms.","f":["Bogotá sits on a high plateau of this range at about 2,600 m.","Pico Bolívar, 4,978 m, in the Mérida Andes, is the highest peak in Venezuela.","Over the south-western shore of Lake Maracaibo, storms flash on more than a hundred nights a year — the Catatumbo lightning."],"q":{"q":"Which lake lies between the two arms of this cordillera?","a":"Lake Maracaibo","x":["Lake Titicaca","Lake Nicaragua"]}},"Q233770":{"t":"Australia’s wild northern wedge, pointing at New Guinea","s":"Cape York is one of the last great wild places in Australia: a long triangle of savanna, woodland and rainforest reaching north towards New Guinea, with few sealed roads. From above, look for red earth, braided rivers that flood in the wet season, and, off the east coast, the turquoise shallows of the Great Barrier Reef lying close to shore.","w":"Red earth and grey-green woodland cut by braided rivers, with the Great Barrier Reef’s turquoise shallows just off the east coast.","f":["The tip of Cape York is the northernmost point of mainland Australia.","The Torres Strait, about 150 km wide, separates the peninsula from New Guinea.","Towering termite mounds, some several metres tall, dot the savanna."],"q":{"q":"Which island lies across the Torres Strait from Cape York?","a":"New Guinea","x":["Timor","Tasmania"]}},"Q7835":{"t":"A diamond of steppe with a mountain rim on the Black Sea","s":"Crimea hangs from the mainland by a narrow neck, the Isthmus of Perekop, and from above its shape is unmistakable. Most of it is flat, dry steppe patterned with fields and shallow salt lakes; along the south coast the Crimean Mountains rise in a rim of limestone cliffs above the sea. To the north-east lie the shallow, sometimes pink-tinted lagoons of the Sivash.","w":"A diamond-shaped peninsula of flat steppe and salt lakes, with a mountain rim along the south coast and shallow lagoons to the north-east.","f":["The Isthmus of Perekop, which joins Crimea to the mainland, is only about 7 km wide at its narrowest.","Roman-Kosh, 1,545 m, is the highest peak of the Crimean Mountains.","The Sivash lagoons are so salty that in places their water turns pink with algae."],"q":{"q":"What is the narrow neck joining Crimea to the mainland called?","a":"The Isthmus of Perekop","x":["The Kerch Strait","The Tuzla Spit"]}},"Q165608":{"t":"Ancient rock, tundra and lakes beyond the Arctic Circle","s":"Almost the whole Kola Peninsula lies north of the Arctic Circle, yet thanks to the warm North Atlantic Current its northern coast stays ice-free all year. From the window it is a rolling mosaic of tundra, dark forest and countless lakes on some of the oldest rock in Europe, with the bare Khibiny Mountains rising in the middle. On winter nights, look for the northern lights.","w":"Low, rolling tundra and dark forest dotted with countless lakes, and the bare, flat-topped Khibiny massif near the centre.","f":["Murmansk, on the Kola coast, is the largest city north of the Arctic Circle.","The Kola Superdeep Borehole reached about 12.2 km — the deepest hole ever drilled.","The Khibiny Mountains are rich in apatite, which is mined to make fertiliser."],"q":{"q":"What record does the Kola Superdeep Borehole hold?","a":"The deepest hole ever drilled","x":["The coldest mine on Earth","The oldest tunnel in Europe"]}},"Q1394226":{"t":"Vietnam’s southern tip, still growing into the sea","s":"Mainland Vietnam ends here in a low, green wedge of mangroves and mudflats, with the sea on three sides. From cruise height it is a watery maze: winding channels, neat rectangles of shrimp ponds and dense dark-green forest. The land is still growing, as silt carried by the Mekong settles among the mangrove roots and pushes the shore outwards.","w":"A low green tip of land laced with channels, dark mangrove forest and rectangular shrimp ponds, surrounded by muddy brown sea.","f":["Cape Cà Mau is the southernmost point of mainland Vietnam.","Its mangroves are protected in Mũi Cà Mau National Park, a Ramsar wetland of international importance."],"q":{"q":"What is slowly pushing Cà Mau’s shore out to sea?","a":"Mekong silt trapped by mangroves","x":["Volcanic lava flows","Growing coral reefs"]}},"Q178543":{"t":"A 1,200-km desert finger between ocean and gulf","s":"Baja California runs about 1,200 km south from the US border, a slender desert finger between the Pacific and the Gulf of California. From 11 km up the contrast is striking: rugged, bare mountains down its spine, cactus-dotted plains, and on the gulf side water of an almost unreal blue. Grey whales come each winter to calve in its Pacific lagoons.","w":"A long, narrow desert peninsula: bare brown mountains, pale lagoons on the Pacific side and deep-blue gulf waters to the east.","f":["Its Pacific lagoons, such as Ojo de Liebre and San Ignacio, are among the grey whale’s main breeding grounds.","The Gulf of California is often called “the world’s aquarium”, a phrase attributed to Jacques Cousteau.","The gulf is slowly widening as the peninsula moves north-west with the Pacific plate."],"q":{"q":"Which whales come each winter to calve in Baja’s Pacific lagoons?","a":"Grey whales","x":["Humpback whales","Blue whales"]}},"Q130978":{"t":"A flat limestone slab with no rivers, only cenotes","s":"The Yucatán is as flat as a table: a vast slab of limestone covered in low green forest, with almost no rivers on the surface. Rain sinks straight through the rock into underground rivers, which surface in sinkholes called cenotes. At its northern tip lies the buried Chicxulub crater, left by the asteroid that ended the age of the dinosaurs 66 million years ago.","w":"Endless flat, low green forest with no hills or rivers; turquoise Caribbean coast to the east, cities like Mérida in the north.","f":["A curved ring of cenotes in the north of the peninsula traces the buried rim of the Chicxulub crater.","Calakmul, one of the greatest Maya cities, lies hidden in the forest near the peninsula’s southern base."],"q":{"q":"What are the Yucatán’s water-filled sinkholes called?","a":"Cenotes","x":["Tepuis","Arroyos"]}},"Q60064":{"t":"A volcanic arm reaching out towards the Aleutians","s":"The Alaska Peninsula stretches about 800 km south-west from the mainland, a rugged arm of volcanoes, lakes and tundra that leads into the Aleutian chain. From above, snowy cones rise one after another along its spine. In Katmai, in 1912, Novarupta produced the largest volcanic eruption of the 20th century, filling a valley with ash that steamed for years.","w":"A long, narrow arm of tundra and big lakes with a spine of snow-capped volcanoes, between the Pacific and Bristol Bay.","f":["The 1912 eruption created the Valley of Ten Thousand Smokes, named for its countless steaming vents.","Brooks Falls in Katmai National Park is famous for brown bears catching leaping salmon.","Aniakchak, a caldera about 10 km across, is one of the least-visited national monuments in the US."],"q":{"q":"Which valley was created by the 1912 Novarupta eruption?","a":"The Valley of Ten Thousand Smokes","x":["Death Valley","The Valley of Geysers"]}},"Q812":{"t":"A low limestone peninsula brimming with water","s":"Florida is one of the flattest places in the United States — its highest point is just 105 m above sea level — and from above it seems as much water as land. Countless round lakes dot the centre, Lake Okeechobee shines like a coin in the south, and below it the Everglades spread as a vast, slow-moving river of grass. Long sandy barrier islands line both coasts.","w":"Flat green land freckled with round lakes, the big disc of Lake Okeechobee, and thin white barrier islands along both coasts.","f":["Britton Hill, Florida’s highest point at 105 m, is the lowest state high point in the US.","The Everglades are a shallow sheet of water creeping south through sawgrass, often called a “river of grass”.","Many of central Florida’s round lakes are sinkholes in the limestone beneath."],"q":{"q":"What are the Everglades often called?","a":"A river of grass","x":["A sea of reeds","A forest on water"]}},"Q213237":{"t":"Patagonia’s axe-head peninsula, a nursery for whales","s":"On the map Península Valdés looks like an axe head attached to the coast of Patagonia by a thin handle, the Carlos Ameghino Isthmus. The gulfs on either side, San José and Nuevo, are sheltered nurseries where southern right whales gather to calve from about June to December. The land itself is flat, dry steppe, with white salt pans in the middle.","w":"A roughly square peninsula of pale brown steppe on a narrow neck, with sheltered gulfs on either side and white salt pans inside.","f":["Península Valdés is a UNESCO World Heritage Site for its marine mammals.","At Punta Norte, orcas deliberately beach themselves to snatch sea lion pups from the shore."],"q":{"q":"Which whales gather to calve in the gulfs of Valdés?","a":"Southern right whales","x":["Humpback whales","Sperm whales"]}},"Q858306":{"t":"China’s rocky thumb between the Bohai and the Yellow Sea","s":"The Shandong Peninsula juts east like a thumb between the Bohai Sea and the Yellow Sea, pointing towards Korea. From the window it is a patchwork of orchards and fields on rolling hills, with granite mountains near the coast and busy port cities strung along it. Qingdao sits on its southern shore beside the almost enclosed Jiaozhou Bay, crossed by one of the world’s longest sea bridges.","w":"A hilly peninsula of fields and orchards between two seas, with the rounded Jiaozhou Bay and Qingdao on the southern coast.","f":["Laoshan, a granite mountain near Qingdao, rises about 1,130 m straight from the sea and is famous for its Taoist temples.","Qingdao’s famous brewery was founded in 1903.","The Jiaozhou Bay Bridge runs for about 26 km over the water."],"q":{"q":"Which city lies on Jiaozhou Bay?","a":"Qingdao","x":["Dalian","Tianjin"]}},"Q131377":{"t":"Red dunes meeting a cold, foggy ocean","s":"The Namib may be the oldest desert on Earth, dry for tens of millions of years. In its central sand sea, around Sossusvlei, the dunes rise over 300 m and glow brick-red in low sun, lined up in rows that run almost to the Atlantic. The cold ocean current brings fog instead of rain, and beetles, lizards and plants survive by drinking it.","w":"Endless rows of red-orange dunes running towards the coast, pale clay pans between them, and a white line of surf and fog.","f":["The Namib Sand Sea is a UNESCO World Heritage Site.","In Deadvlei, a white clay pan among the dunes, stand camel thorn trees that died centuries ago.","Fog-basking beetles tilt their backs into the wind so that droplets roll down into their mouths."],"q":{"q":"Where does the Namib get most of its moisture?","a":"Fog from the cold Atlantic","x":["Summer monsoon rain","Melting mountain snow"]}},"Q14314106":{"t":"Egypt’s great desert of oases and white chalk towers","s":"West of the Nile, Egypt is almost all desert: flat sand and stone plains broken by deep depressions, where oases such as Siwa, Bahariya, Farafra, Dakhla and Kharga drink from ancient groundwater. From cruise height, look for green specks of fields and palm groves in these basins. Near Farafra lies the White Desert, where the wind has carved chalk into mushroom-shaped towers.","w":"Endless beige plains and escarpments, deep basins with green specks of oasis farms, and in places dazzling white chalk ground.","f":["The Qattara Depression in the north drops to about 133 m below sea level, one of the lowest points in Africa.","The oases draw on the Nubian Sandstone Aquifer, one of the largest stores of fossil groundwater on Earth."],"q":{"q":"What carved the White Desert’s mushroom-shaped rocks?","a":"The wind","x":["Glaciers","An ancient river"]}},"Q464909":{"t":"The Nile’s great bend through a stony desert","s":"East of the Nile in northern Sudan lies the Nubian Desert, an almost rainless plain of sandstone and granite crossed by dry wadis. From 11 km up, the most striking sight is the Nile itself: a thin green ribbon looping through the desert in a huge S-shaped bend. Along its banks stand the pyramids of Meroë and of Nubia’s other ancient kingdoms.","w":"A flat, pale stony plain with dark hills and dry wadis; to the west, the Nile’s green ribbon winding through a great bend.","f":["Sudan has more pyramids than Egypt, many of them at Meroë.","The railway from Wadi Halfa to Abu Hamad, built in the late 1890s, runs almost straight across the desert."],"q":{"q":"Which ancient Nubian city is famous for its steep pyramids?","a":"Meroë","x":["Aksum","Carthage"]}},"Q326896":{"t":"Endless parallel red dunes in Australia’s north-west","s":"From the air the Great Sandy Desert looks combed: long, straight red dunes run side by side for hundreds of kilometres, separated by corridors of pale spinifex grass. It is one of the emptiest parts of Australia, home mainly to Aboriginal communities. Near its northern edge lies Wolfe Creek Crater, a near-perfect ring about 900 m across.","w":"Long, straight, parallel red dune ridges with pale grey-green spinifex between them, and the occasional white salt lake.","f":["Wolfe Creek Crater is one of the best-preserved meteorite craters on Earth.","Australia’s dunes line up with the prevailing winds, forming a giant swirl around the centre of the continent."],"q":{"q":"What made Wolfe Creek Crater?","a":"A meteorite impact","x":["A volcanic eruption","A collapsed cave"]}},"Q145165":{"t":"Australia’s largest desert, red sand under blue-green scrub","s":"Australia’s largest desert covers some 350,000 km² — more than Italy. Yet it is far from bare: from above, its red dunes and sandhills are speckled with grey-green spinifex, mulga and mallee eucalypts. Dazzling white salt lakes mark its edges, and to the south the land flattens into the treeless Nullarbor Plain.","w":"Red sand ridges speckled with grey-green scrub, scattered white salt lakes, and flat, treeless plain to the south.","f":["The explorer Ernest Giles named it in 1875 after Queen Victoria.","The Anangu Pitjantjatjara people have lived in and around this desert for many thousands of years."],"q":{"q":"Which is Australia’s largest desert?","a":"The Great Victoria Desert","x":["The Simpson Desert","The Tanami Desert"]}},"Q172691":{"t":"India’s Great Desert, surprisingly full of people","s":"The Thar, or Great Indian Desert, spreads across Rajasthan and into Pakistan, a land of wind-rippled dunes, thorn scrub and scattered villages. It is one of the most densely populated deserts in the world. Look for the long green line of the Indira Gandhi Canal cutting through the sand, bringing Himalayan river water to new fields.","w":"Tawny dunes and scrub dotted with villages, and a straight green corridor of canal and fields cutting across the sand.","f":["Jaisalmer, the “Golden City”, is built of yellow sandstone and rises from the desert around a hilltop fort.","The Indira Gandhi Canal, one of the longest in India, carries water from the Sutlej and Beas rivers into the desert."],"q":{"q":"Which city in the Thar is known as the Golden City?","a":"Jaisalmer","x":["Jaipur","Udaipur"]}},"Q339162":{"t":"A vast stony plain between the Levant and the Euphrates","s":"Between the green coast of the Levant and the Euphrates valley stretches the Syrian Desert, a vast plain of gravel and basalt rather than sand. From above it is flat and beige, crossed by straight roads, with dark lava fields in the south-west. For millennia caravans crossed it between the Mediterranean and Mesopotamia, stopping at the oasis of Palmyra.","w":"A flat, featureless beige plain with straight roads and dry wadis, and dark patches of basalt lava fields in the south-west.","f":["The Harrat al-Sham basalt field in the south-west is one of the largest volcanic fields in the Middle East.","Ancient “desert kites” — huge stone-walled traps for hunting gazelles — were first noticed by pilots in the 1920s."],"q":{"q":"Which ancient caravan city grew in an oasis of the Syrian Desert?","a":"Palmyra","x":["Petra","Byblos"]}},"Q172702":{"t":"The Black Sands, and a crater burning for decades","s":"Karakum means “black sands”, though from above the dunes look tawny-grey, pinned down by saxaul and grass. The desert covers most of Turkmenistan. Near Derweze a gas crater about 70 m wide has been burning for decades, and at night its orange glow is a startling sight. Along the south runs the long green thread of the Karakum Canal.","w":"Grey-tawny dunes pinned by scrub, the green Karakum Canal to the south, and at night a small orange glow near Derweze.","f":["The Karakum Canal, over 1,000 km long, carries Amu Darya water across the desert — one of the world’s longest irrigation canals.","The Karakum covers about 70% of Turkmenistan."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Karakum mean?","a":"Black sands","x":["Red sands","Dry river"]}},"Q184364":{"t":"The Red Sands between Central Asia’s two great rivers","s":"Kyzylkum means “red sands”, and the desert fills the land between the Amu Darya and the Syr Darya, the two rivers that once fed the Aral Sea. From the window it is a reddish-beige plain of dunes and scrub, broken by bare rocky hills and salt flats. Here too lies Muruntau, one of the largest open-pit gold mines on Earth — a giant grey hole plain to see from the air.","w":"Reddish-beige dunes and scrub, bare rocky hills and white salt flats, and in places the huge grey stepped pit of the Muruntau mine.","f":["The Muruntau gold mine’s open pit is more than 3 km long and hundreds of metres deep.","Since the 1960s the Aral Sea to the north-west has shrunk dramatically, as river water was taken to irrigate fields."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Kyzylkum mean?","a":"Red sands","x":["Black sands","Hungry steppe"]}},"Q47141":{"t":"The driest place outside the poles, under crystal skies","s":"Some weather stations in the Atacama have never recorded rain. From cruise height it is a moonscape of salt flats, bare brown ranges and pastel-coloured volcanoes, with the Andes on one side and the cold Pacific on the other. The air is so dry and clear that some of the world’s largest telescopes have been built on its peaks.","w":"Bare brown and ochre ranges, white salt flats such as the Salar de Atacama, and snow-capped volcanoes along the eastern skyline.","f":["The Salar de Atacama is Chile’s largest salt flat and one of the world’s main sources of lithium.","NASA has tested Mars rovers in the Atacama because its soil is so much like that of Mars.","ALMA, a giant array of radio telescopes, stands on a plateau at about 5,000 m."],"q":{"q":"Which metal is extracted in large amounts from the Salar de Atacama?","a":"Lithium","x":["Tin","Silver"]}},"Q392250":{"t":"Mexico’s narrow waist, where winds rush between oceans","s":"Here Mexico narrows to its waist: only about 200 km of land separate the Atlantic from the Pacific. The mountains dip low into a wide gap, and in winter strong northerly winds, the Tehuanos, funnel through it and burst out over the Pacific, whipping up the sea for hundreds of kilometres offshore. Near the Pacific coast, wide, shallow lagoons shine silver.","w":"A narrow land bridge with low hills in the middle, green lowlands to the north and dry plains and lagoons along the Pacific.","f":["The Tehuano winds can blow at more than 100 km/h and are felt far out over the Pacific.","A railway has linked the two coasts across the isthmus since 1907.","The isthmus is often taken as the geographical boundary between North and Central America."],"q":{"q":"About how wide is the Isthmus of Tehuantepec?","a":"About 200 km","x":["About 50 km","About 600 km"]}},"Q182139":{"t":"The land bridge that joined two continents","s":"This slender, S-shaped strip of land joins two continents and parts two oceans. When it rose from the sea about 3 million years ago, animals crossed between the Americas and ocean currents changed course. In the west the Panama Canal cuts through it; in the east the rainforest of the Darién is so wild that the Pan-American Highway stops at its edge.","w":"A narrow, winding strip of green between two seas: unbroken rainforest in the east, the canal and Gatún Lake in the west.","f":["The Panama Canal, opened in 1914, is about 80 km long.","The Darién Gap is the only break in the Pan-American Highway between Alaska and the far south of South America.","Because the isthmus curves, the canal runs from the Atlantic in the north-west to the Pacific in the south-east."],"q":{"q":"In which year did the Panama Canal open?","a":"1914","x":["1869","1936"]}},"Q322356":{"t":"The Malay Peninsula’s narrowest neck, between two seas","s":"At the Kra Isthmus the Malay Peninsula pinches to its narrowest, about 44 km wide, with the Andaman Sea to the west and the Gulf of Thailand to the east. From 11 km up you may see both coasts at once: mangrove-fringed estuaries on one side, long sandy beaches on the other, and rubber and oil palm plantations in between. For centuries people have dreamed of cutting a canal here.","w":"A narrow neck of green hills and plantations with the sea visible on both sides, and the Kra River estuary to the west.","f":["A canal across the Kra has been proposed since at least the 17th century, but it has never been built.","At the narrowest point the Kraburi River forms part of the border between Thailand and Myanmar."],"q":{"q":"Roughly how wide is the Kra Isthmus at its narrowest?","a":"About 44 km","x":["About 4 km","About 140 km"]}},"Q426977":{"t":"Red-clay hills between the Appalachians and the coast","s":"Between the Blue Ridge and the flat coastal plain lies the Piedmont — literally the “foot of the mountains” — a rolling upland of red clay, pine woods and farms. From above, look for big reservoirs with branching, finger-like arms, such as Lake Hartwell and Lake Thurmond on the Savannah River. At its seaward edge rivers tumble over the Fall Line, where cities like Richmond, Columbia and Augusta grew up.","w":"Rolling green hills and woods with reddish fields, and large reservoirs with long, branching arms along the rivers.","f":["The Fall Line cities grew where river boats could go no further upstream.","The Piedmont’s red colour comes from iron-rich clay soils.","Atlanta and Charlotte, two of the largest cities in the American South, stand on the Piedmont."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Piedmont mean?","a":"Foot of the mountains","x":["Land of red clay","Between the rivers"]}},"Q743261":{"t":"Cloud forest tumbling from the Andes to the Amazon","s":"On the eastern flank of the Bolivian Andes the land drops from icy ridges to steamy lowlands within a few dozen kilometres, and the Yungas fill that slope with cloud forest. From the window, look for a wall of dark green veiled in cloud, cut by deep, winding valleys where rivers rush down towards the Amazon basin. Coffee, coca and citrus grow on its terraces.","w":"Steep, dark-green slopes veiled in cloud, dropping from the snowy Andes to the flat Amazon lowlands and cut by deep valleys.","f":["The Yungas descend more than 3,000 m, from the high Andes to the Amazon lowlands.","Their cloud forests are among the richest on Earth in species, especially orchids and birds."],"q":{"q":"Where do the Yungas lie?","a":"On the eastern slopes of the Andes","x":["On the Pacific coast","On the high Altiplano"]}},"Q206650":{"t":"New Zealand’s glacier-draped spine along a great fault","s":"The Southern Alps run the length of New Zealand’s South Island, rising abruptly along the Alpine Fault, where two tectonic plates meet. Westerly winds dump huge amounts of rain and snow on them, so glaciers reach down almost into the rainforest of the west coast, while the east lies in a dry rain shadow. On the eastern side, look for lakes of an unreal milky turquoise, such as Pukaki and Tekapo.","w":"A snowy, glacier-streaked spine with rainforest to the west, and tawny plains and turquoise lakes to the east.","f":["Aoraki / Mount Cook, 3,724 m, is New Zealand’s highest peak.","The Franz Josef and Fox glaciers descend to within a few hundred metres of sea level, into temperate rainforest.","The Tasman Glacier, about 23 km long, is the longest in New Zealand."],"q":{"q":"Which is New Zealand’s highest peak?","a":"Aoraki / Mount Cook","x":["Mount Aspiring / Tititea","Mount Tasman"]}},"ne-rg-1730071499":{"t":"Remote sandstone hills above Central Africa’s savannas","s":"In the far north-east of the Central African Republic, a rugged sandstone massif rises above the savanna — the Bongo Massif, also called the Chaîne des Mongos. It is one of the remotest places in Africa, with no towns and hardly any roads. From cruise height, look for dark, wooded escarpments over a pale-green sea of grass, threaded with darker lines of forest along the rivers.","w":"Dark, wooded sandstone escarpments rising from pale savanna, with thin dark lines of gallery forest along the rivers.","f":["North of the massif lies Manovo-Gounda St Floris, the largest national park in Central Africa’s savannas and a UNESCO site.","More than 300 bird species have been recorded on the park’s northern floodplains."],"q":{"q":"Which UNESCO national park stretches north of the Bongo Massif?","a":"Manovo-Gounda St Floris","x":["Dzanga-Sangha","Garamba"]}},"Q2961771":{"t":"Hidden valleys where a million people lived unseen","s":"A great mountain spine runs the length of New Guinea, and hidden among its ridges lie wide, fertile valleys. When outsiders first flew over them and trekked in during the 1930s, they found around a million people farming there, unknown to the wider world. From 11 km up, look for patchworks of gardens on the valley floors, framed by ridges of cloud forest.","w":"Steep, cloud-wrapped forested ridges with broad green valleys below, their floors patterned with small garden plots.","f":["Kuk Swamp in these highlands is one of the world’s earliest centres of farming and a UNESCO World Heritage Site.","Mount Wilhelm, 4,509 m, is the highest peak in Papua New Guinea.","Puncak Jaya, 4,884 m, in the range’s western part, is the highest island peak on Earth and still holds small equatorial glaciers."],"q":{"q":"When did outsiders first reach the populated highland valleys?","a":"In the 1930s","x":["In the 1780s","In the 1960s"]}},"Q189915":{"t":"Limestone mountains that gave the world the word “karst”","s":"Running parallel to the Adriatic from Slovenia to Albania, the Dinaric Alps are built of pale grey limestone, and from above they look bleached and pitted. Rain vanishes into the rock, leaving few surface rivers but countless sinkholes, caves and flat-floored basins called poljes. The word “karst”, used by geologists worldwide, comes from the Kras plateau at their northern end.","w":"Pale grey, pitted limestone ridges with green flat-floored basins, deep canyons, and the island-fringed Adriatic coast to the south-west.","f":["The Tara River Canyon in Montenegro is about 1,300 m deep, one of the deepest in Europe.","The Plitvice Lakes, a chain of turquoise lakes linked by waterfalls, formed behind travertine dams in these mountains."],"q":{"q":"Which geological term comes from a plateau in the Dinaric Alps?","a":"Karst","x":["Fjord","Tundra"]}},"ne-rg-1730072787":{"t":"A sandstone rampart south of the Hoggar mountains","s":"South of the dark volcanic Ahaggar lies a very different landscape: the Tassili Oua-n-Ahaggar, a broad sandstone plateau that the wind has carved into towers, arches and narrow corridors. From above it looks like a crumbling brown fortress wall, half drowned in drifting orange sand. It runs from southern Algeria towards the border with Niger, through one of the emptiest stretches of the Sahara.","w":"A broad brown plateau broken into blocks and towers by sand-filled corridors, with orange dunes lapping at its edges.","f":["In the Tuareg language, “tassili” means “plateau”.","The plateau curves around the southern side of the Ahaggar massif."],"q":{"q":"What rock is this plateau mostly made of?","a":"Sandstone","x":["Basalt","Granite"]}},"Q190048":{"t":"A stone forest holding the Sahara’s greatest rock art","s":"Near the Libyan border, the Tassili n’Ajjer rises as a vast sandstone plateau eroded into a “forest” of pillars, arches and canyons. Its rock shelters hold more than 15,000 paintings and engravings — giraffes, hippos, herds of cattle, dancers — recording thousands of years of a greener Sahara. Look for a dark, ragged plateau edge with orange dunes piled at its foot.","w":"A dark, deeply eroded plateau cut into a maze of pillars and canyons, with orange dunes piled against its edges.","f":["Tassili n’Ajjer has been a UNESCO World Heritage Site since 1982.","Its name means “plateau of rivers” in the Tuareg language.","A few hundred ancient Saharan cypresses survive in its canyons; some are thought to be about 2,000 years old."],"q":{"q":"How many ancient paintings and engravings does Tassili n’Ajjer hold?","a":"More than 15,000","x":["About 150","About 1,500"]}},"ne-rg-1730072795":{"t":"A sea of sand dunes in the empty heart of Libya","s":"In eastern Libya, the Calanshio Sand Sea spreads long ridges of pale sand across a land with almost no rain and no permanent settlements. From the window, the dunes line up in parallel rows running roughly north to south, shaped by the prevailing wind, with flat gravel plains — sarirs — along their edges. Beneath this emptiness lie major oilfields and vast stores of ancient groundwater.","w":"Long parallel ridges of pale sand running north–south, flat grey gravel plains along the edges, and almost no sign of life.","f":["Water pumped from beneath this desert flows north to Libya’s coast through the Great Man-Made River, a network of giant pipes.","The Sarir oilfield, one of Libya’s largest, lies on the edge of the sand sea."],"q":{"q":"What are the flat gravel plains around the Calanshio dunes called?","a":"Sarirs","x":["Ergs","Wadis"]}},"Q3056157":{"t":"A remote dune sea, and a meteorite older than Earth","s":"Erg Chech is one of the loneliest corners of the Sahara: a great sea of dunes on the border of Algeria and Mali, far from any road or oasis. From cruise height, look for long, orderly dune ridges broken by darker gravel plains and rocky outcrops. In 2020 a meteorite found here, Erg Chech 002, turned out to be one of the oldest volcanic rocks ever studied — about 4.56 billion years old.","w":"Long, orderly orange dune ridges alternating with darker gravel plains and rocky outcrops, with no roads or oases in sight.","f":["Erg Chech 002 is a piece of volcanic crust from an early protoplanet that formed before Earth itself.","Hot deserts are among the best places to find meteorites: dark stones stand out on pale ground and weather slowly."],"q":{"q":"What was found in Erg Chech in 2020?","a":"A meteorite older than Earth","x":["A dinosaur skeleton","A cave full of rock art"]}},"Q84127":{"t":"Treeless tundra with a perfectly round crater lake","s":"At the northern tip of Quebec, the Ungava Peninsula is a vast, treeless tundra scraped bare by ice sheets, scattered with thousands of lakes and cut by rivers flowing to Hudson Bay and Ungava Bay. Near its northern end lies one of Canada’s most striking sights: Pingualuit Crater, a perfectly round lake about 3.4 km wide, punched out by a meteorite 1.4 million years ago.","w":"Bare, rolling tundra with countless lakes and rocky ridges; in the north, a single perfectly circular crater lake.","f":["The lake in Pingualuit Crater holds some of the clearest fresh water in the world.","Nunavik, the Inuit homeland that covers the peninsula, has no road link to southern Quebec."],"q":{"q":"About how old is Pingualuit Crater?","a":"About 1.4 million years","x":["About 14,000 years","About 140 million years"]}},"Q1898305":{"t":"The last ridges before the Sahara begins","s":"Across Algeria runs the Saharan Atlas, a series of parallel ridges — the Ksour, Djebel Amour and Ouled Naïl mountains — that marks the northern edge of the Sahara. North of them lie the High Plateaus with their shallow salt lakes, the chotts; south of them the desert begins. From 11 km up, look for folded, rust-coloured ridges running south-west to north-east, with stony plains stretching beyond.","w":"Parallel folded ridges running south-west to north-east, pale salt lakes to the north and open desert to the south.","f":["Oasis towns such as Laghouat grew at the range’s southern foot, where water flows out of the mountains.","The Chott ech Chergui, north of the range, is one of the largest salt lakes in North Africa."],"q":{"q":"What are the shallow salt lakes north of the Saharan Atlas called?","a":"Chotts","x":["Oases","Wadis"]}},"ne-rg-1730073347":{"t":"The western wall of Africa’s great rift lakes","s":"Along the eastern edge of the Congo, the Mitumba Mountains rise as the western shoulder of the Albertine Rift, a great crack in Africa’s crust. From above they are a long, forested wall dropping steeply to a string of rift lakes — Albert, Edward, Kivu and Tanganyika. Near their southern end, look for the long, dark-blue trough of Lake Tanganyika below the ridges.","w":"A long, steep forested escarpment with the deep blue of Lake Tanganyika or Lake Kivu in the rift valley below.","f":["Lake Tanganyika, at the foot of the range, is the second-deepest lake in the world, about 1,470 m.","Lake Tanganyika is also the longest freshwater lake on Earth, about 670 km."],"q":{"q":"Which deep rift lake lies below the southern Mitumba Mountains?","a":"Lake Tanganyika","x":["Lake Malawi","Lake Victoria"]}},"ne-rg-1730073365":{"t":"Red-earth plateaus that fill the world’s coffee cups","s":"Behind Vietnam’s coast, the land climbs onto the Central Highlands, a series of basalt plateaus with rich red soil. From the window, look for a green-and-rust patchwork: rows of coffee bushes, pepper and rubber plantations, and scattered volcanic cones and lakes. Most of Vietnam’s coffee grows here, helping make the country the world’s second-largest coffee producer.","w":"Rolling red-earth plateaus patterned with green plantations, a few rounded volcanic cones, and forested mountains to the east.","f":["Buôn Ma Thuột is often called Vietnam’s coffee capital.","Most of the coffee grown here is robusta, a hardier species with a stronger taste than arabica."],"q":{"q":"Which crop has made Vietnam’s Central Highlands famous?","a":"Coffee","x":["Tea","Cocoa"]}},"ne-rg-1730073373":{"t":"Siberia’s wild ridges between the Yenisei and Baikal","s":"The Eastern Sayan stretch about 1,000 km from the Yenisei towards Lake Baikal, a wilderness of sharp, bare peaks, deep valleys and taiga. From cruise height you see ridge after ridge streaked with snow for much of the year, and rivers such as the Kan and the Oka running out towards the Yenisei and the Angara. The highest peak, Munku-Sardyk, stands on the border with Mongolia.","w":"Ridge after ridge of sharp, snow-streaked peaks with dark taiga in the valleys, stretching south-east towards Baikal.","f":["Munku-Sardyk, 3,491 m, is the highest peak of the Eastern Sayan.","The Tofalars, one of Russia’s smallest peoples, live in the remote northern valleys of the range.","In the Valley of Volcanoes on the Oka plateau, young lava fields and cones stand among the taiga."],"q":{"q":"Which is the highest peak of the Eastern Sayan?","a":"Munku-Sardyk","x":["Belukha","Pobeda Peak"]}},"Q201678":{"t":"Where Siberia’s rivers part for two oceans","s":"The Yablonovy Range runs some 650 km across Transbaikalia, a line of rounded, forested ridges that hardly looks like a great barrier. Yet it is one: rain on its western slopes flows through the Selenga and Baikal towards the Arctic Ocean, while rain on the east ends up in the Amur and the Pacific. The city of Chita lies at its eastern foot.","w":"Low, rounded, dark-forested ridges running north-east, wide valleys, and the city of Chita at the south-eastern foot.","f":["The Trans-Siberian Railway crosses the range at the Yablonovy Pass, just west of Chita.","The name sounds like the Russian word for “apple”, but its real origin is disputed."],"q":{"q":"Which city lies at the eastern foot of the Yablonovy Range?","a":"Chita","x":["Irkutsk","Ulan-Ude"]}},"ne-rg-1730073417":{"t":"Dark mountains and lava fields along the Red Sea","s":"Along Arabia’s Red Sea coast, the Hejaz Mountains rise steeply from a narrow coastal plain and slope gently east into the interior. From 11 km up they are a jumble of dark granite and brown sandstone, with great black lava fields — harrats — spread across the plateau behind them. Near Al-Ula, look for warm-coloured sandstone cliffs and valleys where the Nabataeans carved their tombs.","w":"Steep dark mountains above a thin coastal strip, black lava fields on the plateau behind, and orange sandstone around Al-Ula.","f":["Hegra, near Al-Ula, has more than 100 Nabataean tombs cut into sandstone and was Saudi Arabia’s first UNESCO site.","The Hejaz Railway, opened in 1908, ran through these mountains from Damascus to Medina."],"q":{"q":"What are Arabia’s black lava fields called?","a":"Harrats","x":["Sabkhas","Ergs"]}},"Q730673":{"t":"Arabia’s green, misty highlands above the Red Sea","s":"Surprisingly for Arabia, the Asir is green. These mountains in the south-west of the peninsula rise to about 3,000 m and catch monsoon rain and mist from the Red Sea, so their slopes carry juniper forest and ancient farming terraces. From above, look for a dark, rugged escarpment dropping sharply to the hot coastal plain of the Tihamah, with desert stretching away on the other side.","w":"A dark, rugged escarpment with a green tinge and terraced slopes, dropping steeply west to a hot coastal plain.","f":["Jabal Sawda, near Abha, is about 3,000 m high and usually counted as Saudi Arabia’s highest peak.","Al-Qatt Al-Asiri, the bright geometric wall painting done by Asiri women, is on UNESCO’s intangible heritage list."],"q":{"q":"What makes the Asir Mountains unusually green for Arabia?","a":"Monsoon rain and mist","x":["Meltwater from glaciers","Water piped from the Nile"]}},"Q484725":{"t":"A wall between the green Caspian and Iran’s dry plateau","s":"The Alborz form a sharp wall between two worlds: on the north, slopes of dense green Hyrcanian forest drop to the Caspian Sea; on the south, bare brown ridges face the dry Iranian plateau and the sprawl of Tehran. Rising above them all is Damavand, a lone, near-perfect volcanic cone about 5,600 m high, often white with snow even in summer.","w":"A lone, snow-capped volcanic cone above a long ridge, green forest on the Caspian side and bare brown slopes towards Tehran.","f":["Damavand is the highest peak in Iran and the highest volcano in Asia.","The Hyrcanian forests on the northern slopes, relics of woodland millions of years old, are a UNESCO World Heritage Site.","Tehran spreads along the southern foot of the Alborz, its northern districts climbing the slopes."],"q":{"q":"Which lone volcanic cone is the highest peak in Iran?","a":"Damavand","x":["Sabalan","Taftan"]}},"Q143014":{"t":"Where the Andes step down towards the Amazon and Chaco","s":"The Eastern Cordillera is the Andes’ eastern flank, where the mountains begin their long descent to the lowlands. In Bolivia it is a maze of red, ochre and green ridges cut by deep valleys, with the white colonial city of Sucre set among them at about 2,800 m. Further east the ridges soften into forested foothills and then the flat plains of the Chaco and the Amazon.","w":"Folded red, ochre and green ridges with deep valleys, fading eastwards into forested foothills and flat lowland plains.","f":["Near Sucre, a quarry wall called Cal Orck’o preserves thousands of dinosaur footprints, tilted almost upright.","Sucre, Bolivia’s constitutional capital, is known as the White City for its whitewashed colonial buildings."],"q":{"q":"Which Bolivian city in the Eastern Cordillera is called the White City?","a":"Sucre","x":["Potosí","Cochabamba"]}},"ne-rg-1730073543":{"t":"Aboriginal stone country at the top of Australia","s":"Arnhem Land, in the north-east of Australia’s Northern Territory, is one of the country’s largest Aboriginal-owned regions, and visitors need a permit to enter. From the window you see a rugged sandstone plateau — the “stone country” — cut by gorges, then wide floodplains and mangrove-lined rivers that turn into shining sheets of water in the wet season. Its rock art is among the oldest and richest on Earth.","w":"A rugged, gorge-cut sandstone plateau, wide green floodplains and winding, mangrove-lined rivers flowing north.","f":["The Yolŋu bark petitions, sent to Australia’s parliament in 1963, were a milestone in the Aboriginal land rights movement.","Arnhem Land’s rock art includes “X-ray” paintings that show the bones and organs of animals."],"q":{"q":"What do Arnhem Land’s famous “X-ray” rock paintings show?","a":"The bones and organs of animals","x":["Maps of the stars","Ocean currents"]}},"Q5513":{"t":"The deepest lake on Earth, a blue crescent in Siberia","s":"Look for a long blue crescent pressed between dark mountain ridges — that's Baikal, more than 600 km from end to end. Its deepest point is 1,642 m down, deeper than any other lake on Earth. In late winter the ice turns glassy and pale turquoise, split by cracks that run for kilometres.","w":"A narrow crescent of deep blue, or white ice in winter, running south-west to north-east between mountains.","f":["Baikal holds roughly a fifth of all the unfrozen fresh water on Earth's surface.","More than 300 rivers flow into it, but only one — the Angara — flows out.","It is home to the nerpa, a seal that lives nowhere else in the world."],"q":{"q":"Which river is the only one flowing out of Lake Baikal?","a":"The Angara","x":["The Selenga","The Yenisei"]}},"Q23883":{"t":"The lowest water on Earth, and it keeps sinking","s":"The shore of the Dead Sea is the lowest dry land on the planet, more than 430 m below sea level — and the water drops by roughly a metre a year. It is so salty that people float without trying. From above, the deep-blue northern basin gives way to pale geometric salt pans in the south.","w":"A slim blue lake in a deep desert trough east of Jerusalem, with turquoise and white salt ponds at its southern end.","f":["Its water is roughly ten times saltier than the ocean — too salty for fish.","The Jordan River is its main source, and nothing flows out: water leaves only by evaporation.","The southern basin is now mostly a patchwork of evaporation ponds for potash and salt."],"q":{"q":"Roughly how far below sea level is the Dead Sea's shore?","a":"More than 400 m","x":["About 100 m","About 250 m"]}},"Q788":{"t":"A frozen ocean where the ice itself drifts","s":"From a polar flight the Arctic Ocean looks like a cracked white plate: pack ice broken by dark lanes of open water called leads. It is the smallest and shallowest ocean, yet under the North Pole the sea is more than 4 km deep. In summer, pools of meltwater dot the ice with blue.","w":"Endless white pack ice laced with dark cracks; in summer, turquoise meltwater ponds and patches of open sea.","f":["There is no land at the North Pole — only drifting sea ice a few metres thick.","In 1893–1896 Nansen's ship Fram was deliberately frozen in and drifted with the ice across the Arctic.","Near the pole the sun stays up for about six months, then stays below the horizon for six."],"q":{"q":"What lies under the ice at the North Pole?","a":"Ocean more than 4 km deep","x":["A rocky island","A shallow shelf sea"]}},"Q7354":{"t":"The ocean that runs all the way around the world","s":"Few flights ever cross it, which makes this view rare. The Southern Ocean rings Antarctica with no land in its way, so its current circles the globe endlessly from west to east. Look for flat-topped icebergs — some are tens of kilometres long — and, in winter, a white apron of sea ice larger than the continent itself.","w":"Dark, wind-streaked water with scattered white icebergs; farther south, a solid rim of sea ice around Antarctica.","f":["The Antarctic Circumpolar Current carries more water than any other current on Earth.","Sailors named its stormy latitudes the Roaring Forties, the Furious Fifties and the Screaming Sixties.","Its northern boundary is usually drawn at 60° South."],"q":{"q":"In which direction does the Antarctic Circumpolar Current flow?","a":"West to east","x":["East to west","South to north"]}},"Q98":{"t":"Bigger than all the world's land put together","s":"Hours of blue with barely a speck of land: this is the Pacific, covering about a third of the planet. Its deepest point, the Challenger Deep, lies nearly 11 km down — roughly as far below the surface as you are above it. Magellan called it the “peaceful sea” after the calm water he met in 1520.","w":"Deep navy water to every horizon, broken only by rows of cloud and, now and then, the pale ring of an atoll.","f":["The Pacific is larger than all the land on Earth combined.","Volcanoes and earthquake zones rim much of it — the so-called Ring of Fire.","Across the tropics it stretches nearly halfway around the globe."],"q":{"q":"Who named the Pacific after its calm, “peaceful” water?","a":"Ferdinand Magellan","x":["James Cook","Vasco da Gama"]}},"Q1239":{"t":"The warm ocean that powers the monsoon","s":"Sun glint on the Indian Ocean can blaze like hammered metal for minutes on end. This is the warmest of the oceans, and it is closed off in the north by Asia rather than open to the Arctic. That shape drives the monsoon: each summer the heating continent pulls in moist ocean air, bringing the rains to South Asia.","w":"Open blue ocean with a bright path of sun glint; in the tropics, rows of puffy cumulus over the water.","f":["It covers about a fifth of the world's ocean area.","The Maldives, in its north, are some 1,200 small coral islands grouped into 26 atolls.","It is the only ocean named after a country — India."],"q":{"q":"How does the Indian Ocean rank in size among the oceans?","a":"Third largest","x":["Second largest","Fourth largest"]}},"Q97":{"t":"An ocean still growing, a few centimetres a year","s":"Down the middle of the Atlantic, hidden under kilometres of water, runs the Mid-Atlantic Ridge — a volcanic seam where the sea floor is pulling apart. The ocean grows wider by a few centimetres every year, roughly as fast as your fingernails. At the surface, the sun's glint picks out the long, even lines of swell.","w":"Open ocean ribbed with long swell, under drifting cloud fields; the sun's reflection shows the texture of the waves.","f":["Iceland and the Azores are parts of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge that rise above the waves.","The North Atlantic is the busiest stretch of oceanic airspace in the world.","Its name comes from Atlas, the Titan of Greek myth who held up the sky."],"q":{"q":"Which island sits right on the Mid-Atlantic Ridge?","a":"Iceland","x":["Ireland","Madeira"]}},"Q4918":{"t":"The sea in the middle of the land","s":"Its famous deep blue comes from poverty, not richness: the water carries few nutrients and little plankton, so sunlight sinks deep. The Mediterranean joins the Atlantic only through the 14-km gap at Gibraltar. About six million years ago its link to the ocean closed and the sea almost dried out, leaving thick salt layers still buried under its floor.","w":"Intense cobalt-blue water between Europe and Africa, often with a sharp silver sun glint and scattered rocky islands.","f":["Its name comes from the Latin for “in the middle of the land”.","Tides across most of it are small, usually well under half a metre.","More water evaporates than rivers bring in, so it is saltier than the open Atlantic."],"q":{"q":"About how wide is the Strait of Gibraltar at its narrowest?","a":"About 14 km","x":["About 3 km","About 50 km"]}},"Q5484":{"t":"A “sea” that is really the world's largest lake","s":"Call it a sea, but it is the largest body of water on Earth with no outlet to the ocean — bigger than Germany. Its two ends are nothing alike: the shallow north, fed by the Volga, often freezes in winter, while the south plunges past 1,000 m. On the eastern shore look for Kara-Bogaz-Gol, a vivid turquoise lagoon.","w":"A huge inland sea with a pale, silty north and deep blue south; on its east side, the round turquoise Kara-Bogaz-Gol.","f":["Its surface lies about 28 m below the level of the world ocean.","The Volga brings in roughly four-fifths of all the river water the Caspian receives.","For centuries it has been the world's main source of sturgeon and black caviar."],"q":{"q":"Which river supplies most of the Caspian Sea's water?","a":"The Volga","x":["The Ural","The Kura"]}},"Q166":{"t":"A turquoise surface over an oxygen-free deep","s":"In late spring the Black Sea can turn milky turquoise, as blooms of chalk-plated plankton swirl across it. The depths are another world: below about 200 m there is no oxygen at all, and ancient wooden ships lie there almost untouched. It reaches the Mediterranean only through the narrow Bosphorus and Dardanelles.","w":"A broad oval sea between Turkey, the Balkans and the Caucasus; in early summer, pale turquoise swirls on dark water.","f":["Nearly 90% of its water holds no oxygen, only dissolved hydrogen sulphide.","In 2018 an intact Greek trading ship about 2,400 years old was found on its floor.","The Danube reaches it through a vast delta, one of Europe's largest wetlands."],"q":{"q":"What is missing from the Black Sea's deep water?","a":"Oxygen","x":["Salt","Nitrogen"]}},"Q159183":{"t":"A warm, deep nursery for typhoons","s":"This is some of the warmest open water on the planet, and it has a stormy talent: many of the Pacific's fiercest typhoons are born here. The sea is fenced by island chains — the Philippines, Taiwan, the Ryukyus and Japan to the west and north, the Marianas to the east. Far below the surface, trenches cut more than 10 km deep.","w":"Deep indigo ocean dotted with towering cumulus; island arcs mark its edges — Luzon and Taiwan to the west, the Marianas to the east.","f":["The warm Kuroshio current sets off along its western edge and runs north past Japan.","The Philippine Trench, along its western side, drops to more than 10 km.","The sea sits on a tectonic plate of its own, the Philippine Sea Plate."],"q":{"q":"Which warm current runs north along the Philippine Sea's western edge?","a":"The Kuroshio","x":["The Oyashio","The Humboldt Current"]}},"Q82931":{"t":"Home of the Great Barrier Reef","s":"The Coral Sea earns its name on its western side, where the Great Barrier Reef runs for about 2,300 km along Australia's coast. From above, the reefs look like turquoise brushstrokes and pale rings scattered on deep blue, with white surf on their seaward edges. Farther east the water deepens to a dark indigo.","w":"Turquoise reef patches and white surf lines off north-east Australia, fading into deep open blue to the east.","f":["The Great Barrier Reef is made of nearly 3,000 separate reefs and hundreds of islands.","It is the largest structure built by living things, and it can be seen from orbit.","The reef has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1981."],"q":{"q":"About how long is the Great Barrier Reef?","a":"About 2,300 km","x":["About 600 km","About 5,000 km"]}},"Q33254":{"t":"“The Ditch” between Australia and New Zealand","s":"Australians and New Zealanders call it “the Ditch”, but it is roughly 2,000 km across — about three hours in the air. The Tasman is known for rough water, stirred up by westerly winds from the Southern Ocean. Its name honours Abel Tasman, the Dutch navigator who crossed it in 1642 and sighted New Zealand.","w":"Open grey-blue water between two coastlines, often streaked with whitecaps and bands of cloud moving in from the west.","f":["Lord Howe Island, in its western part, is a UNESCO World Heritage site.","Nearby Ball's Pyramid, a volcanic stack 562 m tall, rises straight out of the sea.","On its floor lies the Lord Howe Rise, part of Zealandia, a continent that is mostly underwater."],"q":{"q":"What do Australians and New Zealanders nickname the Tasman Sea?","a":"The Ditch","x":["The Puddle","The Gap"]}},"Q38684":{"t":"The world's largest bay, fed by the Ganges","s":"At the head of the bay, the Ganges and Brahmaputra pour out so much silt that the sea turns café-au-lait brown far from shore. This is the largest bay on Earth, and at its top lies the world's biggest delta, fringed by the tangled green of the Sundarbans mangroves. Farther south the silt settles and the water turns deep blue.","w":"A vast triangle of sea between India and Myanmar; near Bangladesh, brown silt plumes swirl into the blue.","f":["The Sundarbans on its northern shore are the largest mangrove forest in the world.","Beneath the bay lies the Bengal Fan, a pile of river sediment some 3,000 km long.","River water makes its surface noticeably less salty than the open ocean."],"q":{"q":"Which rivers build the huge delta at the head of the Bay of Bengal?","a":"Ganges and Brahmaputra","x":["Indus and Narmada","Irrawaddy and Salween"]}},"Q37660":{"t":"One of the busiest seaways on the planet","s":"In clear weather, look for faint white scratches on the blue — the wakes of ships. A huge share of the world's sea trade crosses this water between the Strait of Malacca, China, Japan and Korea. Scattered across the middle are low coral reefs and atolls, which show as turquoise rings where deep water suddenly gives way to shallows.","w":"Wide blue sea between Vietnam, the Philippines and southern China, with ship wakes and small turquoise reef rings.","f":["Roughly a third of all global shipping passes through it.","The Mekong and the Pearl River both empty into it — the latter right beside Hong Kong.","Its deepest basin goes down more than 5,000 m."],"q":{"q":"Which great river's delta opens onto the South China Sea?","a":"The Mekong","x":["The Irrawaddy","The Ganges"]}},"Q27092":{"t":"Cloud streets by day, squid-boat lights by night","s":"On a winter flight, look for neat parallel lines of cloud streaming across the water from the Asian mainland: icy monsoon winds pick up moisture here and dump it as deep snow on Japan's western coast. At night the sea can sparkle with points of light — fishing boats using bright lamps to lure squid.","w":"An enclosed sea between Japan and the Asian mainland; in winter, rows of cloud stripes blowing towards Japan.","f":["It connects to the open ocean only through a handful of narrow straits.","Its deepest basin reaches about 3,700 m.","In winter, sea ice forms in its far north, near the Tatar Strait."],"q":{"q":"What makes the Sea of Japan glitter at night?","a":"Lamps on squid-fishing boats","x":["Offshore oil platforms","Floating solar farms"]}},"Q58705":{"t":"Monsoon sea between Arabia and India","s":"Every summer the south-west monsoon sweeps across the Arabian Sea, stirring cold, nutrient-rich water to the surface off Oman — plankton blooms can then paint the sea green. For thousands of years sailors rode these seasonal winds between Arabia and India in wooden dhows. In the north, the Indus fans out into a muddy delta.","w":"A wide sea between the Arabian Peninsula and India; in summer, greenish plankton swirls may show off Oman.","f":["The Indus reaches the sea through a broad delta on Pakistan's coast.","Below the surface lies one of the largest oxygen-poor zones in any ocean.","A 1st-century Greek sailing guide, the Periplus of the Erythraean Sea, describes trade across it."],"q":{"q":"Which great river flows into the Arabian Sea?","a":"The Indus","x":["The Ganges","The Brahmaputra"]}},"Q131274":{"t":"Where the Mackenzie meets the Arctic ice","s":"For most of the year the Beaufort Sea is a white plain of drifting ice, turned slowly clockwise by a vast ocean eddy. In summer the ice pulls back from the coast, and the Mackenzie River spills a brown plume of silt from a delta braided with countless lakes. Look for that tea-coloured stain against the ice.","w":"Sea ice north of Alaska and Canada; in summer, a brown river plume and a lake-speckled delta on the Canadian shore.","f":["It is named after Admiral Francis Beaufort, who devised the wind-force scale.","The Mackenzie, Canada's longest river, builds a huge delta on its shore.","Bowhead whales pass through it every year on their seasonal migration."],"q":{"q":"The Beaufort Sea's namesake is known for devising what?","a":"A wind-force scale","x":["A map projection","A tide table"]}},"Q1247":{"t":"Turquoise shallows beside deep blue water","s":"The famous colours are real: over sandbanks and reefs the Caribbean glows turquoise and jade, with white sand showing through. Just off those shallows the floor drops away to deep blue — in the Cayman Trough it lies about 7,700 m down. The sea is fenced in by the arc of the Antilles, a chain of islands curving from Cuba almost to Venezuela.","w":"Turquoise and emerald shallows around islands, white sand bars, and a sharp edge where the water turns deep blue.","f":["It takes its name from the Caribs, a people of the Lesser Antilles.","The Mesoamerican Barrier Reef off Belize and Mexico is the largest reef system in the Americas.","The Panama Canal links it to the Pacific across a narrow isthmus."],"q":{"q":"Which people is the Caribbean Sea named after?","a":"The Caribs","x":["The Maya","The Taíno"]}},"Q12630":{"t":"A warm basin with a bird's-foot delta","s":"Where the Mississippi reaches the Gulf, its delta splays out like a bird's foot, trailing brown silt into the blue. The Gulf is a warm, nearly round basin, and at night hundreds of offshore oil and gas platforms scatter lights across it. Buried off Yucatán lies the crater of the asteroid that ended the age of the dinosaurs.","w":"A broad, rounded gulf; off Louisiana, the Mississippi's fingered delta and brown plume; at night, scattered platform lights offshore.","f":["The Chicxulub crater, about 180 km across, lies partly beneath its floor.","Padre Island, off the Texas coast, is the longest barrier island in the world.","The warm Loop Current flows in between Yucatán and Cuba and leaves past Florida."],"q":{"q":"What shape is the Mississippi Delta famous for?","a":"A bird's foot","x":["A perfect fan","A crescent"]}},"Q184189":{"t":"Iceberg highway between Greenland and Labrador","s":"Many flights between Europe and North America cross this cold sea between Greenland and Labrador. In spring and early summer, look for white specks on dark water: icebergs calved from Greenland's glaciers, riding the Labrador Current south towards Newfoundland. In winter, surface water here grows so cold and dense that it sinks deep, helping drive the ocean's slow global circulation.","w":"Dark open water between Greenland's southern tip and the Labrador coast; in spring, scattered white icebergs and drifting pack ice.","f":["Norse sagas describe Markland, a forested coast thought to be Labrador, reached around AD 1000.","The cold Labrador Current flows south along its western side, carrying icebergs and sea ice.","Cape Farewell, the southern tip of Greenland, marks its north-eastern corner."],"q":{"q":"Where do most icebergs in the Labrador Sea come from?","a":"Greenland's glaciers","x":["Antarctica","Hudson Bay"]}},"Q3040":{"t":"An inland sea that freezes over every winter","s":"Hudson Bay is a true sea set inside a continent, and each winter it freezes over almost completely. Look at its eastern coast: it bends in a smooth, near-perfect arc, as if traced around a giant plate. The land around the bay is still slowly rising, springing back now that the weight of the ice-age glaciers is gone.","w":"A vast, roughly round bay in northern Canada — white ice in winter, dark water in late summer; note the smooth eastern arc.","f":["The smooth curve of its eastern shore is known as the Nastapoka Arc.","The land around it is still rising by about a centimetre a year since the last ice age.","Each autumn polar bears gather near Churchill, waiting for the bay to freeze so they can hunt seals."],"q":{"q":"What are Churchill's polar bears waiting for each autumn?","a":"The bay to freeze over","x":["The salmon run","The first snowfall"]}},"Q37040":{"t":"Iceberg-strewn water between Baffin Island and Greenland","s":"On a clear day Baffin Bay is all contrast: Greenland's white ice cap and glacier tongues on one side, the snowy mountains of Baffin Island on the other. Icebergs from glaciers such as Ilulissat drift across the water like scattered sugar cubes. In winter most of the bay freezes — except the North Water in the far north, which stays open.","w":"A long bay between Greenland's ice-covered coast and Baffin Island; white icebergs dot the dark water in summer.","f":["The North Water is one of the largest patches of Arctic sea that stays open through the winter.","Ilulissat Icefjord on its Greenland shore, a UNESCO site, sends huge icebergs into the bay.","It is named after William Baffin, who sailed and charted it in 1616."],"q":{"q":"What is the North Water in Baffin Bay?","a":"Sea that stays ice-free in winter","x":["A deep undersea canyon","A freshwater lake on an island"]}},"Q180531":{"t":"Where glaciers pour down to the sea","s":"Few coasts look wilder from the air. Along the Gulf of Alaska, ice fields spill glaciers straight towards the sea, and the Malaspina Glacier spreads across the coastal plain in a huge lobe striped like marble cake. Where silty meltwater meets clear ocean, you may see a sharp line between milky green and deep blue.","w":"The curving southern coast of Alaska: snowy peaks, glacier tongues, and the vast rounded lobe of the Malaspina Glacier.","f":["The Malaspina is one of the world's largest piedmont glaciers — ice that spreads out over flat land.","Mount Saint Elias, 5,489 m high, rises close to the gulf's northern shore.","Kodiak Island, on its western side, is home to the huge Kodiak brown bear."],"q":{"q":"About how high is Mount Saint Elias, above the Gulf of Alaska?","a":"About 5,500 m","x":["About 3,500 m","About 7,500 m"]}},"Q23406":{"t":"A young ocean being born between Africa and Arabia","s":"The Red Sea is an ocean in the making: Africa and Arabia are slowly pulling apart, and the crack between them has flooded. From the air it's a long, narrow blue channel between desert coasts, fringed with turquoise reefs. In the north it splits around the Sinai into two horns — the Gulfs of Suez and Aqaba.","w":"A long, slender sea between brown desert shores, with turquoise coral fringes; at its top, two arms around the Sinai triangle.","f":["No permanent river flows into it, and it is one of the saltiest seas connected to the ocean.","Africa and Arabia are drifting apart here by roughly a centimetre a year.","Since 1869 the Suez Canal has linked its northern tip to the Mediterranean."],"q":{"q":"Which two gulfs form the Red Sea's northern “horns”?","a":"Suez and Aqaba","x":["Aden and Oman","Aqaba and Sidra"]}},"Q41602":{"t":"A northern sea whose ice reaches Hokkaido","s":"In winter the Sea of Okhotsk turns white far to the south: its drift ice reaches the coast of Hokkaido, at roughly the latitude of Venice. The sea is walled in by Kamchatka to the east, Sakhalin to the west and the long chain of Kuril volcanoes to the south-east. In summer, fog often hides the water.","w":"A broad northern sea between Kamchatka, Sakhalin and the Kuril arc; white with ice in winter, often under fog in summer.","f":["Winter drift ice here reaches Hokkaido's coast at around 44° North.","The Kuril Islands, a chain of volcanoes, separate it from the open Pacific.","Okhotsk, founded on its shore in the 1640s, was the first Russian settlement on the Pacific."],"q":{"q":"Which island's coast does Okhotsk drift ice reach in the south?","a":"Hokkaido","x":["Honshu","Kyushu"]}},"Q173343":{"t":"Home of the clearest seawater ever measured","s":"The Weddell Sea is ruled by ice. Its pack ice turns in a slow clockwise gyre that trapped Shackleton's ship Endurance in 1915 and crushed it; the crew all survived, and the wreck was found in 2022 some 3 km down. To the south lies the Filchner–Ronne Ice Shelf, a floating plain of ice ending in cliffs tens of metres high.","w":"A vast sheet of pack ice east of the Antarctic Peninsula, ending at the flat white cliff of a floating ice shelf.","f":["In 1986 a white disc lowered into its water stayed visible to 79 m — the clearest seawater ever recorded.","The Filchner–Ronne Ice Shelf at its southern end is one of the largest floating ice shelves on Earth.","It is named after James Weddell, a British sealer who sailed deep into it in 1823."],"q":{"q":"Whose ship was crushed by the Weddell Sea's ice in 1915?","a":"Ernest Shackleton's","x":["Roald Amundsen's","Robert Scott's"]}},"Q34675":{"t":"Shallow, warm, and dotted with man-made islands","s":"This is a shallow, bath-warm sea — on average only about 50 m deep, with summer surface water often above 30 °C. Look off Dubai for the unmistakable fronds of the palm-shaped artificial islands, and for Qatar jutting north like a thumb. At night, gas flares and city lights ring the water. Before oil, its wealth came from pearls.","w":"A shallow turquoise gulf between Iran and Arabia; spot Qatar's thumb-shaped peninsula and Dubai's palm-shaped islands.","f":["The Tigris and Euphrates reach it together through the Shatt al-Arab.","Its only outlet to the ocean is the Strait of Hormuz, less than 40 km wide at its narrowest.","For centuries pearl diving was the main livelihood of towns on its Arabian shore."],"q":{"q":"Which peninsula juts into the Persian Gulf like a thumb?","a":"Qatar","x":["Sinai","Kathiawar"]}},"Q164466":{"t":"The sea at the foot of the great ice barrier","s":"The Ross Sea ends in a wall. Its southern edge is the Ross Ice Shelf, a floating slab of ice roughly the size of France, whose cliffs early explorers simply called “the Barrier”. On Ross Island, Mount Erebus sends up steam from a lava lake in its crater. Nowhere does open sea reach closer to the South Pole.","w":"Dark water and pack ice ending at a flat, sharp-edged white plateau of floating ice; to the west, snowy volcanic Ross Island.","f":["The ice shelf's front rises tens of metres above the sea and runs for some 600 km.","Mount Erebus, 3,794 m high, is the southernmost active volcano on Earth.","Much of the Ross Sea has been a marine protected area since 2017."],"q":{"q":"The Ross Ice Shelf is roughly the size of which country?","a":"France","x":["Belgium","Ireland"]}},"Q5525":{"t":"The biggest lake wholly in Canada, on the Arctic Circle","s":"Great Bear Lake sprawls across the northern forest and tundra in five long arms, like a starfish pressed into the land. The Arctic Circle crosses its northern part, and ice often lingers on the water into July. It is the largest lake lying entirely inside Canada, and it is almost untouched: only one community, Déline, sits on its shore.","w":"A huge, many-armed lake in the far north-west of Canada, white or streaked with ice for much of the year.","f":["Its outflow, the Great Bear River, runs into the Mackenzie.","A 33-kg lake trout caught here in 1995 set a rod-and-reel world record.","The lake and its watershed form Tsá Tué, a UNESCO biosphere reserve."],"q":{"q":"Which line crosses Great Bear Lake?","a":"The Arctic Circle","x":["The 60th parallel","The Continental Divide"]}},"Q5539":{"t":"North America's deepest lake","s":"Great Slave Lake has two faces. The main western basin is broad and open, while the East Arm is a maze of cliffs, islands and deep channels — the lake reaches 614 m there, deeper than any other in North America. The territorial capital, Yellowknife, sits on the north shore, and the Mackenzie River slips out of the western end.","w":"A wide lake with a long, island-studded eastern arm; at night, Yellowknife's lights on the north shore.","f":["Its name comes from the Slavey, a Dene people, not from slavery.","In winter an ice road across the frozen lake links Yellowknife with the community of Dettah.","Ice covers the lake for roughly half of every year."],"q":{"q":"How deep is Great Slave Lake at its deepest?","a":"614 m","x":["214 m","1,014 m"]}},"Q3272":{"t":"A shallow giant left behind by an ice-age lake","s":"Lake Winnipeg stretches north for over 400 km, pinched at the waist where its northern and southern basins meet at the Narrows. For all its size it is shallow — about 12 m on average — and in summer, algae blooms can swirl across it in shades of green visible from the air. Its name means “muddy water” in Cree.","w":"A long lake north of the city of Winnipeg, pinched in the middle; often greenish in late summer.","f":["It is a remnant of glacial Lake Agassiz, which once covered more area than all of today's Great Lakes.","Its outflow, the Nelson River, carries its water north to Hudson Bay.","Its drainage basin reaches all the way to the Rocky Mountains."],"q":{"q":"What does the Cree name “Winnipeg” mean?","a":"Muddy water","x":["Big water","Lake of spirits"]}},"Q5492":{"t":"The shallow, warm Great Lake that feeds Niagara","s":"Lake Erie is the shallowest of the Great Lakes, so it warms fastest in summer and is the one most likely to freeze over in winter. From above, look for Long Point, a slender sand spit curving about 40 km out from the Canadian shore. The lake's water leaves eastward, over Niagara Falls.","w":"A long oval lake between Ontario and Ohio; look for the thin hooked spit of Long Point on the northern shore.","f":["It averages only about 19 m deep — far less than the other Great Lakes.","Point Pelee, jutting into its western part, is the southern tip of mainland Canada.","In late summer, green algae blooms in its western basin can be seen from space."],"q":{"q":"Which of the Great Lakes is the shallowest?","a":"Lake Erie","x":["Lake Ontario","Lake Huron"]}},"Q1062":{"t":"The last Great Lake before the sea","s":"This is the final step in the Great Lakes' staircase: water arrives down the Niagara River and leaves through the St Lawrence towards the Atlantic. Lake Ontario is the smallest of the five by area, yet deep enough that it rarely freezes over. At night, look for the dense glow of Toronto and its suburbs wrapped around the western shore.","w":"An oval lake with Toronto's urban sprawl on its north-west shore; at its eastern end, a scatter of islands.","f":["The St Lawrence leaves it among the Thousand Islands — in fact there are more than 1,800.","Its deepest point is about 244 m.","Winds crossing it dump heavy lake-effect snow on New York's Tug Hill Plateau."],"q":{"q":"Which river carries Lake Ontario's water to the Atlantic?","a":"The St Lawrence","x":["The Hudson","The Ottawa"]}},"Q1066":{"t":"The largest freshwater lake on Earth by area","s":"Lake Superior is big enough to have its own horizon: at 563 km long, even from cruise height you may not see it end to end. No freshwater lake on Earth covers more area, and its cold, clear water holds about a tenth of all the fresh water on the planet's surface. In winter, ice and snow trace its rocky outline in white.","w":"A vast lake shaped a little like a wolf's head facing west, with the long Keweenaw Peninsula jutting from its southern shore.","f":["A drop of water that enters it stays, on average, for almost 200 years.","Isle Royale, in its north-west, has hosted a study of wolves and moose since 1958 — one of the longest of its kind.","Its deepest point lies 406 m below the surface."],"q":{"q":"On average, how long does water stay in Lake Superior?","a":"Almost 200 years","x":["About 20 years","About 2 years"]}},"Q1169":{"t":"The only Great Lake wholly in the United States","s":"Lake Michigan hangs down from the Straits of Mackinac like a long blue pendant, with Chicago's grid pressed against its south-western corner. Its eastern shore is lined with pale sand dunes, some rising over 100 m above the water. Strictly speaking, it is one lake with Huron: the two meet at the Straits and share the same level.","w":"A long north–south lake: Chicago's sprawl at the south-west, pale dune shores along the east, the Mackinac straits at the top.","f":["Its eastern shore holds the largest freshwater sand dunes in the world.","The Mackinac Bridge, about 8 km long, spans the straits that link it to Lake Huron.","Its name comes from an Ojibwe word meaning “great water”."],"q":{"q":"Which lake is Lake Michigan hydrologically joined with?","a":"Lake Huron","x":["Lake Superior","Lake Erie"]}},"Q1383":{"t":"The Great Lake with tens of thousands of islands","s":"Lake Huron is the island lake of the five. Along its north and east, the granite shore breaks into thousands of rocky islets, and Manitoulin — the largest island in any freshwater lake on Earth — divides the main lake from the North Channel. Georgian Bay, almost a lake in its own right, opens to the east.","w":"A broad lake east of Michigan's mitten; the long island of Manitoulin in the north and Georgian Bay to the east.","f":["Counting all its islands, it has the longest shoreline of the Great Lakes.","Saginaw Bay cuts into Michigan's shore, separating the “thumb” from the rest of the mitten.","The French named it after the Huron, or Wendat, people."],"q":{"q":"Which Lake Huron island is the largest in any freshwater lake?","a":"Manitoulin","x":["Isle Royale","Mackinac Island"]}},"Q5532":{"t":"A lake with more fish species than any other","s":"Lake Malawi fills a long trench of the East African Rift, a slim blue blade about 580 km long hemmed in by steep escarpments. Beneath the surface swim more fish species than in any other lake on Earth — hundreds of brightly coloured cichlids, most of them found nowhere else. At night, fishermen's lamps can speckle the dark water.","w":"A long, narrow lake running north–south in the Rift Valley, with mountains rising steeply along its northern shores.","f":["Its deepest point is more than 700 m down.","Lake Malawi National Park, at its southern end, is a UNESCO World Heritage site.","It is also called Lake Nyasa, and Lago Niassa in Mozambique."],"q":{"q":"Which fish make up most of Lake Malawi's famous variety?","a":"Cichlids","x":["Salmon","Sturgeons"]}},"Q5511":{"t":"The longest freshwater lake in the world","s":"Lake Tanganyika runs for about 670 km along the floor of the Rift Valley, farther than any other freshwater lake. It is narrow — about 50 km wide on average — but plunges to 1,470 m, second only to Baikal. Its long, steep shores are shared by four countries, and the water is a deep, clear blue.","w":"A very long, narrow lake running north–south in a deep rift, lined by steep mountain walls on both sides.","f":["Tanzania, DR Congo, Burundi and Zambia share its shores.","In 1871 Stanley found Livingstone at Ujiji, on its eastern shore.","It is among the oldest lakes on Earth, formed roughly 10 million years ago."],"q":{"q":"Which lake is deeper than Lake Tanganyika?","a":"Lake Baikal","x":["Lake Malawi","Lake Superior"]}},"Q5505":{"t":"Africa's largest lake, where the White Nile begins","s":"Lake Victoria is so wide it looks like an inland sea, with a ragged coastline of bays, peninsulas and green islands. It is Africa's largest lake, yet surprisingly shallow — only about 80 m at its deepest. The equator crosses its northern waters, and from its northern shore the White Nile slips out on its long journey to the Mediterranean.","w":"A huge lake straddling the equator, with a jagged shoreline, many islands and afternoon thunderclouds building over the water.","f":["It is the largest tropical lake in the world and the second-largest freshwater lake by area.","John Hanning Speke named it after Queen Victoria in 1858; “Nyanza” simply means “lake” in several local languages.","Uganda, Kenya and Tanzania share its waters."],"q":{"q":"Which great river flows out of Lake Victoria?","a":"The White Nile","x":["The Congo","The Blue Nile"]}},"Q173862":{"t":"A freshwater sea with a two-volcano island","s":"Look for Ometepe: an island shaped like a figure of eight, formed by two volcanic cones joined by a low neck of land. It rises from the largest lake in Central America, so wide that the Spanish called it the Mar Dulce, the sweet sea. Bull sharks have long swum here from the Caribbean, up the San Juan River.","w":"A broad lake in south-west Nicaragua; spot the twin volcanic cones of Ometepe island near its western shore.","f":["Ometepe's taller volcano, Concepción, is active and rises to about 1,600 m.","Near the city of Granada, hundreds of tiny islets, Las Isletas, are scattered along the shore.","The lake drains to the Caribbean through the San Juan River."],"q":{"q":"What forms the island of Ometepe?","a":"Two volcanoes","x":["A coral reef","A river delta"]}},"Q1134446":{"t":"A clear, island-studded lake of the Canadian Shield","s":"Reindeer Lake is pure Canadian Shield: a long sheet of dark, clear water scattered with rocky, forested islands, its shores carved into countless bays by ice-age glaciers. It stretches north–south through almost roadless forest. Its water drains south through the Reindeer River into the Churchill, and on to Hudson Bay.","w":"A long north–south lake with ragged shores and many rocky islands, set in dark forest in north-eastern Saskatchewan.","f":["It is the second-largest lake in Saskatchewan, after Lake Athabasca.","The Cree community of Southend sits at its southern tip.","Its cold, clear water is home to big lake trout and northern pike."],"q":{"q":"Where does Reindeer Lake's water finally end up?","a":"Hudson Bay","x":["The Pacific Ocean","The Gulf of Mexico"]}},"Q845876":{"t":"A maze of islands with a notch in the border","s":"From above, Lake of the Woods looks like two lakes in one: an open, rounded basin in the south, and in the north a shattered maze of some 14,500 rocky, forested islands. On its western side sits the Northwest Angle, a quirk of old map-making that leaves a piece of Minnesota north of the 49th parallel, reachable by land only through Canada.","w":"A lake on the Canada–US border: an open southern bay and a northern labyrinth of wooded islands.","f":["The Northwest Angle is the only part of the contiguous United States north of the 49th parallel.","Its water drains north through the Winnipeg River.","The town of Kenora, Ontario, sits at its northern outlet."],"q":{"q":"About how many islands does Lake of the Woods have?","a":"About 14,500","x":["About 1,500","About 4,500"]}},"Q122184":{"t":"The big lake that feeds Lake Superior","s":"Just north of Lake Superior lies a big, ragged lake scattered with hundreds of islands. Lake Nipigon is the largest lake entirely inside Ontario, and it drains south through the Nipigon River — the biggest river flowing into Superior. Around it stretches almost unbroken boreal forest, dark green in summer and white in winter.","w":"A large, island-dotted lake a short way north of Lake Superior's northern shore, surrounded by dark forest.","f":["The world-record brook trout, 6.6 kg, was caught in the Nipigon River in 1915.","The lake covers about 4,800 km²."],"q":{"q":"Into which Great Lake does Lake Nipigon drain?","a":"Lake Superior","x":["Lake Huron","Lake Michigan"]}},"Q645901":{"t":"The lake that gave a province its name","s":"Lake Manitoba is long and shallow, and halfway along it narrows to a slim neck called the Narrows. There, waves striking limestone shores make eerie booming sounds, which people heard as the drum of a spirit — the likely origin of the lake's name, and of the province named after it. It is nowhere much deeper than 7 m.","w":"A long, slender lake west of Lake Winnipeg, narrowing sharply halfway up; marshes fringe its southern end.","f":["Delta Marsh, on its southern shore, is one of the largest freshwater marshes in North America.","Its water flows on, through smaller rivers and lakes, into Lake Winnipeg."],"q":{"q":"What was named after Lake Manitoba?","a":"A Canadian province","x":["A U.S. state","A mountain range"]}},"Q934638":{"t":"Winnipeg's “little muddy” sister","s":"Three big lakes lie side by side in Manitoba, all remnants of the same vanished ice-age lake. Winnipegosis is the westernmost: long, narrow and shallow, with a shoreline of low points and quiet bays. Even its name is the little sister of Winnipeg's — roughly “little muddy water” in Cree. Its water flows south into Lake Manitoba.","w":"The westernmost of Manitoba's three great lakes: long and narrow, running north–south, west of Lake Winnipeg.","f":["It drains south into Lake Manitoba through the Waterhen River.","Lakes Winnipegosis, Manitoba and Winnipeg are all remnants of glacial Lake Agassiz.","It covers more than 5,000 km², yet over most of that area it is only a few metres deep."],"q":{"q":"Which lake does Lake Winnipegosis flow into directly?","a":"Lake Manitoba","x":["Lake Athabasca","Lake of the Woods"]}},"Q272463":{"t":"A northern lake with a desert on its shore","s":"Along the south shore of Lake Athabasca, the dark forest suddenly gives way to bare, pale sand: the Athabasca Sand Dunes, among the most northerly large active dunes on Earth. At the lake's western end, the Peace and Athabasca rivers build a vast green delta. The north shore is rocky Canadian Shield, pitted with small lakes.","w":"A long east–west lake on the Alberta–Saskatchewan border; look for pale patches of sand along its southern shore.","f":["The Peace–Athabasca Delta is one of the largest freshwater deltas in the world.","Fort Chipewyan, founded on its shore in 1788, is one of Alberta's oldest settlements.","Its water flows north via the Slave River towards Great Slave Lake and the Arctic."],"q":{"q":"What unusual landscape lies on Lake Athabasca's southern shore?","a":"Active sand dunes","x":["Hot springs","A volcanic crater"]}},"Q1458051":{"t":"Quebec's largest natural lake, split by islands","s":"Lake Mistassini looks as if it was raked by a giant comb: long and narrow, it runs north-east for more than 150 km, with a chain of slender islands dividing it lengthwise. The Cree community of Mistissini sits on its southern shore, and the lake drains west through the Rupert River into James Bay.","w":"A long, narrow lake running south-west to north-east in the forests of central Quebec, split down the middle by islands.","f":["Its name comes from the Cree for “big rock”.","It covers about 2,300 km², more than any other natural lake in Quebec."],"q":{"q":"Where does Lake Mistassini's water end up?","a":"James Bay","x":["The Gulf of St Lawrence","Lake Ontario"]}},"Q1267413":{"t":"Lake Huron's big bay of thirty thousand islands","s":"Georgian Bay is so large it is sometimes called the sixth Great Lake, though it is really an arm of Lake Huron. The Bruce Peninsula walls it off in the west, a finger of limestone cliffs above turquoise water. Along the eastern shore, pinkish granite breaks into the Thirty Thousand Islands, a glittering scatter from the air.","w":"A large bay east of Lake Huron, closed off by the finger of the Bruce Peninsula, with a maze of islands on its eastern shore.","f":["The Thirty Thousand Islands form the world's largest freshwater archipelago.","The Bruce Peninsula is part of the Niagara Escarpment, the same rock ridge that Niagara Falls pours over.","It was named in 1822 after King George IV."],"q":{"q":"Which peninsula separates Georgian Bay from the rest of Lake Huron?","a":"The Bruce Peninsula","x":["The Keweenaw Peninsula","The Door Peninsula"]}},"Q15288":{"t":"Europe's largest lake, rocky in the north","s":"Europe's largest lake has two faces. Its southern shore is low and marshy, but the north breaks into skerries — rocky islands and narrow inlets gouged by glaciers — where the water plunges to over 200 m. On the Valaam islands, in the north, stands a monastery founded in the Middle Ages.","w":"A huge lake north-east of St Petersburg, low-shored in the south; in the north, a rocky maze of islands and inlets.","f":["The Neva carries its water only about 74 km to the Baltic, straight through St Petersburg.","It is home to the Ladoga ringed seal, a subspecies found nowhere else.","The lake covers about 17,700 km² — more than half the size of Belgium."],"q":{"q":"Which river carries Lake Ladoga's water to the Baltic?","a":"The Neva","x":["The Svir","The Volkhov"]}},"Q134485":{"t":"A lake that is half fresh and half salty","s":"Lake Balkhash is a long, thin arc across the Kazakh steppe, some 600 km from end to end, and it is two lakes in one. The western half, fed by the Ili River, is nearly fresh; the eastern half, linked to it only by a narrow strait, is salty. From above, the two halves often show slightly different shades.","w":"A long, thin arc of water across the dry steppe of eastern Kazakhstan, with the green Ili delta near its western end.","f":["The Ili River, flowing in from the south, supplies most of its water.","The strait linking its two halves is only a few kilometres wide.","It has no outlet: water leaves only by evaporation."],"q":{"q":"Which river feeds Lake Balkhash?","a":"The Ili","x":["The Irtysh","The Syr Darya"]}},"Q125888":{"t":"A rift-valley lake the Nile passes through","s":"Lake Albert lies in a deep trough of the western Rift Valley, walled by steep escarpments — on the Congo side they rise abruptly for hundreds of metres. The Nile only brushes its northern tip: the Victoria Nile flows in below Murchison Falls, and the Albert Nile leaves a few kilometres away. The lake stretches about 160 km from south-west to north-east.","w":"A long, narrow lake between steep escarpments on the Uganda–Congo border, with green papyrus flats at its northern end.","f":["Samuel Baker reached it in 1864 and named it after Prince Albert, Queen Victoria's late husband.","The Semliki River flows in at its southern end, carrying water from the Rwenzori Mountains."],"q":{"q":"Who named Lake Albert in 1864?","a":"Samuel Baker","x":["John Hanning Speke","Henry Morton Stanley"]}},"Q173596":{"t":"Sweden's inland sea, split into two basins","s":"Sweden's biggest lake has two parts: the broad main basin and, to the south-west, the smaller Dalbosjön, almost cut off by two peninsulas reaching towards each other. Rocky islands and skerries dot the water, and at Karlstad on the northern shore the Klarälven river fans out into a delta. Vänern is the largest lake in the European Union.","w":"A huge lake in south-west Sweden, pinched into two basins by peninsulas, with rocky islands and a delta at Karlstad in the north.","f":["Its water leaves through the Göta älv, reaching the sea at Gothenburg.","The Göta Canal links it eastwards to Lake Vättern and on to the Baltic.","It covers about 5,600 km²."],"q":{"q":"Which city stands at the mouth of the river draining Vänern?","a":"Gothenburg","x":["Stockholm","Malmö"]}},"Q166162":{"t":"Europe's second-largest lake, with fingers in the north","s":"Look at Lake Onega's northern end: it splits into long, narrow bays lying side by side like the fingers of a hand, shaped by the ice sheets. The south is a broad, open basin. Among the northern islands lies Kizhi, whose wooden Church of the Transfiguration is crowned with 22 domes — far too small to spot from here, but worth knowing about.","w":"A big lake north-east of Ladoga, its northern end split into long parallel bays; Petrozavodsk on the western shore.","f":["The wooden churches of Kizhi are a UNESCO World Heritage site.","More than a thousand rock carvings, thousands of years old, cover granite ledges on its eastern shore.","The Svir River carries its water west into Lake Ladoga."],"q":{"q":"How many domes crown the Church of the Transfiguration on Kizhi?","a":"22","x":["12","33"]}},"Q116685":{"t":"The lake where the Blue Nile begins","s":"High on the Ethiopian plateau, about 1,800 m up, Lake Tana is the source of the Blue Nile, which slips out of its southern shore at Bahir Dar. Its islands and peninsulas shelter old monasteries with painted churches, and fishermen still paddle papyrus boats called tankwa. From the air it's a broad, rounded lake ringed by green farmland.","w":"A broad, roundish lake on the green Ethiopian highlands, with Bahir Dar and the Blue Nile's outflow at its southern tip.","f":["The Blue Nile supplies most of the water that flows down the Nile through Egypt.","About 30 km downstream, the Blue Nile plunges over the Tis Issat falls.","Some of its island monasteries date back to the 14th century."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows out of Lake Tana?","a":"The Blue Nile","x":["The White Nile","The Awash"]}},"Q35342":{"t":"A deep-blue lake at 3,800 m, with floating islands","s":"Lake Titicaca sits at about 3,810 m — more than a third of the way up to your cruising height — and its water looks an intense, dark blue in the thin mountain air. Beyond it rise the snowy peaks of Bolivia's Cordillera Real. On its Peruvian side, the Uros people live on floating islands built from totora reeds.","w":"A large, irregular deep-blue lake on the bare high plateau, with snow-capped mountains rising along its eastern side.","f":["It is often called the highest navigable lake in the world.","Its two basins are joined by the narrow Strait of Tiquina.","In Inca legend, the founders of the Inca dynasty rose from its waters."],"q":{"q":"What are the Uros people's floating islands made of?","a":"Totora reeds","x":["Balsa logs","Pumice stone"]}},"Q192770":{"t":"A labyrinth of water and 14,000 islands","s":"From above, Saimaa hardly looks like a lake at all — more like a jigsaw of blue channels, pine-dark islands and narrow sounds spread across eastern Finland. It has around 14,000 islands and a shoreline so convoluted that it runs to about 15,000 km. In winter the whole maze freezes into a white puzzle.","w":"A sprawling maze of lakes, channels and forested islands across south-eastern Finland, with no single open expanse.","f":["The Saimaa ringed seal lives here and nowhere else; only a few hundred remain.","The Saimaa Canal links it to the Gulf of Finland at Vyborg.","It is the largest lake in Finland."],"q":{"q":"Which rare animal lives only in Lake Saimaa?","a":"A ringed seal","x":["A freshwater dolphin","A giant salamander"]}},"Q375029":{"t":"A man-made “sea” over a drowned town","s":"Locals call it the Rybinsk Sea, and from the air it earns the name: a broad sheet of grey-blue water spreading north from the Volga over flat forest and fields. Created in the 1940s by damming the Volga and Sheksna, it was then the largest man-made lake in the world. Beneath it lies the old town of Mologa.","w":"A wide, shallow, many-armed reservoir north of Rybinsk, filling the flooded valleys of the Volga, Sheksna and Mologa.","f":["Its average depth is only about 5–6 m.","Rafts of floating peat, sometimes carrying bushes and small trees, drift across it.","The Darwin Nature Reserve on its shore was set up to study how the flooding changed nature."],"q":{"q":"Which town was flooded to create the Rybinsk Reservoir?","a":"Mologa","x":["Kostroma","Vologda"]}},"Q21890573":{"t":"Australia's lowest point, a salt pan that rarely fills","s":"Most of the time Kati Thanda–Lake Eyre is a vast, glaring-white salt pan about 15 m below sea level — the lowest point in Australia. Only after big rains far to the north does water spread across it, sometimes tinted pink by salt-loving algae. Then pelicans fly in from the coast, hundreds of kilometres away, to breed.","w":"A huge white salt pan in the South Australian outback, sometimes streaked with shallow blue or pink water.","f":["Its catchment covers about a sixth of Australia.","In 1964 Donald Campbell set a land speed record of about 649 km/h on its salt.","It has filled completely only a few times since Europeans first saw it."],"q":{"q":"How far below sea level is the bed of Kati Thanda–Lake Eyre?","a":"About 15 m","x":["About 150 m","About 400 m"]}},"Q178741":{"t":"A lake cut in two: pink on one side, blue on the other","s":"Look for a dead-straight line across the water: a railway causeway completed in 1959 splits Great Salt Lake in two. North of it the brine is so salty that microbes turn it pink or rusty red; south of it the water stays blue-green. The lake is what remains of Lake Bonneville, an ice-age lake that once covered much of western Utah.","w":"A large, shallow lake west of Salt Lake City, crossed by a straight causeway: pinkish-red water north, blue-green south.","f":["It is the largest saltwater lake in the Western Hemisphere.","Antelope Island, its largest island, is home to a free-roaming bison herd.","Tiny brine shrimp thrive in it, and their eggs are harvested as fish-farm feed."],"q":{"q":"What turns the north arm of Great Salt Lake pink?","a":"Salt-loving microbes","x":["Iron-rich mud","Reflected desert sand"]}},"Q276776":{"t":"A vanishing sea, turned into a new desert","s":"What you see is the ghost of a sea. In 1960 the Aral was the world's fourth-largest lake, but its rivers were diverted to irrigate cotton, and it shrank. The southern part split into remnants: a deep western basin and a pale, shifting eastern one that dried out almost entirely in 2014. Its old seabed is now a new desert, the Aralkum.","w":"A narrow strip of dark water in the west and pale salt flats to the east, ringed by old shorelines in the desert.","f":["The old fishing port of Moynaq now lies more than 100 km from the water, with rusting ships in the sand.","The former Vozrozhdeniya Island is now joined to the mainland.","Salt and dust blown off the dry seabed can travel hundreds of kilometres."],"q":{"q":"Why did the Aral Sea shrink?","a":"Its rivers were diverted for irrigation","x":["An earthquake drained it","A canal emptied it into the Caspian"]}},"Q876280":{"t":"The “little” lagoon that is 180 km long","s":"Its name means “little” in Tupi-Guarani, yet Lagoa Mirim stretches about 180 km along the coastal plain, shared by Brazil and Uruguay. Only a strip of sand dunes and marshes separates it from the Atlantic. From the air, look for its brownish, silty water beside the ocean's blue, with green rice paddies spread along its western shore.","w":"A long lagoon parallel to the Atlantic coast near the Brazil–Uruguay border, separated from the sea by a sandy strip.","f":["The São Gonçalo Channel links it to the even larger Lagoa dos Patos to the north-east.","The Taim wetlands, a reserve rich in waterbirds, lie between the lagoon and the sea."],"q":{"q":"What does “Mirim” mean in Tupi-Guarani?","a":"Small","x":["Deep","Salty"]}},"Q42191":{"t":"The “warm lake” of the Tian Shan that never freezes","s":"Issyk-Kul is a long sapphire oval held between two snowy ranges of the Tian Shan, about 1,600 m above sea level. Winters in these mountains are harsh, yet the lake never freezes — its name means “warm lake” in Kyrgyz. It is deep, slightly salty and strikingly clear, and for most of the year its blue is framed by white peaks.","w":"A long east–west oval of deep blue between two parallel snow-capped ranges in north-eastern Kyrgyzstan.","f":["It reaches about 668 m deep.","No river flows out of it, which is why its water is slightly salty.","It is one of the largest mountain lakes in the world."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Issyk-Kul mean?","a":"Warm lake","x":["Blue lake","Salt lake"]}},"Q3358037":{"t":"The part of the Aral that is coming back","s":"Unlike its southern neighbour, the North Aral Sea is a rare story of recovery. In 2005 the Kokaral Dam sealed it off from the shrinking south, so the Syr Darya's water now stays here. The level rose, the water grew less salty, and fish returned. From above it's a lobed blue lake set in pale desert, still well short of its old shores.","w":"A lobed blue lake in the desert of south-western Kazakhstan; a dam at its southern tip, the Syr Darya entering from the east.","f":["The Kokaral Dam, about 13 km long, holds its water back.","The water has crept back towards Aralsk, a port that had been left far inland.","Commercial fishing has returned to its waters."],"q":{"q":"What helped the North Aral Sea to recover?","a":"A dam","x":["Pumping from the Caspian","A desalination plant"]}},"Q19270":{"t":"A deep tropical basin in the Coral Triangle","s":"Ringed by Mindanao, Sulawesi and Borneo, this almost enclosed basin drops to more than 6,000 m. Its open water is a dark, velvety blue, but the edges shine turquoise wherever reefs fringe the islands. The Celebes Sea is part of the Coral Triangle, where more kinds of coral and reef fish live than anywhere else on Earth.","w":"A deep-blue sea between Mindanao, Sulawesi's long northern arm and north-east Borneo; volcanic islands along its eastern edge.","f":["“Celebes” is the old European name for the island of Sulawesi.","The Sulu Archipelago, a chain of small islands, separates it from the Sulu Sea."],"q":{"q":"“Celebes” is the old name of which island?","a":"Sulawesi","x":["Borneo","Mindanao"]}},"Q160194":{"t":"Coral atolls in the heart of the Philippine seas","s":"The Sulu Sea is ringed by islands. Look for the long, thin island of Palawan — it forms the north-western wall, like a green spine laid on the blue. In the middle of the open water, far from any coast, lie the Tubbataha Reefs: two atolls whose turquoise lagoons and white sand cays stand out sharply against the deep blue.","w":"A deep-blue sea between Palawan, Mindanao and the central Philippine islands, with small turquoise atolls in the middle.","f":["The Tubbataha Reefs have been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1993.","Its deep basin plunges to more than 5,000 m.","The Sulu Archipelago, which gives the sea its name, runs along its south-eastern edge."],"q":{"q":"Which long island forms the Sulu Sea's north-western edge?","a":"Palawan","x":["Luzon","Samar"]}},"Q47545":{"t":"An Arctic sea kept open by warm Atlantic water","s":"Although much of it lies north of the Arctic Circle, the Norwegian Sea stays almost free of ice all year: a branch of the Gulf Stream's warm water flows through it towards the Arctic. On the Norwegian side, look for the jagged peaks of the Lofoten Islands rising straight from the water like a row of shark's teeth.","w":"Open grey-blue sea off Norway's coast; to the east, the saw-toothed Lofoten chain and a coastline shredded into fjords.","f":["Off the tip of Lofoten swirls the Moskstraumen, the maelstrom that inspired Edgar Allan Poe and Jules Verne.","The Røst Reef off Lofoten is the largest cold-water coral reef yet discovered.","Its deep basins go down more than 3,000 m."],"q":{"q":"Which famous whirlpool swirls off the Lofoten Islands?","a":"The Moskstraumen","x":["The Corryvreckan","Charybdis"]}},"Q132868":{"t":"The Arctic's exit door for drifting sea ice","s":"Most of the sea ice that leaves the Arctic Ocean does so through here. Floes squeeze out through Fram Strait, between Greenland and Svalbard, then ride the East Greenland Current south along the coast in a long white band. East of that band the sea is dark and open. Far to the south rises Jan Mayen, crowned by the snowy volcano Beerenberg.","w":"Between Greenland and Svalbard: a white band of drifting pack ice along Greenland's coast, with open dark water to the east.","f":["Beerenberg on Jan Mayen, 2,277 m high, is the northernmost active volcano on land.","Fram Strait, at its northern end, is the main gateway through which Arctic sea ice drifts out.","In winter its surface water sinks deep, helping to drive the Atlantic's circulation."],"q":{"q":"Through which strait does most ice leave the Arctic Ocean?","a":"Fram Strait","x":["Bering Strait","Davis Strait"]}},"Q171510":{"t":"Deep blue sea of the original Spice Islands","s":"The Banda Sea is ringed by a great curving arc of volcanic islands, and in places its floor drops to more than 7,000 m. Scattered in it are the tiny Banda Islands — for centuries the only place on Earth where nutmeg grew, a spice so valuable that European powers fought over these specks of land.","w":"Deep indigo sea in eastern Indonesia, rimmed by volcanic islands; look for tiny green specks and cones in the open water.","f":["Under a 1667 treaty, England gave up its claim to Run, a tiny Banda nutmeg island, and kept Manhattan.","The Weber Deep, in the east of the sea, is more than 7 km deep."],"q":{"q":"Which spice once grew only on the Banda Islands?","a":"Nutmeg","x":["Cinnamon","Black pepper"]}},"Q41573":{"t":"A stormy bay between France's straight coast and Spain","s":"Look east across the Bay of Biscay and you may see one of Europe's straightest coastlines: over 200 km of almost unbroken sand along France's Landes coast, with the Dune du Pilat piled up near its northern end. The southern shore is the opposite — Spain's green, mountainous north coast. The bay is famous for sudden storms and big Atlantic swell.","w":"A wide bay framed by France's long, straight sandy coast to the east and Spain's mountainous shore to the south.","f":["The Dune du Pilat, about 100 m high, is the tallest sand dune in Europe.","Beyond the continental shelf its floor drops to more than 4,000 m.","Dolphins and whales feed here and are often seen from the ferries that cross it."],"q":{"q":"What is the tallest sand dune in Europe, on the Bay of Biscay?","a":"The Dune du Pilat","x":["The Råbjerg Mile","The Łeba dunes"]}},"Q165100":{"t":"The strait between Africa and Madagascar","s":"This broad channel separates Madagascar from mainland Africa by at least 400 km of deep blue water. It opened more than 100 million years ago, as Madagascar drifted away from Africa. From above, look for the Zambezi's brown plume on the African side, and at the northern end the volcanic Comoros.","w":"Wide blue water between Mozambique's low coast and Madagascar's western shore; the Comoros islands mark its northern end.","f":["Coelacanths, fish once thought extinct for 66 million years, live in deep water off the Comoros.","The channel is about 1,600 km long.","Humpback whales come here to breed during the southern winter."],"q":{"q":"Which “living fossil” fish lives off the Comoros?","a":"The coelacanth","x":["The lungfish","The sturgeon"]}},"Q41430":{"t":"Where the equator meets the prime meridian","s":"Somewhere in this warm, hazy gulf lies 0° latitude, 0° longitude — the spot where the equator crosses the prime meridian, marked only by a weather buoy. To the north the Niger spreads into a vast delta of mangroves and creeks, and at night its oil and gas flares glow like embers. Offshore, volcanic islands rise from the sea.","w":"A broad, warm gulf off West Africa; the Niger Delta's green maze in the north, and volcanic islands such as Bioko offshore.","f":["Bioko, São Tomé and Príncipe are volcanoes of the Cameroon Line, which runs from the mainland out to sea.","The Niger Delta is the largest river delta in Africa.","European sailors once named stretches of its shore the Gold Coast and the Ivory Coast."],"q":{"q":"Which two lines cross in the Gulf of Guinea?","a":"Equator and prime meridian","x":["Tropic of Cancer and prime meridian","Equator and 180th meridian"]}},"Q545":{"t":"A young, half-fresh sea born after the ice age","s":"The Baltic is barely a sea at all: fed by more than 200 rivers and linked to the ocean only through the narrow Danish straits, its water is brackish, and in the far north almost fresh. It formed after the last ice age, and for a time it was a lake. Look for Gotland and Öland, long limestone islands off Sweden.","w":"A large grey-blue inland sea in northern Europe; the long islands of Gotland and Öland lie off Sweden's south-east coast.","f":["Most of the world's amber is found along its south-eastern shores.","In cold winters its northern gulfs freeze over completely.","Its average depth is only about 55 m."],"q":{"q":"What washes up on the Baltic's south-eastern shores?","a":"Amber","x":["Jade","Coral"]}},"Q45823":{"t":"An Arctic sea kept half open by the Atlantic","s":"The Barents Sea is a sea of two halves. Warm Atlantic water keeps its south-western part open all winter — the port of Murmansk never freezes — while the north is locked in ice for much of the year. In August, plankton blooms can marble the water with milky turquoise, a sight satellites often capture.","w":"Open dark water in the south-west, pack ice in the north; the long, curved island of Novaya Zemlya walls it off in the east.","f":["It is named after Willem Barentsz, who wintered on Novaya Zemlya in 1596–97.","Its waters hold one of the world's largest stocks of cod.","Svalbard, Franz Josef Land and Novaya Zemlya frame it to the west, north and east."],"q":{"q":"Why does Murmansk's port stay ice-free in winter?","a":"Warm Atlantic water","x":["Hot springs under the bay","Salt from nearby mines"]}},"Q1693":{"t":"A shallow sea that was once dry land","s":"Much of the southern North Sea was dry land until about 8,000 years ago: hunters roamed a plain now called Doggerland, which joined Britain to the continent. Today the sea is shallow and busy. On clear days you may make out the regular grids of offshore wind farms and, along the Dutch, German and Danish coasts, the grey-brown tidal flats of the Wadden Sea.","w":"Grey-green shallow sea between Britain, Norway and the continent; tidal flats and barrier islands along the southern shore.","f":["The Wadden Sea is the largest unbroken system of tidal flats in the world.","Its average depth is only about 95 m.","Trawlers still sometimes haul up mammoth bones from the Dogger Bank."],"q":{"q":"What is the drowned land under the southern North Sea called?","a":"Doggerland","x":["Atlantis","Lyonesse"]}},"Q41735":{"t":"Between Britain and Ireland, with Man in the middle","s":"Right in the middle of the Irish Sea sits the Isle of Man, green and hilly, with its own ancient parliament. On clear days you can pick out Anglesey and the mountains of Snowdonia to the east, Dublin Bay to the west, and wide bays where the ebbing tide uncovers kilometres of sand.","w":"A grey-green sea between Ireland and Britain; the Isle of Man near its centre, Anglesey off the Welsh coast.","f":["The Isle of Man's parliament, Tynwald, traces its origins back more than a thousand years.","Morecambe Bay, on its English side, holds one of Britain's largest expanses of tidal sand and mud.","An old saying claims that from Snaefell, the Isle of Man's 621 m summit, you can see six kingdoms."],"q":{"q":"Which island sits near the middle of the Irish Sea?","a":"The Isle of Man","x":["The Isle of Wight","Arran"]}},"Q49364":{"t":"A shallow tropical sea over a drowned plain","s":"The Java Sea is remarkably shallow — on average less than 50 m deep — because it floods a former plain. In the ice ages, when sea levels were lower, you could have walked from Java to Borneo. Look for Jakarta's sprawl on the southern shore and, just offshore, the scatter of coral islets called the Thousand Islands.","w":"A warm, shallow, often greenish sea between Java and Borneo, busy with ships; Jakarta spreads along its southern shore.","f":["The Thousand Islands off Jakarta actually number a little over a hundred.","Its seabed is part of the Sunda Shelf, one of the largest continental shelves on Earth.","Karimunjawa, a group of coral islands in its middle, is a national park."],"q":{"q":"In the ice ages, you could walk from Java to which island?","a":"Borneo","x":["Sulawesi","New Guinea"]}},"Q47632":{"t":"Limestone towers, island chains and a lone volcano","s":"The Andaman Sea is framed by islands: the long Andaman and Nicobar chain in the west and, off Myanmar, the hundreds of forested isles of the Mergui Archipelago. Near Phuket, in Phang Nga Bay, sheer limestone towers rise straight out of green water. The Irrawaddy's muddy delta spreads across the north, staining the sea brown.","w":"Warm blue sea west of Thailand and Myanmar; island chains on both sides and brown river water in the north.","f":["Barren Island, in its western part, is the only confirmed active volcano in South Asia.","The Mergui Archipelago off Myanmar counts some 800 islands."],"q":{"q":"Which river's delta spreads into the northern Andaman Sea?","a":"The Irrawaddy","x":["The Mekong","The Chao Phraya"]}},"Q37960":{"t":"A sea tinted by the silt of China's rivers","s":"The name is no exaggeration: near the Chinese coast, silt from great rivers turns the water a murky yellow-brown that fades to green offshore. The Yellow Sea is shallow — about 44 m on average — and on the Korean side the tides are huge, retreating twice a day to bare kilometres of glistening mudflats.","w":"A shallow sea between China and Korea: yellow-brown water near the Chinese coast, broad grey mudflats along Korea's west.","f":["At Incheon the tide can rise and fall by more than 8 m.","Its tidal flats are a vital stopover for millions of migrating shorebirds.","Near the island of Jindo, very low tides briefly expose a path of seabed almost 3 km long."],"q":{"q":"What gives the Yellow Sea its colour?","a":"River silt","x":["Algae blooms","Sulphur springs"]}},"Q45341":{"t":"Where the Yangtze pours into the Pacific","s":"At the mouth of the Yangtze, beside Shanghai, the East China Sea turns the colour of milky tea: Asia's longest river pours out a vast plume of silt. South of the city, look for the thin line of the Hangzhou Bay Bridge running some 36 km across the water. Farther east the sea deepens and clears towards the Ryukyu Islands.","w":"Brown water fanning out from the Yangtze mouth near Shanghai, turning blue-green offshore towards the Ryukyu chain.","f":["The warm Kuroshio current runs north-east along its eastern edge.","Its wide continental shelf is mostly less than 200 m deep.","The Ryukyu Islands, strung out between Kyushu and Taiwan, form its eastern boundary."],"q":{"q":"Which great river empties into the East China Sea?","a":"The Yangtze","x":["The Yellow River","The Pearl River"]}},"Q159252":{"t":"The Arctic sea where the last mammoths lived","s":"North of the Bering Strait, the Chukchi Sea lies between Chukotka and Alaska, and the International Date Line runs straight through it — cross it and the calendar jumps a day. The sea is frozen for most of the year. On its western edge lies Wrangel Island, where woolly mammoths survived until roughly 4,000 years ago, long after they vanished elsewhere.","w":"Ice or dark open water north of the Bering Strait, between the hills of Chukotka and Alaska's flat north-west coast.","f":["Wrangel Island is a UNESCO site and a major denning ground for polar bears.","In autumn, thousands of Pacific walruses haul out on its beaches."],"q":{"q":"Until roughly when did mammoths survive on Wrangel Island?","a":"About 4,000 years ago","x":["About 40,000 years ago","About 400 years ago"]}},"Q61304":{"t":"The oil-rich southern pocket of the Gulf of Mexico","s":"Curving from Veracruz round to the Yucatán Peninsula, the Bay of Campeche is the Gulf of Mexico's southern pocket. Under its waters lies Cantarell, once one of the largest oil fields on Earth, and at night platform lights scatter across the sea. Along the southern shore, rivers spread into lagoons and mangrove-lined wetlands.","w":"The southern bight of the Gulf of Mexico, from Veracruz east to the Yucatán; platform lights offshore at night.","f":["Laguna de Términos, on its southern shore, is one of Mexico's largest coastal lagoons.","Veracruz, on its western shore, was founded by Hernán Cortés in 1519.","The Grijalva and Usumacinta rivers reach it through a shared delta of swamps and channels."],"q":{"q":"Who founded Veracruz on the Bay of Campeche?","a":"Hernán Cortés","x":["Francisco Pizarro","Christopher Columbus"]}},"Q128880":{"t":"A shallow sea over the land bridge to New Guinea","s":"Between northern Australia and New Guinea spreads the Arafura Sea — broad, warm and surprisingly shallow, mostly less than 80 m deep. In the ice ages it was dry land, and people and animals could walk between the two. From the air, look for green-brown water near the muddy coasts and the low, forested Aru Islands in the north.","w":"Wide, shallow, often greenish sea between Australia's Top End and New Guinea, with the low Aru Islands to the north.","f":["The Gulf of Carpentaria opens off its southern side.","Torres Strait, a maze of islands and reefs, links it to the Coral Sea."],"q":{"q":"What did the Arafura Sea floor connect during the ice ages?","a":"Australia and New Guinea","x":["Australia and Timor","New Guinea and Borneo"]}},"Q131418":{"t":"A sea named after the “eastern” island","s":"The Timor Sea takes its name from Timor, and Timor's name comes from the Malay word for “east”. Most of it is a broad, shallow shelf off northern Australia, but close to Timor the floor plunges into a trough more than 3,000 m deep. On the Australian side, big tides wash in and out among rust-red cliffs and mangrove creeks.","w":"Open blue-green sea between the mountainous island of Timor and Australia's red Kimberley and Top End coasts.","f":["The city of Darwin lies at its eastern edge.","Joseph Bonaparte Gulf, on its Australian side, is named after Napoleon's elder brother."],"q":{"q":"What does the Malay word behind the name “Timor” mean?","a":"East","x":["Island","Mountain"]}},"Q131217":{"t":"A warm, shallow gulf ringed by tropical islands","s":"Warm and shallow — about 45 m deep on average — the Gulf of Thailand often looks jade-green rather than blue. Look for the islands of Ko Samui and Ko Pha Ngan off the western shore, and the large island of Phu Quoc near the eastern one. At night, fishing boats' lamps can scatter the dark water with light.","w":"A broad, often greenish gulf south of Bangkok, with the Malay Peninsula to the west and scattered islands off both shores.","f":["The Chao Phraya, the river of Bangkok, empties into its northern tip.","Its old name, the Gulf of Siam, is still used in some languages, including Russian.","Its seabed was dry land during the last ice age."],"q":{"q":"Which river of Bangkok flows into the Gulf of Thailand?","a":"The Chao Phraya","x":["The Mekong","The Irrawaddy"]}},"Q544914":{"t":"Warm sea between India's tip and the coral atolls","s":"South-west of India lies a warm, bright-blue sea dotted with coral atolls. The Lakshadweep islands — the name means “a hundred thousand islands”, though there are only about three dozen — rise in its west, with the Maldives to the south. On the Indian side, look for the green, palm-lined Kerala coast with its maze of backwater lagoons.","w":"Bright blue water south-west of India, between the Kerala coast, Sri Lanka and the small ring-shaped atolls of Lakshadweep.","f":["Kanyakumari, the southern tip of mainland India, looks out over it.","The Gulf of Mannar, between India and Sri Lanka, opens off its eastern side.","It is also known as the Lakshadweep Sea."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Lakshadweep mean?","a":"A hundred thousand islands","x":["Islands of coconuts","Islands of the moon"]}},"Q183455":{"t":"A remote icy sea off the Antarctic Peninsula","s":"Few people ever see the Bellingshausen Sea: it lies off the western side of the Antarctic Peninsula, far from regular shipping lanes. In winter it is a plain of pack ice; in summer, open water scattered with icebergs laps against ice shelves and the snowy mountains of Alexander Island, the largest island in Antarctica.","w":"Pack ice or iceberg-dotted water west of the Antarctic Peninsula, with the white bulk of Alexander Island to the east.","f":["It is named after Fabian Gottlieb von Bellingshausen, whose Russian expedition sighted Antarctica in 1820.","The waters west of the Antarctic Peninsula are among the fastest-warming places on Earth."],"q":{"q":"Bellingshausen's 1820 expedition sailed for which country?","a":"Russia","x":["Britain","Norway"]}},"Q72154":{"t":"Where West Antarctica's great glaciers meet the sea","s":"The Amundsen Sea is remote even by Antarctic standards, often choked with thick pack ice. Yet scientists watch it closely: two of the continent's biggest, fastest-changing glaciers, Thwaites and Pine Island, flow into it, and warm deep water is melting them from below. What happens here could raise sea levels around the world.","w":"Heavy pack ice and floating glacier tongues along the coast of West Antarctica, with nothing beyond but the ice sheet.","f":["Thwaites Glacier is roughly as big as Great Britain.","The sea is named after Roald Amundsen, the first person to reach the South Pole, in 1911."],"q":{"q":"Which large glacier flows into the Amundsen Sea?","a":"Thwaites Glacier","x":["Lambert Glacier","Beardmore Glacier"]}},"Q189262":{"t":"The wide gateway between Greenland and Baffin Island","s":"Many transatlantic flights cross the Davis Strait, the wide channel between Greenland and Baffin Island. Even at its narrowest it is more than 300 km across. On the Greenland side, glaciers and bare rock line a fjord-cut coast; in spring and summer, icebergs drift south through the strait.","w":"Wide dark water between Greenland's glacier-cut west coast and the mountains of Baffin Island, often dotted with ice.","f":["It is named after John Davis, an English navigator who sailed it in the 1580s.","Nuuk, Greenland's capital, lies on its eastern shore.","It links Baffin Bay in the north with the Labrador Sea in the south."],"q":{"q":"Which city lies on the Greenland shore of the Davis Strait?","a":"Nuuk","x":["Reykjavík","Iqaluit"]}},"Q33629":{"t":"A frozen sea fed by two of Siberia's great rivers","s":"The Kara Sea is one of the coldest seas on Earth, iced over for most of the year. Two giant Siberian rivers, the Ob and the Yenisei, pour into it through estuaries hundreds of kilometres long, freshening its surface. To the west, the long mountain spine of Novaya Zemlya walls it off from the Barents Sea.","w":"Ice-covered sea north of western Siberia, between Novaya Zemlya and the Taymyr Peninsula; long river estuaries in the south.","f":["The Gulf of Ob, the estuary of the Ob, reaches some 800 km inland.","It takes its name from the small Kara River, which flows into its south-western corner.","Severnaya Zemlya, on its eastern edge, was one of the last archipelagos to be mapped, in 1930–32."],"q":{"q":"Which two great rivers flow into the Kara Sea?","a":"The Ob and the Yenisei","x":["The Lena and the Kolyma","The Volga and the Pechora"]}},"Q7988":{"t":"The Arctic's ice factory, off the Lena Delta","s":"The Laptev Sea is one of the Arctic's great ice factories: offshore winds push ice away from the coast, the open water refreezes, and the new ice drifts off towards the pole. On its southern shore spreads the Lena Delta, the largest in the Arctic — in summer, a green fan laced with thousands of lakes and channels.","w":"Pack ice or grey water off northern Siberia; to the south, the huge fan of the Lena Delta, speckled with lakes.","f":["It is named after the cousins Khariton and Dmitry Laptev, who charted this coast in the 1730s and 1740s.","The New Siberian Islands mark its eastern edge."],"q":{"q":"Which river's delta lies on the Laptev Sea?","a":"The Lena","x":["The Ob","The Yenisei"]}},"Q186733":{"t":"A huge bite out of Australia, edged by cliffs","s":"From the air, Australia's southern coast looks as if a giant bite was taken out of it: this is the Great Australian Bight. Along its head, the flat, treeless Nullarbor Plain ends abruptly in the Bunda Cliffs, pale walls of limestone that run unbroken for about 100 km above the dark ocean swell.","w":"A vast curve in Australia's southern coastline; look for a long, straight line of pale cliffs at the edge of a flat desert plain.","f":["The Bunda Cliffs rise up to about 120 m straight out of the sea.","Southern right whales gather at the Head of Bight each winter to give birth.","Nullarbor comes from the Latin for “no tree”."],"q":{"q":"What does the name of the Nullarbor Plain mean?","a":"No tree","x":["Red earth","Great plain"]}},"Q44725":{"t":"A rich, cold sea fenced by a chain of volcanoes","s":"Along its southern edge runs the Aleutian arc: a chain of volcanic islands, many topped with snowy cones, curving nearly 2,000 km towards Kamchatka and sealing off the Bering Sea. North of it lies one of the richest fishing grounds on Earth. In the ice ages its shallow northern part was dry land — the bridge by which people first reached the Americas.","w":"Wide grey-blue sea between Alaska and Russia's Far East, bounded in the south by the long chain of the Aleutian volcanoes.","f":["It is named after Vitus Bering, a Danish navigator in Russian service who explored it in 1728.","The Pribilof Islands hold the largest breeding colony of northern fur seals.","In winter its sea ice can spread hundreds of kilometres south."],"q":{"q":"What was the Bering Sea's northern floor during the ice ages?","a":"A land bridge","x":["A glacier","A freshwater lake"]}},"Q169274":{"t":"The only sea without a single shore","s":"No coast bounds the Sargasso Sea: its edges are ocean currents, including the Gulf Stream, circling slowly around it. Inside that loop the water is warm, calm and startlingly clear and blue. Mats of golden-brown sargassum seaweed drift on the surface, and eels from rivers in Europe and North America swim thousands of kilometres here to spawn.","w":"Deep, clear blue open ocean east of Bermuda; on a calm day, streaks of golden-brown seaweed may show on the surface.","f":["Bermuda lies on its western edge.","Its name comes from the Portuguese word for the floating seaweed.","Columbus's crews sailed through its weed in 1492 and feared they would run aground."],"q":{"q":"What forms the borders of the Sargasso Sea?","a":"Ocean currents","x":["Coral reefs","Volcanic islands"]}},"ne-lk-1159106739":{"t":"The far eastern arm of Great Slave Lake","s":"McLeod Bay is the easternmost reach of Great Slave Lake's East Arm, a long corridor of dark, cold water between the rocky, cliff-lined shores of the Canadian Shield. Beyond its eastern tip the forest thins towards the treeless Barren Lands. The water here is clear and ice-bound for much of the year, and very few people live along its shores.","w":"A long, narrow arm of water at the eastern end of Great Slave Lake, with dark rocky shores and scattered islands.","f":["At its eastern tip, the explorer George Back built Fort Reliance in 1833.","The Lockhart River flows into its eastern end.","Its shores lie within Thaidene Nëné, a national park reserve created in 2019."],"q":{"q":"Which lake is McLeod Bay part of?","a":"Great Slave Lake","x":["Great Bear Lake","Lake Athabasca"]}},"Q736707":{"t":"The small round lake between Huron and Erie","s":"Between the huge lakes Huron and Erie sits this small, roundish lake, a link in the chain. Its water arrives from the north through the St Clair River, which splits into a bird's-foot delta of marshes and channels, and leaves past Detroit down the Detroit River. For its size it is astonishingly shallow — about 3 m on average.","w":"A small round lake between Detroit and Ontario, with a fan of marshy delta channels on its north-eastern shore.","f":["French explorers named it in 1679, on the feast day of Saint Clare.","A channel is dredged across it so that big lake freighters can pass.","Its delta, the St Clair Flats, is the largest delta in the Great Lakes system."],"q":{"q":"Which city lies on Lake Saint Clair's south-western shore?","a":"Detroit","x":["Cleveland","Toronto"]}},"Q202905":{"t":"Florida's big, round, shallow “inland sea”","s":"In the middle of southern Florida sits a nearly round lake so big it looks like a patch of sea — Okeechobee, “big water” in the Hitchiti language. It is remarkably shallow, under 3 m deep on average, and is ringed by a grassy dike. Historically its overflow spilled south as a slow sheet of water through the Everglades.","w":"A big, almost circular lake in south-central Florida, ringed by a levee, with green sugar-cane fields to the south.","f":["It is the largest freshwater lake in Florida, covering about 1,900 km².","The Herbert Hoover Dike runs for about 230 km around it.","Canals link it to both the Atlantic and the Gulf of Mexico."],"q":{"q":"What does the name “Okeechobee” mean?","a":"Big water","x":["Sweet water","Sunny lake"]}},"Q1640500":{"t":"A lake that drains two ways at once","s":"Wollaston Lake does something almost no lake does: it drains in two directions at once. One outlet, the Fond du Lac River, flows towards Lake Athabasca and on to the Mackenzie; the other, the Cochrane River, heads for Reindeer Lake and eventually Hudson Bay. It is the largest lake on Earth with two natural outlets like this.","w":"A large, island-studded lake in north-eastern Saskatchewan, set in dark forest on the rocky Canadian Shield.","f":["Water from its two outlets ends up thousands of kilometres apart — in the Beaufort Sea and in Hudson Bay.","Some of the world's richest uranium deposits lie near its shores."],"q":{"q":"What is unusual about Wollaston Lake?","a":"It drains in two directions","x":["It never freezes","It lies below sea level"]}},"Q965436":{"t":"Alaska's largest lake, home to freshwater seals","s":"Iliamna is Alaska's largest lake, a long sheet of cold water between tundra hills and the volcanoes of the Aleutian Range. It holds a rare population of harbour seals that live in fresh water all year. Every summer millions of sockeye salmon swim up from Bristol Bay to spawn along its shores and streams, and in winter it freezes white.","w":"A long lake near the base of the Alaska Peninsula, with snowy volcanoes rising to the east.","f":["Its water flows out through the Kvichak River into Bristol Bay.","Bristol Bay's sockeye salmon run is the largest in the world.","Local legend tells of “Illie”, a giant creature said to live in its depths."],"q":{"q":"Which unusual animals live in Iliamna Lake year-round?","a":"Harbour seals","x":["Beluga whales","Sea otters"]}},"Q169962":{"t":"A deep, clear alpine lake high in the Sierra Nevada","s":"Lake Tahoe sits almost 1,900 m up in the Sierra Nevada, a deep blue oval ringed by pine forest and, for much of the year, snowy peaks. Its colour comes from depth and clarity: the lake drops to 501 m, and you can often see more than 20 m down into it. The California–Nevada border runs right through it.","w":"A deep-blue oval lake in the mountains west of Reno, surrounded by forest and snow-capped peaks.","f":["It is the second-deepest lake in the United States, after Crater Lake.","The California–Nevada border bends at an angle in the middle of the lake.","Emerald Bay, on its south-western shore, is famous for its green-blue water."],"q":{"q":"Which two states share Lake Tahoe?","a":"California and Nevada","x":["Oregon and Nevada","California and Arizona"]}},"Q1801110":{"t":"Wisconsin's big lake, beside a famous airshow town","s":"Pilots know this lake: on its western shore lies Oshkosh, where each summer the EAA AirVenture fly-in fills the sky with thousands of aircraft, making its airport briefly one of the busiest on Earth. Lake Winnebago is the largest lake entirely within Wisconsin, and in winter anglers cut holes in its ice to spear giant lake sturgeon.","w":"A large, oblong lake in eastern Wisconsin, with Oshkosh on the west shore and Fond du Lac at the southern tip.","f":["It is shallow for its size, averaging only about 5 m deep.","The Fox River carries its water north to Green Bay and Lake Michigan.","A limestone ridge, part of the Niagara Escarpment, runs along its eastern shore."],"q":{"q":"Which famous aviation event takes place beside Lake Winnebago?","a":"EAA AirVenture Oshkosh","x":["The Farnborough Airshow","The Reno Air Races"]}},"Q1132211":{"t":"The largest lake on any island, astride the Arctic Circle","s":"Nettilling Lake lies on Baffin Island, and no lake on any island in the world is larger. The Arctic Circle runs right across it, and ice covers it for most of the year. Its name comes from the Inuktitut word for the ringed seal — and seals do sometimes swim up into it from the sea.","w":"A huge, irregular lake on the flat tundra of southern Baffin Island, white with ice for much of the year.","f":["It covers about 5,500 km², roughly twice the size of Luxembourg.","It drains west through the Koukdjuak River into Foxe Basin.","It is the largest lake in Nunavut."],"q":{"q":"What makes Nettilling Lake special?","a":"The largest lake on any island","x":["The deepest lake in Canada","It never freezes"]}},"Q1262967":{"t":"A remote tundra lake in Canada's Barren Lands","s":"Dubawnt Lake lies deep in the Barren Lands, the treeless tundra west of Hudson Bay, far from any road or town. From above, look at the land around it: long, winding gravel ridges called eskers snake across the tundra, left by rivers that once flowed beneath the ice sheet. Ice often lingers on the lake itself into July.","w":"A large, irregular lake on the bare tundra of southern Nunavut; look for long, winding esker ridges crossing the land.","f":["In 1893 Joseph Tyrrell crossed the Barren Lands by canoe, travelling down the Dubawnt River.","Its water flows by the Dubawnt and Thelon rivers to Baker Lake and on to Hudson Bay."],"q":{"q":"What are the long, winding ridges around Dubawnt Lake?","a":"Eskers","x":["Lava flows","Sand dunes"]}},"Q1391737":{"t":"A tundra lake near the very centre of Canada","s":"Baker Lake sits almost at the geographic centre of Canada, on open tundra where the Thelon River ends its long journey from the west. On its shore stands the hamlet of Baker Lake, the only inland community in Nunavut and a well-known centre of Inuit art. Eastward, the long Chesterfield Inlet carries its water to Hudson Bay.","w":"A broad lake on flat tundra west of Hudson Bay, joined to the bay by the long, narrow Chesterfield Inlet.","f":["The hamlet's Inuktitut name, Qamani'tuaq, means roughly “where the river widens”.","Its artists are known worldwide for prints and colourful wall hangings.","Chesterfield Inlet, a fjord-like arm of Hudson Bay, reaches more than 150 km inland to the lake."],"q":{"q":"What makes the hamlet of Baker Lake unique in Nunavut?","a":"It is the only inland community","x":["It is the territorial capital","It is the northernmost town"]}},"Q68467":{"t":"A long valley lake between two mountain ranges","s":"Lake Champlain lies in a long valley like a blue ribbon, about 190 km from end to end, with the Adirondacks rising to the west and Vermont's Green Mountains to the east. Samuel de Champlain reached it in 1609 and gave it his name. It drains north: its water leaves through the Richelieu River into Quebec and the St Lawrence.","w":"A long, narrow north–south lake between the Adirondacks and the Green Mountains, with Burlington on its eastern shore.","f":["In 1998 a US law briefly declared it a Great Lake; the label was dropped within weeks.","Local legend tells of “Champ”, a monster said to live in its depths.","Its northern tip reaches into Quebec."],"q":{"q":"What did a 1998 US law briefly declare Lake Champlain to be?","a":"A Great Lake","x":["A national park","An inland sea"]}},"Q1089891":{"t":"Where the 1,600-km Churchill River begins","s":"Here, in a long lake in north-western Saskatchewan, the Churchill River begins its 1,600-km journey east to Hudson Bay. The lake is joined to its neighbour, Peter Pond Lake, by a narrow channel at the village of Buffalo Narrows. Around it spreads the boreal forest, a dark carpet broken by countless smaller lakes that flash in the sun.","w":"A long lake in the forests of north-western Saskatchewan, joined at a narrow waist to Peter Pond Lake.","f":["The Churchill River was a major highway of the fur trade, linking Hudson Bay with the far north-west.","The channel at Buffalo Narrows is called the Kisis Channel."],"q":{"q":"Where does the river flowing out of Churchill Lake end?","a":"Hudson Bay","x":["The Beaufort Sea","Lake Winnipeg"]}},"Q1516443":{"t":"The “lake of the marten” in the northern forest","s":"Lac la Martre — French for “lake of the marten” — sprawls across the forest north-west of Great Slave Lake, its shores ragged with bays and islands. The Tłı̨chǫ community of Whatì, whose name also means “marten lake”, stands on its shore. In winter the lake becomes a flat white plain; in summer, its dark water mirrors the sky.","w":"A sprawling, many-bayed lake in the forest north-west of Great Slave Lake, scattered with islands.","f":["Whatì was itself called Lac La Martre until 1996, when it took back its Tłı̨chǫ name.","In 2021 an all-season road reached Whatì for the first time."],"q":{"q":"Which animal is Lac la Martre named after?","a":"The marten","x":["The beaver","The otter"]}},"Q12341531":{"t":"Lake Superior's doorway to the Soo Locks","s":"Whitefish Bay is the funnel at Lake Superior's eastern end: every freighter entering or leaving the lake passes through it, heading for the St Marys River and the Soo Locks at Sault Ste. Marie. From above, look for the sandy hook of Whitefish Point at its north-western tip, with the forested Ontario shore opposite.","w":"A broad bay at the south-eastern corner of Lake Superior, between Michigan's Upper Peninsula and Ontario.","f":["The lighthouse at Whitefish Point, first lit in 1849, is the oldest active light on Lake Superior.","In spring, thousands of migrating hawks and other birds funnel past Whitefish Point."],"q":{"q":"Which locks do ships head for after crossing Whitefish Bay?","a":"The Soo Locks","x":["The Welland Canal locks","The Erie Canal locks"]}},"Q2211837":{"t":"The notch that gives Michigan's mitten its thumb","s":"Look at Michigan from above and the Lower Peninsula really is shaped like a mitten — and Saginaw Bay is the gap between the thumb and the fingers. The bay is shallow and warms quickly in summer, when its water can turn greenish. The Saginaw River, draining the largest river basin in Michigan, empties into its head.","w":"A long bay cutting south-west into Michigan from Lake Huron, between the Thumb and the rest of the Lower Peninsula.","f":["Its inner part is only a few metres deep.","Its shores hold some of the largest coastal wetlands on the Great Lakes."],"q":{"q":"Saginaw Bay separates the rest of Michigan from which feature?","a":"The Thumb","x":["The Upper Peninsula","The Keweenaw Peninsula"]}},"Q2000567":{"t":"Lake Huron's sheltered channel behind Manitoulin","s":"The North Channel is Lake Huron's quiet back room: a long, sheltered stretch of water tucked between Manitoulin Island and the rocky Ontario shore. Its coast is a maze of stony islands and coves loved by sailors. Near its eastern end, look for the La Cloche hills, whose white quartzite ridges can look like snow even in summer.","w":"A long channel of Lake Huron between big Manitoulin Island and the mainland, with white-ridged hills near its eastern end.","f":["Manitoulin Island, on its southern side, is the largest island in any freshwater lake.","Killarney Provincial Park protects the white quartzite ridges of the La Cloche Mountains."],"q":{"q":"Which island separates the North Channel from the rest of Lake Huron?","a":"Manitoulin Island","x":["Isle Royale","Wolfe Island"]}},"Q125309":{"t":"A shallow Sahel lake that breathes with the rains","s":"Lake Chad is less a lake than a vast, shifting marsh at the edge of the Sahara. It is so shallow that its size swings with each rainy season, and since the 1960s it has shrunk to a fraction of its former extent. From above, look for a patchwork of open water, green reed beds and islands, fed from the south by the Chari River.","w":"A patchwork of open water, green marsh and sandy islands at the southern edge of the Sahara, where four countries meet.","f":["The Chari River brings about 90% of its water.","Chad, Cameroon, Niger and Nigeria all border it.","Several thousand years ago a vast inland sea, known as Mega-Chad, spread across this basin."],"q":{"q":"Which river supplies most of Lake Chad's water?","a":"The Chari","x":["The Niger","The Nile"]}},"Q503842":{"t":"A turquoise lake with two names and marble caves","s":"This long lake reaches from the Andes out into the dry Patagonian steppe, its water a vivid glacial turquoise, and it answers to two names: General Carrera in Chile, Buenos Aires in Argentina. On its Chilean shore, waves have carved the Marble Caves, whose swirled stone reflects the blue water. It empties west through the Baker River, Chile's mightiest, to the Pacific.","w":"A long, vivid turquoise lake running east from the snowy Andes into the dry Patagonian steppe.","f":["It is the largest lake in Chile.","The Marble Caves can be reached only by boat."],"q":{"q":"What is Lago General Carrera called in Argentina?","a":"Lago Buenos Aires","x":["Lago Argentino","Lago Nahuel Huapi"]}},"Q826464":{"t":"A big blue lake beneath a perfect volcanic cone","s":"Look for Osorno: a snow-capped volcano so symmetrical it is often compared to Fuji, rising to 2,652 m above the eastern shore of Lake Llanquihue. Its neighbour Calbuco, rougher and broader, stands to the south-east. The lake itself is a broad blue sheet ringed by farmland, where German settlers arrived in the 1850s and founded towns such as Frutillar and Puerto Varas.","w":"A large, roundish blue lake in southern Chile, with the white cone of Osorno volcano on its eastern shore.","f":["It is the second-largest lake in Chile.","Glaciers cap Osorno's summit all year round."],"q":{"q":"Which volcano rises on Lake Llanquihue's eastern shore?","a":"Osorno","x":["Villarrica","Lanín"]}},"Q31845950":{"t":"A “suspended” lake held above the farmland by a dike","s":"Hongze Lake sits higher than the towns and fields beside it: its water is held back by the old Gaojia Dike along its eastern shore. Some nine hundred years ago there was no big lake here at all. It grew after the Yellow River shifted south and choked the Huai River's route to the sea with silt, and engineers then raised the dike to store the Huai's water.","w":"A broad, shallow, roughly rounded lake on the flat plain of northern Jiangsu, with a long dike along its eastern side.","f":["It is one of China's five largest freshwater lakes.","The Huai River flows in from the west and leaves towards the Yangtze and the sea."],"q":{"q":"Why is Hongze called a “suspended” lake?","a":"Its water sits above nearby land","x":["It lies in a mountain crater","It floats on salt water"]}},"Q192215":{"t":"Mongolia's “blue pearl”, little sister of Baikal","s":"Khövsgöl is a long, deep-blue lake in the mountains of northern Mongolia, some 200 km west of Baikal's southern tip — and in a sense its little sister, since its water flows out through the Egiin Gol and Selenga rivers into Baikal. It lies about 1,645 m up, fringed by larch forest, and in winter its ice is often more than a metre thick.","w":"A long north–south lake of deep blue among forested mountains near Mongolia's northern border, white with ice in winter.","f":["It holds about 70% of Mongolia's fresh water.","Its deepest point is 262 m."],"q":{"q":"Into which famous lake does Khövsgöl's water eventually flow?","a":"Lake Baikal","x":["Lake Balkhash","Issyk-Kul"]}},"Q213769":{"t":"Finland's deepest lake — and Helsinki's tap","s":"Päijänne is a long, narrow lake stretching north–south for about 120 km through the forests of central Finland. It is the country's deepest, and its clean water supplies Helsinki through a tunnel blasted 120 km through bedrock. In its southern part, look for Pulkkilanharju, a slender gravel ridge left by the ice age that carries a road across the water.","w":"A long, slender, island-dotted lake running north–south through central Finland, from Jyväskylä towards Lahti.","f":["At about 95 m, it is the deepest lake in Finland.","The Päijänne Water Tunnel has carried water to the Helsinki region since 1982.","It is the second-largest lake in Finland, after Saimaa."],"q":{"q":"Which city gets its drinking water from Päijänne by tunnel?","a":"Helsinki","x":["Tampere","Turku"]}},"Q504433":{"t":"A lake on the long road of the Congo River","s":"Lake Mweru is one link in the vast chain of the Congo River system. At its southern end the Luapula River fans out into a swampy green delta; at the north, the Luvua carries the water on towards the Congo. The border between Zambia and the Democratic Republic of the Congo runs down the middle, and fishing villages line both shores.","w":"A broad lake on the Zambia–Congo border, with a green, swampy delta at its southern end.","f":["David Livingstone reached its shores in 1867.","Its outflow, the Luvua, joins the Lualaba, as the upper Congo is called."],"q":{"q":"Which great river system does Lake Mweru belong to?","a":"The Congo","x":["The Nile","The Zambezi"]}},"Q201843":{"t":"A shallow border lake of lotus and cranes","s":"Lake Khanka is broad but astonishingly shallow — only a few metres deep — so the wind keeps its water a cloudy, sandy colour. The border between Russia and China crosses it, and on the Chinese side a narrow sand bar walls off a smaller companion lake. Its marshy shores hold nesting cranes and, in summer, pink lotus in bloom.","w":"A broad, pale, roundish lake on the Russia–China border north of Vladivostok, ringed by flat marshland.","f":["Its water drains through the Sungacha River to the Ussuri and on to the Amur.","Wild lotus, rare this far north, blooms in its bays.","The wetlands around it are protected on both sides of the border."],"q":{"q":"Which two countries share Lake Khanka?","a":"Russia and China","x":["Russia and Mongolia","China and North Korea"]}},"Q199938":{"t":"The lake that swells as its river runs backwards","s":"Tonlé Sap does something extraordinary: each rainy season the Mekong rises so high that the river draining this lake reverses and flows back into it. The lake swells to several times its dry-season size, drowning forests and fields in brown water. Floating villages rise and fall with it, and the temples of Angkor lie just north of its shore.","w":"A large, brown, shallow lake in the heart of Cambodia; much larger in the wet season, with flooded forest around it.","f":["Its fish are a main source of protein for millions of Cambodians.","In the dry season much of it is only about a metre deep.","Each autumn Cambodia's Water Festival marks the moment the river turns back towards the Mekong."],"q":{"q":"What happens to the Tonlé Sap River in the rainy season?","a":"It reverses direction","x":["It dries up completely","It turns salty"]}},"Q1144905":{"t":"The shallow southern end of the Peipus lake system","s":"Three lakes stand in a row here: huge Lake Peipus in the north, the narrow Lämmijärv in the middle and, at the southern end, Lake Pskov — Pihkva in Estonian. The Estonia–Russia border crosses it. The lake is shallow, under 4 m on average, and freezes over in winter; the Velikaya River flows in after passing the old city of Pskov.","w":"A shallow lake south of the huge Lake Peipus, joined to it by a narrow strait; the Velikaya delta at its south-eastern corner.","f":["Together with Lake Peipus, it forms one of the largest lakes in Europe.","The small Talabsk Islands in its middle hold old fishing villages."],"q":{"q":"Which larger lake is Lake Pskov joined to?","a":"Lake Peipus","x":["Lake Ladoga","Lake Ilmen"]}},"ne-lk-1159113481":{"t":"A Karelian lake at the cradle of the Kalevala","s":"Sredneye Kuito — “Middle Kuito” — is the central link in a chain of three lakes stretching some 140 km across the forests of northern Karelia, filling long cracks in the ancient bedrock. On its northern shore stands the settlement of Kalevala, called Ukhta until 1963. In the villages around here, Elias Lönnrot collected many of the old songs he wove into the Finnish epic Kalevala.","w":"A long, island-studded lake among the dark forests of northern Karelia, part of a chain of lakes running west to east.","f":["Its water flows east through Lower Kuito into the Kem River and on to the White Sea.","The Kuito lakes are tectonic: they fill cracks in the Earth's crust."],"q":{"q":"Which epic is linked to the villages around the Kuito lakes?","a":"The Kalevala","x":["The Edda","Beowulf"]}},"Q184492":{"t":"The sea bay that became a lake as Sweden rose","s":"Mälaren is a sprawling lake of bays and islands stretching about 120 km west from Stockholm. A thousand years ago it was still an arm of the Baltic; as the land slowly rose after the ice age, it was cut off and turned fresh. Stockholm grew up exactly where lake meets sea, and the city's islands scatter across the water at the lake's eastern end.","w":"A long, branching lake of bays and wooded islands running west from Stockholm, with the city's islands at its eastern end.","f":["Birka, a Viking-age trading town on the island of Björkö, is a UNESCO World Heritage site.","Drottningholm, home of the Swedish royal family, stands on one of its islands.","It supplies drinking water to Stockholm and the surrounding region."],"q":{"q":"What was Mälaren a thousand years ago?","a":"An arm of the Baltic Sea","x":["A glacier","A river valley"]}},"Q216621":{"t":"A steppe lake where the Irtysh sets off on its long trip","s":"Water that enters Lake Zaysan is at the start of a very long journey: the Black Irtysh flows in from China and leaves as the Irtysh, bound for the Ob and the Arctic Ocean. The lake lies in a broad basin in eastern Kazakhstan, with the Altai to the north and the Saur and Tarbagatai ranges to the south. Since the 1960s a dam downstream has raised its level.","w":"A broad, elongated lake in the steppe of eastern Kazakhstan, between the Altai and the Saur–Tarbagatai ranges.","f":["The Bukhtarma Dam on the Irtysh has held its level higher since the 1960s, making it part of a vast reservoir.","It freezes over for several months every winter."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows out of Lake Zaysan?","a":"The Irtysh","x":["The Ili","The Yenisei"]}},"Q1640519":{"t":"A desert basin filled to keep Baghdad dry","s":"Lake Tharthar is a lake by design. It fills a natural depression in the desert between the Tigris and the Euphrates, and since the 1950s engineers have diverted the Tigris's floodwater into it to protect Baghdad downstream. From above it's a large sheet of blue in the tan desert, its shores bare and pale.","w":"A large blue lake in the desert north-west of Baghdad, between the Tigris and the Euphrates.","f":["It is the largest lake in Iraq.","A canal from the Tigris near Samarra feeds it.","Other canals can send its water on to the Euphrates."],"q":{"q":"Why was Tigris water diverted into Lake Tharthar?","a":"To protect Baghdad from floods","x":["To irrigate date farms","To make a shipping route"]}},"Q756106":{"t":"The “Eye of Quebec”: a ring lake in an ancient crater","s":"Few lakes are as unmistakable from the air: a near-perfect ring of water about 70 km across, circling a huge island. It marks an impact crater blasted by an asteroid around 214 million years ago and worn down ever since. The ring filled to its present size after a dam was built in the 1960s, earning it the nickname “the Eye of Quebec”.","w":"An unmistakable ring of water around a large, round island in the forests of eastern Quebec.","f":["The Daniel-Johnson Dam, which raised the lake, is the largest multiple-arch-and-buttress dam in the world.","Astronauts often photograph it — the ring is easy to spot from orbit."],"q":{"q":"What formed the Manicouagan ring?","a":"An asteroid impact","x":["A collapsed volcano","A melting glacier"]}},"Q1638191":{"t":"A white salt lake in the heart of the Western Desert","s":"In the red sand country of Western Australia's deserts, Kumpupintil shows up as a vast white pan, glaring under the sun and usually dry. Its former English name, Lake Disappointment, dates from 1897, when the explorer Frank Hann followed creeks here hoping for fresh water and found salt. For the Martu people, whose country this is, it is a place of great significance.","w":"A large white salt pan dotted with dark islands, surrounded by dune desert in inland Western Australia.","f":["The Canning Stock Route, one of the world's most remote tracks, passes nearby.","After rare heavy rains, shallow water can spread across it for months.","In 2020 its Martu name, Kumpupintil, became the lake's official name."],"q":{"q":"Why did Frank Hann name it Lake Disappointment?","a":"He found salt, not fresh water","x":["He lost his horses there","It was smaller than on his map"]}},"Q1074063":{"t":"A glittering salt lake on the WA–NT border","s":"From cruise height Lake Mackay looks like a giant white stain spilled across red desert, its edges lacy with inlets and its surface dotted with small islands. It is one of Australia's largest salt lakes, yet most years it holds little or no water. The ruler-straight border between Western Australia and the Northern Territory runs right across it.","w":"A huge, ragged white salt pan in the red dune desert where Western Australia meets the Northern Territory.","f":["The Pintupi people call it Wilkinkarra.","It is named after explorer Donald Mackay, who reached it in 1930 and was among the first to cycle around Australia.","It covers about 3,500 km² and is roughly 100 km across."],"q":{"q":"What do the Pintupi people call Lake Mackay?","a":"Wilkinkarra","x":["Kumpupintil","Kati Thanda"]}},"Q965973":{"t":"A dazzling salt pan where racers chase speed","s":"Lake Gairdner is a salt pan some 160 km long, so white it can hurt the eyes, lying at the northern foot of the rust-red Gawler Ranges. Its crust is more than a metre thick in places, hard and flat enough that each year drivers and riders come to race across it during Speed Week, chasing records on the salt.","w":"A long, dazzling white salt lake in South Australia, just north of the reddish Gawler Ranges.","f":["It lies within Lake Gairdner National Park.","It is South Australia's third-largest salt lake, after Eyre and Torrens."],"q":{"q":"Why do racers come to Lake Gairdner?","a":"Its hard, flat salt crust","x":["Its steep sand dunes","Its frozen winter surface"]}},"Q163855":{"t":"A long salt lake that almost never fills","s":"Lake Torrens runs as a long, pale streak along the western foot of the Flinders Ranges, a salt pan that has filled with water only a handful of times in recorded history. Early colonial explorers, seeing it from the hills, imagined a vast horseshoe of salt lakes barring the way north — a belief that held for years.","w":"A long, narrow white salt pan running north–south just west of the rugged Flinders Ranges in South Australia.","f":["It is named after Robert Torrens, chairman of the commission that founded the colony of South Australia.","It is South Australia's second-largest salt lake, after Kati Thanda–Lake Eyre.","The lake and its shores form Lake Torrens National Park."],"q":{"q":"Which mountain range rises just east of Lake Torrens?","a":"The Flinders Ranges","x":["The MacDonnell Ranges","The Gawler Ranges"]}},"Q626907":{"t":"A salt pan between the Flinders Ranges and red dunes","s":"On the far side of the Flinders Ranges from Lake Torrens lies its twin: Lake Frome, a flat white salt pan about 100 km long. To its west rise the rugged ridges of the northern Flinders; to its east, lines of red sand dunes march away into the Strzelecki Desert. Water covers it only after rare heavy rains.","w":"A long white salt pan east of the northern Flinders Ranges, bordered on the east by parallel red dunes.","f":["It is named after Edward Frome, Surveyor-General of South Australia in the 1840s.","The Arkaroola wilderness sanctuary lies in the ranges just to its west."],"q":{"q":"Which desert's red dunes border Lake Frome to the east?","a":"The Strzelecki Desert","x":["The Gibson Desert","The Great Victoria Desert"]}},"ne-lk-1159126435":{"t":"A super-salty lagoon behind a ribbon of sand","s":"Along the coast of Tamaulipas, a thin ribbon of sandy barrier islands runs for well over 100 km, and behind it lies Laguna Madre: a long, shallow lagoon that is saltier than the sea, because little fresh water flows in and the sun evaporates much of it. In winter it teems with ducks, including a large share of the world's redheads.","w":"A long, shallow, greenish lagoon on Mexico's Gulf coast south of the Rio Grande, walled off from the sea by thin sandy islands.","f":["It is one of the few hypersaline coastal lagoons in the world.","The Rio Grande delta separates it from its twin, the Laguna Madre of Texas."],"q":{"q":"Why is Laguna Madre saltier than the sea?","a":"Strong evaporation, little fresh water","x":["Salt springs on its floor","Salt mines drain into it"]}},"Q503301":{"t":"An accidental lake in a desert below sea level","s":"The Salton Sea is an accident. In 1905 the Colorado River burst through an irrigation canal and poured into this desert basin for almost two years, creating California's largest lake. Its surface lies about 70 m below sea level, and with no outlet it has grown saltier than the Pacific. Look for green farm fields crowding its southern shore.","w":"A long blue lake in the desert south-east of Palm Springs, with the green grid of the Imperial Valley to the south.","f":["It is a vital stop for migrating birds on the Pacific Flyway.","As it shrinks, it exposes wide, pale bands of dried lake bed."],"q":{"q":"How did the Salton Sea form in 1905–1907?","a":"The Colorado River broke into it","x":["A glacier melted","An earthquake opened a spring"]}},"Q4121":{"t":"A sea turned into a lake by one long dam","s":"Until 1932 this was the Zuiderzee, a stormy arm of the North Sea. Then the Afsluitdijk, a dam 32 km long, sealed it off, and the salt water slowly turned fresh. From above, the dam is a razor-straight line across the water, and to the east and south lie Flevoland's polders — ruler-drawn fields on what was once the seabed.","w":"A broad, grey-green lake in the Netherlands, closed off from the Wadden Sea in the north by a long, perfectly straight dam.","f":["Flevoland, built on land reclaimed from it, became the youngest Dutch province in 1986.","It is one of the largest freshwater lakes in Western Europe.","A second dam, the Houtribdijk, splits off its southern part, the Markermeer."],"q":{"q":"What was the IJsselmeer before 1932?","a":"A sea bay, the Zuiderzee","x":["A peat bog","A river valley"]}},"ne-lk-1159126643":{"t":"The pink lagoons of the “Rotten Sea”","s":"Few sights from the air are stranger than Syvash: a maze of shallow lagoons between Crimea and the mainland, streaked pink, rust-red and milky green. The water is so salty that salt-loving algae tint it, and in summer heat it smells — hence its other name, the Rotten Sea. To the east, the thin Arabat Spit seals it off from the Sea of Azov.","w":"A maze of pink, red and green lagoons between Crimea and the mainland, fenced off from the Sea of Azov by a thin spit.","f":["The Arabat Spit, which separates it from the Sea of Azov, is about 110 km long.","Much of it is less than a metre deep.","Salt has been gathered along its shores for centuries."],"q":{"q":"What gives Syvash its pink and red colours?","a":"Salt-loving algae","x":["Iron-rich sand","Red clay from rivers"]}},"Q182719":{"t":"The Jade Sea in the desert of northern Kenya","s":"Lake Turkana is called the Jade Sea: in the right light, algae turn its water a striking green against the brown lava desert around it. It is the largest permanent lake in any desert, and its shores have yielded some of the most important fossils of early humans. Volcanic islands with crater lakes rise from its middle.","w":"A long, narrow green-blue lake in the barren north of Kenya, reaching north into Ethiopia, with dark volcanic islands.","f":["The Omo River, flowing in from Ethiopia, brings almost all of its water.","The skeleton of “Turkana Boy”, about 1.5 million years old, was found near its western shore in 1984.","Its old name, Lake Rudolf, was given in 1888 in honour of Crown Prince Rudolf of Austria."],"q":{"q":"What is Lake Turkana's nickname?","a":"The Jade Sea","x":["The Pearl of Africa","The Sea of Stars"]}},"Q5605":{"t":"A shallow lagoon behind a long ribbon of dunes","s":"Look for a long, slender sand spit, crested with forest and dunes, running parallel to the Baltic coast — and behind it a shallow, brownish-green lagoon. The Vistula Lagoon is on average less than 3 m deep, and its natural link to the sea is a single narrow strait at Baltiysk. At its eastern end, the Pregolya River flows in from Kaliningrad.","w":"A long, shallow lagoon along the Baltic coast near Kaliningrad, cut off from the sea by a thin wooded spit.","f":["The Vistula Spit that shelters it runs for about 65 km.","In winter the lagoon often freezes over.","Its water is brackish, a mix of river water and the Baltic."],"q":{"q":"Which city stands on the river entering the lagoon's eastern end?","a":"Kaliningrad","x":["Gdańsk","Klaipėda"]}},"Q81150":{"t":"Mongolia's biggest lake, a salty oval in the steppe","s":"Uvs Nuur is Mongolia's largest lake: a broad, salty oval in a closed basin where desert dunes, steppe, taiga and snowy peaks all lie within sight of one another. No river flows out. Winters here are bitterly cold and the lake stays frozen for months; in summer, its blue shimmers against the brown steppe.","w":"A large, oval, salty lake in the dry steppe of north-western Mongolia, ringed by the mountains of its basin.","f":["The Uvs Nuur Basin, shared with Russia's Tuva, is a UNESCO World Heritage site.","It lies about 750 m above sea level, at the lowest point of its basin."],"q":{"q":"Uvs Nuur is the largest lake in which country?","a":"Mongolia","x":["Kazakhstan","China"]}},"Q201294":{"t":"China's largest lake, blue on the high plateau","s":"At about 3,200 m on the edge of the Tibetan Plateau lies China's largest lake, a vast salty sheet of vivid blue. Its Chinese name, Qinghai, means “blue sea”, and the Mongolian name Kokonor means “blue lake” — the whole province is named after it. In July its shores glow with yellow rapeseed fields, framed by bare brown mountains.","w":"A huge, bright blue oval lake on the high, bare north-eastern edge of the Tibetan Plateau, west of Xining.","f":["Bird Island is a nesting ground for bar-headed geese, famous for flying over the Himalayas.","Its water is salty, though less so than the sea.","The Qinghai–Tibet Railway runs along its northern shore."],"q":{"q":"What does the Mongolian name Kokonor mean?","a":"Blue lake","x":["Salt sea","Sky mirror"]}},"Q2007652":{"t":"The “Lake of Salt” west of Karbala","s":"Lake Milh — Buhayrat al-Milh, the “Lake of Salt” — spreads across a desert depression west of Karbala. Floodwater from the Euphrates reaches it by canal via Lake Habbaniyah, but in recent decades it has shrunk sharply, leaving wide white salt flats around a smaller sheet of blue-green water. It is also known as Lake Razzaza.","w":"A shallow blue-green lake ringed by white salt flats in the desert west of Karbala, south-west of Baghdad.","f":["It is one of the largest lakes in Iraq.","As its water shrank, it grew too salty for most fish."],"q":{"q":"What does Buhayrat al-Milh mean?","a":"Lake of Salt","x":["Lake of Palms","Lake of Stars"]}},"Q204703":{"t":"A stormy sea ringed by a loop of sub-Antarctic islands","s":"The Scotia Sea is one of the stormiest stretches of water on Earth, swept by the Antarctic Circumpolar Current and dotted with icebergs. Its edges are traced by the Scotia Arc, a great loop of islands from South Georgia to the South Shetlands. In 1916 Ernest Shackleton crossed it in a small open boat to fetch help for his stranded crew.","w":"Grey, wind-whipped open ocean between South America and Antarctica, with icebergs and the snowy peaks of South Georgia.","f":["It is named after the Scotia, ship of the Scottish National Antarctic Expedition of 1902–04.","Shackleton's boat journey from Elephant Island to South Georgia covered more than 1,200 km.","Its cold waters swarm with Antarctic krill, food for whales and penguins."],"q":{"q":"What did Shackleton cross the Scotia Sea in, in 1916?","a":"A small open boat","x":["A steamship","A seaplane"]}},"Q41837":{"t":"The seaway between Arabia and the Horn of Africa","s":"Between the bare mountains of Yemen and the dry coast of Somalia stretches a long wedge of deep blue: the Gulf of Aden. Ships heading to and from the Suez Canal funnel through it. Beneath it runs a spreading ridge where Arabia and Africa are slowly pulling apart. At its eastern mouth lies Socotra, an island of strange, umbrella-shaped dragon's blood trees.","w":"A long, deep-blue gulf between Yemen's rugged coast and the Horn of Africa, narrowing west towards the Bab-el-Mandeb strait.","f":["The Bab-el-Mandeb strait at its western end is only about 30 km wide.","Socotra and its unique plants are a UNESCO World Heritage site."],"q":{"q":"Which famous trees grow on Socotra, off the Gulf of Aden?","a":"Dragon's blood trees","x":["Giant sequoias","Monkey puzzle trees"]}},"Q79948":{"t":"Gateway to the Gulf, beneath the Hajar Mountains","s":"The Gulf of Oman is the entrance hall to the Persian Gulf: tankers file through it towards the Strait of Hormuz. Along its south-western shore, the jagged, rust-brown Hajar Mountains drop almost straight into the sea, with Muscat tucked between them. In the north-west, the Musandam Peninsula frays into fjords that earned it the nickname “Norway of Arabia”.","w":"A wide gulf between Oman's rugged mountain coast and Iran, narrowing north-west towards the Strait of Hormuz.","f":["Tankers carrying a large share of the world's oil pass through it every day.","Musandam is an exclave of Oman, cut off from the rest of the country by the UAE."],"q":{"q":"Which peninsula is nicknamed the “Norway of Arabia”?","a":"Musandam","x":["Qatar","Sinai"]}},"Q38882":{"t":"A volcanic sea between Italy and its great islands","s":"The Tyrrhenian is ringed by volcanoes and islands: Italy's west coast, Corsica, Sardinia and Sicily enclose it. Above the Bay of Naples rises Vesuvius, and in the south the Aeolian Islands include Stromboli, which throws up small bursts of lava every few minutes. On a night flight, you might even catch its red glow.","w":"A deep-blue sea west of mainland Italy, framed by Corsica and Sardinia; volcanic islands north of Sicily.","f":["Its name comes from the Tyrrhenians, as the ancient Greeks called the Etruscans.","Sailors have long called Stromboli the “Lighthouse of the Mediterranean”.","Its deepest point lies about 3,800 m down."],"q":{"q":"Which volcano is known as the “Lighthouse of the Mediterranean”?","a":"Stromboli","x":["Etna","Vesuvius"]}},"Q44133":{"t":"Russia's northern inland sea, white for half the year","s":"The White Sea cuts deep into northern Russia, a branching inland sea that lives up to its name: it is covered with ice for about half the year. Near its western shores lie the Solovetsky Islands, where a monastery founded in the 15th century still stands. In the south-east, the Northern Dvina splits into a delta around Arkhangelsk, once Russia's chief seaport.","w":"A branching inland sea in Russia's far north, white with ice in winter; the Solovetsky Islands near its western shores.","f":["The Solovetsky Islands are a UNESCO World Heritage site.","Tides in its Mezen Bay can reach about 10 m.","The White Sea–Baltic Canal links it to Lake Onega and the Baltic."],"q":{"q":"Which city stands at the Northern Dvina delta on the White Sea?","a":"Arkhangelsk","x":["Murmansk","Petrozavodsk"]}},"Q223810":{"t":"Hudson Bay's shallow, river-fed southern pocket","s":"James Bay is the shallow southern pocket of Hudson Bay, fed by so many big rivers that its water is only mildly salty. Its shores are low and flat — a wide fringe of marsh and tidal mudflats where huge flocks of geese and shorebirds stop on migration. In winter it freezes over completely, and the frozen coast blurs into the white land beyond.","w":"A shallow bay at the southern end of Hudson Bay, between Ontario and Quebec, with low, marshy shores and many river mouths.","f":["It is named after Captain Thomas James, who explored it in 1631.","Moose Factory, a Hudson's Bay Company post founded in 1673, stands near its southern shore.","Akimiski, its largest island, is part of Nunavut."],"q":{"q":"James Bay is the southern extension of which body of water?","a":"Hudson Bay","x":["Baffin Bay","The Gulf of St Lawrence"]}},"Q180844":{"t":"Home of the Morning Glory roll clouds","s":"In September and October, glider pilots travel to the southern shore of the Gulf of Carpentaria for a rare sight: the Morning Glory, a tube-shaped roll cloud that can stretch for hundreds of kilometres and rolls in at dawn. The gulf itself is a broad, shallow, warm sea between Cape York and Arnhem Land, fringed with mangroves and mudflats.","w":"A huge, squarish bay in northern Australia, between Cape York Peninsula and Arnhem Land; muddy green near the coasts.","f":["It is named after Pieter de Carpentier, a governor-general of the Dutch East Indies.","Most of it is less than 70 m deep.","The Morning Glory is best seen near the small town of Burketown."],"q":{"q":"What is the Morning Glory over the Gulf of Carpentaria?","a":"A giant roll cloud","x":["A flowering vine","A red tide"]}},"Q132811":{"t":"The long sea behind Baja California","s":"The bare mountains of the Baja California peninsula wall off a long, narrow sea from the Pacific — about 1,100 km from end to end. It opened as Baja slowly tore away from the continent along the San Andreas fault system. Its water is famously rich in life; Jacques Cousteau called it “the aquarium of the world”.","w":"A long, narrow blue sea between the desert mountains of Baja California and mainland Mexico, dotted with bare islands.","f":["The vaquita, the world's smallest porpoise, lives only in its northern end.","Its islands and protected areas are a UNESCO World Heritage site.","It is also called the Sea of Cortés."],"q":{"q":"Which tiny, rare animal lives only in the Gulf of California?","a":"The vaquita porpoise","x":["The dugong","The Amazon river dolphin"]}},"Q207702":{"t":"The icy gateway into Hudson Bay","s":"Every ship bound for Hudson Bay from the Atlantic must pass through this long, icy strait between Baffin Island and northern Quebec. Henry Hudson sailed it in 1610. Its tides are fierce: in Ungava Bay, on its southern side, the sea can rise and fall by more than 15 m. For much of the year, drifting pack ice fills it from shore to shore.","w":"A long, wide strait between Baffin Island's rugged south coast and Quebec's Ungava Peninsula, often full of drifting ice.","f":["It is about 750 km long.","Ungava Bay has some of the highest tides on Earth."],"q":{"q":"Which bay on the Hudson Strait has some of the world's highest tides?","a":"Ungava Bay","x":["James Bay","Baffin Bay"]}},"Q34640":{"t":"The narrow, busy sea between England and France","s":"At its narrowest, the Strait of Dover, only about 33 km separate England from France — on a clear day the white chalk cliffs on both sides are easy to pick out. It is one of the busiest shipping lanes on Earth; look for the white wakes of ships lined up like traffic. Beneath the seabed, the Channel Tunnel links the two coasts.","w":"A long sea narrowing eastwards between England's south coast and northern France, busy with ships; chalk cliffs at the Dover end.","f":["The Channel Tunnel, opened in 1994, runs for about 38 km beneath the sea.","About 450,000 years ago a huge ice-age flood broke through a chalk ridge here, helping to cut Britain off.","The French call it La Manche, “the sleeve”."],"q":{"q":"About how wide is the Strait of Dover at its narrowest?","a":"About 33 km","x":["About 8 km","About 80 km"]}},"Q795608":{"t":"Turquoise cays where three countries meet the Caribbean","s":"Where the coasts of Belize, Guatemala and Honduras meet lies a sheltered corner of the Caribbean, the Gulf of Honduras. Scattered across it are low mangrove cays and the southern tip of the great Mesoamerican Barrier Reef, bright turquoise against the deep blue. Rivers from the green mountains inland tint the water brown near their mouths.","w":"A broad Caribbean inlet south of Belize, with turquoise reefs and small cays; the Bay Islands of Honduras lie to the east.","f":["Whale sharks, the largest fish on Earth, visit the waters off Utila in the Bay Islands.","Belize's Sapodilla Cayes, near its mouth, mark the southern end of the barrier reef.","The Motagua River from Guatemala empties into it."],"q":{"q":"Which three countries share the shores of the Gulf of Honduras?","a":"Belize, Guatemala, Honduras","x":["Mexico, Belize, Honduras","Honduras, Nicaragua, Guatemala"]}},"Q122574":{"t":"A nearly fresh northern gulf where the land still rises","s":"In the Baltic's long northern arm, between Sweden and Finland, the water is so fresh that in the far north it is barely salty. Each winter ice spreads across it, and in some years it freezes from shore to shore. The land here is still rising after the ice age, adding new islets and skerries to the Kvarken archipelago every century.","w":"A long grey-blue gulf between Sweden and Finland, white with ice in winter; an island-strewn narrow waist at Kvarken.","f":["Sweden's High Coast has risen about 286 m since the ice sheet melted — more than anywhere else known.","The High Coast and the Kvarken archipelago form a joint UNESCO World Heritage site.","Around Kvarken the land still rises by up to about 8 mm a year."],"q":{"q":"Why do new islands keep appearing in the Gulf of Bothnia?","a":"The land is still rising","x":["Volcanoes are erupting","Coral reefs are growing"]}},"Q13924":{"t":"A long blue sea between Italy and a coast of islands","s":"The Adriatic stretches for about 800 km between Italy's smooth coastline and the Balkans' jagged one. On the eastern side, the Croatian shore dissolves into long islands lying parallel to the coast — more than a thousand of them, counting islets and reefs. In the north the sea is shallow and greenish, with the Po delta and the lagoon of Venice on its western side.","w":"A long sea between Italy and the Balkans; the Croatian coast breaks into rows of long islands parallel to the shore.","f":["The Bora, a fierce cold wind, can sweep down from the mountains onto its eastern coast.","Its name comes from the ancient port of Adria, near the Po delta.","The Strait of Otranto, about 70 km wide, links it to the Ionian Sea."],"q":{"q":"Which ancient port gave the Adriatic Sea its name?","a":"Adria","x":["Aquileia","Ancona"]}},"Q81136":{"t":"The legendary sea route through the Arctic islands","s":"For centuries sailors dreamed of a shortcut from Europe to Asia over the top of North America. The Northwest Passage is that route: a maze of channels threading between the frozen islands of the Canadian Arctic. From above it's white ice cracked into floes, with dark open lanes in late summer. Roald Amundsen was the first to sail it, in 1903–06.","w":"Ice-choked channels winding between the brown and white islands of the Canadian Arctic; open water only in late summer.","f":["Amundsen's ship, the Gjøa, was a small former herring boat.","In 1944 the Canadian police schooner St. Roch became the first ship to cross it in a single season.","There are several possible routes, depending on where the ice allows a passage."],"q":{"q":"Who first sailed the Northwest Passage, in 1903–06?","a":"Roald Amundsen","x":["Fridtjof Nansen","Robert Peary"]}},"Q37495":{"t":"The deepest part of the Mediterranean","s":"Between the heel of Italy and the west coast of Greece lies the Ionian Sea, a clear, deep-blue expanse. Off Greece's south-western coast the floor plunges to about 5,270 m — the deepest point of the whole Mediterranean. Along its eastern side, look for the green, mountainous Ionian Islands: Corfu, Kefalonia and Zakynthos, fringed with pale cliffs.","w":"Deep-blue sea between southern Italy, Sicily and western Greece, with the green Ionian Islands along the Greek side.","f":["Loggerhead sea turtles nest on the beaches of Zakynthos.","One Greek legend links its name to Io, who swam across it.","The Strait of Otranto links it to the Adriatic in the north."],"q":{"q":"About how deep is the Mediterranean's deepest point, in the Ionian Sea?","a":"About 5,300 m","x":["About 2,000 m","About 9,000 m"]}},"Q169523":{"t":"A northern sea of red-cliffed islands and winter ice","s":"The Gulf of St Lawrence is where the Great Lakes' water finally meets the Atlantic — a broad inland sea ringed by Quebec, the Maritime provinces and Newfoundland. Look for Prince Edward Island's red cliffs and green fields, and the long, low Anticosti Island. In late winter, drifting pack ice fills much of the gulf, and harp seals give birth on it.","w":"A broad gulf east of Quebec; the crescent of Prince Edward Island in the south, long Anticosti Island in the north.","f":["Jacques Cartier sailed into it in 1534.","Anticosti Island is larger than the whole province of Prince Edward Island.","The Magdalen Islands, near its centre, are strung together by long sand dunes."],"q":{"q":"Which animals give birth on the gulf's pack ice in late winter?","a":"Harp seals","x":["Polar bears","Walruses"]}},"Q185291":{"t":"A sea squeezed between two volcanic arcs","s":"The Molucca Sea is being swallowed from both sides: the sea floor beneath it is sinking under two volcanic arcs at once, a rare arrangement geologists call double subduction. Look for perfect volcanic cones rising from the water, like Ternate and Tidore off Halmahera — islands where cloves originally grew.","w":"Deep-blue sea between Sulawesi's long northern arm and Halmahera, dotted with steep volcanic cone islands.","f":["Cloves originally grew on only a handful of islands here, including Ternate and Tidore.","The equator runs right across it."],"q":{"q":"Which spice originally grew on Ternate and Tidore?","a":"Cloves","x":["Vanilla","Saffron"]}},"Q199436":{"t":"A volcanic sea framed by the Bismarck Archipelago","s":"The Bismarck Sea is enclosed by a great arc of islands — New Britain, New Ireland and the Admiralty Islands — with the north coast of New Guinea to the south. Volcanoes rise along its edges, among them Manam, a near-perfect cone just off the mainland. Where the Sepik River meets the sea, its brown plume spreads into the blue.","w":"Deep-blue sea north of New Guinea, ringed by the long, mountainous islands of New Britain and New Ireland.","f":["It was named after Otto von Bismarck when the region became a German colony in the 1880s.","Manam, a volcano island off New Guinea, is one of the most active volcanoes in the region."],"q":{"q":"Which river's brown plume spreads into the Bismarck Sea?","a":"The Sepik","x":["The Fly","The Mekong"]}},"Q199479":{"t":"A deep tropical sea between New Guinea and the Solomons","s":"Framed by New Guinea's long eastern tail, the mountainous island of New Britain and the Solomon Islands, the Solomon Sea is warm, deep and dotted with coral islands. Along its northern edge, the New Britain Trench drops to more than 8,000 m. In the west lie the low, flat Trobriand Islands, ringed with turquoise reefs.","w":"Deep-blue sea east of New Guinea's tail, bounded by New Britain to the north and the Solomon Islands to the east.","f":["The Trobriand Islands became world-famous through the anthropologist Bronisław Malinowski, who lived there in the 1910s.","Its warm waters lie within the Coral Triangle, one of the richest reef regions on Earth."],"q":{"q":"Which anthropologist made the Trobriand Islands famous?","a":"Bronisław Malinowski","x":["Margaret Mead","Claude Lévi-Strauss"]}},"Q48359":{"t":"The narrow sea lane that links two oceans","s":"This long funnel of water between Sumatra and the Malay Peninsula links the Indian Ocean with the Pacific, and tens of thousands of ships squeeze through it every year. Look for their wakes, often in long lines. Near Singapore, where it meets the Singapore Strait, the shipping channel narrows to less than 3 km. The strait takes its name from Malacca, once a great trading city.","w":"A long, narrowing strait between Sumatra's forested coast and the Malay Peninsula, streaked with the wakes of many ships.","f":["It is about 900 km long.","In the 15th century the Sultanate of Malacca controlled the trade passing through it."],"q":{"q":"Which two land masses does the Strait of Malacca separate?","a":"Sumatra and the Malay Peninsula","x":["Java and Borneo","Sumatra and Java"]}},"Q194477":{"t":"The invisible line between Asian and Australian wildlife","s":"Down the middle of the Makassar Strait, between Borneo and Sulawesi, runs an invisible boundary: the Wallace Line. In the 1850s Alfred Russel Wallace noticed how different the animals on either side were — mostly Asian species to the west, more and more Australasian ones to the east. The strait is deep, and most of the Pacific water flowing through Indonesia into the Indian Ocean passes through it.","w":"A wide, deep strait between Borneo's green coast and the spidery outline of Sulawesi.","f":["Deep water here kept Borneo and Sulawesi apart even when ice-age sea levels were low.","Makassar, on Sulawesi's south-western coast, has been a major port for centuries."],"q":{"q":"Which famous biological boundary runs through the Makassar Strait?","a":"The Wallace Line","x":["The Humboldt Line","The Darwin Line"]}},"Q210855":{"t":"A remote sea between Seram and New Guinea's Bird's Head","s":"The Ceram Sea stretches between the long, mountainous island of Seram and the western tip of New Guinea, known as the Bird's Head. On its northern side lie the limestone islands of Misool, part of Raja Ampat, where reefs hold some of the richest marine life on Earth. From above, look for jungle-clad ridges plunging straight into deep blue water.","w":"Deep-blue sea between the long green island of Seram and the western tip of New Guinea, with small limestone islands to the north.","f":["Seram's highest mountain, Binaiya, rises to about 3,000 m.","“Ceram” is an old spelling of Seram, the island on its southern side."],"q":{"q":"What is the western tip of New Guinea, by the Ceram Sea, nicknamed?","a":"The Bird's Head","x":["The Dragon's Tail","The Lion's Paw"]}},"Q639399":{"t":"A remote Pacific gulf with record-breaking tides","s":"Shelikhov Gulf is the wild north-eastern corner of the Sea of Okhotsk, wedged between Kamchatka and the mainland. At its head, Penzhina Bay has the highest tides in the Pacific: the water can rise and fall by as much as 13 m, exposing vast mudflats. In winter the whole gulf is locked in drifting ice.","w":"A long gulf between Kamchatka's mountains and the mainland of Russia's Far East, white with ice in winter.","f":["It is named after Grigory Shelikhov, the merchant who founded the first permanent Russian settlement in Alaska in 1784.","Penzhina Bay's tides are the highest anywhere in Russia."],"q":{"q":"Where are the highest tides in the Pacific Ocean?","a":"Penzhina Bay","x":["Tokyo Bay","San Francisco Bay"]}},"Q163434":{"t":"A shallow, ice-bound sea off Siberia's far north-east","s":"The East Siberian Sea stretches from the New Siberian Islands to Wrangel Island, and for most of the year it is a white, frozen plain. It is also remarkably shallow, mostly less than 50 m deep, and in summer the brown water of the Indigirka and Kolyma spreads across its surface. Pack ice often clings to the coast even in August.","w":"Ice or grey water off the low tundra coast of north-eastern Siberia, between the New Siberian Islands and Wrangel Island.","f":["The Bear Islands, a small archipelago, lie off the mouth of the Kolyma.","The port of Pevek on its shore is often called Russia's northernmost town."],"q":{"q":"Which two rivers flow into the East Siberian Sea?","a":"The Indigirka and Kolyma","x":["The Ob and Yenisei","The Lena and Khatanga"]}},"Q845217":{"t":"A shallow Arctic basin with an island found from the air","s":"Foxe Basin is a shallow, ice-bound sea north of Hudson Bay, and its ice is sometimes tinged brown by sediment stirred up from the seafloor. It kept a secret for a long time: Prince Charles Island, roughly the size of Cyprus, was put on the map only in 1948, after it was spotted from an aircraft.","w":"A broad, shallow, often ice-covered sea west of Baffin Island, with low, flat islands in its eastern part.","f":["It is named after Luke Foxe, who explored it in 1631.","Its shallow waters are a key home for Atlantic walruses.","Igloolik, an Inuit community, lies on an island at its north-western corner."],"q":{"q":"How was Prince Charles Island in Foxe Basin discovered in 1948?","a":"From an aircraft","x":["By a whaling ship","By satellite"]}},"Q509321":{"t":"An iceberg-filled sea off East Antarctica","s":"The Davis Sea is a remote sea on the edge of East Antarctica, crowded with icebergs and fast ice for most of the year. Along its coast, the ice sheet ends in high white cliffs and the broad Shackleton Ice Shelf. On its shore stands Mirny, founded in 1956 as the first Soviet station in Antarctica.","w":"Pack ice and scattered icebergs along the edge of the East Antarctic ice sheet, which ends here in white ice cliffs.","f":["It is named after John King Davis, captain of the Aurora on Douglas Mawson's 1911–14 expedition.","Mirny, on its shore, still serves as a Russian research station."],"q":{"q":"Which station, founded in 1956, stands on the Davis Sea?","a":"Mirny","x":["McMurdo","Vostok"]}},"Q7347":{"t":"Five inland seas holding a fifth of Earth's fresh water","s":"From cruise height the Great Lakes look like seas, their far shores often lost in haze. Superior, Michigan, Huron, Erie and Ontario form the largest group of freshwater lakes on Earth, holding about a fifth of the world's fresh surface water. In winter, cold air crossing them builds bands of cloud and dumps heavy lake-effect snow on their shores.","w":"Five huge interconnected lakes on the US–Canada border, from Superior in the west to Ontario in the east.","f":["Water from Lake Superior drops about 100 m on its way to Lake Ontario, most of it along the Niagara River.","Together they hold about 84% of North America's fresh surface water.","Ships can sail from Lake Superior all the way to the Atlantic through locks and the St Lawrence Seaway."],"q":{"q":"Which of the Great Lakes is the largest by area?","a":"Lake Superior","x":["Lake Huron","Lake Michigan"]}},"Q7343":{"t":"Turquoise lace stitched along Australia's coast","s":"Look for patches of turquoise and pale lace scattered over deep navy water, far off the Queensland coast. Each is a reef built by living coral, and together they form the largest coral reef system on Earth, running for about 2,300 km. It is so big that astronauts can pick it out from orbit.","w":"Turquoise and cream patches on dark blue sea, well offshore, lined up parallel to the Queensland coast.","f":["It is made up of roughly 3,000 separate reefs and about 900 islands.","UNESCO listed the reef as a World Heritage site in 1981.","Once a year, a few nights after a full moon in October or November, its corals release their eggs almost all at once."],"q":{"q":"Roughly how long is the Great Barrier Reef?","a":"About 2,300 km","x":["About 800 km","About 5,000 km"]}},"Q118841":{"t":"A mile-deep gash with Earth's history on its walls","s":"Even from 11 km up the Grand Canyon looks like a wound in the plateau: a jagged chasm of red, orange and cream layers, with the Colorado River a thin green-brown thread at the bottom. It is about 446 km long and up to 29 km wide. In places the river runs some 1,800 metres below the rim.","w":"A branching maze of red and cream cliffs cutting the flat plateau, the river hidden deep inside.","f":["The oldest rocks at the bottom of the canyon are nearly two billion years old.","The North Rim is about 300 m higher than the South Rim, gets far more snow and closes to visitors in winter.","The Grand Canyon became a US national park in 1919."],"q":{"q":"Which river carved the Grand Canyon?","a":"The Colorado","x":["The Rio Grande","The Snake"]}},"Q43278":{"t":"A waterfall that announces itself with a cloud","s":"Before you see the falls you may see their cloud: a white plume of spray rising above the Zambezi, visible from dozens of kilometres away when the river is high. Below it the water drops into a narrow chasm about 1.7 km long, then zigzags through a chain of gorges. Locally it is called Mosi-oa-Tunya, 'the smoke that thunders'.","w":"A white mist above the Zambezi, then a sharp zigzag of gorges below it, on the Zambia–Zimbabwe border.","f":["About 1,700 m wide and over 100 m high, it is one of the largest sheets of falling water in the world.","David Livingstone reached the falls in 1855 and named them after Queen Victoria.","The zigzag gorges downstream are former lips of the falls, left behind as the water cut its way back upstream."],"q":{"q":"What does the local name Mosi-oa-Tunya mean?","a":"The smoke that thunders","x":["The river that sings","The great white wall"]}},"Q34221":{"t":"Where Lake Erie spills over a cliff towards Ontario","s":"From the air, look for a white smudge of spray between Lake Erie and Lake Ontario: that is the Horseshoe Falls, curved just like its name. The Niagara River carries the outflow of four Great Lakes over the edge of the Niagara Escarpment here. The falls formed roughly 12,000 years ago, as the ice sheets melted back.","w":"A short river linking Lake Erie and Lake Ontario; the falls are a white patch where it bends.","f":["There are three falls: the big Horseshoe Falls on the Canadian side, and the American and Bridal Veil Falls next door.","The falls are about 50 m high; creeping upstream, they have cut a gorge some 11 km long.","At night much of the river is diverted to hydroelectric plants, so less water goes over the falls."],"q":{"q":"Which two lakes does the Niagara River connect?","a":"Erie and Ontario","x":["Huron and Erie","Superior and Michigan"]}},"Q36332":{"t":"Some 275 falls strung along one jungle cliff","s":"Iguaçu is not one waterfall but a whole horseshoe of them: around 275 separate cascades spread along nearly 3 km of cliff, on the border of Brazil and Argentina. From above, look for a brown river breaking into white foam inside green forest. The deepest point, the U-shaped Devil's Throat, swallows about half of the river's flow.","w":"White foam in green forest, just east of where the Iguaçu River meets the wide Paraná.","f":["The river's name comes from the Guarani words for 'big water'.","Both the Argentine and the Brazilian national parks around the falls are UNESCO World Heritage sites.","Downstream the Iguaçu joins the Paraná at a point where Brazil, Argentina and Paraguay meet."],"q":{"q":"Which two countries share Iguaçu Falls?","a":"Brazil and Argentina","x":["Brazil and Paraguay","Argentina and Uruguay"]}},"Q3783":{"t":"A café-au-lait river in an ocean of green","s":"Below you the Amazon, here still called the Solimões, is a broad café-au-lait band wandering through unbroken green, with side channels, islands and lakes along both banks. It carries about a fifth of all the river water that reaches the world's oceans. Not a single bridge crosses its main stream.","w":"A wide brown river with pale sandbars and dark lakes on either side, cutting through unbroken forest.","f":["Its average flow is around 200,000 cubic metres per second, more than the next several largest rivers combined.","At Manaus the dark Rio Negro joins it, and the two waters run side by side for kilometres before mixing.","In 1500 Vicente Yáñez Pinzón found fresh water at sea off its mouth and named the river the 'Sweet Sea'."],"q":{"q":"What is the Amazon called in Brazil above Manaus?","a":"Solimões","x":["Madeira","Tapajós"]}},"Q3392":{"t":"A green thread stitched through the Sahara","s":"Few sights from the air are as stark as this: a single blue-green line with a narrow fringe of fields and palms, and bare desert on both sides. Here in northern Sudan the Nile makes its Great Bend, flowing south-west for a few hundred kilometres before turning north again. Nubian kings built pyramids along this stretch.","w":"A thin dark line with a green fringe across pale desert, curving in a big S.","f":["At about 6,650 km, the Nile is one of the two longest rivers on Earth, alongside the Amazon.","Sudan has more pyramids than Egypt, around two hundred of them, smaller and steeper than Giza's.","Its water comes from two sources: the White Nile from the lakes of East Africa and the Blue Nile from Ethiopia."],"q":{"q":"Where do the White Nile and the Blue Nile meet?","a":"Khartoum","x":["Aswan","Juba"]}},"Q932289":{"t":"Africa's great green heart, cut by brown rivers","s":"For hundreds of kilometres there is almost nothing but forest: a dense green carpet, cut by brown rivers that loop and braid through it. This is the heart of the Congo basin, home to the second-largest tropical rainforest on Earth after the Amazon. By afternoon towering thunderclouds often build over it, fed by moisture the trees themselves breathe out.","w":"Unbroken dark green forest with meandering brown rivers; in the afternoon, clusters of tall white storm clouds.","f":["The Congo River crosses the equator twice as it curves round the basin.","Bonobos live only here, south of the Congo River.","In 2017 scientists mapped a huge peatland beneath its swamp forests, the largest tropical peatland known."],"q":{"q":"Which ape lives only south of the Congo River?","a":"Bonobo","x":["Chimpanzee","Orangutan"]}},"Q244451":{"t":"The greatest river basin on Earth, roofed in forest","s":"Look down and count the shades of green: canopy, swamp forest, pale patches of grassland, and rivers the colour of tea or milky coffee. This is the Amazon lowland, where forest still runs to the horizon in most directions. In places, straight roads with side tracks cut into it in a fishbone pattern, the mark of clearing for farms.","w":"Endless forest crossed by winding rivers; near roads, rectangular clearings arranged like a fishbone.","f":["The Amazon's drainage basin covers about 7 million km², the largest of any river on Earth.","Much of the rain here was first evaporated by the forest itself, recycled again and again as air moves west.","Dust blown across the Atlantic from the Sahara helps fertilise the forest's poor soils."],"q":{"q":"Where does much of the dust that fertilises the Amazon come from?","a":"The Sahara","x":["The Kalahari","The Atacama"]}},"Q797":{"t":"America's biggest state, and its emptiest","s":"Below you is the Alaskan interior: dark spruce forest, a glitter of lakes and the wide braided channels of the Yukon and its tributaries, with hardly a road in sight. Alaska is more than twice the size of Texas. On a clear day the white bulk of Denali, North America's highest peak at 6,190 m, may stand out far to the south.","w":"Dark forest, silvery lakes and braided rivers with almost no roads; snowy ranges on the southern horizon.","f":["The United States bought Alaska from Russia in 1867 for 7.2 million dollars.","Alaska has a longer coastline than all the other US states combined.","In Fairbanks, in the interior, midsummer days last almost 22 hours."],"q":{"q":"From which country did the US buy Alaska in 1867?","a":"Russia","x":["Britain","Denmark"]}},"Q5428":{"t":"Taiga from horizon to horizon, for hours on end","s":"You can fly for hours over Siberia and see little but taiga: dark larch and pine forest, winding rivers and, in winter, snow that turns the whole land white and blue. Here, on the Central Siberian Plateau, the Lena, the Lower Tunguska and their tributaries cut deep valleys. Siberia covers roughly three-quarters of Russia.","w":"Endless dark forest and looping rivers in deep valleys; in winter, white snow with grey larch woods showing through.","f":["Siberia covers about 13 million km², more than Canada or China.","Much of the taiga here is larch, which sheds its needles, so in winter the forest looks grey rather than green.","Lake Baikal, in southern Siberia, holds about a fifth of the world's unfrozen fresh surface water."],"q":{"q":"Which Siberian lake is the deepest on Earth?","a":"Baikal","x":["Ladoga","Taymyr"]}},"Q866405":{"t":"The bigger, older half of Antarctica's ice","s":"If you ever see this, it will be a white emptiness without edges: the East Antarctic ice sheet, more than 4 km thick in places and rising to over 4,000 m. Near here stands Russia's Vostok Station, where −89.2 °C was recorded in 1983, the lowest air temperature ever measured by a weather station.","w":"Flat white ice to every horizon, with no landmarks at all; blinding in the summer sun.","f":["About 4 km beneath Vostok Station lies Lake Vostok, a body of liquid water sealed under the ice.","The East Antarctic ice sheet holds enough water to raise world sea level by more than 50 metres.","This ice sheet has been building up for more than 30 million years."],"q":{"q":"What lies about 4 km beneath Vostok Station?","a":"A liquid lake","x":["An active volcano","A buried meteorite"]}},"Q530917":{"t":"An ice sheet resting on a floor below the sea","s":"West Antarctica looks like its eastern twin from above, white, silent and endless, but beneath the ice it is very different. Much of its bedrock lies far below sea level, so the ice sheet rests in a deep basin that the ocean could reach. Scientists watch its glaciers closely, because they are among the fastest-changing on Earth.","w":"An unbroken white plain; towards the coast, dark mountain ridges and crevassed glaciers break through the ice.","f":["Vinson Massif, Antarctica's highest peak at 4,892 m, rises in West Antarctica's Ellsworth Mountains.","If all its ice melted, world sea level would rise by several metres.","The Transantarctic Mountains separate it from East Antarctica."],"q":{"q":"What is Antarctica's highest peak?","a":"Vinson Massif","x":["Mount Erebus","Mount Kirkpatrick"]}},"Q76034":{"t":"Billions of years old, and speckled with lakes","s":"From the air the Canadian Shield looks as if someone had shattered a mirror over the land: thousands of lakes of every shape, bare pink-grey rock and dark patches of spruce or open tundra, all scraped and polished by ice-age glaciers. This is the ancient core of North America. Some of its rocks formed when Earth was barely half a billion years old.","w":"A jigsaw of countless lakes and bare rock with streaks of forest; in winter, white lakes framed by dark trees.","f":["The shield covers roughly half of Canada, wrapped around Hudson Bay like a horseshoe.","The Acasta Gneiss in the Northwest Territories, about 4 billion years old, is among the oldest known rocks on Earth.","Its rocks hold rich deposits of nickel, gold, copper and diamonds."],"q":{"q":"Which bay does the Canadian Shield wrap around?","a":"Hudson Bay","x":["Bay of Fundy","Gulf of St. Lawrence"]}},"Q147304":{"t":"Land built grain by grain by the Yellow River","s":"Below spreads one of the flattest and most crowded farmlands on Earth: a fine mosaic of fields, villages scattered like grains of rice, and often a grey-brown haze over it all. Most of the plain was laid down as silt by the Yellow River, which has shifted its course across it many times. In June, ripe winter wheat turns it gold.","w":"A vast flat patchwork of small fields and villages, often under haze; green in spring, gold in early summer.","f":["The Grand Canal, the longest artificial waterway in the world, crosses the plain between Beijing and Hangzhou.","In places the Yellow River flows higher than the fields around it, held between tall levees.","Around 1855 the Yellow River moved its mouth from south of the Shandong Peninsula to north of it."],"q":{"q":"Which river laid down most of the North China Plain?","a":"The Yellow River","x":["The Yangtze","The Pearl River"]}},"Q602925":{"t":"Grassland to every horizon, in the heart of Eurasia","s":"Here the land opens up completely: rolling grassland, green in May and straw-gold by midsummer, crossed by dirt tracks and dotted with salt lakes that flash white in the sun. The huge rectangular fields in the north date from the Soviet Virgin Lands campaign of the 1950s. In winter everything is white and scoured by wind.","w":"Flat to rolling tan grassland, straight dirt tracks, white salt pans and huge rectangular fields further north.","f":["In the Virgin Lands campaign, begun in 1954, millions of hectares of Kazakh steppe were ploughed for wheat.","The lakes of Korgalzhyn, west of Astana, hold one of the world's most northerly flamingo colonies.","Saiga antelope, with their swollen, trunk-like noses, still roam the Kazakh steppe."],"q":{"q":"Which antelope with a trunk-like nose roams the Kazakh Steppe?","a":"Saiga","x":["Oryx","Nilgai"]}},"Q560549":{"t":"Flat land from the North Sea all the way to the Urals","s":"You are over the north-eastern end of Europe's great lowland, which runs from the North Sea through Germany and Poland and across Russia to the Urals. Out here it is taiga and bog: dark forest, brown peatland glinting with pools, and rivers like the Pechora wandering in broad loops. In the west the same plain is a quilt of fields and towns.","w":"Dark taiga, brown bogs with glinting pools and looping rivers, stretching flat to the horizon.","f":["On average the plain lies less than 200 m above sea level.","Its gentle hills and chains of lakes in Germany and Poland are moraines left by Ice Age glaciers.","The Pechora, which drains its north-eastern part, flows into the Barents Sea."],"q":{"q":"Which mountains mark the plain's eastern end?","a":"The Urals","x":["The Carpathians","The Caucasus"]}},"Q12707":{"t":"Where the land is ruled into squares a mile wide","s":"Look for the grid: roads a mile apart, square fields in green, gold and brown, and here and there perfect circles where pivot sprinklers water the crops. The Great Plains roll from Canada to Texas, east of the Rockies. Once shortgrass prairie grazed by tens of millions of bison, they are now one of the great grain belts of the world.","w":"A ruler-straight grid of roads and fields; to the west, the crumpled badlands of the Little Missouri River.","f":["Much of the southern Plains is watered from the Ogallala Aquifer, one of the largest aquifers in the world.","The mile-square grid goes back to the Land Ordinance of 1785, which set out how US land was surveyed.","The Plains tilt gently east, losing well over a kilometre of height from the foot of the Rockies."],"q":{"q":"Why do many fields on the Great Plains look like perfect circles?","a":"Centre-pivot irrigation","x":["Ancient earthworks","Grazing patterns"]}},"Q200952":{"t":"A river that ties itself in loops","s":"Few rivers show their restlessness as plainly as the Ucayali. From above it is a brown ribbon coiling through the Peruvian rainforest, with a sandbar on every bend and abandoned loops lying beside it as crescent lakes. Its channel shifts year after year. Further north it meets the Marañón, and together they become the Amazon.","w":"Tight brown loops with pale sandbars and green crescent lakes beside them, in flat forest east of the Andes.","f":["The Amazon's most distant source, high in the Andes of southern Peru, drains towards the sea through the Ucayali.","Pucallpa, the main city on its banks, is linked to Lima by a road across the Andes.","Riverboats run from Pucallpa down to Iquitos, a city with no road link to the rest of Peru."],"q":{"q":"Which river does the Ucayali meet to form the Amazon?","a":"Marañón","x":["Napo","Huallaga"]}},"Q46841":{"t":"A northern giant, braided around wooded islands","s":"Here, below Yakutsk, the Lena has already taken in the Aldan and the Vilyuy, and it spreads into a maze of channels and wooded islands, several kilometres from bank to bank. For about seven months a year it lies frozen, a white road through the taiga. In late May or early June the ice breaks up with a roar and the spring flood covers the islands.","w":"A broad river split into channels around long wooded islands and sandbars; in winter, a white band through dark taiga.","f":["The Lena is more than 4,000 km long and flows entirely within Russia.","In winter lorries drive for hundreds of kilometres along the frozen river on an ice road.","Upstream of Yakutsk stand the Lena Pillars, rock towers along the bank listed by UNESCO in 2012."],"q":{"q":"Into which sea does the Lena flow?","a":"The Laptev Sea","x":["The Kara Sea","The East Siberian Sea"]}},"Q26076":{"t":"Myanmar's great river, braided with sandbanks","s":"Through the dry heart of Myanmar the Irrawaddy is a wide sheet of brown water braided around pale sandbanks that grow and vanish with the seasons. Near here it takes in its biggest tributary, the Chindwin. A little downstream, on the east bank, lies the plain of Bagan, where more than two thousand old temples and pagodas still stand.","w":"A broad river with pale sandbanks in a dry, brownish plain; a second large river joins it from the north-west.","f":["The Irrawaddy runs for more than 2,000 km, almost the whole length of Myanmar, to the Andaman Sea.","It gives its name to the Irrawaddy dolphin, a small, round-headed dolphin of rivers and coastal waters.","Its delta, laced with channels, is one of the great rice-growing regions of Asia."],"q":{"q":"Which old temple city lies on the Irrawaddy's east bank?","a":"Bagan","x":["Angkor","Ayutthaya"]}},"Q7821148":{"t":"The Yangtze in its youth, on the roof of the world","s":"Long before it becomes China's mightiest river, the Yangtze crosses the high Qinghai plateau as the Tongtian, 'the river that reaches to heaven'. From above it is a braided silver strand, spread in many channels over a broad gravel valley more than 4,000 m up. Around it lie brown-green pastures, snow-dusted hills and hardly any people.","w":"A silvery river split into many channels across a wide gravel valley, among bare rounded hills of a high plateau.","f":["In the classic novel 'Journey to the West', the monk Xuanzang crosses the Tongtian on the back of a giant turtle.","It lies in Sanjiangyuan, the 'Source of Three Rivers', where the Yangtze, the Yellow River and the Mekong all begin.","Below Yushu the Tongtian takes a new name: the Jinsha, the 'River of Golden Sand'."],"q":{"q":"The Tongtian is the upper course of which river?","a":"The Yangtze","x":["The Yellow River","The Mekong"]}},"Q1497":{"t":"A river of islands between tall wooded bluffs","s":"Here, on its upper course, the Mississippi is not one channel but a whole valley of water: islands, sloughs and backwaters between steep wooded bluffs on both sides. The bluffs stand tall because this corner of the Midwest, the Driftless Area, escaped the last ice sheets. Every few dozen kilometres a lock and dam holds the river in a chain of broad pools.","w":"A wide valley of braided water and islands between steep forested bluffs, running north–south through farmland.","f":["With the Missouri, the Mississippi drains about 40 percent of the contiguous United States.","Between Minneapolis and St. Louis, some 29 locks and dams turn the river into a navigable staircase.","Its source is Lake Itasca in Minnesota, where you can cross the young river on stepping stones."],"q":{"q":"In which state does the Mississippi begin?","a":"Minnesota","x":["Wisconsin","Iowa"]}},"Q1038418":{"t":"The 'River of Golden Sand' in its deep valleys","s":"Jinsha means 'golden sand', after the gold once panned from its gravels. This is the upper Yangtze, carving deep valleys through the mountains of Yunnan and Sichuan; from the air it is a green-brown thread at the bottom of steep, dry slopes. Today a chain of giant dams has turned long stretches of it into narrow, winding reservoirs.","w":"A winding river deep in steep, brownish mountain valleys, widening in places into long, snake-like reservoirs.","f":["Upstream, in Tiger Leaping Gorge, the mountainsides rise more than 3,000 m above the rushing water.","At the 'First Bend' near Shigu the river swings sharply from south to north-east.","The Baihetan Dam on the Jinsha is one of the most powerful hydroelectric plants in the world."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Jinsha mean?","a":"Golden sand","x":["Black water","Jade river"]}},"Q4814791":{"t":"A slow, pale river across the Sudanese plain","s":"The White Nile is in no hurry. On these flat Sudanese plains it has almost no slope left, so it spreads wide and lazy between low banks, its water pale with fine clay, hence the name. Irrigated fields and reed beds line the river; beyond them, dry savanna runs to the horizon.","w":"A broad, pale river with low banks and green irrigated strips, running north across a flat, dry plain.","f":["At Khartoum it meets the Blue Nile, and for a while their different colours run side by side.","Its steady flow comes mostly from Lake Victoria and other East African lakes, filtered through the Sudd swamps.","The Jebel Aulia Dam, south of Khartoum, was built across the White Nile in the 1930s."],"q":{"q":"What gives the White Nile its name?","a":"Pale clay in its water","x":["Snow-fed sources","White sandy banks"]}},"Q5413":{"t":"Asia's longest river, held back by a giant dam","s":"Around here the Yangtze no longer flows freely: this is the Three Gorges reservoir, a green-brown lake some 600 km long, winding between steep hills. Towns that stood on its banks were rebuilt higher up the slopes as the water rose. Downstream, the reservoir ends at the Three Gorges Dam, the largest power station in the world by capacity.","w":"A long, winding, lake-like river between steep green hills, with towns climbing its slopes.","f":["At about 6,300 km, the Yangtze is the longest river in Asia and the third longest in the world.","The reservoir behind the Three Gorges Dam reaches almost to Chongqing.","About a third of China's people live in the Yangtze basin."],"q":{"q":"Which dam holds back the Yangtze downstream of here?","a":"Three Gorges Dam","x":["Xiaolangdi Dam","Longtan Dam"]}},"Q2750140":{"t":"The river that feeds Lake Victoria, and so the Nile","s":"A river winding through a wide valley of papyrus swamp, with lakes strung along it like beads: this is the Kagera, the largest river flowing into Lake Victoria, and its headwaters are often counted as the most distant source of the Nile. Along this stretch it forms Rwanda's eastern border, with Akagera National Park on its western bank.","w":"A winding river in a broad green swamp with a chain of lakes beside it, among rolling savanna hills.","f":["Its headwaters rise in Rwanda's Nyungwe forest and in the hills of Burundi.","Akagera National Park, named after the river, protects savanna, lakes and papyrus swamps along its banks.","The Kagera enters Lake Victoria in Tanzania, north of the town of Bukoba."],"q":{"q":"Which lake does the Kagera flow into?","a":"Lake Victoria","x":["Lake Tanganyika","Lake Kivu"]}},"Q5419":{"t":"The 'Big Muddy', tamed into smooth curves","s":"The Missouri earned its nickname, the Big Muddy, from the silt it once carried. Here, along the Kansas–Missouri line, it swings in wide, smooth curves between levees, a channel shaped by engineers for barges. Look beside it for the ghosts of older courses: crescent lakes and curving field boundaries in the flat floodplain.","w":"A brown river in smooth, regular bends across a flat floodplain of fields, with crescent lakes and old channel scars.","f":["At about 3,700 km, the Missouri is the longest river in the United States.","In 1804 Lewis and Clark set off up the Missouri on their expedition to the Pacific.","The Missouri joins the Mississippi just north of St. Louis."],"q":{"q":"What is the Missouri's nickname?","a":"The Big Muddy","x":["Old Man River","The Father of Waters"]}},"Q284780":{"t":"China's lowest, hottest hollow, full of vineyards","s":"Look for a dark green oasis at the foot of red, furrowed hills, beside a white salt flat. This is the Turpan Depression, where the ground sinks to about 154 m below sea level at Lake Aydingkol, the lowest point in China. Its summers are the hottest in the country, yet Turpan's vineyards thrive on meltwater from the Tian Shan.","w":"A green oasis and a white salt flat in a brown desert basin, south of the snowy Tian Shan.","f":["The Flaming Mountains, a bare red-sandstone ridge, run along its northern side and glow in the afternoon sun.","Karez, underground channels dug centuries ago, still carry mountain water to Turpan's fields.","Turpan is famous for grapes and raisins, dried in airy, open-walled brick houses."],"q":{"q":"About how far below sea level is the Turpan Depression's lowest point?","a":"About 150 m","x":["About 50 m","About 400 m"]}},"Q191333":{"t":"A gateway through the mountains, used for millennia","s":"West of Peshawar the green, crowded plain ends abruptly against bare brown ridges, and a narrow valley winds up into them: the Khyber Pass. For thousands of years traders, pilgrims and armies came this way between Central Asia and the Indian subcontinent. Its highest point is only about 1,070 m, but the slopes around it are steep and dry.","w":"Where the green Peshawar plain meets a wall of bare, brown, folded hills to the west.","f":["The Khyber is one of the main routes between Pakistan and Afghanistan, leading to the border crossing at Torkham.","A railway through the pass, with dozens of tunnels and bridges, was completed in the 1920s.","Gandhara, an ancient land of Buddhist art and learning, lay around Peshawar at the foot of the pass."],"q":{"q":"Which city lies at the eastern foot of the Khyber Pass?","a":"Peshawar","x":["Lahore","Quetta"]}},"Q118388":{"t":"North America's lowest, hottest, driest valley","s":"Look for a long white streak between dark mountains: the salt pan of Badwater Basin, at 86 m below sea level the lowest point in North America. Fans of gravel spill from every canyon onto the valley floor like poured sand. In 1913 a thermometer here read 56.7 °C, the highest air temperature ever officially recorded on Earth.","w":"A long white salt flat between steep, bare brown ranges, with fan-shaped gravel slopes below each canyon.","f":["Mount Whitney, the highest peak in the contiguous US at 4,421 m, stands less than 140 km away.","On Racetrack Playa, rocks slide across the mud when wind pushes thin sheets of ice, leaving long trails.","Death Valley is the largest national park in the contiguous United States."],"q":{"q":"How far below sea level is Badwater Basin?","a":"About 86 m","x":["About 28 m","About 154 m"]}},"Q534290":{"t":"Australia's food bowl, threaded by slow brown rivers","s":"From above, the Murray–Darling basin is a vast, flat plain in ochre and khaki, crossed by slow rivers traced in the dark green of river red gums. In the Riverina, near here, irrigation turns the plain into bright green squares of rice, cotton and orchards. The basin covers about a seventh of Australia.","w":"A flat ochre plain crossed by winding rivers lined with dark green trees; bright green irrigated fields near them.","f":["The Murray and the Darling together form Australia's longest river system, well over 3,000 km.","River red gums along its banks can live for several hundred years.","At dry Lake Mungo, in the west of the basin, people left traces of their lives more than 40,000 years ago."],"q":{"q":"Roughly what share of Australia does the Murray–Darling basin cover?","a":"About a seventh","x":["About a half","About a twentieth"]}},"Q191566":{"t":"Bog, taiga and gas flares on an endless flat","s":"Few places on Earth are as flat as this. Below you, rivers crawl across the West Siberian Plain in huge loops, and between them spread the bogs, a brown and olive patchwork of peat and countless small lakes. At night the gas flares and lights of the oil towns glow in the darkness.","w":"Flat olive-brown bogland spotted with small lakes, with looping rivers and dark ribbons of forest along them.","f":["The Vasyugan Swamp, in the south of the plain, is one of the largest bogs in the world.","Across the plain the Ob drops only a few centimetres per kilometre, so its floods spread wide and linger.","The plain holds some of Russia's biggest oil and gas fields, among them Samotlor near Nizhnevartovsk."],"q":{"q":"Which huge bog lies in the south of the West Siberian Plain?","a":"Vasyugan Swamp","x":["Pinsk Marshes","Meshchera Marshes"]}},"Q966943":{"t":"A land of ridges where no river reaches the sea","s":"Look down on Nevada and you see a rhythm: a long dark mountain range, a flat pale valley, another range, another valley, repeating to the horizon, all running north–south. This is the Great Basin, where no river finds its way to the ocean; water ends in salt lakes or simply evaporates. Many valley floors are dry lake beds, dazzling white.","w":"Parallel north–south mountain ridges separated by flat, pale valleys, some with white dry lake beds.","f":["Great Basin bristlecone pines, some almost 5,000 years old, are among the oldest living trees on Earth.","The Great Salt Lake in Utah is a remnant of a much larger Ice Age lake, Lake Bonneville.","Explorer John C. Frémont named the Great Basin in the 1840s, realising it had no outlet to the sea."],"q":{"q":"Where do the Great Basin's rivers end?","a":"In lakes and sinks inland","x":["In the Pacific Ocean","In the Gulf of California"]}},"Q656985":{"t":"A hidden sea of ancient water under the red outback","s":"Nothing on the surface gives it away: below you is the red and ochre outback, crossed by the braided dry channels of the Channel Country. But beneath it lies the Great Artesian Basin, one of the largest underground stores of fresh water on Earth. Some of its water fell as rain more than a million years ago.","w":"Rusty red plains laced with braided, mostly dry river channels that turn green after rare floods.","f":["The basin lies beneath about a fifth of Australia.","Water from some deep bores comes out hot, close to boiling point.","Where the water seeps out naturally, mound springs form small green oases in the desert."],"q":{"q":"How old is some of the Great Artesian Basin's water?","a":"Over a million years","x":["About 1,000 years","About 10,000 years"]}},"Q28809720":{"t":"Black-earth fields under a long white winter","s":"The Manchurian Plain is northern China's great granary: a flat expanse of huge fields laid out in long strips, with poplar windbreaks drawn across them like pencil lines. Its soil is a rich black earth, darker than almost anywhere else in China. From November to March it lies frozen and white, then turns green with maize and soybeans.","w":"Flat farmland in long strips divided by thin lines of trees; black in spring, green in summer, white in winter.","f":["It is one of the world's few great black-soil regions, along with the Ukrainian steppe and the US Corn Belt.","The Songhua drains the plain north to the Amur, while the Liao flows south to the Bohai Sea.","Harbin, on its northern edge, is famous for its winter festival of giant ice sculptures."],"q":{"q":"Which crop, with maize, dominates the Manchurian Plain?","a":"Soybeans","x":["Tea","Cotton"]}},"Q575516":{"t":"Two deserts, two rivers and a vanishing sea","s":"Two green threads cross a sea of sand here: the Amu Darya and the Syr Darya, winding through the Kyzylkum and Karakum deserts of the Turan lowland. Along them spread irrigated fields and oasis towns. Both rivers once filled the Aral Sea, which has shrunk dramatically since the 1960s as their water was drawn off for cotton.","w":"Ochre desert with long dune ridges, crossed by green ribbons of irrigated land along two big rivers.","f":["In Turkic languages Kyzylkum means 'red sand' and Karakum 'black sand'.","Khiva, whose walled inner town is a UNESCO site, stands in an oasis on the lower Amu Darya.","Much of the Aral Sea's exposed bed has become a new desert, the Aralkum."],"q":{"q":"What does 'Kyzylkum' mean?","a":"Red sand","x":["Black sand","White sand"]}},"Q23945221":{"t":"The shore of the Sahara, green for only a few months","s":"Sahel comes from the Arabic for 'shore': this is the coast of the Sahara, a belt of dry grass and thorny acacia running right across Africa from the Atlantic to the Red Sea. Its colours follow the rain. From July to September it turns green almost overnight; the rest of the year it fades to straw and dust, dotted with dark trees.","w":"Tan or green savanna dotted with dark trees, fading into bare sand to the north, crossed by dry riverbeds.","f":["The belt runs for roughly 5,000 km across Africa but is only a few hundred kilometres wide.","The Great Green Wall project aims to restore a band of land across the Sahel with trees and grasses.","Gum arabic, used in sweets and soft drinks, comes mostly from Sahel acacias, above all in Sudan."],"q":{"q":"What does the Arabic word 'sahel' mean?","a":"Shore","x":["Sand","Thirst"]}},"Q756832":{"t":"Flat pinewoods and farms between the hills and the sea","s":"Dark green pine forest, sandy fields in neat rectangles and slow rivers the colour of strong tea: this is the flat southern end of the Atlantic coastal plain. Once it was seabed, built of sand and clay laid down when the ocean reached further inland. Towards the coast it dissolves into salt marshes and a chain of barrier islands.","w":"Flat country of dark pinewoods and rectangular fields with winding tea-coloured rivers; marshes and islands near the coast.","f":["Richmond, Raleigh, Columbia and Augusta grew on the Fall Line, where rivers tumble off the hills onto the plain.","The Okefenokee Swamp, on the Georgia–Florida border, is one of North America's largest blackwater swamps.","Georgia grows more peanuts than any other US state, much of them on this plain."],"q":{"q":"Why are many rivers here the colour of tea?","a":"Tannins from swamp plants","x":["Red clay silt","Iron from springs"]}},"Q208347":{"t":"A hot, thorny forest-plain at the heart of South America","s":"Olive-green felt, flat to every horizon: from above, the dry thorn forest of the Gran Chaco looks almost uniform. Look closer and you see the wandering scars of rivers such as the Pilcomayo and the Bermejo, and huge rectangular clearings for cattle and soybeans, edged with strips of trees left standing. The Chaco spreads across Argentina, Paraguay and Bolivia.","w":"Flat olive-green scrub forest with winding pale river scars and big rectangular clearings with tree-lined edges.","f":["Its name is often traced to the Quechua 'chaku', a communal hunt or hunting ground.","It is the second-largest forest region in South America, after the Amazon.","The Chaco peccary was known only from fossils until living animals were identified here in the 1970s."],"q":{"q":"Which animal, once known only from fossils, was found alive in the Chaco?","a":"Chaco peccary","x":["Giant armadillo","Maned wolf"]}},"Q141501":{"t":"Plains that turn from dust to inland sea each year","s":"The Llanos swing between two faces. In the dry season, roughly December to April, this lowland along the Orinoco is golden grassland dotted with palms and crossed by dusty tracks. When the rains come, the rivers overflow and vast stretches become a shallow sheet of water shining in the sun. It is cowboy country, where llaneros still drive cattle across the plains.","w":"Flat grassland with lines of trees along winding rivers; in the rainy season, shining sheets of floodwater.","f":["The Llanos stretch across Venezuela and Colombia, between the Andes and the Orinoco.","They are home to capybaras, the world's largest rodents, and to Orinoco crocodiles.","Joropo, the fast harp-and-maracas music of the llaneros, is Venezuela's national dance."],"q":{"q":"What are the cowboys of the Llanos called?","a":"Llaneros","x":["Gauchos","Huasos"]}},"Q184382":{"t":"Flat as a table, and ruled into giant fields","s":"Pampa comes from a Quechua word for a plain, and few plains are flatter. From above you see a quilt of enormous rectangular fields, dead-straight roads, shallow lakes shining silver and dark clumps of trees marking each estancia. Once tall grassland ridden by gauchos, it is now one of the world's great farm belts for soy, wheat, maize and beef.","w":"An immense flat quilt of rectangular fields, straight roads and silvery shallow lakes, with dark tree clumps around farmsteads.","f":["Almost all the trees you see were planted: the natural pampas had grass but hardly any trees.","The deep, fertile soils of the Pampas are among the richest in the world.","Charles Darwin rode across the Pampas in the 1830s during the voyage of the Beagle."],"q":{"q":"What does the Quechua word 'pampa' mean?","a":"Plain","x":["Grass","Wind"]}},"Q192305":{"t":"A plain of Himalayan mud, home to hundreds of millions","s":"Here the Ganges, now heading for the Bay of Bengal as the Padma, swings in wide braided loops across a plain so flat that its rivers wander at will. Every field, village and pond below you sits on mud carried down from the Himalayas. The Indo-Gangetic Plain is among the most densely populated places on Earth, and at night it glitters with countless lights.","w":"A wide braided river with pale sandbars in a flat green patchwork of fields, ponds and villages.","f":["In places the river-laid sediments beneath the plain are more than a kilometre thick.","In winter a thick fog often settles over the plain, sometimes blanketing it from Pakistan to Bangladesh.","The plain runs for more than 2,500 km, from Pakistan across northern India to Bangladesh."],"q":{"q":"Which mountains supplied the mud that built this plain?","a":"The Himalayas","x":["The Western Ghats","The Vindhya Range"]}},"Q486324":{"t":"A green oval oasis walled in by snowy mountains","s":"Look for a green oval set among mountains: the Fergana Valley, about 300 km long, ringed by the Tian Shan to the north and the Alay range to the south. Rivers pouring down from the snow feed a dense patchwork of cotton fields, orchards, mulberry trees and towns. The Syr Darya is born here, where the Naryn and the Kara Darya meet.","w":"A green, densely farmed basin surrounded on almost all sides by high, often snow-capped mountains.","f":["The valley is shared by Uzbekistan, Kyrgyzstan and Tajikistan.","More than 2,000 years ago Chinese emperors sought the famous 'heavenly horses' of Fergana.","Margilan, in the valley, has been known for its silk weaving for centuries."],"q":{"q":"Which river is born in the Fergana Valley?","a":"Syr Darya","x":["Amu Darya","Zeravshan"]}},"Q51614":{"t":"A high plateau of wheat, salt and ancient towns","s":"The heart of Anatolia is a high, open plateau about a kilometre above the sea, where wheat fields turn from green in spring to gold in summer and bare ochre by autumn. Just to the south-west lies Lake Tuz, a vast salt lake that dries into a blinding white crust in summer. Further south rise the cones of old volcanoes such as Hasan and Erciyes.","w":"A wide ochre and golden plateau; look for the huge white patch of Lake Tuz and distant volcanic cones.","f":["The name comes from the Greek 'anatolē', meaning sunrise, and so the east.","Çatalhöyük, further south on the plateau, was a town of mud-brick houses more than 9,000 years ago.","Lake Tuz supplies a large share of Turkey's salt."],"q":{"q":"Which salt lake lies on the central Anatolian plateau?","a":"Lake Tuz","x":["Lake Iznik","Lake Urmia"]}},"Q1507":{"t":"Wind, steppe and sky at the end of the Americas","s":"Patagonia from the air is mostly emptiness: grey-brown steppe and flat-topped mesetas, cut by a few river valleys that run like green threads from the Andes to the Atlantic. To the west the snowy Andes rise above deep blue lakes. The wind blows hard and almost constantly from the west, bending the few trees there are.","w":"Vast grey-brown steppe with flat mesas and canyons; to the west, snowy Andes and long blue lakes.","f":["Welsh settlers reached the Chubut valley in 1865, and Welsh is still spoken in towns such as Gaiman.","Some of the largest known dinosaurs, including Argentinosaurus and Patagotitan, were found in Patagonia.","The name is usually linked to Magellan's crew, who in 1520 called the local people 'Patagones'."],"q":{"q":"Which European language is still spoken in Patagonia's Chubut valley?","a":"Welsh","x":["Irish","Basque"]}},"Q605779":{"t":"A vast slope of ice falling to the Southern Ocean","s":"From the coast of Wilkes Land the ice rises in a long, gentle slope towards the high plateau of East Antarctica, hundreds of kilometres inland. Near the sea, glaciers speed up and crack into crevasses, and their surface looks like crumpled paper. Beneath the ice lies the deep Aurora Subglacial Basin, much of it below sea level.","w":"A seamless white slope; near the coast, crevassed glaciers, ice cliffs and sea ice scattered on dark water.","f":["It is named after Charles Wilkes, who charted this coast in 1840 on a US Navy expedition.","The Totten Glacier, on its coast, drains a large part of the ice sheet and is closely watched by scientists.","Australia's Casey Station stands on its coast, in the Windmill Islands."],"q":{"q":"Who is Wilkes Land named after?","a":"An American naval officer","x":["A British whaling captain","A Norwegian king"]}},"Q1340442":{"t":"Ice over some of the oldest rocks on Earth","s":"Ice runs from horizon to horizon here, broken near the coast by dark ridges and nunataks, rocky peaks poking through the ice sheet like islands in a white sea. Some of those rocks are among the oldest on Earth. The coast was first sighted in 1831 by John Biscoe, a sealing and whaling captain sailing out of London.","w":"A white ice sheet with dark rocky peaks near the coast poking through it like islands.","f":["It takes its name from Enderby Brothers, the London whaling firm that sent Biscoe south.","Rocks of its Napier Complex are among the oldest in Antarctica, formed some 3.8 billion years ago.","Molodyozhnaya station, on its coast, was once the main Soviet base in Antarctica."],"q":{"q":"What kind of business gave Enderby Land its name?","a":"A whaling company","x":["A tea merchant","A coal company"]}},"Q189999":{"t":"Mountains drowning in ice, named after a queen","s":"Near the coast of Queen Maud Land, jagged mountain chains break through the ice sheet, their dark peaks and granite spires half-buried in white. Further south, where you are now, the ice rises into the high polar plateau and the mountains vanish beneath it. The land's name honours Queen Maud of Norway, wife of King Haakon VII.","w":"A white plateau inland; towards the coast, dark mountain ranges and needle-like peaks sticking out of the ice.","f":["It stretches for more than 2,000 km along the Antarctic coast.","Several countries run research stations here, among them Norway's Troll, Germany's Neumayer and India's Maitri.","Queen Maud was born a British princess, a daughter of King Edward VII."],"q":{"q":"Queen Maud, who gave this land its name, was queen of which country?","a":"Norway","x":["Denmark","Sweden"]}},"Q745767":{"t":"Volcanoes under ice, named after an explorer's wife","s":"Here and there the ice of Marie Byrd Land is pierced by the snowy cones of volcanoes, some rising more than 4 km high. Mount Sidley, about 4,285 m, is the highest volcano in Antarctica. Around them lie hundreds of kilometres of flat white ice. The American aviator Richard Byrd named this remote corner of West Antarctica after his wife, Marie, in 1929.","w":"A white expanse with isolated, broad volcanic cones rising above the ice towards the coast.","f":["No country has ever made a territorial claim to Marie Byrd Land.","In 1929 Byrd was among the first to fly over the South Pole.","Its coast faces the Amundsen and Ross seas, often choked with sea ice."],"q":{"q":"Who was Marie Byrd Land named after?","a":"An explorer's wife","x":["A British queen","A ship's captain"]}},"Q80299":{"t":"The world's highest waterfall, off a table mountain","s":"Angel Falls drops 979 m from the edge of Auyán-tepui, a flat-topped sandstone mountain rising sheer out of the Venezuelan rainforest. It is the highest uninterrupted waterfall in the world; in the dry season much of the water turns to mist before it reaches the bottom. Look for dark tabletop mountains with vertical walls, often wrapped in cloud.","w":"Dark, flat-topped mountains with sheer cliff walls rising from green forest, often half-hidden in cloud.","f":["It is named after Jimmie Angel, an American pilot who flew over it in 1933 and landed on the tepui in 1937.","In the local Pemón language it is called Kerepakupai Merú.","The falls lie in Canaima National Park, a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1994."],"q":{"q":"How high is Angel Falls?","a":"About 979 m","x":["About 450 m","About 1,500 m"]}},"Q599529":{"t":"The steppe where the horse was probably first tamed","s":"From the Danube to the Urals, the Pontic-Caspian steppe was once a sea of feather grass. Today, near the Volga, it is mostly ploughed into vast fields of black earth, crossed by shelter belts of trees and cut by branching ravines. In early summer the wheat is green; by August the whole land turns gold and dusty.","w":"Huge fields in green, gold or black, divided by thin lines of trees and cut by branching ravines; the Volga to the west.","f":["A genetic study published in 2021 traced modern domestic horses to this steppe, some 4,200 years ago.","Its black soils, chernozems, are among the most fertile on Earth.","For thousands of years nomads such as the Scythians and Sarmatians roamed this grassland."],"q":{"q":"Which animal was probably first domesticated on this steppe?","a":"The horse","x":["The camel","The yak"]}},"ne-rg-1730073989":{"t":"The Corn Belt's heartland, ironed flat by glaciers","s":"A checkerboard to the horizon: this is the heart of America's Corn Belt, land flattened and enriched by ice-age glaciers and wind-blown dust, then ruled into square-mile sections. In summer the squares are green with maize and soybeans; after harvest they turn brown and tan. Rivers such as the Mississippi wind through it in wooded valleys.","w":"A square-mile grid of fields and roads, green in summer and brown after harvest, cut by wooded river valleys.","f":["Iowa and Illinois, both in the Central Lowland, together grow roughly a third of the US maize crop.","Much of the soil formed on loess, fine dust blown off glacial outwash at the end of the Ice Age.","Before farming, most of this land was tallgrass prairie, with grasses taller than a person."],"q":{"q":"Which two crops fill the Corn Belt's summer checkerboard?","a":"Maize and soybeans","x":["Wheat and cotton","Rice and sugar beet"]}},"Q2016601":{"t":"The 'Big Land' tundra of lakes and reindeer","s":"East of the Pechora River spreads the Bolshezemelskaya Tundra, the 'Big Land' tundra: a treeless plain of moss, dwarf birch and low hills, glittering with thousands of lakes. For most of the year it is white; in summer it turns green and brown, and the lakes shine like scattered mirrors. Nenets herders still move their reindeer across it with the seasons.","w":"A flat, treeless plain spattered with countless lakes and low rounded hills; white for most of the year.","f":["Permafrost lies beneath the tundra, which is why meltwater gathers in so many shallow lakes.","Vorkuta, a coal-mining city, stands on its south-eastern edge.","Its name sets it against the smaller Malozemelskaya, or 'Small Land', Tundra west of the Pechora."],"q":{"q":"Which people herd reindeer on the Bolshezemelskaya Tundra?","a":"The Nenets","x":["The Sámi","The Yakuts"]}},"Q252609":{"t":"A slice of Antarctica first mapped from the air","s":"In 1938–39 a German expedition catapulted seaplanes off the ship Schwabenland and photographed this stretch of Queen Maud Land from the air, naming it New Swabia after the ship. Behind the flat coastal ice shelves the land climbs into dark mountain ranges, then vanishes under the polar plateau. Near the coast lies the Schirmacher Oasis, a rare strip of bare rock and lakes.","w":"Flat ice shelves at the coast, then dark mountain chains poking through the ice, and white plateau beyond.","f":["The Schirmacher Oasis, about 17 km long, holds dozens of lakes and two stations, Russia's Novolazarevskaya and India's Maitri.","The ship Schwabenland was named after Swabia, a region of south-western Germany.","Novolazarevskaya honours Mikhail Lazarev, who sailed with Bellingshausen when Antarctica was sighted in 1820."],"q":{"q":"What gave New Swabia its name?","a":"An expedition ship","x":["A research station","A mountain range"]}},"Q973":{"t":"A wandering giant across the Siberian bogs","s":"The Ob does not so much flow as wander: here, in the middle of the West Siberian Plain, it spreads into a broad floodplain of channels, oxbow lakes and willow-covered islands, in places tens of kilometres wide. In May and June the spring flood turns much of it into one shining sheet. Then the water slowly retreats to the main channel.","w":"A broad silvery floodplain with many channels, oxbow lakes and islands, winding through flat bog and taiga.","f":["With the Irtysh, the Ob forms one of Asia's longest river systems, about 5,400 km.","It ends in the Gulf of Ob, an estuary some 800 km long opening onto the Kara Sea.","The river lies under ice for around half the year."],"q":{"q":"Into which sea does the Gulf of Ob open?","a":"The Kara Sea","x":["The Laptev Sea","The Barents Sea"]}},"Q1538813":{"t":"The first thread of the Yangtze, below the glaciers","s":"This braided stream, shining in a wide stony valley more than 4,500 m up, is the Tuotuo, the headwater usually counted as the official source of the Yangtze. It is born in the glaciers of Geladaindong in the Tanggula Mountains. Near the tiny settlement of Tuotuohe, the Qinghai–Tibet railway and highway cross it on long bridges.","w":"A thin, braided silver stream spread across a broad grey-brown valley, with snowy peaks to the south-west.","f":["Geladaindong, the peak whose glaciers feed the Tuotuo, rises to about 6,600 m.","Winters here are long and bitter, and the river freezes for several months each year.","Some surveys find the Dangqu, another headstream, to be longer, so the Yangtze's true source is still debated."],"q":{"q":"Which great river begins with the Tuotuo?","a":"The Yangtze","x":["The Yellow River","The Salween"]}},"ne-rv-1159113693":{"t":"The Nile's western arm, and the stone that spoke","s":"Below Cairo the Nile splits in two, and this is its western arm, the Rosetta Branch, winding north through the delta to the Mediterranean. From above it is a dark thread through a dense green quilt of fields and villages, sharply edged by desert to the west. Near its mouth, at Rosetta, French soldiers found the Rosetta Stone in 1799.","w":"A winding dark river through a dense green delta of fields and villages, with desert close by to the west.","f":["The Rosetta Stone bears one decree in hieroglyphs, demotic and Greek, which helped scholars decipher hieroglyphs.","The town at its mouth is called Rashid in Arabic; Rosetta is its European name.","With the Damietta Branch, it frames the Nile Delta, one of the oldest farmed landscapes on Earth."],"q":{"q":"What famous object was found near the mouth of the Rosetta Branch?","a":"The Rosetta Stone","x":["The Narmer Palette","Tutankhamun's mask"]}},"ne-rv-1159113949":{"t":"The Nile's eastern arm through a crowded green delta","s":"Where the Nile fans out into its delta, the Damietta Branch takes the eastern route to the sea. From the air it is a dark, gently winding line through an intensely green and crowded land, where fields, canals and villages leave hardly a gap. At night the whole delta sparkles with lights, framed by the black of the desert.","w":"A dark winding river through an intensely green, crowded delta; at night, a web of lights edged by dark desert.","f":["The city of Mansoura stands on its banks, and Damietta lies near its mouth on the Mediterranean.","Damietta has long been Egypt's great centre of furniture-making.","Since the Aswan High Dam, the Nile's floods no longer reach the delta, and in places its coast is retreating."],"q":{"q":"Which of the Nile's two delta branches is the eastern one?","a":"Damietta","x":["Rosetta","Canopic"]}},"Q1653":{"t":"Europe's great river squeezed through the Iron Gates","s":"Here the Danube forces its way through the Carpathians in the Iron Gates, a chain of gorges on the border of Serbia and Romania. From above the wide river suddenly narrows between steep wooded cliffs, then broadens again into a long reservoir behind the Iron Gate dam. At its narrowest, in the Kazan gorge, it is only about 150 m wide.","w":"A broad river pinched between steep forested mountains into a narrow, winding gorge, with a large dam downstream.","f":["The Danube flows through or along ten countries, more than any other river in the world.","On a cliff near Orșova, the face of the Dacian king Decebalus is carved about 55 m high.","The river runs some 2,850 km, from Germany's Black Forest to the Black Sea."],"q":{"q":"How many countries does the Danube flow through or along?","a":"Ten","x":["Six","Eight"]}},"Q26301":{"t":"The far-flung Mongolian source of the Yenisey","s":"The Ider starts high in the Khangai Mountains of Mongolia and winds north-east through treeless steppe and valleys dotted with larch. From above it looks modest, a blue-grey thread with a thin green edge, but it is the most distant headstream of an enormous system: through the Selenga, Lake Baikal, the Angara and the Yenisey, its water reaches the Arctic Ocean.","w":"A thin winding river with a narrow green fringe in a broad valley of brownish steppe, dark larch on north slopes.","f":["Where the Ider meets the Delgermörön, the Selenga River begins.","Measured from the Ider's source, the Yenisey is one of the world's longest river systems, over 5,000 km.","The river lies frozen for about half the year."],"q":{"q":"Which lake does the Ider's water pass through on its way to the Arctic?","a":"Lake Baikal","x":["Lake Khövsgöl","Lake Balkhash"]}},"Q75745":{"t":"The river that fills Lake Baikal","s":"The Selenga gathers the rivers of northern Mongolia and carries them north into Russia and on to Lake Baikal. Here, near the border, it winds through a broad valley between steppe and pine-clad hills, splitting around islands and gravel bars. It brings in about half of all the water that flows into Baikal.","w":"A winding river with islands and gravel bars in a wide valley between steppe and dark pine-covered hills.","f":["Where it enters Baikal, the Selenga has built a large delta, a maze of channels, reeds and lakes.","It is the largest river in Mongolia by volume of water.","Ulan-Ude, the capital of Buryatia, stands on the Selenga."],"q":{"q":"About how much of Lake Baikal's inflow comes from the Selenga?","a":"About half","x":["About a tenth","Nearly all"]}},"Q15954437":{"t":"The Brahmaputra's first steps, at the 'Horse Spring'","s":"Far out in western Tibet, a young river wanders over a wide, windswept valley more than 4,500 m up, braided into silver channels between sand and gravel. This is the Maquan, the uppermost course of the Yarlung Tsangpo, which later becomes the Brahmaputra. Its source lies in glaciers south-east of sacred Mount Kailash and Lake Manasarovar.","w":"A braided silver river spread over a broad, dry, sandy valley, with snowy Himalayan peaks to the south.","f":["Maquan means 'Horse Spring', echoing its Tibetan name Tamchok Khabab, 'from the horse's mouth'.","In Tibetan tradition four great rivers rise near Kailash, from the mouths of a horse, a lion, an elephant and a peacock.","Below the town of Saga the river takes the name Yarlung Tsangpo."],"q":{"q":"Which great river begins as the Maquan?","a":"The Brahmaputra","x":["The Ganges","The Indus"]}},"Q162737":{"t":"The only river that escapes from Lake Baikal","s":"More than 300 rivers flow into Lake Baikal, but only one flows out: the Angara. Here, far downstream, it crosses dark taiga as a broad, lake-like reservoir, one step in a staircase of dams that turn its fall into power. Its water is famously clear, and below the dams it often steams in winter instead of freezing.","w":"A broad, lake-like reservoir with branching bays in dark taiga, narrowing to a river below the dams.","f":["The Angara is the largest tributary of the Yenisey, which it joins far to the west.","The Bratsk reservoir on the Angara is one of the largest artificial lakes in the world by volume.","A Buryat legend tells how Angara, Baikal's daughter, ran away to her beloved, the Yenisey."],"q":{"q":"How many rivers flow out of Lake Baikal?","a":"One","x":["Three","Twelve"]}},"Q2220":{"t":"A deep green valley through Canada's northern farmland","s":"Flat farmland runs to the horizon, then suddenly drops into a deep valley: the Peace River, winding in broad curves some 200 m below the prairie, its slopes dark with aspen and spruce. Around it lies the Peace Country, some of the northernmost farmland in North America, gold with grain in late summer. Downstream the river swings north towards Lake Athabasca and the Arctic.","w":"A deep, winding, forested valley slicing through flat patchwork farmland of gold and green fields.","f":["Its name recalls a peace made in the 18th century between the Cree and the Dane-zaa, or Beaver, people.","Near Lake Athabasca it feeds the Peace–Athabasca Delta, one of the largest inland freshwater deltas on Earth.","In British Columbia the W. A. C. Bennett Dam holds its water in Williston Lake, the province's largest lake."],"q":{"q":"Where does the Peace River's name come from?","a":"A peace between two peoples","x":["A missionary's motto","Its calm, slow current"]}},"Q3411":{"t":"Canada's longest river, on its way to the Arctic","s":"The Mackenzie flows north for more than 1,700 km from Great Slave Lake to the Beaufort Sea, and here it is a wide, grey-brown river between dark spruce forest and the ridges of the Franklin Mountains. Nearby the clear Great Bear River flows into it. In winter the Mackenzie freezes hard and becomes an ice road linking remote communities.","w":"A broad, muddy river winding north through dark spruce forest, with low mountain ridges along its eastern side.","f":["With its headwaters, the Mackenzie system runs about 4,200 km, the second longest in North America.","Its Dene name, Deh Cho, means 'big river'.","It is named after Alexander Mackenzie, who travelled down it to the Arctic Ocean in 1789."],"q":{"q":"Which lake does the Mackenzie flow out of?","a":"Great Slave Lake","x":["Great Bear Lake","Lake Athabasca"]}},"ne-rv-1159117999":{"t":"The Mekong as a mountain stream on the Tibetan Plateau","s":"It is hard to believe that this stream, winding through high grassland and rocky gorges in Qinghai, will one day become the mighty Mekong. The Za Qu, as the upper Mekong is called here, flows more than 4,000 m above the sea, past yak pastures and steep valley walls. It rises in small glaciers on the plateau and runs south-east towards Tibet.","w":"A winding river in a deep, narrow valley cut into high, bare grassland hills, often dusted with snow.","f":["At Qamdo in Tibet it joins the Ang Qu, and from there it is called the Lancang, China's name for the Mekong.","The Mekong then flows through six countries on its way to the South China Sea.","Its source, above 5,000 m, lies in Zadoi County, within the Sanjiangyuan 'Source of Three Rivers' area."],"q":{"q":"The Za Qu is the upper course of which river?","a":"The Mekong","x":["The Salween","The Yangtze"]}},"Q21847787":{"t":"South America's second great river, laced with islands","s":"After the Amazon, the Paraná is South America's mightiest river, draining a huge basin across Brazil, Paraguay and Argentina together with its great tributary, the Paraguay. From above it is a broad brown band split by islands and sandbars, with floodplain lakes and marshes glinting on either side. It ends in a vast delta near Buenos Aires, feeding the Río de la Plata.","w":"A broad brown river laced with islands and sandbars, with lagoons and marshes spreading across its floodplain.","f":["At about 4,900 km, it is the second-longest river in South America.","The Itaipu Dam on the Paraná, between Brazil and Paraguay, is one of the world's largest hydroelectric plants.","The Paraná Delta, just north of Buenos Aires, is a maze of islands and channels where houses stand on stilts."],"q":{"q":"Which estuary does the Paraná flow into?","a":"Río de la Plata","x":["Gulf of Paria","Bahía Blanca"]}},"Q836812":{"t":"The upper Congo, once thought to be the Nile","s":"The Lualaba is the Congo River in its youth, rising on the Katanga plateau and flowing north through savanna, gorges and a chain of marshy lakes. From the air it is a dark ribbon edged with swamp and pale floodplain grass. In the 1870s explorers still argued whether it fed the Nile or the Congo; Henry Morton Stanley settled it by following it to the Atlantic.","w":"A dark winding river edged by swamps and pale floodplains, with lakes strung along it, in green-brown savanna.","f":["David Livingstone believed the Lualaba might be the upper Nile.","At Boyoma Falls, near Kisangani, the Lualaba ends and the river is called the Congo.","Upemba National Park, along its upper course, protects a chain of lakes and wetlands."],"q":{"q":"Which explorer followed the Lualaba all the way to the Atlantic?","a":"Henry Morton Stanley","x":["David Livingstone","Richard Burton"]}},"Q2226":{"t":"A short, wide river where pelicans nest on the rapids","s":"The Slave River carries the water of the Peace River and Lake Athabasca north to Great Slave Lake, a wide brown river winding through spruce forest and wetlands. Near Fort Smith it tumbles over a series of rapids, the main barrier on the long waterway from Alberta to the Arctic. On rocky islands in those rapids, American white pelicans nest.","w":"A broad, muddy river meandering through green forest and wetland, with white rapids near the Alberta border.","f":["The pelicans at Fort Smith form the most northerly breeding colony of American white pelicans.","The river takes its name from 'Slavey', an old name for the Dene people living along it.","It flows along the edge of Wood Buffalo National Park, the largest national park in Canada."],"q":{"q":"Which bird nests on islands in the Slave River rapids?","a":"American white pelican","x":["Sandhill crane","Snow goose"]}},"Q41179":{"t":"Six countries, one river, starting in Yunnan's gorges","s":"In Yunnan the Mekong, known in China as the Lancang, runs south through deep, forested valleys, much of it now stepped into long, narrow reservoirs behind a chain of dams. From above it looks like a green-brown snake coiled between steep ridges. From here it still has thousands of kilometres to go, past Laos, Thailand, Cambodia and Vietnam to its delta.","w":"A long, narrow, winding river or reservoir deep between steep green ridges, running roughly north–south.","f":["The Mekong runs for more than 4,000 km, through or along six countries.","Its river fisheries are among the largest in the world and feed millions of people.","Each rainy season the Mekong's flood pushes water backwards up the Tonlé Sap river into Cambodia's great lake."],"q":{"q":"What is the Mekong called in China?","a":"Lancang","x":["Nu Jiang","Jinsha"]}},"Q2599627":{"t":"A great river flowing more than 3.5 km above the sea","s":"If you are landing at Lhasa, this is the river beside the runway: the Yarlung Tsangpo, braided across a broad valley floor some 3,600 m up, with golden sand dunes piled against the slopes. It flows east along the Himalayas, then bursts through them in one of the deepest canyons on Earth and becomes the Brahmaputra in India.","w":"A wide, braided river with pale sandbars and dunes in a broad valley between bare brown mountains.","f":["Its Great Canyon, around the peak Namcha Barwa, is often called the deepest in the world.","Much of its Tibetan course lies above 3,000 m, making it one of the highest major rivers on Earth.","The Lhasa River, which flows past the Tibetan capital, joins it just west of here."],"q":{"q":"Which city's airport lies in the Yarlung Tsangpo valley?","a":"Lhasa","x":["Kathmandu","Xining"]}},"Q128102":{"t":"A tributary longer than the river it joins","s":"The Irtysh rises in the Mongolian Altai, crosses China and Kazakhstan and enters Russia, winding north across the West Siberian Plain to meet the Ob. Here, below Omsk, it loops lazily through a green floodplain of meadows, oxbow lakes and birch groves, with fields beyond. Remarkably, this tributary is longer than the river it flows into.","w":"A winding river in wide, regular loops through a green floodplain of meadows, oxbow lakes and birch woods.","f":["At about 4,250 km, the Irtysh is longer than the Ob, into which it flows.","It meets the Ob near Khanty-Mansiysk.","Tobolsk, on the Irtysh, was the first capital of Siberia; its white stone kremlin stands above the river."],"q":{"q":"Which river does the Irtysh flow into?","a":"The Ob","x":["The Yenisey","The Tobol"]}},"Q3503":{"t":"The deepest river on Earth, crossing the equator twice","s":"Around the equator the Congo spreads into a vast, slow river several kilometres wide, dotted with long forested islands and fringed by flooded forest. From above it looks like a brown lake threaded with green. It is the second-largest river on Earth by flow, and on its great arc through Central Africa it crosses the equator twice.","w":"A broad brown river full of long wooded islands, bordered by dark flooded forest on both banks.","f":["In its lower gorges the Congo is more than 200 m deep, the deepest river known.","It carries more water than any other river except the Amazon.","Kinshasa and Brazzaville face each other across it, the closest pair of capitals apart from Rome and Vatican City."],"q":{"q":"Which two capitals face each other across the Congo?","a":"Kinshasa and Brazzaville","x":["Kinshasa and Bangui","Brazzaville and Luanda"]}},"Q45403":{"t":"A river so wide it looks like a sea of sandbars","s":"In Assam the Brahmaputra is less a river than a braided plain of water and sand several kilometres wide, its channels shifting with every monsoon. From above, look for pale sandbars and green islands scattered across it, with tea gardens and rice fields on the higher ground beyond. Upstream lies Majuli, one of the largest river islands in the world.","w":"A very wide, braided river with pale sandbars and green islands, and the hills of Meghalaya to the south.","f":["Its name means 'son of Brahma'; it is one of the few Indian rivers with a male name.","Its islands and sandbars, called chars, are farmed and even lived on, though floods can wash them away.","Kaziranga National Park, on its south bank, holds about two-thirds of the world's greater one-horned rhinos."],"q":{"q":"Which animal is Kaziranga, on the Brahmaputra, famous for?","a":"One-horned rhinoceros","x":["Snow leopard","Asiatic lion"]}},"Q947477":{"t":"A trout river running from Yellowstone to the Missouri","s":"The Madison is born inside Yellowstone, where the Firehole and Gibbon rivers meet, and runs north through Montana in a broad, open valley between two mountain ranges. From above it is a bright, braided stream with a thin line of willows, crossing sage-grey ranchland. At Three Forks it joins the Jefferson and the Gallatin, and together they form the Missouri.","w":"A bright, winding stream in a wide sage-coloured valley between snowy ranges, lined with a thin green band.","f":["Lewis and Clark named it in 1805 after James Madison, then the US Secretary of State.","Anglers rank it among the finest trout rivers in North America.","The Firehole, one of its sources, is warmed by the hot springs and geysers of Yellowstone."],"q":{"q":"Which great river do the Madison, Jefferson and Gallatin form?","a":"The Missouri","x":["The Yellowstone","The Snake"]}},"Q619802":{"t":"The dustiest place on Earth, an ancient lake bed","s":"Pale, almost white ground streaked by dunes marks the Bodélé Depression in Chad, the dried-up bed of an ancient lake. Its floor is diatomite, the fine skeletons of tiny algae, and winter winds funnelled between the Tibesti and Ennedi highlands lift it into vast plumes. Bodélé is the largest single source of dust on Earth; some of it crosses the Atlantic to the Amazon.","w":"A pale, whitish basin crossed by rows of crescent dunes; in winter, long plumes of dust streaming south-west.","f":["The basin is a remnant of Mega-Chad, a lake that once covered an area comparable to the Caspian Sea.","The strongest winds blow here from November to March, the season of the Harmattan.","Bodélé dust carries phosphorus and other nutrients that help fertilise the Amazon rainforest."],"q":{"q":"What is the pale floor of the Bodélé Depression made of?","a":"Remains of tiny algae","x":["Salt crystals","Volcanic ash"]}},"Q1914":{"t":"A land of lakes, all combed the same way","s":"Karelia looks as if a giant rake had been dragged across it: long lakes and ridges all run in the same direction, north-west to south-east, the way the ice sheet moved. Between them lie dark pine and spruce forest, bogs and bare granite. In winter the lakes become white brushstrokes on the dark forest.","w":"Countless long lakes and ridges lined up north-west to south-east among dark forest; Lake Onega to the east.","f":["The Republic of Karelia has around 60,000 lakes.","On Kizhi Island in Lake Onega stands the wooden, 22-domed Church of the Transfiguration, built in 1714.","Many of the songs that became the Finnish epic Kalevala were recorded from singers in Karelia."],"q":{"q":"In which direction do most Karelian lakes stretch?","a":"North-west to south-east","x":["East to west","North-east to south-west"]}},"Q137555":{"t":"A red-brown bushland plateau in the Horn of Africa","s":"The Ogaden is dry country: a red-brown plateau of thorn scrub and scattered acacias, sloping gently from the Ethiopian highlands towards Somalia. From above it looks like a rust-coloured carpet with a fine stipple of bushes, cut by pale, mostly dry riverbeds. After the short rains it flushes green for a few weeks.","w":"A rust-red plain with a fine speckle of bushes and pale dry riverbeds; green only for weeks after the rains.","f":["The Shebelle River, rising in the Ethiopian highlands, crosses the Ogaden on its way to Somalia.","Rain comes here in two short seasons, in spring and in autumn.","Most people here are Somali herders who keep camels, goats and sheep."],"q":{"q":"Which river crosses the Ogaden on its way to Somalia?","a":"The Shebelle","x":["The Awash","The Omo"]}},"Q181103":{"t":"Folded bare mountains in every shade of brown","s":"Balochistan is a wild tangle of bare mountains and dry basins, where rock is folded into long parallel ridges that curve like the grain of polished wood. There is little green: a few oases, dry riverbeds and salt flats in the hollows. The region spans parts of Pakistan, Iran and Afghanistan. To the north-west rises Taftan, a volcano almost 4,000 m high that still steams.","w":"Long, parallel, curving ridges of bare brown and grey rock, with pale dry riverbeds and salt flats between them.","f":["Balochistan province covers about 44 percent of Pakistan, more than any other province.","Mehrgarh, in Balochistan, is one of the oldest farming villages in South Asia, some 9,000 years old.","On the Makran coast, in the south, small mud volcanoes bubble close to the sea."],"q":{"q":"About what share of Pakistan's area is Balochistan province?","a":"About 44 percent","x":["About 10 percent","About 70 percent"]}},"Q5700":{"t":"Forest, fells and bogs under the northern lights","s":"Lapland from the air is dark forest, pale bogs and rounded, treeless fells, laced with rivers and lakes. North of the Arctic Circle the sun does not set for weeks around midsummer, and in midwinter it barely rises. On a clear winter night you may see the aurora from your window, a green veil moving over the snow below.","w":"Dark conifer forest, pale bogs and bare rounded hilltops with winding rivers; in winter, white with dark forest patches.","f":["Lapland spans the north of Norway, Sweden and Finland and Russia's Kola Peninsula, the homeland of the Sámi.","Reindeer roam freely across much of Finnish Lapland; herding them is a traditional Sámi livelihood.","Rovaniemi, the capital of Finnish Lapland, lies right on the Arctic Circle."],"q":{"q":"Which Indigenous people live in Lapland?","a":"The Sámi","x":["The Inuit","The Nenets"]}},"Q1528035":{"t":"A bird's foot of land reaching into the Gulf","s":"At the end of its long journey the Mississippi has built a strange shape: a bird's foot, with long thin fingers of marsh and levee stretching into the Gulf of Mexico. From above, look for brown water spreading from their tips into the blue sea, and a maze of green marsh, lakes and channels around them. The whole landscape is made of river mud.","w":"Long thin fingers of land reaching into the Gulf, with brown river water fanning into blue sea at their tips.","f":["Over the last 7,000 years the river has built southern Louisiana by switching between several delta lobes.","Coastal Louisiana has lost roughly 5,000 km² of land since the 1930s as the delta sinks and erodes.","Head of Passes, where the river splits into the bird's-foot channels, lies about 150 km below New Orleans."],"q":{"q":"What is the Mississippi's modern delta often compared to?","a":"A bird's foot","x":["A fan","A triangle"]}},"Q29412":{"t":"A maze of mangroves and creeks at the Niger's mouth","s":"Here the Niger breaks into countless creeks and channels before reaching the Atlantic, forming the largest delta in Africa. From above it is a dark green maze of mangrove and swamp forest, laced with brown waterways that glint in the sun. At night the orange points of gas flares dot the darkness.","w":"A dark green maze of mangroves and swamp forest threaded by winding brown creeks; at night, scattered orange flares.","f":["Nigeria's mangrove forests, most of them in this delta, are the largest in Africa.","The delta covers some 70,000 km² of wetlands, creeks and islands.","The Niger arrives here after flowing some 4,200 km from the highlands of Guinea."],"q":{"q":"Which river forms this delta?","a":"The Niger","x":["The Volta","The Senegal"]}},"Q1431507":{"t":"Europe's largest delta, where lotus blooms in August","s":"Before it reaches the Caspian, the Volga fans out into Europe's largest delta: hundreds of channels, reed beds and islands, green against the dry, pale steppe on either side. From above it looks like the branching veins of a leaf. In late summer, lotus flowers bloom across its shallow outer waters in whole pink fields.","w":"A huge green fan of branching channels and reed beds spreading into the Caspian, framed by pale, dry steppe.","f":["The delta is made up of some 500 branches, channels and small streams.","The Astrakhan Nature Reserve, founded here in 1919, is one of the oldest in Russia.","The Caspian lies about 28 m below sea level, so the delta itself lies below the level of the world ocean."],"q":{"q":"Which pink flower blooms across the outer Volga Delta in late summer?","a":"Lotus","x":["Tulip","Peony"]}},"Q184429":{"t":"Reed beds, pelicans and three arms to the sea","s":"Just before the Black Sea, the Danube splits into three arms and spreads into a vast wetland of reeds, lakes and winding channels, one of the largest reed beds on Earth. From above it is a green-gold mosaic stitched with silver water, with the ruler-straight Sulina channel cutting through the middle. In spring and summer it is home to huge colonies of pelicans.","w":"A green-gold mosaic of reeds, lakes and winding channels, cut by one ruler-straight channel, beside the Black Sea.","f":["The Danube Delta was listed as a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1991.","It is Europe's second-largest delta, after the Volga's.","Most of the delta lies in Romania; its northern edge belongs to Ukraine."],"q":{"q":"How many main arms does the Danube split into in its delta?","a":"Three","x":["Two","Seven"]}},"Q1052867":{"t":"A rice bowl of nine dragons and endless canals","s":"The Mekong ends here in a flat, vivid green world: rice paddies in endless rectangles, orchards, fish ponds and villages strung along canals, all laced by wide brown branches of the river. The Vietnamese call it Cửu Long, the 'Nine Dragons', after its many mouths. At harvest time whole districts turn from green to gold.","w":"Vivid green rectangles of rice paddies laced with brown river branches and straight canals, all perfectly flat.","f":["The delta grows about half of Vietnam's rice.","Most of it lies less than 2 m above sea level.","Floating markets, where traders sell straight from boats, still gather near Cần Thơ."],"q":{"q":"What does the Vietnamese name Cửu Long mean?","a":"Nine Dragons","x":["Great River","Golden Water"]}},"Q81591":{"t":"Where Africa is slowly tearing apart","s":"Here, in the Afar region, the Great Rift Valley opens into a scorched lowland of lava fields, salt flats and volcanoes, parts of it more than 100 m below sea level. This is where three plates of the Earth's crust are pulling apart, and one day the sea may flood in. From here the rift runs south for thousands of kilometres, marked by a chain of long lakes.","w":"Black lava fields, white salt pans and scattered volcanic cones in a hot lowland, walled by high escarpments to the west.","f":["In 1974 the fossil skeleton known as Lucy, about 3.2 million years old, was found at Hadar in Afar.","Erta Ale, a volcano in the Danakil Depression, held a lake of lava in its crater for much of the last century.","Dallol, in the north of the depression, has one of the highest average yearly temperatures on Earth."],"q":{"q":"Which famous fossil was found in the Afar region in 1974?","a":"Lucy","x":["Turkana Boy","Taung Child"]}},"Q4863367":{"t":"Treeless tundra where the caribou roam","s":"North of the treeline, Canada's forest gives way to the Barren Grounds: open tundra of moss, lichen and bare rock, scattered with more lakes than anyone has counted. Look for eskers, long snaking gravel ridges left by rivers that once flowed inside the ice sheet. Every year great herds of caribou migrate across this land.","w":"Treeless land speckled with countless lakes, crossed by long, winding gravel ridges; white for most of the year.","f":["The Thelon Wildlife Sanctuary, created in 1927, protects musk oxen and caribou in the heart of the Barren Grounds.","Nearby Baker Lake is Nunavut's only inland community.","Some eskers here run for hundreds of kilometres."],"q":{"q":"What are the long, winding gravel ridges of the Barren Grounds called?","a":"Eskers","x":["Drumlins","Moraines"]}},"Q381050":{"t":"No trees, one straight line, and cliffs above the sea","s":"Nullarbor is Latin for 'no tree', and from above the name fits: a vast, flat, olive-grey plain of saltbush and bluebush without a single tree in sight. It is the world's largest single slab of limestone, riddled with caves underneath. Along its southern edge the Bunda Cliffs drop sheer into the Great Australian Bight.","w":"A dead-flat grey-green plain without trees, one straight railway across it, and sheer cliffs along the southern sea.","f":["The Trans-Australian Railway crosses it on a dead-straight stretch of about 478 km, the longest in the world.","The limestone formed on an ancient seabed and covers some 200,000 km².","The Bunda Cliffs are up to about 120 m high and run for roughly 100 km."],"q":{"q":"What does 'Nullarbor' mean?","a":"No tree","x":["Red earth","Great plain"]}},"Q1807196":{"t":"Tundra of countless lakes, all pointing the same way","s":"Between the Brooks Range and the Arctic Ocean, the North Slope is a flat, treeless tundra covered in lakes, many of them oval and aligned in the same direction, as if combed by the wind. For about nine months the land is white; in summer it thaws into a glittering sheet of water and green. Beneath it lies permafrost hundreds of metres thick.","w":"Flat, treeless tundra with countless oval lakes all aligned the same way; the snowy Brooks Range to the south.","f":["Prudhoe Bay, on the North Slope, is the largest oilfield in North America.","In Utqiaġvik, the northernmost town in the US, the sun does not set for more than 80 days in summer.","The Trans-Alaska Pipeline carries oil about 1,300 km from here to the port of Valdez."],"q":{"q":"Which mountains form the southern edge of the North Slope?","a":"The Brooks Range","x":["The Alaska Range","The Chugach Mountains"]}},"Q271294":{"t":"A red basin under a lid of cloud","s":"Ringed by mountains, the Sichuan Basin often hides beneath a flat lid of cloud or haze; when it clears, you see low reddish hills carved into countless terraced fields, ponds and villages. Its soils come from purple-red sandstone, which gave it the name 'Red Basin'. Chengdu lies on the fertile plain in the west, Chongqing where the Jialing meets the Yangtze.","w":"Low, rounded reddish hills covered in tiny terraced fields and ponds, ringed by mountains; often under a sheet of cloud.","f":["A Chinese saying, 'Sichuan dogs bark at the sun', jokes that the sun shows itself so rarely here that dogs are startled.","The Dujiangyan irrigation system, begun in the 3rd century BC, still waters the Chengdu plain.","Around 100 million people live in the basin."],"q":{"q":"Which colour gives the Sichuan Basin its nickname?","a":"Red","x":["Yellow","Black"]}},"Q216622":{"t":"A sea of sand ringed by snowy mountains and oases","s":"The Tarim Basin holds the Taklamakan, one of the largest sandy deserts on Earth: a sea of dunes in ochre and gold, some ridges more than 100 m tall. Around the rim, snowy ranges, the Tian Shan, the Pamirs and the Kunlun, feed rivers that die in the sand and a necklace of green oases such as Kashgar and Hotan. The Silk Road ran along both edges.","w":"An immense sea of golden dunes, ringed by snowy mountains, with green oases dotted along its edges.","f":["The Tarim, China's longest inland river, flows along the desert's northern edge and never reaches the sea.","Mummies found in the basin, some about 4,000 years old, were preserved by the dry, salty sand.","A highway crosses the Taklamakan, lined for hundreds of kilometres with watered shrubs that hold back the sand."],"q":{"q":"Which desert fills the Tarim Basin?","a":"Taklamakan","x":["Gobi","Karakum"]}},"Q157603":{"t":"The world's largest tropical wetland, breathing with floods","s":"The Pantanal breathes with the seasons. From about December to March, rain swells the Paraguay River and its tributaries until much of this vast plain lies under shallow water, dotted with islands of forest. In the dry months the water retreats into lagoons and winding channels, and grassland and cattle reappear. It is the largest tropical wetland on Earth.","w":"A flat mosaic of shining lagoons, winding channels and green islands of forest; drier and browner from June to October.","f":["It spreads across Brazil, Bolivia and Paraguay and covers roughly 150,000 km².","It is one of the best places in the world to see wild jaguars.","Part of the Pantanal has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 2000."],"q":{"q":"Which river drains the Pantanal?","a":"The Paraguay","x":["The Amazon","The São Francisco"]}},"Q1347148":{"t":"Endless lake-speckled tundra on the edge of the Arctic","s":"Between the Byrranga Mountains of Taymyr and the Central Siberian Plateau lies the North Siberian Lowland, a flat, treeless tundra stretching for more than a thousand kilometres. From above it is a vast canvas speckled with lakes, crossed by slow, looping rivers such as the Anabar and the Olenyok. For most of the year it is white; the short summer paints it brown and green.","w":"Flat, treeless tundra peppered with lakes and crossed by looping rivers; white for most of the year.","f":["The permafrost here reaches hundreds of metres deep.","In midwinter the polar night lasts for weeks; in summer the sun circles the sky without setting.","The rivers here are locked in ice for about eight months a year."],"q":{"q":"Which mountains rise along the lowland's northern edge?","a":"The Byrranga Mountains","x":["The Putorana Plateau","The Verkhoyansk Range"]}},"Q737789":{"t":"A lowland below sea level, rimmed with salt lakes","s":"Around the northern Caspian the land sinks below the level of the world ocean. From above it is a dry, pale-brown plain of steppe and semi-desert, streaked with sand and dotted with glittering white salt lakes, with the green Volga cutting across it. At its lowest, along the Caspian shore, it lies about 28 m below sea level.","w":"A flat, pale-brown plain with white salt lakes and sandy patches, crossed by the green ribbon of the Volga.","f":["Lake Elton, in the north of the lowland, is the largest salt lake in Europe.","Lake Baskunchak has supplied Russia with salt for centuries.","Bolshoye Bogdo, only about 150 m high, is the lowland's highest point and a sacred place for the Kalmyks."],"q":{"q":"Roughly how far below sea level does the Caspian Depression reach?","a":"About 28 m","x":["About 5 m","About 90 m"]}},"Q654544":{"t":"A green labyrinth that swallows half the White Nile","s":"Here the White Nile loses its way in the Sudd, one of the largest wetlands in the world: a vast green labyrinth of papyrus, reeds and floating plants, threaded by channels and lagoons that glint in the sun. So much water evaporates that roughly half the river's flow never leaves the swamp. Its size swells and shrinks with the rainy season.","w":"A vast, flat green swamp laced with shining channels and lagoons, with the main river winding through it.","f":["Its name comes from the Arabic for 'barrier': the swamp long blocked boats travelling up the Nile.","Around AD 60 explorers sent by the Roman emperor Nero were turned back by the swamp.","The Jonglei Canal, begun in 1978 to carry water around the swamp, was never finished."],"q":{"q":"What does the Arabic name 'Sudd' mean?","a":"Barrier","x":["Lake","Green sea"]}},"Q11767":{"t":"The land between two rivers, where writing began","s":"Mesopotamia means 'between the rivers' in Greek, and from above you can see why: the Tigris and the Euphrates run roughly side by side across a brown, dry plain, each edged with a thin green strip of fields and palms. Between them lies open steppe that turns briefly green after the winter rains. Along these rivers some of the first cities, and the first writing, appeared.","w":"A dry brown plain with two big rivers, each lined by a thin green strip of farmland, running south-east.","f":["Cuneiform, one of the earliest writing systems, appeared here more than 5,000 years ago.","Farming here depended on irrigation canals, some of the oldest in the world.","In southern Iraq the two rivers finally meet and flow on to the Gulf as the Shatt al-Arab."],"q":{"q":"What does the name 'Mesopotamia' mean?","a":"Between the rivers","x":["Land of palms","Fertile plain"]}},"Q208965":{"t":"Where the Transantarctic Mountains meet the Ross Sea","s":"Victoria Land is Antarctica at its most dramatic: the Transantarctic Mountains rise straight from the Ross Sea, their peaks over 4,000 m, with great glaciers pouring between them onto the sea ice. Further south lie the McMurdo Dry Valleys, bare brown valleys among the driest places on Earth. James Clark Ross named this land after Queen Victoria in 1841.","w":"Jagged dark mountains and broad glaciers running down to a white, ice-covered sea; further south, bare brown valleys.","f":["The McMurdo Dry Valleys are the largest ice-free area in Antarctica.","At Cape Adare, in the north, stand huts built in 1899, the first buildings on the Antarctic continent.","Italy's Mario Zucchelli Station stands on its coast, at Terra Nova Bay."],"q":{"q":"Who is Victoria Land named after?","a":"Queen Victoria","x":["A ship called Victoria","An explorer's daughter"]}},"Q1389935":{"t":"Named after a chocolate maker who paid for the voyage","s":"Inland from the coast, the dark peaks of the Prince Charles Mountains break through the ice of Mac. Robertson Land, while beside them the Lambert Glacier, often called the largest glacier on Earth, flows slowly north into the Amery Ice Shelf. From above, look for the long, streaked surface of moving ice between the mountains. The land honours Macpherson Robertson, an Australian chocolate maker who helped fund Mawson's 1929–31 expedition.","w":"Dark mountain ridges poking through the ice, beside a vast, streaked glacier flowing towards a flat ice shelf.","f":["Robertson's company made sweets such as the Freddo Frog chocolate.","The Lambert Glacier is more than 400 km long and up to about 100 km wide.","Australia's Mawson Station stands on its coast."],"q":{"q":"What did the man who gave Mac. Robertson Land its name make?","a":"Chocolate and sweets","x":["Whaling ships","Aircraft"]}},"Q2607094":{"t":"Where Australia set up home in Antarctica in 1954","s":"Along the Mawson Coast the ice sheet ends in blue-white cliffs, broken by a few dark rocky headlands and islands. On one of them, in 1954, Australia built Mawson Station, the oldest station still running south of the Antarctic Circle. Behind it the Framnes Mountains poke through the ice, and fierce winds rush down the slope towards the sea.","w":"Blue-white ice cliffs with dark rocky islands and headlands, sea ice offshore, and dark peaks poking through the ice inland.","f":["The coast and the station are named after Douglas Mawson, the Australian geologist and polar explorer.","Nearby, at Auster, emperor penguins raise their chicks on the winter sea ice.","Katabatic winds pouring off the ice here often blow at gale force."],"q":{"q":"When was Mawson Station founded?","a":"1954","x":["1911","1985"]}},"Q1576051":{"t":"An icy coast with India's first Antarctic base","s":"A flat ice shelf fringes the Princess Astrid Coast and meets the sea in low white cliffs. Just behind it lies the Schirmacher Oasis, a low strip of dark rock dotted with lakes that thaw in summer. Further south, where you are now, the jagged spires of the Wohlthat Mountains break through the ice, and beyond them the polar plateau begins.","w":"A flat white ice shelf along the sea, a small dark strip of rock and lakes, then jagged mountain spires inland.","f":["India's first Antarctic base, Dakshin Gangotri, was built on the ice shelf here in 1983.","By 1989 Dakshin Gangotri had been abandoned, slowly buried by snow and ice.","Russia's Novolazarevskaya and India's Maitri stations stand side by side in the Schirmacher Oasis."],"q":{"q":"Which country's first Antarctic base was built here in 1983?","a":"India","x":["China","Brazil"]}},"Q1663286":{"t":"Ice-free hills and salty lakes beside a giant ice shelf","s":"Along the Ingrid Christensen Coast the ice sheet parts in places to reveal low hills of bare brown rock scattered with lakes, some of them saltier than the sea. These oases, the Vestfold and Larsemann Hills, are rare in Antarctica, and several countries have built stations among them. To the west lies the vast, flat Amery Ice Shelf.","w":"White ice with patches of bare brown hills and small lakes along the coast; a vast, flat ice shelf to the west.","f":["Deep Lake in the Vestfold Hills is so salty that it stays liquid at around −18 °C.","Australia's Davis Station stands in the Vestfold Hills; Russia, China and India have stations in the Larsemann Hills.","The coast is named after Ingrid Christensen, wife of the Norwegian whaling magnate Lars Christensen."],"q":{"q":"What makes the Vestfold Hills unusual in Antarctica?","a":"They are largely free of ice","x":["They are active volcanoes","They are its highest peaks"]}},"Q21471424":{"t":"Where the first Soviet Antarctic station was built","s":"The Queen Mary Coast is edged by the Shackleton Ice Shelf, a flat white platform of floating ice that ends at the sea in cliffs, with icebergs crowding below. Behind it the ice sheet rises gently inland. In 1956 the Soviet Union built Mirny here, its first Antarctic station, and Russia still runs it today.","w":"A flat white ice shelf ending in cliffs over dark water scattered with icebergs; the ice sheet rising gently behind.","f":["Mawson's Australasian Antarctic Expedition of 1911–14 named it after Queen Mary, wife of King George V.","Mawson's western party spent the winter of 1912 on the Shackleton Ice Shelf.","Emperor penguins breed on the sea ice near Mirny."],"q":{"q":"Which station, founded here in 1956, was the first Soviet base in Antarctica?","a":"Mirny","x":["Vostok","Novolazarevskaya"]}},"Q2944697":{"t":"An oasis of lakes spotted from the air in 1947","s":"Along the Knox Coast the ice sheet flows down to the sea in broad glaciers, but at its western end lies a surprise: the Bunger Hills, a patch of bare, low rock dotted with dozens of lakes. From the air it looks like a brown island in a white sea. US Navy pilot David Bunger spotted it in 1947, and it now bears his name.","w":"White ice sloping down to the sea, with a striking patch of bare brown hills and lakes near the western end.","f":["The coast is named after Samuel Knox, a ship's commander on the US Exploring Expedition of 1838–42.","The Bunger Hills are one of the largest ice-free areas on the Antarctic coast.","In 1956 the Soviet Union opened a station called Oazis in the Bunger Hills."],"q":{"q":"What did a pilot discover on this coast in 1947?","a":"An ice-free oasis with lakes","x":["An active volcano","A lake buried under ice"]}},"Q124971":{"t":"Where a great East Antarctic glacier meets the sea","s":"On the Sabrina Coast the ice of East Antarctica pours into the ocean through the Totten Glacier, one of the largest on the continent, which ends in a floating tongue of ice. Scientists have found warm ocean water reaching beneath it, and they watch it closely. From above, look for streaked, crevassed ice meeting a sea scattered with icebergs.","w":"Crevassed, streaked ice flowing into a floating ice tongue, with icebergs and sea ice on the dark water beyond.","f":["The coast takes its name from the Sabrina, a cutter of John Balleny's British expedition of 1839.","The Totten Glacier drains a basin holding enough ice to raise world sea level by several metres.","The Moscow University Ice Shelf also lies along this coast."],"q":{"q":"What is the Sabrina Coast named after?","a":"A small sailing ship","x":["An explorer's daughter","A whaling station"]}},"Q21489984":{"t":"The windy coast that gave its name to a penguin","s":"The Adélie Coast is a short stretch of ice cliffs and rocky islets, where fierce winds pour off the ice sheet towards the sea. The French explorer Jules Dumont d'Urville landed here in 1840 and named the land after his wife, Adèle. The Adélie penguin, which nests on its rocky islands, carries the same name.","w":"Ice cliffs broken by dark rocky islets, with sea ice and icebergs offshore and the ice sheet rising steadily inland.","f":["France's Dumont d'Urville Station stands on a small island just off this coast.","Its katabatic winds are among the strongest in Antarctica.","The film 'March of the Penguins' was shot among emperor penguins near Dumont d'Urville Station."],"q":{"q":"Who was the Adélie Coast named after?","a":"The explorer's wife","x":["A French queen","The captain's daughter"]}},"Q728390":{"t":"The icy coast where the ozone hole was found","s":"Coats Land runs down the eastern side of the Weddell Sea, where the ice sheet slides off the land into broad ice shelves. On one of them, the Brunt Ice Shelf, stands Britain's Halley station, whose measurements revealed the Antarctic ozone hole in 1985. From above there is little but white: flat shelves, long cracks and the edge of the sea ice.","w":"Flat white ice shelves with long cracks meeting the frozen Weddell Sea; the ice sheet rising gently inland.","f":["Halley VI stands on legs with skis so it can be towed; in 2017 it was moved away from a growing crack.","The land was named in 1904 after James and Andrew Coats, Scottish sponsors of William Speirs Bruce's expedition.","Emperor penguins breed on the sea ice along this coast."],"q":{"q":"What did measurements at Halley station reveal in 1985?","a":"The ozone hole","x":["Lake Vostok","The Gamburtsev Mountains"]}},"Q1134547":{"t":"Home of Antarctica's highest mountains","s":"In Ellsworth Land the Ellsworth Mountains rise from the ice like a great wall some 350 km long, and among them stands Vinson Massif, at 4,892 m the highest point in Antarctica. Around them the ice sheet stretches flat and white towards the Ronne Ice Shelf and the Bellingshausen Sea. Lincoln Ellsworth crossed this land in 1935 on the first flight across Antarctica.","w":"A long wall of dark, jagged mountains rising from the white ice sheet, with flat ice stretching away on all sides.","f":["The Sentinel Range, the northern part of the Ellsworth Mountains, holds Antarctica's highest peaks.","Climbers reach Vinson from a camp on the ice at Union Glacier, served by flights from Chile.","Vinson was first climbed only in 1966."],"q":{"q":"How high is Vinson Massif?","a":"4,892 m","x":["3,794 m","6,190 m"]}},"Q136270":{"t":"A lonely coast first glimpsed by a sealer in 1833","s":"Kemp Land is one of the quietest corners of East Antarctica: a smooth white slope of ice running down to low ice cliffs, bays and a scatter of small rocky islands. Few people have ever set foot here. The coast was first sighted in 1833 by Peter Kemp, a British sealing captain searching the Southern Ocean for new hunting grounds.","w":"A smooth white ice slope ending in low ice cliffs and bays, with a scatter of dark rocky islets and sea ice.","f":["Kemp was sailing the brig Magnet when he sighted the coast.","Mawson's BANZARE expedition charted its coastline in 1930 from the ship Discovery.","It lies between Enderby Land to the west and Mac. Robertson Land to the east."],"q":{"q":"Who was Peter Kemp, who sighted this coast in 1833?","a":"A sealing captain","x":["A Royal Navy admiral","A whaling-station owner"]}},"Q4084201":{"t":"The glacier road Shackleton took towards the Pole","s":"Here the Transantarctic Mountains meet the western edge of the Ross Ice Shelf, and huge glaciers such as the Nimrod and the Beardmore squeeze between the peaks down onto the flat ice. In 1908–09 Ernest Shackleton's party climbed the Beardmore towards the South Pole and turned back only about 180 km short. From above, look for long rivers of ice striped with dark moraine.","w":"Dark, snow-streaked mountains with broad glaciers flowing down onto the flat, white Ross Ice Shelf.","f":["The coast was named after Sir Ernest Shackleton in 1961.","The Nimrod Glacier is named after the ship of Shackleton's 1907–09 expedition.","The Beardmore Glacier, at the coast's southern end, is about 200 km long, one of the largest valley glaciers on Earth."],"q":{"q":"About how far from the South Pole did Shackleton's party turn back?","a":"About 180 km","x":["About 20 km","About 600 km"]}},"Q2983463":{"t":"A coast named after an expedition's initials","s":"Walls of blue-white ice cliffs mark the Banzare Coast, where the ice sheet slides into the sea, with icebergs drifting offshore and a band of sea ice for much of the year. The name is an acronym: BANZARE, the British Australian and New Zealand Antarctic Research Expedition. Led by Douglas Mawson, it charted this coast in 1931.","w":"Blue-white ice cliffs along the sea, icebergs and a belt of sea ice offshore, smooth white ice rising behind.","f":["BANZARE sailed in the Discovery, the ship Robert Falcon Scott had taken to Antarctica in 1901.","Far inland the ice rises to Dome C, over 3,200 m high, home to the French–Italian Concordia station.","The coast lies close to the Antarctic Circle, so in midsummer the sun barely sets."],"q":{"q":"Where does the name Banzare come from?","a":"An expedition's initials","x":["A ship's name","An explorer's surname"]}},"Q2983473":{"t":"A line of ice cliffs named after a captain's wife","s":"The Clarie Coast is a long line of ice cliffs where the East Antarctic ice sheet meets the sea, broken here and there by glacier tongues and stranded icebergs. In January 1840 Jules Dumont d'Urville's ships sailed along these cliffs. He named the coast after Claire, wife of Charles Jacquinot, captain of his second ship, the Zélée.","w":"A long line of white ice cliffs with jutting glacier tongues and stranded icebergs, sea ice spreading offshore.","f":["An American expedition under Charles Wilkes was charting the same coast that very month.","Australia calls this stretch the Wilkes Coast.","It lies between the Banzare Coast to the west and Adélie Land to the east."],"q":{"q":"Who was Claire, after whom the Clarie Coast is named?","a":"A ship captain's wife","x":["A French princess","A scientist's daughter"]}},"Q2576481":{"t":"Rocky islands where Australia's Casey Station stands","s":"Ice cliffs and glaciers line the Budd Coast, but a scatter of low rocky islands, the Windmill Islands, breaks the white. On a rocky peninsula facing them stands Australia's Casey Station, one of its three permanent bases on the continent. Offshore, the sea freezes into a wide belt of fast ice in winter.","w":"Low dark rocky islands and peninsulas at the edge of white ice cliffs, with sea ice spreading offshore.","f":["The coast is named after Thomas Budd, an officer on Charles Wilkes's US expedition of 1838–42.","Casey Station is named after Richard Casey, an Australian politician who later became governor-general.","Adélie penguins nest on the Windmill Islands in summer."],"q":{"q":"Which country runs Casey Station on the Budd Coast?","a":"Australia","x":["New Zealand","Chile"]}},"Q21489993":{"t":"A lone extinct volcano on an icy coast","s":"The Wilhelm II Coast is mostly smooth ice meeting the sea, but it holds one oddity: Gaussberg, a dark, rounded extinct volcano about 370 m high, standing alone at the edge of the ice. It was found in 1902 by Erich von Drygalski's German expedition, whose ship Gauss was frozen into the sea ice nearby for almost a year. The coast is named after the German emperor Wilhelm II.","w":"Smooth white ice meeting the sea, with a single dark, dome-shaped hill standing out near the coast.","f":["The Gauss stayed locked in the ice from February 1902 to February 1903.","Drygalski's team sent up a tethered balloon to look inland, one of the first flights in Antarctica.","Gaussberg is named after the ship, which honoured the mathematician Carl Friedrich Gauss."],"q":{"q":"What is Gaussberg?","a":"An extinct volcano","x":["A research station","An ice dome"]}},"Q1634150":{"t":"Japan's Antarctic home on an icy bay","s":"The Prince Harald Coast wraps around Lützow-Holm Bay, a wide inlet usually packed with sea ice and dotted with rocky islands. On one of them, East Ongul Island, stands Japan's Syowa Station, opened in 1957. Inland the ice sheet rises steadily towards the polar plateau, broken near the coast by small patches of bare rock.","w":"A broad bay filled with white sea ice and dotted with dark islands, the ice sheet rising behind it.","f":["It is named after Prince Harald of Norway, who later became King Harald V.","Japan's icebreaker Shirase often has to ram its way through thick sea ice to reach Syowa.","Lützow-Holm Bay is named after Finn Lützow-Holm, a Norwegian naval pilot who flew over this region."],"q":{"q":"Which country runs Syowa Station on this coast?","a":"Japan","x":["South Korea","Norway"]}},"Q1578672":{"t":"A quiet coast named after a future king","s":"East of Lützow-Holm Bay, the Prince Olav Coast is a stretch of low ice cliffs and glacier tongues where the ice sheet meets the sea, with sea ice clinging to the shore much of the year. Inland, the white slope climbs steadily towards the high plateau, broken only by a few dark nunataks. It is named after Crown Prince Olav of Norway.","w":"Low ice cliffs and glacier tongues with sea ice along the shore, and a white slope rising gently inland.","f":["Olav later reigned as King Olav V of Norway, from 1957 to 1991.","Norwegian expeditions of the 1930s named many coasts of Queen Maud Land after members of the royal family.","Japanese scientists from nearby Syowa Station have surveyed and mapped much of this coast."],"q":{"q":"Who was Prince Olav, after whom this coast is named?","a":"A future king of Norway","x":["A Danish explorer","A Swedish scientist"]}},"Q928344":{"t":"Where Antarctica was first sighted, in 1820","s":"The Princess Martha Coast is fringed by broad, flat ice shelves that end at the sea in white walls. Off this coast, in January 1820, the Russian expedition of Bellingshausen and Lazarev sighted a vast ice barrier, often counted as the first sighting of Antarctica. Today Germany's Neumayer station sits on the ice shelf, and South Africa's SANAE IV on a rocky outcrop inland.","w":"Flat white ice shelves ending in low cliffs at the sea; inland, dark rocky outcrops and mountains break the ice.","f":["The coast is named after Crown Princess Märtha of Norway, wife of Crown Prince Olav.","Neumayer Station III stands on hydraulic legs, so it can be raised as the snow builds up around it.","SANAE IV, South Africa's Antarctic base, perches on a nunatak called Vesleskarvet."],"q":{"q":"Who sighted an ice shelf off this coast in January 1820?","a":"Bellingshausen and Lazarev","x":["Cook and Furneaux","Ross and Crozier"]}},"Q6111648":{"t":"Where the vast Thwaites Glacier meets the sea","s":"On the Walgreen Coast the West Antarctic ice sheet reaches the Amundsen Sea through some of the fastest-changing glaciers on Earth, above all the Thwaites Glacier, about the size of Florida. From above you would see a jumble of crevassed ice breaking into icebergs, with pack ice beyond. The coast is named after Charles Walgreen of the drugstore chain, who helped fund Richard Byrd's 1933–35 expedition.","w":"A chaos of crevassed, broken ice and icebergs where glaciers meet a sea choked with pack ice.","f":["Thwaites Glacier rests on a bed far below sea level, which makes it sensitive to warm ocean water.","Scientists drill through the ice and send robots beneath it to measure how fast it is melting.","Pine Island Glacier, another fast-flowing giant, lies just to the east."],"q":{"q":"Which glacier on this coast is about the size of Florida?","a":"Thwaites Glacier","x":["Beardmore Glacier","Lambert Glacier"]}},"Q5255259":{"t":"The far edge of the Ross Ice Shelf, below the mountains","s":"At the Gould Coast the flat Ross Ice Shelf, the largest ice shelf on Earth, meets the foot of the Transantarctic Mountains, where glaciers such as the Scott Glacier pour down between dark peaks. This is one of the most remote places on the planet, only about 650 km from the South Pole. It is named after Laurence Gould, the geologist who led a dog-sledge party here in 1929.","w":"The flat white ice shelf meeting a wall of dark, snowy mountains, with broad glaciers flowing out between them.","f":["Gould was second-in-command of Richard Byrd's 1928–30 expedition; his party mapped some 280 km of this coast.","The Scott Glacier, discovered by Gould's party in 1929, is named after Robert Falcon Scott, who never saw it.","The Ross Ice Shelf is roughly the size of France and hundreds of metres thick."],"q":{"q":"Who was Laurence Gould, after whom this coast is named?","a":"A geologist and explorer","x":["A ship's captain","An aircraft maker"]}},"Q1551170":{"t":"The high, icy base of the Antarctic Peninsula","s":"Palmer Land is the broad southern part of the Antarctic Peninsula, where the narrow mountain spine widens into a high, ice-covered plateau cut by glaciers flowing to both coasts. To the west, look for George VI Sound, a long, straight channel filled with an ice shelf, separating it from Alexander Island. It is named after Nathaniel Palmer, an American sealer who sailed these waters in 1820.","w":"A high white plateau with glaciers running off both sides; to the west, a long, straight ice-filled channel.","f":["The US Palmer Station is not in Palmer Land at all, but far north on Anvers Island, off Graham Land.","Its mountains rise to more than 3,000 m.","Nathaniel Palmer was only 21 when he sailed his sloop Hero into these waters."],"q":{"q":"Where is the US Palmer Station?","a":"On Anvers Island","x":["In Palmer Land","On Alexander Island"]}},"Q618370":{"t":"Antarctica's mountainous arm reaching north","s":"Graham Land is the northern arm of the Antarctic Peninsula, a narrow, rugged spine of mountains draped in glaciers that reaches up towards South America. Its west coast is a maze of channels, islands and ice cliffs visited by most tourist ships to Antarctica. On its east coast lie the Larsen ice shelves, parts of which broke apart in 1995 and 2002.","w":"A narrow spine of jagged, glacier-draped mountains between two icy seas, with ice shelves along the eastern side.","f":["John Biscoe named it in 1832 after Sir James Graham, First Lord of the Admiralty.","The tip of the peninsula lies only about 1,000 km from South America, across the Drake Passage.","The Lemaire Channel on its west coast is so narrow and dramatic that it is nicknamed 'Kodak Gap'."],"q":{"q":"Which passage separates Graham Land from South America?","a":"The Drake Passage","x":["The Strait of Magellan","The Beagle Channel"]}},"Q1145030":{"t":"The very tip of the Horn of Africa","s":"Here Africa comes to a point. At Cape Guardafui the coast of the Horn, running east along the Gulf of Aden, turns sharply south into the Indian Ocean. From above, look for a bare, tan headland with pale cliffs and beaches, where turquoise shallows meet the deep blue sea. Ships bound to and from the Red Sea pass it every day.","w":"A bare, tan headland where the coast turns sharply from east to south, pale cliffs above turquoise and deep blue sea.","f":["Ancient Greek sailors knew it as Aromata, the 'Cape of Spices'.","Africa's easternmost point is not here but at Ras Hafun, about 150 km to the south.","The cape marks the boundary between the Gulf of Aden and the open Indian Ocean."],"q":{"q":"Which body of water lies north of Cape Guardafui?","a":"The Gulf of Aden","x":["The Red Sea","The Persian Gulf"]}},"Q193401":{"t":"A hook of sand where rockets leave the Earth","s":"Look for a hook of sand jutting from Florida's Atlantic coast, with lagoons and marshes behind it: Cape Canaveral. Rockets have launched from here since the 1950s, and the Apollo missions to the Moon left from the Kennedy Space Center next door, on Merritt Island. With luck you might even see a launch plume climbing from the coast.","w":"A pointed hook of sand on Florida's Atlantic coast, backed by broad lagoons and marshes.","f":["From 1963 to 1973 the cape was officially called Cape Kennedy.","Its name comes from the Spanish 'cañaveral', a place where cane grows.","Rockets launch eastwards over the Atlantic, so spent stages fall into the sea rather than on land."],"q":{"q":"Which Moon missions launched from beside Cape Canaveral?","a":"Apollo","x":["Gemini","Vostok"]}},"Q780339":{"t":"California's westernmost point, where three plates meet","s":"At Cape Mendocino the rugged Lost Coast of northern California juts furthest into the Pacific, its steep green hills dropping straight into the surf. Just offshore, three plates of the Earth's crust meet at the Mendocino Triple Junction, making this one of the most earthquake-prone corners of the United States. Look for a dark headland with a long white line of breakers.","w":"A dark, steep headland where the northern California coast bulges furthest west, fringed with white surf.","f":["Cape Mendocino is the westernmost point of California.","The Lost Coast around it is so steep that the coastal highway turns inland to avoid it.","Spanish galleons sailing from Manila to Mexico often used the cape as their first landmark in America."],"q":{"q":"How many tectonic plates meet off Cape Mendocino?","a":"Three","x":["Two","Four"]}},"Q1020776":{"t":"The rocky tip of Baja California, where two seas meet","s":"At the very end of the long, dry Baja California peninsula, granite cliffs break into the sea at Land's End, where the Gulf of California meets the open Pacific. From above, look for the sharp southern tip of the peninsula, a curved bay with white beaches and the resort town around it. In winter humpback whales gather in these warm waters.","w":"The sharp southern tip of a long desert peninsula: pale granite cliffs, a curved bay and a town along its shore.","f":["El Arco, a natural stone arch at Land's End, is the town's best-known landmark.","The Baja California peninsula stretches some 1,200 km from the US border to this cape.","The Tropic of Cancer passes just north of the town."],"q":{"q":"Which waters meet at Cabo San Lucas?","a":"Pacific and Gulf of California","x":["Gulf of Mexico and Pacific","Caribbean and Pacific"]}},"ne-rg-1159105013":{"t":"Where polar bears wait for the sea to freeze","s":"On the flat western shore of Hudson Bay, Cape Churchill pushes into the sea, a low point of tundra, lakes and gravel beaches. Every autumn polar bears gather here and near the town of Churchill, waiting for the bay to freeze so they can hunt seals on the ice. Much of the land around lies in Wapusk National Park, one of the largest polar bear denning areas in the world.","w":"A low, flat point of tundra dotted with countless lakes, jutting into Hudson Bay; white with ice much of the year.","f":["The nearby town of Churchill calls itself the 'Polar Bear Capital of the World'.","Each summer thousands of beluga whales gather in the Churchill River estuary.","The treeline runs close by, so forest, tundra and sea ice all meet in this corner of Manitoba."],"q":{"q":"Why do polar bears gather near Cape Churchill in autumn?","a":"To wait for the sea ice","x":["To catch salmon","To mate on the beaches"]}},"Q49152":{"t":"A sandy arm flexing into the Atlantic","s":"Cape Cod is one of the easiest shapes to recognise from the air: a long sandy arm bent at the elbow, curling north into the Atlantic like a flexed bicep, with Provincetown in its fist. Glaciers built it at the end of the last ice age, and waves have been reshaping it ever since. Its outer shore is lined with tall sand cliffs and long, empty beaches.","w":"A long, narrow sandy peninsula bent like an arm and hooking north, with Cape Cod Bay inside its curve.","f":["In 1620 the Mayflower first anchored off Provincetown, at the tip of the cape, before the Pilgrims settled at Plymouth.","The Cape Cod Canal, opened in 1914, cuts across its base, technically making it an island.","Bartholomew Gosnold named it in 1602 for the cod his crew caught offshore."],"q":{"q":"What is the shape of Cape Cod often compared to?","a":"A bent arm","x":["A boot","A lobster claw"]}},"Q2065437":{"t":"New Jersey's southern tip, a crossroads for migrants","s":"At Cape May, New Jersey runs out of land: a blunt peninsula of beaches, marsh and small towns between the Atlantic and Delaware Bay. Every autumn huge numbers of migrating birds and monarch butterflies funnel down the peninsula and gather here before crossing the bay. The town at its tip, full of Victorian houses, is one of the oldest seaside resorts in the United States.","w":"The blunt southern tip of New Jersey, between the open Atlantic and the wide mouth of Delaware Bay.","f":["It is named after Cornelius Jacobsen Mey, a Dutch captain who explored this coast around 1620.","The whole town of Cape May is a US National Historic Landmark for its Victorian buildings.","A car ferry links Cape May across Delaware Bay with Lewes in Delaware."],"q":{"q":"Which bay lies west of Cape May?","a":"Delaware Bay","x":["Chesapeake Bay","Cape Cod Bay"]}},"Q4078":{"t":"The storm-beaten southern end of the Americas","s":"A dark, rocky island at the very end of the Americas: Cape Horn stands on Hornos Island, near the southern edge of the Tierra del Fuego archipelago. Beyond it lies only the Drake Passage and, far to the south, Antarctica. For sailors it was long the most feared headland on Earth, where westerly gales and huge waves sweep unchecked around the globe.","w":"A dark, steep island at the southern edge of a scatter of rugged islands, facing open and often stormy sea.","f":["Dutch navigators Schouten and Le Maire named it in 1616 after the town of Hoorn in the Netherlands.","By old tradition, a sailor who had rounded the Horn under sail earned the right to wear a gold earring.","The cape belongs to Chile; a lighthouse and a small naval post stand on the island."],"q":{"q":"What is Cape Horn named after?","a":"A Dutch town","x":["A ship's foghorn","Its horn-like shape"]}},"Q4092":{"t":"The famous cape that isn't Africa's southern tip","s":"At the end of the Cape Peninsula, south of Cape Town, rocky cliffs and slopes of fynbos scrub drop into a restless sea: this is the Cape of Good Hope. The Portuguese navigator Bartolomeu Dias rounded it in 1488, opening the way for the sea route to India. From above, look for a slender finger of land pointing south, with False Bay on its eastern side.","w":"A slender, rocky peninsula pointing south from Cape Town, with the broad False Bay to its east.","f":["It is not Africa's southernmost point: that is Cape Agulhas, about 150 km to the south-east.","Tradition says Dias called it the Cape of Storms before the Portuguese king renamed it the Cape of Good Hope.","It belongs to the Cape Floral Region, one of the richest areas for plants in the world."],"q":{"q":"Which cape is actually Africa's southernmost point?","a":"Cape Agulhas","x":["Cape Point","Cape Hangklip"]}},"Q1034489":{"t":"The corner where West Africa's coast turns east","s":"At Cape Palmas the coast of West Africa, which has been running south-east, swings round to head almost due east along the Gulf of Guinea. From above, look for a low rocky point with sandy beaches, dark green forest behind it and the mouth of the Cavalla River close by. Portuguese sailors charting this coast in the 15th century named it for its palm trees.","w":"A low point where the coastline turns from south-east to east, with forest behind and a river mouth nearby.","f":["Cape Palmas marks the western limit of the Gulf of Guinea.","The Cavalla River, which reaches the sea close by, forms the border between Liberia and Côte d'Ivoire.","European traders once called the shore west of the cape the Grain Coast, and the shore east of it the Ivory Coast."],"q":{"q":"Which gulf begins at Cape Palmas?","a":"The Gulf of Guinea","x":["The Gulf of Aden","The Gulf of Sidra"]}},"Q216889":{"t":"Africa's westernmost point, crowned by a capital","s":"The Cap-Vert peninsula sticks out from Senegal into the Atlantic like a hook, and its tip, Pointe des Almadies, is the westernmost point of mainland Africa. Almost all of it is covered by Dakar, Senegal's capital, spreading over the low headland right to the sea. Just off its sheltered southern shore lies the small island of Gorée.","w":"A hook-shaped peninsula jutting west into the Atlantic, densely built over, with a tiny island off its south shore.","f":["Portuguese sailors named it the 'Green Cape' in 1444, struck by its greenery after the desert coast to the north.","The Cape Verde Islands, some 600 km to the west, take their name from this cape.","Gorée Island has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1978."],"q":{"q":"Which city covers most of the Cap-Vert peninsula?","a":"Dakar","x":["Banjul","Saint-Louis"]}},"Q849879":{"t":"Tunisia's finger pointing towards Sicily","s":"Cap Bon juts north-east from Tunisia like a finger pointing at Sicily, separating the Gulf of Tunis from the Gulf of Hammamet. From above it is a patchwork of green orchards, vineyards and fields between low hills, edged with sandy beaches. At its tip, only about 140 km of sea separates Africa from Sicily.","w":"A green peninsula pointing north-east into the Mediterranean between two wide gulfs, with farmland and beaches.","f":["The Punic town of Kerkouane, on its coast, is a UNESCO World Heritage site.","The peninsula is famous for its citrus groves and for harissa, the fiery chilli paste.","In Roman times it was known as the Promontory of Mercury."],"q":{"q":"Which island lies about 140 km north-east of Cap Bon?","a":"Sicily","x":["Malta","Sardinia"]}},"ne-rg-1730072123":{"t":"Dry savanna of baobabs, thorn scrub and cattle","s":"The Maasai Steppe spreads south of Arusha as a vast, dry plateau of grass, thorn scrub and scattered baobabs, cut by seasonal riverbeds. For most of the year it is tawny and dusty; after the rains, roughly November to May, it turns green almost overnight. Maasai herders still graze their cattle here, and on its north-western edge lies Tarangire National Park.","w":"A vast tawny plain dotted with scrub and baobabs and crossed by dry riverbeds; green after the rains.","f":["Tarangire, on its edge, is famous for its baobabs and for big herds of elephants in the dry season.","Kilimanjaro rises about 300 km to the north-east.","The steppe is named after the Maasai, pastoral people known for their red cloth and their cattle."],"q":{"q":"Which massive-trunked tree is a landmark of the Maasai Steppe?","a":"Baobab","x":["Cedar","Eucalyptus"]}},"Q4778813":{"t":"Dunes where a Saharan lake once shone","s":"The Aoukâr is a great basin of sand in south-eastern Mauritania, its dunes lined up in long, parallel ridges of orange and ochre. Several thousand years ago a large, reedy lake lay here. On the sandstone cliffs that ring the basin, people of the Tichitt culture built hundreds of stone villages looking out over the water, which has long since vanished.","w":"Long parallel orange dune ridges filling a wide basin, walled to the north by dark sandstone cliffs.","f":["Remains of about 400 stone villages have been found beneath the cliffs of Dhar Tichitt and Dhar Oualata.","The old caravan towns of Tichitt and Oualata are UNESCO World Heritage sites.","The dunes formed after the lakes dried up, between about 7,000 and 5,000 years ago."],"q":{"q":"What filled the Aoukâr basin thousands of years ago?","a":"A large lake","x":["A glacier","An arm of the sea"]}},"Q17515152":{"t":"Greenland's highest peaks above east-coast glaciers","s":"On flights between Europe and North America you may cross this stretch of east Greenland: a wild coast of fjords choked with icebergs, and behind it rows of black peaks rising out of the ice sheet. Among them stands Gunnbjørn Fjeld, about 3,700 m high, the tallest mountain in Greenland and in the whole Arctic. Inland the ice sheet rolls on, white and endless.","w":"Black, jagged peaks and glaciers poking through the white ice sheet, with iceberg-filled fjords along the coast.","f":["Tasiilaq, the largest town on Greenland's east coast, lies in the south of this land.","The land is named after Christian IX, king of Denmark from 1863 to 1906.","Gunnbjørn Fjeld honours Gunnbjörn Ulfsson, a Norseman said to have sighted Greenland around the year 900."],"q":{"q":"Which is the highest mountain in Greenland?","a":"Gunnbjørn Fjeld","x":["Mont Forel","Petermann Peak"]}},"ne-rg-1730072913":{"t":"Flood plains and thorn scrub in Tanzania's dry heart","s":"In the dry interior of Tanzania, the land flattens into the Wembere, or Iwembere, Steppe: a broad plain of grass and acacia scrub drained by the Wembere River. In the rainy season, from about December to April, water spreads across its floodplain in shining sheets laced with channels. By the dry months it has faded to tawny grass, with dark lines of thorn trees marking the old flood edges.","w":"A flat tawny plain with dark scrub, crossed by winding channels; shining floodwater in the rainy season.","f":["The Wembere floodplain is about 105 km long and up to 20 km wide.","The river drains north into Lake Kitangiri.","The steppe is one of the few Tanzanian homes of the Karamoja apalis, a rare small warbler."],"q":{"q":"What happens to the Wembere floodplain in the rainy season?","a":"It floods with shallow water","x":["It is covered in snow","It turns into sand dunes"]}},"ne-rg-1730072927":{"t":"Ancient worn-down mountains and a ring-shaped lake","s":"North of the St. Lawrence, the Laurentians roll away as a sea of low, rounded, dark-forested hills and countless lakes. They are among the oldest mountains on Earth, worn down over a billion years to their roots. To the north-east, look for the Manicouagan reservoir, a ring of water some 70 km across that fills an ancient impact crater.","w":"Endless dark forest on rounded hills with countless lakes; to the north-east, a huge ring-shaped lake.","f":["The Manicouagan crater was formed by an asteroid impact about 214 million years ago.","The Laurentians belong to the Canadian Shield and were raised more than a billion years ago.","Their rivers feed large hydroelectric dams that supply much of Quebec's electricity."],"q":{"q":"What fills the ring-shaped Manicouagan crater today?","a":"A reservoir lake","x":["A glacier","A salt flat"]}},"ne-rg-1730073003":{"t":"Taiga, bogs and the long loops of the Vychegda","s":"Here, in the Komi Republic, the land lies low and flat, and the Vychegda River wanders across it in long, lazy loops, leaving oxbow lakes and sandy beaches behind. Around it spreads dark taiga of spruce and pine, broken by pale bogs and clearings. In winter the whole lowland turns white, and the frozen river shows as a smooth ribbon.","w":"Dark taiga crossed by a looping river with oxbow lakes and sandbanks; pale bogs scattered through the forest.","f":["The Vychegda flows into the Northern Dvina at Kotlas, and on to the White Sea.","Syktyvkar, capital of the Komi Republic, stands where the Sysola meets the Vychegda.","Forests cover most of the Komi Republic."],"q":{"q":"Which river does the Vychegda flow into?","a":"The Northern Dvina","x":["The Pechora","The Volga"]}},"ne-rg-1730073065":{"t":"Green land held between two great rivers","s":"Entre Ríos means 'between rivers', and from above the name explains itself: a long wedge of green land held between the broad Paraná to the west and the Uruguay to the east. In the north it dissolves into the Iberá wetlands, a vast mosaic of lagoons, floating islands and marsh. Further south, gentle hills carry cattle ranches, rice fields and citrus groves.","w":"Green lowland between two wide rivers, with the shining lagoons and marshes of the Iberá wetlands in the north.","f":["The Iberá wetlands are among the largest in South America, home to capybaras, caimans and marsh deer.","Jaguars, long gone from Iberá, have been reintroduced there since 2021.","Argentines call this region 'the Mesopotamia', for the same reason as the land between the Tigris and Euphrates."],"q":{"q":"What does 'Entre Ríos' mean?","a":"Between rivers","x":["Two waters","Green land"]}},"Q10264912":{"t":"An island the size of Switzerland at the river's mouth","s":"Where the Amazon meets the Atlantic, the land dissolves into a maze of islands and muddy channels, and the sea turns brown far out from the coast. The largest island, Marajó, is roughly the size of Switzerland, half flooded savanna and half forest. On its southern side, the city of Belém stands on the Pará River, where the Tocantins also reaches the sea.","w":"A maze of forested islands and brown channels opening into a brown-stained sea, with one huge island at the centre.","f":["Water buffalo are so common on Marajó that police in some towns patrol on them.","At some river mouths here a tidal bore, the pororoca, surges upstream as a wave several metres high.","Including its islands, the mouth of the Amazon is more than 200 km wide."],"q":{"q":"Which huge island lies in the mouth of the Amazon?","a":"Marajó","x":["Bananal","Trinidad"]}},"Q3820265":{"t":"The dry, thorny, empty half of Paraguay","s":"North of the Pilcomayo River the Chaco turns drier and emptier: this is the Chaco Boreal, a flat expanse of thorn forest, cactus and scrub stretching across western Paraguay into Bolivia. From above it is grey-green felt cut by pale sandy riverbeds and, near the few towns, by huge rectangular clearings for cattle. Summers here are among the hottest in South America.","w":"Flat grey-green thorn forest with pale, dry riverbeds and, near towns, big rectangular cattle clearings.","f":["The Chaco covers more than half of Paraguay, but only a few percent of Paraguayans live there.","Mennonite settlers founded farming colonies here from the 1920s, around towns such as Filadelfia.","Defensores del Chaco National Park protects jaguars, pumas and giant armadillos in the far north."],"q":{"q":"Which river marks the southern edge of the Chaco Boreal?","a":"The Pilcomayo","x":["The Paraná","The Amazon"]}},"Q3775198":{"t":"Forest, farmland and a field of fallen iron","s":"South of the Bermejo River lies the Chaco Austral, the southern Chaco of northern Argentina: a flat land of dry forest, palm savanna and marsh, much of it now cleared into vast fields of cotton and soybeans. From above look for rectangles of farmland cut out of dark forest, and slow rivers winding east towards the Paraná. Not far from here lies Campo del Cielo, a field of fallen iron meteorites.","w":"Flat country of dark forest remnants and large rectangular fields, crossed by slow, winding rivers.","f":["At Campo del Cielo, iron meteorites fell some 4,000 years ago; one, El Chaco, weighs about 30 tonnes.","The quebracho tree, whose name means 'axe-breaker', was logged here on a huge scale for tannin.","The Bermejo River, which marks its northern edge, carries so much red silt that its name means 'reddish'."],"q":{"q":"What fell at Campo del Cielo about 4,000 years ago?","a":"Iron meteorites","x":["Volcanic bombs","Giant hailstones"]}},"ne-rg-1730073787":{"t":"The ice-bound north-west, named after a great explorer","s":"In far north-west Greenland the ice sheet reaches the sea in broad glaciers, and across a narrow strait rise the mountains of Canada's Ellesmere Island. This remote land carries the name of Knud Rasmussen, the Greenland-born explorer who knew the peoples of the Arctic better than almost anyone. In summer the sun never sets here; in winter it does not rise for months.","w":"White ice sheet ending in wide glaciers at a frozen sea, with the mountains of Ellesmere Island across the strait.","f":["Rasmussen set up a trading station at Thule in 1910, which became the base for his famous expeditions.","On the Fifth Thule Expedition, 1921–24, he crossed Arctic North America by dog sledge to Alaska.","The Humboldt Glacier, to the south-west, has one of the widest ice fronts in the Northern Hemisphere, about 100 km."],"q":{"q":"How did Knud Rasmussen cross Arctic North America in 1921–24?","a":"By dog sledge","x":["By airship","By icebreaker"]}},"Q608383":{"t":"Ice, jagged mountains and a station run on wind and sun","s":"Behind the flat ice shelves of the Princess Ragnhild Coast, the jagged Sør Rondane Mountains break out of the ice sheet in dark ridges and spires. Katabatic winds pour through their valleys towards the sea. In their shelter, on a granite ridge, Belgium built the Princess Elisabeth station, designed to run largely on wind and solar power.","w":"Dark, jagged mountain ridges poking through white ice, with flat ice shelves along the distant coast.","f":["The coast is named after Princess Ragnhild of Norway, the eldest child of Crown Prince Olav.","Princess Elisabeth station opened in 2009.","The coast ends in the east at the Riiser-Larsen Peninsula, where the Prince Harald Coast begins."],"q":{"q":"Which country's station here runs largely on wind and sun?","a":"Belgium","x":["Japan","Norway"]}},"Q1389914":{"t":"The windswept 'home of the blizzard'","s":"Cold air from the polar plateau rushes down to the George V Coast with astonishing force, sweeping over long ice cliffs and floating glacier tongues. Douglas Mawson's expedition wintered at Cape Denison here in 1912 and 1913, in winds so relentless that he called the place 'the home of the blizzard'. The coast is named after King George V.","w":"Long ice cliffs and floating glacier tongues reaching into a sea of pack ice, with icebergs scattered offshore.","f":["Mawson's Huts, the expedition's wooden base at Cape Denison, still stand and are carefully preserved.","In 2010 the long floating tongue of the Mertz Glacier broke off after being struck by a huge iceberg.","Commonwealth Bay, beside Cape Denison, is often called one of the windiest places at sea level on Earth."],"q":{"q":"What did Mawson call this windswept coast?","a":"The home of the blizzard","x":["The white desert","The land of silence"]}},"Q1389591":{"t":"A remote coast first seen from Scott's ship","s":"Oates Land is a remote coast of East Antarctica where the ice sheet flows down to the sea between dark mountain spurs, feeding glaciers that end in floating tongues. Pack ice often keeps ships away, and few people have ever landed here. It was first sighted in 1911 from the Terra Nova, the ship of Robert Falcon Scott's last expedition.","w":"Dark mountain spurs and glaciers running down to a sea crowded with pack ice and icebergs.","f":["It is named after Captain Lawrence Oates, a member of Scott's South Pole party.","The Soviet station Leningradskaya stood on a rocky summit here from 1971 to 1991.","The Terra Nova sighted the coast in February 1911, while dropping off parties for Scott's expedition."],"q":{"q":"From which ship was Oates Land first sighted in 1911?","a":"Terra Nova","x":["Endurance","Fram"]}},"Q171847":{"t":"The river that wraps around the largest river island","s":"Look for a wide, sandy river splitting in two around an enormous island: this is the Araguaia, and the island is Bananal, the largest river island in the world. The river's golden beaches appear in the dry season, from about June to September, when its level drops. Around it spreads the Cerrado, Brazil's tropical savanna, dotted with farms.","w":"A broad river with pale sandy beaches dividing around a huge flat island of savanna, wetland and forest.","f":["Bananal Island covers about 19,000 km².","The Araguaia joins the Tocantins at the 'Parrot's Beak', where three Brazilian states meet.","The Karajá people live along the river and on Bananal Island."],"q":{"q":"What is Bananal, in the Araguaia River?","a":"The largest river island","x":["A great waterfall","A crater lake"]}},"Q191832":{"t":"The Lena's great right-hand river through the taiga","s":"The Aldan winds through the endless larch taiga of Yakutia, sometimes between steep rocky cliffs, sometimes spreading into channels around wooded islands. It is the longest right-bank tributary of the Lena, more than 2,000 km long, and it lies frozen for over half the year. In autumn the larches along its banks turn gold before dropping their needles.","w":"A winding river through dark larch taiga, with rocky cliffs and wooded islands; gold in autumn, white in winter.","f":["Gold was found in its basin in the 1920s, and the town of Aldan grew out of the rush.","The Aldan joins the Lena north of Yakutsk.","Its upper basin lies on the Aldan Shield, one of the oldest parts of Siberia's crust."],"q":{"q":"Which great river does the Aldan flow into?","a":"The Lena","x":["The Amur","The Yenisey"]}},"Q160636":{"t":"A desert river cutting canyons along a border","s":"Through the Chihuahuan Desert the Rio Grande is a thin green ribbon, sometimes almost dry, that has carved deep canyons into bare, tan limestone. Here, near Big Bend, it forms the border between Texas and Mexico and swings round in a great curve. Look for the dark slot of Santa Elena Canyon, whose cliffs rise some 450 m above the water.","w":"A thin, dark-green thread winding through bare tan desert and mountains, vanishing into narrow canyons.","f":["In Mexico it is called the Río Bravo del Norte.","At about 3,000 km it is one of North America's longest rivers, flowing from Colorado to the Gulf of Mexico.","So much of its water is taken for irrigation that in dry years some stretches almost run dry."],"q":{"q":"What is the Rio Grande called in Mexico?","a":"Río Bravo","x":["Río Conchos","Río Colorado"]}},"Q78707":{"t":"The river that splits Siberia in two","s":"The Yenisey is Siberia's great divide. Look at its two banks: to the west, the low, boggy West Siberian Plain; to the east, the higher, forested edge of the Central Siberian Plateau. Here, far to the north, the river is a broad sheet of water, often several kilometres wide, heading straight for the Kara Sea.","w":"A very broad river flowing north, flat bogland on its west bank and higher, darker forested hills on its east.","f":["The Yenisey carries more water into the Arctic Ocean than any other river.","In spring the breaking ice can pile up in walls several metres high along its banks.","Seagoing ships sail up it from the Kara Sea to Igarka, hundreds of kilometres inland."],"q":{"q":"What lies along the Yenisey's east bank?","a":"The Central Siberian Plateau","x":["The West Siberian Plain","The Ural Mountains"]}},"Q78879":{"t":"A far-eastern river of gold, frozen most of the year","s":"The Kolyma winds through the mountains of Russia's far north-east, a pale ribbon between dark larch-covered slopes and wide gravel bars. For about eight months of the year it lies under ice, and the land around it under snow. The region is famous for its gold, washed from the river gravels since the 1930s.","w":"A pale, winding river with wide gravel bars in a valley between dark, larch-covered mountains.","f":["The Kolyma flows some 2,100 km to the East Siberian Sea.","The Kolyma hydroelectric station dams it high in its upper course.","Frozen remains of mammoths have been found in the permafrost of its lower basin."],"q":{"q":"Into which sea does the Kolyma flow?","a":"The East Siberian Sea","x":["The Sea of Okhotsk","The Bering Sea"]}},"Q8319":{"t":"From Rocky Mountain gorges to the Great Plains","s":"The Arkansas begins high in the Colorado Rockies, then runs east for more than 2,000 km across the Great Plains to the Mississippi. Here, in northern Oklahoma, it is a wide, sandy river braided between low wooded banks, winding through a patchwork of wheat fields and pastures. Dams hold it in broad reservoirs such as Kaw Lake.","w":"A wide, sandy, braided river with thin wooded banks, crossing a patchwork of fields and grassland.","f":["At about 2,350 km, the Arkansas is one of the longest tributaries of the Mississippi.","In Colorado it flows through the Royal Gorge, more than 300 m deep.","In Kansas many people pronounce the river's name 'ar-KAN-zas', unlike the name of the state."],"q":{"q":"Where does the Arkansas River begin?","a":"In the Colorado Rockies","x":["In the Ozarks","In the Black Hills"]}},"Q26422":{"t":"The 'Angry River' deep in its gorge","s":"In western Yunnan the Salween, known in China as the Nu Jiang, the 'Angry River', races through a gorge so deep that its walls rise well over a thousand metres above the water. From above it is a thin dark line at the bottom of a steep, forested trench running north to south. Close by, the Mekong and the Yangtze flow parallel to it through gorges of their own.","w":"A thin, dark river at the bottom of a very deep, steep-sided gorge running north–south between high ridges.","f":["Where the Salween, Mekong and Yangtze run side by side lies a UNESCO site, the Three Parallel Rivers.","It flows for more than 2,000 km, through China and Myanmar, to the Andaman Sea.","In Myanmar the river is called the Thanlwin."],"q":{"q":"What does the Chinese name Nu Jiang mean?","a":"Angry River","x":["Golden River","Black River"]}},"Q26428":{"t":"A wild river in the deep valleys of the plateau","s":"The Lower Tunguska crosses the Central Siberian Plateau in a deep, winding valley, with steep forested slopes and rocky ledges of dark basalt. It flows through almost empty taiga for nearly 3,000 km before joining the Yenisey. Near its source it runs close to the Lena, yet turns away and heads north and then west to the other great river.","w":"A winding river in a deep valley of dark forest and rocky ledges, with flat-topped uplands on either side.","f":["Vyacheslav Shishkov's novel 'Gloomy River' draws on life along the Lower Tunguska.","Tura, the main town of Evenkia, stands on its banks.","The river is frozen from about October to May."],"q":{"q":"Which great river does the Lower Tunguska flow into?","a":"The Yenisey","x":["The Lena","The Ob"]}},"Q171840":{"t":"A long Andean river winding through untouched forest","s":"Born in the Colombian Andes, the Caquetá, as the Japurá is called in Colombia, winds east through some of the least disturbed rainforest on Earth. From above it is a brown ribbon through unbroken green, broken here and there by rapids where it cuts through old sandstone. In Brazil it becomes the Japurá and joins the Amazon through a maze of channels and lakes.","w":"A brown winding river through unbroken rainforest, with occasional rapids and flat-topped sandstone hills nearby.","f":["At Araracuara the river squeezes through a narrow canyon of ancient sandstone.","To the north lies Chiribiquete National Park, with tabletop mountains and ancient rock paintings, a UNESCO site.","Before meeting the Amazon, the Japurá splits into channels and lakes spread over dozens of kilometres."],"q":{"q":"What is the Japurá called in Colombia?","a":"Caquetá","x":["Putumayo","Guaviare"]}},"ne-rv-1159114071":{"t":"The Lena delta's shipping lane to Tiksi","s":"The Lena reaches the Laptev Sea through a delta of more than 1,500 islands, and the Bykovskaya channel is its eastern exit, the one ships use to reach the port of Tiksi. From above it is a broad silver band winding between flat, lake-spotted tundra islands. For most of the year it is frozen and white, open to ships only from about June to September.","w":"A broad silver channel between flat tundra islands dotted with lakes, at the delta's eastern edge by the sea.","f":["The Bykovskaya is the main shipping channel between the Lena and the port of Tiksi.","The Lena Delta, around 30,000 km², is the largest delta in the Arctic.","Much of the delta's tundra, lakes and bird colonies is protected in the Ust-Lensky Nature Reserve."],"q":{"q":"Which port do ships reach through the Bykovskaya channel?","a":"Tiksi","x":["Dikson","Pevek"]}},"Q188773":{"t":"A river that never reaches the sea","s":"The Okavango rises in the highlands of Angola and flows south-east as a dark, winding river through dry woodland, towards a fate unlike most rivers: it never reaches the sea. Instead it spreads into the Okavango Delta in Botswana, a green fan of channels, islands and lagoons in the middle of the Kalahari, where almost all its water evaporates or sinks into the sand.","w":"A dark, winding river with a green floodplain through dry, pale woodland, heading south-east.","f":["In 2014 the Okavango Delta became the 1,000th site on UNESCO's World Heritage list.","Its flood reaches the delta months after the rains, peaking in the dry winter season.","In Angola the river is called the Cubango."],"q":{"q":"Where does the Okavango River end?","a":"In a delta in the Kalahari","x":["In the Atlantic Ocean","In Lake Malawi"]}},"ne-rv-1159114387":{"t":"The Lena delta's quiet western channel","s":"The Olenekskaya is the westernmost of the Lena delta's great channels, winding west through flat tundra towards the Olenyok Gulf. From above it is a thin silver thread between islands spattered with thousands of small lakes, the ground around them patterned like a honeycomb. In winter all of it vanishes under snow and ice for some eight months.","w":"A narrow winding channel through flat tundra patterned with polygons and countless small lakes, heading west to the sea.","f":["Only a small share of the Lena's water takes this channel; most flows out through the eastern ones.","The honeycomb pattern comes from ice wedges that split the frozen ground into polygons.","Near its start lies Samoylov Island, where a Russian–German research station studies the permafrost."],"q":{"q":"Which gulf does the Olenekskaya channel flow into?","a":"The Olenyok Gulf","x":["The Gulf of Ob","Chaun Bay"]}},"Q134750":{"t":"The great waterway from the Great Lakes to the sea","s":"Here, below the Thousand Islands, the St. Lawrence is a broad blue river dotted with islands, running north-east between Ontario and New York State. It carries the outflow of all the Great Lakes towards the Atlantic. Look for the locks and dams of the St. Lawrence Seaway, which since 1959 have let ocean ships sail deep into the heart of North America.","w":"A broad blue river with islands and a straight shipping channel, flowing north-east between farmland and woods.","f":["The Thousand Islands, upstream near Kingston, actually number more than 1,800.","The Seaway lets ships travel some 3,700 km inland, from the Atlantic to Lake Superior.","On the way from the Atlantic to Lake Superior, ships are lifted about 180 m."],"q":{"q":"What opened on the St. Lawrence in 1959?","a":"The St. Lawrence Seaway","x":["The Erie Canal","The Rideau Canal"]}},"Q26433":{"t":"A taiga river in Russia's diamond country","s":"The Vilyuy winds east across Yakutia to join the Lena, a broad river looping through larch taiga and meadows, with long sandbars in summer. This is diamond country: the Mir mine at Mirny, a vast open pit about 1.2 km across, lies in the Vilyuy basin to the west. Upstream, a dam holds the river in a long reservoir.","w":"A looping river with sandbars and meadows through dark larch taiga; far to the west, a huge round open pit.","f":["The Vilyuy is the longest left-bank tributary of the Lena, about 2,650 km long.","The Mir pit near Mirny is one of the largest man-made holes on Earth, more than 500 m deep.","Nyurba, on the Vilyuy, is another centre of diamond mining."],"q":{"q":"Which gems are mined in the Vilyuy basin?","a":"Diamonds","x":["Emeralds","Rubies"]}},"Q26271":{"t":"One of the most twisting rivers on Earth","s":"Few rivers wander as much as the Purus. From above it is a tight coil of brown loops laid across the Amazon forest, some bends almost touching, with crescent lakes left behind wherever a loop has been cut off. A boat following it covers far more distance than a plane flying straight overhead.","w":"Tight brown loops and crescent-shaped lakes coiling through unbroken dark green forest.","f":["The Purus flows more than 3,000 km from Peru to the Amazon.","Each year its floods spread into the forest, creating the seasonally flooded várzea woods.","It is one of the Amazon's major southern tributaries, joining the main river upstream of Manaus."],"q":{"q":"What forms where a loop of the Purus is cut off?","a":"An oxbow lake","x":["A delta","A waterfall"]}},"Q26799":{"t":"A long, lonely river across the far north of Yakutia","s":"The Olenyok runs for more than 2,000 km across north-western Yakutia, first through sparse larch forest and then open tundra, with hardly a village along its banks. From above it is a winding silver ribbon with sandbars and cliffs, set in a vast and silent land. It reaches the Laptev Sea just west of the Lena delta.","w":"A winding silver river with sandbars and occasional cliffs through sparse larch forest and tundra.","f":["The river lies frozen for about eight months a year.","Diamonds have been found in its basin, part of Yakutia's diamond province.","Almost its entire basin lies on permafrost."],"q":{"q":"Into which sea does the Olenyok flow?","a":"The Laptev Sea","x":["The Kara Sea","The White Sea"]}},"Q156054":{"t":"A savanna river that gave its name to a state","s":"The Tocantins flows north through the Cerrado, Brazil's tropical savanna, a broad river of sandbars and rocky rapids between patches of dry woodland and farmland. It gave its name to Tocantins, one of Brazil's youngest states, created in 1988. Downstream it passes the giant Tucuruí dam and reaches the sea near Belém.","w":"A wide river with pale sandbars and rapids winding north through dry, brownish savanna and farmland.","f":["Palmas, the state capital, was founded in 1989 as a planned city.","The Tucuruí Dam on its lower course is one of Brazil's largest hydroelectric plants.","It meets the Araguaia at the Bico do Papagaio, the 'Parrot's Beak'."],"q":{"q":"In which year was the Brazilian state of Tocantins created?","a":"1988","x":["1960","2001"]}},"Q916349":{"t":"A border river of wetlands and an old stone fort","s":"For hundreds of kilometres the Guaporé, called the Iténez in Bolivia, forms the border between Bolivia and Brazil, winding slowly through flat wetland, savanna and forest. From above it is a dark, meandering thread edged with flooded grassland that shines after the rains. On its Brazilian bank stands Real Forte Príncipe da Beira, a Portuguese stone fortress built in the 1770s.","w":"A dark, meandering river through flat wetlands and forest, with shining flooded grasslands along its banks.","f":["The Guaporé flows into the Mamoré, whose waters later help form the Madeira.","Noel Kempff Mercado National Park, on the Bolivian side, is a UNESCO World Heritage site.","The Portuguese fort was built to guard the colonial frontier with Spain."],"q":{"q":"What is the Guaporé called in Bolivia?","a":"Iténez","x":["Beni","Pilcomayo"]}},"Q1344617":{"t":"The Danube's biggest arm, building its own delta","s":"The Chilia is the northernmost and largest of the Danube's three arms, carrying more than half of the river's water to the Black Sea. It winds between reed beds, lakes and willow woods, and for much of its length it forms the border between Romania and Ukraine. At its mouth it keeps building a small new delta of its own, pushing out into the sea.","w":"A broad, winding river arm through green reed beds and lakes, fanning into a small delta at the Black Sea.","f":["The Chilia is the longest and the youngest of the delta's three main arms.","It carries around 60 percent of the Danube's flow.","Vylkove, on its Ukrainian bank, is laced with canals and nicknamed the 'Danube Venice'."],"q":{"q":"How much of the Danube's water flows through the Chilia arm?","a":"More than half","x":["About a tenth","About a quarter"]}},"Q1668781":{"t":"The Danube's oldest and wildest arm","s":"The Sfântu Gheorghe is the southernmost of the Danube's three main arms and the oldest, winding south-east through the wildest part of the delta. Around it spread reed beds, lagoons and sand dunes, home to pelicans and countless other birds. It carries a little over a fifth of the river's water to the sea, ending near the fishing village of Sfântu Gheorghe.","w":"A winding river arm through reeds, lagoons and willow woods, with old cut-off loops lying beside it.","f":["In recent decades several of its big loops were bypassed by straight canals, shortening it by tens of kilometres.","It is the most sparsely populated of the delta's three main arms.","Its name, like the village's, means Saint George."],"q":{"q":"Which is the southernmost of the Danube's three main arms?","a":"Sfântu Gheorghe","x":["Sulina","Chilia"]}},"Q1369180":{"t":"The Danube arm straightened like a ruler","s":"Look for the one line in the Danube Delta that is almost perfectly straight: the Sulina branch. In the late 19th century engineers cut through its loops and dredged it into a shipping canal, and today seagoing ships use it to enter the Danube. It runs between reed beds and lakes for about 60 km to the little port of Sulina on the Black Sea.","w":"A ruler-straight channel cutting through green reed beds and lakes in the middle of the delta, out to the sea.","f":["The work was carried out by the European Commission of the Danube, set up in 1856.","Straightening shortened the branch from about 84 km to about 63 km.","Sulina, at its mouth, is the easternmost town in Romania."],"q":{"q":"Why is the Sulina branch so straight?","a":"Engineers cut and dredged it","x":["It follows a fault line","The Romans dug it"]}},"Q12722938":{"t":"A long side-arm of the Danube among the fields","s":"In south-eastern Romania the Danube splits in two for roughly a hundred kilometres: the main river on the east and the narrower Borcea branch on the west. Between them lies a long island of fields, once a vast floodplain wetland that was drained for farming. From above, look for two parallel ribbons of water with a patchwork of rectangular fields in between.","w":"Two roughly parallel river channels with a long strip of rectangular fields between them, in flat farmland.","f":["The island between the two arms is known as Balta Ialomiței.","At Fetești the Bucharest–Constanța railway crosses the Borcea, then the main Danube at Cernavodă.","The first bridges here, designed by Anghel Saligny, opened in 1895."],"q":{"q":"What lies between the Borcea branch and the main Danube?","a":"A long island of farmland","x":["A mountain ridge","A large lake"]}},"Q104437":{"t":"A great northern river crossing Alaska to the sea","s":"By the time it crosses western Alaska, the Yukon is a broad, silty river, several kilometres wide in places, braided around islands and looping through flat land of spruce, tundra and countless lakes. It rises in British Columbia, not far from the Pacific, yet takes the long way round across Yukon and Alaska to the Bering Sea. It is frozen for more than half the year.","w":"A broad grey-brown river split around long islands, looping through flat land of dark spruce, tundra and lakes.","f":["At about 3,200 km, it is one of the longest rivers in North America.","Chinook salmon swim nearly 3,000 km up the Yukon to spawn, one of the longest salmon runs on Earth.","The Klondike Gold Rush of 1896–99 brought tens of thousands of prospectors down the Yukon to Dawson City."],"q":{"q":"Which sea does the Yukon flow into?","a":"The Bering Sea","x":["The Beaufort Sea","The Chukchi Sea"]}},"Q5089":{"t":"India's sacred river, broad and slow across the plain","s":"Across the plains of Uttar Pradesh the Ganges is a broad, slow river, swinging in great curves between wide banks of pale sand. On its outer bends towns and villages crowd the high banks; on the inner ones sandbars stretch for kilometres. Just downstream lies Varanasi, one of the oldest living cities in the world, whose riverside steps draw pilgrims from all over India.","w":"A broad river curving across a flat plain, with wide pale sandbars inside each bend and towns on the outer banks.","f":["The Ganges is sacred in Hinduism and is revered as the goddess Ganga.","It flows about 2,500 km from the Himalayas to the Bay of Bengal.","In Varanasi the ghats line only the western bank; the eastern bank is left almost empty."],"q":{"q":"Which holy city lies on the Ganges just downstream of here?","a":"Varanasi","x":["Amritsar","Madurai"]}},"Q7348":{"t":"A lifeline of green across Pakistan's dry plains","s":"The Indus crosses the dry plains of Pakistan as a broad, braided river of grey-brown water and pale sand, with a thin band of green on either side and desert beyond. Barrages divert its water into a vast web of canals, one of the largest irrigation systems in the world. Along this river, some 4,500 years ago, the cities of the Indus Valley civilisation flourished.","w":"A wide, braided grey-brown river with a green strip of fields and canals on each side, and dry land beyond.","f":["The Indus river dolphin, which is almost blind, lives only in this river system.","Mohenjo-daro, one of the great cities of the Indus Valley civilisation, stands near the river in Sindh.","The river rises in Tibet and flows some 3,000 km to the Arabian Sea."],"q":{"q":"Which ancient city stood near the Indus in Sindh?","a":"Mohenjo-daro","x":["Babylon","Persepolis"]}},"Q3542":{"t":"A river that turns its back on the sea, then returns","s":"The Niger rises in the highlands of Guinea, only about 240 km from the Atlantic, yet flows away from the ocean, north-east to the edge of the Sahara, before swinging south to the Gulf of Guinea. Here in the Sahel it is a broad, braided band of water and green islands, the only line of life across a dry, tan landscape.","w":"A broad river braided around green islands and rice fields, crossing dry, tan Sahel plains.","f":["Its great curve carries it past Timbuktu, at the edge of the Sahara.","Upstream, the Inner Niger Delta floods a vast area of Mali every year.","At about 4,200 km, it is Africa's third-longest river."],"q":{"q":"Where does the Niger River rise?","a":"In the highlands of Guinea","x":["In the Atlas Mountains","In the Ethiopian Highlands"]}},"Q171649":{"t":"A border river winding through rainforest to the Congo","s":"The Ubangi winds south through the rainforest, forming the border between the Democratic Republic of the Congo and its neighbours, first the Central African Republic and then the Republic of the Congo. From above it is a broad brown ribbon with sandbanks and forested islands, set in unbroken green. It is the largest right-bank tributary of the Congo.","w":"A broad brown river with sandbanks and forested islands, winding through unbroken rainforest.","f":["It is formed where the Mbomou and the Uele rivers meet.","Bangui, the capital of the Central African Republic, stands on its bank.","In the dry season, low water over the rapids near Bangui can stop river boats."],"q":{"q":"Which capital stands on the Ubangi?","a":"Bangui","x":["Brazzaville","Kinshasa"]}},"Q284495":{"t":"A gold-rush route through the Yukon wilderness","s":"The Teslin flows north out of long, narrow Teslin Lake and winds through spruce forest and low hills to join the Yukon River. From above it is a clear, meandering ribbon in an almost roadless wilderness, deep green in summer and white in winter. During the Klondike Gold Rush of 1897–98, prospectors floated down it on their way to Dawson City.","w":"A winding river through dark spruce forest and low hills, far from roads, flowing north to a larger river.","f":["The name comes from a Tlingit word usually translated as 'long, narrow waters', describing Teslin Lake.","Teslin Lake is well over 100 km long but only a few kilometres wide.","It joins the Yukon at Hootalinqua, once a stop for paddle steamers."],"q":{"q":"Which river does the Teslin flow into?","a":"The Yukon","x":["The Mackenzie","The Stikine"]}},"Q34589":{"t":"A green ribbon through the desert of western Iraq","s":"In western Iraq the Euphrates is a slow, winding river bordered by a narrow strip of date palms and fields, sharply cut off by pale desert on both sides. Here and there it widens into lakes and reservoirs. With the Tigris, this river fed the cities of ancient Mesopotamia; Babylon stood on the Euphrates further downstream.","w":"A winding river with a narrow green fringe of palms and fields, pale desert right up to it, and lakes nearby.","f":["At about 2,800 km, the Euphrates is the longest river in western Asia.","It rises in the mountains of eastern Turkey and flows through Syria into Iraq.","It meets the Tigris in southern Iraq to form the Shatt al-Arab."],"q":{"q":"Which ancient city stood on the Euphrates?","a":"Babylon","x":["Nineveh","Persepolis"]}},"Q7666170":{"t":"The Indus begins as the 'Lion River' in Tibet","s":"High on the Tibetan Plateau, a small river winds through a wide, bare valley of brown and ochre hills: the Sengge Zangbo, the 'Lion River', the uppermost Indus. Its source lies north of sacred Mount Kailash. The town of Shiquanhe, whose Chinese name means 'Lion Spring River', stands beside it at about 4,300 m.","w":"A thin, winding river with a narrow green edge in a wide, bare valley between brown and ochre mountains.","f":["In Tibetan tradition the Indus flows from a lion's mouth, one of four rivers born near Kailash.","From here the Indus flows north-west into Ladakh and on for some 3,000 km to the Arabian Sea.","Ngari Gunsa airport, near Shiquanhe, is one of the highest airports in the world."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Sengge Zangbo mean?","a":"Lion River","x":["Horse River","Peacock River"]}},"Q2251":{"t":"A great river stepped into lakes by dams","s":"In central Washington the Columbia winds through a deep, dry canyon of dark basalt, and dam after dam turns it into a chain of long blue lakes. The land around is sagebrush and wheat, broken by bright green blocks of irrigated orchards along the river. Upstream stands Grand Coulee, one of the largest concrete dams in the world.","w":"A long, blue, lake-like river in a dry, dark basalt canyon, with green irrigated orchards along its banks.","f":["At the end of the last ice age, colossal floods from glacial Lake Missoula scoured the Channeled Scablands nearby.","The Columbia carries more water to the Pacific than any other river in North America.","Lewis and Clark followed the Columbia to the Pacific in 1805."],"q":{"q":"Which huge dam stands on the Columbia upstream of here?","a":"Grand Coulee","x":["Hoover","Glen Canyon"]}},"Q7355":{"t":"A muddy river between desert dunes and loess hills","s":"Here the Yellow River loops along the edge of the Tengger Desert, a muddy ribbon between golden dunes and bare, furrowed hills of loess. Along its banks, strips of irrigated fields glow green against the sand. The river takes its colour and its name from the fine yellow silt it scours from the Loess Plateau; it carries more silt than almost any other river on Earth.","w":"A brownish-yellow river winding between golden dunes and furrowed hills, lined with bright green irrigated fields.","f":["At Shapotou, near Zhongwei, the river runs right beside the dunes of the Tengger Desert.","At about 5,460 km, the Yellow River is the second-longest river in China.","Chinese civilisation grew up along its middle and lower reaches, often called its cradle."],"q":{"q":"What gives the Yellow River its colour?","a":"Silt from the Loess Plateau","x":["Iron-rich springs","Algae in the water"]}},"Q626":{"t":"Europe's longest river, splitting in two across the steppe","s":"Below Volgograd the Volga splits in two: the main river and its side-arm, the Akhtuba, run side by side for hundreds of kilometres across dry steppe. Between them lies the Volga–Akhtuba floodplain, a green maze of channels, lakes, oak woods and melon fields, flooded every spring. From above it looks like a bright green ribbon laid across a brown land.","w":"Two parallel rivers with a wide green floodplain of lakes and channels between them, across brown, dry steppe.","f":["At about 3,500 km, the Volga is the longest river in Europe.","It flows into the Caspian Sea, which has no outlet to the ocean.","The sandy soils of the lower Volga are famous for their watermelons."],"q":{"q":"Into which body of water does the Volga flow?","a":"The Caspian Sea","x":["The Black Sea","The Sea of Azov"]}},"Q43106":{"t":"A river that floods a whole plain every year","s":"In western Zambia the Zambezi spreads across the Barotse floodplain, a flat, grassy plain up to about 30 km wide that becomes a shining lake in the rainy season. From above, look for a winding river with oxbows and channels in pale green grassland, edged by darker woodland. When the water rises, the Lozi king moves to higher ground by royal barge in the Kuomboka ceremony.","w":"A winding river with oxbows in a wide, flat, pale green floodplain, bordered by darker woodland.","f":["Downstream the Zambezi plunges over Victoria Falls and fills Lake Kariba, one of the largest reservoirs in the world.","At about 2,600 km, it is Africa's fourth-longest river.","It is the largest African river flowing into the Indian Ocean."],"q":{"q":"What is the Lozi ceremony of moving to higher ground called?","a":"Kuomboka","x":["Umhlanga","Timkat"]}},"Q2279579":{"t":"The only river that drains Lake Malawi","s":"The Shire is Lake Malawi's only outlet, flowing south for about 400 km to join the Zambezi in Mozambique. On the way it tumbles through the Murchison Cataracts, then slows in the hot lowlands into a broad, marshy valley. Here, near the Elephant Marsh, it winds through reed beds, lagoons and sugar-cane fields.","w":"A winding river in a broad, green, marshy valley with lagoons and fields, between hills to the east and west.","f":["David Livingstone travelled up the Shire in 1859 and went on to reach Lake Malawi.","Hydroelectric plants on its rapids supply most of Malawi's electricity.","The Elephant Marsh is a protected wetland, listed under the Ramsar Convention."],"q":{"q":"Which lake does the Shire drain?","a":"Lake Malawi","x":["Lake Tanganyika","Lake Chilwa"]}},"Q6862":{"t":"The 'Black Dragon River' between two countries","s":"For well over 1,500 km the Amur forms the border between Russia and China, and here it is a broad, winding river laced with islands, set in a wide floodplain of meadows and marsh lakes. The Chinese call it Heilong Jiang, the 'Black Dragon River', after its dark water. Downstream it takes in the Songhua, whose paler water runs beside the dark Amur for a long way before they mix.","w":"A broad river with islands and a wide green floodplain of meadows and marsh lakes, in flat farmland and forest.","f":["The Amur is home to the kaluga, a giant sturgeon that can grow longer than 5 m.","Amur tigers still live in the forests of its lower basin.","Counting its headwaters, the Amur system runs more than 4,000 km to the Strait of Tartary."],"q":{"q":"What does the Chinese name Heilong Jiang mean?","a":"Black Dragon River","x":["Golden Sand River","River of Clouds"]}},"Q131792":{"t":"A great river with a hidden link to the Amazon","s":"The Orinoco sweeps in a great arc through Venezuela, and here it runs along the Colombian border past dark granite domes and rapids, with savanna on one bank and forest on the other. Its most curious feature lies upstream: the Casiquiare, a natural channel that links the Orinoco with the Amazon basin. Alexander von Humboldt travelled it in 1800 and confirmed the link.","w":"A broad brown river with rocky rapids and dark granite domes, savanna on one side and forest on the other.","f":["The Casiquiare is the largest natural channel on Earth linking two major river basins.","The Orinoco delta, on the Atlantic, is a vast maze of channels and mangroves.","At about 2,100 km, the Orinoco is one of the longest rivers in South America."],"q":{"q":"Which natural channel links the Orinoco with the Amazon basin?","a":"The Casiquiare","x":["The Apure","The Caroní"]}},"Q2556345":{"t":"A quiet inland river feeding the Darling","s":"The Weir River rises on the western slopes of the Great Dividing Range in southern Queensland and winds south-west towards the inland plains. From above it is a thin, dark line of river red gums wandering through scrub and wheat and cotton fields, often more a chain of waterholes than a flowing river. After heavy rain, though, it can spread far across its floodplain.","w":"A thin, winding line of dark green trees across flat farmland and scrub; open water shows mainly after rain.","f":["The Weir is the largest tributary of the Macintyre River, which it joins near Talwood.","Downstream the Macintyre becomes the Barwon, one of the headwaters of the Darling.","Its water belongs to the vast Murray–Darling basin, which drains to the sea in South Australia."],"q":{"q":"Which river system does the Weir River belong to?","a":"The Murray–Darling","x":["The Burdekin","The Fitzroy"]}},"Q1519602":{"t":"A wide, sandy river sweeping past a lowland city","s":"Coming down from the Andes, the Río Grande loops around the east of Santa Cruz de la Sierra and heads north across the lowlands, a broad, pale bed of sand and braided channels that shifts with every flood. From above it looks like a wide scar of bright sand through green forest and farmland. Downstream it joins the Mamoré, in the Amazon basin.","w":"A broad, pale, sandy riverbed with braided channels, crossing flat green forest and large rectangular farms.","f":["In Bolivia the river is also known as the Guapay.","Large soybean farms cover much of the flat land east of Santa Cruz, near the river.","Its water eventually reaches the Amazon through the Mamoré and the Madeira."],"q":{"q":"Which Bolivian city does this Río Grande loop around?","a":"Santa Cruz de la Sierra","x":["La Paz","Sucre"]}},"Q118771":{"t":"A black-water river the colour of strong tea","s":"The Rio Negro is one of the world's great blackwater rivers: its water, stained by tannins from decaying leaves, looks as dark as strong tea, almost black from above. Here in its upper course it winds through the rainforest past rocky rapids and white sandy beaches that stand out sharply against the dark water in the dry season. Far downstream, at Manaus, it meets the Amazon.","w":"A very dark, almost black river with white sandbanks and islands, winding through unbroken rainforest.","f":["It is the largest blackwater river in the world and one of the Amazon's biggest tributaries.","Near Manaus lies the Anavilhanas archipelago, some 400 islands scattered in the river.","Its upper course is linked to the Orinoco through the Casiquiare channel."],"q":{"q":"What makes the Rio Negro's water so dark?","a":"Tannins from rotting leaves","x":["Volcanic ash","Oil seeps"]}},"Q1120933":{"t":"A forest river that gave its name to a gold mine","s":"The Kibali flows west through the forests and savannas of north-eastern Congo, from the hills near Lake Albert towards the town of Dungu. There it meets the Dungu River, and together they form the Uele, which feeds the Ubangi and, in the end, the Congo. Near here, along its banks, lies the Kibali gold mine, one of the largest in Africa.","w":"A winding brown river through green forest and savanna, with large pale mine workings near its banks.","f":["The Kibali and the Dungu meet at the town of Dungu to form the Uele.","Garamba National Park lies just north of Dungu.","Its source lies in the hills west of Lake Albert, not far from the border with Uganda."],"q":{"q":"Which river do the Kibali and the Dungu form together?","a":"The Uele","x":["The Kasai","The Lualaba"]}},"Q686021":{"t":"A forested river valley where the oil age began","s":"The Allegheny winds through the wooded hills of north-western Pennsylvania in deep, curving valleys, a dark ribbon under a canopy of green that turns red and gold in October. Near here, on Oil Creek, a small tributary, Edwin Drake drilled the first commercial oil well in the United States in 1859. Downstream the river meets the Monongahela at Pittsburgh to form the Ohio.","w":"A dark, curving river in a deep valley between forested hills; brilliant red and gold in autumn.","f":["The river flows through the Allegheny National Forest, Pennsylvania's only national forest.","Kinzua Dam, upstream, holds it back in the long Allegheny Reservoir.","Titusville, where Drake's well stood, became one of the first oil boom towns in the world."],"q":{"q":"Which river does the Allegheny meet at Pittsburgh to form the Ohio?","a":"The Monongahela","x":["The Susquehanna","The Delaware"]}},"Q186541":{"t":"A long southern tributary through diamond country","s":"The Kasai rises in the highlands of Angola and flows north into the Democratic Republic of the Congo, winding through savanna and ribbons of gallery forest. Along this stretch it forms the border between Angola and the DRC. The region around it is famous for diamonds, washed from the river gravels near the town of Tshikapa. It joins the Congo far to the north-west.","w":"A winding brown river with sandbars, lined by dark ribbons of forest across a green-brown savanna plateau.","f":["At more than 2,000 km, the Kasai is the Congo's largest left-bank tributary.","Its last stretch, before it enters the Congo, is called the Kwa.","Diamonds have been dug by hand from its gravels for decades."],"q":{"q":"Which precious stones is the Kasai region famous for?","a":"Diamonds","x":["Sapphires","Emeralds"]}},"Q809666":{"t":"The upper Darling, fringed by red gums on the plains","s":"The Barwon is the upper course of the Darling, winding west across the flat black-soil plains of northern New South Wales. From above it is a narrow, dark green line of river red gums looping through a vast patchwork of cotton and wheat fields and grey-green grazing land. Below Brewarrina, where the Culgoa joins it, the river becomes the Darling.","w":"A narrow, winding line of dark trees across flat, light brown plains and big rectangular fields.","f":["At Brewarrina, stone fish traps built by Aboriginal people in the Barwon are among Australia's oldest human-made structures.","In its upper reaches it forms part of the border between New South Wales and Queensland.","It is not the Barwon River of Victoria, which flows through Geelong."],"q":{"q":"Which river does the Barwon become downstream?","a":"The Darling","x":["The Murray","The Lachlan"]}},"Q171436":{"t":"A long forest river on its way to the Ubangi","s":"The Uele winds west across the north of the Democratic Republic of the Congo, a brown river through a patchwork of rainforest and savanna, dotted with rocky rapids and islands. Formed where the Kibali and the Dungu meet, it flows for more than 1,000 km before joining the Mbomou to become the Ubangi. Few roads reach its banks, and the river is still a main route.","w":"A winding brown river with rocky rapids and islands, running through a mosaic of forest and savanna.","f":["In the 19th century explorers argued over whether the Uele flowed to the Nile, the Congo or Lake Chad.","At Gangala-na-Bodio, in the Uele region, a station once trained African elephants to work.","Its basin lies right beside the watershed of the Nile."],"q":{"q":"Which two rivers join to form the Ubangi?","a":"The Uele and the Mbomou","x":["The Kasai and the Kwango","The Lualaba and the Lomami"]}},"Q26439":{"t":"A steppe river along the Russia–China border","s":"The Argun winds north-east through wide steppe and then larch-covered hills, marking for hundreds of kilometres the border between Russia and China. From above it is a looping, silver-green river with oxbow lakes and broad meadows, crossing an almost empty land. Where it meets the Shilka, the Amur begins.","w":"A looping river with oxbow lakes and wide meadows, through rolling steppe and larch-covered hills.","f":["In China its upper course is called the Hailar River.","In wet years it is connected to Hulun Lake, one of the largest lakes in China.","Together with the Amur, it forms one of the longest river systems in Asia."],"q":{"q":"Which river meets the Argun to form the Amur?","a":"The Shilka","x":["The Zeya","The Ussuri"]}},"Q882739":{"t":"A gorge over a kilometre deep through the highlands","s":"Leaving Lake Tana, the Blue Nile, the Abay to Ethiopians, curves south and then west through the highlands in a vast gorge more than a kilometre deep, cut into dark basalt. From above, look for a winding brown thread far below the green edges of the plateau. Every summer the monsoon rains swell it into a torrent that carries most of the Nile's flood.","w":"A thin brown river at the bottom of a deep, branching gorge cut into a high green plateau.","f":["Near its source the Blue Nile Falls, called Tis Issat, 'smoking water', pour over a basalt ledge.","In the flood season the Blue Nile supplies most of the water that reaches Egypt.","It meets the White Nile at Khartoum."],"q":{"q":"Which lake does the Blue Nile flow out of?","a":"Lake Tana","x":["Lake Victoria","Lake Turkana"]}},"Q181482":{"t":"A red-gum ribbon across the Australian outback","s":"The Darling crosses the dry plains of western New South Wales as a slow, olive-brown river, its course traced by a narrow band of river red gums through red earth and grey saltbush. In drought it can shrink to a chain of pools; after big rains upstream, floodwaters spread out for dozens of kilometres. It joins the Murray at Wentworth.","w":"A thin, winding line of dark green trees across red-brown, flat outback plains, sometimes with shining floodwater.","f":["In the 19th century paddle steamers carried wool down the Darling from outback sheep stations.","The Menindee Lakes, near its lower course, were turned into water storages in the 1960s.","Wilcannia, on its banks, was once one of the busiest inland river ports in Australia."],"q":{"q":"Which river does the Darling flow into?","a":"The Murray","x":["The Barwon","The Murrumbidgee"]}},"Q117615":{"t":"A meandering river across Bolivia's flooded savannas","s":"The Mamoré winds north across the Llanos de Moxos, flat savannas in Bolivia's Beni lowlands that flood for months every rainy season. From above, look for a brown river looping between oxbow lakes and islands of forest. On the plains around it lie the faint lines of ancient raised fields and mounds, built by people who farmed here long before the Spanish arrived.","w":"A brown meandering river with oxbow lakes through flat savanna dotted with islands of forest.","f":["Pink Bolivian river dolphins live in the Mamoré.","Trinidad, the capital of Beni, stands near its banks.","Further north the Mamoré meets the Beni, and together they form the Madeira."],"q":{"q":"Which river does the Mamoré form together with the Beni?","a":"The Madeira","x":["The Purus","The Tapajós"]}},"Q1054325":{"t":"A short, fast river pouring out of Lake Huron","s":"The St. Clair River is short, only about 64 km, yet it carries the whole outflow of Lakes Superior, Michigan and Huron south towards Lake St. Clair and Lake Erie. From above it is a clear, blue, fast-flowing ribbon between Sarnia in Canada and Port Huron in the United States, crossed at its head by the Blue Water Bridge. Lake freighters pass along it all summer.","w":"A narrow, bright blue river running south from Lake Huron, a big bridge at its head and towns on both banks.","f":["It forms part of the border between Ontario and Michigan.","At its mouth it has built a broad delta of marshy islands out into Lake St. Clair.","A railway tunnel under the river, opened in 1891, was one of the first of its kind in North America."],"q":{"q":"Which lake does the St. Clair River flow out of?","a":"Lake Huron","x":["Lake Erie","Lake Michigan"]}},"Q118251":{"t":"The Amazon's biggest tributary, heavy with Andean silt","s":"The Madeira winds north-east through the forest as a wide, milky-brown river, heavy with silt washed down from the Andes of Bolivia and Peru. From above it is a broad, gently meandering band with long sandbanks and forested islands. It is the Amazon's largest tributary by volume, and it brings in a large share of the Amazon's sediment.","w":"A wide, milky-brown river with long sandbanks and islands, meandering gently through unbroken forest.","f":["Its name is Portuguese for 'wood', after the logs it carries in the rainy season.","Two large hydroelectric dams, Santo Antônio and Jirau, stand on it near Porto Velho.","Counting the Mamoré, its main headstream, it runs for more than 3,000 km."],"q":{"q":"What does the Portuguese word 'madeira' mean?","a":"Wood","x":["Mud","Gold"]}},"Q4915":{"t":"A broad river along the edge of Appalachia","s":"The Ohio winds between wooded hills along the borders of West Virginia, Ohio and Kentucky, a broad, calm river with towns, power plants and farms on its narrow floodplain. Long strings of barges push coal and grain along it. It carries so much water that where it meets the Mississippi at Cairo, Illinois, it is the larger of the two.","w":"A broad, winding river between steep wooded hills, with small towns and long strings of barges along it.","f":["It is formed at Pittsburgh, where the Allegheny and the Monongahela meet.","Its name comes from a Seneca word usually translated as 'good river' or 'great river'.","A chain of locks and dams keeps it navigable along its whole length of about 1,580 km."],"q":{"q":"Which two rivers meet at Pittsburgh to form the Ohio?","a":"Allegheny and Monongahela","x":["Allegheny and Susquehanna","Kanawha and Monongahela"]}},"Q166966":{"t":"Where the Tigris and Euphrates reach the sea together","s":"The Tigris and the Euphrates end their long journey as one river, the Shatt al-Arab, which flows about 200 km past Basra to the Persian Gulf. From above it is a broad, brown waterway lined on both banks by dark green groves of date palms, sharply edged by pale desert and marsh. Ships have sailed up it to Basra for centuries.","w":"A broad brown river lined by dark green bands of date palms, with pale desert and marshland beyond.","f":["The two rivers meet at Al-Qurnah, which local tradition links with the Garden of Eden.","Its banks once held millions of date palms, one of the largest date forests on Earth.","Near its mouth the Karun River, flowing from Iran, also joins it."],"q":{"q":"Which two rivers join to form the Shatt al-Arab?","a":"Tigris and Euphrates","x":["Euphrates and Jordan","Tigris and Orontes"]}},"Q183078":{"t":"Australia's longest river, winding through red-gum country","s":"The Murray winds across the flat inland plains of south-eastern Australia in tight, tangled loops, marked from above by a dark band of river red gum forest and a scatter of billabongs, the crescent lakes left by old bends. Here, near Swan Hill, it forms the border between Victoria and New South Wales. Around it, irrigation turns the dry plain into green squares of vines and orchards.","w":"A tightly winding river inside a dark band of red gum forest, with crescent lakes and green irrigated fields around it.","f":["At about 2,500 km, the Murray is Australia's longest river.","Paddle steamers still run on the Murray at towns such as Echuca, once Australia's largest inland port.","The Barmah–Millewa forest upstream is the largest river red gum forest in the world."],"q":{"q":"What are the crescent lakes beside the Murray called in Australia?","a":"Billabongs","x":["Gullies","Wadis"]}},"Q181475":{"t":"A green thread of vineyards through dry country","s":"In South Africa's dry Northern Cape the Orange River is a thin blue-green line, flanked by a bright green strip of vineyards and orchards, with bare brown semi-desert right up to its edge. Near here it plunges into the granite gorge of Augrabies Falls, whose Khoi name means 'place of great noise'. Further west it forms the border with Namibia on its way to the Atlantic.","w":"A thin river lined with a bright green strip of vineyards, cutting through bare brown semi-desert and a granite gorge.","f":["Robert Gordon named it in 1779 after the Dutch House of Orange, not for its colour.","At some 2,200 km, it is the longest river in South Africa.","Grapes grown along it here go largely into raisins and sultanas."],"q":{"q":"What is the Orange River named after?","a":"The Dutch House of Orange","x":["The colour of its water","Orange groves on its banks"]}},"Q223":{"t":"An island that is mostly one enormous dome of ice","s":"On flights between Europe and North America you often cross an island that is almost all ice. The ice sheet covers about four-fifths of Greenland and is up to 3 km thick, so its crest sits more than 3,000 m above the sea. At the edges, dark peaks and fjords break through, and in summer the ice is freckled with turquoise meltwater lakes.","w":"A vast featureless white plateau, edged by dark mountains, deep fjords and icebergs drifting off the coast.","f":["Greenland is the world's largest island, about 2.2 million km² — roughly four times the size of France.","If all its ice melted, the world's oceans would rise by about 7 metres.","Only about 56,000 people live here, nearly all on the ice-free coast."],"q":{"q":"What share of Greenland is covered by its ice sheet?","a":"About four-fifths","x":["About half","About a third"]}},"Q35942":{"t":"Thousands of kilometres of ocean, stitched with atolls","s":"Below you is mostly deep blue, broken now and then by a ring of turquoise — an atoll — or the green ridges of a volcanic island. Polynesia is a vast triangle with Hawaii, New Zealand and Easter Island at its corners. People settled all of it in canoes, reading stars, swells and birds, long before Europeans arrived.","w":"Scattered turquoise rings of reef with pale lagoons, and now and then a dark-green volcanic peak wrapped in cloud.","f":["The name comes from the Greek for 'many islands'.","New Zealand, the triangle's south-western corner, was settled only about 700 years ago — one of the last large lands people reached.","Polynesian languages are so close that 'moana', the ocean, is the same word in Hawaii and New Zealand."],"q":{"q":"Which island marks a corner of the Polynesian Triangle?","a":"Easter Island","x":["Fiji","Guam"]}},"Q669037":{"t":"Columbus's mistaken 'Indies', strung across turquoise seas","s":"The islands curve for thousands of kilometres from the Bahamas and Cuba to Trinidad, and from above the colours give them away: shallow banks glowing turquoise, white sand, green mountains. The name is an old mistake — Columbus believed he had reached the Indies of Asia when he landed here in 1492. The big islands lie to the west; a chain of small volcanic ones closes off the Caribbean to the east.","w":"Big mountainous islands to the west, a curving chain of small volcanic ones to the east, bright turquoise shallows in between.","f":["The highest peak, Pico Duarte on Hispaniola, rises to about 3,100 m.","The Caribbean Sea takes its name from the Caribs, a people of the Lesser Antilles."],"q":{"q":"On which island is the highest peak of the West Indies?","a":"Hispaniola","x":["Cuba","Jamaica"]}},"Q928":{"t":"More than 7,000 islands, reefs and volcano cones","s":"Green, mountainous islands break the sea into straits and bays, each coast fringed with a pale line of reef. There are more than 7,000 of them, though Luzon in the north and Mindanao in the south hold over two-thirds of the land. The archipelago sits on the Pacific Ring of Fire, so look for volcanic cones and crater lakes among the forested ridges.","w":"A maze of green islands and narrow straits, with pale reef edges along the coasts.","f":["The islands were named in the 1540s after Prince Philip of Spain, later King Philip II.","On Bohol more than 1,200 rounded hills turn brown in the dry season — the famous Chocolate Hills.","The Philippines lies in the Coral Triangle, the region with the richest variety of reef life on Earth."],"q":{"q":"Which Spanish king gave his name to the Philippines?","a":"Philip II","x":["Charles V","Ferdinand II"]}},"Q17":{"t":"A 3,000-km arc of mountains rising from the Pacific","s":"From cruise height Japan is a long, crumpled arc of forested mountains, with cities squeezed onto the narrow plains between the slopes and the sea. Four big islands carry almost everyone, and thousands of smaller ones are scattered around them. At night the Pacific coast from Tokyo to Osaka glows as one nearly unbroken ribbon of light.","w":"Rugged green mountains down the middle, crowded plains along the coast, and white-capped Fuji standing alone south-west of Tokyo.","f":["About three-quarters of Japan is mountainous, which is why its cities crowd onto the coastal plains.","The country has more than 100 active volcanoes.","A 2023 recount using digital maps found about 14,000 islands — twice the long-quoted figure of 6,852."],"q":{"q":"Roughly how much of Japan is mountainous?","a":"About three-quarters","x":["About a third","About half"]}},"Q85377":{"t":"A frozen jigsaw at the top of North America","s":"Seen from above, this is a jigsaw of brown and white land fitted together with channels of sea ice. Tens of thousands of islands spread north of the Canadian mainland towards Greenland, and for much of the year the sea between them is frozen solid. Explorers spent centuries looking for a way through; the Northwest Passage threads these channels.","w":"Big, flat islands split by straight channels, white with sea ice most of the year; glaciers and ice caps in the east.","f":["Three of the world's ten largest islands are here: Baffin, Victoria and Ellesmere.","Roald Amundsen was the first to sail the whole Northwest Passage, in 1903–1906.","More than 36,000 islands make up the archipelago."],"q":{"q":"Which sea route winds between these islands?","a":"The Northwest Passage","x":["The Northern Sea Route","The Drake Passage"]}},"Q37394":{"t":"Steep green islands where hundreds of languages are spoken","s":"Here the Pacific islands turn tall and green: steep volcanic ridges cloaked in rainforest and rimmed by reef, from New Guinea east to Fiji. Some volcanoes still smoke, so look for a grey plume drifting off a summit. Melanesia is also the most linguistically diverse corner of the planet — villages a few ridges apart can speak unrelated languages.","w":"Large, mountainous, forested islands with reef-fringed coasts; now and then a volcanic plume trailing downwind.","f":["Papua New Guinea alone has more than 800 living languages.","Vanuatu has about 110 languages for some 300,000 people — the densest mix of languages per person on Earth."],"q":{"q":"Papua New Guinea alone has roughly how many languages?","a":"More than 800","x":["About 100","About 300"]}},"Q3359409":{"t":"Thousands of tiny islands above the deepest sea","s":"Micronesia is mostly ocean: thousands of small islands, many of them low atolls whose rings of reef glow turquoise against deep blue. Many rise only a few metres above the waves. On its western edge lies the Mariana Trench, where the sea floor drops almost 11 km — about as far below the surface as you are above it.","w":"Small rings of reef with pale-green lagoons, scattered far apart across dark-blue ocean; occasionally a higher, forested island.","f":["The name means 'small islands' in Greek.","On Yap, huge stone discs called rai, some wider than a person is tall, served as money."],"q":{"q":"What does the name 'Micronesia' mean?","a":"Small islands","x":["Black islands","Many islands"]}},"Q208643":{"t":"The world's largest archipelago, where Asia meets Australia","s":"Between mainland Asia and Australia lies the largest group of islands on Earth — about 25,000 of them, from Sumatra to the Philippines. Look for chains of volcanic cones, rainforest cut into clearings, and shallow seas glowing green over coral. Alfred Russel Wallace travelled here for eight years and noticed that the animals on either side of a narrow strait belonged to different worlds.","w":"Big green islands and strings of volcanic peaks in warm, shallow seas; brown river plumes spreading into turquoise water.","f":["The Wallace Line runs between Bali and Lombok, dividing mostly Asian wildlife to the west from Australian-type wildlife to the east.","While travelling in these islands in 1858, Wallace hit on natural selection independently of Darwin."],"q":{"q":"Between which two islands does the Wallace Line run?","a":"Bali and Lombok","x":["Java and Sumatra","Borneo and Java"]}},"Q38272":{"t":"A green, cloud-dappled cluster off Europe's north-west edge","s":"Two large islands and thousands of small ones sit off the north-west coast of Europe, and on a clear day they look intensely green, patched with fields and fringed by rocky headlands. Cloud is common: Atlantic air brings frequent rain. Until roughly 8,000 years ago you could have walked here from the continent, across a plain now drowned beneath the North Sea.","w":"Great Britain's long north–south shape with Ireland to its west, a patchwork of green fields, and ragged island clusters in the north.","f":["The group has more than 6,000 islands, most of them small and uninhabited.","The drowned land that once joined Britain to Europe is called Doggerland, after the Dogger Bank.","Warm water carried by the Gulf Stream keeps winters here mild for such a northern latitude."],"q":{"q":"What is the drowned land between Britain and Europe called?","a":"Doggerland","x":["Lyonesse","Hyperborea"]}},"Q664":{"t":"One of the last large lands people reached","s":"Two long islands and hundreds of small ones lie alone in the South Pacific, about 2,000 km east of Australia. Look for the snowy spine of the Southern Alps in the south and volcanic cones in the north. Polynesian voyagers arrived only about 700 years ago, making this one of the last large lands on Earth to be settled.","w":"Snowy peaks in the south, glacier-blue lakes and fjords in the south-west, green farmland on the plains.","f":["Cook Strait, between the two main islands, is only about 22 km wide at its narrowest.","New Zealand sits on Zealandia, a sunken continent that is about 94% under the sea.","Aotearoa, the Māori name, is often translated as 'land of the long white cloud'."],"q":{"q":"What is the Māori name for New Zealand?","a":"Aotearoa","x":["Rapa Nui","Hawaiki"]}},"Q3740828":{"t":"Fire and ice on a rift in the Atlantic","s":"Iceland looks unfinished from above: black lava fields and grey outwash plains, white ice caps, and a thin green rim along the coast. The island sits right on the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, where North America and Eurasia pull apart, which is why it has so many volcanoes. Glacial rivers spread across the sand in braids, like pale veins.","w":"Dazzling white ice caps inland, dark lava and sand plains, braided rivers and a narrow green strip by the sea.","f":["The two plates move apart by about 2 cm a year, and Iceland slowly widens with them.","Vatnajökull, the largest ice cap, covers about 8% of the country and hides active volcanoes beneath it.","Geologists everywhere use the Icelandic word 'sandur' for a glacial outwash plain."],"q":{"q":"Which two plates pull apart across Iceland?","a":"North American and Eurasian","x":["African and Eurasian","Pacific and North American"]}},"Q7463928":{"t":"The Red Island, where life went its own way","s":"Madagascar's nickname, the Red Island, makes sense from above: bare laterite soil shows through in rust and orange, and after rain the rivers pour red silt into the sea. It split from Africa, and later from India, tens of millions of years ago, and life here evolved in isolation. Look for green rainforest along the east coast and dry plains in the west.","w":"A huge island with a green eastern escarpment, rust-red highlands, and rivers bleeding red sediment into the sea.","f":["At about 587,000 km², it is the world's fourth-largest island.","Lemurs evolved only here, and about 90% of Madagascar's wildlife is found nowhere else.","Malagasy, the island's language, is closest to languages of southern Borneo, more than 6,000 km away."],"q":{"q":"Malagasy is most closely related to languages from which island?","a":"Borneo","x":["Sri Lanka","Zanzibar"]}},"Q710":{"t":"A country of atolls in all four hemispheres","s":"Kiribati is 33 tiny islands scattered across thousands of kilometres of ocean. From above, most are thin rings of land around pale lagoons, often only a few metres above the sea. It is the only country in all four hemispheres, and in 1995 it moved the date line so that all its islands share the same calendar day.","w":"Thin rings of land enclosing jade-green lagoons, tiny against the deep blue, spread far apart along the equator.","f":["'Kiribati' is pronounced 'Kiribas' — the local form of 'Gilberts', the islands' old British name.","About half of the country's people live on South Tarawa, the capital atoll."],"q":{"q":"'Kiribati' is the local form of which name?","a":"Gilbert Islands","x":["Caroline Islands","Marshall Islands"]}},"Q192626":{"t":"Volcanoes on a conveyor belt across the Pacific","s":"The islands line up from south-east to north-west, and their shapes tell the story. In the south-east the Big Island is broad and young, its volcanoes still active; further north-west the islands grow older, more eroded and more deeply cut by green valleys. Beyond them the chain dwindles into atolls and sunken seamounts. The chain was built as the Pacific Plate crept over a hotspot that stays put.","w":"A diagonal line of islands: broad dark volcanoes to the south-east, older, green, deeply furrowed islands to the north-west.","f":["Hawaii is about 3,800 km from California, one of the most isolated island groups on Earth.","Kauai, near the north-western end of the main islands, is about 5 million years old; the Big Island is under 1 million.","A new volcano, Kamaʻehuakanaloa (formerly Lōʻihi), is growing on the sea floor south-east of the Big Island."],"q":{"q":"What created the Hawaiian Islands?","a":"A hotspot under the Pacific Plate","x":["A subduction zone","A mid-ocean ridge"]}},"Q234796":{"t":"Where the new year arrives before anywhere else","s":"A loose line of coral atolls runs across the equator in the middle of the Pacific, hundreds of kilometres apart. The largest, Kiritimati, is hard to miss: a huge ring of land with a maze of shallow, milky-blue lagoons inside. The islands that belong to Kiribati keep time 14 hours ahead of Greenwich, so they greet each new year first.","w":"Tiny, isolated atolls strung north to south; Kiritimati stands out as a big pale ring full of patchy lagoons.","f":["Kiritimati has the largest land area of any coral atoll, about 390 km².","Its name is the local spelling of 'Christmas': Captain Cook reached it on Christmas Eve 1777.","Of the 11 Line Islands, 8 belong to Kiribati; the rest are United States territories."],"q":{"q":"Which Line Island is the largest coral atoll by land area?","a":"Kiritimati","x":["Tabuaeran","Palmyra"]}},"Q118350":{"t":"Hundreds of atolls and a few high green islands","s":"The Carolines are scattered over some 3,000 km of the western Pacific, just north of the equator. Most are low atolls, rings of reef around turquoise lagoons, but Pohnpei, Kosrae and the peaks of Chuuk rise as green, cloud-capped islands. Their navigators crossed open ocean without instruments, and their knowledge helped revive Polynesian voyaging in the 1970s.","w":"Small turquoise-ringed atolls far apart; Chuuk shows as a huge reef ring with green peaks inside.","f":["The islands are named after King Charles II of Spain.","Nan Madol, a town of basalt built on about 100 artificial islets off Pohnpei, is a UNESCO World Heritage site.","Master navigator Mau Piailug of Satawal guided the canoe Hōkūleʻa from Hawaii to Tahiti in 1976."],"q":{"q":"Which king are the Caroline Islands named after?","a":"Charles II of Spain","x":["Charles I of England","Charles III of Spain"]}},"Q165481":{"t":"Four giant islands on a drowned edge of Asia","s":"Borneo, Sumatra, Java and Sulawesi make up the Greater Sunda Islands, and three of them rise from a shallow shelf that was dry land during the ice ages. The seas between them are rarely deeper than about 100 m, so the water often looks green rather than deep blue. Look for volcano chains along Sumatra and Java and dark rainforest on Borneo.","w":"Big green islands in shallow, greenish seas; lines of volcanic cones along Sumatra and Java.","f":["Java is the world's most populous island, home to more than 150 million people.","In the ice ages people and animals could walk from mainland Asia to Java, Sumatra and Borneo.","Sulawesi is the odd one out: it lies beyond the Wallace Line and has a strange mix of wildlife."],"q":{"q":"Which is the largest of the Greater Sunda Islands?","a":"Borneo","x":["Sumatra","Sulawesi"]}},"Q1042":{"t":"Granite islands left behind by a drifting continent","s":"Most islands in open ocean are volcanoes or coral, but the Seychelles' inner islands are granite — a sliver of an ancient continent, stranded when India drifted away to the north. From above, Mahé and Praslin show steep green hills and grey boulders tumbling into turquoise bays. Far to the south-west, the outer islands are flat coral atolls lost in the blue.","w":"Steep green granite islands with turquoise bays in the north-east; low, far-flung coral atolls to the south-west.","f":["The coco de mer palm, native to Praslin and Curieuse, bears the heaviest seed of any plant.","Aldabra atoll is home to roughly 100,000 giant tortoises.","The Seychelles has the smallest population of any African country."],"q":{"q":"What rock are Mahé and Praslin made of?","a":"Granite","x":["Basalt","Coral limestone"]}},"Q4526612":{"t":"A green teardrop off India's southern tip","s":"Sri Lanka hangs off the tip of India like a teardrop, its narrow northern end pointing at a chain of sandbanks that almost reaches the mainland. The south-central highlands rise as a dark-green knot, with tea estates climbing their slopes. Around them lie rice paddies, reservoirs built by ancient kings, and a golden rim of beaches.","w":"A teardrop-shaped island: mountains in the south-centre, and a thin chain of sandbanks reaching toward India from the north-west.","f":["The word 'serendipity' comes from Serendip, an old name for the island.","Adam's Bridge, a chain of shoals about 50 km long, almost links Sri Lanka to India.","A footprint-shaped hollow on top of Adam's Peak is revered by Buddhists, Hindus, Muslims and Christians alike."],"q":{"q":"Which English word comes from an old name for Sri Lanka?","a":"Serendipity","x":["Cinnamon","Paradise"]}},"Q3593416":{"t":"A heart-shaped island facing the Southern Ocean","s":"Tasmania is shaped a little like a heart, and from above its west is wild: dark forest, buttongrass plains, jagged mountains and lakes, with hardly a road. The east is gentler, with farmland and white-sand bays. The next land to the south is Antarctica, and the westerlies of the Roaring Forties arrive straight off thousands of kilometres of open ocean.","w":"A heart-shaped island south of Australia: rugged dark-green west, drier farmland and bright bays along the east coast.","f":["Abel Tasman sighted it in 1642, and for two centuries it was called Van Diemen's Land.","Air measured at Cape Grim, on the north-west tip, is among the cleanest on Earth.","The Tasmanian devil now survives in the wild only here."],"q":{"q":"What was Tasmania's old European name?","a":"Van Diemen's Land","x":["New Holland","Terra Australis"]}},"Q40285":{"t":"Rainforest, great rivers and ice beside the equator","s":"The world's second-largest island is a green giant with a mountain spine running its full length, usually wrapped in cloud. Rivers such as the Sepik and the Fly loop through swamp forest in wide brown meanders you can follow for hundreds of kilometres. In the west the peaks reach almost 4,900 m, high enough for small glaciers to have survived right beside the equator.","w":"A huge green island with a long, cloud-wrapped mountain spine and wide, snaking brown rivers through lowland swamp.","f":["Puncak Jaya, 4,884 m, is the highest mountain on any island in the world.","At about 786,000 km², New Guinea is second only to Greenland among the world's islands.","Most of the world's birds-of-paradise, famous for their dancing males, are found on New Guinea."],"q":{"q":"Which is the highest peak on New Guinea?","a":"Puncak Jaya","x":["Mount Wilhelm","Mount Kinabalu"]}},"Q36117":{"t":"One island, three countries and a granite giant","s":"Borneo is the third-largest island on Earth, and from the air you can read its story in its textures. Deep, crinkled rainforest covers the interior, brown rivers coil towards the coast, and across many lowlands neat grids of oil-palm plantations have replaced the jungle. In the north-east, Mount Kinabalu's bare granite summit pokes above the clouds at 4,095 m.","w":"Dark rainforest, coiling brown rivers and geometric plantation grids; in the north-east, Kinabalu's rocky summit above the clouds.","f":["Borneo is shared by three countries: Indonesia, Malaysia and Brunei.","The Kapuas, about 1,140 km long, is Indonesia's longest river.","Wild orangutans live only on Borneo and Sumatra."],"q":{"q":"How many countries share the island of Borneo?","a":"Three","x":["Two","Four"]}},"Q3492":{"t":"Home of Toba, the world's largest volcanic lake","s":"Sumatra stretches almost 1,800 km across the equator, with a chain of volcanoes running down its western side. The unmissable landmark is Lake Toba: a blue oval about 100 km long, filling the crater of a supervolcano that erupted some 74,000 years ago. In its middle sits Samosir, nearly as big as Singapore — an island in a lake on an island.","w":"A long green island with a volcanic ridge along its west; look for Toba, a vast blue lake with a big island inside.","f":["It is the only place where tigers, rhinos, orangutans and elephants still live side by side in the wild.","At about 473,000 km², Sumatra is the world's sixth-largest island.","The equator crosses Sumatra, so the island lies in both hemispheres."],"q":{"q":"What is the island in the middle of Lake Toba called?","a":"Samosir","x":["Nias","Siberut"]}},"Q13989":{"t":"Fuji, the Japanese Alps and Tokyo on one long island","s":"Honshu is the long, bent heart of Japan, and from the window its variety is striking: snow on the Japanese Alps down the middle, rice plains and city sprawl along the coasts. On a clear day Fuji's lone white cone is unmistakable, south-west of Tokyo's vast grey-brown carpet. Tunnels and bridges tie Honshu to the other three main islands.","w":"A long curved island: snowy peaks down the centre, Fuji's solitary cone, and dense cities crowding the Pacific coast.","f":["About 100 million people live on Honshu — more than on any island except Java.","At about 228,000 km², it is the world's seventh-largest island.","The Seikan Tunnel, which runs under the sea to Hokkaido, is about 54 km long."],"q":{"q":"Which island has more people than Honshu?","a":"Java","x":["Great Britain","Luzon"]}},"Q22890":{"t":"The Emerald Isle lives up to its name","s":"On a clear day Ireland really is emerald: small fields in a hundred shades of green, stitched together by hedges and, in the west, by grey stone walls. The Atlantic coast is ragged with bays and headlands; the midlands are low, dotted with lakes and brown bog. Look for the River Shannon swelling into long lakes as it runs south towards the ocean.","w":"A green island west of Great Britain: ragged Atlantic coast, flat lake-dotted midlands and the long thread of the Shannon.","f":["The Shannon, about 360 km long, is the longest river in Ireland or Britain.","Ireland has no native snakes: after the Ice Age the sea cut it off before they could arrive.","Lough Neagh, in the north-east, is the largest lake in the British Isles."],"q":{"q":"What is the longest river in Ireland?","a":"The Shannon","x":["The Liffey","The Boyne"]}},"Q23666":{"t":"Europe's largest island, chalk cliffs to Highland lochs","s":"Great Britain runs almost 1,000 km from south to north, and flying along it you watch the land change. The south is a soft patchwork of fields and towns around London's huge sprawl; the Pennines form a brown spine through northern England; Scotland breaks up into mountains and long, narrow lochs. At night the cities blaze orange while the Highlands go almost dark.","w":"A long island narrowing northward: green farmland in the south, a brown upland spine, then Scotland's mountains and slender lochs.","f":["It is the ninth-largest island in the world and the largest in Europe.","Ben Nevis, its highest mountain at 1,345 m, is still almost 10 km below cruising height.","The Channel Tunnel, about 50 km long, links it to France beneath the Strait of Dover."],"q":{"q":"What is Great Britain's highest mountain?","a":"Ben Nevis","x":["Snowdon","Scafell Pike"]}},"Q186233":{"t":"A forested chain between the Bay of Bengal and Andaman Sea","s":"A narrow chain of islands, almost entirely cloaked in dark tropical forest, runs north to south between the Bay of Bengal and the Andaman Sea. It belongs to India but lies much closer to Myanmar. The water around it is exceptionally clear, so reefs show as pale turquoise halos. East of the main chain, tiny Barren Island is South Asia's only active volcano.","w":"A slim north–south chain of densely forested islands fringed by turquoise reef, with scattered small islets to the east.","f":["Barren Island, only about 3 km across, is the sole confirmed active volcano in South Asia.","North Sentinel Island is home to one of the last peoples on Earth living without contact with the outside world."],"q":{"q":"Which active volcano lies in the Andaman Islands?","a":"Barren Island","x":["Krakatoa","Mount Popa"]}},"Q157286":{"t":"Scattered green islands at the gateway to Malacca","s":"South of the Andamans, across the Ten Degree Channel, the Nicobars scatter in a loose line towards Sumatra. Some are flat coral islands ringed with palms; others, like Great Nicobar, are hilly and cloaked in rainforest. Most are closed to tourists to protect the Nicobarese and Shompen peoples, so the dark-green shapes and turquoise reefs below are seen by few outsiders.","w":"A loose chain of islands between the Andamans and Sumatra: flat, palm-fringed ones in the north, hilly rainforest ones in the south.","f":["The Ten Degree Channel, about 150 km wide, separates them from the Andamans along the 10th parallel.","The Nicobars lie far closer to Sumatra than to mainland India."],"q":{"q":"Which channel separates the Nicobars from the Andamans?","a":"Ten Degree Channel","x":["Six Degree Channel","Nine Degree Channel"]}},"Q3827":{"t":"The original Spice Islands","s":"For centuries these scattered islands were the world's only source of cloves and nutmeg, and ships sailed halfway round the globe to reach them. From above they are green cones and ridges rising steeply out of very deep blue water, their shores rimmed with coral. Ternate and Tidore, two volcanic cones facing each other across a narrow strait, are the classic view.","w":"Steep green volcanic islands and cones, rimmed with coral, rising from unusually dark-blue water.","f":["Under a 1667 treaty the Dutch kept Run, a tiny nutmeg island here, and the English kept Manhattan.","Nutmeg and mace both come from the same fruit, native to the Banda Islands.","The Banda Sea plunges to more than 7 km deep in the Weber Deep."],"q":{"q":"Which spice came originally only from the Banda Islands?","a":"Nutmeg","x":["Cinnamon","Black pepper"]}},"Q119285":{"t":"A 1,900-km arc of volcanoes reaching toward Asia","s":"Flights between North America and East Asia often skirt the Aleutians, a 1,900-km arc of volcanic islands trailing from Alaska towards Kamchatka. Fog and cloud hide them much of the time; when it clears, look for snow-streaked cones — Shishaldin on Unimak Island is one of the most symmetrical on Earth. North of the chain lies the Bering Sea, south of it the open Pacific.","w":"A long curving chain of rugged islands and snowy volcanic cones, often poking through a lid of fog.","f":["The chain crosses the 180th meridian, so Alaska reaches into the Eastern Hemisphere.","The arc has dozens of volcanoes, and some erupt every few years.","The Unangan (Aleut) people have lived on these islands for about 9,000 years."],"q":{"q":"Which sea lies north of the Aleutian Islands?","a":"The Bering Sea","x":["The Sea of Okhotsk","The Chukchi Sea"]}},"Q216533":{"t":"The top of Canada: ice caps, bare rock, almost no one","s":"At the northern edge of Canada lies a cluster of large islands with bare brown plateaus, glaciers spilling from ice caps, and channels choked with sea ice for most of the year. Only a few hundred people live here, in two small hamlets. The group was named in 1953 to mark the coronation of Queen Elizabeth II.","w":"Big, flat-topped islands of bare rock, white ice caps and glaciers, separated by channels of pack ice.","f":["Devon Island is the largest uninhabited island on Earth.","On Axel Heiberg Island, stumps of trees that grew about 45 million years ago still stand, preserved but not fossilised.","Scientists use Devon Island's Haughton impact crater to rehearse exploring Mars."],"q":{"q":"Which is the largest uninhabited island on Earth?","a":"Devon Island","x":["Ellesmere Island","Melville Island"]}},"Q68740":{"t":"Taller than Everest, if you start from the sea floor","s":"The Big Island is made of five volcanoes fused together, and from above you can see their broad, gentle domes and dark lava flows streaking down to the sea. Mauna Kea and Mauna Loa both top 4,100 m, and in winter their summits can be white with snow. Measured from its base on the ocean floor, Mauna Kea is over 10,000 m tall — more than Everest.","w":"Two huge, gentle domes, snow-dusted in winter, with black lava flows running to the coast and perhaps a volcanic plume.","f":["Mauna Loa is the largest active volcano on Earth by volume.","Lava from Kīlauea has added new land to the island's south-east coast within recent decades.","The Big Island is larger than all the other Hawaiian islands combined."],"q":{"q":"How many volcanoes make up the island of Hawaii?","a":"Five","x":["Two","Eight"]}},"Q120755":{"t":"Snowy Alps, turquoise lakes and fjords","s":"The Southern Alps run like a white wall down the length of the island, and on their dry eastern side glacial lakes glow an unreal milky turquoise — Tekapo and Pukaki are the brightest. To the west, glaciers tumble towards rainforest and the sea; in the south-west, Fiordland's drowned valleys cut deep into the mountains. East of the range, braided rivers fan across the Canterbury Plains.","w":"A white mountain wall down the island, vivid turquoise lakes on its eastern side, fjords slicing the south-west coast.","f":["Aoraki / Mount Cook, 3,724 m, is New Zealand's highest mountain.","The lakes' colour comes from 'rock flour', fine silt ground by glaciers and suspended in the water.","At about 150,000 km², it is the world's 12th-largest island."],"q":{"q":"What gives South Island's glacial lakes their turquoise colour?","a":"Rock flour from glaciers","x":["Algae blooms","Copper minerals"]}},"Q118863":{"t":"Volcanoes, a caldera lake and a perfect lone cone","s":"The centre of the North Island is a volcanic plateau, and it shows. Lake Taupō, the country's largest lake, fills a caldera left by enormous eruptions; south of it rise Ruapehu, Tongariro and the neat cone of Ngauruhoe, often snow-capped. Out on the west coast, Taranaki stands alone: a near-perfect cone ringed by a sharp circle of forest amid green farmland.","w":"A large lake in the centre with snowy volcanoes to its south; on the west coast, a lone cone inside a circle of forest.","f":["Lake Taupō, about 616 km², fills a volcanic caldera; its last great eruption was around 1,800 years ago.","About three-quarters of New Zealanders live on the North Island.","Auckland is built on a field of some 50 small volcanoes."],"q":{"q":"Which lake fills a volcanic caldera in the centre of the North Island?","a":"Lake Taupō","x":["Lake Wakatipu","Lake Tekapo"]}},"Q709":{"t":"Two chains of atolls, barely above the waves","s":"The Marshalls are two parallel chains of coral atolls — the 'sunrise' and 'sunset' chains — strung across the central Pacific. From above, each atoll is a necklace of narrow islets around a lagoon, bright turquoise inside and deep blue outside. The land averages only about 2 m above sea level, so the islands can look like lines painted on the water.","w":"Necklaces of thin islets around wide turquoise lagoons, lined up in two roughly parallel north-west to south-east chains.","f":["Marshallese navigators made stick charts — frames of sticks and shells mapping the ocean swells around the islands.","The country has 29 atolls and 5 single islands, with only about 180 km² of land in all."],"q":{"q":"What did Marshallese navigators map with sticks and shells?","a":"Ocean swells","x":["Star positions","Fish migrations"]}},"Q686":{"t":"A chain of volcanoes, some of them still glowing","s":"Vanuatu is a chain of about 80 islands running some 1,300 km north to south, and many of them are volcanoes. Steep green slopes drop to fringing reef, and in places bare ash plains and smoking craters break the forest. On Tanna, in the south, Mount Yasur has been erupting almost without pause for centuries; at night its crater glow can sometimes be seen from far away.","w":"A long chain of green volcanic islands with reef edges; look for grey ash slopes and drifting plumes, especially in the south.","f":["The land divers of Pentecost Island, who leap from wooden towers with vines tied to their ankles, inspired bungee jumping.","Vanuatu has an underwater post office, in the reef off Hideaway Island near Port Vila."],"q":{"q":"Which sport was inspired by Pentecost Island's land divers?","a":"Bungee jumping","x":["Cliff diving","Skydiving"]}},"Q80389":{"t":"A chain of volcanic cones stepping across the sea","s":"Between Kamchatka and Hokkaido the Kurils cross the sea like a line of stepping stones about 1,200 km long. Many are simply volcanoes poking out of the water, their slopes streaked with snow well into summer. Fog is the rule here, but on a clear day look for Alaid, a lone cone rising straight from the waves at the northern end of the chain.","w":"A line of small, steep volcanic islands and cones between two seas, often half hidden by fog.","f":["Alaid, on Atlasov Island, rises to about 2,300 m and is the highest volcano in the chain.","On Onekotan, a volcanic cone rises from a lake that fills an older, larger crater.","Just offshore, the Kuril–Kamchatka Trench plunges more than 9 km deep."],"q":{"q":"Which sea lies to the west of the Kuril Islands?","a":"The Sea of Okhotsk","x":["The Sea of Japan","The Bering Sea"]}},"Q1964385":{"t":"Volcanic islands that are, officially, part of Tokyo","s":"South of Tokyo Bay a thin line of volcanic islands runs more than 1,000 km out into the Pacific: first the Izu Islands, then the Ogasawara group, then the Volcano Islands. Many are single cones with steep, dark shores and a collar of white surf. However far they lie from the capital, all of them are governed by the Tokyo Metropolis.","w":"A long north–south dotted line of small, steep volcanic islands, some with craters, fringed by white surf.","f":["The Ogasawara Islands have never been joined to a continent, so many of their species live nowhere else; they are a UNESCO site.","Nishinoshima has grown many times over since eruptions began in 2013, swallowing the old island.","Ogasawara has no airport: the ferry from Tokyo takes about 24 hours."],"q":{"q":"Which city governs the Nanpō Islands?","a":"Tokyo","x":["Osaka","Yokohama"]}},"Q11832316":{"t":"Remote atolls of black pearls and empty lagoons","s":"Far north of Rarotonga, a handful of small atolls lie scattered across hundreds of kilometres of ocean. From above they are classic rings: a thin rim of coconut palms around a lagoon that glows every shade from jade to turquoise. A few hundred people at most live on each inhabited island, and cultured black pearls from Manihiki's lagoon are the local treasure.","w":"Isolated rings of land around jade and turquoise lagoons, widely scattered, with no high ground at all.","f":["New Zealander Tom Neale lived alone on Suwarrow for years and wrote 'An Island to Oneself' about it.","Suwarrow is now a national park with no permanent residents, only seasonal caretakers."],"q":{"q":"What is Manihiki atoll famous for?","a":"Black pearls","x":["Vanilla","Sea salt"]}},"Q1011":{"t":"Dry volcanic islands where Atlantic hurricanes are born","s":"Some 500 km off West Africa, ten volcanic islands rise from the Atlantic in shades of rust, ochre and grey — the Sahara's dry breath reaches this far. Sal and Boa Vista in the east are flat and sandy. In the south-west, Fogo is one enormous volcano, with a young black cone standing inside a broad, collapsed caldera.","w":"Brown, arid islands in deep-blue sea; Fogo is round, with a dark cone inside a caldera; the eastern islands are flat and sandy.","f":["The islands were uninhabited until the Portuguese arrived around 1460.","Pico do Fogo, 2,829 m, is the highest point; it last erupted in 2014–15.","Many of the strongest Atlantic hurricanes begin as storms near these islands."],"q":{"q":"Which Cape Verde island is a single volcano with a cone inside its caldera?","a":"Fogo","x":["Sal","Boa Vista"]}},"Q891593":{"t":"An island that was mapped as a peninsula until 1908","s":"At the mouth of the Khatanga Gulf, on the edge of the Laptev Sea, lies a low, roughly square island of tundra about 60 km across. For most of the year it is white and merges with the sea ice around it; in the short summer it turns brown-green and glitters with small lakes. Rivers run outwards from its central upland like the spokes of a wheel.","w":"A squarish, low tundra island at the mouth of a long gulf, lake-speckled in summer, often fused with sea ice.","f":["Until 1908 it was mapped as a peninsula; Nikifor Begichev showed it was an island, and it now bears his name.","Its highest point, on the central Kiryaka upland, is under 200 m.","Begichev had earlier sailed on Eduard Toll's Russian polar expedition of 1900–1902."],"q":{"q":"What was Bolshoy Begichev thought to be before 1908?","a":"A peninsula","x":["Two separate islands","A sandbank"]}},"Q219774":{"t":"India's southernmost tip, wrapped in rainforest","s":"Great Nicobar is the largest and wildest of the Nicobars: hilly, almost wholly covered in rainforest, its rivers winding out through mangroves to the sea. Its southern tip, Indira Point, is the southernmost point of India, and Indonesia's Sumatra lies less than 200 km beyond it. The Shompen, a small forest people, live in the interior.","w":"A large, hilly, dark-green island at the southern end of the Nicobar chain, with pale beaches and mangrove creeks.","f":["Much of the island is a biosphere reserve that includes two national parks.","Giant leatherback turtles come ashore on its beaches to nest."],"q":{"q":"What is Indira Point, on Great Nicobar, known for?","a":"India's southernmost point","x":["India's highest island peak","India's oldest lighthouse"]}},"Q1473243":{"t":"The Andamans' busy hub, ringed by reef and mangroves","s":"Most flights to the Andamans land here, on the island that holds the capital and most of the islands' people. From the air South Andaman is a ragged shape of forested hills and deep, winding creeks lined with mangroves. Off its south-west coast, a marine national park protects reefs and a scatter of small islands set in pale turquoise water.","w":"A ragged, hilly green island with long mangrove creeks; the town and airport on its east coast, small reef islands to the south-west.","f":["The Mahatma Gandhi Marine National Park, founded in 1983, covers some 15 small islands and their reefs.","Chidiya Tapu, at the island's southern tip, is known for its birdlife and sunsets over the sea."],"q":{"q":"Which marine national park lies off South Andaman?","a":"Mahatma Gandhi Marine National Park","x":["Gulf of Mannar National Park","Sundarbans National Park"]}},"Q222853":{"t":"The largest Andaman island, forest from shore to shore","s":"Middle Andaman is the largest island of the chain, and from above it is almost unbroken forest from coast to coast. Its eastern shore is cut by long, branching creeks fringed with mangroves; the western side, facing the open sea, is wilder still and part of the Jarawa people's reserve. Look for small islands and turquoise reef patches off the east coast.","w":"An elongated island of near-continuous dark forest, with mangrove-lined creeks on the east and small islets offshore.","f":["At about 1,500 km², it is the largest of the Andaman Islands.","Just to the south, on Baratang Island, small mud volcanoes bubble up through the forest."],"q":{"q":"What unusual feature is found on Baratang, just south of Middle Andaman?","a":"Mud volcanoes","x":["A salt lake","A crater lake"]}},"Q2000510":{"t":"The Andamans' highest peak rises from this forested isle","s":"At the northern end of the Great Andaman chain, this island rises higher than any other: Saddle Peak, 732 m, stands near its east coast in a national park of dense forest. Around it, creeks and mangroves eat into the shore, and reefs trace pale lines offshore. Beyond, open sea stretches north towards Myanmar's Coco Islands.","w":"The northernmost large island of the Andaman chain: forested hills rising to a high ridge near the east coast.","f":["Saddle Peak, 732 m, is the highest point of the Andaman and Nicobar Islands.","Off Diglipur, the little islands of Ross and Smith are joined by a narrow sandbar, making a pair of twins."],"q":{"q":"What is the highest point of the Andaman Islands?","a":"Saddle Peak","x":["Mount Harriet","Mount Thullier"]}},"Q82601":{"t":"Magellan's 'Land of Fire' at the bottom of the Americas","s":"At the very tip of South America the Andes bend east and crumble into islands, glaciers and fjords. The main island of Tierra del Fuego is two landscapes in one: flat, tawny steppe with pale lakes in the north, and snowy mountains with glaciers spilling towards the sea in the south. The Strait of Magellan cuts it off from the mainland.","w":"A big triangular island: yellow-brown plains in the north, snowy peaks and glaciers in the south, the Beagle Channel along its southern edge.","f":["Magellan named it in 1520 after the fires he saw burning along its shores.","Cape Horn lies on a small island at the archipelago's southern edge.","The Beagle Channel is named after HMS Beagle, the ship that later carried Charles Darwin here."],"q":{"q":"Which strait separates Tierra del Fuego from the mainland?","a":"The Strait of Magellan","x":["The Drake Passage","The Beagle Channel"]}},"Q1952":{"t":"Almost an island, hugged by the world's highest tides","s":"Nova Scotia is a long peninsula tied to the mainland only by a narrow neck of land, and nowhere in it is more than about 67 km from the sea. The Atlantic coast is a ragged fringe of coves, rocks and islets. On the north-west side, the Bay of Fundy has the highest tides in the world: at low water, look for vast red mudflats where there was sea a few hours before.","w":"A long peninsula parallel to the mainland, a deeply indented Atlantic coast, and reddish tidal flats along the Bay of Fundy.","f":["Fundy's tides can rise and fall by about 16 m, the greatest range on Earth.","The name is Latin for 'New Scotland'.","Sable Island, a thin crescent of sand far out in the Atlantic off Nova Scotia, is home to wild horses."],"q":{"q":"Which bay beside Nova Scotia has the world's highest tides?","a":"The Bay of Fundy","x":["Hudson Bay","Chesapeake Bay"]}},"Q170479":{"t":"A mountainous island between the ocean and the mainland","s":"Vancouver Island runs about 460 km along Canada's Pacific coast, its spine of snowy mountains dividing two worlds. The west side faces the open ocean with deep sounds, surf and temperate rainforest; the sheltered east side has farmland and towns along the Strait of Georgia. At its southern tip is Victoria, capital of British Columbia — the city of Vancouver is across the water on the mainland.","w":"A long mountainous island parallel to the coast: snowy peaks down the middle, fjord-like inlets on the ocean side.","f":["It is the largest island on the Pacific coast of North America.","Its highest peak, the Golden Hinde, reaches 2,195 m.","It is named after George Vancouver, who charted this coast in 1792."],"q":{"q":"Which capital city stands on Vancouver Island?","a":"Victoria","x":["Vancouver","Olympia"]}},"Q158129":{"t":"A jagged Arctic giant riddled with lakes","s":"Victoria Island is the eighth-largest island in the world, and one of the oddest-looking: its coast splays into long peninsulas and deep bays like a spilled blot of ink. Inland, the low tundra is spattered with countless lakes that stay frozen for much of the year. Only about 2,000 people live here, mostly in Cambridge Bay and Ulukhaktok.","w":"A huge, low, ragged island with spreading peninsulas, deep bays and tundra freckled with lakes, often under sea ice.","f":["At about 217,000 km², it is bigger than Great Britain.","The border between Nunavut and the Northwest Territories runs across it.","It holds the largest known island in a lake on an island in a lake on an island."],"q":{"q":"Victoria Island is larger than which of these islands?","a":"Great Britain","x":["Honshu","Sumatra"]}},"Q380307":{"t":"A wild, lake-spattered land where icebergs drift south","s":"Labrador is the wild mainland half of Newfoundland and Labrador: a rocky plateau scoured by ice-age glaciers and spattered with thousands of lakes. In the north the Torngat Mountains rise in sharp peaks straight from the sea, with small glaciers tucked into their hollows. Offshore, the cold Labrador Current carries icebergs south from Greenland in spring and early summer.","w":"Barren rocky upland dotted with countless lakes, a fjorded coast and sharp peaks in the north; icebergs offshore in spring.","f":["Mount Caubvick in the Torngats, 1,652 m, is the highest point of mainland Canada east of the Rockies.","The shimmering gemstone labradorite is named after this region.","Norse sagas mention a forested 'Markland', which many historians place here."],"q":{"q":"Which gemstone takes its name from Labrador?","a":"Labradorite","x":["Larimar","Amazonite"]}},"Q146841":{"t":"Canada's northern tip, under 800 km from the Pole","s":"Ellesmere is the northernmost part of Canada, and from above it looks as if the Ice Age never quite ended: ice caps and glaciers pour through dark mountains into fjords sealed with sea ice. Its northern tip, Cape Columbia, is less than 800 km from the North Pole. Only around 150 people live here, most of them in the Inuit hamlet of Grise Fiord.","w":"Dark mountains buried in ice caps, glaciers spilling into fjords, and frozen sea on every side.","f":["Alert, on its north-east coast, is the northernmost permanently staffed place on Earth.","Barbeau Peak, 2,616 m, is the highest point in Nunavut.","Fossils of Tiktaalik, a fish with limb-like fins, were found here in 2004."],"q":{"q":"What is the northernmost permanently staffed place on Earth?","a":"Alert","x":["Longyearbyen","Qaanaaq"]}},"Q81178":{"t":"The world's fifth-largest island, cut by fjords and ice","s":"On polar routes between Europe and western North America you may cross Baffin, Canada's biggest island. Its east coast is dramatic: mountains, ice caps and deep fjords walled with granite, often edged by sea ice or icebergs. The west is lower and flatter, dotted with lakes. Iqaluit, the capital of Nunavut, sits at the head of Frobisher Bay in the south-east.","w":"A huge island with a mountainous, fjord-slashed, ice-capped east coast and low, lake-dotted tundra to the west.","f":["At about 507,000 km², Baffin is Canada's largest island and the fifth-largest in the world.","Mount Thor's sheer granite face, about 1,250 m high, is the world's greatest vertical drop.","Norse sagas describe a stony 'Helluland', usually identified with Baffin."],"q":{"q":"Which territorial capital is on Baffin Island?","a":"Iqaluit","x":["Yellowknife","Whitehorse"]}},"Q48335":{"t":"The Rock, where Vikings landed a thousand years ago","s":"Transatlantic flights often make landfall over Newfoundland, a big, rugged island of rock, bog, forest and countless ponds. Its coast is a maze of long bays and headlands, and in spring icebergs from Greenland drift past. Near its northern tip, around the year 1000, Norse sailors built a small settlement at L'Anse aux Meadows — the only confirmed Viking site in North America outside Greenland.","w":"A big, rough-edged island of dark forest and bog, speckled with ponds, its coast a maze of deep bays.","f":["Newfoundland keeps its own time zone, half an hour ahead of Atlantic time.","In Gros Morne National Park, the Tablelands expose rock from the Earth's mantle.","Cape Spear, near St. John's, is the easternmost point of North America outside Greenland."],"q":{"q":"What is the Norse site near Newfoundland's northern tip called?","a":"L'Anse aux Meadows","x":["Port aux Basques","Trois-Rivières"]}},"Q3812":{"t":"The island shaped like a sprawling letter K","s":"Sulawesi is instantly recognisable on a map and from the air: four long, mountainous arms reach out from a central knot, so nowhere is very far from the sea. Deep gulfs lie between the arms, and inland the ridges hide clear, deep lakes. The island sits beyond the Wallace Line, and many of its animals, like the tusked babirusa pig, live nowhere else.","w":"Four long, narrow, mountainous peninsulas spreading from a centre like a starfish, with deep gulfs between them.","f":["Lake Matano, about 590 m deep, is the deepest lake in South-East Asia.","In the highlands of Tana Toraja, traditional houses carry huge upswept roofs shaped like boats.","At about 180,000 km², Sulawesi is the world's 11th-largest island."],"q":{"q":"Which letter is Sulawesi's outline often compared to?","a":"K","x":["S","Y"]}},"Q3757":{"t":"A string of volcanoes on the world's most crowded island","s":"Java is about 1,000 km long, and down its spine marches a line of volcanic cones, many of them active — on a clear day you can count them one after another. Between them, rice fields and towns fill every valley: more than 150 million people live here. In the east, look for the Tengger caldera, a grey 'sea of sand' with the smoking crater of Bromo inside.","w":"A long island with a row of volcanic cones along its length; in the east, a grey sandy caldera holding smaller cones.","f":["Semeru, 3,676 m, is Java's highest volcano and one of its most active.","At night Java glows almost from end to end with the lights of its towns and cities.","Borobudur, in central Java, is the largest Buddhist temple in the world."],"q":{"q":"Which is Java's highest volcano?","a":"Semeru","x":["Merapi","Bromo"]}},"Q124873":{"t":"Mount Apo, rare eagles and a 10-km-deep trench offshore","s":"Mindanao, the Philippines' big southern island, is a sprawling, many-armed shape of forested mountains, volcanoes and wide river plains. Near Davao, Mount Apo rises to 2,954 m, the highest point in the country. Just off the east coast the sea floor drops into the Philippine Trench, more than 10 km deep — nearly as far down as you are up.","w":"A large, many-lobed island of green mountains and plains, with a big lake on its north-central plateau and Mount Apo near Davao.","f":["Lake Lanao, on the north-central plateau, is the second-largest lake in the Philippines.","Mindanao's forests are the last stronghold of the Philippine eagle, one of the largest eagles in the world.","It is the second-largest island in the Philippines, after Luzon."],"q":{"q":"What is the highest mountain in the Philippines?","a":"Mount Apo","x":["Mayon Volcano","Mount Pulag"]}},"Q125384":{"t":"Mayon's perfect cone and a volcano inside a lake","s":"Luzon is the largest island of the Philippines: a long shape with a mountain spine in the north and a ragged tail curling to the south-east. Look south of Manila's sprawl for two lakes — broad Laguna de Bay, and Taal, with a volcanic island in its middle. Near the south-eastern tip stands Mayon, a cone so symmetrical it looks drawn with a ruler.","w":"Mountains in the north, Manila's sprawl around its bay, two big lakes to the south, and a lone perfect cone near the south-eastern tip.","f":["Mayon, 2,462 m, is famous as one of the most perfectly shaped volcanic cones on Earth.","Laguna de Bay, just south-east of Manila, is the largest lake in the Philippines.","The rice terraces of the Cordillera, in northern Luzon, are a UNESCO World Heritage site."],"q":{"q":"Which lake near Manila has a volcanic island in its middle?","a":"Taal Lake","x":["Laguna de Bay","Lake Lanao"]}},"Q22502":{"t":"Mountains higher than Fuji on a leaf-shaped island","s":"Taiwan is shaped like a leaf, and its two sides could hardly be more different. The east is a wall of mountains dropping almost straight into the Pacific, with more than 200 peaks above 3,000 m. The west slopes gently down to a crowded plain of cities and fields that blazes with light at night. The Tropic of Cancer runs across the middle.","w":"A leaf-shaped island: steep forested mountains packed along the east, a busy flat plain along the west coast.","f":["Yushan, or Jade Mountain, reaches 3,952 m — higher than Mount Fuji.","Portuguese sailors called it Ilha Formosa, 'beautiful island'.","Sun Moon Lake, in the central mountains, is the island's largest body of water."],"q":{"q":"What does 'Formosa', an old name for Taiwan, mean?","a":"Beautiful island","x":["Green island","Mountain island"]}},"Q13987":{"t":"Where volcanoes smoke above the city streets","s":"Kyushu, the southernmost of Japan's main islands, is volcano country. In its centre, Aso's caldera is so vast that towns, farms and a railway fit inside it. In the south, Sakurajima rises from Kagoshima Bay just across the water from the city, often trailing a plume of ash. The coast is ragged with bays and islands, especially towards Nagasaki in the west.","w":"A mountainous island with a huge round caldera in its middle and a smoking volcano in a southern bay.","f":["The name Kyushu means 'nine provinces', after its old divisions.","During Japan's long isolation, the islet of Dejima in Nagasaki harbour was its only window for European traders.","Aso's caldera measures roughly 25 by 18 km."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Kyushu mean?","a":"Nine provinces","x":["Southern island","Land of fire"]}},"Q13991":{"t":"Japan's quiet island of 88 temples","s":"Shikoku is the smallest and least crowded of Japan's four main islands, mostly steep, forested mountains with towns strung along the coast. To the north lies the Seto Inland Sea, calm water studded with islands and crossed by long bridges. In the Naruto Strait, on the east side, the turning tide spins whirlpools that can reach about 20 m across.","w":"A compact mountainous island south of the island-dotted Seto Inland Sea, linked to Honshu by long bridges.","f":["Pilgrims walk a circuit of 88 Buddhist temples around the island, about 1,200 km in all.","Mount Ishizuchi, 1,982 m, is the highest peak in western Japan.","Three bridge routes link Shikoku to Honshu across the Seto Inland Sea."],"q":{"q":"How many temples are on Shikoku's famous pilgrimage?","a":"88","x":["33","108"]}},"Q35581":{"t":"Japan's snowy north, where sea ice reaches the shore","s":"Hokkaido feels different from the rest of Japan: wider fields, bigger forests, fewer people and far more snow. From above, look for round caldera lakes — Shikotsu, Tōya, Kussharo — set among volcanic hills. In late winter, drift ice from the Sea of Okhotsk packs against the north-east coast and the Shiretoko Peninsula, turning the sea white.","w":"A big island of broad farmland, forested mountains and round blue caldera lakes; white in winter, with sea ice along the north-east.","f":["The Shiretoko Peninsula in the north-east has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 2005.","It covers over a fifth of Japan's land but holds only about 4% of its people.","Sapporo hosted the Winter Olympics in 1972."],"q":{"q":"Which indigenous people have their homeland on Hokkaido?","a":"The Ainu","x":["The Ryukyuans","The Nivkh"]}},"Q7792":{"t":"A long, thin island almost touching the mainland","s":"Sakhalin stretches nearly 950 km from north to south but is never more than about 160 km wide — from the air it looks like a long green-and-brown fish. Two mountain ranges run along it with a valley in between. In the north-west, the Nevelskoy Strait narrows to about 7 km from the mainland, and in winter it freezes right across.","w":"A very long, narrow island parallel to the coast, with two mountain ridges and a central valley; icy seas in winter.","f":["It is Russia's largest island, at about 76,000 km².","Anton Chekhov travelled here in 1890 and wrote the book 'Sakhalin Island'.","At its southern tip, La Pérouse Strait separates Sakhalin from Hokkaido by about 40 km."],"q":{"q":"Which writer travelled to Sakhalin in 1890 and wrote a book about it?","a":"Anton Chekhov","x":["Leo Tolstoy","Fyodor Dostoevsky"]}},"Q1462":{"t":"Emerald coves and 7,000 ancient stone towers","s":"Sardinia is the second-largest island in the Mediterranean, a rugged block of hills and plateaus with surprisingly few towns. Watch the coast: in the north-east, the Costa Smeralda's granite coves glow emerald and turquoise, while long white beaches line the south. Just to the north, across a narrow strait, Corsica rises much higher.","w":"A large, roughly rectangular island with a rugged interior; bright turquoise coves in the north-east and Corsica just to the north.","f":["Some 7,000 nuraghi — Bronze Age stone towers — are scattered across the island.","The mountain villages of Ogliastra are famous for an unusually high number of people living past 100.","The Strait of Bonifacio, only about 11 km wide, separates Sardinia from Corsica."],"q":{"q":"What are Sardinia's ancient stone towers called?","a":"Nuraghi","x":["Trulli","Talayots"]}},"Q14112":{"t":"A mountain range rising straight from the sea","s":"The French call Corsica 'the mountain in the sea', and from above you see why: a jagged granite range fills almost the whole island, streaked with snow well into spring and rising to 2,706 m. The west coast is a run of deep gulfs and red cliffs; the east is a flatter strip of lagoons. At the northern tip, the thin finger of Cap Corse points towards Genoa.","w":"A mountainous island with a finger-like peninsula pointing north; deep gulfs on the west coast, flat lagoons on the east.","f":["Napoleon Bonaparte was born in Ajaccio, on the west coast, in 1769.","The GR20 trail along Corsica's mountain spine is often called Europe's toughest long-distance hike.","The red cliffs of Scandola, on the Gulf of Porto, are a UNESCO World Heritage site."],"q":{"q":"Which emperor was born on Corsica?","a":"Napoleon Bonaparte","x":["Charlemagne","Napoleon III"]}},"Q4951156":{"t":"A triangular island crowned by smoking Etna","s":"Sicily is the largest island in the Mediterranean, a rough triangle that earned it the ancient name Trinacria. Its landmark is unmissable: Etna, a broad, dark volcano on the east coast, snow-capped in winter and often trailing a plume. To the north, look for the Aeolian Islands, where Stromboli has been throwing up small bursts of lava for thousands of years.","w":"A triangular island; on its east coast, Etna's huge dark cone, often with a plume; small volcanic islands to the north.","f":["Etna, about 3,300 m, is the tallest active volcano in Europe, and its height changes with its eruptions.","The Strait of Messina separates Sicily from mainland Italy by only about 3 km.","Stromboli's regular glowing bursts earned it the nickname 'Lighthouse of the Mediterranean'."],"q":{"q":"Which ancient name for Sicily refers to its shape?","a":"Trinacria","x":["Hesperia","Taprobane"]}},"Q337524":{"t":"Vanuatu's biggest island, once taken for a continent","s":"Espiritu Santo is the largest island of Vanuatu: a rugged, forested west side rising to the country's highest peaks, and a lower east side of plantations, reefs and white beaches. In 1606 the navigator Pedro Fernández de Quirós landed here and believed he had found the great southern continent. Look for turquoise reef flats along its eastern shores.","w":"A large green island with a high mountain wall along its west side and flatter, reef-fringed shores to the east.","f":["Mount Tabwemasana, 1,879 m, is the highest point in Vanuatu.","Spring-fed 'blue holes' in its eastern forest are pools of startlingly clear, bright-blue water."],"q":{"q":"What did Quirós believe Espiritu Santo was in 1606?","a":"The great southern continent","x":["The coast of Asia","Part of New Guinea"]}},"Q148966":{"t":"A double chain of jungle islands and vast lagoons","s":"The Solomons form two long, roughly parallel chains of high, jungle-covered islands with a sheltered sea between them. Their coasts are fringed by reefs and mangroves, and in the west the Marovo Lagoon spreads as a maze of islets and turquoise channels. Far to the south, Rennell is a raised coral atoll with a huge lake where its lagoon used to be.","w":"Two parallel lines of mountainous, dark-green islands, fringed by reef, with a turquoise maze of lagoon in the west.","f":["The Spanish explorer Álvaro de Mendaña named the islands in 1568, hoping they held King Solomon's riches.","Lake Tegano on Rennell Island is the largest lake in the insular Pacific.","On Malaita, strings of shell money are still used for bride price and ceremonies."],"q":{"q":"Whose legendary wealth inspired the name of the Solomon Islands?","a":"King Solomon's","x":["King Midas's","The Queen of Sheba's"]}},"Q10905515":{"t":"A 1,000-km arc of coral seas from Kyushu to Taiwan","s":"South-west of Kyushu, the Ryukyus trail away in a long arc towards Taiwan, each island ringed by coral reef and water in a dozen shades of blue. Okinawa, the largest, is long and narrow, with busy towns in the south and green hills in the north. For more than four centuries these islands were the independent Ryukyu Kingdom, trading between Japan, China and South-East Asia.","w":"A long chain of small islands wrapped in bright turquoise reef, stretching south-west from Kyushu towards Taiwan.","f":["Karate grew up in Okinawa, from local martial arts blended with Chinese influences.","The Iriomote cat, one of the rarest wild cats on Earth, lives on a single island here.","From Yonaguni, the westernmost island, Taiwan is only about 110 km away."],"q":{"q":"Which martial art originated in Okinawa?","a":"Karate","x":["Judo","Aikido"]}},"Q183963":{"t":"The world's largest chain of atolls","s":"The Tuamotus scatter nearly 80 coral atolls across a vast stretch of the South Pacific — the largest group of atolls on Earth. From above, each is a thin, broken ring of palm-topped land around a lagoon of luminous turquoise, with deep blue all around. The biggest, Rangiroa, is so wide that from one shore of its lagoon you cannot see the other.","w":"Dozens of thin, broken rings of land around glowing turquoise lagoons, strewn across dark-blue ocean.","f":["Early navigators called them the 'Dangerous Archipelago', because the low atolls were so hard to spot.","Rangiroa's lagoon covers about 1,450 km², one of the largest in the world.","Much of French Polynesia's famous black-pearl farming takes place in Tuamotu lagoons."],"q":{"q":"What did early European navigators call the Tuamotus?","a":"The Dangerous Archipelago","x":["The Friendly Islands","The Isles of Thieves"]}},"Q154628":{"t":"A broken ring of volcanic islands off New Guinea","s":"The Bismarck Archipelago curves around the Bismarck Sea like a broken ring. New Britain is the big crescent, mountainous and smoking in places; New Ireland is its long, skinny neighbour; Manus and the Admiralty Islands lie to the north-west. At New Britain's north-eastern tip, look for Rabaul, a town built around a flooded volcanic caldera.","w":"A big crescent island with volcanic peaks, a long thin island to its north-east, and scattered smaller islands.","f":["The islands were named in 1884 after the German chancellor Otto von Bismarck.","New Ireland is about 350 km long but in places less than 10 km wide.","Rabaul's harbour fills a caldera whose volcanoes, such as Tavurvur, still erupt."],"q":{"q":"Who is the Bismarck Archipelago named after?","a":"Otto von Bismarck","x":["Kaiser Wilhelm I","Alfred von Tirpitz"]}},"Q201766":{"t":"Smoking volcanoes on an explorer's namesake island","s":"Bougainville is a big, mountainous island draped in rainforest, with a chain of volcanoes down its spine. Mount Balbi is the highest, at about 2,700 m, while its neighbour Bagana is one of the most active volcanoes in the region, often trailing a plume of steam and ash. The coasts are lined with reef, black-sand beaches and mangroves.","w":"A long mountainous island of dark rainforest with volcanic peaks down the middle, often with a plume drifting from one of them.","f":["It is named after the French explorer Louis-Antoine de Bougainville, who sailed past in 1768.","The bright bougainvillea flower is named after the same explorer.","Geographically, it is the largest island of the Solomon Islands chain."],"q":{"q":"Which garden plant shares its name with this island's explorer?","a":"Bougainvillea","x":["Magnolia","Fuchsia"]}},"Q192767":{"t":"Jungle peaks on one side, grassy plains on the other","s":"Guadalcanal is the largest island of the Solomon Islands, and strikingly lopsided. A wall of jungle-covered mountains, rising to more than 2,300 m, runs along its southern side, where the rainy 'Weather Coast' drops steeply to the sea. The north is gentler, with grassy plains, plantations and the national capital, Honiara.","w":"A long island with steep, cloud-capped mountains along the south coast and pale grassy plains along the north.","f":["It is named after Guadalcanal in Andalusia, the home town of a member of Mendaña's Spanish expedition of 1568.","The south coast is called the Weather Coast for its heavy rain and rough seas."],"q":{"q":"Which city on Guadalcanal is the capital of Solomon Islands?","a":"Honiara","x":["Gizo","Auki"]}},"Q83639":{"t":"Six volcanoes fused into a seahorse on the equator","s":"Isabela, the largest of the Galápagos, is made of six shield volcanoes that have grown together, and from above its outline looks like a seahorse. Broad, dark lava flows streak their flanks, and several summits hold vast calderas. The equator crosses its northern end, yet Galápagos penguins fish in the cold currents along its shores.","w":"A seahorse-shaped island of broad, dark volcanoes with huge summit craters and black lava flows running down to the sea.","f":["Wolf volcano, 1,707 m, is the highest point in the Galápagos.","The pink iguana lives on the slopes of Wolf volcano and nowhere else on Earth.","Sierra Negra's summit caldera measures about 7 by 10 km."],"q":{"q":"What shape is Isabela Island often compared to?","a":"A seahorse","x":["A crescent moon","A boot"]}},"Q42000":{"t":"A figure eight of green peaks inside a reef","s":"Tahiti is two old volcanoes joined by a narrow neck, so from above it looks like a figure eight: big, round Tahiti Nui and smaller Tahiti Iti. Their slopes are sliced into deep green valleys that run from cloud-wrapped peaks down to beaches of black sand. A reef encircles much of the island, holding a pale turquoise lagoon inside.","w":"A large round island joined to a smaller one by a thin isthmus: knife-edge green ridges, a turquoise lagoon and a white line of surf.","f":["Mount Orohena, 2,241 m, is the highest point in French Polynesia.","Captain Cook came here in 1769 to observe Venus crossing the face of the Sun.","The surf break at Teahupo'o hosted the Olympic surfing contest in 2024."],"q":{"q":"What did Captain Cook come to Tahiti to observe in 1769?","a":"The transit of Venus","x":["A total solar eclipse","Halley's Comet"]}},"Q188705":{"t":"The Valley Isle, crowned by Haleakalā's vast crater","s":"Maui is two volcanoes joined by a low isthmus, which earned it the name the Valley Isle. The big eastern one, Haleakalā, rises to 3,055 m, and its summit holds an enormous depression dotted with rust- and ochre-coloured cinder cones, easy to pick out on a clear day. The older West Maui Mountains are carved into sharp green ridges and valleys.","w":"Two lumps joined by a flat neck: the huge, often cloud-ringed dome of Haleakalā to the east, sharp green ridges to the west.","f":["Haleakalā's summit depression is about 11 km long and 800 m deep.","In winter, humpback whales gather to breed in the channels between Maui, Lānaʻi and Molokaʻi.","Haleakalā means 'house of the sun' in Hawaiian."],"q":{"q":"What does 'Haleakalā' mean in Hawaiian?","a":"House of the sun","x":["Mountain of fire","White mountain"]}},"Q208198":{"t":"Fiji's main island: lush in the east, golden in the west","s":"Viti Levu is the largest island of Fiji, roughly round, with rugged forested mountains in the middle. The weather splits it in two: the south-east, around Suva, catches the trade-wind rain and stays dark green, while the drier north-west is a patchwork of sugar-cane fields and pale grassland. Coral reefs and lagoons fringe much of the coast.","w":"A roundish, mountainous island: dark rainforest in the south-east, paler golden cane country in the north-west, reefs along the shore.","f":["Mount Tomanivi, 1,324 m, is the highest point in Fiji.","The Sigatoka Sand Dunes on the south coast became Fiji's first national park.","Most international flights to Fiji land at Nadi, on the island's dry western side."],"q":{"q":"Which city on Viti Levu is Fiji's capital?","a":"Suva","x":["Nadi","Lautoka"]}},"Q205695":{"t":"Home of the dodo, and an 'underwater waterfall'","s":"Three volcanic islands lie in a line east of Madagascar: Réunion, Mauritius and little Rodrigues. Réunion is the tallest and wildest, its highest peak topping 3,000 m, with one of the world's most active volcanoes on its south-east side. Mauritius is lower and ringed by a turquoise lagoon; off its south-west corner, sand pouring down an undersea slope looks from the air like a waterfall.","w":"Réunion's high, round, cloud-capped peaks; lower Mauritius to the north-east, wrapped in a wide turquoise lagoon.","f":["The islands are named after the Portuguese navigator Pedro Mascarenhas.","The dodo, a flightless pigeon, lived only on Mauritius and was extinct by the late 1600s.","Piton des Neiges on Réunion, 3,069 m, is the highest point in the Indian Ocean."],"q":{"q":"On which island did the dodo live?","a":"Mauritius","x":["Réunion","Madagascar"]}},"Q192281":{"t":"The last large land to be put on the world map","s":"Severnaya Zemlya was the last sizeable land on Earth to be discovered: a Russian expedition sighted it in 1913, and it was not properly mapped until 1930–32. From above it is a cluster of big islands where smooth ice caps cover about half the land, their edges calving into the sea, and the rest is bare brown polar desert. Sea ice locks it in for most of the year.","w":"Big islands under smooth white ice domes, bare brown ground at their edges and frozen straits between, north of the Taymyr Peninsula.","f":["The Academy of Sciences Ice Cap on Komsomolets Island is the largest ice cap in Russia.","Georgy Ushakov and Nikolay Urvantsev mapped the archipelago by dog sled in 1930–32.","Cape Arktichesky, its northern tip, is a common starting point for North Pole expeditions."],"q":{"q":"In which year was Severnaya Zemlya discovered?","a":"1913","x":["1878","1596"]}},"Q1249802":{"t":"Windswept, treeless islands full of penguins","s":"Two main islands and hundreds of small ones lie in the South Atlantic east of Patagonia: low, treeless and brown-green under a big sky. The coast is torn into long inlets and sounds. Look for pale streaks on the hillsides — these are stone runs, rivers of boulders left by ice-age frost. Along the shores live huge colonies of penguins and albatrosses.","w":"Two large, low, treeless islands with deeply indented coasts and pale streaks of boulder fields on the hills.","f":["Five species of penguin breed here, including king, gentoo and rockhopper penguins.","About 70% of the world's black-browed albatrosses nest on these islands.","There are no native trees; the land is covered in grass, heath and peat."],"q":{"q":"What are the Falklands' 'stone runs'?","a":"Rivers of boulders","x":["Ancient stone walls","Old lava flows"]}},"Q25231":{"t":"Pointed mountains, flowing glaciers and a vault of seeds","s":"Svalbard is where the Arctic looks most dramatic from the air: dark, sharp-crested mountains, glaciers flowing between them like frozen rivers, and ice fronts calving into fjords. Around 60% of the land lies under ice. Willem Barentsz named the main island Spitsbergen — 'pointed mountains' — when he sighted it in 1596.","w":"Jagged dark peaks separated by broad white glaciers, fjords ending in ice cliffs, and sea ice to the north and east.","f":["Longyearbyen is the northernmost town on Earth with more than 1,000 inhabitants.","The Global Seed Vault, dug into a mountain near Longyearbyen, stores seeds from all over the world.","The midnight sun shines here from about late April to late August."],"q":{"q":"What does the name 'Spitsbergen' mean?","a":"Pointed mountains","x":["White coast","Ice fjords"]}},"Q185018":{"t":"Europe's northernmost islands, locked in pack ice","s":"Franz Josef Land is a scatter of nearly 200 islands high in the Arctic, most of them buried under ice caps, with dark basalt cliffs showing at their edges. From above the ice domes look smooth and rounded, and the channels between them stay frozen for much of the year. Cape Fligely on Rudolf Island is the northernmost point of Europe, about 900 km from the Pole.","w":"Dozens of islands under smooth, rounded ice caps, with dark cliffs at their edges and frozen channels between them.","f":["An Austro-Hungarian expedition discovered the islands in 1873 and named them after Emperor Franz Joseph.","Fridtjof Nansen and Hjalmar Johansen spent the winter of 1895–96 here in a stone hut.","Champ Island is famous for its strange, almost perfectly round stone balls."],"q":{"q":"Which emperor gave his name to Franz Josef Land?","a":"Franz Joseph I of Austria","x":["Wilhelm II of Germany","Alexander II of Russia"]}},"Q80413":{"t":"A 1,000-km wall of ice and rock between two seas","s":"Novaya Zemlya is a long, curved barrier, nearly 1,000 km from end to end, dividing the Barents Sea from the Kara Sea — the Ural Mountains carried on out into the Arctic. Its two main islands are separated by the narrow Matochkin Shar strait. The southern one is tundra and bare hills; the northern one is largely buried under a vast ice cap whose glaciers break off into the sea.","w":"A very long, narrow, curving pair of islands: brown tundra in the south, a continuous white ice cap in the north.","f":["The northern island's ice cap, about 20,000 km², is one of the largest in Europe.","Willem Barentsz and his crew spent the winter of 1596–97 near its northern tip, in a hut of driftwood.","Matochkin Shar, the strait between the two main islands, is about 100 km long."],"q":{"q":"Which two seas does Novaya Zemlya separate?","a":"The Barents and Kara seas","x":["The Kara and Laptev seas","The Barents and White seas"]}},"Q3803":{"t":"Volcano after volcano, from Bali to Timor","s":"East of Java the islands grow smaller and drier — Bali, Lombok, Sumbawa, Flores and on towards Timor — and most are crowned by volcanoes. Some peaks hold striking crater lakes: Rinjani on Lombok cradles a blue crescent of water with a young cone rising inside it. Farther east, green gives way to brown savanna, where Komodo dragons still roam a few islands.","w":"A west–east chain of volcanic islands, greener in the west and browner in the east; look for crater lakes and calderas.","f":["On Flores, the three crater lakes of Kelimutu each have a different colour, and the colours shift over time.","Tambora, on Sumbawa, was left with a caldera about 6 km wide by its great eruption of 1815.","Remains of Homo floresiensis, a tiny extinct human nicknamed the 'hobbit', were found on Flores in 2003."],"q":{"q":"Which Lesser Sunda island has three crater lakes of different colours?","a":"Flores","x":["Lombok","Sumba"]}},"Q215664":{"t":"Hundreds of reefs scattered across the South China Sea","s":"Across the southern South China Sea lie well over a hundred reefs, atolls and sandy cays, many of them awash at high tide. From above they appear as faint turquoise smudges and pale rings on deep blue, with only a few specks of real land among them. Old British charts marked this whole area with a blunt warning: 'Dangerous Ground'.","w":"Faint turquoise rings and patches of reef strewn across dark-blue sea, with very little dry land.","f":["The islands are named after Richard Spratly, a British whaling captain who sighted one of them in 1843.","All their natural dry land put together covers only about 2 km²."],"q":{"q":"How did old British charts label the Spratly area?","a":"Dangerous Ground","x":["Treacherous Shoals","Coral Maze"]}},"Q201012":{"t":"Arctic islands made of ice, sand and mammoth bones","s":"The New Siberian Islands lie low and flat between the Laptev and East Siberian seas, often impossible to tell apart from the sea ice around them. In summer they show as brown tundra and grey sand, with lakes and crumbling cliffs of ancient ground ice. The frozen soil holds so many mammoth bones and tusks that ivory hunters have come here for more than two centuries.","w":"Low, flat, brown and grey islands with many lakes, blending into the surrounding sea ice for much of the year.","f":["Bunge Land, between Kotelny and Faddeyevsky islands, is a low sandy plain that floods during storms.","In 1811 Yakov Sannikov reported a mysterious land to the north; 'Sannikov Land' was never found.","Kotelny is the largest island of the group."],"q":{"q":"Which phantom land was reported north of the New Siberian Islands?","a":"Sannikov Land","x":["Crocker Land","Bunge Land"]}},"Q128011":{"t":"The Caribbean's big islands, from Cuba to Puerto Rico","s":"The Greater Antilles are the heavyweights of the Caribbean: Cuba, Hispaniola, Jamaica and Puerto Rico, lined up from west to east. From above they are large, green and mountainous, edged by pale sand and turquoise shallows, while the Atlantic to the north falls away to deep, dark blue. Cuba, the largest, stretches some 1,200 km from end to end.","w":"A west–east line of big green islands: long, narrow Cuba, then mountainous Hispaniola, with Jamaica to the south and Puerto Rico last.","f":["Cuba is the largest island in the Caribbean, at about 105,000 km².","North of Puerto Rico, the Puerto Rico Trench plunges to about 8,400 m, the deepest point of the Atlantic.","Jamaica's Blue Mountains, rising to about 2,250 m, gave their name to a famous coffee."],"q":{"q":"Which is the largest island in the Caribbean?","a":"Cuba","x":["Hispaniola","Jamaica"]}},"Q21713615":{"t":"Turquoise banks so bright they seem lit from below","s":"In the Bahamas the water steals the show. The islands sit on vast limestone banks only a few metres deep, which glow an almost unreal turquoise, rippled with sand patterns. Cutting into the Great Bahama Bank is the Tongue of the Ocean, a deep trench whose ink-blue water ends abruptly against the pale shallows — one of the sharpest colour lines you will see from a plane.","w":"Huge areas of glowing pale-turquoise shallows with sandy ripples, low islands along their edges, and sharp lines where deep blue begins.","f":["Columbus made his first landfall in the Americas here, in October 1492.","The country counts about 700 islands and more than 2,000 small cays.","Dean's Blue Hole, off Long Island, drops about 200 m, one of the deepest known."],"q":{"q":"In which year did Columbus make his first landfall in the Bahamas?","a":"1492","x":["1498","1502"]}},"Q93259":{"t":"A curved necklace of islands closing off the Caribbean","s":"The Lesser Antilles curve for some 800 km from the Virgin Islands down to Grenada, dividing the Caribbean from the Atlantic. Most are steep, green volcanic islands, each a peak with a skirt of reef; Guadeloupe is shaped like a butterfly. On a clear day, look on Saint Lucia's west coast for the Pitons, two sharp volcanic spires standing side by side at the water's edge.","w":"A curving chain of small, steep, green volcanic islands running north to south, with the open Atlantic to the east.","f":["The inner islands are volcanic; outer ones such as Antigua and Barbados are flatter and made of limestone.","Kick 'em Jenny, north of Grenada, is an active volcano still growing beneath the sea.","The Pitons of Saint Lucia are a UNESCO World Heritage site."],"q":{"q":"Which island is famous for two volcanic spires called the Pitons?","a":"Saint Lucia","x":["Dominica","Grenada"]}},"Q59771":{"t":"A crescent of rainforest and steep volcanic cones","s":"New Britain is a long, curving crescent about 500 km from end to end, the largest island of the Bismarck Archipelago. Along its north coast stands a line of volcanoes; the most striking is Ulawun, a steep, symmetrical cone that is also one of the most active in Papua New Guinea. Inland, the limestone Nakanai Mountains hide giant sinkholes and underground rivers.","w":"A long crescent-shaped island of dark rainforest with volcanic cones along its north coast, one of them tall and symmetrical.","f":["Ulawun, about 2,300 m, is one of the Decade Volcanoes chosen for special study.","It is Papua New Guinea's largest island after New Guinea itself.","Some sinkholes in the Nakanai Mountains are hundreds of metres deep."],"q":{"q":"Which active volcano stands on New Britain's north coast?","a":"Ulawun","x":["Bagana","Manam"]}},"Q17044696":{"t":"The country that gave the world the word 'atoll'","s":"The Maldives look like turquoise beads scattered on dark-blue velvet: 26 atolls in a double chain some 870 km long, each a ring of reefs and tiny islands around a pale lagoon. Many islands are just a strip of white sand and palms holding a single resort or village. The capital, Malé, fills its small island edge to edge with buildings.","w":"Rings of pale-turquoise reef and dots of white sand on deep-blue ocean, arranged in a long north–south double chain.","f":["'Atoll' comes from 'atholhu', a word in Dhivehi, the Maldivian language.","The Maldives is the world's lowest-lying country: its natural ground rises only about 2.4 m at most.","The country has about 1,200 coral islands, fewer than 200 of them inhabited."],"q":{"q":"Which English word comes from the Maldivian language?","a":"Atoll","x":["Lagoon","Reef"]}},"Q25263":{"t":"Green volcanic islands in the middle of the Atlantic","s":"Nine volcanic islands lie scattered across the mid-Atlantic, about 1,500 km west of Lisbon. From above they are intensely green, patched with small fields, with lakes lying in old craters. The unmistakable one is Pico: a near-perfect cone 2,351 m high, Portugal's tallest mountain, whose summit often pokes out above a sea of cloud.","w":"Small green islands in open ocean; a single tall dark cone on Pico, and round crater lakes on São Miguel.","f":["The Azores sit where the Eurasian, North American and African plates meet.","In the Sete Cidades caldera on São Miguel, two joined lakes show different colours — one blue, one green.","The Azores High, a zone of high pressure named after the islands, shapes much of Europe's summer weather."],"q":{"q":"What is the highest mountain in Portugal?","a":"Pico","x":["Torre, Serra da Estrela","Pico Ruivo"]}},"Q6980542":{"t":"An island you can barely tell from the ice around it","s":"Along the coast of West Antarctica, the Getz Ice Shelf holds a string of islands locked into its floating ice. Wright Island, about 65 km long, is one of them. Completely buried under ice, it shows only as a broad, gentle swell above the flat shelf, easiest to spot when a low sun casts shadows. To the north lies the Amundsen Sea, often choked with sea ice.","w":"A long, smooth white swell rising slightly above the flat ice shelf, with ice-covered sea to the north.","f":["It was first mapped from air photos taken during the US Navy's Operation Highjump in January 1947.","It is named after Admiral Jerauld Wright, in overall command of Operation Deep Freeze in 1957–58.","The Getz Ice Shelf, which holds the island, stretches for more than 500 km along the coast."],"q":{"q":"Which ice shelf holds Wright Island?","a":"The Getz Ice Shelf","x":["The Ross Ice Shelf","The Ronne Ice Shelf"]}},"Q1543171":{"t":"An ice island found by an icebreaker in 1962","s":"Grant Island is one of several islands frozen into the Getz Ice Shelf off Marie Byrd Land, in West Antarctica. About 37 km long and wholly ice-covered, it is hemmed in by the shelf on every side except the north, where a strait opens towards the sea. From above it is a smooth white hump, its outline given away mainly by shading along its slopes.","w":"A smooth white hump rising from the flat ice shelf, with a strait of open water or sea ice along its northern side.","f":["Sailors aboard the icebreaker USS Glacier discovered and charted it on 4 February 1962.","It is named after Commander E. G. Grant, the ship's captain at the time.","The smaller Shepard Island lies only about 9 km to the west."],"q":{"q":"Which ship's crew discovered Grant Island in 1962?","a":"USS Glacier","x":["USS Burton Island","HMS Endurance"]}},"Q6272638":{"t":"A lonely ice island off the Ruppert Coast","s":"On the Ruppert Coast of Marie Byrd Land, far from any regular flight path, the Nickerson Ice Shelf holds a handful of ice-covered islands. Newman Island is about 28 km long and completely buried. From above it looks less like an island than a low, rounded bulge in the ice, with glaciers flowing down from the mainland to the south.","w":"A low, rounded white bulge within a flat ice shelf, with glaciers descending from the land to the south.","f":["It was mapped from US surveys and Navy air photographs taken between 1959 and 1965.","It is named after Commander J. F. Newman of the US Navy, who served on Operation Deep Freeze in 1966."],"q":{"q":"Which ice shelf holds Newman Island?","a":"The Nickerson Ice Shelf","x":["The Getz Ice Shelf","The Ross Ice Shelf"]}},"Q559842":{"t":"An ice-bound island in the shadow of Mount Siple","s":"Dean Island lies in the middle of the Getz Ice Shelf, off the coast of Marie Byrd Land, between Grant Island and Siple Island. It is about 37 km long and 19 km wide, and so thoroughly covered in ice that from above it merges almost seamlessly with the shelf. The landmark nearby is Mount Siple, a huge volcanic dome on the neighbouring island, rising above 3,000 m.","w":"A broad, low white rise within the flat ice shelf; to the north-east, the tall snowy dome of Mount Siple.","f":["It is one of several islands embedded in the Getz Ice Shelf, which runs for more than 500 km along the coast.","Mount Siple, on neighbouring Siple Island, is a shield volcano about 3,100 m high."],"q":{"q":"Which two islands does Dean Island lie between?","a":"Grant and Siple","x":["Shepard and Grant","Carney and Wright"]}},"Q33788":{"t":"A long mountain island inside a vast turquoise lagoon","s":"Grande Terre, New Caledonia's main island, is about 400 km long and only some 50 km wide: a mountainous spine marked by red scars where nickel has been mined. Around it spreads one of the largest lagoons on Earth, bright turquoise and closed off by a barrier reef that traces a thin white line of surf far offshore.","w":"A long, narrow, mountainous island with reddish bare patches, inside a wide turquoise lagoon edged by a distant line of surf.","f":["Captain Cook named it in 1774 because its hills reminded him of Scotland.","Its lagoons and reefs have been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 2008.","The kagu, a grey, nearly flightless bird that barks like a dog, lives only here."],"q":{"q":"Which metal is mined in New Caledonia's red hills?","a":"Nickel","x":["Copper","Gold"]}},"Q712":{"t":"More than 300 islands astride the 180th meridian","s":"Fiji is a scatter of more than 300 islands, from two big, mountainous ones to countless specks ringed by reef. From above the colours are the show: deep-blue ocean, turquoise lagoons and white lines of surf where the reefs break the swell. The 180th meridian passes through the islands, but the date line bends around them so the whole country keeps the same day.","w":"Two large green islands and a scatter of small ones, all fringed by turquoise lagoons and white lines of breaking surf.","f":["Only about a third of the islands are inhabited.","The Great Sea Reef off Vanua Levu is one of the longest barrier reefs in the world.","Viti Levu and Vanua Levu together hold most of the land and nearly all of the people."],"q":{"q":"Which is Fiji's largest island?","a":"Viti Levu","x":["Vanua Levu","Taveuni"]}},"ne-rg-1730072401":{"t":"The Friendly Islands, beside one of the deepest trenches","s":"Tonga is a double line of islands running north to south. On the east lie low coral islands such as flat Tongatapu, ringed by reef; on the west runs a string of volcanoes, some of them little more than peaks breaking the surface. Captain Cook called them the Friendly Islands. East of the chain, the sea floor plunges into the Tonga Trench, one of the deepest places on Earth.","w":"Flat, reef-ringed coral islands in the east, a parallel line of small volcanic islands to the west, and deep ocean beyond.","f":["The Tonga Trench reaches about 10,800 m at the Horizon Deep, the second-deepest point in the oceans.","Tonga is the only kingdom in the Pacific, ruled by its own monarchs right through the colonial era.","In the Vava'u group, humpback whales come to give birth between July and October."],"q":{"q":"What did Captain Cook call Tonga?","a":"The Friendly Islands","x":["The Society Islands","The Navigator Islands"]}},"Q700048":{"t":"The 'sunrise' chain, home of the Marshalls' capital","s":"Ratak means 'sunrise', and this is the eastern of the Marshall Islands' two chains: a line of coral atolls stretching for several hundred kilometres across the ocean. From above each is a narrow necklace of islets around a turquoise lagoon. Majuro, the national capital, lies near the southern end — a thin, crowded ribbon of land curling around a lagoon many kilometres across.","w":"A line of thin rings of land around turquoise lagoons, strung north to south across deep-blue ocean.","f":["On Majuro, the strip of inhabited land is often only a few hundred metres wide.","Bokak, the northernmost atoll, is dry and uninhabited, a haven for seabirds."],"q":{"q":"What does 'Ratak' mean?","a":"Sunrise","x":["Sunset","Big lagoon"]}},"Q697840":{"t":"The 'sunset' chain and its giant lagoons","s":"Ralik means 'sunset', and this is the western of the Marshall Islands' two parallel chains of atolls. Its best-known member is Kwajalein, whose nearly 100 islets enclose one of the largest lagoons in the world, over 2,000 km² of calm turquoise water. From above, the islets look like a broken bracelet dropped on the ocean.","w":"Big rings of tiny islets around vast turquoise lagoons, scattered along a line west of the Ratak chain.","f":["Kwajalein's lagoon covers about 2,170 km².","The bikini swimsuit, launched in 1946, took its name from Bikini Atoll in this chain."],"q":{"q":"Which garment is named after an atoll in the Ralik Chain?","a":"The bikini","x":["The sarong","The muumuu"]}},"Q11826289":{"t":"Rarotonga's green peaks and Aitutaki's glowing lagoon","s":"The southern Cooks hold most of the country's people and its most famous views. Rarotonga is a high volcanic island of jagged green peaks, ringed by a reef and by a coastal road only about 32 km long. To the north, Aitutaki's lagoon is a vast triangle of luminous turquoise dotted with tiny islets; to the south-east, Mangaia is rimmed by cliffs of ancient raised coral.","w":"Rarotonga: a small round island of sharp green peaks inside a reef; to the north, Aitutaki's huge triangular turquoise lagoon.","f":["Mangaia, formed about 18 million years ago, is one of the oldest islands in the Pacific.","Cook Islanders are also citizens of New Zealand."],"q":{"q":"Which southern Cook island is famous for its turquoise lagoon?","a":"Aitutaki","x":["Mangaia","Atiu"]}},"Q244978":{"t":"Perfumed volcanic islands between Africa and Madagascar","s":"The Comoros lie at the northern end of the Mozambique Channel: four volcanic islands that get older from west to east. The biggest, Grande Comore, is dominated by Karthala, a broad active volcano with a huge crater at its summit and dark lava flows on its flanks. At the eastern end Mayotte, the oldest, is wrapped in a wide lagoon behind a barrier reef.","w":"Four dark-green volcanic islands in a row: the western one topped by a vast crater, the eastern one inside a turquoise lagoon.","f":["Karthala, about 2,360 m, has erupted more than 20 times since the 19th century.","Coelacanths, fish once thought to have died out with the dinosaurs, live in the deep water off these islands.","The islands are one of the world's main sources of ylang-ylang oil, used in perfumes."],"q":{"q":"Which 'living fossil' fish lives in deep water off the Comoros?","a":"The coelacanth","x":["The lungfish","The sturgeon"]}},"ne-rg-1730073253":{"t":"800 forested islands where sea nomads still sail","s":"Off Myanmar's southern coast some 800 islands scatter across the Andaman Sea, most of them hilly, cloaked in forest and edged with white beaches and mangroves. There are few towns and little development, so from above you see mostly green islands, pale sand and clear water in every shade of blue. The Moken, sea nomads who spend much of the year in boats, have lived among them for centuries.","w":"Hundreds of steep, forested islands with white beaches, scattered along the coast of southern Myanmar.","f":["The archipelago was largely closed to foreign visitors until the late 1990s.","Lampi Island lies at the heart of Myanmar's only marine national park."],"q":{"q":"What are the Moken of the Mergui Archipelago known as?","a":"Sea nomads","x":["Mountain herders","Salt traders"]}},"Q5765":{"t":"Mountains, coves and water so clear the seabed shows","s":"The Balearics lie off eastern Spain: big Mallorca in the middle, gentler Menorca to the north-east, Ibiza and tiny Formentera to the south-west. Mallorca's north-west coast is a wall of limestone mountains, the Serra de Tramuntana, above deep-blue sea. Around Formentera the water is so clear that underwater meadows of seagrass show as dark patches in the turquoise shallows.","w":"A large island with a mountain ridge along its north-west coast, a flatter one to the north-east, two more to the south-west.","f":["Mallorca's Serra de Tramuntana is a UNESCO World Heritage site.","A seagrass meadow off Formentera may be one of the oldest living things on Earth, possibly tens of thousands of years old.","The whole of Menorca is a UNESCO biosphere reserve."],"q":{"q":"Which is the largest of the Balearic Islands?","a":"Mallorca","x":["Menorca","Ibiza"]}},"ne-rg-1730073309":{"t":"Bare Arctic islands along the Northwest Passage","s":"The Parry Islands form a broad band of Arctic islands along the northern side of the Northwest Passage, from Cornwallis in the east to Prince Patrick in the west. From above they are brown and grey, flat-topped and bare, scored by dry valleys and fringed with sea ice for most of the year. In 1819 William Parry's ships sailed farther west through these channels than anyone before.","w":"Big, bare, brown-grey islands with flat uplands and dry valleys, surrounded by sea ice most of the year.","f":["Parry's ships spent the winter of 1819–20 frozen in at Winter Harbour, on Melville Island.","By sailing past 110° west, Parry's crews won a £5,000 prize offered by the British Parliament.","Resolute, on Cornwallis Island, is one of the northernmost communities in Canada."],"q":{"q":"Who are the Parry Islands named after?","a":"William Edward Parry","x":["John Franklin","John Ross"]}},"Q741243":{"t":"An island the size of Denmark, with no sea in sight","s":"Berkner Island is an island mostly on the map: it is surrounded not by open water but by the floating Filchner–Ronne Ice Shelf, and buried under ice itself. From above it appears as a vast, gentle dome rising from the flat shelf, some 320 km long and less than a kilometre high. It splits the shelf in two, with the Ronne to the west and the Filchner to the east.","w":"A huge, gently rounded ice dome rising above the perfectly flat ice shelf that surrounds it on every side.","f":["At about 44,000 km², it is roughly the size of Denmark.","It was discovered in 1957–58 by a US party from Ellsworth Station, led by Finn Ronne.","It is named after the physicist Lloyd Berkner, a radio engineer on Byrd's 1928–30 Antarctic expedition."],"q":{"q":"Which two ice shelves does Berkner Island separate?","a":"Ronne and Filchner","x":["Ross and Ronne","Filchner and Brunt"]}},"Q65":{"t":"A city poured into a basin between peaks and the Pacific","s":"Los Angeles doesn't so much end as run out of room: its sprawl fills a broad basin from the Pacific shore to the foot of the San Gabriel Mountains, then spills into the valleys beyond. In winter those peaks can wear snow above a city of palm trees. It all began in 1781 as a small Spanish farming pueblo.","w":"A vast grey-beige sprawl between mountains and Santa Monica Bay; the long harbour breakwaters of Long Beach to the south.","f":["The Los Angeles River runs mostly in a concrete channel, lined from the 1930s onward to tame its floods.","The neighbouring ports of Los Angeles and Long Beach together form the busiest container port complex in the US.","The highest of the San Gabriels, Mount San Antonio (about 3,070 m), is known locally as Mount Baldy."],"q":{"q":"Which mountain range walls in Los Angeles to the north?","a":"The San Gabriel Mountains","x":["The Sierra Nevada","The Cascade Range"]}},"Q61":{"t":"A capital drawn on paper first, then built on the Potomac","s":"Washington was planned before it existed: in 1791 Pierre L'Enfant laid broad diagonal avenues over a street grid on farmland beside the Potomac. The district began as a square ten miles a side, set on its corner like a diamond. Virginia took back its share in 1846, so today the river bites off the south-west corner.","w":"Where the Anacostia meets the broad Potomac; a low city without a skyscraper cluster, Reagan National airport right on the riverbank.","f":["Forty stone markers were set along the original diamond-shaped boundary in 1791–92; many still stand.","Congress first met in Washington in 1800.","A law of 1910 caps building heights, which is why the city looks so strikingly low."],"q":{"q":"Which river forms Washington's south-western edge?","a":"The Potomac","x":["The Delaware","The Hudson"]}},"Q60":{"t":"Look for the green rectangle in the middle of the island","s":"Find Manhattan — a long, thin island between the Hudson and the East River — and in its middle a neat green rectangle: Central Park, about four kilometres long. Around it the street grid laid out in 1811 runs block after block up the island. The Dutch called this port New Amsterdam until the English took it in 1664 and renamed it after the Duke of York.","w":"A narrow island pointing south into a wide harbour, a green rectangle at its heart, long bridges fanning out to the east.","f":["Central Park measures about 4 km by 0.8 km — big enough to pick out from cruise on a clear day.","The Verrazzano-Narrows Bridge at the harbour mouth had the world's longest suspension span when it opened in 1964.","Manhattan's street grid was fixed by a plan of 1811, long before most of the island was built up."],"q":{"q":"What was New York called before 1664?","a":"New Amsterdam","x":["New Rotterdam","Fort Orange"]}},"Q1489":{"t":"A megacity on a lake that isn't there any more","s":"The grey-brown sea of roofs below fills an old lake bed 2,240 m above sea level. The Aztecs founded Tenochtitlan on an island in Lake Texcoco around 1325; the Spanish later drained most of the water. The clay beneath is still settling, and parts of the city sink every year. On a clear day, look south-east for two snow-capped volcanoes.","w":"A vast city filling a flat high valley ringed by mountains; Popocatépetl and Iztaccíhuatl rise to the south-east.","f":["Popocatépetl, about 5,400 m high, is an active volcano and often trails a plume of steam.","In places the city sinks by more than 20 cm a year as the old lake clay dries out.","Iztaccíhuatl means 'white woman' in Nahuatl, and its long ridge is said to look like a sleeping figure."],"q":{"q":"What did Mexico City's centre stand on in Aztec times?","a":"An island in a lake","x":["A volcanic crater","A river delta"]}},"Q2841":{"t":"A capital at 2,640 m, pressed against a green mountain wall","s":"Bogotá lies on a high, flat savanna in the Andes, about 2,640 m up, with a steep ridge of hills along its eastern edge. The city fills the plain westward from that wall. Look around it for glinting white patches: greenhouses of the flower farms that make Colombia one of the world's great exporters of cut flowers.","w":"A large city on a flat green plateau, hard against a dark ridge to the east; white greenhouse roofs scattered across the fields.","f":["The Spanish conquistador Gonzalo Jiménez de Quesada founded the city in 1538.","The legend of El Dorado grew from a Muisca ritual at Lake Guatavita, about 50 km north-east of the city.","Monserrate, the hill above the old town, rises to about 3,150 m."],"q":{"q":"At which lake did the El Dorado legend begin?","a":"Lake Guatavita","x":["Lake Titicaca","Lake Tota"]}},"Q2887":{"t":"A city in a bowl, with the Andes rising straight behind","s":"Santiago fills a broad valley between the low Coastal Range and the Andes, which climb to snowy peaks over 5,000 m just east of the suburbs. In winter a haze often pools in the bowl while the summits shine above it. Pedro de Valdivia founded the city in 1541 beside a rocky hill on the Mapocho River.","w":"A large city on a flat valley floor at the foot of the Andes' snowy wall; the thin line of the Mapocho River across it.","f":["Aconcagua, at 6,961 m the highest peak outside Asia, stands about 100 km north-east of the city.","Cerro Santa Lucía, the hill where the city was founded, is now a park in the very centre.","The border with Argentina follows the crest of the Andes less than 100 km from the city."],"q":{"q":"Who founded Santiago in 1541?","a":"Pedro de Valdivia","x":["Francisco Pizarro","Diego de Almagro"]}},"Q8678":{"t":"Green granite domes rising straight out of the sea","s":"Few cities are so tangled up with rock: steep, forested granite domes rise between the neighbourhoods and straight out of Guanabara Bay. Portuguese sailors arrived in January 1502 and took the bay for a river mouth — hence 'River of January'. From 1808 to 1821 the Portuguese court lived here, a European monarchy ruling its empire from the Americas.","w":"A wide bay with a narrow mouth, green domes of rock among the buildings, and long pale beaches facing the open Atlantic.","f":["The Rio–Niterói Bridge crosses Guanabara Bay for about 13 km; from cruise it's a fine line over the water.","Much of the Tijuca Forest above the city was replanted by hand from the 1860s on old coffee land.","Sugarloaf Mountain rises about 396 m straight from the water at the mouth of the bay."],"q":{"q":"Which bay does the Rio–Niterói Bridge cross?","a":"Guanabara Bay","x":["All Saints Bay","Paranaguá Bay"]}},"Q174":{"t":"The Southern Hemisphere's biggest city, up on a plateau","s":"From the window São Paulo looks endless: grey and white rooftops to the horizon, stitched together by rivers in straight concrete channels. It sits on a plateau about 760 m up, only some 70 km from the Atlantic, behind the steep forested wall of the Serra do Mar. Jesuit missionaries founded it as a small mission in 1554.","w":"A boundless grey city; large dark reservoirs on its southern edge, the green escarpment and the coast beyond to the south-east.","f":["The Tietê River rises near the coast yet flows inland, over 1,000 km, to join the Paraná.","The Billings and Guarapiranga reservoirs on the southern edge are easy to spot from cruise.","The coffee trade of the 1800s turned a modest hill town into Brazil's economic engine."],"q":{"q":"Which way does the Tietê River flow from São Paulo?","a":"Inland, towards the Paraná","x":["East, to the Atlantic at Santos","North, to the Amazon"]}},"Q62":{"t":"A hilly peninsula tip where fog pours through the Gate","s":"San Francisco fills the tip of a peninsula, with the Pacific on one side and a great bay on the other, joined by the narrow Golden Gate. On summer afternoons you may see fog pouring through that gap like a slow white river. The gold rush of 1849 turned a village of about a thousand people into a boom town within a couple of years.","w":"A peninsula tip between ocean and bay; thin bridge lines across the water; bright red and green salt ponds far to the south.","f":["The salt ponds of the south bay glow red, orange and green, coloured by salt-loving microorganisms.","The Golden Gate strait was named in 1846, almost a century before the famous bridge was built.","Mission San Francisco de Asís, the city's Spanish seed, was founded in 1776."],"q":{"q":"What colours the salt ponds of south San Francisco Bay?","a":"Salt-loving microorganisms","x":["Iron-rich clay","Copper minerals"]}},"Q16554":{"t":"Where the Great Plains run into the Rockies","s":"Denver marks the line where the flat country stops: to the east the plains roll away for a thousand kilometres, to the west the Front Range rises in a wall of peaks over 4,000 m. The city grew from a gold-rush camp of 1858 and sits about a mile above sea level — hence its nickname, the Mile High City.","w":"A city on flat tan plains at the very foot of the Rockies; its huge airport stands alone far out to the north-east.","f":["Denver International is the largest airport in North America by land area, about 135 km².","The city was named after James W. Denver, governor of Kansas Territory, which then included the site.","Longs Peak, about 4,350 m high, is the prominent summit on the north-western skyline."],"q":{"q":"What brought the first settlers to Denver in 1858?","a":"A gold rush","x":["A new railway line","A silver strike"]}},"Q16555":{"t":"Bayous, refineries and a ship channel on a flat plain","s":"Houston spreads across a coastal plain so flat that its slow brown bayous wander in loops through the suburbs. Look south-east for the Ship Channel, a dredged waterway of about 80 km lined with refineries and tanks, running out to Galveston Bay. Founded in 1836, the city was named after Sam Houston, hero of Texan independence.","w":"Endless flat sprawl ringed by freeway loops; to the south-east, a waterway lined with industry leading to a wide bay.","f":["Houston is famous as the largest US city without a formal zoning code.","NASA's Mission Control for crewed spaceflight is in the city's south-eastern suburbs.","At night the refineries along the Ship Channel glitter as a long, dense band of light."],"q":{"q":"Who is Houston named after?","a":"Sam Houston","x":["Stephen F. Austin","Davy Crockett"]}},"Q8652":{"t":"A thin city between the Everglades and turquoise water","s":"Miami is a long, narrow city pinned between two very different edges: the pale green marshes of the Everglades to the west and the turquoise shallows of Biscayne Bay to the east. Beyond the bay runs the thin barrier island of Miami Beach. The city was incorporated only in 1896, just after Henry Flagler's railway arrived.","w":"A strip of city along the coast; a straight line where streets stop and marsh begins; a bright ribbon of island offshore.","f":["Much of Miami lies only a couple of metres above sea level, on porous limestone.","Miami Beach was largely mangrove swamp before it was cleared and built up in the 1910s and 1920s.","The chain of small Venetian Islands across Biscayne Bay is largely man-made."],"q":{"q":"Which wetland lies directly west of Miami?","a":"The Everglades","x":["The Okefenokee Swamp","The Atchafalaya Basin"]}},"Q23556":{"t":"Towers in a forest, beside a giant of an airport","s":"Atlanta looks surprisingly green from above: a thick canopy of trees hides most of its suburbs, with clusters of towers rising out of it. South of downtown, look for the long parallel runways of Hartsfield–Jackson, for many years the world's busiest airport by passengers. The city began in 1837 as the end point of a railway, simply named Terminus.","w":"Clusters of towers in dense green forest; a huge airport with parallel east–west runways to the south; a pale granite dome to the east.","f":["Stone Mountain, a bare granite dome east of the city, rises about 250 m above the surrounding forest.","The Eastern Continental Divide crosses Atlanta: rain on one side flows to the Atlantic, on the other to the Gulf.","The name Atlanta was adopted in 1845, after a brief spell as Marthasville."],"q":{"q":"What was Atlanta called when it began in 1837?","a":"Terminus","x":["Peachtree","Stone Mountain"]}},"Q1297":{"t":"A perfect grid ruled against a lake like an inland sea","s":"Chicago's edge is one long, straight line: Lake Michigan, so wide that from the ground its far shore lies beyond the horizon. Inland the streets run in a strict grid, and at night it glows orange, block by block, far out across the prairie. The city exists because of a short portage here between the Great Lakes and the Mississippi basin.","w":"A huge city on the south-west shore of Lake Michigan; a ruler-straight lakefront, an even street grid, O'Hare to the north-west.","f":["In 1900 engineers reversed the Chicago River, sending it away from the lake towards the Mississippi.","The name comes from a local Native American word for a wild onion that grew along the river.","Most of Chicago's lakefront is kept as public parks and beaches rather than private land."],"q":{"q":"What did engineers do to the Chicago River in 1900?","a":"Reversed its flow","x":["Filled it in","Dammed it into a lake"]}},"Q1533":{"t":"A valley city, walled off from the sea by one mountain","s":"Caracas stretches for some 20 km along a narrow valley about 900 m up, parallel to the Caribbean coast yet hidden from it. Between city and sea rises the green ridge of El Ávila, over 2,700 m at its highest. So the airport sits on the coast at Maiquetía, on the far side of the mountain. The city was founded in 1567.","w":"A long, narrow city in an east–west valley; a steep green ridge to the north, the blue sea just beyond it.","f":["Simón Bolívar, who led much of South America to independence, was born in Caracas in 1783.","The city's full founding name was Santiago de León de Caracas."],"q":{"q":"What separates Caracas from the Caribbean Sea?","a":"The Ávila mountain ridge","x":["A wide coastal lagoon","A desert plain"]}},"Q2868":{"t":"A capital in the desert, under a lid of grey sea mist","s":"Lima is one of the driest capitals on Earth: a brown city on bare desert that gets barely a couple of centimetres of rain a year. Yet for months it hides under a low, grey sea fog, the garúa. Look for the cliffs where the city drops to the Pacific. Francisco Pizarro founded it in 1535 as the City of Kings.","w":"A vast tan city on a desert coast, edged by sea cliffs; bare brown hills inland and the green thread of the Rímac valley.","f":["For more than two centuries Lima was the capital of a Spanish viceroyalty covering most of Spanish South America.","The port of Callao and the international airport lie just west of the centre, on the same coastal plain.","In winter the garúa can keep the sky grey for weeks while barely wetting the ground."],"q":{"q":"What did Pizarro name Lima when he founded it in 1535?","a":"City of Kings","x":["City of the Sun","New Toledo"]}},"Q81033":{"t":"An industrial city in a ring of jagged grey mountains","s":"Mountains crowd right up to the suburbs of Monterrey, which fills a valley of the Sierra Madre Oriental. The most famous is Cerro de la Silla, whose twin-humped ridge really does look like a saddle. Founded in 1596, the city became Mexico's industrial powerhouse: in 1900 the first large steelworks in Latin America was founded here.","w":"A dense city in a valley hemmed by steep, folded grey ridges; a saddle-shaped mountain on its eastern edge.","f":["Cerro de la Silla rises to about 1,800 m, well over a kilometre above the city.","The Santa Catarina River that crosses the city is dry for most of the year.","The Huasteca Canyon on the western edge cuts deep into folded limestone ridges."],"q":{"q":"Which saddle-shaped mountain is the symbol of Monterrey?","a":"Cerro de la Silla","x":["Pico de Orizaba","Nevado de Toluca"]}},"Q24639":{"t":"A green peninsula city between the inlet and the peaks","s":"Vancouver is all edges: sea on three sides, and snowy Coast Mountains rising almost straight from the northern shore. Look for the dark green thumb of Stanley Park pointing into Burrard Inlet, and the flat, chequered farmland of the Fraser delta to the south, where the airport sits on an island. The city was incorporated in 1886.","w":"A city on a peninsula between two waters; a dark green wooded tip, snowy peaks to the north, flat delta to the south.","f":["Stanley Park covers about 400 hectares — more than New York's Central Park.","The inlet was charted in 1792 by Captain George Vancouver, whose name the city took.","The Canadian Pacific Railway arrived in 1887, making the city Canada's gateway to the Pacific."],"q":{"q":"Which river's delta lies on Vancouver's southern side?","a":"The Fraser","x":["The Columbia","The Skeena"]}},"Q172":{"t":"A lakefront city with islands cupping its harbour","s":"Toronto runs along the north shore of Lake Ontario, and the easiest way to spot it is a string of low green islands just offshore, curling round the harbour. Until a storm broke through in 1858 they were a sandy peninsula. Inland, look for deep wooded ravines snaking through the street grid. Until 1834 the town was called York.","w":"A dense city on the north shore of Lake Ontario; a curl of low islands off downtown, green ravines inland.","f":["One of the islands carries a small city airport, only a stone's throw from downtown.","The name is often traced to a Mohawk word for a place where trees stand in the water.","Toronto's network of wooded ravines is among the most extensive of any city in the world."],"q":{"q":"What was Toronto called before 1834?","a":"York","x":["Kingston","Newark"]}},"Q1486":{"t":"A grid of lights on a brown river as wide as a sea","s":"Buenos Aires faces the Río de la Plata, an estuary so broad that the Uruguayan shore lies roughly 50 km away, out of sight from the waterfront. The water is a milky coffee-brown, stained by silt from the Paraná and Uruguay rivers. Behind the shore, the city's square blocks march out across the flat pampas without a hill in sight.","w":"A vast grid city on the south bank of a wide brown estuary; the green maze of the Paraná Delta to the north-west.","f":["The city was founded twice: first in 1536, then for good by Juan de Garay in 1580.","The Río de la Plata is formed where the Paraná and Uruguay rivers meet.","At night the regular blocks glow like an orange chessboard reaching far inland."],"q":{"q":"Who refounded Buenos Aires for good in 1580?","a":"Juan de Garay","x":["Pedro de Mendoza","Juan Díaz de Solís"]}},"Q36091":{"t":"A city of lakes, split by the young Mississippi","s":"Minneapolis is dotted with lakes, several of them strung together inside the city like blue beads. Through the middle runs the Mississippi, still a modest river here, dropping over St. Anthony Falls, the only natural major waterfall on its whole course. The falls powered the flour mills that, around 1900, made this the milling capital of the world.","w":"A flat city freckled with round lakes; the Mississippi winding through its centre and meeting the Minnesota River by the airport.","f":["Minnesota has more than 11,000 lakes larger than 4 hectares — its '10,000 lakes' slogan undersells it.","St. Paul, the twin city and state capital, lies just downstream on the same river.","The airport sits right beside the confluence of the Mississippi and Minnesota rivers."],"q":{"q":"Which industry made Minneapolis famous around 1900?","a":"Flour milling","x":["Car making","Steelmaking"]}},"Q18094":{"t":"A tropical city between a crater and green fluted ridges","s":"Honolulu is squeezed along Oʻahu's south shore, between the reef-fringed sea and the steep, fluted green ridges of the Koʻolau Range. At its eastern end rises Diamond Head, a crater so round it looks stamped into the coast. The nearest point of mainland North America is roughly 3,800 km away, which makes this one of the remotest big cities on Earth.","w":"A strip of city along an island's south coast; a round crater at the east end, a long runway on the water to the west.","f":["Diamond Head is a volcanic tuff cone; sailors in the 1800s mistook glittering calcite crystals on its slopes for diamonds.","One of the airport's main runways, the Reef Runway, was built on landfill over a coral reef.","The name Honolulu is usually translated from Hawaiian as 'sheltered bay'."],"q":{"q":"What is Diamond Head in Honolulu?","a":"An extinct volcanic crater","x":["A coral island","A sea cliff"]}},"Q5083":{"t":"A city on a narrow neck of land, a giant volcano beyond","s":"Salt water on one side, a long freshwater lake on the other: Seattle occupies a slim strip of hilly land between Puget Sound and Lake Washington. On a clear day the view is stolen by Mount Rainier, a glacier-covered volcano of about 4,390 m some 90 km to the south-east. The city is named after Si'ahl, a leader of the local Duwamish and Suquamish peoples.","w":"A city between a fjord-like sound and a long lake; thin bridges floating across the lake; Rainier's white cone to the south-east.","f":["The bridges over Lake Washington float on concrete pontoons; one is the world's longest floating bridge, about 2.3 km.","The Klondike gold rush of 1897 made Seattle the main outfitting port for prospectors heading north.","Mount Rainier carries more glacier ice than any other peak in the contiguous United States."],"q":{"q":"Which volcano looms south-east of Seattle?","a":"Mount Rainier","x":["Mount Hood","Mount Shasta"]}},"Q16556":{"t":"A mile-square grid stamped across the Sonoran Desert","s":"Phoenix is geometry on sand: a vast grid of arterial roads a mile apart, stamped across a desert valley and broken by bare rocky mountains. Watch for canals and green squares of irrigated land. Long before the city, the Hohokam people dug hundreds of kilometres of canals here, and settlers of the 1860s named their new town after the bird that rises from its ashes.","w":"A huge, pale, regular grid in brown desert, with dark rocky peaks poking through; thin canals and green irrigated patches.","f":["The Hohokam canals, dug from about the 600s to the 1400s, formed one of the largest irrigation networks in pre-Columbian North America.","Summer afternoons here typically climb above 40 °C.","Camelback Mountain, surrounded by suburbs, is named for its hump-backed profile."],"q":{"q":"Why was Phoenix given its name?","a":"It rose on the ruins of ancient canals","x":["A bird-shaped mountain stands nearby","Its founder was called Phoenix"]}},"Q16552":{"t":"A deep blue bay, a sandy spit and Mexico next door","s":"San Diego wraps around a long, sheltered bay, closed off from the Pacific by the flat Coronado peninsula and the high ridge of Point Loma. Planes land almost among the downtown towers, at an airport right on the water. Spanish friars founded California's first mission here in 1769, and the Mexican border is only about 25 km to the south.","w":"A curved bay behind a pale sandy spit and a long ridge; the city spreads over canyon-cut mesas, merging with Tijuana to the south.","f":["In 1542 Juan Rodríguez Cabrillo sailed into this bay, the first European known to reach the US West Coast.","Coronado is not quite an island: a thin sand strip, the Silver Strand, ties it to the mainland."],"q":{"q":"What did Spanish friars found at San Diego in 1769?","a":"California's first mission","x":["California's first university","A silver mine"]}},"Q38022":{"t":"Where the muddy Missouri pours into the Mississippi","s":"Just north of St. Louis two of America's great rivers meet, and from above they often keep their colours for a while: the silt-laden Missouri alongside the darker Mississippi. French fur traders founded the town in 1764 and named it after King Louis IX. In 1804 Lewis and Clark set out from near the confluence to cross the continent.","w":"A city on the west bank of the Mississippi; to the north, two big brown rivers meet in a maze of channels and islands.","f":["The Eads Bridge of 1874 was one of the first major bridges built with steel as its main material.","A little further upstream the Illinois River joins the Mississippi too.","The Gateway Arch on the riverfront is 192 m tall but far too slender to pick out from cruise."],"q":{"q":"Which river joins the Mississippi just north of St. Louis?","a":"The Missouri","x":["The Ohio","The Arkansas"]}},"Q34404":{"t":"The Crescent City, cradled in a bend of the Mississippi","s":"New Orleans grew inside a tight curve of the Mississippi, which is why it's called the Crescent City. To its north spreads the broad, round Lake Pontchartrain, crossed by a bridge nearly 40 km long — a hairline over the water. The French founded the city in 1718; much of it now lies below sea level, ringed by levees.","w":"A city in a sharp bend of a wide brown river; a big round lake to the north crossed by a hairline bridge.","f":["The Lake Pontchartrain Causeway, about 38 km long, is one of the longest bridges over water in the world.","The city is named after Philippe, Duke of Orléans, who ruled France as regent when it was founded.","In 1803 the United States bought New Orleans as part of the vast Louisiana Purchase."],"q":{"q":"Why is New Orleans called the Crescent City?","a":"It sits in a bend of the Mississippi","x":["Its harbour is moon-shaped","Its main square is crescent-shaped"]}},"Q16557":{"t":"A prairie metropolis with an airport bigger than Manhattan","s":"Dallas rises from flat, open prairie, with no coast or mountain to give it shape — just freeways fanning out and a scatter of reservoirs. The Trinity River crosses it in a wide green floodway between levees. Towards Fort Worth, look for the runways of DFW, an airport that covers more ground than Manhattan. It all began with a trading post by a river ford in 1841.","w":"Flat sprawl without natural edges; a broad green floodway along the Trinity; a giant airport with long parallel runways to the north-west.","f":["Dallas–Fort Worth International covers about 70 km², more than the island of Manhattan.","Founder John Neely Bryan picked the spot for its natural ford across the Trinity River.","Almost all the lakes around Dallas are man-made reservoirs."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Dallas?","a":"The Trinity","x":["The Brazos","The Red River"]}},"Q171632":{"t":"On the neck of a vast lake studded with oil wells","s":"At night, look for lights scattered across dark water: countless oil wells stand out on Lake Maracaibo, one of South America's biggest bodies of water. The city lies on the narrow strait where the lake opens to the sea, crossed by a bridge more than 8 km long. In 1499 an expedition sailing for Spain saw stilt houses here and called the land 'Little Venice' — Venezuela.","w":"A city on the west shore of a narrow strait; a long bridge across it; a vast lake opening to the south.","f":["Over the south-west of the lake, near the mouth of the Catatumbo River, lightning flickers on well over a hundred nights a year.","The General Rafael Urdaneta Bridge across the strait is about 8.7 km long and opened in 1962.","The lake is connected to the sea, so its water is slightly salty."],"q":{"q":"What gave Venezuela its name?","a":"Stilt villages on the lake","x":["A Venetian-born explorer","Canals in its capital"]}},"Q100":{"t":"A city that built its own ground out into the harbour","s":"Boston's outline is largely man-made. The Puritans who arrived in 1630 settled a small, hilly peninsula joined to the mainland by a thin neck; in the 1800s the mudflats around it were filled in to create whole districts. Look for the Charles River basin, the island-dotted harbour and the runways stretching into the sea just across from downtown.","w":"A compact city on a harbour full of islands; a wide river basin to the west; runways reaching into the water to the east.","f":["The Back Bay district stands on tidal flats that were filled in with gravel in the 1800s.","Harvard, founded in 1636 across the Charles River, is the oldest university in the United States.","Logan Airport was also built largely on land reclaimed from the harbour."],"q":{"q":"What was Boston's Back Bay before the 1800s?","a":"Tidal mudflats","x":["Farmland","Forest"]}},"Q49255":{"t":"A city at the head of a bay stitched with causeways","s":"From above, the whole Tampa Bay area looks stitched together: long causeways and bridges run straight across shallow water, linking Tampa, at the head of the big, branching bay, with St. Petersburg on the peninsula opposite. In 1886 cigar makers moved their factories here, and hand-rolled cigars made Tampa famous. Look for the dredged shipping channel running out towards the Gulf.","w":"A city at the northern head of a wide, branching bay; several thin causeways across the water; a peninsula city to the west.","f":["Tampa Bay is Florida's largest open-water estuary, covering about 1,000 km².","Ybor City, the old cigar district, once turned out hundreds of millions of cigars a year."],"q":{"q":"Which city faces Tampa across the bay?","a":"St. Petersburg","x":["Orlando","Jacksonville"]}},"Q1345":{"t":"Penn's grid, laid out between two rivers in 1682","s":"Philadelphia began as a plan on paper: in 1682 William Penn drew a neat grid between the Delaware and Schuylkill rivers, with a central square and four smaller ones. That rectangle is still the heart of the city. The Declaration of Independence was adopted here in 1776, and for a decade from 1790 Philadelphia served as the nation's capital.","w":"A dense grid between two rivers; the wide Delaware to the east with the airport on its bank; a long green park along the Schuylkill.","f":["Four of Penn's five original squares are still parks; the fifth holds City Hall.","The name is Greek for 'brotherly love'.","Fairmount Park, along the Schuylkill, is one of the largest urban park systems in the US."],"q":{"q":"Between which two rivers did Penn lay out Philadelphia?","a":"The Delaware and the Schuylkill","x":["The Hudson and the Harlem","The Potomac and the Anacostia"]}},"Q12439":{"t":"The US city with Canada to its south","s":"Detroit comes with a quirk of the map: across the Detroit River, due south of downtown, lies Canada. The river is really a strait linking Lake St. Clair to Lake Erie, and the French word for strait, détroit, gave the city its name. Antoine de la Mothe Cadillac founded a fort here in 1701. Two centuries later carmakers turned it into the Motor City.","w":"A city along a broad river channel between two lakes; Windsor on the opposite bank; a long wooded island in the river.","f":["Belle Isle, the island park in the Detroit River, is larger than New York's Central Park.","The Ambassador Bridge to Windsor carries more trade than any other US–Canada border crossing."],"q":{"q":"In which direction from downtown Detroit does Canada lie?","a":"South","x":["North","West"]}},"Q39450":{"t":"A frontier city between mudflats and the Chugach peaks","s":"Anchorage fills a wedge of flat land between two long arms of Cook Inlet, with the Chugach Mountains rising right behind it. At low tide the inlet drains to glistening grey mudflats: the tides here can rise and fall by more than 9 m. On a clear day, look north for Denali, about 200 km away. The city began in 1914 as a tent camp for railway builders.","w":"A grid city on a lowland wedge between two grey-brown inlets, backed by snowy mountains; a lake full of floatplanes beside the airport.","f":["Lake Hood, next to the international airport, is one of the world's busiest seaplane bases.","Turnagain Arm got its name in 1778, when James Cook's ships had to turn back there.","In Turnagain Arm the incoming tide sometimes arrives as a bore — a single travelling wave."],"q":{"q":"How did Turnagain Arm near Anchorage get its name?","a":"Cook's ships had to turn back there","x":["It bends back on itself","Whalers used it as a turning point"]}},"Q9022":{"t":"Between a deep canyon and Mexico's largest lake","s":"Guadalajara spreads across a high, open valley about 1,560 m above sea level. Its northern edge ends abruptly at a deep canyon cut by the Río Grande de Santiago, and to the south lies Lake Chapala, Mexico's largest freshwater lake. West of the city, look for blue-grey rows of agave: this is the country around the town of Tequila.","w":"A large city on a flat high valley; a dark canyon along its northern edge; a big lake to the south.","f":["The canyon at the northern edge, the Barranca de Huentitán, is roughly 600 m deep.","The city was moved several times before settling on its present site in 1542.","Blue agave for tequila covers the hills around the town of Tequila, about 60 km north-west."],"q":{"q":"Which lake lies just south of Guadalajara?","a":"Lake Chapala","x":["Lake Pátzcuaro","Lake Texcoco"]}},"Q125293":{"t":"A colonial grid ringed by great volcanoes","s":"Few cities have a volcanic horizon like Puebla's. To the west stand snowy Popocatépetl and Iztaccíhuatl, to the north-east the lone cone of La Malinche, and far to the east the white peak of Orizaba, Mexico's highest mountain. The Spanish founded Puebla in 1531 as a brand-new planned town, and its old centre is still a tidy grid.","w":"A large city on a high plain with big volcanoes on the horizon; La Malinche's isolated cone close by to the north-east.","f":["Next door in Cholula stands the largest pyramid in the world by volume, so overgrown it looks like a hill.","Pico de Orizaba, visible to the east on clear days, rises to about 5,600 m.","Puebla is famous for Talavera, a glazed pottery tradition brought from Spain in the 1500s."],"q":{"q":"Which nearby town has the largest pyramid in the world by volume?","a":"Cholula","x":["Teotihuacan","Tula"]}},"Q43509":{"t":"A tidal river port among mangroves and shrimp ponds","s":"Guayaquil spreads along the west bank of the broad, brown Guayas River, where the Pacific tide still reaches. Downstream the estuary dissolves into a maze of mangrove channels and neat grids of rectangular shrimp ponds. Ecuador's biggest city and main port, it was founded on this spot in 1538, after earlier attempts nearby.","w":"A large city beside a wide brown river; rows of rectangular ponds and dark mangroves along the estuary to the south.","f":["The ponds around the Gulf of Guayaquil help make Ecuador one of the world's biggest shrimp exporters.","Rafts of floating water hyacinth drift up and down the Guayas with every tide.","Francisco de Orellana, later the first European to sail down the Amazon, is credited with the 1538 founding."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows past Guayaquil?","a":"The Guayas","x":["The Napo","The Esmeraldas"]}},"Q48278":{"t":"A city filling a green Andean valley from wall to wall","s":"Medellín is poured into the Aburrá Valley, a narrow trough in the Andes about 1,500 m up, and its red-brick neighbourhoods climb high up both slopes. Down the middle runs the Medellín River, heading north. At this height and so close to the equator the air stays mild all year, which is why locals call it the City of Eternal Spring.","w":"A long city in a narrow north–south valley, climbing its green walls; the main airport on a plateau to the east.","f":["The main airport stands on a plateau near Rionegro, at about 2,100 m — higher than the city itself.","The average temperature stays around 22 °C in every month of the year.","Medellín was granted the status of a town in 1675."],"q":{"q":"What is Medellín's nickname, thanks to its climate?","a":"City of Eternal Spring","x":["City of the Winds","City of Mists"]}},"Q51103":{"t":"Salsa city on a plain of sugar cane","s":"Cali sits where the flat, fertile Cauca Valley meets the steep green slopes of the Western Andes. Look out across the valley floor: a patchwork of sugar cane fields in every shade of green stretches north and south for well over a hundred kilometres. Sebastián de Belalcázar founded the city in 1536; today it proudly calls itself the world capital of salsa.","w":"A large city at the foot of a mountain wall to the west; a flat valley of striped green cane fields to the east.","f":["The Cauca Valley is the heart of Colombia's sugar industry.","The Cauca River, one of Colombia's longest, winds along the city's eastern edge.","At about 1,000 m, Cali is lower and warmer than Colombia's highland cities."],"q":{"q":"Which crop blankets the Cauca Valley around Cali?","a":"Sugar cane","x":["Wheat","Tea"]}},"Q1563":{"t":"A pocket harbour behind a narrow, fortified mouth","s":"Havana grew around a bag-shaped bay entered through a single narrow channel, easy to defend and safe from storms. That made it the gathering point for Spain's treasure fleets, which assembled here before crossing the Atlantic. The city moved to this shore in 1519. Look for the harbour's slim neck and the long seafront curving west from it.","w":"A city on Cuba's north coast around a pouch-shaped bay with a slender entrance; the old town on its western side.","f":["Old Havana and its fortifications have been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1982.","Key West, in Florida, lies about 170 km to the north across the Straits of Florida.","The Morro and La Cabaña fortresses guard the harbour mouth from its eastern shore."],"q":{"q":"What gathered in Havana harbour before crossing to Spain?","a":"Spanish treasure fleets","x":["Whaling fleets","Pirate squadrons"]}},"Q34820":{"t":"The Americas' oldest European city, at a river mouth","s":"Santo Domingo lies where the Ozama River meets the Caribbean, and its colonial quarter sits on the west bank right at the river mouth. Founded in 1496, it is the oldest continuously inhabited European-founded city in the Americas. Look for the long, straight seafront running west from the river, and the airport out on the coast to the east.","w":"A large city along a straight stretch of Caribbean coast, split by a river mouth; the airport on the coast to the east.","f":["In 1502 the city moved from the east bank of the Ozama to the west bank.","Its cathedral, completed in the 1540s, is the oldest in the Americas."],"q":{"q":"Which river splits Santo Domingo into west and east?","a":"The Ozama","x":["The Yaque del Norte","The Artibonite"]}},"Q12829733":{"t":"A river port at the gates of the Amazon","s":"Belém faces a vast expanse of muddy water — the Pará River and Guajará Bay, part of the Amazon's maze of mouths. Across it lies Marajó, an island roughly the size of Switzerland. The Portuguese built a fort here in 1616 to guard the way into the Amazon, and the rubber boom of the late 1800s made the city rich.","w":"A dense city on a point between brown waterways, forest and islands all around; the huge, low island of Marajó to the north-west.","f":["Marajó covers about 40,000 km², one of the largest islands ringed by fresh and brackish water.","Belém lies less than 200 km south of the equator, and afternoon downpours are almost daily.","The Ver-o-Peso waterfront market has traded Amazon goods since the 1600s."],"q":{"q":"Which huge island lies across the water from Belém?","a":"Marajó","x":["Bananal","Ilha Grande"]}},"Q2844":{"t":"A capital drawn in the shape of an aeroplane","s":"Brasília is one of the few cities whose design you can read from the window: two curved residential wings crossed by a straight monumental axis, like an aeroplane or a bird. Lúcio Costa drew the plan, and the city was built on almost empty savanna and inaugurated in 1960. An artificial lake wraps around it on the east.","w":"A cross-shaped city — a long straight axis across a curved arc — beside a branching man-made lake on the high savanna.","f":["Lake Paranoá was created by damming a river when the city was built.","The whole Pilot Plan has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1987.","The city stands on a plateau at about 1,100 m, amid Brazil's reddish cerrado savanna."],"q":{"q":"What does Brasília's original plan resemble?","a":"An aeroplane","x":["A star","A spiral"]}},"Q40269":{"t":"A city on a river that might be a lake","s":"Porto Alegre lines the eastern shore of the Guaíba, a broad body of water formed where several rivers meet in a green delta of islands. Locals still argue whether it counts as a river or a lake. To the south it opens into the Lagoa dos Patos, a coastal lagoon more than 200 km long. Settlers from the Azores helped found the town in the 1700s.","w":"A city on the east bank of wide brown water; a delta of green islands to the north, a vast lagoon to the south.","f":["The Lagoa dos Patos covers about 10,000 km², making it Brazil's largest lagoon.","The official founding date is 1772, though Azorean families had settled here some twenty years earlier."],"q":{"q":"Which lagoon stretches away south of Porto Alegre?","a":"Lagoa dos Patos","x":["Lagoa Rodrigo de Freitas","Lagoa da Conceição"]}},"Q4361":{"t":"A cool plateau city among candelabra pines","s":"Frosty winter mornings are part of life in Curitiba, one of Brazil's coolest big cities, set on a high plateau about 900 m up just inland from the forested Serra do Mar. Its name is usually traced to a Tupi phrase for 'many pines', after the araucaria trees whose flat, candelabra crowns still dot the countryside. Look for the lakes in its many parks.","w":"A large city on a green plateau dotted with park lakes; the dark, forested Serra do Mar rising to the east.","f":["Many of the city's park lakes were created as flood-control reservoirs.","A railway opened in 1885 still winds down the Serra do Mar from Curitiba to the port of Paranaguá.","In the 1970s Curitiba pioneered express bus lanes that cities around the world later copied."],"q":{"q":"Which tree gave Curitiba its name?","a":"The araucaria, or Paraná pine","x":["The Brazil nut tree","The rubber tree"]}},"Q43463":{"t":"A sunny fortress city on Brazil's north-east shoulder","s":"Fortaleza faces north onto a warm, turquoise Atlantic, its high-rise seafront curving along a coast of white dunes. The steady trade winds that fill the sails of local jangada rafts also turn rows of wind turbines along the shore. The name simply means 'fortress': the city grew around a fort the Dutch built here in 1649, later taken over by the Portuguese.","w":"A large city on a north-facing coast; long pale beaches and dunes on either side; breakwaters and a port at its eastern end.","f":["Ceará, the state Fortaleza is capital of, abolished slavery in 1884, four years before the rest of Brazil.","The coast here lies only about 4° south of the equator, so days and nights stay nearly equal all year."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Fortaleza mean?","a":"Fortress","x":["Lighthouse","Harbour"]}},"Q36947":{"t":"Brazil's first capital, on a cliff above All Saints Bay","s":"Salvador occupies a blunt peninsula between the open Atlantic and the huge, calm Bay of All Saints. Its old town was built on top of a steep escarpment, with the port crowded at the foot — a city on two levels. Founded in 1549, it served as the capital of Portuguese Brazil for more than two centuries, until 1763.","w":"A big city on a triangular peninsula; a wide, island-studded bay to the west, ocean beaches to the south and east.","f":["The bay was named in 1501 by a Portuguese expedition that arrived on All Saints' Day.","Since 1873 a public lift, the Lacerda Elevator, has carried people between the upper and lower towns.","The bay covers more than 1,000 km² and holds dozens of islands."],"q":{"q":"Which bay did Salvador grow up beside?","a":"The Bay of All Saints","x":["Guanabara Bay","Paranaguá Bay"]}},"Q2096":{"t":"A green river gorge winding through a northern city","s":"Edmonton is cut in two by the North Saskatchewan River, which loops through a deep, wooded valley kept almost entirely as parkland. From above it's a dark green ribbon across the grid of streets. Around the city, flat farmland stretches to the horizon. This is the northernmost metropolis of more than a million people in North America.","w":"A large grid city on flat farmland, split by a deep, winding, tree-lined river valley; refineries on the eastern edge.","f":["The river valley parks form one of the longest continuous stretches of urban parkland in North America.","The Hudson's Bay Company built Fort Edmonton on the river in 1795.","Edmonton is Alberta's capital, though Calgary to the south is the larger city."],"q":{"q":"Which river winds through Edmonton?","a":"The North Saskatchewan","x":["The Athabasca","The Red Deer"]}},"Q340":{"t":"An island city with a mountain at its heart","s":"Montreal fills most of an island in the St. Lawrence, and in its middle rises a dark, wooded hill: Mount Royal, which gave the city its name. Jacques Cartier climbed it in 1535, and in 1642 French settlers founded Ville-Marie below. Just upstream the Lachine Rapids stopped ocean-going ships, so the city grew into a great river port.","w":"A large island city in a broad river, with a wooded hill in the middle; long bridges to the south shore.","f":["The island of Montreal, about 50 km long, is the largest of more than 200 islands in the Hochelaga Archipelago.","Ships now bypass the Lachine Rapids through the St. Lawrence Seaway along the south shore.","Montreal is one of the largest French-speaking cities in the world."],"q":{"q":"What is Montreal named after?","a":"The hill in its centre","x":["A French king","The St. Lawrence River"]}},"Q83189":{"t":"A 1930s planned capital on the red cerrado plateau","s":"Goiânia was drawn on a drawing board and built from 1933 to replace the old colonial town of Goiás as state capital. Its central avenues radiate from a civic square like spokes, a plan a generation older than Brasília's. Around it the cerrado savanna has become a patchwork of soy fields and pasture, with reddish soil showing between them.","w":"A large city on a gently rolling plateau; green and red-brown squares of farmland all round; Brasília about 200 km to the north-east.","f":["The old capital, the town of Goiás, is a UNESCO World Heritage site for its colonial centre.","Many of Goiânia's early public buildings were built in Art Deco style."],"q":{"q":"Which town did Goiânia replace as state capital?","a":"The old town of Goiás","x":["Anápolis","Pirenópolis"]}},"Q48344":{"t":"A city of rivers and bridges behind a natural reef","s":"Recife is threaded with water: the Capibaribe and Beberibe rivers split into channels around islands before reaching the sea, and bridges stitch the districts together. Offshore, a long, straight line of rock runs parallel to the beach and shelters the harbour — the reef that gave the city its name. From 1630 to 1654 this was the capital of Dutch Brazil.","w":"A dense coastal city criss-crossed by river channels; a thin line of reef just offshore; a long beach to the south.","f":["The first synagogue in the Americas, Kahal Zur Israel, opened here in the 1630s under Dutch rule.","Under Johan Maurits of Nassau the Dutch built a new town, Mauritsstad, on one of the islands."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Recife mean?","a":"Reef","x":["Bridge","Island"]}},"Q49219":{"t":"A city planned from scratch on an old cattle ranch","s":"Irvine was drawn up almost from nothing in the 1960s on the Irvine Ranch, once a huge cattle and farming estate stretching from the coast to the mountains. Architect William Pereira laid it out around a new University of California campus, in tidy villages divided by green belts. To the north-east, look for Saddleback, the twin-peaked ridge that shapes the skyline.","w":"Tidy curving suburbs with green belts, between low coastal hills and the Santa Ana Mountains; John Wayne Airport on the western edge.","f":["Saddleback is named for the dip between its two summits; the higher one, Santiago Peak, is about 1,700 m.","Departures from John Wayne Airport climb steeply and throttle back to spare the houses below.","Upper Newport Bay, just to the south-west, is one of southern California's few surviving coastal estuaries."],"q":{"q":"What was the land under Irvine before the city was planned?","a":"A vast private ranch","x":["A national forest","A salt lake"]}},"Q42800":{"t":"A planned capital walled in by iron-rich mountains","s":"Belo Horizonte was built from scratch and inaugurated in 1897 as the new capital of Minas Gerais, replacing the old gold town of Ouro Preto. Its planners overlaid a street grid with diagonal avenues, all wrapped in a ring road. Along the southern edge rises the Serra do Curral, and beyond it lie the red-brown scars of iron-ore mines. The name means 'beautiful horizon'.","w":"A big, hilly city under a long ridge to the south; reddish mine pits in the hills beyond; a lake on the northern side.","f":["The surrounding 'Iron Quadrangle' is one of the world's great iron-ore regions.","Lake Pampulha, an artificial lake in the north, is ringed by 1940s buildings by Oscar Niemeyer, now UNESCO-listed."],"q":{"q":"Which old gold town did Belo Horizonte replace as capital in 1897?","a":"Ouro Preto","x":["Diamantina","Mariana"]}},"Q1335":{"t":"The southernmost capital in the Americas, by a lone hill","s":"Just where the brown Río de la Plata begins to turn into the blue Atlantic, Montevideo spreads over a rocky peninsula on the estuary's north shore. Across its bay rises a lone, rounded hill, the Cerro, which gave the city its name. Spain founded Montevideo in the 1720s as a fortress to hold the estuary's north shore against Portugal.","w":"A city on a peninsula and a long curving shore; a round hill across the bay to the west; brown water shading to blue eastward.","f":["The Cerro de Montevideo is only about 134 m high, but it was the landmark sailors steered by.","Montevideo is the southernmost national capital in the Americas.","A seaside promenade, the Rambla, follows the shore for more than 20 km."],"q":{"q":"What gave Montevideo its name?","a":"A hill across the bay","x":["A river mouth","An island in the harbour"]}},"Q38733":{"t":"A gold-rush capital at the edge of the Rockies","s":"Helena began in 1864, when four worn-out prospectors tried one last creek and struck gold; the town's main street still follows that 'Last Chance Gulch'. Today it's a small state capital where forested mountains meet the flat, open Helena Valley. Look for the Missouri River's reservoirs glinting to the north-east and east.","w":"A small town tucked against wooded hills; a broad, flat valley to the north-east, with the Missouri's blue reservoirs beyond.","f":["In 1805 Meriwether Lewis named a narrow canyon of the Missouri nearby the 'Gates of the Rocky Mountains'.","Helena became Montana's capital in 1875, while Montana was still a territory."],"q":{"q":"What was the gulch called where Helena's gold was found?","a":"Last Chance Gulch","x":["Lucky Strike Gulch","Golden Creek Gulch"]}},"Q37066":{"t":"A prairie capital named to charm German investors","s":"Bismarck lies on the east bank of the Missouri, here a wide river winding through open, rolling plains. The town began in 1872 as Edwinton, a railway crossing; a year later it was renamed after Germany's chancellor, Otto von Bismarck, in the hope of drawing German money and settlers to the Northern Pacific Railway. Its twin town, Mandan, faces it across the river.","w":"A modest city on a broad, winding river with sandbars; a smaller town across the water; wide tan prairie all around.","f":["Lewis and Clark spent the winter of 1804–05 at Fort Mandan, about 60 km up the river.","North Dakota's capitol is a 19-storey tower, often called the 'Skyscraper on the Prairie'."],"q":{"q":"Who is Bismarck named after?","a":"Chancellor Otto von Bismarck","x":["A railway engineer","A Sioux chief"]}},"Q35775":{"t":"A green river of trees across the sagebrush","s":"From above, Boise's origin story makes sense: a ribbon of cottonwoods follows the Boise River out of the mountains and across a dry, sage-grey plain. French-Canadian fur trappers are said to have greeted the trees with 'Les bois!', and the name stuck. Today the city spreads from the foothills onto the irrigated patchwork of the Snake River Plain.","w":"A city at the foot of bare brown foothills; a green, tree-lined river running west through it onto a patchwork of farmland.","f":["Idaho's capitol is the only US state capitol heated by water from underground hot springs.","Boise has one of the largest Basque communities in the United States."],"q":{"q":"Where does the name Boise come from?","a":"The French word for 'woods'","x":["A Shoshone chief's name","A Spanish saint's name"]}},"Q16553":{"t":"The flat heart of Silicon Valley, once all orchards","s":"San Jose spreads across the flat floor of the Santa Clara Valley, between the wooded Santa Cruz Mountains to the west and the bare, rounded Diablo Range to the east. Founded in 1777, it was the first Spanish civilian town in California. Until the mid-1900s the valley was a sea of fruit orchards; now it's the hub of Silicon Valley.","w":"A vast, low city on a flat valley floor at the southern tip of San Francisco Bay; mountains on both sides.","f":["San Jose was California's first state capital, from 1849 to 1851.","On Mount Hamilton to the east, Lick Observatory opened in 1888, one of the first mountaintop observatories."],"q":{"q":"What was the Santa Clara Valley known for before computers?","a":"Fruit orchards","x":["Oil wells","Gold mines"]}},"Q18013":{"t":"A gold-rush capital where two rivers meet","s":"Two rivers meet in the middle of California's flat Central Valley, and Sacramento grew at the junction, where the American River flows into the Sacramento. In 1848 gold was found at a sawmill up the American River, and this riverside settlement became the supply town for the rush. Look west for a wide, straight belt of fields between levees: the Yolo Bypass, which floods on purpose in wet winters.","w":"A tree-filled city at a river junction on a flat valley floor; rice paddies around; a broad, leveed flood channel to the west.","f":["In spring the flooded rice fields around the city can shine like mirrors.","The Central Pacific began building the first transcontinental railway eastward from Sacramento in 1863.","Sacramento has been California's capital since 1854."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows into the Sacramento River in the city?","a":"The American River","x":["The Colorado River","The Klamath River"]}},"Q23768":{"t":"A blaze of light in a black desert","s":"At night Las Vegas is unmistakable: a dense pool of white and gold light in a desert that's otherwise almost completely dark, with one brilliant stripe running north–south through it. By day the valley shows its bones — bare mountains all round, snow on Mount Charleston in winter. The name is Spanish for 'the meadows', after springs that once watered grass here.","w":"A sharply bounded city in a desert basin ringed by bare mountains; the blue of Lake Mead about 40 km to the east.","f":["The city was founded in 1905 with a railway company's auction of town lots.","Lake Mead, formed by the Hoover Dam, is the largest reservoir in the US by capacity.","Mount Charleston, west of the city, rises to about 3,630 m."],"q":{"q":"What does 'Las Vegas' mean in Spanish?","a":"The meadows","x":["The lights","The sands"]}},"Q38555":{"t":"America's oldest and highest state capital","s":"Santa Fe is easy to miss: a low, earth-coloured town of adobe walls that blends into the piñon-dotted hills at the foot of the Sangre de Cristo Mountains. Spanish colonists founded it around 1610, making it the oldest state capital in the US, and at about 2,100 m it is also the highest. The broad Rio Grande valley opens to the west.","w":"A small, low, tan town at the southern tip of a snowy range; dark green juniper hills around; a wide valley to the west.","f":["The Santa Fe Trail, opened in 1821, linked the town to Missouri by a trade route of well over 1,000 km.","The name Sangre de Cristo ('Blood of Christ') is often linked to the mountains' red glow at sunset.","The Palace of the Governors, built in the 1610s, is among the oldest public buildings in the US."],"q":{"q":"When was Santa Fe founded?","a":"Around 1610","x":["In 1776","In 1848"]}},"Q6106":{"t":"A city of bridges under the white cone of Mount Hood","s":"Portland straddles the Willamette River just before it joins the broad Columbia, and a string of bridges stitches its two halves together. On a clear day Mount Hood's snowy cone floats on the eastern horizon. The name was settled in 1845 by a coin toss: one founder wanted Boston, the other Portland, after his home town in Maine.","w":"A city on both banks of a river just above a much wider one; a dark wooded ridge to the west; Mount Hood east.","f":["Forest Park, on the ridge above the city, is one of the largest urban forests in the United States.","Mount Hood, about 3,430 m high, is a volcano and Oregon's highest peak.","The Columbia River here forms the border between Oregon and Washington."],"q":{"q":"How was Portland's name chosen in 1845?","a":"By tossing a coin","x":["By a vote of settlers","By a railway company"]}},"Q23337":{"t":"A grid city between a mountain wall and a salty inland sea","s":"Salt Lake City sits on a flat bench between the abrupt wall of the Wasatch Range and the shallow, glittering Great Salt Lake. Look at the lower mountain slopes for faint horizontal lines: old shorelines of Lake Bonneville, the vast ancient lake of which the Great Salt Lake is a remnant. Mormon pioneers founded the city in 1847 and laid out its grid of very wide streets.","w":"A city at the foot of a steep mountain wall; a vast pale lake to the north-west, with colourful evaporation ponds along its shore.","f":["A railway causeway splits the lake, and the saltier northern part often looks pinkish-red from above.","Street addresses count outward from a single central point, Temple Square.","In places the Great Salt Lake is several times saltier than the ocean."],"q":{"q":"Which ancient lake left terraces on the mountains around Salt Lake City?","a":"Lake Bonneville","x":["Lake Lahontan","Lake Agassiz"]}},"Q39042":{"t":"A railway town that sprang up on the windy high plains","s":"Cheyenne stands alone on open high plains about 1,850 m up, with the low, pine-dark Laramie Range to the west. It was born in 1867, when the Union Pacific tracks arrived, and grew so fast it was nicknamed the Magic City of the Plains. Look for the railway running east–west through town: the reason it exists at all.","w":"A compact town on bare, rolling grassland; a railway line straight through it; low, dark hills to the west.","f":["In 1869 the territorial legislature in Cheyenne gave women the vote — a first in the United States.","Cheyenne Frontier Days, held every July since 1897, is one of the world's largest outdoor rodeos."],"q":{"q":"What did Wyoming's legislature in Cheyenne grant first in 1869?","a":"Votes for women","x":["An eight-hour working day","Free railway travel"]}},"Q39709":{"t":"A capital at a river fork in a sea of corn","s":"Des Moines grew where the Raccoon River flows into the Des Moines, and from above it sits like a knot in a vast chequerboard: Iowa's fields, laid out in one-mile squares of corn and soybeans, run to every horizon. The US Army founded Fort Des Moines at the fork in 1843. Look north for the long reservoir of Saylorville Lake.","w":"A mid-sized city at a river junction, surrounded by a perfect grid of square fields; a long, narrow lake to the north.","f":["The name comes from the river's French name, often translated as 'River of the Monks', though its origin is debated.","Iowa was surveyed into square miles in the 1800s, and its country roads still follow that grid."],"q":{"q":"Which two rivers meet at Des Moines?","a":"The Des Moines and the Raccoon","x":["The Iowa and the Cedar","The Missouri and the Platte"]}},"Q43199":{"t":"A river city where the Missouri once switched sides","s":"Omaha lines the west bank of the Missouri, a brown river looping across a wide floodplain, with Council Bluffs in Iowa on the other side. Look by the airport for a horseshoe-shaped lake: it's an old river bend, cut off when the Missouri changed course in 1877. The town beside it stayed in Iowa, stranded on the Nebraska bank.","w":"A city on bluffs above a winding brown river; a horseshoe lake and the airport on the floodplain; Council Bluffs across the water.","f":["In the 1860s the Union Pacific began building the first transcontinental railway westward from Omaha.","The city, founded in 1854, is named after the Omaha people.","Carter Lake is the only Iowa town west of the Missouri River."],"q":{"q":"Why is Carter Lake, Iowa, on Omaha's side of the Missouri?","a":"The river changed course","x":["Iowa bought it from Nebraska","A treaty swapped it"]}},"Q34863":{"t":"A city that appeared in a single afternoon in 1889","s":"Oklahoma City has an unusual birthday. At noon on 22 April 1889 a gunshot opened the Land Run, and settlers raced in to claim plots; by nightfall around 10,000 people were camped on what had been open prairie. Today the city sprawls across flat, reddish plains, with round reservoirs on its edges and the Oklahoma River running through.","w":"A very spread-out city on flat red-brown plains; round blue reservoirs to the north-west; a river channel just south of downtown.","f":["Oil wells have pumped on the grounds of the State Capitol — a sign of the oil field beneath the city.","Oklahoma City covers about 1,600 km², making it one of the largest US cities by land area."],"q":{"q":"How did Oklahoma City come into being in 1889?","a":"In a single-day land run","x":["As a mining camp","As an army fort"]}},"Q40747":{"t":"A tiny capital below a dam and a 370-km lake","s":"Pierre, pronounced 'peer', is one of the smallest state capitals in the US, a quiet town on the Missouri amid treeless, rolling plains. Just upstream the river is blocked by Oahe Dam, a huge earthen embankment, and behind it Lake Oahe winds north for some 370 km, almost to Bismarck, the capital of North Dakota.","w":"A small town on a river just below a massive earth dam; a long, winding blue lake stretching north through bare brown hills.","f":["In 1743 French explorers buried a lead plate claiming the land for France on a hill across the river; children found it in 1913.","The town is named after Pierre Chouteau Jr., a fur trader based in St. Louis."],"q":{"q":"Which huge reservoir begins just north of Pierre?","a":"Lake Oahe","x":["Lake Sakakawea","Lake Mead"]}},"Q975":{"t":"Where the Texas Hill Country drops to the plains","s":"Around San Antonio you can watch the landscape change: rumpled, wooded hills to the north-west, flat, open farmland to the south-east. The city straddles the Balcones Escarpment, where one gives way to the other. Spanish missionaries and soldiers founded the settlement in 1718 by the springs of the San Antonio River. A chain of five missions along the river, the Alamo among them, is now a World Heritage site.","w":"A big, sprawling city where dark green hills to the north-west meet flat plains to the south-east; freeway rings around the centre.","f":["In 2015 the San Antonio Missions became the first UNESCO World Heritage site in Texas.","Much of the city's water comes from the Edwards Aquifer, a vast limestone reservoir beneath the escarpment."],"q":{"q":"When was San Antonio founded?","a":"In 1718","x":["In 1836","In 1781"]}},"Q820235":{"t":"An Andean terrace city near the Colombian border","s":"Step by step, San Cristóbal climbs a series of terraces above the Torbes River, about 800 m up, in a green valley where Venezuela's Andes begin. The mountains here form a knot between the Colombian ranges to the south-west and the high Cordillera de Mérida to the north-east. Captain Juan Maldonado founded the city in 1561; Venezuelans know it as the City of Courtesy.","w":"A city spread over stepped terraces in a green mountain valley; forested ridges all round; a river winding along the valley floor.","f":["The border with Colombia runs only a few dozen kilometres to the west, near the city of Cúcuta.","To the north-east the Andes climb to Pico Bolívar, Venezuela's highest peak, at about 4,980 m."],"q":{"q":"What do Venezuelans call San Cristóbal?","a":"The City of Courtesy","x":["The City of Eternal Spring","The City of Bridges"]}},"Q54880":{"t":"An industrial city beside a lake with no way to the sea","s":"Valencia spreads over a green valley beside Lake Valencia, a broad lake ringed by mountains with no outlet to the sea. When Alexander von Humboldt came here in 1800, he noticed that the water had been falling and blamed the clearing of forests around it — one of the earliest scientific warnings about human impact on nature. The city itself was founded in 1555.","w":"A large city in a flat valley west of a wide lake; the forested Coastal Range to the north, the sea just beyond it.","f":["Lake Valencia is Venezuela's largest natural freshwater lake.","The port of Puerto Cabello lies about 35 km to the north, on the far side of the coastal mountains."],"q":{"q":"Who studied Lake Valencia's falling water level in 1800?","a":"Alexander von Humboldt","x":["Charles Darwin","Charles Marie de La Condamine"]}},"Q28198":{"t":"A capital on the Pearl River, above a buried volcano","s":"Jackson lies on gently rolling, wooded land beside the Pearl River, with the broad Ross Barnett Reservoir glinting to the north-east. Nothing at the surface hints at it, but about 900 m underground lies an extinct volcano, long buried by sediment. The site began as a trading post called LeFleur's Bluff and was chosen as the state capital in 1821, named after Andrew Jackson.","w":"A mid-sized city in green, rolling woodland; a winding river on its east side; a large reservoir to the north-east.","f":["The buried Jackson Volcano was last active tens of millions of years ago, in the age of the dinosaurs.","The Natchez Trace, an old trail of traders and travellers, passes nearby as a scenic parkway."],"q":{"q":"What lies buried deep beneath Jackson?","a":"An extinct volcano","x":["A meteorite crater","A gold vein"]}},"Q41087":{"t":"The City of Oaks, a capital planned in a forest","s":"Raleigh hides under trees: from above, rooftops and roads peek through an almost continuous canopy of oak and pine. It was planned from scratch in 1792, when North Carolina bought land in the woods for a new capital, and named after Sir Walter Raleigh, sponsor of the Roanoke colony. Look for the branching reservoirs of Falls Lake and Jordan Lake nearby.","w":"A city deep in green forest; a compact downtown; branching reservoirs to the north and west; the airport among trees to the north-west.","f":["Raleigh, Durham and Chapel Hill form the 'Research Triangle', with a large research park between them.","The 1792 plan put the State House on a central square, with four smaller squares around it."],"q":{"q":"Who is Raleigh named after?","a":"Sir Walter Raleigh","x":["Sir Francis Drake","John White"]}},"Q37320":{"t":"A lake port where a crooked river meets Lake Erie","s":"Cleveland spreads along the south shore of Lake Erie, and its heart is where the Cuyahoga River winds through a valley of old industry before slipping into the lake. The name Cuyahoga is often translated as 'crooked river', and from above it's easy to see why. Surveyor Moses Cleaveland laid out the town in 1796; the city later dropped the first 'a'.","w":"A city along a straight lakeshore; a tightly winding river through the centre; a small airport right on the waterfront.","f":["Cuyahoga Valley, just south of the city, is Ohio's only national park.","Burke Lakefront Airport sits on land reclaimed from Lake Erie, next to downtown."],"q":{"q":"Who laid out Cleveland in 1796?","a":"Moses Cleaveland","x":["Grover Cleveland","Anthony Wayne"]}},"Q43196":{"t":"A hilly river city whose airport is in another state","s":"Cincinnati fills a bowl of hills on the north bank of the Ohio River, which swings past in broad curves; across the water is Kentucky. The city's main airport is on the Kentucky side too. Founded in 1788, it was named after the Society of the Cincinnati, which honoured Cincinnatus, the Roman statesman who went back to his plough after saving Rome.","w":"A city in green hills along a curving river; towns on the opposite, Kentucky bank; a big airport on the plateau to the south-west.","f":["The Roebling Suspension Bridge, finished in 1866, was a forerunner of the same engineer's Brooklyn Bridge.","In the 1800s the city packed so much pork that it was nicknamed Porkopolis."],"q":{"q":"In which state is Cincinnati's main airport?","a":"Kentucky","x":["Ohio","Indiana"]}},"Q23197":{"t":"Music City in the loops of the Cumberland River","s":"Nashville grew on the bluffs of the Cumberland River, which coils through the city in wide, lazy loops before heading north. Settlers built Fort Nashborough here in 1779. Look for the reservoirs east of town, Percy Priest and Old Hickory, their arms reaching into wooded hills. The nickname Music City comes from its country-music studios and radio stages.","w":"A city in rolling green country, with a winding river making big loops through it; branching lakes to the east and north-east.","f":["The Grand Ole Opry has broadcast from Nashville every week since 1925, the longest-running radio show in the US.","The city was named after Francis Nash, a general from North Carolina."],"q":{"q":"Which river loops through Nashville?","a":"The Cumberland","x":["The Tennessee","The Ohio"]}},"Q16563":{"t":"A bluff-top river city named after Egypt's ancient capital","s":"High bluffs on the east bank of the Mississippi keep Memphis above the river's floods; across the water, the Arkansas side is flat floodplain scattered with curved oxbow lakes. Its founders in 1819 likened the river to the Nile and named the town after the ancient Egyptian capital. At night its airport is famously busy: it's the main hub of a giant cargo airline.","w":"A city on high ground on the east bank of a broad, winding brown river; flat farmland and oxbow lakes on the Arkansas side.","f":["For years Memphis was the world's busiest cargo airport, thanks to FedEx's overnight hub.","The two steel arches of the Hernando de Soto Bridge over the river form the letter 'M'."],"q":{"q":"Which state lies across the Mississippi from Memphis?","a":"Arkansas","x":["Missouri","Louisiana"]}},"Q49231":{"t":"A harbour city at the mouth of Chesapeake Bay","s":"Several rivers pour into the mouth of Chesapeake Bay at Hampton Roads, a great natural anchorage, and Norfolk lines its southern shore. Look east across the bay entrance for a thin line that suddenly vanishes: the Chesapeake Bay Bridge–Tunnel, which dives under the shipping lanes between man-made islands. South of the city stretches the dark forest of the Great Dismal Swamp.","w":"A low, watery city of peninsulas and inlets on a vast harbour; a long causeway crossing the bay mouth to the north-east.","f":["The Bridge–Tunnel, opened in 1964, runs about 28 km from shore to shore, with two tunnels under the channels.","Lake Drummond, in the middle of the Great Dismal Swamp, is one of only two natural lakes in Virginia.","Hampton Roads is one of the largest natural harbours in the world."],"q":{"q":"What links the two shores at the mouth of Chesapeake Bay?","a":"A bridge–tunnel about 28 km long","x":["A 28-km suspension bridge","Only a car ferry"]}},"Q37836":{"t":"Three rivers, three rival towns and a Great Lake","s":"Three rivers join in Milwaukee and flow together into Lake Michigan behind a long breakwater. Each river bank once belonged to a rival settlement, and when the three merged in 1846 their street grids didn't match — which is why several bridges still cross the river at an angle. German immigrants later made the city famous for its beer.","w":"A city on the western shore of Lake Michigan, where three rivers meet in a harbour sheltered by long breakwaters.","f":["In the late 1800s some of America's biggest breweries were based in Milwaukee.","The opposite, Michigan shore of the lake lies about 130 km away."],"q":{"q":"How many rival settlements merged to form Milwaukee in 1846?","a":"Three","x":["Two","Five"]}},"Q40435":{"t":"Where Lake Erie pours into the Niagara River","s":"Buffalo stands at the eastern tip of Lake Erie, right where the lake drains into the Niagara River and starts its run north to the falls, about 30 km away. In 1825 the Erie Canal linked this spot to the Hudson and New York, and Buffalo boomed as the gateway to the Great Lakes. In winter, look for bands of lake-effect snow.","w":"A city at the eastern corner of Lake Erie; a river flowing north from it along the Canadian border towards Niagara Falls.","f":["The world's first steam-powered grain elevator was built here in 1842.","Wind blowing over the unfrozen lake can dump snow on the city in narrow, very intense bands.","The Peace Bridge links Buffalo with Fort Erie, in Canada, at the head of the river."],"q":{"q":"Which canal ended at Buffalo when it opened in 1825?","a":"The Erie Canal","x":["The Welland Canal","The Ohio and Erie Canal"]}},"Q1342":{"t":"Where two rivers meet to begin the Ohio","s":"Pittsburgh's downtown is a sharp triangle of land where the Allegheny and Monongahela rivers meet to form the Ohio, and from above that point stands out clearly. All around, the rivers run through steep, wooded valleys crossed by bridge after bridge. In 1758 the British named the spot after the statesman William Pitt and soon built Fort Pitt at the point.","w":"A pointed wedge of towers where two rivers merge into one; hills and deep river valleys all around, crossed by many bridges.","f":["Pittsburgh has around 450 bridges, often said to be more than any other city in the world.","For much of the 20th century the city was the heart of American steelmaking."],"q":{"q":"Which two rivers meet at Pittsburgh to form the Ohio?","a":"The Allegheny and the Monongahela","x":["The Susquehanna and the Juniata","The Kanawha and the Elk"]}},"Q507080":{"t":"A planned city where dark water meets muddy water","s":"Look where two great rivers meet: the Caroní, dark as strong tea, flows into the broad, muddy Orinoco, and the two colours run side by side before blending. Ciudad Guayana spreads over both banks of the Caroní, a city planned in 1961 by merging two older towns, Puerto Ordaz and San Félix. Its steel and aluminium plants run on power from dams upstream.","w":"A city on both sides of a dark river where it joins a much wider, pale brown one; industrial plants along the shore.","f":["The Caroní's dark colour comes from tannins washed out of forest soils.","The Guri Dam, upstream on the Caroní, is one of the largest hydroelectric plants in the world."],"q":{"q":"Which two rivers meet at Ciudad Guayana?","a":"The Orinoco and the Caroní","x":["The Orinoco and the Apure","The Amazon and the Negro"]}},"Q79985":{"t":"Russian America's first capital, on a big green island","s":"Kodiak town lies on the north-east corner of Kodiak Island, the second-largest island in the United States, a land of steep green mountains and deep, fjord-like bays. Russian fur traders made their first permanent Alaskan settlement on the island in 1784, and until 1808 this harbour was the capital of Russian America. Today it's one of the busiest fishing ports in the US.","w":"A small harbour town on a green, mountainous island; bays cutting deep into the land; smaller wooded islands just offshore.","f":["Kodiak Island covers about 9,300 km²; in the US only Hawaii's Big Island is larger.","A warehouse built by the Russians around 1808 still stands in town, now the Baranov Museum.","Kodiak bears, a local subspecies of brown bear, are among the largest bears on Earth."],"q":{"q":"What was Kodiak until 1808?","a":"The capital of Russian America","x":["A Hudson's Bay Company fort","A Spanish mission"]}},"Q79523":{"t":"A tiny village with a runway for Pacific emergencies","s":"Cold Bay is barely a village, near the stormy tip of the Alaska Peninsula, yet it has one of the longest runways in Alaska, over 3 km. Airliners crossing the North Pacific keep it on their list of diversion airports. Around it lie treeless tundra, volcanic peaks and Izembek Lagoon, where vast eelgrass beds feed migrating geese every autumn.","w":"A thin neck of land near the peninsula's tip; a long runway beside a wide bay; snow-streaked volcanic cones nearby.","f":["Nearly all the world's Pacific brant geese stop at Izembek Lagoon on their autumn migration.","Frosty Peak, a volcano about 1,900 m high, rises just south-west of the village.","No roads link Cold Bay to the rest of Alaska; everything arrives by sea or air."],"q":{"q":"What does tiny Cold Bay have that transpacific flights value?","a":"A runway over 3 km long","x":["A large passenger terminal","An oil refinery"]}},"Q80022":{"t":"A tundra town in a delta of a thousand lakes","s":"Around Bethel the tundra is so full of ponds and lakes that from above it looks like lace. The town lies on the Kuskokwim River in the Yukon–Kuskokwim Delta, one of the flattest landscapes on the continent, and no road connects it to the rest of Alaska. Moravian missionaries founded it in 1885 beside a Yup'ik village.","w":"A small town on a broad, winding river in flat tundra shattered into countless small lakes.","f":["In winter the frozen Kuskokwim becomes an ice road linking Bethel with villages up and down the river.","The Yukon–Kuskokwim Delta is one of the world's largest river deltas and a huge nesting ground for waterbirds."],"q":{"q":"How do people drive between Bethel and nearby villages in winter?","a":"On the frozen river","x":["On a paved highway","Through a tunnel"]}},"Q79813":{"t":"A gravel finger pointing into the Chukchi Sea","s":"A long, low gravel spit juts westward into the Chukchi Sea, and near its end lies Point Hope — its Iñupiaq name, Tikiġaq, means 'index finger'. It's one of the oldest continuously inhabited places in North America: the nearby Ipiutak site holds the remains of hundreds of houses about 2,000 years old. Bowhead whaling is still at the heart of life here.","w":"A thin, pale triangular spit jutting west into grey sea, with lagoons behind it; flat tundra and low hills inland.","f":["In the 1970s the village moved a few kilometres east because the sea was eroding the tip of the spit.","For much of the year the point is surrounded by sea ice."],"q":{"q":"What does Point Hope's Iñupiaq name, Tikiġaq, mean?","a":"Index finger","x":["Whale's tail","Northern lights"]}},"Q79809":{"t":"The northernmost town in the United States","s":"Utqiaġvik, long known as Barrow, clings to the edge of the Arctic Ocean on flat tundra dotted with lakes. Just to the north-east, Point Barrow is the northernmost tip of the United States, where the Chukchi and Beaufort seas meet. Here the sun stays below the horizon for about two months in winter, and in summer it doesn't set for nearly three months.","w":"A small town on a flat, lake-spotted coastal plain at the Arctic shore; a thin gravel spit curving north-east to the point.","f":["The polar night here lasts from mid-November to late January.","The town was named Barrow after Sir John Barrow of the British Admiralty; in 2016 it officially took back its Iñupiaq name."],"q":{"q":"How long does the polar night last in Utqiaġvik?","a":"About two months","x":["About two weeks","About five months"]}},"Q79996":{"t":"A gold-rush town where the gold lay in the beach","s":"Nome faces the grey waters of Norton Sound from the edge of the treeless Seward Peninsula. In 1899 prospectors discovered gold in the sand of the beach itself, and within a year a tent city of some 20,000 people crowded the shore. No road links Nome to the rest of Alaska, yet every March it is the finish of the Iditarod sled-dog race.","w":"A small grid town on a straight coast of Norton Sound; bare tundra hills and snowy mountains inland.","f":["People still pan and dredge for gold along Nome's beaches in summer.","The Iditarod trail runs more than 1,500 km from near Anchorage to Nome."],"q":{"q":"Where was the gold of Nome's 1899–1900 rush famously found?","a":"In the beach sand","x":["Deep in a mountain mine","Under a glacier"]}},"Q80037":{"t":"A fjord port where the Alaska pipeline meets the sea","s":"Steep, glacier-hung peaks of the Chugach Mountains box in Valdez at the head of a narrow fjord off Prince William Sound. Across the water lies the terminal of the Trans-Alaska Pipeline, which carries oil some 1,300 km from Prudhoe Bay on the Arctic coast to this harbour, ice-free all year. Winters here bring several metres of snow.","w":"A small town at the head of a long, steep-walled fjord; glaciers on the mountains; a tank farm on the opposite shore.","f":["A Spanish expedition named the place in 1790 after Antonio Valdés, Spain's naval minister.","The pipeline was completed in 1977 and crosses three mountain ranges on its way south."],"q":{"q":"Who named Valdez in 1790?","a":"A Spanish naval expedition","x":["Russian fur traders","Captain James Cook"]}},"Q29445":{"t":"A capital between an icefield and the sea, with no road in","s":"Juneau is squeezed onto a thin shelf between Gastineau Channel and mountains that rise almost straight from the rooftops. Behind them spreads the Juneau Icefield, feeding dozens of glaciers; one, the Mendenhall, flows down nearly to the suburbs by the airport. No road connects the city to the rest of North America. It all began with a gold strike in 1880.","w":"A narrow town at the foot of steep mountains on a sea channel; a broad glacier tongue reaching down to a lake near the airport.","f":["Juneau's municipal area is larger than the whole state of Rhode Island.","The 1880 gold find was made by Joe Juneau and Richard Harris, guided by the Tlingit leader Kowee."],"q":{"q":"How do you get to Juneau from the rest of the US?","a":"Only by air or sea","x":["By highway from Anchorage","By railway from Canada"]}},"Q79638":{"t":"A river town under the aurora, in the heart of Alaska","s":"Fairbanks lies deep in interior Alaska, where the Chena River meets the broad, braided Tanana, surrounded by spruce forest and low hills. It began as a trading post in 1901, and a gold strike the next year turned it into a boom town. Its climate is extreme: winters can drop below −40 °C, while summer nights barely get dark.","w":"A spread-out town on flat forested lowland beside a wide, braided river; the white wall of the Alaska Range far to the south.","f":["Since 1906 a Midnight Sun baseball game has been played here around the solstice without floodlights.","In deep winter, cold still air can trap a layer of ice fog over the city.","The town was named after Charles Fairbanks, a senator who later became US vice-president."],"q":{"q":"What is played in Fairbanks near midnight each June without floodlights?","a":"A baseball game","x":["A football match","A golf tournament"]}},"Q1013046":{"t":"An oil field on Arctic tundra, where the pipeline starts","s":"Prudhoe Bay is less a town than a work camp on the flat Arctic coast beside the Beaufort Sea. Beneath it lies the largest oil field in North America, found in 1968, and from here the Trans-Alaska Pipeline begins its journey of about 1,300 km south. Around it the tundra is speckled with thousands of lakes, many stretched in the same direction.","w":"Flat tundra peppered with elongated lakes; a web of gravel roads and pads; sea ice or grey sea to the north.","f":["Sir John Franklin named the bay in 1826 after his friend Algernon Percy, Lord Prudhoe.","The Dalton Highway, one of the most remote roads in the US, ends here at Deadhorse."],"q":{"q":"Who named Prudhoe Bay in 1826?","a":"Sir John Franklin","x":["James Cook","Vitus Bering"]}},"Q3238":{"t":"A capital in a bowl of pine-covered mountains","s":"Tegucigalpa fills a tight mountain basin about 1,000 m up, its houses spilling up the slopes from the Choluteca River. Across the river lies its twin, Comayagüela; together they form the capital. The town grew from a silver-mining settlement founded in 1578 and became Honduras's capital in 1880. Pine-covered ridges ring the whole bowl.","w":"A dense city in a small mountain bowl, climbing the surrounding slopes; a river through the middle; the airport in a valley to the south.","f":["Toncontín airport lies among hills, and pilots must make a tight, low turn on the approach.","The capital moved here from the colonial city of Comayagua, about 80 km to the north-west."],"q":{"q":"Which city faces Tegucigalpa across the Choluteca River?","a":"Comayagüela","x":["Comayagua","San Pedro Sula"]}},"Q10717":{"t":"A Dutch-drained capital hiding behind a seawall","s":"Georgetown lies at the mouth of the Demerara River on a coast so low that much of the city sits below the level of high tide. Dutch colonists drained it with canals and sluices, and a long seawall still holds back the Atlantic. The sea here is brown, stained by silt carried along the coast. The city took its present name in 1812, after King George III.","w":"A flat city at the mouth of a brown river on a straight, muddy coast; a long floating bridge across the river just upstream.","f":["The Demerara Harbour Bridge, nearly 2 km long, rests on floating pontoons.","St. George's Cathedral is one of the tallest wooden churches in the world."],"q":{"q":"What protects low-lying Georgetown from the Atlantic?","a":"A long seawall","x":["Coral reefs","High sand dunes"]}},"Q34261":{"t":"A capital at the head of a vast horseshoe bay","s":"Between Haiti's two long peninsulas the sea forms a great horseshoe, the Gulf of Gonâve, and Port-au-Prince lies at its innermost point. The city climbs from the waterfront up steep slopes towards the mountains of the Massif de la Selle. Out in the gulf lies the long, flat island of Gonâve. The French founded the city in 1749; Haiti won its independence in 1804.","w":"A city at the head of a wide bay, rising up mountain slopes to the south-east; a long island in the gulf to the west.","f":["The bay is said to take its name from a French ship, Le Prince, that anchored here in 1706.","Pic la Selle, Haiti's highest peak at about 2,680 m, stands in the mountains south-east of the city."],"q":{"q":"Which island lies out in the bay off Port-au-Prince?","a":"Gonâve","x":["Tortuga","Île-à-Vache"]}},"Q3001":{"t":"A wooden Dutch colonial capital on a jungle river","s":"Paramaribo lines the west bank of the wide, brown Suriname River, some 15 km from the Atlantic. Its old centre of white wooden houses and churches is a UNESCO World Heritage site. In 1667, under the Treaty of Breda, the Dutch kept this colony while England kept New Amsterdam — today's New York. Beyond the city, rainforest covers most of the country.","w":"A low city on the west bank of a broad brown river near the coast; a long bridge across it; unbroken green forest inland.","f":["The Jules Wijdenbosch Bridge across the Suriname River is about 1.5 km long.","Forest covers more than 90 per cent of Suriname, one of the highest shares of any country."],"q":{"q":"Under the 1667 treaty the Dutch kept Suriname. What did England keep?","a":"New Amsterdam (New York)","x":["Jamaica","Barbados"]}},"Q5582862":{"t":"The Inca capital, 3,400 m up in an Andean valley","s":"Cuzco fills a narrow valley high in the Andes, about 3,400 m above sea level, a sea of terracotta roofs between bare, brown-green slopes. This was the capital of the Inca Empire, which stretched along the Andes from today's Ecuador to Chile. The Spanish arrived in 1533 and built their churches on Inca stone walls. Machu Picchu lies about 75 km to the north-west.","w":"A city of red-brown roofs filling a high, narrow valley; bare mountains around; a runway squeezed along the valley floor.","f":["Tradition says the Inca city was laid out in the shape of a puma.","The historic centre has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1983.","The snowy peak of Ausangate, over 6,300 m high, rises about 100 km to the south-east."],"q":{"q":"Which empire had its capital at Cuzco?","a":"The Inca Empire","x":["The Aztec Empire","The Maya"]}},"Q272795":{"t":"A green oasis at Peru's southern tip","s":"Tacna is a patch of green in one of the driest places on Earth, the northern edge of the Atacama Desert, where rain may add up to only a few millimetres a year. The city lives off the thin Caplina River coming down from the Andes, whose snowy peaks line the eastern horizon. The border with Chile runs only about 35 km to the south.","w":"A mid-sized city in otherwise bare, beige desert; green irrigated fields along a narrow valley; the Andes rising to the east.","f":["Tacna is the southernmost city in Peru.","Olive groves around the city make the region one of Peru's main olive producers."],"q":{"q":"Which country's border lies just south of Tacna?","a":"Chile","x":["Bolivia","Argentina"]}},"Q214173":{"t":"An oval colonial core beside a vast mud-brick ruin","s":"On the desert coast of northern Peru, Trujillo keeps its colonial outline: an oval avenue traces the line of the old city wall around the centre. Just west, towards the sea, sprawl the eroded mud-brick ruins of Chan Chan, capital of the Chimú kingdom and the largest adobe city of the pre-Columbian Americas. The Spanish founded Trujillo in 1534.","w":"A city on a desert coastal plain between the sea and bare mountains; green irrigated valley fields around it.","f":["Chan Chan's ruins cover about 20 km² and have been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1986.","The city is named after Trujillo in Spain, home town of Francisco Pizarro.","Fishermen at nearby Huanchaco still head out on small reed boats called caballitos de totora."],"q":{"q":"Which people built Chan Chan, near Trujillo?","a":"The Chimú","x":["The Inca","The Nazca"]}},"Q840712":{"t":"Vineyards and giant dunes in the Peruvian desert","s":"Out of the coastal desert of southern Peru, Ica's valley shows up as a strip of green, watered by a river from the Andes and hemmed in by huge sand dunes. The irrigated valley is the heart of Peru's grape growing and the home of pisco, the local grape brandy. Just west of the city, the tiny lagoon of Huacachina sits in a hollow ringed by towering dunes.","w":"A city in a green valley strip cutting through pale desert; big, sharp-crested dunes to the west.","f":["The famous Nazca Lines lie roughly 100 km to the south-east, though they are too fine to see from cruise height.","Rain is so rare here that the valley depends almost entirely on water from the Andes."],"q":{"q":"What is Huacachina, near Ica?","a":"A small lagoon among dunes","x":["A volcanic crater lake","A salt flat"]}},"Q2933":{"t":"The 'Mother of Cities' on a bend of the Paraguay River","s":"Asunción spreads along the east bank of the Paraguay River where it curves around a wide bay. Across the water begins the Chaco, a flat, green-brown plain of marshes and scrub running west for hundreds of kilometres. Founded in 1537, it is one of the oldest cities in South America, and its settlers went on to found many others, including Buenos Aires for good in 1580.","w":"A city on the outside of a big river bend, beside a rounded bay; flat, wet, scrubby plains across the river to the west.","f":["The Pilcomayo River joins the Paraguay just downstream of the city.","Santa Cruz de la Sierra, now in Bolivia, was also founded by an expedition from Asunción, in 1561."],"q":{"q":"Why is Asunción called the 'Mother of Cities'?","a":"Its settlers founded many other cities","x":["It is the oldest city in the Americas","It has the oldest cathedral"]}},"Q3274":{"t":"A lakeside capital dotted with round crater lakes","s":"Managua stretches along the southern shore of Lake Managua, and within the city itself you can spot small, round crater lakes — old volcanic vents now filled with water. At the far north-western end of the lake rises the near-perfect cone of Momotombo. The city became Nicaragua's capital in 1852, a compromise between the rival cities of León and Granada.","w":"A low, sprawling city on a big lake's south shore; round blue crater lakes in and around it; a volcanic cone to the north-west.","f":["Laguna de Asososca, one of the crater lakes, supplies part of the city's drinking water.","Lake Nicaragua, the largest lake in Central America, lies about 30 km to the south-east."],"q":{"q":"Why was Managua made Nicaragua's capital in 1852?","a":"As a compromise between rival cities","x":["It had the best harbour","It was the largest city"]}},"Q3306":{"t":"Where ships queue for the Panama Canal","s":"Look out to sea off Panama City and you'll often see dozens of ships at anchor, waiting their turn for the canal. The city's towers line a curving bay right beside the canal's Pacific entrance. Founded in 1519, the original town was the first European city on the Pacific coast of the Americas. The canal itself opened in 1914 and cuts about 80 km across the isthmus.","w":"A high-rise city along a curving bay; ships dotted offshore; a canal channel and a bridge just west of the city.","f":["Oddly, the canal's Pacific end lies further east than its Atlantic end.","The Amador Causeway, linking small islands at the canal mouth, was built with rock dug out for the canal.","Locks lift ships about 26 m to Gatún Lake in the middle of the isthmus."],"q":{"q":"Which end of the Panama Canal lies further east?","a":"The Pacific end","x":["The Atlantic end","Both lie on the same longitude"]}},"Q124739":{"t":"A city pressed right up against the US border","s":"Tijuana fills a jumble of mesas and canyons in Mexico's far north-west corner, and its northern edge is a hard, straight line: the border with the United States, with San Diego beyond. The airport's runway runs just south of that line, so close that a footbridge lets passengers walk straight in from the US side. The city was officially founded in 1889.","w":"A dense city on hilly, canyon-cut ground, cut off to the north by a sharp straight line; the Pacific to the west.","f":["The San Ysidro crossing between Tijuana and San Diego is one of the busiest land border crossings in the world.","The Tijuana River flows north across the border and reaches the Pacific in the US."],"q":{"q":"What runs right alongside Tijuana airport's runway?","a":"The US–Mexico border","x":["The Pacific shore","An irrigation canal"]}},"Q61302":{"t":"A high-desert capital, gateway to the Copper Canyon","s":"Bare, rocky ranges ring the wide, dry plateau where Chihuahua stands, about 1,400 m up; it is the capital of Mexico's largest state. It was founded in 1709 as a silver-mining town. From here the Chihuahua–Pacific railway heads south-west across the Sierra Madre and through the Copper Canyon, a system of gorges that is deeper in places than the Grand Canyon.","w":"A city on a flat, tan high-desert basin, encircled by bare rocky ranges; the dark Sierra Madre far to the west.","f":["The Chihuahua–Pacific railway, completed in 1961, crosses dozens of bridges and runs through dozens of tunnels.","The tiny dog breed takes its name from the state of Chihuahua."],"q":{"q":"What sets out from Chihuahua through the Copper Canyon?","a":"A railway to the Pacific coast","x":["A navigable river","A cable car line"]}},"Q40194":{"t":"A Pacific port on a peninsula, just south of the tropic","s":"Mazatlán's rocky peninsula juts into the Pacific, with a long seafront curving north along the beach and three small islands just offshore. The Tropic of Cancer passes only about 25 km to the north, so at the June solstice the midday sun here is almost directly overhead. The name comes from Nahuatl and means 'place of deer'.","w":"A city on a knobbly peninsula and along a long, straight beach; small islands offshore; green hills and the Sierra Madre inland.","f":["The lighthouse on Cerro del Crestón shines from about 157 m above the sea, one of the highest in the world.","Ferries cross the Gulf of California from here to La Paz, in Baja California."],"q":{"q":"What does 'Mazatlán' mean in Nahuatl?","a":"Place of deer","x":["Place of fish","Place of the sun"]}},"Q273014":{"t":"An oil port among lagoons at the mouth of the Pánuco","s":"Tampico lies a few kilometres up the Pánuco River from the Gulf of Mexico, and water is everywhere: lagoons within the city, marshes around it, the wide brown river on its southern side. In the early 1900s oil struck nearby turned it into one of the busiest oil ports in the world. Look for the long cable-stayed bridge across the river.","w":"A city between lagoons and a wide river near the coast; marshes all around; a long bridge across the river to the south.","f":["The modern city was refounded in 1823 near the site of an older colonial settlement.","Laguna del Carpintero, a lagoon right in the city, is home to crocodiles."],"q":{"q":"What made Tampico boom in the early 1900s?","a":"Oil","x":["Silver","Sugar"]}},"Q81398":{"t":"A horseshoe bay ringed by a hillside amphitheatre","s":"Acapulco wraps around one of the most perfectly shaped bays on the Pacific, a deep blue horseshoe with the city climbing the steep hills on all sides like the seats of an amphitheatre. For some 250 years, from 1565, Spanish galleons sailed from here to Manila, carrying silver west and bringing back silk and porcelain from Asia.","w":"A deep, rounded bay almost closed by headlands; white buildings climbing the green hills around it; long beaches to the east.","f":["Cliff divers at La Quebrada plunge about 35 m into a narrow rocky inlet.","The star-shaped Fort of San Diego was built in the 1600s to guard the galleon harbour."],"q":{"q":"Which Asian port did Acapulco's galleons sail to?","a":"Manila","x":["Macau","Nagasaki"]}},"Q173270":{"t":"Where Cortés came ashore, with Orizaba on the horizon","s":"Veracruz lies on a low, sandy stretch of the Gulf of Mexico coast, with a busy port and, offshore, the pale shapes of coral reefs in turquoise water. Hernán Cortés landed on this coast in 1519 and founded Villa Rica de la Vera Cruz, the first Spanish town in what is now Mexico. On clear days, look west for the white cone of Pico de Orizaba.","w":"A flat port city on a straight Gulf coast; breakwaters and docks; pale reef patches offshore; a snowy volcano far inland to the west.","f":["The fortress of San Juan de Ulúa, begun in the 1500s, stands on a former island at the harbour mouth.","The reefs off the city are protected as a national marine park.","The town's site shifted more than once before it settled here around 1600."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Veracruz mean?","a":"True Cross","x":["Green Coast","Holy Harbour"]}},"Q207429":{"t":"A valley city on the edge of a 1,000-metre canyon","s":"Tuxtla Gutiérrez spreads across a warm, green valley in Chiapas, and just to its north-east the land splits open: the Sumidero Canyon, where the Grijalva River runs between cliffs rising up to about 1,000 m. From above it looks like a dark crack in the plateau. The name Tuxtla comes from a Nahuatl word usually translated as 'place of rabbits'.","w":"A long city in an east–west valley; to the north-east, a deep, winding gorge with a green-blue river at the bottom.","f":["Downstream, the Chicoasén Dam turns the river into a long reservoir winding through the mountains.","The second half of the name honours Joaquín Miguel Gutiérrez, a 19th-century politician from Chiapas."],"q":{"q":"Which canyon lies just north-east of Tuxtla Gutiérrez?","a":"The Sumidero Canyon","x":["The Copper Canyon","The Huasteca Canyon"]}},"Q8969":{"t":"A resort shaped like a 7 on turquoise water","s":"Cancún's hotel zone is easy to spot: a thin strip of white sand shaped like the number 7, with the turquoise Caribbean on one side and the calm, darker Nichupté Lagoon on the other. Until about 1970 there was almost nothing here; the resort was planned from scratch. Inland stretches the flat green Yucatán, without a single river on its surface.","w":"A narrow white island shaped like a 7 enclosing a lagoon; bright turquoise sea to the east; flat green jungle inland.","f":["Just offshore begins the Mesoamerican Reef, the second-largest barrier reef system in the world.","On the Yucatán's limestone, rainwater drains underground into sinkholes called cenotes.","Isla Mujeres, a small island to the north, lies about 13 km across the water."],"q":{"q":"What shape is the island of Cancún's hotel zone?","a":"The number 7","x":["A crescent","A ring"]}},"Q165204":{"t":"The White City above a buried asteroid crater","s":"Mérida spreads over the dead-flat limestone plain of the Yucatán, with no river, hill or lake to break the scrub. Beneath it lies a secret: the buried Chicxulub crater, blasted by the asteroid that helped end the age of dinosaurs, is centred on the coast just north of the city. The Spanish founded Mérida in 1542 on the site of the Maya city of T'hó.","w":"A large, low, pale city on a perfectly flat green plain; the coast and the port of Progreso about 35 km to the north.","f":["A ring of cenotes, water-filled sinkholes, traces part of the crater's buried rim around the city.","In the late 1800s sisal fibre from henequen plantations made Mérida one of Mexico's richest cities.","The crater is about 180 km across, but it lies buried and cannot be seen from the air."],"q":{"q":"What lies buried beneath the land around Mérida?","a":"An asteroid impact crater","x":["A volcanic caldera","An ancient salt lake"]}},"Q2900":{"t":"A long, thin capital on a volcano, just below the equator","s":"Quito is squeezed into a narrow Andean valley about 2,850 m up, stretching some 40 km from north to south along the flank of the Pichincha volcano. The equator runs only about 25 km to the north. On a clear day, look south for the perfect snow cone of Cotopaxi. In 1978 Quito's old town was among the very first places named a World Heritage site.","w":"A long, narrow city pressed against the slopes of a large volcano; deep ravines to the east; snowy cones on the horizon.","f":["Cotopaxi, about 5,900 m high, is one of the highest active volcanoes in the world.","The airport stands on a plateau east of the city, lower than Quito itself."],"q":{"q":"How far is Quito from the equator?","a":"About 25 km","x":["About 250 km","It lies right on it"]}},"Q3070":{"t":"A capital in a green valley ringed by volcanoes","s":"San José fills the heart of Costa Rica's Central Valley, about 1,150 m up: a bowl of red roofs and coffee-green hills rimmed by volcanoes. To the north rise Poás and Barva, to the east Irazú and Turrialba. From Irazú's summit, on a rare clear day, both the Pacific and the Caribbean are visible. The city became the national capital in 1823.","w":"A sprawling city filling a green valley, with volcanic mountains to the north and east; coffee plantations on the slopes.","f":["Irazú, about 3,430 m high, is Costa Rica's highest volcano.","Coffee grown on the volcanic slopes in the 1800s paid for much of the city's early grandeur, including its National Theatre."],"q":{"q":"From which volcano near San José can both oceans be seen?","a":"Irazú","x":["Arenal","Masaya"]}},"Q3110":{"t":"A capital between a volcano and a caldera lake","s":"San Salvador spreads across a valley at the foot of the San Salvador volcano, whose summit crater, El Boquerón, is a gaping round pit about 1.5 km wide. East of the city lies Lake Ilopango, filling the caldera of a colossal eruption some 1,500 years ago. Spanish settlers moved the town to this valley in the 1500s, and today it is the nation's capital.","w":"A dense city at the eastern foot of a big volcano with a round summit crater; a large blue caldera lake to the east.","f":["The Boquerón crater is roughly 500 m deep, with a small cone inside it.","Lake Ilopango covers about 70 km²."],"q":{"q":"Which lake fills the caldera east of San Salvador?","a":"Lake Ilopango","x":["Lake Atitlán","Lake Coatepeque"]}},"Q34692":{"t":"A harbour city behind a long, thin sand spit","s":"Kingston faces one of the world's great natural harbours, sealed off from the Caribbean by the Palisadoes, a sand spit more than 10 km long and barely wide enough for a road and the airport runway. At its tip lies Port Royal, once a notorious haunt of buccaneers. Behind the city rise the Blue Mountains, home of one of the world's most famous coffees.","w":"A wide, calm harbour enclosed by a long, curving spit with a runway; a sprawling city below mountains to the north.","f":["Blue Mountain Peak, about 2,260 m high, is the highest point in Jamaica.","Kingston was laid out in 1692 and became Jamaica's capital in 1872."],"q":{"q":"What sits on the long spit enclosing Kingston Harbour?","a":"The international airport","x":["A railway line","Salt works"]}},"Q657461":{"t":"A walled colonial port among bays and lagoons","s":"Cartagena is a maze of water: a large, sheltered bay with islands, inner lagoons and channels, and a narrow peninsula lined with white towers. At its heart the old town is still ringed by colonial stone walls, about 11 km of them, built to protect a treasure port. Pedro de Heredia founded the city in 1533; its walled centre is a World Heritage site.","w":"A coastal city spread around bays and lagoons; a thin peninsula of high-rises; islands in the bay; coral islets to the south-west.","f":["The Rosario Islands, a coral archipelago south-west of the city, are protected as a national park.","Spanish treasure fleets called here to load silver and gold bound for Spain."],"q":{"q":"What surrounds Cartagena's old town?","a":"Colonial stone walls","x":["A wide ring canal","Earthen dykes"]}},"Q130242":{"t":"A river town lost in an ocean of Amazon forest","s":"In every direction from Mitú, unbroken rainforest runs to the horizon. This small town on the Vaupés River, in Colombia's far south-east, has no road link to the rest of the country; people and goods arrive by plane or by boat. From above, the river winds through the green like a dark ribbon, with the town's clearing and airstrip on its bank.","w":"A small clearing with an airstrip beside a winding dark river; endless green forest canopy all around.","f":["The Vaupés flows east into Brazil, where, as the Uaupés, it joins the Rio Negro.","Most of the region's inhabitants belong to indigenous peoples such as the Cubeo and Tukano."],"q":{"q":"How do you reach Mitú from the rest of Colombia?","a":"Only by air or by river","x":["By highway","By railway"]}},"Q1555":{"t":"A ravine-cut plateau capital watched by volcanoes","s":"Deep green ravines slice the highland plateau of Guatemala City, about 1,500 m up, so the city spreads along flat fingers of land between them. To the south-west stands a row of volcanoes: Agua, Acatenango and Fuego, which often trails a plume of ash. The capital moved here in 1776 from Antigua, in the next valley to the west.","w":"A large city on a plateau split by deep green ravines; tall volcanic cones to the south-west, one often smoking.","f":["The ruins of Kaminaljuyú, a large Maya settlement founded more than 2,000 years ago, lie within today's city.","Pacaya, another active volcano, rises just south of the city."],"q":{"q":"Which city was Guatemala's capital before 1776?","a":"Antigua Guatemala","x":["Quetzaltenango","Tikal"]}},"Q44401":{"t":"A slice of France on the edge of the Amazon forest","s":"Muddy brown sea in front, rainforest behind: Cayenne occupies a small, hilly peninsula at the mouth of the Cayenne River. French Guiana is part of France, so this is a European Union city in South America, and it uses the euro. About 60 km up the coast lies Kourou, where Europe's rockets are launched close to the equator.","w":"A small city on a knobbly peninsula at a river mouth; brown, muddy sea; dense green forest inland.","f":["The brown coastal water is stained by Amazon silt, carried north-west along the shore by currents.","Forest covers the vast majority of French Guiana's land."],"q":{"q":"Which currency is used in Cayenne?","a":"The euro","x":["The Brazilian real","The Guyanese dollar"]}},"Q51599":{"t":"A windswept port on the Strait of Magellan","s":"Punta Arenas stretches along the western shore of the Strait of Magellan, facing the low, tawny hills of Tierra del Fuego across the water. Ferdinand Magellan's ships threaded this passage in 1520. Before the Panama Canal opened, the town was a busy coaling stop for steamers passing between the oceans. Today planes leave from here for Antarctica.","w":"A city strung along a wide grey-blue strait; open, treeless steppe inland; the flat shores of Tierra del Fuego across the water.","f":["Chile founded the city in 1848.","It is one of the southernmost cities in the world with more than 100,000 people."],"q":{"q":"Which strait does Punta Arenas face?","a":"The Strait of Magellan","x":["The Drake Passage","The Beagle Channel"]}},"Q14440":{"t":"A desert port wedged under a cliff and a giant dune","s":"Iquique occupies a narrow ledge between the Pacific and a coastal escarpment that rises some 600 m straight behind it — the edge of the Atacama Desert. Inside the city sits a huge sand dune, Cerro Dragón. Up on the bare plateau inland lie the ghost towns of the 19th-century nitrate boom that made this port rich.","w":"A city on a thin coastal strip under a tall tan escarpment; a large pale dune among the houses; utterly bare desert above.","f":["The abandoned Humberstone and Santa Laura nitrate works inland are a UNESCO World Heritage site.","Rain is so rare here that years can pass without a single shower."],"q":{"q":"What unusual natural feature stands inside Iquique?","a":"A huge sand dune","x":["A volcano","A glacier"]}},"Q3612":{"t":"A copper port on a desert coast by the tropic","s":"Antofagasta stretches for kilometres along a narrow strip of coast, hemmed in by the bare brown wall of the Coastal Range and the cold blue Pacific. The Tropic of Capricorn crosses just north of the city. Inland lies some of the driest land on Earth, and some of the world's biggest copper mines, whose metal is shipped out through this port.","w":"A long, thin city along a straight coast beneath a steep brown escarpment; completely bare desert beyond.","f":["The desert skies are so clear that major observatories, including Paranal, stand about 120 km to the south.","The city was founded in 1868 to serve mining in the desert.","La Portada, a natural sea arch just north of the city, is a protected natural monument."],"q":{"q":"Which line of latitude passes just north of Antofagasta?","a":"The Tropic of Capricorn","x":["The Tropic of Cancer","The equator"]}},"Q33986":{"t":"A port city climbing dozens of hills round a bay","s":"Valparaíso wraps around a wide bay in a steep amphitheatre, its houses climbing more than forty hills that rise straight from a thin strip of flat land by the port. For decades before the Panama Canal it was the main stop for ships rounding Cape Horn. Its maze of hillside neighbourhoods is a World Heritage site, and Viña del Mar merges with it to the north.","w":"A crescent bay with a port; the city rising steeply up many hills behind it; a resort city continuing along the coast to the north.","f":["Some of the funicular lifts that carry people up the hills date from the late 1800s.","Santiago lies about 110 km to the south-east, beyond the Coastal Range."],"q":{"q":"How do many people in Valparaíso ride up the hills?","a":"On old funicular lifts","x":["On a cable car","On a rack railway"]}},"Q160687":{"t":"A city of rivers in Chile's green, rainy south","s":"Valdivia is laced with water: several rivers meet here and wind west through wetlands and forest to the sea at Corral Bay, about 15 km away. Around it grows dense, evergreen temperate rainforest, fed by heavy rain. Pedro de Valdivia founded the city in 1552 and named it after himself. From the 1850s German settlers arrived and left their mark on the town.","w":"A city on meandering dark rivers amid green wetlands and forest; the rivers widening west into a bay on the Pacific.","f":["In the 1600s and 1700s the Spanish ringed Corral Bay with forts to guard the river route to the city.","Sea lions often lounge beside the riverside fish market in the city centre."],"q":{"q":"Who is Valdivia named after?","a":"Its founder, Pedro de Valdivia","x":["A Chilean president","A Mapuche chief"]}},"Q1880":{"t":"A university city at the mouth of the broad Biobío","s":"The Biobío, one of Chile's longest and widest rivers, spreads out before reaching the Pacific at Concepción, and several long bridges cross it here. Lagoons dot the city, and the port of Talcahuano lies on the bay just to the north. For centuries the Biobío marked the frontier between Spanish Chile and the lands of the Mapuche.","w":"A city between low hills and a very wide river mouth crossed by long bridges; a large bay with a port to the north.","f":["In the mid-1700s the city moved to its present site from the nearby coast at Penco.","The Universidad de Concepción, founded in 1919, is one of Chile's oldest universities."],"q":{"q":"Which river reaches the sea at Concepción?","a":"The Biobío","x":["The Maule","The Mapocho"]}},"Q36214":{"t":"Where Chile's mainland breaks into fjords and islands","s":"Puerto Montt marks the point where Chile's long Central Valley meets the sea and the coast begins to shatter into fjords, channels and islands. The city sits on the Reloncaví Sound, with Chiloé Island to the south-west and, to the north-east, the snowy, near-perfect cone of Osorno volcano rising beyond Lake Llanquihue. German settlers founded it in 1853.","w":"A city on a sheltered sound full of islands; a big lake just to the north; snow-capped volcanic cones to the north-east.","f":["The Carretera Austral, a road of more than 1,200 km into Patagonia, begins here.","Lake Llanquihue, just north of the city, covers about 860 km²."],"q":{"q":"Which famous road begins at Puerto Montt?","a":"The Carretera Austral","x":["Ruta 40","The Inca Road"]}},"Q226":{"t":"A small Arctic capital at the mouth of a giant fjord","s":"Nuuk clings to a rocky headland at the mouth of a vast fjord system that reaches more than 100 km inland towards the Greenland ice sheet. Across the water rises the sharp peak of Sermitsiaq. The name simply means 'headland'. The missionary Hans Egede founded the colony here in 1728, but Norse farmers had lived in these fjords centuries earlier.","w":"A small town on a bare rocky peninsula between fjord arms; dark peaks and icebergs around; the white ice sheet far inland.","f":["Norse settlers farmed the fjords near Nuuk from about the year 1000 until the mid-1300s.","Nuuk is one of the northernmost capitals in the world.","Sermitsiaq, the landmark peak across the fjord, rises about 1,200 m straight from the sea."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Nuuk mean?","a":"Headland","x":["Iceberg","Harbour"]}},"Q941136":{"t":"A far-west Amazon town on a wildly winding river","s":"Cruzeiro do Sul lies near Brazil's western edge, on the Juruá River, one of the most tightly meandering rivers on Earth. From above, the Juruá loops back on itself again and again, leaving crescent-shaped oxbow lakes in the forest. To the west, the low green hills of the Serra do Divisor rise towards the border with Peru. The name means 'Southern Cross'.","w":"A small town on a river looping in tight curves through unbroken forest; crescent lakes left by old bends; hills to the west.","f":["The westernmost point of Brazil lies in the Serra do Divisor, west of the town.","It is the second-largest town in the state of Acre."],"q":{"q":"What is Cruzeiro do Sul named after?","a":"The Southern Cross constellation","x":["A Portuguese ship","A mission church"]}},"Q214913":{"t":"Where three countries meet on the Amazon","s":"The Colombian town of Leticia runs straight into Brazil's Tabatinga, with only a street marking the border, while Peru lies across the wide, island-studded Amazon. This is the point where the three countries meet. No road reaches Leticia from the rest of Colombia; the river and the airport are its lifelines, and forest stretches for hundreds of kilometres all around.","w":"Twin towns on the bank of a very wide brown river with long forested islands; endless forest on every side.","f":["Thanks to Leticia, Colombia holds a stretch of Amazon riverbank about 100 km long.","The town was founded in 1867."],"q":{"q":"Which Brazilian town is joined to Leticia?","a":"Tabatinga","x":["Manaus","Iquitos"]}},"Q40236":{"t":"Where black water and brown water flow side by side","s":"Just below Manaus, look for one of the great sights of the Amazon: the Meeting of the Waters. The dark, tea-coloured Rio Negro meets the pale, muddy Solimões, and for several kilometres the two run side by side in one channel without mixing. The city itself, deep in the rainforest, grew rich in the rubber boom of the late 1800s.","w":"A big city on the north bank of a dark river; downstream, a sharp line where black and coffee-coloured water meet.","f":["The waters stay apart because they differ in temperature, speed and the silt they carry.","Ocean-going ships sail some 1,500 km up the Amazon to reach the city's port.","The Teatro Amazonas opera house, opened in 1896, was built with rubber-boom wealth."],"q":{"q":"Which two rivers form the Meeting of the Waters at Manaus?","a":"The Rio Negro and the Solimões","x":["The Madeira and the Tapajós","The Xingu and the Tocantins"]}},"Q997107":{"t":"A river town among babassu palms in Maranhão","s":"Caxias lies on the Itapecuru River in the interior of Maranhão, where the dry cerrado savanna gives way to the wetter forests of the Amazon basin. The country around it is dotted with babassu palms, whose oily nuts are gathered by hand. The poet Gonçalves Dias, author of 'Song of Exile', one of Brazil's best-loved poems, was born near here in 1823.","w":"A mid-sized town on a winding river in rolling green country; scattered palm groves and patches of savanna.","f":["Brazil's famous military leader, the Duke of Caxias, took his noble title from this town.","The Itapecuru River flows north to the Atlantic near São Luís."],"q":{"q":"Which famous Brazilian poet was born near Caxias?","a":"Gonçalves Dias","x":["Carlos Drummond de Andrade","Machado de Assis"]}},"Q659450":{"t":"Where the blue Tapajós meets the muddy Amazon","s":"Santarém sits right on a colour line: the clear, blue-green Tapajós flows into the brown, silt-laden Amazon in front of the city, and the two waters run side by side for kilometres. Upstream the Tapajós widens into a lake-like reach fringed with white sand beaches. Jesuits founded a mission here in 1661, roughly halfway between Manaus and Belém.","w":"A town on a point between a blue-green river and a much wider brown one; pale sandbanks and forest around.","f":["In 1928 Henry Ford built a rubber-plantation town, Fordlândia, far up the Tapajós.","In the dry season the falling Tapajós uncovers white beaches at nearby Alter do Chão."],"q":{"q":"Which river meets the Amazon at Santarém?","a":"The Tapajós","x":["The Xingu","The Madeira"]}},"Q509818":{"t":"A frontier city where two Amazon rivers meet","s":"On a point of land where the Itacaiúnas River flows into the broad Tocantins stands Marabá, in the south-east of Pará. The town grew in the early 1900s on the Brazil-nut trade. Look around it for the patchwork of pasture and remnant forest. To the west rise the hills of Carajás, home to one of the world's largest iron-ore mines.","w":"A city on a point where two broad rivers meet; flooded islands and sandbars; pastures and forest patches all around.","f":["The Carajás Railway, carrying iron ore about 900 km to the coast near São Luís, crosses the Tocantins here.","In the rainy season the rivers rise several metres and often flood the low parts of town."],"q":{"q":"Which huge iron-ore district lies west of Marabá?","a":"Carajás","x":["Itabira","The Iron Quadrangle"]}},"Q1749958":{"t":"A plateau town at the gateway to Rondônia","s":"Vast rectangles of soy and maize, with strips of forest along the streams, surround Vilhena on the broad Parecis plateau, about 600 m up, at the south-eastern entrance to the state of Rondônia. The town began as a station on the telegraph line that Cândido Rondon's expeditions pushed into the Amazon in the early 1900s.","w":"A town on a flat plateau among huge rectangular fields; a highway running straight through; forest strips along the streams.","f":["It is named after Álvaro Vilhena, a director of Brazil's telegraph service.","The BR-364 highway through town is the main road link between Rondônia and the rest of Brazil."],"q":{"q":"What did Vilhena begin as?","a":"A telegraph station","x":["A rubber port","A gold camp"]}},"Q983815":{"t":"A river town amid Rondônia's fishbone clearings","s":"Ji-Paraná straddles the Machado River, also called the Ji-Paraná, in the heart of Rondônia. Around it lies one of the most striking patterns on the planet: long, straight side roads branching off the main highway, each lined with clearings, so the forest looks cut into a giant fishbone. Most of this settlement dates from the 1970s onward.","w":"A city on both banks of a winding river; a ladder-like pattern of fields and roads cut into the surrounding forest.","f":["It is the second-largest city in Rondônia, after the capital, Porto Velho.","The river flows north to join the Madeira, one of the Amazon's great tributaries."],"q":{"q":"What pattern do forest clearings form around Ji-Paraná?","a":"A fishbone","x":["Concentric circles","A honeycomb"]}},"Q168929":{"t":"The 'Brown City' on red soil, gateway to the Pantanal","s":"Campo Grande spreads across a gentle plateau in Brazil's centre-west, laid out with broad, tree-lined avenues. The soil here is a deep reddish-brown, which earned it the nickname Cidade Morena, the 'Brown City'. Around it lie cattle pastures and soy fields, and a couple of hundred kilometres west begins the Pantanal, the world's largest tropical wetland.","w":"A spacious city on a gently rolling plateau; red-brown soil showing between the fields and pastures all around.","f":["Sobá, a noodle soup brought by immigrants from Okinawa, is an official part of the city's cultural heritage.","The city became capital of the new state of Mato Grosso do Sul, created in 1977."],"q":{"q":"What is Campo Grande's nickname, inspired by its soil?","a":"The Brown City","x":["The Marvellous City","The City of Light"]}},"Q132997":{"t":"An island capital of lagoons, dunes and beaches","s":"Most of Florianópolis lies on Santa Catarina Island, a green, hilly island about 50 km long, joined to the mainland by bridges. Look for the Lagoa da Conceição, a long lagoon behind white dunes, and dozens of beaches strung along the coast. Settlers from the Azores arrived in the 1700s, and the city took its present name in 1894.","w":"A long, hilly island parallel to the coast, with a lagoon and pale dunes on its east side; bridges to the mainland.","f":["The Hercílio Luz Bridge, opened in 1926, is Brazil's longest suspension bridge.","The name honours Floriano Peixoto, one of the first presidents of the Brazilian republic."],"q":{"q":"Where does most of Florianópolis lie?","a":"On an island","x":["In a river delta","On a mountain plateau"]}},"Q194343":{"t":"A crossroads city that grew from a cattle fair","s":"About 100 km inland from Salvador, where Bahia's green coastal belt starts giving way to the dry sertão, Feira de Santana spreads over a gentle plateau. The town grew in the 1700s around a cattle fair beside a chapel of Saint Anne, which gave it its name. Today several of north-east Brazil's main highways meet here, making it one of the region's busiest road junctions.","w":"A large, compact city on a flat plateau; highways radiating out in several directions; greener land east, drier scrub west.","f":["It is the second-largest city in the state of Bahia.","Locals call it the 'Princess of the Sertão'."],"q":{"q":"What gave Feira de Santana its name?","a":"A cattle fair","x":["A fish market","A salt market"]}},"Q2135":{"t":"A prairie city on the bed of a vanished glacial lake","s":"The land around Winnipeg is so flat because it is the floor of an ancient lake: glacial Lake Agassiz, which covered much of this region at the end of the last ice age. The Red and Assiniboine rivers meet in the city centre, at a spot people have used as a meeting place for some 6,000 years. East of the city, look for a long, straight channel: the Red River Floodway.","w":"A city on dead-flat plains at a river junction; a long flood channel curving round its eastern side; square fields beyond.","f":["The Floodway, about 47 km long, carries Red River floodwater around the city.","The name comes from a Cree word for 'muddy water', first given to Lake Winnipeg to the north."],"q":{"q":"What does the Cree word 'Winnipeg' mean?","a":"Muddy water","x":["Big prairie","Meeting place"]}},"Q744553":{"t":"The polar bear town on the shore of Hudson Bay","s":"Every autumn polar bears gather on the shore around Churchill, waiting for Hudson Bay to freeze so they can hunt seals on the ice. The tiny town stands where the Churchill River meets the bay, right at the edge of the treeline: stunted spruce to the south, open tundra to the north. In summer thousands of beluga whales crowd into the river mouth.","w":"A small town on a rocky point at a river mouth on a huge grey bay; flat tundra and countless ponds inland.","f":["No road reaches Churchill; it is linked to the south only by rail and by air.","Across the river mouth stands Prince of Wales Fort, a stone fort built by the Hudson's Bay Company between 1731 and 1771."],"q":{"q":"Why do polar bears gather near Churchill every autumn?","a":"To wait for the bay to freeze","x":["To follow caribou herds","To find caves to hibernate in"]}},"Q2123":{"t":"A capital planted by hand on the treeless prairie","s":"Regina rises from some of the flattest, most open prairie in Canada, where the only trees are the ones people planted. Before 1882 the spot was known as Pile o' Bones, after heaps of bison bones by the creek. That year Princess Louise renamed it Regina, Latin for 'queen', after her mother, Queen Victoria. Look for the man-made Wascana Lake in the city's green heart.","w":"A compact city on a vast, flat, chequered prairie; a curving lake and parkland in its centre; railway lines running straight out.","f":["Wascana Centre, the park around the lake, is larger than New York's Central Park.","Canada's national police, the RCMP, has trained its recruits in Regina since the 1880s."],"q":{"q":"What was Regina's site called before 1882?","a":"Pile o' Bones","x":["Buffalo Crossing","Fort Qu'Appelle"]}},"Q10566":{"t":"The Bridge City on the South Saskatchewan River","s":"Saskatoon straddles the South Saskatchewan River, which winds through the city in a wooded valley crossed by a string of bridges — hence the nickname, the Bridge City. Around it, flat farmland stretches to the horizon in tidy squares of wheat and canola. The town was founded in 1883 as a temperance colony and named after a sweet local berry.","w":"A city on both banks of a winding river with several bridges close together; flat squares of cropland all around.","f":["The saskatoon berry takes its name from the Cree word misâskwatômina.","Potash mines around the city tap one of the world's largest deposits of this fertiliser mineral."],"q":{"q":"What is Saskatoon named after?","a":"A local berry","x":["A Cree chief","A fur trader"]}},"Q36312":{"t":"Where the prairie runs up against the Rockies","s":"On a clear day the white wall of the Rocky Mountains fills Calgary's western horizon; the city stands where the flat prairie begins to ripple into foothills. The Bow and Elbow rivers meet downtown, where the North-West Mounted Police built Fort Calgary in 1875. In winter, look for a band of clear sky over the mountains: the chinook arch, sign of a warm wind.","w":"A large city on rolling land where plains meet foothills; two rivers joining in the centre; snowy mountains to the west.","f":["A chinook wind can raise the temperature here by more than 20 °C in a few hours.","In 1988 Calgary hosted the first Winter Olympics held in Canada."],"q":{"q":"What is the warm winter wind that sweeps into Calgary called?","a":"The chinook","x":["The mistral","The Santa Ana"]}},"Q1015639":{"t":"A rainy island port on Canada's northern Pacific coast","s":"Dark, forested mountains and fjord-like inlets surround Prince Rupert on Kaien Island, near the mouth of the Skeena River, with the Alaska Panhandle just to the north. It is one of the rainiest cities in Canada. Its deep harbour is among the closest North American ports to Asia, which is why a transcontinental railway was pushed through to it, reaching the coast in 1914.","w":"A small town on a hilly island among forested mountains and channels; a large, deep harbour with container docks.","f":["The name was chosen in a 1906 contest and honours Prince Rupert of the Rhine, first governor of the Hudson's Bay Company.","The Skeena, whose mouth lies just to the south-east, is one of British Columbia's great salmon rivers."],"q":{"q":"How was the name Prince Rupert chosen in 1906?","a":"Through a public contest","x":["After a royal visit","After a ship's name"]}},"Q2132":{"t":"A garden capital at the southern tip of Vancouver Island","s":"From the southern tip of Vancouver Island, Victoria looks across the Strait of Juan de Fuca to the snowy Olympic Mountains in the United States. Sheltered in their rain shadow, it enjoys one of the mildest, driest climates in Canada. The Hudson's Bay Company built Fort Victoria here in 1843, and the town boomed with the gold rush of 1858.","w":"A city on a rocky, indented shore at an island's southern tip; a busy inner harbour; snowy mountains across the strait to the south.","f":["Victoria is the capital of British Columbia, though Vancouver is far larger.","Floatplanes take off and land in the Inner Harbour, right beside downtown."],"q":{"q":"When was Fort Victoria founded?","a":"In 1843","x":["In 1792","In 1886"]}},"Q638017":{"t":"An Inuit hamlet in a pocket of the Arctic coast","s":"Arctic Bay nestles in a small, round bay almost enclosed by steep, bare hills on northern Baffin Island — its Inuktitut name, Ikpiarjuk, means 'the pocket'. It opens onto Admiralty Inlet, a giant fjord more than 300 km long, where narwhals gather in summer. The English name comes from the whaling ship Arctic, which called here in 1872.","w":"A tiny settlement in a nearly enclosed bay; brown, flat-topped hills; a long, wide fjord covered in ice for much of the year.","f":["In winter the sun stays below the horizon here for more than two months.","It is one of the northernmost communities in Canada."],"q":{"q":"What does Arctic Bay's Inuktitut name, Ikpiarjuk, mean?","a":"The pocket","x":["The whale","The ice"]}},"Q606622":{"t":"The 'place with no dawn' on a gravel polar desert","s":"Resolute lies on the south coast of Cornwallis Island, deep in Canada's Arctic archipelago, on a barren plain of grey gravel with barely a plant in sight. Its Inuktitut name, Qausuittuq, means 'place with no dawn', for the long winter darkness. The English name comes from HMS Resolute, one of the British ships that explored these channels in the 1850s.","w":"A tiny settlement and airstrip on a bare, grey-brown coastal plain; sea ice or dark channels between flat islands.","f":["Timber from HMS Resolute was later made into a desk that still stands in the White House.","So little rain or snow falls here that the land counts as a polar desert.","Expeditions heading for the North Pole often set off from the airstrip here."],"q":{"q":"Where did timber from the ship HMS Resolute end up?","a":"In a desk in the White House","x":["In Big Ben's clock tower","In a Canadian cathedral"]}},"Q1723761":{"t":"A hamlet sitting right on the Arctic Circle","s":"Naujaat lies almost exactly on the Arctic Circle, on the north shore of a bay at the base of the Melville Peninsula. Its Inuktitut name means 'seagull nesting place', after the cliffs nearby. Its old English name, Repulse Bay, dates from 1742, when the explorer Christopher Middleton found that the bay was a dead end rather than the hoped-for Northwest Passage.","w":"A small settlement on a bare, rocky shore of a wide bay; low tundra hills and countless lakes inland.","f":["The Scottish explorer John Rae wintered here in 1846–47, living largely off the land as the Inuit did.","The hamlet officially took the name Naujaat in 2015."],"q":{"q":"Why did Middleton name this place Repulse Bay in 1742?","a":"It was a dead end, not a passage","x":["Local people drove his ships away","A storm forced him back"]}},"Q2061":{"t":"A gold town on the shore of North America's deepest lake","s":"The lake on Yellowknife's doorstep, Great Slave Lake, plunges to about 614 m, the deepest in North America. All around, ice-age glaciers scraped the Canadian Shield down to pink-grey rock and thousands of lakes. The town grew from gold strikes in the 1930s, and on clear winter nights it's one of the best places on Earth to watch the northern lights.","w":"A small city on the rocky shore of a vast lake; bare rock, scattered spruce and countless small lakes all around.","f":["The name comes from the copper knives traded by the local Dene people, the Yellowknives.","In winter an ice road runs across the frozen bay to the nearby community of Dettah."],"q":{"q":"Which lake does Yellowknife stand beside?","a":"Great Slave Lake","x":["Great Bear Lake","Lake Athabasca"]}},"Q201313":{"t":"An old fur post on the Mackenzie, near the Arctic Circle","s":"Just south of the Arctic Circle, Fort Good Hope looks out over the Mackenzie, Canada's longest river, from its east bank. A little upstream the river squeezes through the Ramparts, a gorge between limestone cliffs. The North West Company opened a fur-trading post here in 1805, one of the oldest on the lower Mackenzie. Around it stretch spruce forest, muskeg and lakes.","w":"A tiny settlement on a very wide river; a narrow gorge just upstream; flat forest and bog dotted with lakes all around.","f":["Together with its headwaters, the Mackenzie forms Canada's longest river system, over 4,000 km long.","Our Lady of Good Hope church, with its hand-painted 19th-century interior, is a National Historic Site."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows past Fort Good Hope?","a":"The Mackenzie","x":["The Yukon","The Peace"]}},"Q2058":{"t":"A river capital named for rapids like white horses","s":"Whitehorse lies in a broad valley of the Yukon River, ringed by rounded, forested mountains. The name comes from the rapids just upstream, whose foam reminded gold seekers of the manes of galloping white horses; a dam has since tamed them into a lake. In 1898 thousands of Klondike stampeders passed through, building boats to float some 700 km downriver to Dawson City.","w":"A small city on a terrace beside a winding river; a canyon and a dammed lake upstream; forested mountains all around.","f":["The White Pass and Yukon Route railway from Skagway, on the coast, reached Whitehorse in 1900.","About two-thirds of all the Yukon's people live in Whitehorse."],"q":{"q":"What gave Whitehorse its name?","a":"Foaming river rapids","x":["A white mountain","A famous horse"]}},"Q181056":{"t":"A fan-shaped city north of the equator in Brazil","s":"Boa Vista is laid out like an open fan: its avenues radiate from a civic centre near the bank of the Rio Branco, a plan drawn up in the 1940s that stands out clearly from the air. It is the only Brazilian state capital lying wholly in the Northern Hemisphere. Around it, instead of rainforest, stretches the lavrado, an open savanna of grass and scattered palms.","w":"A semicircular city whose streets fan out from a riverbank; open, pale green savanna dotted with small lakes around it.","f":["Mount Roraima, the flat-topped tepui where Brazil, Venezuela and Guyana meet, lies about 250 km to the north.","The Rio Branco flows south to join the Rio Negro and, through it, the Amazon."],"q":{"q":"What shape is Boa Vista's street plan?","a":"A fan spreading from the river","x":["A perfect grid","Concentric rings"]}},"Q180215":{"t":"A city on the equator, facing the Amazon's mouth","s":"Macapá sits right on the equator, on the north bank of the Amazon where the great river opens into its vast, island-strewn mouth. The water in front of the city is brown and many kilometres wide. The equator is taken seriously here: the local football stadium's halfway line follows it, so each team defends a goal in a different hemisphere.","w":"A city on a straight riverbank facing a huge brown estuary full of islands; flat forest and savanna inland.","f":["The Fortress of São José de Macapá, finished in 1782, is one of the largest forts the Portuguese built in Brazil.","No road links Macapá with the rest of Brazil; people cross the Amazon by boat or plane."],"q":{"q":"Which line runs along the halfway line of Macapá's stadium?","a":"The equator","x":["The Tropic of Cancer","The Greenwich meridian"]}},"Q1930":{"t":"A capital on a river border, with a canal you can skate","s":"The broad Ottawa River divides Ontario from Quebec here, with Ottawa on its south bank and the city of Gatineau facing it on the north. Through the middle of Ottawa runs the Rideau Canal, opened in 1832, and in winter a long stretch of it freezes into a skating rink nearly 8 km long. Queen Victoria chose the town as Canada's capital in 1857.","w":"A city on a wide river with Gatineau opposite; a thin canal winding south through the centre; the wooded Gatineau Hills to the north-west.","f":["The Rideau Canal has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 2007.","Three rivers meet here: the Ottawa, the Rideau and the Gatineau."],"q":{"q":"Who chose Ottawa as Canada's capital in 1857?","a":"Queen Victoria","x":["John A. Macdonald","King George III"]}},"Q3078079":{"t":"Ontario's northernmost community, on Hudson Bay","s":"No settlement in Ontario lies further north than Fort Severn, a small Cree community near the mouth of the Severn River on Hudson Bay. Around it spread the Hudson Bay Lowlands, one of the largest wetlands on Earth: a flat mosaic of peat bog, ponds and stunted spruce. The Hudson's Bay Company first set up a trading post at the river mouth in 1689.","w":"A tiny settlement by a river mouth on a flat, grey bay coast; endless bog and ponds inland in every direction.","f":["Polar Bear Provincial Park, Ontario's largest park, stretches along the coast to the east.","For most of the year the community can be reached only by air."],"q":{"q":"What sets Fort Severn apart from other Ontario communities?","a":"It is the northernmost","x":["It is the oldest city","It is the provincial capital"]}},"Q34116":{"t":"A lake port watched over by a Sleeping Giant","s":"Thunder Bay lines the north-west shore of Lake Superior, the largest freshwater lake in the world by area. Across its bay lies the long Sibley Peninsula, whose flat-topped cliffs look from the city like a giant lying on his back: the Sleeping Giant of Ojibwe legend. The city was created in 1970 by merging two rival ports, Fort William and Port Arthur.","w":"A city along a curving bay of a huge lake; a long, high peninsula enclosing the bay to the east; dark forest inland.","f":["In the early 1800s Fort William was the inland headquarters of the North West Company's fur trade.","Kakabeka Falls, about 30 km to the west, drops some 40 m over dark shale cliffs."],"q":{"q":"Which two towns merged in 1970 to form Thunder Bay?","a":"Fort William and Port Arthur","x":["Fort Erie and Port Elgin","Fort Frances and Port Hope"]}},"Q2145":{"t":"A walled city on a cliff where the river narrows","s":"Quebec City stands on a high cliff above the point where the St. Lawrence suddenly narrows — which is exactly what its Algonquian name means. Samuel de Champlain founded a trading post here in 1608. Its old town is still enclosed by stone ramparts, the only surviving city walls north of Mexico. Downstream, the river splits around the long, green Île d'Orléans.","w":"A city on high ground above a narrowing river; bridges upstream; a long, farmed island downstream where the river widens.","f":["The Quebec Bridge, just upstream, has the longest cantilever span in the world, 549 m.","Montmorency Falls, just east of the city, are about 83 m high — some 30 m taller than Niagara."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Québec mean?","a":"Where the river narrows","x":["Land of lakes","Cold wind"]}},"Q2141":{"t":"A deep natural harbour with a great basin behind it","s":"One of the world's great natural harbours cradles Halifax: a long, deep inlet that narrows and then opens into Bedford Basin, a broad inner bay. The city fills a peninsula on its western side, and two suspension bridges cross to Dartmouth opposite. The British founded the town in 1749 as a naval base, and for decades immigrants arriving by ship first set foot in Canada here.","w":"A city on a peninsula beside a long, deep harbour that opens into a wide inner basin; two bridges across the water.","f":["Between 1928 and 1971 about a million immigrants entered Canada through Pier 21 in Halifax.","The harbour stays free of ice all winter."],"q":{"q":"What is the large inner bay of Halifax Harbour called?","a":"Bedford Basin","x":["Minas Basin","Mahone Bay"]}},"Q2082":{"t":"North America's easternmost city, facing the Atlantic","s":"St. John's clusters around a snug harbour on the east coast of Newfoundland, reached from the ocean only through the Narrows, a slot between high cliffs. On Signal Hill above it, Guglielmo Marconi received the first transatlantic radio signal in 1901. A few kilometres to the south-east, Cape Spear is the easternmost point of the North American continent.","w":"A hilly town around a small, almost enclosed harbour with a narrow entrance; rugged cliffs and open Atlantic to the east.","f":["Newfoundland keeps its own time zone, half an hour ahead of Atlantic time.","In spring and early summer icebergs from Greenland often drift past this coast."],"q":{"q":"What happened on Signal Hill in St. John's in 1901?","a":"First radio signal across the Atlantic","x":["First telephone call to Europe","First steamship sailing to Europe"]}},"Q3335210":{"t":"Labrador's northernmost town, among islands and fjords","s":"Hundreds of rocky islands and long fjords break up Labrador's rugged north coast around Nain, and sea ice lingers well into spring. The town lies on a sheltered bay; Moravian missionaries founded a mission here in 1771. It is the northernmost permanent community in Labrador and the gateway to the Torngat Mountains further north.","w":"A small settlement on a bay behind a maze of rocky islands; bare, rounded hills and fjords; sea ice for much of the year.","f":["Labradorite, a stone that flashes blue and green, was first described from Paul's Island near Nain around 1770.","Nain is the administrative centre of Nunatsiavut, the Inuit region of Labrador."],"q":{"q":"Which shimmering mineral was first described near Nain?","a":"Labradorite","x":["Moonstone","Opal"]}},"Q2127":{"t":"A small capital on an island of brick-red fields","s":"The first thing you notice on Prince Edward Island is the colour of the land: vivid brick-red soil shows in ploughed fields and along the cliffs and beaches. Charlottetown lies in the middle of the island, on a harbour where three small rivers meet. In 1864 a conference here set in motion the union of provinces that became Canada.","w":"A small town on a branching harbour; a quilt of green and red-brown fields; red cliffs along the island's shore.","f":["The red colour comes from iron oxide — rust — in the island's sandstone and soil.","The Confederation Bridge, about 12.9 km long, is the world's longest bridge over ice-covered water.","The town was named after Queen Charlotte, wife of King George III."],"q":{"q":"Why is the soil of Prince Edward Island so red?","a":"Iron oxide","x":["Volcanic ash","Crushed coral"]}},"Q923541":{"t":"A Patagonian port on a drowned river valley","s":"On Argentina's windswept Patagonian coast the sea has flooded an old river valley, forming a long, narrow ría that reaches some 40 km inland, and Puerto Deseado stands at its mouth. Its cliffs and islets teem with seabirds, sea lions and penguins. The English navigator Thomas Cavendish named the harbour after his ship, the Desire, in 1586.","w":"A small town at the mouth of a long, narrow inlet cut into bare, tawny plateau; rocky islets off the coast.","f":["Charles Darwin explored the ría during the voyage of the Beagle in 1833.","Rockhopper penguins nest on Isla Pingüino, a little way down the coast to the south."],"q":{"q":"Which ship gave Puerto Deseado its name?","a":"Thomas Cavendish's Desire","x":["Magellan's Victoria","Darwin's Beagle"]}},"Q44242":{"t":"A windswept steppe capital on a tidal estuary","s":"Río Gallegos lies on the south bank of a wide estuary near Argentina's southern tip, surrounded by flat, treeless Patagonian steppe. Its tides are among the highest on the Argentine coast, and at low water huge grey mudflats appear, where migrating shorebirds feed. Founded in 1885, the town grew as a port for the wool of the vast sheep ranches around it.","w":"A low town beside a broad estuary with wide mudflats; flat, tawny steppe stretching to the horizon.","f":["It is the capital of Santa Cruz, one of Argentina's largest and least populated provinces.","Strong westerly winds sweep across the steppe here for much of the year."],"q":{"q":"What made the Río Gallegos region rich in the late 1800s?","a":"Sheep ranching","x":["Silver mining","Sugar cane"]}},"Q221921":{"t":"An oil town born when a search for water struck oil","s":"Comodoro Rivadavia is squeezed between the Gulf of San Jorge and the steep slopes of Cerro Chenque, a flat-topped hill looming over the centre. In 1907 workers drilling for fresh water in this parched corner of Patagonia struck oil instead, and the town became Argentina's first great oil centre. Relentless winds sweep the steppe, now turning rows of wind turbines.","w":"A city strung along a wide gulf beneath bare, flat-topped hills; oil wells dotted across the brown steppe behind.","f":["The town was founded in 1901 and named after the naval officer Martín Rivadavia.","The Gulf of San Jorge is one of the largest bays on Argentina's Atlantic coast."],"q":{"q":"What were drillers looking for when they struck oil here in 1907?","a":"Water","x":["Gold","Coal"]}},"Q44237":{"t":"A green oasis of vineyards beneath the high Andes","s":"Mendoza is an oasis: the land around it is semi-desert, yet the city and its vineyards are green thanks to irrigation channels fed by Andean snowmelt, a system begun by the Huarpe people long before the Spanish founded the city in 1561. To the west the Andes rise in a massive wall, and Aconcagua, the highest peak outside Asia, stands about 110 km away.","w":"A large green city and patchwork of vineyards on a dry plain at the foot of a towering, snow-streaked mountain wall.","f":["The Mendoza region produces most of Argentina's wine and is especially famous for Malbec.","The streets are lined with open irrigation ditches, called acequias, that water the trees."],"q":{"q":"Which grape variety is Mendoza most famous for?","a":"Malbec","x":["Pinot Noir","Riesling"]}},"Q2907":{"t":"Bolivia's white colonial capital, with a dinosaur wall","s":"Whitewashed walls and red-tiled roofs make Sucre stand out among the dry, rolling hills around it, at about 2,800 m. It is Bolivia's constitutional capital, although the government sits in La Paz, and it was here that Bolivian independence was declared in 1825. On the city's edge, a quarry cliff called Cal Orck'o is covered with thousands of dinosaur footprints.","w":"A compact city of pale buildings and red roofs in a bowl of brown hills; eroded valleys all around.","f":["Cal Orck'o holds one of the largest collections of dinosaur tracks in the world, on an almost vertical limestone wall.","Founded in 1538, the city has had several names, including La Plata and Chuquisaca."],"q":{"q":"What covers the Cal Orck'o cliff near Sucre?","a":"Dinosaur footprints","x":["Inca rock paintings","Silver veins"]}},"Q941357":{"t":"The Brazil-nut capital, where two jungle rivers meet","s":"Riberalta stands on a high bank where the Madre de Dios River flows into the Beni, deep in the lowland forests of northern Bolivia. From above, both rivers wind in wide brown loops, leaving crescent-shaped lakes behind. The town was founded in 1894 during the rubber boom and today lives largely off Brazil nuts, gathered wild in the surrounding forest.","w":"A small town on a bank where two broad, meandering brown rivers meet; oxbow lakes and dense forest all around.","f":["Bolivia is the world's largest exporter of Brazil nuts, and much of the processing happens in Riberalta.","Brazil nuts come from giant wild trees that rarely thrive in plantations."],"q":{"q":"Which nut is processed in huge quantities in Riberalta?","a":"The Brazil nut","x":["The cashew","The macadamia"]}},"Q54108":{"t":"The 'White Bay', a maze of tidal channels and salt","s":"At the head of a broad estuary, tidal channels wind through salt marshes and mudflats; early sailors called the place 'White Bay' after the salt crusts glinting on the shore. Bahía Blanca began in 1828 as a frontier fort and grew into one of Argentina's main deep-water ports, shipping grain from the pampas. The low Sierra de la Ventana rises to the north.","w":"A city beside a vast estuary laced with winding channels and pale flats; docks and industry along the shore; flat farmland inland.","f":["In 1832 Charles Darwin dug up fossils of giant extinct mammals at Punta Alta, on this bay.","Cerro Tres Picos, in the Sierra de la Ventana, is the highest point in Buenos Aires province."],"q":{"q":"Where does the name Bahía Blanca, 'White Bay', come from?","a":"Salt crusts on the shore","x":["White sand dunes","Chalk cliffs"]}},"Q52607":{"t":"Argentina's beach city, where the pampas meet the sea","s":"Mar del Plata is where the endless flat pampas finally reach the Atlantic, and here the coast turns rocky, with low cliffs and headlands between long sandy beaches. Founded in 1874, it became the summer playground of Buenos Aires, and in January its population swells enormously. Look for the long breakwaters of its port, home to a colony of sea lions.","w":"A large coastal city curving along beaches and rocky points; a harbour with long breakwaters; flat green farmland inland.","f":["A colony of South American sea lions lives right in the fishing port.","Buenos Aires lies about 400 km to the north-west."],"q":{"q":"Which animals live in a colony right in Mar del Plata's port?","a":"Sea lions","x":["Flamingos","Pelicans"]}},"Q44210":{"t":"Argentina's second city, where plains meet the sierras","s":"Córdoba spreads along the Suquía River where the flat Pampas begin to rise into the Sierras de Córdoba, a range of rounded granite hills to the west. Founded in 1573, it became a centre of learning: its university, founded by Jesuits in 1613, is Argentina's oldest. The Jesuit buildings in the centre and their ranches in the hills are a World Heritage site.","w":"A large city on a plain, with hills rising close by to the west; a river winding through the centre.","f":["Cerro Champaquí, the highest summit of the sierras, reaches about 2,790 m.","Mar Chiquita, Argentina's largest salt lake, lies about 170 km to the north-east."],"q":{"q":"Which is the oldest university in Argentina?","a":"The University of Córdoba","x":["The University of Buenos Aires","The University of La Plata"]}},"Q44238":{"t":"A red-earth river city facing Paraguay","s":"Across the broad Paraná from Posadas lies Encarnación, in Paraguay, linked to it by a long bridge; a dam downstream has widened the river into a lake-like expanse. The soil of the province of Misiones is a startling brick red, showing in roadsides and fields between patches of forest and yerba mate plantations.","w":"A city on a very wide river with another city opposite, joined by a long bridge; red soil, green fields and forest around.","f":["The Yacyretá Dam, downstream, raised the river and widened it into a reservoir.","The ruins of San Ignacio Miní, a Jesuit mission of the 1600s and 1700s, stand about 60 km to the east."],"q":{"q":"Which Paraguayan city faces Posadas across the Paraná?","a":"Encarnación","x":["Ciudad del Este","Pilar"]}},"Q3043":{"t":"A small, purpose-built capital in the Belizean jungle","s":"Blink and you might miss Belmopan, one of the smallest national capitals in the world: a tidy town planted in the green, rolling country of the Belize River valley. It was built in the 1960s, about 80 km inland, to replace low-lying Belize City, which is exposed to Caribbean hurricanes; the government moved here in 1970. To the south rise the forested Maya Mountains.","w":"A small, low town among green hills and forest near a winding river; dark, forested mountains to the south.","f":["Off the coast lies the Belize Barrier Reef, part of the second-largest reef system in the world.","The name combines 'Belize' and 'Mopan', two of the country's rivers."],"q":{"q":"Why was Belmopan built far from the coast?","a":"To be safer from hurricanes","x":["To be nearer the oil fields","To guard the border"]}},"Q1491":{"t":"The world's highest seat of government, in a canyon","s":"La Paz is poured into a deep canyon cut into the edge of the Altiplano, its buildings spilling down from the rim at about 4,000 m towards a floor several hundred metres lower. Up on the flat plateau sprawls El Alto, with one of the highest international airports in the world. Above it all, to the south-east, rise the three snowy summits of Illimani, about 6,440 m high.","w":"A city filling a deep, branching canyon below a flat, high plateau; another city on the rim; a triple-peaked snow mountain to the south-east.","f":["At around 3,600 m, La Paz is the highest seat of government in the world.","Lake Titicaca lies about 60 km to the north-west across the Altiplano.","The Spanish founded the city in 1548."],"q":{"q":"Which snowy mountain towers over La Paz?","a":"Illimani","x":["Aconcagua","Chimborazo"]}},"Q170688":{"t":"Bolivia's biggest city, laid out in concentric rings","s":"Santa Cruz de la Sierra lies not in the Andes but on the hot, flat lowlands east of them, and its plan is unmistakable from the air: ring roads, called anillos, circle the central square one inside another like the rings of a tree. It is now Bolivia's largest city. Around it stretch vast, geometric soybean fields carved out of the lowland forest.","w":"A large city of concentric ring roads on a flat green plain; a river along its western edge; huge rectangular fields beyond.","f":["An expedition from Asunción first founded the city in 1561, at a site far to the east; it moved to its present spot later that century.","At about 400 m above sea level, it lies more than 3 km lower than La Paz."],"q":{"q":"What shape is Santa Cruz's road plan?","a":"Concentric rings","x":["A fan from the river","A strict grid"]}},"Q41211":{"t":"An old walled city on an islet at the harbour mouth","s":"San Juan's colonial core sits on a narrow islet guarding the entrance to a large bay, its stone forts facing the open Atlantic. Spanish settlers moved here in 1521, making it one of the oldest European-founded cities in the Americas. Eastward, the modern city curves along beaches and lagoons to the airport. Offshore, the ocean floor drops away into a deep trench.","w":"A long islet with fortifications at the mouth of a broad bay; hotels, lagoons and beaches running east to the airport.","f":["About 120 km to the north lies the Puerto Rico Trench, the deepest part of the Atlantic, more than 8 km down.","The forts and old city of San Juan are a UNESCO World Heritage site."],"q":{"q":"What lies deep beneath the sea north of San Juan?","a":"The Atlantic's deepest point","x":["A mid-ocean ridge","A giant blue hole"]}},"Q12245":{"t":"A tiny harbour town on treeless, windswept islands","s":"Treeless moorland, low rocky ridges and a jagged coast surround Stanley, a small town on a sheltered harbour of East Falkland. The islands are famous for their penguins: gentoo, king, rockhopper and Magellanic. Look inland for pale streaks on the hillsides — 'stone runs', rivers of boulders left by ice-age frost. The town was laid out in the 1840s.","w":"A small town on the south shore of a narrow harbour; bare green-brown moorland, rocky ridges and pale boulder streams around.","f":["Hulks of 19th-century sailing ships that limped in after rounding Cape Horn still lie in and around the harbour.","The islands have no native trees, and strong winds sweep them for most of the year."],"q":{"q":"What are the 'stone runs' seen near Stanley?","a":"Streams of ice-age boulders","x":["Old stone walls","Lava flows"]}},"Q30985":{"t":"A fishhook of islands alone in the Atlantic","s":"Bermuda appears out of the empty Atlantic as a thin fishhook of islands wrapped in turquoise shallows and coral reefs, about 1,000 km from the nearest mainland. Hamilton, the capital since 1815, sits at the head of a sheltered harbour in the middle. Look closely at the houses: their stepped roofs are painted white and built to catch rainwater, the islands' main source of fresh water.","w":"A curving chain of green islands inside a wide ring of turquoise reef; tiny white roofs everywhere; a harbour in the middle.","f":["The islands are named after the Spanish navigator Juan de Bermúdez, who came upon them in the early 1500s.","Bermuda has no rivers and no freshwater lakes."],"q":{"q":"Why are Bermuda's roofs white and stepped?","a":"To collect rainwater","x":["To deflect lightning","To guide pilots"]}},"Q38834":{"t":"One of the first capitals to greet each new day","s":"Nuku'alofa lies on the north shore of Tongatapu, a low, flat coral island ringed by reefs, with a large, shallow lagoon cutting into it from the north. Its name means 'abode of love'. Tonga is a kingdom and one of the first countries to greet each new day, lying just west of the date line. Captain Cook called these the Friendly Islands in the 1770s.","w":"A small town on a flat green island's north coast; a wide, shallow lagoon behind it; reefs and islets in turquoise water offshore.","f":["The Ha'amonga 'a Maui, a stone trilithon built around the 1200s, stands at the island's eastern end.","About three-quarters of Tonga's people live on Tongatapu."],"q":{"q":"What did Captain Cook call the islands of Tonga?","a":"The Friendly Islands","x":["The Society Islands","The Sandwich Islands"]}},"Q36260":{"t":"Where the calendar once skipped a whole day","s":"Apia strings along the north coast of Upolu, a lush volcanic island whose forested ridges rise steeply behind the town. Its harbour opens to the sea through a gap in the fringing reef. Samoa once lay on the other side of the date line, but in 2011 it skipped 30 December entirely to share a calendar day with Australia and New Zealand, and now it is among the first to see each new day.","w":"A town along a curving bay on a green, mountainous island; a white line of reef offshore with a gap for ships.","f":["Robert Louis Stevenson spent his last years at Vailima, above Apia, and is buried on nearby Mount Vaea.","Upolu and its larger neighbour, Savai'i, are both volcanic islands."],"q":{"q":"Which writer spent his last years in the hills above Apia?","a":"Robert Louis Stevenson","x":["Herman Melville","Jack London"]}},"Q2467":{"t":"Turquoise banks beside a deep blue ocean trench","s":"Around Nassau the sea puts on its most dazzling show: pale turquoise and cream over the shallow Bahama banks, then a sudden plunge into the dark blue of the Tongue of the Ocean, a trench more than 1,500 m deep just south of the island. Nassau itself lines the north shore of New Providence, facing Paradise Island across its harbour.","w":"A small island city on a narrow harbour; brilliant turquoise shallows around; an abrupt edge of dark blue deep water to the south.","f":["In the early 1700s Nassau was a notorious base for pirates, Blackbeard among them.","The town was named in 1695 after King William III, of the House of Orange-Nassau."],"q":{"q":"What is the 'Tongue of the Ocean' near Nassau?","a":"A deep-water trench","x":["A long coral reef","A sandbar"]}},"Q84":{"t":"A dark river coiling through a sea of lights","s":"Find the Thames and London falls into place: a brown ribbon looping east through an enormous grey-green sprawl, then widening towards the North Sea. The M25 motorway draws a rough ring around it all. The Romans bridged the river here almost two thousand years ago, and the city has spread outward from that crossing ever since.","w":"The looping Thames running east to its estuary; square reservoirs glint beside Heathrow on the western edge.","f":["The M25 ring motorway is about 188 km long; at night it traces a faint loop of lights around the city.","The Thames is tidal right through London: in the centre the water rises and falls by several metres twice a day.","The Romans founded Londinium on the north bank of the Thames around AD 50."],"q":{"q":"What was London called in Roman times?","a":"Londinium","x":["Lutetia","Eboracum"]}},"Q406":{"t":"One city, two continents, one blue thread between them","s":"Look for a thin blue line cutting a vast city in two: the Bosphorus, with Europe on one bank and Asia on the other. The Golden Horn curls into the old town on the European side. As Constantinople, this was an imperial capital for more than a thousand years.","w":"A narrow strait running north–south between the Black Sea and the Sea of Marmara.","f":["The Bosphorus is about 30 km long and well under a kilometre wide at its narrowest.","Three suspension bridges cross the strait; from cruise they show as fine pale lines.","At night the two shores glow as one ribbon of light along the water."],"q":{"q":"Which strait splits Istanbul between Europe and Asia?","a":"The Bosphorus","x":["The Dardanelles","The Strait of Messina"]}},"Q3692":{"t":"A grid of lights dropped into the Arabian desert","s":"Hundreds of kilometres from any sea, a vast beige grid of broad avenues spreads over the Najd plateau: this is Riyadh, near the middle of the Arabian Peninsula. On its western side runs the green thread of Wadi Hanifa. The city's name comes from the Arabic for 'gardens' — the oases this valley once watered.","w":"A huge rectangular street grid in brown desert, with the long Tuwaiq escarpment running north–south to the west.","f":["The Tuwaiq escarpment west of the city curves for roughly 800 km across central Arabia.","On Riyadh's north-western edge, the mud-brick At-Turaif district of Diriyah is a UNESCO World Heritage site.","The city stands about 600 metres above sea level on the Najd plateau."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Riyadh mean in Arabic?","a":"Gardens","x":["Sands","Stars"]}},"Q5465":{"t":"A flat-topped mountain near the tip of Africa","s":"Look for a mountain sliced flat like a table, with the city curled around its feet and the sea on both sides. South of it, a rocky finger of land runs down to the Cape of Good Hope. On many summer days a white cloud spills over the summit like a tablecloth, then melts away on the way down.","w":"A flat grey mountain block above a curved bay, a rocky peninsula running south, and a low island out in the bay.","f":["Table Mountain's flat summit reaches about 1,085 metres — roughly a tenth of your cruising height.","The low island in Table Bay is Robben Island, a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1999.","Cape Town is South Africa's oldest city, founded in 1652 as a supply station for Dutch ships."],"q":{"q":"In which year did the Dutch East India Company found Cape Town?","a":"1652","x":["1588","1714"]}},"Q649":{"t":"Rings within rings, like a cut tree trunk","s":"Moscow is laid out like a tree trunk seen in cross-section: ring roads nested one inside another around the old centre, with radial highways running out like spokes. At night the whole pattern glows amber and white. Through it winds the Moskva River in big, lazy loops. The city first appears in a chronicle in 1147.","w":"Concentric road rings with straight spokes radiating out; the winding Moskva River crosses the centre from north-west to south-east.","f":["The MKAD, Moscow's outer ring motorway, is about 109 km long.","The inner Garden Ring runs for about 16 km around the historic centre.","The city and its river share one name: in Russian, both are called Moskva."],"q":{"q":"In which year is Moscow first mentioned in a chronicle?","a":"1147","x":["1240","1380"]}},"Q8673":{"t":"A megacity stitched together across a lagoon","s":"Lagos is as much water as land. Look for a broad lagoon behind a long, straight Atlantic beach, with the city spilling over the islands and shores all around it. A thin bridge strides across the water for kilometres. Portuguese sailors named the place after these lakes, and today it is one of Africa's largest cities.","w":"A wide brown-grey lagoon behind a ruler-straight surf coast, dense city on its islands and a long bridge across the water.","f":["The Third Mainland Bridge runs about 11.8 km across Lagos Lagoon, linking the islands to the mainland.","Lagos was Nigeria's capital until 1991, when the government moved to Abuja.","In Yoruba, the city's own name is Eko."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Lagos mean in Portuguese?","a":"Lakes","x":["Harbours","Palms"]}},"Q220":{"t":"The Eternal City, framed by old volcanoes","s":"Look for round lakes first. To the north-west and south-east of Rome they fill the craters of extinct volcanoes; between them, about 25 km from the sea, the Tiber snakes through the city in tight bends. A motorway ring, the Grande Raccordo Anulare, loops around it all. Tradition dates the city's founding to 753 BC.","w":"A sprawl on a winding river, framed by the round crater lakes of Bracciano to the north-west and the Alban Hills to the south-east.","f":["The Grande Raccordo Anulare ring road is about 68 km long.","Vatican City, the world's smallest country, lies entirely inside Rome.","Lake Bracciano, north-west of the city, fills an old volcanic basin about 9 km across."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Rome?","a":"The Tiber","x":["The Arno","The Po"]}},"Q3870":{"t":"Lions and giraffes within sight of the skyline","s":"Lions and giraffes roam within sight of Nairobi's towers: the open grassland on the city's southern edge is a national park. The city sits on a high plateau at about 1,800 metres, just east of the Great Rift Valley. It began in 1899 as a depot on the railway from Mombasa to Lake Victoria, and its name comes from a Maasai phrase for 'cool water'.","w":"A dense city with an open tawny plain — the national park — on its southern side; the Ngong Hills rise to the south-west.","f":["Nairobi National Park begins only about 7 km from the city centre.","West of the city the land drops sharply into the Great Rift Valley.","The city lies less than 150 km south of the equator, yet its altitude keeps nights cool."],"q":{"q":"What does the Maasai name behind 'Nairobi' mean?","a":"Cool water","x":["Red earth","High hill"]}},"Q85":{"t":"Where the green Nile valley opens into the delta","s":"Cairo marks the hinge of Egypt. South of the city the Nile runs in a narrow green strip through desert; just to the north it fans out into the delta. The city fills both banks, with the Giza plateau and its pyramids on the western edge, where the houses stop and the Sahara begins.","w":"A huge grey-beige city on the Nile, exactly where the thin green valley widens into the triangle of the delta.","f":["Cairo was founded as al-Qahira, 'the Victorious', by the Fatimid dynasty in 969.","The line between irrigated fields and bare desert around Cairo is razor-sharp, even from cruise height.","The Great Pyramid of Giza was the tallest human-made structure on Earth for more than 3,800 years."],"q":{"q":"Which dynasty founded Cairo in 969?","a":"The Fatimids","x":["The Ottomans","The Mamluks"]}},"Q90":{"t":"An oval city inside a ring, with a river in loops","s":"Paris proper is compact: a neat oval held inside the Boulevard Périphérique, with suburbs sprawling far beyond it. The Seine enters from the south-east, curves through the centre past two small islands, then leaves in a series of great loops towards the Channel. The Romans called the settlement Lutetia.","w":"An oval ring road enclosing the dense centre, with the Seine snaking through it and meandering west in big loops.","f":["The Boulevard Périphérique is about 35 km long and follows the line of the city's 19th-century fortifications.","Inside the ring, Paris covers only about 105 km².","The city began on the Île de la Cité, the larger of the two islands in the Seine."],"q":{"q":"About how long is the Boulevard Périphérique around Paris?","a":"About 35 km","x":["About 70 km","About 12 km"]}},"Q1899":{"t":"Green hills on one bank, flat plain on the other","s":"The Dnipro splits Kyiv in two, and its banks could hardly differ more: steep wooded hills on the west, where the old city grew, and low flat land to the east. Sandy islands and side channels lie between them. Upstream, the river swells into a long reservoir. A thousand years ago this was the capital of Kyivan Rus.","w":"A broad river with sandy islands running north–south; the hilly old city on the west bank, newer districts on the flat east bank.","f":["Just north of the city the Dnipro widens into the Kyiv Reservoir, more than 100 km long.","The Kyiv Pechersk Lavra, a cave monastery founded in 1051, is a UNESCO World Heritage site.","Large wooded islands in the Dnipro, such as Trukhaniv, sit right opposite the city centre."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Kyiv?","a":"The Dnipro","x":["The Dniester","The Southern Bug"]}},"Q612":{"t":"A palm tree drawn on the sea in sand","s":"Few cities are so easy to spot. Off Dubai's coast, a palm tree has been drawn on the sea in sand, its fronds ringed by a crescent breakwater. Further out, a scatter of islets imitates a map of the world. Onshore, towers line the coast for kilometres, and the old town hugs a winding creek.","w":"The palm-shaped island off a straight desert coast, with the cluster of 'World' islands further out to sea.","f":["Palm Jumeirah was built from sand dredged from the sea floor and is roughly 5 km across.","The World islands are an archipelago of about 300 artificial islands laid out like a world map.","Dubai Creek, a natural inlet, was the heart of the old pearling and trading town."],"q":{"q":"What shape is Dubai's best-known artificial island?","a":"A palm tree","x":["A seashell","A falcon"]}},"Q2807":{"t":"Spain's high-plateau heart, snowy peaks to the north-west","s":"In winter a white-capped mountain wall rises north-west of Madrid: the Sierra de Guadarrama. The city itself lies almost at the centre of Spain, on a dry plateau more than 600 metres above the sea, circled by ring motorways that draw ever wider loops. Philip II moved his royal court to this small town in 1561.","w":"A dense city on a tan plateau ringed by motorways, with the Guadarrama mountains rising to the north-west.","f":["At about 650 metres, Madrid is the highest capital city in the European Union.","The Puerta del Sol in the centre is Kilometre Zero for Spain's radial national roads.","The city's own river, the Manzanares, is so small it barely shows from the air."],"q":{"q":"Which mountains rise north-west of Madrid?","a":"Sierra de Guadarrama","x":["Sierra Nevada","Picos de Europa"]}},"Q71":{"t":"Where the Rhône slips out of a crescent lake","s":"Where a great crescent-shaped lake narrows to a point and the Rhône slips out towards France, you will find Geneva. The city is almost surrounded by French territory. On a clear day look south-east to the snowy Mont Blanc massif, and north-west to the long, dark wall of the Jura.","w":"The narrow pointed end of a large crescent-shaped lake, with a river leaving it; the Jura ridge lies to the north-west.","f":["The canton of Geneva shares about 100 km of border with France and only a few kilometres with the rest of Switzerland.","Underground, just west of the city, CERN's particle accelerator runs in a 27 km ring beneath the French border.","Lake Geneva is about 73 km long, curving like a crescent between Switzerland and France."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows out of Lake Geneva at Geneva?","a":"The Rhône","x":["The Rhine","The Aare"]}},"Q1754":{"t":"Fourteen islands, one lake and a sea of skerries","s":"Stockholm is spread over fourteen islands at the point where Lake Mälaren drains into the Baltic. From above it is a jigsaw of water, granite and dark pine forest. To the east the archipelago takes over: tens of thousands of islands and skerries scattered towards the open sea. The city is first recorded in 1252.","w":"A jigsaw of islands and channels between a big lake to the west and a vast archipelago spreading east to the Baltic.","f":["The Stockholm archipelago contains roughly 30,000 islands, islets and rocks.","Lake Mälaren sits slightly higher than the Baltic, and its water drains out through the city's channels.","The land here is still rising after the last Ice Age, by around half a centimetre a year."],"q":{"q":"About how many islands make up the Stockholm archipelago?","a":"About 30,000","x":["About 300","About 3,000"]}},"Q3718":{"t":"The westernmost city of mainland Africa","s":"Dakar crowns the Cap-Vert peninsula, a hook of land pushing out into the Atlantic, the farthest west that mainland Africa reaches. From above, the city fills the whole triangle, with sea on three sides and the ochre Sahel behind. Just offshore lies the small island of Gorée, a UNESCO site and memorial to the Atlantic slave trade.","w":"A triangular peninsula jutting west into the Atlantic, built over almost entirely, with a small island off its south-eastern shore.","f":["Pointe des Almadies, on Dakar's peninsula, is the westernmost point of mainland Africa.","Two low volcanic hills, the Mamelles, rise near the western shore; one carries a lighthouse.","Gorée Island became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1978."],"q":{"q":"Dakar stands on which peninsula?","a":"Cap-Vert","x":["Cap Bon","Sinai"]}},"Q34647":{"t":"Built on gold: follow the line of old mine dumps","s":"Johannesburg exists because of gold. It sprang up in 1886, after a rich reef was found along the Witwatersrand, a long east–west ridge on the high veld. Look along the city's southern side for flat-topped yellowish mounds — old mine dumps — strung out in a line that traces the reef buried below.","w":"A sprawling city on a high grassy plateau; a line of pale yellow mine dumps runs east–west along its south.","f":["Johannesburg lies about 1,750 metres above sea level.","Witwatersrand means 'ridge of white waters' in Afrikaans.","Some Witwatersrand gold mines reach about 4 km below the surface, among the deepest in the world."],"q":{"q":"Which discovery led to the founding of Johannesburg in 1886?","a":"Gold","x":["Diamonds","Coal"]}},"Q727":{"t":"A canal city on land won from the water","s":"Amsterdam grew from a 13th-century dam on the river Amstel, and water still shapes everything you see: the broad IJ channel along its north side, the grey expanse of the Markermeer to the east, and a flat patchwork of polders all around. Many of those green rectangles lie below sea level.","w":"Flat green polders cut by straight ditches, the city on a broad channel, and a large grey lake to its east.","f":["Schiphol airport lies about 3 metres below sea level, on the bed of a lake drained in the mid-1800s.","The ring of 17th-century canals became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2010.","The North Sea Canal, opened in 1876, links the city's harbour with the sea about 25 km to the west."],"q":{"q":"Which river gave Amsterdam its name?","a":"The Amstel","x":["The Maas","The IJssel"]}},"Q7903":{"t":"A white city strung along the Atlantic surf","s":"From the air, Casablanca lives up to its name: Spanish for 'white house', echoing the Arabic Dar al-Bayda. A pale spread of buildings stretches along a flat Atlantic shore, pressed right up to the surf line, and the long breakwaters of its port, one of the largest in Africa, curve out to sea.","w":"A pale city along a straight Atlantic coast, with a big harbour enclosed by long breakwaters.","f":["The minaret of the Hassan II Mosque, on the seafront, is about 210 metres tall.","Part of the Hassan II Mosque stands on a platform built out over the Atlantic.","Casablanca is Morocco's largest city, though the capital is Rabat, about 90 km up the coast."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Casablanca mean?","a":"White house","x":["White sands","Blue harbour"]}},"Q64":{"t":"A flat city of lakes, forests and one big green void","s":"Water and forest are everywhere in Berlin: the Spree winding through the centre, a chain of Havel lakes in the west, the broad Müggelsee in the east, all on a flat, sandy plain left by Ice Age glaciers. Dark pine woods reach deep into the city. Look too for a vast open rectangle south of the centre — Tempelhof, an airport turned park.","w":"A huge flat city scattered with lakes and dark forest; a large open green rectangle lies just south of the centre.","f":["The A10 Berliner Ring motorway around the city is about 196 km long, one of Europe's longest ring roads.","Tempelhofer Feld, the old Tempelhof airport, is now an open park of about 300 hectares.","Berlin covers about 890 km², more than eight times the area of Paris inside its ring road."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through the centre of Berlin?","a":"The Spree","x":["The Elbe","The Oder"]}},"Q3838":{"t":"Two capitals facing each other across a great river","s":"Two capitals face each other across the river here. Kinshasa fills the south bank of the Congo, where the river spreads into a vast, lake-like pool dotted with sandy islands; Brazzaville sits on the far shore. Just downstream, the river plunges into a long run of rapids. Henry Morton Stanley set up a trading post here in 1881 and named it Léopoldville.","w":"A sprawling city on the south shore of a broad, island-studded widening of the Congo River, with another city on the opposite bank.","f":["Apart from Rome and the Vatican, Kinshasa and Brazzaville are the world's closest pair of capital cities.","Pool Malebo, the river's widening here, is about 35 km long and more than 20 km wide.","Below the city, the Livingstone Falls — a chain of rapids — lower the Congo by about 270 metres."],"q":{"q":"Which capital faces Kinshasa across the Congo River?","a":"Brazzaville","x":["Bangui","Luanda"]}},"Q1524":{"t":"A white city poured into a basin down to the sea","s":"Athens pours across a basin ringed by bare mountains, spilling down to the Saronic Gulf at the port of Piraeus. From above it is an almost unbroken white-and-beige carpet. Look at the mountain to the north-east: pale scars on its flanks are marble quarries, worked since antiquity, when they supplied the stone for the Parthenon.","w":"A dense white city in a mountain-rimmed basin opening south-west onto the sea, with ship wakes fanning out from Piraeus.","f":["The Parthenon's marble came from Mount Penteli, about 15 km north-east of the Acropolis.","Piraeus has served as the port of Athens since the 5th century BC, when the Long Walls linked the two.","The city takes its name from the goddess Athena, its patron in Greek myth."],"q":{"q":"Which mountain supplied the marble for the Parthenon?","a":"Mount Penteli","x":["Mount Olympus","Mount Parnassus"]}},"Q1530":{"t":"Threaded on the great loops of the Tigris","s":"Baghdad is threaded on the Tigris, which swings through it in great looping bends, with dark palm groves along both banks. To the west the Euphrates comes within a few dozen kilometres: the two rivers of Mesopotamia draw closer here than almost anywhere else. The Abbasid caliph al-Mansur founded the city in 762 as a perfectly round walled town.","w":"A flat, dust-coloured city on a wide, deeply looping river, with dark green palm groves along its banks.","f":["Al-Mansur named his new round city Madinat al-Salam, the City of Peace.","Near Baghdad the Tigris and the Euphrates run less than 50 km apart.","In the 9th century Baghdad's House of Wisdom drew scholars who translated Greek science into Arabic."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Baghdad?","a":"The Tigris","x":["The Euphrates","The Orontes"]}},"Q3624":{"t":"At 2,350 metres, among eucalyptus hills","s":"Few capitals sit higher: Addis Ababa lies at about 2,350 metres on the Ethiopian Highlands. The hills to its north are dark with eucalyptus, a tree brought in during the 1890s to give the young city firewood. Emperor Menelik II and Empress Taytu founded the city in 1886, near a group of hot springs.","w":"A sprawling city on a high green plateau, with dark eucalyptus-covered hills rising along its northern edge.","f":["The African Union has its headquarters in Addis Ababa.","The eucalyptus around the city came from Australia and now covers much of the Entoto hills.","At this altitude water boils at about 92 °C rather than 100 °C."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Addis Ababa mean in Amharic?","a":"New flower","x":["High city","Spring water"]}},"Q3616":{"t":"Snowy Damavand looming over a tilted city","s":"A lone white cone floats above the mountains north-east of Tehran: Damavand, the highest peak in Iran. The city lies at the foot of the Alborz, a steep wall that shuts it off from the Caspian Sea, and the whole city tilts: its northern districts sit hundreds of metres higher than the southern ones, which fade into dry plain.","w":"A huge grey city pressed against a high mountain wall to its north; a lone snowy volcanic cone stands out to the north-east.","f":["Mount Damavand, about 70 km north-east of the city, reaches roughly 5,600 metres.","Tehran became the capital in 1786 under Agha Mohammad Khan, founder of the Qajar dynasty.","Damavand is a volcano, and the highest in Asia."],"q":{"q":"Which volcano rises north-east of Tehran?","a":"Damavand","x":["Ararat","Sabalan"]}},"Q1741":{"t":"Where the Danube runs in straight parallel lines","s":"Vienna's river is easy to find but strange to look at: the Danube passes the city in straight parallel channels with a long, thin island between them. That is the work of engineers; the old river once wandered freely across its floodplain. To the west, the wooded hills of the Vienna Woods are the last foothills of the Alps.","w":"The Danube as two straight parallel channels with a slim island between them, north-east of the centre; forested hills to the west.","f":["Danube Island, the thin strip between the channels, is about 21 km long and was built for flood protection.","Vienna has working vineyards inside its city limits, and wine-making here goes back centuries.","For centuries Vienna was the main seat of the Habsburg dynasty."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Vienna?","a":"The Danube","x":["The Elbe","The Vltava"]}},"Q3640":{"t":"A capital on the bare steppe of Anatolia","s":"Ankara spreads over a bowl of hills in the middle of the Anatolian plateau, nearly 900 metres up and ringed by dry, tawny steppe. Its old name, Angora, lives on in the soft wool of goats, cats and rabbits once bred here. Far to the south you may catch a gleam of white: Lake Tuz, a vast, shallow salt lake.","w":"A large city in a bowl of low hills on bare, tan steppe; far to the south, the white sheet of a salt lake.","f":["Ankara replaced Istanbul as Turkey's capital in 1923.","In Roman times the city was Ancyra, capital of the province of Galatia.","Lake Tuz is so shallow that in summer much of it dries into a crust of white salt."],"q":{"q":"Which old name of Ankara was given to a soft wool?","a":"Angora","x":["Merino","Cashmere"]}},"Q1781":{"t":"Buda's hills and Pest's plain, stitched by bridges","s":"The Danube runs straight north to south through Budapest and splits it into two characters: the wooded hills of Buda on the west bank and the flat, dense grid of Pest on the east. A slim, leafy island, Margaret Island, sits in mid-river near the centre. The towns on the two banks were only merged into one city in 1873.","w":"A broad river flowing south, green hills on its west bank, flat city to the east, and a narrow island in mid-stream.","f":["Budapest sits on a geological fault that feeds more than a hundred thermal springs.","South of the city the Danube splits around Csepel Island, which is nearly 50 km long.","Margaret Island is about 2.5 km long and once held a medieval convent."],"q":{"q":"In which year were Buda, Óbuda and Pest united?","a":"1873","x":["1848","1896"]}},"Q2471":{"t":"An ancient capital on a high mountain plateau","s":"Tall tower houses of mud brick and stone, trimmed in white, have filled Sanaa's old city for centuries, and people have lived here for more than 2,500 years. From above, the city is an earth-coloured patch between bare, folded ridges, set in a high basin among the mountains of Yemen at about 2,250 metres.","w":"An earth-toned city in a broad upland basin, hemmed in by bare, rugged mountains on its east and west.","f":["The Old City of Sanaa has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1986.","West of the city rises Jabal an-Nabi Shu'ayb, at about 3,660 m the highest peak in the Arabian Peninsula.","Sanaa's tower houses are several storeys tall, with white gypsum friezes framing their windows."],"q":{"q":"Roughly how high above sea level is Sanaa?","a":"About 2,250 m","x":["About 750 m","About 4,000 m"]}},"Q1492":{"t":"A perfect grid between the hills and the sea","s":"Barcelona fills a coastal plain boxed in by two rivers and a line of wooded hills. Its heart is unmistakable: the Eixample, a vast chessboard of near-identical blocks laid out in the 1860s. Look for one broad avenue slicing across it at an angle, and for the hill of Montjuïc rising straight up from the port.","w":"A grid of square blocks between green hills and the Mediterranean, crossed by a long diagonal avenue, with a rounded hill by the port.","f":["The Eixample was planned in 1859 by the engineer Ildefons Cerdà.","Each Eixample block is about 113 metres across, with its corners cut off at an angle.","The Avinguda Diagonal cuts across the grid for about 11 km."],"q":{"q":"Which hill rises beside Barcelona's port?","a":"Montjuïc","x":["Tibidabo","Montserrat"]}},"Q19660":{"t":"A flat city with a string of lakes along its north","s":"A chain of lakes strung like beads along a small river marks Bucharest's northern edge; look for it on the flat Wallachian plain, about 60 km north of the Danube. The first document to mention the city was signed here in 1459 by Vlad III, better known as Vlad the Impaler.","w":"A large, flat city on an open plain, with a curving chain of small lakes along its northern edge.","f":["Its elegant boulevards once earned Bucharest the nickname 'Little Paris'.","Bucharest became the capital of Wallachia in 1659.","The Dâmbovița river crosses the centre in a straight, walled channel."],"q":{"q":"Which ruler signed the first document naming Bucharest, in 1459?","a":"Vlad the Impaler","x":["Stephen the Great","Michael the Brave"]}},"Q41183":{"t":"One of the world's oldest cities, on a limestone plain","s":"Aleppo is among the oldest continuously inhabited cities on Earth, a stop on caravan routes between the Mediterranean and Mesopotamia for thousands of years. From above it is a large pale city on a rolling limestone plain. Its old centre grew up around a citadel on an ancient mound. South-east of the city lies a shallow salt lake, Jabbul.","w":"A large pale city on a dry, rolling plain; a shallow salt lake glints to the south-east.","f":["The Ancient City of Aleppo has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1986.","The city has long been famous for soap made with olive and laurel oil, known worldwide as Aleppo soap.","Aleppo lies roughly 100 km inland from the Mediterranean."],"q":{"q":"Which salt lake lies south-east of Aleppo?","a":"Lake Jabbul","x":["Lake Tuz","Lake Urmia"]}},"Q3766":{"t":"An ancient oasis city beneath a bare mountain","s":"Damascus owes its existence to one small river. The Barada flows down from the Anti-Lebanon mountains and spreads into the Ghouta, an oasis of orchards that has fed the city for thousands of years. The city lies at the foot of bare Mount Qasioun, and in winter the snowy ridge of Mount Hermon stands out to the south-west.","w":"A large city between green orchards and desert, backed by a bare mountain on its north-west side.","f":["From 661 to 750 Damascus was the capital of the Umayyad Caliphate, which stretched from Spain to Central Asia.","Mount Hermon, south-west of the city, rises to about 2,800 metres and often keeps snow into spring.","The Ancient City of Damascus has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1979."],"q":{"q":"Which fabric takes its name from Damascus?","a":"Damask","x":["Muslin","Calico"]}},"Q72":{"t":"At the tip of a slender lake, the Alps beyond","s":"A slim lake curves about 40 km south-east towards the Alps, and at its northern tip sits Zürich, with the river Limmat draining out straight through the centre. On a clear day, look past the lake to the snowy wall of the Glarus Alps. The Romans knew the settlement as Turicum.","w":"The northern end of a long, narrow lake curving south-east, a river leaving it through the city, and the Alps beyond.","f":["Lake Zurich is about 40 km long but nowhere more than about 4 km wide.","The Uetliberg, the wooded ridge on the city's western edge, rises to about 870 metres.","Zürich is Switzerland's largest city, although the federal government sits in Bern."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows out of Lake Zurich through the city?","a":"The Limmat","x":["The Aare","The Reuss"]}},"Q597":{"t":"Mainland Europe's westernmost capital, on a wide estuary","s":"Lisbon climbs its hills on the north shore of the Tagus, just where the river's broad inland lake narrows into a channel to the Atlantic. Two long bridges cross the water: a red suspension bridge at the narrows and, upstream, a thin line of a bridge that runs on for kilometres. No capital in mainland Europe lies further west.","w":"A hilly city on the north shore of a broad estuary, with a long bridge across its upper reaches and another at the narrows.","f":["The Vasco da Gama Bridge across the Tagus is about 12 km long, one of the longest in Europe.","The inland part of the estuary is so wide that locals call it the Mar da Palha, the 'Sea of Straw'.","Cabo da Roca, west of Lisbon, is the westernmost point of mainland Europe."],"q":{"q":"Which river forms the broad estuary at Lisbon?","a":"The Tagus","x":["The Douro","The Guadiana"]}},"Q1963":{"t":"Where the Blue Nile and the White Nile become one","s":"Khartoum stands at one of the great river meetings of the world. The Blue Nile, coming down from the Ethiopian Highlands, joins the White Nile, which has travelled all the way from Lake Victoria. Their waters can run side by side in two shades for a while before they mix. Around the confluence, three cities face each other across the rivers.","w":"Two large rivers meeting in a Y around a green island, with a city on each of the three banks.","f":["Tuti Island sits right at the confluence of the two Niles.","Most of the water in the Nile below Khartoum comes from the Blue Nile, especially in the flood season.","The three cities around the confluence are Khartoum, Omdurman and Khartoum North."],"q":{"q":"Which two rivers meet at Khartoum?","a":"The Blue Nile and White Nile","x":["The Nile and the Atbara","The White Nile and the Sobat"]}},"Q374365":{"t":"The Red Sea gateway, fringed by turquoise reefs","s":"Turquoise shallows fringe the Red Sea along Jeddah's shore: those pale greens and blues are coral reefs. The city runs for kilometres, long and narrow, between the reefs and the desert. For centuries pilgrims bound for Mecca, about 70 km inland, have landed here, and the old port town grew rich on their arrival.","w":"A long city along a straight Red Sea coast, edged by bright turquoise reefs, with brown desert and hills behind.","f":["Historic Jeddah, an old quarter of houses built from coral stone, became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2014.","King Fahd's Fountain, off the city's seafront, throws water more than 250 metres into the air.","The reefs along this coast are part of one of the warmest and saltiest seas on Earth."],"q":{"q":"Which sea does Jeddah face?","a":"The Red Sea","x":["The Arabian Sea","The Persian Gulf"]}},"Q5806":{"t":"A holy city in a valley of dark, bare mountains","s":"East of Mecca, a whole valley is carpeted with rows of white tents: Mina, where pilgrims stay during the Hajj. The city itself lies in narrow valleys among dark, jagged mountains, about 70 km inland from the Red Sea. For Muslims it is the holiest city of Islam: they turn towards it in prayer, and millions come each year on pilgrimage.","w":"A city squeezed into winding valleys between bare black hills, with a valley of white tents stretching away to the east.","f":["The Prophet Muhammad was born in Mecca around AD 570.","The tent city in Mina holds tens of thousands of fireproof tents in neat rows.","The plain of Arafat, a key stop of the pilgrimage, lies about 20 km south-east of the city."],"q":{"q":"What covers the valley of Mina, east of Mecca?","a":"Rows of white tents","x":["Solar panels","Date palm groves"]}},"Q585":{"t":"A capital at the end of a long, island-dotted fjord","s":"A long arm of the sea, dotted with small islands, reaches far inland and finally runs out between forested hills; there, at the head of the fjord, sits Oslo. The city fills a green amphitheatre, with dark woods and lakes right at its edge. For three centuries it was called Christiania, after King Christian IV, who rebuilt it in 1624.","w":"The inner end of a long fjord scattered with small islands, with the city in a bowl of dark, forested hills.","f":["The Oslofjord runs about 100 km from the city south to the Skagerrak.","The city took back its medieval name, Oslo, in 1925.","Much of the municipality is protected forest, the Marka, laced with lakes and ski trails."],"q":{"q":"What was Oslo called from 1624 until 1925?","a":"Christiania","x":["Nidaros","Bjørgvin"]}},"Q5468":{"t":"A warm harbour city on the Indian Ocean","s":"Durban wraps around a big natural harbour on South Africa's east coast, sheltered by a long, wooded ridge called the Bluff. North of the port, a straight line of beaches faces the Indian Ocean, where the warm Agulhas Current keeps the water mild all year. Inland, rolling green hills are patched with sugar-cane fields.","w":"A near-enclosed bay with a narrow entrance, a green ridge along its south side, and a long straight beach to the north.","f":["The city was named in 1835 after Sir Benjamin D'Urban, then governor of the Cape Colony.","Durban's harbour is one of the busiest ports in Africa.","The warm Agulhas Current flows south-west along this coast, one of the fastest ocean currents on Earth."],"q":{"q":"Which ocean does Durban face?","a":"The Indian Ocean","x":["The Atlantic Ocean","The Southern Ocean"]}},"Q656":{"t":"Peter's city on the islands of the Neva delta","s":"A thin line crosses the eastern end of the Gulf of Finland: a barrier about 25 km long, running through Kotlin Island, that shields Saint Petersburg from storm surges. Behind it, the city spreads over the islands of the Neva delta. Peter the Great founded it on this marshy ground in 1703.","w":"A big city on a branching river delta at the head of a gulf, with a long dam crossing the water through an island.","f":["The Neva is only about 74 km long, yet it drains Lake Ladoga, the largest lake in Europe.","The city is built on dozens of islands, divided by rivers and canals.","Around midsummer, the 'white nights' keep the sky light even at midnight."],"q":{"q":"In which year did Peter the Great found Saint Petersburg?","a":"1703","x":["1682","1721"]}},"Q182984":{"t":"An ancient Sahel trading city on the savanna","s":"Low, earth-coloured roofs spread like a vast carpet across the dusty savanna of northern Nigeria: this is Kano, a trading city for a thousand years and a southern end of the caravan routes across the Sahara. In its old walled heart, cloth is still dyed in indigo pits that have been in use since the 15th century.","w":"A huge low city of sandy browns on flat, dry savanna, with green fields and reservoirs scattered around it.","f":["The Kofar Mata dye pits have been worked for more than 500 years.","Kano's Kurmi Market dates back to the 15th century.","The rainy season here lasts only about four months, from June to September."],"q":{"q":"Which dye colours cloth in Kano's old dye pits?","a":"Indigo blue","x":["Saffron yellow","Madder red"]}},"Q270":{"t":"Sandbanks and willows right through a capital","s":"Warsaw stands on the Vistula, and the river is the surprise: while most capitals have tamed theirs, here broad sandbanks, willow thickets and beaches line the water right through the city. The centre sits on a low escarpment on the west bank; the Praga district faces it across the river. The royal court moved here from Kraków around 1596.","w":"A wide river with pale sandbanks and green, wooded banks winding north through a large city on a flat plain.","f":["The Vistula, Poland's longest river, runs just over 1,000 km from the Carpathians to the Baltic.","Frédéric Chopin was born in 1810 at Żelazowa Wola, about 50 km west of Warsaw.","Warsaw's historic Old Town is a UNESCO World Heritage site."],"q":{"q":"From which city did Poland's royal court move to Warsaw around 1596?","a":"Kraków","x":["Gdańsk","Poznań"]}},"Q1960":{"t":"A 'haven of peace' on a sheltered harbour","s":"Dar es Salaam curls around a narrow, sheltered harbour on the Indian Ocean, with coral islets and turquoise reefs scattered just offshore. Sultan Majid of Zanzibar founded the town in the 1860s and gave it an Arabic name meaning 'haven of peace'. Today it is Tanzania's largest city, sprawling far inland across the coastal plain.","w":"A sprawling coastal city around a narrow-mouthed harbour, with small green islands and reefs just offshore to the north.","f":["Tanzania's official capital is Dodoma, but Dar es Salaam remains its largest city and main port.","Zanzibar Town lies about 70 km to the north, across a channel of the Indian Ocean.","The islands just north of the city, such as Bongoyo and Mbudya, are part of a marine reserve."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Dar es Salaam mean?","a":"Haven of peace","x":["Gate of the sea","House of spices"]}},"Q1761":{"t":"A Viking harbour between Howth and the Wicklow hills","s":"Dublin rings the inner curve of Dublin Bay, with the Liffey running straight east through its heart into the sea. The rocky peninsula of Howth closes the bay to the north, and the Wicklow Mountains rise dark and green just to the south. Vikings set up a fortified harbour here in 841, beside a dark tidal pool — the dubh linn.","w":"A city around a rounded bay, a river running east through its centre, a knobbly peninsula to the north and hills to the south.","f":["Phoenix Park, on the city's western side, covers about 700 hectares — one of Europe's largest enclosed city parks.","The city's Irish name, Baile Átha Cliath, means 'town of the hurdled ford'.","Two long sea walls reach into the bay from the river mouth; the southern one was finished in the 1790s."],"q":{"q":"What does 'dubh linn', the source of Dublin's name, mean?","a":"Black pool","x":["Green hill","Fair harbour"]}},"Q3748":{"t":"A rain-soaked capital on a green cape","s":"Few capitals get more rain than Monrovia, and in the wet season the land around it glows a deep green. The city stretches along Cape Mesurado, a narrow peninsula between the Atlantic and a maze of lagoons and mangrove creeks. It was founded in 1822 by freed African Americans and named after US president James Monroe.","w":"A narrow cape between the ocean and a mangrove-fringed river mouth, with the city running along its spine.","f":["Monrovia gets more than 4,500 mm of rain a year, several times as much as London.","The first settlers landed in 1822 on Providence Island, in the mouth of the Mesurado River.","The city's port lies on Bushrod Island, wrapped by the Saint Paul and Mesurado rivers."],"q":{"q":"Which US president was Monrovia named after?","a":"James Monroe","x":["Thomas Jefferson","James Madison"]}},"Q2634":{"t":"Vesuvius on one side, a field of craters on the other","s":"Naples curves around one of the most famous bays in the world, and its landmark is impossible to miss: the cone of Vesuvius, cradled in the broken rim of an older volcano just south-east of the city. To the west lies another volcanic area, the Phlegraean Fields, pitted with craters. Out in the bay float Capri and Ischia.","w":"A broad bay with a big volcanic cone on its south-eastern side, islands at its mouth, and the city along the north shore.","f":["Vesuvius rises to about 1,280 metres straight from the shore of the bay.","The Phlegraean Fields west of the city form a large volcanic caldera, partly under the sea.","Greek settlers named the city Neapolis, and its historic centre is now a UNESCO World Heritage site."],"q":{"q":"What does the Greek name Neapolis, Naples' origin, mean?","a":"New city","x":["Sea city","Holy city"]}},"Q490":{"t":"A flat city on the Po plain, the Alps on its horizon","s":"On a clear day north of Milan the Alps rise like a white wall, with the great lakes, Como and Maggiore, glinting at their feet. The city itself lies in the flat heart of the Po plain, rings of streets around a medieval core, with motorways and railways fanning out in every direction. In late Roman times, as Mediolanum, it was an imperial capital.","w":"A dense, roughly round city on a perfectly flat plain, with the Alps and long glacial lakes to the north.","f":["Milan Cathedral was begun in 1386 and took nearly six centuries to finish.","With no big river of its own, Milan dug canals, the Navigli, from the 12th century to reach the Ticino and Adda.","On clear days Monte Rosa, more than 4,600 metres high, can be seen from the city."],"q":{"q":"What was Milan called in Roman times?","a":"Mediolanum","x":["Augusta Taurinorum","Placentia"]}},"Q87":{"t":"A long ribbon of a city between sea and lake","s":"A thin ribbon of pale buildings, squeezed between the Mediterranean and the shallow Lake Mariout, runs for kilometres along the coast: this is Alexandria. Look for the curve of its eastern harbour. Alexander the Great founded the city in 331 BC, and its lighthouse, the Pharos, was one of the Seven Wonders of the ancient world.","w":"A narrow city strung along a straight coast, with a curved harbour and a flat lake and marshes just inland.","f":["The city runs more than 30 km along the coast but is only a few kilometres deep.","The ancient Library of Alexandria was one of the great centres of learning of the classical world.","The Pharos stood on an island at the harbour mouth for well over 1,500 years."],"q":{"q":"Who founded Alexandria in 331 BC?","a":"Alexander the Great","x":["Ptolemy I","Julius Caesar"]}},"Q1794":{"t":"Runways carved from forest beside the Main","s":"Pale runway stripes cut into a great dark forest mark one of Europe's busiest airports, just south of Frankfurt. The city lies on the Main about 30 km before it joins the Rhine, with the wooded Taunus hills rising to the north-west. For centuries this was where German kings were elected.","w":"A compact centre on a river running east–west, with a big forest to the south cut by long, straight airport runways.","f":["From 1356 the Holy Roman Emperors were elected in Frankfurt, and from 1562 they were crowned here too.","Johann Wolfgang von Goethe was born in Frankfurt in 1749.","The Taunus range to the north-west rises to about 880 metres at the Großer Feldberg."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Frankfurt?","a":"The Main","x":["The Neckar","The Mosel"]}},"Q1055":{"t":"A great port far up the Elbe, with a lake at its heart","s":"About 100 km up the Elbe from the North Sea lies one of Europe's largest ports. From above, look for Hamburg's maze of docks and container terminals on the south bank, and for a bright lake right in the city centre: the Alster, a small river dammed centuries ago. Hamburg was a leading city of the Hanseatic League.","w":"A wide river with a maze of harbour basins on its south bank and a broad lake in the city centre just north of it.","f":["The Alster lakes were created in the Middle Ages by damming a small river to drive a mill.","The Speicherstadt, a warehouse district built on oak piles, is a UNESCO World Heritage site.","The tide reaches Hamburg: here the Elbe rises and falls by more than three metres."],"q":{"q":"Which lake lies in the centre of Hamburg?","a":"The Alster","x":["The Müritz","The Steinhuder Meer"]}},"Q1726":{"t":"A Bavarian city with the Alps on its doorstep","s":"When the warm, dry Föhn wind blows, the Alps look close enough to touch from Munich, though the wall of peaks is about 60 km to the south. The city spreads across a flat gravel plain on the green Isar. South-west of it, two big lakes, Starnberg and Ammersee, glint among fields and woods.","w":"A large city on a flat plain split by a green river valley, with lakes to the south-west and the Alps beyond.","f":["Munich's name comes from the monks, 'Mönche', who lived here before Henry the Lion founded the town in 1158.","The first Oktoberfest was held in 1810 to celebrate a royal wedding.","At about 520 metres, Munich is one of the highest large cities in Germany."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Munich?","a":"The Isar","x":["The Inn","The Lech"]}},"Q1085":{"t":"A river bending in S-curves through a hundred spires","s":"The Vltava gives Prague its shape. From above, the river swings through the city in a broad S, with the wooded rise of Petřín and the castle ridge on the west bank and the dense old town on the east. Green islands sit in the stream. About 30 km further north, the Vltava ends its journey in the Elbe.","w":"A river making big S-shaped bends through a dense city, wooded hills on its west bank and small islands mid-stream.","f":["Prague Castle is often listed as the largest ancient castle complex in the world.","Charles Bridge was begun in 1357 under Emperor Charles IV.","The historic centre of Prague has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1992."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Prague?","a":"The Vltava","x":["The Elbe","The Morava"]}},"Q35178":{"t":"A desert city curled around a wide, shallow bay","s":"Half-rings of motorway arc out from the old town into flat, almost featureless desert. Kuwait City wraps around the southern shore of Kuwait Bay, a broad inlet at the north-western corner of the Persian Gulf. The name comes from the Arabic for 'little fort', and the town grew on pearling and seafaring.","w":"A city on the south shore of a wide bay, with semicircular ring roads arcing out into flat desert.","f":["The city's ring roads, numbered First to Seventh, curve out from the old centre in concentric arcs.","On Failaka Island, at the mouth of the bay, stand the remains of a Greek temple from the 3rd century BC.","Before oil, pearl diving was the backbone of Kuwait's economy."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Kuwait mean?","a":"Little fort","x":["Pearl bay","Sweet water"]}},"Q3761":{"t":"Almost exactly at zero longitude","s":"You are very close to zero longitude here: just east of Accra, at the port of Tema, the Greenwich meridian comes ashore. The city spreads along a flat stretch of the Gulf of Guinea, its low roofs running inland over a grassy coastal plain, only about 600 km north of the point where the meridian crosses the equator.","w":"A broad, low city on a flat, gently curving coast, with a long ridge of hills rising inland to the north.","f":["The Greenwich meridian passes through Tema, about 25 km east of central Accra.","Accra's name is thought to come from 'nkran', an Akan word for ants.","Accra became the capital of the British Gold Coast colony in 1877."],"q":{"q":"Which port near Accra lies on the Greenwich meridian?","a":"Tema","x":["Takoradi","Lomé"]}},"Q3579":{"t":"An old Mediterranean port between sea and desert","s":"Tripoli spreads along the Libyan coast, a pale city on a low shore where a belt of gardens and palm groves gives way inland to desert. Phoenicians founded it as Oea in the 7th century BC. Its Greek name means 'three cities', after Oea and its neighbours Sabratha and Leptis Magna, both now famous Roman ruins.","w":"A pale city on a straight Mediterranean shore, a harbour at its old centre, green farmland fading into desert behind.","f":["The Arch of Marcus Aurelius, built around AD 165, still stands in Tripoli's old town.","Leptis Magna, some 120 km east of the city, is one of the best-preserved Roman cities in the Mediterranean.","Tripoli's walled old town sits right beside the harbour."],"q":{"q":"What does the Greek name Tripoli mean?","a":"Three cities","x":["Three hills","Sea gate"]}},"Q33935":{"t":"A white city on the dunes, an ancient port at its tip","s":"A dense band of pale buildings presses right up to a straight, sandy Mediterranean beach, and at its southern tip, on a low rocky headland, sits Jaffa, a port used for thousands of years. Tel Aviv itself is young: it was founded on the dunes north of Jaffa in 1909.","w":"A pale, dense city along a ruler-straight sandy coast, a small headland at its southern end, a river mouth to the north.","f":["Tel Aviv's 'White City' holds thousands of Bauhaus-style buildings and has been a UNESCO site since 2003.","The name Tel Aviv means 'spring hill' — spring as in the season.","The Yarkon River reaches the sea at the city's northern edge."],"q":{"q":"Which ancient port forms Tel Aviv's southern end?","a":"Jaffa","x":["Acre","Caesarea"]}},"Q1757":{"t":"A granite peninsula city fringed with islands","s":"In a cold winter the sea around Helsinki freezes, and the whole archipelago turns white. The city sits on a ragged granite peninsula in the Gulf of Finland, among bays, inlets and more than three hundred islands; at the harbour mouth, a cluster of islets carries the old sea fortress of Suomenlinna. Tallinn lies only about 80 km across the gulf.","w":"A rocky peninsula ragged with bays and islands, facing south across the gulf, with forest and lakes inland.","f":["Helsinki became Finland's capital in 1812, taking over from Turku.","The Suomenlinna sea fortress, begun in 1748, is a UNESCO World Heritage site.","Helsinki is the northernmost capital in the European Union."],"q":{"q":"Which king founded Helsinki in 1550?","a":"Gustav I of Sweden","x":["Christian IV of Denmark","Peter the Great"]}},"Q121157":{"t":"A pilgrimage city in a valley between two ranges","s":"Mashhad fills a broad valley in north-eastern Iran, with the Binalud mountains to the south-west and the Hezar Masjed range to the north-east. It grew around the shrine of Imam Reza, and its name means 'place of martyrdom'. Millions of pilgrims arrive every year, making it one of the great pilgrimage centres of the Muslim world.","w":"A large city in a wide valley, between a high, often snowy range to the south-west and bare mountains to the north-east.","f":["The tomb of Ferdowsi, author of the Persian epic Shahnameh, stands at Tus, about 25 km from the city.","Mashhad is Iran's second-largest city after Tehran.","The Binalud range south-west of the city rises above 3,000 metres."],"q":{"q":"Which poet's tomb stands at Tus, near Mashhad?","a":"Ferdowsi","x":["Hafez","Saadi"]}},"Q1748":{"t":"Where a bridge dives into a tunnel under the sea","s":"Copenhagen faces the Øresund, the strait between Denmark and Sweden. Look south of the city for one of Europe's great engineering lines: a bridge runs out from Sweden, lands on an artificial island and then vanishes into a tunnel under the sea to reach the Danish shore. The city itself spreads over the edge of Zealand and the flat island of Amager.","w":"A long bridge crossing the strait to an artificial island, where it disappears; the city and airport lie on the Danish side.","f":["The Øresund Bridge is about 8 km long, and the link continues in a 4 km tunnel under the sea.","Peberholm, where the bridge meets the tunnel, is an artificial island built for the crossing.","Bishop Absalon built a castle here around 1167, traditionally taken as the city's founding."],"q":{"q":"What does the Danish name København mean?","a":"Merchants' harbour","x":["King's castle","Salt bay"]}},"Q1515":{"t":"A lagoon city with rainforest inside its limits","s":"Abidjan wraps around the Ébrié Lagoon, a long body of calm water behind a narrow sand bar on the Gulf of Guinea. The city's districts sit on peninsulas and islands, joined by bridges. Look for the straight cut through the sand bar — the Vridi Canal — that lets ships into the lagoon. Within the city, a block of dark green is protected rainforest.","w":"A city spread around a branching lagoon behind a straight sandy coast, cut through by a narrow canal to the sea.","f":["The Vridi Canal, opened in 1950, turned Abidjan into one of West Africa's main ports.","Banco National Park, a remnant of rainforest, lies inside the city.","Abidjan is Côte d'Ivoire's largest city, though the official capital is Yamoussoukro."],"q":{"q":"Which lagoon does Abidjan surround?","a":"The Ébrié Lagoon","x":["The Aby Lagoon","Lagos Lagoon"]}},"Q239":{"t":"Where did the river go? Under the boulevards","s":"Don't look for a river in Brussels: in the 19th century the Senne was vaulted over and hidden beneath the boulevards. What you can see is a city spread over gentle hills in the middle of Belgium, ringed by a motorway, and the Sonian Forest on its south-eastern edge, a large dark block of old beech woods.","w":"A large city in rolling farmland, circled by a ring motorway, with a big dark forest on its south-eastern edge.","f":["The Sonian Forest covers more than 4,000 hectares, and parts of it are UNESCO-listed for their ancient beeches.","The Senne was covered over in the 1860s and 1870s and still flows in tunnels under the city.","The Grand-Place, the old market square, is a UNESCO World Heritage site."],"q":{"q":"What does Bruocsella, the old name of Brussels, mean?","a":"Home in the marsh","x":["Bridge town","Hill fort"]}},"Q3897":{"t":"A bay city sheltered by a long finger of sand","s":"Luanda curves around a bay on Angola's Atlantic coast, sheltered by the Ilha, a long, thin sand spit lined with beaches. Further south, another spit, the Mussulo peninsula, encloses a calm turquoise lagoon for kilometres. Portuguese settlers founded the city in 1576, making it one of the oldest European-founded cities south of the Sahara.","w":"A city curving around a bay closed by a thin sandy spit, with a longer spit and lagoon further south.","f":["The Mussulo peninsula south of the city is a sand spit nearly 30 km long.","The cold Benguela Current keeps this coast surprisingly dry for the tropics.","The Kwanza River, which gave Angola's currency its name, reaches the sea some 70 km to the south."],"q":{"q":"Which European country founded Luanda in 1576?","a":"Portugal","x":["The Netherlands","Spain"]}},"Q3561":{"t":"A white city stacked on the hills above a bay","s":"Algiers rises from a wide, curving bay in white tiers, climbing the steep hills behind the port; the French called it Alger la Blanche, 'Algiers the White'. Its Arabic name refers to small islets offshore, long ago joined to the mainland to form the harbour. The old Casbah clings to the slope above the sea.","w":"A white city climbing steep green hills around a broad, curving bay, with a long harbour breakwater.","f":["The Casbah of Algiers has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1992.","The town was refounded in the 10th century by the Berber ruler Buluggin ibn Ziri, on the site of ancient Icosium.","Algeria is Africa's largest country by area, and Algiers is both its capital and largest city."],"q":{"q":"What does Algiers' Arabic name, al-Jazair, mean?","a":"The islands","x":["The fortress","The harbour"]}},"Q3792":{"t":"A capital pressed right against a border","s":"The edge of Lomé runs straight into a border: the city lies at the western end of Togo's short coastline, so close to Ghana that the two practically touch. Behind the straight, surf-washed beach, a chain of lagoons runs parallel to the coast. The town became the capital of German Togoland in 1897, and later of the independent country.","w":"A low city on a straight sandy coast with lagoons behind it and harbour breakwaters jutting out to sea.","f":["Togo's entire coastline is only just over 50 km long.","Lomé's western districts run right up to the border with Ghana.","The city's Grand Marché is famous for its cloth traders, especially of printed wax fabrics."],"q":{"q":"Which country's border runs along Lomé's western edge?","a":"Ghana","x":["Benin","Nigeria"]}},"Q3572":{"t":"Two shallow lakes, with Carthage next door","s":"Tunis lies on a narrow neck of land between two shallow lakes, where the Gulf of Tunis cuts deep into North Africa. East of the old town, the Lake of Tunis is split by a straight channel that lets ships through to the city's port. On the gulf's shore just to the north-east lie the ruins of Carthage, once Rome's great rival.","w":"A city between a shallow lagoon crossed by a straight channel to the east and a salt flat to the south-west.","f":["The ruins of Carthage, some 15 km from the centre, have been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1979.","The Medina of Tunis, a maze of souks and alleys, is UNESCO-listed too.","The Lake of Tunis is only about a metre deep in places, and flamingos wade in its shallows."],"q":{"q":"Which ancient city's ruins lie beside Tunis?","a":"Carthage","x":["Leptis Magna","Cyrene"]}},"Q7525":{"t":"A city of deep bays near the tip of Crimea","s":"Sevastopol lies near the south-western tip of the Crimean peninsula, on a long, deep bay that cuts several kilometres inland, with smaller bays branching off it like fingers. Beyond the city, the land rises towards the Crimean Mountains. On a headland to the west are the ruins of Chersonesus, a Greek colony founded some 2,400 years ago.","w":"A long, narrow bay reaching inland from the Black Sea, with smaller side bays branching off and the city between them.","f":["The main bay reaches about 7 km inland and is one of the best natural harbours on the Black Sea.","Chersonesus was founded by Greek colonists in the 5th century BC.","The modern city was founded in 1783."],"q":{"q":"Which ancient Greek city's ruins lie at Sevastopol?","a":"Chersonesus","x":["Olbia","Tanais"]}},"Q1519":{"t":"An island city in a maze of mangroves and shallows","s":"Abu Dhabi stands on an island just off the Gulf coast, separated from the mainland by a narrow channel and joined to it by bridges. Around it, the sea is a puzzle of turquoise shallows, sandbanks, dark mangroves and low islands. The city's central grid ends abruptly at the water, with a long corniche curving along its north-western shore.","w":"A grid-planned island city ringed by turquoise shallows, with dark mangroves and many low islands to its east.","f":["Qasr al-Hosn, a fort that began as an 18th-century watchtower, is the oldest stone building in the city.","The emirate of Abu Dhabi covers more than four-fifths of the UAE's territory.","The mangroves east of the island are protected as a national park."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Abu Dhabi mean?","a":"Father of the gazelle","x":["Father of pearls","Gate of the sun"]}},"Q23438":{"t":"A white capital between mountains and the Karakum","s":"Two worlds meet at Ashgabat: the bare Kopet Dag range rises to the south, the Karakum Desert stretches away to the north, and the city lies on a narrow strip of green between them. A thin line of water, the Karakum Canal, curves along the desert's edge, carrying water from the Amu Darya far to the east. The city is known for its white marble facades.","w":"A pale city at the foot of a bare mountain range, with sand desert to the north and a long canal curving past.","f":["The Karakum Canal runs for more than 1,000 km across the desert, one of the longest irrigation canals in the world.","The ruins of Nisa, an early capital of the Parthians, lie just west of the city and are UNESCO-listed.","The Kopet Dag, south of the city, forms Turkmenistan's border with Iran."],"q":{"q":"Which desert stretches north of Ashgabat?","a":"The Karakum","x":["The Kyzylkum","The Gobi"]}},"Q3881":{"t":"A plateau capital that began at a railway siding","s":"The air on Lusaka's plateau is fresher than the latitude suggests: the city lies about 1,280 metres up, on a broad, flat upland in the middle of Zambia. It grew from a small siding on the railway line from the south, built in the early 1900s. In 1935 it became the colonial capital, chosen for its central position and healthy altitude.","w":"A spreading low city on a flat, lightly wooded plateau, with farmland and red-brown earth all around.","f":["The city takes its name from Lusaaka, a local headman whose village stood here.","The Kafue River passes some 50 km south of the city.","Thanks to the altitude, winters here are mild and dry, with cool nights from May to August."],"q":{"q":"What made Lusaka grow in the early 1900s?","a":"A railway siding","x":["A gold mine","A river port"]}},"Q3921":{"t":"A highveld capital among granite hills and jacarandas","s":"Harare spreads over the high grassland of northern Zimbabwe, about 1,500 metres above the sea, where rounded granite hills poke out of the plateau. South-west of the city, look for a winding lake: Lake Chivero, a reservoir on the Manyame River. In October, thousands of jacaranda trees along the avenues burst into purple flower.","w":"A sprawling city on a flat grassy plateau dotted with granite outcrops; a winding reservoir lies to the south-west.","f":["Harare lies at about 1,490 metres, so its summers are warm rather than hot.","Lake Chivero, about 30 km south-west of the centre, supplies much of the city's water.","The first settlement grew beside the Kopje, a granite hill next to today's city centre."],"q":{"q":"What was Harare called until 1982?","a":"Salisbury","x":["Fort Victoria","Umtali"]}},"Q193250":{"t":"A city of wide streets near a land of granite domes","s":"Bulawayo sits on the dry, open plateau of south-western Zimbabwe, a neat grid of unusually broad streets. They were laid out wide enough, it is said, for a wagon pulled by a full span of oxen to turn around. About 35 km south rise the Matobo Hills, a wild landscape of bare granite domes and precariously balanced boulders.","w":"A grid-planned city on a dry, flat plateau, with rough country of bare granite hills to the south.","f":["The Matobo Hills have been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 2003, noted for their ancient rock paintings.","Bulawayo was the royal town of the Ndebele king Lobengula before the colonial city was laid out in 1894.","Bulawayo is Zimbabwe's second-largest city."],"q":{"q":"Which granite hills rise south of Bulawayo?","a":"The Matobo Hills","x":["The Chimanimani Mountains","The Drakensberg"]}},"Q1764":{"t":"The world's northernmost capital, named after steam","s":"Dark lava fields, some of the youngest land in Europe, sweep across the Reykjanes peninsula south-west of Reykjavík. The city sits on a low peninsula on the south shore of Faxaflói bay, with the long, flat-topped ridge of Mount Esja across the water. The first settler, Ingólfur Arnarson, is said to have named the bay after steam rising from hot springs.","w":"A low, compact city on a peninsula, a flat-topped mountain across the bay, and dark lava fields to the south-west.","f":["Reykjavík is the northernmost capital of a sovereign country.","Nearly all homes in the city are heated with hot water from geothermal sources.","Mount Esja, across the bay, rises to about 900 metres."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Reykjavík mean?","a":"Smoky bay","x":["Ice harbour","Whale fjord"]}},"Q4093":{"t":"Scotland's biggest city, on the shipbuilding Clyde","s":"Glasgow spreads along the Clyde, a river that once built a large share of the world's ships. Downstream, the river widens into the long Firth of Clyde, scattered with islands. To the north the low, green Campsie Fells rise, and beyond them, some 30 km away, lies Loch Lomond, the largest lake by area in Great Britain.","w":"A large city on a river flowing west to a long, island-studded firth, with green hills and a big loch to the north-west.","f":["Loch Lomond is the largest lake in Great Britain by surface area.","Glasgow's name is often said to come from Gaelic words meaning 'dear green place'.","Glasgow is Scotland's largest city, bigger than the capital, Edinburgh."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Glasgow?","a":"The Clyde","x":["The Forth","The Tay"]}},"Q3894":{"t":"Green hills on the shore of Africa's largest lake","s":"Kampala is built across a cluster of green hills just north of Lake Victoria, with the city filling the valleys in between. Its name comes from an old Luganda phrase for 'hill of the impala'. You are almost exactly on the equator here: the line runs only about 35 km south of the centre, across the lake.","w":"A green, hilly city spilling down to a ragged bay of a vast lake, with the Entebbe peninsula to the south-west.","f":["Lake Victoria is the largest lake in Africa and the main source of the White Nile.","The Nile leaves Lake Victoria at Jinja, about 80 km east of Kampala.","The Kasubi Tombs, burial place of the kings of Buganda, are a UNESCO World Heritage site."],"q":{"q":"Which lake lies just south of Kampala?","a":"Lake Victoria","x":["Lake Albert","Lake Kyoga"]}},"Q131694":{"t":"A port city built inside an old volcano","s":"Aden grew on a rugged volcanic peninsula on the Gulf of Aden, and its oldest district really does sit in a crater, ringed by the jagged walls of an extinct volcano. Across the harbour, the Little Aden peninsula is another old volcano. The deep, sheltered water between them made Aden one of the great ports on the sea route between Europe and India.","w":"Two dark volcanic peninsulas enclosing a wide natural harbour, with the city tucked into their bays and craters.","f":["The ancient Tawila cisterns, cut into the crater wall, were built to catch the area's rare rain.","In 1839 Aden became a British coaling station for steamships sailing to India.","Jebel Shamsan, the highest point of the crater rim, rises to about 550 metres."],"q":{"q":"What is Aden's old town, Crater, built inside?","a":"An extinct volcano","x":["A meteorite crater","A dried-up lake"]}},"Q8717":{"t":"The inland port that once ruled trade with the Americas","s":"Ocean-going ships once sailed right up the Guadalquivir to Seville, some 80 km from the sea, and from 1503 the city held the royal monopoly on trade with the Americas. Today it spreads across a flat, sun-bleached plain of olive groves and farmland. Downstream, the river winds through the vast Doñana marshes towards the Atlantic.","w":"A large city on a flat plain beside a river, with a straight, canal-like channel and farmland stretching in every direction.","f":["Seville has Spain's only inland river port.","Magellan's expedition, the first to sail around the world, set out from Seville in 1519.","Seville Cathedral is often called the largest Gothic cathedral in the world."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Seville?","a":"The Guadalquivir","x":["The Guadiana","The Ebro"]}},"Q204365":{"t":"An old sultanate town among Sahel granite","s":"Zinder spreads across the dry Sahel of southern Niger, a low, sand-coloured town scattered among outcrops of bare granite. It was the seat of the Sultanate of Damagaram, which grew rich on caravans crossing the Sahara between Kano and the north. Its old walled quarter, the Birni, still surrounds the sultan's palace.","w":"A low, sandy town among bare rocky outcrops on flat, dry Sahel plains, with scattered fields around it.","f":["The Sultanate of Damagaram made Zinder its capital in the 18th century.","The Nigerian city of Kano lies about 200 km to the south.","Zinder served as the capital of the French colony of Niger until 1926."],"q":{"q":"Which city replaced Zinder as Niger's capital in 1926?","a":"Niamey","x":["Agadez","Maradi"]}},"Q3674":{"t":"A Sahel capital on the great river Niger","s":"Green strips of rice fields and gardens line the broad brown Niger at Niamey, the only bright colour in an otherwise dusty land. Only a small village in the late 1800s, the city became the colonial capital in 1926 and has grown fast ever since. East of it, the scrubland is home to the last wild herd of West African giraffes.","w":"A city on a wide brown river flowing south-east, with green ribbons of fields along its banks and dry plateaus beyond.","f":["The last wild West African giraffes live around Kouré, about 60 km south-east of Niamey.","The Niger, Africa's third-longest river, loops through the Sahel past the city.","Downstream lies W National Park, named after a W-shaped bend of the Niger."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows past Niamey?","a":"The Niger","x":["The Senegal","The Volta"]}},"Q208718":{"t":"A young Red Sea harbour with coral reefs offshore","s":"Port Sudan lies on a flat, hot strip of Red Sea coast, where a deep, coral-fringed inlet makes a natural harbour. It is a young city, built in the early 1900s to replace the old port of Suakin, whose channel was too narrow for modern ships. Offshore, look for turquoise patches in the deep blue: reefs and atolls.","w":"A low city around a sheltered inlet on a straight desert coast, with turquoise reefs offshore and dry hills inland.","f":["Sanganeb, an atoll about 25 km offshore, is a UNESCO-listed marine national park.","The Red Sea Hills behind the coastal plain rise to more than 2,000 metres.","Work on the new port began in 1905."],"q":{"q":"Which old port did Port Sudan replace as the main harbour?","a":"Suakin","x":["Massawa","Berbera"]}},"Q3780":{"t":"Lion mountains above one of the world's great harbours","s":"Freetown clings to the northern tip of a mountainous peninsula, wedged between forested hills and the wide estuary of the Sierra Leone River. That estuary is one of the largest natural harbours in the world. Portuguese sailors named the peaks 'Serra Leoa', the lion mountains. The city was founded in 1792 as a home for freed Africans.","w":"A city squeezed between green, mountainous ridges and a very wide river estuary opening onto the Atlantic.","f":["The highest hills of the Freetown Peninsula rise to nearly 900 metres.","Settlers from Nova Scotia, formerly enslaved Africans, founded the city in 1792.","The peninsula's rainforest is protected as the Western Area Peninsula National Park."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Sierra Leone mean?","a":"Lion mountains","x":["Green coast","River of gold"]}},"Q320049":{"t":"A mud-brick gateway to the Sahara","s":"Agadez is a sand-coloured oasis town at the southern edge of the Aïr Mountains, where the Sahel gives way to the Sahara. For centuries it was a crossroads for Tuareg caravans carrying salt and goods across the desert. Its skyline is marked by a mud-brick minaret bristling with wooden beams, first raised in the early 16th century.","w":"A small, earth-coloured town on flat sand, with the dark, rugged Aïr Mountains rising to the north and east.","f":["The minaret of the Grand Mosque is about 27 metres tall, one of the tallest mud-brick structures in the world.","The historic centre of Agadez became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2013.","The Aïr Mountains north of the town rise to just over 2,000 metres."],"q":{"q":"What is Agadez's famous minaret built of?","a":"Mud brick","x":["White marble","Coral stone"]}},"Q851576":{"t":"A savanna city below a volcanic massif","s":"Nyala lies in the rolling savanna of southern Darfur, beside a wide, sandy wadi that runs with water only in the rainy season. From above, the land is dotted with acacia and cultivated patches that turn green after the summer rains. To the north-west rise the Marra Mountains, a volcanic massif crowned by a great caldera.","w":"A spread-out town on flat, dotted savanna beside a broad sandy riverbed, with dark volcanic highlands far to the north-west.","f":["Jebel Marra rises to about 3,000 metres, far above the surrounding plains.","Nyala is the largest city in the Darfur region.","Rain falls here mostly between June and September; the rest of the year is dry."],"q":{"q":"What lies inside the summit caldera of Jebel Marra, near Nyala?","a":"Two crater lakes","x":["A glacier","A salt flat"]}},"Q276534":{"t":"A river town on the red earth of the ironstone plateau","s":"Wau sits on the Jur River in the north-west of South Sudan, where wooded savanna on reddish ironstone soils stretches in every direction. In the rainy season the whole region turns a vivid green and the river swells. Its water flows on towards the Sudd, one of the largest wetlands in the world, before reaching the White Nile.","w":"A modest town on a winding river amid flat, wooded savanna, with patches of red earth showing through.","f":["Wau is one of the largest cities in South Sudan.","The Jur feeds the Bahr el Ghazal, which meets the White Nile at Lake No.","The reddish soils around Wau belong to the ironstone plateau that covers much of western South Sudan."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows past Wau?","a":"The Jur","x":["The Sobat","The Blue Nile"]}},"Q821321":{"t":"A thin green thread of the Nile through the desert","s":"Dongola sits on the west bank of the Nile in northern Sudan, where the river's strip of date palms and fields is only a few kilometres wide and yellow desert presses in on both sides. From above it is the clearest picture of how the Nile makes life possible. Upstream, Old Dongola was once a medieval Nubian royal capital.","w":"A narrow ribbon of dark green along the Nile, with a small town on its west bank and bare desert on both sides.","f":["Kerma, about 50 km downstream, was the centre of one of Africa's earliest kingdoms more than 4,000 years ago.","Between Khartoum and Egypt the Nile makes a huge S-shaped bend, and Dongola lies on its north-flowing stretch.","Rain is so rare here that some years pass with almost none."],"q":{"q":"Old Dongola was the capital of which medieval kingdom?","a":"Makuria","x":["Aksum","Kanem"]}},"Q686813":{"t":"A town at the foot of sudden granite towers","s":"Kassala lies on the flat plain of eastern Sudan, right at the foot of the Taka Mountains, a cluster of bare granite domes that shoot up abruptly from the land and are unmistakable from the air. The seasonal Gash River flows past the town and spreads into an inland delta of farms to the north. The Eritrean border lies just to the east.","w":"A cluster of steep, bare rock domes rising straight out of a flat plain, with a town and green orchards at their base.","f":["The Taka Mountains rise several hundred metres straight up from the surrounding plain.","The Gash River flows only in the rainy season and fans out into an inland delta north of the town.","Orchards watered by the Gash make Kassala known across Sudan for its fruit."],"q":{"q":"What rises abruptly beside Kassala?","a":"Bare granite mountains","x":["Sand dunes","A volcanic cone"]}},"Q26087":{"t":"An Arctic island city under the northern lights","s":"In midwinter the sun doesn't rise here for weeks and the northern lights often dance overhead; in midsummer it doesn't set. Tromsø sits mainly on a small island between steep, snow-streaked mountains and deep sounds, about 350 km north of the Arctic Circle, tied to the mainland by bridges and tunnels. Polar expeditions long set out from here.","w":"A small city on a low island in a narrow sound, ringed by steep, white-topped mountains and fjords.","f":["The midnight sun shines here from about late May to late July.","During the polar night, from late November to mid-January, the sun stays below the horizon.","Tromsø lies under the auroral oval, one of the best places on Earth to see the northern lights."],"q":{"q":"Roughly how far north of the Arctic Circle is Tromsø?","a":"About 350 km","x":["About 50 km","About 1,000 km"]}},"Q25804":{"t":"A Viking-age town cradled in a loop of river","s":"Trondheim sits on the south shore of the long Trondheimsfjord, where the river Nidelva curls around the old town in a tight loop, almost turning it into an island. King Olav Tryggvason founded the town in 997, and for centuries pilgrims came here to the shrine of Saint Olav. Look for the fjord's broad arm reaching far inland.","w":"A city on a long fjord, with a river curling round the compact old centre in a near-complete loop.","f":["Nidaros Cathedral, built over Saint Olav's grave, is the northernmost medieval cathedral in the world.","The Trondheimsfjord is about 130 km long, one of Norway's longest fjords.","Warm Atlantic water keeps the fjord largely free of ice through the winter."],"q":{"q":"What was Trondheim's medieval name?","a":"Nidaros","x":["Bjørgvin","Christiania"]}},"Q26793":{"t":"A rainy port ringed by seven mountains","s":"Rain is Bergen's trademark: squeezed between steep mountains and a maze of islands and fjords on Norway's west coast, it is one of Europe's rainiest cities. Look for a small harbour bay cutting into the centre, framed by mountains. For centuries this was a key Hanseatic port, shipping dried cod from the north across Europe.","w":"A city squeezed around a narrow harbour between steep mountains, with countless islands and fjords spreading out to sea.","f":["Bergen gets roughly 2,250 mm of rain in an average year.","Bryggen, the old Hanseatic wharf, has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1979.","The Sognefjord, Norway's longest and deepest fjord, opens to the sea north of the city."],"q":{"q":"Which Hanseatic wharf is Bergen famous for?","a":"Bryggen","x":["Speicherstadt","Nyhavn"]}},"Q990080":{"t":"A deep-water bay on Mozambique's northern coast","s":"Nacala lies on the shore of a long, deep bay on Mozambique's northern coast, one of the finest natural harbours in East Africa. The water is deep enough for big ships to come close in without dredging. From above, look for a narrow-mouthed inlet reaching inland between green hills, with coral reefs along the ocean shore outside.","w":"A long, deep inlet reaching inland through a narrow mouth, with a town on its shore and green hills around.","f":["Nacala's bay is often described as the deepest natural harbour on Africa's east coast.","A railway links Nacala's port with landlocked Malawi.","Coral reefs fringe the coast outside the bay."],"q":{"q":"Which UNESCO-listed island lies about 60 km south of Nacala?","a":"Mozambique Island","x":["Zanzibar","Lamu"]}},"Q37701":{"t":"The judicial capital on the open Free State plains","s":"Bloemfontein sits in the middle of South Africa's high, flat grasslands, about 1,400 metres up, where a few rocky, flat-topped hills — koppies — break the horizon. It is one of the country's three capitals, home to the Supreme Court of Appeal. It is also where J. R. R. Tolkien was born, in 1892.","w":"A compact city on wide, flat, tawny grassland, with a few small flat-topped hills around its centre.","f":["Naval Hill, in the middle of the city, is a small nature reserve with giraffes.","South Africa has three capitals: Pretoria, Cape Town and Bloemfontein.","The city was founded in 1846 as a British outpost beside a spring."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Bloemfontein mean?","a":"Fountain of flowers","x":["Flower hill","Blooming valley"]}},"Q3926":{"t":"The jacaranda city between long rocky ridges","s":"In October and November, tens of thousands of jacaranda trees bloom along Pretoria's streets, washing whole suburbs in purple. The city lies in valleys between long, parallel east–west ridges of the Magaliesberg, about 50 km north of Johannesburg, and is South Africa's executive capital. It was founded in 1855 and named after Andries Pretorius.","w":"A city spread along valleys between long, straight east–west ridges, merging southwards into the Johannesburg sprawl.","f":["Tswaing, a meteorite crater about 1.1 km across, lies some 40 km north-west of the city.","The rocks of the Magaliesberg are more than two billion years old.","Pretoria's jacarandas are not native: they were brought from South America."],"q":{"q":"Which tree turns Pretoria purple in spring?","a":"Jacaranda","x":["Flame tree","Mimosa"]}},"Q80985":{"t":"A medieval maze still living inside its walls","s":"Fez lies in a green valley between the Middle Atlas and the Rif, and its old medina, Fes el-Bali, is one of the largest car-free urban areas in the world. From above, it is a dense, pale mass in a bowl of hills, with the newer city laid out on the plain beside it. Its university, al-Qarawiyyin, dates back to 859.","w":"A dense, pale old city packed into a bowl of hills, with a grid-planned new town spreading beside it on the plain.","f":["Al-Qarawiyyin, founded in 859, is often cited as the oldest continuously operating university in the world.","The Medina of Fez has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1981.","Fez's tanneries still dye leather in stone vats, much as they did centuries ago."],"q":{"q":"Who founded al-Qarawiyyin in Fez in 859?","a":"Fatima al-Fihri","x":["Idris I","Ibn Battuta"]}},"Q3551":{"t":"Twin towns facing each other across a river mouth","s":"Rabat sits on the Atlantic where the Bou Regreg river reaches the sea, facing its older twin, Salé, across the estuary. On the clifftop at the river mouth stands the walled Kasbah of the Udayas. A little upstream, a 12th-century caliph began a vast mosque whose unfinished minaret, the Hassan Tower, still overlooks the water.","w":"Two cities facing each other across a river mouth on a straight Atlantic coast, with a walled fortress on the southern point.","f":["The Hassan Tower was left unfinished when its builder, the Almohad caliph Yaqub al-Mansur, died in 1199.","Rabat's historic and modern city became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2012.","In the 17th century Salé was a base for Atlantic corsairs, the 'Sallee Rovers'."],"q":{"q":"Which city faces Rabat across the Bou Regreg river?","a":"Salé","x":["Kenitra","Témara"]}},"Q101625":{"t":"A red city with snowy peaks on the horizon","s":"Look south from Marrakesh and the High Atlas rises like a wall some 50 km away, its peaks often white from winter into spring. The city itself spreads across the flat Haouz plain, all ochre-red walls and palm groves. Look for the dark green of the palm grove to the north and the neat rectangles of old garden pools.","w":"A reddish city on a flat, dry plain, with a dark palm grove to the north and the snow-capped High Atlas to the south.","f":["Toubkal, North Africa's highest peak at about 4,170 metres, lies some 60 km south of the city.","The Almoravid dynasty founded Marrakesh around 1070.","The Medina of Marrakesh has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1985."],"q":{"q":"Which mountain range rises south of Marrakesh?","a":"The High Atlas","x":["The Rif","The Middle Atlas"]}},"Q21197":{"t":"A green hill city above endless wine cellars","s":"Chișinău spreads over gentle hills in the middle of Moldova, with an unusual amount of green: parks, lakes and tree-lined avenues show up clearly from above. Around it, rolling farmland is striped with vineyards. Beneath the countryside just north and south of the city run old limestone quarries, many kilometres long, now filled with wine.","w":"A leafy city on rolling hills, dotted with small lakes and parks and surrounded by striped fields and vineyards.","f":["The Mileștii Mici cellars south of the city hold what Guinness World Records listed as the largest wine collection in the world.","The Cricova cellars, north of the city, have about 120 km of underground streets.","Chișinău is first mentioned in a document of 1436."],"q":{"q":"What fills the old limestone tunnels near Chișinău?","a":"Wine cellars","x":["Salt mines","Mushroom farms"]}},"Q211563":{"t":"A low port city on a muddy estuary with big tides","s":"Beira lies on flat, low ground where the Pungwe River meets the Indian Ocean, surrounded by mangroves, mudflats and wide, sandy shallows. The tides here are strong, exposing broad banks of sand twice a day. Founded by the Portuguese in 1887, the city grew as the sea gateway for landlocked lands inland.","w":"A flat coastal city at a wide, muddy estuary, fringed by dark mangroves and pale sandbanks exposed at low tide.","f":["The Beira Corridor links the port with Zimbabwe by road, railway and oil pipeline.","At spring tides the sea here rises and falls by several metres.","The Zambezi delta, Mozambique's largest, lies about 200 km up the coast to the north-east."],"q":{"q":"At the mouth of which river does Beira stand?","a":"The Pungwe","x":["The Zambezi","The Limpopo"]}},"Q125434":{"t":"A windy bay city with elephants just inland","s":"Port Elizabeth curves along the western shore of Algoa Bay, a broad sweep of the Indian Ocean on South Africa's southern coast. It is nicknamed the Windy City, and from above you may spot whitecaps racing across the bay. About 40 km inland, Addo Elephant National Park protects hundreds of elephants in dense bush. The city is now officially called Gqeberha.","w":"A city along the western curve of a wide, open bay, with a small cape at its southern end and dry bush inland.","f":["British settlers who arrived in 1820 laid the foundations of the town.","Fort Frederick, a stone fort built by the British in 1799, overlooks the harbour.","St Croix Island in Algoa Bay has one of the world's largest African penguin colonies."],"q":{"q":"Which bay does Port Elizabeth face?","a":"Algoa Bay","x":["Table Bay","False Bay"]}},"Q3889":{"t":"A capital on a wide bay, a bridge across its estuary","s":"Maputo sits on the western shore of Maputo Bay, a broad inlet of the Indian Ocean, on low red cliffs above the water. Out in the bay, the green island of Inhaca guards the entrance. A tall suspension bridge now crosses the estuary just south of the centre. Until 1976 the city was called Lourenço Marques, after a Portuguese trader who explored the bay in the 1540s.","w":"A city on red cliffs along a wide bay, with a suspension bridge across the estuary and a large island near the bay's mouth.","f":["The Maputo–Katembe Bridge, opened in 2018, has a main span of about 680 metres.","Inhaca Island, at the mouth of the bay, is ringed by coral reefs and seagrass meadows.","Maputo lies less than 100 km from the borders of both South Africa and Eswatini."],"q":{"q":"What was Maputo called until 1976?","a":"Lourenço Marques","x":["Sofala","Vila Pery"]}},"Q1763":{"t":"The largest city beyond the Arctic Circle","s":"No city north of the Arctic Circle is bigger than Murmansk. It stretches along the eastern shore of the Kola Bay, a long, fjord-like inlet running north to the Barents Sea. Thanks to the last warm reach of the North Atlantic Current, its port stays free of ice all winter, while the lakes and tundra around it freeze solid.","w":"A long city on the hilly east shore of a narrow, fjord-like bay, surrounded by low tundra dotted with lakes.","f":["The polar night here lasts about 40 days, from early December to mid-January.","The midnight sun shines over Murmansk from late May to late July.","Murmansk was founded in 1916."],"q":{"q":"What keeps Murmansk's port free of ice in winter?","a":"Warm North Atlantic water","x":["Salt from nearby mines","Hot volcanic springs"]}},"ne-pp-1159150693":{"t":"Russia's first seaport, on a great northern delta","s":"Arkhangelsk lies where the Northern Dvina fans out into a broad delta of channels and islands before meeting the White Sea. Endless dark taiga surrounds it. Founded in 1584 around the monastery of the Archangel Michael, it was for over a century Russia's main gateway to the sea, until Saint Petersburg took over. In winter the frozen river becomes a white highway.","w":"A long city along a wide river splitting into many channels just before a large bay, surrounded by dark forest.","f":["The scientist Mikhail Lomonosov was born in 1711 near Kholmogory, upstream on the Dvina.","Ice covers the sea near the city for roughly half the year.","The Solovetsky Islands, a UNESCO site, lie in the White Sea about 230 km to the west."],"q":{"q":"Which river forms a delta at Arkhangelsk?","a":"The Northern Dvina","x":["The Pechora","The Onega"]}},"Q891":{"t":"Where the Oka pours into the Volga","s":"Nizhny Novgorod stands at one of Russia's great river meetings: the Oka flows into the Volga right at the city's heart. The old town sits high on the steep right bank, looking across the water to a low, forested plain on the far side. The city was founded in 1221 to guard this junction of river routes.","w":"Two wide rivers meeting at a sharp point, with the city on high wooded bluffs and flat forest across the Volga.","f":["A cable car across the Volga links the city with the town of Bor, about 3.6 km away.","From 1817 the city hosted a famous summer trade fair, one of the largest in the Russian Empire.","The Volga, which flows past the city, is Europe's longest river at about 3,500 km."],"q":{"q":"Which river joins the Volga at Nizhny Novgorod?","a":"The Oka","x":["The Kama","The Sheksna"]}},"Q914":{"t":"More than 60 km of city along one riverbank","s":"Volgograd is one of the longest cities in Europe: a narrow ribbon along the high western bank of the Volga, running for more than 60 km. Across the river lies a completely different world, the green Volga–Akhtuba floodplain, laced with channels, lakes and woods. At the city's southern end, the Volga–Don Canal sets off west towards the Don.","w":"A long, narrow city on the west bank of the broad Volga, facing a green, channel-laced floodplain on the east.","f":["The Volga–Don Canal, about 100 km long, links the Volga with the Don and so with the Black Sea.","A dam just north of the city holds back the Volgograd Reservoir, which is about 540 km long.","The Volga–Akhtuba floodplain opposite the city is protected as a nature park."],"q":{"q":"What was Volgograd's original name?","a":"Tsaritsyn","x":["Simbirsk","Kuybyshev"]}},"Q911":{"t":"A city on a ridge between two winding rivers","s":"Ufa stretches along a long, high ridge between two rivers, the Belaya and the Ufa, which wind below it in wide loops before they meet. To the east the land rises gently towards the Southern Urals. The city is the capital of Bashkortostan, a region known for its forested hills and its honey from wild bees.","w":"A long city on a high ridge squeezed between two meandering rivers, with forested hills to the east.","f":["The Belaya, Ufa's main river, is the largest tributary of the Kama.","In Bashkortostan's forests, honey is still gathered from wild bees nesting in hollow trees.","The city grew from a Russian fortress founded in 1574."],"q":{"q":"Ufa stands where the river Ufa meets which other river?","a":"The Belaya","x":["The Chusovaya","The Vyatka"]}},"Q894":{"t":"Where the Volga swings round a loop of wooded hills","s":"Samara lies on the left bank of the Volga beside one of the river's most dramatic features, the Samara Bend: the Volga swings east in a huge loop around the forested Zhiguli Mountains. From above, the loop and its dark wooded hills are unmistakable. The city stretches for kilometres along the river, which is more than a kilometre wide here.","w":"A long city on the east bank of the broad Volga, facing the dark, forested hills inside the river's great loop.","f":["The Zhiguli Mountains inside the loop are protected as a nature reserve and a national park.","Samara was founded in 1586 as a fortress guarding the Volga trade route.","Upstream, the Volga widens into the Kuybyshev Reservoir, the largest reservoir in Europe."],"q":{"q":"Which hills does the Volga loop around near Samara?","a":"The Zhiguli Mountains","x":["The Valdai Hills","The Khibiny"]}},"Q900":{"t":"The Volga spread wide as a sea, and a white kremlin","s":"Kazan stands on the left bank of the Volga where the little Kazanka river flows in, and here the Volga, swollen by a reservoir downstream, is several kilometres wide. The white-walled kremlin sits on a hill above the confluence. For centuries Russian and Tatar, Christian and Muslim cultures have met here, and it is the capital of Tatarstan.","w":"A large city on the east bank of a very wide stretch of the Volga, split by a smaller river flowing in from the north-east.","f":["The Kazan Kremlin has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 2000.","Some 70 km downstream, the Kama, the Volga's largest tributary, joins it.","Kazan celebrated its 1,000th anniversary in 2005."],"q":{"q":"Which small river flows into the Volga at Kazan's kremlin?","a":"The Kazanka","x":["The Oka","The Sura"]}},"Q2449":{"t":"An old Indian Ocean trading port among white dunes","s":"Mogadishu lines the Indian Ocean coast of the Horn of Africa, a pale city backed by sand dunes, with reefs just offshore. For a thousand years it was a trading port linking Africa with Arabia, Persia and India. When the traveller Ibn Battuta visited in 1331, he described a very large and wealthy town whose cloth was sent as far as Egypt.","w":"A pale city along a straight surf coast, backed by sandy dunes, with the green valley of the Shabelle inland to the west.","f":["Mogadishu lies only about 230 km north of the equator.","The Shabelle River passes within about 30 km of the city but never reaches the sea.","In the Middle Ages the Sultanate of Mogadishu minted its own coins."],"q":{"q":"Which famous traveller visited Mogadishu in 1331?","a":"Ibn Battuta","x":["Marco Polo","Vasco da Gama"]}},"Q3826":{"t":"White coves beneath mountains made of ocean floor","s":"Muscat doesn't sprawl like other Gulf capitals: it is threaded through a string of coves and valleys between the rugged Hajar Mountains and the Gulf of Oman. The dark brown rock of those mountains is ancient ocean floor, pushed up onto land. In the old harbour, two Portuguese forts from the 16th century still guard the entrance.","w":"A long string of white districts in valleys and coves between dark, jagged mountains and a blue sea.","f":["The Semail ophiolite, the dark rock around Muscat, is one of the largest exposed slabs of oceanic crust on Earth.","Forts Jalali and Mirani were built by the Portuguese in the 1580s.","For centuries Muscat's sheltered cove served ships trading between Arabia, India and East Africa."],"q":{"q":"What are Muscat's dark mountains largely made of?","a":"Ancient ocean floor","x":["Volcanic ash","Coral limestone"]}},"Q465022":{"t":"Nigeria's coal city below a green escarpment","s":"Enugu lies at the foot of the Udi Hills, a steep, green escarpment rising on its western side in south-eastern Nigeria. Coal was found in those hills in 1909, and the town grew up around the mines, earning the nickname 'Coal City'. From above, look for the city nestled against the ridge, with rolling, wooded farmland all around.","w":"A city at the foot of a steep, green escarpment on its west side, amid rolling, wooded hills.","f":["Coal was discovered in the Udi Hills in 1909, and mining began a few years later.","The Udi Plateau rises a few hundred metres above the plain around the city."],"q":{"q":"What does the Igbo name Enugu mean?","a":"Top of the hill","x":["River bend","Market town"]}},"Q336350":{"t":"Capital of a 19th-century caliphate, on a Sahel river","s":"Sokoto lies in the far north-west of Nigeria, where the Sokoto and Rima rivers meet on a broad, flat floodplain. In the rainy season the valley floor turns green with rice and vegetables; the rest of the year, dusty savanna stretches to the horizon. The city was founded in 1809 as the capital of the Sokoto Caliphate, one of Africa's largest 19th-century states.","w":"A low, dusty city on a flat plain beside a broad river valley, with green fields along the water.","f":["The Sokoto Caliphate stretched from present-day Burkina Faso to Cameroon.","Sokoto is one of Nigeria's hottest cities, with April afternoons often above 40 °C.","The Sultan of Sokoto is still regarded as a spiritual leader by many Nigerian Muslims."],"q":{"q":"Who founded the Sokoto Caliphate?","a":"Usman dan Fodio","x":["Mansa Musa","Sundiata Keita"]}},"Q915":{"t":"The city that gave its name to a geological age","s":"A whole period of Earth's history is named after this region: in 1841 the Scottish geologist Roderick Murchison studied its rocks and called it the Permian. Perm itself stretches for kilometres along the Kama, a broad river that widens into a reservoir just upstream. Endless taiga surrounds it, and to the east the first low ridges of the Urals begin.","w":"A long city on both banks of a wide river, with a reservoir upstream and dark forest in every direction.","f":["The Permian period, roughly 299 to 252 million years ago, ended with the largest mass extinction in Earth's history.","Perm began in 1723 as a copper-smelting works.","The city stretches for about 70 km along the Kama."],"q":{"q":"Which geological period is named after the Perm region?","a":"The Permian","x":["The Devonian","The Jurassic"]}},"Q335548":{"t":"A highland town near a giant iron meteorite","s":"Mbeya sits in a high valley in Tanzania's Southern Highlands, about 1,700 metres up, with the steep Mbeya Range rising right behind it. The land around is intensely green, a patchwork of tea, coffee and maize on rich volcanic soils. South-west of the town, a lump of iron from space weighing around 16 tonnes still rests where it fell.","w":"A town in a green valley at the foot of a steep mountain range, surrounded by a patchwork of highland farms.","f":["The Mbozi meteorite, some 50 km south-west of Mbeya, is one of the largest meteorites ever found.","Mbeya Peak, the highest point of the range above the town, rises to about 2,800 metres.","Further east, the Kitulo Plateau is so rich in wild orchids that it is called the 'Garden of God'."],"q":{"q":"What giant object lies at Mbozi, near Mbeya?","a":"An iron meteorite","x":["A dinosaur skeleton","A fallen satellite"]}},"Q3935":{"t":"A dry-country capital that recycles every drop","s":"Water is so precious in Windhoek that since 1968 the city has turned wastewater directly into drinking water, a world first. It lies in a basin in Namibia's central highlands, about 1,650 metres up, framed by the Auas Mountains to the south and the Eros hills to the north, with dry, thorny savanna all around.","w":"A compact city in a bowl of low, rugged hills on dry, brown highland savanna.","f":["Namibia's Atlantic coast lies about 300 km to the west.","The Auas Mountains south of the city rise to about 2,480 metres, among the highest in Namibia.","Hot springs once bubbled up in the valley, drawing the first settlers."],"q":{"q":"What has Windhoek done with its wastewater since 1968?","a":"Turned it into drinking water","x":["Piped it into the Atlantic","Used it to cool a power plant"]}},"Q986336":{"t":"A quiet farm town beside the heaviest meteorite on Earth","s":"Grootfontein is a small town in the bushland of northern Namibia, named after a large spring that drew settlers and cattle herders. The surprise lies about 20 km west, on a farm: the Hoba meteorite, a slab of iron weighing around 60 tonnes, the largest known on Earth. It is so heavy that it has never been moved from where it landed.","w":"A small town in green, bushy savanna, with low dolomite hills and farmland around it.","f":["The Hoba meteorite fell less than 80,000 years ago and was found by a farmer in 1920.","Grootfontein means 'big spring' in Afrikaans.","The dolomite country around the town hides sinkhole lakes, such as Otjikoto near Tsumeb."],"q":{"q":"What record-breaking object lies near Grootfontein?","a":"The largest known meteorite","x":["The oldest known baobab","Africa's largest crater"]}},"Q2222874":{"t":"A spice-island port of coral stone and turquoise water","s":"Zanzibar City lies on the west coast of Unguja, the main island of the Zanzibar archipelago, facing the African mainland across a channel of turquoise shallows and reefs. Its old heart, Stone Town, is a maze of coral-stone houses. In 1840 the Sultan of Oman made it his capital, and cloves grown on the island were shipped around the world.","w":"A town on the west coast of a flat green island, fringed by turquoise reefs and sandbanks, with the mainland to the west.","f":["Stone Town has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 2000.","Freddie Mercury was born in Stone Town in 1946.","In the 19th century Zanzibar was the world's leading producer of cloves."],"q":{"q":"Which spice made Zanzibar famous in the 19th century?","a":"Cloves","x":["Saffron","Vanilla"]}},"Q8818":{"t":"A green river of parkland through an orange-grove city","s":"A long green ribbon curves through Valencia: the old bed of the river Turia, turned into a park after the river was diverted to the south. Around the city lies the huerta, a patchwork of irrigated orchards and fields on a flat coastal plain. Just south of the city glints the Albufera, a lagoon ringed by rice paddies.","w":"A large city on a flat coast, with a green park snaking through it and a big shallow lagoon and rice fields to the south.","f":["The Turia Gardens, laid out in the old riverbed, run for about 9 km through the city.","The rice fields around the Albufera lagoon are the home of paella.","The Romans founded Valencia in 138 BC."],"q":{"q":"Which dish was born in the rice fields around Valencia?","a":"Paella","x":["Gazpacho","Tortilla"]}},"Q3787":{"t":"A planned capital among giant granite domes","s":"Abuja was built from scratch near the geographic centre of Nigeria and became the capital in 1991. From above, look for its broad avenues fanning out below Aso Rock, a bare granite dome about 400 metres high on the city's eastern edge. The hilly savanna around it is studded with more of these great rounded rocks.","w":"A spacious planned city of wide avenues at the foot of a big bare granite dome, in hilly green savanna.","f":["Zuma Rock, a huge granite monolith about 40 km west of the city, appears on Nigeria's 100-naira note.","The city's master plan was drawn up in the late 1970s.","Abuja lies in the Federal Capital Territory, carved out so the capital would belong to no single state."],"q":{"q":"Which city did Abuja replace as Nigeria's capital in 1991?","a":"Lagos","x":["Ibadan","Kano"]}},"Q3739":{"t":"A river-mouth capital facing a maze of islands","s":"Bissau lies on the wide estuary of the Geba River, where muddy water, mangroves and tidal flats blur the line between land and sea. Offshore spreads the Bijagós archipelago, a scatter of dozens of low, green islands and sandbanks. The tides are strong, and at low water vast banks of mud and sand appear. The Portuguese built a fort here in 1687.","w":"A city on a broad, muddy estuary lined with mangroves, with a scatter of low green islands out to sea.","f":["The Bijagós archipelago has about 88 islands and islets, only around 20 of them permanently inhabited.","On the Bijagós, hippos swim in salt water between the islands.","The Geba estuary is so broad at Bissau that it looks more like an arm of the sea than a river."],"q":{"q":"Which archipelago lies off the coast at Bissau?","a":"The Bijagós","x":["The Los Islands","The Banana Islands"]}},"Q2656":{"t":"The 'golden shell' between bare mountains and the sea","s":"Palermo fills a broad bay on Sicily's north coast, set in a crescent of bare mountains. The plain it stands on is called the Conca d'Oro, the 'golden shell', after the citrus groves that once covered it. To the north, the limestone headland of Monte Pellegrino juts out into the sea. Phoenicians founded the city in the 8th century BC.","w":"A city on a plain curving around a bay, ringed by bare mountains, with a big rocky headland on its northern side.","f":["Goethe called Monte Pellegrino the most beautiful promontory in the world.","Palermo's Arab-Norman buildings became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2015.","The Phoenicians called the city Ziz, usually translated as 'flower'."],"q":{"q":"What is the plain around Palermo called?","a":"The Conca d'Oro","x":["The Campania Felix","The Tavoliere"]}},"Q3805":{"t":"A white-stone city spilling over many hills","s":"Amman spreads over a tangle of steep hills on the edge of the desert plateau, and from above it looks almost entirely white, since most buildings are faced with pale limestone. To the west the land drops away into the Jordan Valley and the Dead Sea, some 40 km off. In Greco-Roman times the city was called Philadelphia.","w":"A pale, almost white city draped over steep hills, with the deep trench of the Jordan Valley and the Dead Sea to the west.","f":["The shore of the Dead Sea, about 430 metres below sea level, is the lowest land on Earth.","Amman's Roman theatre, cut into a hillside, seated about 6,000 people.","The city grew on the site of Rabbath Ammon, capital of the ancient Ammonites."],"q":{"q":"What was Amman called in Greco-Roman times?","a":"Philadelphia","x":["Gerasa","Petra"]}},"Q216":{"t":"A baroque city among forested hills and river bends","s":"Baroque church towers crowd the old town of Vilnius, one of the largest in Eastern Europe, and wooded hills give the whole city a surprisingly green look from above. It lies where the little Vilnia joins the winding Neris. The city is first mentioned in 1323, in letters of Grand Duke Gediminas inviting merchants and craftsmen to settle here.","w":"A green, hilly city on a looping river, with forests reaching right into town and many lakes to the west.","f":["The Old Town of Vilnius has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1994.","In 1989 French geographers placed the geographic centre of Europe about 26 km north of Vilnius.","Trakai, with its island castle on a lake, lies less than 30 km to the west."],"q":{"q":"Which river does the Vilnia flow into at Vilnius?","a":"The Neris","x":["The Nemunas","The Daugava"]}},"Q1773":{"t":"A Hanseatic port where a great river meets the gulf","s":"Riga stands on the Daugava a few kilometres before it flows into the Gulf of Riga, a broad, shallow bay of the Baltic. From above, the wide river divides the compact old town on the east bank from newer districts on the west, and pine forests and bogs close in all around. Bishop Albert founded the city in 1201, and it became a leading Hanseatic port.","w":"A city on a wide river just before it enters a broad bay, with sandy coast and dark pine forest along the shore.","f":["About a third of the buildings in central Riga are Art Nouveau, one of the densest collections anywhere.","The historic centre of Riga has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1997.","West of the city, the resort of Jūrmala stretches for more than 30 km along a sandy beach."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Riga?","a":"The Daugava","x":["The Neman","The Gauja"]}},"Q225641":{"t":"An island port wrapped in two deep creeks","s":"Mombasa's old heart is an island, a low coral block wedged between two deep inlets that reach into the Kenyan coast. On one side, Kilindini Harbour shelters one of East Africa's biggest ports; on the other, the old town faces Tudor Creek. Offshore, a line of white surf marks the fringing reef. For centuries this was a key port of the Swahili coast.","w":"A small island tucked between two winding creeks on a green coast, with white surf breaking on the reef offshore.","f":["Fort Jesus, begun in 1593, is a UNESCO World Heritage site.","Vasco da Gama called at Mombasa in 1498 on his way to India.","The island is joined to the mainland by causeways and bridges, and to the south coast by ferry."],"q":{"q":"Who built Fort Jesus in Mombasa in the 1590s?","a":"The Portuguese","x":["The Omanis","The British"]}},"Q3909":{"t":"The lowland capital of a kingdom in the sky","s":"Even the 'lowland' capital of Lesotho is about 1,600 metres above the sea. Maseru sits on the Caledon River, right on the western border of a country completely surrounded by South Africa. From above, look east: the land climbs into the Maloti Mountains, where most of Lesotho lies above 1,800 metres, often dusted with snow in winter.","w":"A town beside a narrow river border, with flat farmland to the west and folded, bare mountains rising to the east.","f":["Lesotho's lowest point, about 1,400 metres, is the highest 'lowest point' of any country.","Maseru's name means 'red sandstones' in Sesotho.","Thaba Bosiu, the flat-topped mountain stronghold of King Moshoeshoe I, lies about 25 km to the east."],"q":{"q":"Which country completely surrounds Lesotho?","a":"South Africa","x":["Botswana","Eswatini"]}},"Q3915":{"t":"A hill city among rice paddies on the Great Red Island","s":"Antananarivo sprawls over a long granite ridge and the hills around it in the middle of Madagascar, about 1,300 metres up. Between the hills, bright green rice paddies still fill the valleys right inside the city, and the soil around is a striking red. The name means 'city of the thousand', said to recall a thousand warriors who guarded it.","w":"A hilly city with a long ridge at its centre, bright green rice paddies in the valleys and red earth all around.","f":["The Rova, the old royal palace compound, crowns the highest hill in the city.","Lake Anosy in the city centre is ringed by jacaranda trees that bloom purple in spring.","Madagascar's red laterite soils earned it the nickname 'the Great Red Island'."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Antananarivo mean?","a":"City of the thousand","x":["City of rice","Red hill"]}},"Q456":{"t":"Two rivers meeting at the tip of a narrow peninsula","s":"Lyon is built around a meeting of rivers. The broad, fast Rhône, coming from the Alps, and the calmer Saône, from the north, run side by side through the city, enclosing a long, narrow peninsula before they join at its southern tip. West of the Saône rises the hill of Fourvière. The Romans founded Lugdunum here in 43 BC.","w":"Two rivers running side by side through a big city, a long thin strip of land between them, joining at a point to the south.","f":["Lugdunum was a capital of Roman Gaul, and the emperor Claudius was born here.","The historic centre of Lyon has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1998.","From the 16th to the 19th century Lyon was Europe's great silk-weaving city."],"q":{"q":"Which two rivers meet at Lyon?","a":"The Rhône and the Saône","x":["The Loire and the Allier","The Seine and the Marne"]}},"Q1004822":{"t":"A river port near the top of the Congo's great arc","s":"Bumba sits on the north bank of the Congo, near the top of the great arc the river draws across Central Africa. Here the Congo is a braided giant, several kilometres wide and dotted with long wooded islands, flowing through unbroken rainforest. From above, the brown river and the dark green canopy stretch to every horizon.","w":"A small town on the north bank of a huge, braided brown river full of wooded islands, amid endless rainforest.","f":["The Congo crosses the equator twice on its long arc through Central Africa.","Only the Amazon carries more water to the sea than the Congo.","Around Bumba the river reaches the northernmost part of its course."],"q":{"q":"How many times does the Congo River cross the equator?","a":"Twice","x":["Once","Three times"]}},"Q3659":{"t":"A Sahel capital where two rivers meet on a border","s":"N'Djamena stands where the Logone flows into the Chari, on a flat plain at the southern edge of the Sahel. Across the Logone lies Cameroon, so close that the two countries face each other over the water. The combined river flows on north-west to Lake Chad, a shallow lake that swells and shrinks dramatically with the seasons and the years.","w":"A flat city at a river confluence, with a smaller town across the water and floodplains and dry savanna around.","f":["The Chari river system supplies about 90 percent of the water reaching Lake Chad.","The city was founded in 1900 as Fort-Lamy.","The southern shore of Lake Chad lies roughly 100 km north-west of the city."],"q":{"q":"Which lake does the Chari feed, north-west of N'Djamena?","a":"Lake Chad","x":["Lake Victoria","Lake Volta"]}},"Q242587":{"t":"An old sultanate capital on a caravan road","s":"Abéché lies on the dry plateau of eastern Chad, a low, sand-coloured town among scattered rocky hills and seasonal riverbeds. From 1850 it was the capital of the Sultanate of Wadai, a powerful Sahel state. Caravans once set out from here across the Sahara to the Mediterranean coast at Benghazi, some 2,000 km away.","w":"A small, dusty town among low rocky hills and dry, sandy riverbeds on a flat, brown plateau.","f":["The earlier Wadai capital, Ouara, lies in ruins some 50 km to the north.","Most of the year's rain falls in July and August, briefly turning the wadis into rivers."],"q":{"q":"Abéché was the capital of which sultanate?","a":"Wadai","x":["Bornu","Darfur"]}},"Q3818":{"t":"An island capital beneath a 3,000-metre volcano","s":"A forested volcano topping 3,000 metres, often capped in cloud, rises behind Malabo: Pico Basilé. The city lies on the north coast of Bioko, a lush volcanic island in the Gulf of Guinea, separated from the rest of its country on the mainland. Its harbour curves inside the rim of an old volcanic crater, partly flooded by the sea.","w":"A small city on the north shore of a green volcanic island, with a huge forested peak rising behind it.","f":["Pico Basilé, at about 3,010 metres, is the highest point of Equatorial Guinea.","Bioko lies less than 40 km off the coast of Cameroon.","Malabo is one of the few capitals set on an island apart from its country's mainland."],"q":{"q":"What was Malabo called when the British founded it in 1827?","a":"Port Clarence","x":["Fernando Po","Bathurst"]}},"Q130514":{"t":"Ancient Thebes, split by the Nile between life and death","s":"Luxor straddles the Nile in a narrow green valley between bare desert cliffs. On the east bank, where the sun rises, lie the town and the vast temples of Karnak and Luxor. On the west bank, beyond the fields, the land rises to dry limestone hills hiding the Valley of the Kings, where pharaohs of the New Kingdom were buried.","w":"A green strip on both banks of the Nile, a town on the east side and pale desert cliffs rising sharply on the west.","f":["As Thebes, this was Egypt's capital for much of the New Kingdom, more than 3,000 years ago.","The Karnak temple complex is one of the largest religious sites ever built.","Ancient Thebes and its necropolis became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1979."],"q":{"q":"What did the ancient Greeks call the city at Luxor?","a":"Thebes","x":["Memphis","Heliopolis"]}},"Q3642":{"t":"A modernist city on the rim of a great escarpment","s":"Just east of Asmara, the land plunges more than 2,000 metres towards the hot Red Sea coast in only a few dozen kilometres. The city sits at about 2,300 metres on the Eritrean highlands, near the edge of that escarpment. Its streets are lined with early 20th-century modernist buildings, and in 2017 UNESCO listed it as a modernist African city.","w":"A compact city on a high plateau, beside the edge of a steep, deeply cut escarpment falling east towards the Red Sea.","f":["A narrow-gauge railway winds down the escarpment from Asmara to the port of Massawa.","Thanks to its altitude, Asmara has mild temperatures all year despite lying in the tropics.","Asmara's name is often explained as 'they united them', after four villages that joined together."],"q":{"q":"For which style of architecture is Asmara UNESCO-listed?","a":"Modernist","x":["Gothic","Baroque"]}},"Q1435":{"t":"Forested mountain above, the Sava plain below","s":"Zagreb spreads south from the slopes of Medvednica, a forested mountain rising just north of the city, down to the broad plain of the Sava. The historic core sits on the lower hills; newer districts stretch across the flat land beyond the river. The two medieval towns of Gradec and Kaptol were united as one city in 1850.","w":"A city between a dark, forested mountain to the north and a river crossing the flat plain to the south.","f":["Sljeme, the summit of Medvednica, rises to about 1,030 metres.","In the Lower Town, a U-shaped chain of parks and squares is known as the 'Green Horseshoe'.","The Sava, flowing past the city, joins the Danube at Belgrade."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Zagreb?","a":"The Sava","x":["The Drava","The Danube"]}},"Q1770":{"t":"A medieval Hanseatic town on a limestone bay","s":"A limestone hill, Toompea, rises above Tallinn's wide bay, with a medieval old town, one of the best preserved in Northern Europe, clustered around it. The hill is part of the Baltic Klint, a long coastal escarpment. Wooded islands dot the bay, and Helsinki lies about 80 km across the sea. As Reval, the city was a prosperous Hanseatic port.","w":"A compact city curving around a broad bay with a few wooded islands, and flat, forested coast stretching east and west.","f":["The historic centre of Tallinn has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1997.","The Baltic Klint, a limestone escarpment, runs for more than 1,000 km from Sweden into Russia.","Tallinn's name is often explained as 'Danish town', recalling Danish rule in the 13th century."],"q":{"q":"What was Tallinn's old Hanseatic name?","a":"Reval","x":["Dorpat","Memel"]}},"Q261887":{"t":"A river town where grassy plateaus meet forested valleys","s":"Kikwit lies on the Kwilu River in south-western Congo, a busy regional town in a landscape of grassy plateaus sliced by deep, forested valleys. From above, look for the striking pattern: pale savanna on the high ground and dark green ribbons of gallery forest along every stream. The Kwilu flows north towards the Kasai and, eventually, the Congo.","w":"A town on a winding river, with pale grassy plateaus and dark green strips of forest lining the valleys.","f":["The Kwilu joins the Kwango, which feeds the Kasai, one of the Congo's largest tributaries.","The country around Kikwit has long been known for its oil palms."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Kikwit?","a":"The Kwilu","x":["The Kwango","The Lomami"]}},"Q211073":{"t":"A rocky river port where the ocean ships must stop","s":"Matadi clings to steep, rocky hills on the left bank of the Congo, about 150 km from the Atlantic. It is as far as ocean-going ships can sail: just upstream, the river squeezes into gorges and rapids. In the 1890s a railway was built to carry goods around them to Kinshasa. The town's name means 'stones' in Kikongo.","w":"A hilly, rocky town on a narrow, fast stretch of the Congo, crossed by a single long suspension bridge.","f":["The Matadi–Kinshasa railway, built between 1890 and 1898, bypasses the Livingstone Falls.","The Matadi Bridge over the Congo has a main span of about 520 metres.","Upstream at Yelala Falls, the Portuguese navigator Diogo Cão left an inscription on the rocks in 1485."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Matadi mean in Kikongo?","a":"Stones","x":["Harbour","Sandbank"]}},"Q271404":{"t":"A mining town ringed by giant open pits","s":"Terraced craters, grey spoil heaps and pale tailings ponds scar the land around Kolwezi: huge open-pit mines on one of the richest mineral belts on Earth. The town sits on a high plateau in the south of the Congo. The ores here carry copper and cobalt, a metal now in demand for rechargeable batteries.","w":"A town surrounded by vast terraced open-pit mines, grey spoil heaps and flat tailings ponds on a high plateau.","f":["The Democratic Republic of the Congo produces more than half of the world's mined cobalt, much of it around Kolwezi.","The Central African Copperbelt stretches from Kolwezi across the border into Zambia.","Kolwezi lies about 1,450 metres above sea level."],"q":{"q":"Which battery metal is mined in large amounts around Kolwezi?","a":"Cobalt","x":["Lithium","Nickel"]}},"Q187593":{"t":"Copper capital with a black mountain of slag","s":"Lubumbashi spreads across the high, flat plateau of the Copperbelt in the far south-east of the Congo, only a few dozen kilometres from Zambia. Look for a dark hill near the city centre: a huge heap of slag left by a century of copper smelting, beside the smelter's tall chimney. The city was founded in 1910 as Élisabethville.","w":"A grid-planned city on a flat plateau, with a dark, flat-topped slag heap near its centre and dry woodland around.","f":["Lubumbashi is one of the largest cities in the Democratic Republic of the Congo.","The Zambian border lies only some 30 km away.","Miombo woodland, a dry forest of broad-leaved trees, covers much of the plateau around the city."],"q":{"q":"What was Lubumbashi called when it was founded in 1910?","a":"Élisabethville","x":["Léopoldville","Stanleyville"]}},"Q3876":{"t":"A garden capital spread across the central plateau","s":"Lilongwe lies on Malawi's central plateau, about 1,050 metres up, a spread-out city of separate districts divided by green belts and the winding Lilongwe River. Around it, red soil and farmland stretch in every direction, much of it planted with maize and tobacco. About 100 km to the east, the land drops to the shore of Lake Malawi.","w":"A loose, spread-out city of separate districts among trees and red farmland, crossed by a small winding river.","f":["Lilongwe replaced Zomba as Malawi's capital in 1975.","Lake Malawi is home to more species of fish than any other lake on Earth.","Many of Lilongwe's neighbourhoods have numbers instead of names, such as Area 3 or Area 47."],"q":{"q":"Which lake lies about 100 km east of Lilongwe?","a":"Lake Malawi","x":["Lake Tanganyika","Lake Kariba"]}},"Q3825":{"t":"A 'free town' on an estuary just north of the equator","s":"Libreville lies on the north shore of the Komo estuary, where the river opens into the Atlantic, only about 40 km north of the equator. Rainforest reaches almost to the city's edge, and across the estuary the mangroves and beaches of Pongara National Park stay wild. The city was founded in 1849 by freed Africans rescued from a slave ship.","w":"A city along the north shore of a wide estuary opening to the sea, with dense green forest and mangroves on the far side.","f":["The beaches of Pongara, across the estuary, are a nesting ground for leatherback turtles.","Close to 90 percent of Gabon is covered by forest.","Libreville is Gabon's capital and by far its largest city."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Libreville mean?","a":"Free town","x":["New town","River town"]}},"Q501715":{"t":"An island oil town on the edge of a great delta","s":"Port-Gentil stands near the tip of Cape Lopez on Mandji Island, a flat, sandy island at the mouth of the Ogooué, Gabon's great river. Behind it spreads the river's delta, a maze of channels, lagoons and mangroves. The town is the centre of Gabon's oil industry, and at night you may spot the lights of offshore platforms out in the Atlantic.","w":"A small town on the northern tip of a flat, sandy island, with a vast delta of channels and mangroves to the south-east.","f":["The Ogooué is Gabon's longest river, draining most of the country.","Cape Lopez is named after the Portuguese navigator Lopo Gonçalves, who sailed past it in the 1470s."],"q":{"q":"At the mouth of which river does Port-Gentil stand?","a":"The Ogooué","x":["The Congo","The Sanaga"]}},"Q9427":{"t":"The fabled desert city, just beyond the Niger","s":"From above, Timbuktu is a small, sand-coloured town that could be anywhere. Yet from the 14th to the 16th centuries it was a great centre of trade and Islamic scholarship, its libraries filled with manuscripts. It stands where desert meets river: the Sahara reaches its northern edge, while the Niger flows about 15 km to the south.","w":"A small, sand-coloured town on the edge of dunes, with the green floodplain of the Niger a short way to the south.","f":["The Djinguereyber Mosque, built around 1327, is made largely of mud and straw.","Families in Timbuktu have kept tens of thousands of old manuscripts on law, astronomy and medicine.","Timbuktu has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1988."],"q":{"q":"Which Mali emperor had Timbuktu's Djinguereyber Mosque built?","a":"Mansa Musa","x":["Sundiata Keita","Askia Muhammad"]}},"Q3688":{"t":"A desert capital built on the dunes by the Atlantic","s":"In 1958 this was little more than a village; today Nouakchott is home to around a million people. Work began that year on a new capital, planned for about 15,000. It lies where the Sahara meets the Atlantic, a low, sprawling city on flat sand between the ocean and a sea of dunes that still presses against its edges.","w":"A low, sprawling city on flat sand, a straight Atlantic beach to the west and orange dunes pressing in from the east.","f":["Banc d'Arguin National Park, a UNESCO site up the coast, hosts millions of wintering shorebirds.","Much of the city lies only a few metres above sea level.","The name Nouakchott is often translated as 'place of the winds'."],"q":{"q":"In which year did work begin on Nouakchott as a new capital?","a":"1958","x":["1901","1985"]}},"Q3703":{"t":"A capital on the Niger beneath a sandstone escarpment","s":"Bamako spreads along both banks of the Niger, a wide, shallow river braided with sandbanks and small rocky islands. Behind the city to the north rises the edge of the Manding Plateau, a line of sandstone cliffs and hills. Bridges tie the two halves of the city together. The name is often said to mean 'crocodile river' in Bambara.","w":"A broad city on both banks of a wide river with sandbanks and bridges, backed by sandstone hills to the north.","f":["Just downstream of the centre, the Sotuba rapids break the flow of the Niger.","Bamako is one of the fastest-growing cities in Africa.","The Niger rises only about 250 km from the Atlantic, yet flows inland, away from it, past Bamako."],"q":{"q":"What is Bamako's name often said to mean?","a":"Crocodile river","x":["Hippo pool","Red cliff"]}},"Q735645":{"t":"An oasis gateway to the Adrar and the Eye of the Sahara","s":"Atar is an oasis town of date palms in a valley of the Adrar plateau, in the heart of the Mauritanian Sahara. Dark sandstone cliffs and orange dunes surround it. Some 180 km to the north-east lies one of the most famous sights seen from the air: the Richat Structure, or Eye of the Sahara, a vast set of concentric rock rings.","w":"A green strip of palm groves in a dry valley between dark plateau cliffs, with sand dunes spreading around.","f":["The Richat Structure is about 40 km across, big enough to be picked out by astronauts.","Chinguetti, an old caravan town about 80 km to the east, is a UNESCO World Heritage site.","Every summer the date harvest, the guetna, draws families to the oases of the Adrar."],"q":{"q":"What is the Eye of the Sahara, north-east of Atar?","a":"An eroded dome of rock layers","x":["A meteorite crater","A dried-up salt lake"]}},"Q213507":{"t":"A mud-brick town that becomes an island in the flood","s":"Djenné stands in the inland delta of the Niger, on a patch of higher ground that the Bani River's floods turn into an island each rainy season. Its Great Mosque is the largest mud-brick building in the world, and every year the townspeople replaster it together in a festival. From above, look for a compact brown town ringed by water or green floodplain.","w":"A compact, earth-brown town on a low island in a wide, flat floodplain that turns to water in the wet season.","f":["The present Great Mosque dates from 1907, rebuilt on the site of a much older mosque.","Nearby Djenné-Djeno, settled around 250 BC, is one of the oldest known towns in sub-Saharan Africa.","The old towns of Djenné have been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1988."],"q":{"q":"What do Djenné's people replaster together every year?","a":"The Great Mosque","x":["The city walls","The market hall"]}},"Q208389":{"t":"A Sahara oasis city with green circles in the sand","s":"Perfect green circles dot the desert around Sabha: fields watered by rotating irrigation arms that draw on ancient groundwater. The town lies deep in the Libyan Sahara, in the Fezzan, a region of sand seas, stony plateaus and strings of oases. For centuries it was a crossroads for caravans between the Mediterranean and the Sahel.","w":"A town on a flat desert plain, with scattered green circular fields and dark rocky plateaus and sand seas around.","f":["The groundwater pumped around Sabha fell as rain thousands of years ago, when the Sahara was greener.","About 150 km to the west, Germa was the capital of the Garamantes, a desert kingdom of Roman times.","Many years pass in the Fezzan with almost no rain at all."],"q":{"q":"Which ancient people ruled a desert kingdom west of Sabha?","a":"The Garamantes","x":["The Nabataeans","The Phoenicians"]}},"Q40816":{"t":"A Greek-founded port on the Gulf of Sidra","s":"Benghazi spreads along a low coast at the north-eastern corner of the Gulf of Sidra, where salt marshes and lagoons edge the sea. Inland to the east rise the green uplands of the Jebel Akhdar, one of the wettest parts of Libya. The city began as the Greek colony of Euesperides and was later renamed Berenice, after a Ptolemaic queen.","w":"A flat coastal city with lagoons and salt flats along the shore, and a green, wooded upland rising to the north-east.","f":["Greek settlers founded Euesperides here in the 6th century BC.","The Jebel Akhdar, the 'Green Mountain', gets far more rain than almost anywhere else in Libya.","Benghazi is Libya's second-largest city."],"q":{"q":"What was Benghazi called in Ptolemaic times?","a":"Berenice","x":["Cyrene","Apollonia"]}},"Q17151":{"t":"A port city facing Mount Olympus across the gulf","s":"Thessaloniki curves along the head of the Thermaic Gulf, a long waterfront backed by hills. On a clear day, look south-west across the water: the massif of Mount Olympus, home of the Greek gods, rises to nearly 3,000 metres, often capped with snow. West of the city, the Axios River builds a green delta of wetlands and rice fields.","w":"A city along the curving head of a gulf, backed by hills, with a river delta to the west and Olympus across the water.","f":["King Cassander founded the city in 315 BC and named it after his wife Thessalonike, half-sister of Alexander the Great.","Mount Olympus reaches about 2,918 metres, the highest point in Greece.","The city's early Christian and Byzantine monuments are a UNESCO World Heritage site."],"q":{"q":"Thessaloniki is named after the half-sister of which ruler?","a":"Alexander the Great","x":["Julius Caesar","Ptolemy I"]}},"Q3820":{"t":"Snowy peaks and a warm sea, side by side","s":"Beirut sits on a triangular cape pushing into the Mediterranean, with the slopes of Mount Lebanon rising steeply just behind it. In winter the peaks, well over 2,000 metres high, turn white with snow while the sea below stays blue. Off the western tip stand two great sea arches, the Pigeon Rocks. As Roman Berytus, the city was famous for its school of law.","w":"A dense city covering a triangular cape, with steep mountain slopes rising immediately inland to the east.","f":["Beirut has been inhabited for around 5,000 years.","The cedar is Lebanon's emblem, and old cedar groves survive high on Mount Lebanon.","In late winter it is possible to ski on Mount Lebanon and swim in the sea on the same day."],"q":{"q":"What kind of school made Roman Berytus famous?","a":"A school of law","x":["A school of medicine","A school of philosophy"]}},"Q994":{"t":"Warm springs in a river gorge gave it its name","s":"Tbilisi winds along the valley of the Kura, which cuts through the city in a narrow gorge between steep hills. The old town clusters beneath a fortress on a ridge, where hot sulphur springs still feed domed bathhouses. The name comes from the Georgian word for 'warm'. On clear days the snowy wall of the Greater Caucasus stands along the northern horizon.","w":"A long city in a winding river valley between bare hills, with the snowy Greater Caucasus range far to the north.","f":["King Vakhtang Gorgasali is traditionally credited with founding the city in the 5th century.","The Tbilisi Sea, a reservoir on the city's north-eastern edge, is about 9 km long.","The Kura, called the Mtkvari in Georgian, flows on to the Caspian Sea through Azerbaijan."],"q":{"q":"What gave Tbilisi its name?","a":"Its warm springs","x":["Its vineyards","Its river bend"]}},"Q218861":{"t":"A castle-studded royal city above Lake Tana","s":"Gondar sits on the northern Ethiopian Highlands at over 2,100 metres, on hills overlooking the plain that runs down to Lake Tana, some 35 km to the south. In 1636 Emperor Fasilides made it his capital and built a stone castle; his successors added more, forming a walled royal enclosure. To the north-east rise the jagged Simien Mountains.","w":"A hilly town on the highlands with a large lake to the south and a range of jagged peaks far to the north-east.","f":["The royal enclosure of Fasil Ghebbi has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1979.","Gondar remained Ethiopia's capital for more than two centuries.","The Simien Mountains include Ras Dashen, at about 4,550 metres Ethiopia's highest peak."],"q":{"q":"Which lake, source of the Blue Nile, lies south of Gondar?","a":"Lake Tana","x":["Lake Turkana","Lake Abaya"]}},"Q42053":{"t":"'Half the world': a Safavid capital by the Zagros","s":"Isfahan spreads across a high, dry plain at the eastern foot of the Zagros Mountains, strung along the Zayandeh Rud, the river that made this oasis possible. In 1598 Shah Abbas I made it the Safavid capital and filled it with mosques, bridges and a vast public square. A Persian saying from that age calls Isfahan 'half the world'.","w":"A large city on a dry, flat plain, crossed by a river flowing west to east, with the Zagros ranges rising to the west.","f":["Naqsh-e Jahan Square, laid out under Shah Abbas I, is about 560 metres long and a UNESCO World Heritage site.","The Si-o-se-pol bridge over the Zayandeh Rud is named for its 33 arches.","The Zayandeh Rud ends not in the sea but in the Gavkhouni salt marsh to the east."],"q":{"q":"What does an old Persian saying call Isfahan?","a":"Half the world","x":["Pearl of the desert","City of roses"]}},"Q6397066":{"t":"Poets, gardens and a lake that turns pink","s":"Shiraz lies in a green valley of the Zagros Mountains, about 1,500 metres up, between bare, folded ridges. It is Iran's city of poets: Hafez and Saadi are buried here, and their tombs are still visited like shrines. Just to the south-east lies Lake Maharloo, a salt lake that can turn pink in summer. The ruins of Persepolis stand some 60 km to the north-east.","w":"A city in a long valley between bare, folded mountain ridges, with a shallow salt lake, sometimes pink, to the south-east.","f":["Persepolis, the ceremonial capital of the Achaemenid kings, is a UNESCO World Heritage site.","Lake Maharloo gets its pink colour from salt-loving microorganisms.","Shiraz's Eram Garden is part of the UNESCO-listed Persian Garden."],"q":{"q":"Which ancient royal city lies about 60 km north-east of Shiraz?","a":"Persepolis","x":["Susa","Ecbatana"]}},"Q3844":{"t":"Face to face with Kinshasa across the Congo","s":"Brazzaville stretches along the north bank of the Congo, looking straight across the water at the much larger Kinshasa. Just downstream of the city, the river leaves the calm expanse of Pool Malebo and breaks into foaming rapids, white streaks you may spot from above. The city is named after the explorer Pierre Savorgnan de Brazza, who founded a post here in 1880.","w":"A city on the north bank of a broad river, facing a bigger city opposite, with white rapids just downstream.","f":["Brazzaville is the capital of the Republic of the Congo, a different country from its neighbour across the river.","The rapids below the city, the start of the Livingstone Falls, block navigation to the sea.","Mbamou, a large island in Pool Malebo, lies just upstream of the city."],"q":{"q":"Which explorer gave Brazzaville its name?","a":"Pierre Savorgnan de Brazza","x":["Henry Morton Stanley","David Livingstone"]}},"Q3733":{"t":"A long, thin capital on a rain-soaked peninsula","s":"Conakry stretches along the Kaloum peninsula, a narrow finger of land pointing south-west into the Atlantic, with its old centre on what was once Tombo Island at the very tip. Offshore lie the Los Islands, green and rocky. This is one of the rainiest capitals in the world, drenched by several metres of rain a year, mostly between June and September.","w":"A city filling a long, narrow peninsula pointing south-west into the sea, with a cluster of green islands offshore.","f":["Conakry receives about 3,700 mm of rain a year, much of it in July and August.","The Los Islands are the remains of an ancient ring of volcanic rock.","Guinea holds the world's largest reserves of bauxite, the ore of aluminium."],"q":{"q":"Which islands lie just off Conakry?","a":"The Los Islands","x":["The Bijagós","The Banana Islands"]}},"Q3768":{"t":"A village turned capital, with a giant basilica","s":"Yamoussoukro lies in the gently rolling centre of Côte d'Ivoire, where forest gives way to savanna. Once a small village, it became the official capital in 1983 and was laid out with enormously wide avenues and artificial lakes. Its basilica is one of the largest churches in the world. North-west of the city spreads the great branching reservoir of Lake Kossou.","w":"A spacious town of wide avenues and small lakes in green, rolling country, with a large branching reservoir to the north-west.","f":["The Basilica of Our Lady of Peace, completed in 1989, has a dome inspired by St Peter's in Rome.","Lake Kossou, created by a dam on the Bandama River, is the largest lake in Côte d'Ivoire.","Abidjan, the country's economic centre, lies about 230 km to the south-east."],"q":{"q":"Since which year has Yamoussoukro been the official capital?","a":"1983","x":["1960","2001"]}},"Q612713":{"t":"A remote savanna town among rocky hills","s":"N'Délé is a small town in the far north of the Central African Republic, set among rocky hills above a vast plain of wooded savanna. In the late 19th century it was the seat of the Sultanate of Dar al-Kuti, whose rulers built their town against the slopes. To the north-east lies one of Africa's great wilderness parks, crossed by seasonal rivers.","w":"A tiny town among rocky hills, surrounded by endless wooded savanna cut by seasonal rivers.","f":["Manovo-Gounda St Floris National Park, north-east of the town, is a UNESCO World Heritage site.","The rainy season here runs from about May to October, when the savanna turns green."],"q":{"q":"Which UNESCO-listed park lies north-east of N'Délé?","a":"Manovo-Gounda St Floris","x":["Dzanga-Sangha","Garamba"]}},"Q3711":{"t":"A white city on the ridge where the Sava meets the Danube","s":"Belgrade stands on a ridge above one of Europe's great river meetings: the Sava flows into the Danube right below the old fortress. From above, look for the point where the two rivers join, with a wooded island at the confluence and the flat, planned grid of New Belgrade on the far bank of the Sava. The Romans called the town Singidunum.","w":"Two wide rivers meeting below a ridge-top fortress, with a green island at the junction and a flat, planned grid across the water.","f":["Belgrade is one of the oldest continuously inhabited cities in Europe.","The Sava reaches the Danube here after a journey of nearly 1,000 km from the Slovenian Alps.","New Belgrade, across the Sava, was built from 1948 on former marshland."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Belgrade mean?","a":"White city","x":["Stone city","River city"]}},"Q1025617":{"t":"A remote outpost near three countries' borders","s":"Obo is a small town in the far south-eastern corner of the Central African Republic, close to the borders of South Sudan and the Democratic Republic of the Congo. Around it, wooded savanna and dark gallery forest stretch for hundreds of kilometres, crossed by few roads. The streams here drain south towards the Mbomou River, and ultimately into the Congo.","w":"A tiny clearing of settlement in a vast green mosaic of savanna and dark riverside forest.","f":["Bangui, the capital, lies nearly 900 km to the west in a straight line.","The Mbomou, which drains this region, joins the Uele to form the Ubangi."],"q":{"q":"Near the borders of which two countries does Obo lie?","a":"South Sudan and DR Congo","x":["Chad and Sudan","Cameroon and Congo"]}},"Q3832":{"t":"A river capital named after its rapids","s":"Bangui lies on the north bank of the Ubangi, just where the river turns south and tumbles over a line of rapids that gave the city its name. Across the water is the town of Zongo, in the Democratic Republic of the Congo. Wooded hills rise behind the city, and forest and savanna spread far beyond.","w":"A city on the north bank of a wide river bend, with wooded hills behind and another town on the far shore.","f":["The French founded a post here in 1889.","The Ubangi is the largest right-bank tributary of the Congo.","Bangui lies less than 500 km north of the equator."],"q":{"q":"What gave Bangui its name?","a":"The river's rapids","x":["A royal hill","A salt market"]}},"Q818824":{"t":"A Sahel town beside the mountains of spires","s":"Maroua lies on the dry plains of Cameroon's Far North, where two seasonal rivers meet and wide sandy beds fill with water only after the summer rains. To the west rise the Mandara Mountains, a rugged range whose volcanic plugs stand up like spires around villages such as Rhumsiki. To the north, Waza National Park spreads over flat floodplain.","w":"A sprawling town on a flat, dry plain crossed by broad sandy riverbeds, with a rugged mountain range to the west.","f":["The Mandara Mountains run along the border between Cameroon and Nigeria.","Waza National Park, north of the city, protects elephants, giraffes and lions.","Maroua is the largest town in Cameroon's Far North Region."],"q":{"q":"Which mountains rise west of Maroua?","a":"The Mandara Mountains","x":["The Adamawa Plateau","The Aïr Mountains"]}},"Q3808":{"t":"Seven green hills, cooled by altitude","s":"Yaoundé is built across a cluster of forested hills in southern Cameroon, about 750 metres up, which keeps it noticeably cooler than the steamy coast. From above, red-roofed districts fill the valleys between green ridges, with rainforest stretching away to the south. The Germans set up a trading and research post here in 1889.","w":"A city spread over green hills and valleys, with higher forested peaks to the north-west and rainforest all around.","f":["Yaoundé is often called the 'city of seven hills'.","Mount Mbam Minkom, on the north-western edge, rises to about 1,300 metres.","Douala, Cameroon's largest city and main port, lies about 200 km to the west."],"q":{"q":"Which city, not Yaoundé, is Cameroon's largest and main port?","a":"Douala","x":["Kribi","Limbe"]}},"Q19689":{"t":"Mount Dajti on one side, the Adriatic on the other","s":"Tirana spreads across a green plain in central Albania, with the steep wall of Mount Dajti rising right on its eastern edge. The Adriatic lies only about 30 km to the west, beyond a line of low hills. The town was founded in 1614 by an Ottoman general, Sulejman Pasha, and grew slowly until it became the capital in 1920.","w":"A compact city on a green plain, backed to the east by a steep mountain wall, with the sea a short way to the west.","f":["Mount Dajti, east of the city, rises to about 1,610 metres and can be reached by cable car.","An artificial lake, ringed by woodland, lies in the Grand Park on the city's southern edge.","The port of Durrës, about 30 km west, was founded by Greek colonists as Epidamnos."],"q":{"q":"Which mountain rises just east of Tirana?","a":"Mount Dajti","x":["Mount Olympus","Mount Lovćen"]}},"Q1953":{"t":"A pink-stone city beneath the snows of Ararat","s":"Mount Ararat, a huge snow-capped volcano with its smaller twin beside it, fills the southern horizon of Yerevan, about 60 km away. The city lies in a basin on the edge of the Ararat plain, and many of its buildings are made of pinkish volcanic tuff. Yerevan traces its founding to the fortress of Erebuni, built in 782 BC.","w":"A city in a basin with a flat green plain to the south and a massive, lone snowy volcano rising beyond it.","f":["Mount Ararat rises to about 5,137 metres, more than 4 km above the plain.","Aragats, Armenia's highest peak at about 4,090 metres, rises north-west of the city.","Lake Sevan, about 60 km to the north-east, is one of the largest high-altitude lakes in Eurasia."],"q":{"q":"Which mountain towers over the plain south of Yerevan?","a":"Ararat","x":["Aragats","Elbrus"]}},"Q9248":{"t":"The world's lowest capital, on an oil-rich shore","s":"Baku is the lowest-lying national capital on Earth: like the surface of the Caspian Sea beside it, the city lies below the level of the oceans. It curves around a bay on the southern side of the Absheron peninsula. The dry, brown land around it is dotted with oil derricks, and south of the city bubble dozens of mud volcanoes.","w":"A city curving around a wide bay on a dry, brown peninsula jutting into the Caspian, with oil fields and platforms nearby.","f":["Azerbaijan has several hundred mud volcanoes, a large share of all those on Earth.","Some of the world's first industrial oil wells were drilled at Bibi-Heybat, near Baku, in the 1840s.","Baku's walled Old City, with the Maiden Tower, is a UNESCO World Heritage site."],"q":{"q":"Roughly how far below sea level does Baku lie?","a":"About 28 m","x":["About 280 m","About 2 m"]}},"Q818731":{"t":"A plateau town where the Okavango's waters gather","s":"Menongue lies on the high, wooded plateau of south-eastern Angola, beside the Cuebe River. The rivers here never reach the sea: the Cuebe joins the Cubango, which flows south-east past the Namibian border and ends in the Okavango Delta, spreading out into the Kalahari sands of Botswana. Around the town, open woodland runs to the horizon.","w":"A small town beside a winding river on a flat, wooded plateau, with grassy wetlands along the valleys.","f":["The Okavango Delta, where the Cubango ends, is one of the largest inland deltas in the world.","Most of the Okavango's water comes from rain falling on the Angolan highlands.","Menongue stands at about 1,350 metres above sea level."],"q":{"q":"Where do the rivers around Menongue finally end?","a":"In the Okavango Delta","x":["In the Atlantic Ocean","In Lake Malawi"]}},"Q207322":{"t":"A highland city where Angola's great rivers begin","s":"Huambo stands on Angola's central plateau at about 1,700 metres, a cool, green upland where some of southern Africa's great rivers are born: the Kwanza, the Cunene and the Cubango all rise on these highlands and flow off in different directions. The city was founded in 1912 on the Benguela Railway. To the west rises Mount Moco, Angola's highest peak.","w":"A city on a high, open plateau of grassland and farms, with higher mountains rising to the west.","f":["Mount Moco, about 2,620 metres high, is the highest point in Angola.","The Benguela Railway links Huambo with the Atlantic port of Lobito."],"q":{"q":"What was Huambo called in colonial times?","a":"Nova Lisboa","x":["Sá da Bandeira","Silva Porto"]}},"Q131818":{"t":"Bright bay in front, white salt lake behind","s":"Oran curves around a wide bay on Algeria's western coast, guarded on the west by the steep mountain of Murdjadjo. Just inland to the south lies the Sebkha of Oran, a huge, shallow salt lake that glares white in the dry season. Andalusian merchants founded the town in 903, and Spain held it for much of the 16th to 18th centuries.","w":"A city curving around a bay, a steep mountain on its western side, and a large pale salt lake inland to the south.","f":["The Sebkha of Oran often dries into a white crust of salt in summer.","The Santa Cruz fort, built by the Spanish, crowns Murdjadjo above the harbour.","Oran is Algeria's second-largest city."],"q":{"q":"Which music style grew up in Oran?","a":"Raï","x":["Gnawa","Fado"]}},"Q43595":{"t":"Between lagoon and ocean, with a town on stilts nearby","s":"Cotonou sits on a narrow strip of sand between the Atlantic and Lake Nokoué, a broad, shallow lagoon, and a channel cuts through the city to link the two. On the lake's northern shallows stands Ganvié, a town built entirely on stilts, where people get around by canoe. Porto-Novo is the official capital, but Cotonou is Benin's largest city and seat of government.","w":"A city on a sandy strip between a straight ocean beach and a wide, shallow lake, split by a channel joining them.","f":["Ganvié, on Lake Nokoué, is home to some 20,000 people living in houses on stilts.","Ouidah, along the coast to the west, was a major port of the Atlantic slave trade."],"q":{"q":"Which stilt village stands on Lake Nokoué near Cotonou?","a":"Ganvié","x":["Ouidah","Abomey"]}},"Q205792":{"t":"An oasis town in the volcanic heart of the Sahara","s":"Tamanrasset lies in a dry valley on the edge of the Hoggar, a vast upland of dark volcanic peaks and weathered granite in the middle of the Sahara. At about 1,400 metres, it is cooler than the desert around it. From above, look for the black volcanic plugs and basalt plateaus that surround the town, sharp against the orange sand.","w":"A small town on a sandy valley floor, ringed by dark volcanic peaks and plateaus rising out of the desert.","f":["Mount Tahat in the Hoggar, about 2,900 metres high, is Algeria's highest peak.","The Assekrem plateau in the Hoggar is famous for sunrises over a sea of volcanic peaks.","Tamanrasset is almost 2,000 km by road from Algiers."],"q":{"q":"Which mountains rise around Tamanrasset?","a":"The Hoggar","x":["The Tibesti","The Aïr"]}},"Q622841":{"t":"Five hilltop towns in a desert valley","s":"Ghardaia is the largest of five fortified towns built in the M'zab Valley, a dry gorge cut into the rocky plateau of the northern Sahara. Each town climbs a low hill in tight rings of houses, topped by a minaret, with palm groves in the valley below. The Mozabites founded them from the 11th century and devised clever ways to share scarce floodwater.","w":"A winding desert valley with several compact towns, each piled on a low hill, and green palm groves between them.","f":["The M'zab Valley has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1982.","The architect Le Corbusier admired the simple, practical forms of the M'zab towns.","Wells and channels share out the rare flash floods among the valley's palm groves."],"q":{"q":"In which valley do Ghardaia and its sister towns stand?","a":"The M'zab","x":["The Draa","The Souf"]}},"Q472":{"t":"A mountain on the doorstep of a high-basin capital","s":"The rounded dome of Vitosha rises right on Sofia's southern edge, often white with snow in winter, while the long ridge of the Balkan Mountains closes the view to the north. Between them, the city fills a broad, high basin about 550 metres up. In Roman times it was Serdica, and the emperor Constantine is said to have called it 'my Rome'.","w":"A large city in a flat basin, with a big rounded mountain right on its southern edge and a long range to the north.","f":["Cherni Vrah, the summit of Vitosha, rises to about 2,290 metres.","Sofia sits on mineral springs, and public taps in the centre pour out warm water.","Vitosha became the first protected park in the Balkans in 1934."],"q":{"q":"Which mountain rises right on Sofia's southern edge?","a":"Vitosha","x":["Musala","Olympus"]}},"Q2280":{"t":"Broad avenues, a quiet river and a 'sea' that isn't","s":"The Minsk Sea is not a sea at all but the Zaslavl Reservoir, spreading north-west of the Belarusian capital. Minsk itself covers a gently rolling plain in the middle of the country, with dark forests and farmland all around, and the small Svislach River winds through its centre in a chain of ponds. The city is first mentioned in a chronicle in 1067.","w":"A large city of broad, straight avenues on a flat, forested plain, with a winding river and a big reservoir to the north-west.","f":["The ring road around Minsk is about 56 km long.","The Svislach flows on to join the Berezina and then the Dnieper.","Minsk lies close to the geographical centre of Belarus."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Minsk?","a":"The Svislach","x":["The Neman","The Pripyat"]}},"Q3919":{"t":"A young capital on the edge of the Kalahari","s":"Gaborone is one of Africa's youngest capitals, planned in the 1960s beside the Notwane River, only a few kilometres from the South African border. From above, look for a large reservoir on its southern side, held back by the Gaborone Dam, with rocky hills such as Kgale Hill nearby. To the west, the dry scrub of the Kalahari stretches away for hundreds of kilometres.","w":"A spread-out city beside a large reservoir to the south, with rocky hills close by and dry, flat scrubland to the west.","f":["The Kalahari covers about 70 percent of Botswana.","Building began in 1964, just before Botswana's independence in 1966.","The city is named after Kgosi Gaborone, a local chief."],"q":{"q":"Which desert covers most of Botswana, west of Gaborone?","a":"The Kalahari","x":["The Namib","The Karoo"]}},"Q3777":{"t":"A savanna capital of red earth and reservoirs","s":"A string of reservoirs along Ouagadougou's northern edge stores water through the long dry season; look for them on the flat savanna of central Burkina Faso. The city is low, with red laterite streets and tree-lined quarters. For centuries this was the capital of the Mossi kingdom, and its traditional king, the Mogho Naba, still holds court here.","w":"A flat, low, reddish city on open savanna, with a chain of reservoirs along its northern side.","f":["FESPACO, held here every two years, is Africa's largest film festival.","The rainy season lasts only from about June to September."],"q":{"q":"What kind of festival has made Ouagadougou famous?","a":"A film festival","x":["A jazz festival","A camel race"]}},"Q11194":{"t":"A long city in a valley ringed by Olympic mountains","s":"Steep, forested mountains hem Sarajevo in on every side, so the city stretches instead: a long, thin ribbon running east–west along the narrow valley of the Miljacka, with snowy peaks around it in winter. The Ottoman governor Isa-Beg Isaković founded the town in the 1460s, and its old bazaar still carries his imprint.","w":"A long, narrow city squeezed along a river valley, surrounded on all sides by steep, wooded mountains.","f":["The mountains around the city, including Bjelašnica, Jahorina and Igman, hosted Olympic events.","In 1895 Sarajevo opened an electric tram line, one of the first in Europe.","Baščaršija, the old bazaar, dates from the 15th century."],"q":{"q":"Which Olympic Games did Sarajevo host?","a":"The 1984 Winter Olympics","x":["The 1972 Summer Olympics","The 1980 Winter Olympics"]}},"Q168652":{"t":"A highland city near some of Africa's finest rock art","s":"Hargeisa sits on a dry highland plateau in the Horn of Africa, about 1,300 metres up, along a wide seasonal riverbed that fills only after rain. Around it stretch thorn scrub and red earth, grazed by camels and goats. In rock shelters about 50 km to the north-east, at Laas Geel, cattle and herders painted thousands of years ago still glow in red and white.","w":"A spread-out city on a dry, reddish plateau, split by a broad sandy riverbed, with scrubland and low hills around.","f":["The Laas Geel paintings are thought to be at least 5,000 years old.","The city's livestock market is one of the largest in the Horn of Africa.","Its highland air keeps Hargeisa far milder than the scorching coast of the Gulf of Aden."],"q":{"q":"What is found in the rock shelters of Laas Geel, near Hargeisa?","a":"Ancient rock paintings","x":["Dinosaur tracks","Roman coins"]}},"Q3940":{"t":"A tiny capital on ancient granite in the Indian Ocean","s":"Most oceanic islands are volcanic or coral, but the granite mountains behind Victoria are fragments of the ancient supercontinent Gondwana, stranded in mid-ocean. The town clings to the north-east coast of Mahé, the largest island of the Seychelles. Just offshore, small islands and turquoise reefs form a marine national park.","w":"A small town on a green, mountainous granite island, with little islets and turquoise reefs just off its shore.","f":["The granite of the Seychelles is around 750 million years old.","Victoria is one of the smallest national capitals in the world.","The coco de mer palm, which has the world's largest seed, grows wild only in the Seychelles."],"q":{"q":"What rock are the main Seychelles islands made of?","a":"Granite","x":["Coral limestone","Basalt"]}},"Q3932":{"t":"Volcanic peaks and jungle just north of the equator","s":"São Tomé lies on the north-east coast of a lush volcanic island in the Gulf of Guinea, only about 40 km north of the equator. Behind the town, forested mountains rise to over 2,000 metres, and in the island's south a needle of volcanic rock, Pico Cão Grande, stands up out of the jungle. The Portuguese settled the island in the late 15th century.","w":"A small town on the north-east shore of a green, mountainous island, with forest climbing steeply behind it.","f":["Pico de São Tomé, the island's highest point, rises to about 2,020 metres.","In the early 20th century São Tomé was one of the world's leading cocoa producers."],"q":{"q":"Which line crosses the islet of Rolas, off São Tomé's southern tip?","a":"The equator","x":["The Tropic of Capricorn","The prime meridian"]}},"Q23800":{"t":"A fortress city on a peninsula between two harbours","s":"Valletta fills a narrow rocky peninsula on Malta's north-east coast, with a deep natural harbour on each side: Grand Harbour to the south-east and Marsamxett to the north-west. The Knights of St John built it from 1566 as a planned fortress city, a grid of streets inside massive bastions. Around it, Malta is a patchwork of pale stone towns and small fields.","w":"A walled, grid-planned peninsula between two deep, branching harbours, on a small island of pale stone and tiny fields.","f":["Valletta covers less than one square kilometre, the smallest capital in the European Union.","The whole city has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1980.","The city is named after Jean de Valette, Grand Master of the Knights."],"q":{"q":"Which order of knights built Valletta?","a":"The Knights of St John","x":["The Knights Templar","The Teutonic Knights"]}},"Q1218":{"t":"On the ridge where green hills give way to desert","s":"Within about 25 km of Jerusalem, the land drops more than 1,000 metres to the shore of the Dead Sea. The city stands on the crest of the Judean Mountains, about 750 metres up, right on the divide between the Mediterranean slope to the west and the desert to the east. Its walled Old City is holy to Judaism, Christianity and Islam.","w":"A pale stone city on a ridge of hills, green terraces to the west and bare, brown desert falling away to the east.","f":["The walled Old City covers less than one square kilometre.","The Old City and its walls are on the UNESCO World Heritage list.","Winter can bring rain and even snow here, while the Dead Sea shore below stays mild and dry."],"q":{"q":"Which Ottoman sultan built the Old City walls of Jerusalem?","a":"Suleiman the Magnificent","x":["Mehmed II","Selim I"]}},"Q3751":{"t":"An Atlantic island capital on a flat-topped plateau","s":"These islands, some 600 km off the coast of Africa, were uninhabited until the Portuguese arrived around 1460. Praia, the capital of Cape Verde, sits on the southern coast of Santiago, the largest of them, its old centre on a flat-topped plateau above a sheltered bay. Just to the west lies the first Portuguese settlement, Cidade Velha.","w":"A town on a flat plateau above a small bay on the dry, rugged south coast of a volcanic island.","f":["Cidade Velha, founded in 1462, was the first European colonial town in the tropics and is a UNESCO site.","Rain is scarce here; most of it falls in August and September."],"q":{"q":"What does the Portuguese word praia mean?","a":"Beach","x":["Harbour","Plateau"]}},"Q3856":{"t":"A star-shaped old town on the plain of Cyprus","s":"Nicosia's old town is a near-perfect circle, ringed by walls the Venetians built in the 1560s, with arrow-shaped bastions that give it the outline of a star. The city lies in the middle of the Mesaoria, the broad, dry plain at the heart of Cyprus, with the jagged Kyrenia range to the north and the Troodos Mountains to the south-west.","w":"A city on a flat, tawny plain, with a round old core, a jagged ridge to the north and higher mountains to the south-west.","f":["One peak of the Kyrenia range is called Pentadaktylos, 'five fingers', after its jagged outline.","Mount Olympos in the Troodos, the highest point of Cyprus, rises to about 1,950 metres."],"q":{"q":"How many bastions do Nicosia's Venetian walls have?","a":"Eleven","x":["Seven","Twenty"]}},"Q956":{"t":"An imperial grid framed by mountains on two sides","s":"Beijing is laid out like a chessboard: straight avenues, a strict north–south axis and ring roads nested one inside another. Mountains curl around it on the west and north, while the plain opens towards the sea in the south-east. Its name means “northern capital”, and emperors ruled from here for most of the time since the 13th century.","w":"A vast grid on a flat plain, hemmed in by dark mountains to the north and west; at night, rectangular rings of light.","f":["The 2nd Ring Road traces the line of the old city walls, which were largely pulled down in the 20th century.","The Grand Canal, almost 1,800 km long, reaches its northern end on Beijing's eastern side.","The Forbidden City, finished in 1420, sits exactly on the city's north–south axis."],"q":{"q":"What does the name “Beijing” mean?","a":"Northern capital","x":["Southern capital","Western capital"]}},"Q3630":{"t":"Java's great sprawl, with a scatter of islands offshore","s":"A city with no visible edge: Jakarta spreads over a flat, hazy plain where Java meets the shallow Java Sea and simply fades into its neighbours. Offshore, the Thousand Islands dot the water like crumbs. On a clear day, volcanic cones rise to the south. The Dutch called it Batavia and ran their East Indies trade from here.","w":"A huge grey sprawl on Java's north coast, a bay with small islands to the north, volcanoes on the southern horizon.","f":["Despite their name, the Thousand Islands off Jakarta number only a little over a hundred.","The Dutch East India Company founded Batavia here in 1619, on the site of the port of Jayakarta.","The old harbour of Sunda Kelapa still hosts wooden pinisi schooners."],"q":{"q":"What was Jakarta called under Dutch rule?","a":"Batavia","x":["Buitenzorg","Nieuw Amsterdam"]}},"Q8686":{"t":"Where the muddy Yangtze finally meets the sea","s":"Look for brown water turning blue: the Yangtze pours its silt into the East China Sea right beside Shanghai. The city lies flat on the south bank, cut in two by the winding Huangpu River. Out to sea, a hair-thin line runs across the water — a bridge more than 30 km long to an island container port.","w":"A giant flat city on the south side of the brown Yangtze estuary, with a long, low island in the river mouth.","f":["Chongming Island in the Yangtze mouth is built entirely of river silt and is about 80 km long.","Shanghai has been the world's busiest container port since 2010.","The name is usually read as “on the sea” or “upon the sea”."],"q":{"q":"Which river winds through the centre of Shanghai?","a":"The Huangpu","x":["The Pearl River","The Qiantang"]}},"Q1490":{"t":"A city that fills an entire plain, wrapped round a bay","s":"Tokyo does not so much end as run out of flat land: buildings cover the Kanto Plain, Japan's largest, from the mountains to the sea. The city wraps around Tokyo Bay, much of whose shore is reclaimed land. On a clear day Fuji's cone stands about 100 km to the south-west. Until 1868 this was Edo, the shoguns' city.","w":"Continuous city around a wide bay; Fuji's lone cone to the south-west; at night an unbroken carpet of lights.","f":["Odaiba is named after cannon batteries built in the bay in 1853–54, after Commodore Perry's ships arrived.","The name Tokyo means “eastern capital”; the emperor moved here from Kyoto in 1868–69.","The Aqua-Line runs out into Tokyo Bay and seems to stop at an island — the rest of the road is a tunnel under the seabed."],"q":{"q":"What was Tokyo called before 1868?","a":"Edo","x":["Naniwa","Heian-kyō"]}},"Q1156":{"t":"Seven islands stitched into one long peninsula","s":"Mumbai points south into the Arabian Sea like a thumb. It was once seven separate islands, joined from the 18th century onwards by causeways and landfill into one crowded strip. Harbour water lies to the east, open sea to the west. After dark, look for the bright curve of lights along Back Bay — locals call it the Queen's Necklace.","w":"A narrow finger of land jutting south into the sea, a wide harbour on its east side, a green forest patch in the north.","f":["Portugal handed Bombay to England in 1661 as part of Catherine of Braganza's dowry on her marriage to Charles II.","Sanjay Gandhi National Park, a forest with wild leopards, lies inside the city limits.","A sea bridge about 22 km long, opened in 2024, links the city with the mainland across the harbour."],"q":{"q":"Mumbai was formed by joining how many islands?","a":"Seven","x":["Three","Twelve"]}},"Q1348":{"t":"Former capital of British India on a delta river","s":"Kolkata stretches along the east bank of the Hooghly, a working arm of the Ganges, with Howrah facing it across the water. Beyond its eastern edge, a patchwork of ponds and marshes quietly cleans the city's wastewater and raises fish. Further south the land dissolves into the channels and mangroves of the Sundarbans. Until 1911, this was the capital of British India.","w":"A long city on a brown river running north–south; square fish ponds to the east, delta channels to the south.","f":["The East Kolkata Wetlands use the city's sewage to feed fish ponds and are a protected Ramsar site.","Kolkata's port lies on the river, well over 100 km from the open sea.","The Sundarbans to the south are the world's largest mangrove forest, shared with Bangladesh."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Kolkata?","a":"The Hooghly","x":["The Brahmaputra","The Mahanadi"]}},"Q3130":{"t":"A drowned valley of blue fingers, open to the Pacific","s":"Sydney's harbour is a drowned river valley, and from above it looks like a hand of blue fingers reaching deep into the city. Sandstone headlands guard its narrow mouth to the Pacific. South lies Botany Bay, where the airport's runways run out into the water. To the west, the Blue Mountains rise in a haze that really does look blue.","w":"A deep, branching harbour entering the coast between two headlands; a round bay with runways to the south.","f":["The blue haze over the Blue Mountains comes from oils evaporating from eucalyptus leaves.","James Cook landed at Botany Bay in 1770; the First Fleet settled at Sydney Cove in 1788.","The Harbour Bridge, opened in 1932, is nicknamed the Coathanger for the shape of its arch."],"q":{"q":"Where did James Cook land in 1770, on Sydney's south side?","a":"Botany Bay","x":["Moreton Bay","Port Phillip Bay"]}},"Q334":{"t":"An island nation ringed by hundreds of waiting ships","s":"Before the city, you notice the sea: hundreds of ships at anchor in the Singapore Strait, scattered like grains of rice. The diamond-shaped island at the tip of the Malay Peninsula has grown by roughly a quarter through land reclamation, and its straight new shorelines are easy to spot. The equator lies less than 150 km to the south.","w":"A diamond-shaped island just off the Malay Peninsula, joined by a causeway, with anchored ships speckling the strait.","f":["Stamford Raffles set up a British trading post here in 1819.","The name comes from the Malay Singapura, “lion city”.","Jurong Island, the refining hub in the south-west, was made by joining seven small islands with landfill."],"q":{"q":"What does the name “Singapura” mean?","a":"Lion city","x":["Tiger city","City by the sea"]}},"Q8646":{"t":"Green mountains, a crowded shore and a fragrant harbour","s":"From above, Hong Kong is surprisingly green: steep, forested hills and dozens of islands, with the city packed into thin strips along the shore. Victoria Harbour, a narrow blue channel, separates Kowloon from Hong Kong Island. Further west, look for the airport on a flat man-made platform and a long bridge striking out across the Pearl River estuary.","w":"Hilly green islands and a peninsula, dense city lining a narrow harbour; a rectangular airport island to the west.","f":["About 40 percent of Hong Kong's land is protected as country parks.","The bridge to Zhuhai and Macau is about 55 km long and dips into an undersea tunnel between two artificial islands.","The name means “fragrant harbour”, probably after the incense wood once shipped from here."],"q":{"q":"What does the name “Hong Kong” mean?","a":"Fragrant harbour","x":["Pearl harbour","Deep harbour"]}},"Q1353":{"t":"Seven cities in one, on the banks of the Yamuna","s":"Delhi has been rebuilt again and again on the same stretch of the Yamuna, and tradition counts at least seven historic cities layered here. From above, the river runs down the eastern side, braided with sandbars in the dry season. A low, scrubby ridge — the last tip of the ancient Aravalli hills — cuts through the west. At night the glow spreads far across the plain.","w":"A huge sprawl on a flat plain, a braided river along its east side, a dark wooded ridge through the west.","f":["Old Delhi, or Shahjahanabad, was built by Shah Jahan, creator of the Taj Mahal; its Red Fort was finished in 1648.","The Aravalli Range, whose last spur ends in Delhi, is one of the oldest mountain ranges on Earth.","The Iron Pillar near the Qutb Minar has stood for about 1,600 years with remarkably little rust."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows past Delhi?","a":"The Yamuna","x":["The Ganges","The Sutlej"]}},"Q269":{"t":"A green oasis city at the foot of the Tian Shan","s":"Tashkent spreads as a broad, leafy grid across an irrigated plain, fed by canals drawn from the Chirchiq River. To the north-east the land rises into the first ridges of the Tian Shan, snowy in winter and spring, and the turquoise Charvak Reservoir fills a mountain valley. Caravans on the Silk Road knew this oasis as Chach.","w":"A large green grid on a flat plain; mountains rising to the north-east with a turquoise reservoir in a valley.","f":["The name is usually read as “stone city” in Turkic.","Tashkent's metro, opened in 1977, was the first in Central Asia.","It is the most populous city in Central Asia."],"q":{"q":"What does the name “Tashkent” most likely mean?","a":"Stone city","x":["Green city","Sun city"]}},"Q1861":{"t":"City of angels on a looping brown river","s":"The Chao Phraya winds through Bangkok in lazy brown loops, and the whole city sits on a delta so flat it barely rises above the sea. Around it, rice fields and square fish ponds stretch to the Gulf of Thailand, about 30 km south. Thais rarely say Bangkok: they call it Krung Thep, the city of angels.","w":"A vast flat grey city on a looping brown river, with a grid of green fields and ponds between it and the gulf.","f":["Bangkok's full ceremonial name is one of the longest place names in the world.","It became the capital in 1782, when King Rama I moved the court across the river from Thonburi.","Its old network of canals earned Bangkok the nickname “Venice of the East”."],"q":{"q":"What do Thais usually call Bangkok?","a":"Krung Thep","x":["Ayutthaya","Sukhothai"]}},"Q8684":{"t":"A wide river and granite peaks inside one capital","s":"Seoul is a bowl of city among steep granite mountains, split by the broad Han River. Up to a kilometre wide here, the Han is crossed by more than 25 bridges that show as fine stitches across the water. Joseon kings made it their capital in 1394: mountains behind, a river in front — ideal by the rules of geomancy.","w":"A broad river curving west through a dense city ringed by rocky, wooded mountains; bridges cross it every kilometre or two.","f":["Joseon kings ringed their capital with a stone wall about 18 km long, begun in 1396.","Bukhansan, a national park of granite peaks, rises right on the city's northern edge.","In Korean, the word “Seoul” simply means “capital”."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Seoul?","a":"The Han","x":["The Nakdong","The Taedong"]}},"Q1461":{"t":"A great round bay, a river and a lake behind the city","s":"Manila lines the eastern shore of a broad, almost circular bay, famous for its sunsets. The Pasig River threads through the city, linking the bay to Laguna de Bay, the largest lake in the Philippines. For 250 years Spanish galleons sailed from here to Mexico, carrying Chinese silk and porcelain across the Pacific.","w":"A dense city on the east shore of a wide round bay; a river winds east to a huge lake.","f":["The Spanish founded walled Intramuros in 1571; the Manila galleons ran to Acapulco from 1565 to 1815.","The name comes from nilad, a mangrove shrub that once grew along the river.","Laguna de Bay is very shallow: on average it is only about three metres deep."],"q":{"q":"Where did the Manila galleons sail to across the Pacific?","a":"Acapulco","x":["Lima","San Francisco"]}},"Q16959":{"t":"The big city farthest from any sea","s":"No large city on Earth lies farther from the ocean than Ürümqi — the nearest coastline is about 2,500 km away. It fills a valley on the northern foot of the Tian Shan, with the snowy massif of Bogda Peak rising to the east. North of the city the land flattens into the dry Dzungarian Basin.","w":"A long city in a valley below snowy mountains; the glaciers of Bogda Peak to the east, dry basin to the north.","f":["Bogda Peak is 5,445 m high — and still more than 5 km below you.","The Gurbantünggüt, China's second-largest desert, fills the basin north of the city.","The name comes from Mongolian and means “beautiful pasture”."],"q":{"q":"Roughly how far is Ürümqi from the nearest sea?","a":"About 2,500 km","x":["About 900 km","About 5,000 km"]}},"Q30002":{"t":"A round city on a plain watered since 256 BC","s":"Ring roads draw concentric circles on a remarkably flat, green plain: this is Chengdu, on the western edge of the Sichuan Basin. Just west, mountains rise abruptly towards the Tibetan Plateau. The plain owes its fertility to Dujiangyan, an irrigation system built around 256 BC that still splits the Min River into a fan of canals.","w":"A round city of concentric ring roads on a green plain; a wall of mountains rising sharply to the west.","f":["An old saying claims Sichuan's dogs bark at the sun because they see it so rarely through the haze.","Dujiangyan, about 50 km north-west of the city, is a UNESCO World Heritage site.","Chengdu has had a giant panda breeding centre since 1987."],"q":{"q":"Which ancient irrigation system still waters the Chengdu Plain?","a":"Dujiangyan","x":["The Grand Canal","The Lingqu Canal"]}},"Q35765":{"t":"Japan's old kitchen, with an airport out in the bay","s":"Osaka fills the flat land at the head of Osaka Bay, where the Yodo River splits into channels on its way to the sea. The built-up area runs on without a break towards Kobe and Kyoto. Out in the bay, look for a neat rectangle joined to the shore by a single long bridge: Kansai Airport, built on a man-made island.","w":"A continuous city around the head of a bay; a rectangular airport island offshore, linked by one bridge.","f":["Osaka ruled the rice trade in the Edo period so completely that it was called “the nation's kitchen”.","Toyotomi Hideyoshi began building Osaka Castle in 1583.","In neighbouring Sakai, the keyhole-shaped burial mound of Emperor Nintoku is about 486 m long."],"q":{"q":"What was Osaka nicknamed in the Edo period?","a":"The nation's kitchen","x":["The nation's forge","The nation's garden"]}},"Q987":{"t":"A planned imperial capital inside a far older city","s":"Within Delhi's endless sprawl, look for a greener, looser patch south of the old city: that is New Delhi. It was planned from scratch after 1911, when Britain decided to move its Indian capital here from Calcutta. Architects Edwin Lutyens and Herbert Baker laid out broad avenues meeting at roundabouts, with a ceremonial axis running east from Raisina Hill.","w":"A greener, more open patch in the middle of the Delhi sprawl, a few kilometres west of the Yamuna.","f":["New Delhi was formally inaugurated as the capital in 1931, twenty years after the move was announced.","Its plan is built from triangles and hexagons, with avenues radiating out from roundabouts.","The presidential palace on Raisina Hill has about 340 rooms."],"q":{"q":"When was New Delhi formally inaugurated as the capital?","a":"1931","x":["1911","1947"]}},"Q1355":{"t":"A garden city of lakes on a cool high plateau","s":"Dozens of lakes glint across this sprawl on the Deccan Plateau — most of them old reservoirs, or tanks, made centuries ago by damming shallow valleys. Bengaluru sits about 900 m up, which keeps it noticeably cooler than India's coastal cities. A local chieftain, Kempe Gowda, founded the city with a mud fort in 1537.","w":"A sprawling city on rolling upland, freckled with lakes of all sizes; bare granite hills rise to the north.","f":["Its many software companies have earned it the nickname “India's Silicon Valley”.","Lalbagh, a botanical garden begun under Hyder Ali in the 18th century, helped earn it the name “Garden City”.","In 2014 the official name changed from Bangalore to Bengaluru, as it has always been called in Kannada."],"q":{"q":"Roughly how high above sea level is Bengaluru?","a":"About 900 m","x":["About 100 m","About 2,000 m"]}},"Q5838":{"t":"A high mountain valley where Babur chose to rest","s":"Kabul lies about 1,800 m up, in a bowl of bare brown mountains, with rocky ridges rising right through the city itself. The Kabul River winds east from here towards the Khyber Pass and, eventually, the Indus. In winter the snowy wall of the Hindu Kush shines to the north. Babur, founder of the Mughal Empire, loved this valley and chose to be buried here.","w":"A dense city in a high, dry basin with bare ridges poking through it; snowy mountains to the north.","f":["Babur's tomb stands in Bagh-e Babur, a terraced garden he laid out himself.","At about 1,800 m, Kabul stands higher than the summit of Ben Nevis, Britain's highest mountain.","The Kabul River flows east into Pakistan and joins the Indus at Attock."],"q":{"q":"Which Mughal emperor chose to be buried in Kabul?","a":"Babur","x":["Akbar","Aurangzeb"]}},"Q3141":{"t":"A city at the head of a vast, nearly closed bay","s":"Picture a huge sheet of water almost sealed off from the ocean: that is Port Phillip Bay, with Melbourne at its head. The entrance, the Rip, is only about 3 km wide and has a fierce reputation among sailors. The Yarra River curls into the centre from the east, where the forested Dandenong Ranges rise. A gold rush in the 1850s made this one of the richest cities in the world.","w":"A sprawling city around the head of a huge, almost round bay; wooded hills to the east, flat plains to the west.","f":["From 1901 to 1927 Australia's federal parliament sat in Melbourne, until Canberra was ready.","The city was founded in 1835 on the banks of the Yarra.","Port Phillip Bay covers nearly 2,000 km², yet is nowhere deeper than about 24 m."],"q":{"q":"Roughly how wide is the entrance to Port Phillip Bay?","a":"About 3 km","x":["About 30 km","About 300 m"]}},"Q1867":{"t":"A city poured into a basin of green mountains","s":"Taipei fills a flat basin like water in a bowl, with forested mountains rising on every side. To the north, the dormant volcanoes of Yangmingshan steam quietly, feeding hot springs on the city's edge. Three rivers join in the basin and leave through a single gap to the north-west as the Tamsui, heading for the Taiwan Strait.","w":"A dense city filling a round basin, green mountains all around, a river snaking out north-west to the sea.","f":["Taipei 101, finished in 2004, was the world's tallest building until 2010.","Qixing Mountain in Yangmingshan, about 1,120 m high, is a dormant volcano within the city limits.","The hot springs of Beitou, on the north side of the city, are heated by the same volcanic group."],"q":{"q":"Which river drains the Taipei Basin to the sea?","a":"The Tamsui","x":["The Zhuoshui","The Gaoping"]}},"Q37100":{"t":"A city on a narrow neck between two seas","s":"Auckland is squeezed onto a narrow isthmus: the Waitematā Harbour opens east to the Pacific, the Manukau Harbour west to the Tasman Sea. The city sits on a field of about 50 small volcanoes, and many survive as green grassy cones among the suburbs. Just offshore, look for Rangitoto, a symmetrical island that rose from the sea only about 600 years ago.","w":"A city on a thin neck of land between two harbours, dotted with green cones; a round, symmetrical island just offshore.","f":["At its narrowest the isthmus is so thin that Māori once hauled canoes across it from one harbour to the other.","Auckland's Māori name, Tāmaki Makaurau, is often translated as “Tāmaki desired by many”.","Rangitoto is both the youngest and the largest volcano in the Auckland field."],"q":{"q":"Roughly how many volcanoes make up the Auckland volcanic field?","a":"About 50","x":["About 5","About 500"]}},"Q15174":{"t":"A boomtown along Hong Kong's northern edge","s":"Shenzhen runs in a long ribbon along the coast, facing the green hills of Hong Kong across a narrow river. Before 1980 this was a quiet border district of paddies and fishing villages; that year it became one of China's first Special Economic Zones and began growing at astonishing speed. At night its lights run almost unbroken from the Pearl River estuary to the eastern peninsulas.","w":"A long band of city along the coast just north of Hong Kong; a bay with a bridge on the west side.","f":["The Shenzhen River forms much of the boundary with Hong Kong.","A bridge about 5 km long crosses Shenzhen Bay to Hong Kong's New Territories.","Mangroves still line Shenzhen Bay at Futian, a protected refuge for migrating birds."],"q":{"q":"In which year did Shenzhen become a Special Economic Zone?","a":"1980","x":["1949","1997"]}},"Q198370":{"t":"A city of scattered towns, and football's ancient home","s":"Zibo is less one city than a loose cluster of towns, strung out where the hills of central Shandong fall away to the North China Plain. One of them, Linzi, was the capital of the ancient state of Qi and among the largest cities of its age. Another, Boshan, has been known for ceramics and coloured glass for centuries.","w":"Several separate urban patches spread along the edge of low hills, with the flat plain opening to the north.","f":["In 2004 FIFA recognised cuju, played in Linzi over 2,000 years ago, as the earliest known form of football.","Linzi remained the capital of Qi for centuries, until the Qin conquest in 221 BC."],"q":{"q":"Zibo's Linzi district is honoured as the birthplace of which sport?","a":"Football","x":["Table tennis","Badminton"]}},"Q1858":{"t":"A thousand-year capital on the rust-brown river","s":"Thick with silt, the Red River really does look rusty from above, and Hanoi sits on its right bank. The city is freckled with lakes, the biggest being West Lake in the north. All around, the flat, green Red River Delta is laid out in paddies. The Lý dynasty made this their capital in 1010, calling it Thăng Long, “ascending dragon”.","w":"A city on the south-west bank of a broad brown river, with a large lake on its north side, amid flat green paddies.","f":["The name Hà Nội means roughly “inside the river”; the city received it in 1831.","West Lake, the largest lake in Hanoi, covers about 500 hectares."],"q":{"q":"What was Hanoi called when it became the capital in 1010?","a":"Thăng Long","x":["Phú Xuân","Gia Định"]}},"Q1854":{"t":"River loops, a vast delta and a maze of mangroves","s":"The Saigon River loops through the city in tight, shining bends before heading for the sea. South-east of the centre, the land gives way to Cần Giờ, a dark green maze of mangroves and tidal channels. To the south-west spreads the Mekong Delta, one of the great rice bowls of Asia. Many residents still call the city by its old name, Saigon.","w":"A dense city on a looping river; dark mangrove channels to the south-east, flat green delta to the south-west.","f":["The Cần Giờ mangrove forest is a UNESCO biosphere reserve.","Before Vietnamese settlers arrived in the 17th century, a Khmer port called Prey Nokor stood here."],"q":{"q":"Which mangrove forest lies between Ho Chi Minh City and the sea?","a":"Cần Giờ","x":["The Sundarbans","Bhitarkanika"]}},"Q11739":{"t":"A Mughal city of gardens on the Punjab plain","s":"Straight irrigation canals rule lines across the flat Punjab plain around Lahore, and the Ravi River curves past the city's north-western edge. For centuries this was one of the great cities of the Mughal Empire, and Akbar kept his capital here for more than a decade. The old walled city is still a tight knot of lanes near the river.","w":"A large flat city with a river on its north-west side and straight canals running through green fields.","f":["Akbar held his court at Lahore from 1585 to 1598.","Lahore Fort and the Shalimar Gardens are listed together as one UNESCO World Heritage site.","The walled city was once entered through about a dozen gates."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows past Lahore?","a":"The Ravi","x":["The Jhelum","The Kabul"]}},"Q8660":{"t":"A megacity on a dry coast beside the Indus Delta","s":"Karachi sprawls over a dry, sand-coloured coast, with bare hills behind it and the Arabian Sea in front. Its harbour is sheltered by a low spit of land, and to the south-east the shore frays into mangrove creeks where the Indus Delta begins. In the 18th century this was a small fishing settlement; today it is Pakistan's largest city.","w":"A vast beige city on a straight dry coast; a sheltered harbour and mangrove creeks to the south-east, bare hills inland.","f":["Its old name was Kolachi, after a fishing village on this shore.","Karachi was Pakistan's capital from 1947 until 1959.","The Indus Delta mangroves to the south-east are among the largest mangrove forests in a dry climate."],"q":{"q":"What was Karachi's name as an 18th-century fishing village?","a":"Kolachi","x":["Thatta","Gwadar"]}},"Q18808":{"t":"A river-island city once called the capital of willows","s":"Long, low islands split the wide, calm Taedong River as it flows through Pyongyang, and the city lies on both banks. Beyond it, flat farmland and low hills stretch towards the Yellow Sea, about 50 km to the south-west. The name means “flat land”, and in 427 the Goguryeo kingdom moved its capital here.","w":"A city on both banks of a broad river with long islands; the river winds south-west across flat farmland to the sea.","f":["An old name for the city is Ryugyong, “capital of willows”, after the trees along its riverbanks.","Goguryeo ruled from Pyongyang for more than two centuries, from 427 until 668."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Pyongyang?","a":"The Taedong","x":["The Han","The Yalu"]}},"Q5972":{"t":"Sumatra's biggest city, built on tobacco","s":"Vast, tidy grids of plantations surround Medan on the flat coastal plain of north-eastern Sumatra, a few kilometres inland from the Strait of Malacca. In the 19th century the leaf grown here, Deli tobacco, was prized worldwide as cigar wrapper, and a small village grew rich. To the south-west, volcanic cones rise towards the great caldera lake of Toba.","w":"A grey sprawl on a flat plain near the coast, ringed by gridded plantations; volcanic peaks on the south-west horizon.","f":["Medan is the largest city on Sumatra.","Lake Toba, south of the city, fills the caldera of a supervolcano that erupted about 74,000 years ago.","Deli tobacco, named after the local sultanate, was grown here for cigar wrappers from the 1860s."],"q":{"q":"Which crop made Medan rich in the 19th century?","a":"Tobacco","x":["Nutmeg","Cloves"]}},"Q1865":{"t":"A muddy river junction that tin turned into a capital","s":"Kuala Lumpur fills the Klang Valley, with the forested ridges of the Titiwangsa Mountains rising to the east. The city began in the 1850s as a rough camp of tin miners, and many of the lakes glinting around it are old flooded mine pits. To the south, look for Putrajaya, a planned government town wrapped around a large artificial lake.","w":"A dense city in a green valley, forested mountains to the east, scattered lakes around it and a planned town to the south.","f":["Kuala Lumpur means “muddy confluence”: it grew where the Gombak River meets the Klang.","The Petronas Twin Towers were the world's tallest buildings from 1998 to 2004.","Batu Caves, in a limestone hill on the city's northern edge, have been a Hindu shrine since the 1890s."],"q":{"q":"What does the name “Kuala Lumpur” mean?","a":"Muddy confluence","x":["River of tin","Town of hills"]}},"Q183584":{"t":"A long, thin city squeezed along the Yellow River","s":"Lanzhou is a city with nowhere to spread: it runs for tens of kilometres along a narrow valley of the Yellow River, hemmed in by bare, ochre loess hills. From above it looks like a grey ribbon laid in a crease of the land. For Silk Road caravans, this was the key crossing of the Yellow River on the way west into the Hexi Corridor.","w":"A narrow grey ribbon of city following a winding river between bare yellow-brown hills.","f":["Zhongshan Bridge, finished in 1909, was the first permanent bridge across the Yellow River.","Before bridges, travellers crossed the river here on rafts buoyed by inflated sheepskins."],"q":{"q":"Which river runs through the middle of Lanzhou?","a":"The Yellow River","x":["The Yangtze","The Wei River"]}},"Q179608":{"t":"A green subtropical city in the loops of the Yong","s":"Nanning coils around the wide meanders of the Yong River, deep in China's subtropical south. The countryside around it is a patchwork of green hills and fields, many of them planted with sugarcane. The border with Vietnam is less than 200 km to the south-west, and since 2004 the city has hosted a yearly trade fair with South-East Asia.","w":"A city spread around broad river loops, with green hills and fields all around.","f":["Guangxi, whose capital is Nanning, grows more than half of China's sugarcane.","Its year-round greenery has earned Nanning the nickname “Green City”.","The Yong River flows on into the Xi River and eventually reaches the Pearl River Delta."],"q":{"q":"Which crop makes Guangxi China's biggest producer?","a":"Sugarcane","x":["Tea","Cotton"]}},"Q192271":{"t":"A high city among countless cone-shaped karst hills","s":"Limestone cones and hollows crumple the land around Guiyang, about 1,100 m up on the Yunnan–Guizhou Plateau. Districts squeeze in between the hills, so from above the city looks broken into pieces. An old saying claims Guizhou never has three sunny days in a row or three li of flat ground — look down and you'll see why.","w":"Patches of city scattered between green, cone-shaped hills on a high, crumpled plateau, often under cloud.","f":["Thanks to the altitude, Guiyang's summers are mild for southern China.","The name means “south of Mount Gui”: in Chinese, “yang” marks the sunny side of a hill."],"q":{"q":"Guiyang is the capital of which province?","a":"Guizhou","x":["Guangxi","Yunnan"]}},"Q11725":{"t":"A mountain city on the point where two rivers meet","s":"Chongqing's old centre is a steep, crowded peninsula at the meeting of two rivers: the Jialing from the north and the Yangtze. At the tip, their waters often run side by side in two colours before mixing. Around the city, long parallel ridges run north-east like the folds of a rumpled cloth. Hills, fog and hot summers have given it its nicknames: Mountain City and Fog City.","w":"A dense peninsula between two converging rivers, framed by long parallel ridges running north-east to south-west.","f":["The reservoir behind the Three Gorges Dam stretches roughly 600 km upstream, all the way to Chongqing.","Chongqing is counted among China's “furnaces” for its hot, humid summers.","The whole municipality of Chongqing is roughly the size of Austria."],"q":{"q":"Which river joins the Yangtze in central Chongqing?","a":"The Jialing","x":["The Min","The Han"]}},"Q68481":{"t":"Banyan City, in a green basin on the Min River","s":"Banyan trees, first planted here about a thousand years ago, still shade Fuzhou's streets and give it the nickname Banyan City. From above, the city fills a basin ringed by green mountains on the lower Min River, just before the river widens towards the sea. A large island in the river, Nantai, is itself covered in city.","w":"A city in a mountain-ringed basin on a wide river, with a large built-up island in the middle of the river.","f":["The FAO lists Fuzhou's jasmine and tea culture as a Globally Important Agricultural Heritage System.","Hot springs rise beneath the city centre, and bathhouses have used them for centuries.","The Mawei shipyard, founded in 1866, was one of China's first modern shipyards."],"q":{"q":"Fuzhou's nickname comes from which tree?","a":"The banyan","x":["The camphor tree","The willow"]}},"Q16572":{"t":"Old Canton, at the head of the Pearl River Delta","s":"Where the Pearl River breaks into the maze of channels of its delta, Guangzhou merges seamlessly with the cities around it. The green hump of Baiyun Mountain rises on its northern side. From 1757 to 1842 this was the one Chinese port where Western merchants were allowed to trade, and they knew it as Canton.","w":"A huge city on a branching river at the top of the delta, with a green mountain on its northern edge.","f":["A legend of five rams bringing rice to the people gave Guangzhou its nickname, the City of Rams.","The Canton Fair has been held here twice a year since 1957."],"q":{"q":"What did European traders call Guangzhou?","a":"Canton","x":["Amoy","Foochow"]}},"Q59218":{"t":"A patchwork of factory towns on the Pearl River","s":"Dongguan has no single centre: it is a patchwork of about thirty towns, factory estates and fish ponds on the east side of the Pearl River estuary, filling the gap between Guangzhou and Shenzhen. Countless toys, shoes and electronic gadgets have come out of its workshops. At its western edge, the Humen Bridge leaps across the river mouth.","w":"A sprawl of towns and ponds on the east shore of a wide estuary, with a long suspension bridge on its western edge.","f":["At Humen in 1839, Commissioner Lin Zexu had more than a thousand tonnes of seized opium destroyed.","The Humen Bridge, opened in 1997, has a main span of 888 m."],"q":{"q":"What did Commissioner Lin Zexu destroy at Humen in 1839?","a":"Opium","x":["Silver","Tea"]}},"Q10389":{"t":"A cool highland basin ringed by volcanoes","s":"Bandung fills a basin about 700 m up in the highlands of West Java, and the flat floor it sits on was once the bed of a great prehistoric lake. Volcanoes stand all around; the long, flat-topped one to the north is Tangkuban Perahu, the “upturned boat”. Its cool climate and elegant avenues once earned the city the nickname “Paris of Java”.","w":"A city spread across a flat basin floor, volcanoes on every side; a long, flat-topped mountain to the north.","f":["In 1955 Bandung hosted the Asian–African Conference, attended by 29 countries.","Tea plantations cover many of the volcanic slopes around the city.","Legend says Tangkuban Perahu is a boat kicked over in anger by the hero Sangkuriang."],"q":{"q":"What does the volcano name Tangkuban Perahu mean?","a":"Upturned boat","x":["Sleeping giant","Burning mountain"]}},"Q11462":{"t":"A shark, a crocodile and a bridge to Madura","s":"A thin line stitching two islands together is your clue: the Suramadu Bridge, about 5 km long, joins Surabaya, on Java's flat north-east coast, to the island of Madura. Along the shore, square fish ponds glint in rows. The city's emblem shows a shark and a crocodile — sura and baya — locked in a fight.","w":"A large coastal city facing a narrow strait, with a long bridge across to an island and rows of fish ponds along the shore.","f":["When the Suramadu Bridge opened in 2009, it was the longest bridge in Indonesia.","Tanjung Perak, Surabaya's harbour, is one of Indonesia's busiest ports."],"q":{"q":"Surabaya's emblem shows a shark fighting which animal?","a":"A crocodile","x":["A tiger","An eagle"]}},"Q5826":{"t":"Where the Silk Road began, below the Qinling wall","s":"A dark wall of mountains, the Qinling, rises to the south of Xi'an, which lies on the flat Wei River plain. Its streets still follow a strict north–south grid around a rectangular old core. As Chang'an, this was the capital of the Tang dynasty, the eastern starting point of the Silk Road and one of the largest cities on Earth.","w":"A big gridded city on a flat plain; a steep mountain wall to the south, a river to the north.","f":["Xi'an's Ming-era city wall, about 14 km around, is one of the most complete old city walls in China.","The Terracotta Army was found in 1974 by farmers digging a well, about 35 km to the east.","The Qinling range is China's traditional dividing line between north and south."],"q":{"q":"What was Xi'an called as the Tang dynasty capital?","a":"Chang'an","x":["Luoyang","Kaifeng"]}},"Q72778":{"t":"Coal country's capital along the Fen River","s":"Taiyuan stretches north to south along the Fen River, in a long basin pinched between two mountain ranges on the Loess Plateau. The hills around it are dry and furrowed, yellow-brown for much of the year. Beneath Shanxi province lies some of China's richest coal, and the city grew as its industrial heart.","w":"A long city following a river down a narrow basin, bare furrowed hills on both sides.","f":["The first fortified town here, Jinyang, was built around 497 BC.","Shanxi is famous for its dark aged vinegar, made around Taiyuan for centuries."],"q":{"q":"Which river runs through Taiyuan?","a":"The Fen","x":["The Wei","The Luo"]}},"Q11746":{"t":"Three cities split by two rivers and a hundred lakes","s":"Wuhan is really three old cities — Wuchang, Hankou and Hanyang — divided by the meeting of the Yangtze and its tributary, the Han. From above, the rivers form a giant Y, and lakes glitter everywhere between the districts. The largest, East Lake, spreads its many arms over more than 30 km² on the eastern side.","w":"Two big rivers meeting in a Y through a vast city, with many lakes; a large branching lake to the east.","f":["The first bridge ever built across the Yangtze opened here in 1957.","In the 19th century, Russian merchants ran brick-tea factories in Hankou.","Its place at the heart of China's river network earned Wuhan the nickname “thoroughfare of nine provinces”."],"q":{"q":"Which river joins the Yangtze at Wuhan?","a":"The Han","x":["The Jialing","The Xiang"]}},"Q174091":{"t":"A long orange-grove island in the Xiang River","s":"Changsha lines both banks of the Xiang River, and in the middle of the river lies a remarkably long, thin island: Orange Isle, once covered in citrus groves. On the west bank rises the wooded hill of Yuelu. At its foot stands one of China's oldest academies, founded in 976 and still part of a university today.","w":"A city on both banks of a wide river with a long, narrow island in the middle; a wooded hill on the west bank.","f":["Orange Isle is about 5 km long, yet very narrow.","The Han-dynasty tombs of Mawangdui, found in Changsha in 1972, held a remarkably well-preserved noblewoman."],"q":{"q":"What is the long island in the Xiang River at Changsha called?","a":"Orange Isle","x":["Peach Isle","Lotus Isle"]}},"Q182852":{"t":"Eternal spring on the shore of a mountain lake","s":"Almost 1,900 m up on the Yunnan plateau, Kunming spreads from the northern end of Dian Lake, a long sheet of water whose western shore is lined by steep hills. Altitude and latitude together give it mild weather all year, and it is known as the City of Eternal Spring. The fields around it show the deep red soil typical of the plateau.","w":"A city at the north end of a long lake with steep hills along its western shore; red-soiled fields around.","f":["Dian Lake covers about 300 km², making it the largest lake in Yunnan.","Since the 1980s, black-headed gulls from Siberia have come to Kunming every winter.","The Stone Forest, a UNESCO-listed maze of limestone pinnacles, lies less than 100 km to the south-east."],"q":{"q":"What is Kunming's nickname?","a":"City of Eternal Spring","x":["City of Eternal Autumn","City of Winds"]}},"Q30340":{"t":"A rail crossroads beside a river that runs above the plain","s":"Here the Yellow River flows higher than the fields around it: its bed has silted up between high levees, and it runs wide, braided and sandy. Zhengzhou spreads across the flat North China Plain just to the south. People have lived here a long time: parts of the rammed-earth walls of a Shang dynasty city, some 3,500 years old, still stand in the centre.","w":"A big flat grid of a city with a wide, sandy, braided river running east–west just to the north.","f":["The Shaolin Temple, cradle of kung fu legend, lies in the Song Mountains less than 100 km to the west.","Zhengzhou grew up where China's main north–south and east–west railways cross."],"q":{"q":"Zhengzhou is the capital of which province?","a":"Henan","x":["Hebei","Hubei"]}},"Q11720":{"t":"The Manchu capital before Beijing, on a wintry plain","s":"In winter the Hun River freezes and the fields turn white around Shenyang, which spreads across the flat plain of north-east China with the river curving along its southern side. In 1625 the Manchu leader Nurhaci made it his capital, and his successors ruled from here until they took Beijing in 1644. The Manchus called it Mukden.","w":"A large flat city on a wide plain, a river along its southern edge; snow-covered and grey in winter.","f":["The Mukden Palace, built from 1625, is a smaller cousin of Beijing's Forbidden City and a UNESCO site.","The name means “north of the Shen River”, an old name for the Hun."],"q":{"q":"What was Shenyang's Manchu name?","a":"Mukden","x":["Harbin","Kirin"]}},"Q170247":{"t":"City of Springs, between Mount Tai and the Yellow River","s":"Jinan is squeezed between low hills to the south and the Yellow River to the north. Water seeping through the limestone hills bubbles up in the city centre from dozens of springs, which feed Daming Lake and earned Jinan the name City of Springs. On clear days Mount Tai, foremost of China's five sacred mountains, rises about 60 km to the south.","w":"A city between green hills to the south and a wide river to the north, with a lake in its centre.","f":["Tradition counts 72 famous springs in Jinan; the best known is Baotu Spring.","Jinan means “south of the Ji”; since 1855 the Yellow River has flowed in the Ji River's old bed."],"q":{"q":"When did the Yellow River shift into its present course past Jinan?","a":"1855","x":["1755","1911"]}},"Q11736":{"t":"Beijing's gateway to the sea, edged with salt pans","s":"A geometric pattern of salt pans and harbour basins marks the coast where the Hai River reaches Bohai Bay. Tianjin spreads over the flat plain behind them, about 120 km south-east of Beijing. The name means “heavenly ford”, given in 1404 by the Yongle Emperor, who had crossed the river here.","w":"A large city on a flat plain, a winding river to the sea, and a coast of rectangular salt pans and harbour basins.","f":["After 1860 several foreign powers held concessions in Tianjin, and their European-style streets survive.","The Changlu salt works along this coast are among the oldest and largest in China."],"q":{"q":"What does the name “Tianjin” mean?","a":"Heavenly ford","x":["Heavenly gate","Gate to the sea"]}},"Q171943":{"t":"Gateway to a lake that grows and shrinks with the seasons","s":"Nanchang straddles the Gan River just before it spreads out into the marshy plain of Poyang Lake, China's largest freshwater lake. In summer the lake swells into an inland sea; in winter it can shrink to a web of channels and grassland. Those winter wetlands host the great majority of the world's Siberian cranes.","w":"A city on both banks of a wide river; to the north-east, the huge, shifting expanse of Poyang Lake.","f":["Poyang Lake can cover several thousand km² in the wet season and shrink several times over in the dry.","The Tengwang Pavilion on the Gan River was first built in 653."],"q":{"q":"Which lake, China's largest freshwater lake, lies near Nanchang?","a":"Poyang Lake","x":["Dongting Lake","Lake Tai"]}},"Q16666":{"t":"The southern capital, between the Yangtze and green hills","s":"Where the Yangtze swings north-east on its last stretch to the sea, Nanjing sits on the south bank. The forested dome of Purple Mountain rises on the eastern edge, and Xuanwu Lake glints just inside the old walls. The first Ming emperor made this his capital in 1368, and its name still means “southern capital”.","w":"A large city on the south bank of the broad Yangtze, a green mountain to the east and a lake in its northern part.","f":["Nanjing's Ming city wall, about 35 km long, was one of the longest city walls ever built, and much of it survives.","Zhu Yuanzhang, founder of the Ming dynasty, is buried in a tomb on Purple Mountain."],"q":{"q":"Which dynasty built Nanjing's great city wall?","a":"The Ming","x":["The Tang","The Song"]}},"Q4970":{"t":"A poets' lake and a river with a racing tidal wave","s":"Hangzhou wraps around West Lake, a calm sheet of water held on three sides by green hills and praised by Chinese poets for more than a thousand years. South of the city the wide Qiantang River bends towards funnel-shaped Hangzhou Bay. Especially in autumn, a tidal bore charges up the river as a wall of water, drawing crowds to the banks.","w":"A city on the east shore of a lake with hills on three sides; a wide river curving to a funnel-shaped bay.","f":["Hangzhou was the capital of the Southern Song dynasty, and then one of the largest cities in the world.","The Grand Canal, running north to Beijing, begins here.","Longjing, or Dragon Well, one of China's most famous green teas, grows in the hills west of the lake."],"q":{"q":"Which river at Hangzhou is famous for its tidal bore?","a":"The Qiantang","x":["The Huangpu","The Min"]}},"Q34664":{"t":"A city on the fingers of a delta, by an island sea","s":"Hiroshima spreads over the delta of the Ōta River, whose channels fan out towards the sea like the fingers of a hand, splitting the city into long islands. Beyond lies the calm Seto Inland Sea, scattered with green islands. The name means “broad island”, and the city was founded in 1589, when the warlord Mōri Terumoto built his castle on the delta.","w":"A city on a delta of parallel river channels at the head of a bay, with wooded islands scattered offshore.","f":["Hiroshima Prefecture produces more than half of Japan's farmed oysters.","Itsukushima Shrine, on the island of Miyajima about 20 km south-west, is famous for its torii gate standing in the sea."],"q":{"q":"On the delta of which river is Hiroshima built?","a":"The Ōta","x":["The Yodo","The Shinano"]}},"Q92161":{"t":"China's car city on a plain of black earth","s":"For months each winter the fertile black soil of the Songliao Plain lies white under snow, and Changchun sits right in the middle of it, laid out in a neat grid of wide avenues. In 1953 China's first automobile factory was founded here, and the city is still called the cradle of the country's car industry. Its name, ironically for so cold a place, means “long spring”.","w":"A gridded city on an endless flat plain of dark fields; a forested reservoir on its south-eastern edge.","f":["The first trucks built in the People's Republic rolled off the line here in 1956.","Jingyuetan, on the south-eastern edge, is a reservoir ringed by a vast planted forest."],"q":{"q":"What is Changchun best known for making?","a":"Cars","x":["Silk","Porcelain"]}},"Q569907":{"t":"Deer City on the Yellow River's great northern bend","s":"Baotou stretches along the plain between the bare Yin Mountains and the Yellow River, near the top of the river's great loop through Inner Mongolia. Across the water to the south, the dunes of the Kubuqi Desert begin. The city grew into a steel centre in the 1950s, and its Mongolian name is usually translated as “place with deer” — hence its nickname, Deer City.","w":"An industrial city strung along a plain between bare mountains to the north and a wide river to the south; dunes beyond.","f":["Bayan Obo, more than 100 km to the north, is the world's largest known deposit of rare-earth metals.","The Yellow River's loop around the Ordos Plateau is so big that the river flows north, then east, then south."],"q":{"q":"Baotou's Mongolian name refers to which animal?","a":"Deer","x":["Horses","Wolves"]}},"Q42956":{"t":"A railway town on a river that freezes into a festival","s":"Harbin spreads along the south bank of the Songhua River on the flat plains of the north-east. In winter the river freezes solid into a white road, and blocks cut from its ice are carved into the palaces of the famous Ice and Snow Festival. The city grew from 1898 around a railway built by Russia, and its onion-domed churches still recall those years.","w":"A large city on the south bank of a wide river with an island park opposite; the river white and frozen in winter.","f":["The average January temperature here is well below −15 °C.","Harbin's Ice and Snow Festival has been held every winter since 1985."],"q":{"q":"Harbin grew up around a railway built by which country?","a":"Russia","x":["Japan","Britain"]}},"Q37951":{"t":"A snowy grid city at the edge of Hokkaido's mountains","s":"Sapporo was laid out from 1869 on a neat grid, and you can still see its straight lines across the Ishikari Plain, with forested mountains rising right behind it to the west. Few large cities anywhere get as much snow: roughly five metres falls in a typical winter. The name comes from the Ainu language and is often read as “dry, great river”.","w":"A gridded city on a plain at the foot of forested mountains; in winter, everything white.","f":["In 1972 Sapporo hosted the first Winter Olympics held in Asia.","The Sapporo Snow Festival began in 1950 with six snow statues built by local students."],"q":{"q":"When did Sapporo host Asia's first Winter Olympics?","a":"1972","x":["1964","1998"]}},"Q1361":{"t":"A city of pearls and diamonds among granite boulders","s":"Giant granite boulders lie scattered across the rocky Deccan Plateau as if dropped by hand, and Hyderabad has grown up among them. Look for a big lake right in the middle of the city: Hussain Sagar, built in the 16th century, which separates Hyderabad from its twin, Secunderabad. The Qutb Shahi sultans founded the city in 1591, and nearby Golconda was famed for its diamond trade.","w":"A vast city on a rocky plateau dotted with lakes, a large lake in the centre and a river through the south.","f":["The Koh-i-Noor diamond is believed to have come from mines in the Golconda region.","Its centuries-old pearl trade earned Hyderabad the nickname City of Pearls.","The Charminar, the city's four-minareted emblem, was built in 1591, the year Hyderabad was founded."],"q":{"q":"Which famous diamond is thought to come from the Golconda mines?","a":"The Koh-i-Noor","x":["The Cullinan","The Excelsior"]}},"Q1538":{"t":"The Peshwas' city in the lee of the Western Ghats","s":"Where the Mula and Mutha rivers meet, about 560 m up on the Deccan Plateau, lies Pune. Just to the west rise the steep, green Western Ghats, with a chain of reservoirs filling their valleys. In the 18th century this was the seat of the Peshwas, chief ministers who ran the Maratha Empire. Today it has so many colleges that it is called the Oxford of the East.","w":"A large city where two rivers meet on a plateau; green, steep hills and reservoirs to the west.","f":["Shaniwar Wada, the Peshwas' fortified palace, was completed in 1732.","The hill fort of Sinhagad stands about 30 km south-west, on a ridge of the Ghats."],"q":{"q":"Pune was the seat of which 18th-century rulers?","a":"The Peshwas","x":["The Mughals","The Nizams"]}},"Q1513":{"t":"Orange City, at the very heart of India","s":"Somewhere close by lies the geographic centre of India, and a Zero Mile Stone set up by British surveyors in Nagpur still marks the spot. From above, it's a sprawling city on a gently rolling plateau, with several lakes on its western side. Orange orchards surround it, giving it the name Orange City, and the forests further out are home to tigers.","w":"A large city on a flat plateau with a few lakes on its western edge, surrounded by farmland and orchards.","f":["Nagpur's oranges are famous across India and carry a geographical indication tag.","Tiger reserves, including Pench and Tadoba, lie within a few hours' drive."],"q":{"q":"Nagpur's nickname comes from which fruit?","a":"Oranges","x":["Mangoes","Bananas"]}},"Q66485":{"t":"The Pink City, laid out on a grid in 1727","s":"Old forts guard the passes in the rugged Aravalli ridges that rise north and east of Jaipur, a city spread across a dry Rajasthan plain. Maharaja Sawai Jai Singh II founded it in 1727 on a strict grid, unusual for its time. Its old town is painted terracotta pink, first done to welcome the Prince of Wales in 1876.","w":"A large city on a dry plain, rocky ridges with forts to the north and east, a lake at their foot.","f":["Jai Singh II was also an astronomer; his Jantar Mantar observatory is a UNESCO World Heritage site.","The walled city of Jaipur was added to the UNESCO World Heritage list in 2019."],"q":{"q":"For whose visit was Jaipur painted pink in 1876?","a":"The Prince of Wales","x":["Queen Victoria","The Viceroy of India"]}},"Q66568":{"t":"An old mill town on the banks of the Ganges","s":"Kanpur stretches along the south-west bank of the Ganges, which curves past its northern edge in wide, sandy channels. The land all around is the flat, fertile Gangetic plain, laid out in small fields. In the 19th century cotton mills and tanneries turned the town into an industrial hub nicknamed the Manchester of the East. Leather is still one of its big trades.","w":"A large flat city on the south bank of a broad river with sandbars, surrounded by a patchwork of small fields.","f":["Under British rule the city was known as Cawnpore.","Kanpur is one of India's biggest centres of leather tanning."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows past Kanpur?","a":"The Ganges","x":["The Yamuna","The Gomti"]}},"Q80484":{"t":"Ancient Pataliputra, stretched along the wide Ganges","s":"Patna is long and thin: it runs for many kilometres along the south bank of the Ganges, which here spreads several kilometres wide in flood and breaks into channels around sandbars in the dry season. Look for the long bridge striking north across it. More than 2,000 years ago, as Pataliputra, this was the capital of the Maurya Empire and one of the greatest cities of the ancient world.","w":"A long, narrow city along the south bank of a very wide, braided river, with a long bridge crossing it.","f":["Around 300 BC the Greek envoy Megasthenes described Pataliputra's huge timber walls.","The Mahatma Gandhi Setu, opened in 1982, carries a road about 5.7 km across the Ganges."],"q":{"q":"What was Patna called as the Maurya capital?","a":"Pataliputra","x":["Taxila","Ujjain"]}},"Q1352":{"t":"Old Madras, facing the Bay of Bengal across a long beach","s":"Chennai lines a long, straight stretch of the Coromandel Coast, and its signature is Marina Beach: a pale strip of sand running for kilometres beside the Bay of Bengal. Two sluggish rivers, the Cooum and the Adyar, cut through the city to the sea. The English East India Company built Fort St George here in 1644, and the city of Madras grew up around it.","w":"A flat city along a long, straight sandy coast; harbour breakwaters in the north, two rivers entering the sea.","f":["Marina Beach is one of the longest urban beaches in the world.","The city's official name changed from Madras to Chennai in 1996.","Pulicat Lake, a large lagoon about 50 km north, draws flocks of flamingos in winter."],"q":{"q":"Which fort did the English build at Chennai in 1644?","a":"Fort St George","x":["Fort William","Fort St David"]}},"Q1070":{"t":"A sultan's city of kites on the Sabarmati","s":"Ahmedabad spreads across the flat plains of Gujarat on both banks of the Sabarmati, whose course through the city runs straight between long concrete embankments. Sultan Ahmed Shah founded it in 1411, and in 2017 it became the first Indian city on UNESCO's World Heritage list. Each January, at the Uttarayan festival, the sky above it fills with thousands of kites.","w":"A large flat city split by a river running straight between embankments; a planned capital with green avenues to the north.","f":["Gandhi lived at his Sabarmati Ashram on the riverbank from 1917 to 1930.","Gandhinagar, the planned capital of Gujarat, lies about 25 km to the north."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows through Ahmedabad?","a":"The Sabarmati","x":["The Narmada","The Tapi"]}},"Q4629":{"t":"Where most of the world's diamonds are cut","s":"Surat sits on a broad bend of the Tapi River, a few kilometres before it reaches the mudflats of the Arabian Sea coast. In Mughal times it was India's busiest port, and pilgrims sailed from here to Mecca. In the 1610s the English East India Company set up one of its earliest Indian trading posts here. Today the city's workshops cut and polish the great majority of the world's diamonds.","w":"A dense city on a wide river bend near a flat, muddy coast, with new districts spreading towards the sea.","f":["Around nine out of ten of the world's diamonds are cut and polished in Surat.","Surat is also one of India's largest centres of synthetic textiles."],"q":{"q":"Surat is famous for cutting and polishing what?","a":"Diamonds","x":["Emeralds","Pearls"]}},"Q1354":{"t":"A river-ringed megacity, once the capital of muslin","s":"Dhaka is almost an island: the Buriganga, Turag, Balu and Shitalakshya rivers wrap around it, and in the monsoon the fields beyond turn into sheets of water. Inside that ring the city is one of the most densely packed on Earth. In 1610 the Mughals made it the capital of Bengal, and its weavers produced muslin so fine it was likened to woven air.","w":"A very dense city on slightly higher ground ringed by rivers; in the monsoon, flooded fields all around.","f":["Dhaka is often called the rickshaw capital of the world, with hundreds of thousands of cycle rickshaws.","Under the Mughals the city was renamed Jahangirnagar, after Emperor Jahangir.","It has so many mosques that it is nicknamed the City of Mosques."],"q":{"q":"Mughal-era Dhaka was famed for which fine fabric?","a":"Muslin","x":["Silk brocade","Cashmere"]}},"Q376749":{"t":"Bangladesh's great port, where the hills meet the sea","s":"Where the flat delta of Bangladesh finally gives way to green hills rolling east towards Myanmar, Chattogram sits at the mouth of the Karnaphuli River. It is the country's main seaport. North of the city, look along the shore for a row of large ships deliberately run aground on the mudflats: one of the world's biggest shipbreaking coasts. Portuguese traders called this Porto Grande.","w":"A port city at a river mouth, green hills to the east; a line of beached ships on the coast to the north.","f":["In the yards of Sitakunda, north of the city, ocean-going ships are taken apart on the beach, much of it by hand.","In 2018 the official English spelling changed from Chittagong to Chattogram, closer to the Bengali."],"q":{"q":"What did Portuguese traders call Chattogram?","a":"Porto Grande","x":["Porto Pequeno","Porto Novo"]}},"Q3183":{"t":"A lonely capital on a lake-like river of black swans","s":"Perth is one of the most isolated big cities on Earth: Adelaide, the nearest city of similar size, is over 2,000 km away. The Swan River widens here into broad blue basins that look more like lakes, with the city on their banks. To the east the land rises abruptly at the long, straight line of the Darling Scarp; to the west lie white beaches and Rottnest Island.","w":"A city around wide, lake-like river basins; a straight escarpment to the east, white beaches and an island to the west.","f":["Dutch explorer Willem de Vlamingh named the Swan River in 1697, after its black swans.","Rottnest Island, off the coast, is home to the quokka, a small marsupial with a smiling face.","The Swan River Colony, which grew into Perth, was founded in 1829."],"q":{"q":"Who named the Swan River after its black swans in 1697?","a":"Willem de Vlamingh","x":["Abel Tasman","Dirk Hartog"]}},"Q37995":{"t":"A golden-pagoda city between delta rivers","s":"On a tongue of land where the Yangon and Bago rivers meet, some 30 km from the Andaman Sea, lies Yangon. West of it spreads the vast green Irrawaddy Delta, laced with channels. Two large lakes, Inya and Kandawgyi, glint among the northern districts. Near the second, on a low hill, stands the gilded Shwedagon Pagoda, the most sacred Buddhist site in Myanmar.","w":"A city on a point of land between converging rivers, with lakes in its northern half; a green delta stretching west.","f":["King Alaungpaya named the town Yangon, “end of strife”, in 1755.","Under British rule the city was known as Rangoon.","The Shwedagon's stupa is about 100 m tall and covered in gold plates."],"q":{"q":"What does the name “Yangon” mean?","a":"End of strife","x":["City of gold","River mouth"]}},"Q572089":{"t":"Karst peaks, a dammed red river and long lives","s":"Hechi spreads across a rugged corner of north-western Guangxi, where forested limestone peaks crowd so tightly that towns and fields squeeze into the narrow valleys between them. The Hongshui, or Red Water River, winds through deep gorges here and is stepped with big dams. This is a homeland of several minority peoples, among them the Zhuang, Yao, Mulao and Maonan.","w":"A sea of steep, forested limestone peaks with small towns and fields tucked into the valleys; a dammed river in gorges.","f":["Bama county, in Hechi, is famous for its unusually large number of people who live past 100.","Hechi has China's only autonomous counties for the Mulao and the Maonan peoples.","The Longtan Dam on the Hongshui River, in Hechi, feeds one of China's largest hydropower stations."],"q":{"q":"Bama county in Hechi is famous for its many…","a":"Centenarians","x":["Twins","Tea houses"]}},"Q68814":{"t":"Orchards, a delta and round earthen fortresses","s":"A green quilt of orchards and fields, known for lychees, pomelos and narcissus bulbs, covers the fertile plain around Zhangzhou. Here the Jiulong, or Nine Dragon River, widens eastwards into an estuary with Xiamen at its mouth. In 1567 the Ming lifted their ban on overseas trade at Yuegang, a harbour on this river, and Zhangzhou's goods flowed to Manila and beyond.","w":"A city on a green delta plain; the river widens east into an estuary towards Xiamen, hills to the west.","f":["In the hills west of the city stand Fujian tulou, giant round earthen houses listed by UNESCO.","Zhangzhou's narcissus bulbs are sent all over China for the Lunar New Year."],"q":{"q":"What are the giant round earthen houses in Zhangzhou's hills called?","a":"Tulou","x":["Siheyuan","Yaodong"]}},"Q205697":{"t":"Kolkata's twin across the Hooghly, with a giant banyan","s":"Haora, or Howrah, faces Kolkata across the Hooghly River, and from above the two read as one city split by a brown ribbon of water. Bridges stitch them together, and the wide railway yards on this side mark Howrah Station, opened in 1854 and still one of India's busiest. Downstream, the botanic garden at Shibpur shelters the Great Banyan, a single tree grown into a small forest.","w":"The west-bank half of a city split by a brown river, with bridges across and wide railway yards on this side.","f":["The Great Banyan's aerial roots have become thousands of trunks, and its canopy covers well over a hectare.","Howrah Bridge, opened in 1943, was built without nuts and bolts — it is held together by rivets."],"q":{"q":"What famous tree grows in the botanic garden at Howrah?","a":"The Great Banyan","x":["A giant sequoia","A baobab"]}},"Q38283":{"t":"A fishing village that became Japan's great port","s":"Yokohama lines the western shore of Tokyo Bay, and from above it flows into Tokyo without a visible border. Its waterfront is a jigsaw of piers, container terminals and reclaimed land, crossed by the long Bay Bridge. In 1859 this was a small fishing village chosen as a port for foreign trade; within decades it had become Japan's gateway to the world.","w":"A port city on the west side of a large bay, merging into Tokyo; piers, reclaimed land and a bridge over the harbour.","f":["Japan's first railway, opened in 1872, ran between Tokyo's Shimbashi and Yokohama.","Yokohama is Japan's second most populous city, after Tokyo.","Its Chinatown is the largest in Japan."],"q":{"q":"In which year was Yokohama opened to foreign trade?","a":"1859","x":["1603","1905"]}},"Q244898":{"t":"A ring-shaped city wrapped around Taipei","s":"New Taipei is shaped like a ring: it completely surrounds Taipei, filling the rest of the basin and spilling over green mountains to Taiwan's northern coast. Look for the mouth of the Tamsui River, where an old harbour town faces the sunset, and the rocky capes of the far north. With nearly four million people, it is the most populous city in Taiwan.","w":"A band of city and green hills encircling Taipei, reaching the sea at a river mouth and at rocky northern capes.","f":["The Spanish built the first Fort San Domingo at Tamsui in 1628.","Fuguijiao, the northernmost point of Taiwan's main island, lies within the city.","Yehliu, on its north coast, is famous for mushroom-shaped rocks carved by the sea."],"q":{"q":"Who built the first Fort San Domingo at Tamsui in 1628?","a":"The Spanish","x":["The Portuguese","The French"]}},"Q16520":{"t":"A port city wedged between steep hills and the sea","s":"Busan is squeezed into the gaps between steep green ridges and a ragged, bay-cut coastline at Korea's south-eastern tip. To the west, the Nakdong River ends in a delta of sandbars and reed beds that shelters migrating birds. Long bridges leap across the bays. On a clear day, Japan's island of Tsushima is visible about 50 km offshore.","w":"A long city threaded between steep ridges along an indented coast; a river delta with sandbars to the west.","f":["Busan is South Korea's largest port and one of the busiest container ports in the world.","In the Joseon era, Busan hosted a walled Japanese trading post known as the Waegwan."],"q":{"q":"Which Japanese island can be seen from Busan on clear days?","a":"Tsushima","x":["Okinawa","Sado"]}},"Q74881":{"t":"A peninsula-tip port between two seas","s":"Dalian occupies the hilly southern tip of the Liaodong Peninsula, where the Bohai Sea meets the Yellow Sea. Its coast is cut into bays, headlands and harbours. Russia founded the modern city in 1898 under the name Dalny, meaning “distant”, and laid it out with round plazas and streets radiating from them like spokes.","w":"A hilly peninsula tip with a jagged coast of bays and harbours; open sea on both sides.","f":["Dalian's harbour stays largely free of ice in winter, a rarity in northern China.","Lüshun, once known as Port Arthur, sits at the very tip of the peninsula."],"q":{"q":"Under what name did Russia found Dalian in 1898?","a":"Dalny","x":["Port Arthur","Nikolaevsk"]}},"Q11751":{"t":"Castle city on a plain of three parallel rivers","s":"Just west of Nagoya, three big rivers — the Kiso, Nagara and Ibi — run side by side to the sea like parallel strokes of a brush. The city itself spreads over the flat Nōbi Plain at the head of Ise Bay, and offshore to the south the airport sits on its own man-made island. Tokugawa Ieyasu had Nagoya Castle built in the 1610s, and the city grew up around it.","w":"A large city on a flat plain at the head of a bay; three parallel rivers to the west, an airport island to the south.","f":["Atsuta Shrine in Nagoya is said to house the Kusanagi sword, one of Japan's three imperial regalia.","Toyota's home city lies just east of Nagoya, making the region the heart of Japan's car industry."],"q":{"q":"Which legendary sword is said to be kept at Atsuta Shrine?","a":"Kusanagi","x":["Masamune","Muramasa"]}},"Q28729":{"t":"Where Vasco da Gama reached India, under coconut palms","s":"A thick green blanket of coconut palms half-hides Kozhikode, on the Malabar Coast between the Arabian Sea and the rising Western Ghats. As Calicut, it was the busy capital of the Zamorin kings and a hub of the pepper trade. In 1498 Vasco da Gama landed nearby, completing the first sea voyage from Europe to India.","w":"A city under a dense green canopy of palms along a straight coast, with mountains rising to the east.","f":["Calico, a plain cotton cloth, takes its name from Calicut.","Zheng He's Chinese treasure fleets called at Calicut in the early 15th century."],"q":{"q":"Which cloth takes its name from Calicut?","a":"Calico","x":["Muslin","Chintz"]}},"Q171771":{"t":"Temple City at the edge of the Mahanadi delta","s":"Hundreds of stone temples once stood in Bhubaneswar, and it is still called the Temple City. It sits on slightly raised red-earth land at the south-western edge of the Mahanadi delta, where green paddies stretch towards the Bay of Bengal. The modern town was planned from 1948 as the new capital of Odisha, right beside the ancient one.","w":"A city on low reddish uplands, with the green, river-laced delta spreading east and north towards the coast.","f":["The Lingaraja Temple, the city's greatest, dates largely from the 11th century.","The rock-cut caves of Udayagiri and Khandagiri, on the western edge, were carved for Jain monks over 2,000 years ago.","Chilika, India's largest coastal lagoon, lies about 100 km to the south-west."],"q":{"q":"What is Bhubaneswar's nickname?","a":"Temple City","x":["Pink City","Garden City"]}},"Q200054":{"t":"India's steel city, planned around a single works","s":"Jamshedpur grew around a steel plant founded in 1907 on the Chota Nagpur Plateau, where iron ore, coal and water lay close together. From above, look for the dark block of the steelworks near the meeting of the Subarnarekha and Kharkai rivers, with green planned neighbourhoods around it. The forested Dalma Hills to the north shelter wild elephants.","w":"A planned town where two rivers meet, a large dark industrial block at its centre, forested hills to the north.","f":["The city is named after Jamsetji Tata, who dreamed up the steelworks but died before it was built.","It was one of India's first planned industrial cities."],"q":{"q":"Jamshedpur is named after which industrialist?","a":"Jamsetji Tata","x":["G. D. Birla","Walchand Hirachand"]}},"Q5753":{"t":"Timur's blue-domed capital in a green Silk Road oasis","s":"Samarkand is a green oasis in the valley of the Zarafshan, whose name means “gold-scattering”, with brown mountain ridges rising to the south. On the city's northern edge lies Afrasiyab, a bare, empty mound that was the city for its first two thousand years. Timur made Samarkand his capital in 1370 and filled it with turquoise domes.","w":"A green oasis in a wide river valley, brown ridges to the south, a bare earthen mound on the northern edge.","f":["Timur's grandson Ulugh Beg built a great observatory here in the 1420s.","At around 2,700 years old, Samarkand is one of the oldest cities in Central Asia.","From the 8th century, Samarkand paper was prized across the Islamic world."],"q":{"q":"Which ruler made Samarkand his capital in 1370?","a":"Timur","x":["Genghis Khan","Babur"]}},"Q9310":{"t":"A small capital between steep mountains and a reef island","s":"Steep mountains rise close behind Dili, a low, compact city on a narrow strip of flat land along Timor's north coast. Across the strait, about 25 km to the north, lies Atauro Island, fringed with coral reefs. The Portuguese moved their colonial capital here in 1769, and Portuguese is still one of the country's official languages.","w":"A small city on a thin coastal plain, steep mountains behind, a long island across the water to the north.","f":["Surveys around Atauro have recorded some of the highest reef-fish diversity anywhere.","Timor lies in Wallacea, the transition zone between Asian and Australian wildlife."],"q":{"q":"When did the Portuguese move their Timor capital to Dili?","a":"1769","x":["1669","1869"]}},"Q37806":{"t":"A small harbour capital among reefs and lagoons","s":"Port Vila curls around a sheltered harbour on the south-west coast of Efate, one of about 80 islands of Vanuatu. From above, look for the turquoise of reefs and lagoons against dark green hills, and the small islets in the bay. Until 1980 these islands were the New Hebrides, ruled jointly by Britain and France — an unusual arrangement known as a condominium.","w":"A small town around a sheltered bay, turquoise reefs and lagoons along the shore, green hills behind.","f":["Vanuatu's national language, Bislama, is a creole largely based on English.","Land diving on Pentecost Island, to the north, is often described as the forerunner of bungee jumping."],"q":{"q":"What were Vanuatu's islands called before 1980?","a":"The New Hebrides","x":["New Caledonia","The Friendly Islands"]}},"Q9365":{"t":"A city named after a Monday market, below snowy peaks","s":"Dushanbe fills a broad green valley where the Varzob River tumbles out of the Hissar Range, whose snowy ridges rise just north of the city. A century ago this was a village known for its weekly bazaar, held on Mondays — and “dushanbe” is simply Tajik for Monday. East of here, the Vakhsh River is held back by some of the tallest dams on Earth.","w":"A green city in a wide valley at the foot of snowy mountains to the north, with a river flowing down through it.","f":["The Nurek Dam on the Vakhsh, about 75 km east, is around 300 m high.","From 1929 to 1961 the city was called Stalinabad."],"q":{"q":"What does “Dushanbe” mean in Tajik?","a":"Monday","x":["Market","Crossroads"]}},"Q1362":{"t":"A planned grid capital at the foot of the Margalla Hills","s":"Islamabad is one of the world's newer capitals, laid out from the 1960s on a neat grid of sectors, each with its own green belt. From above it looks tidy and leafy, pressed against the forested Margalla Hills — the first foothills of the Himalaya. Just south, its older twin Rawalpindi forms a much denser, busier sprawl.","w":"A neat grid of green sectors along the foot of wooded hills, with a lake to the east and a denser city to the south.","f":["Greek planner Constantinos Doxiadis drew up Islamabad's master plan.","Its sectors are named with a letter and a number, like squares on a chessboard.","Rawal Lake, on the eastern edge, is a reservoir that supplies water to both cities."],"q":{"q":"Who drew up Islamabad's master plan?","a":"Constantinos Doxiadis","x":["Le Corbusier","Edwin Lutyens"]}},"Q185453":{"t":"An ancient City of Saints on the hot Punjab plain","s":"An old Persian couplet lists Multan's four gifts: dust, heat, beggars and graveyards. The city lies on the flat, irrigated plain of southern Punjab, with the braided Chenab River a few kilometres to the west. It is one of the oldest continuously inhabited cities in South Asia, and its many Sufi shrines have earned it the name City of Saints.","w":"A large city on a flat plain of fields and canals, a wide braided river to the west, orchards around.","f":["The tomb of the Sufi saint Shah Rukn-e-Alam, built in the 14th century, is an octagonal brick landmark.","Multan is famous across Pakistan for its mangoes.","Blue-glazed tiles and pottery are a centuries-old local craft."],"q":{"q":"Multan is traditionally known as the City of…","a":"Saints","x":["Gardens","Lights"]}},"Q1640079":{"t":"A city of windcatchers on the banks of the Indus","s":"Rooftop windcatchers once bristled over the old houses of Hyderabad, funnelling breezes indoors. The city sits on a low limestone ridge on the east bank of the Indus, about 150 km upriver from Karachi. The river here is wide and braided, and a barrage beside the city spreads its water into canals that turn the desert green. The Kalhora ruler Ghulam Shah founded Hyderabad in 1768.","w":"A city on a low ridge beside a wide, braided river, with a barrage across it and green canal-fed fields around.","f":["Hyderabad is known as the city of bangles for its many glass-bangle workshops.","The Kotri Barrage beside the city diverts Indus water into irrigation canals."],"q":{"q":"What did old houses in Hyderabad, Sindh, carry on their roofs?","a":"Windcatchers","x":["Water wheels","Bell towers"]}},"Q1113311":{"t":"A frontier city at the foot of the Khyber Pass","s":"Just east of where the Khyber Pass climbs west towards Afghanistan, Peshawar sits in a green, fertile basin ringed by bare brown mountains. The Kabul River loops across the valley to the north. As Purushapura, it was the capital of the Kushan king Kanishka nearly two thousand years ago, and for centuries caravans rested here before or after the pass.","w":"A dense city on a green plain encircled by bare mountains, with a gap leading west into the hills.","f":["Kanishka raised a huge Buddhist stupa here, described by Chinese pilgrims as one of the tallest structures of its day.","The name of the old Qissa Khwani Bazaar means “storytellers' market”."],"q":{"q":"What was Peshawar called as the Kushan capital?","a":"Purushapura","x":["Pataliputra","Taxila"]}},"Q3037":{"t":"A valley that was once a lake, beneath the Himalaya","s":"Kathmandu fills an almost round valley about 1,400 m up, ringed by terraced green hills. Geologists confirm what legend says: the valley floor was once a lake, and its drained sediments made the soil here so fertile. On a clear day, a wall of snowy Himalayan peaks shines along the northern horizon.","w":"A round, densely built valley ringed by terraced hills; on clear days, a wall of snow peaks to the north.","f":["The valley holds seven groups of monuments on the UNESCO World Heritage list.","The city's name is traced to Kasthamandap, a wooden pavilion said to have been built from a single tree.","Everest, about 160 km to the east, is only some 2 km below your cruising altitude."],"q":{"q":"Legend and geology agree the Kathmandu Valley was once…","a":"A lake","x":["A desert","A glacier"]}},"Q36526":{"t":"A tropical capital on a surprisingly dry, brown coast","s":"Port Moresby wraps around a harbour on the south coast of New Guinea, and it looks surprisingly dry for the tropics: its hills turn brown and grassy in the dry season because they lie in a rain shadow. Coral reefs fringe the coast. Inland, the forested Owen Stanley Range rises to peaks of about 4,000 m. Captain John Moresby named the harbour in 1873 after his father.","w":"A town spread around a harbour and bays, brown grassy hills behind, reefs offshore and high mountains inland.","f":["Hanuabada, a village of houses on stilts over the harbour, is the traditional home of the Motu people.","Motu traders once sailed big lakatoi canoes along the coast on trading voyages called hiri."],"q":{"q":"Captain John Moresby named the harbour in 1873 after…","a":"His father","x":["His ship","Queen Victoria"]}},"Q40921":{"t":"A strip of town below Guadalcanal's green mountains","s":"Honiara is a thin strip of town along the north coast of Guadalcanal, squeezed between the sea and grassy foothills that climb towards jungle-covered mountains. The island's highest peaks, over 2,300 m, are the tallest in the Solomon Islands. Spanish navigator Álvaro de Mendaña reached these islands in 1568 and named them after King Solomon, hoping for gold.","w":"A narrow town along a coast, green hills and high mountains behind it, islands across the water to the north.","f":["The name Honiara is often said to come from a local phrase meaning “facing the south-east wind”.","Guadalcanal is named after a town in Andalusia, Spain, home of one of Mendaña's officers."],"q":{"q":"Who named the Solomon Islands in 1568?","a":"Álvaro de Mendaña","x":["Abel Tasman","James Cook"]}},"Q976":{"t":"Siberia's old university town, lace-carved in wood","s":"Dark taiga stretches to every horizon around Tomsk, on the high right bank of the Tom River. Cossacks founded a fort here in 1604 on the orders of Tsar Boris Godunov. In 1888 the city opened the first university in Siberia, and it is still a student town. Many old wooden houses are decorated with carvings as delicate as lace.","w":"A city on the high east bank of a winding river, surrounded by dark coniferous forest; white in winter.","f":["Tomsk State University, opened in 1888, was the first university in Asian Russia.","The city keeps hundreds of historic wooden houses with carved window frames."],"q":{"q":"When did Tomsk open Siberia's first university?","a":"1888","x":["1755","1918"]}},"Q7978":{"t":"Russia's easternmost town, at the mouth of a tundra river","s":"Where the Anadyr River opens into a wide estuary of the Bering Sea, the town of Anadyr sits on a point of land, surrounded by treeless tundra on permafrost that stays white for much of the year. It is the easternmost town in Russia, just two and a half degrees west of the 180th meridian, and about 10,000 people live here.","w":"A small cluster of town on a point of land at a wide estuary, surrounded by flat, treeless tundra.","f":["The town was founded in 1889 under the name Novo-Mariinsk.","The estuary is frozen for much of the year, from autumn well into early summer."],"q":{"q":"What was Anadyr called when it was founded in 1889?","a":"Novo-Mariinsk","x":["Novo-Arkhangelsk","Nikolaevsk"]}},"Q887":{"t":"An ironworks town in the Urals, on the edge of Asia","s":"Yekaterinburg spreads across the low, forested hills of the Middle Urals, just east of the watershed traditionally taken as the border between Europe and Asia. It began in 1723 as an ironworks, and the dam built to power it still holds back the City Pond in the centre. The city is named after Catherine I, wife of Peter the Great.","w":"A big city among low forested hills, a dammed pond on the river in its centre and many lakes around.","f":["Monuments marking the Europe–Asia border stand on the ridge less than 20 km west of the city.","From 1924 to 1991 the city was called Sverdlovsk."],"q":{"q":"Yekaterinburg is named after which empress?","a":"Catherine I","x":["Catherine II","Elizabeth"]}},"Q183002":{"t":"An oil town among bogs and lakes on the great Ob","s":"Surgut stands on the right bank of the Ob, in the middle of the West Siberian Plain — one of the flattest, boggiest places on Earth, speckled with countless lakes. Around it lie some of Russia's largest oilfields; look for straight roads linking square well pads across the swamp. At night, isolated gas flares glow in the darkness.","w":"A city on a broad river amid endless bogs and lakes, crossed by straight roads; flares glowing at night.","f":["Founded in 1594, Surgut is one of the oldest Russian towns in Siberia.","Its bridge over the Ob, opened in 2000, hangs from a single tall pylon."],"q":{"q":"In which year was Surgut founded?","a":"1594","x":["1794","1961"]}},"Q6014":{"t":"A silver town where ribbons of pine cross the steppe","s":"Barnaul stands on the high left bank of the Ob, where fields and steppe begin to replace the taiga. Look for a strange pattern south-west of the city: long, narrow strips of dark pine forest running straight across the farmland, like ribbons laid on a table. The town grew from 18th-century smelting works, and for decades it produced most of Russia's silver.","w":"A city on the high bank of a wide river; long, straight dark strips of pine forest running diagonally across the fields.","f":["The ribbon pine forests follow ancient channels carved by meltwater, and some stretch for hundreds of kilometres.","The first smelting works here was set up in the 1730s by the industrialist Akinfiy Demidov."],"q":{"q":"Which river does Barnaul stand on?","a":"The Ob","x":["The Irtysh","The Yenisei"]}},"Q883":{"t":"Siberia's biggest city, born where a railway crossed the Ob","s":"Novosibirsk straddles the Ob, and its reason to exist is a bridge: in 1893 the Trans-Siberian Railway crossed the river here, and a settlement sprang up beside the works. In barely a century it became the largest city in Siberia. South of the city, a dam has turned the Ob into a reservoir some 200 km long that locals call the Ob Sea.","w":"A big city on both banks of a wide river crossed by several bridges; a vast, long reservoir stretches south.","f":["The city was called Novonikolayevsk until 1926.","Akademgorodok, a science town founded in the 1950s, lies in the pine forest beside the Ob Sea."],"q":{"q":"What was Novosibirsk called when it was founded?","a":"Novonikolayevsk","x":["Nikolsk","Novoarkhangelsk"]}},"Q35381":{"t":"A harbour city with brand-new land built into the sea","s":"Colombo lines the flat west coast of Sri Lanka, its harbour sheltered behind long breakwaters. Right beside it, look for a large, geometric block of new land pushed out into the Indian Ocean — a whole district built on reclaimed sand. Portuguese, Dutch and British traders each held the port in turn. Inland, green hills rise towards the mountains at the island's heart.","w":"A flat coastal city, a harbour behind breakwaters and a geometric block of reclaimed land jutting into the sea.","f":["Sri Lanka's official capital, Sri Jayawardenepura Kotte, borders Colombo on its eastern side.","Colombo's harbour is man-made: work on its first great breakwater began in 1875."],"q":{"q":"Which city is Sri Lanka's official capital, next to Colombo?","a":"Sri Jayawardenepura Kotte","x":["Kandy","Anuradhapura"]}},"Q1467":{"t":"Where Spain's story in the Philippines began","s":"The airport sits on flat Mactan Island, and across a slim channel crossed by bridges lies Cebu City, on the east coast of a long, narrow, mountainous island. Magellan's expedition arrived here in 1521 and planted a cross that is still venerated. In 1565 Miguel López de Legazpi founded the first Spanish settlement in the archipelago on this shore.","w":"A city on the east coast of a long, hilly island, facing a flat island with an airport across a narrow channel.","f":["On Mactan in 1521, Magellan fell in a clash with the chief Lapu-Lapu, now honoured as a national hero.","Cebu is regarded as the oldest city in the Philippines."],"q":{"q":"In which year did Magellan's expedition plant a cross at Cebu?","a":"1521","x":["1492","1565"]}},"Q459787":{"t":"An old sugar port facing a mango island","s":"Iloilo spreads over flat land on the south coast of Panay, facing the hilly island of Guimaras across a strait only a few kilometres wide. Rivers and fish ponds break up the city's edges. When the port opened to world trade in 1855, sugar from nearby Negros poured through it, and grand merchant houses rose along its streets.","w":"A flat city on a coast facing a hilly island across a narrow strait, with rivers and fish ponds around.","f":["Guimaras, across the strait, is famous for its sweet mangoes.","Miag-ao Church, about 40 km to the west, is a UNESCO-listed Baroque church of yellow stone from the 18th century."],"q":{"q":"Iloilo faces which island across a narrow strait?","a":"Guimaras","x":["Mindoro","Bohol"]}},"Q1473":{"t":"A gulf city beneath the highest peak in the Philippines","s":"Davao spreads along the shore of the Davao Gulf in south-eastern Mindanao, with the green island of Samal just across the water. To the west rises Mount Apo, a volcanic peak of 2,954 m and the highest mountain in the Philippines. The land in between is carpeted with banana and fruit plantations, and Davao is famous for its durian.","w":"A long city on the shore of a wide gulf with an island just offshore; a large volcanic mountain to the west.","f":["Davao is the largest city in the Philippines by land area.","The rare Philippine eagle nests in the forests around Mount Apo."],"q":{"q":"Which is the highest mountain in the Philippines?","a":"Mount Apo","x":["Mayon","Mount Pulag"]}},"Q132732":{"t":"A town beside one of the world's largest man-made lakes","s":"Bratsk sits beside an enormous reservoir that fills the valleys of the Angara and its tributaries, reaching through the taiga in long, branching arms. The dam, finished in the 1960s, powered one of the largest hydroelectric stations of its time. The town itself is scattered in separate districts along the shores. A Cossack fort stood here as early as 1631.","w":"A huge, many-armed reservoir winding through dark taiga, with a dam and scattered districts of town along its shores.","f":["The Bratsk Reservoir holds about 170 km³ of water, one of the largest reservoirs in the world by volume.","When it was completed, the Bratsk hydroelectric station was the most powerful in the world."],"q":{"q":"A dam on which river created the Bratsk Reservoir?","a":"The Angara","x":["The Lena","The Yenisei"]}},"Q6576":{"t":"The gateway to Baikal, on the lake's only outflow","s":"Irkutsk lies on both banks of the Angara, the only river that flows out of Lake Baikal, some 60 km upstream. A dam just above the city has widened the river into a long reservoir. On a clear day, look south-east for Baikal itself: a deep-blue crescent framed by mountains, the deepest lake on Earth. Irkutsk began as a fort in 1661 and grew rich on tea and furs.","w":"A city on both banks of a wide river with a reservoir upstream; the long, deep-blue arc of Lake Baikal to the south-east.","f":["Lake Baikal is over 1,600 m deep and holds about a fifth of the world's unfrozen surface fresh water.","Many Decembrists exiled after 1825 settled in Irkutsk and shaped its cultural life."],"q":{"q":"How many rivers flow out of Lake Baikal?","a":"One","x":["Three","More than 300"]}},"Q919":{"t":"A Yenisei city where the river never freezes","s":"Krasnoyarsk lines both banks of the mighty Yenisei, with forested hills and rocky outcrops rising to the south. Upstream, a huge dam releases water that is warmer than the winter air, so the river through the city stays open and steams even at −30 °C. The city began in 1628 as a Cossack fort named after a red clay cliff on the riverbank.","w":"A large city on both banks of a very wide river, forested hills and rock pillars to the south; in winter, open water steaming.","f":["Stolby, a reserve of giant granite pillars in the taiga, begins right on the city's southern edge.","The Krasnoyarsk Dam, about 30 km upstream, is one of the most powerful hydroelectric stations in Russia.","The city's Communal Bridge appears on the Russian 10-rouble banknote."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Krasnoyarsk refer to?","a":"A red riverbank cliff","x":["A beautiful spring","A red forest"]}},"Q678299":{"t":"An Arctic outpost where the Yenisei meets the Kara Sea","s":"Where the vast gulf of the Yenisei opens into the Kara Sea, Dikson clings to bare, rocky shores. For much of the year both sea and land are white, and the settlement is little more than a scatter of buildings on the tundra. Only about a thousand people live here, far above the Arctic Circle, where the sun does not rise for weeks in midwinter.","w":"A tiny scatter of buildings on a rocky Arctic shore at the mouth of a huge, ice-covered gulf.","f":["Nordenskiöld named the harbour in 1875 after Oscar Dickson, the Swedish merchant who funded his expeditions.","The polar night here lasts about two and a half months.","Its port has served ships on the Northern Sea Route."],"q":{"q":"Oscar Dickson, after whom Dikson is named, came from which country?","a":"Sweden","x":["Norway","Denmark"]}},"Q53139":{"t":"A sunny Transbaikal city between low forested ridges","s":"Chita lies in a broad valley where the Chita River meets the Ingoda, framed by low ridges of larch and pine. Transbaikalia is one of Russia's sunniest regions, and in winter the ground often stays bare under a hard, dry frost. From 1827 many exiled Decembrists were held here, and the wives who followed them helped turn a small fort into a town.","w":"A city in a wide valley between low wooded ridges, where two rivers meet; a large lake on its western edge.","f":["The Trans-Siberian Railway passes through Chita on its way to the Pacific.","The sun shines here for well over 2,000 hours a year."],"q":{"q":"Which famous exiles were held in Chita from 1827?","a":"The Decembrists","x":["The Streltsy","The Old Believers"]}},"Q959":{"t":"The Trans-Siberian's end, on a hilly peninsula of bays","s":"Vladivostok climbs over the hills of a peninsula between two bays of the Sea of Japan, and its harbour, the Golden Horn, was named after Istanbul's. Look south for the long, fine line of a bridge to Russky Island. The city was founded in 1860, and it is the eastern end of the Trans-Siberian Railway, about 9,300 km from Moscow.","w":"A hilly city on a peninsula between two bays, a curved harbour in the middle and a long bridge south to an island.","f":["The Russky Bridge, opened in 2012, had the longest cable-stayed span in the world: 1,104 m.","The name joins the Russian words for “rule” and “east”."],"q":{"q":"Vladivostok's harbour is named after which bay in Istanbul?","a":"The Golden Horn","x":["The Silver Horn","The Golden Gate"]}},"Q1993488":{"t":"A remote river port near the delta of the Yana","s":"Nizhneyansk — “Lower Yana” — is a tiny settlement on the tundra near where the Yana River splits into a delta and meets the Laptev Sea. From above, the land around is freckled with countless thaw lakes, frozen and white for most of the year. Only a few hundred people remain in what was once a busier river port on this remote Arctic coast.","w":"A tiny settlement on flat tundra dotted with lakes, near a river breaking into many channels towards the sea.","f":["Far upstream on the Yana lies Verkhoyansk, one of the coldest inhabited places in the Northern Hemisphere.","On the lower Yana, archaeologists have found traces of people living in the Arctic about 32,000 years ago."],"q":{"q":"Which famously cold town lies upstream on the Yana?","a":"Verkhoyansk","x":["Oymyakon","Norilsk"]}},"Q6610":{"t":"The coldest big city, on a river kilometres wide","s":"January here averages near −40 °C, and Yakutsk is often called the coldest major city on Earth; ice fog can hang over its streets for days. It sits on the west bank of the Lena, which spreads into a maze of channels and islands several kilometres wide. The whole city stands on permafrost, so most buildings rest on piles driven deep into the frozen ground.","w":"A city on the west bank of an extremely wide, island-studded river, flat taiga all around; ice fog in winter.","f":["Cossacks led by Pyotr Beketov founded a fort on the Lena here in 1632.","The Lena Pillars, a UNESCO-listed wall of rock towers, stand beside the river about 200 km upstream."],"q":{"q":"Why do most buildings in Yakutsk stand on piles?","a":"Permafrost","x":["Floods","Earthquakes"]}},"Q500895":{"t":"A polar port beside the vast fan of the Lena Delta","s":"Just west of Tiksi spreads one of the great sights of the north: the Lena Delta, a fan of hundreds of channels and thousands of lakes, around 30,000 km² in all. The small Arctic port itself sits on a bay of the Laptev Sea, backed by bare, low mountains. For most of the year the sea here is frozen, and in midwinter the sun stays below the horizon for weeks.","w":"A small port on an icy bay; to the west, a gigantic delta fanning out into countless channels and lakes.","f":["The Lena Delta is the largest delta in the Arctic and a protected nature reserve.","Tiksi has served ships on the Northern Sea Route since the 1930s."],"q":{"q":"Which river's vast delta lies just west of Tiksi?","a":"The Lena","x":["The Yenisei","The Kolyma"]}},"Q7968":{"t":"A port between two bays of the Sea of Okhotsk","s":"Two bays of the Sea of Okhotsk, Nagaev and Gertner, pinch Magadan onto a neck of land, with bare, rounded mountains rising behind it. In winter the sea freezes and the coast turns a uniform white. For most travellers this is where the Kolyma region begins: a vast land of mountains, gold-bearing rivers and larch forest stretching inland.","w":"A city on a narrow neck of land between two bays, bare hills behind; sea ice along the coast in winter.","f":["The Kolyma Highway links Magadan with Yakutsk, about 2,000 km away.","The Kolyma basin inland has been one of Russia's main gold-mining regions since the 1930s."],"q":{"q":"Which sea does Magadan stand on?","a":"The Sea of Okhotsk","x":["The Bering Sea","The Sea of Japan"]}},"Q230628":{"t":"A mining city beside a giant open-pit crater","s":"Erdenet lies among rolling green hills in northern Mongolia, and next to it you can't miss the mine: a vast, terraced grey pit and a broad, pale tailings pond. The town was built from 1974 to serve one of the largest copper deposits in Asia. Its name means roughly “with treasure”.","w":"A small city in green steppe hills, beside a huge terraced grey pit and a large pale tailings pond.","f":["Besides copper, the Erdenet mine produces molybdenum, a metal used to harden steel.","Copper concentrate is one of Mongolia's main exports."],"q":{"q":"What is mainly mined at Erdenet?","a":"Copper","x":["Coal","Gold"]}},"Q23430":{"t":"The world's coldest capital, in a steppe valley","s":"Ulaanbaatar stretches east–west along the valley of the Tuul River, with the forested ridge of Bogd Khan Mountain rising to the south and pale ger districts spilling up the hills to the north. Around it the treeless steppe rolls to the horizon. With an average yearly temperature below freezing, it is the coldest national capital on Earth.","w":"A long city in a wide river valley, a dark forested mountain to the south, bare rolling steppe all around.","f":["The city began in 1639 as a moving monastery camp and only settled on this spot in 1778.","Bogd Khan Mountain has been protected since the 18th century, making it one of the oldest nature reserves in the world.","The name means “red hero”; the city took it in 1924."],"q":{"q":"Among the world's capitals, Ulaanbaatar is the…","a":"Coldest","x":["Highest","Driest"]}},"Q695360":{"t":"A tiny Koryak capital near the Okhotsk shore","s":"A few kilometres from the Sea of Okhotsk, on Kamchatka's remote north-west coast, Palana sits in the valley of the Palana River. Around it lies a wild country of tundra, dwarf pine and snow-streaked ridges rising towards the Sredinny Range. It is the administrative centre of the Koryak district, homeland of the Koryak people, reindeer herders and sea hunters.","w":"A small settlement in a river valley near the sea, amid tundra and low mountains; snow lingers on the ridges into summer.","f":["Palana was the capital of the Koryak Autonomous Okrug until it merged into Kamchatka Krai in 2007.","The settlement is so remote that most visitors arrive by plane."],"q":{"q":"Palana lies in the homeland of which people?","a":"The Koryaks","x":["The Chukchi","The Nenets"]}},"Q7951":{"t":"A harbour city at the foot of two smoking volcanoes","s":"Petropavlovsk-Kamchatsky lines the shore of Avacha Bay, one of the finest natural harbours in the world, and behind it rise two giant cones: Koryaksky, about 3,456 m, and the often steaming Avachinsky. No road links the city to the rest of Russia; everything comes by sea or air. Vitus Bering founded it in 1740 and named it after his two ships.","w":"A city along a wide, nearly enclosed bay, with two big snow-streaked volcanic cones right behind it.","f":["Bering's ships were the St Peter and the St Paul — hence the city's name.","Kamchatka has around 30 active volcanoes, and several can be seen from the city on a clear day."],"q":{"q":"Bering named the city after his two ships: the St Peter and the…","a":"St Paul","x":["St Andrew","St Nicholas"]}},"Q7253":{"t":"Home of rendang, between the ocean and the volcanoes","s":"Roofs that curve up like buffalo horns are the mark of the Minangkabau people, and Padang is their main city. It lies on a narrow coastal plain in West Sumatra, pressed between the Indian Ocean and the steep green wall of the Barisan Mountains, with volcanoes rising inland. Minangkabau cooking, from rendang to fiery curries, is served in “Padang restaurants” all over Indonesia.","w":"A city on a thin coastal plain, steep green mountains behind, the open ocean in front and islands offshore.","f":["The Minangkabau are among the largest matrilineal societies in the world: land passes down through the women.","Legend says the name Minangkabau means “victorious buffalo”."],"q":{"q":"Among the Minangkabau, family land passes down through…","a":"The mothers","x":["The fathers","The eldest sons"]}},"Q9361":{"t":"A leafy grid city beneath a wall of snowy peaks","s":"A wall of snowy peaks above 4,000 m rises immediately south of Bishkek: the Kyrgyz Ala-Too range. The city lies on the flat floor of the Chuy Valley, laid out on a green grid of tree-lined streets. It grew from Pishpek, a clay fortress built by the Khanate of Kokand in 1825.","w":"A green, gridded city on a flat valley floor, with a steep wall of snowy mountains just to the south.","f":["From 1926 to 1991 the city was called Frunze.","Ala Archa National Park, a gorge of glaciers and juniper, begins only about 40 km south of the centre.","The Chuy River, north of the city, forms part of the border with Kazakhstan."],"q":{"q":"What was Bishkek called from 1926 to 1991?","a":"Frunze","x":["Pishpek","Verny"]}},"Q532560":{"t":"Where the Ming Great Wall ends, in the Gobi","s":"Jiayuguan stands at the narrowest point of the Hexi Corridor, a gap between the snowy Qilian Mountains to the south and bare desert hills to the north. This is where the Ming dynasty ended its Great Wall: the fortress of Jiayu Pass, first built in 1372, guarded the road west. Around the modern steel town, green oasis fields fade quickly into stony gobi.","w":"A small city in a narrow corridor of desert, snow-capped mountains to the south, stony plains all around.","f":["Jiayu Pass was nicknamed the “First and Greatest Pass Under Heaven”.","Snowmelt from the Qilian Mountains, which rise above 5,000 m, waters the oases of the Hexi Corridor."],"q":{"q":"Jiayu Pass marks the western end of which Great Wall?","a":"The Ming wall","x":["The Qin wall","The Han wall"]}},"Q69060":{"t":"A high valley city on the edge of the Tibetan Plateau","s":"Xining lies about 2,200 m up, stretched along the narrow valley of the Huangshui River between bare, deeply gullied hills. This is the threshold of the Tibetan Plateau: west of here the land climbs to Qinghai Lake and the high grasslands. For centuries the city was a market where Chinese tea was traded for Tibetan horses. Its name means “western peace”.","w":"A long city squeezed into a narrow valley between bare, eroded hills, with higher mountains to the west.","f":["Kumbum Monastery, about 25 km south-west, marks the birthplace of Tsongkhapa, founder of the Gelug school.","Qinghai Lake, about 150 km to the west, is the largest lake in China.","The railway to Lhasa, crossing passes over 5,000 m high, begins its climb here."],"q":{"q":"Xining is the capital of which province?","a":"Qinghai","x":["Gansu","Sichuan"]}},"Q189633":{"t":"A city among limestone towers, on the 20-yuan note","s":"Guilin is scattered among hundreds of steep, rounded limestone towers rising straight out of flat green fields, and from above they look like a field of giant molehills. The Li River slips south between them towards Yangshuo, through one of the most painted landscapes in China. The name means “forest of sweet osmanthus”, after the trees that scent the city every autumn.","w":"A city among countless steep, rounded limestone peaks, with a river winding south between them through green fields.","f":["A Li River scene near Guilin appears on the back of the 20-yuan banknote.","The Lingqu Canal, dug around 214 BC north of Guilin, is one of the oldest canals in the world.","A Chinese saying claims Guilin's scenery is the finest under heaven."],"q":{"q":"A Li River scene near Guilin appears on which banknote?","a":"The 20-yuan note","x":["The 5-yuan note","The 100-yuan note"]}},"Q360178":{"t":"Coal, tofu and solar panels floating on mine lakes","s":"Huainan stretches along the south bank of the Huai River, the traditional line between northern and southern China. Coal has been mined here for over a century, and where the ground has sunk above old workings it has flooded into shallow lakes; some now carry dark grids of floating solar panels. Legend credits Liu An, a prince of Huainan in the 2nd century BC, with inventing tofu.","w":"An industrial city along the south bank of a big river, with scattered lakes, some covered in dark grids of solar panels.","f":["A battle at the nearby Fei River in 383 gave Chinese the idiom “every bush and tree looks like a soldier”.","Together with the Qinling Mountains, the Huai River is taken as the boundary between north and south China."],"q":{"q":"Legend credits a prince of Huainan with inventing which food?","a":"Tofu","x":["Noodles","Dumplings"]}},"Q59100":{"t":"Teochew heartland on the delta of the Han River","s":"Shantou spreads over the flat delta of the Han River where it meets the South China Sea, wrapped around a sheltered harbour lagoon. The land nearby is a mosaic of fish ponds and paddies, and the big island of Nan'ao lies offshore to the east. This is the heart of Chaoshan, or Teochew, country, famous for its cooking and its elaborate gongfu tea ritual.","w":"A coastal city on a flat river delta around a sheltered harbour, fish ponds around it and a large island to the east.","f":["In 1980 Shantou became one of China's first four Special Economic Zones.","Many Chinese communities in Thailand and across South-East Asia trace their roots to Teochew emigrants from this region."],"q":{"q":"What did Western traders call Shantou?","a":"Swatow","x":["Amoy","Chefoo"]}},"Q14501":{"t":"An oil island among Borneo's mangrove deltas","s":"Tarakan sits on a small island just off the north-east coast of Borneo, where several rivers break into a maze of muddy channels and mangrove forest. From above, the island looks like a green patch ringed by brown water. Oil has been pumped here since the early 1900s, and its wells and refinery made the island a busy port of the Dutch East Indies.","w":"A small green island off a coast of muddy river channels and dark mangroves; the town on its south-western shore.","f":["A mangrove reserve inside the city is home to proboscis monkeys, famous for their long noses.","Tarakan is the largest city of North Kalimantan, a province created in 2012."],"q":{"q":"Which long-nosed monkey lives in Tarakan's mangrove reserve?","a":"The proboscis monkey","x":["The golden snub-nosed monkey","The mandrill"]}},"Q11025":{"t":"A port city climbing from the coast into volcanic hills","s":"Semarang has two levels: a flat lower town along the Java Sea coast and a greener upper town climbing the hills to the south, with the volcano Ungaran rising beyond. The old Dutch quarter near the harbour is nicknamed Little Netherlands. Local tradition says the Chinese admiral Zheng He landed here in the early 15th century, and the Sam Poo Kong temple honours him.","w":"A city on a flat coast that climbs onto green hills to the south; a volcano behind, a harbour in front.","f":["Lawang Sewu, built in 1907 as a Dutch railway office, is called the “Thousand Doors” for its many doors and windows.","Semarang is the capital of Central Java."],"q":{"q":"Semarang's Sam Poo Kong temple honours which Chinese admiral?","a":"Zheng He","x":["Koxinga","Shi Lang"]}},"Q8131":{"t":"Srivijaya's old capital on the wide brown Musi","s":"Palembang straddles the broad, brown Musi River, far inland across the flat swamps and plantations of southern Sumatra. Ships still come upriver from the sea, a journey of close to 100 km. More than a thousand years ago this was the heart of Srivijaya, a maritime empire that controlled trade through the Strait of Malacca. Look for the long bridge joining the two halves of the city.","w":"A city on both banks of a wide brown river winding through flat swampy lowland, with a long bridge across.","f":["The Ampera Bridge, opened in 1965, was built with a centre span that lifted for ships; it was later fixed in place.","Wooden houses floating on rafts still line parts of the riverbank.","The Chinese pilgrim Yijing studied Sanskrit in Srivijaya in the late 7th century."],"q":{"q":"Palembang was the heart of which maritime empire?","a":"Srivijaya","x":["Majapahit","Mataram"]}},"Q14182":{"t":"The city of a thousand rivers and floating markets","s":"Banjarmasin lies in the flat, swampy lowlands of southern Borneo, where the Martapura River joins the wide Barito not far from the sea. Rivers and canals thread through every district, earning it the name City of a Thousand Rivers. At dawn, traders in small boats gather on the water for its famous floating markets, selling fruit and vegetables straight from their canoes.","w":"A flat city laced with rivers and canals beside a wide brown river, surrounded by swampy lowland and rice fields.","f":["The Banjar Sultanate, founded in the 1520s, ruled from here for more than three centuries.","Much of the city lies barely above the level of high tide."],"q":{"q":"Banjarmasin is famous for its…","a":"Floating markets","x":["Stilt temples","Salt pans"]}},"Q14634":{"t":"A seafaring city facing a scatter of coral islands","s":"Makassar lines the flat west coast of South Sulawesi, looking out over the Spermonde Archipelago, a scatter of tiny coral islands ringed with turquoise reefs. Inland to the north-east rise the jagged limestone towers of Maros. For centuries this was the great trading port of the Gowa sultanate, and its sailors ranged across the eastern seas.","w":"A city on a flat coast facing dozens of tiny coral islands; jagged limestone hills inland to the north-east.","f":["From the 18th century, Makassan sailors voyaged each year to northern Australia to gather sea cucumbers.","Fort Rotterdam in the city centre was rebuilt by the Dutch in the 1670s on the site of a Gowa fortress."],"q":{"q":"Makassan sailors once voyaged to northern Australia to collect…","a":"Sea cucumbers","x":["Pearls","Sandalwood"]}},"Q5869":{"t":"A city in a high Himalayan valley, 3,650 m up","s":"Even at 3,650 m above sea level, Lhasa is overlooked by bare brown mountains that rise another 1,500 m or more. The city lies in the flat valley of the Kyi Chu, a tributary of the Yarlung Tsangpo. The air is thin and dry, and sunshine is so plentiful that Lhasa is nicknamed the City of Sunlight. Its name means “place of the gods”.","w":"A green, flat-floored river valley hemmed in by steep bare mountains, with the city spread along its northern side.","f":["The Potala Palace, on a hill in the city, rises 13 storeys and is a UNESCO World Heritage site.","The Jokhang Temple in the old centre dates back to the 7th century.","Lhasa gets about 3,000 hours of sunshine a year."],"q":{"q":"Roughly how high above sea level does Lhasa lie?","a":"About 3,650 m","x":["About 1,650 m","About 5,650 m"]}},"Q650585":{"t":"The melon oasis at the eastern end of the Tian Shan","s":"After days crossing the Gobi, Silk Road caravans from Gansu reached their first great oasis here. Hami is a green patch at the southern foot of the Karlik Mountains, the snowy eastern tail of the Tian Shan, with vast gravel desert stretching away on every side. Its name travels the world on the famous Hami melon, sweet and crisp.","w":"A green oasis at the foot of snowy mountains, surrounded by vast grey gravel desert; large solar farms nearby.","f":["Hami melons were sent as tribute to the Qing emperors from the late 17th century.","Its central district, Yizhou, carries the oasis's Tang-era name; in Uyghur the oasis is called Kumul."],"q":{"q":"Which fruit is named after Hami?","a":"A melon","x":["A grape","A pomegranate"]}},"Q235389":{"t":"A jade oasis on the edge of the Taklamakan","s":"Two braided rivers named after jade, the White Jade and the Black Jade, run down from the snowy Kunlun Mountains on either side of Hotan, then join and push north into the sand sea of the Taklamakan. Between them lies the green oasis. For thousands of years people have picked nephrite jade from the rivers' gravel beds.","w":"A green oasis between two braided rivers, snowy mountains to the south and an endless sea of dunes to the north.","f":["Hotan jade was carried east to the courts of China's rulers for thousands of years.","A legend recorded by the monk Xuanzang says silk reached Khotan when a princess hid silkworm eggs in her headdress.","The Hotan River crosses the Taklamakan only in summer, when snowmelt fills it."],"q":{"q":"Which stone is found in the rivers of Hotan?","a":"Jade","x":["Turquoise","Lapis lazuli"]}},"Q170521":{"t":"A Silk Road crossroads at the foot of the Pamirs","s":"Three great ranges — the Tian Shan, the Pamirs and the Kunlun — close in around the desert at Kashgar, a green oasis at the far western tip of the Tarim Basin. For caravans it was the great junction: routes skirting the Taklamakan to north and south met here before climbing to the high passes westward. On clear days, the snow domes of Kongur and Muztagh Ata, both over 7,500 m, rise to the south-west.","w":"A green oasis at the western end of a desert basin, walls of snowy mountains rising to the west, north and south.","f":["Mahmud al-Kashgari, from this region, compiled the first great dictionary of the Turkic languages in the 1070s.","Kashgar's Sunday livestock market has drawn traders from across the region for generations."],"q":{"q":"Which 11th-century scholar from here compiled a great Turkic dictionary?","a":"Mahmud al-Kashgari","x":["Al-Biruni","Ibn Sina"]}},"Q211243":{"t":"A green plain between a mountain wall and the Yellow River","s":"A dark, jagged mountain wall, the Helan range, shields Yinchuan from the sands of the Tengger Desert to the west, while the Yellow River runs to the east. Between them lies a strip of green, irrigated plain, watered by canals first dug over 2,000 years ago. Its lakes and paddies earned the area the name “Jiangnan beyond the frontier”.","w":"A green, lake-dotted plain between a long, dark mountain range to the west and a broad river to the east; desert beyond.","f":["From the 11th to the 13th century Yinchuan was the capital of the Western Xia kingdom.","The royal tombs of the Western Xia stand at the foot of the Helan Mountains, west of the city.","Ningxia's goji berries are famous across China."],"q":{"q":"Yinchuan was the capital of which medieval kingdom?","a":"Western Xia","x":["Liao","Jin"]}},"Q362865":{"t":"An old coal town in the hills of the Hunan border","s":"Coal built Pingxiang, a city in a valley of green hills on Jiangxi's western border, where the Luoxiao Mountains divide the province from Hunan. The Anyuan mine, opened in 1898, fed one of China's first modern ironworks. To the south-east rises Wugong Mountain, whose rounded summits are covered in grassland rather than forest.","w":"A city in a valley among green, rolling hills, with higher mountains along the border to the west and south.","f":["Coke from Anyuan fed the Hanyang Iron Works in Wuhan, one of China's first modern steel plants.","Wugong Mountain's summit grasslands, over 1,900 m up, turn golden in autumn."],"q":{"q":"What did Pingxiang's Anyuan mine, opened in 1898, produce?","a":"Coal","x":["Tin","Tungsten"]}},"Q38234":{"t":"Japan's window to Europe, round a narrow harbour","s":"Nagasaki wraps around a long, narrow harbour, with houses climbing steep green hills on both sides like the seats of an amphitheatre. Portuguese ships made it a port in 1571. For more than two centuries of Japan's isolation, a small artificial island here, Dejima, was the country's only regular window to Europe. Offshore to the south-west lies a scatter of islands.","w":"A long, narrow harbour like a fjord, the city climbing steep green hills on both sides; islands to the south-west.","f":["From 1641 to 1859, Dutch traders on Dejima were the only Europeans allowed to trade with Japan.","Castella, a sponge cake still sold all over Nagasaki, arrived with Portuguese traders in the 16th century."],"q":{"q":"Which Europeans traded at Dejima from 1641?","a":"The Dutch","x":["The Portuguese","The English"]}},"Q205922":{"t":"The crane city on the marshes of the Nen River","s":"Qiqihar spreads across the flat Songnen Plain beside the Nen River, in a landscape of reed marshes and shallow lakes that shine like scattered mirrors. South-east of the city lies Zhalong, a vast wetland where red-crowned cranes nest in summer; it became one of China's first nature reserves for cranes. The town began as a Qing fortress in 1691.","w":"A city beside a braided river on a flat plain of reed marsh and shallow lakes; white and frozen in winter.","f":["Zhalong's reed marshes cover more than 2,000 km².","In Chinese art, the red-crowned crane is a symbol of long life."],"q":{"q":"Which bird is Zhalong wetland near Qiqihar famous for?","a":"The red-crowned crane","x":["The mute swan","The mandarin duck"]}},"Q200016":{"t":"Where the Eastern Ghats run down to the Bay of Bengal","s":"Visakhapatnam, or Vizag, sits where green hills of the Eastern Ghats come right down to the Bay of Bengal. Its deep harbour is tucked behind a rocky headland called Dolphin's Nose, and long sandy beaches curve away to the north. The city is named after Visakha, a Hindu god of valour. Inland, the hills climb towards the Araku Valley and its coffee plantations.","w":"A coastal city between green hills and the sea, a harbour behind a rocky headland, long beaches to the north.","f":["Visakhapatnam is one of India's largest ports.","Coffee from the Araku Valley, grown largely by tribal farmers, has become known worldwide."],"q":{"q":"Vizag's harbour is sheltered by a headland called…","a":"Dolphin's Nose","x":["Elephant's Trunk","Lion's Head"]}},"Q38807":{"t":"A rainy harbour capital ringed by coral reefs","s":"Look for a pale line of surf on the reef and dark, rain-soaked mountains behind it: that is Suva, on a hilly peninsula on the south-east coast of Viti Levu, Fiji's main island. Clouds gather here often, and it is far wetter than Fiji's western side. Suva replaced Levuka as the capital in 1882.","w":"A city on a hilly peninsula between two bays, a pale line of reef offshore, green cloud-capped mountains behind.","f":["Suva gets around 3,000 mm of rain a year — several times as much as London.","Levuka, the old capital on the island of Ovalau, is a UNESCO World Heritage site."],"q":{"q":"Which town was Fiji's capital before Suva?","a":"Levuka","x":["Nadi","Lautoka"]}},"Q1520":{"t":"A capital of many names on the open steppe","s":"This city rises from a flat, almost treeless steppe on the banks of the winding Ishim River, and in winter everything around it turns white under a hard frost. It began as the fort of Akmolinsk in 1830 and has changed its name more often than almost any capital: Tselinograd, Akmola, Astana, Nur-Sultan and, since 2022, Astana again. Kazakhstan moved its capital here from Almaty in 1997.","w":"A compact city on a flat, treeless steppe, with a winding river through it; white and frozen in winter.","f":["Winter nights here often drop below −30 °C, making it one of the coldest capitals in the world.","The Ishim flows on north into Russia, where it joins the Irtysh."],"q":{"q":"What was the city called from 1961 to 1992?","a":"Tselinograd","x":["Akmolinsk","Semipalatinsk"]}},"Q484706":{"t":"A coal city on the steppe, named after a shrub","s":"Qaraghandy, better known as Karaganda, sprawls across the bare Kazakh steppe, and around it you can spot the cones of old spoil heaps left by its coal mines. Coal was found here in 1833 — by a shepherd boy, local tradition says — and the modern city grew fast from the 1930s. Its name comes from karagan, the hardy caragana shrub that grows across the steppe.","w":"A sprawling city on flat, bare steppe, dotted with conical spoil heaps; a steel town and reservoir to the north-west.","f":["The Karaganda coal basin is one of the largest in Central Asia.","The steel town of Temirtau, about 30 km north-west, grew up beside a reservoir on the Nura River."],"q":{"q":"Karaganda is named after which steppe plant?","a":"The caragana shrub","x":["Wormwood","Feather grass"]}},"Q35493":{"t":"The apple city beneath a wall of snowy peaks","s":"Almaty spreads across a gently sloping plain at the very foot of the Trans-Ili Alatau, whose snowy peaks rise to nearly 5,000 m just south of the city — a wall of white hanging over the rooftops. The forests on these slopes are home to the wild apple from which the world's cultivated apples descend, and the city's name recalls the fruit.","w":"A big city on a slope running up to a steep wall of snowy mountains to the south; flat steppe to the north.","f":["The city began in 1854 as the Russian fort of Verny.","Almaty was the capital of Kazakhstan until 1997.","Medeu, a skating rink in a gorge above the city, lies at about 1,700 m."],"q":{"q":"Wild apples in the mountains near Almaty are the ancestors of…","a":"The cultivated apple","x":["The pear","The quince"]}},"Q48403":{"t":"The city of the Golden Temple and its pool of nectar","s":"At the heart of Amritsar, the Golden Temple, holiest shrine of the Sikh faith, stands in the middle of a sacred pool. Around the city, the flat, fertile plain of Punjab is a patchwork of wheat and rice fields laced with canals. Amritsar grew up around that pool in the 1570s under Guru Ram Das, and its name means “pool of nectar”.","w":"A dense city on a flat green plain of fields and canals stretching to the horizon in every direction.","f":["The Golden Temple's community kitchen serves free meals to tens of thousands of visitors every day.","The temple's upper storeys were covered in gold leaf in the early 19th century under Maharaja Ranjit Singh."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Amritsar mean?","a":"Pool of nectar","x":["City of gold","Holy river"]}},"Q79980":{"t":"A holy city on a crescent bend of the Ganges","s":"Varanasi lines the west bank of the Ganges where the river curves in a great crescent and, unusually, flows northwards. The contrast is striking from above: the city packs the western shore with stone steps, or ghats, while the eastern bank stays an almost empty strip of sand. It is often called one of the oldest continuously inhabited cities in the world.","w":"A dense city on the west side of a crescent-shaped bend of a wide river, with a bare sandy bank opposite.","f":["At Sarnath, about 10 km north-east, the Buddha is said to have given his first sermon.","More than 80 ghats line the river for several kilometres."],"q":{"q":"Who gave his first sermon at Sarnath, near Varanasi?","a":"The Buddha","x":["Mahavira","Guru Nanak"]}},"Q202139":{"t":"Where India first dug for coal","s":"Asansol spreads over rolling red-earth country between the Damodar and Ajay rivers, on the edge of the Chota Nagpur Plateau. This is the Raniganj coalfield, where commercial coal mining in India began in 1774, and the land around is dotted with pits, spoil heaps and flooded workings. To the west, dams on the Damodar and Barakar hold back large reservoirs.","w":"An industrial city on rolling reddish land dotted with pits and ponds, a river to the south, reservoirs to the west.","f":["The Damodar was once called the “Sorrow of Bengal” for its floods, until dams were built on it in the 1950s.","The Grand Trunk Road, one of Asia's oldest highways, runs through the city."],"q":{"q":"When did commercial coal mining in India begin, in the Raniganj field?","a":"1774","x":["1674","1874"]}},"Q242144":{"t":"A steel town built with Soviet help on a rice plain","s":"Bhilai rose in the 1950s around one of India's largest steelworks, built with Soviet help on the flat plain of Chhattisgarh. From above, look for the vast grey rectangle of the plant with its cooling ponds, and a neatly planned town beside it. All around stretches a quilt of paddy fields: Chhattisgarh is often called the rice bowl of India.","w":"A huge grey industrial complex with ponds beside a planned town, set in a flat patchwork of green paddy fields.","f":["The Bhilai plant has long been India's main maker of steel rails.","Bhilai and neighbouring Durg have grown together into a single urban area."],"q":{"q":"Bhilai's steelworks was built in the 1950s with help from…","a":"The Soviet Union","x":["Britain","West Germany"]}},"Q80989":{"t":"The city of lakes, once ruled by queens","s":"Bhopal wraps around two large lakes on the hilly Malwa Plateau, and the bigger one, the Upper Lake, dominates the view from above. Tradition says Raja Bhoj created it in the 11th century by damming a river. For more than a century from 1819, Bhopal was ruled by a line of four women, the Begums — a rarity in Indian history.","w":"A city on low hills around two large lakes, the western one much bigger; plateau and farmland all around.","f":["The Buddhist stupa of Sanchi, a UNESCO site, lies about 45 km to the north-east.","The rock shelters of Bhimbetka, south-east of the city, hold paintings thousands of years old."],"q":{"q":"Bhopal's Upper Lake is traditionally credited to which king?","a":"Raja Bhoj","x":["Ashoka","Akbar"]}},"Q228405":{"t":"Temple city of the Tamils, among granite hills","s":"Madurai spreads along the Vaigai River on a flat plain studded with bare granite hills; one, Yanaimalai, is said to look like a resting elephant. At the city's heart stands the Meenakshi Temple, whose colourful gateway towers rise above the streets. Madurai has been a centre of Tamil culture for over two thousand years, and tradition places the ancient Tamil poets' academies here.","w":"A city on a sandy river amid a flat plain dotted with isolated bare rocky hills, one long and elephant-shaped.","f":["The Meenakshi Temple has 14 gateway towers; the tallest is about 50 m high.","Madurai's jasmine is so prized that it carries a geographical indication tag."],"q":{"q":"Madurai's great temple is dedicated to the goddess…","a":"Meenakshi","x":["Kali","Lakshmi"]}},"Q9885":{"t":"A mill city by the great gap in the Western Ghats","s":"The one big gap in the Western Ghats opens just west of Coimbatore: the Palakkad Gap, some 30–40 km wide, lets winds and roads through to Kerala. Elsewhere the city's plain is hemmed in by mountains, with the Nilgiri Hills rising steeply to the north. Its many cotton mills earned Coimbatore the nickname Manchester of South India.","w":"A large city on a plain, a massive mountain block to the north, steep ranges to the west with a wide gap in them.","f":["The Palakkad Gap is the main break in the Western Ghats, which otherwise run unbroken for over 1,000 km.","Coimbatore is famous across India for its wet grinders, which carry a geographical indication tag."],"q":{"q":"Which gap in the Western Ghats lies just west of Coimbatore?","a":"The Palakkad Gap","x":["The Bhor Ghat","The Thal Ghat"]}},"Q9326":{"t":"A low, leafy capital on a great bend of the Mekong","s":"Vientiane lies low and leafy along a broad bend of the Mekong, and across the water you are looking at Thailand. In the dry season the river shrinks to reveal long sandbars. King Setthathirath moved the capital of the Lan Xang kingdom here from Luang Prabang in 1560. The name is usually translated as “city of sandalwood”.","w":"A low, green city along a wide river bend with sandbars; the far bank belongs to another country.","f":["The golden stupa of Pha That Luang is the national symbol of Laos.","The first Thai–Lao Friendship Bridge, opened in 1994, crosses the Mekong just downstream."],"q":{"q":"King Setthathirath moved his capital to Vientiane in 1560 from…","a":"Luang Prabang","x":["Ayutthaya","Champasak"]}},"Q9279":{"t":"A riverside capital with a whole village on stilts","s":"A whole village on stilts, linked by boardwalks, spreads across the river from the centre of Bandar Seri Begawan: Kampong Ayer, one of the largest water villages in the world. The capital sits on the Brunei River a few kilometres from Brunei Bay, surrounded by dark green rainforest. In 1521 members of Magellan's expedition visited and marvelled at a town built over the water.","w":"A small city on a river winding through rainforest, a large cluster of stilt houses on the water, a long bridge across the bay.","f":["The Temburong Bridge, opened in 2020, runs about 30 km across Brunei Bay to the country's separate eastern part.","The city took its present name in 1970; before that it was called Brunei Town."],"q":{"q":"What is Kampong Ayer?","a":"A village on stilts over water","x":["A floating market","A royal forest"]}},"Q45604":{"t":"A pomegranate oasis between bare ridges and red sands","s":"Reddish dunes of the Registan Desert spread south of Kandahar, which lies on a dry plain about 1,000 m up, among bare, jagged ridges. North-west of the city, the Arghandab River feeds a ribbon of green orchards famous for pomegranates. In 1747 Ahmad Shah Durrani made Kandahar the capital of his new empire, and he is buried here.","w":"A city on a dry brown plain among bare rocky ridges, with a green ribbon of orchards along a river to the north-west.","f":["Many historians link Kandahar with a city founded by Alexander the Great, Alexandria in Arachosia.","Kandahar's pomegranates are prized across the region."],"q":{"q":"The Arghandab valley near Kandahar is famous for which fruit?","a":"Pomegranates","x":["Dates","Oranges"]}},"Q1850":{"t":"A capital where four river arms meet","s":"Phnom Penh stands at a great watery crossroads called Chaktomuk, “four faces”: the Mekong, the Tonle Sap and the Bassac meet here in an X of brown water. Each rainy season the Mekong swells so much that the Tonle Sap River reverses and flows backwards, filling the great lake upstream. The city's name means “hill of Penh”, after a woman who, legend says, founded a shrine here.","w":"A city at an X-shaped meeting of wide brown rivers, surrounded by flat floodplain and paddies.","f":["In the wet season Tonle Sap Lake can grow to several times its dry-season size.","King Norodom moved the royal capital here from Oudong in 1866."],"q":{"q":"What does the Tonle Sap River do each rainy season?","a":"Reverses its flow","x":["Dries up","Freezes over"]}},"Q9270":{"t":"A small capital strung along a Himalayan valley","s":"Thimphu is strung out along the narrow valley of the Wang Chhu, about 2,300 m up, with steep forested mountains rising on either side. It is one of the smallest capitals in Asia and still has no traffic lights: at its busiest junction a police officer directs cars with graceful hand signals. On the valley floor stands Tashichho Dzong, a fortress-monastery that houses the government.","w":"A small, long town in a narrow river valley between steep, densely forested mountainsides.","f":["Thimphu became Bhutan's capital in the 1950s, taking over from Punakha.","Bhutan's only international airport, at Paro, lies in a neighbouring valley about 50 km away."],"q":{"q":"What does Thimphu famously have none of?","a":"Traffic lights","x":["Street lamps","Bridges"]}},"Q11568":{"t":"A tropical harbour town named after Charles Darwin","s":"Dark mangroves fringe a big harbour on Australia's tropical north coast, and Darwin sits on a low peninsula within it. Around it stretch flat savanna woodland and, further east, the floodplains of Kakadu. In the wet season towering thunderstorms build over the land most afternoons. The harbour was named in 1839 by the crew of HMS Beagle, after their former shipmate Charles Darwin.","w":"A small city on a low peninsula in a wide harbour lined with mangroves; flat green savanna beyond.","f":["Darwin is closer to Jakarta than to Sydney.","Saltwater crocodiles live in the rivers and bays around the city."],"q":{"q":"Darwin was named in 1839 by the crew of which ship?","a":"HMS Beagle","x":["HMS Endeavour","HMS Bounty"]}},"Q17872":{"t":"A town in a gap of red ridges at Australia's heart","s":"Alice Springs lies almost at the centre of Australia, tucked into a gap in the MacDonnell Ranges — long, parallel red ridges that run east–west across the desert like the folds of a rug. The Todd River through town is usually just a bed of dry sand. The settlement grew around a station on the Overland Telegraph, finished in 1872, which linked Adelaide with Darwin.","w":"Long, parallel red ridges running east–west through red desert, with a small town sitting in a gap between them.","f":["In the Henley-on-Todd Regatta, crews race bottomless boats by running with them along the dry riverbed.","The springs, and later the town, were named after Alice Todd, wife of the telegraph's builder, Charles Todd."],"q":{"q":"Boats in Alice Springs' Henley-on-Todd Regatta have no…","a":"Bottoms","x":["Oars","Sails"]}},"Q3114":{"t":"A capital drawn on paper, around a man-made lake","s":"Canberra was chosen as a compromise between rivals Sydney and Melbourne and designed on paper by the American architects Walter Burley Griffin and Marion Mahony Griffin. From above, look for the artificial lake at its centre and the geometric pattern of avenues and circles around it. Wooded hills and nature reserves separate the suburbs, earning it the nickname “bush capital”.","w":"A spread-out city among wooded hills, with a long artificial lake in the middle and geometric avenues around it.","f":["The Griffins' design won an international competition in 1912.","Lake Burley Griffin was created in 1963 by damming the Molonglo River."],"q":{"q":"Who won the 1912 competition to design Canberra?","a":"Walter Burley Griffin","x":["Edwin Lutyens","Frank Lloyd Wright"]}},"Q57278":{"t":"Coal ships, a river mouth and a sea of dunes","s":"A line of bulk carriers waiting offshore gives Newcastle away: at the mouth of the Hunter River stands the world's largest coal-export port. North of the harbour, the pale dunes of Stockton Bight stretch for about 30 km along the coast. To the south lies the big, branching Lake Macquarie. Coal was first found here in 1797.","w":"A city at a river mouth with ships anchored offshore; long pale dunes along the coast to the north, a large lake to the south.","f":["The Stockton Bight dunes are the largest moving coastal sand dunes in New South Wales.","The Hunter Valley inland is one of Australia's oldest wine regions."],"q":{"q":"Newcastle is the world's largest export port for…","a":"Coal","x":["Iron ore","Wheat"]}},"Q5112":{"t":"A grid city wrapped in a ring of parkland","s":"An unbroken ring of green parkland frames Adelaide's neat grid centre — a plan laid out by Colonel William Light in 1837 that still shows clearly from above. The city lies on a coastal plain between Gulf St Vincent and the wooded Mount Lofty Ranges. Unlike Sydney or Hobart, South Australia was settled by free colonists, never convicts.","w":"A square grid centre framed by a green belt of parks, on a plain between a gulf to the west and hills to the east.","f":["The city is named after Queen Adelaide, wife of King William IV.","The Barossa Valley, one of Australia's best-known wine regions, lies about 60 km to the north-east."],"q":{"q":"Who laid out Adelaide's grid and parklands?","a":"William Light","x":["Lachlan Macquarie","Walter Burley Griffin"]}},"Q190021":{"t":"A dry-tropics city by a red rock and a magnetic island","s":"Townsville spreads along a flat, dry stretch of the north Queensland coast around Castle Hill, a pink-red granite dome rising right out of the city. Just offshore lies Magnetic Island, mountainous and wooded, and further out the Great Barrier Reef begins. The hills behind catch less rain than the wet coast further north, giving Townsville its drier, brown-grass look.","w":"A city on a flat coast around a red rocky hill, with a hilly island just offshore; brown grassland behind.","f":["James Cook named Magnetic Island in 1770 because it seemed to disturb his ship's compass.","Castle Hill, about 286 m high, rises straight out of the city centre."],"q":{"q":"Why did James Cook call it Magnetic Island?","a":"It seemed to upset his compass","x":["It was rich in iron ore","It drew ships in during storms"]}},"Q34932":{"t":"A snaking river and a bay of giant sand islands","s":"The Brisbane River coils through the city in tight, snaking loops before it reaches Moreton Bay. Out in the bay, Moreton and North Stradbroke islands are among the largest sand islands on Earth, their dunes pale against the Pacific. To the north, look for the Glass House Mountains, a cluster of volcanic plugs rising abruptly from the plain.","w":"A city along a tightly looping river; a wide bay sheltered by long sand islands; volcanic peaks to the north.","f":["James Cook named the Glass House Mountains in 1770; they reminded him of glass furnaces in Yorkshire.","Brisbane began in 1825 as a penal settlement for convicts."],"q":{"q":"Who named the Glass House Mountains north of Brisbane?","a":"James Cook","x":["Matthew Flinders","John Oxley"]}},"Q40191":{"t":"A harbour city under a snow-dusted mountain","s":"Hobart hugs the shores of the broad Derwent estuary, crossed by a long bridge, and directly behind it rises kunanyi / Mount Wellington, 1,271 m high and often dusted with snow. Founded in 1804, it is Australia's second-oldest capital city. Ships leave from here for the Southern Ocean: Hobart is a gateway to Antarctica.","w":"A city along a wide estuary crossed by a long bridge, with a big, dark mountain rising right behind it.","f":["The Sydney to Hobart yacht race, first sailed in 1945, finishes on the Derwent every year.","The Australian Antarctic Division is based just south of the city."],"q":{"q":"In which year was Hobart founded?","a":"1804","x":["1788","1836"]}},"Q37400":{"t":"A new capital of vast roads laid out in the countryside","s":"Naypyidaw was built from scratch in the early 2000s on a plain between the forested Bago Yoma hills and the Shan uplands, and it became Myanmar's capital in 2005–06. From above it looks unlike any other city: very wide, straight roads, widely spaced districts and large gaps of countryside between them. Its name means roughly “abode of kings”.","w":"A sprawling, thinly built city with very wide straight roads and scattered districts on a plain between forested hills.","f":["Some of its main avenues are around 20 lanes wide.","The Uppatasanti Pagoda here is a near-copy of Yangon's Shwedagon, just slightly lower."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Naypyidaw mean?","a":"Abode of kings","x":["City of gold","End of strife"]}},"Q9347":{"t":"A whole capital packed onto one tiny coral island","s":"Malé is one of the most striking capitals to see from the air: a small coral island, only about 2 km long, covered by buildings right to the water's edge. Next to it lie the flat airport island and Hulhumalé, a large island built from dredged sand. All around, atolls scatter turquoise lagoons across the dark blue Indian Ocean.","w":"A tiny island covered edge to edge with buildings, beside a flat airport island; turquoise lagoons and reefs all around.","f":["The word “atoll” comes from Dhivehi, the language of the Maldives.","The Maldives are the world's lowest-lying country, with an average ground level of about 1.5 m.","A bridge opened in 2018 links Malé with the airport island."],"q":{"q":"The word “atoll” comes from which language?","a":"Dhivehi","x":["Hawaiian","Malay"]}},"Q181557":{"t":"A harbour city behind a long, thin island","s":"Kaohsiung stretches along the south-west coast of Taiwan behind Cijin, a long, pencil-thin island that shelters its harbour from the Taiwan Strait. Ships enter through gaps at either end. It is Taiwan's largest port. At the northern end the green hump of Shoushan rises over the water, and far inland the Central Mountain Range lines the eastern horizon.","w":"A long city behind a thin island running parallel to the coast, a green hill at its north end, mountains far to the east.","f":["The city's old name, Takau, is thought to come from an indigenous word for a bamboo forest.","The harbour's second entrance was cut through Cijin in the 1970s."],"q":{"q":"Which long, narrow island shelters Kaohsiung's harbour?","a":"Cijin","x":["Penghu","Liuqiu"]}},"Q23661":{"t":"The southernmost capital, on a windy, round harbour","s":"Wellington climbs steep, green hills around a nearly enclosed round harbour at the southern tip of New Zealand's North Island. Look for the dead-straight edge of the Hutt Valley to the north-east: it follows the Wellington Fault. Just south, the often rough waters of Cook Strait separate you from the South Island, visible on a clear day.","w":"A city on steep hills around a round harbour; a straight-edged valley to the north-east, a strait and a far island to the south.","f":["Wellington is the southernmost capital city of any sovereign country.","Winds funnelled through Cook Strait make it one of the windiest cities on Earth."],"q":{"q":"Which strait lies just south of Wellington?","a":"Cook Strait","x":["Bass Strait","Foveaux Strait"]}},"Q79990":{"t":"A garden city between braided rivers and old volcanoes","s":"Wide braided rivers fan out from the Southern Alps across the flat quilt of the Canterbury Plains, and Christchurch sits at the plains' seaward edge. South-east of the city rises Banks Peninsula, built by two ancient volcanoes whose drowned craters now form the harbours of Lyttelton and Akaroa. English settlers founded the city in 1850.","w":"A flat city beside a round, hilly peninsula; gridded plains and braided rivers inland, snowy mountains far to the west.","f":["Christchurch is a main gateway to Antarctica: supply flights for several national programmes leave from its airport.","The Waimakariri River, north of the city, splits into many channels across a wide gravel bed."],"q":{"q":"What are Lyttelton and Akaroa harbours?","a":"Drowned volcanic craters","x":["Glacial fjords","Coral lagoons"]}},"Q19881964":{"t":"A new capital named after an ancient Buddhist city","s":"Amaravati is being laid out on flat farmland on the south bank of the Krishna River, just upstream of Vijayawada and its long barrage; from above, look for new road grids cut across green fields. The name is borrowed from the ancient town of Amaravathi a little further upstream, where a great Buddhist stupa stood more than 2,000 years ago.","w":"Flat green farmland on the south bank of a wide river, crossed by a new grid of roads, near a city with a river barrage.","f":["Carvings from the Amaravati stupa are among the treasures of the British Museum and of the Government Museum in Chennai.","The Krishna, about 1,400 km long, is one of the great rivers of peninsular India."],"q":{"q":"The ancient stupa of Amaravati stood beside which river?","a":"The Krishna","x":["The Godavari","The Kaveri"]}},"Q120306":{"t":"Canada's highest summit, afloat on a sea of ice","s":"Nothing about Mount Logan is small. Its summit plateau runs for roughly 20 km, and the massif is thought to have the broadest base of any non-volcanic mountain on Earth. At 5,959 m it is the highest point in Canada, rising out of the glaciers of the St. Elias Mountains and still only about five kilometres short of your cruise height.","w":"A huge, flat-topped white massif above a maze of glaciers, inland from the Gulf of Alaska near the Yukon–Alaska border.","f":["It is named after William Logan, founder of the Geological Survey of Canada.","In North America only Denali, in Alaska, is higher.","Its glaciers lie in Kluane National Park, part of a UNESCO World Heritage site shared with Alaska."],"q":{"q":"Which is the only North American peak higher than Mount Logan?","a":"Denali","x":["Mount Whitney","Pico de Orizaba"]}},"Q870063":{"t":"Where the Himalayas make their last snowy stand","s":"At the far eastern end of the Himalayas, just before the mountains bend south into the tropics, one pyramid still carries heavy snow. Hkakabo Razi, 5,881 m, is the highest mountain in Myanmar and in all of Southeast Asia. Its slopes fall from glaciers to dense subtropical forest, and the area is so remote that the first confirmed ascent came only in 1996.","w":"White peaks rising from dark, forested ridges in Myanmar's far north, close to the borders with India and China.","f":["It is the highest mountain in Southeast Asia.","The first confirmed ascent, in 1996, was made by Japanese climber Takashi Ozaki and Tibetan climber Nyima Gyaltsen.","Meltwater from its glaciers feeds the headwaters of the Irrawaddy, Myanmar's great river."],"q":{"q":"When was Hkakabo Razi first climbed?","a":"In 1996","x":["In 1953","In 1978"]}},"ne-pk-1159105475":{"t":"The snowy crown over the Silk Road oases","s":"Silk Road caravans owed their water to ice like this. Kangze'gyai, 5,808 m, is the highest summit of the Qilian Mountains, the snowy wall on the north-eastern rim of the Tibetan Plateau. Its meltwater runs down to the Hexi Corridor, a chain of oases squeezed between these ridges and the Gobi, which the old trade route followed towards Central Asia.","w":"Snow-streaked ridges and small glaciers between the brown Tibetan uplands and a flat, desert-edged corridor to the north.","f":["The Qilian Mountains run for roughly 800 km along the border of Qinghai and Gansu.","Qilian is thought to come from a Xiongnu word for 'sky'.","Jiayuguan, the western end of the Ming Great Wall, stands in the corridor at the foot of the Qilian range."],"q":{"q":"Which ancient trade route ran along the foot of the Qilian Mountains?","a":"The Silk Road","x":["The Tea Horse Road","The Amber Road"]}},"Q578862":{"t":"Permanent snow 40 km from Caribbean beaches","s":"Palm-fringed beaches and permanent snow sit only about 40 km apart here. The Sierra Nevada de Santa Marta climbs straight from the Caribbean to 5,775 m at Pico Cristóbal Colón, which makes it the highest coastal range on Earth. Its twin summit, Pico Simón Bolívar, is practically the same height, and the massif stands alone, cut off from the Andes by lowlands.","w":"An isolated, often cloud-capped mountain block rising from the Caribbean shore behind the city of Santa Marta, east of Barranquilla.","f":["The massif spans almost every climate zone, from tropical coast to glacier.","The Kogi, Arhuaco and Wiwa peoples live on its slopes and call it the heart of the world.","Pico Cristóbal Colón is named after Christopher Columbus."],"q":{"q":"What record does the Sierra Nevada de Santa Marta hold?","a":"Highest coastal range on Earth","x":["Longest range in Colombia","Youngest volcanic range"]}},"Q238147":{"t":"Mexico's 'star mountain', seen from the Gulf coast","s":"On a clear day this white cone shows above the coastal haze near Veracruz, more than 100 km away. Pico de Orizaba, 5,636 m, is the highest mountain in Mexico and the third-highest in North America. Its Nahuatl name, Citlaltépetl, means 'star mountain'. The volcano has been quiet since the 19th century, and it carries the largest glaciers left in Mexico.","w":"A lone, symmetrical white cone east of Puebla, standing well above the volcanic highlands and the green slopes falling towards the Gulf.","f":["Only Denali and Mount Logan are higher in North America.","The summit lies on the border of the states of Puebla and Veracruz.","Its Nahuatl name, Citlaltépetl, means 'star mountain'."],"q":{"q":"What does Citlaltépetl, the Nahuatl name of Pico de Orizaba, mean?","a":"Star mountain","x":["Smoking mountain","White woman"]}},"Q40758":{"t":"Asia's tallest volcano, still breathing sulphur","s":"A white cone rises above the Alborz ridges, so neat it looks set down by hand. Damavand, 5,610 m, is Iran's highest mountain and the tallest volcano in Asia. Near the top, vents still breathe sulphur and warm gas. In the Persian epic Shahnameh, the tyrant Zahhak is chained inside this mountain, and the Caspian Sea lies just beyond the range.","w":"A lone snow-capped cone standing above the brown Alborz range, between Tehran and the green Caspian coast.","f":["On clear days Damavand can be seen from Tehran, about 65 km away.","Yellow sulphur deposits and gas vents mark the summit area.","In the Shahnameh, the hero Fereydun imprisons the tyrant Zahhak inside Damavand."],"q":{"q":"In which mountain range does Damavand stand?","a":"The Alborz","x":["The Zagros","The Kopet Dag"]}},"Q1037396":{"t":"Glaciers above one of the lowest basins on Earth","s":"The contrast here is extreme. Bogda Peak lifts its glaciers to 5,445 m, while the Turpan Depression, about 130 km to the south, sinks to around 150 m below sea level. Ürümqi spreads along the western foot of this eastern arm of the Tian Shan. Tucked into the northern slopes lies Tianchi, the 'Heavenly Lake', a narrow blue lake framed by spruce forest.","w":"A three-headed snowy massif east of Ürümqi, with the pale Turpan basin to the south and the Dzungarian plain to the north.","f":["Bogda means 'holy' in Mongolian.","Tianchi, the lake on its northern side, lies at about 1,900 m.","The Turpan Depression south of the range is one of the deepest dry basins on any continent."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Bogda mean in Mongolian?","a":"Holy","x":["White","Cold"]}},"ne-pk-1159106095":{"t":"The smoking mountain beside the sleeping woman","s":"Look for the plume. Popocatépetl, 'smoking mountain' in Nahuatl, is one of Mexico's most active volcanoes, and a thread of steam or ash often drifts from its crater. Its neat cone, about 5,420 m high, stands beside the long ridge of Iztaccíhuatl, whose outline resembles a woman lying down. Mexico City lies about 70 km to the north-west.","w":"A symmetrical snowy cone, often trailing a plume, next to a long jagged white ridge south-east of Mexico City.","f":["The pass between Popocatépetl and Iztaccíhuatl is named after Hernán Cortés, whose army crossed it in 1519.","Sixteenth-century monasteries on its slopes are a UNESCO World Heritage site.","Its current phase of activity began in 1994, after decades of quiet."],"q":{"q":"Which volcano forms a pair with Popocatépetl?","a":"Iztaccíhuatl","x":["Pico de Orizaba","Nevado de Toluca"]}},"Q2648857":{"t":"Andean glaciers at the edge of the endless plains","s":"On the eastern side of Colombia's Andes, a short chain of glaciers hangs above the Llanos, the grassy plains that roll on towards Venezuela. Ritacuba Blanco, 5,410 m, is the highest of these peaks and the top of the whole Cordillera Oriental. Below the ice lie páramos, wet highlands dotted with frailejones, silver-leaved plants that stand in the mist like people.","w":"A short line of white peaks on the eastern edge of the Andes, with flat green plains stretching away beyond them.","f":["It is the highest point of the Sierra Nevada del Cocuy, in El Cocuy National Park.","The glaciers of El Cocuy have been shrinking fast in recent decades.","Frailejones, the rosette plants of the páramo, grow only about a centimetre a year."],"q":{"q":"Ritacuba Blanco is the highest point of which Andean range?","a":"The Cordillera Oriental","x":["The Cordillera Central","The Cordillera Occidental"]}},"Q233351":{"t":"An ice-capped volcano above Colombia's coffee hills","s":"Ice and fire share this summit. Nevado del Ruiz, 5,311 m, is an active volcano whose broad top is capped by glaciers, and a column of steam often rises from its crater, Arenas. Around it stretch the páramos of Los Nevados National Park. Lower down begin the steep green hills of the coffee region, a landscape listed by UNESCO.","w":"A broad, white-capped dome, sometimes steaming, rising above green ridges south-east of the city of Manizales.","f":["Its summit crater is called Arenas.","Colombia's Coffee Cultural Landscape, on the hills below the volcano, joined the UNESCO list in 2011.","Its ice cap has shrunk sharply since the middle of the 20th century."],"q":{"q":"Which crop shapes the landscape on the hills below Nevado del Ruiz?","a":"Coffee","x":["Tea","Cocoa"]}},"Q21519918":{"t":"A white wall above the lake that never freezes","s":"Behind the town of Karakol the Terskey Ala-Too rises like a white wall, and Pik Karakol, about 5,200 m, is one of its highest summits. To the north lies Issyk-Kul, a deep blue lake some 180 km long whose name means 'warm lake': even in the harsh Tian Shan winter it does not freeze. Snow and deep blue meet sharply here.","w":"Snowy ridges on the south-eastern shore of Issyk-Kul, the long blue lake cradled in the Tian Shan.","f":["Issyk-Kul lies at about 1,600 m and is up to roughly 670 m deep.","Its slightly salty water never freezes, even in winter.","The explorer Nikolai Przhevalsky died in Karakol in 1888, and the town long bore his name."],"q":{"q":"Which lake lies at the foot of Pik Karakol's range?","a":"Issyk-Kul","x":["Balkhash","Son-Kul"]}},"Q217457":{"t":"The crown of a twelve-kilometre wall of ice","s":"Along the main Caucasus ridge runs the Bezengi Wall, some 12 km of ice and rock, and Shkhara, about 5,200 m, is its highest point. It is also the highest summit of Georgia. At its southern foot, the stone-towered villages of Upper Svaneti are among the highest permanently inhabited places in Europe, at around 2,100 m.","w":"A continuous white wall of summits on the main Caucasus ridge, south-east of Elbrus, with deep green valleys to the south.","f":["The Bezengi Wall carries several peaks above 5,000 m.","Upper Svaneti, below Shkhara's southern face, has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1996.","The Enguri River rises from Shkhara's glaciers."],"q":{"q":"Shkhara is the highest summit of which country?","a":"Georgia","x":["Armenia","Azerbaijan"]}},"Q172070":{"t":"Ice and jagged teeth right beside the equator","s":"The equator crosses its northern slopes, yet there is ice up here. Mount Kenya is the worn-down core of an ancient volcano, and its jagged central peaks, Batian at 5,199 m and Nelion close behind, are second in Africa only to Kilimanjaro. The country took its name from the mountain, which the Kikuyu call Kirinyaga and traditionally see as the seat of God.","w":"A broad, dark mountain with a rocky, snow-flecked crown, rising from farmland and forest north of Nairobi.","f":["The equator runs across the mountain's northern slopes.","Its glaciers have lost most of their ice since the early 20th century.","Mount Kenya's national park and forest are a UNESCO World Heritage site."],"q":{"q":"Which is the highest peak of Mount Kenya?","a":"Batian","x":["Nelion","Point Lenana"]}},"Q72303":{"t":"A lone giant with an ice cap and an ancient legend","s":"Few mountains stand so alone. Ararat rises more than four kilometres above the surrounding plains to 5,137 m, a dormant volcano capped with ice, with its smaller twin, Lesser Ararat, beside it like an echo. The Book of Genesis says Noah's ark came to rest 'upon the mountains of Ararat', and tradition has attached the story to this peak for centuries.","w":"A huge white-capped cone with a smaller, sharper cone beside it, standing alone above brown plains near the Aras River.","f":["Lesser Ararat, nearly 3,900 m high, is joined to the main peak by a high saddle.","The summit carries a permanent ice cap.","Its Turkish name is Ağrı Dağı."],"q":{"q":"What is the Turkish name of Mount Ararat?","a":"Ağrı Dağı","x":["Erciyes Dağı","Nemrut Dağı"]}},"Q20191405":{"t":"The high point of the Koh-e Baba, above Bamyan","s":"In the heart of Afghanistan the land folds into the Koh-e Baba, a long, bare range often streaked with snow well into summer. Shāh Fōlādī, 5,048 m, is its highest summit. It towers over the Bamyan valley to the north, where green fields follow the river between red and ochre cliffs. Around the peak there are almost no trees: only stone, snow and sky.","w":"A snowy, bare ridge running east–west in central Afghanistan, with the green thread of the Bamyan valley along its northern side.","f":["The Koh-e Baba is a westward continuation of the Hindu Kush.","The Bamyan valley at its foot lies at about 2,500 m.","Band-e Amir, a chain of vivid blue lakes behind natural travertine dams, lies west of Bamyan."],"q":{"q":"Which valley lies at the northern foot of Shāh Fōlādī?","a":"Bamyan","x":["Panjshir","Wakhan"]}},"Q213704":{"t":"Venezuela's rooftop, towering over Mérida","s":"Mérida fills a long terrace deep in the Venezuelan Andes, and the country's highest peak rises almost straight above it. Pico Bolívar, 4,980 m, is a sharp rock summit named after Simón Bolívar, with a bust of the Liberator on top. From the city, a cable car climbs to neighbouring Pico Espejo, and at over 4,700 m its top station is one of the highest in the world.","w":"A dark, sharp summit above a deep valley with a city on its terraced floor, in the north-eastern arm of the Andes.","f":["It is the highest point of Venezuela.","A bust of Simón Bolívar stands on the summit.","The Mérida cable car, called Mukumbarí, reopened in 2016."],"q":{"q":"Which city lies at the foot of Pico Bolívar?","a":"Mérida","x":["Caracas","Maracaibo"]}},"Q221666":{"t":"Equatorial ice on the Mountains of the Moon","s":"Ice on the equator sounds impossible, yet the Rwenzori range carries glaciers, and Mount Stanley is its highest block. Its top, Margherita Peak, at about 5,000 m, is the third-highest point in Africa after Kilimanjaro and Mount Kenya. Cloud hides the massif on most days, and it has long been linked with Ptolemy's legendary 'Mountains of the Moon', the source of the Nile.","w":"A cloud-wrapped massif with white patches of ice, rising on the Uganda–Congo border west of Lake George.","f":["The mountain is named after the explorer Henry Morton Stanley.","Its main summits were first climbed in 1906 by an expedition led by the Duke of the Abruzzi.","Margherita Peak is named after Queen Margherita of Italy."],"q":{"q":"Who is Margherita Peak, Mount Stanley's summit, named after?","a":"A queen of Italy","x":["An explorer's wife","A Belgian princess"]}},"Q1045888":{"t":"The highest island summit on Earth, with tropical ice","s":"In 1623 the Dutch navigator Jan Carstensz reported snow on a mountain near the equator, and people back home laughed. He was right. Puncak Jaya, 4,884 m, is the highest peak of New Guinea and of any island on Earth. Its grey limestone walls still hold small, fast-shrinking glaciers, surrounded by rainforest that runs down towards the sea.","w":"Grey limestone cliffs with small white patches of ice, rising above a sea of green forest in the centre of New Guinea.","f":["It was first climbed in 1962 by a team led by Heinrich Harrer.","It is often called the highest point between the Himalayas and the Andes.","It is also known as Carstensz Pyramid, after the navigator who reported its snow."],"q":{"q":"Who first reported snow on Puncak Jaya, in 1623?","a":"Jan Carstensz","x":["Abel Tasman","Willem Janszoon"]}},"Q210252":{"t":"Eurasia's tallest active volcano, a near-perfect cone","s":"Eruptions here are almost routine. Klyuchevskaya Sopka, 4,750 m, is the highest active volcano in Eurasia, a steep, nearly perfect cone that often sends ash plumes drifting over Kamchatka. It rises from a crowded family of about a dozen volcanoes, and its snow is frequently streaked grey with fresh ash. From cruise height its crater is only about six kilometres below.","w":"A tall, symmetrical snow cone, often with a plume, in a cluster of volcanoes beside the broad Kamchatka River valley.","f":["It is named after the village of Klyuchi at its foot.","It belongs to the 'Volcanoes of Kamchatka' UNESCO World Heritage site.","Its height changes by tens of metres as eruptions build up or blast away the summit."],"q":{"q":"On which peninsula does Klyuchevskaya Sopka stand?","a":"Kamchatka","x":["Chukotka","Taymyr"]}},"Q13171265":{"t":"A summit named after the hero of a vast epic","s":"The Kyrgyz epic Manas is among the longest poems ever recorded, and this summit carries its hero's name. At 4,482 m, Manas Peak crowns the Talas Ala-Too, a rugged western arm of the Tian Shan with small glaciers and deep gorges. North of the ridge the mountains drop towards the Kazakh plains, where the Aksu-Zhabagly reserve has guarded the slopes since 1926.","w":"A snowy knot of summits at the western end of the Tian Shan, where green valleys open north onto the plains of southern Kazakhstan.","f":["Some versions of the Manas epic run to hundreds of thousands of lines.","Aksu-Zhabagly, founded in 1926, is the oldest nature reserve in Central Asia.","Near the town of Talas, east of the peak, a 14th-century mausoleum is traditionally called the tomb of Manas."],"q":{"q":"Who is Manas in Kyrgyz culture?","a":"The hero of a national epic","x":["A mountain spirit","A Soviet-era poet"]}},"Q20190966":{"t":"A lonely 4,000-metre summit in Afghanistan's highlands","s":"Deep in the south-west of Afghanistan's central highlands, the ridges still climb above 4,000 m before breaking up towards the deserts. Kuh-e Jang Qal'eh reaches 4,171 m, a bare summit that keeps its snow through winter and into spring. At cruise height you are less than seven kilometres above it, looking down on folded brown ridges cut by dry valleys.","w":"Bare, brown folded ridges with snow on the highest crests, where Afghanistan's central mountains fade south-west into lower, drier country.","f":["In Persian, kuh means 'mountain' and qal'eh means 'fortress'.","Snowmelt from these highlands feeds rivers that run south-west towards the Sistan basin on the Iranian border."],"q":{"q":"What does the Persian word kuh, which starts many peak names, mean?","a":"Mountain","x":["River","Fortress"]}},"Q60967":{"t":"Borneo's granite crown above the morning cloud","s":"Kinabalu's top is a jagged plateau of bare granite, grey spires poking through the morning cloud above Borneo's green hills. At about 4,100 m it is the highest mountain on the island. Its slopes pass from lowland rainforest through mossy cloud forest to naked rock, and they hold one of the richest collections of plants on Earth, giant pitcher plants included.","w":"A grey, jagged granite massif rising abruptly from green hills near Borneo's north-west coast, east of Kota Kinabalu.","f":["Kinabalu Park became Malaysia's first UNESCO World Heritage site in 2000.","Its highest point, Low's Peak, is named after Hugh Low, a British official who explored the summit plateau in 1851.","For the Kadazan-Dusun people, the mountain is a resting place for the spirits of the dead."],"q":{"q":"Who is Low's Peak, the summit of Kinabalu, named after?","a":"Hugh Low","x":["James Brooke","Stamford Raffles"]}},"ne-pk-1159105699":{"t":"Snow on the border above Baikal's little sister","s":"Where Siberia meets Mongolia, the Eastern Sayan ends in a snowy peak with small glaciers clinging to its flanks. Mönkh Saridag, 3,491 m, is the highest point of the range, and the Russia–Mongolia border runs across its summit. Just to the south stretches Khövsgöl, a long, clear mountain lake whose water eventually flows north into Lake Baikal.","w":"A white-topped massif just north of the long blue Lake Khövsgöl, at the southern edge of the Siberian taiga.","f":["It is the highest peak of the Eastern Sayan mountains.","Lake Khövsgöl holds roughly 70% of Mongolia's fresh water.","Khövsgöl drains through the Selenga River system into Lake Baikal."],"q":{"q":"Into which lake does water from nearby Khövsgöl eventually flow?","a":"Lake Baikal","x":["Lake Balkhash","Issyk-Kul"]}},"Q120314":{"t":"Five flat summits of a sacred Buddhist mountain","s":"Five broad, almost flat-topped summits give this mountain its name: Wutai means 'five terraces'. At 3,058 m, its northern terrace is often called the roof of northern China. For well over a thousand years it has been one of the four sacred mountains of Chinese Buddhism, and dozens of temples fill the valleys between the terraces, far too small to pick out from here.","w":"A cluster of broad, grassy, rounded summits above the loess hills of north-eastern Shanxi, west of the long Taihang ridges.","f":["Mount Wutai joined the UNESCO World Heritage list in 2009.","The main hall of Nanchan Temple here, dated to 782, is one of the oldest surviving timber buildings in China.","Tradition links the mountain to Manjushri, the bodhisattva of wisdom."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Wutai mean?","a":"Five terraces","x":["Five rivers","Five temples"]}},"Q1061148":{"t":"The high point of Córdoba's rolling sierras","s":"Rising from the flat Pampas, the Sierras de Córdoba look like long waves of grey granite and golden grass. Their highest point is Cerro Champaquí, nearly 2,800 m, a broad rounded summit rather than a sharp peak. To the east lies the Calamuchita valley with its blue reservoirs; to the west, the drier Traslasierra valley. Some winters bring snow to the top.","w":"A broad grassy dome on a north–south line of low sierras, with blue reservoirs in the valley to the east and flat pampas beyond.","f":["It is the highest point of Argentina's Córdoba province.","The Sierras de Córdoba belong to the Sierras Pampeanas, ancient blocks of crystalline rock tilted up above the plains."],"q":{"q":"Champaquí is the highest point of which Argentine province?","a":"Córdoba","x":["San Luis","Mendoza"]}},"Q3321738":{"t":"An Arctic giant demoted by better measurements","s":"For decades this was thought to be the tallest peak of the Brooks Range. Then, in 2014, precise new surveys showed two neighbours to be slightly higher, and Mount Chamberlin, about 2,700 m, slipped to third place. It rises in the Arctic National Wildlife Refuge, well north of the Arctic Circle, where snowy ridges fall away to the tundra of the coastal plain.","w":"Snow-streaked, glaciated peaks on the northern edge of the Brooks Range, with flat tundra stretching north to the Beaufort Sea.","f":["It is named after Thomas Chrowder Chamberlin, an American geologist.","Mount Isto, about 50 km to the east-south-east, is now counted as the highest peak of the Brooks Range.","The Arctic National Wildlife Refuge covers nearly 80,000 km²."],"q":{"q":"Which peak is now counted as the highest in the Brooks Range?","a":"Mount Isto","x":["Mount Hubley","Mount Michelson"]}},"ne-gl-albanov-ice-cap":{"t":"A white dome on one of the last-mapped archipelagos","s":"Severnaya Zemlya was one of the last sizeable archipelagos to reach the map: it was discovered only in 1913. On October Revolution Island, its largest island, several ice domes merge into one frozen landscape, and the Albanov Ice Cap is one of them. It shows as a smooth, pale dome whose edges break up where the ice meets grey rock or the sea.","w":"A smooth white dome among neighbouring ice caps on October Revolution Island, with the Kara Sea to the west.","f":["It is named after Valerian Albanov, navigator of the Arctic ship Svyataya Anna.","Georgy Ushakov and Nikolai Urvantsev first mapped Severnaya Zemlya in detail in 1930–32.","Close to half of Severnaya Zemlya is covered by glaciers."],"q":{"q":"In which archipelago is the Albanov Ice Cap?","a":"Severnaya Zemlya","x":["Franz Josef Land","Novaya Zemlya"]}},"ne-gl-barentsjokulen":{"t":"An ice cap named after the man who found Svalbard","s":"In 1596 the Dutch navigator Willem Barentsz sighted a land of jagged peaks and named it Spitsbergen. This ice cap, Barentsjøkulen, bears his name. It covers the heart of Barentsøya, a quiet island in eastern Svalbard, and spreads as a pale dome between dark, flat-topped mountains. Its only neighbours are reindeer, walruses and polar bears.","w":"A smooth white dome filling the centre of an island east of Spitsbergen, ringed by dark cliffs and, often, sea ice.","f":["Barentsøya, the island it covers, has no permanent inhabitants.","Barentsz reached Spitsbergen in 1596 while searching for a north-east passage to Asia.","The Barents Sea, to the south, is also named after him."],"q":{"q":"Which navigator gave his name to Barentsjøkulen?","a":"Willem Barentsz","x":["Henry Hudson","Vitus Bering"]}},"ne-gl-eyjafjallajokull":{"t":"The glacier volcano that grounded Europe's planes","s":"Every air traveller has a reason to remember this name. In April 2010 the volcano beneath Eyjafjallajökull erupted through its ice, and its fine ash drifted over Europe, closing much of the continent's airspace for days. From above it looks modest: a rounded white cap on a mountain near Iceland's south coast. The name simply means 'glacier of the island mountains'.","w":"A small, rounded ice cap above green coastal lowlands in southern Iceland, just west of the larger Mýrdalsjökull.","f":["About 100,000 flights were cancelled during the 2010 ash crisis.","The mountain under the ice reaches about 1,650 m.","The 2010 eruption began on the Fimmvörðuháls pass beside the glacier, then moved to the summit crater under the ice."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Eyjafjallajökull mean?","a":"Glacier of the island mountains","x":["Glacier of fire and ice","Smoking glacier"]}},"ne-gl-hang-ten-icefield":{"t":"Wild ice in a corner of Canada with no roads","s":"The Coast Mountains of northern British Columbia hide icefields that few people ever hear of, and the Hang Ten Icefield is one of them. About 10 km across, it lies just inland of the vast Juneau Icefield and the Alaska border. It shows as a patch of white among steep, dark peaks, with glaciers spilling into valleys that no road reaches.","w":"A compact white icefield among steep dark peaks, east of the huge Juneau Icefield and south of long Atlin Lake.","f":["It lies in the Boundary Ranges, the northern part of the Coast Mountains.","There are no permanent settlements anywhere near the icefield.","Devils Paw, on the Alaska border to the south-west, is the highest summit of the Juneau Icefield."],"q":{"q":"Which huge icefield lies just west of the Hang Ten Icefield?","a":"The Juneau Icefield","x":["The Columbia Icefield","The Harding Icefield"]}},"ne-gl-hofsjokull":{"t":"A dome of ice hiding a volcano in Iceland's empty heart","s":"In the middle of Iceland, between two famous highland tracks, sits an almost perfectly round dome of ice. Hofsjökull is the country's third-largest glacier, and beneath it lies an active volcano with a caldera. Its meltwater helps feed the Þjórsá, Iceland's longest river, which winds south across black sand and lava to the Atlantic.","w":"A round white dome in the grey-black desert of the central highlands, between Langjökull to the west and Vatnajökull to the east.","f":["Hofsjökull covers roughly 900 km².","The Þjórsá, fed partly by its meltwater, is about 230 km long.","The Kjölur and Sprengisandur highland roads pass on either side of it."],"q":{"q":"Which is Iceland's longest river, fed partly by Hofsjökull?","a":"Þjórsá","x":["Ölfusá","Jökulsá á Fjöllum"]}},"ne-gl-hooker-icefield":{"t":"Ice around the phantom giants of the Rockies","s":"In 1827 the botanist David Douglas crossed Athabasca Pass and declared two nearby peaks, Mount Hooker and Mount Brown, the highest in North America, at over 16,000 feet. The legend lasted for decades until surveyors found them closer to 3,300 m. The Hooker Icefield spreads around Mount Hooker, a white plateau among the sharp ridges of the Canadian Rockies.","w":"A white icefield on the crest of the Rockies south of Jasper, near the Alberta–British Columbia border.","f":["Mount Hooker is named after botanist William Jackson Hooker, later director of Kew Gardens.","Athabasca Pass, nearby, was a main fur-trade route across the Rockies in the early 19th century.","David Douglas also gave his name to the Douglas fir."],"q":{"q":"Who started the legend of giant peaks near the Hooker Icefield?","a":"David Douglas","x":["John Muir","Joseph Banks"]}},"ne-gl-jostedalsbreen":{"t":"The largest glacier on mainland Europe","s":"Between the Sognefjord and the Nordfjord, a long white plateau of ice runs for some 60 km. Jostedalsbreen is the largest glacier in continental Europe, covering nearly 475 km², and dozens of blue-white tongues spill from it into green valleys. Some, like Briksdalsbreen and Nigardsbreen, end in milky meltwater lakes only a few hundred metres above sea level.","w":"A long white ice plateau with blue tongues reaching into valleys, between two of Norway's longest fjords.","f":["The ice is up to about 600 m thick in places.","Jostedalsbreen National Park was established in 1991.","Around 50 outlet glaciers flow from the main ice plateau."],"q":{"q":"Between which two fjords does Jostedalsbreen lie?","a":"Sognefjord and Nordfjord","x":["Hardangerfjord and Sognefjord","Geirangerfjord and Oslofjord"]}},"ne-gl-langjokull":{"t":"Iceland's 'long glacier', mother of Gullfoss","s":"Its name means simply 'long glacier', and that is exactly how it looks: a pale, stretched-out ice cap running some 50 km across Iceland's western highlands. Langjökull is the country's second-largest glacier. At its eastern edge a tongue of ice reaches the lake Hvítárvatn, source of the Hvítá river, which later plunges over the famous Gullfoss waterfall.","w":"An elongated white ice cap running south-west to north-east, with a milky lake at its eastern edge and green farmland to the south.","f":["Visitors can walk inside it through a man-made ice tunnel opened in 2015.","Geysir and Gullfoss, two of Iceland's best-known sights, lie within about 40 km of its southern edge."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Langjökull mean?","a":"Long glacier","x":["Cold glacier","Old glacier"]}},"ne-gl-plateau-icefield":{"t":"A high sheet of ice in the ranges beyond Whistler","s":"A short flight north of Vancouver, the Coast Mountains turn into a wilderness of rock and ice. The Plateau Icefield, about 14 km across, is one of the many ice sheets of the Pacific Ranges, lying some 80 km north-west of Whistler, just south of the larger Lillooet Icecap. It shows as a smooth white table edged by dark, crevassed glacier tongues.","w":"A flat white ice sheet among steep dark peaks, north-west of Whistler and just south of a larger, rounder icecap.","f":["The icefields of the Pacific Ranges are among the largest in the world's temperate latitudes.","The icefield lies about 150 km north-north-west of Vancouver."],"q":{"q":"In which mountain system is the Plateau Icefield?","a":"The Coast Mountains","x":["The Rocky Mountains","The Cascade Range"]}},"ne-gl-rusanov-ice-cap":{"t":"An Arctic ice dome between the Kara and Laptev seas","s":"Severnaya Zemlya is a barrier of islands and ice between the Kara Sea and the Laptev Sea, and the Rusanov Ice Cap sits on its largest island, October Revolution. Some 20 km across, it forms a smooth, gently rounded dome. Sea ice surrounds it for most of the year, and in the brief summer its edges turn grey where meltwater and rock show through.","w":"A broad, rounded white dome on the northern side of October Revolution Island, one of several ice caps lying side by side.","f":["It is named after Vladimir Rusanov, a geologist who explored Novaya Zemlya in the early 1900s.","The islands of Severnaya Zemlya separate the Kara Sea from the Laptev Sea.","The archipelago lies just over 1,000 km from the North Pole."],"q":{"q":"Which two seas does Severnaya Zemlya separate?","a":"The Kara and Laptev seas","x":["The Barents and Kara seas","The Laptev and East Siberian seas"]}},"ne-gl-svartisen":{"t":"The 'black ice' that reaches down towards a fjord","s":"Just north of the Arctic Circle, Norway's second-largest glacier spreads over the mountains in two great sheets. Svartisen means 'the black ice', perhaps for the dark blue sheen of its tongues. One of them, Engabreen, flows down almost to the waters of Holandsfjorden, making it one of the lowest-reaching glaciers on mainland Europe.","w":"Two large white ice sheets on the mountains near Norway's coast, with tongues reaching towards a fjord, just north of the Arctic Circle.","f":["Svartisen covers roughly 370 km² in two main parts, Vestisen and Østisen.","Scientists study the underside of Engabreen from tunnels built for a hydropower scheme beneath the ice.","The Arctic Circle crosses Saltfjellet–Svartisen National Park."],"q":{"q":"What does the Norwegian name Svartisen mean?","a":"The black ice","x":["The white ice","The old ice"]}},"ne-gl-sydkap-ice-cap":{"t":"Ice on the south shore of Canada's northernmost island","s":"Ellesmere Island reaches closer to the North Pole than any other part of Canada, and its southern coast is crowned by the Sydkap Ice Cap, some 40 km across. The Norwegian name means 'south cape', given by Otto Sverdrup's expedition around 1900. Glaciers spill from the dome towards Jones Sound, a channel locked in ice for much of the year.","w":"A broad white ice cap on Ellesmere's southern coast, with glaciers running down to the frozen channel that separates it from Devon Island.","f":["Otto Sverdrup's expedition aboard the Fram explored much of this coast in 1898–1902.","Grise Fiord, one of Canada's northernmost communities, lies roughly 90 km to the east.","Jones Sound, south of the ice cap, separates Ellesmere Island from Devon Island."],"q":{"q":"What does the Norwegian name Sydkap mean?","a":"South cape","x":["Cold cape","Ice cape"]}},"ne-gl-vavilov-ice-cap":{"t":"A sleepy Arctic ice cap that suddenly began to race","s":"For decades the Vavilov Ice Cap moved slowly, as cold polar ice caps do. Then, around 2013, its western edge suddenly sped up and began pushing out across the floor of the Kara Sea, advancing kilometres within a few years. Glaciologists watched in surprise: such a fast surge is rare in the dry, frozen High Arctic. It covers the western part of October Revolution Island.","w":"A white dome on the western side of October Revolution Island, with a broad lobe of ice pushed out into the sea.","f":["Its western outlet began a dramatic surge around 2013, reaching speeds of many metres a day.","It lies on October Revolution Island, the largest island of Severnaya Zemlya."],"q":{"q":"What happened to the Vavilov Ice Cap around 2013?","a":"Its edge surged out into the sea","x":["It split into two islands","It melted away completely"]}},"Q587808":{"t":"The ridge of white waters that built Johannesburg","s":"Gold turned this low ridge into a metropolis. The Witwatersrand, 'ridge of white waters' in Afrikaans, rises only a couple of hundred metres above the Highveld, yet after gold was found here in 1886, Johannesburg grew along it at astonishing speed. The rocks beneath have yielded a huge share of all the gold ever mined, and pale mine dumps still dot the city.","w":"A low east–west ridge on high grassland, with Johannesburg's sprawl and pale yellow mine dumps strung along it.","f":["Rocks of the Witwatersrand basin have produced roughly 40% of all the gold ever mined.","Rain north of the ridge drains to the Indian Ocean via the Limpopo; rain to the south, to the Atlantic via the Vaal and Orange.","Johannesburg was founded in 1886, the year of the gold discovery."],"q":{"q":"When was gold discovered on the Witwatersrand?","a":"In 1886","x":["In 1848","In 1867"]}},"Q27496722":{"t":"Dark heights between a sand sea and the Tibesti","s":"Deep in the Libyan Sahara, south of the dunes of the Murzuq sand sea, the ground rises into the dark, rocky heights of Jabal Bin Ghunaymah. They reach only about 1,000 m, but in this flat emptiness they stand out clearly: brown and black rock cut by dry wadis, with pale sand drifting into every hollow. Beneath some wadi beds, water lies close to the surface.","w":"Dark, rocky uplands cut by pale dry valleys, between the orange dunes of the Murzuq sand sea and the Tibesti massif to the south-east.","f":["The heights reach roughly 1,000 m above sea level.","Rainfall here averages only a few tens of millimetres a year."],"q":{"q":"Which sand sea lies north of Jabal Bin Ghunaymah?","a":"The Murzuq sand sea","x":["The Grand Erg Oriental","The Great Sand Sea"]}},"Q1501766":{"t":"A long volcanic ridge where counting may have begun","s":"For some 800 km a narrow line of hills runs north–south between the high bushveld and the coastal plain of Mozambique. The Lebombo Mountains are made of old volcanic rock and are mostly only a few hundred metres high, yet they form a clear wall. In Border Cave, in the southern Lebombos, archaeologists found a notched baboon bone about 43,000 years old, one of the oldest known counting tools.","w":"A thin, straight line of low hills running north–south, with Maputo and the flat coastal plain to the east and bushveld to the west.","f":["The Lebombo bone is a baboon fibula carrying 29 notches.","The ridge marks much of the eastern edge of Kruger National Park.","Parts of the borders of South Africa, Eswatini and Mozambique follow its crest."],"q":{"q":"What ancient object was found in the Lebombo Mountains?","a":"A notched counting bone","x":["A golden rhinoceros","A carved soapstone bird"]}},"Q1433137":{"t":"Cedar forests and snow in the heart of Morocco","s":"Between Fès and the High Atlas, Morocco turns unexpectedly green and cool. The Middle Atlas is a land of limestone plateaus, extinct volcanic cones and dark forests of Atlas cedar, where Barbary macaques live. Snow falls here most winters, and the town of Ifrane, with its steep red roofs, is nicknamed the Switzerland of Morocco.","w":"Dark patches of cedar forest on limestone plateaus and rounded volcanic cones, between the plain of Fès and the higher, drier Atlas to the south.","f":["Ifrane recorded −23.9 °C in 1935, often cited as the lowest temperature ever measured in Africa.","The Oum Er-Rbia, one of Morocco's longest rivers, rises in the Middle Atlas.","The Barbary macaque is the only wild primate north of the Sahara apart from humans."],"q":{"q":"Which monkey lives in the cedar forests of the Middle Atlas?","a":"The Barbary macaque","x":["The gelada","The hamadryas baboon"]}},"Q1868712":{"t":"Sierra Leone's rooftop above the rainforest","s":"In north-eastern Sierra Leone the forest suddenly climbs onto a granite massif. The Loma Mountains carry the country's highest peak, Bintumani, about 1,945 m, whose rounded top is covered not in trees but in grassland. Montane forest on the slopes shelters chimpanzees, and not far to the north-east, in the highlands of Guinea, the great Niger River begins its journey.","w":"A dark green massif with grassy, rounded tops rising from the forest and farmland of north-eastern Sierra Leone.","f":["Bintumani, also called Loma Mansa, is the highest point of Sierra Leone.","The source of the Niger lies in the Guinea Highlands, just across the border to the north-east."],"q":{"q":"What is the highest peak of Sierra Leone?","a":"Bintumani","x":["Mount Nimba","Mount Loura"]}},"Q533041":{"t":"Blinding salt flats where a great lake once lay","s":"The Makgadikgadi looks like a vast white stain on the Kalahari, blinding under the midday sun. These salt pans are the dried-out floor of a huge ancient lake, and together they rank among the largest salt flats on Earth. After good rains a thin sheet of water returns, and tens of thousands of flamingos arrive to feed and breed.","w":"Brilliant white flats edged by grey-green scrub in north-eastern Botswana, with two main pans lying side by side.","f":["The two largest pans are called Sowa and Ntwetwe.","Kubu Island, a granite outcrop crowned with old baobabs, rises from the salt like a ship.","Large herds of zebra migrate to the grasslands around the pans in the rainy season."],"q":{"q":"What did the Makgadikgadi Pans once hold?","a":"A huge lake","x":["A volcanic caldera","A shallow sea gulf"]}},"Q1067917":{"t":"A black stain of lava in the middle of the Sahara","s":"In the pale centre of Libya lies a dark blot that looks spilled across the sand. Haruj is a vast volcanic field of black basalt, some 40,000 km² of lava flows dotted with more than a hundred cones and craters. Its full Arabic name, al-Haruj al-Aswad, means 'the black Haruj'. Against the surrounding desert, its outline is unmistakable even from orbit.","w":"A huge dark patch of lava fields and small cones in central Libya, sharply outlined against the pale desert around it.","f":["Al-Haruj al-Aswad means 'the black Haruj' in Arabic.","It is the largest volcanic field in Libya."],"q":{"q":"Which colour gives al-Haruj al-Aswad its name?","a":"Black","x":["Red","White"]}},"Q1740661":{"t":"Folded hills where Namibia's plateau drops to the Namib","s":"West of Windhoek the land crumples into endless ridges of grey-brown rock and golden grass. The Khomas Highland sits at around 2,000 m, then breaks off abruptly towards the Namib Desert in a great escarpment, crossed by dizzy passes such as Spreetshoogte. The contrast is striking: dry grassland on top, pale gravel plains far below and, further west, the dunes.","w":"Tightly folded brown ridges west of Windhoek, ending in a steep escarpment above the flat, pale Namib plains.","f":["The H.E.S.S. gamma-ray telescopes stand in the Khomas Highland, which is known for dark, clear skies.","Windhoek, Namibia's capital, lies at about 1,650 m on the eastern side of the highland."],"q":{"q":"Which capital city lies on the edge of the Khomas Highland?","a":"Windhoek","x":["Gaborone","Lusaka"]}},"Q1343339":{"t":"Sandstone arches and desert crocodiles in the Sahara","s":"Wind and ancient water have carved the Ennedi into a maze of sandstone towers, canyons and natural arches rising from the Sahara in north-eastern Chad. One arch, Aloba, stands roughly 120 m tall, among the highest in the world. In a shady gorge, the Guelta d'Archei, camels drink from a permanent pool where a few crocodiles still survive, relics of a greener Sahara.","w":"A dark, deeply dissected block of sandstone with pale sand filling its canyons, rising from the flat desert of north-eastern Chad.","f":["The Ennedi Massif became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2016.","Thousands of rock paintings show cattle, giraffes and people from the time of the green Sahara.","Its crocodiles are among the last left anywhere in the Sahara."],"q":{"q":"Which animals survive in the Guelta d'Archei pool?","a":"Crocodiles","x":["Hippos","Manatees"]}},"Q811142":{"t":"A cool granite island above Nigeria's plains","s":"Rising abruptly from the savanna, the Jos Plateau is a granite tableland around 1,200 m high, noticeably cooler than the country around it. Scattered across it are dozens of small lakes, many of them flooded pits left by more than a century of tin mining. Rivers flow off the plateau in every direction, feeding both the Niger and Lake Chad.","w":"A raised plateau of granite knobs and scattered small lakes, rising sharply from the surrounding savanna in central Nigeria.","f":["The Nok culture, famous for terracotta heads over 2,000 years old, is named after a village near the plateau.","Large-scale tin mining began here in the early 20th century.","The city of Jos lies at about 1,200 m."],"q":{"q":"Which ancient culture, known for terracotta heads, is linked to this area?","a":"Nok","x":["Ife","Benin"]}},"Q1878522":{"t":"Lake-riddled tundra between two Arctic bays","s":"Between the long Gulf of Ob and the Taz Bay, the land seems to dissolve into water. The Taz Peninsula is flat tundra on permafrost, pocked with thousands of lakes that glitter like scattered glass when the sun is low. In winter it all turns into a single white sheet. Beneath it lies Yamburg, one of the giant gas fields of northern Siberia.","w":"Flat, lake-speckled tundra between two wide Arctic bays: the Gulf of Ob to the west and the Taz Bay to the east.","f":["Most of the peninsula lies north of the Arctic Circle.","The Yamburg gas field, one of the largest in the world, lies on the peninsula."],"q":{"q":"Which gulf lies west of the Taz Peninsula?","a":"The Gulf of Ob","x":["The Yenisei Gulf","The Gulf of Bothnia"]}},"Q1470027":{"t":"Where the Great Plains run into a wall of mountains","s":"Flying west across the Great Plains, the first mountains you meet rise like a wall: the Front Range of the Rockies. Denver, Boulder and Colorado Springs line its foot, and several summits top 4,000 m. Pikes Peak, 4,302 m, stands out at the southern end; the view from its top in 1893 inspired the song 'America the Beautiful'.","w":"The first high, often snow-streaked ridges west of the flat plains, with Denver and Colorado Springs spread along their foot.","f":["Katharine Lee Bates wrote 'America the Beautiful' after reaching the top of Pikes Peak in 1893.","The highest summit of the Front Range is Grays Peak, about 4,350 m.","Pikes Peak is named after Zebulon Pike, who explored the region in 1806."],"q":{"q":"Which song was inspired by the view from Pikes Peak?","a":"America the Beautiful","x":["The Star-Spangled Banner","Home on the Range"]}},"Q879014":{"t":"The ridges that nearly stopped Lewis and Clark","s":"In September 1805, Lewis and Clark's expedition struggled over these mountains on snowy trails, short of food. The Bitterroot Range still looks forbidding: long, forested ridges and deep valleys stretching along the Idaho–Montana border, crossed by very few roads. In late spring the valleys bloom with pink bitterroot flowers, the plant that gave the range its name.","w":"Long, dark-green forested ridges running along the Idaho–Montana border, with few clearings and almost no roads.","f":["The bitterroot, Montana's state flower, is called Lewisia rediviva after Meriwether Lewis.","The expedition crossed on the Lolo Trail, an old Nez Perce route.","The Selway-Bitterroot Wilderness here is one of the largest wilderness areas in the lower 48 states."],"q":{"q":"Which expedition crossed the Bitterroot Range in 1805?","a":"Lewis and Clark","x":["Zebulon Pike","John C. Frémont"]}},"Q1585051":{"t":"Europe's largest high plateau, land of wild reindeer","s":"West of Oslo the land rises into a vast, treeless tableland of lakes and bare rock. Hardangervidda is the largest mountain plateau in Europe, mostly between 1,100 and 1,400 m high, and it holds Europe's biggest herd of wild reindeer. In winter it becomes one white plain; in summer, a patchwork of grey stone, green moss and countless blue lakes.","w":"A broad, treeless plateau speckled with lakes, with the Hardangerjøkulen ice cap on its northern edge.","f":["Hardangervidda National Park is the largest national park on the Norwegian mainland.","The Bergen Railway crosses its northern edge, reaching about 1,220 m at Finse."],"q":{"q":"Which wild animals form Europe's largest herd on Hardangervidda?","a":"Reindeer","x":["Elk","Musk oxen"]}},"Q1464606":{"t":"A dark island of mountains in Namibia's dry south","s":"Southern Namibia is mostly flat, stony semi-desert, which makes the Great Karas Mountains stand out like an island. Their dark, flat-topped ridges rise well above the plains, deeply cut by gorges that carry water only after rare storms. The Fish River Canyon, one of the largest canyons in Africa, lies about 130 km to the west.","w":"A dark, deeply cut block of flat-topped mountains standing above pale plains, south-east of the town of Keetmanshoop.","f":["Keetmanshoop, to the north-west, is known for its nearby forest of quiver trees.","Namibia's southernmost region, ǁKaras, takes its name from these mountains."],"q":{"q":"Which famous canyon lies west of the Karas Mountains?","a":"Fish River Canyon","x":["Blyde River Canyon","Olduvai Gorge"]}},"Q886772":{"t":"A volcanic massif with crater lakes in Sudan's west","s":"In the dry savanna of western Sudan rises a mountain island of volcanic rock. Jebel Marra reaches about 3,000 m, the highest point in Sudan, and at its top lies the Deriba caldera, some 5 km wide, holding two small crater lakes. The height catches rain the plains never get, so streams, orchards and green terraces cling to its slopes.","w":"A large, dark volcanic massif with a round summit caldera, rising far above the dry brown plains of western Sudan.","f":["Jebel Marra is the highest point of Sudan.","Citrus and other fruit trees grow on its terraced slopes."],"q":{"q":"What lies in the summit caldera of Jebel Marra?","a":"Two crater lakes","x":["A glacier","A salt pan"]}},"Q275491":{"t":"Morocco's green arc facing the Mediterranean","s":"The Rif curves along Morocco's Mediterranean coast like a drawn bow, green on its seaward slopes and browner inland. Geologically it is only half of an arc: the same mountain belt swings across the Strait of Gibraltar and continues in southern Spain as the Betic Cordillera. On the highest ridges, above 2,000 m, grow forests of cedar and the rare Moroccan fir.","w":"A curved belt of steep green-and-brown ridges along Morocco's Mediterranean shore, between Tangier and the Algerian border.","f":["The highest summit, Jbel Tidirhine, reaches about 2,450 m.","The blue-washed town of Chefchaouen lies in the western Rif.","With Spain's Betic Cordillera, the Rif forms the Gibraltar Arc."],"q":{"q":"Which Spanish mountains continue the Rif across the Strait of Gibraltar?","a":"The Betic Cordillera","x":["The Pyrenees","The Cantabrian Mountains"]}},"Q152294":{"t":"Zimbabwe's misty rooftop in the Eastern Highlands","s":"Along Zimbabwe's eastern border the land rises into cool green uplands of grassland, pine plantations and tea estates. Their summit is Mount Nyangani, 2,592 m, the highest point in the country: a broad, heath-covered hilltop often wrapped in mist. To the south, the Mtarazi Falls drop over the escarpment in one of the highest waterfalls in Africa.","w":"Green rolling uplands with dark pine plantations, rising to a broad summit on Zimbabwe's border with Mozambique.","f":["Mount Nyangani is the highest point of Zimbabwe.","Tea grows in the Honde Valley below the escarpment."],"q":{"q":"Mount Nyangani is the highest point of which country?","a":"Zimbabwe","x":["Zambia","Malawi"]}},"Q745846":{"t":"Rocks that gave their name to a geological period","s":"A giant oval rim of rock rises out of the red land at the heart of the Flinders Ranges: Wilpena Pound, a natural amphitheatre about 17 km long. These folded ridges run for more than 400 km north from the Spencer Gulf. In nearby hills scientists found fossils of some of the earliest complex animals, and in 2004 a whole geological period, the Ediacaran, was named after them.","w":"Parallel red and grey ridges running north from the head of Spencer Gulf, with the oval rim of Wilpena Pound in the middle.","f":["The Ediacaran, named after the Ediacara Hills, was the first new geological period added in over a century.","Wilpena Pound is a bowl-shaped fold of rock layers, not a volcanic crater.","The Adnyamathanha people call Wilpena Pound Ikara."],"q":{"q":"Which geological period is named after hills in the Flinders Ranges?","a":"The Ediacaran","x":["The Cambrian","The Devonian"]}},"Q1475441":{"t":"Red ribs of rock around Alice Springs","s":"Right in the middle of Australia, long red ridges run east and west of Alice Springs like the ribs of a buried giant. The MacDonnell Ranges stretch for about 640 km, sliced by deep gaps and gorges where rivers cut straight through them. Among them rises the Finke, often called one of the oldest rivers in the world, which flows only after rain.","w":"Long, parallel red ridges running east–west through the centre of Australia, with the small grid of Alice Springs in a gap.","f":["Mount Zeil, about 1,530 m, is the highest point in the Northern Territory.","Explorer John McDouall Stuart named the ranges in 1860 after Governor Richard MacDonnell.","The Arrernte people are the traditional owners of the Alice Springs area."],"q":{"q":"Which town lies in a gap of the MacDonnell Ranges?","a":"Alice Springs","x":["Tennant Creek","Katherine"]}},"Q21961599":{"t":"The wooded scarp that walls in Perth","s":"East of Perth, the land rises in a long, dark-green wall: the Darling Scarp, the edge of the Darling Range. It follows the Darling Fault, the boundary between the ancient rocks of the Yilgarn Craton and the younger sands of the coastal plain. On top grow jarrah forests, broken by reservoirs that supply Perth with water and by the red scars of bauxite mines.","w":"A low, forested scarp running north–south just east of Perth, with the city and coastal plain to the west and reservoirs on top.","f":["Its highest point, Mount Cooke, is only about 580 m high.","Jarrah, a eucalyptus with deep red wood, grows naturally only in south-western Australia.","The Yilgarn Craton behind the scarp holds rocks well over 2.5 billion years old."],"q":{"q":"Which city lies at the foot of the Darling Range?","a":"Perth","x":["Adelaide","Darwin"]}},"Q649051":{"t":"A volcanic spine along Sumatra's western shore","s":"Sumatra has a backbone, and it runs close to the island's western coast for some 1,700 km. The Barisan Mountains are a chain of forested ridges and smoking volcanoes, with the Indian Ocean on one side and wide lowland plains on the other. Their highest point, Mount Kerinci, about 3,800 m, is the tallest volcano in Indonesia, and tigers still roam the forests around it.","w":"Steep forested ridges and volcanic cones hugging Sumatra's west coast, with the Indian Ocean close by on one side.","f":["Barisan means 'row' or 'line' in Indonesian.","Three national parks in the range form the UNESCO site Tropical Rainforest Heritage of Sumatra.","The Great Sumatran Fault runs along the length of the range."],"q":{"q":"What does the Indonesian word barisan mean?","a":"Row or line","x":["Fire","Forest"]}},"Q733842":{"t":"Siberian glaciers on the divide of two oceans","s":"Between the frozen uplands around Oymyakon and the Sea of Okhotsk rises one of the harshest ranges in Siberia. The Suntar-Khayata reaches nearly 3,000 m at Mus-Khaya and carries some two hundred small glaciers. It forms part of the divide between rivers draining to the Arctic Ocean and those running to the Sea of Okhotsk. Its grey, snow-streaked summits show no roads at all.","w":"Grey, snow-streaked jagged peaks with small glaciers, between the Yakutian uplands and the Okhotsk coast.","f":["Its highest peak, Mus-Khaya, is about 2,960 m high.","Oymyakon, one of the coldest inhabited places on Earth, lies to the north of the range."],"q":{"q":"Which village, famous for extreme cold, lies near the Suntar-Khayata?","a":"Oymyakon","x":["Dikson","Pevek"]}},"Q1557099":{"t":"Remote ridges above a field of summer ice","s":"A white field of ice lying in green tundra in midsummer: that is the Moma aufeis, one of the largest in the world, built up each winter as seeping river and ground water freezes layer upon layer. Above it rise the Momsky mountains, bare grey ridges running for hundreds of kilometres through north-eastern Yakutia, almost untouched by people.","w":"Bare grey ridges running north-west to south-east in north-eastern Yakutia, parallel to the higher Chersky Range to the south-west.","f":["An aufeis forms when water seeps out onto river ice in winter and freezes in thick layers.","The Moma River flows between the Momsky and Chersky ranges into the Indigirka."],"q":{"q":"What is an aufeis, like the one in the Moma valley?","a":"Layers of frozen seeping water","x":["A glacier from the peaks","A frozen crater lake"]}},"Q11948856":{"t":"Bare ridges named after a surprising language","s":"South of Quetta, Balochistan's ridges run in long, parallel folds of bare rock, grey and ochre under a hard sun. These are the Central Brahui Ranges, named after the Brahui people. Their language is one of the great puzzles of linguistics: it belongs to the Dravidian family, whose other members are spoken mainly in southern India, well over a thousand kilometres away.","w":"Long, parallel folds of bare grey and ochre rock running north–south around the town of Kalat, south of Quetta.","f":["Brahui is a Dravidian language, related to Tamil and Telugu.","These folded ridges were pushed up as the Indian plate collided with Eurasia."],"q":{"q":"To which language family does Brahui belong?","a":"Dravidian","x":["Indo-Aryan","Turkic"]}},"Q2062939":{"t":"The 'black mountains' reaching into the Kazakh steppe","s":"Karatau means 'black mountains', and the ridges do look dark against the pale steppe around them. This long arm of the Tian Shan pushes some 400 km north-west into southern Kazakhstan. Its rocks hold rich phosphate deposits and a famous Jurassic lake bed full of fossil insects. At its south-western foot lies Turkistan, an ancient Silk Road city.","w":"A long, dark ridge running north-west into the pale steppe, with the Syr Darya valley and the city of Turkistan to the south-west.","f":["The mausoleum of Khoja Ahmed Yasawi in Turkistan is a UNESCO World Heritage site.","The range holds large phosphorite deposits, mined for fertiliser.","Fossil insects from Karatau's Jurassic lake beds, about 160 million years old, are studied worldwide."],"q":{"q":"What does the Turkic name Karatau mean?","a":"Black mountains","x":["White mountains","Red mountains"]}},"Q784189":{"t":"The mountain wall on the western edge of the Indus","s":"West of the Indus, the flat green plain of Punjab ends against a long wall of bare, folded rock: the Sulaiman Mountains. They run for hundreds of kilometres north to south, brown and ochre, with snow on the highest crests in winter. Their best-known summit, Takht-e-Sulaiman, about 3,500 m high, is the 'Throne of Solomon', tied by legend to the biblical king.","w":"A long north–south wall of bare brown ridges rising abruptly from the green Indus plain to the east.","f":["Takht-e-Sulaiman means 'Throne of Solomon'.","Dera Ghazi Khan lies on the plain at the range's eastern foot.","Flash floods from its dry valleys spread wide fans of gravel onto the Indus plain."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Takht-e-Sulaiman mean?","a":"Throne of Solomon","x":["Crown of Solomon","Temple of Solomon"]}},"Q5061389":{"t":"Combed ridges where Alexander's army suffered","s":"From above, the Makran looks combed: long, parallel ridges of bare rock, grey, tan and pink, separated by dry valleys. The Central Makran Range runs east–west through southern Balochistan, part of a vast belt of folds pushed up as the Arabian Sea floor slides beneath Asia. In 325 BC Alexander the Great led his army back from India across this land, at a terrible cost.","w":"Parallel, striped ridges of bare rock running east–west, with narrow valleys between them, inland from the Arabian Sea coast.","f":["The ancient Greeks called this dry coastland Gedrosia.","The Makran folds rise above a zone where the Arabian plate dives beneath Eurasia."],"q":{"q":"Which ancient conqueror crossed the Makran desert in 325 BC?","a":"Alexander the Great","x":["Cyrus the Great","Darius I"]}},"Q2140102":{"t":"The dry wall between the Indus and Balochistan","s":"The Kirthar Mountains form a clean edge: to the east, the flat, irrigated Indus plain of Sindh; to the west, rank after rank of bare, tilted ridges. They run north–south for roughly 300 km along the boundary of Sindh and Balochistan. Rain is scarce, yet the range shelters wild goats, urial sheep and one of Pakistan's largest national parks.","w":"Bare, tilted brown ridges running north–south, with the flat green strip of the Indus plain along their eastern side.","f":["Kirthar National Park, created in 1974, protects Sindh ibex and urial.","Gorakh Hill, at about 1,700 m, is one of the highest points in Sindh."],"q":{"q":"Which river's plain lies east of the Kirthar Mountains?","a":"The Indus","x":["The Ganges","The Helmand"]}},"Q328072":{"t":"A long fence between the Karakum and the Iranian plateau","s":"The Kopet Dag rises like a fence between two worlds: to the north, the sands of the Karakum Desert; to the south, the high Iranian plateau. For some 600 km its folded ridges run north-west to south-east, and along the northern foot grew a string of oases fed by mountain springs. Ashgabat, Turkmenistan's white-marble capital, sits right against the range.","w":"A long, straight line of folded brown ridges, with the flat Karakum Desert to the north and Ashgabat at their foot.","f":["The ruins of Nisa, an early Parthian capital near Ashgabat, are a UNESCO World Heritage site.","Akhal-Teke horses, famed for their metallic sheen, were bred in the oases at its foot."],"q":{"q":"Which desert lies north of the Kopet Dag?","a":"The Karakum","x":["The Taklamakan","The Dasht-e Kavir"]}},"Q172556":{"t":"Turkey's limestone wall above the Mediterranean","s":"Behind Turkey's Mediterranean coast rises a long barrier of limestone: the Taurus Mountains, curving for hundreds of kilometres from Antalya towards the Euphrates. Their bare grey peaks hold snow into early summer, and deep gorges cut down to the sea. For millennia a narrow pass, the Cilician Gates, linked Anatolia with the Levant; Alexander the Great marched through it in 333 BC.","w":"Grey limestone ridges with snowy crests running parallel to Turkey's southern coast, between the blue Mediterranean and the Anatolian plateau.","f":["The Cilician Gates pass lies north of the city of Tarsus.","The Tigris rises in the Eastern Taurus.","The highest summits, in the Aladağlar massif, reach about 3,750 m."],"q":{"q":"Which pass through the Taurus did Alexander the Great use?","a":"The Cilician Gates","x":["The Khyber Pass","Thermopylae"]}},"Q11748964":{"t":"The Carpathians' granite crown beyond green Beskid ridges","s":"Along the Polish–Slovak border, soft green ridges of the Beskids roll one behind another, covered in spruce and beech. Further east the land suddenly hardens into the Tatras: a compact block of grey granite peaks, streaked with snow and dotted with glacial lakes. Gerlachovský štít, 2,655 m, in the Slovak Tatras, is the highest point of the entire Carpathian arc.","w":"Rolling forested ridges near the Czech–Polish–Slovak borders, rising further east into a compact cluster of bare grey peaks.","f":["Rysy, about 2,500 m, is Poland's highest mountain.","Morskie Oko is the best known of the Tatras' many glacial lakes.","Babia Góra, about 1,725 m, is the highest summit of the Western Beskids."],"q":{"q":"What is the highest peak of the Tatras and the whole Carpathians?","a":"Gerlachovský štít","x":["Rysy","Babia Góra"]}},"Q201582":{"t":"The rocky backbone of mainland Greece","s":"The Pindus runs down the middle of northern Greece like a backbone, separating Epirus from Thessaly. Its limestone and dark ophiolite ridges hide some of Europe's wildest country, where brown bears and wolves still live. Here too is the Vikos Gorge, famous for its depth compared with its width. On the range's eastern edge, the rock towers of Meteora carry monasteries on their tops.","w":"A long, rugged north–south ridge through northern Greece, with the flat Thessaly plain to the east and Ioannina's lake to the west.","f":["Smolikas, about 2,640 m, is Greece's second-highest mountain after Olympus.","Guinness World Records has listed the Vikos Gorge as the deepest gorge relative to its width."],"q":{"q":"Which monasteries stand on rock towers at the edge of the Pindus?","a":"Meteora","x":["Mount Athos","Hosios Loukas"]}},"Q3658589":{"t":"A cold, wet plateau rising from the boreal forest","s":"In the far north of Alberta, a broad plateau rises several hundred metres above endless boreal lowlands. The Caribou Mountains are not peaks at all but a high tableland of lakes and bogs, cold enough that patches of permafrost survive under the peat, far south of the true Arctic. Woodland caribou, which gave the range its name, still roam its spruce and lichen.","w":"A broad raised plateau speckled with small lakes and bogs in northern Alberta, north of the Peace River.","f":["Much of the plateau is protected as the Caribou Mountains Wildland Provincial Park.","Wood Buffalo National Park, the largest in Canada, lies to the east."],"q":{"q":"What survives under the bogs of the Caribou Mountains?","a":"Patches of permafrost","x":["Hot springs","Salt domes"]}},"Q283784":{"t":"Dark granite spires on the road to the Arctic","s":"North of Dawson City, the Dempster Highway climbs into the Ogilvie Mountains, and the only road in the region becomes a thin gravel thread through the tundra. The most striking peaks stand in Tombstone Territorial Park, where black granite spires rise above green valleys and rust-red slopes. In September the tundra turns scarlet and gold, one of the great autumn shows of the north.","w":"Rugged grey and black peaks with tundra valleys north of the Yukon River, crossed by a single gravel road heading north.","f":["The mountains are named after William Ogilvie, a Canadian surveyor of the Klondike era.","The Dempster Highway runs about 740 km from near Dawson City to Inuvik.","Tombstone Mountain takes its name from its dark, slab-like summit."],"q":{"q":"Which road crosses the Ogilvie Mountains towards the Arctic?","a":"The Dempster Highway","x":["The Alaska Highway","The Dalton Highway"]}},"Q616559":{"t":"Giant volcanoes in America's largest national park","s":"The Wrangells are volcanoes on a colossal scale: broad, ice-covered giants rising above 4,000 m from the Copper River basin. Mount Wrangell, a vast shield volcano, still vents steam near its summit, while Mount Blackburn, the highest at about 5,000 m, is one of the tallest volcanoes in the United States. All of it lies within Wrangell–St. Elias, the country's largest national park.","w":"Massive ice-capped domes rising above a broad river basin, with long glaciers spreading from their flanks, east of the Copper River.","f":["Wrangell–St. Elias National Park covers about 53,000 km², more than Switzerland.","The abandoned Kennecott copper mines lie at the southern foot of the range.","Mount Wrangell has an ice-filled caldera at its summit."],"q":{"q":"Which is the largest national park in the United States?","a":"Wrangell–St. Elias","x":["Yellowstone","Denali"]}},"Q166755":{"t":"Ridge behind ridge, fading into blue haze","s":"On a summer day the Blue Ridge looks exactly like its name: ridge behind ridge, each a paler shade of blue until they merge with the sky. The haze comes partly from natural compounds released by the dense forests. These old, rounded mountains are part of the Appalachians and hold Mount Mitchell, 2,037 m, the highest summit east of the Mississippi.","w":"Layer upon layer of rounded, forested ridges in shades of blue, running north-east to south-west through the southern Appalachians.","f":["The Blue Ridge Parkway winds for about 755 km along the mountains.","Great Smoky Mountains National Park, in the Blue Ridge, is the most visited national park in the United States.","Much of the blue haze comes from isoprene, a gas given off by trees."],"q":{"q":"Which is the highest peak east of the Mississippi?","a":"Mount Mitchell","x":["Mount Washington","Clingmans Dome"]}},"Q1744263":{"t":"A tangle of wild ridges between Shasta and the Pacific","s":"West of Mount Shasta's white cone, the land breaks into a tangle of steep, forested ridges with no obvious pattern: the Klamath Mountains. Unlike the young volcanoes next door, they are built from ancient slices of ocean crust and island arcs pushed onto the continent. Wild rivers such as the Klamath and the Trinity cut deep canyons through them, and their forests are famous for their many kinds of conifer.","w":"A jumble of dark green, steep ridges between the snowy cone of Mount Shasta and the Pacific coast, on the California–Oregon border.","f":["The range includes the Trinity Alps, the Siskiyou Mountains and the Marble Mountains.","Serpentine rock from the old sea floor supports unusual plants found almost nowhere else."],"q":{"q":"Which snowy volcano rises just east of the Klamath Mountains?","a":"Mount Shasta","x":["Mount Hood","Mount Rainier"]}},"Q2033447":{"t":"Rare east–west ridges in the heart of America","s":"Most American mountain ranges run roughly north–south, but the Ouachitas break the rule. Their long, forested ridges run east to west across western Arkansas and south-eastern Oklahoma, like parallel waves frozen in stone. They were folded up some 300 million years ago. At their eastern end, springs at the town of Hot Springs bring water to the surface at over 60 °C.","w":"Long, parallel forested ridges running east–west, with reservoirs in the valleys, west of Little Rock.","f":["The Ouachitas are known for their clear quartz crystals.","The hot springs at their eastern edge were set aside as a federal reservation in 1832."],"q":{"q":"In which direction do the Ouachita ridges run?","a":"East–west","x":["North–south","North-east to south-west"]}},"Q7405538":{"t":"Deep canyons around the 'River of No Return'","s":"Central Idaho is a sea of forested ridges, and the Salmon River Mountains sit at its heart, cut by some of the deepest canyons in North America. The Salmon River loops around them in a gorge that earned it the name 'River of No Return': early boats could float down but not come back up. Much of the range lies inside the largest wilderness area in the lower 48 states.","w":"A maze of steep, forested ridges and deep, twisting canyons in central Idaho, with almost no roads.","f":["The Frank Church–River of No Return Wilderness covers about 9,600 km².","The Middle Fork of the Salmon is one of the classic whitewater rivers of the United States."],"q":{"q":"What nickname does the Salmon River carry?","a":"The River of No Return","x":["The River of Gold","The Big Muddy"]}},"Q186910":{"t":"The long cliff edge of the Colorado Plateau","s":"Here the Colorado Plateau simply stops. The Mogollon Rim is an escarpment running some 300 km across central Arizona, with cliffs and steep slopes dropping hundreds of metres to the lower desert country around Payson. On top spreads one of the largest ponderosa pine forests on Earth; below, the land turns to scrub and cactus. The line between the two is sharp.","w":"A long, dark-green forested edge with a steep drop to the south, running north-west to south-east across central Arizona.","f":["The rim is named after Juan Ignacio Flores Mogollón, an 18th-century Spanish governor of New Mexico.","Novelist Zane Grey set several westerns on the rim, where he kept a cabin."],"q":{"q":"Which tree dominates the forests on top of the Mogollon Rim?","a":"Ponderosa pine","x":["Giant sequoia","Joshua tree"]}},"Q931210":{"t":"The Andes' last high branch, between lake and plains","s":"The Andes make their final high push in Venezuela. The Cordillera de Mérida slants north-east for some 400 km, separating the hot basin of Lake Maracaibo from the grassy Llanos. Its crest carries the country's only peaks near 5,000 m, and below them spread the páramos: cold, wet moors of small lakes and silver-leaved frailejón plants.","w":"A long, high ridge slanting north-east, with the vast blue Lake Maracaibo on one side and flat green plains on the other.","f":["Its three highest summits, Pico Bolívar, Humboldt and Bonpland, all rise above 4,800 m.","The city of Mérida lies deep inside the range, in the valley of the Chama River."],"q":{"q":"Which huge lake lies west of the Cordillera de Mérida?","a":"Lake Maracaibo","x":["Lake Valencia","Lake Titicaca"]}},"Q1143026":{"t":"A gentle 'knife' ridge across Uruguay's grasslands","s":"Uruguay has no real mountains, only long, gentle ridges that locals call cuchillas, 'knives'. The Cuchilla Grande is the main one, running north-east through the east of the country towards Brazil. From the air it is barely a ripple: rolling green pasture, grey rock outcrops, lines of eucalyptus and scattered cattle ranches. Its highest point, Cerro Catedral, is only about 514 m.","w":"Low, grassy, undulating hills with rocky outcrops and eucalyptus stands, running north-east through eastern Uruguay.","f":["Cerro Catedral, about 514 m, is the highest point of Uruguay.","Uruguay has roughly three times as many cattle as people."],"q":{"q":"What does the Spanish word cuchilla literally mean?","a":"Knife","x":["Saddle","Spine"]}},"Q2120979":{"t":"Rainforest mountains named after a Dutch queen","s":"Almost nothing but forest surrounds the Wilhelmina Mountains, a line of granite ridges rising from the unbroken green canopy of central Suriname. Their highest point, Julianatop, a little over 1,200 m, is also the highest point in the country. The whole area lies within the Central Suriname Nature Reserve, one of the largest stretches of untouched tropical forest on Earth.","w":"Dark green forested ridges rising above an unbroken sea of rainforest in the centre of Suriname, with no roads or clearings.","f":["The range is named after Queen Wilhelmina of the Netherlands, and Julianatop after her daughter Juliana.","The Central Suriname Nature Reserve, about 16,000 km², became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2000."],"q":{"q":"Julianatop is the highest point of which country?","a":"Suriname","x":["Guyana","French Guiana"]}},"Q3621380":{"t":"Forest hills where the Essequibo is born","s":"On the southern edge of Guyana, along the border with Brazil, a line of forested hills forms a watershed deep in the rainforest. The Acarai Mountains are modest in height, but they matter: from their northern slopes flows the Essequibo, Guyana's greatest river, while streams on the southern side run towards the Amazon. The green canopy continues unbroken in every direction.","w":"Low, forest-covered hills along the Guyana–Brazil border, with the green canopy stretching unbroken in every direction.","f":["The Essequibo, which rises here, is Guyana's longest river, about 1,000 km long.","The Wai-Wai people live in the forests near the river's headwaters."],"q":{"q":"Which river rises in the Acarai Mountains?","a":"The Essequibo","x":["The Orinoco","The Rio Negro"]}},"Q5761832":{"t":"A sandstone plateau of savanna in the rainforest","s":"In southern Pará the land rises onto the Serra do Cachimbo, a broad sandstone plateau in the Amazon basin. On its poor, sandy soils the rainforest gives way to open savanna and scrub, so it shows as paler patches in the dark green. Rivers drain from it towards the Tapajós and the Xingu, and the long Cuiabá–Santarém highway crosses its back.","w":"Paler patches of open savanna and bare sandstone amid dark rainforest in southern Pará, crossed by a north–south highway.","f":["The BR-163 highway, linking Cuiabá with Santarém on the Amazon, crosses the plateau.","Its sandy soils support savanna vegetation, unusual in the middle of the rainforest."],"q":{"q":"What vegetation covers much of the Cachimbo plateau?","a":"Open savanna","x":["Mangrove","Pine forest"]}},"Q22024985":{"t":"Cerrado highlands between Brasília and Goiânia","s":"Brazil's high central plateau rises here into ridges and flat-topped tablelands about 1,000 m above sea level. This is cerrado country: a savanna of twisted trees and grass, now largely broken up by vast rectangles of soy and cattle pasture, with red earth showing between them. Not far away, near Brasília, the rain divides between three of South America's great river basins.","w":"Red earth, rectangular fields and patches of scrubby savanna on a high plateau between Brasília and Goiânia.","f":["The cerrado is considered the most species-rich savanna on Earth.","Near Brasília, the Águas Emendadas wetland feeds the Tocantins, Paraná and São Francisco basins."],"q":{"q":"What is the savanna of Brazil's central plateau called?","a":"Cerrado","x":["Caatinga","Pantanal"]}},"Q766931":{"t":"A rugged peninsula beside the Pacific's highest tides","s":"At the top of the Sea of Okhotsk, the Taygonos Peninsula juts south between two long bays like a clenched fist of mountains. On its eastern side lies Penzhina Bay, where the tides are the highest in the whole Pacific, rising and falling by more than ten metres. The peninsula itself is roadless and almost uninhabited: bare ridges, tundra and, in winter, ice-choked shores.","w":"A mountainous peninsula pointing south into the northern Sea of Okhotsk, between two long, narrow bays.","f":["Tides in Penzhina Bay can reach about 13 m, the highest in the Pacific Ocean.","The peninsula separates Gizhiga Bay from Penzhina Bay, both arms of Shelikhov Gulf."],"q":{"q":"Which bay beside Taygonos has the Pacific's highest tides?","a":"Penzhina Bay","x":["Gizhiga Bay","Taui Bay"]}},"Q893152":{"t":"Where the magnetic north pole was first found","s":"In June 1831 James Clark Ross stood on the west coast of this peninsula and watched his compass needle point almost straight down: he had reached the North Magnetic Pole. The pole has since wandered far away, but Boothia still holds a record of its own. Its northern tip is the northernmost point of mainland North America.","w":"A broad, tundra-covered peninsula reaching north into the Arctic channels, joined to the mainland by a narrow neck of land.","f":["It is named after Felix Booth, a London gin distiller who funded the expedition.","Its northern tip, on Murchison Promontory, lies at almost 72° N.","The magnetic pole has since drifted across the Arctic Ocean towards Siberia."],"q":{"q":"Who located the North Magnetic Pole on Boothia in 1831?","a":"James Clark Ross","x":["John Franklin","William Parry"]}},"Q60010":{"t":"Icefield, fjords and salmon rivers south of Anchorage","s":"Just south of Anchorage, the Kenai Peninsula packs much of Alaska into one view. Its eastern half is buried under the Harding Icefield, which sends dozens of glaciers down towards the sea, many ending in the fjords of Kenai Fjords National Park. The western half is lower, dotted with lakes and crossed by the turquoise Kenai River, famous for its giant salmon.","w":"A large peninsula south of Anchorage: a broad white icefield on the eastern side, lake-dotted lowlands along Cook Inlet in the west.","f":["The Harding Icefield covers about 1,800 km².","The world-record king salmon, about 44 kg, was caught in the Kenai River in 1985.","The peninsula takes its name from the Kenaitze, a Dena'ina people."],"q":{"q":"Which icefield covers much of the Kenai Peninsula?","a":"The Harding Icefield","x":["The Juneau Icefield","The Stikine Icefield"]}},"Q971586":{"t":"America's tip, facing Asia across the Bering Strait","s":"The Seward Peninsula is where America reaches out towards Asia. Its western tip, Cape Prince of Wales, is the westernmost point of the mainland Americas, only about 82 km from Russia's Chukchi Peninsula across the Bering Strait. In the ice ages the sea floor here was dry land, the Bering Land Bridge, the route by which the first people are thought to have reached the Americas.","w":"A broad, treeless peninsula of tundra and low mountains pointing west towards the narrow Bering Strait.","f":["It is named after William Seward, the US Secretary of State who arranged the purchase of Alaska in 1867.","A gold rush brought thousands of prospectors to the beaches of Nome in 1899–1900.","Bering Land Bridge National Preserve protects part of the peninsula."],"q":{"q":"What is Cape Prince of Wales, the tip of the Seward Peninsula?","a":"Westernmost point of mainland America","x":["Northernmost point of Alaska","Southernmost point of Alaska"]}},"Q850245":{"t":"The water tower of West Africa","s":"Much of West Africa's water starts here. The Fouta Djallon is a highland of sandstone plateaus in Guinea, cut by deep valleys and waterfalls, and its heavy rains feed the headwaters of the Senegal and Gambia rivers, among others. Its flat-topped tablelands end in sheer cliffs, with forest in the valleys and bare, rust-coloured rock on top.","w":"Flat-topped sandstone tablelands with sheer edges and green valleys in central Guinea, inland from the Atlantic coast.","f":["The Bafing, the main headstream of the Senegal River, rises here.","In the 18th century the Fula people founded a Muslim state here, the Imamate of Futa Jallon.","Mount Loura, one of its highest summits, reaches about 1,500 m."],"q":{"q":"Which major river rises in the Fouta Djallon?","a":"The Gambia","x":["The Volta","The Congo"]}},"Q1462530":{"t":"The 'great barrier' of sandstone in the empty Sahara","s":"In Egypt's far south-western corner, a vast sandstone plateau rises some 300 m above the desert. Gilf Kebir means 'great barrier', and its sheer, dark cliffs are cut by valleys choked with golden sand. In a shallow cave on its edge, the Hungarian explorer László Almásy found paintings of people who seem to be swimming, made when the Sahara was green.","w":"A dark, cliff-edged plateau with sand-filled valleys, rising from pale desert near the corner where Egypt, Libya and Sudan meet.","f":["Almásy found the Cave of Swimmers in 1933.","The cave appears in the novel and film 'The English Patient'.","The plateau became part of Gilf Kebir National Park in 2007."],"q":{"q":"What does the Arabic name Gilf Kebir mean?","a":"Great barrier","x":["Black mountain","Red sand"]}},"Q755509":{"t":"A green volcanic tableland above the reef coast","s":"Climb west from Cairns and the rainforest opens onto a rolling green plateau of dairy farms, volcanic cones and deep crater lakes. The Atherton Tableland lies mostly between about 600 and 1,000 m, cooler and gentler than the steamy coast below. Its rich red volcanic soils grow crops from avocados to coffee, and pockets of rainforest shelter tree-kangaroos.","w":"A rolling green plateau with small round lakes and patches of dark forest, just inland from Cairns and the reef coast.","f":["Lake Eacham and Lake Barrine fill craters left by explosive volcanic eruptions.","The rainforests around it belong to the Wet Tropics of Queensland World Heritage site."],"q":{"q":"Which kangaroo lives in the rainforests of the Atherton Tableland?","a":"Lumholtz's tree-kangaroo","x":["The red kangaroo","The quokka"]}},"Q1414064":{"t":"The Volga's high right bank, hundreds of kilometres long","s":"Along the Volga, one bank is high and the other low, and the Volga Upland is the high one. From near Nizhny Novgorod down to Volgograd it rises on the river's western side as a broad, gullied plateau of chalk, clay and limestone, sometimes ending in pale cliffs above the water. At the Samara Bend the Volga loops around the forested Zhiguli Mountains, the upland's most dramatic corner.","w":"A broad upland of fields and wooded ravines along the western bank of the Volga, with the wide river and its reservoirs to the east.","f":["The Zhiguli Mountains in the Samara Bend are protected as a nature reserve and a national park.","Ulyanovsk and Saratov stand on the upland's edge above the Volga."],"q":{"q":"Which mountains does the Volga loop around at the Samara Bend?","a":"The Zhiguli Mountains","x":["The Ergeni Hills","The Ural Mountains"]}},"Q906924":{"t":"Granite and black earth on the Dnieper's high bank","s":"West of the Dnieper the land rises into a broad, gently rolling upland cut by deep river valleys. Beneath its famously fertile black soil lies the granite of the Ukrainian Shield, one of Europe's oldest rock foundations, which breaks through in rapids and small canyons on rivers such as the Southern Bug. It spreads out as a vast patchwork of fields divided by lines of trees.","w":"A gently rolling plain of large fields and wooded ravines west of the Dnieper, with rivers cutting narrow valleys into it.","f":["Its black soils, called chernozem, are among the most fertile in the world.","The poet Taras Shevchenko is buried on a hill above the Dnieper at Kaniv, on the upland's eastern edge.","The granite beneath is well over 1.5 billion years old."],"q":{"q":"What is the region's famous black soil called?","a":"Chernozem","x":["Podzol","Laterite"]}},"Q190695":{"t":"France's volcanic heartland of old, worn mountains","s":"In the middle of southern France rises a great block of old, worn-down mountains, high plateaus and extinct volcanoes. The Massif Central covers about 15% of the country. In its north, the Chaîne des Puys lines up dozens of neat volcanic cones; further south lie bare granite plateaus, deep gorges and limestone causses. The Loire and many other rivers begin here.","w":"A broad upland of rounded hills, high plateaus and volcanic cones in south-central France, between the Rhône valley and Toulouse.","f":["The Chaîne des Puys and the Limagne fault became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2018.","The Puy de Sancy, about 1,885 m, is the highest point of the Massif Central.","The Loire, France's longest river, rises on the slopes of Mont Gerbier de Jonc."],"q":{"q":"Which river rises at Mont Gerbier de Jonc in the Massif Central?","a":"The Loire","x":["The Seine","The Garonne"]}},"Q5072889":{"t":"A flat-topped table packed with Cretaceous fossils","s":"Rising from the dry scrub of north-eastern Brazil, the Chapada do Araripe is a long plateau with a dead-flat top and steep edges, like a table laid across the backlands. Its rock layers are one of the world's great fossil treasuries, holding exquisitely preserved fish, insects and pterosaurs from the Cretaceous. Springs at its foot keep the cities of Crato and Juazeiro do Norte green.","w":"A long plateau with a flat top and steep edges, rising above dry scrubland on the Ceará–Pernambuco border.","f":["Araripe became the first UNESCO Global Geopark in the Americas in 2006.","The Araripe manakin, a small bird with a red crest, lives only at the foot of this plateau.","Many of its fossils come from the Santana Formation, over 100 million years old."],"q":{"q":"What is the Chapada do Araripe famous for?","a":"Cretaceous fossils","x":["Gold mines","Hot springs"]}},"Q141513":{"t":"One of the hottest, lowest, strangest places on Earth","s":"The Danakil is where Africa is slowly tearing apart. Parts of this desert lie more than 100 m below sea level, and it is one of the hottest places on the planet. In its depths, the hot springs of Dallol paint the salt in acid yellows and greens, and the volcano Erta Ale is famous for its long-lived lava lake. Afar camel caravans still carry slabs of salt out of the basin.","w":"A vast pale basin of salt flats and dark lava fields near the Red Sea coast, in the Horn of Africa.","f":["The Danakil lies at a junction of three rifts pulling the crust apart.","Dallol holds the record for the highest average temperature measured at an inhabited place.","Salt has been cut from these flats and carried away by camel for centuries."],"q":{"q":"Which volcano in the Danakil is famous for its lava lake?","a":"Erta Ale","x":["Nyiragongo","Mount Cameroon"]}},"Q23782559":{"t":"A stony plateau where the Sahara has no sand","s":"Not all of the Sahara is sand. The Hamada de Tinrhert is a vast stony plateau near the Algeria–Libya border, swept by wind until only flat rock and a pavement of dark pebbles remain. To the north lie the dunes of the Grand Erg Oriental; to the south the land climbs towards the Tassili n'Ajjer. It looks like dark, wrinkled leather laid across the desert.","w":"A dark, flat stony plateau cut by a few dry valleys, between orange dunes to the north and rocky highlands to the south.","f":["Hamada is the Arabic term for a bare, rocky desert plateau.","The dark sheen on its stones is desert varnish, a thin coating of iron and manganese oxides."],"q":{"q":"What is a hamada?","a":"A bare rocky desert plateau","x":["A field of star dunes","A dry salt lake"]}},"Q1350167":{"t":"Red dune ridges named after a Polish explorer","s":"Long red sand ridges run side by side for tens of kilometres here, like combed hair, with pale clay pans in the hollows between them. The Strzelecki Desert spreads across the corner where South Australia, Queensland and New South Wales meet. It is named after the Polish explorer Paweł Strzelecki, best remembered for climbing and naming Australia's highest mountain, Mount Kosciuszko.","w":"Parallel red dune ridges with white clay pans between them, in the far north-east of South Australia.","f":["Strzelecki named Mount Kosciuszko in 1840 after the Polish-Lithuanian hero Tadeusz Kościuszko.","The Dog Fence, one of the longest fences in the world, crosses this corner of Australia.","The Moomba gas fields lie within the desert."],"q":{"q":"Which mountain did Paweł Strzelecki name?","a":"Mount Kosciuszko","x":["Mount Townsend","Mount Bogong"]}},"Q211839":{"t":"A thousand red dunes marching in parallel lines","s":"Crossing the Simpson Desert by track means climbing over roughly 1,100 sand dunes, one after another. From the air they form an astonishing pattern: long, straight red ridges running north-north-west for tens of kilometres, parallel like the grooves of a record, with pale clay flats in between. The desert covers some 170,000 km² in the heart of Australia, north of Lake Eyre.","w":"Endless parallel red dune ridges running north-north-west, with white salt or clay pans between them, north of Lake Eyre.","f":["Big Red, or Nappanerica, the best-known dune, stands about 40 m high.","It is named after Alfred Simpson, an Adelaide businessman who funded its aerial survey in 1929.","It is often described as the world's largest parallel dune desert."],"q":{"q":"Roughly how many dunes does a west–east crossing of the Simpson involve?","a":"About 1,100","x":["About 100","About 10,000"]}},"Q674262":{"t":"Red sandplains and spinifex in Australia's empty north","s":"The Tanami is less about towering dunes than endless flat red sand, speckled with grey-green hummocks of spinifex grass and scattered termite mounds. It spreads across the Northern Territory and into Western Australia, crossed by one long dirt road, the Tanami Track. After rare heavy rains temporary lakes appear, and the red earth turns briefly green.","w":"Vast flat red plains with a grey-green speckle of spinifex, occasional low ridges and white salt lakes, north-west of Alice Springs.","f":["The Tanami Track runs about 1,000 km from near Alice Springs to Halls Creek.","The desert is the traditional country of the Warlpiri people, among others."],"q":{"q":"Which spiky grass covers much of the Tanami Desert?","a":"Spinifex","x":["Pampas grass","Esparto"]}},"Q2088454":{"t":"A rust-red sea of dunes in southern Afghanistan","s":"South of Kandahar, the ground turns into a sea of red sand. The Registan, whose Persian name means 'land of sand', is a plateau of dunes tens of metres high, coloured rust and orange by iron oxide. Along its western edge the Helmand River draws a green line of irrigated fields through the desert, a vivid stripe against the red.","w":"A broad field of rust-red dunes south of Kandahar, with the green ribbon of the Helmand valley along its western edge.","f":["Registan means 'land of sand' in Persian.","The Helmand, Afghanistan's longest river, skirts the desert's western edge."],"q":{"q":"What does the Persian name Registan mean?","a":"Land of sand","x":["Land of salt","Land of the wind"]}},"Q2356478":{"t":"The 'Desert of Death' between two rivers","s":"Its name, Dasht-e Margo, means 'desert of death', and from above it is easy to see why: a vast expanse of wind-scoured clay, gravel and low dunes in south-western Afghanistan, with barely a track across it. Two rivers frame it, the Helmand along its southern side and the Khash Rud to the north. Both end in the marshy Sistan basin on the Iranian border.","w":"A vast pale expanse of clay flats, gravel and low dunes, bordered by the green thread of the Helmand valley to the south.","f":["In Persian, dasht means 'plain' or 'desert' and marg means 'death'.","The Helmand and the Khash Rud both run out into the lakes and marshes of the Sistan basin."],"q":{"q":"What does the name Dasht-e Margo mean?","a":"Desert of Death","x":["Desert of Salt","Desert of Stones"]}},"Q272514":{"t":"Iran's great salt desert, a crust over hidden mud","s":"In the heart of the Iranian plateau lies a pale, cracked wilderness of salt. The Dasht-e Kavir, the Great Salt Desert, covers roughly 77,000 km². Much of it is a crust of salt over treacherous mud, which is why travellers have always kept to its edges. It looks like frosted glass, streaked with brown where old stream channels and patches of clay break the white.","w":"A huge, pale, salt-crusted basin in north-central Iran, south-east of Tehran, fringed by brown hills.","f":["Kavir is the Persian word for a salt marsh.","Old caravanserais, such as Maranjab, stand on its fringes along former trade routes."],"q":{"q":"Why did caravans keep to the edges of the Dasht-e Kavir?","a":"Its salt crust hides soft mud","x":["Active volcanoes","Constant sandstorms"]}},"Q749205":{"t":"Brick-red dunes above ancient rock art","s":"The Great Nafud is famous for the colour of its sand, a deep brick red from iron oxide, and for its huge crescent-shaped dunes, carved and recarved by strong winds. It fills a broad basin in northern Saudi Arabia. On its southern edge, around the oasis of Jubbah, the rocks carry thousands of carvings of people and animals made over thousands of years.","w":"A broad oval of red sand dunes in northern Saudi Arabia, north of the city of Ha'il.","f":["The rock art of Jubbah and Shuwaymis became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2015.","Its sand owes its red colour to a thin coating of iron oxide."],"q":{"q":"What gives the Nafud's sand its red colour?","a":"Iron oxide","x":["Copper minerals","Red algae"]}},"Q574853":{"t":"Morocco's oldest rocks, bare and coloured like spice","s":"South of the High Atlas, Morocco's mountains grow older and barer. The Anti-Atlas is built from some of the most ancient rocks in North Africa, folded and worn into ridges of ochre, pink and purple, with few trees except argan. Villages of earth-coloured houses cling to the slopes, and around Tafraoute, pink granite boulders pile up like giant marbles.","w":"Bare, colourful ridges of ochre and purple rock south of the snowy High Atlas, fading south-west towards the Atlantic and east into the Sahara.","f":["The argan tree, source of argan oil, grows naturally almost only in south-western Morocco.","Taliouine, near the volcanic Jbel Siroua, is known as Morocco's saffron capital.","Some of its rocks are about 2 billion years old."],"q":{"q":"Which tree, source of a prized oil, grows wild on the Anti-Atlas slopes?","a":"The argan","x":["The shea tree","The jojoba"]}},"Q22071997":{"t":"The 'mountains that weep', between Rio and São Paulo","s":"Mantiqueira is often said to mean 'mountains that weep' in Tupi, and the name fits: countless springs and waterfalls pour off these granite heights between São Paulo, Rio de Janeiro and Minas Gerais. The range climbs to nearly 2,800 m, with rocky summits above highland grassland, and cool towns such as Campos do Jordão draw visitors escaping the tropical heat.","w":"A long line of dark granite ridges and grassy highlands running north-east, between the Paraíba valley and the hills of southern Minas Gerais.","f":["Itatiaia, in the Mantiqueira, became Brazil's first national park in 1937.","Its highest summits, Pedra da Mina and Agulhas Negras, both reach about 2,790 m."],"q":{"q":"Which was Brazil's first national park, created in 1937?","a":"Itatiaia","x":["Iguaçu","Tijuca"]}},"Q27036":{"t":"Cloud forests above the Pacific coast of Chiapas","s":"Along Mexico's southern Pacific coast, a steep wall of mountains rises from a narrow plain of mango and banana plantations. The Sierra Madre de Chiapas climbs quickly into cloud forest, where the resplendent quetzal still lives, and coffee grows in the shade on its slopes. At its south-eastern end stands Tacaná, a volcano over 4,000 m high on the border with Guatemala.","w":"A steep, dark-green mountain wall parallel to the Pacific coast, with a narrow coastal plain between it and the sea.","f":["The El Triunfo Biosphere Reserve protects cloud forest home to quetzals and horned guans.","The Soconusco coffee region lies on its Pacific slopes.","Tacaná volcano is about 4,060 m high."],"q":{"q":"Which famous bird lives in the cloud forests of this range?","a":"The resplendent quetzal","x":["The scarlet macaw","The toco toucan"]}},"Q7448930":{"t":"Wild peaks on the divide between two great rivers","s":"The Selwyn Mountains form a rugged, little-visited wilderness along the border of Yukon and the Northwest Territories. Their grey, glacier-carved peaks and green valleys roughly follow a great divide: water on the western side flows to the Yukon River and the Bering Sea, while on the eastern side it heads for the Mackenzie and the Arctic Ocean. Roads barely touch them.","w":"Jagged grey peaks with small glaciers and long green valleys along the Yukon–Northwest Territories border.","f":["They are named after Alfred Selwyn, an early director of the Geological Survey of Canada.","The Yukon–Northwest Territories border here roughly follows the drainage divide."],"q":{"q":"Where do rivers from the east side of the Selwyn Mountains flow?","a":"To the Arctic Ocean","x":["To the Bering Sea","To Hudson Bay"]}},"Q5644542":{"t":"A high wall along Korea's north-eastern coast","s":"In the far north-east of the Korean Peninsula, a long mountain wall rises steeply from the coast and slopes more gently inland. The Hamgyong Mountains are among the highest in Korea: Kwanmobong, at about 2,540 m, is second only to Paektu. In winter the crest turns white early, and forests of larch and fir cover the lower slopes.","w":"A long, high ridge running north-east to south-west close to the coast, snowy in winter, with forested valleys inland.","f":["Kwanmobong, about 2,540 m, is the second-highest mountain on the Korean Peninsula.","Mount Chilbo, a scenic area of strange rock pillars, lies near the coast east of the range."],"q":{"q":"Which is the only Korean peak higher than Kwanmobong?","a":"Paektu","x":["Hallasan","Jirisan"]}},"Q1429556":{"t":"Kamchatka's long volcanic backbone","s":"Kamchatka has two mountain chains, and the Sredinny Range is the inner one, running for some 900 km down the middle of the peninsula. Its crest is a string of old volcanoes and snowy ridges, crowned by Ichinsky, about 3,600 m, a volcano that still has steaming vents. In the valleys lie hot springs and villages such as Esso, warmed by heat from deep underground.","w":"A long, snowy spine down the middle of Kamchatka, with a broad river valley separating it from the volcanoes of the east coast.","f":["Ichinsky volcano, about 3,600 m, is the highest point of the range.","The village of Esso is heated by water from hot springs.","Bystrinsky Nature Park, in the range, is part of the 'Volcanoes of Kamchatka' UNESCO site."],"q":{"q":"What heats the village of Esso in the Sredinny Range?","a":"Hot springs","x":["A coal power station","Wind turbines"]}},"Q644145":{"t":"Treeless mountains on the Bering Sea coast","s":"Between Kamchatka and Chukotka, a wide band of mountains runs along the Bering Sea for hundreds of kilometres. The Koryak Highlands are rugged and almost treeless, with small glaciers on the highest peaks, tundra in the valleys and fog rolling in from the sea. Few people live here beyond scattered coastal villages and Koryak reindeer herders.","w":"A broad band of bare, rugged ridges with small glaciers along the Bering Sea coast, north-east of Kamchatka.","f":["The highest point, Mount Ledyanaya, whose name means 'icy', is about 2,500 m high.","The mountains are named after the Koryak people, traditional reindeer herders and sea hunters."],"q":{"q":"Which sea washes the coast of the Koryak Mountains?","a":"The Bering Sea","x":["The Laptev Sea","The Barents Sea"]}},"Q1245603":{"t":"The watershed between the Kolyma and the Pacific","s":"North-east of Magadan, the Kolyma Mountains run for hundreds of kilometres towards Chukotka, a sea of bare, rounded summits and steep-sided valleys. They divide the waters: rivers on one side feed the Kolyma and flow to the Arctic Ocean, while those on the other run towards the Pacific. For most of the year this is a white, silent land of snow and sparse larch.","w":"Endless bare, rounded summits and narrow valleys with thin larch forest, stretching from Magadan's hinterland towards Chukotka.","f":["The Omolon, a major tributary of the Kolyma, rises in these mountains.","Winter temperatures below −40 °C are common here."],"q":{"q":"Into which ocean does the Kolyma River carry its water?","a":"The Arctic Ocean","x":["The Pacific Ocean","The Atlantic Ocean"]}},"Q683923":{"t":"A 1,500-km range named after a Polish exile","s":"The Chersky Range sweeps some 1,500 km across north-eastern Siberia, a maze of sharp grey ridges, small glaciers and gorges. It was mapped properly only in the 1920s, when geologist Sergei Obruchev named it after Jan Czerski, a Polish exile who had explored the region decades earlier. The Indigirka River cuts right through it in deep canyons on its way to the Arctic.","w":"Parallel grey, jagged ridges with small glaciers running north-west to south-east across north-eastern Yakutia, cut by a deep river gorge.","f":["Its highest peak, Pobeda, meaning 'Victory', rises above 3,000 m.","Jan Czerski was exiled to Siberia after the Polish uprising of 1863."],"q":{"q":"Who is the Chersky Range named after?","a":"Jan Czerski, a Polish explorer","x":["A Russian tsar","A Soviet pilot"]}},"Q2597340":{"t":"Folded green ridges between India and Myanmar","s":"West of Myanmar's great Irrawaddy plain, the land folds into ridge after ridge of green hills, running north–south towards the Indian border. The Chin Hills are steep and forested, with villages perched on the crests rather than in the narrow valleys. Their highest summit, Mount Victoria, about 3,050 m, rises in the south, often above a sea of morning cloud.","w":"Tightly packed north–south ridges of dark green forest, with clouds pooling in the valleys, west of the Irrawaddy plain.","f":["Mount Victoria, also called Nat Ma Taung, is the highest point of the Chin Hills.","The hills continue north into the Indian states of Mizoram and Manipur."],"q":{"q":"What is the highest summit of the Chin Hills?","a":"Mount Victoria","x":["Mount Popa","Hkakabo Razi"]}},"Q620493":{"t":"Forest on one flank, steppe on the other","s":"The Tannu-Ola is a long east–west ridge where Siberia gives way to Central Asia. Its northern slopes, facing Tuva, are dark with larch and Siberian pine; its southern side, facing Mongolia, is bare steppe dropping towards the salty Uvs Lake. The contrast is striking: green on one flank, tawny brown on the other, with snow on the crest for much of the year.","w":"A long east–west ridge with dark forest on its northern side and bare brown steppe to the south, above a large salty lake.","f":["Uvs Lake, south of the range, is the largest lake in Mongolia by area.","The Uvs Nuur basin is a UNESCO World Heritage site shared by Mongolia and Russia."],"q":{"q":"Which large salty lake lies south of the Tannu-Ola?","a":"Uvs Lake","x":["Khövsgöl","Lake Baikal"]}},"Q284226":{"t":"The mountains a fabled old man tried to move","s":"A Chinese fable tells of Yugong, the 'foolish old man', who set out to dig away the Taihang Mountains because they blocked his door. His task looks hopeless: a long wall of ridges and sheer cliffs divides the high Loess Plateau of Shanxi from the flat North China Plain, running roughly north–south for some 400 km.","w":"A long escarpment of ridges and cliffs, with the flat, crowded North China Plain to the east and higher yellow loess country to the west.","f":["In the fable, the gods were so moved by Yugong's persistence that they carried the mountains away.","Shanxi, the province west of the range, takes its name from it: 'west of the mountains'.","The Red Flag Canal, cut along its cliffs in the 1960s, brought water to farms in Henan."],"q":{"q":"What does Shanxi, the province west of the Taihang, mean?","a":"West of the mountains","x":["West of the river","Mountain pass"]}},"Q1538623":{"t":"Loess ridges between the Yellow River and the Fen","s":"In western Shanxi, the Lüliang Mountains run north–south between two rivers: the Yellow River on the west and its tributary the Fen on the east. Much of the range is draped in thick yellow loess, carved by erosion into a maze of gullies, terraces and flat-topped ridges. On the Yellow River just west of the range, the Hukou Falls pour muddy water into a narrow gorge.","w":"Brown and yellow ridges furrowed by countless gullies, between the Yellow River's gorge to the west and the green Fen valley to the east.","f":["The Hukou Waterfall is the largest waterfall on the Yellow River.","Loess on this plateau can be more than 100 m thick."],"q":{"q":"Which great river flows along the west side of the Lüliang Mountains?","a":"The Yellow River","x":["The Yangtze","The Huai"]}},"Q1158071":{"t":"Karst ridges and red cliffs between Guizhou and Sichuan","s":"Between the Sichuan Basin and the Guizhou Plateau, the Dalou Mountains rise in steep, forested ridges of limestone, riddled with caves, sinkholes and deep gorges. Rivers cut dramatic canyons through them, among them the Chishui, whose banks glow with red sandstone cliffs. On its bank lies the town of Maotai, home of China's most famous baijiu liquor.","w":"Parallel limestone ridges and deep gorges running south-west to north-east, between the red Sichuan Basin and the higher Guizhou Plateau.","f":["The red sandstone landscape of Chishui is part of the 'China Danxia' UNESCO World Heritage site.","Moutai baijiu is distilled from sorghum in the town of Maotai on the Chishui River."],"q":{"q":"Which famous Chinese liquor comes from a town in the Dalou Mountains?","a":"Moutai","x":["Wuliangye","Fenjiu"]}},"Q939398":{"t":"A cliff edge between the Khorat Plateau and the plains","s":"The Dângrêk is less a range than a long step in the land. From the north, Thailand's Khorat Plateau rises gently to its crest; on the south side, cliffs drop sharply to the flat Cambodian plain, and the edge runs for some 300 km. Khmer builders used this dramatic brink: the temple of Preah Vihear, begun more than a thousand years ago, stands right on it.","w":"A long east–west escarpment with a gentle slope to the north and a steep, forested drop to flat plains in the south.","f":["Preah Vihear temple became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2008.","In Khmer, Dângrêk means 'carrying pole'."],"q":{"q":"What does the Khmer name Dângrêk mean?","a":"Carrying pole","x":["Elephant's back","Stone wall"]}},"Q3109835":{"t":"Forested ridges where South Asia meets Southeast Asia","s":"Where India's north-east meets Myanmar, the land buckles into the Naga Hills, a tangle of steep ridges covered in forest and bamboo, with villages crowning the hilltops. Their highest summit, Saramati, about 3,830 m, carries snow in winter. In early summer the high Dzükou Valley near Kohima fills with lilies, and mist lies in the valleys most mornings.","w":"Steep forested ridges running north-east to south-west along the India–Myanmar border, with villages dotting the crests.","f":["Saramati, about 3,830 m, is the highest peak of the Naga Hills.","The Dzükou lily grows only in and around the Dzükou Valley.","Nagaland's Hornbill Festival, held every December, celebrates Naga cultures."],"q":{"q":"Which is the highest peak of the Naga Hills?","a":"Saramati","x":["Japfü","Dapha Bum"]}},"Q625425":{"t":"A monsoon wall between the Bay of Bengal and the Irrawaddy","s":"Running parallel to Myanmar's west coast, the Arakan Mountains form a long, forested wall between the Bay of Bengal and the Irrawaddy valley. Their western slopes take the full force of the monsoon, while the valley beyond lies in their rain shadow. The range does not really end at Cape Negrais: it continues under the sea and reappears as the Andaman and Nicobar Islands.","w":"Long, parallel forested ridges running north–south close to Myanmar's west coast, with the broad Irrawaddy valley to the east.","f":["In Burmese the range is called Rakhine Yoma.","Sittwe, on the coast west of the range, receives several metres of rain a year."],"q":{"q":"Which islands continue the Arakan Mountains beneath the sea?","a":"The Andaman and Nicobar Islands","x":["The Maldives","The Mergui Archipelago"]}},"Q1145416":{"t":"Seven folds between India's west-flowing rivers","s":"Satpura is often translated as 'seven folds', and the range does ripple across central India in long, forested ridges. It runs east–west between two of the few big Indian rivers that flow west to the Arabian Sea: the Narmada to the north and the Tapti to the south. In its sandstone heart lies the hill station of Pachmarhi, with gorges, waterfalls and ancient rock shelters.","w":"Long, parallel forested ridges running east–west across central India, with the Narmada valley to the north and the Tapti to the south.","f":["Dhupgarh, near Pachmarhi, is the range's highest point at about 1,350 m.","Satpura National Park is home to tigers, leopards and sloth bears.","Rock shelters around Pachmarhi hold paintings thousands of years old."],"q":{"q":"Which river flows along the northern side of the Satpura Range?","a":"The Narmada","x":["The Tapti","The Godavari"]}},"Q9177711":{"t":"A Tian Shan wall between the green Ili and dry Dzungaria","s":"In northern Xinjiang, the Borohoro Mountains form a long, snowy wall between two very different lands. To the south lies the Ili valley, one of the greenest corners of Central Asia, watered by moist westerly winds; to the north, the dry Dzungarian basin stretches towards Mongolia. Near the range's western end sits Sayram Lake, a vivid blue alpine lake ringed by meadows.","w":"A long snowy ridge running west to east, with the green Ili valley to the south and the pale Dzungarian plain to the north.","f":["The Ili River flows west into Kazakhstan and ends in Lake Balkhash.","Sayram Lake lies at about 2,070 m."],"q":{"q":"Where does the Ili River, south of the Borohoro, end?","a":"In Lake Balkhash","x":["In the Aral Sea","In Issyk-Kul"]}},"Q3182012":{"t":"Among the wettest hills on Earth, with living bridges","s":"When the monsoon blows north from the Bay of Bengal, the first high ground it meets is the Khasi Hills, and the clouds unload here. Mawsynram and Cherrapunji, on the southern edge, rank among the wettest places on Earth, with more than 11 m of rain a year. Khasi villagers train the roots of rubber trees across streams into living bridges that grow stronger with age.","w":"Rolling green uplands ending in a steep edge above the flat, watery plains of Bangladesh to the south.","f":["Cherrapunji holds the record for rain in a calendar year: about 26 m, in 1861.","Shillong, the capital of Meghalaya, lies on the plateau at about 1,500 m.","Meghalaya means 'abode of the clouds' in Sanskrit."],"q":{"q":"What does Meghalaya, home of the Khasi Hills, mean?","a":"Abode of the clouds","x":["Land of rivers","Land of tea"]}},"Q216593":{"t":"Volcanic plateaus and a high blue lake","s":"South of the great Caucasus ridge, a second, lower mountain system runs across the southern Caucasus: the Lesser Caucasus. It is a jumble of folded ridges, forests and high volcanic plateaus dotted with old lava cones. Its centrepiece is Lake Sevan, a vast blue lake set at about 1,900 m, one of the largest high-altitude freshwater lakes in the world.","w":"Folded ridges and high, bare volcanic plateaus with the large blue Lake Sevan among them, south of the snowy main Caucasus.","f":["Lake Sevan covers roughly 1,250 km².","The Sevanavank monastery stands on a former island in the lake, now a peninsula after its level fell."],"q":{"q":"At about what altitude does Lake Sevan lie?","a":"About 1,900 m","x":["About 400 m","About 3,500 m"]}},"Q519263":{"t":"A green wall between the Black Sea and Anatolia","s":"Along Turkey's Black Sea coast, the Pontic Mountains rise steeply from the shore and wring the moisture out of the sea air. Their northern slopes are among the greenest places in Turkey, covered in forest, tea gardens and hazelnut groves; beyond the crest the land turns dry and brown. In the east the range climbs to about 3,940 m in the Kaçkar Mountains, streaked with snow even in summer.","w":"A steep green mountain wall rising straight from the Black Sea coast, with drier brown uplands behind it.","f":["Turkey grows most of the world's hazelnuts, largely along this coast.","Almost all Turkish tea comes from the slopes around Rize.","The Sumela Monastery clings to a cliff face south of Trabzon."],"q":{"q":"Which crop covers the slopes around Rize?","a":"Tea","x":["Coffee","Cotton"]}},"Q170427":{"t":"The 'old mountain' that named a whole peninsula","s":"Bulgarians call this range Stara Planina, the 'old mountain', and it is the one that gave the whole Balkan Peninsula its name. It runs some 550 km from the Serbian border to the Black Sea, a long green and grey wall across Bulgaria. At its southern foot lies the Rose Valley, where pink damask roses are picked each May and June for rose oil.","w":"A long east–west mountain wall across Bulgaria, with the Danube plain to the north and a narrow valley of fields along its southern foot.","f":["Botev Peak, about 2,380 m, is the highest point of the range.","Bulgaria is one of the world's leading producers of rose oil.","The word Balkan is thought to come from a Turkic word for a wooded mountain range."],"q":{"q":"What does Stara Planina, the Bulgarian name of the range, mean?","a":"Old mountain","x":["Rose mountain","High mountain"]}},"Q208076":{"t":"The wall between 'Green Spain' and the dry Meseta","s":"Along Spain's northern coast, the Cantabrian Mountains trap the moist Atlantic air, and the difference shows: north of the crest, green valleys, meadows and oak woods; south of it, the dry brown plains of the Meseta. In the west they are wild and remote, home to Spain's main population of brown bears; further east rise the jagged limestone Picos de Europa.","w":"A long east–west range close to Spain's north coast, green on the seaward side and brown on the inland side.","f":["Somiedo Natural Park, in the western Cantabrians, is a stronghold of the brown bear.","Torre de Cerredo, about 2,650 m in the Picos de Europa, is the highest summit.","The Picos de Europa became Spain's first national park in 1918."],"q":{"q":"Which large animal survives in the western Cantabrian Mountains?","a":"The brown bear","x":["The Iberian lynx","The moose"]}},"Q26955":{"t":"Snow above Granada, within sight of Africa","s":"Snow on the southern edge of Europe, barely 40 km from the Mediterranean: the Sierra Nevada rises behind Granada to 3,479 m at Mulhacén, the highest point of mainland Spain. In winter its long white crest stands out against the brown hills of Andalusia, and on clear days the mountains of Morocco can be seen from the summit across the sea.","w":"A long, high, often snow-capped ridge just south-east of Granada, with the Mediterranean coast close by to the south.","f":["Mulhacén is named after Muley Hacén, a 15th-century Nasrid ruler of Granada.","Its ski resort is the southernmost in Europe.","The Alpujarras, white villages on its southern slopes, were among the last Moorish strongholds in Spain."],"q":{"q":"What is the highest peak of mainland Spain?","a":"Mulhacén","x":["Aneto","Teide"]}},"Q686362":{"t":"The dark hills where Spain's plateau meets Andalusia","s":"Heading south across Spain, the high Meseta ends at a long line of dark, wooded hills: the Sierra Morena. It runs some 400 km east–west above the Guadalquivir valley, never very high but rugged, covered in holm oak, cork oak and scrub. These quiet hills are a last stronghold of the Iberian lynx, and in their west lie the ancient copper mines of Río Tinto.","w":"A long line of dark, wooded hills running east–west, north of the broad green Guadalquivir valley.","f":["The Despeñaperros pass has long been the main route from Madrid into Andalusia.","The Río Tinto, whose water runs red with dissolved metals, rises in its western hills."],"q":{"q":"Which endangered wild cat survives in the Sierra Morena?","a":"The Iberian lynx","x":["The Eurasian lynx","The caracal"]}},"Q3326866":{"t":"An orange sea of sand in the Libyan Fezzan","s":"In south-western Libya, the Idhan Murzuq is a sea of orange sand hundreds of kilometres across, its dunes lined up in long rows and star-shaped peaks. To the north-west rises the dark Messak plateau, where prehistoric artists engraved giraffes, elephants and crocodiles into the rock, animals of a Sahara that was once green. The oasis town of Murzuq lies on its northern edge.","w":"A vast field of orange dunes in south-western Libya, bordered by dark rocky plateaus to the north-west.","f":["The rock engravings of Wadi Mathendous, in the Messak, are several thousand years old.","Idhan is a local term for a sand sea, like erg in Algeria."],"q":{"q":"What animals appear in the rock art near the Murzuq sand sea?","a":"Giraffes and elephants","x":["Bears and wolves","Bison and reindeer"]}},"Q1537020":{"t":"Star dunes and red oases in the western Sahara","s":"The Grand Erg Occidental is one of the Sahara's great sand seas, covering some 80,000 km² of western Algeria. Its dunes pile up into huge star-shaped massifs and long ridges that glow orange at dawn. Around its edges lie palm oases such as Taghit and Timimoun, whose red mud-brick towns sit right against the sand, watered by underground channels dug centuries ago.","w":"A huge sea of orange dunes in western Algeria, with dark palm oases along its southern and western edges.","f":["Timimoun's gardens are watered by foggaras, underground channels bringing water from far away.","Some of its star dunes rise well over 100 m high."],"q":{"q":"What are Timimoun's underground water channels called locally?","a":"Foggaras","x":["Aflaj","Karez"]}},"Q1548386":{"t":"The salt floor of a vanished giant lake","s":"West of the Great Salt Lake lies a flat, white world: the floor of Lake Bonneville, an ice-age lake that once covered much of Utah. When it dried up it left the Great Salt Lake Desert, with the Bonneville Salt Flats at its western edge, a crust of salt so smooth and hard that land speed records have been set on it for over a century. Interstate 80 crosses it in a dead-straight line.","w":"A vast white and tan salt flat west of the Great Salt Lake, crossed by a dead-straight highway towards the Nevada border.","f":["Lake Bonneville was about 300 m deep at its peak, some 15,000 years ago.","Old shorelines of Lake Bonneville still show as terraces on the surrounding mountains."],"q":{"q":"Which ice-age lake left behind this salt desert?","a":"Lake Bonneville","x":["Lake Lahontan","Lake Agassiz"]}},"Q272577":{"t":"A desert named after a man who vanished in it","s":"In 1874 Alfred Gibson, a member of Ernest Giles's expedition, rode off across this country to get help and was never seen again; Giles named the desert after him. The Gibson Desert is a vast stretch of red sandplains, gravel and low dunes in the heart of Western Australia, dotted with spinifex and shimmering salt lakes. It remains one of the emptiest places on Earth.","w":"Endless red sandplains with gravel ridges, scattered dunes and pale salt lakes in the interior of Western Australia.","f":["The Gibson Desert covers roughly 155,000 km².","Aboriginal peoples such as the Pintupi lived here long before Europeans arrived."],"q":{"q":"Who named the Gibson Desert?","a":"Ernest Giles","x":["John McDouall Stuart","Charles Sturt"]}},"Q767128":{"t":"One of the hottest patches of ground on Earth","s":"Satellites have measured ground temperatures above 70 °C here, among the hottest surface readings on the planet. The Dasht-e Lut in south-eastern Iran is a desert of dark gravel, sand seas and, in the west, the kaluts: rows of giant wind-carved ridges like a fleet of stone ships, all pointing the same way. Together they look like a huge sheet of ribbed fabric.","w":"Rows of long, parallel wind-carved ridges, with dark gravel plains and sand to the east, in south-eastern Iran.","f":["The Lut Desert became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2016.","In 2005 a satellite measured the land surface here at 70.7 °C.","The kaluts are yardangs, ridges sculpted by winds blowing steadily from the north."],"q":{"q":"What are the Lut's giant wind-carved ridges called locally?","a":"Kaluts","x":["Ergs","Wadis"]}},"Q1002":{"t":"Asia's eastern tip, across the strait from America","s":"The Chukchi Peninsula is where Asia ends. Its tip, Cape Dezhnev, is the easternmost point of the Asian mainland, only about 82 km from Alaska across the Bering Strait. Most of the peninsula lies east of the 180th meridian, in the Western Hemisphere. It is a treeless land of bare hills, lagoons and tundra, where Chukchi and Yupik hunters have lived by the sea for millennia.","w":"A bare, hilly peninsula with lagoons along its coasts, reaching east to the narrow Bering Strait opposite Alaska.","f":["Cape Dezhnev is named after Semyon Dezhnev, who sailed around it in 1648.","The two Diomede Islands in the strait lie on either side of the International Date Line.","On Yttygran Island, ancient hunters set up rows of whale bones known as Whale Bone Alley."],"q":{"q":"Who first sailed around Cape Dezhnev, in 1648?","a":"Semyon Dezhnev","x":["Vitus Bering","James Cook"]}},"Q3815319":{"t":"Granite walls in 'the land that never melts'","s":"On the eastern edge of Baffin Island, the Cumberland Peninsula is carved into fjords, glaciers and sheer granite walls. Much of it lies in Auyuittuq National Park, whose Inuktitut name means 'the land that never melts'. Here stands Mount Thor, whose west face drops about 1,250 m, often called the greatest purely vertical drop on Earth. The Arctic Circle runs right through the park.","w":"A deeply fjorded, glacier-covered peninsula on eastern Baffin Island, with a large white ice cap and long fjords on either side.","f":["The Penny Ice Cap covers about 6,000 km² of the peninsula.","Akshayuk Pass, a route of about 100 km through the mountains, crosses the Arctic Circle.","Pangnirtung, at the peninsula's southern gateway, is known for Inuit printmaking and weaving."],"q":{"q":"What does the Inuktitut name Auyuittuq mean?","a":"The land that never melts","x":["The land of whales","The white mountains"]}},"Q837325":{"t":"The northernmost point of the Eurasian mainland","s":"No mainland in Europe or Asia reaches farther north than this. Cape Chelyuskin, at the tip of the Taymyr Peninsula, lies at about 77°43′ N, less than 1,400 km from the North Pole. It is a low, bare shore of gravel and tundra facing the Vilkitsky Strait and the ice-capped islands of Severnaya Zemlya. Even in summer, sea ice often lingers along the coast.","w":"The bare northern tip of the Taymyr Peninsula, facing a narrow strait and the ice-capped islands of Severnaya Zemlya.","f":["It is named after Semyon Chelyuskin, who reached it by sledge in 1742.","In 1878 the Swedish ship Vega, under Adolf Erik Nordenskiöld, became the first vessel to round the cape.","A polar station has operated here since 1932."],"q":{"q":"Who reached Cape Chelyuskin first, in 1742?","a":"Semyon Chelyuskin","x":["Vitus Bering","Khariton Laptev"]}}},"history":{"via-appia":{"name":"Via Appia","era":"from 312 BC","t":"The Romans called it the queen of roads","s":"This is the first of Rome's great highways, begun in 312 BC and pushed south until it reached the Adriatic port of Brindisi. Soldiers, traders and travellers all used it to reach the ships for Greece and the East. The paving stones are far too small to see, but south of Rome modern roads still trace its dead-straight line across the flat Pontine plain.","w":"Look for a ruler-straight road running south-east from Rome across a flat, chequered plain towards the coast at Terracina.","f":["It was begun by the censor Appius Claudius Caecus, who gave the road his name.","Near Rome the old road is still lined with tombs and umbrella pines, and parts of the Roman paving survive.","From Brindisi, ships crossed to Dyrrachium, where the Via Egnatia continued east towards Byzantium."],"q":{"q":"Which port was the final stop of the Via Appia?","a":"Brindisi","x":["Naples","Ostia"]}},"via-egnatia":{"name":"Via Egnatia","era":"2nd c. BC – Middle Ages","t":"Rome's highway across the Balkans to the Bosphorus","s":"Here Rome reached for the East. Built in the 2nd century BC, the Via Egnatia ran from the Adriatic coast of today's Albania over the mountains to Thessaloniki and on to Byzantium. It was the natural sequel to the Via Appia: sail across from Brindisi, then walk. The road itself is invisible from cruise, but you can follow its line past Lake Ohrid and along the Aegean shore.","w":"Find Lake Ohrid among the mountains, then the Gulf of Thessaloniki to the east; the road linked the two and ran on towards Istanbul.","f":["It was named after the Roman proconsul Gnaeus Egnatius, who had it built.","From the Adriatic to Byzantium the road covered roughly 1,100 km.","The Apostle Paul travelled along it on his way through Philippi and Thessaloniki."],"q":{"q":"Which lake did the Via Egnatia pass on its way east?","a":"Lake Ohrid","x":["Lake Balaton","Lake Scutari"]}},"via-francigena":{"name":"Via Francigena","era":"from the 9th–10th c.","t":"A medieval pilgrim road from Canterbury to Rome","s":"This is the road that medieval pilgrims walked from England to Rome. In 990 Archbishop Sigeric of Canterbury wrote down each overnight stop on his way home from the Pope, and his list still defines the route. It crosses France, climbs the Alps at the Great St Bernard Pass and runs down through Tuscany. From above, trace it by the lakes, passes and river plains it threads between.","w":"Look for Lake Geneva, then the high Alpine notch of the Great St Bernard to its south, and the flat Po plain beyond.","f":["Sigeric's travel notes list 79 stages between Rome and the English Channel.","The Great St Bernard Pass, at 2,469 m, is the highest point on the route.","Pilgrims still walk it today, collecting stamps in a pilgrim passport."],"q":{"q":"Who recorded the stages of the Via Francigena in 990?","a":"Archbishop Sigeric","x":["Charlemagne","Pope Gregory the Great"]}},"camino-frances":{"name":"Camino de Santiago (Camino Francés)","era":"from the 9th c.","t":"A thousand years of footsteps to Santiago","s":"This is Europe's best-loved pilgrim road. For more than a thousand years walkers have crossed the Pyrenees here and headed west across northern Spain to the tomb of St James in Santiago de Compostela, about 780 km away. From the air the route is easy to imagine: green mountains at both ends, and between them the Meseta, a high, flat plateau of golden wheat around Burgos and León.","w":"Look for the wide tawny plateau between Burgos and León, with the green Galician hills rising to the west.","f":["The scallop shell, symbol of St James, marks the way on walls and signposts.","In 1987 it became the first Cultural Route of the Council of Europe.","Hundreds of thousands of people now finish the pilgrimage each year."],"q":{"q":"Which symbol marks the Camino de Santiago?","a":"A scallop shell","x":["An olive branch","An oak leaf"]}},"amber-road":{"name":"Amber Road","era":"Bronze Age – Roman times","t":"Baltic gold carried south to Rome","s":"Imagine the Baltic shore as a gold mine. Amber washed up on its beaches, and for thousands of years traders carried it south through the Moravian Gate to the Danube and down to Aquileia on the Adriatic, where Roman workshops turned it into jewellery. The route itself left no line you can see, but its two ends are clear: the long sandy spits of the Baltic and the northern Adriatic coast.","w":"At the northern end, look for the thin sandy spits and lagoons of the Baltic coast near Gdańsk and Kaliningrad.","f":["Amber is fossilised tree resin, and Baltic amber is roughly 40 million years old.","Pliny the Elder tells of a Roman knight sent by Nero to the Baltic to buy amber in bulk.","Most of the world's mined amber still comes from the coast near Kaliningrad."],"q":{"q":"What is amber?","a":"Fossilised tree resin","x":["Petrified sea foam","Volcanic glass"]}},"hadrians-wall":{"name":"Hadrian's Wall","era":"AD 122 – early 5th c.","t":"Rome's northern frontier, coast to coast","s":"Across the narrow neck of northern England, Emperor Hadrian ordered a stone wall in AD 122. It ran from the Tyne to the Solway Firth, with small forts every Roman mile and garrisons drawn from all over the empire. The wall is only a few metres wide and cannot be seen from cruise, but the gap it guarded, between two estuaries, is plain.","w":"Trace the land from the Tyne estuary at Newcastle westward to the broad silver Solway Firth beyond Carlisle.","f":["The wall was about 117 km long, or 80 Roman miles.","Letters on wooden tablets found at nearby Vindolanda include a birthday party invitation from about AD 100.","It became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1987."],"q":{"q":"Roughly how long was Hadrian's Wall?","a":"About 117 km","x":["About 40 km","About 450 km"]}},"limes-germanicus":{"name":"Upper Germanic–Raetian Limes","era":"c. AD 100 – 260","t":"Rome's frontier line from the Rhine to the Danube","s":"Here ran the edge of the Roman world. From the Rhine near Koblenz to the Danube near Regensburg, a palisade, ditch and hundreds of watchtowers marked where the empire ended and free Germania began. Much of it lies in forest today, invisible from any height. What you can see are the two great rivers it joined, and the wooded hills of the Taunus and Odenwald between them.","w":"Find the Rhine and the Danube; the frontier cut across the wooded uplands between them in a long, bent line.","f":["The frontier ran about 550 km and had some 900 watchtowers and 60 forts.","One section runs almost perfectly straight for about 80 km, ignoring hills and valleys.","At Saalburg near Frankfurt, a Roman fort was rebuilt around 1900 on its original foundations."],"q":{"q":"Which two rivers did this Roman frontier connect?","a":"The Rhine and the Danube","x":["The Rhine and the Elbe","The Main and the Oder"]}},"danube-limes":{"name":"Danube Limes","era":"1st – 5th c.","t":"The river that was Rome's frontier","s":"For four centuries this river was a border. The Romans lined the Danube with forts and watchtowers, and several of today's capitals grew from their camps: Vienna, Budapest and Belgrade among them. Unlike most Roman frontiers, this one is easy to follow from the air, because the river itself was the line. Watch for the gorge of the Iron Gates, where the Danube squeezes through the Carpathians.","w":"Follow the broad Danube; the narrow Iron Gates gorge between Serbia and Romania is the most striking stretch.","f":["Vienna grew from the Roman camp of Vindobona, and Budapest from Aquincum.","Trajan's Bridge across the Danube, finished in AD 105, was over a kilometre long.","Its stone piers stood near today's Drobeta-Turnu Severin, and a few stumps still rise on the banks."],"q":{"q":"Which capital grew from the Roman camp of Vindobona?","a":"Vienna","x":["Budapest","Bratislava"]}},"hanseatic-route":{"name":"Hanseatic trade route (London–Novgorod)","era":"13th – 17th c.","t":"Merchant towns that ruled the northern seas","s":"This was the old sea road of the Hanseatic League, a merchant alliance that for centuries controlled trade across the North Sea and the Baltic. Its ships carried cloth west to east, and furs, wax, grain and fish the other way, linking offices in London, Bruges, Bergen and Novgorod. Look for the Baltic coast: the red-brick port towns along it were built on this trade.","w":"Follow the Baltic coast from Lübeck Bay eastward, with the long island of Gotland out to sea.","f":["Lübeck was the league's leading city, often called the Queen of the Hanse.","Hamburg's car plates still begin with HH, for Hansestadt Hamburg.","The Hanseatic Diet, the league's assembly, last met in 1669."],"q":{"q":"What does HH on Hamburg car plates stand for?","a":"Hansestadt Hamburg","x":["Hafen Hamburg","Hauptstadt Hamburg"]}},"via-domitia":{"name":"Via Domitia","era":"from 118 BC","t":"The first Roman road in Gaul, Italy to Spain","s":"This was the first road Rome built in Gaul, laid out from 118 BC to link Italy with Spain. It climbed over the Alps at Montgenèvre, followed the Durance valley, crossed the Rhône near Beaucaire and ran along the coast plain past Nîmes and Narbonne to the Pyrenees. The road is invisible now, but the valleys, the river and the lagoon-fringed coast still show its path.","w":"Look for the Rhône delta and the chain of lagoons along the coast west of it, towards the Pyrenees.","f":["It was named after Gnaeus Domitius Ahenobarbus, the Roman general who had it built.","In Narbonne a stretch of the original paving has been uncovered in the town's main square.","The Pont Julien, a Roman bridge near Apt, carried traffic on this route for about 2,000 years."],"q":{"q":"Which two lands did the Via Domitia link?","a":"Italy and Spain","x":["Italy and Britain","Gaul and Germania"]}},"canal-du-midi":{"name":"Canal du Midi","era":"1666 – 1681","t":"A 17th-century shortcut from Atlantic to Mediterranean","s":"Here a 17th-century engineer joined two seas. Pierre-Paul Riquet's canal runs from Toulouse to the Mediterranean, and with the Garonne it links the Atlantic to the Mediterranean without sailing round Spain. Its genius lies in the water supply, collected from the Montagne Noire and fed in at the canal's summit. At about 20 m wide it is too narrow to spot from cruise, but its corridor is clear.","w":"Look for the lowland corridor between the Pyrenees and the Massif Central, from Toulouse to the Étang de Thau lagoon.","f":["The canal is about 240 km long and still in use, now mostly by holiday boats.","At Béziers a staircase of eight locks lifts boats up a single hillside.","It became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1996."],"q":{"q":"Who built the Canal du Midi?","a":"Pierre-Paul Riquet","x":["Ferdinand de Lesseps","Gustave Eiffel"]}},"kiel-canal":{"name":"Kiel Canal","era":"opened 1895","t":"A shortcut that slices Germany's neck","s":"Look for a straight silver thread crossing the flat farmland of northern Germany. This is the Kiel Canal, opened in 1895 to link the North Sea with the Baltic so that ships no longer had to sail round Denmark. It runs 98 km from the Elbe estuary to Kiel. At about 100 m wide, it can be picked out from cruise height on a clear day.","w":"A thin straight waterline running north-east from the Elbe mouth at Brunsbüttel to the fjord at Kiel.","f":["By the number of ships, it is one of the busiest artificial waterways in the world.","The shortcut saves ships about 460 km compared with the voyage round Denmark.","At Rendsburg a transporter bridge from 1913 carries a gondola hanging beneath the railway."],"q":{"q":"Which peninsula does the Kiel Canal let ships avoid?","a":"Jutland","x":["Cotentin","Istria"]}},"orient-express":{"name":"Orient Express route","era":"1883 – 1977","t":"The legendary sleeper train from Paris to Constantinople","s":"Along this line ran the most famous train in Europe. From 1883 the Orient Express carried passengers in sleeping cars from Paris towards Constantinople, and from 1889 all the way there by rail, through Munich, Vienna, Budapest and Belgrade. It made crossing half a continent feel like a stay in a grand hotel. From above, follow the Danube, then the Balkan valleys down to the Bosphorus.","w":"Follow the Danube past Vienna and Budapest, then valleys through Serbia and Bulgaria towards the Bosphorus.","f":["The train was created by the Belgian entrepreneur Georges Nagelmackers.","Istanbul's Sirkeci station, opened in 1890, was built as its terminus.","The through service from Paris to Istanbul ended in 1977."],"q":{"q":"Who founded the company that ran the Orient Express?","a":"Georges Nagelmackers","x":["George Pullman","Thomas Cook"]}},"bleriot-channel-1909":{"name":"Blériot's Channel flight (1909)","era":"25 July 1909","t":"The flight that shrank the English Channel","s":"On 25 July 1909 Louis Blériot took off from a field near Calais in a small monoplane and landed below Dover Castle about 37 minutes later. It was the first aeroplane flight across the English Channel, just six years after the Wright brothers first flew. He had no compass. The strait, with its white cliffs and busy shipping lanes, suddenly looked much narrower.","w":"Look for the white chalk cliffs at Dover facing the flat French coast near Calais, about 35 km apart.","f":["The flight won Blériot a £1,000 prize from London's Daily Mail.","A granite outline of his aeroplane marks the landing spot near Dover Castle.","His aircraft, the Blériot XI, is now in the Musée des Arts et Métiers in Paris."],"q":{"q":"How long did Blériot's Channel flight take?","a":"About 37 minutes","x":["About 12 minutes","About 2 hours"]}},"paris-meridian-arc":{"name":"Paris meridian arc (the metre survey)","era":"1792 – 1798","t":"Where the metre was measured on the ground","s":"This line gave the world the metre. In the 1790s two astronomers, Delambre and Méchain, measured the distance from Dunkirk to Barcelona by chains of triangles between church towers and hilltops. From it they fixed the metre as one ten-millionth of the distance from pole to equator. The line runs almost due north–south through Paris, invisible but exact.","w":"The arc runs nearly due north–south through Paris, from the Channel coast at Dunkirk to Barcelona.","f":["Delambre measured the northern half and Méchain the southern half, meeting at Rodez.","The result was cast as a platinum bar, the Metre of the Archives, in 1799.","They slightly misjudged the Earth's flattening, so their metre came out about 0.2 mm short."],"q":{"q":"Which unit was this survey meant to define?","a":"The metre","x":["The nautical mile","The kilogram"]}},"ancient-rome":{"name":"Ancient Rome","era":"753 BC – AD 476","t":"The city whose roads led everywhere","s":"This was the heart of an empire that stretched from Scotland to Syria. At its peak ancient Rome was home to perhaps a million people, and its Forum, Colosseum and Pantheon still stand in the middle of the modern city. From cruise height the monuments merge into the sprawl, but the Tiber's lazy S-bends through the centre are the same river the city was founded on.","w":"Find the Tiber winding through the city in tight bends, about 25 km inland from the coast at Ostia.","f":["The Colosseum, opened in AD 80, could hold around 50,000 spectators.","The Pantheon's concrete dome, nearly 2,000 years old, is still the largest unreinforced one in the world.","By tradition, Rome was founded on 21 April 753 BC, and the city still celebrates that date."],"q":{"q":"In which year, by tradition, was Rome founded?","a":"753 BC","x":["509 BC","312 BC"]}},"pompeii":{"name":"Pompeii and Herculaneum","era":"buried AD 79, excavated since 1738","t":"Roman towns kept under ash for 1,700 years","s":"Look for the cone of Vesuvius above the Bay of Naples. In AD 79 it buried two Roman towns under ash and mud, and in doing so kept them almost intact: painted walls, bakeries, shop signs, even scribbled graffiti. Today they are the fullest picture we have of everyday Roman life. The streets are too small to see from here, but the volcano that made them famous is unmistakable.","w":"A broad volcanic cone with a dark crater, rising just east of the Bay of Naples; the ruins lie at its foot.","f":["Excavations at Pompeii began in 1748, and about a third of the town is still unexcavated.","Its walls are covered in ancient graffiti, from election slogans to jokes.","Charred papyrus scrolls from Herculaneum are now being read with X-ray scans and AI."],"q":{"q":"Which volcano buried Pompeii?","a":"Vesuvius","x":["Etna","Stromboli"]}},"acropolis-athens":{"name":"Acropolis of Athens","era":"5th c. BC","t":"The rock where Western architecture found its form","s":"In the middle of the Athens basin rises a flat-topped rock crowned with marble temples. The Parthenon was built on it in the 5th century BC, when Athens was experimenting with democracy, and its proportions have been copied on banks, museums and parliaments ever since. The temple itself is too small to pick out from cruise, but the city's bowl between the mountains and the sea is easy to see.","w":"Look for a sprawling pale city in a bowl of mountains, opening south-west onto the Saronic Gulf and Piraeus.","f":["The Parthenon was built between 447 and 432 BC and dedicated to Athena.","Its columns bulge slightly and its floor curves upward, so it looks perfectly straight to the eye.","The marble came from Mount Pentelicus, about 16 km away."],"q":{"q":"To which goddess was the Parthenon dedicated?","a":"Athena","x":["Hera","Artemis"]}},"delphi":{"name":"Delphi","era":"8th c. BC – 4th c. AD","t":"The ancient Greeks' centre of the world","s":"Look for the massif of Mount Parnassus, often white on top, above the Gulf of Corinth. On its southern slope sat Delphi, where for a thousand years Greeks came to ask Apollo's oracle about voyages, colonies and marriages. They believed it marked the centre of the world. The temple terraces are invisible from here, but the mountain and the silvery olive plain running down to the sea are clear.","w":"A high, often snowy mountain block north of the Gulf of Corinth, with a wide olive plain below its south side.","f":["A carved stone called the omphalos, or navel, marked Delphi as the centre of the world.","The Pythian Games held here included music and poetry contests as well as athletics.","Winners of the Pythian Games were crowned with laurel, the plant sacred to Apollo."],"q":{"q":"Which god's oracle spoke at Delphi?","a":"Apollo","x":["Zeus","Poseidon"]}},"olympia":{"name":"Olympia","era":"776 BC – AD 393","t":"Where the Olympic Games began","s":"In a green valley in the western Peloponnese, the ancient Greeks held the Olympic Games for more than a thousand years. A sacred truce let athletes travel safely, and victors went home with an olive wreath and lasting fame. Every modern Olympic flame is still lit here from the sun's rays. From cruise, look for the wide Alfeios river valley winding to the Ionian Sea.","w":"Find the broad valley of the Alfeios river running west to the Ionian coast in the western Peloponnese.","f":["By tradition the first Games were held in 776 BC.","The stadium track is about 192 m long, the length of an ancient stade.","Its gold-and-ivory statue of Zeus was one of the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World."],"q":{"q":"What did ancient Olympic victors receive?","a":"An olive wreath","x":["A gold medal","A laurel wreath"]}},"knossos":{"name":"Knossos","era":"c. 1900 – 1350 BC","t":"The palace behind the legend of the labyrinth","s":"Just inland from Heraklion on Crete's north coast stood the greatest palace of Europe's first advanced civilisation, the Minoans. Its maze of rooms, light wells and painted walls may lie behind the Greek myth of King Minos, the labyrinth and the Minotaur. From above you won't see the ruins, but you can see Crete's long mountainous spine and the busy coast where the Minoans built their sea-trading world.","w":"On Crete's north coast, look for the city of Heraklion; the palace lies a few kilometres inland to its south.","f":["The British archaeologist Arthur Evans excavated Knossos from 1900.","Evans rebuilt parts of the palace in concrete, which is still debated today.","Clay tablets from Knossos written in Linear B turned out to be an early form of Greek."],"q":{"q":"Who excavated Knossos from 1900?","a":"Arthur Evans","x":["Heinrich Schliemann","Howard Carter"]}},"mycenae":{"name":"Mycenae","era":"c. 1600 – 1100 BC","t":"The citadel of Homer's King Agamemnon","s":"On a hill above the Argolid plain stood Mycenae, the fortress that gave its name to Greece's Late Bronze Age. Homer made it the home of King Agamemnon, leader of the Greeks at Troy. Its walls are built of boulders so huge that later Greeks thought giants had laid them. From cruise, look for the flat green plain of Argos opening onto its gulf.","w":"Find the Gulf of Argolis in the north-east Peloponnese; the citadel stands on the plain's northern edge.","f":["The walls are called Cyclopean, after the one-eyed giants said to have built them.","Its famous Lion Gate, carved around 1250 BC, is one of the oldest monumental sculptures in Europe.","The gold 'Mask of Agamemnon' found here in 1876 is about 300 years older than the Trojan War of legend."],"q":{"q":"What guards the main entrance to Mycenae?","a":"Two carved lions","x":["Two sphinxes","A bronze bull"]}},"stonehenge":{"name":"Stonehenge and Avebury","era":"c. 3000 – 1500 BC","t":"Stone circles aligned with the midsummer sun","s":"On the rolling chalk of Salisbury Plain stands the world's most famous prehistoric monument. Stonehenge was built in stages over some 1,500 years; its axis points to sunrise at midsummer and sunset at midwinter. Nearby Avebury has an even larger stone circle, with a village built inside it. The stones are far too small to see from here, but the pale downland is easy to spot.","w":"Look for open, pale-green chalk downland north of Salisbury, with few trees and scattered military ranges.","f":["Its smaller bluestones were brought about 240 km from the Preseli Hills in Wales.","The biggest sarsen stones weigh around 25–30 tonnes each.","Avebury's outer circle is so big that part of the village sits inside it."],"q":{"q":"Where did Stonehenge's bluestones come from?","a":"The Preseli Hills, Wales","x":["Cornwall","The Lake District"]}},"carnac":{"name":"Carnac stones","era":"c. 4500 – 3300 BC","t":"Rows of standing stones marching across Brittany","s":"On the south coast of Brittany, thousands of standing stones stand in long parallel rows, some stretching more than a kilometre. They were raised in the Neolithic, long before Stonehenge's great sarsens, and nobody knows exactly why. Local legend says they are Roman soldiers turned to stone. The rows are too small to see, but the Quiberon peninsula and the island-studded Gulf of Morbihan are plain.","w":"Look for the thin Quiberon peninsula pointing south, with the island-filled Gulf of Morbihan just east of it.","f":["More than 3,000 standing stones survive around Carnac.","The Ménec alignment alone has over 1,000 stones in 11 rows.","The word 'menhir', for a standing stone, comes from Breton: 'long stone'."],"q":{"q":"Roughly how many standing stones survive at Carnac?","a":"Over 3,000","x":["About 300","About 30,000"]}},"lascaux":{"name":"Lascaux and the Vézère valley","era":"c. 17,000 years ago","t":"Ice Age horses and bulls painted in the dark","s":"Somewhere in these wooded valleys, a cave holds some of the finest art of the Ice Age: horses, deer and huge black bulls painted about 17,000 years ago. It was found in 1940 by four local teenagers. The Vézère valley around it is packed with prehistoric sites. From the air you see only green limestone hills and a winding river, just as the painters did.","w":"Look for the meandering Vézère river among wooded limestone hills, north-east of the wider Dordogne valley.","f":["The cave holds about 600 paintings and around 1,500 engravings.","It closed to visitors in 1963 to protect the art; people now visit exact replicas nearby.","The Vézère valley has 147 prehistoric sites and 25 decorated caves on UNESCO's list."],"q":{"q":"Who found the Lascaux cave in 1940?","a":"Four teenagers","x":["A team of archaeologists","A shepherd"]}},"newgrange":{"name":"Newgrange (Brú na Bóinne)","era":"c. 3200 BC","t":"A tomb older than the pyramids, lit by midwinter sun","s":"In a great bend of the River Boyne, north of Dublin, stands a huge round mound faced with white quartz. Newgrange was built around 3200 BC, before Stonehenge's stone circle and the pyramids of Giza. Once a year, at the midwinter sunrise, a beam of light travels down its passage and lights the inner chamber. The mound is small from here, but the loop of the Boyne is visible.","w":"Find the River Boyne west of Drogheda; the monuments sit inside a tight southern loop of the river.","f":["The sunbeam enters through a 'roof-box' above the doorway and lasts about 17 minutes.","Its passage is about 19 m long and ends in a chamber with a stone corbelled roof.","Neighbouring Knowth has more megalithic art than any other site in western Europe."],"q":{"q":"What lights up Newgrange's chamber each year?","a":"The midwinter sunrise","x":["The midsummer sunset","The spring full moon"]}},"skara-brae":{"name":"Neolithic Orkney (Skara Brae)","era":"c. 3100 – 2500 BC","t":"A stone-age village with its furniture still in place","s":"On Orkney's main island, a Stone Age village lay hidden under sand until a storm uncovered it in 1850. Skara Brae's houses still have their stone beds, hearths and dressers, left as if their owners had just stepped out. Nearby stand the Ring of Brodgar and the great tomb of Maeshowe. From above, look for Orkney's green, treeless islands and the two lochs at their heart.","w":"Look for low green islands north of Scotland's tip; the monuments lie around two joined lochs on the largest one.","f":["Skara Brae was lived in for about 600 years.","At midwinter the setting sun shines down the entrance passage of Maeshowe.","The Ring of Brodgar originally had about 60 stones; around 27 still stand."],"q":{"q":"What uncovered Skara Brae in 1850?","a":"A storm","x":["A farmer's plough","A landslide"]}},"pont-du-gard":{"name":"Pont du Gard","era":"1st c. AD","t":"Rome's aqueduct bridge, three tiers high","s":"In the gorge of the Gardon river, a Roman bridge of three stacked arcades carried water to the city of Nîmes. It was one part of an aqueduct about 50 km long that fell only a few metres over its whole length, a feat of surveying. The bridge is too small to see from cruise, but the green Gardon gorge between Nîmes and Avignon is where to look.","w":"Look between Nîmes and Avignon, where the Gardon river cuts a green gorge before reaching the Rhône.","f":["At about 49 m, it is the tallest aqueduct bridge the Romans built.","The aqueduct drops only about 12 m over its 50 km, around 25 cm per kilometre.","The stones were laid without mortar in the main arches."],"q":{"q":"Which Roman city did the Pont du Gard supply?","a":"Nîmes","x":["Arles","Marseille"]}},"mont-saint-michel":{"name":"Mont-Saint-Michel","era":"from 966","t":"An abbey on a rock, cut off by the tide","s":"Look for a vast grey-and-silver bay on the Normandy–Brittany border. In its middle sits a tiny rocky island topped by a Gothic abbey, founded as a Benedictine monastery in 966. The bay has some of the biggest tides in Europe, and at high water the sea surrounds the rock. From cruise the abbey is a dot, but the patterned sandflats and river channels are striking.","w":"A broad bay of sandflats and winding channels where Normandy meets Brittany, with a small island near its southern shore.","f":["The tidal range in the bay can reach about 14 m.","In 2014 a light bridge replaced the old causeway so the sea can flow around the rock again.","It became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1979, with its bay."],"q":{"q":"How high can the tide rise in Mont-Saint-Michel Bay?","a":"Up to about 14 m","x":["Up to about 2 m","Up to about 40 m"]}},"aachen":{"name":"Aachen, Charlemagne's capital","era":"c. 800","t":"The spa town where Charlemagne ruled Europe","s":"Near the corner where Germany, Belgium and the Netherlands meet lies Aachen, Charlemagne's favourite residence. Around 800 he built a domed chapel here that became the heart of his empire, and for six centuries German kings were crowned in it. The Romans had come for the hot springs, which still flow. From above, look for the city on the edge of the green Eifel uplands.","w":"Look for the city just east of where Germany, Belgium and the Netherlands meet, at the edge of wooded uplands.","f":["About 30 German kings were crowned in Aachen between 936 and 1531.","In 1978 its cathedral was among the first 12 sites on UNESCO's World Heritage list.","The Roman name Aquae Granni referred to the town's hot springs."],"q":{"q":"Whose chapel became Aachen Cathedral?","a":"Charlemagne's","x":["Otto the Great's","Frederick Barbarossa's"]}},"ravenna":{"name":"Ravenna","era":"5th – 6th c.","t":"The last capital of the Western Roman Empire","s":"On the flat Adriatic coast of Italy, Ravenna was for a time the capital of the Western Roman Empire, then of Ostrogothic kings and Byzantine governors. They filled its churches with glittering mosaics that still look fresh after 1,500 years, among the finest anywhere. The city was once a harbour; the sea has since retreated. From above, look for the flat coast, pine woods and lagoons.","w":"Look for a low, straight coastline with dark pine forests and, to the north, the wide lagoons of Comacchio.","f":["The Western Roman emperors moved their court to Ravenna in 402.","The mosaics of San Vitale show Emperor Justinian and Empress Theodora with their courts.","The poet Dante died in Ravenna in 1321 and is buried there."],"q":{"q":"Which poet is buried in Ravenna?","a":"Dante","x":["Petrarch","Virgil"]}},"venice-republic":{"name":"Republic of Venice","era":"c. 697 – 1797","t":"A merchant republic built on a lagoon","s":"Look for a fish-shaped city floating in a lagoon, joined to the mainland by a long bridge. For about a thousand years Venice was an independent republic ruled by elected doges, and its galleys carried spices, silk and glass between Europe and the East. Few places are as easy to recognise from the air: the lagoon, the barrier islands and the city's dense tangle of canals.","w":"A compact fish-shaped city in a shallow lagoon, linked to the mainland by a long straight causeway.","f":["The city stands on more than 100 small islands linked by around 400 bridges.","Its Arsenal was one of the largest shipyards of the pre-industrial world.","Marco Polo, whose travels made China famous in Europe, was a Venetian merchant."],"q":{"q":"Who ended the Venetian Republic in 1797?","a":"Napoleon","x":["Louis XIV","Louis XVI"]}},"split-diocletian":{"name":"Diocletian's Palace, Split","era":"c. AD 305","t":"An emperor's retirement home that became a city","s":"When Emperor Diocletian gave up the throne around AD 305, he retired to a fortress-palace he had built on the Dalmatian coast. After Rome fell, local people moved inside its walls, and a whole town grew within them. Today shops, homes and a cathedral fill the Roman palace. From above, look for Split on its peninsula, facing the long islands of Brač and Hvar.","w":"A city on a peninsula of the Croatian coast, with the long islands of Brač and Hvar lying offshore.","f":["Diocletian was the first Roman emperor to step down of his own accord.","Split's cathedral was built in the emperor's own mausoleum.","Around 3,000 people still live inside the old palace walls."],"q":{"q":"Which emperor retired to a palace at Split?","a":"Diocletian","x":["Constantine","Hadrian"]}},"constantinople":{"name":"Constantinople","era":"AD 330 – 1453","t":"Imperial capital on two continents","s":"This is the city Constantine made the new Rome in 330. As Constantinople it was the capital of the Byzantine Empire for over a thousand years, and then of the Ottoman sultans, guarding the only sea route between the Black Sea and the Mediterranean. Its dome of Hagia Sophia amazed visitors for centuries. From the air, look for the Bosphorus and the curved Golden Horn.","w":"Look for the narrow Bosphorus strait and the curved inlet of the Golden Horn cutting into the old city.","f":["Hagia Sophia, finished in 537, was the world's largest cathedral for nearly a thousand years.","The city's land walls, built in the 5th century, ran about 6 km from sea to sea.","Before Constantine, the city was the Greek colony of Byzantium, founded around 660 BC."],"q":{"q":"Which emperor refounded Byzantium as his capital in 330?","a":"Constantine the Great","x":["Justinian","Theodosius"]}},"alhambra-granada":{"name":"Nasrid Granada and the Alhambra","era":"1238 – 1492","t":"A red palace-city under the snowy Sierra Nevada","s":"Look for the Sierra Nevada, often snow-capped, rising behind the city of Granada. Here the Nasrid dynasty ruled the last Muslim kingdom of the Iberian Peninsula until 1492, and built the Alhambra, a hilltop palace of carved stucco, tiles and courtyard fountains. The palace cannot be seen from cruise, but the mountain wall behind it, higher than any other in mainland Spain, is hard to miss.","w":"A long mountain range, white-topped in winter and spring, with Granada spread on the plain at its north-western foot.","f":["The name Alhambra comes from the Arabic for 'the red one', after the colour of its walls.","The fountain in the Court of the Lions rests on the backs of 12 marble lions.","Mulhacén in the Sierra Nevada, at 3,479 m, is the highest peak in mainland Spain."],"q":{"q":"What does the name 'Alhambra' mean?","a":"The red one","x":["The high one","The garden"]}},"cordoba-umayyad":{"name":"Umayyad Córdoba","era":"756 – 1031","t":"Once one of Europe's greatest cities","s":"In the 10th century Córdoba, on the Guadalquivir, was the capital of an Umayyad caliphate and one of the largest and most learned cities in Europe. Its Great Mosque, with a forest of columns and red-and-white striped arches, still stands, with a cathedral built right in the middle of it. From the air, look for the city on the river, with the dark Sierra Morena rising to the north.","w":"Find the Guadalquivir valley; the city lies on the river at the foot of the wooded Sierra Morena hills.","f":["The mosque's prayer hall has over 800 columns of marble, jasper and granite.","A Renaissance cathedral was built inside the mosque in the 16th century.","The ruined palace-city of Medina Azahara lies just west of Córdoba."],"q":{"q":"What was built inside Córdoba's Great Mosque?","a":"A cathedral","x":["A bullring","A royal palace"]}},"belem-lisbon":{"name":"Belém, Lisbon: the Age of Discovery","era":"15th – 16th c.","t":"The riverbank where the sea route to India began","s":"From this riverbank at the mouth of the Tagus, Portuguese ships set out in the 15th and 16th centuries to map Africa's coast and reach India by sea. Vasco da Gama sailed from here in 1497. Spice money paid for the ornate Jerónimos Monastery and the Belém Tower that guarded the harbour. From above, look for the broad Tagus estuary and the long red suspension bridge across it.","w":"Look for the wide Tagus estuary opening to the Atlantic, crossed by a long suspension bridge west of Lisbon's centre.","f":["Vasco da Gama is buried in the Jerónimos Monastery.","The Belém Tower was completed around 1519 to guard the harbour entrance.","The monastery's intricate style, called Manueline, uses carved ropes, shells and sea creatures."],"q":{"q":"Who sailed from Belém in 1497 to find a sea route to India?","a":"Vasco da Gama","x":["Ferdinand Magellan","Bartolomeu Dias"]}},"bergen-bryggen":{"name":"Bryggen, Bergen","era":"c. 1360 – 1754","t":"The Hanseatic wharf of wooden gables","s":"In the city of Bergen, surrounded by fjords and mountains, German merchants of the Hanseatic League ran one of their four great foreign offices for almost 400 years. They traded grain for stockfish, cod dried on racks in the Arctic wind. Their row of tall wooden gabled houses, Bryggen, still lines the harbour. From above, look for the city on its inlet, ringed by steep hills.","w":"A city on a sheltered inlet of Norway's west coast, ringed by steep hills and fjords, often under cloud.","f":["The stockfish came from the Lofoten Islands, hundreds of kilometres to the north.","Bryggen has burned several times and was rebuilt after a fire in 1702 in the old style.","It became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1979."],"q":{"q":"What did Bergen's Hanseatic merchants mainly export?","a":"Stockfish (dried cod)","x":["Amber","Silver"]}},"visby":{"name":"Visby","era":"12th – 14th c.","t":"A walled Hanseatic town in the middle of the Baltic","s":"On the west coast of Gotland, in the middle of the Baltic, stands Visby, a Hanseatic trading town still enclosed by its medieval wall. Its merchants grew rich on trade between Russia and western Europe, and filled the town with stone churches, many now roofless ruins. From the air, look for the long, low island of Gotland, the largest in Sweden.","w":"A long, low limestone island in the open Baltic; Visby lies on its western shore, facing Sweden.","f":["Visby's ring wall is about 3.4 km long and still has 27 of its towers.","Gotland has yielded more Viking-age silver hoards than anywhere else in Scandinavia.","The town became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1995."],"q":{"q":"On which island does Visby stand?","a":"Gotland","x":["Öland","Bornholm"]}},"thingvellir":{"name":"Þingvellir","era":"930 – 1798","t":"An open-air parliament in a crack between continents","s":"North-east of Reykjavík lies a lake in a rift valley where two tectonic plates pull apart. In 930 Iceland's settlers began meeting here each summer for the Althing, an open-air assembly that made laws and settled disputes. It gathered here until 1798. From above you can see what they could not: the lake and the long fissures running through the lava, where North America and Eurasia drift apart.","w":"Look for Iceland's largest natural lake, north-east of Reykjavík, with dark lava fissures running along its north shore.","f":["The Althing is often called one of the oldest parliaments in the world.","In the year 1000 the assembly here decided that Iceland would adopt Christianity.","Across Iceland, the two plates drift apart by about 2 cm a year."],"q":{"q":"In which year was the Althing founded?","a":"930","x":["874","1262"]}},"gutenberg-mainz":{"name":"Mainz, Gutenberg's press","era":"c. 1450","t":"Where movable type began Europe's print revolution","s":"Look for the point where the Main flows into the Rhine. On its west bank lies Mainz, where around 1450 Johannes Gutenberg began printing with movable metal type. His Bible, finished around 1455, showed books could be made in hundreds of copies instead of one at a time. Within 50 years printing presses were running in hundreds of European towns. The confluence is easy to spot.","w":"Find the confluence of the Main and the Rhine, with Mainz on the west bank and Wiesbaden opposite.","f":["About 180 copies of the Gutenberg Bible were printed; 49 are known to survive.","Gutenberg's system combined metal type, oil-based ink and a press adapted from wine presses.","Mainz began as the Roman fortress of Mogontiacum."],"q":{"q":"Which book did Gutenberg print around 1455?","a":"The Bible","x":["The Iliad","The Quran"]}},"greenwich-meridian":{"name":"Greenwich prime meridian","era":"chosen in 1884","t":"Longitude zero, the line every map counts from","s":"On a hill above the Thames stands the Royal Observatory, founded in 1675. In 1884 delegates from 25 nations agreed that its meridian would be longitude zero for the world. Every position on your flight map is measured east or west of it, and world time was once counted from it. From above, look for the Thames looping round the Isle of Dogs; Greenwich lies on the south bank.","w":"Look for the Thames's tight loop around the Isle of Dogs in east London; Greenwich Park lies just south of it.","f":["The Royal Observatory was founded by King Charles II in 1675.","The zero line used by GPS lies about 100 m east of the brass line at the observatory.","The 1884 decision was made at a conference in Washington, D.C."],"q":{"q":"When was Greenwich chosen as the prime meridian?","a":"1884","x":["1675","1851"]}},"montgolfier-annonay":{"name":"Annonay, the first hot-air balloon","era":"4 June 1783","t":"Where two paper-makers launched the age of flight","s":"In the hills west of the Rhône lies Annonay, home of the Montgolfier brothers, paper-makers. On 4 June 1783 they filled a large cloth-and-paper envelope with hot air in the town square and watched it rise more than a kilometre. Within months their balloons carried a sheep, a duck and a rooster, and then the first human passengers. Every flight since traces back here.","w":"Look for the Rhône valley running south from Lyon; Annonay lies in the wooded hills a little to its west.","f":["The first human flight in a Montgolfier balloon took place over Paris on 21 November 1783.","At Versailles in September 1783, a sheep, a duck and a rooster flew and landed safely.","In French, a hot-air balloon is still called a 'montgolfière'."],"q":{"q":"What was the Montgolfier family's trade?","a":"Paper-making","x":["Silk weaving","Wine-making"]}},"ironbridge":{"name":"Ironbridge Gorge","era":"1709 – 1779","t":"A valley that helped start the Industrial Revolution","s":"In this wooded gorge of the River Severn, the Industrial Revolution found its fuel and its symbol. In 1709 Abraham Darby first smelted iron with coke instead of charcoal, making iron cheap. In 1779 his grandson raised the world's first major cast-iron bridge across the river. The bridge is too small to see from cruise, but the Severn's deep valley west of Telford is visible.","w":"Find the River Severn winding south-east from Shrewsbury; the gorge lies just south of the new town of Telford.","f":["The Iron Bridge has a span of about 30 m and is still standing.","Its parts were joined with carpentry techniques, such as dovetails, because iron building was so new.","The gorge became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1986."],"q":{"q":"What is the Iron Bridge made of?","a":"Cast iron","x":["Wrought iron","Steel"]}},"malta-temples":{"name":"Megalithic temples of Malta","era":"c. 3600 – 2500 BC","t":"Stone temples older than the pyramids","s":"On the small islands of Malta and Gozo, farmers of the Stone Age built great stone temples more than 5,000 years ago, earlier than Stonehenge's circle or the pyramids of Giza. They are among the oldest free-standing buildings on Earth. The temples are too small to see, but the islands themselves, golden-brown in the blue Mediterranean south of Sicily, are easy to find.","w":"Look for two small golden-brown islands, Malta and Gozo, about 90 km south of Sicily.","f":["Local legend says the Ġgantija temples on Gozo were built by a giantess.","The Ħal Saflieni Hypogeum is an underground temple and burial site cut into solid rock.","Seven of the temples are on the UNESCO World Heritage list."],"q":{"q":"Who, in local legend, built the Ġgantija temples?","a":"A giantess","x":["Phoenician sailors","The Knights of St John"]}},"etruria":{"name":"Etruria","era":"8th – 1st c. BC","t":"Land of the Etruscans, Rome's mysterious teachers","s":"This is the land of the Etruscans, who ruled central Italy before Rome and taught the young city much of its religion, engineering and style. They built hill towns, drained marshes and painted their tombs with banquets, dancers and musicians. Their language is still only partly understood. From above, look for the green Tuscan hills and the round volcanic lakes that dot the land north of Rome.","w":"Look for round crater lakes such as Bolsena and Bracciano among green hills between Rome and Florence.","f":["Tuscany takes its name from the Etruscans, whom the Romans called Tusci.","The painted tombs of Tarquinia and Cerveteri are on the UNESCO World Heritage list.","Lake Bolsena fills an old volcanic caldera and is the largest volcanic lake in Europe."],"q":{"q":"Which Italian region is named after the Etruscans?","a":"Tuscany","x":["Umbria","Lombardy"]}},"magna-graecia":{"name":"Magna Graecia","era":"8th – 3rd c. BC","t":"When southern Italy was part of the Greek world","s":"From the 8th century BC, Greek settlers sailed west and founded cities along the coasts of southern Italy and Sicily, so many that the Romans called the region Greater Greece. Pythagoras taught here, and cities like Syracuse and Taranto rivalled Athens. From above, look for the boot's toe and heel and the deep Gulf of Taranto between them, the sea the colonists crossed.","w":"Look for the toe and heel of Italy's boot, with the wide Gulf of Taranto curving between them.","f":["Naples began as the Greek colony of Neapolis, 'the new city'.","The word 'sybarite', for a lover of luxury, comes from the rich colony of Sybaris.","The Doric temples at Paestum are among the best-preserved Greek temples anywhere."],"q":{"q":"What does Naples' Greek name, Neapolis, mean?","a":"New city","x":["Sea city","Holy city"]}},"nuragic-sardinia":{"name":"Nuragic Sardinia","era":"c. 1800 – 238 BC","t":"An island of thousands of Bronze Age stone towers","s":"Sardinia is dotted with stone towers called nuraghi, built by a Bronze Age people who left no writing. Thousands survive, from single cones to fortress-villages with walls and courtyards, and archaeologists still debate what they were for. The towers are too small to see from cruise, but the island's rugged granite mountains and empty uplands, where many of them stand, are easy to see.","w":"A large, rugged, lightly populated island south of Corsica, with granite mountains in the east.","f":["More than 7,000 nuraghi are known across the island.","Su Nuraxi at Barumini, a nuraghe village, became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1997.","Nuragic smiths made small bronze figures of warriors, priests and animals."],"q":{"q":"What is a nuraghe?","a":"A stone tower","x":["A burial mound","A reed boat"]}},"al-andalus":{"name":"Al-Andalus","era":"711 – 1492","t":"Eight centuries of Muslim Iberia","s":"For almost eight centuries much of the Iberian Peninsula was al-Andalus, ruled by Muslim dynasties from Córdoba, Seville and Granada. Its scholars preserved and extended Greek learning, and its farmers brought rice, oranges and sugar cane to Europe. Christians, Muslims and Jews lived side by side, not always peacefully. From above, look for the Guadalquivir valley and the endless dotted rows of olive trees around it.","w":"Look for the broad Guadalquivir valley and hillsides covered in regular dotted rows of olive groves.","f":["The philosophers Averroes and Maimonides were both born in Córdoba in the 12th century.","The name of the region of Andalusia comes from al-Andalus.","Many Spanish words, such as 'aceite' (oil) and 'azúcar' (sugar), come from Arabic."],"q":{"q":"Which Spanish region takes its name from al-Andalus?","a":"Andalusia","x":["Aragon","Extremadura"]}},"dacia":{"name":"Dacia","era":"1st c. BC – AD 271","t":"The gold-rich mountain kingdom inside the Carpathians","s":"Inside the great curve of the Carpathians lay Dacia, a kingdom of mountain fortresses and gold mines. In AD 106 it became a Roman province under Trajan, and for the next 165 years Roman settlers mixed with the Dacians. Their Latin grew into Romanian. From above, look for the arc of the Carpathians, often snow-streaked, wrapped around the green Transylvanian plateau.","w":"Look for the curved chain of the Carpathians enclosing a broad, rolling plateau of farms and forest.","f":["The Dacian capital, Sarmizegetusa Regia, stood at about 1,200 m in the Orăștie Mountains.","Trajan's Column in Rome tells the story of the Roman campaigns in Dacia in a spiral of carved scenes.","The Romans withdrew from Dacia around AD 271."],"q":{"q":"Which language grew from the Latin spoken in Dacia?","a":"Romanian","x":["Hungarian","Albanian"]}},"sapmi":{"name":"Sápmi, land of the Sámi","era":"thousands of years – today","t":"Homeland of the Sámi, across four countries","s":"This is Sápmi, the homeland of the Sámi, the Indigenous people of the far north of Europe. It spans Norway, Sweden, Finland and Russia's Kola Peninsula. For centuries many Sámi followed reindeer between winter forests and summer pastures by the coast and in the fells, and some still do. From above you see their landscape: lakes, bogs, birch and pine, and long winter snow.","w":"Look for a vast land of lakes, low fells and forest, white for much of the year, north of the Gulf of Bothnia.","f":["Sámi National Day, 6 February, marks the first Sámi assembly held in Trondheim in 1917.","Norway, Sweden and Finland each have an elected Sámi Parliament.","The joik is a traditional Sámi song, often sung to evoke a person or a place."],"q":{"q":"What is a joik?","a":"A traditional Sámi song","x":["A reindeer sled","A winter tent"]}},"incense-route":{"name":"Incense Route","era":"7th c. BC – 2nd c. AD","t":"Camel caravans carrying frankincense to Rome","s":"This was the road of frankincense and myrrh. Camel caravans carried these precious resins from the south of Arabia north along the Red Sea hills, oasis to oasis, to Petra and the Mediterranean port of Gaza. Temples across the ancient world burned incense, so the trade made the desert kingdoms rich. From above you see what the caravans crossed: bare mountains, lava fields and sand.","w":"Look for dark volcanic hills and sandy valleys running north–south inland from the Red Sea coast.","f":["Frankincense is the dried resin of a small desert tree, Boswellia.","The Nabataeans of Petra grew rich by taxing and guiding the caravans.","The caravan journey from southern Arabia to Gaza took around two months."],"q":{"q":"What is frankincense?","a":"A tree resin","x":["Ground seashells","Dried flower petals"]}},"royal-road":{"name":"Persian Royal Road","era":"5th c. BC","t":"The highway of the Persian kings, Sardis to Susa","s":"In the 5th century BC the Persian king Darius the Great linked the edges of his empire with a road about 2,700 km long, from Sardis near the Aegean to his capital at Susa. Relay stations with fresh horses lined the way, so royal messengers could cross it in about a week. From above, trace it across the Anatolian plateau, over the Euphrates and Tigris and down to the plains of Iran.","w":"Across eastern Turkey, look for the upper Euphrates and Tigris valleys the road crossed on its way to Mesopotamia.","f":["Herodotus wrote that travellers on foot needed about 90 days for the full journey.","He counted 111 relay stations along the road.","His praise of the couriers, that neither snow nor rain stayed them, is carved on a New York post office."],"q":{"q":"Which Persian king built the Royal Road?","a":"Darius the Great","x":["Cyrus the Great","Xerxes"]}},"suez-canal":{"name":"Suez Canal","era":"opened 1869","t":"A ruler-straight line joining two seas","s":"Look for a thin straight line cutting through the desert between the Mediterranean and the Red Sea. Opened in 1869, the Suez Canal spared ships the long voyage round Africa. It has no locks: the sea simply flows through, pausing in the Bitter Lakes halfway. From cruise height it is one of the clearest human-made lines on Earth, often with a convoy of ships strung along it.","w":"A straight blue line from Port Said south to Suez, widening into the Great Bitter Lake halfway along.","f":["The canal was built under the French diplomat Ferdinand de Lesseps.","It is about 193 km long and was later widened, with a parallel channel added along part of it in 2015.","It cut the sea route between Britain and India by about 40 percent."],"q":{"q":"Who led the building of the Suez Canal?","a":"Ferdinand de Lesseps","x":["Pierre-Paul Riquet","Isambard Kingdom Brunel"]}},"troy":{"name":"Troy","era":"c. 3000 BC – AD 500","t":"The city behind Homer's Iliad","s":"At the mouth of the Dardanelles lies the mound of Troy. For thousands of years people rebuilt a city here, layer on layer, and one of those cities may be the Troy of Homer's Iliad. It guarded the sea route between the Aegean and the Black Sea. The mound is too small to see, but the narrow strait and the river plain beside it are clear.","w":"Look for the entrance of the Dardanelles strait; the site lies just inland on its Asian side, above a flat river plain.","f":["Archaeologists have found at least nine cities built on top of one another.","Heinrich Schliemann's 'Priam's Treasure' turned out to be about a thousand years older than Homer's Troy.","Troy became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1998."],"q":{"q":"Who began excavating Troy in the 1870s?","a":"Heinrich Schliemann","x":["Arthur Evans","Howard Carter"]}},"ephesus":{"name":"Ephesus","era":"10th c. BC – 15th c.","t":"A great Roman port now stranded far inland","s":"Ephesus was one of the largest cities of the Roman East, with a marble main street, a theatre for about 25,000 people and the grand Library of Celsus. It was a port, but the river silted up its harbour, and the coast now lies kilometres away. From above you can read that story: a flat green plain of river silt where the sea once came in, west of the town of Selçuk.","w":"Look for a flat green river plain west of Selçuk that runs out to the sea near Kuşadası.","f":["Its Temple of Artemis was one of the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World.","The front of the Library of Celsus, built in the 2nd century AD, has been rebuilt from its original stones.","Ephesus became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2015."],"q":{"q":"Which Wonder of the Ancient World stood at Ephesus?","a":"The Temple of Artemis","x":["The Colossus of Rhodes","The Pharos lighthouse"]}},"pergamon":{"name":"Pergamon","era":"3rd – 2nd c. BC","t":"A hilltop capital that gave its name to parchment","s":"On a steep hill above the town of Bergama stood Pergamon, capital of a rich Hellenistic kingdom. Its kings built a great library, a famous altar and one of the steepest theatres of the ancient world, cut into the slope. The library needed so much writing material that Pergamon became known for parchment. From above, look for the green plain of the Bakırçay river, inland from the Aegean.","w":"Look for the Bakırçay river plain about 25 km inland from the Aegean coast, north of İzmir.","f":["The word 'parchment' comes from the name of Pergamon.","The Pergamon Altar was taken to Berlin in the 19th century and gave its name to a museum there.","The theatre's seating drops down the hillside in about 80 rows."],"q":{"q":"Which writing material is named after Pergamon?","a":"Parchment","x":["Papyrus","Paper"]}},"sardis":{"name":"Sardis, city of the first coins","era":"7th – 6th c. BC","t":"Where money first became coins","s":"In this fertile valley stood Sardis, capital of the Lydian kingdom. Around the 7th century BC the Lydians began stamping lumps of electrum, a natural gold–silver alloy, into coins, one of the first uses of coinage anywhere. Their last king, Croesus, became a byword for wealth. The city later marked the start of the Persian Royal Road. From above, look for the broad green Gediz valley east of İzmir.","w":"Look for the broad green Gediz valley east of İzmir, with a mountain ridge rising along its southern side.","f":["The river Pactolus, which ran through Sardis, was said to carry gold dust.","The phrase 'as rich as Croesus' comes from the last king of Lydia.","The ruins include one of the largest ancient synagogues ever found."],"q":{"q":"Which king of Sardis became a byword for wealth?","a":"Croesus","x":["Midas","Darius"]}},"gobekli-tepe":{"name":"Göbekli Tepe","era":"c. 9500 – 8000 BC","t":"Temples built before farming began","s":"On a hill above the Harran plain, hunter-gatherers raised great stone pillars carved with foxes, snakes and vultures about 11,500 years ago. That is before pottery, before writing, and before farming had taken hold. Göbekli Tepe turned the old idea that villages came first and monuments later upside down. The pillars are invisible from cruise, but the wide, now irrigated plain around Şanlıurfa is easy to see.","w":"Look for the wide plain north of the Syrian border near Şanlıurfa, green in patches with irrigated fields.","f":["Its T-shaped pillars stand up to about 5.5 m tall.","It is some 6,000 years older than Stonehenge.","The site was recognised as prehistoric by the archaeologist Klaus Schmidt in 1994."],"q":{"q":"Who built Göbekli Tepe?","a":"Hunter-gatherers","x":["Bronze Age farmers","Roman engineers"]}},"catalhoyuk":{"name":"Çatalhöyük","era":"c. 7100 – 5700 BC","t":"A Stone Age town with no streets","s":"On the flat Konya plain stood one of the world's earliest large towns, home to thousands of people more than 9,000 years ago. Its mud-brick houses were packed wall to wall with no streets between them, so people walked across the rooftops and climbed down ladders into their homes. From above, the plain still looks as flat as a table, with the volcano Hasan Dağı rising to the east.","w":"Look for the vast flat Konya plain, with the lone twin-peaked volcano Hasan Dağı standing to its east.","f":["Houses were entered through a hole in the roof.","A wall painting found here may show a town with an erupting volcano behind it.","It became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2012."],"q":{"q":"How did people enter houses at Çatalhöyük?","a":"Through the roof","x":["Through side tunnels","Through front doors"]}},"hattusa":{"name":"Hattusa, the Hittite capital","era":"c. 1650 – 1200 BC","t":"The land where the Hittite empire rose","s":"On these bare hills of central Anatolia stood Hattusa, capital of the Hittites, who ruled much of the Bronze Age Near East and dealt as equals with Egypt. Its walls, gates carved with lions and sphinxes, and an archive of clay tablets survive. Among them is one of the oldest peace treaties known. From above, look for the great loop of the Kızılırmak river that wraps around the Hittite heartland.","w":"Look for the long arc of the Kızılırmak, Turkey's longest river, curving around dry hills east of Ankara.","f":["The Hittite king and Egypt's Ramesses II signed a peace treaty around 1259 BC; a copy hangs at UN headquarters.","Hittite is the oldest written Indo-European language known.","Archaeologists have found around 30,000 clay tablets at Hattusa."],"q":{"q":"Which pharaoh signed a peace treaty with the Hittites?","a":"Ramesses II","x":["Tutankhamun","Akhenaten"]}},"petra":{"name":"Petra","era":"4th c. BC – 7th c. AD","t":"A city carved into rose-red cliffs","s":"In the rugged mountains east of the Wadi Araba lies Petra, capital of the Nabataeans, who grew rich on the incense caravans. They carved temples and tombs straight into the rose-red sandstone and caught every drop of rain in channels and cisterns. You reach its most famous facade, the Treasury, through a narrow gorge. From cruise, look for the jagged, reddish mountain wall above the long, dead-flat rift valley.","w":"Look for jagged reddish mountains rising steeply east of the Wadi Araba, the flat rift valley between the Dead Sea and Aqaba.","f":["The gorge leading into Petra, the Siq, is about 1.2 km long and in places only a few metres wide.","The Swiss traveller Johann Ludwig Burckhardt brought Petra to Europe's attention in 1812.","A Victorian poem called it 'a rose-red city half as old as time'."],"q":{"q":"Who brought Petra to Europe's attention in 1812?","a":"Johann Ludwig Burckhardt","x":["Heinrich Schliemann","Richard Burton"]}},"palmyra":{"name":"Palmyra","era":"1st – 3rd c. AD","t":"The caravan city of the desert palms","s":"In the middle of the Syrian desert, a spring and a grove of palms made Palmyra a stop for caravans travelling between the Mediterranean and the Euphrates. Its merchants grew so rich that they built a long colonnaded avenue, grand temples and tower tombs, mixing Greek, Roman and Persian styles. From above, look for the green patch of the oasis, sharp against the pale desert around it.","w":"A small green oasis in a vast pale desert, at the foot of a line of low bare hills.","f":["In the 3rd century Queen Zenobia made Palmyra the centre of a short-lived empire.","Palmyrene merchants traded in silk, spices and perfumes from as far as China and India.","The city's own language was a dialect of Aramaic, written in its own script."],"q":{"q":"Which queen made Palmyra the heart of an empire?","a":"Zenobia","x":["Cleopatra","Dido"]}},"baalbek":{"name":"Baalbek","era":"1st c. BC – 3rd c. AD","t":"Temples built of some of the largest stones ever moved","s":"In the Beqaa valley, between two mountain ranges, the Romans built one of their grandest temple complexes, Heliopolis, today's Baalbek. Six towering columns of the Temple of Jupiter still stand, and the Temple of Bacchus beside them is among the best preserved in the Roman world. Its foundations include blocks of hundreds of tonnes each. From above, the long flat valley between two ridges is unmistakable.","w":"Look for a long, flat green valley running north–south between two parallel mountain ranges, often snow-capped in winter.","f":["Three stones in the temple's base, the Trilithon, weigh about 800 tonnes each.","Even larger blocks, never moved, still lie in the ancient quarry nearby.","The Temple of Jupiter's surviving columns are about 20 m tall."],"q":{"q":"About how heavy is each stone of Baalbek's Trilithon?","a":"About 800 tonnes","x":["About 8 tonnes","About 80 tonnes"]}},"qumran":{"name":"Qumran and the Dead Sea Scrolls","era":"c. 250 BC – AD 68; found 1947","t":"Caves that kept the oldest Bible manuscripts","s":"Look for the Dead Sea, the lowest water on Earth. In 1947 a Bedouin shepherd, searching the cliffs on its north-western shore, found clay jars holding ancient scrolls. Over the next decade the caves around Qumran yielded hundreds of manuscripts, including the oldest known copies of books of the Hebrew Bible. The caves are invisible from here, but the sea and its steep cliffs are striking.","w":"Look for the Dead Sea's long blue northern basin and the steep bare cliffs along its western shore.","f":["The scrolls were written roughly between 250 BC and AD 68.","The Great Isaiah Scroll is over 7 m long and nearly complete.","The surface of the Dead Sea lies about 430 m below sea level."],"q":{"q":"Who found the first Dead Sea Scrolls in 1947?","a":"A Bedouin shepherd","x":["A British archaeologist","A travelling monk"]}},"babylon":{"name":"Babylon","era":"c. 1900 – 300 BC","t":"The ancient world's byword for a great city","s":"On the Euphrates south of Baghdad lie the ruins of Babylon. Under Hammurabi it gave the world one of its first written law codes; under Nebuchadnezzar II it became a city of blue-glazed gates and a towering ziggurat that may lie behind the story of the Tower of Babel. From above, look for the Euphrates and the dark ribbon of palm groves and fields that follows it through the desert.","w":"Look for the Euphrates winding through dry land, lined with dark green palm groves, about 85 km south of Baghdad.","f":["Hammurabi's law code, from about 1754 BC, was carved on a black stone stele now in the Louvre.","The blue-tiled Ishtar Gate has been rebuilt in a museum in Berlin.","Babylon became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2019."],"q":{"q":"Which king's law code came from Babylon?","a":"Hammurabi's","x":["Gilgamesh's","Sargon's"]}},"ur":{"name":"Ur","era":"c. 3800 – 500 BC","t":"A Sumerian city still watched over by its ziggurat","s":"In the desert of southern Iraq rises the stepped bulk of the Great Ziggurat of Ur, built more than 4,000 years ago. Ur was one of the richest cities of Sumer, a port on the Euphrates close to the Gulf. The river and the coast have since moved away, leaving it stranded in the sand. From cruise the ziggurat is a speck, but the edge of the green river plain against the desert is clear.","w":"Look for the boundary between the green Euphrates plain near Nasiriyah and the empty desert just south-west of it.","f":["The ziggurat was built by King Ur-Nammu around 2100 BC and partly restored in modern times.","Its royal tombs, dug in the 1920s, held gold helmets, lyres and the mosaic box called the Standard of Ur.","Tradition names 'Ur of the Chaldees' as the birthplace of Abraham."],"q":{"q":"Who built the Great Ziggurat of Ur?","a":"Ur-Nammu","x":["Nebuchadnezzar II","Hammurabi"]}},"uruk":{"name":"Uruk","era":"c. 4000 – 300 BC","t":"One of the first cities, where writing began","s":"Here, in what is now desert, stood Uruk, one of the first true cities in the world. By about 3000 BC tens of thousands of people lived inside its walls, and its temple officials began pressing marks into clay tablets to keep accounts: some of the earliest writing anywhere. Legend made Gilgamesh its king. From above, look for the old dry channels of the Euphrates wandering across the plain.","w":"Look for faint old river channels and the edge of green farmland in the desert east of Samawah.","f":["The earliest tablets from Uruk, from about 3300 BC, are mostly records of grain, beer and livestock.","Its city walls, credited in legend to Gilgamesh, ran about 9 km around.","The Epic of Gilgamesh is one of the oldest surviving works of literature."],"q":{"q":"Which legendary hero was king of Uruk?","a":"Gilgamesh","x":["Hammurabi","Sargon"]}},"nineveh":{"name":"Nineveh","era":"c. 700 – 612 BC (as capital)","t":"Assyria's capital and its great library","s":"On the east bank of the Tigris, opposite Mosul, lie the mounds of Nineveh, once the largest city in the world. Assyrian kings lined its palaces with carved stone reliefs and built canals and aqueducts to water its gardens. King Ashurbanipal gathered a library of clay tablets, and among them was the Epic of Gilgamesh. From above, look for the Tigris flowing south through a city on both banks.","w":"Look for the Tigris running north–south through the city of Mosul; the ancient mounds lie on its eastern bank.","f":["Ashurbanipal's library held tens of thousands of clay tablets, now mostly in the British Museum.","In 1872 a tablet from it was found to tell a flood story much like Noah's.","Sennacherib's canals to Nineveh crossed a valley on the Jerwan aqueduct, one of the oldest known."],"q":{"q":"Whose library at Nineveh held the Epic of Gilgamesh?","a":"Ashurbanipal's","x":["Nebuchadnezzar's","Darius's"]}},"persepolis":{"name":"Persepolis","era":"c. 518 – 330 BC","t":"The ceremonial capital of the Persian kings","s":"On a stone terrace at the foot of a mountain, the Persian kings built Persepolis to receive envoys from across their empire. Its stairways are carved with long processions of Medes, Egyptians, Indians and many others bringing gifts. It was burned in 330 BC after Alexander the Great took it, and its columns still stand among the ruins. From above, look for the wide Marvdasht plain north of Shiraz.","w":"Look for the broad, farmed Marvdasht plain about 60 km north-east of Shiraz, edged by bare mountains.","f":["Darius the Great began building Persepolis around 518 BC.","Its reliefs show envoys of more than 20 peoples bringing gifts to the king.","The tomb of Cyrus the Great stands at nearby Pasargadae."],"q":{"q":"Which king founded Persepolis?","a":"Darius the Great","x":["Cyrus the Great","Xerxes"]}},"yazd":{"name":"Yazd, city of windcatchers","era":"5th c. – today","t":"A mud-brick city that learned to live in the desert","s":"Between two great deserts lies Yazd, one of the oldest living cities of Iran. Its old town is built of mud brick, with towers called windcatchers that funnel cool air into the houses below. Water came from qanats, underground channels tunnelled from distant mountains. From cruise, look for a green oasis city on a vast beige plain, with the Shir Kuh mountains to its south-west.","w":"Look for an oasis city on a wide beige desert plain, with the high Shir Kuh mountains to the south-west.","f":["The historic city of Yazd became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2017.","A Zoroastrian fire temple in Yazd keeps a flame said to have burned since AD 470.","Qanat shafts show from the air as long dotted lines across the desert, best seen from lower altitude."],"q":{"q":"What is a badgir in Yazd?","a":"A windcatcher tower","x":["An ice house","A water mill"]}},"giza":{"name":"Pyramids of Giza","era":"c. 2600 – 2500 BC","t":"The last Wonder of the ancient world still standing","s":"Where the green Nile valley meets the desert, on the western edge of Cairo, stand the pyramids of Giza. The Great Pyramid of Khufu was the tallest human-made structure on Earth for about 3,800 years. Nearby Saqqara holds the step pyramid of Djoser, the first of them all. In clear air, look for three small pale triangles at the desert's edge, right beside the city.","w":"Look where Cairo's sprawl meets the desert to the south-west; the pyramids sit on the plateau's edge like pale specks.","f":["The Great Pyramid was originally about 146 m tall.","It is the only one of the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World that still stands.","Its sides face the four compass points with an error of only a fraction of a degree."],"q":{"q":"Which pharaoh built the Great Pyramid?","a":"Khufu","x":["Khafre","Ramesses II"]}},"thebes":{"name":"Thebes: Karnak and the Valley of the Kings","era":"c. 2000 – 1000 BC","t":"Temples on one bank, royal tombs on the other","s":"At Luxor the Nile divides two worlds. On the east bank stood Thebes, capital of Egypt's New Kingdom, with the vast temples of Karnak and Luxor. On the west bank, in desert valleys behind the cliffs, the pharaohs cut their tombs, including Tutankhamun's. From cruise the monuments are too small, but the contrast is striking: a narrow green valley, then sudden bare desert and cliffs.","w":"A thin green strip of fields along the Nile, with the city of Luxor on the east bank and pale cliffs to the west.","f":["Karnak is one of the largest religious complexes ever built.","More than 60 tombs have been found in the Valley of the Kings.","Howard Carter found Tutankhamun's nearly intact tomb there in 1922."],"q":{"q":"Who found Tutankhamun's tomb in 1922?","a":"Howard Carter","x":["Flinders Petrie","Auguste Mariette"]}},"abu-simbel":{"name":"Abu Simbel","era":"c. 1264 BC; moved 1964–1968","t":"Temples cut into a mountain, then moved","s":"Look for the huge blue sheet of Lake Nasser in the Nubian desert. On its shore sit the temples of Ramesses II, fronted by four seated giants, each about 20 m tall. When the Aswan High Dam was about to flood them in the 1960s, an international effort led by UNESCO cut the temples into blocks and rebuilt them higher up. The temples are too small to see, but the lake is vast.","w":"A long, branching blue lake in the desert near Egypt's southern border; the temples lie on its western shore.","f":["The temples were cut into over 1,000 blocks and rebuilt about 65 m higher.","Twice a year, the rising sun reaches deep inside to light statues in the inner sanctuary.","The rescue campaign helped inspire UNESCO's World Heritage list."],"q":{"q":"Why were the Abu Simbel temples moved?","a":"To escape a dam's lake","x":["To avoid earthquakes","To make way for a road"]}},"alexandria-ancient":{"name":"Ancient Alexandria","era":"331 BC – 7th c. AD","t":"The city of the Lighthouse and the Great Library","s":"Alexander the Great founded this city on the Mediterranean in 331 BC, and it became the capital of the Ptolemies and the scholarly centre of the ancient world. Its Great Library gathered books from everywhere, and its lighthouse on the island of Pharos was one of the Seven Wonders. From above, look for a long city stretched along the coast between the sea and a lake, west of the Nile delta.","w":"A city strung out along the coast for many kilometres, between the sea and Lake Mariout, west of the Nile delta.","f":["The Pharos lighthouse stood for centuries; a 15th-century fort now occupies its site.","Eratosthenes estimated the Earth's circumference here around 240 BC, remarkably well.","Cleopatra VII, the last Ptolemaic ruler of Egypt, reigned from Alexandria."],"q":{"q":"Who measured the Earth's size from Alexandria?","a":"Eratosthenes","x":["Archimedes","Pythagoras"]}},"st-catherine-sinai":{"name":"St Catherine's Monastery, Sinai","era":"from the 6th c.","t":"A monastery among red granite peaks","s":"In the jagged red granite mountains of southern Sinai, at the foot of the peak tradition calls Mount Sinai, stands St Catherine's Monastery. It was founded in the 6th century and has been lived in by monks ever since, making it one of the oldest working Christian monasteries in the world. Its library holds priceless early manuscripts. From above, look for the wild knot of mountains between two gulfs.","w":"Look for a knot of jagged reddish mountains at the southern tip of Sinai, between the Gulf of Suez and the Gulf of Aqaba.","f":["The monastery was built on the orders of Emperor Justinian between about 548 and 565.","The 4th-century Codex Sinaiticus, one of the oldest complete Bibles, was long kept here.","Mount Sinai, the peak above it, rises to about 2,285 m."],"q":{"q":"Which emperor founded St Catherine's Monastery?","a":"Justinian","x":["Constantine","Theodosius"]}},"hegra":{"name":"Hegra (Mada'in Salih)","era":"1st c. BC – 1st c. AD","t":"Petra's southern sister, carved into desert rocks","s":"Among sandstone outcrops that rise like islands from the sand near AlUla lies Hegra, the second city of the Nabataeans after Petra. On the Incense Route, it grew rich from passing caravans, and its people carved more than a hundred monumental tombs into the rocks, many with inscriptions naming their owners. From above, look for wind-sculpted sandstone massifs and the green ribbon of the AlUla oasis.","w":"Look for scattered sandstone outcrops rising from a sandy plain, and the green palm valley of AlUla to the south.","f":["Hegra has 111 monumental tombs, 94 of them with decorated facades.","In 2008 it became Saudi Arabia's first UNESCO World Heritage site.","Inscriptions on the tombs record owners' names, dates and fines for anyone who misuses the grave."],"q":{"q":"Which people carved the tombs of Hegra?","a":"The Nabataeans","x":["The Romans","The Hittites"]}},"marib":{"name":"Marib, the land of Sheba","era":"c. 8th c. BC – 6th c. AD","t":"Capital of Saba and its famous dam","s":"Where the mountains of Yemen meet the desert stood Marib, capital of the kingdom of Saba, the biblical Sheba. Its wealth came from incense and from a great dam that stored the seasonal floods and watered a huge oasis. For more than a thousand years it was one of the engineering wonders of Arabia. From above, look for the line where dark highlands give way to open sand.","w":"Look where the dark Yemeni highlands end abruptly at the edge of the sandy Ramlat al-Sab'atayn desert.","f":["The Great Dam of Marib was about 600 m long and fed canals that watered thousands of hectares.","Tradition links the kingdom of Saba with the Queen of Sheba.","In 2023 UNESCO listed Marib's ancient sites as World Heritage."],"q":{"q":"Which legendary queen is linked with Saba?","a":"The Queen of Sheba","x":["Queen Zenobia","Queen Nefertiti"]}},"dilmun":{"name":"Dilmun burial mounds","era":"c. 2200 – 1750 BC","t":"The Bronze Age trading island of the Gulf","s":"Bahrain was the heart of Dilmun, a Bronze Age trading power that linked Mesopotamia with the Indus valley. Sumerian poems describe it as a pure land of fresh water. Its people built tens of thousands of burial mounds, which once covered much of the island. They are too small to see from cruise, but the island itself is clear, tied to Arabia by a long causeway.","w":"Look for a low sandy island in the Gulf, joined to the Saudi coast by a long causeway to its west.","f":["Around 11,000 of the burial mounds survive, and many more once existed.","Dilmun's ships carried copper from Oman to the cities of Mesopotamia.","The mounds became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2019."],"q":{"q":"Which modern country was the heart of Dilmun?","a":"Bahrain","x":["Oman","Kuwait"]}},"mesopotamia":{"name":"Mesopotamia","era":"c. 6000 BC – AD 600","t":"The land between the rivers, where cities began","s":"This is the land between the Tigris and the Euphrates, where people first built cities, wrote on clay and kept laws in writing. Sumer, Akkad, Assyria and Babylon all rose here, fed by irrigation canals in a land of little rain. From cruise you can see why it mattered: two green river ribbons crossing an otherwise brown plain, converging towards the Gulf.","w":"Look for two rivers, each lined with green, running south-east across a brown plain and slowly converging.","f":["Our 60-minute hour and 360-degree circle come from Mesopotamian counting in sixties.","The name is Greek for 'between the rivers'.","The Tigris and Euphrates finally join to form the Shatt al-Arab before reaching the Gulf."],"q":{"q":"What does the name 'Mesopotamia' mean?","a":"Between the rivers","x":["Land of the sun","Between two seas"]}},"cappadocia":{"name":"Cappadocia","era":"4th – 11th c. (rock churches)","t":"A land carved from volcanic ash","s":"Ancient eruptions buried this part of Anatolia in thick volcanic ash, which hardened into soft rock. Wind and rain carved it into cones and pillars, and people carved it too: cave houses, rock-cut churches painted with frescoes, and whole underground towns many levels deep. From cruise the fairy chimneys are invisible, but the snow-capped volcano Erciyes that helped make them is a landmark.","w":"Look for the big snowy cone of Mount Erciyes near Kayseri; the rock valleys lie about 70 km west of it.","f":["The underground city of Derinkuyu reaches about 85 m below ground.","Göreme's rock-cut churches hold frescoes painted between the 10th and 12th centuries.","Cappadocia became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1985."],"q":{"q":"What formed Cappadocia's soft rock?","a":"Volcanic ash","x":["Coral reefs","Glacial clay"]}},"lycia":{"name":"Lycia","era":"c. 1200 BC – AD 400","t":"Temple-fronted tombs cut into seaside cliffs","s":"Where the Taurus mountains plunge into the turquoise Mediterranean lay Lycia, a land of proud city-states. Its people cut tombs into cliff faces with fronts like little temples, and they joined in a league that gave larger cities more votes. Today the same coast is a holiday favourite. From above, look for the rugged peninsula between Fethiye and Antalya, its mountains falling straight into the sea.","w":"Look for a mountainous peninsula jutting into the sea between the bays of Fethiye and Antalya.","f":["The Lycian League was praised by Montesquieu and cited in the Federalist Papers.","St Nicholas, the model for Santa Claus, was bishop of the Lycian city of Myra.","The Lycian Way, a hiking trail of more than 500 km, links many of the ancient sites."],"q":{"q":"Which saint was bishop of Myra in Lycia?","a":"St Nicholas","x":["St George","St Paul"]}},"phoenicia":{"name":"Phoenicia","era":"c. 1500 – 300 BC","t":"The seafarers who gave the world an alphabet","s":"On this narrow strip between the mountains of Lebanon and the sea lived the Phoenicians, the great sailors of the ancient Mediterranean. From Tyre, Sidon and Byblos they traded cedar wood and purple cloth, founded Carthage and settlements as far as Spain. Their simple alphabet of 22 letters spread with them and became the ancestor of Greek, Latin and many others. The coast and its snowy mountains are easy to see.","w":"Look for a thin coastal strip of towns backed by a high mountain range, often snowy in winter and spring.","f":["Tyrian purple dye was made from sea snails and was worth more than its weight in gold.","The word 'Bible' comes via Greek from the Phoenician port of Byblos.","The Phoenician alphabet had 22 letters, all of them consonants."],"q":{"q":"What is the Phoenicians' most lasting gift?","a":"The alphabet","x":["Paper","The wheel"]}},"mesopotamian-marshes":{"name":"Mesopotamian Marshes","era":"c. 3000 BC – today","t":"Reed villages that Sumer would recognise","s":"Where the Tigris and Euphrates approach each other near the Gulf, they spread into a vast maze of lakes and reed beds. For thousands of years the Marsh Arabs have lived here, building arched houses entirely of reeds and herding water buffalo by boat. Carvings from Sumer show almost identical buildings. From above, look for a patchwork of blue water and green-brown reeds in the desert.","w":"A mosaic of lakes, channels and reed beds, blue and green against the desert, between Nasiriyah and Basra.","f":["The marshes became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2016.","Traditional guest houses called mudhif are built of reeds without a single nail.","Some writers have linked the marshes with the biblical Garden of Eden."],"q":{"q":"What is a traditional mudhif built from?","a":"Reeds","x":["Mud brick","Palm trunks"]}},"faiyum":{"name":"Faiyum oasis","era":"c. 1900 BC – AD 300","t":"A green fan in the desert, watered by the Nile","s":"South-west of Cairo, a desert basin is fed by a canal from the Nile, turning it into a green fan of fields around a salty lake. Pharaohs of the Middle Kingdom developed it for farming almost 4,000 years ago, and in Roman times its people were buried with lifelike painted portraits. From cruise it is one of Egypt's clearest sights: a bright green patch alone in the desert.","w":"A bright green fan-shaped oasis in the desert west of the Nile, with a long lake along its northern edge.","f":["The Faiyum mummy portraits, painted on wood, are among the finest portraits to survive from antiquity.","Nearby Wadi Al-Hitan holds fossils of ancient whales that once swam in a sea here.","Lake Qarun, on the oasis's northern edge, lies below sea level."],"q":{"q":"What are the famous Roman-era finds of the Faiyum?","a":"Painted mummy portraits","x":["Gold death masks","Clay soldier statues"]}},"silk-road-northern":{"name":"Silk Road (northern route)","era":"2nd c. BC – 15th c.","t":"The caravan road that tied China to the Mediterranean","s":"This was the main artery of the Silk Road, from Chang'an in China through the Hexi Corridor and the Tarim oases to Samarkand, Persia and the Mediterranean. Silk went west; horses, glass, spices and ideas travelled in every direction, and Buddhism reached China this way. No caravan went end to end. From above, look for oasis towns strung like beads between snowy mountains and desert.","w":"Look for green oases strung along the foot of snowy mountains, with sand or gravel desert on the other side.","f":["The name 'Silk Road' was coined in 1877 by the German geographer Ferdinand von Richthofen.","Paper-making travelled west along these routes from China.","In 2014 the section from Chang'an to the Tian Shan became a UNESCO World Heritage site."],"q":{"q":"Who coined the term 'Silk Road' in 1877?","a":"Ferdinand von Richthofen","x":["Marco Polo","Sven Hedin"]}},"silk-road-southern":{"name":"Silk Road (southern Tarim route)","era":"2nd c. BC – 10th c.","t":"Oasis to oasis between desert and the Kunlun","s":"This branch of the Silk Road skirted the southern edge of the Taklamakan desert, hopping from oasis to oasis along streams that run down from the Kunlun mountains. Khotan, its jewel, was famous for jade and for Buddhist learning. From Kashgar and Tashkurgan caravans climbed over the Pamirs towards Afghanistan. From cruise it is spectacular: a wall of snowy peaks on one side, a sea of dunes on the other.","w":"Look for a line of small green oases between the snowy Kunlun range to the south and the Taklamakan's dunes to the north.","f":["Khotan's jade was carried east to China for thousands of years.","Legend says silkworm eggs were smuggled to Khotan hidden in a princess's headdress.","The monk Xuanzang took this route home from India, reaching Chang'an in 645."],"q":{"q":"Which precious stone made Khotan famous?","a":"Jade","x":["Lapis lazuli","Turquoise"]}},"silk-road-tianshan":{"name":"Silk Road (Tian Shan corridor)","era":"2nd c. BC – 16th c.","t":"Along the northern foot of the Heavenly Mountains","s":"North of the Tian Shan, the Heavenly Mountains, another branch of the Silk Road ran from Turpan past today's Ürümqi and Almaty to the cities of the Syr Darya. It passed through well-watered valleys and grassland, good country for horses and for nomads who traded with the caravans. From cruise, follow the long snowy wall of the Tian Shan with green foothills and cities at its feet.","w":"Follow the long snowy wall of the Tian Shan, with cities and green foothills along its northern base.","f":["The Tang poet Li Bai is often said to have been born near Suyab, a city on this route.","Otrar, near its western end, was the birthplace of the philosopher al-Farabi.","The route passes Sayram Lake, a blue alpine lake high in the mountains."],"q":{"q":"Which mountain lake lies on this route?","a":"Sayram Lake","x":["Lake Balkhash","Lake Baikal"]}},"maritime-silk-road":{"name":"Maritime Silk Road","era":"2nd c. BC – 15th c.","t":"The monsoon highway from China to the Gulf","s":"Long before planes, the sea linked Asia. Ships carried silk, porcelain and tea from southern China through the Strait of Malacca and across the Indian Ocean to India, Arabia and East Africa, returning with spices, ivory and incense. Sailors timed their voyages to the monsoon winds, which reverse each season. Look for the island-studded coasts and straits where ports grew rich on this trade.","w":"Look for busy straits and island coasts, especially the narrow Strait of Malacca between Malaysia and Sumatra.","f":["The Ming admiral Zheng He led seven great fleets across the Indian Ocean between 1405 and 1433.","Marco Polo and Ibn Battuta both described Quanzhou as one of the world's greatest ports.","A 9th-century Arab ship found off Indonesia carried about 60,000 pieces of Chinese pottery."],"q":{"q":"Which Ming admiral led fleets across the Indian Ocean?","a":"Zheng He","x":["Kublai Khan","Ibn Battuta"]}},"trans-caspian-railway":{"name":"Trans-Caspian Railway","era":"1880 – 1898","t":"The first railway across Central Asia's deserts","s":"This is the first railway of Central Asia. The Russian Empire began it on the Caspian shore in 1880 and pushed it east along the foot of the Kopet Dag mountains, across the Karakum desert and over the Amu Darya to Bukhara and Samarkand. Travellers could now reach the Silk Road cities by train. Look for the green strip at the mountains' foot and the empty desert beyond.","w":"Look for the Kopet Dag range with a strip of green towns along its northern foot, and the Karakum sands beyond.","f":["It was the first railway built in Central Asia.","The line reached Samarkand in 1888 and Tashkent in 1898.","It was built under the direction of General Mikhail Annenkov."],"q":{"q":"Which desert did the Trans-Caspian Railway cross?","a":"The Karakum","x":["The Gobi","The Taklamakan"]}},"samarkand":{"name":"Samarkand","era":"7th c. BC – today","t":"The blue-domed crossroads of the Silk Road","s":"Samarkand is more than 2,500 years old and was for centuries the great meeting point of the Silk Road. Under Timur and his descendants in the 14th and 15th centuries it was filled with turquoise domes and tiled madrasas, and its Registan square is still one of the grandest in the Islamic world. From above, look for the city spread across the green Zeravshan valley, with mountains rising to the south.","w":"Look for a large city in the green, irrigated Zeravshan valley, with mountain ridges to the south and desert beyond.","f":["The ruler Ulugh Beg built an observatory here in the 1420s and catalogued about a thousand stars.","Samarkand paper was famous across the Islamic world from the 8th century.","The city became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2001."],"q":{"q":"Which ruler built an observatory in Samarkand?","a":"Ulugh Beg","x":["Omar Khayyam","Al-Biruni"]}},"bukhara":{"name":"Bukhara","era":"5th c. BC – today","t":"A holy city of scholars, domes and bazaars","s":"In an oasis at the end of the Zeravshan river lies Bukhara, one of the best-preserved medieval cities of Central Asia. In the 9th and 10th centuries it was the capital of the Samanids and a centre of Persian poetry and science. Its old centre still has covered bazaars, madrasas and a 12th-century minaret. From above, look for a green oasis surrounded by desert, west of Samarkand.","w":"Look for a green irrigated oasis ringed by desert, about 220 km west of Samarkand.","f":["The physician and philosopher Avicenna was born near Bukhara in 980.","The Kalyan Minaret, built in 1127, rises about 46 m above the old town.","The Samanid Mausoleum, from the 10th century, is a masterpiece of decorative brickwork."],"q":{"q":"Which great physician was born near Bukhara?","a":"Avicenna","x":["Galen","Al-Razi"]}},"khiva":{"name":"Khiva","era":"from the 10th c.","t":"A walled desert town like a museum under open sky","s":"On the edge of the Karakum desert, in the delta oasis of the Amu Darya, stands Khiva. Its inner town, Itchan Kala, is enclosed by mud-brick walls and crammed with minarets, madrasas and palaces, mostly from the 18th and 19th centuries. It was the last stop before caravans crossed the desert to Persia. From above, look for the green irrigated oasis sharp against sand on every side.","w":"Look for a large green irrigated oasis on the Amu Darya, with bare desert pressing in on all sides.","f":["Itchan Kala became Uzbekistan's first UNESCO World Heritage site in 1990.","The Kalta Minor minaret was left unfinished and stands short and wide, covered in turquoise tiles.","The mathematician al-Khwarizmi, whose name gave us the word 'algorithm', came from this region."],"q":{"q":"Which word comes from the name of Khwarazm's mathematician?","a":"Algorithm","x":["Alchemy","Almanac"]}},"merv":{"name":"Merv","era":"6th c. BC – 13th c.","t":"Once one of the largest cities in the world","s":"In the delta of the Murghab river, in the Karakum desert, lie the ruins of Merv. For 2,000 years a succession of walled cities rose side by side here, each built next to the last. In the 12th century, as capital of the Seljuk sultans, it was among the largest cities on Earth. From above, look for the green oasis of Mary amid the desert; the ruins lie just east of the town.","w":"Look for the green delta oasis of the Murghab river in the Karakum; the ruins lie just east of the city of Mary.","f":["The mausoleum of Sultan Sanjar, from the 12th century, still has its great brick dome.","Its oldest walled city, Erk Kala, dates to the Achaemenid Persian era.","Ancient Merv became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1999."],"q":{"q":"In which river's delta did Merv grow?","a":"The Murghab","x":["The Amu Darya","The Syr Darya"]}},"ani":{"name":"Ani","era":"10th – 14th c.","t":"The medieval city of a thousand and one churches","s":"On a triangle of plateau above a river gorge stand the ruins of Ani, capital of a medieval Armenian kingdom. In the 11th century it was one of the great cities of its age, its skyline crowded with churches. Many still stand, roofless, on the open grassland. From above, look for the high plateau and the deep gorge that today marks the border between Turkey and Armenia.","w":"Look for a high green-brown plateau cut by a deep river gorge, east of the town of Kars.","f":["Ani was nicknamed 'the city of 1,001 churches'.","Its cathedral, finished around 1001, uses pointed arches long before Gothic Europe.","Ani became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2016."],"q":{"q":"What was Ani's nickname?","a":"City of 1,001 churches","x":["City of 100 towers","City of seven hills"]}},"mtskheta":{"name":"Mtskheta","era":"from the 3rd c. BC","t":"Georgia's ancient capital where two rivers meet","s":"Just north of Tbilisi, where the Aragvi flows into the Kura, lies Mtskheta, the ancient capital of the Georgian kingdom of Iberia. Here Georgia adopted Christianity in the 4th century, one of the first countries to do so. A small 6th-century monastery, Jvari, watches over the confluence from a hilltop. From above, look for the two rivers meeting in a green valley, often in two different colours.","w":"Look for two rivers joining in a narrow green valley about 20 km north of Tbilisi.","f":["Svetitskhoveli Cathedral in Mtskheta was built in the 11th century on an older church site.","Jvari Monastery gave its name to a whole style of Georgian church architecture.","Mtskheta's churches became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1994."],"q":{"q":"Which two rivers meet at Mtskheta?","a":"The Aragvi and the Kura","x":["The Rioni and the Kura","The Terek and the Aragvi"]}},"derbent":{"name":"Derbent","era":"6th c. – today","t":"The Gate of Gates between mountains and sea","s":"Here the Caucasus mountains come within a few kilometres of the Caspian Sea, leaving a narrow pass along the coast. In the 6th century Persian kings built two parallel stone walls from the mountain fortress down into the sea to control it. Arab geographers called it the Gate of Gates. From above, the reason is obvious: a thin coastal strip squeezed between blue water and steep ridges.","w":"Look for a narrow coastal plain squeezed between the Caspian Sea and the steep foothills of the Caucasus.","f":["Derbent is often called the oldest city in Russia.","Its walls once ran out into the sea, forming a guarded harbour.","The citadel and old city became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2003."],"q":{"q":"Which sea do Derbent's walls run down to?","a":"The Caspian Sea","x":["The Black Sea","The Sea of Azov"]}},"baikonur":{"name":"Baikonur, launch site of the first human spaceflight","era":"from 1955","t":"Where the first human left for space","s":"On the flat steppe of Kazakhstan, near the Syr Darya river, lies the world's first spaceport. The first artificial satellite, Sputnik, was launched from here in 1957. On 12 April 1961 Yuri Gagarin lifted off from the same pad to become the first human in space. From above, look for the ribbon of the Syr Darya across the dry steppe, and the town of Baikonur beside it.","w":"Look for the thin green ribbon of the Syr Darya crossing flat, dry steppe, with a town and scattered launch complexes north of it.","f":["Gagarin's flight lasted 108 minutes and made one orbit of the Earth.","His launch pad, known as Gagarin's Start, stayed in use for more than 60 years.","The cosmodrome is leased by Russia from Kazakhstan."],"q":{"q":"How long did Gagarin's first spaceflight last?","a":"108 minutes","x":["12 minutes","24 hours"]}},"arkaim":{"name":"Arkaim","era":"c. 2000 – 1700 BC","t":"A ring-shaped Bronze Age town in the Ural steppe","s":"On the open steppe east of the southern Urals lie the remains of Arkaim, a fortified settlement built around 4,000 years ago as two rings of walls with houses set against them. It belongs to the Sintashta culture, known for some of the world's earliest spoked-wheel chariots. The rings are too faint to see from cruise, but the rolling grassland around them has changed little since.","w":"Look for open, rolling steppe with river valleys and few trees, south of Chelyabinsk and east of the Ural ridges.","f":["Arkaim was found in 1987 by archaeologists surveying land for a planned reservoir.","The discovery led to the reservoir plan being dropped and the land becoming a reserve.","The settlement was about 150 m across."],"q":{"q":"What nearly flooded Arkaim soon after it was found?","a":"A planned reservoir","x":["A mining project","A river's change of course"]}},"khorezm":{"name":"Khwarazm","era":"6th c. BC – 19th c.","t":"An oasis kingdom on the Amu Darya delta","s":"Where the Amu Darya fans out before the Aral Sea, irrigation created a green island between two deserts: Khwarazm, home to kingdoms for 2,500 years. Dozens of ancient mud-brick fortresses still stand on the desert edge, and its old capital, Konye-Urgench, keeps a soaring minaret. From cruise the oasis is one of the region's clearest sights: a big patch of green and water in beige desert.","w":"A large green patchwork of irrigated fields and canals on the lower Amu Darya, between the Karakum and Kyzylkum deserts.","f":["The scholar al-Biruni, born here in 973, calculated the Earth's radius with remarkable accuracy.","The Kutlug Timur minaret in Konye-Urgench is about 60 m tall.","The fortresses of ancient Khwarazm are among the oldest in Central Asia."],"q":{"q":"Which scholar born in Khwarazm measured the Earth's radius?","a":"Al-Biruni","x":["Avicenna","Ulugh Beg"]}},"bactria":{"name":"Bactria","era":"6th c. BC – 7th c. AD","t":"Where Greek, Persian and Indian worlds met","s":"Between the Hindu Kush and the Amu Darya lay Bactria, a rich land of oasis cities. It was a Persian province, then a Greek kingdom founded by Alexander's successors, then the heart of the Kushan empire, which carried Buddhism north along the Silk Road. Its chief city, Balkh, was called the mother of cities. From above, look for the Amu Darya's green valley with the Hindu Kush to the south.","w":"Look for the green valley of the Amu Darya, with the high, often snowy Hindu Kush rising to the south.","f":["A Greek city with a theatre and gymnasium, Ai-Khanoum, stood on the Amu Darya in the 3rd century BC.","The two-humped Bactrian camel is named after this region.","The poet Rumi is known in Persian as Balkhi, 'from Balkh'."],"q":{"q":"What was Balkh called in ancient times?","a":"The mother of cities","x":["The city of kings","The pearl of the east"]}},"scythian-steppe":{"name":"Scythian steppe","era":"7th – 3rd c. BC","t":"Grasslands of the nomads and their golden kurgans","s":"North of the Black Sea stretches the steppe where the Scythians once lived: horse-riding nomads who moved with their herds and buried their leaders under great earth mounds called kurgans. Their goldsmiths made some of the finest animal-style jewellery of the ancient world. Today the land is farmed in huge fields, and from above it looks like a patchwork quilt on a flat table.","w":"A vast flat plain of large rectangular fields, cut by broad rivers flowing south to the Black Sea.","f":["Herodotus devoted a whole book of his Histories to the Scythians.","A gold pectoral found in a kurgan in 1971 is one of the masterpieces of Scythian art.","Kurgans still rise from the fields, some nearly 20 m high."],"q":{"q":"Which Greek historian described the Scythians?","a":"Herodotus","x":["Thucydides","Plutarch"]}},"colchis":{"name":"Colchis","era":"c. 13th – 1st c. BC","t":"The land of the Golden Fleece","s":"Between the Caucasus and the Black Sea lies the green, humid lowland of Colchis, in today's western Georgia. Greek myth sent Jason and the Argonauts here to seize the Golden Fleece. Historians note that people in the nearby mountains once caught gold dust from rivers on sheepskins, which may lie behind the legend. From above, look for the flat green plain of the Rioni river opening to the sea.","w":"Look for a flat, very green lowland along the eastern Black Sea, where the Rioni river reaches the coast at Poti.","f":["Excavations at Vani have uncovered rich gold jewellery made in Colchis.","The region is one of the wettest in the Caucasus, which keeps it green all year.","Greek colonists founded trading towns on the Colchian coast from the 6th century BC."],"q":{"q":"What did Jason seek in Colchis?","a":"The Golden Fleece","x":["The Holy Grail","Achilles' armour"]}},"urartu":{"name":"Urartu","era":"9th – 6th c. BC","t":"The Iron Age kingdom of Lake Van and Ararat","s":"Around the vast blue Lake Van lay Urartu, a mountain kingdom of the Iron Age. Its kings built fortresses on rocky crags, cut irrigation canals through the valleys and left inscriptions in cuneiform. Its name survives in Ararat, the snow-capped volcano on its northern edge. From cruise this is superb country: a huge lake, volcano cones and high, bare plateaus.","w":"Look for the huge blue Lake Van, and to its north-east the lone white cone of Mount Ararat.","f":["Yerevan grew from the Urartian fortress of Erebuni, founded in 782 BC.","A canal built by King Menua in the 8th century BC still carries water to the Van area.","Lake Van is a salty soda lake where only one kind of fish can live."],"q":{"q":"Which city grew from the fortress of Erebuni?","a":"Yerevan","x":["Tbilisi","Baku"]}},"volga-trade-route":{"name":"Volga trade route","era":"8th – 11th c.","t":"Viking traders on Europe's longest river","s":"In the Viking Age, traders from Scandinavia rowed their boats down the Volga to the markets of Bulgar and the Caspian, exchanging furs, wax and honey for Arab silver. Some went on to Baghdad. The river still shows the way: follow its wide blue course, now broken by long reservoirs, as it curves south across Russia towards the Caspian.","w":"Follow the Volga, broad and blue with long reservoirs, curving south towards the Caspian lowlands.","f":["Tens of thousands of Arab silver coins have been found in Viking-age hoards in Scandinavia.","In 922 the Arab envoy Ibn Fadlan described meeting Rus traders on the Volga.","The Volga is Europe's longest river, about 3,500 km."],"q":{"q":"What coins flowed north along the Volga route?","a":"Arab silver dirhams","x":["Roman gold solidi","Chinese bronze coins"]}},"varangians-to-greeks":{"name":"Route from the Varangians to the Greeks","era":"9th – 12th c.","t":"The river road from the Baltic to Constantinople","s":"Here ran the great river road of early Rus, from the Baltic to Constantinople. Traders and travellers sailed up the Volkhov through Novgorod to Lake Ilmen, dragged their boats overland between rivers, then floated down the Dnieper past Kyiv to the Black Sea. The route helped bind the first East Slavic state together. From above, follow the chain of lakes and the broad Dnieper as it runs south.","w":"Look for Lake Ilmen and the Volkhov in the north, and far to the south the broad Dnieper flowing to the Black Sea.","f":["The route is described in the Primary Chronicle, written in Kyiv in the early 12th century.","Where rivers did not connect, boats were hauled overland on rollers along portages.","A 10th-century Byzantine emperor recorded the names of the Dnieper rapids in two languages, Slavic and Norse."],"q":{"q":"Which chronicle describes this route?","a":"The Primary Chronicle","x":["The Tale of Igor's Campaign","The Domostroy"]}},"trans-siberian-railway":{"name":"Trans-Siberian Railway","era":"built 1891 – 1916","t":"The longest railway on Earth, Moscow to the Pacific","s":"This is the longest railway line in the world, about 9,300 km from Moscow to Vladivostok. Built between 1891 and 1916, it tied Siberia and the Pacific coast to European Russia and turned a journey of months into one of days. From cruise, look for towns strung along a line through endless taiga, and for its great bridges over the Ob, the Yenisei and the Amur.","w":"Look for a chain of towns and clearings along a line through dark forest, crossing great rivers on long bridges.","f":["Construction was formally begun in Vladivostok in May 1891 by the future Tsar Nicholas II.","The line along the southern shore of Lake Baikal has dozens of tunnels cut into the cliffs.","The full journey by train takes about a week and crosses seven time-zone changes."],"q":{"q":"Where was construction formally begun in 1891?","a":"Vladivostok","x":["Moscow","Irkutsk"]}},"chinese-eastern-railway":{"name":"Chinese Eastern Railway","era":"built 1897 – 1903","t":"A railway shortcut across Manchuria","s":"Instead of following the long Amur bend, Russia built a shortcut straight across Manchuria to Vladivostok between 1897 and 1903. The line crossed the Greater Khingan mountains and the wide Manchurian plain, and at its junction on the Songhua river a new city grew: Harbin. From above, look for the forested Khingan ridges giving way to a vast flat farmland plain.","w":"Look for forested Greater Khingan ridges in the west, then a vast, flat patchwork of farmland around Harbin.","f":["Harbin was founded in 1898 as the railway's hub.","Harbin's old centre still has streets of Russian-style buildings from the railway era.","The line crossed the Greater Khingan through a tunnel about 3 km long."],"q":{"q":"Which city grew up as the railway's hub in 1898?","a":"Harbin","x":["Shenyang","Changchun"]}},"siberian-trakt":{"name":"Great Siberian Road (Siberian Trakt)","era":"18th – 19th c.","t":"The post road across Siberia before the railway","s":"Before the railway, this was the road to Siberia. From the 18th century the Siberian Trakt ran from Moscow over the Urals, through Tyumen, Tomsk and Irkutsk to Kyakhta on the Chinese border. Mail coaches, tea caravans and settlers used it, changing horses at post stations. The modern highway and railway follow much of its line through birch forest and taiga, still visible as a thin thread.","w":"Look for a road and railway threading east through birch forest and taiga, linking towns on the big rivers.","f":["Anton Chekhov travelled it in 1890 on his way to Sakhalin and wrote about its mud and its people.","Tea from China came west along the road from Kyakhta.","It was one of the longest roads in the world when it was completed."],"q":{"q":"Which writer crossed Siberia on this road in 1890?","a":"Anton Chekhov","x":["Leo Tolstoy","Ivan Turgenev"]}},"great-tea-road":{"name":"Great Tea Road","era":"17th – 19th c.","t":"Tea by camel from China to Russia","s":"For about two centuries, tea from the hills of southern China travelled north by river boat, cart and camel caravan across the Gobi and the Mongolian steppe to the border town of Kyakhta. There it was traded for furs and cloth and carried on across Siberia to Moscow. From above, look for the vast empty Gobi and the green valleys of the Selenga north of Ulaanbaatar.","w":"Look for the pale, empty Gobi giving way to the green Mongolian steppe and river valleys near Ulaanbaatar.","f":["A treaty in 1727 made Kyakhta the official place of trade between Russia and China.","Pressed tea bricks were used as money on the steppe.","Kyakhta's merchants became some of the richest people in the Russian Empire."],"q":{"q":"Which border town was the hub of the tea trade?","a":"Kyakhta","x":["Irkutsk","Nerchinsk"]}},"veliky-novgorod":{"name":"Veliky Novgorod","era":"9th – 15th c.","t":"A medieval merchant republic that wrote on birch bark","s":"On the Volkhov river, just north of Lake Ilmen, stands Novgorod, one of the oldest cities of Russia. For more than three centuries it was a merchant republic whose town assembly, the veche, chose its princes. Its soggy soil preserved over a thousand letters scratched on birch bark by ordinary people. From above, look for the lake and the river flowing north out of it through the city.","w":"Look for Lake Ilmen, with the Volkhov river flowing north out of it through the city.","f":["The first birch-bark letter was found here in 1951; more turn up every year.","St Sophia Cathedral, built in 1045–1050, is one of the oldest churches in Russia.","Novgorod's monuments became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1992."],"q":{"q":"What did archaeologists find in Novgorod in 1951?","a":"A birch-bark letter","x":["A Viking sword","A hoard of gold coins"]}},"staraya-ladoga":{"name":"Staraya Ladoga","era":"from the mid-8th c.","t":"Where the story of Rus begins","s":"Just before the Volkhov river enters Lake Ladoga lies Staraya Ladoga, a village that was once a busy trading town of Scandinavians, Slavs and Finns. Tree-ring dating puts its first buildings in the mid-8th century, making it one of the oldest towns of Rus. Chronicles link it with Rurik, the legendary founder of the first princely dynasty. From above, look for the Volkhov's final stretch and Europe's largest lake.","w":"Look for the Volkhov river running north into the south shore of the huge Lake Ladoga.","f":["Its earliest timber buildings have been dated by their tree rings to around 753.","Lake Ladoga, just to the north, is the largest lake in Europe.","The stone fortress by the river dates from the 12th and 16th centuries."],"q":{"q":"Which lake lies just north of Staraya Ladoga?","a":"Lake Ladoga","x":["Lake Onega","Lake Ilmen"]}},"kizhi":{"name":"Kizhi Pogost","era":"1714 – 1874","t":"Twenty-two wooden domes on a northern island","s":"On a small island in Lake Onega stands one of the wonders of wooden architecture: the Church of the Transfiguration, built in 1714, with 22 onion domes covered in silvery aspen shingles. Beside it rise a smaller church and a bell tower. Legend says the master builder threw his axe into the lake when he finished. From above, look for Lake Onega's northern bays, scattered with long narrow islands.","w":"Look for the northern part of Lake Onega, a maze of long, narrow islands and bays in the Karelian forest.","f":["The Church of the Transfiguration is about 37 m tall and built entirely of wood.","Its neighbour, the Church of the Intercession, has nine domes.","Kizhi Pogost became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1990."],"q":{"q":"How many domes crown the Church of the Transfiguration?","a":"22","x":["9","33"]}},"solovki":{"name":"Solovetsky Islands","era":"from 1436","t":"A fortress monastery on the White Sea","s":"Far out in the White Sea, just below the Arctic Circle, lie the Solovetsky Islands. Monks founded a monastery here in the 15th century, and it grew into a fortress of huge boulders, a trading power and a centre of northern culture. The islands also hold ancient stone labyrinths laid out by earlier peoples. From above, look for a cluster of forested islands dotted with lakes in the open sea.","w":"Look for a cluster of low, forested islands full of small lakes, in the western part of the White Sea.","f":["The monastery walls are built of boulders, some weighing several tonnes.","The monks dug canals linking dozens of the island's lakes.","The islands' monuments became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1992."],"q":{"q":"Which sea surrounds the Solovetsky Islands?","a":"The White Sea","x":["The Barents Sea","The Kara Sea"]}},"bolghar":{"name":"Bolghar","era":"10th – 14th c.","t":"The northern trading capital on the Volga","s":"Near where the Kama joins the Volga stood Bolghar, capital of Volga Bulgaria and one of the great markets of medieval Eurasia. Furs from the northern forests met silver and silks from the south. In 922 its ruler received an embassy from the Caliph of Baghdad and officially adopted Islam. From above, look for the vast Kuybyshev Reservoir, where two great rivers meet.","w":"Look for the enormous Kuybyshev Reservoir where the Kama joins the Volga, south of Kazan.","f":["The Arab envoy Ibn Fadlan wrote a vivid account of his journey here in 922.","Bolghar became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2014.","The Kuybyshev Reservoir beside it is the largest reservoir in Europe."],"q":{"q":"In which year did Volga Bulgaria adopt Islam?","a":"922","x":["988","1236"]}},"tobolsk":{"name":"Tobolsk","era":"from 1587","t":"The old capital of Siberia","s":"Where the Tobol flows into the Irtysh, Russian settlers founded Tobolsk in 1587, and for about two centuries it was the capital of all Siberia. On a high bluff above the rivers stands the only stone kremlin in Siberia, with white walls and church domes. The cartographer Semyon Remezov, who helped build it, also drew the first atlas of Siberia. From above, look for two rivers meeting in the taiga.","w":"Look for the meeting of the Tobol and the Irtysh, with the old town on a high bluff above the floodplain.","f":["The chemist Dmitri Mendeleev was born in Tobolsk in 1834.","Its kremlin was built of stone around 1700.","Tobolsk was the starting point for many expeditions further east."],"q":{"q":"Which scientist was born in Tobolsk?","a":"Dmitri Mendeleev","x":["Mikhail Lomonosov","Ivan Pavlov"]}},"mangazeya":{"name":"Mangazeya","era":"1601 – 1672","t":"A lost Arctic boomtown of the fur trade","s":"In the tundra on the Taz river, above the Arctic Circle, stood Mangazeya, the first Russian town in the Siberian Arctic. For a few decades in the 17th century it boomed on sable furs, reached by sea from the White Sea and by river. Then trade moved away and the town was abandoned. From above, look for the Taz winding through flat, lake-speckled tundra towards the Gulf of Ob.","w":"Look for the Taz river winding through flat tundra full of lakes, east of the long Gulf of Ob.","f":["Contemporaries called it 'gold-seething Mangazeya' for its riches.","In 1619 the tsar banned the sea route there, fearing foreign ships would follow it.","Excavations have found leather shoes, chess pieces and seals from across Europe."],"q":{"q":"What made Mangazeya rich?","a":"Sable furs","x":["Gold","Salt"]}},"gagarin-landing":{"name":"Gagarin's landing site","era":"12 April 1961","t":"Where the first spaceman came back to Earth","s":"On the morning of 12 April 1961, after one orbit of the Earth, Yuri Gagarin came down by parachute in a field on the Volga steppe, near the city of Engels. As planned, he had ejected from his capsule, which landed separately nearby. The first people he met were a forester's wife and her granddaughter. From above, look for the wide Volga at Saratov, with Engels on its eastern bank.","w":"Look for the broad Volga at Saratov; the landing site lies in the steppe a little south of Engels on the east bank.","f":["Gagarin landed about 108 minutes after launch.","The site is now a memorial park with a tall monument.","Vostok 1 was designed so that the pilot landed apart from the capsule by parachute."],"q":{"q":"How did Gagarin land?","a":"By parachute, apart from the capsule","x":["Inside the capsule, at sea","On an airfield runway"]}},"golden-ring":{"name":"Golden Ring of Russia","era":"12th – 17th c.","t":"Old Russian towns of white stone and onion domes","s":"North-east of Moscow lies a loop of old towns: Vladimir, Suzdal, Rostov, Yaroslavl, Kostroma and others. Between the 12th and 17th centuries they were princely capitals and monastery centres, and their white-stone churches, kremlins and onion domes still stand among fields and forests. From above, look for the Volga and for round Lake Pleshcheyevo and Lake Nero among the woods.","w":"Look for the Volga at Yaroslavl, and smaller round lakes, Pleshcheyevo and Nero, among forest and fields to the south.","f":["The name 'Golden Ring' was coined by a journalist in 1967.","The white-stone monuments of Vladimir and Suzdal became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1992.","The Church of the Intercession on the Nerl, from 1165, stands alone in a water meadow."],"q":{"q":"When was the name 'Golden Ring' coined?","a":"1967","x":["1812","1913"]}},"grand-trunk-road":{"name":"Grand Trunk Road","era":"3rd c. BC – today","t":"Two thousand years of traffic across the Ganges plain","s":"This is one of Asia's oldest and longest roads, crossing the Indo-Gangetic plain from Bengal to Kabul, some 2,500 km. It follows an ancient route, but the 16th-century ruler Sher Shah Suri rebuilt it with rest houses, wells and shade trees. Rudyard Kipling called it a river of life. From cruise, look for the flat green plain with the Ganges and cities strung along the line.","w":"Look for the vast flat green plain of northern India, with the Ganges and a line of big cities running north-west.","f":["Sher Shah Suri marked distances along it with pillars called kos minars, some still standing.","The road crosses the Khyber Pass on its way to Kabul.","Much of it is still a main highway, now national roads in several countries."],"q":{"q":"Which 16th-century ruler rebuilt the Grand Trunk Road?","a":"Sher Shah Suri","x":["Babur","Shah Jahan"]}},"grand-canal":{"name":"Grand Canal of China","era":"5th c. BC – today","t":"The world's longest canal, Beijing to Hangzhou","s":"For more than a thousand years this canal carried rice from the fertile south to feed the capital in the north. Begun in pieces as early as the 5th century BC and joined into one system in the early 7th, it runs about 1,800 km from Hangzhou to Beijing, crossing the Yangtze and the Yellow River. In the south it is still busy with barges, and in clear weather its line can be traced.","w":"Between Hangzhou and Yangzhou, look for a waterway threading past Suzhou and Wuxi among lakes and fields.","f":["It is the longest artificial waterway in the world.","The pound lock, with gates at both ends, was invented in China in the 10th century for canals like this.","The Grand Canal became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2014."],"q":{"q":"Which two cities does the Grand Canal link?","a":"Beijing and Hangzhou","x":["Beijing and Shanghai","Xi'an and Nanjing"]}},"great-wall":{"name":"Great Wall of China","era":"7th c. BC – 17th c.","t":"Two thousand years of walls across mountains and desert","s":"Chinese states and dynasties built walls along their northern frontier for about 2,000 years, and the stone-and-brick wall most people picture was raised by the Ming after 1368. It runs from the Gobi at Jiayuguan to the sea at Shanhaiguan, riding mountain ridges. It is only a few metres wide, so it cannot be seen from cruise height, but the ridges and deserts it crossed are easy to see.","w":"North of Beijing, look for steep, wrinkled mountain ridges; far to the west, the gravel desert of the Hexi Corridor.","f":["A 2012 survey measured all sections built over the centuries at about 21,000 km.","The idea that the wall is visible from the Moon is a myth.","At Old Dragon's Head near Shanhaiguan the wall runs down into the sea."],"q":{"q":"How long are all sections of the Great Wall combined?","a":"About 21,000 km","x":["About 2,100 km","About 210,000 km"]}},"tea-horse-road":{"name":"Tea Horse Road","era":"7th – 20th c.","t":"Tea from Yunnan carried over mountains to Tibet","s":"For more than a thousand years, caravans of mules and porters carried bricks of tea from Yunnan and Sichuan up into Tibet, and brought back horses. The road climbed through some of the deepest gorges on Earth, where the Yangtze, the Mekong and the Salween run side by side, and over passes above 4,000 m. From cruise the land is breathtaking: parallel river canyons and snowy peaks.","w":"Look for three deep river gorges running side by side from north to south, between ranges of snowy peaks.","f":["Tibetans drank tea churned with butter and salt, so demand was constant.","Old market towns such as Lijiang and Shaxi grew up as caravan stops.","Mule caravans still used parts of the route in the mid-20th century."],"q":{"q":"What did Tibet trade for Chinese tea?","a":"Horses","x":["Silk","Porcelain"]}},"tokaido":{"name":"Tōkaidō road","era":"17th – 19th c.","t":"The old coastal highway from Edo to Kyoto","s":"In the Edo period the Tōkaidō was Japan's busiest road, running about 500 km along the Pacific coast from Edo, today's Tokyo, to the imperial city of Kyoto. Lords, merchants and pilgrims walked it in about two weeks, stopping at 53 post towns. The artist Hiroshige made it famous in a series of prints. Today the bullet train follows almost the same line, with Mount Fuji watching over it.","w":"Follow the Pacific coast west from Tokyo; Mount Fuji rises just inland, and the route passes the Hakone hills.","f":["Hiroshige's print series 'The Fifty-three Stations of the Tōkaidō' appeared in the 1830s.","A barrier at Hakone checked travellers' papers on the road.","Parts of the old stone-paved road still survive near Hakone."],"q":{"q":"How many post stations were on the Tōkaidō?","a":"53","x":["69","88"]}},"kyoto":{"name":"Kyoto, the old imperial capital","era":"794 – 1868","t":"A thousand years as Japan's capital","s":"From 794 to 1868 Kyoto was the seat of Japan's emperors. Laid out on a grid modelled on the Chinese capital Chang'an, it became the heart of Japanese court culture: temples, gardens, tea ceremony and the tale of Genji. It still holds well over a thousand Buddhist temples and hundreds of Shinto shrines. From above, look for the city filling a flat basin ringed by wooded mountains, with Lake Biwa to the east.","w":"Look for a city in a flat basin closed by mountains on three sides, with the large Lake Biwa just to the east.","f":["When founded in 794, the city was named Heian-kyō, 'capital of peace'.","UNESCO's listing for ancient Kyoto covers 17 temples, shrines and a castle.","Its streets still follow the grid laid out more than 1,200 years ago."],"q":{"q":"What was Kyoto called when it was founded in 794?","a":"Heian-kyō","x":["Heijō-kyō","Edo"]}},"nara":{"name":"Nara","era":"710 – 784","t":"Japan's first permanent capital and its Great Buddha","s":"In 710 Japan's court settled in Nara, its first permanent capital, and for most of the 8th century it was the eastern end of the Silk Road. Buddhism flourished, and Emperor Shōmu cast a giant bronze Buddha and housed it in a vast wooden hall at Tōdai-ji. Tame deer still wander its parks. From above, look for a smaller city in a green basin south of Kyoto.","w":"Look for a city in a green basin about 40 km south of Kyoto, backed by wooded hills to the east.","f":["The Great Buddha of Tōdai-ji, cast in 752, is about 15 m tall.","Its hall is one of the largest wooden buildings in the world.","Nearby Hōryū-ji contains some of the oldest wooden buildings in the world."],"q":{"q":"Which animals roam freely in Nara's parks?","a":"Deer","x":["Monkeys","Cranes"]}},"gyeongju":{"name":"Gyeongju","era":"57 BC – AD 935","t":"The thousand-year capital of Silla","s":"For almost a thousand years Gyeongju was the capital of the Korean kingdom of Silla, which united most of the peninsula in the 7th century. Its royal tombs are grassy hills right in the city, and nearby stand the temple of Bulguksa and the stone Buddha of Seokguram. Koreans call it a museum without walls. From above, look for a city in a green basin near the east coast.","w":"Look for a city in a green basin among hills near Korea's south-east coast, north of Ulsan.","f":["Cheomseongdae, a 7th-century stone tower, is one of the oldest surviving observatories in East Asia.","Gold crowns found in its tombs are among Korea's national treasures.","Bulguksa and Seokguram became UNESCO World Heritage sites in 1995."],"q":{"q":"Which kingdom had its capital at Gyeongju?","a":"Silla","x":["Goguryeo","Joseon"]}},"xian-changan":{"name":"Chang'an and the Terracotta Army","era":"3rd c. BC – 10th c.","t":"The start of the Silk Road and an army of clay","s":"Today's Xi'an was Chang'an, capital of the Qin, Han and Tang dynasties and the eastern starting point of the Silk Road. Under the Tang it may have been the largest city in the world. Just east lies the tomb of China's first emperor, guarded by thousands of life-size clay soldiers. From above, look for the city's grid on the Wei river plain, with the Qinling mountains rising to the south.","w":"Look for a large grid-planned city on the flat Wei river plain, with the Qinling range rising steeply to the south.","f":["The Terracotta Army was found in 1974 by farmers digging a well.","Archaeologists estimate there are around 8,000 figures, their faces individually finished.","Xi'an's Ming city wall, about 14 km around, still stands complete."],"q":{"q":"Who found the Terracotta Army in 1974?","a":"Farmers digging a well","x":["Archaeologists on a survey","Railway builders"]}},"forbidden-city":{"name":"Forbidden City, Beijing","era":"1420 – 1912","t":"The palace of 24 emperors","s":"At the heart of Beijing lies the Forbidden City, the palace of the Ming and Qing emperors from 1420 to 1912. Walled and moated, it holds some 980 buildings under yellow-tiled roofs, and ordinary people could not enter without permission. It sits exactly on the axis that runs through the old city. In clear air its rectangle can be picked out in the middle of Beijing's grid.","w":"In clear weather, look for a large rectangle at the exact centre of Beijing, with a park and hill on its north side.","f":["Twenty-four emperors lived here over almost 500 years.","It is the largest surviving palace complex in the world.","Its yellow roof tiles were a colour reserved for the emperor."],"q":{"q":"How many emperors lived in the Forbidden City?","a":"24","x":["12","60"]}},"anyang-yinxu":{"name":"Yinxu, the Shang capital","era":"c. 1300 – 1046 BC","t":"Where the oldest Chinese writing was found","s":"Near today's Anyang on the North China Plain lay the last capital of the Shang dynasty. Its kings asked questions of their ancestors by cracking bones and turtle shells with heat, then carved the questions and answers on them. These oracle bones are the oldest mature Chinese writing, and many of their characters can still be recognised today. From above, look for the flat, intensely farmed plain north of the Yellow River.","w":"Look for the flat, densely farmed North China Plain, with the Taihang mountains rising to the west.","f":["Oracle bones were identified in 1899, when a scholar noticed writing on 'dragon bones' sold as medicine.","The intact tomb of Fu Hao, a Shang queen and military leader, was found here in 1976.","Yinxu became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2006."],"q":{"q":"On what are the oldest Chinese writings from Yinxu?","a":"Oracle bones","x":["Bamboo slips","Silk scrolls"]}},"dunhuang-mogao":{"name":"Mogao Caves, Dunhuang","era":"4th – 14th c.","t":"A thousand years of Buddhist art in the desert","s":"Where the Silk Road entered China from the western deserts lies the oasis of Dunhuang. In a cliff nearby, monks and patrons carved hundreds of cave shrines over a thousand years and covered them with murals and statues. A sealed library cave, opened in 1900, held tens of thousands of manuscripts. From above, look for the green oasis pressed against enormous golden sand dunes.","w":"Look for a small green oasis pressed against a range of huge golden sand dunes, south of the town of Dunhuang.","f":["About 490 caves still hold murals and sculptures.","The library cave held the Diamond Sutra of 868, the oldest dated printed book known.","The Mogao Caves became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1987."],"q":{"q":"Which dated printed book was found at Mogao?","a":"The Diamond Sutra","x":["The Analects","The Art of War"]}},"qufu":{"name":"Qufu, home of Confucius","era":"from 551 BC","t":"The hometown of Confucius and his descendants","s":"In this small city on the Shandong plain, Confucius was born in 551 BC. After his death his house became a temple, and over the centuries emperors enlarged it into one of China's grandest complexes. His descendants lived beside it in a family mansion, and are buried in a vast forest cemetery. From above, look for the flat plain south of Mount Tai, the sacred mountain about 70 km to the north.","w":"Look for the flat Shandong plain, with the sacred Mount Tai rising alone about 70 km to the north.","f":["The Kong family tree records more than 80 generations of descendants.","The Confucius family cemetery has been in use for over 2,000 years.","Qufu's temple, mansion and cemetery became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1994."],"q":{"q":"How many generations does the Kong family tree record?","a":"More than 80","x":["About 20","About 200"]}},"turpan-jiaohe":{"name":"Turpan oasis: Jiaohe and the karez","era":"2nd c. BC – today","t":"An oasis below sea level, watered from underground","s":"The Turpan basin is one of the lowest and hottest places in Asia, yet it is green with vineyards. The secret is the karez: tunnels dug to carry snowmelt from the Tian Shan underground, safe from evaporation. On a narrow island between two rivers stand the ruins of Jiaohe, a Silk Road city carved down into the earth. From above, look for the red Flaming Mountains and the oasis beside them.","w":"Look for a deep desert basin with a long red ridge, the Flaming Mountains, and green oasis fields along it.","f":["The lowest point of the basin lies about 150 m below sea level.","Turpan's karez tunnels together run for thousands of kilometres.","Much of Jiaohe was built by digging rooms out of the ground rather than building upward."],"q":{"q":"What is a karez?","a":"An underground water channel","x":["A mud-brick watchtower","A cave temple"]}},"loulan":{"name":"Loulan","era":"2nd c. BC – 4th c. AD","t":"A lost oasis kingdom at the edge of Lop Nur","s":"On the shores of Lop Nur, once a lake at the eastern end of the Tarim basin, stood Loulan, a small kingdom on the Silk Road. When the rivers shifted and the lake shrank, the city was left to the desert, and sand buried its houses and records. From above, look for the vast dry bed of Lop Nur, whose pale concentric rings look like a giant ear.","w":"Look for a huge pale lakebed with concentric rings like an ear, in the desert east of the Taklamakan.","f":["The Swedish explorer Sven Hedin found Loulan's ruins in 1900.","Documents found there are written on wood and paper in several languages.","The 'Ear of Lop Nur' rings are shorelines left as the lake dried."],"q":{"q":"Which explorer found the ruins of Loulan in 1900?","a":"Sven Hedin","x":["Nikolai Przhevalsky","Francis Younghusband"]}},"karakorum":{"name":"Karakorum","era":"1235 – 1260 (as capital)","t":"Capital of the Mongol Empire on the open steppe","s":"In the green Orkhon valley of central Mongolia stood Karakorum, capital of the Mongol Empire in the mid-13th century. Envoys, merchants and craftsmen came from Europe, Persia and China. A European friar who visited in 1254 described a silver tree in the khan's palace that poured out drinks. Little is left today, but the wide Orkhon valley and its rolling steppe are easy to see.","w":"Look for the broad green Orkhon river valley on open steppe, with forested Khangai mountains to the west.","f":["The silver drinking fountain was made by a goldsmith from Paris, Guillaume Boucher.","The Erdene Zuu monastery was built in 1585, partly from the city's ruins.","The Orkhon valley became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2004."],"q":{"q":"What fountain stood in Karakorum's palace?","a":"A silver tree pouring drinks","x":["A golden lion","A jade dragon"]}},"xanadu":{"name":"Xanadu (Shangdu)","era":"1256 – 1368","t":"Kublai Khan's summer capital on the grassland","s":"On the grasslands of Inner Mongolia, Kublai Khan built his summer capital, Shangdu, in 1256. Here the Mongol court hunted and held great feasts, and here Marco Polo described a palace of marble and a bamboo pavilion held by silk cords. Five centuries later a poem turned its name, Xanadu, into a byword for splendour. From above, look for open, rolling grassland dotted with lakes north of Beijing's mountains.","w":"Look for open, rolling grassland with small lakes and river meanders, about 270 km north of Beijing.","f":["Samuel Taylor Coleridge's poem 'Kubla Khan' begins: 'In Xanadu did Kubla Khan a stately pleasure-dome decree'.","The city's layout combined Chinese planning with open space for Mongol tents.","The site of Xanadu became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2012."],"q":{"q":"Which poet made Xanadu famous?","a":"Samuel Taylor Coleridge","x":["William Wordsworth","Lord Byron"]}},"taj-mahal-agra":{"name":"Taj Mahal, Agra","era":"1632 – 1653","t":"A white marble tomb built for love","s":"On a bend of the Yamuna at Agra stands the Taj Mahal, built by the Mughal emperor Shah Jahan as a tomb for his wife Mumtaz Mahal. Thousands of craftsmen worked for about 20 years in white marble inlaid with coloured stones. Its colour shifts with the light, from pink at dawn to gold at sunset. The tomb is small from cruise height, but the Yamuna's broad curve through Agra is easy to find.","w":"Look for the broad Yamuna curving past the city of Agra, about 180 km south-east of Delhi.","f":["The marble came from Makrana in Rajasthan, over 300 km away.","Shah Jahan himself was later buried beside Mumtaz Mahal inside the tomb.","The Taj Mahal became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1983."],"q":{"q":"For whom did Shah Jahan build the Taj Mahal?","a":"Mumtaz Mahal","x":["Nur Jahan","Jodha Bai"]}},"hampi":{"name":"Hampi (Vijayanagara)","era":"14th – 16th c.","t":"An imperial city among giant boulders","s":"Among piles of giant granite boulders on the Tungabhadra river lie the ruins of Vijayanagara, capital of a great south Indian empire. Around 1500 it was one of the largest and richest cities in the world, visited by Persian and Portuguese travellers who marvelled at its bazaars. After 1565 it was abandoned. From above, look for a strange rocky landscape of boulder hills along the river, near a large reservoir.","w":"Look for rocky boulder-strewn hills along the Tungabhadra river, just downstream of a large reservoir.","f":["The stone pillars of the Vittala temple ring with musical notes when tapped.","A stone chariot in the Vittala temple courtyard appears on India's 50-rupee note.","Hampi became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1986."],"q":{"q":"What rings when tapped at Hampi's Vittala temple?","a":"Its stone pillars","x":["Its copper roof","Its water tanks"]}},"mohenjo-daro":{"name":"Mohenjo-daro","era":"c. 2500 – 1900 BC","t":"A planned city with drains, 4,500 years ago","s":"On the Indus plain in Sindh stood Mohenjo-daro, one of the largest cities of the Indus civilisation, built about 4,500 years ago. Its streets were laid out in a grid, its houses had bathrooms, and covered brick drains ran beneath the lanes, a level of planning few ancient cities matched. Its writing has still not been deciphered. From above, look for the green ribbon of the Indus winding through dry land.","w":"Look for the Indus river's broad green valley winding south through the dry plains of Sindh.","f":["Its 'Great Bath', a watertight brick pool, may be the earliest public water tank known.","The city may have been home to around 40,000 people.","Mohenjo-daro became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1980."],"q":{"q":"Which famous structure was found at Mohenjo-daro?","a":"The Great Bath","x":["The Great Library","The Great Stadium"]}},"bodh-gaya":{"name":"Bodh Gaya","era":"5th c. BC – today","t":"Where the Buddha found enlightenment","s":"On the plains of Bihar, beside a slow river, Buddhist tradition holds that Siddhartha Gautama sat beneath a fig tree and became the Buddha. A tree descended from it still grows beside the tall Mahabodhi Temple, and pilgrims come from all over Asia. It is the holiest place in the Buddhist world. From above, look for the flat green Gangetic plain south of Patna, with low rocky hills near the town of Gaya.","w":"Look for the flat green plain south of Patna, with scattered low rocky hills around the town of Gaya.","f":["The Mahabodhi Temple, rebuilt many times, is one of the oldest brick temples in India.","The present tree grew from a cutting linked, by tradition, to the original.","The temple complex became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2002."],"q":{"q":"Under what kind of tree did the Buddha find enlightenment?","a":"A bodhi (sacred fig) tree","x":["A banyan tree","A mango tree"]}},"nalanda":{"name":"Nalanda","era":"5th – 12th c.","t":"One of the world's first great universities","s":"For some 700 years, Nalanda was one of the great centres of learning in Asia. Thousands of monks and students lived in its brick monasteries, studying philosophy, medicine, logic and grammar, and its libraries were famous. Students came from China, Korea, Tibet and South-East Asia. The ruins lie in the green plain of Bihar, south-east of Patna, among fields and villages.","w":"Look for the flat green plain of Bihar, south-east of Patna, with the low Rajgir hills just to the south.","f":["The Chinese monk Xuanzang studied at Nalanda in the 7th century.","It is often called one of the world's earliest residential universities.","Its ruins became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2016."],"q":{"q":"Which Chinese monk studied at Nalanda?","a":"Xuanzang","x":["Jianzhen","Zheng He"]}},"ajanta-ellora":{"name":"Ajanta and Ellora caves","era":"2nd c. BC – 10th c. AD","t":"Temples carved straight out of the Deccan rock","s":"In the dark volcanic rock of the Deccan plateau, monks and craftsmen cut temples and monasteries straight into cliffs. At Ajanta, Buddhist caves hold paintings up to 2,000 years old. At Ellora, Buddhist, Hindu and Jain shrines stand side by side, including a whole temple carved from a single rock. From above, look for the flat-topped hills and dark soil of the Deccan north of Aurangabad.","w":"Look for flat-topped basalt hills and dark farmland north of the city of Aurangabad.","f":["Ellora's Kailasa temple was carved from the top down out of one piece of rock.","The Ajanta caves were brought to wide attention in 1819 by a British officer out hunting.","Both sites became UNESCO World Heritage sites in 1983."],"q":{"q":"What is special about Ellora's Kailasa temple?","a":"It is carved from one rock","x":["It is built of bricks","It is built of marble"]}},"sigiriya":{"name":"Sigiriya","era":"5th c. AD","t":"A palace on top of a giant rock","s":"From the jungle of central Sri Lanka rises a lone rock about 200 m high. In the 5th century King Kashyapa built a palace on its summit, reached through the paws of a colossal brick lion. Its walls were painted with graceful women, and gardens with pools spread around its base. From above, look for a single dark rock standing out of the green forest, north of Dambulla.","w":"Look for a single steep rock rising from flat green forest in the centre of Sri Lanka, near reservoirs.","f":["Only the giant paws of the Lion Gate remain.","Visitors scratched poems on the polished Mirror Wall from about the 8th century.","Sigiriya became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1982."],"q":{"q":"What remains of Sigiriya's Lion Gate?","a":"Two giant paws","x":["A lion's head","A stone mane"]}},"lumbini":{"name":"Lumbini","era":"c. 5th c. BC","t":"The birthplace of the Buddha","s":"On the flat Terai plain at the foot of the Himalaya lies Lumbini, where Buddhist tradition says Siddhartha Gautama, the future Buddha, was born. More than two centuries later Emperor Ashoka visited and set up a stone pillar to mark the spot. Today monasteries from many countries surround the sacred garden. From above, look for the flat green plain with the white wall of the Himalaya on the northern horizon.","w":"Look for the flat green Terai plain in southern Nepal, with the snowy Himalaya on the northern horizon.","f":["Ashoka's pillar, from 249 BC, bears an inscription saying the Buddha was born here.","The pillar was rediscovered in 1896.","Lumbini became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1997."],"q":{"q":"Who set up the inscribed pillar at Lumbini?","a":"Emperor Ashoka","x":["Emperor Akbar","King Kanishka"]}},"kathmandu-valley":{"name":"Kathmandu Valley","era":"from the 5th c.","t":"Three royal cities in a Himalayan bowl","s":"In a green bowl among the foothills of the Himalaya lie three old royal cities, Kathmandu, Patan and Bhaktapur. Their Newar craftsmen filled them with pagoda temples, carved wooden windows and palace squares, and the valley also holds two of the great stupas of Buddhism. From above, look for a round valley filled with city, ringed by green hills, with snow peaks behind.","w":"Look for a round valley filled with city and ringed by green hills, with the snowy Himalaya to the north.","f":["Legend says the valley was once a lake, drained when the bodhisattva Manjushri cut a gorge.","The white dome of Boudhanath is one of the largest stupas in the world.","Seven groups of monuments in the valley are on the UNESCO World Heritage list."],"q":{"q":"According to legend, what was the valley once?","a":"A lake","x":["A glacier","A desert"]}},"taxila":{"name":"Taxila","era":"6th c. BC – 5th c. AD","t":"Where Greek and Buddhist worlds met in stone","s":"Near today's Islamabad lay Taxila, a crossroads city of the ancient Gandhara region, on routes linking India, Central Asia and Persia. Alexander the Great passed through, and later Buddhist monasteries and a famous seat of learning flourished here. Its sculptors mixed Greek and Indian styles, giving the Buddha one of his earliest human portraits. From above, look for the green hills just north-west of Islamabad.","w":"Look for the green Margalla Hills and the valley just north-west of Islamabad and Rawalpindi.","f":["Alexander the Great arrived at Taxila in 326 BC.","Remains of three successive cities have been excavated here.","Taxila became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1980."],"q":{"q":"Which art style mixed Greek and Buddhist forms here?","a":"Gandhara","x":["Mughal","Chola"]}},"angkor":{"name":"Angkor","era":"9th – 15th c.","t":"Temple-mountains and giant reservoirs of the Khmer","s":"North of the great lake Tonlé Sap lie the ruins of Angkor, capital of the Khmer Empire. Its kings built temple-mountains of stone, and Angkor Wat, from the 12th century, is the largest religious monument in the world. They also dug huge rectangular reservoirs to store the monsoon rains. Those shapes are visible from cruise: look for long straight-edged lakes and moats in the forest.","w":"Look for a long rectangular reservoir and a square moat in the forest, just north of Siem Reap and the Tonlé Sap lake.","f":["The West Baray reservoir is about 8 km long and 2 km wide, and still holds water.","The moat around Angkor Wat is about 190 m wide.","The towers of the Bayon temple carry more than 200 giant stone faces."],"q":{"q":"Which lake lies just south of Angkor?","a":"Tonlé Sap","x":["Inle Lake","Lake Toba"]}},"bagan":{"name":"Bagan","era":"11th – 13th c.","t":"A plain of more than two thousand temples","s":"On a dry plain beside the Irrawaddy, the kings of Bagan and their subjects built more than 10,000 temples and pagodas between the 11th and 13th centuries. Over 2,000 still stand, brick and white against the ochre earth, turning gold at sunrise. The temples are too small to see from cruise, but look for the wide bend of the Irrawaddy on a flat, dry plain.","w":"Look for a wide bend of the Irrawaddy on a flat, dry ochre plain in central Myanmar.","f":["Bagan was the capital of the first kingdom to unite most of Myanmar.","The Ananda temple, from about 1105, is one of its best preserved.","Bagan became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2019."],"q":{"q":"Roughly how many temples still stand at Bagan?","a":"Over 2,000","x":["About 200","About 20,000"]}},"borobudur":{"name":"Borobudur","era":"9th c.","t":"A Buddhist mountain of stone between volcanoes","s":"On the plain of central Java, ringed by volcanoes, stands Borobudur, the largest Buddhist temple in the world. Built in the 9th century, it rises in terraces like a stepped mountain, and pilgrims walking round it pass thousands of carved panels telling stories of the Buddha. It lay half-buried for centuries. From above, look for the green Kedu plain framed by the volcanoes Merapi, Merbabu and Sumbing.","w":"Look for a green plain in central Java framed by big volcanic cones, including the often-smoking Merapi.","f":["It has 504 Buddha statues and more than 2,600 relief panels.","In 1814 the British governor Stamford Raffles had the overgrown site surveyed.","Borobudur became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1991."],"q":{"q":"Who had Borobudur surveyed in 1814?","a":"Stamford Raffles","x":["James Cook","Warren Hastings"]}},"ayutthaya":{"name":"Ayutthaya","era":"1351 – 1767","t":"The river-island capital of old Siam","s":"For four centuries Ayutthaya was the capital of Siam and one of the great trading cities of Asia, visited by merchants from Europe, Persia, China and Japan. The city stands on an island where three rivers meet, and its brick temples and prang towers still rise among the modern streets. From above, look for a river-ringed island in the flat rice plain north of Bangkok.","w":"Look for an island ringed by rivers in the flat, watery rice plain about 70 km north of Bangkok.","f":["Around 1700 it may have been one of the largest cities in the world.","Foreign traders, including Dutch, Portuguese and Japanese, had their own quarters outside the walls.","Ayutthaya became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1991."],"q":{"q":"What makes old Ayutthaya an island?","a":"Three rivers meeting","x":["A crater lake","A sea channel"]}},"hoi-an":{"name":"Hội An","era":"15th – 19th c.","t":"A lantern-lit trading port frozen in time","s":"Near the mouth of the Thu Bồn river on Vietnam's central coast, Hội An was a busy international port where Japanese, Chinese, Portuguese and Dutch merchants traded silk, porcelain and spices. When the river silted up, trade moved to Da Nang, and the old town was left almost untouched, with wooden shophouses, assembly halls and a covered bridge. From above, look for the river mouth and the Cham Islands offshore.","w":"Look for the Thu Bồn river reaching the sea about 25 km south of Da Nang, with the small Cham Islands offshore.","f":["Its covered bridge was built by Japanese merchants in the late 16th century.","Chinese merchant communities built assembly halls that are still used today.","Hội An's old town became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1999."],"q":{"q":"Who built Hội An's covered bridge?","a":"Japanese merchants","x":["Portuguese sailors","Dutch traders"]}},"zhongyuan":{"name":"Central Plain, cradle of Chinese civilisation","era":"c. 2000 BC – 12th c.","t":"Where Chinese civilisation took shape on the Yellow River","s":"Along the middle Yellow River lies the Central Plain, where many early Chinese states and dynasties rose. Luoyang and Kaifeng were capitals for centuries, the Shaolin Temple stands on Mount Song, and Bronze Age palaces have been found at Erlitou. From above, look for the broad, silt-brown Yellow River braiding across a vast flat plain, held between high levees.","w":"Look for the wide, muddy Yellow River braiding across a flat plain, with the Song mountains to its south-west.","f":["Near Kaifeng the Yellow River's bed lies higher than the surrounding land, held in by levees.","Luoyang's Longmen Grottoes hold tens of thousands of Buddhist statues carved into river cliffs.","Kaifeng, the Northern Song capital, was one of the largest cities in the world around 1100."],"q":{"q":"Which temple on Mount Song is famous for kung fu?","a":"Shaolin","x":["Wudang","Longmen"]}},"ifugao-terraces":{"name":"Rice Terraces of the Philippine Cordilleras","era":"centuries old – today","t":"Mountains sculpted by hand into rice fields","s":"In the mountains of northern Luzon, the Ifugao people have carved whole hillsides into steps of rice paddies, fed by water from the forests above. The terraces follow the contours of the slopes like giant staircases, built and maintained by hand over generations. They are too fine to see from cruise, but the steep green Cordillera where they lie is easy to find north of Manila.","w":"Look for the steep, green Cordillera range in northern Luzon, about 250 km north of Manila.","f":["In 1995 they were among the first sites listed by UNESCO as a living cultural landscape.","The forests above the terraces are kept as shared watersheds to supply the fields.","The Ifugao's Hudhud chants are on UNESCO's list of intangible heritage."],"q":{"q":"Which people built the Banaue rice terraces?","a":"The Ifugao","x":["The Tagalog","The Visayans"]}},"trans-saharan-west":{"name":"Trans-Saharan caravan route (Sijilmasa–Timbuktu)","era":"8th – 16th c.","t":"Salt south, gold north, across the great desert","s":"For centuries camel caravans crossed the western Sahara between Morocco and the Niger. Salt, cloth and horses went south; gold, ivory and leather came north, and the gold of West Africa ended up in coins across the Mediterranean. Books and scholars travelled with them. From above you see the scale of the crossing: weeks of sand seas, gravel plains and bare plateaus with barely a trace of green.","w":"Look for endless dunes and gravel plains, dark rocky plateaus, and rare oases strung along dry valleys.","f":["The crossing could take around two months, with long stretches between wells.","Mansa Musa of Mali spent so much gold on his pilgrimage in 1324 that its value fell in Cairo.","Salt caravans still carry slabs from the Taoudenni mines to Timbuktu."],"q":{"q":"What did caravans carry south to trade for gold?","a":"Salt","x":["Ivory","Pepper"]}},"trans-saharan-bornu":{"name":"Trans-Saharan route: Tripoli–Lake Chad","era":"1st millennium BC – 19th c.","t":"The oldest road across the Sahara, via the Fezzan","s":"From Tripoli, one of the oldest routes across the Sahara ran south through the oases of the Fezzan to Lake Chad and the kingdom of Kanem-Bornu. The Garamantes, who farmed the Fezzan with underground channels, controlled it in Roman times. Later, salt from Bilma and goods from the Mediterranean moved along it. From above, look for dark rocky plateaus, sand seas and, in the far south, the shallow waters of Lake Chad.","w":"Look for dark plateaus and sand seas in southern Libya, and far south the shallow, marshy basin of Lake Chad.","f":["The Garamantes dug long networks of underground water channels, called foggaras.","Kanem-Bornu, at Lake Chad, lasted about a thousand years.","In 1823 British travellers on this route became the first Europeans known to see Lake Chad."],"q":{"q":"Which ancient people farmed the Fezzan with underground canals?","a":"The Garamantes","x":["The Carthaginians","The Nubians"]}},"vasco-da-gama-route":{"name":"Vasco da Gama's sea route to India","era":"1497 – 1498","t":"The voyage that joined Europe to India by sea","s":"In July 1497 four ships left Lisbon under Vasco da Gama. Instead of hugging the African coast, he swung far out into the South Atlantic, then rounded the Cape of Good Hope, sailed up the east African coast and, with a local pilot from Malindi, crossed the Indian Ocean. In May 1498 he reached Calicut in India. The sea route he opened changed world trade for centuries.","w":"Look for the Cape Peninsula at Africa's south-western tip, and the long east African coast running north from it.","f":["His loop through the South Atlantic kept the fleet out of sight of land for about three months.","He named Natal, in South Africa, after passing it on Christmas Day 1497.","Bartolomeu Dias had first rounded the Cape of Good Hope ten years earlier, in 1488."],"q":{"q":"Which region did da Gama name on Christmas Day 1497?","a":"Natal","x":["Transvaal","Zululand"]}},"carthage":{"name":"Carthage","era":"c. 814 BC – AD 698","t":"The Phoenician city that rivalled Rome","s":"On a headland of the Gulf of Tunis, Phoenician settlers from Tyre founded Carthage, which grew into the great trading power of the western Mediterranean. After Rome destroyed it in 146 BC, the Romans rebuilt it as one of the largest cities of their empire. Its round military harbour can still be traced. From cruise, look for the lagoon-edged Gulf of Tunis and the city of Tunis spread around it.","w":"Look for the Gulf of Tunis and its shallow lagoon, with the capital's suburbs covering the headland to the north-east.","f":["Legend says Queen Dido founded the city on land she marked out with strips of an oxhide.","The circular Punic harbour once had sheds for more than 200 warships around its edge.","Carthage became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1979."],"q":{"q":"Which legendary queen founded Carthage?","a":"Dido","x":["Zenobia","Cleopatra"]}},"leptis-magna":{"name":"Leptis Magna","era":"7th c. BC – 7th c. AD","t":"A Roman city kept by the sand","s":"On the Libyan coast east of Tripoli lies Leptis Magna, one of the most complete Roman cities in the Mediterranean. It began as a Phoenician port, grew rich on olive oil, and was lavishly rebuilt by Emperor Septimius Severus, who was born here. Later the sand covered it, keeping its forum, basilica, theatre and harbour. From above, look for the coast near Khoms and the dry hills behind it.","w":"Look for the Libyan coastline about 120 km east of Tripoli, near the town of Khoms.","f":["Emperor Septimius Severus was born in Leptis Magna in AD 145.","Its theatre faces the sea, and its stage wall still stands tall.","Leptis Magna became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1982."],"q":{"q":"Which Roman emperor was born in Leptis Magna?","a":"Septimius Severus","x":["Hadrian","Trajan"]}},"el-jem":{"name":"El Jem amphitheatre","era":"c. AD 238","t":"A colossal Roman arena in an olive-grove town","s":"In the middle of the Tunisian plain, a Roman amphitheatre towers over the small town of El Jem. It was built around AD 238 in Thysdrus, a town made rich by olive oil, and could seat some 35,000 people. It is among the best-preserved Roman amphitheatres in the world. The arena is small from cruise height, but the vast plain of olive groves in neat rows is striking.","w":"Look for a flat plain covered in regular dotted rows of olive trees, inland from the Tunisian coast between Sousse and Sfax.","f":["It is one of the largest amphitheatres the Romans ever built.","It was built of stone blocks brought from quarries some 30 km away.","El Jem became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1979."],"q":{"q":"Which product made Roman Thysdrus rich?","a":"Olive oil","x":["Wine","Dates"]}},"volubilis":{"name":"Volubilis","era":"3rd c. BC – 11th c. AD","t":"A Roman town at the edge of the Empire in Morocco","s":"On a fertile plain below the Zerhoun hills lie the ruins of Volubilis, a town at the far south-western edge of the Roman Empire. Its olive presses, triumphal arch and houses with fine floor mosaics still stand among the fields. Centuries later, the founder of Morocco's first Muslim dynasty came here in 788. From above, look for green farmland and hills north of Meknes.","w":"Look for green farmland and the rounded Zerhoun hills, about 25 km north of Meknes.","f":["Dozens of olive presses have been found in the town.","Idris I, founder of the Idrisid dynasty, was received here in 788.","Volubilis became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1997."],"q":{"q":"Who founded a dynasty after arriving at Volubilis in 788?","a":"Idris I","x":["Yusuf ibn Tashfin","Tariq ibn Ziyad"]}},"ait-benhaddou":{"name":"Aït Benhaddou","era":"17th c. (on older foundations)","t":"An earthen fortress village on the caravan road","s":"On a hill above a river south of the High Atlas stands Aït Benhaddou, a fortified village of red earth houses and towers. It guarded the caravan route that brought goods from the Sahara over the mountains to Marrakesh. Its silhouette has starred in many films. From above, look for the snowy High Atlas and the green river valleys on its southern side near Ouarzazate.","w":"Look south of the snowy High Atlas for a green river valley and a large reservoir near Ouarzazate.","f":["Its buildings are made of rammed earth and mud brick, repaired after heavy rains.","It has appeared in films including 'Lawrence of Arabia' and 'Gladiator'.","Aït Benhaddou became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1987."],"q":{"q":"What are Aït Benhaddou's buildings made of?","a":"Earth and mud brick","x":["Cut limestone","Timber"]}},"timbuktu":{"name":"Timbuktu","era":"12th – 16th c. (golden age)","t":"A desert city of scholars and manuscripts","s":"Where the Sahara meets the great bend of the Niger stands Timbuktu, a meeting point of camel caravans and river boats. In the 15th and 16th centuries it was a centre of Islamic learning, with mud-brick mosques and scholars whose libraries grew over generations. Europe imagined it as a city of gold. From above, look for the edge where the Niger's green floodplain meets the desert sand.","w":"Look for the northern edge of the Niger's bend, where green floodplain and channels give way to desert.","f":["Its Djinguereber Mosque dates back to the 14th century and is built of earth.","Families in Timbuktu have kept hundreds of thousands of manuscripts.","In 1828 René Caillié became the first European to reach Timbuktu and return."],"q":{"q":"Who was the first European to reach Timbuktu and return?","a":"René Caillié","x":["Mungo Park","David Livingstone"]}},"djenne":{"name":"Djenné","era":"3rd c. BC – today","t":"The largest mud-brick building in the world","s":"On an island in the floodplain of the Bani river, in Mali's inland Niger delta, stands Djenné, one of the oldest towns in West Africa. Its Great Mosque is the largest mud-brick building in the world, bristling with wooden beams. Every year the whole town replasters it with fresh mud in a festival. From above, look for a watery maze of channels and floodplain, green or blue in the wet season.","w":"Look for the inland Niger delta, a vast floodplain of channels and lakes, especially after the summer rains.","f":["The present Great Mosque was built in 1907 on the site of earlier mosques.","Nearby Djenné-Djeno was a town from around 250 BC, one of the oldest known south of the Sahara.","Old Djenné became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1988."],"q":{"q":"What is Djenné's Great Mosque built of?","a":"Mud brick","x":["Stone","Timber"]}},"great-zimbabwe":{"name":"Great Zimbabwe","era":"11th – 15th c.","t":"The stone city that gave a country its name","s":"In the granite hills of southern Zimbabwe stand the ruins of Great Zimbabwe, capital of a medieval Shona kingdom. Its builders fitted granite blocks into curving walls up to 11 m high without any mortar. Its rulers grew rich trading gold and ivory with the Swahili coast; Chinese porcelain and Persian glass have been found here. From above, look for granite hills and the reservoir of Lake Mutirikwi.","w":"Look for rounded granite hills and the large Lake Mutirikwi reservoir near the town of Masvingo.","f":["'Zimbabwe' comes from Shona words usually translated as 'houses of stone'.","Carved soapstone birds from the site appear on Zimbabwe's flag.","Great Zimbabwe became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1986."],"q":{"q":"What does the name 'Zimbabwe' mean?","a":"Houses of stone","x":["Land of gold","Great river"]}},"mapungubwe":{"name":"Mapungubwe","era":"c. 1075 – 1220","t":"The hilltop kingdom of the golden rhino","s":"Where the Limpopo and Shashe rivers meet, at today's meeting point of South Africa, Botswana and Zimbabwe, stood Mapungubwe, the capital of one of southern Africa's first kingdoms. Its rulers lived on a flat-topped sandstone hill above their people and traded gold and ivory towards the Indian Ocean. From above, look for the confluence of two broad, often sandy rivers in dry bush country.","w":"Look for the confluence of the Limpopo and Shashe, two wide sandy rivers, where three countries meet.","f":["A small rhinoceros of gold foil over a wooden core was found in a royal grave.","Mapungubwe is seen as a forerunner of Great Zimbabwe.","It became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2003."],"q":{"q":"Which golden animal was found at Mapungubwe?","a":"A rhinoceros","x":["A lion","An elephant"]}},"aksum":{"name":"Aksum","era":"1st – 7th c. AD","t":"Giant stone stelae of an African empire","s":"In the highlands of northern Ethiopia stood Aksum, capital of a trading empire that dealt with Rome, Persia and India and minted its own gold coins. Its kings raised towering carved stone stelae over royal tombs, and in the 4th century King Ezana adopted Christianity. Ethiopian tradition holds that the Ark of the Covenant rests in a chapel here. From above, look for rugged highland plateaus cut by deep valleys.","w":"Look for high, rugged plateaus cut by deep valleys in the far north of Ethiopia.","f":["The tallest stela still standing is about 24 m high.","A fallen stela about 33 m long may be the largest single stone ancient people tried to raise.","Aksum became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1980."],"q":{"q":"Which king of Aksum adopted Christianity?","a":"Ezana","x":["Lalibela","Menelik"]}},"lalibela":{"name":"Lalibela","era":"late 12th – early 13th c.","t":"Churches carved down into solid rock","s":"High in the mountains of Ethiopia, eleven churches were carved not upward but downward, out of solid volcanic rock, around 800 years ago. King Lalibela is said to have wanted a new Jerusalem, and pilgrims still fill the churches at Christmas. The most famous, St George, is shaped like a cross when seen from above. From cruise, look for rugged highland ridges and deep valleys.","w":"Look for high, rugged mountains and deep gorges in the Ethiopian highlands, east of Lake Tana.","f":["Most of the churches were cut free from the surrounding rock, windows and doors included.","Lalibela was among the first 12 sites on UNESCO's World Heritage list in 1978.","The churches are still used for worship every day."],"q":{"q":"What shape is Lalibela's Church of St George?","a":"A cross","x":["A circle","A star"]}},"meroe":{"name":"Meroë","era":"c. 590 BC – AD 350","t":"The steep pyramids of the Kush kingdom","s":"On the east bank of the Nile in Sudan, clusters of steep, narrow pyramids rise from the sand. They belong to Meroë, capital of the kingdom of Kush, which once ruled Egypt itself. Its rulers, including powerful queens, were buried under more than 200 pyramids. Meroë also smelted iron on a large scale. From above, look for the Nile's green ribbon, north of Khartoum, in wide desert.","w":"Look for the thin green line of the Nile winding through desert, about 200 km north-east of Khartoum.","f":["Sudan has more pyramids than Egypt, though they are smaller and steeper.","Kush's ruling queens held the title Kandake, the origin of the name Candace.","Meroitic writing can be read aloud, but the language is still barely understood."],"q":{"q":"What title did the ruling queens of Kush hold?","a":"Kandake","x":["Sultana","Begum"]}},"kilwa":{"name":"Kilwa Kisiwani","era":"9th – 16th c.","t":"The richest of the Swahili city-states","s":"On a small island off the coast of Tanzania stand the coral-stone ruins of Kilwa, a Swahili city-state that grew rich on the gold trade of the Indian Ocean. Its sultans built a great domed mosque and a vast palace overlooking the sea. The traveller Ibn Battuta visited in 1331 and praised it as one of the most beautiful towns in the world. From above, look for islands, mangroves and turquoise shallows.","w":"Look for small islands, mangrove channels and turquoise shallows on the coast about 300 km south of Dar es Salaam.","f":["The Great Mosque of Kilwa dates back to the 11th century.","Kilwa's coins have been found as far away as northern Australia.","Kilwa became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1981."],"q":{"q":"Which traveller praised Kilwa in 1331?","a":"Ibn Battuta","x":["Marco Polo","Zheng He"]}},"olduvai":{"name":"Olduvai Gorge","era":"c. 2 million years ago – today","t":"A gorge that opened a window on human origins","s":"Between the Ngorongoro highlands and the Serengeti plains runs Olduvai Gorge, where layers of ancient lakeside sediment hold nearly two million years of human prehistory. Here Mary and Louis Leakey found early human fossils and some of the oldest stone tools known. From above, look for the vast round Ngorongoro crater and the pale Serengeti plains to its west; the gorge lies between them.","w":"Look for the huge round Ngorongoro crater; the gorge cuts the plains just north-west of it towards the Serengeti.","f":["Mary Leakey found the skull known as 'Nutcracker Man' here in 1959.","Its simple stone tools gave their name to the Oldowan tool-making tradition.","Nearby at Laetoli, footprints about 3.6 million years old were found in volcanic ash."],"q":{"q":"Who found the 'Nutcracker Man' skull in 1959?","a":"Mary Leakey","x":["Jane Goodall","Dian Fossey"]}},"cradle-of-humankind":{"name":"Cradle of Humankind","era":"c. 3.5 million years ago – today","t":"Limestone caves full of our ancestors","s":"In the rolling hills north-west of Johannesburg, a cluster of limestone caves has yielded a large share of the world's early human fossils. Famous finds include 'Mrs Ples', a skull more than two million years old, the nearly complete skeleton called 'Little Foot', and a species new to science, Homo naledi. From above, look for the Johannesburg sprawl and the Hartbeespoort reservoir among the ridges to its north-west.","w":"Look north-west of Johannesburg for grassy ridges and the Hartbeespoort Dam reservoir.","f":["'Mrs Ples' was found at Sterkfontein Caves in 1947.","Homo naledi was announced in 2015 from fossils found in the Rising Star cave system.","The Cradle of Humankind became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1999."],"q":{"q":"What was the skull found at Sterkfontein in 1947 called?","a":"Mrs Ples","x":["Lucy","Toumaï"]}},"hadar-lucy":{"name":"Hadar, where Lucy was found","era":"3.2 million years ago; found 1974","t":"The badlands where 'Lucy' came to light","s":"In the hot Afar region of Ethiopia, along the Awash river, eroding badlands expose layers millions of years old. In 1974 a team found here a partial skeleton of an early human relative who lived about 3.2 million years ago. They called her Lucy, and she showed that our ancestors walked upright long before their brains grew large. From above, look for the green Awash valley in dry, volcanic land.","w":"Look for the green thread of the Awash river winding through dry, dark volcanic land in north-eastern Ethiopia.","f":["About 40 percent of Lucy's skeleton was recovered.","In Ethiopia she is also known as Dinkinesh, meaning 'you are marvellous'.","She belongs to the species Australopithecus afarensis, named after the Afar region."],"q":{"q":"Which song gave Lucy her name?","a":"Lucy in the Sky with Diamonds","x":["Hey Jude","Yellow Submarine"]}},"swahili-coast":{"name":"Swahili coast","era":"9th c. – today","t":"Coral-stone towns of the monsoon trade","s":"Along the coast of East Africa grew a chain of trading towns, from Lamu and Mombasa to Zanzibar and Kilwa. Their merchants used the monsoon winds to trade with Arabia, Persia and India, and built in coral stone with carved wooden doors. The Swahili language and culture grew from African roots with many words borrowed from Arabic. From above, look for reefs, lagoons and white beaches along a green coast.","w":"Look for coral reefs, turquoise lagoons and islands along a green coast between southern Somalia and Tanzania.","f":["'Swahili' comes from an Arabic word meaning 'coasts'.","Lamu Old Town is the oldest and best-preserved Swahili town, a UNESCO site since 2001.","Dhows, wooden sailing ships, still use the monsoon winds along this coast."],"q":{"q":"What does the Arabic root of 'Swahili' mean?","a":"Coasts","x":["Islands","Traders"]}},"tassili-rock-art":{"name":"Tassili n'Ajjer rock art","era":"c. 6000 BC – 1st c. AD","t":"Paintings from the time when the Sahara was green","s":"On a sandstone plateau in the Algerian Sahara, carved into a maze of rock pillars, are thousands of paintings and engravings. They show giraffes, hippos and elephants, and herders with cattle, in a land that is now desert. The art records how the Sahara changed from green savanna to sand over thousands of years. From above, look for a dark, eroded plateau above the pale dunes near Djanet.","w":"Look for a dark, deeply eroded sandstone plateau rising above pale sand seas in south-eastern Algeria.","f":["More than 15,000 paintings and engravings have been recorded here.","The plateau's rock pillars are often called a 'forest of stone'.","Tassili n'Ajjer became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1982."],"q":{"q":"What do the Tassili paintings reveal about the Sahara?","a":"It was once green","x":["It was once colder","It was always desert"]}},"inca-road":{"name":"Inca road (Qhapaq Ñan)","era":"15th – 16th c.","t":"An empire tied together by mountain roads","s":"The Incas built a road network of some 30,000 km along the Andes, from today's Colombia to Chile and Argentina. Its main spine ran through the mountains from Quito to Cusco and on south, with stairways cut into cliffs, rope bridges over gorges and way stations every day's walk. Relay runners carried messages along it. From above, look for high plateaus, snowy volcanoes and Lake Titicaca.","w":"Look for the high, bare Andean plateau, snowy volcano cones and the big blue Lake Titicaca.","f":["The Incas had no wheeled vehicles; the roads served walkers and llama caravans.","The Q'eswachaka rope bridge is still rebuilt by hand every year.","The road system became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2014, shared by six countries."],"q":{"q":"Which Inca rope bridge is still rebuilt every year?","a":"Q'eswachaka","x":["Ollantaytambo","Sacsayhuamán"]}},"camino-real-tierra-adentro":{"name":"Camino Real de Tierra Adentro","era":"1598 – 1880s","t":"The royal silver road from Mexico City to Santa Fe","s":"For almost three centuries this was the main road north from Mexico City, running some 2,500 km through the silver-mining towns of Zacatecas and Durango, across the Chihuahuan desert and up the Rio Grande to Santa Fe. Wagons, mule trains and missionaries moved along it. From above, look for the high desert basins of northern Mexico and the green ribbon of the Rio Grande.","w":"Look for the high, dry basins of northern Mexico, and further north the green line of the Rio Grande.","f":["The Mexican part of the road became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2010.","Santa Fe, at its northern end, is the oldest state capital in the United States.","Silver from the mines along it flowed south to Mexico City and on to Spain."],"q":{"q":"Which metal drove traffic on the Camino Real?","a":"Silver","x":["Copper","Tin"]}},"oregon-trail":{"name":"Oregon Trail","era":"1840s – 1860s","t":"Two thousand miles by covered wagon","s":"In the mid-19th century hundreds of thousands of settlers crossed the continent by covered wagon, on trails to Oregon, California and Utah. The Oregon Trail ran about 3,500 km from Missouri along the Platte River, over the Rockies at South Pass and down the Snake and Columbia rivers. The journey took four to six months. From above, look for the braided Platte River and the high plains of Wyoming.","w":"Look for the wide, braided Platte River across Nebraska, then the high, dry basins of Wyoming.","f":["Wagon ruts cut into rock are still visible near Guernsey, Wyoming.","Travellers aimed to reach Independence Rock by 4 July, hence its name.","South Pass let wagons cross the Continental Divide on a gentle slope."],"q":{"q":"How did Independence Rock get its name?","a":"Travellers aimed to reach it by 4 July","x":["It marked the US border","A treaty was signed there"]}},"route-66":{"name":"Route 66","era":"1926 – 1985","t":"America's Mother Road, Chicago to the Pacific","s":"Opened in 1926, Route 66 ran about 3,900 km from Chicago to Santa Monica, across prairie, the Texas Panhandle and the south-western deserts. It carried migrants west in the 1930s and holidaymakers in the 1950s, and its motels, diners and neon signs became American icons. Much of it is now replaced by interstates, but from above you can follow its line along the railway across the desert.","w":"Across Arizona and New Mexico, look for a highway and railway running side by side through red desert and mesas.","f":["John Steinbeck called it 'the Mother Road' in 'The Grapes of Wrath'.","The 1946 song '(Get Your Kicks on) Route 66' made it famous around the world.","It was officially removed from the US highway system in 1985."],"q":{"q":"Which writer called Route 66 'the Mother Road'?","a":"John Steinbeck","x":["Jack Kerouac","Mark Twain"]}},"pony-express":{"name":"Pony Express","era":"1860 – 1861","t":"Mail at a gallop across the West","s":"For about 18 months in 1860 and 1861, riders on fast horses carried mail across the American West, from Missouri to California in about ten days. They changed horses at relay stations every few dozen kilometres, across plains, deserts and the Sierra Nevada. Then the telegraph reached California and the service closed. From above, look for the ridges and dry basins of Nevada that the riders crossed.","w":"Look for Nevada's parallel mountain ridges and dry basins, and the Sierra Nevada rising to the west.","f":["The route ran about 3,000 km from St. Joseph, Missouri, to Sacramento.","There were around 190 stations along the way.","The service ended two days after the transcontinental telegraph was completed in October 1861."],"q":{"q":"What put the Pony Express out of business?","a":"The transcontinental telegraph","x":["The transcontinental railroad","Steamships round Cape Horn"]}},"lewis-and-clark":{"name":"Lewis and Clark expedition route","era":"1804 – 1806","t":"Up the Missouri and over the Rockies to the Pacific","s":"In 1804 Meriwether Lewis and William Clark set out from near St. Louis with the Corps of Discovery to find a route to the Pacific. They paddled up the Missouri, crossed the Rocky Mountains with horses from the Shoshone, and floated down the Columbia to the ocean. Along the way they mapped rivers and described hundreds of plants and animals. From above, follow the Missouri's wide reservoirs across the plains.","w":"Look for the Missouri River's long reservoirs across the northern plains, and the Columbia River gorge in the far west.","f":["Sacagawea, a Shoshone woman, travelled with them carrying her baby son.","They spent the winter of 1805–06 at Fort Clatsop, near the Pacific coast.","The round trip covered about 13,000 km."],"q":{"q":"Which Shoshone woman travelled with Lewis and Clark?","a":"Sacagawea","x":["Pocahontas","Nancy Ward"]}},"transcontinental-railroad":{"name":"First Transcontinental Railroad","era":"1863 – 1869","t":"The line that stitched America coast to coast","s":"In the 1860s two companies raced to build a railway across the American West: one east from Sacramento over the Sierra Nevada, the other west from the Missouri River across the plains. They met at Promontory Summit in Utah on 10 May 1869, where a golden spike was driven. A journey that had taken months now took about a week. From above, look for the Great Salt Lake and the high Wyoming basins.","w":"Look for the Great Salt Lake, with the Promontory peninsula on its north shore, and the dry basins of Wyoming to the east.","f":["Chinese workers made up most of the Central Pacific's workforce in the Sierra Nevada.","The news of the golden spike was sent across the country by telegraph as it happened.","The line ran roughly 3,000 km from Council Bluffs to the San Francisco Bay."],"q":{"q":"Where was the golden spike driven in 1869?","a":"Promontory Summit, Utah","x":["Omaha, Nebraska","Cheyenne, Wyoming"]}},"erie-canal":{"name":"Erie Canal","era":"opened 1825","t":"The ditch that made New York the Empire State","s":"When it opened in 1825, the Erie Canal linked the Hudson River at Albany with Lake Erie at Buffalo, about 580 km away. Barges could now carry grain and timber from the Great Lakes to New York City far more cheaply than wagons, and the city became America's leading port. Sceptics had called it Clinton's Ditch. From above, look for the Mohawk valley and the chain of Finger Lakes to its west.","w":"Look for the Mohawk valley west of Albany, then the long, thin Finger Lakes south of the canal's route.","f":["The original canal had 83 locks to climb about 170 m.","At Lockport a staircase of five locks, the 'Flight of Five', lifted boats up a ridge.","It cut the cost of shipping goods from Buffalo to New York by around 90 percent."],"q":{"q":"Which lake does the Erie Canal link to the Hudson?","a":"Lake Erie","x":["Lake Huron","Lake Michigan"]}},"panama-canal":{"name":"Panama Canal","era":"opened 1914","t":"A staircase of water between two oceans","s":"Opened in 1914, the Panama Canal lets ships cross the isthmus instead of sailing round South America. Locks lift them 26 m into Gatún Lake, a huge reservoir made by damming a river, then lower them to the other ocean. A French attempt had failed; the United States finished the work. From cruise it is easy to see: a lake full of islands, a cut through green hills and ships queueing at both ends.","w":"Look for Gatún Lake with its many islands, the narrow cut through hills, and lines of ships waiting off both coasts.","f":["Because the isthmus curves, ships going from the Atlantic to the Pacific actually head south-east.","When filled, Gatún Lake was the largest artificial lake in the world.","In 1928 the writer Richard Halliburton swam the canal and paid a toll of 36 cents."],"q":{"q":"Which lake forms much of the Panama Canal?","a":"Gatún Lake","x":["Lake Nicaragua","Lake Maracaibo"]}},"canadian-pacific-railway":{"name":"Canadian Pacific Railway","era":"1881 – 1885","t":"The railway that held a new country together","s":"In 1871 British Columbia agreed to join Canada on one condition: a railway to the Pacific. The Canadian Pacific was finished in 1885, running about 4,600 km from Montreal along the rocky shore of Lake Superior, across the prairies and through the Rockies at Kicking Horse Pass. It carried settlers, wheat and tourists. From above, look for the grid of prairie farms and the wall of the Rockies beyond Calgary.","w":"Look for the vast grid of prairie fields, then the Rockies rising west of Calgary, with Banff in the valley.","f":["The last spike was driven at Craigellachie, British Columbia, on 7 November 1885.","The Spiral Tunnels, opened in 1909, loop inside mountains to ease the steep grade.","The railway built grand hotels, such as the Banff Springs, to attract travellers."],"q":{"q":"Where was the Canadian Pacific's last spike driven?","a":"Craigellachie","x":["Kicking Horse Pass","Winnipeg"]}},"columbus-1492":{"name":"Columbus's first voyage (1492)","era":"August – October 1492","t":"The crossing that joined two halves of the world","s":"In August 1492 three small ships left Palos in Spain under Christopher Columbus, stopped in the Canary Islands, then sailed west with the trade winds. After about five weeks at sea they reached an island in the Bahamas on 12 October. Columbus believed he had reached Asia. His voyage began lasting contact between Europe and the Americas, with profound consequences for the peoples of both. From above, look for the turquoise shallows of the Bahamas.","w":"Look for the bright turquoise banks and low islands of the Bahamas, set in deep blue ocean.","f":["His ships were the Santa María, the Pinta and the Niña.","The route along the north-east trade winds is still used by sailing boats crossing the Atlantic.","The Taíno people called the island where he landed Guanahani."],"q":{"q":"What did the Taíno call Columbus's first landfall?","a":"Guanahani","x":["Borinquen","Xaymaca"]}},"alcock-brown-1919":{"name":"Alcock and Brown, first non-stop transatlantic flight (1919)","era":"14–15 June 1919","t":"The first non-stop flight across the Atlantic","s":"On 14 June 1919 John Alcock and Arthur Whitten Brown took off from a field in St. John's, Newfoundland, in a modified Vickers Vimy biplane. They flew through fog, snow and ice for about 16 hours, navigating by sextant when they glimpsed the sun and stars, and came down in a bog near Clifden, Ireland. It was the first non-stop flight across the Atlantic, and both men walked away unhurt.","w":"At the western end, look for Newfoundland's rugged east coast; at the eastern end, the rocky coast of Connemara.","f":["They mistook the bog for a green field, so their plane tipped onto its nose on landing.","They won a £10,000 prize from the Daily Mail and were both knighted.","Their flight covered about 3,000 km."],"q":{"q":"Where did Alcock and Brown land?","a":"In a bog near Clifden, Ireland","x":["On a beach in Cornwall","In a field near Paris"]}},"lindbergh-1927":{"name":"Lindbergh's New York–Paris flight (1927)","era":"20–21 May 1927","t":"Alone across the Atlantic in 33½ hours","s":"On 20 May 1927 Charles Lindbergh took off from Long Island in the single-engined Spirit of St. Louis, bound for Paris. He flew alone, fighting sleep, over Nova Scotia, Newfoundland and the open ocean, reached Ireland's coast almost exactly where he had planned, and landed at Le Bourget after about 33½ hours. It was the first solo non-stop flight across the Atlantic, and it made him world-famous overnight.","w":"Look for the long coast of Nova Scotia at the American end, and south-west Ireland and Cornwall at the European end.","f":["An extra fuel tank blocked his view ahead; he looked forward through a periscope or out of the side windows.","He won the $25,000 Orteig Prize for the first non-stop flight between New York and Paris.","A huge crowd, estimated at over 100,000, met him at Le Bourget."],"q":{"q":"How long did Lindbergh's flight take?","a":"About 33½ hours","x":["About 16 hours","About 3 days"]}},"transatlantic-telegraph":{"name":"First transatlantic telegraph cable","era":"1858; lasting link 1866","t":"The wire that let two continents talk in minutes","s":"In 1866 the giant steamship Great Eastern laid a telegraph cable along the floor of the Atlantic, from Valentia Island in Ireland to Heart's Content in Newfoundland. News that had taken about ten days by ship now crossed the ocean in minutes. An earlier cable in 1858 had worked only for a few weeks. Today's internet still crosses the ocean by cable, along much the same seabed.","w":"At the ends, look for the rugged tip of south-west Ireland and the deep bays of eastern Newfoundland.","f":["The American businessman Cyrus Field spent twelve years on the project.","The 1858 cable carried a message from Queen Victoria to the US President that took about 16 hours to send.","The Great Eastern was the only ship then big enough to carry the whole cable."],"q":{"q":"Which ship laid the 1866 Atlantic cable?","a":"The Great Eastern","x":["The Great Western","The Cutty Sark"]}},"machu-picchu":{"name":"Machu Picchu","era":"c. 1450 – 1570","t":"An Inca estate on a mountain saddle above the clouds","s":"On a ridge high above the looping Urubamba river, the Inca emperor Pachacuti built a royal estate of granite temples, terraces and houses around 1450. The Spanish never found it, and it drifted out of memory beyond the valley until 1911, when local farmers led the explorer Hiram Bingham there. The site is small from cruise, but look for the deep river gorges and the snowy peaks around it.","w":"Look for the deep, twisting Urubamba gorge north-west of Cusco, with the snowy peak of Salcantay to the south.","f":["It lies at about 2,430 m, between the peaks of Machu Picchu and Huayna Picchu.","Its stones were cut to fit so tightly that no mortar was needed.","Machu Picchu became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1983."],"q":{"q":"Who brought Machu Picchu to world attention in 1911?","a":"Hiram Bingham","x":["Alexander von Humboldt","Francisco Pizarro"]}},"cusco":{"name":"Cusco, the Inca capital","era":"c. 1200 – today","t":"The navel of the Inca world","s":"In a high Andean valley at about 3,400 m lies Cusco, capital of the Inca Empire. Roads ran from it to every corner of the realm, and its temples were covered with gold. After the Spanish arrived they built churches and mansions on the Inca walls, and the fitted stone foundations still line the streets. From above, look for a city filling a green mountain valley, with red-tiled roofs.","w":"Look for a city spread along a high green valley ringed by bare mountains, south-east of the Urubamba valley.","f":["Cusco's 'twelve-angled stone' fits its neighbours perfectly without mortar.","Legend says the city was laid out in the shape of a puma.","Cusco became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1983."],"q":{"q":"Which animal's shape was Cusco said to follow?","a":"A puma","x":["A condor","A llama"]}},"nazca-lines":{"name":"Nazca Lines","era":"c. 500 BC – AD 500","t":"Giant drawings scratched into the desert","s":"On the dry plateau of southern Peru, the Nazca people drew hundreds of straight lines, geometric shapes and animal figures by moving the dark surface stones to reveal paler ground. Some are hundreds of metres long, and the dry, windless climate has kept them for 2,000 years. The animal figures need a low-flying plane, but from cruise the longest lines can sometimes be made out as faint pale scratches.","w":"Look for a flat, pale-brown desert plateau between river valleys, inland from the coast of southern Peru.","f":["About 70 figures show animals and plants, including a hummingbird, a monkey and a spider.","The German mathematician Maria Reiche studied and protected the lines for about 50 years.","The Nazca Lines became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1994."],"q":{"q":"How were the Nazca Lines made?","a":"By moving dark surface stones","x":["By burning the ground","By painting with lime"]}},"chan-chan":{"name":"Chan Chan","era":"c. 850 – 1470","t":"The largest mud-brick city of the Americas","s":"On the desert coast of northern Peru stood Chan Chan, capital of the Chimú kingdom. Its walled compounds, built of adobe mud brick and decorated with friezes of fish, birds and waves, covered some 20 km², making it the largest adobe city in the Americas. The Incas conquered it around 1470. From above, look for the green Moche valley meeting the sea beside the city of Trujillo.","w":"Look for the green Moche valley reaching the Pacific beside Trujillo, with bare desert on either side.","f":["Rain is so rare here that mud walls have survived for centuries.","Each Chimú ruler is thought to have built his own walled palace compound.","Chan Chan became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1986."],"q":{"q":"What is Chan Chan built of?","a":"Adobe mud brick","x":["Cut granite","Coral stone"]}},"caral":{"name":"Caral","era":"c. 2600 – 1800 BC","t":"A city as old as the Egyptian pyramids","s":"In the Supe valley north of Lima stood Caral, one of the oldest cities in the Americas. Around 2600 BC, while the pyramids of Giza were rising, its people built stepped pyramids and sunken circular plazas here, without pottery or writing. They farmed cotton and traded with fishing villages on the coast. From above, look for a thin green valley crossing the coastal desert towards the Pacific.","w":"Look for a narrow green valley cutting through the coastal desert, about 180 km north of Lima.","f":["The archaeologist Ruth Shady has led excavations at Caral since 1994.","Flutes made of pelican and condor bones were found in one of its temples.","The Sacred City of Caral-Supe became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2009."],"q":{"q":"Who has led excavations at Caral since 1994?","a":"Ruth Shady","x":["Hiram Bingham","Maria Reiche"]}},"tiwanaku":{"name":"Tiwanaku","era":"c. 500 – 1000","t":"A high-altitude capital near Lake Titicaca","s":"Near the southern shore of Lake Titicaca, at about 3,850 m, stood Tiwanaku, capital of a civilisation that flourished centuries before the Incas. Its builders shaped stone with astonishing precision and fed the city with raised fields that protected crops from frost. The Incas later saw it as a sacred ancestral place. From above, look for the vast blue lake and the flat, cold altiplano around it.","w":"Look for the southern end of the huge blue Lake Titicaca and the flat high plain stretching away from it.","f":["Its Gateway of the Sun was carved from a single block of stone.","Its raised-field farming has been revived in some villages around the lake.","Tiwanaku became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2000."],"q":{"q":"What is Tiwanaku's famous carved gateway called?","a":"The Gateway of the Sun","x":["The Lion Gate","The Ishtar Gate"]}},"potosi":{"name":"Potosí and Cerro Rico","era":"from 1545","t":"A mountain of silver that fed an empire","s":"High on the Bolivian plateau, at about 4,000 m, rises Cerro Rico, the rich hill. After silver was found here in 1545 it became one of the richest sources of silver ever known, largely worked by forced Indigenous labour. The city at its foot grew into one of the largest in the Americas, and its coins spread across the world. From above, look for a reddish cone above a city on the bare high plain.","w":"Look for a reddish, cone-shaped mountain rising above a city on the bare, high Bolivian plateau.","f":["The Spanish phrase 'vale un Potosí' still means 'it's worth a fortune'.","Potosí is one of the highest cities in the world.","The city became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1987."],"q":{"q":"What does the saying 'vale un Potosí' mean?","a":"It's worth a fortune","x":["It's very high up","It's very old"]}},"teotihuacan":{"name":"Teotihuacan","era":"c. 100 BC – AD 550","t":"A city of pyramids that awed even the Aztecs","s":"North-east of today's Mexico City lie the ruins of Teotihuacan, one of the largest cities in the world around AD 500. A broad avenue several kilometres long runs past the great Pyramids of the Sun and the Moon, lined with temples and palaces. Centuries after its fall, the Aztecs believed the gods had been born here. In clear air the Pyramid of the Sun can sometimes be picked out in the valley.","w":"Look north-east of Mexico City's sprawl for a farmed valley with a long straight avenue and two large pyramids.","f":["The Pyramid of the Sun is about 65 m tall, one of the largest in the Americas.","At its peak the city may have had around 100,000 inhabitants.","Teotihuacan became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1987."],"q":{"q":"What does 'Teotihuacan' mean in Nahuatl?","a":"Place where the gods were made","x":["City of the sun","Home of the eagles"]}},"tenochtitlan":{"name":"Tenochtitlan","era":"1325 – 1521","t":"The Aztec capital built on a lake","s":"Mexico City stands on the site of Tenochtitlan, the Aztec capital founded in 1325 on an island in Lake Texcoco. Causeways linked it to the shore, canals ran between its districts, and floating gardens fed its people. When Spanish travellers first saw it, they compared it to Venice. The lake was later drained, and from above you now see only a vast city filling the old lakebed, ringed by mountains and volcanoes.","w":"Look for an immense city filling a high basin ringed by mountains, with the volcanoes Popocatépetl and Iztaccíhuatl to the south-east.","f":["The eagle on a cactus on Mexico's flag comes from the city's founding legend.","Remains of the Great Temple were uncovered in the city centre after 1978.","The canals of Xochimilco are a surviving piece of the old lake landscape."],"q":{"q":"Which image on Mexico's flag comes from Tenochtitlan's legend?","a":"An eagle on a cactus","x":["A jaguar on a pyramid","A serpent in a lake"]}},"chichen-itza":{"name":"Chichén Itzá","era":"c. 600 – 1200","t":"A Maya calendar in stone on the Yucatán plain","s":"On the flat limestone plain of the Yucatán rises Chichén Itzá, one of the greatest cities of the Maya. Its step pyramid, El Castillo, works as a calendar in stone, and at the spring and autumn equinoxes shadows form a serpent descending its stairs. There are no rivers here; water came from cenotes, natural wells in the rock. From above, look for flat green forest stretching to every horizon.","w":"Look for flat, unbroken green scrub forest across the Yucatán, about 180 km west of Cancún.","f":["El Castillo's four stairways plus its top platform add up to 365 steps.","Its Great Ball Court is the largest known in Mesoamerica.","Chichén Itzá became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1988."],"q":{"q":"What appears on El Castillo's stairs at the equinoxes?","a":"A serpent of light and shadow","x":["A rainbow","A double sunrise"]}},"tulum":{"name":"Tulum","era":"13th – 16th c.","t":"A Maya port on a cliff above the Caribbean","s":"On a low cliff above the turquoise Caribbean stands Tulum, a walled Maya port that was still busy when the first Spanish ships sailed by. Its temples looked out over a gap in the reef, and traders in canoes carried jade, obsidian and salt along the coast. From above, look for the long white-sand coast of the Yucatán and the line of the barrier reef just offshore.","w":"Look for the white-sand coast south of Cancún, with a thin line of surf marking the reef offshore.","f":["A stone wall protected the city on three sides; the sea guarded the fourth.","Its name is Maya for 'wall'.","The Mesoamerican Barrier Reef off this coast is the largest reef in the Americas."],"q":{"q":"What protected Tulum on three sides?","a":"A stone wall","x":["A moat","A river"]}},"palenque":{"name":"Palenque","era":"c. 500 – 800","t":"A Maya royal city in the rainforest","s":"Where forested hills meet the lowland plain of Chiapas stands Palenque, a Maya city famous for its elegant temples and carved inscriptions. Its greatest king, Pakal, ruled for almost 70 years in the 7th century. In 1952 archaeologists found his tomb deep inside the Temple of the Inscriptions, with a jade mask and a magnificently carved stone lid. From above, look for green hills rising from the flat plain.","w":"Look where the forested hills of Chiapas rise abruptly from the flat, wet plain of Tabasco to the north.","f":["Pakal became king at the age of 12.","The tomb was reached by a hidden stairway inside the pyramid.","Palenque became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1987."],"q":{"q":"Whose tomb was found in the Temple of the Inscriptions?","a":"King Pakal's","x":["Moctezuma's","Atahualpa's"]}},"tikal":{"name":"Tikal","era":"4th c. BC – 10th c. AD","t":"Maya temples rising above the jungle canopy","s":"Deep in the rainforest of northern Guatemala, the temple-pyramids of Tikal rise above the treetops. It was one of the most powerful Maya cities, home to tens of thousands of people, with plazas, palaces and reservoirs. Laser scans from aircraft have since revealed thousands more buildings hidden under the forest. From above, look for unbroken green jungle and Lake Petén Itzá to the south.","w":"Look for unbroken green rainforest in northern Guatemala, with the long Lake Petén Itzá to the south.","f":["Its tallest temple, Temple IV, is about 65 m high.","Tikal appears as a rebel base in the original 'Star Wars' film of 1977.","It became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1979, for both nature and culture."],"q":{"q":"Which film used Tikal as a rebel base?","a":"Star Wars (1977)","x":["Indiana Jones","Jurassic Park"]}},"mesa-verde":{"name":"Mesa Verde","era":"c. 550 – 1300","t":"Villages tucked into cliff alcoves","s":"On a green tableland in south-western Colorado, Ancestral Puebloan people farmed for more than 700 years. In the 1200s they built villages of stone into alcoves in the canyon walls, some with more than a hundred rooms. Around 1300 they moved away, probably driven by drought. From above, look for a flat-topped mesa cut by deep canyons, near the Four Corners where four states meet.","w":"Look for a large flat-topped mesa, green with juniper and cut by canyons, in the far south-west of Colorado.","f":["Cliff Palace, the largest cliff dwelling, has around 150 rooms.","In 1906 it became the first US national park created to protect human works.","In 1978 it was among the first 12 sites on UNESCO's World Heritage list."],"q":{"q":"What is Mesa Verde's largest cliff dwelling?","a":"Cliff Palace","x":["Pueblo Bonito","Taos Pueblo"]}},"chaco-canyon":{"name":"Chaco Canyon","era":"c. 850 – 1150","t":"Great houses aligned with the sun and moon","s":"In a dry canyon in north-western New Mexico, Ancestral Puebloans built great houses of stone, some several storeys high with hundreds of rooms. Chaco was a centre of trade and ceremony, linked to distant settlements by straight roads, and several buildings are aligned with the sun and moon. From above, look for dry mesas and washes in empty desert south of the San Juan River.","w":"Look for dry mesas, canyons and washes in open desert in north-western New Mexico.","f":["Pueblo Bonito, the largest great house, had around 650 rooms.","Chaco is also an International Dark Sky Park, with some of the darkest night skies in the US.","Chaco Culture became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1987."],"q":{"q":"What is Chaco Canyon's largest great house?","a":"Pueblo Bonito","x":["Cliff Palace","Taos Pueblo"]}},"cahokia":{"name":"Cahokia","era":"c. 1050 – 1350","t":"The great city of the Mississippi mound builders","s":"Just across the Mississippi from St. Louis lie the mounds of Cahokia, the largest city north of Mexico before Europeans arrived. Around 1100, thousands of people lived here among plazas, houses and more than a hundred earthen mounds. Its central mound, built basket by basket, is the largest earthwork of its kind in the Americas. From above, look for where the Missouri and Mississippi rivers meet near the city.","w":"Look for the confluence of the Missouri and Mississippi just north of St. Louis; the mounds lie on the Illinois side.","f":["Monks Mound is about 30 m high and covers some 6 hectares.","A circle of wooden posts, now called Woodhenge, marked sunrise at solstices and equinoxes.","Cahokia became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1982."],"q":{"q":"What is Cahokia's largest earthwork called?","a":"Monks Mound","x":["Serpent Mound","Grave Creek Mound"]}},"kitty-hawk":{"name":"Kitty Hawk: the first powered flight","era":"17 December 1903","t":"Where the Wright brothers first flew","s":"On the windy sand dunes of the Outer Banks, near Kitty Hawk, Orville and Wilbur Wright made the first controlled, powered flights of an aeroplane on 17 December 1903. The first flight lasted 12 seconds. The brothers, bicycle makers from Ohio, chose the spot for its steady winds and soft sand. Every flight since, including yours, traces back here. From above, look for the long, thin barrier islands off North Carolina.","w":"Look for a long, narrow chain of sandy barrier islands off North Carolina, with wide sounds behind them.","f":["The first flight covered about 37 m, less than the wingspan of many airliners.","Their best flight that day lasted 59 seconds and covered about 260 m.","Neil Armstrong carried a piece of the Wright Flyer's fabric to the Moon in 1969."],"q":{"q":"How long did the first powered flight last?","a":"12 seconds","x":["2 seconds","3 minutes"]}},"l-anse-aux-meadows":{"name":"L'Anse aux Meadows","era":"c. 1000; found 1960","t":"The Vikings in America, 500 years before Columbus","s":"At the northern tip of Newfoundland lie the remains of a Norse settlement from around the year 1000: turf buildings, a smithy and bits of iron and bronze. It is the only confirmed Viking site in the Americas, and it matches the sagas' stories of Leif Erikson reaching a land they called Vinland. From above, look for the northern tip of Newfoundland facing the Strait of Belle Isle, with icebergs in spring.","w":"Look for the northern tip of Newfoundland beside the narrow Strait of Belle Isle, often with icebergs in spring.","f":["It was found in 1960 by the Norwegian explorers Helge Ingstad and Anne Stine Ingstad.","Tree rings in wood cut by Norse tools show the timber was felled in 1021.","It was among the first 12 sites on UNESCO's World Heritage list in 1978."],"q":{"q":"Who found L'Anse aux Meadows in 1960?","a":"Helge and Anne Stine Ingstad","x":["Farley Mowat","Thor Heyerdahl"]}},"signal-hill":{"name":"Signal Hill, St. John's","era":"1901","t":"Where the first radio signal crossed the Atlantic","s":"On a headland above the harbour of St. John's, Newfoundland, Guglielmo Marconi listened through an earphone on 12 December 1901 and heard three faint clicks sent from Cornwall, about 3,500 km away. It was the first wireless signal across the Atlantic, and it opened the age of radio. His aerial was a wire held up by a kite. From above, look for the narrow harbour entrance of St. John's on Newfoundland's east coast.","w":"Look for a narrow gap in the cliffs on Newfoundland's east coast, opening into the harbour of St. John's.","f":["The three clicks were the Morse code for the letter S.","Cabot Tower on the hill marks the 400th anniversary of John Cabot's voyage of 1497.","St. John's is the easternmost city in North America."],"q":{"q":"Which Morse letter did Marconi hear in 1901?","a":"S","x":["O","E"]}},"ellis-island":{"name":"Ellis Island and the Statue of Liberty","era":"1886 – 1954","t":"The gateway for millions of new Americans","s":"In New York Harbor, two small islands tell the story of American immigration. The Statue of Liberty, a gift from France, was dedicated in 1886, and from 1892 to 1954 around 12 million immigrants were processed at the station on neighbouring Ellis Island. For many, the statue was their first sight of America. From above, look for the Upper Bay between Manhattan, Brooklyn and New Jersey.","w":"Look for the Upper Bay just south-west of Manhattan's tip, with two small islands near the New Jersey shore.","f":["The statue's copper skin was designed by Frédéric Bartholdi; Gustave Eiffel designed its iron frame.","Its copper turned green over about 20 years as it weathered.","Ellis Island's main building is now a museum of immigration."],"q":{"q":"Which country gave the Statue of Liberty to the US?","a":"France","x":["Britain","Italy"]}},"kennedy-space-center":{"name":"Kennedy Space Center: the road to the Moon","era":"from 1962","t":"The launch pads that sent people to the Moon","s":"On Merritt Island, beside Cape Canaveral on Florida's Atlantic coast, stand the launch pads from which Apollo astronauts left for the Moon. Apollo 11 lifted off from Launch Complex 39A on 16 July 1969, and four days later two people walked on another world for the first time. Space Shuttles and today's rockets launch here too. From above, look for the cape and its lagoons jutting out from the Florida coast.","w":"Look for a broad cape and flat island between long lagoons on Florida's Atlantic coast, east of Orlando.","f":["The Vehicle Assembly Building, where rockets are stacked, is one of the largest buildings in the world by volume.","Much of the land around the pads is a wildlife refuge.","Launch Complex 39A has sent astronauts to the Moon, Space Shuttles into orbit and modern rockets to the space station."],"q":{"q":"Which mission launched here for the first Moon landing?","a":"Apollo 11","x":["Apollo 8","Gemini 4"]}},"sutters-mill":{"name":"Sutter's Mill, Coloma","era":"24 January 1848","t":"The flake of gold that started the California Gold Rush","s":"In January 1848 a carpenter building a sawmill for John Sutter on the American River noticed flakes of gold in the water. Over the next few years some 300,000 people poured into California, and the small port of San Francisco grew into a city almost overnight. California became a state in 1850. From above, look for the Sierra Nevada foothills and Folsom Lake east of Sacramento.","w":"Look for the oak-covered Sierra Nevada foothills and the Folsom Lake reservoir, east of Sacramento.","f":["The carpenter who found the gold was James Marshall.","The gold-seekers of 1849 became known as the 'forty-niners'.","San Francisco grew from about 1,000 people in 1848 to around 25,000 by 1850."],"q":{"q":"What were the gold-seekers of 1849 called?","a":"Forty-niners","x":["Sooners","Klondikers"]}},"santo-domingo":{"name":"Colonial Santo Domingo","era":"from 1498","t":"The oldest European city in the Americas","s":"At the mouth of the Ozama river on the south coast of Hispaniola, Bartholomew Columbus founded Santo Domingo in the 1490s. It became the first permanent European city in the Americas, and the first cathedral and hospital in the New World were built here. Its old stone quarter still stands beside the river. From above, look for a large city where a river meets the Caribbean, on the island's southern shore.","w":"Look for a large coastal city on the south shore of Hispaniola, where the Ozama river meets the Caribbean.","f":["Its cathedral, finished around 1540, is the oldest in the Americas.","Its streets were laid out on a grid that became a model for Spanish cities in the Americas.","The colonial city became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1990."],"q":{"q":"Who founded Santo Domingo?","a":"Bartholomew Columbus","x":["Hernán Cortés","Francisco Pizarro"]}},"hohokam-canals":{"name":"Hohokam canals of the Salt River valley","era":"c. 450 – 1450","t":"The ancient canals beneath Phoenix","s":"Long before Phoenix, the Hohokam people turned the Salt River valley green. Over a thousand years they dug hundreds of kilometres of canals by hand, the largest prehistoric irrigation network in North America, and grew corn, beans and cotton in the desert. Some of today's canals follow their routes. From above, look for the vast grid of Phoenix, laced with straight canals, in the Sonoran desert.","w":"Look for the huge grid of Phoenix in the desert, crossed by long straight canals and ringed by bare mountains.","f":["The Hohokam canal network is estimated at more than 500 km.","Phoenix was named in the 1860s for a new town rising on the ruins of an old one.","The Pueblo Grande museum in Phoenix preserves a Hohokam platform mound."],"q":{"q":"Why was Phoenix given its name?","a":"It rose on an ancient town's ruins","x":["A rare bird was seen there","It was named after a steamboat"]}},"ouro-preto":{"name":"Ouro Preto","era":"18th c.","t":"A baroque gold-rush town in the Brazilian hills","s":"In the steep hills of Minas Gerais, a gold rush at the end of the 17th century created Ouro Preto, then called Vila Rica. Its gold helped pay for an age of splendour in Portugal, and its miners and merchants filled the town with baroque churches decorated by the sculptor Aleijadinho. From above, look for hilly green country scarred with red earth from mining, south-east of Belo Horizonte.","w":"Look for steep green hills and red-earth mine scars in Minas Gerais, south-east of Belo Horizonte.","f":["'Ouro Preto' means 'black gold', after the dark iron-oxide coating on its gold.","In the 18th century Minas Gerais made Brazil one of the world's leading gold producers.","In 1980 it became Brazil's first UNESCO World Heritage site."],"q":{"q":"Which sculptor decorated Ouro Preto's churches?","a":"Aleijadinho","x":["Oscar Niemeyer","Candido Portinari"]}},"hokulea-1976":{"name":"Hōkūleʻa's voyage to Tahiti (1976)","era":"May – June 1976","t":"A canoe that sailed Polynesian history back to life","s":"In 1976 the double-hulled voyaging canoe Hōkūleʻa sailed from Hawaiʻi to Tahiti, over 4,000 km of open ocean, without compass or charts. Its navigator, Mau Piailug from Micronesia, read the stars, swells and birds, as Polynesian navigators had for centuries. The voyage helped show that the settlement of the Pacific was deliberate exploration, not drift. Thousands of people greeted the canoe in Papeete. The ocean it crossed looks endless from above.","w":"Open ocean between Hawaiʻi and Tahiti; near Tahiti, look for the high green island and the atolls of the Tuamotus.","f":["The crossing took about a month.","Mau Piailug learned traditional navigation on the tiny island of Satawal.","Between 2013 and 2017 Hōkūleʻa sailed around the world."],"q":{"q":"Who navigated Hōkūleʻa to Tahiti in 1976?","a":"Mau Piailug","x":["Tupaia","Thor Heyerdahl"]}},"aotearoa-voyage":{"name":"Polynesian voyage to Aotearoa","era":"c. 1250 – 1300","t":"The last great land on Earth to be settled","s":"Around 1250 to 1300, voyagers from eastern Polynesia sailed south-west across thousands of kilometres of ocean in double-hulled canoes and found a large, cool, forested land: Aotearoa, New Zealand. They navigated by stars, swells and the flight of birds, and carried plants and animals to start new lives. It was the last major land mass on Earth to be settled by people. From above, the scale of that ocean is humbling.","w":"Open ocean south-west of the Cook Islands; at the end, look for the green hills and harbours of New Zealand's North Island.","f":["Māori tradition names the homeland of the first voyagers Hawaiki.","Many Māori tribes trace their ancestry to particular voyaging canoes.","The voyagers brought plants such as the kūmara, a sweet potato, with them."],"q":{"q":"What is the ancestral homeland called in Māori tradition?","a":"Hawaiki","x":["Aotearoa","Rapa Nui"]}},"rapa-nui-voyage":{"name":"Polynesian voyage to Rapa Nui","era":"c. 1150 – 1250","t":"Across empty ocean to the loneliest island","s":"Around 800 years ago, Polynesian voyagers sailing east from the islands of Mangareva found a tiny speck of land far out in the Pacific: Rapa Nui, Easter Island. It lies more than 2,000 km from the nearest inhabited island, and finding it on purpose required extraordinary navigation. The settlers went on to carve the famous moai. From above you sense the achievement: nothing but ocean, in every direction.","w":"Endless open ocean east of Pitcairn; at the end, a small triangular island with three volcanoes.","f":["The sweet potato, a South American crop, reached Polynesia before Europeans did.","Genetic studies suggest Polynesians and Native South Americans met around 800 years ago.","Pitcairn, on the way, is now home to fewer than 50 people."],"q":{"q":"Which South American crop reached Polynesia before Europeans?","a":"The sweet potato","x":["The potato","Maize"]}},"cook-first-voyage":{"name":"James Cook's first voyage (1768–1771)","era":"1768 – 1771","t":"Charting the Pacific in the Endeavour","s":"In 1768 James Cook sailed from Plymouth in HMS Endeavour to observe the planet Venus crossing the face of the Sun from Tahiti. He then sailed round both main islands of New Zealand, charting their coasts, and followed the east coast of Australia for the first time by a European ship. The voyage brought back charts, plants and animals unknown to European science. From above, look for New Zealand's long coasts.","w":"Look for New Zealand's two long islands and, further west, the east coast of Australia along the Coral Sea.","f":["The botanist Joseph Banks collected thousands of plant specimens on the voyage.","The Tahitian navigator Tupaia joined the ship and drew a chart of many Pacific islands.","The Endeavour struck the Great Barrier Reef in 1770 and was repaired at today's Cooktown."],"q":{"q":"What did Cook observe in Tahiti in 1769?","a":"The transit of Venus","x":["A total solar eclipse","Halley's Comet"]}},"overland-telegraph":{"name":"Australian Overland Telegraph Line","era":"1870 – 1872","t":"The wire that linked Australia to the world","s":"In 1872 a single telegraph wire was completed from Adelaide across the heart of the continent to Darwin, where it joined an undersea cable to Java and on to London. News from Europe, which had taken months by ship, now arrived in hours. Repeater stations were built every few hundred kilometres, and one of them grew into Alice Springs. From above, look for red desert and the highway that follows the line.","w":"Look for red desert crossed by a lone highway and railway, and the long parallel ridges of the MacDonnell Ranges at Alice Springs.","f":["The line was about 3,200 km long and needed some 36,000 poles.","The work was led by Charles Todd, South Australia's postmaster and astronomer.","Alice Springs was named after his wife, Alice Todd."],"q":{"q":"Who was Alice Springs named after?","a":"Alice Todd, Charles Todd's wife","x":["Princess Alice","A governor's daughter"]}},"kingsford-smith-1928":{"name":"First trans-Pacific flight (1928)","era":"31 May – 9 June 1928","t":"The first flight across the Pacific, in three hops","s":"In 1928 Charles Kingsford Smith, Charles Ulm and two American crewmen flew the three-engined Fokker Southern Cross from California to Australia. They stopped in Hawaiʻi and Fiji, and the leg from Hawaiʻi to Suva, over 5,000 km of open ocean, was the longest flight over water yet made. After about 83 hours in the air they landed in Brisbane to a huge welcome. From above, the Pacific they crossed looks endless.","w":"Open Pacific between Hawaiʻi and Fiji; at the end, the Queensland coast and Brisbane River.","f":["The crew flew through storms at night, navigating by stars, compass and drift sight.","The Southern Cross is preserved in a memorial at Brisbane Airport.","The whole journey covered about 11,600 km."],"q":{"q":"What was Kingsford Smith's aircraft called?","a":"Southern Cross","x":["Spirit of St. Louis","Friendship"]}},"uluru-kata-tjuta":{"name":"Uluṟu-Kata Tjuṯa","era":"tens of thousands of years – today","t":"A living cultural landscape of the Aṉangu","s":"In the red heart of Australia rises Uluṟu, a single great sandstone rock, and 25 km to the west the domes of Kata Tjuṯa. For the Aṉangu, the traditional owners, these places are bound up with Tjukurpa, the law and knowledge that shape their lives and land. Their stories are held in the rock's features. From cruise, look for a lone red rock on a flat red plain, and a cluster of domes beside it.","w":"A single red monolith rising from a flat red plain, with a cluster of rounded domes about 25 km to its west.","f":["The park was handed back to the Aṉangu in 1985 and is jointly managed with them.","At the traditional owners' request, climbing Uluṟu stopped for good in 2019.","UNESCO listed the park for its nature in 1987 and as a cultural landscape in 1994."],"q":{"q":"In which year did climbing Uluṟu end?","a":"2019","x":["1985","2000"]}},"rapa-nui":{"name":"Rapa Nui (Easter Island)","era":"c. 1200 – today","t":"The moai of the most remote inhabited island","s":"Rapa Nui, Easter Island, is a small triangle of volcanoes in the south-eastern Pacific, more than 2,000 km from the nearest inhabited island. Its people carved hundreds of great stone figures, the moai, most of them from a single volcanic quarry, and stood many on platforms with their backs to the sea. From cruise the island is a small green-brown triangle alone in the ocean, with a volcano at each corner.","w":"A small triangular island in open ocean, with a volcanic crater at each corner.","f":["Nearly 900 moai are known on the island.","Most were carved in the quarry of the volcano Rano Raraku.","Rapa Nui National Park became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1995."],"q":{"q":"Where were most moai carved?","a":"In the Rano Raraku quarry","x":["At Orongo village","On Anakena beach"]}},"nan-madol":{"name":"Nan Madol","era":"c. 1180 – 1628","t":"A city of basalt islets on a Pacific reef","s":"Off the coast of Pohnpei in Micronesia lie the ruins of Nan Madol, built on about a hundred artificial islets on a coral reef. Its walls are made of long black basalt columns stacked crosswise like logs, some weighing several tonnes. It was the seat of the Saudeleur rulers, who governed the island for centuries. From above, look for a green high island ringed by reef and mangroves.","w":"Look for the lush high island of Pohnpei ringed by reef and mangroves; the ruins lie off its eastern shore.","f":["Canals between the islets have led people to call it the Venice of the Pacific.","How the builders moved the heavy basalt columns is still debated.","Nan Madol became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2016."],"q":{"q":"What are Nan Madol's walls built from?","a":"Stacked basalt columns","x":["Mud bricks","Timber logs"]}},"taputapuatea":{"name":"Taputapuātea marae","era":"c. 1000 – today","t":"The sacred heart of Polynesian voyaging","s":"On the island of Raiatea stands Taputapuātea, a complex of stone ceremonial platforms, or marae, at the centre of the Polynesian world. Navigators set out from here and priests from distant islands came to meet, taking stones from it to found new marae across the Pacific. Its setting of forest, lagoon and a pass through the reef is part of its meaning. From above, look for two islands sharing one lagoon.","w":"Look for two green islands, Raiatea and Tahaʻa, enclosed together in one turquoise lagoon, north-west of Tahiti.","f":["Raiatea's ancient name, Havaiʻi, recalls the ancestral homeland of Polynesian traditions.","Voyaging canoes such as Hōkūleʻa have been welcomed here in modern times.","Taputapuātea became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2017."],"q":{"q":"What was Raiatea's ancient name?","a":"Havaiʻi","x":["Hiva Oa","Aotearoa"]}},"budj-bim":{"name":"Budj Bim eel traps","era":"at least 6,600 years","t":"One of the world's oldest fish farms","s":"In the lava fields of south-western Victoria, the Gunditjmara people built channels, weirs and ponds to trap, store and harvest eels. Parts of the system are dated to about 6,600 years ago, making it one of the oldest aquaculture systems known. Stone house foundations stand nearby. From above, look for the volcanic plains and lakes near the coast, with the low cone of Budj Bim.","w":"Look for flat volcanic plains, lakes and wetlands inland from Portland, near the low cone of Budj Bim.","f":["The lava flow the channels are built in came from the Budj Bim volcano about 30,000 years ago.","The eels were smoked and traded far beyond the area.","Budj Bim became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2019."],"q":{"q":"What did the Budj Bim channels trap?","a":"Eels","x":["Trout","Crabs"]}},"lake-mungo":{"name":"Lake Mungo","era":"c. 50,000 years – today","t":"A dry lake that holds 40,000 years of human story","s":"In the outback of New South Wales lie the dry beds of the Willandra Lakes, full of water in the last Ice Age. On their crescent-shaped dunes, archaeologists found the burials of Mungo Lady and Mungo Man, about 40,000 years old, among the oldest known anywhere, with signs of careful ritual. From cruise the landscape is striking: pale, flat lakebeds with white crescent dunes along their eastern shores.","w":"Look for a chain of pale, dry, flat lakebeds, each with a white crescent-shaped dune on its eastern side.","f":["The white crescent dune at Lake Mungo is called the Walls of China.","Mungo Lady is the oldest known ritual cremation in the world.","Mungo Man was returned to his traditional owners at Lake Mungo in 2017."],"q":{"q":"What are Lake Mungo's white crescent dunes called?","a":"The Walls of China","x":["The Pinnacles","The Olgas"]}},"kakadu-rock-art":{"name":"Kakadu rock art","era":"tens of thousands of years – today","t":"A painted gallery thousands of years deep","s":"Along the sandstone escarpment of Arnhem Land and the rock outcrops of Kakadu, generations of Bininj and Mungguy artists have painted animals, people and ancestral beings, layer upon layer, over thousands of years. Some paintings show the inside of fish and animals, bones and all. The traditional owners still care for the sites. From above, look for a long sandstone cliff line above green wetlands and floodplains.","w":"Look for a long sandstone escarpment above wide green floodplains and winding rivers, east of Darwin.","f":["Some paintings show the thylacine, which vanished from mainland Australia thousands of years ago.","Later paintings record the arrival of sailing ships and European visitors.","Kakadu became a UNESCO World Heritage site in 1981, for both nature and culture."],"q":{"q":"Which style shows animals' bones and organs?","a":"X-ray style","x":["Dot painting","Hand stencils"]}},"nansen-greenland-1888":{"name":"Nansen's crossing of Greenland (1888)","era":"August – October 1888","t":"The first crossing of the Greenland ice sheet","s":"In 1888 Fridtjof Nansen and five companions landed on the empty east coast of Greenland and set off on skis across the ice sheet, pulling their sledges. They chose the east so that there would be no turning back. After about six weeks, having climbed to some 2,700 m, they came down to the fjords near Godthåb, today's Nuuk. From cruise the ice sheet looks just as they saw it: white to every horizon.","w":"Look for the Greenland ice sheet, flat and white to the horizon, with dark mountains and fjords along its coasts.","f":["Two of the team, Samuel Balto and Ole Ravna, were Sámi.","They missed the last ship home and spent the winter in Godthåb, living among the Greenlanders.","Nansen later wrote a book about Greenlandic life and customs."],"q":{"q":"Why did Nansen cross Greenland from east to west?","a":"So there was no way back","x":["To follow the sun","Because the wind blew west"]}},"nansen-fram":{"name":"Nansen's Fram drift (1893–1896)","era":"1893 – 1896","t":"A ship frozen into the ice on purpose","s":"Nansen believed that sea ice drifted across the Arctic Ocean, so in 1893 he let his specially built ship Fram freeze into the pack north of Siberia and drift with it. With a companion he then left the ship on skis and reached farther north than anyone before. Three years later Fram came free near Svalbard, and every member of the expedition came home. From above, the frozen ocean still looks as it did.","w":"Over the Arctic Ocean, look for white pack ice broken by dark leads of open water, north of Siberia and Svalbard.","f":["Fram's rounded hull was designed so that the ice would lift it up instead of crushing it.","Nansen and Hjalmar Johansen reached 86°14′ N in April 1895.","Fram is preserved in its own museum in Oslo."],"q":{"q":"How far north did Nansen and Johansen reach?","a":"86°14′ N","x":["84°04′ N","90° N"]}},"amundsen-northwest-passage":{"name":"Amundsen's Northwest Passage (1903–1906)","era":"1903 – 1906","t":"The first ship through the Northwest Passage","s":"Sailors had long sought a sea route round the top of North America. In 1903 Roald Amundsen set out in Gjøa, a small herring boat, with six men. They spent two winters on King William Island, learning from the Netsilik Inuit how to live on the ice. In 1906 Gjøa reached Nome in Alaska, the first ship to sail through the passage. From above, look for the maze of Arctic islands and channels.","w":"Look for the maze of islands and ice-choked channels of the Canadian Arctic, north of the mainland coast.","f":["The harbour where they wintered is now the Inuit community of Gjoa Haven.","Amundsen skied about 800 km to a telegraph station in Alaska to send the news home.","Gjøa was only about 21 m long."],"q":{"q":"Where did Gjøa spend two winters?","a":"Gjoa Haven, King William Island","x":["Resolute Bay","Pond Inlet"]}},"amundsen-south-pole":{"name":"Amundsen's journey to the South Pole (1911)","era":"October 1911 – January 1912","t":"The first people at the South Pole","s":"In October 1911 Roald Amundsen and four companions set out from their hut on the Ross Ice Shelf with dog sledges. They climbed a great glacier through the Transantarctic Mountains to the polar plateau and reached the South Pole on 14 December 1911, the first people ever to do so. All five returned safely. From above, Antarctica is a blinding white plateau, with mountain ranges breaking through the ice.","w":"A blinding white plateau of ice, with dark peaks of the Transantarctic Mountains breaking through.","f":["Their base, Framheim, stood on the Ross Ice Shelf at the Bay of Whales.","They climbed to the plateau up the Axel Heiberg Glacier.","At the Pole they left a tent with a letter for the King of Norway."],"q":{"q":"When did Amundsen reach the South Pole?","a":"14 December 1911","x":["17 January 1912","6 April 1909"]}},"nordenskiold-vega":{"name":"Nordenskiöld's Vega expedition (1878–1879)","era":"1878 – 1880","t":"The first ship through the Northeast Passage","s":"In 1878 the Swedish-Finnish explorer Adolf Erik Nordenskiöld sailed the steamship Vega east along the Arctic coast of Siberia. It became the first ship to round Cape Chelyuskin, the northernmost point of Eurasia, and after being frozen in for ten months near the Bering Strait it passed into the Pacific in July 1879. Returning via Suez, Vega was the first ship to sail around Eurasia. From above, look for the Siberian coast and sea ice.","w":"Look for the long, low Arctic coast of Siberia, with sea ice offshore for much of the year.","f":["Vega rounded Cape Chelyuskin on 19 August 1878.","While frozen in, the crew got to know their Chukchi neighbours.","Stockholm still celebrates Vega Day on 24 April, the date of the ship's return in 1880."],"q":{"q":"Which cape was Vega the first ship to round?","a":"Cape Chelyuskin","x":["Cape Dezhnev","North Cape"]}},"norge-1926":{"name":"Airship Norge over the North Pole (1926)","era":"11–14 May 1926","t":"The first undisputed flight over the North Pole","s":"In May 1926 the airship Norge left Svalbard with 16 people aboard, among them Roald Amundsen, the American Lincoln Ellsworth and its Italian designer and pilot Umberto Nobile. It crossed the North Pole on 12 May and, after about 70 hours in the air, landed at Teller in Alaska. It was the first undisputed flight over the Pole and the first air crossing of the Arctic Ocean.","w":"Over the Arctic Ocean, look for pack ice to every horizon; at the ends, Svalbard's mountains and Alaska's coast.","f":["At the Pole the crew dropped the flags of Norway, the United States and Italy.","Its mooring mast still stands at Ny-Ålesund in Svalbard.","Nobile's small dog, Titina, flew with them."],"q":{"q":"Who designed and piloted the airship Norge?","a":"Umberto Nobile","x":["Ferdinand von Zeppelin","Richard Byrd"]}},"chkalov-1937":{"name":"Chkalov's transpolar flight (1937)","era":"18–20 June 1937","t":"Moscow to America over the top of the world","s":"In June 1937 Valery Chkalov, Georgy Baidukov and Alexander Belyakov flew a single-engined ANT-25 non-stop from Moscow over the North Pole to North America. After 63 hours, much of it in cloud and icing, they landed at Pearson Field in Vancouver, Washington. It was the first non-stop flight between Europe and America across the pole, pioneering the kind of polar route many flights use today. From above, look for pack ice, then Canada's Arctic.","w":"Over the Arctic, look for pack ice, then the islands of the Canadian Arctic and the coastal mountains of the Pacific North-West.","f":["They had aimed for San Francisco but landed early, short of fuel.","The flight covered roughly 9,000 km.","A monument to the flight stands in Vancouver, Washington."],"q":{"q":"Where did Chkalov's crew land in 1937?","a":"Vancouver, Washington","x":["San Francisco","Seattle"]}},"beagle-voyage":{"name":"Voyage of the Beagle (1831–1836)","era":"1831 – 1836","t":"The voyage that shaped Darwin's big idea","s":"In December 1831 HMS Beagle sailed from Plymouth to survey the coasts of South America, with a 22-year-old naturalist named Charles Darwin aboard. Over five years he collected fossils, birds and beetles, crossed the Andes on horseback and visited the Galápagos Islands, before the ship returned home round the world. What he saw led him to the theory of evolution by natural selection. From above, look for the long coasts of South America.","w":"Look for the long Atlantic and Pacific coasts of South America, and the maze of channels at Tierra del Fuego.","f":["Darwin published 'On the Origin of Species' in 1859, 23 years after the voyage ended.","The Beagle Channel at Tierra del Fuego is named after the ship.","Its captain, Robert FitzRoy, later issued some of the first public weather forecasts."],"q":{"q":"Who was the captain of HMS Beagle?","a":"Robert FitzRoy","x":["James Cook","William Bligh"]}},"magnetic-north-pole-1831":{"name":"North Magnetic Pole, first reached 1831","era":"1 June 1831","t":"Where compass needles pointed straight down","s":"On 1 June 1831 the British explorer James Clark Ross reached the North Magnetic Pole on the Boothia Peninsula, in the Canadian Arctic. There the needle of a dip compass pointed straight down. The pole does not stay put: it has wandered more than a thousand kilometres since, across the Arctic Ocean towards Siberia. From above, look for the Boothia Peninsula and the frozen channels around it.","w":"Look for the Boothia Peninsula, the northernmost tip of mainland North America, among frozen straits.","f":["Ross was the nephew of the expedition's leader, John Ross.","Amundsen measured the pole's position again from Gjoa Haven in 1904.","The magnetic pole is now much closer to Siberia than to Canada."],"q":{"q":"How far has the magnetic pole moved since 1831?","a":"More than 1,000 km","x":["About 10 km","About 100 km"]}}}}